  "2 James Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "2 James Gin's herbal-botanical edge meets at register with the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the juniper-led botanicals carry the halibut, and the floral-and-herb register brightens the plate. Strong; the gin option when the gold call is spoken for.",
  "2 James Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals sits alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "2 James Gin|Seafood Tower": "2 James with the seafood tower — Detroit local distillery against the raw bar. Excellent; 2 James is the most distinctive Michigan-pride tower pairing — the gin choice for guests who want something local and story-driven.",
  "2 James Gin|Seared Scallops": "2 James with seared scallops — Detroit local craft against scallop sweetness. Excellent; 2 James's American interpretation frames scallops with Midwest-craft character — less refined than Tanq 10's citrus-chamomile, more hometown than any British London Dry.",
  "2 James Gin|Seasonal Soup": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals leans against the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "2 James Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "2 James Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "2 James with shrimp bisque — Detroit local gin against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; 2 James cuts the bisque with American-craft character.",
  "2 James Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "2 James with shrimp cocktail — Detroit-distilled local gin against cold shrimp. Excellent; 2 James delivers the classic pairing with local-pride distinction — the answer when a guest asks for something Michigan.",
  "2 James Gin|Swordfish": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the botanicals lift the savory weight. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "2 James Gin|The Tomahawk": "2 James with the Tomahawk — Detroit local gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "2 James Gin|Tomato Basil": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the tomato basil's bright acid — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals lift the savory weight. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "2 James Gin|Truffle Fries": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "2 James Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals finds register with the tuna's seared rare crust — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "2 James Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Asparagus": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the asparagus, and the smoke threads the side. Works; JD Bonded's backbone handles the richness at Bonded register.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Au Gratin Potatoes": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with au gratin potatoes — rare craft release against cheese-and-cream. Works; boutique over-specified.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Beignets": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the malt-driven body wraps the beignets, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; boutique-blend depth gives composed dessert character.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Bone Marrow": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with bone marrow — rare craft release against umami fat. The boutique-release character meets marrow with literary-evocative authority. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Bone-In Filet": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's oak-spice character matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Broccoli Cheddar": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the scotch depth settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the oak softens the cream. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Broccolini": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the scotch depth settles into the broccolini, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; boutique character doesn't drive.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Brussels and Belly": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with Brussels + Belly — rare limited-release meets maple-glazed pork belly. The boutique-craft character cuts the glaze with rare-release distinctiveness. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Burrata": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with burrata — fresh dairy against rare limited-release. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Butternut Squash": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the scotch depth settles into the squash bisque, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; BIB structure is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Carrot Cake": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with carrot cake — rare limited-release against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; boutique-craft finds cake spice with rare-release character.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Cheesecake": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the scotch backbone matches the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Chilean Seabass": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. A Midnight's Winter Dram lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Chocolate Brownie": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the chocolate brownie — rare release against cocoa. Strong; boutique-craft meets the brownie at rare-release register.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Chocolate Cake": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the malt-and-oak threads the chocolate cake, and the malt edge frames the dessert. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Clam Chowder": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the malt-driven body wraps the chowder, and the oak softens the cream. Works; the aging is wasted on the salad.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Cowboy Ribeye": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the Cowboy Ribeye — rare limited-release against cap fat. The literary-evocative craft character handles the ribeye at boutique-release register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Crab Cake": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with crab cake — rare craft release against delicate crab. Avoid; save the rare bottle.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Creamed Spinach": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with creamed spinach — rare limited-release against dairy. Strong; boutique-craft sits with richness.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Creamy Potato": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the Highland register carries the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Creme Brulee": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch backbone matches the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; regional craft at matched register.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Escargot": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character rests at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the malt-and-oak threads the buttered escargot, and the heather brightens the starter. Strong; Fortuna frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Faroe Island Salmon": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the A Midnight's Winter Dram for the steak course.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Filet Mignon": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the filet — rare limited-release against the 10oz cut. Strong; boutique-craft is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|French Onion": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the malt-driven body wraps the French onion, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Grilled Caesar": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the grilled Caesar — rare release against charred romaine. Works; boutique-craft doesn't drive.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Gumbo": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the Highland register carries the gumbo, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; boutique-sourced wasted on salad.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Honey Roasted Carrots": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the scotch backbone matches the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; mid-proof pour is slightly over-specified.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|House Wedge": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the House Wedge — rare limited-release against iceberg and bleu. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Kansas City": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets A Midnight's Winter Dram — rare limited-release craft bourbon. Evocative literary name; boutique-brand distinctiveness. The strip gets a rare-release craft frame that stands out for bottle-story tableside. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Loaded Potato": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the scotch depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Lobster Mac": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the scotch depth settles into the lobster mac, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Market Fish": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. A Midnight's Winter Dram lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Mocha Creme": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the scotch depth settles into the mocha creme, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; premium proof overpowers this dish.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Mushroom Bisque": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the scotch backbone matches the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the umami. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Mushrooms": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the mushrooms — rare craft release against earthy umami. Strong; boutique-release layers into mushroom depth distinctively.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Peanut Butter Brownie": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the PB brownie — rare limited-release against peanut butter and cocoa. The boutique character gives the dessert rare-release craft depth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Porterhouse": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the porterhouse — rare craft release across strip and filet. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Prime Tartare": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's oak-spice character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Rainbow Trout": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the A Midnight's Winter Dram for the steak course.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Roast Half Chicken": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the malt-driven body wraps the roast chicken, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the plate. Works; extended-aged premium wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the scotch backbone matches the chickpea soup, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Salt-Cured Halibut": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. A Midnight's Winter Dram belongs on the steak course.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the scotch depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; GTS is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Seafood Tower": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the A Midnight's Winter Dram for the steak course.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Seared Scallops": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against rare release. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Seasonal Soup": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the seasonal soup, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; annual-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Seasonal Vegetables": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the scotch depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Shrimp Bisque": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the malt-and-oak threads the shrimp bisque, and the oak threads the cream. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Shrimp Cocktail": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against rare craft release. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Swordfish": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the A Midnight's Winter Dram for the steak course.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|The Tomahawk": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the Tomahawk — rare limited-release against bone-in ribeye. Strong; boutique-craft covers the cut with rare-release character.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Tomato Basil": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the tomato basil, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; the complex character gives the dessert a refined frame.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Truffle Fries": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with truffle fries — rare craft release against truffle-parmesan. The literary-brand character gives the side a boutique-release frame. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the A Midnight's Winter Dram on the steak course instead.",
  "A Midnight's Winter Dram|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the malt-driven body wraps the curry, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; the private select's layered profile underserves the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Aberlour 16|Asparagus": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the malt-and-oak threads the asparagus, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; GTS is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Aberlour 16|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Aberlour 16 with the au gratin — 16yr ex-bourbon + Oloroso Speyside against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Aberlour 16's balance handles the richness.",
  "Aberlour 16|Beignets": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon-plus-sherry depth wraps the beignets, and the balanced sweetness frames the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Aberlour 16|Bone Marrow": "Aberlour 16 with bone marrow — double-matured Speyside against umami fat. Strong; Aberlour 16's balanced bourbon-plus-sherry character sits alongside marrow; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Aberlour 16|Bone-In Filet": "Aberlour 16 with the bone-in filet — double-matured 16yr Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Aberlour 16's bourbon-plus-sherry balance frames the cut with approachable-premium character.",
  "Aberlour 16|Broccoli Cheddar": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the scotch backbone matches the broccoli cheddar, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; blend character covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Aberlour 16|Broccolini": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the malt-driven body wraps the broccolini, and the smoke threads the side. Works; wine-cask over-specified.",
  "Aberlour 16|Brussels and Belly": "Aberlour 16 with Brussels + Belly — 16yr double-matured Speyside against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; Aberlour 16's balance meets the glaze; Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask is the featured match.",
  "Aberlour 16|Burrata": "Aberlour 16 with burrata — ex-bourbon + Oloroso Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Aberlour 16|Butternut Squash": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the malt-driven body wraps the squash bisque, and the oak threads the cream. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "Aberlour 16|Carrot Cake": "Aberlour 16 with carrot cake — 16yr double-matured Speyside against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Aberlour 16's sherry-cask raisin-and-spice marries the cake naturally at balanced-premium register.",
  "Aberlour 16|Cheesecake": "Aberlour 16 with cheesecake — ex-bourbon + Oloroso Speyside against custard. Strong; Aberlour 16's balance threads the cheesecake at approachable-premium register.",
  "Aberlour 16|Chilean Seabass": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Aberlour 16 on the steak course instead.",
  "Aberlour 16|Chocolate Brownie": "Aberlour 16 with the chocolate brownie — double-matured Speyside against cocoa. Excellent; Aberlour 16's sherry-side dried-fruit marries cocoa at balanced-premium register.",
  "Aberlour 16|Chocolate Cake": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the malt-driven body wraps the chocolate cake, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; gentle match.",
  "Aberlour 16|Clam Chowder": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the malt-and-oak threads the chowder, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; annual-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Aberlour 16|Cowboy Ribeye": "Aberlour 16 with the cowboy ribeye — 16yr double-matured Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Aberlour 16 frames the ribeye with balanced depth; Balvenie 21 Portwood is the featured ribeye match.",
  "Aberlour 16|Crab Cake": "Aberlour 16 with crab cake — double-matured Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Aberlour 16|Creamed Spinach": "Aberlour 16 with creamed spinach — double-matured Speyside against dairy-rich side. Works; Aberlour 16's bourbon-side vanilla rides the cream.",
  "Aberlour 16|Creamy Potato": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the malt-driven body wraps the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Aberlour 16|Creme Brulee": "Aberlour 16 with creme brulee — double-matured 16yr Speyside against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; Aberlour 16's balance supports the brulee; Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-caramel is the featured brulee match.",
  "Aberlour 16|Escargot": "Aberlour 16 -- double-matured Speyside, bourbon-plus-sherry, balanced. The bourbon-plus-sherry depth wraps the buttered escargot, and the balanced sweetness frames the starter. Strong; the curated single-barrel is capable but single-distillery aged bourbons bring more traditional focused character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Aberlour 16|Faroe Island Salmon": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Aberlour 16 for the steak course.",
  "Aberlour 16|Filet Mignon": "Aberlour 16 with the filet — 16yr Speyside double-matured in ex-bourbon and Oloroso sherry casks, balanced premium approachable-16yr character. Excellent; Aberlour 16's double-matured balance frames tenderloin with both vanilla-bourbon and dried-fruit-sherry notes — the most balanced Sherried Speyside-filet option, where Macallans go pure-sherry and Caribbean Cask goes pure-rum.",
  "Aberlour 16|French Onion": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the malt-and-oak threads the French onion, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; Fortuna handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Aberlour 16|Grilled Caesar": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance sits alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the bourbon-plus-sherry depth wraps the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Aberlour 16|Gumbo": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the malt-driven body wraps the gumbo, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; the richer pour finds more with the dessert than Original brings.",
  "Aberlour 16|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Highland register carries the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; own-plus-sourced covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Aberlour 16|House Wedge": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the scotch backbone matches the wedge, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; Prohibition narrative wasted on salad.",
  "Aberlour 16|Kansas City": "Aberlour 16 with the KC — double-matured 16yr Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Aberlour 16 handles the strip with balanced character; Macallan 18 and Balvenie 21 Portwood are the featured Sherried Speyside-KC matches.",
  "Aberlour 16|Loaded Potato": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the scotch backbone matches the loaded potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the blend's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Aberlour 16|Lobster Mac": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the scotch backbone matches the lobster mac, and the oak softens the cream. Works; sweet character distinctive but not driving.",
  "Aberlour 16|Market Fish": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance flattens the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Aberlour 16 on the steak course instead.",
  "Aberlour 16|Mocha Creme": "Aberlour 16 with mocha creme — double-matured Speyside against coffee-chocolate dessert. Works; Aberlour 16's sherry-side threads the mocha; Balvenie 21 Portwood is the featured mocha match.",
  "Aberlour 16|Mushroom Bisque": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the malt-and-oak threads the mushroom bisque, and the oak depth meets the umami. Works; single-barrel depth gives the dessert more than Small Batch.",
  "Aberlour 16|Mushrooms": "Aberlour 16 with mushrooms — 16yr double-matured Speyside against earthy umami. Excellent; Aberlour 16's sherry-and-bourbon balance threads mushroom earthiness with both dimensions.",
  "Aberlour 16|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet 16yr double-matured Speyside. Strong; Aberlour 16 handles the dessert density at balanced-premium register; Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-cask brown-sugar is the featured PB-brownie match.",
  "Aberlour 16|Porterhouse": "Aberlour 16 with the porterhouse — 16yr double-matured Speyside against strip-and-filet. Works; Aberlour 16's balance frames the two-cut plate at approachable-premium register.",
  "Aberlour 16|Prime Tartare": "Aberlour 16's chocolate-cocoa character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Aberlour 16|Rainbow Trout": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Aberlour 16 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Aberlour 16|Roast Half Chicken": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the malt-and-oak threads the roast chicken, and the heather brightens the plate. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for the side — save the rare-cask bottle for the protein course.",
  "Aberlour 16|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the malt-and-oak threads the chickpea soup, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; the premium pour is wasted on the salad's simplicity.",
  "Aberlour 16|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Aberlour 16 for the steak course.",
  "Aberlour 16|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the malt-and-oak threads the garlic spinach, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Aberlour 16|Seafood Tower": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Aberlour 16 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Aberlour 16|Seared Scallops": "Aberlour 16 with seared scallops — 16yr double-matured Speyside against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Aberlour 16|Seasonal Soup": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the Highland register carries the seasonal soup, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; boutique at matched register.",
  "Aberlour 16|Seasonal Vegetables": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the scotch backbone matches the seasonal vegetables, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the side. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side; save the rare bottle for the main.",
  "Aberlour 16|Shrimp Bisque": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance leans against the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the bourbon-plus-sherry depth wraps the shrimp bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the intensity overpowers this dish.",
  "Aberlour 16|Shrimp Cocktail": "Aberlour 16 with shrimp cocktail — double-matured Speyside against cold shrimp. Avoid; Scotch wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Aberlour 16|Swordfish": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Aberlour 16 for the steak course.",
  "Aberlour 16|The Tomahawk": "Aberlour 16's chocolate-cocoa character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Aberlour 16|Tomato Basil": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the Highland register carries the tomato basil, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; Caribbean Cask frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Aberlour 16|Truffle Fries": "Aberlour 16 with truffle fries — ex-bourbon + Oloroso sherry Speyside against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Aberlour 16's double-matured character meets the earthy-rich with balanced depth.",
  "Aberlour 16|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Aberlour 16 for the steak course.",
  "Aberlour 16|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the scotch depth settles into the curry, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Asparagus": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the aged-agave weight wraps the asparagus, and the caramel-agave register brightens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the au gratin, and the oak threads the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Beignets": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- aged tequila has nothing to bridge into dessert. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Adictivo Cristalino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Bone Marrow": "Adictivo Cristalino with bone marrow — filtered aged against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms cristalino despite its aged depth.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Bone-In Filet": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the añejo register carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the caramel-agave register brightens the cut. Works; brightness can cut the glaze but wheated bourbons sit better with this dish.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Broccoli Cheddar": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the oak-aged tequila body threads the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; refinement is underserved by the dish density.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Broccolini": "Adictivo Cristalino's rested-agave body leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the barrel-rested body handles the broccolini, and the oak threads the side. Works; ceremonial pour over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Brussels and Belly": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the oak-aged tequila body threads the pork-belly plate, and the soft-spice note softens the side. Works; brightness can cut the glaze but wheated bourbons sit better with this dish.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Burrata": "Adictivo Cristalino with burrata — filtered cristalino against fresh mozzarella. The aged-but-clear distinctive character lifts the burrata with craft depth. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Butternut Squash": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak-aged tequila body threads the squash bisque, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; single-estate character handles richness at craft register.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Carrot Cake": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- aged tequila has nothing to bridge into dessert. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Adictivo Cristalino for the steak course.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Cheesecake": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- añejo weight and custard tang refuse each other. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Adictivo Cristalino on the steak course instead.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Chilean Seabass": "Adictivo Cristalino's rested-agave body sits alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the barrel-rested body handles the seabass, and the oak threads the plate. Strong; modern cleanness is capable but the starter earns a more character-forward reposado.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Chocolate Brownie": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- aged-agave register fights chocolate sweetness without bridging. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Adictivo Cristalino on the steak course instead.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Chocolate Cake": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- aged tequila has nothing to bridge into dessert. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Adictivo Cristalino for the steak course.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Clam Chowder": "Adictivo Cristalino's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the barrel-rested body handles the chowder, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Cowboy Ribeye": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- this expression lands short of the cut's register. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Adictivo Cristalino for another course.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Crab Cake": "Crab Cake: lump crab meets Adictivo Cristalino — filtered cristalino, clear liquid carrying aged character (reposado/añejo filtered to blanco appearance). Distinctive in the blanco cluster; technically aged but presents clear. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Creamed Spinach": "Adictivo Cristalino's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the rested-agave depth sits with the creamed spinach, and the caramel-agave register brightens the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Creamy Potato": "Adictivo Cristalino's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the potato soup, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Creme Brulee": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- añejo weight and custard tang refuse each other. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Adictivo Cristalino on the steak course instead.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Escargot": "Adictivo Cristalino with escargot — filtered aged against garlic-butter snails. Strong; cristalino depth cuts the garlic-butter with craft-theatrical distinctiveness.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Faroe Island Salmon": "Adictivo Cristalino with the salmon — filtered aged against salmon's richness. Strong; aged-but-clear frames the fish with distinctive craft character.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Filet Mignon": "Adictivo Cristalino with the filet — filtered aged tequila against the 10oz cut. Strong; the cristalino's aged-but-clear depth gives the filet more than standard blancos can — where Don Julio Blanco works at best, Adictivo Cristalino earns Strong.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|French Onion": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the aged-agave weight wraps the French onion, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; the 70th's clarity gets overwhelmed by the dish's density.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Grilled Caesar": "Adictivo Cristalino with the grilled Caesar — filtered cristalino against charred romaine. Strong; the aged-but-clear character earns the Caesar with distinctive craft depth.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Gumbo": "Adictivo Cristalino's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-aged tequila body threads the gumbo, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Adictivo Cristalino's rested-agave body reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the aged-agave weight wraps the honey carrots, and the cooked-agave note underlines the side. Works; ceremonial depth is composed with the dessert.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|House Wedge": "Adictivo Cristalino with the House Wedge — filtered aged against iceberg and bleu. The distinctive cristalino character cuts the bleu with aged depth blanco can't normally deliver. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Kansas City": "Adictivo Cristalino's rested-agave body sits alongside the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the oak-aged tequila body threads the strip steak, and the oak threads the cut. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Loaded Potato": "Adictivo Cristalino's rested-agave body sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Lobster Mac": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak-aged tequila body threads the lobster mac, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the side. Works; the pour is a touch over-specified for the side.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Market Fish": "Adictivo Cristalino with the market fish — filtered cristalino against catch of the day. Works; distinctive craft character lifts the fish depending on preparation.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Mocha Creme": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- aged-agave register fights chocolate sweetness without bridging. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Adictivo Cristalino for the steak course.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Mushroom Bisque": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the aged-agave weight wraps the mushroom bisque, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Mushrooms": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami weight: the rested-agave depth sits with the mushrooms, and the cooked-agave note underlines the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- aged tequila has nothing to bridge into dessert. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Adictivo Cristalino on the steak course instead.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Porterhouse": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the oak-aged tequila body threads the dual-cut steak, and the oak threads the cut. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Adictivo Cristalino|Prime Tartare": "Adictivo Cristalino with the tartare — filtered aged tequila against raw beef. The aged-but-clear character is a distinctive craft counterpoint to tartare's mineral depth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
