  "Adictivo Reposado|Rainbow Trout": "Adictivo Reposado's aged-agave character finds neutral with the trout's delicate flesh: the aged-agave weight wraps the trout, and the oak threads the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Roast Half Chicken": "Adictivo Reposado with the roast half chicken — aged reposado against roasted chicken. Works; the rich oak is slightly over-specified for the dish.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Adictivo Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak-aged tequila body threads the chickpea soup, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Adictivo Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the añejo register carries the halibut, and the caramel-agave register brightens the plate. Works; iconic extra añejo is composed with the dessert.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Adictivo Reposado's aged-agave character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the añejo register carries the garlic spinach, and the caramel-agave register brightens the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Seafood Tower": "Adictivo Reposado's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the rested-agave depth sits with the seafood tower, and the cooked-agave note underlines the starter. Works; multi-cask complexity composed with this dish at distinctive register.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Seared Scallops": "Adictivo Reposado with seared scallops — near-añejo reposado against caramelized scallop. The aged depth meets the sear with more conviction than younger reposados bring. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Seasonal Soup": "Adictivo Reposado's añejo-pour depth leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the oak-aged tequila body threads the seasonal soup, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; ceremonial over-specified.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Seasonal Vegetables": "Adictivo Reposado's rested-agave body leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rested-agave depth sits with the seasonal vegetables, and the cooked-agave note underlines the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Shrimp Bisque": "Adictivo Reposado with shrimp bisque — extended-aged reposado against cream-based seafood. Strong; the aged depth sits with the soup's richness.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Shrimp Cocktail": "Adictivo Reposado with shrimp cocktail — long-aged reposado against cold shrimp. The aged character gives the cocktail a weightier counterpoint than standard reposados bring. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Swordfish": "Adictivo Reposado's aged-agave character leans against the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the añejo register carries the swordfish, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the plate. Works; brightness can cut the glaze but wheated bourbons sit better with this dish.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|The Tomahawk": "Adictivo Reposado with the Tomahawk — extended-aged reposado against massive cut. Avoid; even near-añejo is bourbon-or-scotch territory here.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Tomato Basil": "Adictivo Reposado's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the añejo register carries the tomato basil, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Truffle Fries": "Adictivo Reposado with truffle fries — long-aged reposado against truffle-parmesan. Works; the extended maturation gives the side more than standard reposados bring, though not a standout.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Adictivo Reposado's rested-agave body sits alongside the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the oak-aged tequila body threads the tuna, and the caramel-agave register brightens the plate. Works; distinctive-bottle over-specified.",
  "Adictivo Reposado|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Adictivo Reposado's añejo-pour depth leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the añejo register carries the curry, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; accessible aged composes with the plate.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Asparagus": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Beignets": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Alexander Valley Chardonnay lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Bone Marrow": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Bone-In Filet": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Alexander Valley Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Broccoli Cheddar": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight aligns with the soup's cheddar-cream richness and the apple-pear lifts the broccoli base. Peak accessible Chardonnay for broccoli cheddar — California oak on the classic. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Broccolini": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Brussels and Belly": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity reads alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Burrata": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth texture. The oak-vanilla weight meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the apple-pear fruit plays against basil-tomato. Strong California Chard for burrata — accessible oak weight on the delicate starter.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Butternut Squash": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The apple-pear mirrors butternut's autumn fruit character and the oak-vanilla plays against caramelized squash. Strong accessible Chardonnay for butternut squash — California oak on the classic.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Carrot Cake": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Alexander Valley Chardonnay on the steak course instead.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Cheesecake": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Alexander Valley Chardonnay on the steak course instead.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Chilean Seabass": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight aligns with seabass's buttery flesh and the apple-pear lifts the rich texture. Peak accessible Chardonnay for Chilean seabass — California oak on the buttery classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Chocolate Brownie": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Alexander Valley Chardonnay lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Chocolate Cake": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a white wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Alexander Valley Chardonnay lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Clam Chowder": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight aligns with the chowder's cream weight and the apple-pear lifts the clam-potato base. Strong accessible Chardonnay for clam chowder — California oak on the classic.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Cowboy Ribeye": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character falls short of the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- crisp white reads thin against 26+ ounces of cap-fat ribeye. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Alexander Valley Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Crab Cake": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced. The oak rounds the pairing against crab's sweet meat and the apple-pear fruit plays cleanly against romesco. Strong accessible Chardonnay for crab cake — approachable California weight on the classic starter.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Creamed Spinach": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Creamy Potato": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight aligns with the soup's cream-potato richness and the apple-pear lifts the dairy base. Strong accessible Chardonnay for creamy potato — California oak on the classic.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Creme Brulee": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Alexander Valley Chardonnay on the steak course instead.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Escargot": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay — Sonoma with ripe apple, pear, vanilla oak, smooth texture. The oak-vanilla weight cuts escargot's garlic-parsley butter and the apple-pear fruit lifts the snail richness. Strong California Chardonnay for escargot — accessible pairing on the French classic.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Faroe Island Salmon": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight handles salmon's oil-rich flesh and the apple-pear fruit plays against the flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak Chardonnay for salmon across most seasonal builds. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Filet Mignon": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay — Sonoma with ripe apple, pear, vanilla oak. The oak body can handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the vanilla echoes escargot-butter toppers. Works as accessible white-with-steak when guest wants Chardonnay over Cab; classic reds peak higher for filet.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|French Onion": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Grilled Caesar": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, vanilla oak, smooth balanced. The oak weight handles anchovy-parmesan dressing and the apple-pear plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as accessible California Chard for grilled Caesar — approachable weight on the layered salad.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Gumbo": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The apple-pear plays against carrots' honey-glaze sweetness and the oak-vanilla weight handles the roasted char. Strong accessible Chardonnay for honey-roasted carrots — California oak on the sweet-root classic.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|House Wedge": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak body cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the apple-pear fruit plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong accessible pairing for the wedge — California Chard reframes the classic steakhouse salad.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Kansas City": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Alexander Valley Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Loaded Potato": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight aligns with the soup's cheddar-bacon richness and the apple-pear lifts the loaded base. Strong accessible Chardonnay for loaded potato — California oak on the classic.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Lobster Mac": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay — Sonoma, ripe apple, pear, vanilla oak, smooth texture. The oak weight aligns with lobster-mac's cream-cheese richness and the apple-pear fruit cuts against the dense pasta. Peak accessible Chardonnay for the lobster mac side — California oak meets indulgence. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Market Fish": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity reads alongside the fish's clean flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Mocha Creme": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a white wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Alexander Valley Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Mushroom Bisque": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight aligns with the bisque's cream-mushroom richness and the apple-pear lifts the umami base. Strong accessible Chardonnay for the bisque — California oak on the earthy classic.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Mushrooms": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Alexander Valley Chardonnay belongs on the steak course.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Porterhouse": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- white wine can't stand up to the marbled char — the cut earns a structured red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Alexander Valley Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Prime Tartare": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity reads alongside the tartare's raw beef and brine — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Rainbow Trout": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity holds the line with the trout's delicate flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Roast Half Chicken": "* Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character runs straight into the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bright acid lifts the roast chicken, and the chalky minerality plays against the plate. Peak Napa Chardonnay for chicken across most seasonal accompaniments. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the caramel rounds the earthy plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay -- Sonoma with ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth texture. The bright acid lifts the halibut, and the citrus cuts the plate. Strong; the call reads at register without overshooting.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak. The oak-vanilla weight cuts the sauté butter and the apple-pear fruit plays against garlic without clashing with the spinach's earth. Works as accessible California Chardonnay for the sauté side — oak rounds the pairing.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Seafood Tower": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay -- Sonoma with ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth texture. The bright acid lifts the seafood tower, and the citrus cuts the starter. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Seared Scallops": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth texture. The oak-vanilla frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness without overpowering and the apple-pear adds lift against the pan-sear. Strong Chardonnay for scallops — oak-vanilla echoes the pan-butter beautifully.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Seasonal Soup": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity sits alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Seasonal Vegetables": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity finds neutral with the seasonal garden plate — the crisp acidity sits with the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Shrimp Bisque": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced. The oak-vanilla texture aligns with the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and the apple-pear fruit lifts the butter-roux weight. Peak approachable Chardonnay for bisque — California oak on California-style soup. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Shrimp Cocktail": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity finds register with the shrimp's chilled sweetness — the crisp acidity lifts the shellfish without weight, and the juniper carries the shellfish. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Swordfish": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight aligns with swordfish's meaty flesh and the apple-pear lifts the grilled char. Strong accessible Chardonnay for swordfish — California oak on the grilled classic.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|The Tomahawk": "Sonoma Chardonnay against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even oaked California Chard has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Alexander Valley for salmon, chicken, lobster mac, or the bisque course.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Tomato Basil": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity reads alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Truffle Fries": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity stays alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay -- Sonoma with ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth texture. The bright acid lifts the tuna, and the citrus cuts the plate. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Alexander Valley Chardonnay|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity leans against the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Alexander Valley Cyrus|Asparagus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Alexander Valley Cyrus|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Alexander Valley Cyrus|Beignets": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Alexander Valley Cyrus belongs on the steak course.",
  "Alexander Valley Cyrus|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Alexander Valley Cyrus|Bone-In Filet": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the velvety body lifts the lean cut, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Alexander Valley Cyrus|Broccoli Cheddar": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Alexander Valley Cyrus|Broccolini": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Alexander Valley Cyrus|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Alexander Valley Cyrus|Burrata": "Sonoma Meritage against burrata's creamy-delicate center — full-body Bordeaux-style structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Cyrus for the steak course.",
  "Alexander Valley Cyrus|Butternut Squash": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Alexander Valley Cyrus|Carrot Cake": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Alexander Valley Cyrus lands on the steak course — not here.",
