  "Alexander Valley Homestead Red|Shrimp Bisque": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin sits alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Alexander Valley Homestead Red|Shrimp Cocktail": "Sonoma Bordeaux blend against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — red wine and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Homestead Red for the steak course.",
  "Alexander Valley Homestead Red|Swordfish": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Alexander Valley Homestead Red on the steak course instead.",
  "Alexander Valley Homestead Red|The Tomahawk": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Alexander Valley Homestead Red|Tomato Basil": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin stays alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cassis frames the herb butter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Alexander Valley Homestead Red|Truffle Fries": "Alexander Valley Sonoma Bordeaux blend — plum, blackberry, mocha, soft tannin, smooth. The soft tannin cuts truffle-fry potato fat and the mocha plays against the truffle-oil umami. Strong crowd-pleaser Bordeaux blend for truffle fries — Sonoma softness on the umami hit.",
  "Alexander Valley Homestead Red|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Alexander Valley Homestead Red for the steak course.",
  "Alexander Valley Homestead Red|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Asparagus": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness doubles down on the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Amaro Nonino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Beignets": "Amaro Nonino with beignets — Friulian amaro against fried dough and powdered sugar. Works; the botanical complexity adds refinement to the casual dessert.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Bone Marrow": "Amaro Nonino with bone marrow — grappa-based amaro against umami fat. Strong; the Friulian botanicals cut through the marrow's richness as a post-bite digestif.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Bone-In Filet": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- bitter botanicals on the cut fight the savory char without payoff. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Amaro Nonino belongs on another course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Broccoli Cheddar": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Amaro Nonino on the steak course instead.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Broccolini": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Amaro Nonino on the steak course instead.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet grappa-based amaro. Works; Friulian amaro cuts the glaze's sweetness with digestif brightness.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Burrata": "Amaro Nonino with burrata — amaro against fresh dairy. Avoid; botanical bitterness doesn't find burrata.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Butternut Squash": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Amaro Nonino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Carrot Cake": "Amaro Nonino with carrot cake — Friulian amaro against cream cheese and cinnamon. The grappa-based elegance and botanical notes find the cake's raisins cleanly. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Cheesecake": "Amaro Nonino with cheesecake — elegant Friulian amaro against custard. The grappa-based structure and orange peel thread cleanly through the cheesecake's richness; premium digestif. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Chilean Seabass": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Amaro Nonino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Chocolate Brownie": "Amaro Nonino with the chocolate brownie — grappa-based Friulian amaro, elegant and lighter than Averna. Orange peel and botanical complexity against cocoa; the grappa base gives the digestif more structure than sweeter amari bring to dessert. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Chocolate Cake": "Amaro Nonino -- orange peel, grappa-base, bitter herb. The bitter herb edge cuts the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; the pairing lands at register without strain.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Clam Chowder": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness fights the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- bitter herbs cut the bisque's round cream without lifting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Amaro Nonino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Cowboy Ribeye": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Amaro Nonino on another course instead.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Crab Cake": "Amaro Nonino with crab cake — grappa-based amaro against delicate crab. Avoid; amari aren't built for seafood.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Creamed Spinach": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Amaro Nonino on the steak course instead.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Creamy Potato": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Amaro Nonino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Creme Brulee": "Amaro Nonino with creme brulee — grappa-based amaro against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. The orange peel and botanical complexity echo the brulee's caramel; refined digestif pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Escargot": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness doubles down on the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- aperitivo herbs and a herb-bright opener compound the bitter register — no contrast. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Amaro Nonino on the steak course instead.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Faroe Island Salmon": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bitter herbs on oily fish read metallic — the rich flesh deserves a crisp white. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Filet Mignon": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- amaro bitterness flattens the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Amaro Nonino for another course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|French Onion": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness jars against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Grilled Caesar": "Amaro Nonino with the grilled Caesar — Friulian amaro against charred romaine. Strong; botanical complexity complements the Caesar's anchovy-and-char flavors.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Gumbo": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- aperitivo bitterness and the glaze's sugar refuse each other — bitter wins, dessert reads cloying. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Amaro Nonino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Amaro Nonino|House Wedge": "Amaro Nonino with the House Wedge — elegant amaro against iceberg and bleu. Works; the botanical complexity is a post-salad digestif option.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Kansas City": "Amaro Nonino with the KC — elegant amaro as post-steak digestif. Strong; the grappa-base and orange peel settle the meal after the strip.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Loaded Potato": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Lobster Mac": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Amaro Nonino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Market Fish": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- aperitivo bitterness fights gentle flesh without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Mocha Creme": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness holds steady against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the bitter herb edge cuts dessert sweetness, and the orange peel echoes the chocolate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Mushroom Bisque": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Amaro Nonino on the steak course instead.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Mushrooms": "Amaro Nonino with the mushrooms — grappa-based amaro against earthy umami. Strong; the botanicals layer into mushroom depth at digestif register.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet Friulian grappa-based amaro. Orange-peel and botanical complexity lift the dessert with elegant digestif character. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Porterhouse": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Amaro Nonino for another course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Prime Tartare": "Amaro Nonino with the tartare — Friulian amaro against raw beef. Strong; the grappa-base and orange peel lift the tartare's mineral notes.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Rainbow Trout": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- aperitivo bitterness fights gentle flesh without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Amaro Nonino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Roast Half Chicken": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness overwhelms the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- amaro bitterness buries the chicken's mild herbed-skin frame. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Amaro Nonino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bitter herbs flatten delicate fish — the protein can't lift the amaro's register. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Amaro Nonino on the steak course instead.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness doubles down on the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Seafood Tower": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- amaro bitterness on cold shellfish flattens the delicate brine — needs sparkling instead. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Amaro Nonino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Seared Scallops": "Amaro Nonino with seared scallops — Friulian amaro against scallop delicacy. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Seasonal Soup": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Seasonal Vegetables": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness doubles down on the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Shrimp Bisque": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Amaro Nonino on the steak course instead.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Shrimp Cocktail": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- amaro bitterness on cold shellfish flattens the delicate brine — needs sparkling instead. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Amaro Nonino on the steak course instead.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Swordfish": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bitter botanicals fight the savory sear — bitter against char without resolution. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|The Tomahawk": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Amaro Nonino belongs on another course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Tomato Basil": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Amaro Nonino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Truffle Fries": "Amaro Nonino with truffle fries — elegant amaro against truffle-parmesan. Works; grappa-based botanicals are a gentle counterpoint to the side.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Amaro Nonino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Amaro Nonino|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Amaro Nonino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Asparagus": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Ancho Reyes on the steak course instead.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Beignets": "Beignets: sugar-dusted pastry meets Ancho Reyes. Excellent; smoky-sweet-chile plays with sugar-dust at Mexican register.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Bone Marrow": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Ancho Reyes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Bone-In Filet": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Ancho Reyes lands on another course — not here.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Broccoli Cheddar": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Ancho Reyes on the steak course instead.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Broccolini": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Brussels and Belly": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness plays cleanly against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Burrata": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur and fresh dairy compound the richness without contrast — heavy too early. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Butternut Squash": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Ancho Reyes. Works; chile-sweet-smoke amplifies cake spice with heat-dimension at Mexican register.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Cheesecake": "Ancho Reyes: Mexican chile liqueur from Puebla, 40% ABV, late-harvest ancho poblano chiles — smoky-sweet-spicy. Excellent on cheesecake; the chile-heat-smoky-sweet character brings unexpected dimension to cheesecake — bolder pairing, chile-meets-cream at Mexican register.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Chilean Seabass": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert-grade sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs a crisp white instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Ancho Reyes. Excellent; chile-and-chocolate is a textbook Mexican pairing — Ancho Reyes brings ancho-poblano smoky-sweet-spice to the brownie at 40% register.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Chocolate Cake": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the digestif body mirrors the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Clam Chowder": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Ancho Reyes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Cowboy Ribeye": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character flattens the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Ancho Reyes on another course instead.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Crab Cake": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character smothers the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sugar buries the delicate shellfish without any acid to balance. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Ancho Reyes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Creamed Spinach": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Creamy Potato": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Creme Brulee": "Creme Brulee: caramelized custard meets Ancho Reyes. Excellent; the smoky-sweet-chile character plays with burnt-sugar-cream at Mexican-liqueur register — the chile-dessert classic.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Escargot": "Escargot: garlic-herb butter meets Ancho Reyes. Excellent; the chile-heat brings unusual dimension to garlic-herb butter at Mexican-liqueur register — unexpected but earns its place through spice.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Faroe Island Salmon": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Ancho Reyes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Filet Mignon": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness plays cleanly against the filet's tender lean cut — the sweet character meets the cut at digestif register. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Ancho Reyes|French Onion": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: char-romaine with anchovy-parmesan meets Ancho Reyes. Excellent; the smoky-sweet-chile character doubles with grill-char at Mexican-liqueur register — smoke meets smoke, heat meets char.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Gumbo": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Ancho Reyes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sweet liqueur and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no contrast left. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Ancho Reyes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|House Wedge": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness holds steady against the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the agave smoke frames the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Kansas City": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness finds neutral with the strip's lean char — the sweet character meets the cut at digestif register. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Loaded Potato": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Ancho Reyes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Lobster Mac": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Market Fish": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- sugar on delicate fish is wrong direction — the plate needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Mocha Creme": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness holds steady against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the smoke crosses the cocoa. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Mushroom Bisque": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Ancho Reyes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Mushrooms": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness matches the mushrooms' earthy umami — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness matches the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the smoke crosses the cocoa. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Porterhouse": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Ancho Reyes on another course instead.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Prime Tartare": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the sweet character meets the savory plate at digestif register. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Rainbow Trout": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Roast Half Chicken": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character buries the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- sugar smothers the chicken's savory frame — wait for dessert. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Ancho Reyes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Ancho Reyes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Seafood Tower": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Ancho Reyes on the steak course instead.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Ancho Reyes. Avoid; chile-liqueur overwhelms scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Seasonal Soup": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Ancho Reyes on the steak course instead.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Seasonal Vegetables": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Shrimp Bisque": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness sits with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the smoke wraps the briny shellfish. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Shrimp Cocktail": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Swordfish": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sugar dulls the crust's clean char instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|The Tomahawk": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character flattens the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Ancho Reyes lands on another course — not here.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Tomato Basil": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Ancho Reyes on the steak course instead.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Truffle Fries": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character flattens the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- sugar on starch is dessert territory — wrong course for the supporting plate. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Ancho Reyes|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Ancho Reyes on the steak course instead.",
  "Angel's Envy Cask Strength|Asparagus": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the bourbon's register is wrong for a clean vegetable side — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Angel's Envy Cask Strength for the steak course.",
