  "Angel's Envy|Seafood Tower": "Angel's Envy's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Angel's Envy lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Angel's Envy|Seared Scallops": "Angel's Envy with seared scallops — flagship AE port-finish against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops.",
  "Angel's Envy|Seasonal Soup": "Angel's Envy's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Angel's Envy|Seasonal Vegetables": "Angel's Envy's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon weight handles the seasonal vegetables, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Angel's Envy|Shrimp Bisque": "Angel's Envy with shrimp bisque — port-finished bourbon against cream-shrimp soup. Avoid; port-fruit clashes with seafood soup.",
  "Angel's Envy|Shrimp Cocktail": "Angel's Envy with shrimp cocktail — port-finished Louisville against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Angel's Envy|Swordfish": "Angel's Envy's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Angel's Envy belongs on the steak course.",
  "Angel's Envy|The Tomahawk": "Angel's Envy's crisp vanilla character lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Angel's Envy|Tomato Basil": "Angel's Envy's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the oak depth lays under the tomato basil, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Angel's Envy|Truffle Fries": "Angel's Envy with truffle fries — port-finished Louisville bourbon against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; AE's softer-oak + port-fruit meets the earthy-rich with wine-finish distinction.",
  "Angel's Envy|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Angel's Envy's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Angel's Envy on the steak course instead.",
  "Angel's Envy|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Angel's Envy's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon body sits with the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; spice over-specified for side's character.",
  "Aperol|Asparagus": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Aperol on the steak course instead.",
  "Aperol|Beignets": "Aperol with beignets — soft aperitivo against fried dough. Works; Aperol's gentle character doesn't overwhelm the casual sweet but doesn't feature.",
  "Aperol|Bone Marrow": "Aperol with bone marrow — 11% soft bitter against umami fat. Avoid; Aperol is far too light to cut marrow richness — wrong tool. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Aperol|Bone-In Filet": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Aperol for another course.",
  "Aperol|Broccoli Cheddar": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Broccolini": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge doubles down on the broccolini's tender green stalks -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Brussels and Belly": "Aperol with Brussels + Belly — soft orange-rhubarb against maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; Aperol's orange meets the glaze's sweetness rather than cutting it.",
  "Aperol|Burrata": "Aperol with burrata — soft orange-rhubarb against fresh dairy. Excellent; this is the one amaro pairing where burrata lands — Aperol's 11% softness threads the cream where every other amaro in the family is too aggressive. Classic aperitivo move.",
  "Aperol|Butternut Squash": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Carrot Cake": "Aperol with carrot cake — soft orange-rhubarb against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Aperol's orange marries the cinnamon at a brunch-register that works cleaner than the heavier amari.",
  "Aperol|Cheesecake": "Aperol with cheesecake — 11% soft bitter against custard. Strong; the gentle orange-rhubarb threads the cheesecake with brunch-dessert character.",
  "Aperol|Chilean Seabass": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Aperol on the steak course instead.",
  "Aperol|Chocolate Brownie": "Aperol with the chocolate brownie — soft orange-rhubarb against cocoa. Works; Aperol is too light to feature vs cocoa where the sipping amari shine.",
  "Aperol|Chocolate Cake": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge plays cleanly against the cake's layered chocolate — the bitter herb edge cuts dessert sweetness, and the orange peel echoes the chocolate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Aperol|Clam Chowder": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Aperol belongs on the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Cowboy Ribeye": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Aperol lands on another course — not here.",
  "Aperol|Crab Cake": "Aperol with crab cake — softest amaro against delicate crab. Works; this is the one amaro in the family gentle enough to sit alongside crab — not a featured match, but it doesn't overpower.",
  "Aperol|Creamed Spinach": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Aperol lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Aperol|Creamy Potato": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Creme Brulee": "Aperol with creme brulee — soft aperitivo against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; the soft orange finds the caramel at a lower intensity than Campari brings.",
  "Aperol|Escargot": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge overwhelms the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Aperol on the steak course instead.",
  "Aperol|Faroe Island Salmon": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bitter herbs on oily fish read metallic — the rich flesh deserves a crisp white. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Filet Mignon": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- amaro bitterness flattens the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Aperol for another course.",
  "Aperol|French Onion": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge jars against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Aperol on the steak course instead.",
  "Aperol|Grilled Caesar": "Aperol with the grilled Caesar — soft aperitivo against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Aperol's orange-rhubarb lifts the Caesar at a softer register than Campari — spritz-friendly, brunch-adjacent match.",
  "Aperol|Gumbo": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Aperol belongs on the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Aperol belongs on the steak course.",
  "Aperol|House Wedge": "Aperol with the House Wedge — soft orange-rhubarb at 11% ABV against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; the lightest, most approachable amari pairing on the menu — Aperol's gentle bitter complements the wedge without overpowering it, where Campari's 24% edge reads heavier.",
  "Aperol|Kansas City": "Aperol with the KC — brunch-register bitter against an 18oz strip. Avoid; Aperol has no business closing a heavy steak. Redirect to Fernet Branca.",
  "Aperol|Loaded Potato": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Aperol belongs on the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Lobster Mac": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Market Fish": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Aperol on the steak course instead.",
  "Aperol|Mocha Creme": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge holds steady against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the bitter herb edge cuts dessert sweetness, and the orange peel echoes the chocolate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Aperol|Mushroom Bisque": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge fights the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- bitter herbs cut the bisque's round cream without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Aperol lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Aperol|Mushrooms": "Aperol with mushrooms — soft aperitivo against earthy umami. Strong; Aperol softens the earthiness rather than cutting or deepening it.",
  "Aperol|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet 11% soft bitter. Works; too light for dessert density at this level.",
  "Aperol|Porterhouse": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Aperol for another course.",
  "Aperol|Prime Tartare": "Aperol with the tartare — 11% soft bitter against raw beef. Strong; Aperol is lighter than Campari here but the orange-rhubarb still lifts tartare's mineral notes at aperitivo register.",
  "Aperol|Rainbow Trout": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bitter herbs flatten delicate fish — the protein can't lift the amaro's register. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Roast Half Chicken": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge overwhelms the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- amaro bitterness buries the chicken's mild herbed-skin frame. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Aperol lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Aperol|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bitter herbs flatten delicate fish — the protein can't lift the amaro's register. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Aperol belongs on the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Seafood Tower": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Aperol belongs on the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Seared Scallops": "Aperol with seared scallops — soft aperitivo against scallop delicacy. Avoid; even the softest amaro is still wrong for scallops.",
  "Aperol|Seasonal Soup": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Aperol belongs on the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Seasonal Vegetables": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge doubles down on the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Shrimp Bisque": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Shrimp Cocktail": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Aperol belongs on the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Swordfish": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Aperol belongs on the steak course.",
  "Aperol|The Tomahawk": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Aperol on another course instead.",
  "Aperol|Tomato Basil": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Aperol on the steak course instead.",
  "Aperol|Truffle Fries": "Aperol with truffle fries — soft aperitivo bitter against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; the gentle orange-rhubarb brightens the earthy side without overwhelming it the way Branca would.",
  "Aperol|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bitter botanicals fight the savory sear — bitter against char without resolution. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Aperol|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Aperol lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Asparagus|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon depth settles into the asparagus, and the rounded sweetness softens the side. Works; pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Asparagus|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the brown-spirit warmth threads the asparagus, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Asparagus|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's bold cherry-coffee cassis finds neutral with the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Asparagus|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals holds with the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Asparagus|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the asparagus, and the smoke threads the side. Works; JD Bonded's backbone handles the richness at Bonded register.",
  "Asparagus|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the malt-and-oak threads the asparagus, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; GTS is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Asparagus|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the aged-agave weight wraps the asparagus, and the caramel-agave register brightens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Asparagus|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's rested-agave body finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the añejo register carries the asparagus, and the cooked-agave note underlines the side. Works; crystallino-filtered complexity handles richness at trendy-luxury register.",
  "Asparagus|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Asparagus|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Asparagus|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin sits alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Asparagus|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness doubles down on the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Asparagus|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Asparagus|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak depth lays under the asparagus, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Asparagus|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the bourbon's register is wrong for a clean vegetable side — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Angel's Envy Cask Strength for the steak course.",
  "Asparagus|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak depth lays under the asparagus, and the oak frames the side. Works; Old World complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Asparagus|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Asparagus|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Asparagus|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge doubles down on the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Asparagus|Aviation": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Asparagus|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals stays alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Asparagus|Avion 44": "Avion 44's barrel-aged tequila weight finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the aged-agave weight wraps the asparagus, and the caramel-agave register brightens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
