  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique with au gratin potatoes — retailer-label against cheese-and-cream. Works; boutique over-specified.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Roast Half Chicken": "The chicken and the au gratin on the plate -- the herbed crisp skin meets the gruyere-baked potato. Strong; the side holds the chicken's weight without competing.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The chickpea soup and the au gratin on the same meal -- spice and cream share the table, the contrast settles round. Works; sequence or share, the build holds.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs's crisp apple-char acidity reads alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Roku Gin": "Roku Gin's crisp floral-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey premium-sipping rum sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the rich pour wraps the au gratin, and the heavy-pour register grips the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with au gratin potatoes — aged Wild Turkey against cheese-and-cream. Strong; sophisticated Kentucky non-wheated match.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Salt-Cured Halibut": "The halibut alongside the au gratin -- the halibut's salt-cured flesh meets the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust cleanly. Strong; the pairing carries its register cleanly against the main.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Sambuca": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|San Simeon Stormwatch": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the mocha plays against the lactic richness. Strong Paso value Bordeaux blend for au gratin — solid full-body on the cheesy side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Sazerac": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the whiskey-build weight settles into the rich side, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye with au gratin potatoes — BT rye against cheese-and-cream. Works; the rye's bright character is slightly at odds with the side's richness.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the au gratin — New Zealand single malt against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Fortuna handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts gruyère-cream weight and the leather plays against the lactic richness. Works as King-of-Italian for au gratin — Scavino prestige on the cheesy side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the dark chocolate plays against the lactic richness. Strong by-the-glass Cab for au gratin — accessible California value on the cheesy side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Seafood Tower": "The seafood tower and the au gratin -- acid lifts the richness, the meal gets the contrast it needs. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Seared Scallops": "The scallops and the au gratin -- cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Seasonal Soup": "The seasonal soup and the au gratin on the same meal -- neither course pulls focus, the table reads even. Works; sequence or share, the build holds.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Au Gratin Potatoes: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Shafer 1.5": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin. The plush tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the vanilla-mocha plays against the lactic richness. Works as Stags Leap Cab for au gratin — Shafer opulence on the cheesy side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Shafer Hillside Select": "Stags Leap collector's Cab — Hillside Select 32-month French oak, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violet, graphite, dense yet silky, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the dark chocolate plays against the lactic richness. Works as collector's Napa Cab for au gratin — Hillside Select pairs better with the main cut.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Shrimp Bisque": "The shrimp bisque and the au gratin -- the meal doubles the cream, neither course gets to play off the other. Works; sequence them or share the table, the call holds either way.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Shrimp Cocktail": "Shrimp Cocktail alongside the au gratin -- the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish meets the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: bright-acid breaks the weight, the plate finds its lift. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the au gratin, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; ceremonial pairing depends on preparation.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado's rested-agave body finds neutral with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the rested-agave depth sits with the au gratin, and the oak threads the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak — Alexander Valley Cab with American-oak vanilla, coconut, dill, ripe cassis, plum. The lush body cuts au gratin's cheese-and-cream weight and the vanilla-dill plays against the golden crust. Works as recognizable Cab for au gratin — American-oak signature on the decadent side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Boutique Napa family estate Cab — dark plum, cassis, toasted oak, long refined finish, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the toasted oak plays against the lactic richness. Works as boutique Napa Cab for au gratin — Simon Family estate polish on the cheesy side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "St. Helena Napa Cab — Spottswoode organic family estate, plum, dark cherry, violet, silky medium tannin. The silky tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the violet plays against the lactic richness. Strong organic Napa Cab for au gratin — Spottswoode estate polish on the cheesy side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry — Napa Cab with blackberry, cassis, mocha, full body. The firm tannin cuts au gratin's cheese-and-cream weight and the mocha plays against the golden crust. Strong by-the-glass Cab for au gratin — Rutherford character frames the indulgent side at accessible price.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the espresso plays against the lactic richness. Strong estate Meritage for au gratin — Napa structure on the cheesy side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the au gratin — BT cask-strength against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Stagg Jr. handles the richness at barrel-proof register.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, green, light-bodied. Wrong weight class entirely for the 180°C 90-minute-baked manchego gratin; the wine vanishes underneath. Keep Stoneleigh for the wedge salad or shrimp cocktail where it has a job to do. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Swordfish": "Swordfish with the au gratin -- the dense fish steak sits cleanly with the golden-crust gratin. Strong; reliable side for the swordfish.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals stays alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals finds neutral with the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with au gratin potatoes — triple-cask aged against cheese-and-cream. Works; multi-cask complexity over-specified.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Polished Napa Cab — rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth lingering finish. The crowd-pleaser body cuts au gratin's cheese-and-cream weight and the mocha plays against the golden crust. Works as mid-tier Napa Cab for au gratin — approachable concentration on the decadent side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Au Gratin Potatoes: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|The BG": "The BG's lemon-driven cocktail body sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the bright build frames the au gratin, and the citrus cuts the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|The Happy Wife": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|The Manhattan": "Maker's Mark private barrel with Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The vanilla-sweet bourbon wraps around the manchego-baked cream; the cocktail's weight matches the gratin's richness directly. Decadent cocktail route when the guest's committed to indulgence. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|The Prisoner Red Blend": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The integrated tannin cuts gruyere-cream and the full body handles dairy weight. Works for au gratin — a reliable recognized-label match on the side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk alongside the au gratin -- the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char carries cleanly into the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust. Excellent; the headline side pairing for the Tomahawk.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the au gratin, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; premium single-barrel wasted on the salad.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the au gratin — chardonnay-cask bourbon against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; TSM Chardonnay Cask handles the richness with chardonnay-butter distinctiveness.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Titos Vodka": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality plays cleanly against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character finds neutral with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the au gratin, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the blend's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Tomato Basil": "The tomato basil and the au gratin on the same meal -- the bright register cleaves the heavy course, the table breathes. Works; sequence or share, the build holds.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Transfusion": "Transfusion's crisp citrus-ginger build plays cleanly against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado's aged-agave character sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the rested-agave depth sits with the au gratin, and the oak threads the side. Works; aged depth is refined but not a standout.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Truffle Fries": "The au gratin and the truffle fries share potato as the primary character. Avoid; the meal doubles down without contrast. Route the au gratin with Bone-In Filet, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Keenan Chardonnay or the truffle fries with The Tomahawk, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka instead.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s gentle Irish blend leans against the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the malt-grain blend handles the au gratin, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "The tuna alongside the au gratin -- delicate gives way to rich, the table earns its weight. Works; the side sits without pulling focus.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas and the au gratin -- the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth meets the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: heat plays against dairy, the meal earns the cooler register. Works; no conflict however the table orders them.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Venge Family Reserve": "Kirk Venge flagship Oakville Cab — concentrated blackcurrant, dark chocolate, vanilla, seamless polished finish, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the vanilla plays against the lactic richness. Works as Oakville flagship for au gratin — Venge opulence on the cheesy side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Venge Scout's Honor": "Napa Zin-led blend — Kirk Venge's Scout's Honor, blackberry, boysenberry, black pepper, vanilla, full body, generous. The vanilla plays with manchego-cream richness and the black pepper cuts gratin weight. Works for au gratin — Cabernet peaks higher on the dense dairy side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Venge Silencieux": "Kirk Venge single-vineyard Napa — dark cherry, cassis, chocolate, floral violet, silky soft tannin. The silky tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the chocolate plays against the lactic richness. Works as winemaker's Napa Cab for au gratin — Venge precision on the cheesy side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Vesper": "Vesper's crisp citrus-juniper build sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the apple notes lift the custard. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Dom Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. NOLA classic — the rye-cognac split and Bénédictine's herbal depth meet manchego's aged character. Complex cocktail for a complex potato side. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Villon Cognac": "Villon with the au gratin — cocktail-ready cognac against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Villon handles the richness at approachable register.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Weller 107": "Weller 107 with au gratin potatoes — bigger-body wheated meets cheese-and-cream. Strong; the heat adds interest where Weller Special Reserve just sits beside the side.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year with the au gratin potatoes — cheese and cream meet twelve years of oak-softened wheat. The age gives the pour enough weight to sit with the side's richness. A restrained pairing; the side still leads, the 12-year adds dimension. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Weller Millennium": "Au Gratin Potatoes: cheese and cream richness meet Weller Millennium's honeyed depth. The side holds its own beside the Millennium without either elevating; strong, not celebratory. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve with au gratin potatoes — approachable wheated meets cheese-and-cream depth. Cleanly matched registers; not revelatory, just composed. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the rye spice cuts the au gratin, and the oak softens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the rye-and-oak weight handles the au gratin, and the oak softens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character leans against the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the rye spice cuts the au gratin, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; AE CS handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character reads alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the au gratin, and the oak softens the cream. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the rye-and-oak weight handles the au gratin, and the oak threads the cream. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year with au gratin potatoes — Canadian-sourced rye against cheese-and-cream. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with au gratin potatoes — craft 94-proof against cheese-and-cream. Strong; oak spice gives the side a distinctive frame.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with au gratin potatoes — fruit-forward small-batch against cheese-and-cream. Strong; Woodford's complexity adds something BT's straight-line approach doesn't.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Au Gratin Potatoes: cheese-and-cream potatoes meet iconic Double-Oaked. Works; Woodford Double Oaked handles the richness at KY-benchmark register.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with au gratin potatoes — elegant small-batch against cheese-and-cream. Works; fruit-forward at matched register.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with au gratin potatoes — local-grain rye against cheese-and-cream. Works; regional craft at matched register.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the restrained whisky body threads the au gratin, and the oak softens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the restrained whisky body threads the au gratin, and the oak softens the cream. Works; gentle match.",
  "Au Gratin Potatoes|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Asparagus": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: the structured red's grip carries the rich side's dairy load; oak-driven body absorbs the cream and tannin clears the finish. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Beignets": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone-In Filet": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-coffee cassis frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the velvety body lifts the lean cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccoli Cheddar": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccolini": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the broccolini's tender green stalks -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Burrata": "Bold Paso Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — plush warm-spice body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with ripe plum-cherry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Austin Hope for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Butternut Squash": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Carrot Cake": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Cheesecake": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Chilean Seabass": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Brownie": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Cake": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Clam Chowder": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Cowboy Ribeye": "Austin Hope — Paso Robles Cab with ripe plum, dark cherry, mocha, warm spice, plush full finish. Paso produces bigger warmer reds than Napa. The plush body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the warm spice meets rendered fat. Peak Paso Cab for Cowboy — maximum richness for the big ribeye. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Crab Cake": "Bold Paso Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — plush warm-spice body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Austin Hope for the steak course where its Paso warmth shines.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamy Potato": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Creme Brulee": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Escargot": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-coffee cassis holds the line with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Faroe Island Salmon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Filet Mignon": "Austin Hope — Paso Cab with ripe plum, dark cherry, mocha, warm spice, plush. The bold body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the warm-spice-mocha echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong Paso Cab for filet — bigger than filet usually needs, though guest-choice when maximum warmth is the ask.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|French Onion": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-coffee cassis holds with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee meets the caramel. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Grilled Caesar": "Austin Hope — Paso Cab, ripe plum, dark cherry, mocha, warm spice. The bold body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the warm-spice plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as Paso Cab for grilled Caesar — warm-climate richness on the classic layered salad.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Gumbo": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|House Wedge": "Austin Hope — Paso Cab, ripe plum, dark cherry, mocha, warm spice. The plush body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the warm-spice plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as Paso Cab for the wedge — unconventional red with warmth, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Kansas City": "Paso Robles Cab — ripe plum, dark cherry, mocha, warm spice, plush finish. The bold body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the warm spice-mocha plays against the beefy char. Peak Paso Cab for Kansas City — warmer-climate richness frames the strip with a different register than Napa. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Loaded Potato": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's firm cassis-and-cocoa tannin leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the structured body wraps the loaded potato soup, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Lobster Mac": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-coffee cassis leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the opulent body matches the indulgent side, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Market Fish": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Mocha Creme": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushroom Bisque": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-coffee cassis leans against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushrooms": "Austin Hope — Paso Cab, ripe plum, dark cherry, mocha, warm spice. The plush body meshes with mushroom umami and the warm-spice-mocha frames the sauté earth. Strong Paso Cab for mushrooms — warm-climate richness on the earthy side, though savory Cabs like Gundlach peak similarly with less warmth.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Prime Tartare": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-coffee cassis reads alongside the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Rainbow Trout": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Roast Half Chicken": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's powerful blackcurrant body finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the structured body wraps the roast chicken, and the cassis frames the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Seafood Tower": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Seared Scallops": "Austin Hope Paso Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — plush warm-spice obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Austin Hope for the steak course where its Paso richness peaks.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Soup": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Vegetables": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Bisque": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-coffee cassis sits beside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Cocktail": "Austin Hope Paso Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — plush warm-spice is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Austin Hope for steak where its Paso warmth belongs.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Swordfish": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|The Tomahawk": "Paso Robles Cab — ripe plum, dark cherry, mocha, warm spice, plush. The bold body can meet Tomahawk fat and char but the warmer-climate softness is rounder than the biggest marbled steak demands firm-tannin-wise. Strong Paso Cab for Tomahawk — Opus, Shafer, or Caymus peak higher for the theatrical bone-in.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Tomato Basil": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the mushroom's umami depth meets the bold red's structured tannin; dark fruit threads the truffle-and-earth without crowding. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Asparagus": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge doubles down on the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Averna Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Averna Amaro|Beignets": "Beignets: fried dough and powdered sugar meet Sicilian amaro. Works; caramel-orange classic-amaro character pairs with sugar-coated beignets.",
  "Averna Amaro|Bone Marrow": "Averna with bone marrow — fuller-body Sicilian amaro against umami fat. Strong; the caramel-orange cuts through the marrow's richness at classic-digestif register.",
  "Averna Amaro|Bone-In Filet": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Averna Amaro for another course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Broccoli Cheddar": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Broccolini": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge doubles down on the broccolini's tender green stalks -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Averna Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Averna Amaro|Brussels and Belly": "Averna with Brussels + Belly — Sicilian amaro against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; caramel-orange doubles the dish's sweetness; Nonino cuts it better.",
  "Averna Amaro|Burrata": "Averna with burrata — amaro against fresh dairy. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Averna Amaro|Butternut Squash": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge fights the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- bitter herbs cut the bisque's round cream without lifting it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Averna Amaro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Carrot Cake": "Averna with carrot cake — Sicilian amaro against cream cheese and cinnamon. The caramel-and-orange threads with the cake's raisins in a classic post-dessert register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Averna Amaro|Cheesecake": "Averna with cheesecake — Sicilian amaro against custard. The caramel-and-orange character sits with the cheesecake's richness; classic post-dessert digestif. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Averna Amaro|Chilean Seabass": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Averna Amaro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Chocolate Brownie": "Averna with the chocolate brownie — Sicilian classic amaro, caramel-and-orange-forward with herbal complexity. Fuller body than Nonino's grappa approach. The caramel-orange notes thread into the brownie's cocoa with the classic Sicilian amaro-dessert character. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Averna Amaro|Chocolate Cake": "Averna Amaro's caramel-orange sweetness plays cleanly against the cake's layered chocolate — the bitter herb edge cuts dessert sweetness, and the caramel deepens the cocoa. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Averna Amaro|Clam Chowder": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Averna Amaro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Cowboy Ribeye": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- aperitivo bitterness fights the headline cut's marbled char — bitter and savory pull apart. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Averna Amaro for another course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Crab Cake": "Averna with crab cake — Sicilian amaro against delicate crab. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Averna Amaro|Creamed Spinach": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Creamy Potato": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge fights the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- bitter herbs cut the bisque's round cream without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Averna Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Averna Amaro|Creme Brulee": "Averna with creme brulee — Sicilian caramel-orange amaro against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. The amaro's caramel notes echo the brulee's burnt sugar; classic pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Averna Amaro|Escargot": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Faroe Island Salmon": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bitter herbs on oily fish read metallic — the rich flesh deserves a crisp white. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Averna Amaro on the steak course instead.",
  "Averna Amaro|Filet Mignon": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Averna Amaro belongs on another course.",
  "Averna Amaro|French Onion": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Averna Amaro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Grilled Caesar": "Averna with the grilled Caesar — fuller-body amaro against charred romaine. Strong; caramel-orange finds the Caesar's anchovy register cleanly.",
  "Averna Amaro|Gumbo": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- aperitivo bitterness and the glaze's sugar refuse each other — bitter wins, dessert reads cloying. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|House Wedge": "Averna with the House Wedge — Sicilian amaro against iceberg and bleu. Works; caramel-orange is a post-salad digestif option.",
  "Averna Amaro|Kansas City": "Averna with the KC — Sicilian classic as post-steak digestif. Strong; caramel-orange settles the meal.",
  "Averna Amaro|Loaded Potato": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Lobster Mac": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Market Fish": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Mocha Creme": "Averna Amaro's caramel-orange sweetness holds steady against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the bitter herb edge cuts dessert sweetness, and the caramel pairs into the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Averna Amaro|Mushroom Bisque": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Mushrooms": "Averna with the mushrooms — Sicilian amaro against earthy umami. Strong; caramel-orange layers into mushroom depth.",
  "Averna Amaro|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet Sicilian caramel-orange amaro. Fuller-body Averna meets the dessert with classic-amaro authority. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Averna Amaro|Porterhouse": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Averna Amaro for another course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Prime Tartare": "Averna with the tartare — fuller-body classic amaro against raw beef. Strong; caramel-orange lifts the tartare's mineral depth.",
  "Averna Amaro|Rainbow Trout": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- aperitivo bitterness fights gentle flesh without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Roast Half Chicken": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Averna Amaro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bitter herbs flatten delicate fish — the protein can't lift the amaro's register. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge doubles down on the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Seafood Tower": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- amaro bitterness on cold shellfish flattens the delicate brine — needs sparkling instead. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Seared Scallops": "Averna with seared scallops — amaro against scallop delicacy. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Averna Amaro|Seasonal Soup": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Seasonal Vegetables": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge doubles down on the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Averna Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Averna Amaro|Shrimp Bisque": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge fights the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- bitter herbs cut the bisque's round cream without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Shrimp Cocktail": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- amaro bitterness on cold shellfish flattens the delicate brine — needs sparkling instead. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Swordfish": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bitter botanicals fight the savory sear — bitter against char without resolution. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Averna Amaro on the steak course instead.",
  "Averna Amaro|The Tomahawk": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- aperitivo bitterness fights the headline cut's marbled char — bitter and savory pull apart. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Averna Amaro for another course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Tomato Basil": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Truffle Fries": "Averna with truffle fries — fuller-body amaro against truffle-parmesan. Works; caramel-orange is a gentle counterpoint.",
  "Averna Amaro|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Averna Amaro|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Aviation Gin|Asparagus": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals stays alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Aviation Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals stays alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Aviation Gin|Beignets": "Aviation with beignets — smooth American craft gin against fried dough. Excellent; Aviation's soft lavender-cardamom is the crowd-pleasing craft-gin-beignets pairing — Empress 1908's rose notes edge it on distinctiveness.",
  "Aviation Gin|Bone Marrow": "Aviation with bone marrow — soft-juniper craft against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Aviation Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Aviation Gin for another course.",
  "Aviation Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Aviation Gin|Broccolini": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Aviation Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals leans against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Aviation Gin|Burrata": "Aviation with burrata — Portland soft-juniper craft against fresh dairy. Excellent; Aviation's lavender-orange-peel softness complements cream at crowd-pleasing register — Empress's rose-floral and Inverroche's fynbos are more distinctive.",
  "Aviation Gin|Butternut Squash": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals sits alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Aviation Gin|Carrot Cake": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Aviation Gin for the steak course.",
  "Aviation Gin|Cheesecake": "Aviation with cheesecake — American craft gin against custard. Works; Aviation's lavender-orange-peel is a soft dessert finisher.",
  "Aviation Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals holds steady against the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Aviation Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Aviation Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Aviation Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Aviation Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Aviation Gin|Clam Chowder": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Aviation Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Aviation with the cowboy ribeye — Portland craft gin against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Aviation Gin|Crab Cake": "Crab Cake: delicate lump crab meets Portland OR American craft, soft juniper with lavender, cardamom, and orange peel. Aviation's smooth-balanced profile is the crowd-pleasing gin-crab pairing — where Bluecoat citruses and Empress floralizes, Aviation just delivers the expected with softer American character. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Aviation Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Aviation Gin|Creamy Potato": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals stays alongside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Aviation Gin|Creme Brulee": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Aviation Gin for the steak course.",
  "Aviation Gin|Escargot": "Aviation with escargot — smooth Portland craft against garlic-butter snails. Works; Aviation's soft juniper meets butter at crowd-pleasing register; Mahón and Eastern Kille's sharper juniper cut it harder.",
  "Aviation Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Aviation with the salmon — Portland Oregon soft-juniper craft against oily salmon. Excellent; Aviation's balanced approach frames the fish at crowd-pleasing register; Empress 1908's floral and Inverroche's fynbos are more distinctive choices.",
  "Aviation Gin|Filet Mignon": "Aviation with the filet — soft American craft against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Aviation Gin|French Onion": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the orange brightens the caramel. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Aviation Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Aviation with the grilled Caesar — Portland lavender-cardamom against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Aviation meets the char at balanced register; Bluecoat's 4-citrus cuts char sharper, Tanq 10 still leads.",
  "Aviation Gin|Gumbo": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the orange meets the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Aviation Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals reads alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Aviation Gin|House Wedge": "Aviation with the House Wedge — lavender-cardamom American craft against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Aviation's soft juniper threads the wedge at balanced register.",
  "Aviation Gin|Kansas City": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Aviation Gin on another course instead.",
  "Aviation Gin|Loaded Potato": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals leans against the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Aviation Gin|Lobster Mac": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Aviation Gin|Market Fish": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift reads register-aligned with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh: the bright botanical edge handles the market fish, and the dry gin lift plays against the plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Aviation Gin|Mocha Creme": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Aviation Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Aviation Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Aviation Gin|Mushrooms": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Aviation Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Aviation Gin for the steak course.",
  "Aviation Gin|Porterhouse": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Aviation Gin on another course instead.",
  "Aviation Gin|Prime Tartare": "Aviation with the tartare — soft-juniper Portland craft against raw beef. Strong; Aviation supports tartare at crowd-pleasing register; Monkey 47's 47-botanical complexity is the featured craft-gin-tartare match.",
  "Aviation Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals holds steady against the trout's delicate flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Aviation Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Aviation with the chicken — Portland American dry gin against roasted poultry. Strong; Aviation's soft juniper supports chicken cleanly at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Aviation Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the orange meets the spice. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Aviation Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals lifts the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Aviation Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Aviation Gin|Seafood Tower": "Aviation with the seafood tower — smooth American craft gin against the raw bar. Strong; Aviation handles the tower at crowd-pleasing register; Gray Whale's ocean-conservation-California-botanicals is the featured craft-tower match.",
  "Aviation Gin|Seared Scallops": "Aviation with seared scallops — smooth American craft against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Aviation's soft juniper frames scallops with approachable balance — less cut than flagship Tanqueray, less botanical than Monkey 47.",
  "Aviation Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Aviation Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals stays alongside the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Aviation Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Aviation with shrimp bisque — smooth Oregon craft against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Aviation rides the bisque at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Aviation Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Aviation with shrimp cocktail — soft-juniper Portland craft gin against cold shrimp. Excellent; Aviation's balanced lavender-orange-peel profile delivers the classic cold app with crowd-pleasing character.",
  "Aviation Gin|Swordfish": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals matches the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Aviation Gin|The Tomahawk": "Aviation with the Tomahawk — crowd-pleasing craft against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Aviation Gin|Tomato Basil": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright acid — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Aviation Gin|Truffle Fries": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals sits alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Aviation Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals lifts the tuna's seared rare crust — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the juniper sharpens the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Aviation Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the orange meets the spice. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Aviation|Asparagus": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Aviation|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build reads alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Aviation|Beignets": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a light cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Aviation for the steak course.",
  "Aviation|Bone Marrow": "Aviation gin, Luxardo, crème de violette, ginger syrup. The cocktail's floral lightness has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat richness — violet and cherry don't meet bone depth. Never pair — keep the Aviation for starters and raw bar.",
  "Aviation|Bone-In Filet": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- cocktail citrus reads thin against the cut's structure. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Aviation on another course instead.",
  "Aviation|Broccoli Cheddar": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Aviation|Broccolini": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build leans against the broccolini's tender green — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Aviation|Brussels and Belly": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build plays cleanly against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Aviation|Burrata": "Aviation gin, Luxardo maraschino, crème de violette, ginger syrup — pale purple in the glass. The floral-cherry-almond profile meets burrata's creamy-delicate build and the visual appeal doubles the presentation. Peak starter pairing — both elegant, both visually striking on the table. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Aviation|Butternut Squash": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build leans against the squash soup's silky sweet — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Aviation|Carrot Cake": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Aviation lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Aviation|Cheesecake": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Aviation for the steak course.",
  "Aviation|Chilean Seabass": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build plays cleanly against the seabass's buttery flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Aviation|Chocolate Brownie": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Aviation for the steak course.",
  "Aviation|Chocolate Cake": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Aviation on the steak course instead.",
  "Aviation|Clam Chowder": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Aviation|Cowboy Ribeye": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Aviation on another course instead.",
  "Aviation|Crab Cake": "Aviation gin with Luxardo maraschino, crème de violette, ginger syrup. The cocktail's floral-cherry profile plays against crab's sweet-delicate meat and the ginger adds warmth against romesco. Peak cocktail for crab cake when guest wants visual drama alongside the pairing.",
  "Aviation|Creamed Spinach": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Aviation|Creamy Potato": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build sits alongside the potato soup's silky body — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Aviation|Creme Brulee": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Aviation for the steak course.",
  "Aviation|Escargot": "Escargot: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this gin cocktail's citrus brightness and botanical lift. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
