  "Bacardi Rum|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Bacardi Rum -- Carta Blanca, workhorse light rum, clean. The bright cane-and-citrus weight carries the halibut, and the Caribbean register brightens the plate. Strong; clean additive-free cuts the garlic-butter at craft register.",
  "Bacardi Rum|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the cane-and-citrus body lifts the garlic spinach, and the sugarcane lift underlines the side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Bacardi Rum|Seafood Tower": "Bacardi Rum -- Carta Blanca, workhorse light rum, clean. The bright cane-and-citrus weight carries the seafood tower, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the starter. Excellent; workhorse light rum on the seafood tower -- the kind of pour that earns a regular. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Bacardi Rum|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallop delicacy meets Bacardi Rum. Excellent; the ultra-clean Bacardi register is the textbook light-rum answer to scallops — neutral enough to let the sear speak.",
  "Bacardi Rum|Seasonal Soup": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bright cane-and-citrus weight carries the seasonal soup, and the sugarcane lift underlines the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bacardi Rum|Seasonal Vegetables": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the Caribbean-rum body carries the seasonal vegetables, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bacardi Rum|Shrimp Bisque": "* Bacardi Rum -- Carta Blanca, workhorse light rum, clean. The unaged-cane register matches the shrimp bisque, and the cane-and-citrus thread frames the bowl. The premium presentation elevates the whole tower; pour when the table wants the full experience. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Bacardi Rum|Shrimp Cocktail": "* Bacardi Rum -- Carta Blanca, workhorse light rum, clean. The unaged body brightens the chilled shrimp, and the light-rum lift plays against the starter. Excellent on shrimp cocktail; Bacardi's neutral-clean profile is the textbook light-rum move on cold shellfish — the workhorse answer when guest wants something light with seafood. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Bacardi Rum|Swordfish": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character finds register with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bacardi Rum|The Tomahawk": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a light spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Bacardi Rum for another course.",
  "Bacardi Rum|Tomato Basil": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the cane-and-citrus body lifts the tomato basil, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bacardi Rum|Truffle Fries": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character leans against the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight: the bright cane-and-citrus weight carries the truffle fries, and the cane register underlines the side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Bacardi Rum|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character sits with the tuna's seared rare crust — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bacardi Rum|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the silver-rum body cuts the curry, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Asparagus": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Beignets": "Baileys Irish Cream's after-dinner liqueur weight leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the digestif body mirrors the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Bone Marrow": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Bone-In Filet": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on another course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Broccoli Cheddar": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Baileys Irish Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Broccolini": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Brussels and Belly": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Baileys Irish Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Burrata": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Butternut Squash": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Baileys. Excellent; the vanilla-cocoa-cream profile amplifies the cream cheese frosting at classic-Baileys register.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Cheesecake": "Baileys Irish Cream's herbal-and-sugar liqueur register lifts the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the herbal-sweet weight matches the cheesecake, and the honey threads the cream. Excellent; the starter sets up the cut at full register. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Chilean Seabass": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Baileys. Excellent; the pairing earns its featured slot.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Chocolate Cake": "★ Baileys Irish Cream's sweet cream-cocoa character commands the meeting with the cake's layered chocolate — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the cocoa underlines the chocolate. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Clam Chowder": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Baileys Irish Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Cowboy Ribeye": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Baileys Irish Cream for another course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Crab Cake": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Baileys Irish Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Creamed Spinach": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Creamy Potato": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Baileys Irish Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Creme Brulee": "Baileys Irish Cream: the original cream liqueur (Ireland, 1974), Irish whiskey + Irish cream base, 17% ABV, vanilla-cocoa-cream profile. Excellent on creme brulee; the classic-cream-liqueur character threads the burnt-sugar custard at dessert-time register — the most universally-recognized cream liqueur dessert pairing.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Escargot": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character buries the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- sweet liqueur smothers the herb-bright opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Baileys Irish Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Faroe Island Salmon": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Filet Mignon": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the liqueur's sugar buries the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Baileys Irish Cream for another course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|French Onion": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Grilled Caesar": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character jars against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Baileys Irish Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Gumbo": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Baileys Irish Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|House Wedge": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Kansas City": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Baileys Irish Cream on another course instead.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Loaded Potato": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Lobster Mac": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Market Fish": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Mocha Creme": "Mocha Creme: coffee-chocolate dessert meets Baileys. Excellent; Baileys and coffee is the textbook pairing — the Irish-whiskey-cream meets mocha at iconic register.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Mushroom Bisque": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Baileys Irish Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Mushrooms": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Baileys Irish Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut-chocolate stack meets Baileys. Excellent; the vanilla-cocoa-cream profile wraps peanut-chocolate at classic-Baileys dessert register.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Porterhouse": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Baileys Irish Cream on another course instead.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Prime Tartare": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Baileys Irish Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Rainbow Trout": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Roast Half Chicken": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Seafood Tower": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Baileys. Avoid; cream-liqueur wrong for scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Seasonal Soup": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Seasonal Vegetables": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Baileys Irish Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Shrimp Bisque": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Shrimp Cocktail": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Swordfish": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|The Tomahawk": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Baileys Irish Cream for another course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Tomato Basil": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Truffle Fries": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Baileys Irish Cream|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Baileys Irish Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Baker's|Asparagus": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the caramel-spice register matches the asparagus, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Baker's|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Baker's with au gratin potatoes — 107-proof Beam small-batch against cheese-and-cream. Strong; bold character at premium register.",
  "Baker's|Beignets": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon body sits with the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Voyage 23 frames the cut at ocean-finish register.",
  "Baker's|Bone Marrow": "Baker's with bone marrow — 107-proof small-batch against umami fat. The premium proof and rich vanilla depth meet marrow's intensity. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Baker's|Bone-In Filet": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the bourbon depth settles into the bone-enhanced filet, and the vanilla softens the cut. Strong; Marian McLain L.E.",
  "Baker's|Broccoli Cheddar": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon backbone carries the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for the side — save the rare-cask bottle for the protein course.",
  "Baker's|Broccolini": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the broccolini, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; EHT Full Proof is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Baker's|Brussels and Belly": "Baker's with Brussels + Belly — 107-proof Beam small-batch meets maple-glazed pork belly. The high-proof rich vanilla cuts the glaze with small-batch authority. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Baker's|Burrata": "Baker's with burrata — fresh dairy against 107-proof small-batch. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Baker's|Butternut Squash": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the squash bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Baker's|Carrot Cake": "Baker's with carrot cake — 107-proof small-batch against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; bold intensity slightly over-specified.",
  "Baker's|Cheesecake": "Baker's with cheesecake — 107-proof high-rye against clean custard. Works; bold proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Baker's|Chilean Seabass": "Baker's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Baker's for the steak course.",
  "Baker's|Chocolate Brownie": "Baker's with the chocolate brownie — 107-proof small-batch against cocoa. Works; rich vanilla depth meets the brownie with small-batch character.",
  "Baker's|Chocolate Cake": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the brown-spirit warmth threads the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a gentle composed match.",
  "Baker's|Clam Chowder": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon depth settles into the chowder, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Baker's|Cowboy Ribeye": "Baker's with the Cowboy Ribeye — 107-proof against cap fat. The higher proof and bold high-rye character earn the ribeye with Beam small-batch authority. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Baker's|Crab Cake": "Baker's with crab cake — 107-proof small-batch against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Baker's|Creamed Spinach": "Baker's with creamed spinach — 107-proof small-batch against dairy. Strong; premium proof sits with richness at bold register.",
  "Baker's|Creamy Potato": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Baker's|Creme Brulee": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the 10-year depth gives the dessert substantial premium frame.",
  "Baker's|Escargot": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon depth settles into the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; collector intensity at allocated register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Baker's|Faroe Island Salmon": "Baker's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Baker's lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Baker's|Filet Mignon": "Baker's with the filet — Jim Beam's 107-proof 7-year small-batch premium. Rich vanilla, bold high-rye character, named for Baker Beam. Distinct from Knob Creek 12 (12-year aged) — Baker's is younger but higher-proof, more immediate intensity. Premium Beam-side for filet. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Baker's|French Onion": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon body sits with the French onion, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Baker's|Grilled Caesar": "Baker's with the grilled Caesar — 107-proof small-batch against charred romaine. Strong; the bold proof earns the Caesar with small-batch authority.",
  "Baker's|Gumbo": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon backbone carries the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; annual-release complexity over-specified.",
  "Baker's|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; Remus 15 handles dessert density at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Baker's|House Wedge": "Baker's with the House Wedge — bold 107-proof against iceberg and bleu. Works; the intensity overpowers the salad.",
  "Baker's|Kansas City": "Baker's with the KC — 107-proof 7-year Beam premium against the strip's marbling. The extra proof handles the cut with Beam small-batch intensity. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Baker's|Loaded Potato": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon backbone carries the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the bourbon's character is distinctive but the pairing isn't a standout.",
  "Baker's|Lobster Mac": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the brown-spirit warmth threads the lobster mac, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; HH 20 is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Baker's|Market Fish": "Baker's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Baker's on the steak course instead.",
  "Baker's|Mocha Creme": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "Baker's|Mushroom Bisque": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the oak depth lays under the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; AE Triple Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Baker's|Mushrooms": "Baker's with the mushrooms — bold high-rye 107-proof against earthy umami. The extra proof layers into mushroom depth with Beam small-batch character. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Baker's|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet 107-proof Beam small-batch. Works; rich vanilla and high-proof give the dessert bolder match than standard Beam brings.",
  "Baker's|Porterhouse": "Baker's with the porterhouse — 107-proof high-rye across strip and filet. Strong; bold proof leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "Baker's|Prime Tartare": "Baker's bold caramel-spice character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Baker's|Rainbow Trout": "Baker's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Baker's on the steak course instead.",
  "Baker's|Roast Half Chicken": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the oak depth lays under the roast chicken, and the oak frames the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Baker's|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the caramel-spice register matches the chickpea soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Baker's|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Baker's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Baker's lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Baker's|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon body sits with the garlic spinach, and the oak frames the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Baker's|Seafood Tower": "Baker's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Baker's for the steak course.",
  "Baker's|Seared Scallops": "Baker's with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 107-proof. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Baker's|Seasonal Soup": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the oak depth lays under the seasonal soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; craft-blender complexity over-specified.",
  "Baker's|Seasonal Vegetables": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the brown-spirit warmth threads the seasonal vegetables, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Baker's|Shrimp Bisque": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the bourbon weight handles the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Baker's|Shrimp Cocktail": "Baker's with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against 107-proof Beam. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Baker's|Swordfish": "Baker's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Baker's lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Baker's|The Tomahawk": "Baker's with the Tomahawk — 107-proof 7-year against bone-in ribeye. The extra proof handles the cut's scale where standard Knob Creek fades; Baker's and Four Roses Small Batch Select are the premium-small-batch Tomahawk calls. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Baker's|Tomato Basil": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; boutique-sourced wasted on salad.",
  "Baker's|Truffle Fries": "Baker's with truffle fries — 107-proof small-batch against truffle-parmesan. The bold proof gives the side real grip with Beam premium character. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Baker's|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Baker's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Baker's belongs on the steak course.",
  "Baker's|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; EC 18 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Asparagus": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the scotch backbone matches the asparagus, and the smoke threads the side. Works; boutique covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the au gratin — vanilla-forward Speyside against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Strong; Balvenie's sweet-oak handles the richness at handcrafted register.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Beignets": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the beignets, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the dessert. Works; craft-blend over-specified.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Bone Marrow": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with bone marrow — American Oak Speyside against umami fat. Works; Balvenie is too light to cut marrow (Fernet Branca does that); Balvenie sits alongside the marrow course comfortably.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Bone-In Filet": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the bone-in filet — American Oak vanilla-forward Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Balvenie's sweet-oak richness frames the cut's depth with handcrafted Speyside character.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Broccoli Cheddar": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon-cask vanilla settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; AE Triple Cask's layered character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Broccolini": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the malt-and-oak threads the broccolini, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; 100-proof complexity sits beside the soup.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Brussels and Belly": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with Brussels + Belly — first-fill American Oak Speyside against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; Balvenie's vanilla meets the glaze with sweet-oak resonance.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Burrata": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with burrata — handcrafted Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Butternut Squash": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon-cask vanilla settles into the squash bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; wine-cask over-specified.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Carrot Cake": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with carrot cake — vanilla-forward bourbon-cask Speyside against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; the American Oak vanilla marries the cake's spice-and-raisins naturally — the featured Speyside-carrot-cake pairing over Fiddich's apple or Glenlivet's honey approach.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Cheesecake": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with cheesecake — vanilla-forward bourbon-cask Speyside against custard dessert. Excellent; Balvenie's American Oak vanilla threads cheesecake custard — the featured Speyside-cheesecake match; Fiddich's apple and Glenlivet's honey are the alternatives.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Chilean Seabass": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Balvenie 12 American Oak for the steak course.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Chocolate Brownie": "Balvenie 12 American Oak: bourbon-barrel Speyside. Excellent on chocolate brownie; vanilla-honey-sweet Speyside frames cocoa at American-oak register.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Chocolate Cake": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the scotch depth settles into the chocolate cake, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the dessert. Works; extended-aged home-state pour wasted on salad.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Clam Chowder": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the malt-driven body wraps the chowder, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; boutique-craft doesn't drive.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Cowboy Ribeye": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the cowboy ribeye — first-fill American Oak Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Balvenie's sweet-oak depth frames the ribeye at handcrafted register.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Crab Cake": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with crab cake — Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Creamed Spinach": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with creamed spinach — handcrafted American Oak Speyside against dairy-rich side. Strong; Balvenie's vanilla rides the cream at bourbon-cask register.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Creamy Potato": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the Highland register carries the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Creme Brulee": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Escargot": "Balvenie 12 American Oak -- Speyside, bourbon cask, vanilla, honey. The bourbon-cask vanilla settles into the buttered escargot, and the honey rounds the starter. Strong; the 10-year oak gives the side backbone distinct from wheated approaches; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Faroe Island Salmon": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Balvenie 12 American Oak lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Filet Mignon": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the filet — first-fill American Oak bourbon-cask only, vanilla-forward, handcrafted Speyside. Excellent; Balvenie's vanilla-forward American Oak character frames tenderloin with the richest sweet-oak depth in the batch — where Fiddich is apple-crisp and Glenlivet is honey-soft, Balvenie is vanilla-caramel.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|French Onion": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the malt-driven body wraps the French onion, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; a gentle composed match.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Grilled Caesar": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the grilled Caesar — handcrafted Speyside against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Balvenie's vanilla-oak meets the char with sweet-oak depth, though Fiddich's crisp-apple and Glenlivet's honey-floral are more traditional Speyside-Caesar approaches.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Gumbo": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the scotch depth settles into the gumbo, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; novelty maturation at matched register.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|House Wedge": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the House Wedge — handcrafted Speyside against iceberg and bleu. Works; Balvenie's sweet-oak is over-specified for the wedge but functional.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Kansas City": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the KC — vanilla-forward Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Balvenie handles the strip with handcrafted American Oak depth; Speyside 18 or Balvenie 16 (next batch) are the featured Speyside-KC choices.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Loaded Potato": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon-cask vanilla settles into the loaded potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Lobster Mac": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the malt-and-oak threads the lobster mac, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; boutique at matched register.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Market Fish": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Balvenie 12 American Oak for the steak course.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Mocha Creme": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the Highland register carries the mocha creme, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Marian McLain L.E.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Mushroom Bisque": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the malt-driven body wraps the mushroom bisque, and the oak threads the umami plate. Works; Sinatra Select handles the richness at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Mushrooms": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with mushrooms — first-fill American Oak Speyside against earthy umami. Excellent; Balvenie's vanilla-forward character marries mushroom depth with sweet-oak resonance.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the PB brownie — vanilla-forward Speyside against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; Balvenie's American Oak vanilla marries the dessert density — the best Speyside match in the batch for PB brownie.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Porterhouse": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the porterhouse — vanilla-forward Speyside against strip-and-filet. Strong; Balvenie's American Oak richness frames the two-cut plate with more oak depth than the benchmark siblings.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Prime Tartare": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's crisp char-honey character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Rainbow Trout": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Balvenie 12 American Oak lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Roast Half Chicken": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the roast chicken, and the smoke threads the plate. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for the side — save the rare-cask bottle for the protein course.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the chickpea soup, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Balvenie 12 American Oak for the steak course.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the garlic spinach, and the smoke threads the side. Works; bonded weight pushes past the soup's register slightly.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Seafood Tower": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Balvenie 12 American Oak lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Seared Scallops": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with seared scallops — vanilla-forward Speyside against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Seasonal Soup": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon-cask vanilla settles into the seasonal soup, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; AE Triple Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Seasonal Vegetables": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the scotch depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; high proof over-specified.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Shrimp Bisque": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with shrimp bisque — vanilla-forward Speyside against cream-shrimp soup. Works; Balvenie rides the bisque at handcrafted register.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Shrimp Cocktail": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with shrimp cocktail — bourbon-cask Speyside against cold shrimp. Avoid; Scotch wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Swordfish": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Balvenie 12 American Oak belongs on the steak course.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|The Tomahawk": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's crisp char-honey character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Tomato Basil": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the scotch backbone matches the tomato basil, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; OGD 16 handles dessert density at distinctive register.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Truffle Fries": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with truffle fries — handcrafted American Oak Speyside against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Balvenie's sweet-oak vanilla meets the earthy-rich with more depth than Fiddich's lighter bourbon-cask profile.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Balvenie 12 American Oak lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Balvenie 12 American Oak|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the curry, and the smoke threads the bowl. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Asparagus": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the scotch backbone matches the asparagus, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the side. Works; the match is gentle and approachable.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the au gratin — Caribbean-rum-cask Speyside against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Caribbean Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Beignets": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the malt-driven body wraps the beignets, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the dessert. Works; the match is gentle and approachable.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Bone Marrow": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with bone marrow — rum-cask-finished Speyside against umami fat. Strong; Caribbean Cask's brown-sugar-and-vanilla sits alongside marrow with tropical character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Bone-In Filet": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the bone-in filet — Caribbean rum-cask-finished Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; the tropical-sweet rum-cask character frames the cut's marrow-adjacent richness with distinctive finish-cask story.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Broccoli Cheddar": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the Highland register carries the broccoli cheddar, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; Remus 15 frames the two-cut plate at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Broccolini": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the Highland register carries the broccolini, and the heather brightens the side. Works; single-barrel depth gives the dessert more than Small Batch.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet tropical-sweet Caribbean-rum-cask Speyside. The rum-cask brown-sugar-and-tropical-sweetness meets maple glaze with featured resonance — maple-and-rum-cask is the textbook pairing, and the Caribbean Cask brings it uniquely in the Sherried Speyside cluster. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Burrata": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with burrata — tropical-sweet Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Butternut Squash": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the malt-and-oak threads the squash bisque, and the oak softens the cream. Works; craft-blend over-specified.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Carrot Cake": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with carrot cake — Caribbean-rum-cask Speyside against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; the rum-cask-derived brown sugar and vanilla marry the cake's raisins and spice with tropical-finish distinction.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Cheesecake": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with cheesecake — tropical-sweet rum-cask Speyside against custard. Strong; Caribbean Cask's vanilla-caramel character threads the cheesecake at distinctive finish-cask register.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Chilean Seabass": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask for the steak course.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Chocolate Brownie": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the chocolate brownie — rum-cask-finished 14yr Speyside against cocoa. Strong; Caribbean Cask's rum-cask-derived brown sugar marries cocoa; Balvenie 21 Portwood's port-cask is the featured Sherried Speyside-chocolate match.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Chocolate Cake": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the Highland register carries the chocolate cake, and the heather brightens the dessert. Works; the aging is wasted on the salad.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Clam Chowder": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the scotch backbone matches the chowder, and the oak threads the cream. Works; complexity wasted on salad.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Cowboy Ribeye": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the cowboy ribeye — 14yr rum-cask-finished Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Caribbean Cask's tropical-sweetness frames the ribeye; Balvenie 21 Portwood's port-cask richness is the featured ribeye match.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Crab Cake": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with crab cake — Caribbean-rum-cask Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Creamed Spinach": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with creamed spinach — tropical-sweet rum-cask Speyside against dairy-rich side. Works; Caribbean Cask rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Creamy Potato": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the malt-and-oak threads the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Creme Brulee": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with creme brulee — rum-cask-finished Speyside against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Excellent; caramel-meets-caramel is the textbook match — Caribbean Cask's rum-cask-derived caramel is the featured Sherried Speyside-brulee pairing.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Escargot": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's caramel-honey character matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Faroe Island Salmon": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Filet Mignon": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the filet — 14yr Speyside finished in Caribbean rum casks, tropical-sweetness with vanilla-caramel-brown-sugar layers. Excellent; Caribbean Cask's rum-cask-derived sweetness frames tenderloin with distinctive tropical character — different from Macallan's dried-fruit-Oloroso approach and Balvenie 16's pure American Oak vanilla.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|French Onion": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the French onion, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Grilled Caesar": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the malt-and-oak threads the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Gumbo": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the malt-and-oak threads the gumbo, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rum-cask depth meets the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; milestone-edition over-specified.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|House Wedge": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the malt-and-oak threads the wedge, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for a side — save the bottle for the main course.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Kansas City": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the KC — Caribbean-rum-cask-finished Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Caribbean Cask handles the strip with rum-cask-tropical character; Macallan 18 or Balvenie 21 Portwood are the featured Sherried Speyside-KC matches.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Loaded Potato": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the scotch depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; the boldness pushes slightly past what the soup asks for.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Lobster Mac": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the rum-cask depth meets the lobster mac, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; sweet character distinctive but not driving.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Market Fish": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask on the steak course instead.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Mocha Creme": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with mocha creme — rum-cask-finished Speyside against coffee-chocolate dessert. Works; Caribbean Cask's vanilla-caramel threads the mocha; Balvenie 21 Portwood's port-cask is the featured mocha match.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Mushroom Bisque": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the rum-cask depth meets the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the umami. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Mushrooms": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with mushrooms — Caribbean-rum-cask Speyside against earthy umami. Strong; Caribbean Cask's tropical-sweet character threads the mushrooms at distinctive rum-cask register.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the PB brownie — rum-cask-finished 14yr Speyside against peanut butter and cocoa. Excellent; Caribbean Cask's brown-sugar-and-vanilla is the featured Sherried Speyside-PB-brownie pairing — rum-cask sweetness marries PB fat and cocoa at a register Macallan's dried-fruit-sherry doesn't reach.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Porterhouse": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the porterhouse — 14yr rum-cask-finished Speyside against strip-and-filet. Works; Caribbean Cask frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Prime Tartare": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's caramel-honey character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Rainbow Trout": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask belongs on the steak course.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Roast Half Chicken": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the scotch backbone matches the roast chicken, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the plate. Works; the pour's delicacy doesn't overpower the soup, but doesn't particularly elevate it either.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the rum-cask depth meets the chickpea soup, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; BIB slightly over-specified.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the malt-driven body wraps the garlic spinach, and the honey rounds the side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Seafood Tower": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask for the steak course.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Seared Scallops": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with seared scallops — rum-cask-finished Speyside against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Seasonal Soup": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the seasonal soup, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; curated single-barrel covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Seasonal Vegetables": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the scotch depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; extended-aged premium wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Shrimp Bisque": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the malt-and-oak threads the shrimp bisque, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Shrimp Cocktail": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with shrimp cocktail — Caribbean-rum-cask Speyside against cold shrimp. Avoid; Scotch wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Swordfish": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask for the steak course.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|The Tomahawk": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask -- Speyside, rum-finished, caramel. The rum-cask depth meets the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; decennial-release complexity sits with richness.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Tomato Basil": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the malt-driven body wraps the tomato basil, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Truffle Fries": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with truffle fries — Caribbean-rum-cask Speyside against truffle-parmesan. Works; Caribbean Cask's tropical-sweetness meets the earthy-rich at distinctive register; Macallan or Aberlour's sherry-forward profile is more traditional.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask on the steak course instead.",
  "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the scotch backbone matches the curry, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; bonded weight slightly over-specified.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Asparagus": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the Highland register carries the asparagus, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; blend character covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with the au gratin — hand-picked American Oak against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Strong; Balvenie 16's depth handles the richness at collector register.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Beignets": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the malt-and-oak threads the beignets, and the malt edge frames the dessert. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Bone Marrow": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with bone marrow — American Oak Speyside against umami fat. Works; Balvenie 16's single-barrel depth sits alongside marrow comfortably; Fernet Branca remains the featured marrow pairing.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Bone-In Filet": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with the bone-in filet — hand-picked First Fill American Oak Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; the single-barrel vanilla-forward depth frames the cut with collector-register character.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Broccoli Cheddar": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the scotch backbone matches the broccoli cheddar, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Broccolini": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's First Fill American Oak reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the scotch depth settles into the broccolini, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; boutique covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Brussels and Belly": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with Brussels + Belly — single-barrel Speyside against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; Balvenie 16's depth meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Burrata": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with burrata — hand-picked First Fill American Oak against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Butternut Squash": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the scotch backbone matches the squash bisque, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a soup; save the rare bottle.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet hand-picked First Fill American Oak. Balvenie 16's deeper vanilla and American-Oak character marry the cake's spice-and-raisins with collector-register gravitas — where Balvenie 12 Am Oak opens the conversation, 16 finishes it. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Cheesecake": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with cheesecake — hand-picked First Fill American Oak against custard dessert. Excellent; Balvenie 16's deeper vanilla threads cheesecake with single-barrel character — Balvenie 12 opens this pairing, 16 deepens it.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Chilean Seabass": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's First Fill American Oak swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Balvenie 16 Single Barrel for the steak course.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Chocolate Brownie": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brownie, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; Sinatra Select handles the richness at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Chocolate Cake": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the chocolate cake, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; Gran Cru's brioche meets the glaze at refined register.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Clam Chowder": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the Highland register carries the chowder, and the oak threads the cream. Works; OGD 16 frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Cowboy Ribeye": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with the cowboy ribeye — hand-picked American Oak against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Balvenie 16 handles the ribeye with single-barrel vanilla depth; Fiddich 18's Oloroso is better for heaviest cuts.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Crab Cake": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with crab cake — collector-register Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Creamed Spinach": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with creamed spinach — First Fill American Oak against dairy-rich side. Strong; Balvenie 16's vanilla rides the cream with collector-register depth.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Creamy Potato": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's First Fill American Oak finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the scotch depth settles into the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Creme Brulee": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the Highland register carries the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; gentle match.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Escargot": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's First Fill American Oak leans against the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the scotch depth settles into the buttered escargot, and the malt edge frames the starter. Strong; Fortuna frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Faroe Island Salmon": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's First Fill American Oak smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Balvenie 16 Single Barrel lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets First Fill American Oak, hand-picked single barrel (fewer than 300 bottles per barrel), deeper vanilla than the 12. Balvenie 16's single-barrel depth and American-Oak vanilla are the collector-register Speyside-filet pairing — where Balvenie 12 Am Oak is handcrafted, the 16 is hand-selected. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|French Onion": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the malt-and-oak threads the French onion, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; Discovery complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Grilled Caesar": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with the grilled Caesar — hand-picked American Oak against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Balvenie 16's vanilla-depth meets the char; Cragganmore 12's subtle smoke leads the Speyside-Caesar pairing.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Gumbo": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the scotch backbone matches the gumbo, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; the boldness pushes slightly past what the soup asks for.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the scotch depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak threads the caramelized sugar. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|House Wedge": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with the House Wedge — hand-picked American Oak against iceberg and bleu. Works; Balvenie 16 is over-specified for the wedge — save for the filet or dessert course.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Kansas City": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with the KC — First Fill American Oak against an 18oz strip. Strong; Balvenie 16's depth handles the strip at collector register; Fiddich 18's Oloroso depth is still the featured Speyside-KC match.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Loaded Potato": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the loaded potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the Millennium's register overshoots the dessert's.",
  "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel|Lobster Mac": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the lobster mac, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; curated single-barrel slightly over-specified.",
