  "Beignets|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand with beignets — unblended vintage-style against fried dough. Works; Ferrand's craft honey-floral is a thoughtful finisher.",
  "Beignets|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club 2015 — prestige grower Champagne with citrus, toasted brioche, and creamy texture. The vintage depth cuts beignets' fried-dough weight and the brioche complements pastry directly. Works as elegant Champagne for dessert — though younger NV vintages suit beignets more economically.",
  "Beignets|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the high-rye spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Beignets|Pineau des Charentes": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac finish. The warm nutty Cognac clings to hot choux-fried dough and the honey lifts powdered-sugar. Peak Pineau for beignets — honey-on-sugar on the light airy bite. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Beignets|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pio Cesare Barbaresco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Beignets|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth with beignets — softer Plymouth-style against fried dough. Works; Plymouth's sweet notes make the juniper-sugar pairing more harmonious than Bombay's angular version.",
  "Beignets|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo on the steak course instead.",
  "Beignets|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery 2004 Cuvée Louise — 20-year aged Champagne. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich. The aged brioche complements beignets' fried-dough pastry directly and honey plays against powdered sugar. Works as occasion dessert Champagne — though this bottle deserves a more savory course.",
  "Beignets|Porterhouse": "After the Porterhouse, the beignets -- the dual strip-and-tender yields to warm choux and powdered sugar, the dipping sauce closing light. Works; the table-share steak hands off to a measured close.",
  "Beignets|Prime Tartare": "The tartare and the beignets -- the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami bookends with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Beignets|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Beignets|RAEN Royal St Robert": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "Beignets|Rainbow Trout": "After the trout, the beignets -- the delicate flesh yields to warm choux and powdered sugar, the dipping sauce closing light. Strong; the lighter main and the light close share register, the meal stays composed.",
  "Beignets|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Spanish rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit, clean acid. The rosé fruit plays against the beignet's choux-and-sugar profile; bubbles lift the fried dough. Peak pour when guest wants sparkling that's less sweet than Moscato to finish the meal. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Beignets|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the approachable Irish character carries the beignets, and the gentle barley note frames the dessert. Works; boutique character doesn't drive.",
  "Beignets|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the malt-grain blend handles the beignets, and the grain register softens the dessert. Works; the pour's assertiveness is a bit much for the soup but doesn't clash.",
  "Beignets|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon depth settles into the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "Beignets|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Rémy VSOP with beignets — elegant Grande Champagne against fried dough. Works; Rémy's refinement is over-specified for the casual dessert.",
  "Beignets|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo's rested-agave body leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the barrel-rested body handles the beignets, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; the wine-cask character adds dimension but not standout.",
  "Beignets|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reyka Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Beignets|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the caramel-spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Remus 15 handles dessert density at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Beignets|Roast Half Chicken": "After the chicken, the beignets -- the crisp-skin roast yields to warm choux and powdered sugar, dipping sauce closing light. Works; the table closes easy after a comfort main.",
  "Beignets|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea and the beignets -- two registers in contrast, the meal earns its shape. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Beignets|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs — Le Mesnil Grand Cru Chardonnay. Chalk, lemon curd, green apple, no dosage. The austere acidity cuts beignets' fried-dough weight and the precision plays against powdered sugar. Works as sommelier Champagne for dessert — though this bottle shines more with savory courses.",
  "Beignets|Roku Gin": "Roku Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- gin's herbal edge has no foil against warm pastry sugar — sharp meets sweet, neither wins. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Roku Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Beignets|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum -- Solera 23, Guatemalan, honey-vanilla-caramel. The Solera honey wraps the beignets, and the caramel rounds the dessert. Excellent; the herbal-bitter-complex digestif cuts through sugar-and-fried-dough at 55% register — textbook green-digestif move. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Beignets|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon backbone carries the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Beignets|Salt-Cured Halibut": "After the halibut, the beignets -- the salt-cured firm flesh yields to warm choux and powdered sugar, dipping sauce closing the meal light. Works; the cure-and-pastry contrast lifts the close cleanly.",
  "Beignets|Sambuca": "Beignets: warm sugar-dusted pastry meets Sambuca. Excellent; the sweet-anise character plays with fresh sugar-dust at Italian-digestif register — sweeter pairing than Mata Hari's 60% punch.",
  "Beignets|San Simeon Stormwatch": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the San Simeon Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "Beignets|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Beignets|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The garlic spinach and the beignets -- the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens hands off cleanly to the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: bright meets sweet, the contrast stays light. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Beignets|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes — Sémillon-Sauvignon Blanc with noble rot (botrytis cinerea), apricot, honey, orange marmalade, luscious unctuous, bright acid. The honey clings to hot choux-fried dough and the bright acid cuts powdered-sugar sweetness. Peak Sauternes for beignets — botrytis-and-acid lift on the airy bite. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Beignets|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The anise-rye edge clashes with powdered-sugar beignets; spirit-forward classics belong pre-dinner or with tartare/marrow. Save Sazerac for those moments; route dessert guest to sparkling or coffee cocktails. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Beignets|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the dry rye body wraps the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Beignets|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the caramel-spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Beignets|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Nebbiolo — tar, dried rose, firm tannin. Barolo crushes the beignet on every axis — tannin against fried choux is incoherent. Never pair. Route beignet guest to sparkling wine, tawny, or a coffee cocktail.",
  "Beignets|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads — Rheingau Riesling, apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry fruit complements beignets' berry jam directly and the acid cuts fried-dough weight; petrol adds unexpected dessert complexity. Strong Riesling-with-beignets pairing — off-dry fruit meets sweet pastry.",
  "Beignets|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Beignets|Seafood Tower": "Seafood Tower and the beignets -- bright meets sweet, the contrast stays light. Works; the bookends compose neutrally.",
  "Beignets|Seared Scallops": "The scallops alongside the beignets -- the entree yields to the dessert, the meal arc reads on-key. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Beignets|Seasonal Soup": "The seasonal soup and the beignets -- the seasonal soup's house seasonal build bookends with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Beignets|Seasonal Vegetables": "Seasonal Vegetables and the beignets -- a quiet call, the meal carries without strain. Works; the meal closes cleanly from the side.",
  "Beignets|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Beignets|Shafer 1.5": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Shafer 1.5 on the steak course instead.",
  "Beignets|Shafer Hillside Select": "Shafer Hillside Select's Stags Leap allocated Cab power flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Shafer Hillside Select for the steak course.",
  "Beignets|Shrimp Bisque": "Shrimp Bisque and the beignets -- rich cream gives way to sweet, the close lands generously. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Beignets|Shrimp Cocktail": "Shrimp Cocktail and the beignets -- bright meets sweet, the contrast stays light. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Beignets|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a light spirit. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Siete Leguas Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Beignets|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the barrel-rested body handles the beignets, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Beignets|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon's American-oak Cab character jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Beignets|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Simon Family Estate Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Simon Family Estate Reserve lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Beignets|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "Spottswoode Lyndenhurst's Napa estate Cab with structured cassis jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Spottswoode Lyndenhurst on the steak course instead.",
  "Beignets|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Beignets|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a white wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc for the steak course.",
  "Beignets|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the St Supéry Élu Meritage for the steak course.",
  "Beignets|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the caramel-spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; curated character over-specified.",
  "Beignets|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Stoli Elit Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Beignets|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp NZ Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc for the steak course.",
  "Beignets|Swordfish": "After the swordfish, the beignets -- the meaty grilled steak yields to warm choux and powdered sugar, the dipping sauce closing the meal light. Works; the table closes easy after the firm main.",
  "Beignets|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 with beignets — premium Tanq against fried dough. Works; Tanq 10's citrus-chamomile is a refined finish — more martini-gin finesse than flagship can offer.",
  "Beignets|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray with beignets — high-proof London Dry against fried dough. Works; the juniper-sugar contrast is historically British-pub territory.",
  "Beignets|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Classic Douro Tawny — Taylor Fladgate (founded 1692), smooth balanced, dried fruit, almond, warm clean finish. The warm clean finish clings to hot choux-fried dough and the almond lifts powdered-sugar simplicity. Peak heritage-Tawny for beignets — balanced lift on the light airy bite. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Beignets|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Flagship Vintage Port — Taylor Fladgate bottle, extraordinarily rich, dark fruit, chocolate, tobacco, minutes-long evolving finish. The extraordinarily rich structure overwhelms hot choux-fried dough and powdered-sugar simplicity. Strong flagship-Vintage for beignets — the celebrated bottle is over-spent on the light airy bite.",
  "Beignets|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the aged-agave weight wraps the beignets, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Beignets|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Beignets|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico for the steak course.",
  "Beignets|The BG": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build plays cleanly against the beignet's warm sugar-dusted pastry — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Beignets|The Happy Wife": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build finds neutral with the beignet's warm sugar-dusted pastry — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Beignets|The Manhattan": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build reads alongside the beignet's warm sugar-dusted pastry — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Beignets|The Prisoner Red Blend": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour The Prisoner Red Blend on the steak course instead.",
  "Beignets|The Tomahawk": "After the Tomahawk, the beignets -- the showpiece cut yields to warm fried choux and powdered sugar, the dipping sauce closing the meal cleanly. Works; the table closes light after the table-defining main.",
  "Beignets|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the rye backbone drives the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Beignets|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon weight handles the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Beignets|Titos Vodka": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a vodka. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Titos Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Beignets|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the beignets, and the precision rounds the dessert. Works; pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Beignets|Tomato Basil": "Tomato Basil and the beignets -- bright meets sweet, the contrast stays light. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Beignets|Transfusion": "Transfusion's crisp citrus-ginger build plays cleanly against the beignet's warm sugar-dusted pastry — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Beignets|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the beignets, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; Ultra's ceremonial depth composed with dessert.",
  "Beignets|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries and the beignets -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Beignets|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s gentle Irish blend reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the Irish whiskey body sits with the beignets, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the dessert. Works; bold character slightly over-specified for the soup.",
  "Beignets|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "After the tuna, the beignets -- the seared-rare yellowfin yields to warm choux and powdered sugar, the dipping sauce closing light. Strong; the cleaner main and the lighter close share register across the courses.",
  "Beignets|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas and the beignets -- separate registers share the plate, the contrast settles the build. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Beignets|Venge Family Reserve": "Venge Family Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Venge Family Reserve lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Beignets|Venge Scout's Honor": "Venge Scout's Honor's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Venge Scout's Honor on the steak course instead.",
  "Beignets|Venge Silencieux": "Venge Silencieux's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Venge Silencieux on the steak course instead.",
  "Beignets|Vesper": "Vesper's crisp citrus-juniper build sits alongside the beignet's warm sugar-dusted pastry — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Beignets|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut — yellow label Champagne. Ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse, lively acidity. The biscuit complements beignets' fried-dough pastry directly and the peach plays against berry jam. Strong celebration Champagne for beignets — everyone's favorite meets dessert course.",
  "Beignets|Vieux Carré": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build sits alongside the beignet's warm sugar-dusted pastry — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Beignets|Villon Cognac": "Villon with beignets — contemporary cognac against fried dough. Works; Villon's approachable sweetness is a casual-dessert finisher.",
  "Beignets|Vin Santo": "Tuscan Vin Santo — Trebbiano-Malvasia straw-dried, sealed-barrel years, amber, honey, dried apricot, walnut, nutty oxidative. The honey clings to hot choux-fried dough and the walnut lifts powdered-sugar. Peak Vin Santo for beignets — traditional Tuscan dessert wine on the airy bite. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Beignets|Weller 107": "Weller 107's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon depth settles into the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Beignets|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the caramel-spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; complexity wasted on salad.",
  "Beignets|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the oak depth lays under the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a composed but not remarkable match.",
  "Beignets|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the brown-spirit warmth threads the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; BIB over-specified for side.",
  "Beignets|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Wheatley Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Beignets|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the high-rye spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; spice over-specified for side's character.",
  "Beignets|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the rye spice cuts the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "Beignets|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the dry rye body wraps the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pour's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Beignets|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the rye backbone drives the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; BIB slightly over-specified.",
  "Beignets|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the rye spice cuts the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a composed but not remarkable match.",
  "Beignets|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the high-rye spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for the dessert's register.",
  "Beignets|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon weight handles the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a soup — save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Beignets|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pour's sweetness meets the dessert without pushing past.",
  "Beignets|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon body sits with the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Beignets|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the high-rye spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Beignets|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the high-rye spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Beignets|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the beignets, and the precision rounds the dessert. Works; AE meets the char at wine-finish register.",
  "Beignets|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the beignets, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the dessert. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th handles the richness at annual-release register.",
  "Beignets|Yellow Chartreuse": "Beignets: warm sugar-dusted pastry meets Yellow Chartreuse. Excellent; the honey-saffron-herbal character plays with sugar-dust at Carthusian-sister register — sweeter, softer Chartreuse pairing.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Asparagus": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality stays alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Beignets": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Belvedere Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Bone Marrow": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality sits beside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Bone-In Filet": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- clean vodka reads thin against the cut's weight — needs a wine or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Belvedere Vodka for another course.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Broccoli Cheddar": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Broccolini": "Belvedere Vodka's neutral vodka register reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the unflavored body sits with the broccolini, and the crisp profile carries the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Brussels and Belly": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality leans against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Burrata": "Belvedere with burrata — aromatic Polish rye against fresh dairy. Excellent; Belvedere's vanilla notes complement cream while the rye pepper adds textural contrast.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Butternut Squash": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality sits beside the squash soup's silky sweet — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Carrot Cake": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Belvedere Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Cheesecake": "Belvedere with cheesecake — aromatic rye vodka against custard. Works; the vanilla notes echo the dessert at distinctive register.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Chilean Seabass": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality sits with the seabass's buttery flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Chocolate Brownie": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Belvedere Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Chocolate Cake": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a vodka. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Belvedere Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Clam Chowder": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Cowboy Ribeye": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Belvedere Vodka belongs on another course.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Crab Cake": "Belvedere with crab cake — Polish rye against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Belvedere's subtle pepper adds dimension to the crab that softer vodkas miss.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Creamed Spinach": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Creamy Potato": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality stays alongside the potato soup's silky body — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Creme Brulee": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Belvedere Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Escargot": "Belvedere with escargot — aromatic Polish rye against garlic-butter snails. Excellent; Belvedere's pepper cuts butter harder than the wheat-based siblings can, lifting the garlic notes cleanly.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Faroe Island Salmon": "Belvedere with the salmon — Polish rye against oily salmon. Strong; the rye pepper lifts the fish's richness with dimension the softer vodkas lack.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Filet Mignon": "Belvedere with the filet — aromatic Polish rye against tenderloin. Works; vodka still over-specified, but Belvedere's rye character is the least wrong vodka for a steak cut.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|French Onion": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Grilled Caesar": "Belvedere with the grilled Caesar — rye vodka against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Belvedere's character engages the char; Ketel's citrus still wins this pairing for vodka.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Gumbo": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality reads alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|House Wedge": "Belvedere with the House Wedge — Polish rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; the rye pepper engages the bleu sharpness differently than wheat vodkas do.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Kansas City": "Belvedere with the KC — Polish rye against an 18oz strip. Avoid; even rye-character vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon or rye.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Loaded Potato": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Lobster Mac": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Market Fish": "Belvedere with the market fish — Polish rye against featured fish. Works; Belvedere's character handles fish with more dimension than the softer vodkas.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Mocha Creme": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a vodka. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Belvedere Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Mushroom Bisque": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality reads alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Mushrooms": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Belvedere Vodka lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Porterhouse": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Belvedere Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Prime Tartare": "* Belvedere Vodka's silky vodka body meets head-on the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the unflavored body sits with the tartare, and the unobtrusive backbone supports the starter. Peak vodka for the starter — clean neutrality lets the cold-app proteins lead.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Rainbow Trout": "Belvedere Vodka -- Polish rye-based vodka, premium clean-slightly-sweet profile. The unflavored body sits with the trout, and the crisp profile carries the plate. Strong; Belvedere's character is distinctive but Goose's softness is the flagship tower choice.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Roast Half Chicken": "Belvedere with the chicken — single-estate rye against roasted poultry. Works; Belvedere's rye character gives chicken more support than wheat vodkas offer.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality matches the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality leans against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Seafood Tower": "Belvedere with the seafood tower — single-estate rye against the raw bar. Strong; Belvedere's character is distinctive but Goose's softness is the flagship tower choice.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Seared Scallops": "Belvedere with seared scallops — Polish rye vodka against scallop sweetness. Excellent; the slight rye pepper threads the scallop's natural sweetness at a distinctive register — more character than Tito's round or Goose's silk.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Seasonal Soup": "Belvedere Vodka's unflavored crystalline pour sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the unflavored body sits with the seasonal soup, and the crisp profile carries the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Seasonal Vegetables": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality sits alongside the seasonal garden plate — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Shrimp Bisque": "Belvedere with shrimp bisque — aromatic Polish rye against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; the pepper cuts the bisque's richness where wheat vodkas ride it.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Shrimp Cocktail": "Belvedere with shrimp cocktail — single-estate rye vodka against cold shrimp. Excellent; aromatic rye character brings brightness the wheat vodkas don't match.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Swordfish": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality reads alongside the swordfish's meaty flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|The Tomahawk": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Belvedere Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Tomato Basil": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality leans against the tomato basil's bright acid — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Truffle Fries": "Belvedere Vodka: Polish rye-based vodka, premium clean-slightly-sweet profile. Excellent on truffle fries; clean vodka frames truffle-salt-fat at Polish-rye register. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Belvedere Vodka -- Polish rye-based vodka, premium clean-slightly-sweet profile. The unflavored body sits with the tuna, and the crisp profile carries the plate. Strong; the silkiness sits with butter; Belvedere's pepper still cuts the dish harder.",
  "Belvedere Vodka|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Blade & Bow|Asparagus": "Blade & Bow's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the asparagus, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; the unique maturation story adds interest to a gentle dessert match.",
  "Blade & Bow|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Blade & Bow with au gratin potatoes — heritage blend against cheese-and-cream. Strong; honey and vanilla sit with richness cleanly.",
  "Blade & Bow|Beignets": "Blade & Bow's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the brown-spirit warmth threads the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Blade & Bow|Bone Marrow": "Blade & Bow with bone marrow — Stitzel-Weller revival blend against umami fat. The heritage whiskey in the blend gives the starter a different vocabulary than pure-distillery pours. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Blade & Bow|Bone-In Filet": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth matches register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the oak depth lays under the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak frames the cut. Strong; the 10-year oak gives the side backbone distinct from wheated approaches.",
  "Blade & Bow|Broccoli Cheddar": "Blade & Bow's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon body sits with the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the pour's assertiveness is a bit much for the soup but doesn't clash.",
  "Blade & Bow|Broccolini": "Blade & Bow's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the caramel-spice register matches the broccolini, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Blade & Bow|Brussels and Belly": "Blade & Bow with Brussels + Belly — Stitzel-Weller revival blend meets maple-glazed pork belly. The blend's honey threads through the dish's maple; a heritage-story small-plate pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Blade & Bow|Burrata": "Blade & Bow with burrata — fresh dairy against blended bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Blade & Bow|Butternut Squash": "Blade & Bow's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak depth lays under the squash bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; richer profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Blade & Bow|Carrot Cake": "Blade & Bow with carrot cake — Stitzel-Weller revival blend against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; the blend's honey finds the cake's sweetness at matched registers.",
  "Blade & Bow|Cheesecake": "Blade & Bow with cheesecake — blend's honey against custard. Works; gentle match at matched registers.",
  "Blade & Bow|Chilean Seabass": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Blade & Bow lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Blade & Bow|Chocolate Brownie": "Blade & Bow with the chocolate brownie — heritage blend against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast.",
  "Blade & Bow|Chocolate Cake": "Blade & Bow's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon depth settles into the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "Blade & Bow|Clam Chowder": "Blade & Bow's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon backbone carries the chowder, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; concentrated blend slightly over-specified.",
  "Blade & Bow|Cowboy Ribeye": "Blade & Bow with the Cowboy Ribeye — blended heritage against cap fat. Strong; the blend's honey is slightly underpowered for the ribeye's scale — Weller 12 or Knob Creek bring more grip.",
  "Blade & Bow|Crab Cake": "Blade & Bow with crab cake — blended bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Blade & Bow|Creamed Spinach": "Blade & Bow with creamed spinach — blended bourbon's honey against dairy. Strong; a composed heritage-blend match.",
  "Blade & Bow|Creamy Potato": "Blade & Bow's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the caramel-spice register matches the potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Blade & Bow|Creme Brulee": "Blade & Bow's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon backbone carries the brulee, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the distinctive character is underused by the salad's simplicity.",
  "Blade & Bow|Escargot": "Blade & Bow's caramel-honey character lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Blade & Bow|Faroe Island Salmon": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Blade & Bow lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Blade & Bow|Filet Mignon": "Blade & Bow with the filet — Diageo's Stitzel-Weller revival brand. Blended bourbon with some old-stock Stitzel-Weller heritage whiskey in the blend. Honey, vanilla, caramel; the bottle's five keys reference the Stitzel-Weller distillery heritage. A storytelling pour for the filet. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Blade & Bow|French Onion": "Blade & Bow's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the French onion, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; Prohibition narrative wasted on salad.",
  "Blade & Bow|Grilled Caesar": "Blade & Bow with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine against blended bourbon. Works; a composed but not remarkable match.",
  "Blade & Bow|Gumbo": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon depth settles into the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; BH Red Wine Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Blade & Bow|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the brown-spirit warmth threads the honey carrots, and the oak threads the caramelized sugar. Works; annual-release complexity over-specified.",
  "Blade & Bow|House Wedge": "Blade & Bow with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against heritage blend. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "Blade & Bow|Kansas City": "Blade & Bow with the KC — blended bourbon with Stitzel-Weller heritage against the strip's marbling. The honey-forward blend carries the char; a heritage-story pour for everyday premium orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Blade & Bow|Loaded Potato": "Blade & Bow's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the oak depth lays under the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Blade & Bow|Lobster Mac": "Blade & Bow's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon depth settles into the lobster mac, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; Boss Hog's allocation story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Blade & Bow|Market Fish": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Blade & Bow for the steak course.",
  "Blade & Bow|Mocha Creme": "Blade & Bow's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the caramel-spice register matches the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; oak sits beside the dessert gently.",
  "Blade & Bow|Mushroom Bisque": "Blade & Bow's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Blade & Bow|Mushrooms": "Blade & Bow with the mushrooms — blended bourbon against earthy umami. Honey and vanilla layer into mushroom depth; distinct from pure-distillery pours. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Blade & Bow|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Blade & Bow's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon backbone carries the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Blade & Bow|Porterhouse": "Blade & Bow with the porterhouse — heritage blend across strip and filet. Strong; the blend covers both cuts cleanly at the everyday premium register.",
  "Blade & Bow|Prime Tartare": "Blade & Bow's caramel-honey character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Blade & Bow|Rainbow Trout": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Blade & Bow lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Blade & Bow|Roast Half Chicken": "Blade & Bow's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the roast chicken, and the caramel rounds the plate. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "Blade & Bow|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Blade & Bow|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Blade & Bow lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Blade & Bow|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Blade & Bow's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the garlic spinach, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; HH 20 frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Blade & Bow|Seafood Tower": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Blade & Bow lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Blade & Bow|Seared Scallops": "Blade & Bow with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against blend. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Blade & Bow|Seasonal Soup": "Blade & Bow's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon backbone carries the seasonal soup, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; Gran Cru's brioche meets the glaze at refined register.",
  "Blade & Bow|Seasonal Vegetables": "Blade & Bow's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Blade & Bow|Shrimp Bisque": "Blade & Bow with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood against blended bourbon. Works neutrally.",
  "Blade & Bow|Shrimp Cocktail": "Blade & Bow with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against blended bourbon. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Blade & Bow|Swordfish": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Blade & Bow for the steak course.",
  "Blade & Bow|The Tomahawk": "Blade & Bow's caramel-honey character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the caramel-and-oak weight rises with the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Blade & Bow|Tomato Basil": "Blade & Bow's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the brown-spirit warmth threads the tomato basil, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Blade & Bow|Truffle Fries": "Blade & Bow with truffle fries — heritage-blend honey against truffle-parmesan. The storytelling pour gives the side a narrative beyond flavor; a conversation-starter everyday premium pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Blade & Bow|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Blade & Bow for the steak course.",
  "Blade & Bow|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Blade & Bow's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon backbone carries the curry, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the bourbon-and-side match sits without pulling focus.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Asparagus": "Blanton's Gold's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon weight handles the asparagus, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; the pour's brightness is a gentle counterpoint to the dessert's richness.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Blanton's Gold with au gratin potatoes — richer single-barrel against cheese-and-cream. Strong; the 103-proof sits with the richness at the bolder register.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Beignets": "Blanton's Gold's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the brown-spirit warmth threads the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Bone Marrow": "Blanton's Gold with bone marrow — 103-proof against umami fat. The extra concentration meets the marrow's intensity where Original's 93-proof balance doesn't quite reach. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Bone-In Filet": "Blanton's Gold's bold caramel-juniper character matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey character holds register with the tenderloin, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Broccoli Cheddar": "Blanton's Gold's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the oak depth lays under the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the 10-year's oak adds dimension but the match is still gentle.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Broccolini": "Blanton's Gold's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the caramel-spice register matches the broccolini, and the oak frames the side. Works; the pour's delicacy doesn't overpower the soup, but doesn't particularly elevate it either.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Brussels and Belly": "Blanton's Gold with Brussels + Belly — 103-proof Blanton's meets maple-glazed pork belly. The richer profile cuts the glaze with more authority than Original. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Burrata": "Blanton's Gold with burrata — fresh dairy against 103-proof. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Butternut Squash": "Blanton's Gold's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak depth lays under the squash bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Carrot Cake": "Blanton's Gold with carrot cake — 103-proof single-barrel against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; richer profile is slightly over-specified for the dessert.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Cheesecake": "Blanton's Gold with cheesecake — 103-proof Blanton's against clean custard. Works; richer profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Chilean Seabass": "Blanton's Gold's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Blanton's Gold belongs on the steak course.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Chocolate Brownie": "Blanton's Gold with the chocolate brownie — 103-proof richer Blanton's against cocoa. Works; the extra depth gives the brownie more than Blanton's Original does.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Chocolate Cake": "Blanton's Gold's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon depth settles into the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; concentrated blend slightly over-specified.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Clam Chowder": "Blanton's Gold's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon backbone carries the chowder, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the complex character gives the dessert a refined frame.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Cowboy Ribeye": "Blanton's Gold with the Cowboy Ribeye — 103-proof against cap fat. The richer Blanton's and extra proof handle the ribeye with more conviction than Original's 93-proof; the stronger single-barrel ribeye call. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Crab Cake": "Blanton's Gold with crab cake — 103-proof richer Blanton's against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Creamed Spinach": "Blanton's Gold with creamed spinach — 103-proof richer Blanton's against dairy. Strong; extra proof adds dimension at the premium register.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Creamy Potato": "Blanton's Gold's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the caramel-spice register matches the potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Creme Brulee": "Blanton's Gold's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon backbone carries the brulee, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the pour's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Escargot": "Blanton's Gold's bold caramel-juniper character lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Faroe Island Salmon": "Blanton's Gold's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Blanton's Gold for the steak course.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Filet Mignon": "Blanton's Gold with the filet — the 103-proof export-market Blanton's. Richer, bolder than Blanton's Original; the extra 10 proof points and deeper concentration give the filet a more assertive single-barrel frame. Premium-plus single-barrel for guests who want more depth than Original brings. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Blanton's Gold|French Onion": "Blanton's Gold's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the French onion, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Grilled Caesar": "Blanton's Gold with the grilled Caesar — 103-proof Blanton's against charred romaine. Strong; the extra proof earns this Caesar where Original only works.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Gumbo": "Blanton's Gold's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon depth settles into the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; neutral, not elevating.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Blanton's Gold's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon backbone carries the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; the pour's toasted warmth threads through the char without overpowering.",
  "Blanton's Gold|House Wedge": "Blanton's Gold with the House Wedge — richer single-barrel against iceberg and bleu. Works; the extra proof overpowers the salad.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Kansas City": "Blanton's Gold with the KC — 103-proof export Blanton's against the strip's marbling. The richer profile and extra proof carry the strip where Original is balanced; a bolder single-barrel strip call. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Loaded Potato": "Blanton's Gold's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon body sits with the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; collector-bottle depth gives ultra-premium character.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Lobster Mac": "Blanton's Gold's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon depth settles into the lobster mac, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; bold intensity slightly over-specified.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Market Fish": "Blanton's Gold's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Blanton's Gold for the steak course.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Mocha Creme": "Blanton's Gold's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the caramel-spice register matches the mocha creme, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the 10-year depth gives the dessert substantial premium frame.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Mushroom Bisque": "Blanton's Gold's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon weight handles the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; Portwood is over-specified for a side — save for main course.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Mushrooms": "Blanton's Gold with the mushrooms — richer Blanton's against earthy umami. The deeper single-barrel profile layers into mushroom depth with more weight than Original brings. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Blanton's Gold with the PB brownie — 103-proof single-barrel against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; the richer pour finds more with the dessert than Original brings.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Porterhouse": "Blanton's Gold with the porterhouse — 103-proof across strip and filet. Strong; the extra proof leans toward strip side — Blanton's Original is the more balanced porterhouse call.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Prime Tartare": "Blanton's Gold's bold caramel-juniper character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Blanton's Gold|Rainbow Trout": "Blanton's Gold's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Blanton's Gold on the steak course instead.",
