  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Clam Chowder": "Blood Oath Pact 9's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the oak depth lays under the chowder, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the premium pour is wasted on the salad's simplicity.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Cowboy Ribeye": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the Cowboy Ribeye — Pact 9 wine-cask blend against cap fat. The fruit-wine character layers over the bourbon blend with master-blender distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Crab Cake": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with crab cake — Pact 9 wine-cask against delicate crab. Avoid; save the Blood Oath bottle.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Creamed Spinach": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with creamed spinach — Pact 9 blend against dairy. Strong; wine-cask complexity sits with richness.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Creamy Potato": "Blood Oath Pact 9's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon backbone carries the potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Creme Brulee": "Blood Oath Pact 9's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the intensity overpowers this dish.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Escargot": "Blood Oath Pact 9's cherry-spice character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Faroe Island Salmon": "Blood Oath Pact 9's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Blood Oath Pact 9 for the steak course.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Filet Mignon": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the filet — Pact 9 wine-cask blend against the 10oz cut. Strong; annual-release capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|French Onion": "Blood Oath Pact 9's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Grilled Caesar": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the grilled Caesar — Pact 9 wine-cask against charred romaine. Works; fruit-cask doesn't drive.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Gumbo": "Blood Oath Pact 9's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon weight handles the gumbo, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; annual-release wasted on salad.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Blood Oath Pact 9's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|House Wedge": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the House Wedge — Pact 9 wine-cask against iceberg and bleu. Works; annual-release wasted on salad.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Kansas City": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the KC — master blender John Rempe's 9th annual Blood Oath, 2023 release from Lux Row. Three bourbons blended with a wine-cask finish component. The Pact 9 wine-cask character gives the strip a layered annual-release frame distinct from Pact 8's Mizunara approach. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Loaded Potato": "Blood Oath Pact 9's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon backbone carries the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Lobster Mac": "Blood Oath Pact 9's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the lobster mac, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; pour is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Market Fish": "Blood Oath Pact 9's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Blood Oath Pact 9 on the steak course instead.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Mocha Creme": "Blood Oath Pact 9's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon backbone carries the mocha creme, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Mushroom Bisque": "Blood Oath Pact 9's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the complex profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Mushrooms": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the mushrooms — Pact 9 wine-cask against earthy umami. Strong; fruit-cask layers into mushroom depth with annual-release distinctiveness.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the PB brownie — Pact 9 wine-cask against peanut butter and cocoa. The fruit-cask character gives the dessert layered annual-Pact depth. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Porterhouse": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the porterhouse — Pact 9 across strip and filet. Works; annual-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Prime Tartare": "Blood Oath Pact 9's cherry-spice character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Rainbow Trout": "Blood Oath Pact 9's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Blood Oath Pact 9 for the steak course.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Roast Half Chicken": "Blood Oath Pact 9's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Blood Oath Pact 9's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; 100-proof complexity sits beside the soup.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Blood Oath Pact 9's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Blood Oath Pact 9 on the steak course instead.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Blood Oath Pact 9's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the oak frames the side. Works; Caribbean Cask frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Seafood Tower": "Blood Oath Pact 9's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Blood Oath Pact 9 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Seared Scallops": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against Pact 9 blend. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Seasonal Soup": "Blood Oath Pact 9's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the light body doesn't overshoot the salad.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Seasonal Vegetables": "Blood Oath Pact 9's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; Voyage 23 handles the richness at ocean-finish register.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Shrimp Bisque": "Blood Oath Pact 9's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the bourbon weight handles the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; Remus 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Shrimp Cocktail": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against annual-release. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Swordfish": "Blood Oath Pact 9's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Blood Oath Pact 9 on the steak course instead.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|The Tomahawk": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the Tomahawk — Pact 9 against bone-in ribeye. Strong; annual-release wine-cask covers the cut with master-blender character.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Tomato Basil": "Blood Oath Pact 9's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon body sits with the tomato basil, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Truffle Fries": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with truffle fries — Pact 9 wine-cask blend against truffle-parmesan. The fruit-finish character gives the side layered annual-release dimension. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Blood Oath Pact 9's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Blood Oath Pact 9 for the steak course.",
  "Blood Oath Pact 9|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Blood Oath Pact 9's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the oak depth lays under the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; oak sits beside the dessert gently.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Asparagus": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals holds with the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals sits alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Beignets": "Bluecoat with beignets — Philadelphia 4-citrus craft against fried dough. Works; Bluecoat's citrus is a functional finisher; Empress 1908's rose is more distinctive.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Bone Marrow": "Bluecoat with bone marrow — American citrus gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Bluecoat Gin for another course.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the floral edge frames the broccoli cheddar, and the juniper threads the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Broccolini": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals leans against the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals plays cleanly against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Burrata": "Bluecoat with burrata — 4-organic-citrus Philly gin against fresh dairy. Excellent; Bluecoat's citrus lift brightens the cream cleanly at American-craft register.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Butternut Squash": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals leans against the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Carrot Cake": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Bluecoat Gin for the steak course.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Cheesecake": "Bluecoat with cheesecake — 4-organic-citrus craft against custard. Works; Bluecoat's citrus threads the cheesecake at American-artisan register.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Bluecoat Gin's gin lift with floral-and-pepper sits alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the floral edge frames the seabass, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Neptunia's briny character engages the bleu with distinctive maritime profile.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Bluecoat Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Bluecoat Gin for the steak course.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Clam Chowder": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals sits beside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Bluecoat with the cowboy ribeye — Philly patriotic-artisan against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Crab Cake": "Bluecoat with crab cake — Philadelphia American dry gin with 4 organic citrus peels (lemon, orange, and more). Excellent; Bluecoat's citrus-forward patriotic-artisan profile frames delicate crab with bright American-citrus character — more lifting than Aviation's lavender softness.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Creamy Potato": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Creme Brulee": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Bluecoat Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Escargot": "Bluecoat with escargot — patriotic-artisan citrus gin against garlic-butter snails. Works; Bluecoat's citrus meets butter with American-craft character; Mahón's Mediterranean juniper cuts it harder.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Bluecoat with the salmon — Philadelphia citrus-forward craft against oily salmon. Excellent; Bluecoat's 4-citrus profile is the featured Smooth/Citrus Craft Gin-salmon pairing — citrus-and-fish is textbook, Bluecoat brings it with American-artisan distinction.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Filet Mignon": "Bluecoat with the filet — Philadelphia citrus-forward gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|French Onion": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the orange brightens the caramel. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Bluecoat with the grilled Caesar — 4-organic-citrus American dry gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Bluecoat's citrus-forward profile is the featured Smooth/Citrus Craft Gin-Caesar pairing — parallel to Tanq 10's citrus approach from London Dry, the patriotic-artisan equivalent.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Gumbo": "Bluecoat Gin's coriander-and-juniper register leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the floral edge frames the gumbo, and the juniper threads the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals sits alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|House Wedge": "Bluecoat with the House Wedge — Philly patriotic-artisan gin against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Bluecoat's citrus cuts the bleu with American-artisan distinction.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Kansas City": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Bluecoat Gin for another course.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Loaded Potato": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals sits alongside the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Lobster Mac": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Market Fish": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals plays cleanly against the fish's clean flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Mocha Creme": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Bluecoat Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals sits alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Mushrooms": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Bluecoat Gin for the steak course.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Porterhouse": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Bluecoat Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Prime Tartare": "Bluecoat with the tartare — 4-organic-citrus gin against raw beef. Strong; Bluecoat's citrus brightens tartare's minerality at American-craft register.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Bluecoat Gin's herbal-botanical edge reads register-aligned with the trout's delicate flesh: the bright botanical edge handles the trout, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Bluecoat Gin earns its place next to the trout.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Bluecoat with the chicken — 4-organic-citrus Philly craft against roasted poultry. Strong; Bluecoat's citrus supports chicken at American-artisan register.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals lifts the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals leans against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Seafood Tower": "Bluecoat with the seafood tower — Philadelphia citrus-forward craft against the raw bar. Strong; Bluecoat's 4-citrus profile lifts the tower at American-artisan register; Gray Whale's ocean-botanicals is still the featured craft-tower match.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Seared Scallops": "Bluecoat with seared scallops — Philadelphia citrus-forward craft against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Bluecoat's 4-citrus profile mirrors scallop sweetness with American-artisan character.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals sits alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals sits beside the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Bluecoat with shrimp bisque — Philadelphia citrus-forward craft against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Bluecoat's 4-citrus profile cuts the bisque's richness with American-artisan lift.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Bluecoat with shrimp cocktail — 4-citrus Philly organic craft against cold shrimp. Excellent; Bluecoat's American citrus-forward profile elevates the classic cold app with patriotic-artisan lift.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Swordfish": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals reads alongside the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|The Tomahawk": "Bluecoat with the Tomahawk — citrus-forward craft against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Tomato Basil": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals reads alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Truffle Fries": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals sits beside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals reads alongside the tuna's seared rare crust — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bluecoat Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Asparagus": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis sits beside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: structured red meets dairy-driven side: the bold tannin scrubs the cream, the dark fruit lifts the indulgence into balance. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Beignets": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Bodega Noemia A Lisa on the steak course instead.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Bone Marrow": "Patagonia Argentina Malbec — Noemia A Lisa, cooler-climate than Mendoza, blackberry, violet, graphite, full body, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts roasted-marrow unctuousness without tearing and the graphite echoes beefy-bone depth. Peak Patagonian-Malbec for bone marrow — silky polish on the richness. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Bone-In Filet": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the velvety body lifts the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Broccoli Cheddar": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the structured body wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; the dessert sits cleanly against the meal's register.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Broccolini": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Burrata": "Never pair Noemia with burrata — blackberry, violet, and full body clash with fresh cream and the graphite buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Butternut Squash": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis holds with the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the floral notes lift the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Carrot Cake": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Bodega Noemia A Lisa for the steak course.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Cheesecake": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Bodega Noemia A Lisa for the steak course.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Chilean Seabass": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Bodega Noemia A Lisa for the steak course.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Chocolate Brownie": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Bodega Noemia A Lisa for the steak course.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Chocolate Cake": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Bodega Noemia A Lisa for the steak course.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Clam Chowder": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis stays alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the floral edge frames the shellfish. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Cowboy Ribeye": "Patagonia Argentina Malbec — Noemia A Lisa, cooler-climate than Mendoza, blackberry, violet, graphite, full body, soft tannin. The full body matches Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the soft tannin won't tear against rendered fat. Peak Patagonian-Malbec for ribeye — silky elegance on the marbled cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Crab Cake": "Never pair Noemia with crab cake — blackberry, graphite, and full body bury delicate lump crab and the silky tannin can't lift the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Creamed Spinach": "Patagonia Argentina Malbec — Noemia A Lisa, cooler-climate than Mendoza, blackberry, violet, graphite, full body, soft tannin. The full body handles dairy-heavy cream and the soft tannin won't tear against spinach-iron. Strong Patagonian-Malbec for creamed spinach — silky polish on the rich side.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Creamy Potato": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the floral notes lift the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Creme Brulee": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Bodega Noemia A Lisa on the steak course instead.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Escargot": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Faroe Island Salmon": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Bodega Noemia A Lisa on the steak course instead.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Filet Mignon": "Patagonia Argentina Malbec — Noemia A Lisa, cooler-climate than Mendoza, blackberry, violet, graphite, full body, soft tannin, silky elegance. The soft tannin matches butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the violet plays with escargot-butter. Strong Patagonian-Malbec for filet — Mendoza's jam-forward lift reads heavier here.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|French Onion": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Tannin and char, umami and dark fruit — the bold red and the Caesar punch at the same level. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Gumbo": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis finds neutral with the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|House Wedge": "Patagonia Argentina Malbec — Noemia A Lisa, cooler-climate than Mendoza, blackberry, violet, graphite, full body, soft tannin. The soft tannin holds against blue-cheese funk, but the elegant style gets muted by cold iceberg. Works for wedge — a lighter Mencía or Chianti lifts higher.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Kansas City": "Kansas City: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Loaded Potato": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis sits beside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the floral notes lift the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Lobster Mac": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the match holds without overshooting.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Market Fish": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Bodega Noemia A Lisa lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Mocha Creme": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Bodega Noemia A Lisa for the steak course.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Mushroom Bisque": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis holds with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit anchors the mushroom's umami weight; firm tannin and oak hold their register. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Bodega Noemia A Lisa for the steak course.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Prime Tartare": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Rainbow Trout": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Bodega Noemia A Lisa lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Roast Half Chicken": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Bodega Noemia A Lisa on the steak course instead.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis reads alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Seafood Tower": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Bodega Noemia A Lisa on the steak course instead.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Noemia with seared scallops — graphite and full body overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and the soft tannin still fights browned-butter sweetness. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Seasonal Soup": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis sits beside the seasonal soup's house build — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Seasonal Vegetables": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis finds neutral with the seasonal garden plate — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Shrimp Bisque": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis sits alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the floral edge frames the shellfish. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Noemia with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by full body, blackberry, and graphite, and the silky elegance has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Swordfish": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Bodega Noemia A Lisa lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|The Tomahawk": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the velvet-on-fat coherence runs through the plate, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Tomato Basil": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis holds with the tomato basil's bright acid — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis frames the herb butter. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: Cab tannin frames the earthy mushroom-and-truffle depth; dark fruit lifts the umami into the wine's body. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Bodega Noemia A Lisa for the steak course.",
  "Bodega Noemia A Lisa|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Asparagus": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals sits alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Beignets": "Bombay Dry with beignets — angular London Dry against fried dough. Works; the juniper-sugar contrast is sharper than Beefeater's approach.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Bone Marrow": "Bombay Dry with bone marrow — 8-botanical juniper against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Bombay Dry Gin for another course.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals leans against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Broccolini": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Burrata": "Bombay Dry with burrata — 8-botanical juniper against fresh dairy. Strong; Bombay Dry's angular juniper cuts cream more aggressively than Plymouth's soft approach — less textural match, more contrast.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Butternut Squash": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals sits beside the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Carrot Cake": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Bombay Dry Gin for the steak course.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Cheesecake": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Bombay Dry Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Bombay Dry Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Bombay Dry Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Clam Chowder": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Bombay Dry Gin on another course instead.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Crab Cake": "Bombay Dry with crab cake — 8-botanical London Dry against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Bombay Dry's juniper-forward character frames crab with angular precision.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Creamy Potato": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals leans against the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Creme Brulee": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Bombay Dry Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Escargot": "Bombay Dry with escargot — angular 8-botanical against garlic-butter snails. Excellent; Bombay Dry's juniper cuts butter with purity the softer siblings don't match.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Bombay Dry with the salmon — 8-botanical London Dry against oily salmon. Strong; Bombay Dry's juniper lifts the fish with traditional-gin character.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Filet Mignon": "Bombay Dry with the filet — original-recipe gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified; Bombay Dry's juniper is the traditional case for guests who insist.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|French Onion": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Bombay Dry with the grilled Caesar — angular juniper against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Bombay Dry's pine cuts the char at traditional register.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Gumbo": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals sits alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals sits alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|House Wedge": "Bombay Dry with the House Wedge — original-recipe London Dry against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Bombay Dry's juniper engages the bleu with traditional character.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Kansas City": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Bombay Dry Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Loaded Potato": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals leans against the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Lobster Mac": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Market Fish": "Bombay Dry with the market fish — juniper-assertive London Dry against featured fish. Works; Bombay Dry's pine handles most fish with distinctive character.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Mocha Creme": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Bombay Dry Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals leans against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Mushrooms": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals sits alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Bombay Dry Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Porterhouse": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Bombay Dry Gin on another course instead.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Prime Tartare": "Bombay Dry with the tartare — juniper-assertive original recipe against raw beef. Excellent; Bombay Dry's 8-botanical angular profile lifts tartare's minerality with distinctive British pine notes no other gin in the batch brings as sharply.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals sits with the trout's delicate flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Bombay Dry with the chicken — 8-botanical London Dry against roasted poultry. Works; Bombay Dry's juniper supports chicken at traditional register.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Bombay Dry Gin's herbal-botanical edge sits alongside the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the floral edge frames the halibut, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Neptunia's briny character engages the bleu with distinctive maritime profile.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals sits beside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Seafood Tower": "Bombay Dry with the seafood tower — juniper-assertive London Dry against the raw bar. Excellent; Bombay Dry's traditional character delivers the raw bar classic-gin experience.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Seared Scallops": "Bombay Dry with seared scallops — original 8-botanical recipe, juniper-assertive London Dry. Excellent; Bombay Dry's angular juniper is the sharpest gin-scallop lift in the batch — more assertive than Beefeater's balance, less proof-heavy than Tanq.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals stays alongside the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Bombay Dry with shrimp bisque — juniper-assertive London Dry against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Bombay Dry's pine notes cut the bisque with angular precision.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Bombay Dry with shrimp cocktail — original-recipe juniper against cold shrimp. Excellent; Bombay Dry's assertiveness makes the pairing feel distinctive rather than default.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Swordfish": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals lifts the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|The Tomahawk": "Bombay Dry with the Tomahawk — juniper-assertive against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Tomato Basil": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright acid — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Truffle Fries": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals reads alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Bombay Dry Gin's juniper-and-citrus lift leans against the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the floral edge frames the tuna, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Sapphire's softness meets the bleu at accessible register.",
  "Bombay Dry Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Asparagus": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals plays cleanly against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals reads alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Beignets": "Bombay Sapphire with beignets — vapor-infused floral gin against fried dough. Works; Sapphire's softer character makes the juniper-sugar pairing more accessible than Bombay Dry's angular version.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Bone Marrow": "Bombay Sapphire with bone marrow — 10-botanical floral against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Bombay Sapphire Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Broccolini": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals sits beside the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals reads alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Burrata": "Bombay Sapphire with burrata — 10-botanical gin (grains of paradise + cubeb pepper), vapor-infused process (botanicals suspended above spirit in basket). Excellent; Sapphire's floral-softer profile meets burrata's cream with a different approach than Plymouth's sweet-earthy or Tanq 10's citrus — the floral-pepper notes are distinctive.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Butternut Squash": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals reads alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Carrot Cake": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- juniper-citrus and warm cake spices pull in opposite directions. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Bombay Sapphire Gin for the steak course.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Cheesecake": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Bombay Sapphire Gin for the steak course.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's juniper-and-citrus lift leans against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the floral edge frames the seabass, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Neptunia's briny character engages the bleu with distinctive maritime profile.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Bombay Sapphire Gin for the steak course.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Bombay Sapphire Gin for the steak course.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Clam Chowder": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Bombay Sapphire Gin for another course.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Crab Cake": "Bombay Sapphire with crab cake — vapor-infused floral gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Sapphire's softness is the gentlest approach to crab in the London Dry batch — less aggression than Tanq, more floral than Beefeater.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Creamy Potato": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals sits beside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Creme Brulee": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Bombay Sapphire Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Escargot": "Bombay Sapphire with escargot — floral-softer gin against garlic-butter snails. Excellent; the cubeb-pepper notes cut through butter while the floral botanicals complement it — a distinctive escargot pairing vs Tanq's proof-cutting approach.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Bombay Sapphire with the salmon — vapor-infused 10-botanical against oily salmon. Strong; Sapphire's softer character frames the fish without the aggression of flagship London Drys.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Filet Mignon": "Bombay Sapphire with the filet — 10-botanical softer London Dry against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified; Sapphire's softness is a functional case.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|French Onion": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals plays cleanly against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Bombay Sapphire with the grilled Caesar — softer floral against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Sapphire meets the char gently; Tanq 10's citrus is still the sharper gin-Caesar.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Gumbo": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals holds with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals reads alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|House Wedge": "Bombay Sapphire with the House Wedge — vapor-infused floral gin against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Sapphire's softness meets the bleu at accessible register.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Kansas City": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Bombay Sapphire Gin on another course instead.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Loaded Potato": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Lobster Mac": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals plays cleanly against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Market Fish": "Bombay Sapphire with the market fish — 10-botanical softer London Dry against featured fish. Works; Sapphire's floral character handles most fish gently.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Mocha Creme": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Bombay Sapphire Gin for the steak course.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's gin lift with floral-and-pepper reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the floral edge frames the mushroom bisque, and the juniper threads the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Mushrooms": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Bombay Sapphire Gin for the steak course.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Porterhouse": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Bombay Sapphire Gin for another course.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Prime Tartare": "Bombay Sapphire with the tartare — 10-botanical softer London Dry against raw beef. Strong; Sapphire's floral character supports tartare, though sibling Bombay Dry's angular juniper is the featured gin-tartare match.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals holds the line with the trout's delicate flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Bombay Sapphire with the chicken — vapor-infused floral against roasted poultry. Works; Sapphire's softer character supports chicken at accessible register.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals plays cleanly against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals plays cleanly against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals stays alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Seafood Tower": "Bombay Sapphire with the seafood tower — vapor-infused 10-botanical against the raw bar. Excellent; Sapphire's softer floral character is the accessible luxury pairing — where Tanq is serious and Plymouth is English-traditional, Sapphire is approachable.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Seared Scallops": "Bombay Sapphire with seared scallops — vapor-infused 10-botanical against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Sapphire's softer floral character frames scallops where Bombay Dry's angular juniper contrasts them — the softer sibling for a different pairing philosophy.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals sits alongside the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Bombay Sapphire with shrimp bisque — 10-botanical softer London Dry against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Sapphire's floral notes ride the bisque softer than angular Bombay Dry would cut it.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Bombay Sapphire with shrimp cocktail — 10-botanical softer floral against cold shrimp. Excellent; Sapphire's accessibility makes this the approachable gin-shrimp pairing — the blue-bottle icon delivering the expected.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Swordfish": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals sits with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|The Tomahawk": "Bombay Sapphire with the Tomahawk — softer floral gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Tomato Basil": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals stays alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Truffle Fries": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals reads alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift sits alongside the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the floral edge frames the tuna, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Bombay Dry's pine cuts the char at traditional register.",
  "Bombay Sapphire Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bone Marrow|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with bone marrow — Barton spicy-sweet against umami fat. Strong; 93.7-proof balance is capable against the starter but Knob Creek 12 or Baker's bring more depth.",
  "Bone Marrow|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat with bone marrow — sweet-wheat bourbon against umami fat. The distinctive sweet-wheat character meets the starter differently than Wellers or Maker's; a character-forward wheated. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|1881 Napa Valley": "Napa Cab — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, smooth medium tannin. The approachable body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness but the smoother tannin is rounder than the bone-depth demands. Strong entry Napa Cab for marrow — firm-tannin Cabs like Silver Oak or Ghost Block peak higher for the starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|2 James Gin": "2 James with bone marrow — Michigan craft gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with bone marrow — rare craft release against umami fat. The boutique-release character meets marrow with literary-evocative authority. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with bone marrow — double-matured Speyside against umami fat. Strong; Aberlour 16's balanced bourbon-plus-sherry character sits alongside marrow; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino with bone marrow — filtered aged against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms cristalino despite its aged depth.",
  "Bone Marrow|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado with bone marrow — long-aged reposado against umami fat. The near-añejo depth meets the marrow's intensity where 8-month reposados don't quite reach. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bone Marrow|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's elegant cherry-and-pepper body meets at register with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness: the elegant red-fruit register matches the rich marrow, and the red-fruit edge brightens the starter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone Marrow|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino with bone marrow — grappa-based amaro against umami fat. Strong; the Friulian botanicals cut through the marrow's richness as a post-bite digestif.",
  "Bone Marrow|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Ancho Reyes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with bone marrow — port-finished bourbon against umami fat. Works; AE at 86.6 proof is under-specified for marrow intensity; AE Cask Strength handles marrow better.",
  "Bone Marrow|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with bone marrow — 117+ proof port-finish against umami fat. Works; AE CS's cask-strength engages marrow better than flagship AE; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "AE Triple Cask with bone marrow — triple-cask AE against umami fat. Works; AE Triple Cask's layered character sits alongside marrow; AE Cask Strength handles marrow intensity better.",
  "Bone Marrow|Aperol": "Aperol with bone marrow — 11% soft bitter against umami fat. Avoid; Aperol is far too light to cut marrow richness — wrong tool. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Asparagus": "Bone Marrow alongside the asparagus -- the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness meets the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: acid plays against richness, the contrast holds across the courses. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Bone Marrow|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The bone marrow opens the meal, the au gratin with the entree -- the marrow-and-gremolata plate wraps the cheese-and-cream side. Strong; the starter sets the table before the side lands.",
  "Bone Marrow|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Averna Amaro": "Averna with bone marrow — fuller-body Sicilian amaro against umami fat. Strong; the caramel-orange cuts through the marrow's richness at classic-digestif register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Aviation": "Aviation gin, Luxardo, crème de violette, ginger syrup. The cocktail's floral lightness has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat richness — violet and cherry don't meet bone depth. Never pair — keep the Aviation for starters and raw bar.",
  "Bone Marrow|Aviation Gin": "Aviation with bone marrow — soft-juniper craft against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Avion 44": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets 44-month aged Avion. Extended maturation depth meets marrow at premium Avion register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver with bone marrow — filtered blanco against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms blanco.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character underclubs the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- light spirit reads thin against the rich meat opener. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a light spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Bacardi Rum lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bone Marrow|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Baker's": "Baker's with bone marrow — 107-proof small-batch against umami fat. The premium proof and rich vanilla depth meet marrow's intensity. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with bone marrow — American Oak Speyside against umami fat. Works; Balvenie is too light to cut marrow (Fernet Branca does that); Balvenie sits alongside the marrow course comfortably.",
  "Bone Marrow|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with bone marrow — rum-cask-finished Speyside against umami fat. Strong; Caribbean Cask's brown-sugar-and-vanilla sits alongside marrow with tropical character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with bone marrow — American Oak Speyside against umami fat. Works; Balvenie 16's single-barrel depth sits alongside marrow comfortably; Fernet Branca remains the featured marrow pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood with bone marrow — 21yr port-cask-finished Speyside against umami fat. Strong; Portwood's port red-berry depth frames marrow at collector register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with bone marrow — French-wine-cask Bardstown against umami fat. Works; Bardstown Château Laboude's French-wine character sits alongside marrow at contemporary-craft register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with bone marrow — sourced-blend Discovery against umami fat. The multi-source complexity meets marrow with craft-blender authority. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with bone marrow — KBS-finished against umami fat. The coffee-and-stout character meets marrow with distinctive beer-barrel authority. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare with bone marrow — Barbados rum-cask Bardstown against umami fat. Excellent; Bardstown Foursquare's rum-cask + marrow is distinctive — the tropical-sweetness riding marrow richness at Caribbean-finish register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with bone marrow — Fusion blend against umami fat. The own-plus-sourced complexity meets marrow with Bardstown distillery-proud authority. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with bone marrow — curated Bardstown single-barrel against umami fat. The specifically-selected barrel character meets marrow with retailer-pick authority. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with bone marrow — rye spice against umami fat. Capable, but the 80-proof light body can't quite match the marrow's intensity. Reach for Weller 12 or Maker's Mark Cellar Aged for more alignment; Basil Hayden's is the lighter alternate. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year with bone marrow — aged depth gives the pour the weight the starter demands. Where standard Basil Hayden's is outmatched, 10-year's maturity meets the marrow's umami fat cleanly. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with bone marrow — bready malted grain against umami fat. Distinctive but outmatched by Weller 12 on weight; the pour's character is the draw, not the match for marrow's intensity. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Bone Marrow|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with bone marrow — Beam 80-proof BH against umami fat. Works; BH Red Wine Cask is under-specified for marrow at softer proof; AE Cask Strength handles marrow better.",
  "Bone Marrow|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast with bone marrow — toasted caramel notes against umami fat. The toast adds dessert-adjacent sweetness to the starter; more interesting than standard Basil Hayden's but still outmatched by Weller 12's depth. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bee's Knees": "Bee's Knees's crisp butter-citrus build plays cleanly against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the cocktail lift refreshes the starter, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater with bone marrow — balanced gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Beignets": "The bone marrow alongside the beignets -- the savory course resolves into sugar, the meal closes with contrast. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Bone Marrow|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality sits beside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bone Marrow|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with bone marrow — Stitzel-Weller revival blend against umami fat. The heritage whiskey in the blend gives the starter a different vocabulary than pure-distillery pours. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with bone marrow — 103-proof against umami fat. The extra concentration meets the marrow's intensity where Original's 93-proof balance doesn't quite reach. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with bone marrow — single-barrel against umami fat. Strong; the marrow's intensity pushes against the 93-proof balance — Blanton's Gold or E.H. Taylor handle marrow more fully.",
  "Bone Marrow|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's SfB with bone marrow — export cask-strength Blanton's against umami fat. Excellent; Blanton's SfB cuts marrow at barrel-proof register with distinctive Blanton's character.",
  "Bone Marrow|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with bone marrow — 10th-Pact milestone against umami fat. The decennial release complexity meets marrow with collector-edition authority. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with bone marrow — Pact 8 blend against umami fat. The three-bourbon master-blender complexity meets marrow with Mizunara-finish distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with bone marrow — Pact 9 wine-cask-finished blend against umami fat. The fruit-cask depth meets marrow with annual-release authority. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat with bone marrow — American citrus gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Patagonia Argentina Malbec — Noemia A Lisa, cooler-climate than Mendoza, blackberry, violet, graphite, full body, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts roasted-marrow unctuousness without tearing and the graphite echoes beefy-bone depth. Peak Patagonian-Malbec for bone marrow — silky polish on the richness. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry with bone marrow — 8-botanical juniper against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire with bone marrow — 10-botanical floral against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bone-In Filet": "The bone-in filet after the bone marrow -- both courses speak the same beefy-marrow language, the unctuous opener priming the palate for the 14oz cut's bone-deepened butter. Strong; the marrow line carries through the meal.",
  "Bone Marrow|Booker's": "Booker's with bone marrow — Beam flagship cask-strength against umami fat. Excellent; Booker's uncut intensity cuts marrow at barrel-proof register — one of the rare spirits that actually matches marrow's weight without needing Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Botanist Gin": "Botanist with bone marrow — 31-botanical Islay gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace muddled with our rum-soaked cherries, orange, and Angostura. The bourbon's caramel and vanilla wrap around the roasted marrow's richness; the citrus salt on top meets the orange and cherries. The card explicitly calls out spirit-friendliness — lead with this. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with bone marrow — the custom-selected oak-stave profile adds a dimension to Maker's that standard doesn't have. A pour that reflects what Bowdie's values; the house's wheated expression for this starter. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with bone marrow — Virginia smooth small-batch against umami fat. Strong; the smooth character is capable but marrow earns aged bonded pours like Henry McKenna 10 BIB or E.H. Taylor.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bowmore 12": "Bone Marrow: the rich umami fat meets Bowmore 12's sherry-touched, honey-forward peat. Different frame than the other two Islays — softer, more integrated with the marrow's mineral finish. Still peated, but less brawling than Lagavulin 8 or Laphroaig 10. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin holds with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bone Marrow|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Broccoli Cheddar": "The bone marrow with the broccoli cheddar -- the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness meets the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: cream and protein share the meal, the build reads full. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Bone Marrow|Broccolini": "The bone marrow and the broccolini -- the bright cuts the rich cleanly. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich: this Islay Scotch's intense peat smoke, medicinal phenol, and bold weight meets this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth. Whiskey depth meets the starter's fat and umami — both land heavy. Avoid.",
  "Bone Marrow|Brussels and Belly": "The bone marrow opens the meal, Brussels + Belly with the entree -- the beefy bone-broth richness meets the maple-glazed belly. Strong; both items.",
  "Bone Marrow|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with bone marrow — flagship bourbon against umami fat. BT's oak-and-caramel clean profile meets the marrow without the wheated softness; a balanced, non-wheated premium pour for this starter. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character flattens the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with bone marrow — 90-proof high-rye spice against umami fat. Strong; rye brightness doesn't match the marrow's intensity the way Weller 12's aged depth does, but offers a distinctive take.",
  "Bone Marrow|Butternut Squash": "The bone marrow preceded by the butternut squash -- the savory course resolves into sugar, the meal closes with contrast. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "Bone Marrow|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila with bone marrow — value reposado against umami fat. Strong; the accessible character is outmatched by marrow's intensity.",
  "Bone Marrow|Campari": "Campari with bone marrow — aperitivo bitter against umami fat. Avoid; aperitivo is the wrong register for the biggest appetizer — Campari at 24% can't cut marrow the way Branca does. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend sits alongside the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness: the Canadian whisky weight matches the rich marrow, and the soft profile rounds the starter. Works; the Millennium's register overshoots the dessert's; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Canadian Club 43 Year": "Canadian Club 43 Year's Canadian whisky character finds neutral with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness: the easygoing Canadian character handles the rich marrow, and the soft profile rounds the starter. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a soup — save the collector bottle for the main course; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- this light spirit's register is wrong for the starter — try a different pour. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Captain Morgan Rum for the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness: the easygoing Canadian character handles the rich marrow, and the gentle rye thread underlines the starter. Works; a gentle composed match at the premium tier; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Carrot Cake": "Bone Marrow and the carrot cake -- savory gives way to sweet, the close lands cleanly. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Bone Marrow|Casa LBV Port": "Never pair Casa LBV with bone marrow — entry-port residual sugar drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and the smooth structure clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. LBV belongs at dessert, not the start of the meal.",
  "Bone Marrow|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal: the Clooney-Gerber gateway to agave smoke. Excellent on marrow; Casamigos Mezcal's softened-smoke character meets the umami fat without overwhelming — the entry point for a guest who wants mezcal but dialed back.",
  "Bone Marrow|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus — Napa Cab, concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the cocoa frames the bone depth directly. Peak iconic Napa Cab for marrow — the name-recognition plus lushness makes this a confirmed-right-choice pairing for guests. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Cheesecake": "The bone marrow alongside the cheesecake -- savory gives way to sweet, the close lands cleanly. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Bone Marrow|Chilean Seabass": "Chilean Seabass with the bone marrow as the lead-in -- the pan-finished seabass wraps the rich marrow. Strong; the starter sets the table for the seabass.",
  "Bone Marrow|Chocolate Brownie": "Bone Marrow and the brownie -- the entree yields to the dessert, the meal arc reads on-key. Works; the bookends compose neutrally.",
  "Bone Marrow|Chocolate Cake": "Bone Marrow and the chocolate cake -- savory-into-sweet, the classic close lands intact. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Bone Marrow|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality reads alongside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding, and the oak frames the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bone Marrow|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with bone marrow — sweeter American brandy against umami fat. Strong; Christian Bros's sweetness rides the marrow, though Courvoisier's drier oak cuts it sharper.",
  "Bone Marrow|Château Batailley": "Pauillac 5ème Cru Classé Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the graphite-cedar plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak classified Bordeaux for marrow — Pauillac structure on the rich starter. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Château Beaucastel": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The starter's fat and umami meet the bold red's dark fruit — both land on the palate with real weight. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The velvety Merlot wraps rendered-marrow unctuousness and truffle echoes beefy-bone depth. Works for marrow — plush Bordeaux reads softer than the char-forward wines that peak on the starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|Château Haut Segottes": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the truffle plays against the smoky mineral depth. Strong right-bank Bordeaux for marrow — Merlot-led approachability on the rich starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|Château de Rouillac": "Pessac-Léognan left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, tobacco, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the cedar plays against the smoky mineral depth. Works as left-bank Bordeaux for marrow — French structure on the rich starter; bolder Napa Cabs peak higher.",
  "Bone Marrow|Clam Chowder": "The bone marrow preceded by the chowder -- cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "Bone Marrow|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's caramel-butterscotch character sits with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango with bone marrow — Durango-artisanship extra añejo against umami fat. The ceramic-bottle ceremony meets marrow at the ultra-premium Clase Azul register. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold with bone marrow — joven-plus-mezcal blend with 24K gold against umami fat. The mezcal-accented smoke adds dimension to the marrow; ceremonial luxury marrow pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal: Guerrero-state terroir in a ceramic decanter. Excellent on marrow; the softer-fruitier smoke character meets the umami fat at luxury-mezcal register — save the bottle for the guest who wants ceremony with their smoke.",
  "Bone Marrow|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata with bone marrow — luxury blanco against umami fat. Avoid; marrow's intensity overwhelms blanco regardless of tier.",
  "Bone Marrow|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado with bone marrow — luxury reposado against umami fat. Strong; the smooth agave is distinct against the marrow's intensity — ceremony paired with the starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra with bone marrow — 5-year top-tier Clase Azul against umami fat. The Ultra's aged depth meets marrow at the absolute top of the Clase Azul ceremonial register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep with bone marrow — Beam wheated against umami fat. The wheated profile meets the starter at the approachable register; Jim Beam's answer to the wheated style. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin lifts the marrow's umami-fat depth — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Bone Marrow|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with bone marrow — Napa Cab-cask reposado against umami fat. Strong; the wine-fruit influence is a distinctive counterpoint.",
  "Bone Marrow|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's bitter-herb aperitivo edge finds neutral with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness: the herbal cut works the rich marrow, and the amaro backbone frames the starter. Works; grappa-based botanicals are a gentle counterpoint to the side.",
  "Bone Marrow|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco with bone marrow — highland-agave against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms blanco.",
  "Bone Marrow|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado with bone marrow — single-estate reposado against umami fat. Strong; the highland-agave brightness is a distinctive counterpoint to marrow's intensity.",
  "Bone Marrow|Corazon Sazerac": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets Corazon Sazerac. Excellent; a confident headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Corazon Stagg": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets Corazon Stagg. Excellent; the ex-GTS-barrel bold-caramel-oak depth handles marrow's umami at Expresiones register — the intense expression matches the plate's intensity.",
  "Bone Marrow|Corazon Weller": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets Corazon Weller. Excellent; the wheated-soft-sweet-bourbon character handles marrow's umami at rounded-Expresiones register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Corpse Reviver": "Warner's gin, Lillet, Pierre Ferrand cognac, lemon, absinthe-rinsed glass. The cocktail's elegance has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat weight. Never pair — keep the Corpse Reviver for starters, route marrow to whiskey cocktails or big Cabs.",
  "Bone Marrow|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier with bone marrow — drier traditional cognac against umami fat. Excellent; Courvoisier's oak-forward profile cuts marrow harder than Hennessy's dried-fruit rounder approach — the featured cognac-marrow pairing in the batch.",
  "Bone Marrow|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye after the bone marrow -- the unctuous marrow opens the meal, the 26oz cap-and-fat richness following with rendered fat and char that share the marrow's beefy language without competing. Strong; both courses speak fat in the same register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 with bone marrow — Classic Malt Speyside against umami fat. Works; Cragganmore's subtle smoke meets marrow better than the lighter siblings, but Fernet Branca is still the featured marrow pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Creamed Spinach": "The bone marrow opens the meal, the creamed spinach with the entree -- the marrow-and-gremolata plate sits cleanly with the creamed spinach. Strong; both items.",
  "Bone Marrow|Creamy Potato": "The bone marrow with the potato soup -- the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness meets the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the meal layers rich on rich, the table carries weight cleanly when the registers match. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Bone Marrow|Creme Brulee": "Bone Marrow and the brulee -- savory gives way to sweet, the close lands cleanly. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Bone Marrow|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Willamette Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — graceful Oregon Pinot lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Cristom for the chicken, salmon, or seafood course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness sits alongside the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the rich marrow, and the easy Canadian register softens the starter. Works; the intensity overpowers this dish; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Cucumber Gimlet": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda. Garden-fresh cocktail has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat richness — the Gimlet is built for raw bar, not bone dishes. Never pair — route marrow to whiskey cocktails or big reds.",
  "Bone Marrow|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12 with bone marrow — Highland luxury sherry-finish against umami fat. Strong; Dalmore's sherry-finish depth sits alongside marrow with chocolate-orange character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Loire red Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — light-body Sancerre lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Sancerre Rouge for the chicken, salmon, or seafood course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the savory herb plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak rare-Napa CF for marrow — Darioush Persian-influenced elegance on the rich starter. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silverado Trail Cab — blackcurrant, dark plum, mocha, spice, rich plush. The opulent body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the mocha-spice frames the bone depth. Peak opulent Napa Cab for marrow — Darioush Persian-inspired story plus plush concentration handles the rich starter. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin with bone marrow — Detroit juniper against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City Vodka's crisp grain-juniper neutrality stays alongside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Bone Marrow|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15 with bone marrow — aged Highland blend against umami fat. Works; Dewar's 15's sherry-finish sits alongside marrow at premium register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label with bone marrow — entry Highland blend against umami fat. Works; Dewar's White is too light to cut marrow; JW Red's smoke brings more dimension, Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character flattens the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Disaronno on the steak course instead.",
  "Bone Marrow|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with bone marrow — craft sourced rye against umami fat. The boutique-blend character meets marrow with craft distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets Doctor Bird Jamaica. Excellent; the intense Jamaican hogo stands up to marrow's umami depth at high-ester register — the rum equivalent of a peated scotch on this plate.",
  "Bone Marrow|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Willamette flagship Pinot Blend against pure collagen-fat marrow — silky layered Pinot lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Grand Cheval for the filet, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé against marrow's rendered-fat richness — even serious gun-flint structure is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Pouilly-Fumé for scallops, escargot, crab cake, or seafood tower.",
  "Bone Marrow|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts roasted-marrow unctuousness and the black pepper plays with toasted-bread char. Peak Châteauneuf for bone marrow — GSM balances the richness. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Don Fulano Anejo": "Don Fulano Anejo's agave-apricot character finds register with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel softens the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco with bone marrow — refined craft against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms blanco at any register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado with bone marrow — premium highland against umami fat. Strong; the refinement is a distinctive counterpoint to marrow's intensity.",
  "Bone Marrow|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942 with bone marrow — benchmark extra añejo against umami fat. The aged depth and vanilla meet marrow's intensity at the 1942 marquee register. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th with bone marrow — filtered reposado against umami fat. Strong; the clarity is a distinctive counterpoint to the marrow's richness.",
  "Bone Marrow|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco with bone marrow — bright blanco against umami fat. Avoid; the marrow's intensity overwhelms blanco's clean brightness. Reach for bourbon or peated Islay.",
  "Bone Marrow|Don Julio Reposado": "Don Julio Reposado with bone marrow — Don Julio's balance against umami fat. Strong; the reposado's vanilla and oak add dimension but the marrow's intensity earns a higher-proof or longer-aged reposado.",
  "Bone Marrow|Drambuie": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Drambuie belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Dunville's Single Malt": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets Dunville's. Excellent; revived Belfast Irish single-malt brings craft-heritage character to the marrow plate at Irish-single-malt register.",
  "Bone Marrow|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with bone marrow — BT BiB 100-proof against umami fat. Excellent; EHT Full Proof engages marrow at historic Kentucky register.",
  "Bone Marrow|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye with bone marrow — 100-proof BIB BT rye against umami fat. The bonded weight and aging meet marrow's intensity with premium authority; the BT-side premium BIB rye for marrow. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with bone marrow — 100-proof BIB against umami fat. The bonded weight and rich oak meet the marrow's intensity head-on; the premium BIB marrow pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Bone Marrow: the rich umami fat meets Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged oak and vanilla. Ten years of barrel influence carries marrow without being heavy; refined rather than brawling. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Eastern Kille with bone marrow — classic London Dry against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|El Cabo": "El Cabo with bone marrow — craft small-batch reposado against umami fat. Strong; the boutique character gives dimension but the starter earns a heavier pour.",
  "Bone Marrow|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "El Mayor Extra Anejo with bone marrow — accessible aged El Mayor against umami fat. Value-adjacent premium depth meets marrow at accessible register. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado with bone marrow — value reposado against umami fat. Strong; the light-oak character is outmatched by marrow's intensity; Adictivo or aged reposados bring more weight.",
  "Bone Marrow|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin with bone marrow — African-botanical gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 with bone marrow — aged HH single-barrel against umami fat. Excellent; EC 18's 18yr depth engages marrow at aged-premium register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel with bone marrow — private-barrel Heaven Hill against umami fat. The house pick's specifically-chosen depth meets the marrow's intensity. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel with bone marrow — concentrated single-barrel against umami fat. The 94-proof single-barrel intensity meets the marrow where Small Batch only works strong. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with bone marrow — small-batch caramel-vanilla against umami fat. The 94 proof gives the pour the weight to meet the marrow; a sturdy everyday match. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with bone marrow — 94-proof HH rye against umami fat. The rye spice and HH character meet marrow's intensity cleanly at the premium HH register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Willamette Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — medium-tannin Oregon Pinot still lacks the grip needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Elk Cove for the filet, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's apple-char acidity reads alongside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bone Marrow|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee with bone marrow — smooth single-barrel against umami fat. Strong; namesake balance sits with marrow cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 with bone marrow — rose-and-floral BC gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Epoch Ingenuity": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Espresso Martini": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The espresso cuts marrow's rendered fat and Averna's bitter herbal backbone meets the bone's savory depth. Strong unconventional pairing — coffee cocktail with marrow works for guests wanting different.",
  "Bone Marrow|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The espresso cuts marrow's fat weight directly and Frangelico's hazelnut adds aromatic depth against roasted bone savor. Peak cocktail for marrow when guest wants the coffee-chocolate register alongside.",
  "Bone Marrow|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Willamette Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — savory Eola-Amity Pinot's medium tannin still falls short of the render's demand. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Evening Land for the filet, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The starter's fat and umami meet the bold red's dark fruit — both land on the palate with real weight. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity sits beside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bone Marrow|Faroe Island Salmon": "The salmon after the bone marrow -- the pan-finished salmon frames the beefy unctuous opener. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Bone Marrow|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. Graphite echoes beefy-bone mineral depth and tobacco plays with rendered-marrow unctuousness. Strong Super Tuscan for marrow — Maremma structure on the rich starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Faust — Napa Cab with blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the tobacco-chocolate frames the bone depth. Peak value Napa Cab for marrow — layered brooding complexity at a price that makes the starter pairing easy. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone Marrow|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca with bone marrow — nuclear menthol-saffron bitter against umami fat. Excellent; the 27-herb digestif cuts marrow richness harder than any sipping amaro in the family, and the bartender's-handshake reputation is earned exactly here. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta with bone marrow — cooling mint bitter against umami fat. Excellent; Menta's mint cuts marrow richness with a brighter lift than Branca's saffron does.",
  "Bone Marrow|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon after the bone marrow -- the marrow's beefy unctuous richness adds the dimension the 10oz lean cut leaves room for, the opener anchoring what the filet keeps clean. Strong; the marrow primes the palate the filet finishes.",
  "Bone Marrow|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character flattens the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — silky Pinot lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Fisher Unity for the chicken, salmon, or seafood course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with bone marrow — 104-proof premium Four Roses against umami fat. The concentrated blend meets the marrow with premium small-batch weight. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label with bone marrow — entry-level 80-proof against umami fat. Outmatched by the starter; Weller 12 or Buffalo Trace carry the marrow more fully. Capable everyday choice. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with bone marrow — craft boutique against umami fat. Strong; the small-production character is distinctive but marrow earns more depth from aged bonded pours.",
  "Bone Marrow|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Fox & Oden Double Oaked with bone marrow — Detroit-brand Double Oaked against umami fat. Strong; Fox & Oden's second-char character engages marrow at Double-Oaked register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Rutherford Cab — Bosché Vineyard since 1970, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the graphite-tobacco plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak Bosché Vineyard Cab for marrow — 1970-legacy Rutherford on the rich starter. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|French 75": "Inverroche gin, lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top. The bubbly, bright cocktail has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat richness — citrus and bubbles don't cut that deep. Never pair — keep the 75 for raw bar and starters, route marrow to whiskey cocktails.",
  "Bone Marrow|French Onion": "Bone Marrow alongside the French onion -- the savory course rounds the protein, the meal reads layered. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "Bone Marrow|Frias Block 5": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, high acid. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the tar plays against the smoky mineral depth. Works as classic Barolo for marrow — Vajra prestige on the rich starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Piedmont sweet sparkling against roasted bone marrow — sweet-dessert bubbles against rich umami fat is the wrong axis for a savory starter. Never pair. Route Moscato to dessert; take bone marrow with structured reds like Scavino, Muga, or Opus One.",
  "Bone Marrow|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco with bone marrow — craft blanco against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms blanco.",
  "Bone Marrow|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof with bone marrow — 104-proof agave against umami fat. The concentrated pour meets the marrow's intensity head-on; the highest-impact reposado-marrow pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness: the gentle Irish body threads the rich marrow, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the starter. Strong; aged Beam matches richness cleanly; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|George T. Stagg": "GTS with bone marrow — BTAC holy-grail collector against umami fat. Excellent; GTS's 15+yr cask-strength is one of the few spirits on the menu that meets marrow as an equal — the featured BTAC-marrow pairing at collector register. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Oakville Cabernet — firm structure, classic Napa fruit profile. Cuts the marrow fat with the dust-and-dark-fruit character Oakville is known for. Good mid-tier Cab when the guest wants serious without Caymus-level weight. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts roasted-marrow unctuousness and the cracked pepper echoes toasted-bread char. Peak California-Zin for bone marrow — Napa bold-on-rich harmony. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12 with bone marrow — gateway Speyside against umami fat. Works; Fiddich is too light to cut marrow the way Fernet Branca does — reach for Fernet for marrow, Fiddich if the guest specifically wants Speyside alongside.",
  "Bone Marrow|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14 with bone marrow — new-charred-oak Speyside against umami fat. Works; Fiddich 14 is still too light to cut marrow the way Fernet Branca does; sits alongside comfortably.",
  "Bone Marrow|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18 with bone marrow — Oloroso-cask Speyside against umami fat. Works; Fiddich 18's dried-fruit depth sits alongside marrow comfortably — closer to working than the 12/14 but Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23 with bone marrow — 23yr Cuvée-cask Speyside against umami fat. Works; Gran Cru's refinement sits alongside marrow but doesn't cut it; Fernet Branca remains the featured marrow pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12 with bone marrow — Sandend-coast Highland against umami fat. Strong; Glenglassaugh 12's tropical-fruit-and-honey sits alongside marrow at distinctive register; Oban 14's maritime character still leads Highland-marrow pairings.",
  "Bone Marrow|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12 with bone marrow — softer Speyside against umami fat. Works; Glenlivet is too light to cut marrow the way Fernet Branca does — Fernet is the featured marrow pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10 with bone marrow — tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask against umami fat. Strong; Glenmorangie 10's honeysuckle-vanilla sits alongside marrow at entry-Highland register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18 with bone marrow — aged Highland with Oloroso finish against umami fat. Strong; Glenmorangie 18's Oloroso-cask depth sits alongside marrow at premium register; Oban 14's maritime character still leads the Highland-marrow pairings.",
  "Bone Marrow|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Never pair Graham's 10 Year Tawny with bone marrow — sweet oxidative port drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and the toffee sugar clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée for marrow. Tawny belongs at dessert, not the start of the meal.",
  "Bone Marrow|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Never pair Graham's 20 Year Tawny with bone marrow — sweet oxidative tawny drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and twenty-year concentration flattens beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. Tawny belongs at dessert.",
  "Bone Marrow|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Never pair Graham's 2017 Vintage Port with bone marrow — dense inky sugar drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and the declared-vintage intensity clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. Vintage Port is a dessert pour, not an appetizer.",
  "Bone Marrow|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum with bone marrow — refined blanco against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms blanco at any tier.",
  "Bone Marrow|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Grand Marnier belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale with bone marrow — ocean-botanical California gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's cask-strength herbal-high-proof sweetness finds register with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the sweet character meets the savory plate at digestif register, and the herbs cut the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose Vodka's crisp French-wheat-soft neutrality leans against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Bone Marrow|Grilled Caesar": "The bone marrow with the Caesar -- the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness meets the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the savory course rounds the protein, the meal reads layered. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Bone Marrow|Gumbo": "The bone marrow preceded by the gumbo -- spice plays against rich protein, clean contrast. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "Bone Marrow|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Gundlach Bundschu — Sonoma Cab, blackberry, cedar, dried herb, firm tannin. The savory body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the cedar-herb frames the bone depth. Strong savory Sonoma Cab for marrow — restrained Rhinefarm character offers a different register than Napa lushness.",
  "Bone Marrow|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality sits beside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bone Marrow|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 Year -- Suntory Hakushu, lightly peated, forest. The lightly-peated Hakushu meets the rich marrow, and the forest-floor frames the starter. Strong; more body than White Label brings, but still underpowered vs; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel with bone marrow — BT single-barrel against umami fat. Strong; the complex profile is capable but the marrow's intensity earns Blanton's Gold or E.H. Taylor.",
  "Bone Marrow|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 Vodka's crisp char-floral neutrality holds with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding, and the floral notes lift the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bone Marrow|Head Fake": "Wheatley vodka, limoncello, triple sec, lemon, simple syrup. The cocktail's sweet-bright profile has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat richness — lemon and sugar don't cut bone depth. Never pair — keep Head Fake for starters and raw bar.",
  "Bone Marrow|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with bone marrow — HH 20yr collector against umami fat. Excellent; HH 20's 20-year-aged depth engages marrow at collector register — the featured aged-HH marrow pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with bone marrow — 100-proof bond against umami fat. The BIB weight meets the marrow's intensity cleanly; classic Kentucky approach, distinct from the Wellers' wheated take. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with bone marrow — aged 10-year 100-proof against umami fat. The aging depth meets marrow's intensity at the premium register; a Dylan-brand marrow pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Richness from the plate and structure from the wine share the same register — a savory weight-on-weight setup. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "Heitz Trailside — Rutherford Cab with dark cherry, blackcurrant, mint, cedar, refined tannin. The body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the mint-cedar frames the bone depth. Peak historic-house Cab for marrow — Heitz provenance plus Trailside approachable balance on the rich starter. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Flora Adora with bone marrow — floral-garden Hendrick's against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks with bone marrow — perfumed Scottish gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Grand Cabaret with bone marrow — fruit-forward Hendrick's against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Hendricks Neptunia": "Neptunia with bone marrow — coastal-release Hendrick's against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Hendricks Orbium": "Orbium with bone marrow — quinine-wormwood Hendrick's against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy with bone marrow — ubiquitous cognac against umami fat. Excellent; Hennessy's vanilla-and-oak depth cuts marrow richness at benchmark register — where Courvoisier's drier character does it more aggressively, Hennessy rounds it more comfortably.",
  "Bone Marrow|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB with bone marrow — aged 100-proof bonded single-barrel against umami fat. The 10-year depth meets marrow's intensity with BIB authority; premium HH BIB marrow pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Japanese Harmony -- blended Japanese, harmony, silky. The Japanese whisky balance frames the rich marrow, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the starter. Strong; the pairing lands at register without strain against the cut; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Bone Marrow and the honey carrots -- the entree yields to the dessert, the meal arc reads on-key. Works; both items hold their register, no clash.",
  "Bone Marrow|House Wedge": "Bone Marrow and the wedge together -- protein meets umami, the table builds substance. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Bone Marrow|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 with bone marrow — 15yr historic-Kentucky bourbon against umami fat. Excellent; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak-aged character engages marrow at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Light Glera Prosecco against marrow's rendered-fat richness — bubbles crumple under the weight and no structural hook exists. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned, and keep Prosecco for starters, seafood tower, or dessert service.",
  "Bone Marrow|Inhibited": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build plays cleanly against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the cocktail lift refreshes the starter, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bone Marrow|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche with bone marrow — fynbos-floral South African gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish with bone marrow — Thompsonville estate gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon Mezcal Casks with bone marrow — northern MI farm-to-bottle bourbon finished in mezcal casks against umami fat. Excellent; Ironfish Mezcal's smoky-agave character engages marrow with distinctive finish — the mezcal-smoke + marrow-umami is a featured Michigan-hometown pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish with bone marrow — A. Smith Bowman port-finish against umami fat. Works; Isaac Bowman's port character sits alongside marrow at Virginia-heritage register.",
  "Bone Marrow|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "Anderson Valley Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — concentrated Mendocino Pinot still lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep J Davies Ferrington for the filet, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "Bone Marrow|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "Diamond Mountain Cab — volcanic dark fruit, graphite, mineral, firm tannin. The structured body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the graphite-mineral frames the bone depth. Strong mountain Cab for marrow — Schramsberg estate complexity and firm structure handle the rich starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year with bone marrow — 12yr aged Tennessee against umami fat. Excellent; JD 12's aged vanilla-caramel is the featured JD-marrow pairing — the aged depth finally cuts marrow where the entry JDs just sit alongside.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded with bone marrow — Bottled-in-Bond Tennessee against umami fat. Strong; JD Bonded's 100-proof engages marrow at Bonded register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7 with bone marrow — Lincoln County-Process Tennessee against umami fat. Strong; Old No. 7's vanilla-caramel sits alongside marrow at iconic register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder; JD 12 Year's aged depth is the featured JD-marrow match.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "JD Sinatra Select with bone marrow — Sinatra-tribute Tennessee against umami fat. Excellent; Sinatra Select's richer oak engages marrow at Sinatra-tribute register — tied with JD 12 Year as the featured JD-marrow match.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's crisp triple-distilled-smooth character matches the marrow's umami-fat depth — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Russian River Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — smooth Pinot lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Jax Calesa for the chicken, salmon, or seafood course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Grossot Chablis against marrow's rendered-fat richness — lean flint-chalk precision is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Chablis for scallops, escargot, crab cake, or the seafood tower course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with bone marrow — Jefferson's Ocean L.E. against umami fat. Excellent; Marian McLain L.E.'s salt-sea character engages marrow at collector register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with bone marrow — ship-aged Jefferson's against umami fat. Excellent; Voyage 23's salt-tinged sea-aged character engages marrow with distinctive ocean-finish resonance — salt + marrow is textbook.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve with bone marrow — blended honey and vanilla against umami fat. The blend's smoothness meets marrow without the sweet-on-fat pattern of wheated takes; a refined starter pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with bone marrow — double-barrel oak and deep vanilla against umami fat. The Twin Oak finish gives the pour the weight standard Jefferson's doesn't quite reach for marrow. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label with bone marrow — aged Beam against umami fat. Strong; the oak gives some match but Weller 12 or Heaven Hill BIB carry the marrow more fully.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label with bone marrow — 80-proof entry against umami fat. Capable; the starter earns a heavier pour. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Johnnie Walker Black": "JW Black with bone marrow — 12yr balanced JW against umami fat. Strong; JW Black's aged smoke + sherry depth sits alongside marrow at premium-blend register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Johnnie Walker Blue": "JW Blue with bone marrow — hand-selected rare-cask JW against umami fat. Works; JW Blue's silky-complex character sits alongside marrow at collector register but doesn't cut it; save the bottle for filet or KC — reach for Fernet Branca if the guest wants the marrow-cutting experience.",
  "Bone Marrow|Johnnie Walker Red": "JW Red with bone marrow — peat-influenced entry JW against umami fat. Strong; JW Red's Islay-malt smoke engages marrow with distinctive depth — closer to cutting it than the honey-vanilla Dewar's, though Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Sonoma Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin, elegant balance. The refined body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness but the fine-grained tannin is gentler than the bone-depth demands. Works as Jordan for marrow — firmer Cabs like Silver Oak or Frias peak higher for the rich starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Loire Sancerre against marrow's rendered-fat richness — refined tension-filled mineral is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Sancerre for scallops, crab cake, tartare, escargot, or seafood tower.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the espresso-chocolate plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak cult Napa for marrow — Colgin prestige on the rich starter with reverence. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets Jung and Wulff Guyana. Excellent; the wooden-still earthiness and heavy molasses stand up to marrow's depth — Demerara terroir meets bovine umami at historical register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets Jung and Wulff Trinidad. Excellent; the moderate-ester refined character meets marrow at medium-aged register — pairs where Guyana would bring heavier wood-still weight.",
  "Bone Marrow|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Kahlua lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bone Marrow|Kansas City": "Kansas City after the bone marrow -- the marrow's beefy unctuous depth opens the meal, the 18oz strip's savory grain following with clean lean character that the marrow primes without overlap. Strong; both courses honor beef in different keys.",
  "Bone Marrow|Keenan Chardonnay": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity stays alongside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bone Marrow|Keenan Mernet": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye with bone marrow — premium allocated KO against umami fat. The 10-year KO character meets marrow with allocated authority. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with bone marrow — KO Confiscated against umami fat. The narrative-bourbon character meets marrow with KO mainstream authority. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's: Irish-luxury-icon limited release. Excellent on bone marrow; sherry-cask Irish depth handles umami-fat at allocation-luxury register — save the bottle.",
  "Bone Marrow|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register leans against the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness: the Rhone-blend body wraps the rich marrow, and the peppery edge frames the starter. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Bone Marrow|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality finds neutral with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bone Marrow|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker with bone marrow — brewer's-heritage gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek with bone marrow — 100-proof nutty bourbon against umami fat. The extra proof and high-rye body carry the marrow with real weight; a bolder call than the Wellers' wheated softness brings. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year with bone marrow — aged 100-proof Beam against umami fat. The 12 years of aging meets marrow's intensity where standard Knob Creek is only strong. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 with bone marrow — 15yr Beam KC against umami fat. Excellent; KC 15's aged 100-proof character engages marrow at aged-Beam register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 with bone marrow — 18yr Beam aged KC against umami fat. Excellent; KC 18's deeper aged character engages marrow at collector register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with bone marrow — blended bourbon-and-rye against umami fat. The layered complexity threads through marrow's mineral finish with more vocabulary than straight bourbon brings. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye with bone marrow — 100-proof bold rye against umami fat. The high-proof Beam rye meets marrow's intensity with authority; one of the strongest rye-marrow pairings. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva's wine-finish-caramel character frames the marrow's umami-fat depth — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bone Marrow|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo's crisp char-floral character lifts the marrow's umami-fat depth — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bone Marrow|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Añejo with bone marrow — aged Komos against umami fat. The Riedel-bottle luxury and extra añejo depth meet marrow at ceremonial register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado with bone marrow — wine-cask pink reposado against umami fat. Strong; the strawberry-cherry character is a distinctive counterpoint to marrow's intensity.",
  "Bone Marrow|Lagavulin 8": "* Lagavulin 8 -- Islay peat, younger-bottling, salt. The scotch backbone matches the rich marrow, and the smoke threads the starter. Gold standard; Lagavulin 8's salt-and-smoke is the ceremonial peated-Islay match for the marrow's umami fat — pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Napa Cab — Lail entry-level, polished blackcurrant, cedar, dark cherry, refined medium tannin. The refined tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the cedar plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak historically-pedigreed Napa Cab for marrow — Lail family structure on the rich starter. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the graphite plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak historically-significant Napa Cab for marrow — Daniel Cuvée pedigree on the rich starter. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver with bone marrow — family-heritage craft against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms blanco.",
  "Bone Marrow|Laphroaig 10": "Laphroaig 10 with bone marrow — medicinal iodine, tar, and band-aid TCP meet the marrow's fat in a pairing that's either heaven or a hard no. Nothing subtle about it. Pour for guests who know what they're asking for; the most decisive peated-Islay-and-fat pairing on the menu. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch with bone marrow — Heaven Hill wheated caramel against the marrow's umami fat. Capable and approachable; Weller 12 is richer, Larceny is the friendly alternate. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Pinot Noir rosé Champagne against marrow's rendered-fat richness — the silky strawberry profile is crushed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned and save the rosé for the raw bar or tartare opener.",
  "Bone Marrow|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne against marrow's rendered-fat richness — precise acidity is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned and keep LP Le Cuvée for the seafood tower, tartare, or filet opener.",
  "Bone Marrow|Le Garenne Rosé": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé against marrow's rendered-fat richness — delicate fresh-fruit has no weight to meet bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Le Garenne for burrata, wedge, crab cake, scallops, or summer starter service.",
  "Bone Marrow|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Tuscany Brunello di Montalcino DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Le Ragnaie, five years minimum aging, dried cherry, tobacco, leather, rose petal, full body, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the leather plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak Italy's-most-prestigious for marrow — Brunello on the rich starter. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Richness from the plate and structure from the wine share the same register — a savory weight-on-weight setup. Works, though neither flavor is showcased.",
  "Bone Marrow|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The cocktail's floral lightness has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat weight — lavender-and-honey don't cut bone depth. Never pair — keep the martini for starters and raw bar.",
  "Bone Marrow|Les Pallières Raciné": "Gigondas Rhône Valley Grenache — Les Pallières Raciné, Brunier-family old-vine, wild red fruit, garrigue, iron, earthy mineral, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts roasted-marrow unctuousness and the iron echoes beefy-bone depth. Peak Gigondas for bone marrow — old-vine structure balances the richness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Liberator Gin": "Liberator with bone marrow — Ferndale MI craft gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Licor 43 on the steak course instead.",
  "Bone Marrow|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Limoncello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. Bone marrow is pure collagen fat needing tannin to cut — Pinot's low tannin can't clear the palate. Works only when guest specifically wants Pinot with marrow; Cabs and bold reds peak higher for this rich starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|Little Book": "Little Book with bone marrow — Beam Fred Noe annual against umami fat. Excellent; Little Book cuts marrow at barrel-proof register with chapter-series distinction.",
  "Bone Marrow|Loaded Potato": "Bone Marrow and the loaded potato together -- two rich courses on the same plate, the table earns its substance. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Bone Marrow|Lobster Mac": "The bone marrow and the lobster mac -- cream rounds the protein, the plate composes thick. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Bone Marrow|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year with bone marrow — 20yr complex Highland against umami fat. Strong; Loch Lomond 20's aged depth sits alongside marrow at collector register; Oban 14 still leads the Highland-marrow pairings.",
  "Bone Marrow|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with bone marrow — sourced boutique rye against umami fat. The premium-blended character meets marrow with boutique-brand authority. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Long Road Gin": "Long Road with bone marrow — Grand Rapids Michigan-botanical against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal: 'The Neighbors' — Oaxacan artisanal pit-roasted expressions from multiple mezcaleros. Excellent on marrow; the authentic-traditional-smoke meets umami fat at craft register — the mezcal the purist reaches for.",
  "Bone Marrow|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII with bone marrow — 40-100 year aged Grande Champagne against umami fat. Excellent; the marrow's deep umami is the rare flavor intense enough to meet Louis XIII without the bottle overpowering or being overpowered. The benchmark cognac-marrow experience at legendary register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano with bone marrow — Basilicata amaro against umami fat. Strong; floral-anise cuts through marrow distinctively.",
  "Bone Marrow|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin lifts the marrow's umami-fat depth — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Bone Marrow|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry with bone marrow — Oloroso-cask Speyside against umami fat. Strong; Macallan 12's sherry-cask depth frames the marrow where the bourbon-cask Speysides just sit — but Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18 with bone marrow — 18yr premium Oloroso Speyside against umami fat. Strong; Macallan 18's aged-sherry depth frames marrow at collector register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Bone Marrow|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate with bone marrow — Easter Elchies Estate-oak Macallan against umami fat. Strong; Macallan Estate's distinctive depth frames marrow; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin cuts roasted-marrow unctuousness and the dark chocolate echoes toasted-bread char. Peak Petite-Sirah for bone marrow — powerful Napa on the richness. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — light-body soft-tannin Pinot lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Macauley for the chicken, salmon, or seafood course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The starter's fat and umami meet the bold red's dark fruit — both land on the palate with real weight. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Mahon Gin": "Mahón with bone marrow — PDO Menorcan juniper gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark with bone marrow — soft caramel and vanilla against umami fat. Approachable and well-matched; Maker's Mark Cellar Aged brings more weight, but standard Maker's hits the mark cleanly for everyday pours. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Bone Marrow: the rich umami fat meets Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's cellar-aged depth. Standard Maker's is pleasant but thin here; the Cellar Aged earns the marrow. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- light spirit reads thin against the rich meat opener. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a light spirit. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Malibu Rum belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye with bone marrow — extended-aged Michigan rye against umami fat. The 16-year depth meets marrow with distinctive Michigan-distillery authority. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Marc Brédif Chinon's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Margarita": "Margarita's crisp agave-brine build leans against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the cocktail lift refreshes the starter, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bone Marrow|Marimar Estate Christina": "Russian River Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — plush Pinot lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Marimar Christina for the chicken, salmon, or seafood course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Market Fish": "Market Fish with the bone marrow as the lead-in -- the kitchen-driven fish plays against the marrow-and-bread starter. Strong; the starter sets the table for the market fish.",
  "Bone Marrow|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Mata Hari Absinthe for the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Bourbon with bone marrow — Michter's flagship single-barrel against umami fat. Excellent; Michter's 10's 10yr depth engages marrow at allocated-prestigious register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye with bone marrow — premium aged Michter's against umami fat. The 10-year polished character meets marrow with small-batch aged authority. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye with bone marrow — smooth Michter's rye against umami fat. The pot-still smooth character frames marrow differently than BT's Sazerac Rye — more polished, less sharp pepper. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco with bone marrow — additive-free craft against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms blanco.",
  "Bone Marrow|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads register-aligned with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness: the aged-agave weight wraps the rich marrow, and the soft-spice note softens the starter. Strong; ceremonial presentation sits with richness at ultra-premium register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado with bone marrow — triple-distilled reposado against umami fat. Strong; modern cleanness is capable but the starter earns a more character-forward reposado.",
  "Bone Marrow|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam with bone marrow — Sazerac Seagram's-tribute blend against umami fat. Excellent; Mister Sam's complex blended character engages marrow at Seagram's-tribute register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Mocha Creme": "The bone marrow alongside the mocha creme -- protein hands off to dessert, the close builds without conflict. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Bone Marrow|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 with bone marrow — 47-botanical German gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder with bone marrow — Glenfiddich-Balvenie-Kininvie blend against umami fat. Works; Monkey Shoulder's fruit-and-vanilla sits alongside marrow; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro with bone marrow — approachable amaro against umami fat. Strong; the vanilla-orange is a gentler digestif than Averna's caramel approach.",
  "Bone Marrow|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- light spirit reads thin against the rich meat opener. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a light spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Mount Gay Rum belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Muga Reserva": "Muga Reserva's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the oak frames the cream. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Bone Marrow|Mushroom Bisque": "Bone Marrow and the mushroom bisque together -- protein and umami share the plate, the build reads weighty and clean. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Bone Marrow|Mushrooms": "The bone marrow and the mushrooms -- protein meets umami, the table builds substance. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Bone Marrow|Myers's Rum": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets Myers's. Excellent; the molasses-heavy profile handles marrow's umami at workhorse register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Negroni": "Gin, Contratto, Campari — equal parts, stirred. The bitter-orange backbone cuts marrow's rendered-fat richness directly and the herbal vermouth complexity plays against roasted bone savor. Peak cocktail for marrow when guest wants bitter structure rather than bourbon sweetness.",
  "Bone Marrow|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye with bone marrow — novelty UK-aged rye against umami fat. The distinctive maturation character threads through marrow's intensity with a voice no domestic rye matches. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch with bone marrow — novelty UK-aged against umami fat. The distinctive maturation gives the starter a conversation angle that standard bourbons don't bring. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with bone marrow — Michigan beer-barrel bourbon against umami fat. Excellent; NH Beer Barrel's hop-derived bitterness engages marrow with distinctive Michigan brewer-heritage character.",
  "Bone Marrow|New York Sour": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- the cocktail's weight is forward for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. New York Sour lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bone Marrow|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Oakville prestige Cab — deep cassis, blackberry, graphite, subtle oak, concentrated. The structured body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the graphite frames the bone depth. Peak prestige Oakville Cab for marrow — Nickel & Nickel's concentrated terroir handles the rich starter with serious structure. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolet's with bone marrow — Turkish rose/peach gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Not a Paper Plane": "Bulleit bourbon, Nonino Quintessential, Aperol, Angostura. The bourbon cuts marrow's rendered fat and Nonino's herbal-bitter backbone adds complexity against savory bone; Aperol softens the edges. Peak spirit-forward cocktail for marrow when guest wants amaro-driven over pure bourbon.",
  "Bone Marrow|Oban 14": "Oban 14 with bone marrow — tiny-distillery coastal Highland against umami fat. Excellent; Oban's sea-salt-and-honey character is the featured Highland-marrow pairing — the maritime-umami resonance is distinctive, more lively than the sherry-forward or honey-floral alternatives.",
  "Bone Marrow|Oban 18": "Oban 18 with bone marrow — premium 18yr coastal Highland against umami fat. Excellent; Oban 18's aged maritime character is the featured Highland-marrow pairing at premium register — the coastal-umami + sherry-depth combination is richer than Oban 14's simpler maritime approach.",
  "Bone Marrow|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo's agave-char character holds the line with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone Marrow|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with bone marrow — Old Elk distillery cigar-paired bourbon against umami fat. Excellent; Old Elk Cigar Cut's cigar-companion character engages marrow — built for rich flavors, it finds its native pairing here.",
  "Bone Marrow|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer with bone marrow — wheated bourbon from emmer wheat against umami fat. Excellent; Old Emmer's wheated softness sits alongside marrow with craft distinction; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year with bone marrow — 100-proof wheated BIB against umami fat. The bonded wheat gives the starter a different frame than Wellers' 90-proof softness or Heaven Hill BIB's non-wheated structure. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year with bone marrow — 9-year wheated BIB against umami fat. The deeper oak and mature honey meet the marrow with more weight than Old Fitz 7 carries. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with bone marrow — 16yr OGD high-rye against umami fat. Excellent; OGD 16's rye-forward aged spice engages marrow at distinctive aged-Beam register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Opus One": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the mocha-cedar plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak collector Napa blend for marrow — Mondavi-Rothschild prestige on the rich starter. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Willamette Trappist Chard against marrow's rendered-fat richness — even rich oak-butterscotch is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Acolytes for salmon, chicken, scallops, crab cake, or the bisque course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Pablo Sour": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla, egg white. The Pablo's silky foam has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat richness — lemon and foam don't cut bone depth. Never pair — keep the Pablo for starters and raw bar, route marrow to whiskey cocktails.",
  "Bone Marrow|Paloma": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The Paloma's clean light structure has no match for marrow's rendered-fat richness — grapefruit and vanilla don't cut bone depth. Never pair — keep the Paloma for starters, route marrow to whiskey cocktails or big reds.",
  "Bone Marrow|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye with bone marrow — unicorn Pappy against umami fat. The Van Winkle legacy intensity meets marrow with allocation-story authority; a once-a-table pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage 16th Ed with bone marrow — HH collector cask-strength against umami fat. Excellent; Parker's Heritage 16th cuts marrow at annual-release register — its cask-strength weight actually matches marrow.",
  "Bone Marrow|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's crisp oak-agave character sits with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone Marrow|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver with bone marrow — iconic blanco against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms clean blanco.",
  "Bone Marrow|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character flattens the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Patron XO Cafe belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Bouzy Grand Cru rosé Champagne against marrow's rendered-fat richness — Burgundian body is overwhelmed by bone depth and the red-fruit profile has no hook into the marrow's savory center. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned.",
  "Bone Marrow|Peanut Butter Brownie": "The bone marrow and the PB brownie -- the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness bookends with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Bone Marrow|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Peju — Rutherford Cab, dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the cocoa frames the bone depth. Strong elegant Napa Cab for marrow — family-estate refinement, though firmer Cabs like Ghost Block or Silver Oak peak higher for the rich starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand with bone marrow — craft Grande Champagne against umami fat. Excellent; Ferrand's floral-honey artisanal profile cuts marrow with unblended character — distinct from Courvoisier's drier oak approach; the thinking-person's cognac-marrow pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "Pierre Gimonnet 2015 grower Champagne against marrow's rendered-fat richness — even vintage depth is overwhelmed by bone intensity. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep the Special Club for tartare, seafood tower, or the filet course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye with bone marrow — 110-proof bold Maryland-style rye against umami fat. The high proof and bold rye spice meet marrow with authority at higher impact than standard approachable ryes. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Pineau des Charentes": "Never pair Pineau des Charentes with bone marrow — fortified honey drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and the warm nutty Cognac clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. Pineau belongs at aperitif or dessert.",
  "Bone Marrow|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth with bone marrow — softer English gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Aldo Conterno Bussia Monforte d'Alba, rose petal, tar, dried cherry, orange peel, iron minerality, full body, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the iron minerality plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak serious-Barolo for marrow — iron-on-iron mineral match. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery 2004 Cuvée Louise against marrow's rendered-fat richness — aged complexity is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep the Cuvée Louise for tartare, scallops, seafood tower, or filet.",
  "Bone Marrow|Porterhouse": "The porterhouse preceded by the bone marrow -- protein on protein, the table holds register without softening. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding the cut.",
  "Bone Marrow|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt — Napa Cab with blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft tannin. The lush body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the chocolate frames the bone depth. Strong polished Napa Cab for marrow — indulgent match, though firm-tannin Cabs like Silver Oak or Frias peak higher for the starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|RAEN Royal St Robert": "Sea-influenced Sonoma Coast Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — silky RAEN lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Royal St Robert for the chicken, salmon, or seafood course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Rainbow Trout": "Rainbow Trout with the bone marrow as the lead-in -- the lemon-bright trout plays against the rendered-marrow plate. Strong; the starter sets the table for the trout.",
  "Bone Marrow|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Catalan Cava rosé against marrow's rendered-fat richness — saline-berry bubbles have no weight to meet bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Cava for raw bar, tartare, or starter course where it excels.",
  "Bone Marrow|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 Year -- single pot still Irish, sherry-cask, spice. The pot-still depth wraps the rich marrow, and the sherry-cask spice frames the starter. Strong; curated character covers the cut; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Redbreast 21 Year": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets Redbreast 21 Year. Excellent; extended-aged single-pot-still Irish character handles marrow at Redbreast-luxury register — the Irish flagship on the umami plate.",
  "Bone Marrow|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Gatsby with bone marrow — Gatsby-era tribute MGP 15yr against umami fat. Excellent; Remus 15's aged rye-spice + cherry engages marrow at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Rémy VSOP with bone marrow — refined Grande Champagne against umami fat. Strong; Rémy's elegance supports marrow, though Courvoisier's drier oak cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo with bone marrow — Moorish-inspired aged tequila against umami fat. The distinctive-packaging extra añejo meets marrow with ceremonial presentation. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality sits alongside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bone Marrow|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique with bone marrow — aged retailer-label against umami fat. The boutique-sourced character meets marrow with retailer-label authority. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Roast Half Chicken": "Roast Half Chicken with the bone marrow as the lead-in -- the jus-finished bird plays against the bone-enhanced richness. Strong; the starter sets the table for the chicken.",
  "Bone Marrow|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The bone marrow with the chickpea soup -- the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness meets the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: heat lifts the rich course, the table earns the warmer note. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Bone Marrow|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Le Mesnil Grand Cru blanc de blancs against marrow's rendered-fat richness — austere precision is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Moncuit for tartare, seafood tower, scallops, or filet course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Roku Gin": "Roku with bone marrow — Suntory sakura-yuzu-sansho gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets Ron Zacapa. Excellent; the aged-solera character with its depth meets the marrow at luxury register — one of the few rums that earns its place against bourbon on this plate. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with bone marrow — aged Wild Turkey against umami fat. The 90-proof and 10 years of oak give the pour the structure to meet the starter cleanly; a classic Kentucky approach to the marrow. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Salt-Cured Halibut": "The halibut after the bone marrow -- the salt-cured flesh threads the marrow-and-bread starter. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Bone Marrow|Sambuca": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sambuca belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bone Marrow|San Simeon Stormwatch": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis sits with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the coffee threads the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Santa Barbara Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — layered Sta. Rita Hills Pinot still lacks the grip needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Sanford & Benedict for the filet, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The bone marrow and the garlic spinach -- the bright register cleaves the heavy course, the table breathes. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Bone Marrow|Sauternes Glass": "Never pair Sauternes Glass with bone marrow — luscious unctuous honey drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and noble-rot sweetness clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. Sauternes is a dessert or cheese-course pour.",
  "Bone Marrow|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The rye's spice cuts marrow fat and the absinthe-edge nose lifts the savory-bone richness; Peychaud's floral-cherry softens the minerality. Peak spirit-forward pour for marrow with historical cocktail weight.",
  "Bone Marrow|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye with bone marrow — iconic rye against umami fat. The rye's pepper and floral notes cut through marrow's richness in a way wheated bourbons don't; a classic rye-starter pairing at the benchmark register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna with bone marrow — New Zealand single malt against umami fat. Strong; Fortuna's NZ terroir sits alongside marrow at distinctive register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the leather plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak King-of-Italian for marrow — Scavino prestige on the rich starter. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Rheingau Riesling against marrow's rendered-fat richness — off-dry German white has no weight to meet bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Schloss Vollrads for bisque, crab cake, scallops, burrata, or the chicken course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis sits with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone Marrow|Seared Scallops": "Bone Marrow with the scallops -- the roasted bone matches the sweet scallop plate. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Bone Marrow|Seasonal Soup": "Bone Marrow alongside the seasonal soup -- the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "Bone Marrow|Seasonal Vegetables": "Bone Marrow alongside the seasonal vegetables -- the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness meets the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: neither course pulls focus, the table reads even. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Bone Marrow|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Shafer 1.5": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin. The plush tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the mocha-vanilla plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak Stags Leap Cab for marrow — Shafer opulence on the rich starter. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Shafer Hillside Select": "Stags Leap collector's Cab — Hillside Select 32-month French oak, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violet, graphite, dense yet silky, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the graphite plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak collector's Napa Cab for marrow — Hillside Select dense-yet-silky structure on the rich starter. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Shrimp Bisque": "Bone Marrow alongside the shrimp bisque -- cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "Bone Marrow|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco with bone marrow — rustic craft against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms blanco.",
  "Bone Marrow|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado with bone marrow — traditional agave against umami fat. Strong; the craft character gives the pairing more texture than Don Julio brings.",
  "Bone Marrow|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak — Alexander Valley Cab, American-oak vanilla, coconut, dill, ripe cassis, plum. The lush body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the vanilla-dill frames the bone depth distinctively. Strong recognizable Cab for marrow — American-oak signature, though firmer Cabs like Frias or Ghost Block peak higher for the starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Boutique Napa family estate Cab — dark plum, cassis, toasted oak, long refined finish, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the toasted oak plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak boutique Napa Cab for marrow — Simon Family reserve-level structure on the rich starter. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "St. Helena Napa Cab — Spottswoode organic family estate, plum, dark cherry, violet, silky medium tannin. The silky tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the violet plays against the smoky mineral depth. Strong organic Napa Cab for marrow — Spottswoode estate polish on the rich starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab — blackberry, cassis, mocha, firm tannin. The full body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the mocha-tannin frames the bone depth. Works as by-the-glass Cab for marrow — classic Napa character, though premium Cabs like Silver Oak or Caymus peak higher for the starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry Napa Sauv Blanc against marrow's rendered-fat richness — rounded Sauv Blanc has no weight to meet bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep St Supéry for crab cake, scallops, shrimp cocktail, wedge, or seafood tower.",
  "Bone Marrow|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the espresso plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak estate Meritage for marrow — age-integrated Napa on the rich starter. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with bone marrow — BT cask-strength against umami fat. Excellent; Stagg Jr.'s cask-strength cuts marrow at barrel-proof register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality leans against the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness: the unflavored body sits with the rich marrow, and the crisp profile carries the starter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bone Marrow|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc against marrow's rendered-fat richness — zesty passionfruit-grass has no weight to meet bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Stoneleigh for wedge, burrata, crab cake, shrimp cocktail, or seafood starters.",
  "Bone Marrow|Swordfish": "The swordfish after the bone marrow -- the firm-fish flesh meets the bone-enhanced richness. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Bone Marrow|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 with bone marrow — whole-citrus Tanq against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Bone Marrow|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray with bone marrow — 47.3% juniper against umami fat. Avoid; juniper can't cut marrow the way Fernet Branca does.",
  "Bone Marrow|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Never pair Taylor Fladgate Tawny with bone marrow — smooth balanced sugar drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and the almond oxidative note clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. Tawny belongs at dessert.",
  "Bone Marrow|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Never pair Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle with bone marrow — flagship sweet structure drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and dark-fruit intensity clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. The Vintage Bottle is a dessert pour.",
  "Bone Marrow|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with bone marrow — triple-cask aged against umami fat. The Scotch-sherry-cognac cask complexity meets marrow with multi-cask distinctiveness. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter — Napa Cab with rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth lingering finish. The polished body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the mocha frames the bone depth. Peak mid-tier Napa Cab for marrow — crowd-pleaser concentration handles the rich starter confidently. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tuscany Chianti Classico DOCG Sangiovese — Arceno vibrant cherry, dried herbs, tomato leaf, savory earth, medium body, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the savory earth plays against the smoky mineral depth. Works as Tuscan Sangiovese for marrow — Italian table wine on the rich starter.",
  "Bone Marrow|The BG": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth meets the starter's fat and umami — both land heavy. A headliner opener. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|The Happy Wife": "Tito's, orange juice, ginger syrup. The cocktail's simple light profile has no match for marrow's rendered-fat richness — orange juice can't cut bone depth. Never pair — keep the Happy Wife for starters and approachable openers.",
  "Bone Marrow|The Manhattan": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The bourbon's vanilla-oak depth cuts marrow's fat richness and the herbal vermouth complexity adds depth against the savory-bone profile. Peak spirit-forward pour for marrow when guest wants classic whiskey cocktail over Old Fashioned.",
  "Bone Marrow|The Prisoner Red Blend": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The integrated tannin cuts roasted-marrow unctuousness and the layered fruit echoes toasted-bread char. Peak Prisoner for bone marrow — Napa lush-on-rich harmony. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk preceded by the bone marrow -- the 36oz showpiece meets cleanly the beefy unctuous opener, and the smoke frames the herb butter. Excellent; the starter sets up the cut at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with bone marrow — BTAC barrel-proof against umami fat. The uncut Sazerac intensity meets marrow with allocated BT authority; a collector-pour marrow pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with bone marrow — Barton chardonnay-cask against umami fat. Works; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay character sits alongside marrow; wine-finish is under-specified for marrow intensity.",
  "Bone Marrow|Titos Vodka": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality sits alongside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bone Marrow|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky -- Suntory entry, light, citrus. The entry Suntory body sits with the rich marrow, and the citrus lifts the starter. Strong; polished aged character sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Tomato Basil": "Bone Marrow and the tomato basil together -- bright-acid breaks the weight, the plate finds its lift. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Bone Marrow|Transfusion": "Hangar 1, muddled black grapes, lime, ginger beer top. The cocktail's sessionable fruit profile has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat richness — lime and grape don't cut bone depth. Never pair — keep the Transfusion for starters and raw bar.",
  "Bone Marrow|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado with bone marrow — cedar-aged reposado against umami fat. Strong; the cedar is a distinctive counterpoint to marrow's richness.",
  "Bone Marrow|Truffle Fries": "The bone marrow opens the meal, the truffle fries with the entree -- the unctuous marrow matches the parsley-finished fries. Strong; both items.",
  "Bone Marrow|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s crisp grain-malt character holds steady against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "The tuna and the bone marrow across courses -- the sesame crust anchors against the bone-enhanced richness. Strong; the opener carries its register before the main.",
  "Bone Marrow|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "The bone marrow with the curry -- the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness meets the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth. Strong; the opening courses share the table at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Venge Family Reserve": "Kirk Venge flagship Oakville Cab — concentrated blackcurrant, dark chocolate, vanilla, seamless polished finish, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the dark chocolate plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak Oakville flagship for marrow — Venge craftsman precision on the rich starter. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Venge Scout's Honor": "Napa Zin-led blend — Kirk Venge's Scout's Honor, blackberry, boysenberry, black pepper, vanilla, full body, generous finish. Black pepper cuts roasted-marrow unctuousness and full body handles beefy-bone depth. Strong Zin-led for marrow — Californian fruit wraps the rendered richness.",
  "Bone Marrow|Venge Silencieux": "Kirk Venge single-vineyard Napa — dark cherry, cassis, chocolate, floral violet, silky soft tannin. The silky tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the chocolate plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak winemaker's Napa Cab for marrow — Venge precision on the rich starter. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Vesper": "Nolet's, Ketel One, Lillet. The martini's clean dryness has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat richness — Lillet's citrus can't cut that deep. Never pair — keep the Vesper for raw bar and starters, route marrow to whiskey cocktails.",
  "Bone Marrow|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Yellow label Veuve against marrow's rendered-fat richness — even the iconic Reims Champagne is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Veuve for tartare, seafood tower, or the celebration toast.",
  "Bone Marrow|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The rye-cognac split handles marrow's fatty weight from both sides — spice cuts, richness meets. Bénédictine's herbal edge adds complexity. Peak cocktail route for guest wanting sophistication with the marrow course.",
  "Bone Marrow|Villon Cognac": "Villon with bone marrow — saffron/elderflower Courvoisier-sibling against umami fat. Strong; Villon's modern profile cuts marrow at contemporary register, though Courvoisier proper's drier oak cuts it harder.",
  "Bone Marrow|Vin Santo": "Never pair Vin Santo with bone marrow — oxidative honey drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and the dried apricot clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. Vin Santo is a Tuscan finishing pour, not an appetizer.",
  "Bone Marrow|Weller 107": "Bone Marrow: the rich umami fat meets Weller 107's heat and body. The highest-impact Weller for this starter; Weller 12 is more refined, Weller 107 matches the marrow's intensity. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year with bone marrow — aged wheated sweetness against the marrow's umami fat. The 12-year's oak depth threads into the marrow's mineral finish. A richer pour than Weller Special Reserve for this starter; the extra age earns its place. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium with bone marrow — the Millennium's extended oak and deep honey meet marrow's umami fat at a premium register. A ceremonial pour; the marrow's mineral finish picks up the bourbon's aged complexity cleanly. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve with bone marrow — soft wheated sweetness against the marrow's umami fat. A clean, approachable match; Weller 12 is richer, Millennium is ceremonial — this is the everyday pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality leans against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bone Marrow|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with bone marrow — 100-proof aged WP rye against umami fat. The aged Vermont-finished character meets marrow with premium single-barrel authority. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with bone marrow — wine-cask-finished WP against umami fat. The Sauternes/Madeira/Port complexity gives marrow a layered rye-and-fruit counterpoint. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with bone marrow — extended-aged 15-year WP against umami fat. The Vermont-oak-finished depth meets marrow with flagship premium authority. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone Marrow|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with bone marrow — barrel-proof allocated WP against umami fat. The Siren's Song intensity meets marrow at the ultra-premium WP register; a collector-pour starter pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with bone marrow — 10th-edition allocated WP against umami fat. The X Commandments intensity meets marrow at ultra-premium register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone Marrow|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year with bone marrow — 100-proof Canadian-sourced rye against umami fat. The WhistlePig character meets marrow with Vermont-brand distinctiveness at the 100-proof register. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with bone marrow — craft high-rye oak spice against umami fat. The pot-still character gives the marrow a distinctive frame; a conversation-starter pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with bone marrow — small-batch character against umami fat. Woodford's fruit and floral notes thread through the marrow's mineral depth in a way Buffalo Trace's cleaner profile doesn't. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with bone marrow — iconic Double-Oaked benchmark against umami fat. Strong; Woodford Double Oaked's second-char character engages marrow at iconic-benchmark register.",
  "Bone Marrow|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with bone marrow — fruit-forward rye against umami fat. The Woodford elegance and fruit notes thread through marrow's intensity with small-batch refinement. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with bone marrow — PNW craft rye against umami fat. The Washington State character meets marrow with regional-craft distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone Marrow|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Japanese-sherry-touched character holds steady against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the dried-fruit notes meet the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone Marrow|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year: Suntory's Japanese flagship. Excellent on marrow; the sherry-cask-driven profile meets umami fat at refined register — save the bottle for the guest who treats whisky like art.",
  "Bone Marrow|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge mis-pairs with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- this bitter's register fights the rich meat starter — needs warmth or fat, not herbs. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Yellow Chartreuse lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's butter-caramel character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's bold cherry-coffee cassis finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the velvety body lifts the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the 2 James Gin on another course instead.",
  "Bone-In Filet|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's oak-spice character matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with the bone-in filet — double-matured 16yr Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Aberlour 16's bourbon-plus-sherry balance frames the cut with approachable-premium character.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the añejo register carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the caramel-agave register brightens the cut. Works; brightness can cut the glaze but wheated bourbons sit better with this dish.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the rested-agave depth sits with the bone-enhanced filet, and the soft-spice note softens the cut. Strong; the extra proof cuts through the dairy with more conviction than softer reposados.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Alexander Valley Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the velvety body lifts the lean cut, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- bitter botanicals on the cut fight the savory char without payoff. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Amaro Nonino belongs on another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Ancho Reyes lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with the bone-in filet — flagship AE port-finish against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; AE's port-finish character frames the cut at wine-finished register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with the bone-in filet — AE cask-strength port-finish against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; AE CS's cask-strength character frames the cut at premium wine-finish register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "AE Triple Cask with the bone-in filet — AE triple-cask against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; AE Triple Cask's layered-finish character frames the cut at distinctive register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Aperol for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Asparagus": "The bone-in filet with the asparagus -- the 14oz bone-deepened cut meets the asparagus's tender green-vegetal edge, the spear's lift balancing the marrow-touched richness. Strong; the side carries cleanly against the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Au Gratin Potatoes": "* The bone-in filet with the au gratin -- the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness frames the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust. Gold standard; the side that defines the steak course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-coffee cassis frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the velvety body lifts the lean cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Averna Amaro for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Aviation": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- cocktail citrus reads thin against the cut's structure. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Aviation on another course instead.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Aviation Gin for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Avion 44": "Bone-In Filet: bone-in cut meets 44-month aged Avion. Extended maturation frames the cut with premium Avion authority. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's unaged silver-spirit body sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the unaged body brightens the bone-enhanced filet, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- light spirit reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light spirit. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Bacardi Rum for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Baker's": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the bourbon depth settles into the bone-enhanced filet, and the vanilla softens the cut. Strong; Marian McLain L.E.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the bone-in filet — American Oak vanilla-forward Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Balvenie's sweet-oak richness frames the cut's depth with handcrafted Speyside character.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the bone-in filet — Caribbean rum-cask-finished Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; the tropical-sweet rum-cask character frames the cut's marrow-adjacent richness with distinctive finish-cask story.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with the bone-in filet — hand-picked First Fill American Oak Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; the single-barrel vanilla-forward depth frames the cut with collector-register character.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood with the bone-in filet — 21yr port-cask-finished Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Portwood's port-richness and 21-year aging frame the cut with ultra-premium gravitas.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with the bone-in filet — Bardstown French-wine-cask finish against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Bardstown Château Laboude's French-wine character frames the cut at contemporary-craft register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6's oak-vanilla character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth supports the lean cut without overpowering, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS's chocolate-cocoa character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare with the bone-in filet — rum-cask Bardstown against tenderloin-with-bone. Works; Bardstown Foursquare frames the cut at Caribbean-finish register.",
