  "Bone-In Filet|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the rye spice cuts the bone-enhanced filet, and the rye edge frames the cut. Strong; accessible everyday match.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with the bone-in filet — Beam's BH red-wine finish against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; BH Red Wine Cask's rye-spice + red-wine-fruit frames the cut at distinctive register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast's crisp caramel-smoke character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth supports the lean cut without overpowering, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Bee's Knees": "Bee's Knees' citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Bee's Knees for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Beefeater Gin on another course instead.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Beignets": "After the bone-in filet, the beignets -- the elevated cut hands off to warm choux and powdered sugar, the dipping sauce easing the close. Works; the meal closes light after the marrow-edged main.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- clean vodka reads thin against the cut's weight — needs a wine or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Belvedere Vodka for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth matches register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the oak depth lays under the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak frames the cut. Strong; the 10-year oak gives the side backbone distinct from wheated approaches.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold's bold caramel-juniper character matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey character holds register with the tenderloin, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original's single-barrel-caramel character lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey character holds register with the tenderloin, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's Straight from the Barrel's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cut. Strong; JW Blue's silky-complex character meets the earthy-rich at collector register; consider whether the side deserves a $200 pour or if Blue is better spent on the main.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the bourbon backbone carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak-and-spice register settles the cut. Strong; EHT Full Proof meets the earthy-rich at historic Kentucky register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8's molasses-tropical character lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth supports the lean cut without overpowering, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9's cherry-spice character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth supports the lean cut without overpowering, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
