  "Bone-In Filet|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Bombay Sapphire Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Bone Marrow": "The bone-in filet after the bone marrow -- both courses speak the same beefy-marrow language, the unctuous opener priming the palate for the 14oz cut's bone-deepened butter. Strong; the marrow line carries through the meal.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Booker's": "Booker's cask-strength caramel-juniper character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Botanist Gin": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Botanist Gin for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned: this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth meets the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge. Oak, caramel, and spirit heat stand up to the beef's render; the caramelized sweetness mirrors the crust. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the caramel-spice register matches the bone-enhanced filet, and the vanilla softens the cut. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's juniper character lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the juniper sharpens the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Bowmore 12": "Bowmore 12's peat-sherry character lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's bold earth cassis lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the concentrated body carries the strip, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Broccoli Cheddar": "Bone-In Filet into the broccoli cheddar -- cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Broccolini": "The bone-in filet with the broccolini -- the bright cuts the rich cleanly. Works; the side sits without pulling focus.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich's crisp char-citrus character lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth carries the cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Brussels and Belly": "The bone-in filet alongside Brussels + Belly -- the 14oz bone-enhanced cut's deeper marrow character meets the maple-pork-belly glaze, sweet-savory richness against bone-deepened beef. Strong; the side reads built for the bone-in version.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace -- vanilla, toffee, oak. The bourbon depth settles into the bone-enhanced filet, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cut. Strong; Sinatra Select's grooved-barrel character frames the two-cut plate at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Buffalo Trace Cream lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon's bold spice-vanilla character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Burrata": "The bone-in filet after the burrata -- fresh milky basil-tomato cream opens the meal, the 14oz cut following with bone-deepened butter that meets the cream's richness on the same plane. Strong; the dairy opener sets the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Butternut Squash": "Bone-In Filet with the butternut squash alongside -- protein hands off to dessert, the close builds without conflict. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cade Cabernet Sauvignon -- Howell Mountain Cab, cassis, mountain tannin. The Howell-Mountain Cab tannin frames the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis grips the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila's crisp character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the añejo weight grips the lean cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Campari": "Campari's aperitivo at its most textbook mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- bitter botanicals on the cut fight the savory char without payoff. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Campari belongs on another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend finds neutral with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the blended whisky body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the soft profile rounds the cut. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Canadian Club 43 Year": "Canadian Club 43 Year's Canadian whisky character finds neutral with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the bone-enhanced filet, and the soft profile rounds the cut. Works; grain notes don't really engage the soup but don't clash.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Captain Morgan Rum lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the easygoing Canadian character handles the bone-enhanced filet, and the light-grain note brightens the cut. Works; curated single-barrel slightly over-specified.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Carrot Cake": "The bone-in filet into the carrot cake -- the marrow-edged butter-tender yields to spiced cake and cream-cheese frosting, walnut crunch on the close. Works; the spice-and-frosting reads heavier than the cut needed, but the meal builds clean across courses.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- dessert wine is post-meal — wrong register for the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Casa LBV Port for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight matches register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the earthen-pit smoke carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the earthy backbone threads the cut. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge. The dark-fruit frame supports the filet, but the tannin rides harder than the lean profile demands. Pour when the guest wants the bold-red experience. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Chambord for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Cheesecake": "The bone-in filet closes with the cheesecake -- the marrow-laced butter-tender yields to dense dairy custard on graham crust, berry compote brightening the close. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Chocolate Brownie": "The bone-in filet closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the marrow-laced finish yields to dense cocoa fudge, the warm vanilla lifting the close. Strong; bone-depth into chocolate-depth, the meal arc reads thoughtful.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Chocolate Cake": "After the bone-in filet, the chocolate cake -- the marrow-edged butter-tender flesh hands off to layered cocoa-and-ganache, the vanilla scoop framing the close. Strong; the elevated cut earns the layered dessert across the meal.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- clean vodka reads thin against the cut's weight — needs a wine or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Chopin Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Bros Brandy's rancio-edged cognac weight leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the brandy depth wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the caramel rounds the cut. Works; Ferrand's craft character handles the richness.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Château Batailley": "Château Batailley's powerful blackcurrant body leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Château Beaucastel": "Château Beaucastel's bold earth-leather cassis finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the firm tannin reads cleanly against the lean cut, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The velvety Merlot wraps butter-tender bone-enhanced filet and the truffle plays with bone mineral depth. Strong Pomerol for bone-in filet — plush Bordeaux on the elevated cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Château Haut Segottes": "Château Haut Segottes's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Château de Rouillac": "Left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, and tobacco with firm tannin and a structured, old-world profile. The bone-in filet has enough richness from the bone and bold marble for the firmer tannin to grab. A non-Napa alternative worth offering to guests who know Bordeaux. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Clam Chowder": "Bone-In Filet preceded by the chowder -- two rich courses on the same plate, the table earns its substance. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's aged-agave character leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the barrel-rested body handles the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak threads the cut. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango with the bone-in filet — ceramic-bottle luxury against bone-in cut. The Durango-artisanship ceremony frames the cut with regional-Mexican craft presentation. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold with the bone-in filet — golden-ceramic joven blend against bone-in cut. The ceremonial presentation and multi-style blend create a distinctive pairing experience. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the wood-fire body meets the bone-enhanced filet, and the earthy backbone threads the cut. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata's silky Jalisco blanco with ceramic-decanter presentation finds neutral with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; premium craft composes cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado -- ceramic-bottle reposado, caramel, agave. The ceramic-icon reposado depth wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the agave-and-caramel rounds the cut. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra with the bone-in filet — top-tier 5-year Clase Azul against bone-in cut. The Ultra's extended aging and platinum-gold ceramic create the most ceremonial extra-añejo pairing on the menu. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep's crisp caramel-char character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth carries the cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's crisp agave-floral character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the añejo weight grips the lean cut, and the juniper sharpens the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's amaro-bitter register leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the herbal cut works the bone-enhanced filet, and the amaro backbone frames the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the unaged-cane note plays against the cut. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado's agave-char character sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the añejo weight grips the lean cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's agave-pepper character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the añejo weight grips the lean cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg's bold agave-caramel character lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-tequila depth carries the cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's añejo-pour depth leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the barrel-rested body handles the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak threads the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's structured dark-fruit weight sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Corpse Reviver": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- cocktail citrus reads thin against the cut's structure. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Corpse Reviver lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac finds neutral with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the eau-de-vie body carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak-and-vanilla edge brightens the cut. Works; Rémy's elegance meets the char at functional register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Crab Cake": "The bone-in filet after the crab cake -- the panko-and-lump crab opens with delicate sweetness, the 14oz bone-enhanced cut following with marrow-touched butter that doesn't crowd the opener's memory. Strong; refined opener into bone-deepened cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 with the bone-in filet — Classic Malt Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Cragganmore's meaty depth and subtle smoke find the cut's marrow-adjacent richness with distinctive character.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Creamed Spinach": "The bone-in filet with the creamed spinach -- the 14oz bone-deepened butter holds with the rich bechamel-laced greens, the cream complementing the marrow-touched richness. Strong; the call composes without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Creamy Potato": "Bone-In Filet with the potato soup alongside -- cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Creme Brulee": "After the bone-in filet, the brulee -- the marrow-edged finish gives way to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the elevated cut into the steakhouse classic.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée -- Willamette Pinot, red cherry, silky tannins. The silky red-fruit body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cherry-fruit frames the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the Canadian-whisky body sits with the bone-enhanced filet, and the caramel rounds the cut. Works; craft-blend covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Cucumber Gimlet": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Cucumber Gimlet lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the sherry-cask depth wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the orange-and-chocolate plays against the cut. Works; extended-aged home-state pour wasted on salad.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's cherry-floral tannin holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon's bold coffee-mocha cassis frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the firm tannin reads cleanly against the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin's Detroit urban-craft gin underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Detroit City Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City Vodka's hometown craft vodka mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- clean vodka reads thin against the cut's weight — needs a wine or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Detroit City Vodka for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15's Highland-scotch character leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the malt-driven body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the smoke threads the cut. Works; Discovery complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
