  "Bone-In Filet|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 with the bone-in filet — HH aged single-barrel against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; EC 18's 18yr character frames the cut with aged-HH polish.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel's bold char-oak character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the bourbon depth settles into the bone-enhanced filet, and the vanilla softens the cut. Strong; the decennial release covers the cut with milestone-collector character.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the oak depth lays under the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak frames the cut. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the rye-and-oak weight handles the bone-enhanced filet, and the dry-spice register brightens the cut. Strong; BH Red Wine Cask's rye + red-wine character threads mushrooms at distinctive register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the Pinot body brightens the bone-enhanced filet, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the cut. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's crisp white-wine character underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Elk Cove Pinot Blanc on another course instead.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee -- Buffalo Trace single-barrel, 90 proof. The bourbon depth settles into the bone-enhanced filet, and the vanilla softens the cut. Strong; AE is under-specified for cap-fat at flagship proof; AE Cask Strength handles this better.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Empress 1908 Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Epoch Ingenuity": "Epoch Ingenuity's structured dark-fruit weight leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; the call reads at register without overshooting.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Escargot": "The bone-in filet after the escargot -- the herb-butter snail plate opens with garlic-and-parsley lift, the 14oz bone-deepened cut meeting it with a richer butter that the herbs cut through. Strong; the snail opener primes the marrow cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Espresso Martini": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Espresso Martini for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Espresso Old Fashioned for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone, medium tannin. The medium tannin handles bone-in filet's intensified beefy edge and the smoked-meat plays against the bone-deepened cut. Peak serious Willamette Pinot for bone-in filet — structured enough for the elevated filet. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Oakville luxurious Cab — blackcurrant, dark chocolate, espresso, violet, smooth powerful, long finish. The opulent body meets bone-in filet's added depth and the chocolate-espresso frames the steak-flavor directly. Peak luxurious Cab for bone-in filet — Far Niente's long finish aligns with the premium tenderloin cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Far Niente Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Strong tier. Bone-in concentration gives the Saffredi's firm tannin and graphite a target. Maremma Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot wraps the marrow-edged cut with blackcurrant and tobacco, while Mediterranean herbs dovetail the flame-grilled char. Italian structure where the Napa Cabs lean ripe.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge. The dark-fruit frame supports the filet, but the tannin rides harder than the lean profile demands. A serviceable option; not the cleanest bold-red + filet alignment. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity sits beside the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bitter herb edge cleans the cut, and the bitter cleans the char. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta's crisp mint-menthol edge stays alongside the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bitter herb edge cleans the cut. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Fireball Whisky belongs on another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
