  "Bone-In Filet|French Onion": "Bone-In Filet with the French onion alongside -- umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Frias Block 5": "Frias Block 5: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge. The dark-fruit frame supports the filet, but the tannin rides harder than the lean profile demands. A strong match — the tannin and filet tenderness align cleanly. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Bone-In Filet|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's structured dark-fruit weight rests at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the dark-fruit body sits with the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; the pairing carries its register cleanly against the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Sweet, effervescent — peach blossom, apricot, lychee. This is a dessert wine. Against a bone-in filet it reads sugary and thin. Fine to pour at the end; don't pair it with the course. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Bone-In Filet|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift finds neutral with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the blanco-tequila body matches the bone-enhanced filet, and the bright blanco register underlines the cut. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof's bold agave-char character sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the añejo weight grips the lean cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the gentle Irish body threads the bone-enhanced filet, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the cut. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|George T. Stagg": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength barrel-proof-leather character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-oak cassis reads alongside the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the Cab structure frames the tenderloin, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's bold pepper-spice cassis finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the velvety body lifts the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12 with the bone-in filet — gateway Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Fiddich's apple/pear lightness frames the cut's marrow-adjacent depth without overwhelming the filet's finesse.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14 with the bone-in filet — Bourbon Barrel Reserve against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; the new-charred-oak finish adds spice and vanilla depth that frames the cut's marrow-adjacent richness better than the 12's lighter profile.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18 with the bone-in filet — Small Batch Reserve against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; the Oloroso dried-fruit + nutmeg notes frame the cut's marrow-adjacent depth with premium aged character.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23 with the bone-in filet — Cuvée-cask-finished 23yr Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Gran Cru's refined brioche-pastry character lifts the cut with unique champagne-cask elegance the other Speysides can't match.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the scotch depth settles into the bone-enhanced filet, and the malt edge frames the cut. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12 with the bone-in filet — honey-floral Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Glenlivet's softer profile frames the cut's marrow-adjacent depth at benchmark register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the scotch backbone matches the bone-enhanced filet, and the smoke threads the cut. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the malt-driven body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the smoke threads the cut. Works; aging wasted on the salad.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Graham's 20 Year Tawny for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Graham's 2017 Vintage Port's structured vintage port with dark-fruit weight clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Graham's 2017 Vintage Port belongs on another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum's agave-citrus character lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the light body is outweighed by the cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Grand Marnier lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Gray Whale Gin for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's cask-strength herbal-high-proof sweetness sits alongside the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the sweet character meets the cut at digestif register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Grey Goose Vodka belongs on another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Grilled Caesar": "The bone-in filet preceded by the Caesar -- the elevated filet meets the parmesan-Caesar plate. Strong; the opener composes cleanly before the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Gumbo": "The bone-in filet after the gumbo -- the gumbo's spiced creole stew sets the table for the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: heat lifts the rich course, the table earns the warmer note. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's bold earth-umami cassis matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the firm tannin reads cleanly against the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bone-In Filet|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the HDW 100 Vodka for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 with the bone-in filet — Japanese Alps single malt against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Hakushu's lightly-peated pine-forest profile frames the cut with Forest-Distillery distinction.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel's crisp oak-vanilla character frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey character holds register with the tenderloin, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
