  "Bone-In Filet|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy Cognac's classic dried-fruit-and-vanilla cognac reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the eau-de-vie body carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the cut. Works; Rémy's elegance meets the char at functional register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond's rounded-oak bourbon character rests at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the bourbon backbone carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the caramel rounds the cut. Strong; smooth character sits with richness at matched register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Harmony with the bone-in filet — premium refined Japanese blend against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Hibiki's elegant harmony of cask types frames the cut at premium Japanese register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Honey Roasted Carrots": "The bone-in filet into the honey carrots -- the savory course resolves into sugar, the meal closes with contrast. Works; safe alongside, but not the headline pick.",
  "Bone-In Filet|House Wedge": "Bone-In Filet with the wedge alongside -- protein and umami share the plate, the build reads weighty and clean. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 with the bone-in filet — 15yr historic-Kentucky brand against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak character frames the cut at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "White peach, green apple, crisp bubbles — a lighter opener or celebration wine. Against the bone-in filet the bubbles flatten and the wine loses its footing. Serve before the course, not with it. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Inhibited": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Inhibited for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Inverroche Gin for another course.",
