  "Bone-In Filet|Johnnie Walker Black": "Johnnie Walker Black's Highland-scotch character leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the Highland register carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the cut. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Johnnie Walker Blue": "Johnnie Walker Blue's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the scotch backbone matches the bone-enhanced filet, and the honey rounds the cut. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Johnnie Walker Red": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the malt-and-oak threads the bone-enhanced filet, and the malt edge frames the cut. Works; the premium pour is wasted on the salad's simplicity.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Alexander Valley Sonoma Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine-grained tannin. The elegant body meets bone-in filet's added depth and the cedar-mint frames the steak-flavor directly. Peak elegant Cab for bone-in filet — Jordan's decades of consistency and balance align perfectly with the premium tenderloin cut. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Joseph Mellot Sancerre lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles bone-in filet's intensified beefy edge and the espresso plays against the bone-deepened cut. Peak cult Napa for bone-in filet — Pritchard Hill structure for the elevated cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's bold char-earth character matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth supports the lean cut without overpowering, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's caramel-char character frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the light body is outweighed by the cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Kahlua belongs on another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Keenan Chardonnay": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Keenan Chardonnay on another course instead.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Keenan Mernet": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the Cab structure frames the tenderloin, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
