  "Bone-In Filet|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Añejo with the bone-in filet — Riedel-bottle luxury against bone-in cut. The aged Komos character frames the cut at ceremonial luxury register. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads register-aligned with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the añejo register carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the cooked-agave note underlines the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Lagavulin 8": "Lagavulin 8 -- Islay peat, younger-bottling, salt. The peat-smoke meets the bone-enhanced filet, and the saline edge frames the cut. Strong; the 10-year gives the pour weight the side can accept.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Lail Vineyards Blueprint's bold cherry cassis matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the concentrated body carries the strip, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's dense blackberry-and-cassis tannin sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver's clean silver register leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the unaged body brightens the bone-enhanced filet, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; ceremonial pairing depends on preparation.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Laphroaig 10": "Laphroaig 10's bold peat-iodine character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch's bold caramel-honey character sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth supports the lean cut without overpowering, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Bone-In Filet: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this versatile sparkling's bright acidity, toasty lees character, and cleansing bubbles. Bubbles scrub fat well, but sparkling lacks the dark-fruit weight and tannin for steak. Strong — the interaction lands despite the axis challenge.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Le Garenne Rosé": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity plays cleanly against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the white-wine body is outweighed by the cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's powerful blackcurrant body leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- cocktail citrus reads thin against the cut's structure. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Lemon Lavender Gin Martini belongs on another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Les Pallières Raciné": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the velvety body lifts the lean cut. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Liberator Gin": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Liberator Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Licor 43 for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Limoncello on another course instead.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Dark cherry, forest floor, low tannin — elegant Willamette Pinot. Doesn't match the bone-in's richness the way a Cab would, but the savory depth and light structure make it a clean, non-obvious pour. Good for a Pinot-committed guest. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Little Book": "Little Book's cask-strength oak character sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth carries the cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Loaded Potato": "Bone-In Filet with the loaded potato alongside -- the meal layers rich on rich, the table carries weight cleanly when the registers match. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Lobster Mac": "The bone-in filet alongside the lobster mac -- the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness frames the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence. Excellent; one of the strong calls on the list.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the malt-and-oak threads the bone-enhanced filet, and the heather brightens the cut. Works; retailer-pick wasted on salad.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's pepper-spice character holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Long Road Gin": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Long Road Gin for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the wood-fire body meets the bone-enhanced filet, and the earthy backbone threads the cut. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- an icon cognac is best as a separate occasion — pair the steak with a Cab or bourbon, then close with the cognac. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not an icon cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Louis XIII Cognac for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- bitter botanicals on the cut fight the savory char without payoff. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Lucano Amaro lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's spice-driven red elegance leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the silky body carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the cherry-pepper note frames the cut. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry with the bone-in filet — 100% Oloroso Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Macallan 12's sherried-dried-fruit complements the cut's marrow-adjacent richness with benchmark depth.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18 with the bone-in filet — 18yr premium Oloroso Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Macallan 18's deeper sherry-cask depth frames the cut's marrow-adjacent richness with aged-premium gravitas.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate with the bone-in filet — Estate-grown-oak Macallan against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Macallan Estate's distinctive cask selection frames the cut's marrow-adjacent richness with distillery-estate-terroir distinction.",
