  "Bone-In Filet|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character meets at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cut. Strong; PNW craft capable but higher-proof Kentucky ryes bring more grip for this cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with the bone-in filet — aged OGD high-rye against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; OGD 16's rye-forward aged character frames the cut with distinctive spice at aged-Beam register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Opus One": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles bone-in filet's intensified beefy edge and the cedar plays against the bone-deepened cut. Peak collector Napa blend for bone-in filet — Mondavi-Rothschild prestige for the elevated cut. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Pablo Sour": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pablo Sour lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Paloma": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build holds with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the cocktail is outweighed by the cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye -- allocated, aged rye, caramel-spice. The dry rye body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the cut. Strong; Vermont-brand character cuts the glaze with distinctive rye voice.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads register-aligned with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak-and-spice register settles the cut. Strong; wine-cask complexity sits with richness.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's aged-agave character sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the añejo register carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the cooked-agave note underlines the cut. Strong; the boutique character gives dimension but the starter earns a heavier pour.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver's polished silver tequila with agave-forward lift sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the unaged body brightens the bone-enhanced filet, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the cut. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Patron XO Cafe lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Paul Bara Grand Cru Pinot Noir rosé — structured Champagne with ripe raspberry, red currant, and minerally finish. The Burgundian weight handles bone-enhanced filet's deeper beef character and the red-fruit depth complements the bone. Strong rosé Champagne alternative to classic Cab for bone-in filet.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Peanut Butter Brownie": "After the bone-in filet, the PB brownie -- the marrow-edged cut gives way to peanut-and-chocolate fudge, the vanilla scoop tempering the heat. Works; the warm dessert lands cleanly after the elevated main.",
