  "Bone-In Filet|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye's cask-strength citrus-pepper character frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth carries the cut, and the citrus brightens the cream. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Pineau des Charentes": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Pineau des Charentes for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's bold Nebbiolo-cherry cassis holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the firm tannin reads cleanly against the lean cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Plymouth Gin for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's powerful blackcurrant body reads register-aligned with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the dark-fruit body sits with the bone-enhanced filet, and the iron-mineral plays against the cut. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery Cuvée Louise 2004 — 20-year Reims aged Champagne. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich. The aged weight handles bone-enhanced filet and the honey-brioche complements bone depth. Strong occasion Champagne for bone-in filet — rare special-moment pairing worth the pour.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Prime Tartare": "The bone-in filet after the tartare -- raw beef into bone-deepened flame-grilled beef, the tartare's bright caper-and-shallot edge priming the palate for the 14oz cut's marrow-touched richness. Strong; the opener carries cleanly into the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Ripe blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla with polished tannins and an indulgent finish. Parallels the bone-in filet's richness without adding contrast — a match rather than a lift. A default-good pour for guests who asked for \"a nice Cab\" without a specific label in mind. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|RAEN Royal St Robert": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the Irish whiskey body sits with the bone-enhanced filet, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the cut. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the Irish whiskey body sits with the bone-enhanced filet, and the grain register softens the cut. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Gatsby with the bone-in filet — MGP high-rye 15yr Gatsby Reserve against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Remus 15's aged rye-spice frames the cut at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Remy VSOP Cognac's VSOP fine-champagne cognac leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the Limousin-oak weight handles the bone-enhanced filet, and the dried-fruit note underlines the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo with the bone-in filet — sun-shaped Moorish bottle against bone-in cut. The distinctive packaging ceremony frames the cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Reyka Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique's grain-and-oak bourbon register rests at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the caramel rounds the cut. Strong; the premium character sits with the side at matched register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Bone-In Filet preceded by the chickpea soup -- spice and substance share the plate, the contrast settles the meal. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs' bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a sparkling. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs on another course instead.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Roku Gin": "Roku Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Roku Gin for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey character sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the light body is outweighed by the cut, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year's caramel-honey character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Sambuca": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Sambuca for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|San Simeon Stormwatch": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the Cab structure frames the tenderloin, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the Pinot body brightens the bone-enhanced filet, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the cut. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The bone-in filet alongside the garlic spinach -- the 14oz bone-enhanced cut meets the wilted garlic-buttered greens, the garlic anchoring the marrow's natural depth. Strong; cleaner option than creamed, equally confident.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Sauternes Glass lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Sazerac": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build plays cleanly against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey-build weight frames the lean cut, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye's crisp floral-pepper character holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the agave-pepper carries the char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the bourbon weight handles the bone-enhanced filet, and the caramel rounds the cut. Works; bonded weight overpowers this dish.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid, full body. The powerful tannin handles bone-in filet's bone-deep beef flavor and the tar-leather plays against the marrow-infused finish. Peak King-of-Italian for bone-in — Scavino top producer on the aged-beef cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's off-dry Rheingau Riesling with lime-petrol edge underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Schloss Vollrads Riesling for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' concentrated dark-fruit and tannin sits at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the concentrated dark-fruit weight handles the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Seafood Tower": "The bone-in filet after the seafood tower -- the iced shellfish cleans the palate, the 14oz cut following with bone-marrow depth the opener leaves room for. Strong; the contrast in temperature and weight reads composed.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Seasonal Soup": "Bone-In Filet with the seasonal soup alongside -- a quiet call, the meal carries without strain. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Seasonal Vegetables": "Bone-In Filet with the seasonal vegetables -- a quiet call, the meal carries without strain. Works; the side carries cleanly with the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's deep dark-fruit and grippy tannin leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Shafer 1.5": "Shafer 1.5's bold coffee-juniper cassis holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the concentrated body carries the strip, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Shafer Hillside Select": "Shafer Hillside Select -- Stags Leap allocated icon, cassis, graphite. The allocated-Cab depth wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the graphite frames the cut. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Shrimp Bisque": "Bone-In Filet into the shrimp bisque -- the buttery beef meets the shrimp bisque. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Shrimp Cocktail": "The bone-in filet after the shrimp cocktail -- the chilled shrimp's citrus-horseradish snap refreshes the palate, the 14oz bone-deepened cut following with marrow-touched butter. Strong; the opener resets for the cut's richness.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the silver-tequila register lifts the bone-enhanced filet, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado's crisp agave-earth character lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-tequila depth carries the cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "American-oak vanilla, coconut, and dill against the bone-in filet's richer marble. The oak's sweetness has more surface to meet here than on the standard filet. Soft tannin keeps it approachable — a familiar label for first-time steakhouse guests. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Simon Family Estate Reserve's bold oak cassis finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the velvety body lifts the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "Spottswoode Lyndenhurst's bold cherry-floral cassis sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the Cab structure frames the tenderloin, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-coffee cassis sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the concentrated body carries the strip, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's bold cherry-coffee cassis holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the structured tannin frames the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's cask-strength cask-strength-oak character sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey character holds register with the tenderloin, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Stoli Elit Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty acid. Light white wine can't hold up against a bone-in cut with bold marble. The wine's acid cuts but the body is nowhere. Redirect to a red. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Tanq 10 Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Tanqueray Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- dessert wine is post-meal — wrong register for the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for another course.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with the bone-in filet — triple-cask aged (Scotch/sherry/cognac) against bone-in cut. The multi-cask complexity gives the cut a frame no single-cask extra añejo can deliver. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's bold coffee-mocha cassis finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the firm tannin reads cleanly against the lean cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity rests at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the savory Tuscan red carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the savory red register plays against the cut. Strong; the match stays in its register against the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|The BG": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the cocktail is outweighed by the cut, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bone-In Filet|The Happy Wife": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the cocktail is outweighed by the cut, and the citrus brightens the cream. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|The Manhattan": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The bone-enhancement deepens the filet's profile and the Manhattan's vanilla-softened structure meets it cleanly; Contratto's Italian vermouth adds herbal complexity. Peak cocktail route for the bone-in filet's elevated preparation.",
  "Bone-In Filet|The Prisoner Red Blend": "The Prisoner Red Blend's structured dark-fruit weight sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's cask-strength spice character frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey character holds register with the tenderloin, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the bone-in filet — Barton chardonnay-cask bourbon against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; TSM Chardonnay Cask's white-wine-finish crisp-apple character frames the cut distinctively.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Titos Vodka": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Titos Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory with the bone-in filet — entry Japanese blend against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Toki's light-floral Suntory-blend character frames the cut at crowd-pleasing Japanese register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Tomato Basil": "Bone-In Filet preceded by the tomato basil -- acid plays against richness, the contrast holds across the courses. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
