  "Bone-In Filet|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's crisp caramel-spice character frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's caramel-floral character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with the bone-in filet — Brown-Forman Double Oaked against tenderloin-with-bone. Works; Woodford Double Oaked frames the cut at KY-benchmark register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the dry rye body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the rye-grain bite cuts the cut. Strong; sophisticated everyday match.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's crisp grain-oak character matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 with the bone-in filet — iconic Suntory flagship single malt against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak sandalwood-honey character frames the cut with signature Japanese distinction.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 with the bone-in filet — aged Yamazaki against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Yamazaki 18's aged Mizunara-oak sandalwood-coconut frames the cut at collector register.",
  "Bone-In Filet|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Yellow Chartreuse for another course.",
  "Booker's|Asparagus": "Booker's cask-strength bourbon depth crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the spirit needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Booker's belongs on the steak course.",
  "Booker's|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Booker's with the au gratin — Beam cask-strength against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Booker's intensity handles the richness at barrel-proof register.",
  "Booker's|Beignets": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Booker's|Bone Marrow": "Booker's with bone marrow — Beam flagship cask-strength against umami fat. Excellent; Booker's uncut intensity cuts marrow at barrel-proof register — one of the rare spirits that actually matches marrow's weight without needing Fernet Branca.",
  "Booker's|Bone-In Filet": "Booker's cask-strength caramel-juniper character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Booker's|Broccoli Cheddar": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon depth settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; Old Fitz 9 brings more depth here.",
  "Booker's|Broccolini": "Booker's cask-strength bourbon depth overshadows the broccolini's tender green stalks -- sweet-oak depth has nothing to bridge into green vegetable freshness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Booker's for the steak course.",
  "Booker's|Brussels and Belly": "Booker's with Brussels + Belly — cask-strength Beam against maple-glazed pork belly. Excellent; Booker's 125+ proof meets the glaze's sweetness with barrel-proof intensity — the bourbon that originated the category meets one of its featured dessert-adjacent pairings.",
  "Booker's|Burrata": "Booker's with burrata — uncut Beam against fresh dairy. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates burrata.",
  "Booker's|Butternut Squash": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the caramel-spice register matches the squash bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Booker's|Carrot Cake": "Booker's with carrot cake — Beam barrel-proof against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; Booker's intensity meets the cake at barrel-proof register.",
  "Booker's|Cheesecake": "Booker's with cheesecake — 120-130 proof Beam against custard. Avoid; barrel-proof intensity is wrong for custard's delicate character.",
  "Booker's|Chilean Seabass": "Booker's cask-strength bourbon depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Booker's for the steak course.",
  "Booker's|Chocolate Brownie": "Booker's with the chocolate brownie — uncut/unfiltered Beam against cocoa. Excellent; Booker's 120-130 proof and uncut character is the featured barrel-proof-chocolate pairing — the intensity of both meet at the same register.",
  "Booker's|Chocolate Cake": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon body sits with the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Booker's|Clam Chowder": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the brown-spirit warmth threads the chowder, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; milestone-edition over-specified.",
  "Booker's|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets Beam flagship cask-strength bourbon, uncut/unfiltered 120-130 proof, Booker Noe originated the barrel-proof category in 1992. Booker's uncut-unfiltered intensity is the featured barrel-proof-ribeye pairing — the 125+ proof handles 26oz cap-fat with the original-barrel-proof gravitas that started the whole category. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Booker's|Crab Cake": "Booker's with crab cake — 120-130 proof Beam against delicate lump crab. Avoid; barrel-proof intensity obliterates crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Booker's|Creamed Spinach": "Booker's with creamed spinach — uncut/unfiltered Beam against dairy-rich side. Strong; Booker's cask-strength cuts the cream at barrel-proof register.",
  "Booker's|Creamy Potato": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon body sits with the potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Booker's|Creme Brulee": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon backbone carries the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the spicy-sweet character gives the dessert a Barton frame.",
  "Booker's|Escargot": "Booker's cask-strength caramel-juniper character frames the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Booker's|Faroe Island Salmon": "Booker's with the salmon — Beam 120-130 proof against oily salmon. Avoid; barrel-proof bourbon is too intense for fish.",
  "Booker's|Filet Mignon": "Booker's with the filet — 120-130 proof Beam against tenderloin. Works; Booker's intensity is over-specified for filet's finesse — save the barrel-proof for the ribeye/Tomahawk.",
  "Booker's|French Onion": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon backbone carries the French onion, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the light body doesn't overshoot the salad.",
  "Booker's|Grilled Caesar": "Booker's with the grilled Caesar — uncut Beam against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Booker's 125+ proof is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Booker's|Gumbo": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon body sits with the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Booker's|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Booker's cask-strength bourbon depth overwhelms the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- bourbon weight buries the glaze's sweetness instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Booker's on the steak course instead.",
  "Booker's|House Wedge": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon body sits with the wedge, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; neutral, not elevating.",
  "Booker's|Kansas City": "Booker's with the KC — uncut/unfiltered Beam cask-strength against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Booker's 120-130 proof handles strip marbling with the original-barrel-proof bourbon that defined the category.",
  "Booker's|Loaded Potato": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; Boss Hog's allocation story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Booker's|Lobster Mac": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon body sits with the lobster mac, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; 100-proof complexity sits beside the soup.",
  "Booker's|Market Fish": "Booker's cask-strength bourbon depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Booker's belongs on the steak course.",
  "Booker's|Mocha Creme": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon body sits with the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Booker's|Mushroom Bisque": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the 10-year depth gives the dessert substantial premium frame.",
  "Booker's|Mushrooms": "Booker's with mushrooms — 120-130 proof Beam against earthy umami. Strong; Booker's cask-strength intensity threads mushrooms at barrel-proof register.",
  "Booker's|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet cask-strength Beam. Strong; Booker's 120+ proof handles dessert density at barrel-proof register.",
  "Booker's|Porterhouse": "Booker's with the porterhouse — Beam flagship cask-strength against strip-and-filet. Strong; Booker's uncut intensity frames the two-cut plate at barrel-proof register.",
  "Booker's|Prime Tartare": "Booker's cask-strength caramel-juniper character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Booker's|Rainbow Trout": "Booker's cask-strength bourbon depth flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Booker's belongs on the steak course.",
  "Booker's|Roast Half Chicken": "Booker's with the chicken — uncut Beam against roasted poultry. Avoid; barrel-proof is over-specified for chicken.",
  "Booker's|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon weight handles the chickpea soup, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; Marian McLain L.E.",
  "Booker's|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Booker's cask-strength bourbon depth flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Booker's belongs on the steak course.",
  "Booker's|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Booker's cask-strength bourbon depth smothers the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side reads small under oak-and-vanilla density. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Booker's for the steak course.",
  "Booker's|Seafood Tower": "Booker's cask-strength bourbon depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Booker's lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Booker's|Seared Scallops": "Booker's with seared scallops — cask-strength Beam against scallop delicacy. Avoid; barrel-proof wrong for scallops.",
  "Booker's|Seasonal Soup": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon weight handles the seasonal soup, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "Booker's|Seasonal Vegetables": "Booker's cask-strength bourbon depth overshadows the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- sweet-oak depth has nothing to bridge into green vegetable freshness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Booker's for the steak course.",
  "Booker's|Shrimp Bisque": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the cask-strength power drives the shrimp bisque, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Booker's|Shrimp Cocktail": "Booker's with shrimp cocktail — Beam cask-strength against cold shrimp. Avoid; barrel-proof wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Booker's|Swordfish": "Booker's cask-strength bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Booker's lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Booker's|The Tomahawk": "Booker's with the Tomahawk — Beam cask-strength 125-130 proof against 32oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Booker's is the featured barrel-proof-Tomahawk match — Booker Noe's original cask-strength expression was designed for exactly this kind of intensity meeting.",
  "Booker's|Tomato Basil": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the brown-spirit warmth threads the tomato basil, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Booker's|Truffle Fries": "Booker's with truffle fries — uncut Beam cask-strength against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Booker's intensity meets the earthy-rich at barrel-proof register.",
  "Booker's|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Booker's cask-strength bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Booker's for the steak course.",
  "Booker's|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the curry, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; Boss Hog's allocation story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Botanist Gin|Asparagus": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Botanist Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals reads alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Botanist Gin|Beignets": "Botanist with beignets — Bruichladdich Islay craft gin against fried dough. Works; the foraged-herbal profile is over-specified for casual dessert.",
  "Botanist Gin|Bone Marrow": "Botanist with bone marrow — 31-botanical Islay gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Botanist Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Botanist Gin for another course.",
  "Botanist Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Botanist Gin|Broccolini": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals leans against the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Botanist Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals leans against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Botanist Gin|Burrata": "Botanist with burrata — Bruichladdich Islay craft gin against fresh dairy. Strong; Botanist's wildcrafted-herbal complexity complements cream at Hebridean-terroir register; Inverroche's fynbos-floral is still the featured craft-gin-burrata match.",
  "Botanist Gin|Butternut Squash": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Botanist Gin|Carrot Cake": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- juniper-citrus and warm cake spices pull in opposite directions. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Botanist Gin for the steak course.",
  "Botanist Gin|Cheesecake": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Botanist Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Botanist Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals reads alongside the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Botanist Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Botanist Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Botanist Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Botanist Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Botanist Gin|Clam Chowder": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits beside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Botanist Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Botanist with the cowboy ribeye — Hebridean craft gin against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Botanist Gin|Crab Cake": "Botanist with crab cake — Bruichladdich Islay 31-botanical craft gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Botanist's wildcrafted herbal complexity lifts crab with Hebridean character no classic London Dry can replicate.",
  "Botanist Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Botanist Gin|Creamy Potato": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits alongside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Botanist Gin|Creme Brulee": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Botanist Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Botanist Gin|Escargot": "Botanist with escargot — 22 foraged Islay botanicals against garlic-butter snails. Excellent; the wildcrafted herbal lift cuts butter with distinctive Hebridean character — a different depth than the French/British traditional approaches.",
  "Botanist Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Botanist with the salmon — Bruichladdich Islay botanical gin against oily salmon. Strong; the Hebridean-coastal botanicals find resonance with Faroe Island fish — coastal gin meets coastal fish thematically.",
  "Botanist Gin|Filet Mignon": "Botanist with the filet — Islay foraged-botanical gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Botanist Gin|French Onion": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Botanist Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Botanist with the grilled Caesar — 22-foraged-botanical Islay craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Botanist's wildcrafted herbs meet the char at Hebridean register.",
  "Botanist Gin|Gumbo": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals plays cleanly against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Botanist Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Botanist Gin|House Wedge": "Botanist with the House Wedge — 31-botanical Islay craft against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Botanist's wildcrafted complexity meets the bleu with Hebridean distinction.",
  "Botanist Gin|Kansas City": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Botanist Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Botanist Gin|Loaded Potato": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits alongside the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Botanist Gin|Lobster Mac": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits beside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Botanist Gin|Market Fish": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals holds the line with the fish's clean flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Botanist Gin|Mocha Creme": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Botanist Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Botanist Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Botanist Gin|Mushrooms": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Botanist Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Botanist Gin for the steak course.",
  "Botanist Gin|Porterhouse": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Botanist Gin for another course.",
  "Botanist Gin|Prime Tartare": "Botanist with the tartare — Islay foraged-herbal craft gin against raw beef. Excellent; Botanist's 22 wildcrafted Hebridean botanicals add terroir depth to tartare's minerality that no urban-craft gin offers.",
  "Botanist Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals lifts the trout's delicate flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Botanist Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Botanist with the chicken — Bruichladdich Islay craft gin against roasted poultry. Works; Botanist's herbal complexity supports chicken at craft register.",
  "Botanist Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits beside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Botanist Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals holds steady against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Botanist Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits beside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Botanist Gin|Seafood Tower": "Botanist with the seafood tower — Bruichladdich Islay craft gin, 22 foraged Islay botanicals + 9 classic. Excellent; Botanist's wildcrafted Islay-herb character frames the raw bar with a coastal-Scotland story no other gin on the menu can claim — same island that makes Islay Scotch, but in gin form. The most thematically right tower gin.",
  "Botanist Gin|Seared Scallops": "Botanist with seared scallops — Islay-foraged craft gin against scallop sweetness. Excellent; the 22 wildcrafted Hebridean botanicals frame scallops with oceanic-island terroir — coastal Scotland meets the scallop's marine origin.",
  "Botanist Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Botanist Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits beside the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Botanist Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Botanist with shrimp bisque — Islay wildcrafted craft gin against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Botanist's foraged-herbal character cuts the bisque with Hebridean terroir.",
  "Botanist Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Botanist with shrimp cocktail — Islay-foraged craft against cold shrimp. Strong; Botanist's Hebridean botanicals elevate the classic cold app with oceanic-island character.",
  "Botanist Gin|Swordfish": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift sits alongside the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the floral edge frames the swordfish, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Orbium's bitter-tonic profile cuts the fish's richness with distinctive contrast.",
  "Botanist Gin|The Tomahawk": "Botanist with the Tomahawk — Islay craft gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Botanist Gin|Tomato Basil": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits beside the tomato basil's bright acid — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Botanist Gin|Truffle Fries": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals stays alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Botanist Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Botanist Gin's juniper-and-citrus lift leans against the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the floral edge frames the tuna, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Botanist's Hebridean botanicals elevate the classic cold app with oceanic-island character.",
  "Botanist Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Asparagus": "Buffalo Trace Old Fashioned against asparagus — bourbon vanilla-caramel has no hook into a sulfur-forward vegetable side. Never layer. Route the cocktail to fatted steaks or marrow; take asparagus with Sauvignon Blanc, Sancerre, or Chablis.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's bold caramel-char build finds neutral with the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the whiskey-build weight settles into the rich side, and the oak frames the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Beignets": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, Angostura. Bourbon weight overwhelms the beignet's airy choux interior; the cocktail belongs with marrow or Tomahawk. Route beignet guest to Champagne, Moscato, Prosecco, or an Espresso Martini. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Bone Marrow": "Buffalo Trace muddled with our rum-soaked cherries, orange, and Angostura. The bourbon's caramel and vanilla wrap around the roasted marrow's richness; the citrus salt on top meets the orange and cherries. The card explicitly calls out spirit-friendliness — lead with this. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Bone-In Filet": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned: this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth meets the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge. Oak, caramel, and spirit heat stand up to the beef's render; the caramelized sweetness mirrors the crust. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Broccoli Cheddar": "Buffalo Trace Old Fashioned against a cream-and-cheddar broccoli soup — bourbon vanilla-caramel has no hook into a vegetable-cheese classic. Never layer. Route the cocktail to fatted steaks or marrow; take broccoli cheddar with Chardonnay, Pinot Blanc, or Chablis.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Broccolini": "Buffalo Trace Old Fashioned against charred broccolini — bourbon vanilla-caramel has no hook into a vegetable side. Never layer. Route the cocktail to fatted steaks or marrow; take broccolini with Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Noir.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Brussels and Belly": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, bitters, large cube. The bourbon's vanilla-caramel profile meets pork belly's rendered fat and brussels char; cherry sweetness bridges the sweet-savory glaze. Peak pairing when guest wants whiskey alongside the belly's smoke-and-sear depth. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Burrata": "Buffalo Trace, rum-infused cherries, orange, bitters. The bourbon's weight crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile; vanilla-caramel has no hook into the cheese-and-basil build. Never pair — burrata begs for Prosecco or a crisp white, not spirit-forward whiskey.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Butternut Squash": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's bold caramel-char build finds neutral with the squash soup's silky sweet — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the oak frames the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Carrot Cake": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, Angostura. Bourbon weight fights cream-cheese frosting; the cocktail belongs with marrow, Brussels and Belly, or Tomahawk. Route dessert guest to Espresso Old Fashioned (brown sugar echoes) or Baileys neat instead. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Cheesecake": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, Angostura. Bourbon weight overwhelms cream cheese delicacy; the cocktail belongs with marrow, Brussels and Belly, or Tomahawk. Route dessert guest to Espresso Martini, Baileys, or a tawny. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Chilean Seabass": "Buffalo Trace Old Fashioned against buttery Chilean seabass — bourbon vanilla-caramel has no hook into the delicate melt-in-mouth fish. Never layer. Route the cocktail to fatted steaks or marrow; take seabass with Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, or Chablis.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Chocolate Brownie": "Buffalo Trace, rum-infused cherries, orange, bitters. The bourbon's vanilla-caramel meets the brownie's fudge base but the cocktail wasn't built for dessert — the Espresso Old Fashioned is. Works if the guest wants to continue the cocktail; otherwise route to coffee cocktails or tawny.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Chocolate Cake": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's bold caramel-char build reads alongside the cake's layered chocolate — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Clam Chowder": "Buffalo Trace Old Fashioned against a cream-based clam chowder — bourbon vanilla-caramel has no hook into shellfish-cream classic. Never layer. Route the cocktail to fatted steaks or marrow; take chowder with Chardonnay, Chablis, or Pinot Blanc.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Cowboy Ribeye": "Buffalo Trace muddled with rum-soaked cherries, orange, bitters over a large cube. The vanilla-caramel backbone and brûléed-orange nose cut the Cowboy's marbled fat; cherry sweetness meets the crust caramelization. Peak spirit-forward pour for the fattiest cut on the menu.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Crab Cake": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, bitters. Spirit-forward bourbon overwhelms the crab's sweet-delicate meat and the cocktail's weight doesn't meet the light romesco. Never pair — crab cake belongs with Sauvignon Blanc, Champagne, or a lighter cocktail.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Creamed Spinach": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, bitters. The cocktail's vanilla-caramel softens against the creamed spinach's dairy weight; bourbon stands with the side without fighting it. Works — though the spinach peaks better against the Cab pour guests usually already have.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Creamy Potato": "Buffalo Trace Old Fashioned — bourbon vanilla-caramel with cherries, orange, bitters — against a cream-based potato soup. Spirit-weight whiskey is the wrong axis for a silky dairy starter. Never layer. Route the cocktail to fatted steaks or marrow; take creamy potato with Chardonnay, Pinot Blanc, or Chablis.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Creme Brulee": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, Angostura over a large cube. Bourbon weight fights vanilla custard's delicacy; the cocktail belongs with marrow or Tomahawk. Route guest to Espresso Martini or Baileys for a dessert-appropriate cocktail. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Escargot": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned: this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth meets this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth. Whiskey depth meets the starter's fat and umami — both land heavy. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Faroe Island Salmon": "Buffalo Trace muddled with our rum-soaked cherries, orange, and Angostura over a large cube. Bourbon weight buries the Faroe's delicate flavor and fat. Reserve this cocktail for the steak or marrow course. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|French Onion": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's bold caramel-char build reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the whiskey-build depth matches the hearty soup, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Grilled Caesar": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's bold caramel-char build leans against the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the whiskey-build weight is misaligned with the leaf-and-acid plate, and the oak frames the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Gumbo": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's bold caramel-char build sits alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's bold caramel-char build stays alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|House Wedge": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, bitters. Cocktail weight is heavier than the iceberg-and-blue-cheese wedge strictly needs, but the vanilla depth plays with bacon and the cherry brightens the dressing. Works as an opener when guest leads with whiskey.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Kansas City": "Kansas City: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Loaded Potato": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, bitters. The bourbon's vanilla-caramel meets the soup's bacon-cheddar weight and cherry sweetness balances the savory loaded toppings. Works as bourbon cocktail for loaded potato — whiskey route on the indulgent classic.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Lobster Mac": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned: this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth meets this rich, creamy side's dairy-driven indulgence. Whiskey's oak and sweetness mirror the side's dairy and umami depth. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Market Fish": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's weight and vanilla-caramel depth smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the bitters-and-bourbon register has no foil against gentle flesh. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Bowdie's Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Mocha Creme": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's weight and vanilla-caramel depth leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bold-mix depth handles the mocha creme, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Mushroom Bisque": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's bold caramel-char build sits alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the whiskey-build depth matches the hearty soup, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Mushrooms": "Buffalo Trace muddled with rum-infused cherries, orange, bitters. The vanilla-caramel meets the mushroom side's earthy butter-sauté richness; brûléed-orange nose adds aromatic lift. Strong pairing — the bourbon handles mushroom depth without the clash a lighter cocktail might bring.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, bitters. The vanilla backbone meets peanut butter's richness but the cocktail isn't dessert-built — Frangelico or Espresso Old Fashioned hooks into the hazelnut-PB axis more directly. Works only when guest stays on the house OF through dessert.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Porterhouse": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned: this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth meets this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char. Oak, caramel, and spirit heat stand up to the beef's render; the caramelized sweetness mirrors the crust. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Prime Tartare": "Buffalo Trace muddled with rum-infused cherries and bitters. The cocktail's vanilla-caramel base and brûléed-orange nose stands against the raw prime beef's iron-tinged richness without dominating; cherry sweetness plays off the tartare's seasoning. Peak pre-dinner pairing with tartare. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Rainbow Trout": "Buffalo Trace Old Fashioned against delicate rainbow trout — bourbon vanilla-caramel has no hook into freshwater fish delicacy. Never layer. Route the cocktail to fatted steaks or marrow; take trout with Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Roast Half Chicken": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned: this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth meets this roasted chicken's savory, rich poultry-skin flavor. Bold whiskey against roasted chicken — the drink outweighs but oak and savory can mesh. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's bold caramel-char build holds with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Buffalo Trace Old Fashioned against salt-cured halibut with sweet soy glaze — bourbon vanilla-caramel has no hook into cured fish and soy-glaze delicacy. Never layer. Route the cocktail to fatted steaks or marrow; take halibut with Chardonnay, Chablis, or Pinot Blanc.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Buffalo Trace muddled with our rum-soaked cherries, orange, and Angostura. Bourbon's caramel weight buries the light spinach and dominates the escargot butter's garlic-shallot character. Reserve for beef sides or the marrow course. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Seafood Tower": "Buffalo Trace muddled with our rum-soaked cherries, orange, and Angostura. Bourbon and cold shellfish don't meet well — the caramel weight buries the brine and delicate textures. Reserve for the steak course. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Seared Scallops": "Buffalo Trace, rum-infused cherries, orange, bitters. The spirit-forward bourbon obliterates the scallops' delicate sweetness and the vanilla-caramel muddies the clean seared profile. Never pair — scallops want crisp white or bubbles, not a heavy whiskey cocktail.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Seasonal Soup": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's weight and vanilla-caramel depth sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bold-mix depth handles the seasonal soup, and the bitters edge frames the bowl. Works; the close composes without strain.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Seasonal Vegetables": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's bold caramel-char build leans against the seasonal garden plate — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Shrimp Bisque": "Buffalo Trace muddled with our rum-soaked cherries, orange, and Angostura over a large cube. The cocktail's weight overwhelms the delicate seafood cream base and buries the smoked paprika finish. Reserve this cocktail for the beef course instead. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Shrimp Cocktail": "Buffalo Trace muddled with our rum-soaked cherries, orange, and Angostura. Bourbon's caramel weight buries cold shrimp; the cocktail-sauce heat gets swallowed too. Reserve this cocktail for the beef course where it earns its spot. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Swordfish": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's weight and vanilla-caramel depth fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- cocktail bitterness pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Bowdie's Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|The Tomahawk": "Buffalo Trace, rum-infused cherries, orange, bitters, large cube. The house Old Fashioned's weight and vanilla-caramel depth stands up to the Tomahawk's size and marbling; brûléed orange adds smoky nose to the crust char. Peak cocktail for the table's statement cut.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Tomato Basil": "Buffalo Trace Old Fashioned against tomato basil's fresh-acidity classic — bourbon vanilla-caramel has no hook into a bright herb-and-tomato soup. Never layer. Route the cocktail to fatted steaks or marrow; take tomato basil with Sauvignon Blanc, Sancerre, or Chablis.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Truffle Fries": "Buffalo Trace, rum-infused cherries, orange, bitters. The cocktail's vanilla-caramel plays against truffle oil's earthy richness; brûléed-orange nose lifts the parmesan-truffle aroma. Strong cocktail-side pairing when the fries anchor the table — holds up to the side's full flavor weight.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Buffalo Trace Old Fashioned against rare-seared yellowfin tuna — bourbon vanilla-caramel has no hook into sesame-crust and honey-sambal brightness. Never layer. Route the cocktail to fatted steaks or marrow; take tuxedo tuna with Pinot Noir, Pinot Blanc, or Chablis.",
  "Bowdie's Old Fashioned|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Buffalo Trace Old Fashioned against a coconut-milk vegetable curry — bourbon vanilla-caramel has no hook into turmeric-cumin heat. Never layer. Route the cocktail to fatted steaks or marrow; take the curry with Riesling, rosé, or Pinot Noir.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Asparagus": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the asparagus, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; richer profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with au gratin potatoes — cheese-and-cream meets house-pick oak. Strong; matched registers with the private-select's extra depth.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Beignets": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon weight handles the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Bone Marrow": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with bone marrow — the custom-selected oak-stave profile adds a dimension to Maker's that standard doesn't have. A pour that reflects what Bowdie's values; the house's wheated expression for this starter. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Bone-In Filet": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the caramel-spice register matches the bone-enhanced filet, and the vanilla softens the cut. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Broccoli Cheddar": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon depth settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; neutral, not elevating.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Broccolini": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the oak depth lays under the broccolini, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; the unique maturation story adds interest to a gentle dessert match.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Brussels and Belly": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with Brussels + Belly — house-pick wheated depth against maple-glazed pork belly. The stave selection gives this pairing a signature Bowdie's character; richer than standard Maker's, more specific than Cellar Aged. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Burrata": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with burrata — fresh dairy cannot carry house-pick wheated depth. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Butternut Squash": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the caramel-spice register matches the squash bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Carrot Cake": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with carrot cake — the stave-tailored pour against cream cheese frosting and cinnamon. Strong; the house selection layers with the cake's sweetness in a way that's specifically Bowdie's.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Cheesecake": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with cheesecake — custard meets house-pick caramel. Works; the private select's layered profile underserves the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Chilean Seabass": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark on the steak course instead.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Chocolate Brownie": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with chocolate brownie — house-pick wheated against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Weller Millennium is the call.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Chocolate Cake": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon depth settles into the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; high proof over-specified.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Clam Chowder": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon backbone carries the chowder, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: the cut's cap fat and char meet the house pick's bigger body from custom staves. Carries the ribeye where standard Maker's fades; Bowdie's signature wheated. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Crab Cake": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with crab cake — house-pick depth against delicate crab. Avoid; the pour's specificity doesn't rescue the register mismatch.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Creamed Spinach": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with creamed spinach — the house selection's deeper caramel against dairy. Strong; the private-select character adds dimension beyond standard Maker's.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Creamy Potato": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak depth lays under the potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the match is gentle and approachable.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Creme Brulee": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Escargot": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's char-oak character matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Faroe Island Salmon": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark on the steak course instead.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Filet Mignon": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with the filet — the house's chosen stave profile brings distinct character to the standard Maker's expression. Richer oak, deeper caramel, tailored to what Bowdie's wanted. A house-signature pour for filet; the Maker's expression designed specifically for this menu. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|French Onion": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the French onion, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Grilled Caesar": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with the grilled Caesar — the house pick's character earns this salad where standard Maker's only works. Strong; the private select's depth gives the Caesar something specific.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Gumbo": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak depth lays under the gumbo, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; IW Harper 15 handles dessert density at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon backbone carries the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|House Wedge": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against house-pick wheat. Works as a separate sip; the private select's depth is a waste on the wedge.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Kansas City": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with the KC — the house-picked stave profile gives the strip a pour made for this room. More depth than standard Maker's, more specific than Maker's Mark Cellar Aged; Bowdie's answer to 'what should I drink with my strip.'. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Loaded Potato": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the bourbon-and-soup pairing holds — neither soars nor fights.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Lobster Mac": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak depth lays under the lobster mac, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a soup; save the rare bottle.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Market Fish": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark for the steak course.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Mocha Creme": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the oak depth lays under the mocha creme, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the pour's toasted warmth threads through the char without overpowering.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Mushroom Bisque": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon backbone carries the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; curated single-barrel slightly over-specified.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Mushrooms": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with the mushrooms — the house selection's richer oak meets umami depth. Distinct from standard Maker's here; the private select's tailored profile layers with the side. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon weight handles the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; richer profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Porterhouse": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with the porterhouse — house-pick depth across strip and filet sides. The stave selection gives both cuts something to work with; more appropriate than standard Maker's for this plate. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Prime Tartare": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's char-oak character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Rainbow Trout": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Roast Half Chicken": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon weight handles the chickpea soup, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark for the steak course.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the brown-spirit warmth threads the garlic spinach, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Seafood Tower": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark on the steak course instead.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Seared Scallops": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with seared scallops — custom-stave wheated against scallop delicacy. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Seasonal Soup": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the seasonal soup, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; richer profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Seasonal Vegetables": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; milestone-edition over-specified.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Shrimp Bisque": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood meets house-pick caramel. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Shrimp Cocktail": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against house-pick oak. Avoid; separate pours.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Swordfish": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|The Tomahawk": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's char-oak character finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Tomato Basil": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon backbone carries the tomato basil, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the toast adds a warmth the dessert can accept.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Truffle Fries": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with truffle fries — house-pick depth against truffle-parmesan fat. The stave selection earns this side beyond what standard Maker's reaches; a signature-menu pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the curry, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Asparagus": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon backbone carries the asparagus, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; the light body doesn't overshoot the salad.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with au gratin potatoes — 90-proof Virginia small-batch against cheese-and-cream. Strong; smooth match at the small-batch register.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Beignets": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the Millennium's register overshoots the dessert's.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Bone Marrow": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with bone marrow — Virginia smooth small-batch against umami fat. Strong; the smooth character is capable but marrow earns aged bonded pours like Henry McKenna 10 BIB or E.H. Taylor.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Bone-In Filet": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's juniper character lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the juniper sharpens the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Broccoli Cheddar": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon depth settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Broccolini": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon depth settles into the broccolini, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; private-selection wasted on the salad.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet Virginia-aged small-batch. Smooth character handles the dish at everyday register. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Burrata": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with burrata — fresh dairy against Virginia small-batch. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Butternut Squash": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak depth lays under the squash bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Carrot Cake": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with carrot cake — Virginia small-batch against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Cheesecake": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with cheesecake — Virginia small-batch against clean custard. Works; smooth gentle match.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Chilean Seabass": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Bowman Brothers Small Batch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Chocolate Brownie": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the chocolate brownie — 90-proof Virginia small-batch against cocoa. Works; smooth character meets the brownie cleanly.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Chocolate Cake": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the caramel-spice register matches the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for the side — save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Clam Chowder": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon weight handles the chowder, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Cowboy Ribeye": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the Cowboy Ribeye — 90-proof Virginia small-batch against cap fat. The smooth Bowman character handles the cut at the everyday small-batch register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Crab Cake": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with crab cake — Virginia small-batch against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Creamed Spinach": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with creamed spinach — Virginia-aged small-batch against dairy. Strong; smooth character sits with richness at matched register.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Creamy Potato": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; the toast notes don't really interplay with the soup but don't clash.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Creme Brulee": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon weight handles the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; light body doesn't overshoot.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Escargot": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's juniper character holds the line with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the botanicals lift the herb butter. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Faroe Island Salmon": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Bowman Brothers Small Batch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Filet Mignon": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the filet — A. Smith Bowman's Virginia-aged small-batch, Sazerac-owned. 90-proof, smooth, distinctively Virginia-matured (the distillery ages in a climate distinct from Kentucky). A non-Kentucky small-batch voice for the filet. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|French Onion": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the French onion, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Grilled Caesar": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the grilled Caesar — Virginia-aged small-batch against charred romaine. Works; the smooth 90-proof doesn't quite drive the Caesar.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Gumbo": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the caramel-spice register matches the gumbo, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon weight handles the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; AE CS handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|House Wedge": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the House Wedge — Virginia small-batch against iceberg and bleu. Works; smooth character is slightly over-specified for the salad.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Kansas City": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets Virginia-aged 90-proof small-batch. Smooth Bowman character carries the cut at small-batch register; Sazerac-family alternate to BT's flagship voice. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Loaded Potato": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the oak depth lays under the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; boutique-sourced doesn't drive.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Lobster Mac": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the caramel-spice register matches the lobster mac, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Market Fish": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Bowman Brothers Small Batch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Mocha Creme": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon depth settles into the mocha creme, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; Remus 15 handles dessert density at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Mushroom Bisque": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the 10-year depth gives the dessert substantial premium frame.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Mushrooms": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the mushrooms — Virginia-aged small-batch against earthy umami. The smooth character layers into mushroom depth at the approachable register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the PB brownie — Virginia-aged small-batch against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Porterhouse": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the porterhouse — Virginia 90-proof small-batch across strip and filet. Strong; smooth character covers both cuts cleanly.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Prime Tartare": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's juniper character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Rainbow Trout": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Bowman Brothers Small Batch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Roast Half Chicken": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon backbone carries the roast chicken, and the rounded sweetness brightens the plate. Works; the 10-year holds without lifting.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the chickpea soup, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Bowman Brothers Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the brown-spirit warmth threads the garlic spinach, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Seafood Tower": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Bowman Brothers Small Batch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Seared Scallops": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 90-proof Virginia. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Seasonal Soup": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon weight handles the seasonal soup, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Seasonal Vegetables": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal vegetables, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Shrimp Bisque": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the oak depth lays under the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Shrimp Cocktail": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against Virginia small-batch. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Swordfish": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Bowman Brothers Small Batch on the steak course instead.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|The Tomahawk": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the Tomahawk — 90-proof Virginia small-batch against bone-in ribeye. Strong; cut's scale pushes against the smooth balance — Baker's, Four Roses Small Batch Select, or E.H. Taylor are stronger Tomahawk calls.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Tomato Basil": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the tomato basil, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; bonded weight pushes past the soup's register slightly.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Truffle Fries": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with truffle fries — 90-proof Virginia small-batch against truffle-parmesan. The smooth Bowman character gives the side a Sazerac-family frame distinct from BT. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Bowman Brothers Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Bowman Brothers Small Batch|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the curry, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the pour's toasted warmth threads through the char without overpowering.",
  "Bowmore 12|Asparagus": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the asparagus, and the honey rounds the side. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "Bowmore 12|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character reads alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the Highland register carries the au gratin, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Bowmore 12|Beignets": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the malt-driven body wraps the beignets, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the dessert. Works; complex profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Bowmore 12|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: the rich umami fat meets Bowmore 12's sherry-touched, honey-forward peat. Different frame than the other two Islays — softer, more integrated with the marrow's mineral finish. Still peated, but less brawling than Lagavulin 8 or Laphroaig 10. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bowmore 12|Bone-In Filet": "Bowmore 12's peat-sherry character lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Bowmore 12|Broccoli Cheddar": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the malt-and-oak threads the broccoli cheddar, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; bold proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Bowmore 12|Broccolini": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the Highland register carries the broccolini, and the heather brightens the side. Works; the pour's delicacy doesn't overpower the soup, but doesn't particularly elevate it either.",
  "Bowmore 12|Brussels and Belly": "Bowmore 12 with Brussels + Belly — sherry-finish honeyed notes meet the maple glaze cleanly. Gentler peat than Lagavulin or Laphroaig lets the dish's sweetness still lead. A guest-friendly Islay pour with a savory-sweet small plate. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bowmore 12|Burrata": "Bowmore 12 with burrata — fresh dairy and basil oil can't find Islay peat on any spectrum. The gentler profile of Bowmore doesn't shift this; the register mismatch is structural. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Bowmore 12|Butternut Squash": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the scotch depth settles into the squash bisque, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; Old Fitz 9 brings more depth here.",
  "Bowmore 12|Carrot Cake": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character finds neutral with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the carrot cake, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; wine-cask over-specified.",
  "Bowmore 12|Cheesecake": "Bowmore 12 with cheesecake — the custard's clean dairy register doesn't pick up peat. Bowmore's sherry finish actually comes closest of the three (and for guests committed to the pairing, it's the least bad), but the conflict is still there. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Bowmore 12|Chilean Seabass": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Bowmore 12 for the steak course.",
  "Bowmore 12|Chocolate Brownie": "Bowmore 12 with the chocolate brownie — the sherry cask finish gives the dram a dried-fruit depth that threads into the cocoa. Softer, more balanced than Laphroaig's version; the easier pour for guests less committed to a full peat experience. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bowmore 12|Chocolate Cake": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the scotch depth settles into the chocolate cake, and the malt edge frames the dessert. Works; X Commandments story wasted on salad.",
  "Bowmore 12|Clam Chowder": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the malt-driven body wraps the chowder, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Bowmore 12|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: the cut's cap fat and bold char meet Bowmore 12's honeyed peat and sherry notes. The gentlest Islay of the three with this cut — capable and refined. Lagavulin 8 and Laphroaig 10 both hit harder; Bowmore is for guests who want sherry-touched balance with a big cut. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Bowmore 12|Crab Cake": "Crab Cake: the delicate crab cake gets overpowered by any peat, Bowmore's gentle profile included. Sherry-soft doesn't rescue it. Avoid across all three Islays for delicate seafood.",
  "Bowmore 12|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: dairy-rich side meets Bowmore 12's sherry depth. The honey threads into the cream; the gentler peat doesn't flatten the side the way Lagavulin or Laphroaig do. Works cleanly but won't move a guest.",
  "Bowmore 12|Creamy Potato": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the malt-and-oak threads the potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive but adds interest.",
  "Bowmore 12|Creme Brulee": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch backbone matches the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; bold intensity slightly over-specified.",
  "Bowmore 12|Escargot": "Bowmore 12's peat-sherry character lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Bowmore 12|Faroe Island Salmon": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Bowmore 12 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bowmore 12|Filet Mignon": "Bowmore 12 with the filet — the gentlest of the Islays, with honey and orchard fruit from the sherry finish, is the one peated scotch that actually lifts the filet cleanly. The other two (Lagavulin 8, Laphroaig 10) overpower the cut's subtlety; Bowmore brings character without crushing it. This is the Islay pour for filet orders. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bowmore 12|French Onion": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the scotch backbone matches the French onion, and the smoke threads the bowl. Works; premium single-barrel wasted on the salad.",
  "Bowmore 12|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: charred romaine and anchovy dressing meet Bowmore 12's honeyed sherry finish. The softer peat lets the Caesar lead; this is the Caesar pairing for guests who aren't sure how much smoke they want. The most versatile Islay-Caesar pour of the three. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bowmore 12|Gumbo": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the lighter peat meets the gumbo, and the sea-salt frames the bowl. Works; EHT Full Proof handles the richness at historic Kentucky register.",
  "Bowmore 12|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; EC 18 handles dessert density at aged-HH register.",
  "Bowmore 12|House Wedge": "House Wedge: iceberg with bleu dressing meets Bowmore 12's honey-tinged peat. The honey notes offer something the dressing can pick up, but the peat still overwhelms the crisp base. Works as a side-sip, not a driver pairing.",
  "Bowmore 12|Kansas City": "Kansas City: the strip's char and marbling meet Bowmore 12's sherry-finished honeyed peat. The gentlest Islay with the strip — the gentler peat favors the beef over the dram. Pour when a guest wants the strip to lead the conversation. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Bowmore 12|Loaded Potato": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the scotch depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Bowmore 12|Lobster Mac": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the Highland register carries the lobster mac, and the oak softens the cream. Works; distinctive character is worth mentioning tableside, even if the pairing isn't marquee.",
  "Bowmore 12|Market Fish": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Bowmore 12 for the steak course.",
  "Bowmore 12|Mocha Creme": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the malt-and-oak threads the mocha creme, and the oak threads the cream. Works; save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Bowmore 12|Mushroom Bisque": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the lighter peat meets the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the umami. Works; milestone-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Bowmore 12|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the earthy, umami-forward side meets Bowmore 12's honey, orchard fruit, and sherry-softened peat. The pairing opens the dish up instead of covering it — distinct from Lagavulin 8 or Laphroaig 10 with the same side. Pour when a guest wants peated Islay but the rest of the table skews lighter. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Bowmore 12|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the scotch backbone matches the PB brownie, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Bowmore 12|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the cut's split of strip and filet meets Bowmore 12's sherry-touched gentle peat. The filet side actually improves with Bowmore (where Lagavulin and Laphroaig overpower); the strip side handles the gentler profile cleanly. The most versatile peated Islay for this two-cut plate. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Bowmore 12|Prime Tartare": "Bowmore 12 with the tartare — gentle peat and honey against raw beef's mineral depth. The softness actually underserves the tartare (Lagavulin's vibrancy is a better match), but Bowmore holds its own cleanly. Works without surprising.",
  "Bowmore 12|Rainbow Trout": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Bowmore 12 for the steak course.",
  "Bowmore 12|Roast Half Chicken": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the roast chicken, and the smoke threads the plate. Works; Discovery complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Bowmore 12|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the scotch backbone matches the chickpea soup, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; Marian McLain L.E.",
  "Bowmore 12|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Bowmore 12 for the steak course.",
  "Bowmore 12|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the malt-driven body wraps the garlic spinach, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the side. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Bowmore 12|Seafood Tower": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Bowmore 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bowmore 12|Seared Scallops": "Bowmore 12 with seared scallops — scallops' sweet delicacy gets buried by any peat, Bowmore's gentleness included. Avoid; reach for gin, vodka, or a light white wine.",
  "Bowmore 12|Seasonal Soup": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the seasonal soup, and the smoke threads the bowl. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Bowmore 12|Seasonal Vegetables": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the scotch depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Bowmore 12|Shrimp Bisque": "Bowmore 12 with the shrimp bisque — honeyed peat and orchard-fruit notes meet the bisque's sea-driven cream. The sherry touch lifts the soup's sweetness; softer peat doesn't flatten the seafood depth. Strong — the most refined Islay-bisque pour.",
  "Bowmore 12|Shrimp Cocktail": "Bowmore 12 with shrimp cocktail — cold poached shrimp and Islay peat don't bridge regardless of how gentle the peat is. Bowmore 12's honey doesn't rescue it. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Bowmore 12|Swordfish": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Bowmore 12 on the steak course instead.",
  "Bowmore 12|The Tomahawk": "Bowmore 12 with the Tomahawk — the Tomahawk's scale pushes past Bowmore's gentler peat, so the pairing is capable but not transcendent. Guests who want Bowmore with a big cut will still enjoy this; for max-impact, reach for Lagavulin 8 or Laphroaig 10. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Bowmore 12|Tomato Basil": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the tomato basil, and the smoke threads the bowl. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side; save the rare bottle for the main.",
  "Bowmore 12|Truffle Fries": "Bowmore 12 with truffle fries — honey and sherry notes against truffle-parmesan fat. The softer peat lets truffle's earthiness lead. Strong; cleaner than Lagavulin 8, beautifully lighter than Laphroaig's medicinal approach.",
  "Bowmore 12|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Bowmore 12 on the steak course instead.",
  "Bowmore 12|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the lighter peat meets the curry, and the sea-salt frames the bowl. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Asparagus": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin. The high acid cuts gruyère-cream weight and the blackberry plays against the lactic richness. Works as benchmark Barbera for au gratin — food-wine acidity on the cheesy side.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Beignets": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Braida Montebruna Barbera lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Bone Marrow": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin holds with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Bone-In Filet": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Broccoli Cheddar": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin leans against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Broccolini": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Brussels and Belly": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin. The high acid cuts pork-belly fat and the blackberry plays against charred brussels. Strong benchmark Barbera for Brussels and Belly — food-wine acidity on the rich starter.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Burrata": "Italian food-wine Barbera against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Braida's juicy dark cherry overwhelms the starter despite light tannin. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Braida for red meat or pasta where Italian food-wine acidity peaks.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Butternut Squash": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin leans against the squash soup's silky sweet — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Carrot Cake": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Braida Montebruna Barbera lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Cheesecake": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Braida Montebruna Barbera for the steak course.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Chilean Seabass": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin holds with the seabass's buttery flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Chocolate Brownie": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Braida Montebruna Barbera for the steak course.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Chocolate Cake": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Braida Montebruna Barbera for the steak course.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Clam Chowder": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Cowboy Ribeye": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin, medium body. The medium body handles ribeye's big marbled build though low tannin reads softer than firm-tannin Cabs on the cut. Works as benchmark Barbera for ribeye — Italian food-wine alternative on the bold cut.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Crab Cake": "Italian food-wine Barbera against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Braida's high-acid juicy-cherry profile could theoretically match seafood, but dark cherry and blackberry still overpower the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Braida for red meat and pasta.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Creamed Spinach": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin. The high acid cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the blackberry plays against the spinach base. Strong benchmark Barbera for creamed spinach — food-wine acidity reframes the rich side.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Creamy Potato": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the potato soup, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Creme Brulee": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Braida Montebruna Barbera for the steak course.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Escargot": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Faroe Island Salmon": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin, medium body. The high acid and low tannin play against salmon's rich omega-three oils; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong benchmark Barbera for salmon — food-wine acid handles fish across most seasonal preparations.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Filet Mignon": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin, medium body. The high acid and low tannin meet butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the dark cherry plays against escargot-butter. Strong benchmark Barbera for filet — Italian food-wine on the delicate cut.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|French Onion": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Grilled Caesar": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin. The high acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the dark cherry plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak benchmark Barbera for grilled Caesar — food-wine acidity reframes the layered salad. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Gumbo": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin. The high acid cuts gumbo's dark-roux fat and the juicy fruit plays against andouille-shrimp spice. Strong Barbera for gumbo — Italian food-wine acidity on the Cajun classic.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|House Wedge": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin. The high acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong benchmark Barbera for the wedge — food-wine acidity reframes the classic salad.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Kansas City": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin, medium body. The juicy acid meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the dark cherry plays against beefy char. Peak benchmark Barbera for the strip — Italian alternative to Barolo on the American cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Loaded Potato": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Lobster Mac": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Market Fish": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the fish's clean flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Mocha Creme": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Braida Montebruna Barbera on the steak course instead.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Mushroom Bisque": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the mushroom bisque, and the cherry-leather edge frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Mushrooms": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin, medium body. The high acid cuts through roasted-mushroom glutamate and the blackberry plays against the earth depth. Peak benchmark Barbera for mushrooms — food-wine acidity on the earthy side. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Braida Montebruna Barbera belongs on the steak course.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Porterhouse": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin sits with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Prime Tartare": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the tartare, and the cherry-leather edge frames the starter. Works; the call holds at neutral register.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Rainbow Trout": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin holds with the trout's delicate flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Roast Half Chicken": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin, medium body. The high acid and light tannin play against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak Italian-food-wine for chicken across most seasonal preparations. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Seafood Tower": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Braida Montebruna Barbera on the steak course instead.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Seared Scallops": "Italian food-wine Barbera against scallop sweetness — Braida's dark cherry and blackberry buries the delicate shellfish despite light tannin. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Braida for red meat, pasta, or rich starters where the food-wine role peaks.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Seasonal Soup": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the seasonal soup's house build — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Seasonal Vegetables": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Shrimp Bisque": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin holds with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Shrimp Cocktail": "Italian food-wine Barbera against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — Braida's juicy cherry-blackberry and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Braida for red meat.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Swordfish": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity leans against the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the swordfish, and the cherry-leather edge frames the plate. Works; the call holds at neutral register.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|The Tomahawk": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin holds with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Tomato Basil": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cassis frames the herb butter. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Truffle Fries": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin. The high acid cuts truffle-fry potato fat and the blackberry plays against the truffle-oil umami. Peak benchmark Barbera for truffle fries — Italian food-wine on the umami hit. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the tuna's seared rare crust — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Braida Montebruna Barbera|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin leans against the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Asparagus": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Beignets": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone-In Filet": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's bold earth cassis lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the concentrated body carries the strip, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccoli Cheddar": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccolini": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Burrata": "Mount Veeder mountain Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm graphite-earth body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with dark blackberry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Brandlin for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Butternut Squash": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Carrot Cake": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Cheesecake": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Chilean Seabass": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Brownie": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Cake": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Clam Chowder": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Cowboy Ribeye": "Brandlin — Mount Veeder mountain Cab, dense and concentrated with dark blackberry, graphite, earth, firm age-worthy tannin. Higher elevation means smaller berries, thicker skins, more structure. The dense body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the graphite meets rendered fat. Peak mountain Cab for Cowboy — serious food-driven structure. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Crab Cake": "Mount Veeder mountain Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm-tannin graphite-earth crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Brandlin for the steak course where its Mount Veeder food-driven character shines.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the structured red's grip carries the rich side's dairy load; oak-driven body absorbs the cream and tannin clears the finish. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamy Potato": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Creme Brulee": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Escargot": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's bold earth cassis lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Faroe Island Salmon": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Filet Mignon": "Mount Veeder mountain Cab — dark blackberry, graphite, earth, firm age-worthy tannin. The dense body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the graphite-earth echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong mountain Cab for filet — Mount Veeder structure, though lighter Cabs like Jordan peak higher on the delicate cut.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|French Onion": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's bold earth cassis sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Grilled Caesar": "Brandlin — Mount Veeder Cab with dark blackberry, graphite, earth, firm tannin. The dense body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the graphite-earth plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as mountain Cab for grilled Caesar — Mount Veeder structure on the classic layered salad.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Gumbo": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|House Wedge": "Brandlin — Mount Veeder Cab, dark blackberry, graphite, earth, firm tannin. The dense body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the graphite plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as mountain Cab for the wedge — Mount Veeder food-driven red, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Kansas City": "Brandlin — Mount Veeder Cab with dark blackberry, graphite, earth, firm tannin. The dense mountain-fruit body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the graphite-earth plays against the beefy char. Peak mountain Cab for Kansas City — Mount Veeder structure on the classic strip. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Loaded Potato": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's bold earth cassis stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis cuts the cheese richness. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Lobster Mac": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's bold earth cassis reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the opulent body matches the indulgent side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Market Fish": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Mocha Creme": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushroom Bisque": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's bold earth cassis plays cleanly against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit anchors the mushroom's umami weight; firm tannin and oak hold their register. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the Cab's firm tannin cuts the strip's char and the dark fruit lifts the savory edge — steakhouse symmetry. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Prime Tartare": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's bold earth cassis lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Rainbow Trout": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Roast Half Chicken": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's bold earth cassis holds with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Seafood Tower": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Seared Scallops": "Brandlin Mount Veeder Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — firm graphite-earth obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Brandlin for the steak course where its Mount Veeder character peaks.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Soup": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Vegetables": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Bisque": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's bold earth cassis sits alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis frames the herb butter. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Cocktail": "Brandlin Mount Veeder Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — firm graphite-earth is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Brandlin for steak where its Mount Veeder character belongs.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Swordfish": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|The Tomahawk": "Mount Veeder mountain Cab — dark blackberry, graphite, earth, firm age-worthy tannin. The dense mountain-fruit body meets Tomahawk fat and char directly and the graphite-earth frames the theatrical bone-in cut. Peak mountain Cab for Tomahawk — Mount Veeder structure stands up to the biggest marbled steak on the menu. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Tomato Basil": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit anchors the mushroom's umami weight; firm tannin and oak hold their register. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon body sits with the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the 10-year holds without lifting.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the caramel-spice register matches the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's bold cherry-coffee cassis finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the scotch depth settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the oak softens the cream. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the scotch backbone matches the broccoli cheddar, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; blend character covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the oak-aged tequila body threads the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; refinement is underserved by the dish density.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the aged-agave weight wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; the smoothness doesn't drive the pairing but the theatrics do.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight aligns with the soup's cheddar-cream richness and the apple-pear lifts the broccoli base. Peak accessible Chardonnay for broccoli cheddar — California oak on the classic. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin leans against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Amaro Nonino on the steak course instead.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Ancho Reyes on the steak course instead.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon depth settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon depth settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a soup; save the rare bottle.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Asparagus": "Broccoli Cheddar and the asparagus -- the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream meets the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: bright-acid breaks the weight, the plate finds its lift. Works; no conflict however the table orders them.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The broccoli cheddar and the au gratin share cheese as the primary character. Avoid; the meal doubles down without contrast. Route the broccoli cheddar with Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay or the au gratin with Bone-In Filet, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Keenan Chardonnay instead.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Aviation": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Avion 44": "Avion 44's aged-agave character finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; distinctive ceremony over-specified.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's clean silver register leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the blanco-tequila body matches the broccoli cheddar, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Broccoli Cheddar|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the silver-rum body cuts the broccoli cheddar, and the unaged-cane note plays against the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
