  "Brussels and Belly|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The rye-cognac split handles the belly's rendered fat and the Bénédictine adds herbal depth against brussels char. Works — though the Bowdie's or Espresso Old Fashioned peak higher with the belly's glaze.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Villon Cognac": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge reads alongside Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the brandy depth wraps the pork-belly plate, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Vin Santo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Weller 107": "Weller 107 with Brussels + Belly — higher-proof wheat meets maple-glazed pork belly. The heat cuts through the dish's sweetness in a way Weller Special Reserve's softness can't; a more assertive small-plate pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year -- aged wheated, honey, vanilla. The oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the pork-belly plate, and the vanilla rounds the flame-grilled char. Excellent; EHT Full Proof meets the glaze at historic Kentucky register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium with Brussels + Belly — the Millennium's honeyed depth against maple-glazed pork belly. More polished than Weller 12's version; the celebratory small-plate pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Weller Special Reserve": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet Weller Special Reserve's soft wheated sweetness. Approachable without being underwhelming; the gateway Weller handles the small plate cleanly. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality holds with the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Brussels and Belly|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with Brussels + Belly — aged WP single-barrel meets maple-glazed pork belly. The 10-year Vermont-finished depth cuts the glaze with premium rye authority. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Brussels and Belly|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with Brussels + Belly — fruit-cask WP meets maple-glazed pork belly. The wine-cask fruit character and maple find each other in Old World finishing logic; a complex small-plate pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Brussels and Belly|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with Brussels + Belly — 15-year flagship WP meets maple-glazed pork belly. The extended maturation cuts the glaze with premium flagship depth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Brussels and Belly|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with Brussels + Belly — allocated Boss Hog meets maple-glazed pork belly. The barrel-proof WP cuts the glaze with unicorn rye authority. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Brussels and Belly|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with Brussels + Belly — X Commandments meets maple-glazed pork belly. The 10th-edition Boss Hog cuts the glaze with allocated annual authority. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Brussels and Belly|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year with Brussels + Belly — WP 100-proof meets maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; Vermont-brand character cuts the glaze with distinctive rye voice.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with Brussels + Belly — craft 94-proof high-rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. The oak spice and rye character cut the glaze's sweetness in a way Heaven Hill BIB's straight oak can't. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with Brussels + Belly — fruit-forward bourbon meets maple-glazed pork belly. Woodford's fruit notes play with the dish's maple in a way BT or Maker's can't; a refined small-plate pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet Brown-Forman Double Oaked. Works; Woodford Double Oaked's intensified-caramel meets the glaze at KY-benchmark register.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with Brussels + Belly — elegant small-batch rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; fruit-forward character cuts the glaze with refined distinction.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with Brussels + Belly — PNW craft rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; regional character cuts the glaze with non-Kentucky distinctiveness.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character sits alongside Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the Japanese-whisky precision frames the pork-belly plate, and the silky body rounds the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the pork-belly plate, and the green-fruit edge brightens the side. Works; Remus 15 frames the two-cut plate at Gatsby-tribute register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Brussels and Belly|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness matches the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Asparagus": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Buffalo Trace Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Beignets": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet bourbon-cream-caramel character holds steady against the beignet's warm sugar-dusted pastry — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the caramel deepens the dessert sugar. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Bone Marrow": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character flattens the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Bone-In Filet": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Buffalo Trace Cream lands on another course — not here.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Broccoli Cheddar": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Buffalo Trace Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Broccolini": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Buffalo Trace Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Brussels and Belly": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character doubles down on Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- sweet liqueur and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no contrast left. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Burrata": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Butternut Squash": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Buffalo Trace Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Buffalo Trace Cream. Excellent; the bourbon-vanilla-caramel amplifies the cream cheese frosting with deeper character than Baileys — Kentucky-cream register.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Cheesecake": "Cheesecake: custard-dairy dessert meets Buffalo Trace Cream. Excellent; the bourbon-base-caramel-cream frames cheesecake at Kentucky-cream register — caramel-vanilla on dairy, bourbon-drinkers' dessert liqueur.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Chilean Seabass": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert-grade sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs a crisp white instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Buffalo Trace Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Buffalo Trace Cream. Excellent; bourbon-caramel-cream threads chocolate at deeper register than Baileys — bourbon depth meets cocoa warmth.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Chocolate Cake": "Buffalo Trace Cream's herbal-and-sugar liqueur register rests at register with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the herbal-sweet weight matches the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Clam Chowder": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Buffalo Trace Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Cowboy Ribeye": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Buffalo Trace Cream for another course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Crab Cake": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Creamed Spinach": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Creamy Potato": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Creme Brulee": "Buffalo Trace Cream: bourbon-base cream liqueur from Buffalo Trace Distillery (Kentucky), vanilla-caramel-cream with bourbon depth. Excellent on creme brulee; the bourbon-caramel-cream character amplifies burnt-sugar-custard at bourbon-cream register — richer register than Baileys' Irish-whiskey base.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Escargot": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Faroe Island Salmon": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Filet Mignon": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the liqueur's sugar buries the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Buffalo Trace Cream lands on another course — not here.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|French Onion": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Buffalo Trace Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Grilled Caesar": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character jars against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Buffalo Trace Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Gumbo": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Buffalo Trace Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Buffalo Trace Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|House Wedge": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Buffalo Trace Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Kansas City": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Buffalo Trace Cream lands on another course — not here.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Loaded Potato": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Lobster Mac": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Market Fish": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- sugar on delicate fish is wrong direction — the plate needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Mocha Creme": "Mocha Creme: coffee-chocolate dessert meets Buffalo Trace Cream. Excellent; bourbon-caramel-cream meets mocha at Kentucky-dessert register — bourbon-coffee is a classic pairing, cream amplifies it.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Mushroom Bisque": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Mushrooms": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character fights the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- the liqueur's sweet register pulls against the mushroom's umami without bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut-chocolate stack meets Buffalo Trace Cream. Excellent; the bourbon-cream wraps peanut-chocolate at bourbon-dessert register — where Baileys brings cocoa-cream lightness, Buffalo Trace Cream brings caramel depth.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Porterhouse": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Buffalo Trace Cream for another course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Prime Tartare": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character flattens the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Rainbow Trout": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Buffalo Trace Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Roast Half Chicken": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character buries the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- sugar smothers the chicken's savory frame — wait for dessert. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Buffalo Trace Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Buffalo Trace Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Seafood Tower": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Buffalo Trace Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Buffalo Trace Cream. Avoid; bourbon-cream weight wrong for scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Seasonal Soup": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Buffalo Trace Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Seasonal Vegetables": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Shrimp Bisque": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Shrimp Cocktail": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Swordfish": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sugar dulls the crust's clean char instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Buffalo Trace Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|The Tomahawk": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character flattens the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Buffalo Trace Cream for another course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Tomato Basil": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Buffalo Trace Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Truffle Fries": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character flattens the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- sugar on starch is dessert territory — wrong course for the supporting plate. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Buffalo Trace Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- sugar dulls the crust's clean char instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Buffalo Trace Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace Cream|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Asparagus": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the brown-spirit warmth threads the asparagus, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Buffalo Trace with au gratin potatoes — flagship bourbon against cheese-and-cream. Strong; clean match at the register.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Beignets": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the brown-spirit warmth threads the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Bone Marrow": "Buffalo Trace with bone marrow — flagship bourbon against umami fat. BT's oak-and-caramel clean profile meets the marrow without the wheated softness; a balanced, non-wheated premium pour for this starter. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Bone-In Filet": "Buffalo Trace -- vanilla, toffee, oak. The bourbon depth settles into the bone-enhanced filet, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cut. Strong; Sinatra Select's grooved-barrel character frames the two-cut plate at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Broccoli Cheddar": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the caramel-spice register matches the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Broccolini": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon body sits with the broccolini, and the oak frames the side. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Brussels and Belly": "Buffalo Trace with Brussels + Belly — flagship bourbon meets maple-glazed pork belly. BT's caramel and oak handle the dish's sweetness without over-matching; a reliable, clean small-plate pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Burrata": "Buffalo Trace with burrata — fresh dairy against flagship bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Butternut Squash": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon depth settles into the squash bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; milestone-edition over-specified.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Carrot Cake": "Buffalo Trace with carrot cake — BT caramel and vanilla against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Cheesecake": "Buffalo Trace with cheesecake — clean custard against BT caramel. Works; gentle match.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Chilean Seabass": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Buffalo Trace on the steak course instead.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Chocolate Brownie": "Buffalo Trace with the chocolate brownie — flagship oak against cocoa. Works; for more interplay, Weller 107 or Weller Millennium for wheated, or Eagle Rare 10 for non-wheated.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Chocolate Cake": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the oak depth lays under the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing is pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Clam Chowder": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the chowder, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Marian McLain L.E.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Cowboy Ribeye": "Buffalo Trace with the Cowboy Ribeye — 90-proof flagship against cap fat and char. The BT profile earns the ribeye where lighter wheated pours fade. A clean, non-wheated everyday call for this cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Crab Cake": "Buffalo Trace with crab cake — flagship bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Creamed Spinach": "Buffalo Trace with creamed spinach — BT's oak and vanilla against dairy richness. Strong; the non-wheated profile gives the side a different frame than Maker's or Larceny.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Creamy Potato": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon body sits with the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; AE Triple Cask's layered character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Creme Brulee": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon weight handles the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Escargot": "Buffalo Trace's caramel-workhorse character frames the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Faroe Island Salmon": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Buffalo Trace belongs on the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Filet Mignon": "Buffalo Trace with the filet — the flagship of the distillery, 90 proof, non-wheated mash #1. Caramel, vanilla, and clean oak give the filet a frame distinct from the Wellers and Maker's (no wheat softness). The workhorse premium bourbon — always a safe, well-composed call. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Buffalo Trace|French Onion": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon weight handles the French onion, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for a side — save the bottle for the main course.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Grilled Caesar": "Buffalo Trace with the grilled Caesar — flagship oak against charred romaine and anchovy. BT's balance earns the Caesar in a way softer wheated pours can't; strong. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Gumbo": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the vanilla settles into the gumbo, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon weight handles the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Buffalo Trace|House Wedge": "Buffalo Trace with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against flagship bourbon. Works; no driver pairing.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Kansas City": "Buffalo Trace with the KC — BT flagship against the strip's char. The 90-proof balance handles the marbling cleanly; distinct from wheated pours, more oak-spice and less honey. A versatile everyday premium call. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Loaded Potato": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the caramel-spice register matches the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Lobster Mac": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak depth lays under the lobster mac, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Market Fish": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Buffalo Trace for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Mocha Creme": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon body sits with the mocha creme, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; BH Red Wine Cask's softer-proof character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Mushroom Bisque": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon weight handles the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; a gentle composed match at the premium tier.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Mushrooms": "Buffalo Trace with the mushrooms — BT flagship against earthy umami. The 90-proof non-wheated profile gives mushroom depth a clean frame; a versatile side pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon backbone carries the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Porterhouse": "Buffalo Trace with the porterhouse — flagship versatility across both strip and filet sides. BT handles the plate cleanly; a middle-ground pour between the Wellers and Eagle Rare 10 Year. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Prime Tartare": "Buffalo Trace's caramel-workhorse character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Rainbow Trout": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Buffalo Trace belongs on the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Roast Half Chicken": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the vanilla settles into the roast chicken, and the oak threads the plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon backbone carries the chickpea soup, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; Remus 15 frames the two-cut plate at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Buffalo Trace for the steak course.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon weight handles the garlic spinach, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; EHT Full Proof handles the richness at historic Kentucky register.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Seafood Tower": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Buffalo Trace lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Seared Scallops": "Buffalo Trace with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against BT oak. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Seasonal Soup": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the vanilla settles into the seasonal soup, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Seasonal Vegetables": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the vanilla settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the oak threads the side. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Shrimp Bisque": "Buffalo Trace with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood against flagship bourbon. Works neutrally.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Shrimp Cocktail": "Buffalo Trace with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against BT flagship. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Swordfish": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Buffalo Trace on the steak course instead.",
  "Buffalo Trace|The Tomahawk": "Buffalo Trace's caramel-workhorse character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Tomato Basil": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon weight handles the tomato basil, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; the complex profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Truffle Fries": "Buffalo Trace with truffle fries — BT's oak against truffle-parmesan. The flagship's cleanness lets truffle lead; distinct from wheated takes — less honey, more structure. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Buffalo Trace on the steak course instead.",
  "Buffalo Trace|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon weight handles the curry, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; EC 18 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Asparagus": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the rye-spice edge cuts the asparagus, and the oak underlines the side. Works; richer profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Bulleit Bourbon with au gratin potatoes — 90-proof high-rye against cheese-and-cream. Strong; rye spice distinct from wheated-caramel approaches.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Beignets": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon weight handles the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Bone Marrow": "Bulleit Bourbon with bone marrow — 90-proof high-rye spice against umami fat. Strong; rye brightness doesn't match the marrow's intensity the way Weller 12's aged depth does, but offers a distinctive take.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Bone-In Filet": "Bulleit Bourbon's bold spice-vanilla character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Broccoli Cheddar": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the oak depth lays under the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; Gran Cru's brioche meets the glaze at refined register.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Broccolini": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon depth settles into the broccolini, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; the rye's bright character is slightly at odds with the side's richness.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Brussels and Belly": "Bulleit Bourbon with Brussels + Belly — high-rye spice meets maple-glazed pork belly. The rye cuts the glaze's sweetness in a way wheated can't; a distinctive small-plate pairing at the approachable tier. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Burrata": "Bulleit Bourbon with burrata — fresh dairy against high-rye bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Butternut Squash": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak depth lays under the squash bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; AE Triple Cask's layered character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Carrot Cake": "Bulleit Bourbon with carrot cake — bold spice against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; rye spice and cake spice find each other.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Cheesecake": "Bulleit Bourbon with cheesecake — high-rye spice against clean custard. Works; rye's brightness is a gentle counterpoint.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Chilean Seabass": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Bulleit Bourbon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Chocolate Brownie": "Bulleit Bourbon with the chocolate brownie — high-rye against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Chocolate Cake": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the caramel-spice register matches the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; distinctive finish meets the cake with beer-barrel character.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Clam Chowder": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon weight handles the chowder, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; spice over-specified for side's character.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Cowboy Ribeye": "Bulleit Bourbon with the Cowboy Ribeye — 90-proof high-rye against cap fat and char. Strong; slightly underpowered for the ribeye's scale — Knob Creek (100-proof) and Willett (94-proof craft) are the higher-impact high-rye calls. Bulleit holds the cut at approachable tier.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Crab Cake": "Bulleit Bourbon with crab cake — high-rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Creamed Spinach": "Bulleit Bourbon with creamed spinach — high-rye spice against dairy. Strong; rye's brightness gives the side a distinctive counterpoint to wheated caramel takes.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Creamy Potato": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the unique maturation story adds interest to a gentle dessert match.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Creme Brulee": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon weight handles the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Escargot": "Bulleit Bourbon's bold spice-vanilla character holds the line with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Faroe Island Salmon": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Bulleit Bourbon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Filet Mignon": "Bulleit Bourbon with the filet — 90-proof high-rye with distinctive bold spice. The rye-forward character gives the filet a different vocabulary than the wheated pours or the BT flagship; distinct from Knob Creek's bigger-body 100-proof — more accessible, still spice-driven. The high-rye filet pour at the approachable register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|French Onion": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the French onion, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Grilled Caesar": "Bulleit Bourbon with the grilled Caesar — high-rye spice against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; the rye earns this Caesar in a way Wellers' wheated softness doesn't.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Gumbo": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the caramel-spice register matches the gumbo, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; IW Harper 15 handles dessert density at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon weight handles the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; wine-cask complexity gives the dessert layered premium character.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|House Wedge": "Bulleit Bourbon with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against high-rye bourbon. Works; the spice is underused by the salad.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Kansas City": "Bulleit Bourbon with the KC — bold high-rye spice against the strip's marbling. The 90-proof rye-forward profile cuts the char differently than wheated — more spice, more grip. A distinctive strip pour at the approachable tier. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Loaded Potato": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the oak depth lays under the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; smooth gentle match.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Lobster Mac": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the caramel-spice register matches the lobster mac, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; KBS distinctiveness is slightly over-specified for the two-cut plate.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Market Fish": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Bulleit Bourbon for the steak course.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Mocha Creme": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon depth settles into the mocha creme, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Mushroom Bisque": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon backbone carries the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Mushrooms": "Bulleit Bourbon with the mushrooms — 90-proof high-rye against earthy umami. The spice-forward character layers with mushroom depth; distinct from wheated or flagship approaches. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the distinctive character is underused by the salad's simplicity.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Porterhouse": "Bulleit Bourbon with the porterhouse — high-rye spice across strip and filet sides. Strong; both cuts take the rye-forward character cleanly, though higher-proof high-ryes (Knob Creek) bring more grip.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Prime Tartare": "Bulleit Bourbon's bold spice-vanilla character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Rainbow Trout": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Bulleit Bourbon on the steak course instead.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Roast Half Chicken": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the brown-spirit warmth threads the roast chicken, and the caramel rounds the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon backbone carries the chickpea soup, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Bulleit Bourbon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the brown-spirit warmth threads the garlic spinach, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; the single-barrel depth is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Seafood Tower": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Bulleit Bourbon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Seared Scallops": "Bulleit Bourbon with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against rye spice. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Seasonal Soup": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon weight handles the seasonal soup, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; KBS distinctiveness is slightly over-specified for the two-cut plate.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Seasonal Vegetables": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal vegetables, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Shrimp Bisque": "Bulleit Bourbon with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood against high-rye. Works; rye spice doesn't particularly elevate the soup.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Shrimp Cocktail": "Bulleit Bourbon with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against high-rye. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Swordfish": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Bulleit Bourbon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|The Tomahawk": "Bulleit Bourbon's bold spice-vanilla character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Tomato Basil": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the tomato basil, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for this dish's register.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Truffle Fries": "Bulleit Bourbon with truffle fries — high-rye spice against truffle-parmesan. The rye-forward character gives the side a different dimension than wheated caramel; a distinctive approachable-high-rye pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Bulleit Bourbon for the steak course.",
  "Bulleit Bourbon|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon weight handles the curry, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; curated single-barrel covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Burrata|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with burrata — fresh dairy against Barton small-batch. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat with burrata — fresh dairy against sweet-wheat. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|1881 Napa Valley": "Entry Napa Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — smooth-tannin body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with cherry-currant. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep 1881 for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|2 James Gin": "2 James with burrata — Detroit local craft gin against fresh dairy. Excellent; 2 James's juniper-forward American profile frames burrata with hometown character; Plymouth still wins on sheer textural softness match.",
  "Burrata|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with burrata — fresh dairy against rare limited-release. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with burrata — ex-bourbon + Oloroso Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino with burrata — filtered cristalino against fresh mozzarella. The aged-but-clear distinctive character lifts the burrata with craft depth. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Burrata|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's aged-agave character sits alongside the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the burrata, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the starter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Burrata|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth texture. The oak-vanilla weight meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the apple-pear fruit plays against basil-tomato. Strong California Chard for burrata — accessible oak weight on the delicate starter.",
  "Burrata|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Sonoma Meritage against burrata's creamy-delicate center — full-body Bordeaux-style structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Cyrus for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Sonoma Bordeaux blend against burrata's creamy-delicate center — plum-blackberry-mocha structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Homestead Red for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino with burrata — amaro against fresh dairy. Avoid; botanical bitterness doesn't find burrata.",
  "Burrata|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur and fresh dairy compound the richness without contrast — heavy too early. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with burrata — port-finished bourbon against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with burrata — cask-strength port-finish against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "AE Triple Cask with burrata — AE triple-cask against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Aperol": "Aperol with burrata — soft orange-rhubarb against fresh dairy. Excellent; this is the one amaro pairing where burrata lands — Aperol's 11% softness threads the cream where every other amaro in the family is too aggressive. Classic aperitivo move.",
  "Burrata|Asparagus": "Burrata alongside the asparagus -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy meets the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: bright and delicate share air, the meal carries lift without weight. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Burrata|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The burrata alongside the au gratin -- both bring cheese as the headline. Avoid; one is enough. Pair the burrata with Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc; pair the au gratin with Bone-In Filet, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Keenan Chardonnay.",
  "Burrata|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Bold Paso Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — plush warm-spice body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with ripe plum-cherry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Austin Hope for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Averna Amaro": "Averna with burrata — amaro against fresh dairy. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Burrata|Aviation": "Aviation gin, Luxardo maraschino, crème de violette, ginger syrup — pale purple in the glass. The floral-cherry-almond profile meets burrata's creamy-delicate build and the visual appeal doubles the presentation. Peak starter pairing — both elegant, both visually striking on the table. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Burrata|Aviation Gin": "Aviation with burrata — Portland soft-juniper craft against fresh dairy. Excellent; Aviation's lavender-orange-peel softness complements cream at crowd-pleasing register — Empress's rose-floral and Inverroche's fynbos are more distinctive.",
  "Burrata|Avion 44": "Avion 44 with burrata — fresh dairy against aged Avion. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Burrata|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver with burrata — filtered 80-proof against fresh dairy. The clean highland agave lifts the burrata at the accessible register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Burrata|Bacardi Rum": "Burrata: fresh dairy meets Bacardi Rum. Excellent; the neutral-accessible character frames burrata's cream at gateway-rum register — clean meets clean.",
  "Burrata|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Baker's": "Baker's with burrata — fresh dairy against 107-proof small-batch. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with burrata — handcrafted Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with burrata — tropical-sweet Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with burrata — hand-picked First Fill American Oak against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood with burrata — port-cask ultra-premium Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with burrata — French-wine-cask Bardstown against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with burrata — fresh dairy against Discovery blend. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with burrata — fresh dairy against stout-barrel bourbon. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare with burrata — Barbados rum-cask Bardstown against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with burrata — fresh dairy against own-plus-sourced. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with burrata — fresh dairy against curated single-barrel. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with burrata — fresh dairy against rye spice. Avoid; even 80-proof light-bodied bourbon is too much for the burrata's register.",
  "Burrata|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year with burrata — fresh dairy can't carry aged rye spice. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Burrata|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against bready malted. Avoid; the pour's character can't find the burrata's gentleness.",
  "Burrata|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with burrata — BH high-rye red-wine finish against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast with burrata — fresh dairy against toasted caramel. Avoid; the pour's warmth doesn't find the burrata's coolness.",
  "Burrata|Bee's Knees": "Empress Indigo gin with lemon and honey lavender syrup. The honey works seasonally with whatever fruit the kitchen has on the plate; the Indigo's butterfly-pea color-shift is a detail to mention alongside a vegetarian starter. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Burrata|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater with burrata — 40% balanced London Dry against fresh dairy. Excellent; Beefeater threads the cream cleanly without Tanq's intensity or Plymouth's sweetness.",
  "Burrata|Beignets": "Burrata and the beignets -- the delicate course resolves into the close, the meal stays unburdened. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Burrata|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere with burrata — aromatic Polish rye against fresh dairy. Excellent; Belvedere's vanilla notes complement cream while the rye pepper adds textural contrast.",
  "Burrata|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with burrata — fresh dairy against blended bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Burrata|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with burrata — fresh dairy against 103-proof. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Burrata|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with burrata — fresh dairy against premium single-barrel. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Burrata|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's SfB with burrata — cask-strength Blanton's against fresh dairy. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates burrata.",
  "Burrata|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with burrata — fresh dairy against decennial Pact 10. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with burrata — fresh dairy against Pact 8. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with burrata — fresh dairy against Pact 9 wine-cask. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Burrata|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat with burrata — 4-organic-citrus Philly gin against fresh dairy. Excellent; Bluecoat's citrus lift brightens the cream cleanly at American-craft register.",
  "Burrata|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Never pair Noemia with burrata — blackberry, violet, and full body clash with fresh cream and the graphite buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Burrata|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry with burrata — 8-botanical juniper against fresh dairy. Strong; Bombay Dry's angular juniper cuts cream more aggressively than Plymouth's soft approach — less textural match, more contrast.",
  "Burrata|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire with burrata — 10-botanical gin (grains of paradise + cubeb pepper), vapor-infused process (botanicals suspended above spirit in basket). Excellent; Sapphire's floral-softer profile meets burrata's cream with a different approach than Plymouth's sweet-earthy or Tanq 10's citrus — the floral-pepper notes are distinctive.",
  "Burrata|Bone-In Filet": "The bone-in filet after the burrata -- fresh milky basil-tomato cream opens the meal, the 14oz cut following with bone-deepened butter that meets the cream's richness on the same plane. Strong; the dairy opener sets the table.",
  "Burrata|Booker's": "Booker's with burrata — uncut Beam against fresh dairy. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates burrata.",
  "Burrata|Botanist Gin": "Botanist with burrata — Bruichladdich Islay craft gin against fresh dairy. Strong; Botanist's wildcrafted-herbal complexity complements cream at Hebridean-terroir register; Inverroche's fynbos-floral is still the featured craft-gin-burrata match.",
  "Burrata|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, rum-infused cherries, orange, bitters. The bourbon's weight crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile; vanilla-caramel has no hook into the cheese-and-basil build. Never pair — burrata begs for Prosecco or a crisp white, not spirit-forward whiskey.",
  "Burrata|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with burrata — fresh dairy cannot carry house-pick wheated depth. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with burrata — fresh dairy against Virginia small-batch. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Burrata|Bowmore 12": "Bowmore 12 with burrata — fresh dairy and basil oil can't find Islay peat on any spectrum. The gentler profile of Bowmore doesn't shift this; the register mismatch is structural. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Italian food-wine Barbera against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Braida's juicy dark cherry overwhelms the starter despite light tannin. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Braida for red meat or pasta where Italian food-wine acidity peaks.",
  "Burrata|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Mount Veeder mountain Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm graphite-earth body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with dark blackberry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Brandlin for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Broccoli Cheddar": "The burrata and the broccoli cheddar -- both cheese-forward, the table doubles up on cheese. Avoid; pick one or the other. Pair the burrata with Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc; pair the broccoli cheddar with Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay.",
  "Burrata|Broccolini": "Burrata alongside the broccolini -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy meets the broccolini's tender green stalks: both pulled back, the table reads composed and open. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Burrata|Bruichladdich": "Burrata: the burrata's creamy, fresh dairy mildness meets this Islay Scotch's intense peat smoke, medicinal phenol, and bold weight. Bold whiskey's weight meets the starter — register depends on the plate's richness. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Burrata|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with burrata — fresh dairy against flagship bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with burrata — fresh dairy against high-rye bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|Butternut Squash": "Burrata and the butternut squash together -- delicate gives way to sweet, the close lands light. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Burrata|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Howell Mountain Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm iron-mineral body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackberry-plum. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Cade for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the rested-agave depth sits with the burrata, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the starter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Burrata|Campari": "Campari with burrata — 24% bitter against fresh dairy. Avoid; Campari's bitterness is still too heavy for burrata. Reach for Aperol instead.",
  "Burrata|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club with burrata — heritage Canadian against fresh dairy. Avoid; Canadian Club's grain-forward character is wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Canadian Club 43 Year": "CC 43 Year with burrata — rare aged Canadian against fresh dairy. Avoid; save the collector bottle — burrata doesn't deserve a 43yr pour.",
  "Burrata|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's unaged silver-rum body sits alongside the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the unaged body brightens the burrata, and the Caribbean register brightens the starter. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Burrata|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing with burrata — single-barrel Canadian against fresh dairy. Avoid; Caribou Crossing's craft character is wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Carrot Cake": "Burrata and the carrot cake -- the delicate course resolves into the close, the meal stays unburdened. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Burrata|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Casa LBV Port lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Burrata|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- smoke and fresh dairy refuse each other — texture reads broken. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a mezcal. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Casamigos Mezcal on the steak course instead.",
  "Burrata|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon's cocoa-mocha velvety-tannin opulence clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- red structure on burrata curdles the cream's clean delicacy. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Burrata|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Cheesecake": "Burrata and the cheesecake -- the lighter course hands off to dessert, the meal closes clean. Works; the bookends compose neutrally.",
  "Burrata|Chilean Seabass": "Chilean Seabass preceded by the burrata -- the miso-glazed seabass holds with the fresh-cream-and-tomato opener. Strong; reliable opener for the main.",
  "Burrata|Chocolate Brownie": "The burrata and the brownie -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy bookends with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Burrata|Chocolate Cake": "The burrata and the chocolate cake -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy bookends with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Burrata|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin with burrata — Polish potato vodka, single-ingredient, creamy/round mouthfeel. Excellent; a textbook match that delivers.",
  "Burrata|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with burrata — sweeter American brandy against fresh dairy. Avoid; brandy is too heavy for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Château Batailley": "Classified Pauillac Bordeaux against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm-tannin graphite-cedar structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Château Beaucastel": "Never pair Beaucastel with burrata — leather, truffle, and full body clash with fresh cream and the Provençal herbs bury tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Burrata|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Never pair Château Gombaude-Guillot with burrata — velvety Pomerol's dark plum and mocha obliterate basil-tomato-fresh-cheese delicacy and milky texture. Lead with Il Colle Prosecco, Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc. Gombaude-Guillot is a steak-and-truffle wine.",
  "Burrata|Château Haut Segottes": "Right-bank Bordeaux against burrata's creamy-delicate center — plum-truffle Merlot overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Château de Rouillac": "Left-bank Bordeaux against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm-tannin blackcurrant-cedar overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Château de Rouillac for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Clam Chowder": "Burrata and the chowder together -- restrained yields to cream, the table arcs cleanly. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Burrata|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Clase Azul Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango with burrata — fresh dairy against hand-painted ceramic. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold with burrata — fresh dairy against ceremonial blend. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- mezcal smoke is too forward for the table's opener. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a mezcal. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata with burrata — ultra-premium blanco against fresh dairy. The smooth agave and luxury presentation lift the burrata with ceremony. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Burrata|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado's ceramic-icon reposado character finds neutral with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the barrel-rested body handles the burrata, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the starter. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Burrata|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra with burrata — fresh dairy against 5-year top-tier ceremony. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Burrata|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep with burrata — fresh dairy against Beam wheated. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Burrata|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body, low tannin. The wild raspberry bridges tomato-basil brightness and the low tannin won't tear against fresh cream. Works for burrata — a Rosé handles the dairy more cleanly.",
  "Burrata|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the barrel-rested body handles the burrata, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the starter. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Burrata|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's vermouth-wine-base sweetness sits beside the burrata's fresh cream — the bitter herb edge cuts the umami fat. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Burrata|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco with burrata — Sazerac single-estate against fresh mozzarella. The highland character lifts the burrata distinctly from the big-brand blancos. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Burrata|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado's añejo-pour depth leans against the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the rested-agave depth sits with the burrata, and the cooked-agave note underlines the starter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Burrata|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's aged-agave character fights the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- agave and fresh dairy refuse each other texturally. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Corazon Sazerac for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg's añejo tequila finished in ex-George-T fights the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- agave and fresh dairy refuse each other texturally. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Corazon Stagg for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's aged-agave character overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Corazon Weller belongs on the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Never pair Corazón Malbec with burrata — jam-forward plum and violet clash with fresh cream and the full body buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Burrata|Corpse Reviver": "Warner's gin, Lillet, cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. The cocktail's layered citrus-and-anise profile cuts burrata's cream and cognac plays against basil-tomato depth. Peak starter pairing for guests who want something historically significant with their burrata.",
  "Burrata|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier with burrata — drier cognac against fresh dairy. Avoid; cognac weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye preceded by the burrata -- delicate-into-bold, the meal earns its register lift, the courses arc cleanly. Works; the call holds, the starter primes the cut without strain.",
  "Burrata|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 with burrata — Classic Malt Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Creamed Spinach": "The burrata and the creamed spinach -- the lighter course primes the richer, the meal carries register lift. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Burrata|Creamy Potato": "The burrata preceded by the potato soup -- delicate gives way to rich, the table earns its weight. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "Burrata|Creme Brulee": "The burrata and the brulee -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy bookends with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Burrata|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral, low tannin. The low tannin meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the raspberry plays against basil-tomato. Works as benchmark Pinot for burrata — accessible red; whites and rosés peak higher.",
  "Burrata|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal with burrata — iconic smooth Canadian against fresh dairy. Avoid; Crown Royal is too light-vanilla-sweet to match burrata.",
  "Burrata|Cucumber Gimlet": "Hendrick's gin, cucumber slices, lime, ginger syrup, soda top. Fresh cucumber against burrata's creamy-basil delicacy is a direct match; lime brightness cuts the cream and ginger adds subtle warmth. Peak starter pairing — both lean into garden-fresh, clean profiles. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Burrata|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12 with burrata — Highland luxury sherry-finish against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body. The chalk-minerality meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the dried-herbs play against basil-tomato. Strong Loire red for burrata — unconventional French Pinot on the delicate starter.",
  "Burrata|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Rare Napa CF against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Darioush's medium-tannin violet-herb structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Darioush for the steak course where the Persian-estate story earns the bottle.",
  "Burrata|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Opulent Silverado Trail Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — plush mocha-spice body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackcurrant-plum. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Darioush for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin with burrata — Detroit City Distillery urban craft, juniper-forward American interpretation. Excellent; Detroit City is the hometown-Detroit burrata pairing — sibling to Detroit City Vodka on the spirits program, parallel to 2 James as the Detroit gin answer.",
  "Burrata|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City with burrata — Detroit City Distillery against fresh dairy. Excellent; hometown craft vodka sits alongside cream at Michigan-pride register.",
  "Burrata|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15 with burrata — aged Highland blend against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label with burrata — honey-vanilla blend against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Disaronno on the steak course instead.",
  "Burrata|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against boutique craft rye. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Willamette flagship Pinot Blend — dark cherry, Bing cherry, toasted spice, forest floor, silky soft tannin. The silky tannin meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the Bing cherry plays against basil-tomato. Works as flagship Willamette Pinot for burrata — Oregon elegance; whites peak higher for the starter.",
  "Burrata|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Never pair Tempier with burrata — game, olive, and smoky mineral clash with fresh cream and the full-body firm tannin buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Burrata|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — Sauv Blanc with gun-flint minerality, grapefruit, lime zest. The serious body can handle burrata's cream but the smoky-flint profile is stronger than the delicate salad needs. Works as unconventional pairing; Sancerre or St Supéry peak higher for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Never pair Grand Tinel with burrata — garrigue, black pepper, and full body clash with fresh cream and the herbal earth buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Burrata|Don Fulano Anejo": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character fights the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- agave and fresh dairy refuse each other texturally. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Don Fulano Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco with burrata — highland craft blanco against fresh mozzarella. The clean refined agave lifts the burrata with premium craft elegance. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Burrata|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight finds neutral with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the barrel-rested body handles the burrata, and the oak threads the starter. Works; iconic extra añejo is composed with the dessert.",
  "Burrata|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942 with burrata — fresh dairy against benchmark extra añejo. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the rested-agave depth sits with the burrata, and the caramel-agave register brightens the starter. Works; aged-but-clear character composes with the dish.",
  "Burrata|Don Julio Blanco": "Burrata: fresh mozzarella and basil oil meet iconic blanco. Citrus and clean agave lift the burrata where brown spirits overwhelm; industry-standard call. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Burrata|Don Julio Reposado": "Don Julio Reposado's rested-agave body finds neutral with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the barrel-rested body handles the burrata, and the oak threads the starter. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Burrata|Drambuie": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Drambuie for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Dunville's Single Malt": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bourbon weight buries the milky cheese plate's delicate frame. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Dunville's Single Malt for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with burrata — Col. E.H. Taylor Jr. tribute against fresh dairy. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates burrata.",
  "Burrata|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against bonded BT rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Burrata|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with burrata — fresh dairy against bonded 100-proof. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Burrata|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with burrata — fresh dairy can't carry Buffalo Trace's aged oak. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Burrata: fresh dairy meets Eastern Kille Distilling, Grand Rapids, classic London Dry style, small-batch family-operated. Eastern Kille's juniper-citrus classic character frames burrata with Grand Rapids hometown distinction — the Grand Rapids-pride gin pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Burrata|El Cabo": "El Cabo's rested-agave body finds neutral with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the oak-aged tequila body threads the burrata, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the starter. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Burrata|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "El Mayor Extra Anejo with burrata — fresh dairy against aged El Mayor. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado's aged-agave character finds neutral with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the añejo register carries the burrata, and the caramel-agave register brightens the starter. Works; accessible match.",
  "Burrata|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin with burrata — African craft gin against fresh dairy. Excellent; the baobab and lion's tail botanicals complement cream with distinctive contemporary character — distinct from Plymouth's sweetness or Nolet's rose-peach approach.",
  "Burrata|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 with burrata — HH 18yr against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel with burrata — fresh dairy against house-pick single-barrel. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Burrata|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel with burrata — fresh dairy against single-barrel Heaven Hill. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Burrata|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with burrata — fresh dairy against small-batch. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Burrata|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against HH rye. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — cherry, violet, clove, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the violet plays against basil-tomato. Works as step-up Pinot for burrata — structured Willamette red; whites and rosés peak higher.",
  "Burrata|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Oregon Pinot Blanc — pear and apple, clean acid. Fresh fruit against the creamy burrata and whatever seasonal fruit component the kitchen adds today. Reliable mid-tier white that always works with fresh cheese and fruit preparations. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Burrata|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee with burrata — fresh dairy against single-barrel. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 with burrata — Victoria BC color-changing gin (butterfly pea flower + rose + 8 botanicals). Excellent; Empress's rose-and-floral profile is the featured Smooth/Citrus Craft Gin-burrata pairing — rose-with-cream is classically beautiful, and the indigo-to-pink color change makes this a visual conversation-starter at the table.",
  "Burrata|Epoch Ingenuity": "Never pair Epoch with burrata — smoked meat, olive, and crushed pepper clash with fresh cream and the full body buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Burrata|Espresso Martini": "Tito's, espresso, Grind liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Coffee-forward cocktail crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile; espresso has no hook into basil-tomato-cheese. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco, French 75, or the light gin cocktails.",
  "Burrata|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Dessert cocktail weight crushes the burrata's creamy-delicate profile; coffee has no hook into basil-tomato. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco or a crisp white opener, not a coffee cocktail.",
  "Burrata|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- red structure on burrata curdles the cream's clean delicacy. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Evening Land Seven Springs lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Burrata|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Far Niente Oakville Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — opulent chocolate-espresso body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackcurrant-violet. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Far Niente for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Far Niente — Oakville Chardonnay with buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush texture. The opulent oak is heavier than burrata needs — the lush weight threatens to crush the delicate cheese, though the hazelnut does play against pistachio. Strong with restraint — lighter Chards peak higher.",
  "Burrata|Faroe Island Salmon": "The salmon and the burrata across courses -- the oil-rich fish anchors against the heirloom burrata plate. Strong; the opener carries its register before the main.",
  "Burrata|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Never pair Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi with burrata — Maremma firm-tannin blackcurrant obliterates basil-tomato-fresh-cheese delicacy and milky texture. Lead with Il Colle Prosecco, Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc. Saffredi is a Tomahawk wine.",
  "Burrata|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Brooding Napa Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm-tannin tobacco-chocolate crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackcurrant-blueberry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Faust for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid. The high acid cuts fresh cream and the red-cherry bridges tomato-basil brightness. Works for burrata — a Rosé or Pinot Grigio handles the dairy more cleanly, but Mencía's lightness keeps it fresh.",
  "Burrata|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca with burrata — 27-herb bitter against fresh dairy. Avoid; Branca is too aggressive for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta with burrata — Fernet against fresh dairy. Avoid; even the softer mint sibling is wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon after the burrata -- fresh basil-tomato-cream opens the meal, the 10oz lean cut following with restrained beef butter that pairs to the cream's milky note. Strong; refined opener into refined cut.",
  "Burrata|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The silky tannin meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the cherry plays against basil-tomato. Works as by-the-glass Pinot for burrata — accessible red for the delicate starter; whites and rosés peak higher.",
  "Burrata|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with burrata — fresh dairy against 104-proof premium. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label with burrata — fresh dairy against entry bourbon. Avoid; register mismatch.",
  "Burrata|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with burrata — fresh dairy against craft small-batch. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Burrata|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Fox & Oden Double Oaked with burrata — Fox & Oden Double Oaked against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register doubles down on the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur and fresh dairy compound the richness without contrast — heavy too early. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Frangelico on the steak course instead.",
  "Burrata|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Rutherford Bosché Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm-tannin graphite-tobacco structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Freemark Abbey Bosché for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|French 75": "Inverroche gin shaken with lemon and vanilla syrup, topped with Prosecco. Bubbles, vanilla, and lemon against the burrata's cream and the seasonal fruit on the plate. Refined cocktail route for a light starter. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Burrata|French Onion": "The burrata alongside the French onion -- both bring cheese as the headline. Avoid; one is enough. Pair the burrata with Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc; pair the French onion with Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée.",
  "Burrata|Frias Block 5": "Spring Mountain Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm iron-herbal body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackberry-plum. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "Classic Piedmont Barolo against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Vajra's firm-tannin tar-rose structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Vajra for aged beef courses.",
  "Burrata|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Piedmont sweet sparkling — peach, honey, floral, low alcohol. The sweetness and fruit can work with the seasonal fruit on the plate, but it's dessert-adjacent. Position carefully — best for a table sharing the burrata after cocktails. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Burrata|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco with burrata — 80-proof craft blanco against fresh mozzarella. The agave-forward Camarena character lifts the burrata with craft distinctiveness. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Burrata|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof's rested-agave body sits alongside the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the aged-agave weight wraps the burrata, and the caramel-agave register brightens the starter. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Burrata|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness crowds out the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet-oak register curdles next to fresh dairy without acid to balance. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Garavogue Irish Whiskey for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|George T. Stagg": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bourbon weight buries the milky cheese plate's delicate frame. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the George T. Stagg for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Oakville Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm graphite-oak body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackcurrant-cherry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Ghost Block for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Never pair Ghost Block with burrata — jammy fruit, cracked pepper, and full body clash with fresh cream and the preserves bury tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Burrata|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12 with burrata — entry Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14 with burrata — new-charred-oak Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18 with burrata — sherry-cask Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23 with burrata — 23yr champagne-cask Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata — save the Gran Cru bottle.",
  "Burrata|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12 with burrata — recently-reopened Sandend Highland against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12 with burrata — softer Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10 with burrata — Highland bourbon-cask against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18 with burrata — aged bourbon + Oloroso Highland against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Graham's 10 Year Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Burrata|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Graham's 20 Year Tawny lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Burrata|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Graham's 2017 Vintage Port's structured vintage port with dark-fruit weight doubles down on the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet wine and fresh dairy compound the richness with no foil. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Graham's 2017 Vintage Port for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum with burrata — premium Patron against fresh dairy. The smooth triple-distilled agave lifts the burrata with refined luxury. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Burrata|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur and fresh dairy compound the richness without contrast — heavy too early. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Grand Marnier belongs on the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale with burrata — California 6-botanical craft (Sonoma mint + Temecula limes + more) against fresh dairy. Excellent; Gray Whale's mint-and-citrus California terroir brightens cream at ocean-conservation register.",
  "Burrata|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Green Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose with burrata — soft wheat vodka against fresh dairy. Excellent; the silky texture meets cream cleanly, luxury-register pairing the other vodkas can't quite touch on softness.",
  "Burrata|Grilled Caesar": "The burrata alongside the Caesar -- both bring cheese as the headline. Avoid; one is enough. Pair the burrata with Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc; pair the Caesar with Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo.",
  "Burrata|Gumbo": "The burrata with the gumbo -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy meets the gumbo's spiced creole stew: spice plays against the delicate, the contrast holds. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Burrata|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Savory Sonoma Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm-tannin cedar-herb crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackberry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Gundlach Bundschu for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 with burrata — higher-proof craft vodka against fresh dairy. Strong; HDW 100's backbone cuts cream rather than riding it; Chopin-style creamy match it is not, but the proof gives it distinctive character.",
  "Burrata|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 with burrata — lightly-peated Japanese single malt against fresh dairy. Works; Hakushu's crisp-green and subtle peat can sit with burrata at edge-of-category register; not featured but distinctive.",
  "Burrata|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel with burrata — fresh dairy against BT single-barrel. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Burrata|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 with burrata — California craft blended vodka against fresh dairy. Excellent; Hangar 1's grape-component floral notes complement cream with distinctive craft character — parallel to but different from Nolet's rose-peach approach in the gin category.",
  "Burrata|Head Fake": "Wheatley vodka, limoncello, triple sec, lemon, simple syrup — sugar rim. The limoncello's Italian-citrus rounds the cocktail and plays against burrata's creamy-basil delicacy; lemon cuts without crushing. Peak bright cocktail for burrata — citrus-on-cream plus sugar-rim elegance.",
  "Burrata|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with burrata — HH 20yr collector against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with burrata — fresh dairy against 100-proof BIB. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Burrata|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with burrata — fresh dairy against 10-year premium. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Burrata|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "Iconic Napa Cab against fresh burrata — Martha's Vineyard's tannin and mint-cedar intensity annihilates the creamy-fresh dairy starter. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Champagne, or crisp Italian whites; save Heitz for the beef course.",
  "Burrata|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "Rutherford Heitz Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — refined mint-cedar body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with dark cherry-blackcurrant. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Heitz Trailside for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Flora Adora with burrata — Hendrick's Spring release, \"lavish floral garden\" profile (honeysuckle, chamomile, primrose, elderflower, hibiscus, meadowsweet). Excellent; a confident headline pairing.",
  "Burrata|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks with burrata — flagship Hendrick's Scottish gin, cucumber-rose signature, low-juniper perfumed profile. Excellent; Hendrick's rose-cucumber signature complements cream at the crowd-pleasing register — the default Hendrick's-burrata pairing, where Flora Adora floralizes harder and Grand Cabaret brings fruit.",
  "Burrata|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Grand Cabaret with burrata — Hendrick's cocktail-culture release featuring black currant + rhubarb, absinthe-style stage-show inspired. Excellent; the pairing earns its featured slot.",
  "Burrata|Hendricks Neptunia": "Neptunia with burrata — coastal-release Hendrick's against fresh dairy. Excellent; Neptunia's briny-coastal profile complements cream with distinctive maritime character.",
  "Burrata|Hendricks Orbium": "Orbium with burrata — bitter-tonic Hendrick's against fresh dairy. Excellent; the pairing earns its featured slot.",
  "Burrata|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy with burrata — cognac against fresh dairy. Avoid; cognac is too heavy for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB with burrata — fresh dairy against 10-year BIB. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Harmony with burrata — refined Japanese blend against fresh dairy. Works; Japanese whisky's delicate category + Hibiki's refinement make it whisky-curious-burrata-possible at premium register.",
  "Burrata|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Burrata alongside the honey carrots -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy meets the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: delicate gives way to sweet, the close lands light. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Burrata|House Wedge": "The burrata alongside the wedge -- both bring cheese as the headline. Avoid; one is enough. Pair the burrata with Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc; pair the wedge with St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé.",
  "Burrata|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 with burrata — 15yr IW Harper against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Glera from Veneto — bright Prosecco with white peach, green apple, and crisp refreshing bubbles carrying a clean citrus finish. The light floral profile meets burrata's creamy-delicate center without crushing basil-tomato, and the bubbles refresh between bites. Peak approachable opener for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Inhibited": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. Dessert cocktail crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile; chocolate has no hook into basil-tomato. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco, French 75, or light gin cocktails.",
  "Burrata|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche with burrata — South African gin built on fynbos (Western Cape native flora), distinctive floral-herbal terroir. Excellent; Inverroche's fynbos-floral profile is the featured craft-gin-burrata pairing in the cluster — the Cape-floral character meets cream with a register no other gin on the menu can touch.",
  "Burrata|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish with burrata — Iron Fish Distillery, Thompsonville (Benzie County) northern Michigan, farm-to-bottle with estate-grown botanicals and Michigan honey. Excellent; Iron Fish's farm-forward character frames burrata with northern-Michigan-pride distinction — the farm-to-bottle gin pairing no other Local MI gin replicates.",
  "Burrata|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon Mezcal Casks with burrata — Iron Fish MI mezcal-cask against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish with burrata — A. Smith Bowman port-finish against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "Anderson Valley Pinot — dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, refined tannin. The medium tannin meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the mocha plays against basil-tomato. Works as Anderson Valley Pinot for burrata — concentrated Mendocino; whites and rosés peak higher for the delicate starter.",
  "Burrata|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "Diamond Mountain Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm graphite-mineral body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with volcanic dark fruit. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep J. Davies for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year with burrata — aged Tennessee against fresh dairy. Avoid; Tennessee weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded with burrata — 100-proof Tennessee against fresh dairy. Avoid; Tennessee weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7 with burrata — iconic Tennessee against fresh dairy. Avoid; Tennessee weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "JD Sinatra Select with burrata — Sinatra-tribute Tennessee against fresh dairy. Avoid; Tennessee weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness crowds out the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Jameson Irish Whiskey for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, smooth, low tannin. The soft tannin meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the pomegranate plays against basil-tomato. Works as food-friendly Pinot for burrata — reliable red; whites and rosés peak higher.",
  "Burrata|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Grossot Chablis — unoaked Burgundy Chard, flint, chalk, lemon, green apple. The lean acidity cuts burrata's cream but the mineral-heavy profile can feel austere for the delicate Italian salad. Works as precision pairing; Sancerre, St Supéry, or Elk Cove peak higher for burrata's basil-tomato register.",
  "Burrata|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with burrata — limited-edition ocean-finish against fresh dairy. Avoid; save the collector bottle.",
  "Burrata|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with burrata — sea-aged Jefferson's against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve with burrata — fresh dairy against blended bourbon. Avoid; register mismatch.",
  "Burrata|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with burrata — fresh dairy against Twin Oak depth. Avoid; save the pour.",
  "Burrata|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label with burrata — fresh dairy against aged Beam. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label with burrata — fresh dairy against entry bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Burrata|Johnnie Walker Black": "JW Black with burrata — iconic blended JW against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Johnnie Walker Blue": "Johnnie Walker Blue's Highland-scotch character fights the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Johnnie Walker Blue on the steak course instead.",
  "Burrata|Johnnie Walker Red": "JW Red with burrata — 80-proof smoky JW against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance fights the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- tannin and fresh dairy curdle texture-wise — the milky plate reads sour. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre — Loire Sauv Blanc, grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension. The fresh-herb profile plays directly against burrata's basil and the chalk minerality cuts through the cream without crushing. Strong benchmark Sauv Blanc for burrata — herb-on-herb match done precisely.",
  "Burrata|Jubilation by Colgin": "Cult Napa Cab blend against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Jubilation's firm-tannin Pritchard-Hill structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Jubilation for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Jung and Wulff Guyana belongs on the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Jung and Wulff Trinidad on the steak course instead.",
  "Burrata|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Kahlua on the steak course instead.",
  "Burrata|Kansas City": "Kansas City after the burrata -- fresh basil-tomato-cream opens with milky delicacy, the 18oz lean-bold strip following with clean savory grain that the cream cleaned the palate for. Strong; the dairy opener lifts the strip's middle weight.",
  "Burrata|Keenan Chardonnay": "Keenan Chardonnay — Spring Mountain, green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak. The bright acid cuts burrata's cream and the subtle oak meets the delicate cheese without crushing; lemon curd plays against basil-tomato. Strong balanced Chardonnay for burrata — mountain structure without overpowering the starter.",
  "Burrata|Keenan Mernet": "Spring Mountain Napa Mernet against burrata's creamy-delicate center — mountain-grown Merlot-Cab structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Keenan Mernet for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against allocated KO. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with burrata — fresh dairy against KO Confiscated. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness crowds out the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet-oak register curdles next to fresh dairy without acid to balance. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Never pair Kermit Lynch Rhône with burrata — garrigue and pepper clash with fresh cream and the wild herbs fight tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Burrata|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One with burrata — citrus-clean Dutch wheat against fresh dairy. Excellent; Ketel's finish adds lift to the cream where softer vodkas just sit.",
  "Burrata|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker with burrata — New Holland Artisan Spirits (Holland, MI), hop-influenced gin with brewer's-heritage character. Excellent; Knickerbocker's hop-botanical profile complements burrata with a register no other Local MI Gin touches — brewer's heritage meets cream.",
  "Burrata|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek with burrata — fresh dairy against high-rye 100 proof. Avoid; register mismatch, hard.",
  "Burrata|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year with burrata — fresh dairy against aged Beam. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 with burrata — 15yr Beam single-barrel against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 with burrata — 18yr aged Beam KC against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against blended 100-proof. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Burrata|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against 100-proof bold rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Burrata|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva's aged-agave character overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Komos Anejo Reserva belongs on the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Komos Crystallino Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Añejo with burrata — fresh dairy against aged Komos. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the rested-agave depth sits with the burrata, and the caramel-agave register brightens the starter. Works; clean and composed pairing.",
  "Burrata|Lagavulin 8": "Lagavulin 8 with burrata — fresh dairy delicacy against peat is a hard mismatch. Even Lagavulin's balanced profile overpowers burrata's cream and basil oil. Avoid across all Islays; reach for gin or a light white wine.",
  "Burrata|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Historically-pedigreed Napa Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Blueprint's full-body cedar-blackcurrant structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Blueprint for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Historically-significant Napa Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Daniel Cuvée's firm-tannin full-body structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Daniel Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver with burrata — family-heritage craft blanco against fresh dairy. The clean additive-free agave lifts the burrata with Lalo González craft distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Burrata|Laphroaig 10": "Laphroaig 10 with burrata — fresh dairy cream and basil oil get annihilated by Laphroaig's tar and band-aid TCP. Hard avoid; don't let a guest try this. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch with burrata — fresh dairy against wheated caramel. Avoid; register mismatch.",
  "Burrata|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Pinot Noir Champagne rosé — silky mousse, red berry aromas, long elegant finish. The strawberry-raspberry cuts burrata's cream and plays cleanly against basil-tomato-pistachio. Peak rosé Champagne for burrata — the berry-fruit framework meets the salad's delicate build beautifully. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Burrata|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne — green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid structure cuts burrata's cream and plays cleanly against basil-tomato; brioche echoes pistachio crunch. Peak precise Champagne for burrata — classical starter pour at benchmark quality. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Burrata|Le Garenne Rosé": "Provence rosé — strawberry, citrus, clean. Rosé with cheese-and-fruit is textbook; the strawberry note echoes whatever seasonal fruit the kitchen has on today, and the acid cuts the burrata cream. Peak pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Burrata|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Italy's-most-prestigious Brunello against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Le Ragnaie's firm-tannin tobacco-leather weight overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Le Ragnaie for aged beef courses.",
  "Burrata|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Tuscan Merlot-driven blend against fresh burrata — Le Volte's structure and dark-fruit weight crush the creamy-fresh dairy starter. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Champagne, or crisp Italian whites; save Le Volte for heavier Italian-leaning courses.",
  "Burrata|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. Plymouth's softer juniper profile meets burrata's creamy-basil delicacy and the honey-lavender adds floral complexity; the Prosecco float lifts the finish. Peak floral-martini pairing for burrata — both elegant and aromatic.",
  "Burrata|Les Pallières Raciné": "Never pair Raciné with burrata — garrigue, iron, and earthy mineral clash with fresh cream and the old-vine depth buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Burrata|Liberator Gin": "Liberator with burrata — Valentine Distilling Ferndale MI craft against fresh dairy. Excellent; Liberator's smooth approachable profile complements cream at hometown register.",
  "Burrata|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur and fresh dairy compound the richness without contrast — heavy too early. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Licor 43 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Limoncello lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Burrata|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, low tannin. Red wine with burrata is unusual but the low-tannin profile doesn't fight the creamy cheese or the delicate fruit. Good pivot for committed Pinot drinkers. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Burrata|Little Book": "Little Book with burrata — Jim Beam Little Book against fresh dairy. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates burrata.",
  "Burrata|Loaded Potato": "Burrata alongside the loaded potato -- restrained yields to cream, the table arcs cleanly. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "Burrata|Lobster Mac": "The burrata and the lobster mac -- cheese-on-cheese, the meal goes redundant. Avoid; the table reads heavy without payoff. Suggest Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc for the burrata, or Cowboy Ribeye, Far Niente Chardonnay, or The Tomahawk for the lobster mac.",
  "Burrata|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year with burrata — 20yr varied-still Highland against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against premium sourced rye. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Long Road Gin": "Long Road with burrata — Long Road Distillers, Grand Rapids, ultra-local with Michigan botanicals and estate pride. Excellent; Long Road is Grand Rapids-proud gin pairing — parallel to Eastern Kille as the GR-craft tandem, complementing burrata with hometown register.",
  "Burrata|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- smoke and fresh dairy refuse each other — texture reads broken. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a mezcal. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- a luxury cognac is wrong moment for the opener — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an icon cognac. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Louis XIII Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Burrata|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano with burrata — Basilicata amaro against fresh dairy. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Rustic Abruzzo Montepulciano against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Lunaria's earthy grip overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Lunaria for the meat course where rustic grip earns the bottle.",
  "Burrata|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry with burrata — benchmark sherried Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18 with burrata — premium aged sherried Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate with burrata — Easter Elchies Estate-oak Macallan against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Never pair Macauley with burrata — inky color, dense tannin, and black pepper clash with fresh cream and the full body buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Burrata|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- red structure on burrata curdles the cream's clean delicacy. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Macauley Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Atlas Peak Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — powerful iron-espresso body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackberry-blueberry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Macauley Stagecoach for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Mahon Gin": "Mahón with burrata — PDO juniper-heavy Spanish gin against fresh dairy. Strong; Mahón's sharp juniper contrasts cream more than the complex-layered siblings; Inverroche's fynbos-floral is the featured craft-gin-burrata match.",
  "Burrata|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark with burrata — fresh dairy against wheated caramel. Avoid; register mismatch.",
  "Burrata|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with burrata — fresh dairy and cellar-aged depth don't bridge. Avoid; the cream can't accommodate the pour.",
  "Burrata|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's unaged unaged-cane body leans against the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the unaged body brightens the burrata, and the cane register underlines the starter. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Burrata|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against extended-aged Michigan. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Loire Cab Franc — Chinon light body, raspberry, red cherry, bell pepper, forest floor, supple low tannin, bright high acid. The bright acid handles burrata's creamy-delicate center and the red cherry plays against the fresh cheese. Strong Loire CF for burrata — unusual red for cream-forward starter, food-wine acid reframes the dish.",
  "Burrata|Margarita": "Corazon Blanco, lemon, lime, orange, Cointreau. The triple-citrus lift cuts burrata's cream and the blanco's agave-forward character adds dimension against basil-tomato; Cointreau softens the finish. Peak starter cocktail for burrata — classic citrus structure against soft cheese.",
  "Burrata|Marimar Estate Christina": "Russian River Green Valley Pinot — ripe strawberry, cherry compote, warm spice, silky, low tannin. The silky structure meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the strawberry plays against basil-tomato. Works as plush Pinot for burrata — Russian River opulence; whites and rosés peak higher.",
  "Burrata|Market Fish": "The market fish and the burrata across courses -- the simply-prepared fish threads the basil-tomato burrata. Strong; the opener carries its register before the main.",
  "Burrata|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe's bitter-herb aperitivo edge fights the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bitter herbs and fresh dairy refuse each other — the milky plate reads sour back. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Mata Hari Absinthe for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Bourbon with burrata — Michter's single-barrel 10yr against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against aged Michter's. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against smooth rye. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco with burrata — Maestra-Tequilera craft against fresh mozzarella. The clean additive-free agave lifts the burrata with premium craft integrity. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Burrata|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo's aged-agave character fights the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- agave and fresh dairy refuse each other texturally. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Milagro Anejo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado's aged-agave character reads alongside the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the burrata, and the cooked-agave note underlines the starter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Burrata|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam with burrata — Sazerac blended tribute against fresh dairy. Avoid; whiskey weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Mocha Creme": "The burrata and the mocha creme -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy bookends with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Burrata|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 with burrata — lingonberry-featuring 47-botanical German gin against fresh dairy. Excellent; Monkey 47's lingonberry-and-forest-herbal complements cream with distinctive Schwarzwald character — tied with Inverroche's fynbos-floral as the featured craft-gin-burrata match.",
  "Burrata|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder with burrata — cocktail-friendly Speyside-malt blend against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro with burrata — amaro against fresh dairy. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|Mount Gay Rum": "Burrata: fresh dairy meets Mount Gay Rum. Excellent; the subtle-molasses character adds quiet warmth to burrata's cream at Barbados-tradition register.",
  "Burrata|Muga Reserva": "Never pair Muga with burrata — oak-vanilla and leather clash with fresh cream and the savory-earthy finish buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Burrata|Mushroom Bisque": "The burrata with the mushroom bisque -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy meets the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the lighter plate hands off to the savory, the table arcs clean. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Burrata|Mushrooms": "Burrata alongside the mushrooms -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy meets the mushrooms' earthy umami weight: the lighter plate hands off to the savory, the table arcs clean. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Burrata|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Myers's Rum belongs on the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Negroni": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — stirred. The bitter-orange crushes burrata's creamy-delicate build and the cocktail's structure has no match for basil-tomato-cheese delicacy. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco, French 75, or light gin cocktails.",
  "Burrata|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against Yorkshire-matured rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Burrata|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch with burrata — fresh dairy against novelty bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with burrata — MI beer-barrel against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|New York Sour": "Jameson, lemon, simple, egg white, Daou Cab float — off menu. The Cab float's tannin crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile and the egg-white foam doesn't bridge the cheese-basil axis. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco or a crisp white, not a red-wine-floated sour.",
  "Burrata|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Prestige Oakville Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — concentrated graphite-cassis body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with deep blackberry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Nickel & Nickel for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolet's with burrata — Dutch Nolet distillery (Ketel One family), Turkish rose + peach + raspberry botanicals, floral-contemporary. Excellent; Nolet's rose-peach-raspberry profile is the distinctive gin-burrata pairing in the batch — where Plymouth softens and Sapphire is floral-softer, Nolet's is overtly fruit-forward, reading almost like a floral companion to the cream.",
  "Burrata|Not a Paper Plane": "Bulleit bourbon, Nonino amaro, Aperol, Angostura. Spirit-forward amaro-bourbon cocktail crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile; the cocktail has no hook into basil-tomato-cheese. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco, French 75, or light gin cocktails.",
  "Burrata|Oban 14": "Oban 14 with burrata — West Highland against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Oban 18": "Oban 18 with burrata — 18yr aged coastal Highland against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Ocho Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Refined Stags Leap Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — velvet-glove sage-cedar body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with dark fruit. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Odette for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with burrata — Fort Collins craft cigar-paired against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer with burrata — emmer-wheat wheated bourbon against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year with burrata — fresh dairy against wheated BIB. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year with burrata — fresh dairy against aged wheated BIB. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with burrata — 16yr OGD against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Opus One": "Collector Napa Cab blend against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Opus One's firm-tannin Bordeaux-blend structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Opus One for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Willamette Trappist Chardonnay — rich pear, butterscotch, toasted oak. The rich oak weight is heavier than burrata needs — the opulence threatens to overpower the delicate cheese. Strong with restraint; Elk Cove Pinot Blanc or Sancerre peak higher for burrata's light basil-tomato register.",
  "Burrata|Pablo Sour": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla syrup, egg white. The foam-and-vanilla profile meets burrata's creamy-basil delicacy and lemon cuts through without crushing; reposado adds structure. Strong starter pairing — both dishes share silky, elegant texture as their defining trait.",
  "Burrata|Paloma": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The bright grapefruit cuts burrata's cream and vanilla adds aromatic softness against basil-tomato; reposado's light oak keeps it from reading thin. Peak starter-tier agave cocktail for burrata — clean on both sides of the pairing.",
  "Burrata|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against Van Winkle Family Reserve. Avoid; unicorn rye deserves better.",
  "Burrata|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage 16th Ed with burrata — HH annual cask-strength against fresh dairy. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates burrata.",
  "Burrata|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's aged-agave character fights the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- agave and fresh dairy refuse each other texturally. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Patron Anejo on the steak course instead.",
  "Burrata|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver with burrata — Patron's clean profile against fresh dairy. The citrus lifts the cream cleanly; an accessible everyday blanco-burrata pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Burrata|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Patron XO Cafe belongs on the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Paul Bara Grand Cru rosé Champagne — structured Pinot Noir with ripe raspberry, red currant, and minerally finish. The red-fruit weight and fine bubbles cut burrata's cream and play cleanly against basil-tomato-pistachio. Strong grower-rosé for burrata — more depth than typical sparkling pours.",
  "Burrata|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Burrata and the PB brownie -- delicate gives way to sweet, the close lands light. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Burrata|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Elegant Rutherford Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — refined dusty-cocoa body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with dark cherry-cassis. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Peju for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand with burrata — artisanal cognac against fresh dairy. Avoid; cognac weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club — 2015 grower Champagne. Citrus zest, toasted brioche, white flowers, creamy texture. The vintage brioche and cream meet burrata's creamy-delicate center and the Côte des Blancs minerality plays against basil-tomato. Strong prestige Champagne pour for burrata — elegance meets cream.",
  "Burrata|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against 110-proof bold rye. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Pineau des Charentes": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The honey bridges fresh cream density and the white grape lifts tomato-basil brightness. Peak Pineau for burrata — aperitif-mode classic bridge on the caprese composition. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Burrata|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Approachable-Barolo-sibling Barbaresco against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Pio Cesare's firm-tannin tar weight overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Pio Cesare for aged beef where Barbaresco elegance earns the bottle.",
  "Burrata|Plymouth Gin": "Burrata: fresh dairy meets Plymouth's protected-origin style, softer and earthier than London Dry proper. Plymouth's sweet notes and softer juniper meet burrata's cream where Tanq would cut it and Bombay would contrast it — textural harmony, the gin answer for burrata. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Burrata|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Serious-Barolo Aldo Conterno against burrata's creamy-delicate center — decade-built firm tannin and tar weight overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Aldo Conterno for the grand-cut steak course.",
  "Burrata|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "2004 Pommery Cuvée Louise — 20-year Reims vintage. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich. The aged complexity cuts burrata's cream and the almond-honey plays against basil-tomato-pistachio. Strong occasion Champagne for burrata — elevated pairing when the meal is a celebration.",
  "Burrata|Porterhouse": "The porterhouse after the burrata -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy sets up the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: restrained yields to weighty, the meal arcs from light to full. Works; the starter composes before the cut.",
  "Burrata|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Polished Napa Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — lush chocolate-vanilla crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with ripe blackberry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Quilt for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|RAEN Royal St Robert": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, crushed rock, silky. The silky structure meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the crushed-rock plays against basil-tomato. Works as Burgundian California Pinot for burrata — elegant red; whites and rosés peak higher.",
  "Burrata|Rainbow Trout": "Rainbow Trout preceded by the burrata -- the herb-finished trout anchors against the fresh-cream-and-tomato opener. Strong; reliable opener for the main.",
  "Burrata|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Rosé Cava — red-fruit character, sparkling acid. The color and fruit character echo the seasonal fruit garnish, and the bubbles cut the burrata cream. Non-obvious sparkling route with real logic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Burrata|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character crowds out the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Redbreast 12 Year for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish crowds out the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Redbreast 21 Year for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Gatsby with burrata — George Remus tribute against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Rémy VSOP with burrata — elegant cognac against fresh dairy. Avoid; cognac weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo with burrata — fresh dairy against Moorish-inspired aged. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka with burrata — Icelandic glacial vodka against fresh dairy. Strong; Reyka's mineral cuts cream rather than matching it — cleaner than Chopin's texture-match approach.",
  "Burrata|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique with burrata — fresh dairy against boutique retailer-label. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Roast Half Chicken": "The chicken after the burrata -- the herbed crisp skin sits cleanly with the basil-tomato burrata. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Burrata|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The burrata preceded by the chickpea soup -- the delicate course meets the spiced, the table reads bright across the meal. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "Burrata|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs — Le Mesnil Grand Cru blanc de blancs with chalky minerality, lemon curd, green apple, no dosage. The austere acidity cuts burrata's cream and the chalk plays against basil-tomato-pistachio cleanly. Peak sommelier Champagne for burrata — technical elegance. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Burrata|Roku Gin": "Roku with burrata — 6-Japanese-botanical gin (sakura flower + leaf) against fresh dairy. Excellent; one of the strong calls on the list.",
  "Burrata|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey premium-sipping rum overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Ron Zacapa Rum for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with burrata — fresh dairy against aged bourbon. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Salt-Cured Halibut": "The halibut after the burrata -- the butter-laced halibut carries into the heirloom burrata plate. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Burrata|Sambuca": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Sambuca belongs on the steak course.",
  "Burrata|San Simeon Stormwatch": "Paso Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend against burrata's creamy-delicate center — full-body Bordeaux-style structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sta. Rita Hills Santa Barbara Pinot — rich, layered, dark cherry, earth, dried herbs, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the dried-herbs play against basil-tomato. Works as legendary Pinot for burrata — Santa Barbara depth; whites peak higher for the starter.",
  "Burrata|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Burrata and the garlic spinach -- bright and delicate share air, the meal carries lift without weight. Works; both items hold their register, no clash.",
  "Burrata|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes — Sémillon-Sauvignon Blanc with noble rot (botrytis cinerea), apricot, honey, orange marmalade, luscious unctuous, bright acid. The honey bridges fresh cream and the bright acid cuts tomato-basil-sweetness cleanly. Strong Sauternes for burrata — Sauternes-on-cheese classic applied to caprese.",
  "Burrata|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. Anise-rye profile crushes burrata's creamy-delicate build; the cocktail has no hook into basil-tomato-cheese. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco or a crisp white opener, not the Sazerac.",
  "Burrata|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against rye's bright pepper. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna with burrata — NZ craft single malt against fresh dairy. Avoid; whisky weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Scavino Barolo": "King-of-Italian Barolo against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Scavino's firm-tannin tar-leather weight overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Scavino for aged beef where the prestige of the King-of-Italian earns the bottle.",
  "Burrata|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Rheingau Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The delicate off-dry fruit meets burrata's creamy-delicate center without crushing and the apricot-peach plays against basil-tomato-pistachio. Peak gateway Riesling for burrata — fruit-lift without the heaviness of Chardonnay. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Burrata|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "By-the-glass California Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — full-body cassis-chocolate structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Seared Scallops": "Burrata with the scallops -- the basil-finished burrata wraps the scallops. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Burrata|Seasonal Soup": "Burrata and the seasonal soup together -- a clean call, no friction either way. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Burrata|Seasonal Vegetables": "Burrata alongside the seasonal vegetables -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy meets the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: a quiet call, the meal carries without strain. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Burrata|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Never pair Sesti with burrata — firm tannin and high acid clash against fresh cream and the sandalwood earth buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Burrata|Shafer 1.5": "Stags Leap Shafer Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — 1.5's opulent blackberry-vanilla structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Shafer Hillside Select": "Stags Leap collector's Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Hillside Select's firm-tannin dense graphite structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Hillside Select for the steak course — this bottle deserves the main cut.",
  "Burrata|Shrimp Bisque": "The burrata with the shrimp bisque -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy meets the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the lighter course primes the richer, the meal carries register lift. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Burrata|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco with burrata — rustic traditional blanco against fresh mozzarella. The craft character gives the burrata more texture than polished blancos provide. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Burrata|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado's rested-agave body sits alongside the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the burrata, and the soft-spice note softens the starter. Works; the smoothness doesn't drive the pairing but the theatrics do.",
  "Burrata|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak American-oak Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — lush vanilla-dill crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with ripe cassis-plum. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Silver Oak for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Boutique Napa estate Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Estate Reserve's firm-tannin full-body structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Estate Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "Organic Napa Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Lyndenhurst's full-body plum-violet structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Lyndenhurst for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm-tannin full body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with cassis-mocha. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep St Supéry for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "Napa Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, citrus-forward. Bright acid handles the burrata's creaminess and doesn't fight the balsamic-and-fruit sweetness on the plate. California pivot for guests who want American producers over Loire when pairing with cheese. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Burrata|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "Napa Meritage against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm-tannin five-varietal structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Élu for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with burrata — BT cask-strength against fresh dairy. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates burrata.",
  "Burrata|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit with burrata — glacial-clean Russian wheat against fresh dairy. Excellent; Elit's purity lets the burrata speak — the crystalline approach vs Chopin's creamy-match approach.",
  "Burrata|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Stoneleigh — Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc, passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty. The zesty acid cuts burrata's cream and the grass-grapefruit plays directly against basil-tomato-pistachio — tropical meets Italian in a bright way. Peak accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for burrata — reliable by-the-glass pairing. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Burrata|Swordfish": "The swordfish and the burrata across courses -- the meaty steak meets the burrata. Strong; the opener carries its register before the main.",
  "Burrata|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 with burrata — whole-citrus-distilled premium gin against fresh dairy. Excellent; the chamomile notes meet cream softer than Bombay Dry's angular juniper while the citrus adds lift Plymouth's sweetness doesn't.",
  "Burrata|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray with burrata — high-proof juniper against fresh dairy. Excellent; Tanq's botanical spine cuts cream with precision; Plymouth's sweetness matches it softer but Tanq's contrast wins here.",
  "Burrata|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness doubles down on the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet wine and fresh dairy compound the richness with no foil. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Burrata|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with burrata — fresh dairy against triple-cask aged. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Burrata|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Polished Napa Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — smooth mocha body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with rich dark fruit. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Teeter Totter for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tuscan Chianti Classico against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Arceno's medium-tannin cherry-earth structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Arceno for red meat or Italian table pairings.",
  "Burrata|The BG": "Traveller whiskey, honey lavender syrup, whole grapefruit squeeze, black walnut bitters, ginger beer. The whiskey weight and black walnut earth crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco, French 75, or light gin cocktails, not a whiskey cocktail.",
  "Burrata|The Happy Wife": "Tito's, orange juice, ginger syrup. The orange brightness cuts burrata's cream and ginger adds subtle warmth against basil-tomato; Tito's clean base doesn't fight the delicacy. Strong approachable cocktail for burrata — simple pairing that works.",
  "Burrata|The Manhattan": "Maker's Mark private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Spirit-forward Manhattan weight crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile; bourbon has no hook into basil-tomato-cheese. Never pair — burrata begs for Prosecco or a crisp white, not a classic whiskey cocktail.",
  "Burrata|The Prisoner Red Blend": "Never pair Prisoner with burrata — layered dark fruit and full body clash with fresh cream and the lush finish buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Burrata|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the smoky bone-in weight crowds out the burrata, and the marbled char fights the milky cheese plate. Avoid; the courses can't share a meal cleanly. Pair the burrata with Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc on a different visit; pour Opus One, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or George T. Stagg alongside the Tomahawk.",
  "Burrata|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against BTAC barrel-proof. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with burrata — white-wine-finished bourbon against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata even with chardonnay finish.",
  "Burrata|Titos Vodka": "Tito's with burrata — slightly-sweet American vodka against fresh dairy. Excellent; round mouthfeel sits alongside the cream without friction.",
  "Burrata|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory with burrata — light-floral Japanese entry against fresh dairy. Works; Toki's delicate character is one of the few whiskies gentle enough to sit with burrata; not featured but distinctive.",
  "Burrata|Tomato Basil": "The burrata alongside the tomato basil -- both bring tomato as the headline. Avoid; one is enough. Pair the burrata with Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc; pair the tomato basil with Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé.",
  "Burrata|Transfusion": "Hangar 1, muddled black grapes, lime, ginger beer top. The grape's slight tannin adds complexity against burrata's cream and lime cuts through without crushing basil-tomato delicacy. Peak refreshing cocktail for burrata — fruit on the cocktail side meets fruit on the plate.",
  "Burrata|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the burrata, and the soft-spice note softens the starter. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Burrata|Truffle Fries": "The burrata and the truffle fries -- cheese-on-cheese, the meal goes redundant. Avoid; the table reads heavy without payoff. Suggest Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc for the burrata, or The Tomahawk, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka for the truffle fries.",
  "Burrata|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s gentle Irish blend clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bourbon weight buries the milky cheese plate's delicate frame. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Tullamore D.E.W. belongs on the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "The tuna and the burrata across courses -- the seared-rare yellowfin holds with the basil-tomato burrata. Strong; the opener carries its register before the main.",
  "Burrata|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Burrata and the curry together -- the fresh cream anchors against the chickpea curry plate, and the spice meets the herb butter. Strong; both openers earn their place on the meal.",
  "Burrata|Venge Family Reserve": "Oakville flagship Napa Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Family Reserve's full-body blackcurrant-chocolate weight overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Family Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Venge Scout's Honor": "Never pair Venge Scout's Honor with burrata — Napa Zin-led full-body fruit obliterates basil-tomato-fresh-cheese delicacy and generous alcohol flattens milky texture. Lead with Il Colle Prosecco, Paul Bara Grand Rosé, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc. Scout's Honor is a steak wine.",
  "Burrata|Venge Silencieux": "Winemaker's Napa Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — full-body Silencieux structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Silencieux for the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Vesper": "Nolet's, Ketel One, Lillet. The cocktail's elegant clean profile plays against burrata's creamy-basil delicacy and Nolet's rose-peach botanicals add aromatic layering. Strong martini pairing — cocktail's dryness balances the burrata's softness.",
  "Burrata|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Yellow label Veuve Clicquot — Reims Champagne, ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse. The creamy body cuts burrata's cream and the biscuit plays against basil-tomato; lively acidity lifts the cheese. Peak crowd-pleaser Champagne for burrata — celebration meets classic starter. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Burrata|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The cocktail's layered weight crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile; rye-cognac-Bénédictine has no hook into basil-tomato-cheese. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco or a crisp white, not the Vieux Carré.",
  "Burrata|Villon Cognac": "Villon with burrata — contemporary cognac against fresh dairy. Avoid; cognac weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Burrata|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet wine and fresh dairy compound the richness with no foil. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Vin Santo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Burrata|Weller 107": "Weller 107 with burrata — fresh dairy cannot carry 107-proof wheat. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year with burrata — twelve years of oak against fresh dairy and basil oil is a register mismatch. Avoid; the cream can't accommodate the pour's depth.",
  "Burrata|Weller Millennium": "Burrata: fresh dairy and basil oil meet Weller Millennium's aged oak. Avoid; don't pour the Millennium against something that can't carry it.",
  "Burrata|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve with burrata — fresh dairy against gateway wheated caramel. Avoid; the register mismatch is structural across all wheated bourbons.",
  "Burrata|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley with burrata — BT wheat vodka against fresh dairy. Excellent; Wheatley's cleanness supports burrata without Chopin's texture-match or Goose's silk — straightforward value.",
  "Burrata|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with burrata — fresh dairy against aged single-barrel rye. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with burrata — fresh dairy against fruit-cask 12-year. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Burrata|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with burrata — fresh dairy against extended-aged rye. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with burrata — fresh dairy against allocated barrel-proof WP. Avoid; ceremonial bottles deserve ceremonial pairings.",
  "Burrata|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with burrata — fresh dairy against 10th-edition allocated WP. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Burrata|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year with burrata — fresh dairy against Canadian-sourced rye. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with burrata — fresh dairy against craft high-rye. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with burrata — fresh dairy against fruit-forward bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Burrata|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with burrata — iconic KY Double Oaked against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Burrata|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against fruit-forward rye. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Burrata|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against Washington craft rye. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Burrata|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 with burrata — iconic Suntory flagship against fresh dairy. Works; Japanese whisky's delicate category + Yamazaki 12's refinement let it sit with burrata at flagship register; not featured but distinctive.",
  "Burrata|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 with burrata — aged flagship Japanese single malt against fresh dairy. Works; save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Burrata|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Butternut Squash|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon body sits with the squash bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; wine-cask complexity gives the dessert layered premium character.",
  "Butternut Squash|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon depth settles into the squash bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Butternut Squash|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's bold cherry-coffee cassis leans against the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, no friction.",
  "Butternut Squash|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals reads alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Butternut Squash|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the scotch depth settles into the squash bisque, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; BIB structure is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "Butternut Squash|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the malt-driven body wraps the squash bisque, and the oak threads the cream. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "Butternut Squash|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak-aged tequila body threads the squash bisque, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; single-estate character handles richness at craft register.",
  "Butternut Squash|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the barrel-rested body handles the squash bisque, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; multi-cask complexity composed with this dish at distinctive register.",
  "Butternut Squash|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The apple-pear mirrors butternut's autumn fruit character and the oak-vanilla plays against caramelized squash. Strong accessible Chardonnay for butternut squash — California oak on the classic.",
  "Butternut Squash|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Butternut Squash|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin holds with the squash soup's silky sweet — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Butternut Squash|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Amaro Nonino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Butternut Squash|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Butternut Squash|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon depth settles into the squash bisque, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Butternut Squash|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the caramel-spice register matches the squash bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Butternut Squash|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon weight handles the squash bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; extended-aged home-state pour wasted on salad.",
  "Butternut Squash|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Butternut Squash|Asparagus": "Butternut Squash and the asparagus -- the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream meets the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: bright meets sweet, the contrast stays light. Works; no conflict however the table orders them.",
  "Butternut Squash|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The butternut squash and the au gratin on the same meal -- rich dairy yields to dessert weight, the close composes thickly. Works; sequence or share, the build holds.",
  "Butternut Squash|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Butternut Squash|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge fights the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- bitter herbs cut the bisque's round cream without lifting it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Averna Amaro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Butternut Squash|Aviation": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build leans against the squash soup's silky sweet — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Butternut Squash|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals sits alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Butternut Squash|Avion 44": "Avion 44's añejo-pour depth leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the añejo register carries the squash bisque, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Butternut Squash|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's green-agave-and-cane lift sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the squash bisque, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Butternut Squash|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the silver-rum body cuts the squash bisque, and the unaged-cane register underlines the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Butternut Squash|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Butternut Squash|Baker's": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the squash bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Butternut Squash|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon-cask vanilla settles into the squash bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; wine-cask over-specified.",
  "Butternut Squash|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the malt-and-oak threads the squash bisque, and the oak softens the cream. Works; craft-blend over-specified.",
  "Butternut Squash|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the scotch backbone matches the squash bisque, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a soup; save the rare bottle.",
  "Butternut Squash|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood's aged Port-finished Speyside reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the malt-driven body wraps the squash bisque, and the oak threads the cream. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Butternut Squash|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the caramel-spice register matches the squash bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; bonded weight slightly over-specified.",
  "Butternut Squash|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon weight handles the squash bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Butternut Squash|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon depth settles into the squash bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; milestone-collector wasted on salad.",
  "Butternut Squash|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare's rum-cask + cocoa is textbook — tropical-rum + chocolate sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon depth settles into the squash bisque, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; Old World complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Butternut Squash|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak depth lays under the squash bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the VR Twin Oak is over-specified for the soup's register.",
