  "Canadian Club|Loaded Potato": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the easygoing Canadian character handles the loaded potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; high proof over-specified for the soup.",
  "Canadian Club|Lobster Mac": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the lobster mac, and the oak threads the cream. Works; boutique character gives the dessert a craft frame.",
  "Canadian Club|Market Fish": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Canadian Club belongs on the steak course.",
  "Canadian Club|Mocha Creme": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the smooth Canadian register carries the mocha creme, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; AE Triple Cask's layered character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Canadian Club|Mushroom Bisque": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the mushroom bisque, and the oak threads the umami plate. Works; richer profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Canadian Club|Mushrooms": "Canadian Club with mushrooms — heritage Canadian against earthy umami. Strong; Canadian Club's grain-forward character threads mushrooms at heritage register.",
  "Canadian Club|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the PB brownie, and the light caramel edge frames the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Canadian Club|Porterhouse": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the smooth Canadian register carries the dual-cut steak, and the soft profile rounds the cut. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Canadian Club|Prime Tartare": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend reads alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the Canadian whisky weight matches the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Works; Remus 15 frames the two-cut plate at Gatsby-tribute register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Canadian Club|Rainbow Trout": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Canadian Club belongs on the steak course.",
  "Canadian Club|Roast Half Chicken": "Canadian Club with the chicken — traditional Canadian blend, 1858-founded Hiram Walker, grain-forward easy-drinking heritage brand. Excellent; Canadian Club's grain-forward heritage character frames chicken with traditional Canadian distinction — where Crown Royal is smooth-iconic, CC is grain-forward-heritage.",
  "Canadian Club|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the Canadian whisky weight matches the chickpea soup, and the gentle rye thread underlines the bowl. Works; complex profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Canadian Club|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Canadian Club for the steak course.",
  "Canadian Club|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the blended whisky body wraps the garlic spinach, and the light caramel edge frames the side. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Canadian Club|Seafood Tower": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Canadian Club for the steak course.",
  "Canadian Club|Seared Scallops": "Canadian Club with seared scallops — traditional Canadian against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Canadian Club's grain-forward profile is wrong for scallops.",
  "Canadian Club|Seasonal Soup": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the smooth Canadian register carries the seasonal soup, and the light-grain note brightens the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Canadian Club|Seasonal Vegetables": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the Canadian-blend body sits with the seasonal vegetables, and the gentle caramel rounds the side. Works; premium KO story wasted on salad.",
  "Canadian Club|Shrimp Bisque": "Canadian Club with shrimp bisque — heritage Canadian against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Canadian Club's grain-forward character rides the bisque at traditional register.",
  "Canadian Club|Shrimp Cocktail": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend drowns out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Canadian Club on the steak course instead.",
  "Canadian Club|Swordfish": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Canadian Club lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Canadian Club|The Tomahawk": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the easygoing Canadian character handles the 36oz showpiece, and the light caramel edge frames the cut. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "Canadian Club|Tomato Basil": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the easygoing Canadian character handles the tomato basil, and the gentle rye thread underlines the bowl. Works; Fortuna handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Canadian Club|Truffle Fries": "Canadian Club with truffle fries — 1858 Hiram Walker Canadian against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Canadian Club's grain-forward character meets the earthy-rich at heritage register.",
  "Canadian Club|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Canadian Club lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Canadian Club|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the blended whisky body wraps the curry, and the easy Canadian register softens the bowl. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Captain Morgan Rum|Asparagus": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- light spirit needs the cut, not the supporting plate. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a light spirit. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Captain Morgan Rum for the steak course.",
  "Captain Morgan Rum|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Captain Morgan Rum's green-agave-and-cane lift finds neutral with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the unaged-cane register matches the au gratin, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Captain Morgan Rum|Beignets": "Captain Morgan Rum: Diageo's iconic Original Spiced rum, Caribbean-origin blend with warm-spice profile — vanilla, cinnamon, clove, molasses. Excellent on beignets; the warm-spice character plays directly into the fresh sugar-dust and pastry warmth at cocktail-workhorse register — spice meets sugar textbook.",
  "Captain Morgan Rum|Bone Marrow": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- this light spirit's register is wrong for the starter — try a different pour. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Captain Morgan Rum for the steak course.",
