  "Caribou Crossing|Chilean Seabass": "Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; the better calls are above. Caribou Crossing lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Chocolate Brownie": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the smooth Canadian register carries the brownie, and the light-grain note brightens the dessert. Works; doesn't drive.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Chocolate Cake": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the Canadian whisky weight matches the chocolate cake, and the gentle rye thread underlines the dessert. Works; the extra proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Clam Chowder": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the Canadian whisky weight matches the chowder, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; bonded weight pushes past the soup's register slightly.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Cowboy Ribeye": "Caribou Crossing with the cowboy ribeye — single-barrel Canadian against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Works; Caribou Crossing is lighter than ribeye demands; CC 43 Year handles it better.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Crab Cake": "Caribou Crossing with crab cake — Sazerac single-barrel Canadian against delicate lump crab. Works; Caribou Crossing's craft character sits with crab at single-barrel register; not featured.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Creamed Spinach": "Caribou Crossing with creamed spinach — single-barrel Canadian against dairy-rich side. Strong; Caribou Crossing's craft character rides the cream at single-barrel register.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Creamy Potato": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the smooth Canadian register carries the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; richer profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Creme Brulee": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the blended whisky body wraps the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Escargot": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the Canadian whisky weight matches the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Works; multi-distillery complexity doesn't drive; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Faroe Island Salmon": "Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Caribou Crossing on the steak course instead.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets Sazerac-owned single-barrel Canadian whisky (distinctive — most Canadian is blended), hand-selected character in smaller bottle format. Caribou Crossing's single-barrel distinction is the featured Canadian-filet pairing at craft register — where Crown Royal is iconic-smooth and CC is heritage-grain, Caribou Crossing is single-barrel-distinctive. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Caribou Crossing|French Onion": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the blended whisky body wraps the French onion, and the light caramel edge frames the bowl. Works; EHT Full Proof handles the richness at historic Kentucky register.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Grilled Caesar": "Caribou Crossing with the grilled Caesar — Sazerac-owned single-barrel Canadian against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Caribou Crossing's hand-selected character meets the char at craft register.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Gumbo": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the blended whisky body wraps the gumbo, and the easy Canadian register softens the bowl. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Canadian whisky weight matches the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; BIB slightly over-specified.",
  "Caribou Crossing|House Wedge": "Caribou Crossing with the House Wedge — distinctive single-barrel Canadian against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Caribou Crossing's craft character threads the wedge at single-barrel register.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Kansas City": "Caribou Crossing with the KC — single-barrel Canadian against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Caribou Crossing's hand-selected single-barrel character handles strip marbling at craft register.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Loaded Potato": "Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the Canadian whisky weight matches the loaded potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Boss Hog's allocation story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Lobster Mac": "Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the Canadian whisky weight matches the lobster mac, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Market Fish": "Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops flattens the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Caribou Crossing for the steak course.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Mocha Creme": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the mocha creme, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the pour's assertiveness is a bit much for the soup but doesn't clash.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Mushroom Bisque": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the smooth Canadian register carries the mushroom bisque, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Mushrooms": "Caribou Crossing with mushrooms — single-barrel Canadian against earthy umami. Excellent; Caribou Crossing's hand-selected character threads mushrooms at craft register.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the easygoing Canadian character handles the PB brownie, and the easy Canadian register softens the dessert. Works; HH 20 is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Porterhouse": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the dual-cut steak, and the gentle rye thread underlines the cut. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Prime Tartare": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character reads alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the blended whisky body wraps the tartare, and the oak balances the umami register. Works; aged premium slightly over-specified; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Rainbow Trout": "Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Caribou Crossing for the steak course.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Roast Half Chicken": "Caribou Crossing with the chicken — Sazerac-owned single-barrel Canadian against roasted poultry. Excellent; Caribou Crossing's distinctive craft character frames chicken at single-barrel register.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the chickpea soup, and the light-grain note brightens the bowl. Works; Portwood is over-specified for a side — save for main course.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Caribou Crossing on the steak course instead.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the Canadian whisky weight matches the garlic spinach, and the light-grain note brightens the side. Works; the complex character gives the dessert a refined frame.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Seafood Tower": "Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Caribou Crossing for the steak course.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Seared Scallops": "Caribou Crossing with seared scallops — Sazerac single-barrel Canadian against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Seasonal Soup": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the easygoing Canadian character handles the seasonal soup, and the soft profile rounds the bowl. Works; the richer pour finds more with the dessert than Original brings.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Seasonal Vegetables": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the seasonal vegetables, and the gentle rye thread underlines the side. Works; annual-release wasted on salad.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Shrimp Bisque": "Caribou Crossing with shrimp bisque — Sazerac-owned Canadian against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Caribou Crossing's hand-selected character rides the bisque at craft register.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Shrimp Cocktail": "Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops smothers the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Caribou Crossing for the steak course.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Swordfish": "Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Caribou Crossing for the steak course.",
  "Caribou Crossing|The Tomahawk": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the blended whisky body wraps the 36oz showpiece, and the light-grain note brightens the cut. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Tomato Basil": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the blended whisky body wraps the tomato basil, and the light caramel edge frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Truffle Fries": "Caribou Crossing with truffle fries — single-barrel Canadian against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Caribou Crossing's craft character meets the earthy-rich at single-barrel register.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Caribou Crossing for the steak course.",
  "Caribou Crossing|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the easygoing Canadian character handles the curry, and the easy Canadian register softens the bowl. Works; aged premium slightly over-specified.",
  "Carrot Cake|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with carrot cake — Barton's spicy-sweet against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; the rye spice and cake spice find each other.",
  "Carrot Cake|1792 Sweet Wheat": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet 1792's sweet-wheat. Bourbon's distinctive sweetness mirrors cake's raisins and frosting; standout sweet-wheat dessert. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Carrot Cake|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. 1881 Napa Valley lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Carrot Cake|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- juniper-citrus and warm cake spices pull in opposite directions. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the 2 James Gin for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with carrot cake — rare limited-release against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; boutique-craft finds cake spice with rare-release character.",
  "Carrot Cake|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with carrot cake — 16yr double-matured Speyside against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Aberlour 16's sherry-cask raisin-and-spice marries the cake naturally at balanced-premium register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- aged tequila has nothing to bridge into dessert. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Adictivo Cristalino for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the añejo register carries the carrot cake, and the soft-spice note softens the dessert. Works; benchmark extra añejo is slightly ambitious for the two-cut plate.",
  "Carrot Cake|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Alexander Valley Chardonnay on the steak course instead.",
  "Carrot Cake|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Alexander Valley Cyrus lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Carrot Cake|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Alexander Valley Homestead Red for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino with carrot cake — Friulian amaro against cream cheese and cinnamon. The grappa-based elegance and botanical notes find the cake's raisins cleanly. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Carrot Cake|Ancho Reyes": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Ancho Reyes. Works; chile-sweet-smoke amplifies cake spice with heat-dimension at Mexican register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with carrot cake — port-finished bourbon against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; AE's port-derived jammy-fruit is the featured wine-finished-carrot-cake pairing — the port-and-cake bridge works where straight-bourbon doesn't.",
  "Carrot Cake|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with carrot cake — 117+ proof port-finish against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; AE CS's cask-strength port-fruit threads the cake at premium wine-finish register; flagship AE still holds the featured carrot-cake position.",
  "Carrot Cake|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "AE Triple Cask with carrot cake — multi-cask-finished AE against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; AE Triple Cask's layered-cask character threads the cake with more complexity than flagship AE's port-only approach.",
  "Carrot Cake|Aperol": "Aperol with carrot cake — soft orange-rhubarb against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Aperol's orange marries the cinnamon at a brunch-register that works cleaner than the heavier amari.",
  "Carrot Cake|Asparagus": "The asparagus and the carrot cake -- the asparagus' green-vegetal edge hands off cleanly to the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: acid plays against sweetness, the close stays unburdened. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Carrot Cake|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The au gratin and the carrot cake -- the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust hands off cleanly to the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: cream-and-sugar build the close, the meal finishes generous. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Carrot Cake|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Averna Amaro": "Averna with carrot cake — Sicilian amaro against cream cheese and cinnamon. The caramel-and-orange threads with the cake's raisins in a classic post-dessert register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Carrot Cake|Aviation": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Aviation lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Carrot Cake|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Aviation Gin for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Avion 44": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet 44-month aged Avion. Strong; extended maturation meets the cake at premium Avion register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Avion Silver belongs on the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character sits alongside the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the cane-and-citrus body lifts the carrot cake, and the cane-and-citrus thread frames the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Carrot Cake|Baileys Irish Cream": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Baileys. Excellent; the vanilla-cocoa-cream profile amplifies the cream cheese frosting at classic-Baileys register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Baker's": "Baker's with carrot cake — 107-proof small-batch against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; bold intensity slightly over-specified.",
  "Carrot Cake|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with carrot cake — vanilla-forward bourbon-cask Speyside against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; the American Oak vanilla marries the cake's spice-and-raisins naturally — the featured Speyside-carrot-cake pairing over Fiddich's apple or Glenlivet's honey approach.",
  "Carrot Cake|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with carrot cake — Caribbean-rum-cask Speyside against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; the rum-cask-derived brown sugar and vanilla marry the cake's raisins and spice with tropical-finish distinction.",
  "Carrot Cake|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet hand-picked First Fill American Oak. Balvenie 16's deeper vanilla and American-Oak character marry the cake's spice-and-raisins with collector-register gravitas — where Balvenie 12 Am Oak opens the conversation, 16 finishes it. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood with carrot cake — 21yr port-finished Speyside against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; Portwood's port-richness threads the cake's spice at collector register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with carrot cake — French-wine-cask Bardstown against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Bardstown Château Laboude's French-wine-fruit threads the cake with contemporary-craft character.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with carrot cake — sourced-blend against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; craft-blender finds the cake spice with layered character.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with carrot cake — KBS coffee-chocolate against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; distinctive finish meets the cake with beer-barrel character.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bardstown Foursquare": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Bardstown rum-cask. Works; Bardstown Foursquare's tropical-rum-cask threads the cake at Caribbean-finish register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with carrot cake — own-plus-sourced against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; fusion character finds cake spice with layered distinctiveness.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with carrot cake — retailer-pick Bardstown against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; curated single-barrel finds cake spice with retailer-select character.",
  "Carrot Cake|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with carrot cake — rye spice against cream cheese frosting and cinnamon. The spice in the pour picks up the cake's warmth; a distinct dessert pairing from the wheated takes — less sweet-on-sweet, more spice-on-spice. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Carrot Cake|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year with carrot cake — aged rye spice against cream cheese and cinnamon. The 10-year's maturity picks up the cake's raisins; more layered than standard Basil Hayden's with this dessert. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Carrot Cake|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with carrot cake — spicy-sweet malted rye against cream cheese and cinnamon. The malted character and cake warmth find common ground; a distinctive within-family dessert pairing. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with carrot cake — high-rye BH red-wine finish against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; BH Red Wine Cask's rye-spice marries cinnamon + the red-wine fruit marries raisins — a dual-complement featured pairing.",
  "Carrot Cake|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast with carrot cake — toasted marshmallow sweetness against cream cheese frosting and cinnamon. The toast character and cake spice find each other; a distinct dessert pairing within the Basil Hayden's family. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bee's Knees": "Bee's Knees' citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Bee's Knees lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Carrot Cake|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Beefeater Gin for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Beignets": "The beignets alongside the carrot cake -- the powdered-sugar beignets reads beside the spiced carrot close, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the dessert split holds at neutral register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Belvedere Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with carrot cake — Stitzel-Weller revival blend against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; the blend's honey finds the cake's sweetness at matched registers.",
  "Carrot Cake|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with carrot cake — 103-proof single-barrel against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; richer profile is slightly over-specified for the dessert.",
  "Carrot Cake|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with carrot cake — single-barrel caramel-vanilla against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; a gentle composed match at the premium tier.",
  "Carrot Cake|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet cask-strength Blanton's. Works; Blanton's SfB meets the cake at barrel-proof register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with carrot cake — decennial Pact 10 against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; milestone-release finds cake spice with 10th-Pact collector character.",
  "Carrot Cake|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with carrot cake — Pact 8 against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; Mizunara-finish gives cake spice layered annual-release character.",
  "Carrot Cake|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with carrot cake — Pact 9 wine-cask against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; fruit-cask and cake spice find each other with annual-release character.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Bluecoat Gin for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Bodega Noemia A Lisa for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Bombay Dry Gin for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- juniper-citrus and warm cake spices pull in opposite directions. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Bombay Sapphire Gin for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow and the carrot cake -- savory gives way to sweet, the close lands cleanly. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bone-In Filet": "The bone-in filet into the carrot cake -- the marrow-edged butter-tender yields to spiced cake and cream-cheese frosting, walnut crunch on the close. Works; the spice-and-frosting reads heavier than the cut needed, but the meal builds clean across courses.",
  "Carrot Cake|Booker's": "Booker's with carrot cake — Beam barrel-proof against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; Booker's intensity meets the cake at barrel-proof register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Botanist Gin": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- juniper-citrus and warm cake spices pull in opposite directions. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Botanist Gin for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, Angostura. Bourbon weight fights cream-cheese frosting; the cocktail belongs with marrow, Brussels and Belly, or Tomahawk. Route dessert guest to Espresso Old Fashioned (brown sugar echoes) or Baileys neat instead. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with carrot cake — the stave-tailored pour against cream cheese frosting and cinnamon. Strong; the house selection layers with the cake's sweetness in a way that's specifically Bowdie's.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with carrot cake — Virginia small-batch against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bowmore 12": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character finds neutral with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the carrot cake, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; wine-cask over-specified.",
  "Carrot Cake|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Braida Montebruna Barbera lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Carrot Cake|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Broccoli Cheddar": "Broccoli Cheddar and the carrot cake -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Carrot Cake|Broccolini": "The broccolini alongside the carrot cake -- acid and sugar share the table, neither course pulls heavy. Works; the contrast holds, neither course overshadows the other.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich: this Islay Scotch's intense peat smoke, medicinal phenol, and bold weight meets the carrot cake's warm-spice, cream-cheese indulgence. Bold whiskey meets rich chocolate — cross-category depth that plays. Strong — dark-on-dark.",
  "Carrot Cake|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels + Belly and the carrot cake -- Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts hands off cleanly to the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: savory-into-sweet, the classic close lands intact. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Carrot Cake|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with carrot cake — BT caramel and vanilla against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Carrot Cake|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Buffalo Trace Cream. Excellent; the bourbon-vanilla-caramel amplifies the cream cheese frosting with deeper character than Baileys — Kentucky-cream register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with carrot cake — bold spice against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; rye spice and cake spice find each other.",
  "Carrot Cake|Burrata": "Burrata and the carrot cake -- the delicate course resolves into the close, the meal stays unburdened. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Carrot Cake|Butternut Squash": "Butternut Squash and the carrot cake -- two sweets stacked, the close loses dimension. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Carrot Cake|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cade Cabernet Sauvignon's Howell Mountain Cab character jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Cade Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Carrot Cake|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the rested-agave depth sits with the carrot cake, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Carrot Cake|Campari": "Campari with carrot cake — bitter orange-rhubarb against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; the orange notes marry the cinnamon and the bitterness threads cream cheese cleanly — a featured dessert match for the aperitivo cornerstone.",
  "Carrot Cake|Canadian Club": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet 1858 Hiram Walker Canadian. Works; Canadian Club's heritage character meets the cake at traditional register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Canadian Club 43 Year": "CC 43 Year with carrot cake — 43yr aged Canadian against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; CC 43 Year's aged depth is the featured Canadian-carrot-cake pairing at collector register — the aged dried-fruit notes marry the cake's spice.",
  "Carrot Cake|Captain Morgan Rum": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Captain Morgan. Excellent; the warm-spice rum profile (cinnamon, clove) meets carrot-cake's own spice register — spice amplified by spice at cocktail-workhorse level.",
  "Carrot Cake|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing with carrot cake — single-barrel Canadian against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; Caribou Crossing's craft character meets the cake at single-barrel register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Casa LBV Port": "Late Bottled Vintage Port — Casa LBV, 4-6 year cask, dark cherry, plum, smooth moderate structure, entry-port approachable. The dark cherry lifts allspice-cinnamon spice and the smooth structure plays with cream-cheese frosting. Peak LBV for carrot cake — approachable port on the spiced bake. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's earthen-pit smoke profile reads alongside the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the smoky agave register handles the carrot cake, and the campfire register frames the dessert. Works; a measured close to the meal.",
  "Carrot Cake|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus — Napa Cab, concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body meets carrot cake's spice-and-cream-cheese and the cocoa plays against the warm dessert. Works as iconic Napa Cab for carrot cake — unconventional red-with-dessert, though the crowd-pleasing Caymus name sells it.",
  "Carrot Cake|Chambord": "Carrot Cake: the carrot cake's warm-spice, cream-cheese indulgence meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Works as an indulgence.",
  "Carrot Cake|Chilean Seabass": "The seabass into the carrot cake -- the butter-rich silky flesh hands off to spiced cake and cream-cheese frosting, walnut on the close. Works; the indulgent fish and the spiced close share the meal across registers.",
  "Carrot Cake|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Chopin Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with carrot cake — California brandy against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Christian Bros's sweetness meets the raisins and spice at value-workhorse register.",
  "Carrot Cake|Château Batailley": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Château Beaucastel": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Château Beaucastel lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Carrot Cake|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Carrot Cake|Château Haut Segottes": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Château Haut Segottes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Carrot Cake|Château de Rouillac": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Château de Rouillac on the steak course instead.",
  "Carrot Cake|Clam Chowder": "Clam Chowder and the carrot cake -- cream-and-sugar build the close, the meal finishes generous. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
