  "Casamigos Mezcal|Mushroom Bisque": "Casamigos Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the smoky agave register handles the mushroom bisque, and the mineral smoke plays against the bowl. Works; the call holds at neutral register.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Mushrooms": "Casamigos Mezcal's earthen-pit smoke profile sits at register with the mushrooms' earthy umami weight: the rustic mezcal weight sits with the mushrooms, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Casamigos Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the wood-fire body meets the PB brownie, and the earthy backbone threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Porterhouse": "Casamigos Mezcal's wood-fire mezcal body rests at register with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the espadín-and-smoke body wraps the dual-cut steak, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: hand-cut raw beef meets Casamigos Mezcal. Excellent; the softened-smoke character frames the tartare at approachable-mezcal register — guest gets mezcal intrigue without full-smoke punch.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Rainbow Trout": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- mezcal smoke buries the delicate flesh — the fish has no foil against agave + smoke. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a mezcal. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Casamigos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Roast Half Chicken": "Casamigos Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the smoky agave register handles the roast chicken, and the campfire register frames the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a mezcal. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Casamigos Mezcal lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the main fish course. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a mezcal. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Casamigos Mezcal lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Casamigos Mezcal's earthen-pit smoke profile reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the earthen-pit smoke carries the garlic spinach, and the mineral smoke plays against the side. Works; the two sides share the meal without competing.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Seafood Tower": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- mezcal smoke obliterates delicate shellfish — needs sparkling or sancerre. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a mezcal. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Casamigos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Casamigos Mezcal. Avoid; smoke overwhelms scallops. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Seasonal Soup": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a mezcal. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Casamigos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Seasonal Vegetables": "Casamigos Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the wood-fire body meets the seasonal vegetables, and the mineral smoke plays against the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Shrimp Bisque": "Casamigos Mezcal's bold agave-soft-smoke character holds steady against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the agave smoke threads the broth, and the smoke wraps the briny shellfish. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Shrimp Cocktail": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- mezcal smoke obliterates delicate shellfish — needs sparkling or sancerre. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a mezcal. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Casamigos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Swordfish": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character doubles down on the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- mezcal smoke and the seared crust double up smoky char without contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a mezcal. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Casamigos Mezcal belongs on the steak course.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk: 36oz bone-on showpiece meets Diageo-backed mezcal polish. Excellent; Casamigos Mezcal's gateway-smoke energy works the dramatic cut at celebrity-brand register — approachable enough to round the table.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Tomato Basil": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- mezcal's smoke crowds the broth's clean register without lifting it. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a mezcal. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Casamigos Mezcal lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Truffle Fries": "Casamigos Mezcal's bold agave-soft-smoke character lifts the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the agave smoke layers into the rich side, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the main fish course. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a mezcal. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Casamigos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Casamigos Mezcal|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- mezcal's smoke crowds the broth's clean register without lifting it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a mezcal. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Casamigos Mezcal on the steak course instead.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Asparagus": "Napa Rutherford Cab against asparagus — concentrated cocoa-mocha body is the wrong axis for a sulfur-forward vegetable. Tannin-meets-sulfur is a textbook clash. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take asparagus with Sauvignon Blanc, Sancerre, or Chablis.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Napa Rutherford Cab — concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body cuts au gratin's cheese-and-cream weight and the cocoa-mocha plays against the golden crust. Strong iconic Napa Cab for au gratin — Caymus lushness meets the decadent side.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Beignets": "Caymus — cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. Cab tannin against a light choux beignet is catastrophically wrong; the wine obliterates the dessert's delicacy. Save Caymus for the steak course; route beignet pairing to Champagne, Moscato, or Prosecco. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone Marrow": "Caymus — Napa Cab, concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the cocoa frames the bone depth directly. Peak iconic Napa Cab for marrow — the name-recognition plus lushness makes this a confirmed-right-choice pairing for guests. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone-In Filet": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge. The dark-fruit frame supports the filet, but the tannin rides harder than the lean profile demands. Pour when the guest wants the bold-red experience. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccoli Cheddar": "Napa Rutherford Cab against a cream-and-cheddar broccoli soup — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the vegetable-cheese classic. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take broccoli cheddar with Chardonnay, Pinot Blanc, or Chablis.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccolini": "Napa Rutherford Cab against charred broccolini — concentrated cocoa-mocha body is the wrong axis for a vegetable side. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take broccolini with Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Noir.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Brussels and Belly": "Napa Rutherford Cab — concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body handles pork-belly fat and the cocoa-mocha plays against charred brussels directly. Peak iconic Napa Cab for Brussels and Belly — the 2020 vintage opulence frames the rich starter with recognizable Napa character. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Burrata": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon's cocoa-mocha velvety-tannin opulence clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- red structure on burrata curdles the cream's clean delicacy. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Butternut Squash": "Napa Rutherford Cab against a caramelized-squash autumn soup — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the sweet-cream classic. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take butternut squash with Riesling, Chardonnay, or Pinot Noir.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Carrot Cake": "Caymus — Napa Cab, concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body meets carrot cake's spice-and-cream-cheese and the cocoa plays against the warm dessert. Works as iconic Napa Cab for carrot cake — unconventional red-with-dessert, though the crowd-pleasing Caymus name sells it.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Cheesecake": "Caymus — Napa Cab, concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body meets cheesecake's dense cream and the cocoa plays against the tangy richness. Works as iconic Napa Cab for cheesecake — dessert pairing that leverages the Caymus name-recognition for a memorable finish.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Chilean Seabass": "Napa Rutherford Cab against buttery Chilean seabass — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the delicate melt-in-mouth fish. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take seabass with Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, or Chablis.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Brownie": "Napa Rutherford Cab — concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The cocoa-mocha profile meshes directly with chocolate-brownie fudge and the opulent fruit rounds out the dessert. Works as iconic Napa Cab for brownie — Cab-on-chocolate is an established classic the crowd-pleasing Caymus name plays into.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Cake": "Caymus — cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. Close to chocolate cake profile on paper, but tannin clashes with dessert sweetness and the structure is wrong for cake. Save Caymus for steak course; route chocolate-dessert guest to Espresso Martini or tawny. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Clam Chowder": "Napa Rutherford Cab against a cream-based clam chowder — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the delicate shellfish soup. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take chowder with Chardonnay, Chablis, or Pinot Blanc.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Cowboy Ribeye": "* Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon -- cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. The concentrated dark-fruit weight handles the cap-fat ribeye, and the tannin grips the cut. Peak boutique Napa Cab for ribeye — Simon Family reserve-level option for guests who want less well-known but equally serious.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Crab Cake": "Caymus Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — opulent cocoa-mocha crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep the iconic Napa label for the steak course where its Rutherford character shines.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamed Spinach": "Caymus — Napa Cab with concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. The opulent body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the cocoa plays against the earthy sauce. Peak iconic Napa Cab for creamed spinach — crowd-pleasing opulence on the classic steakhouse side. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamy Potato": "Napa Rutherford Cab against a cream-and-potato soup — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the silky dairy base. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take creamy potato with Chardonnay, Pinot Blanc, or Chablis.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Creme Brulee": "Caymus — cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. The Cab's structure destroys vanilla custard; tannin and cream don't meet on any axis. Peak Cab belongs with the steak course, not the crème brûlée. Route guest to Moscato, Vin Santo, or Sauternes. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Escargot": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. Not the sharpest pairing. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Faroe Island Salmon": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon's cocoa-mocha velvety-tannin opulence clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Filet Mignon": "Caymus — Napa Rutherford Cab, concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the cocoa-mocha echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak iconic Napa Cab for filet — the name, the opulence, the long warming finish all line up for the classic cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|French Onion": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cassis-cocoa tannin reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Grilled Caesar": "Caymus — Napa Cab, concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the cocoa plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as iconic Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — unconventional red pairing, though the name recognition sells the moment.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Gumbo": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon's cocoa-mocha velvety-tannin opulence jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Napa Rutherford Cab against a caramelized-honey roasted vegetable — concentrated cocoa-mocha body is the wrong axis for a sweet-root side. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take honey carrots with Pinot Noir, rosé, or Chardonnay.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|House Wedge": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cassis-cocoa tannin leans against the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the opulent body overshoots the leaf-and-acid plate, and the cassis frames the herb butter. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Kansas City": "Napa Rutherford Cab — concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. The opulent body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the cocoa frames the beefy char. Peak Napa Cab for Kansas City — Caymus is the recognizable pour that confirms a good choice for guests who know the label. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Loaded Potato": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cassis-cocoa tannin reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis cuts the cheese richness. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Lobster Mac": "Napa Rutherford Cab — concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body meets lobster-mac's cream-cheese richness and the cocoa frames the dense pasta. Strong iconic Napa Cab for lobster mac — crowd-pleasing lushness works on the indulgent side, though opulent Chardonnay often peaks higher.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Market Fish": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon's cocoa-mocha velvety-tannin opulence clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Mocha Creme": "Caymus — cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. The mocha profile is name-adjacent to the dessert, but Cab tannin clashes with cream-based custard desserts entirely. Save Caymus for steak course; route guest to Espresso Martini or tawny. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushroom Bisque": "Napa Rutherford Cab against a cream-based mushroom bisque — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the delicate umami soup. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take the bisque with Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, or Chablis.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushrooms": "Caymus — Napa Cab with concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body meshes with mushroom umami and the cocoa frames the sauté earth. Strong iconic Napa Cab for mushrooms — the name recognition plus earth-on-earth match works, though smaller Cabs like Jordan or Peju peak similarly with less opulence.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Caymus — cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. The cocoa profile is dessert-adjacent but the tannin clashes with peanut butter frosting and dessert in general. Save Caymus for the steak course; steer PB-brownie guest to Espresso Martini or Frangelico. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Porterhouse": "Caymus — Napa Cab, concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the cocoa plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong iconic Napa Cab for Porterhouse — crowd-pleasing lushness on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Prime Tartare": "Iconic Napa Cab against prime tartare's raw-iron richness — opulent cocoa-mocha obliterates the delicate seasoned beef. Never pair. Route tartare to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Caymus for the steak course where its velvety Napa Rutherford character peaks.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Rainbow Trout": "Napa Rutherford Cab against delicate rainbow trout — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the freshwater fish. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take trout with Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Roast Half Chicken": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon's cocoa-mocha velvety-tannin opulence reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the opulent body cuts the roast chicken, and the cocoa-mocha plays against the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Napa Rutherford Cab against a smoky roasted-pepper chickpea soup — concentrated cocoa-mocha body overwhelms the Mediterranean classic. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take the soup with Pinot Noir, rosé, or Rioja.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Napa Rutherford Cab against salt-cured halibut with sweet soy glaze — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the delicate cured fish. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take halibut with Chardonnay, Chablis, or Pinot Blanc.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Caymus — cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. Too heavy for the lightest sharing side; the wine buries the spinach and makes the escargot butter's garlic-shallot nuance disappear. Save Caymus for steaks or heavier sides. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Seafood Tower": "Caymus's cocoa, mocha, and velvety tannins — a full Napa Cab with no business near cold oysters and lobster. The structural mismatch is extreme. Save the bottle for the main course, steer Cab-only guests to Champagne here. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Seared Scallops": "Iconic Napa Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — velvety cocoa-mocha obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Caymus for the steak course where the crowd-pleasing Rutherford Cab peaks.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Soup": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon's cocoa-mocha velvety-tannin opulence overwhelms the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Vegetables": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon's cocoa-mocha velvety-tannin opulence overshadows the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Bisque": "Caymus — Napa Cab with concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body can meet the bisque's cream-shellfish richness but the cocoa-mocha profile is the wrong axis for shellfish soup. Works as iconic Napa Cab for bisque — Chardonnay, Champagne, or Riesling peak higher on cream-soup.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Cocktail": "Caymus Napa Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — opulent cocoa-mocha is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save the iconic Napa name for steak.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Swordfish": "Napa Rutherford Cab against grilled swordfish — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the fish even given swordfish's meaty character. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take swordfish with Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, or rosé.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|The Tomahawk": "Napa Rutherford Cab — concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin, long warming finish. The opulent body can meet Tomahawk fat and char but the velvety profile is rounder than the biggest marbled steak demands. Strong Caymus for Tomahawk — Opus, Shafer, or Heitz Martha's peak higher for theatrical bone-in.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Tomato Basil": "Napa Rutherford Cab against tomato basil's fresh-herb-and-acidity classic — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the bright soup. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take tomato basil with Sauvignon Blanc, Sancerre, or Chablis.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Truffle Fries": "Napa Rutherford Cab — concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body frames truffle-fry earth and the cocoa-mocha cuts the crisp oil-and-salt. Peak iconic Napa Cab for truffle fries — Caymus lushness meets the decadent side with the recognizability of a crowd-pleasing label. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Napa Rutherford Cab against rare-seared yellowfin tuna with sesame crust and honey sambal — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the delicate fish. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take tuxedo tuna with Pinot Noir, Pinot Blanc, or Chablis.",
  "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Napa Rutherford Cab against a coconut-milk vegetable curry — concentrated cocoa-mocha body is the wrong axis for spiced chickpeas. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take the curry with Riesling, rosé, or Pinot Noir.",
  "Chambord|Asparagus": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Beignets": "Chambord: this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness meets the beignets' light, airy, powdered-sugar sweetness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Chambord|Bone Marrow": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Bone-In Filet": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Chambord for another course.",
  "Chambord|Broccoli Cheddar": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Chambord on the steak course instead.",
  "Chambord|Broccolini": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Brussels and Belly": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Chambord on the steak course instead.",
  "Chambord|Burrata": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Butternut Squash": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Chambord on the steak course instead.",
  "Chambord|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: the carrot cake's warm-spice, cream-cheese indulgence meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Works as an indulgence.",
  "Chambord|Cheesecake": "Cheesecake: the cheesecake's dense, tangy dairy richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Chambord|Chilean Seabass": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Chambord on the steak course instead.",
  "Chambord|Chocolate Brownie": "Chambord with a chocolate brownie — the raspberry-chocolate pairing is a dessert menu standard for a reason. Black-raspberry sweetness picks up the brownie's cocoa depth and the acidity balances the sugar. One of the cleanest liqueur-plus-dessert moves on the card. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Chambord|Chocolate Cake": "Chocolate Cake: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chambord|Clam Chowder": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Cowboy Ribeye": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character flattens the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Chambord for another course.",
  "Chambord|Crab Cake": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Chambord lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chambord|Creamed Spinach": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Creamy Potato": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Creme Brulee": "Chambord: this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness meets this delicate custard's creamy, vanilla sweetness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Chambord|Escargot": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character buries the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- sweet liqueur smothers the herb-bright opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Chambord on the steak course instead.",
  "Chambord|Faroe Island Salmon": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert-grade sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs a crisp white instead. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Filet Mignon": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Chambord lands on another course — not here.",
  "Chambord|French Onion": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Grilled Caesar": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Gumbo": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sweet liqueur and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no contrast left. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Chambord lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chambord|House Wedge": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Chambord on the steak course instead.",
  "Chambord|Kansas City": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Chambord for another course.",
  "Chambord|Loaded Potato": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Chambord lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chambord|Lobster Mac": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Chambord belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Market Fish": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- sugar on delicate fish is wrong direction — the plate needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Mocha Creme": "Mocha Creme: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chambord|Mushroom Bisque": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Chambord on the steak course instead.",
  "Chambord|Mushrooms": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Chambord|Porterhouse": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Chambord for another course.",
  "Chambord|Prime Tartare": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Rainbow Trout": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- sugar on delicate fish is wrong direction — the plate needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Chambord belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Roast Half Chicken": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character buries the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- sugar smothers the chicken's savory frame — wait for dessert. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- sugar on delicate fish is wrong direction — the plate needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Chambord lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chambord|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Chambord lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chambord|Seafood Tower": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Seared Scallops": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Chambord on the steak course instead.",
  "Chambord|Seasonal Soup": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Chambord on the steak course instead.",
  "Chambord|Seasonal Vegetables": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Chambord belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Shrimp Bisque": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Chambord belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Shrimp Cocktail": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Swordfish": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sugar dulls the crust's clean char instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Chambord on the steak course instead.",
  "Chambord|The Tomahawk": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Chambord lands on another course — not here.",
  "Chambord|Tomato Basil": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Chambord belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Truffle Fries": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Chambord lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chambord|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- sugar dulls the crust's clean char instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Chambord|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with cheesecake — Barton 1792 against clean custard. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Cheesecake|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat with cheesecake — sweet-wheat against clean custard. Works; the pour's sweetness meets the dessert without pushing past.",
  "Cheesecake|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the 1881 Napa Valley for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the 2 James Gin for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the scotch backbone matches the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Cheesecake|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with cheesecake — ex-bourbon + Oloroso Speyside against custard. Strong; Aberlour 16's balance threads the cheesecake at approachable-premium register.",
  "Cheesecake|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- añejo weight and custard tang refuse each other. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Adictivo Cristalino on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the oak-aged tequila body threads the cheesecake, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Cheesecake|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Alexander Valley Chardonnay on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Alexander Valley Cyrus for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Alexander Valley Homestead Red for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino with cheesecake — elegant Friulian amaro against custard. The grappa-based structure and orange peel thread cleanly through the cheesecake's richness; premium digestif. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cheesecake|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes: Mexican chile liqueur from Puebla, 40% ABV, late-harvest ancho poblano chiles — smoky-sweet-spicy. Excellent on cheesecake; the chile-heat-smoky-sweet character brings unexpected dimension to cheesecake — bolder pairing, chile-meets-cream at Mexican register.",
  "Cheesecake|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with cheesecake — flagship AE port-finish against custard. Strong; AE's jammy-fruit threads the cheesecake at wine-finish register.",
  "Cheesecake|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with cheesecake — 117+ proof port-finish against custard. Strong; AE CS's cask-strength jammy-fruit threads the cheesecake at premium register.",
  "Cheesecake|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "AE Triple Cask with cheesecake — AE triple-cask against custard. Strong; AE Triple Cask's layered-cask character threads the cheesecake with distinctive complexity.",
  "Cheesecake|Aperol": "Aperol with cheesecake — 11% soft bitter against custard. Strong; the gentle orange-rhubarb threads the cheesecake with brunch-dessert character.",
  "Cheesecake|Asparagus": "The asparagus and the cheesecake -- the asparagus' green-vegetal edge hands off cleanly to the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: acid and sugar share the table, neither course pulls heavy. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Cheesecake|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes and the cheesecake -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Cheesecake|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Averna Amaro": "Averna with cheesecake — Sicilian amaro against custard. The caramel-and-orange character sits with the cheesecake's richness; classic post-dessert digestif. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cheesecake|Aviation": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Aviation for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Aviation Gin": "Aviation with cheesecake — American craft gin against custard. Works; Aviation's lavender-orange-peel is a soft dessert finisher.",
  "Cheesecake|Avion 44": "Avion 44 with cheesecake — 44-month aged against custard. Works; maturation depth composed with dessert.",
  "Cheesecake|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Avion Silver belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the bright cane-and-citrus weight carries the cheesecake, and the Caribbean-rum register underlines the dessert. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Cheesecake|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's herbal-and-sugar liqueur register lifts the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the herbal-sweet weight matches the cheesecake, and the honey threads the cream. Excellent; the starter sets up the cut at full register. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Cheesecake|Baker's": "Baker's with cheesecake — 107-proof high-rye against clean custard. Works; bold proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Cheesecake|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with cheesecake — vanilla-forward bourbon-cask Speyside against custard dessert. Excellent; Balvenie's American Oak vanilla threads cheesecake custard — the featured Speyside-cheesecake match; Fiddich's apple and Glenlivet's honey are the alternatives.",
  "Cheesecake|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with cheesecake — tropical-sweet rum-cask Speyside against custard. Strong; Caribbean Cask's vanilla-caramel character threads the cheesecake at distinctive finish-cask register.",
  "Cheesecake|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with cheesecake — hand-picked First Fill American Oak against custard dessert. Excellent; Balvenie 16's deeper vanilla threads cheesecake with single-barrel character — Balvenie 12 opens this pairing, 16 deepens it.",
  "Cheesecake|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood with cheesecake — 21yr port-cask Speyside against custard. Strong; Portwood's port red-berry frames the cheesecake at collector register.",
  "Cheesecake|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with cheesecake — French-wine-cask Bardstown against custard. Strong; Bardstown Château Laboude's French-wine-fruit threads the cheesecake at contemporary-craft register.",
  "Cheesecake|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the caramel-spice register matches the cheesecake, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the Millennium's register overshoots the dessert's.",
  "Cheesecake|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the cheesecake, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; bold proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Cheesecake|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare with cheesecake — Foursquare rum-cask Bardstown against custard. Works; Bardstown Foursquare's tropical character threads the cheesecake at Caribbean-finish register.",
  "Cheesecake|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the cheesecake, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Cheesecake|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the bourbon depth settles into the cheesecake, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; JD Bonded's backbone threads the cake at Bonded register.",
  "Cheesecake|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with cheesecake — rye spice against clean custard. Works; the pour's brightness is a gentle counterpoint to the dessert's richness.",
  "Cheesecake|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year with cheesecake — aged high-rye against custard. Works; the 10-year's oak adds dimension but the match is still gentle.",
  "Cheesecake|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with cheesecake — bready malted notes against custard. Works; the bread-and-custard pairing is capable without being remarkable.",
  "Cheesecake|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with cheesecake — high-rye BH red-wine finish against custard. Strong; BH Red Wine Cask's red-wine-fruit threads the cheesecake with rye-spice dimension.",
  "Cheesecake|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast with cheesecake — toast-barrel caramel against clean custard. Works; the toast adds a warmth the dessert can accept.",
  "Cheesecake|Bee's Knees": "Bee's Knees' citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Bee's Knees for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Beefeater Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere with cheesecake — aromatic rye vodka against custard. Works; the vanilla notes echo the dessert at distinctive register.",
  "Cheesecake|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with cheesecake — blend's honey against custard. Works; gentle match at matched registers.",
  "Cheesecake|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with cheesecake — 103-proof Blanton's against clean custard. Works; richer profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Cheesecake|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with cheesecake — iconic single-barrel against clean custard. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Cheesecake|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's SfB with cheesecake — cask-strength Blanton's against custard. Avoid; barrel-proof intensity wrong for custard's delicate character.",
  "Cheesecake|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the oak depth lays under the cheesecake, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for the dessert's register.",
  "Cheesecake|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the cheesecake, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th meets the cake at annual-release register.",
  "Cheesecake|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the caramel-spice register matches the cheesecake, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Cheesecake|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat with cheesecake — 4-organic-citrus craft against custard. Works; Bluecoat's citrus threads the cheesecake at American-artisan register.",
  "Cheesecake|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Bodega Noemia A Lisa for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Bombay Dry Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Bombay Sapphire Gin for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Bone Marrow": "The bone marrow alongside the cheesecake -- savory gives way to sweet, the close lands cleanly. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Cheesecake|Bone-In Filet": "The bone-in filet closes with the cheesecake -- the marrow-laced butter-tender yields to dense dairy custard on graham crust, berry compote brightening the close. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Cheesecake|Booker's": "Booker's with cheesecake — 120-130 proof Beam against custard. Avoid; barrel-proof intensity is wrong for custard's delicate character.",
  "Cheesecake|Botanist Gin": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Botanist Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, Angostura. Bourbon weight overwhelms cream cheese delicacy; the cocktail belongs with marrow, Brussels and Belly, or Tomahawk. Route dessert guest to Espresso Martini, Baileys, or a tawny. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Cheesecake|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with cheesecake — custard meets house-pick caramel. Works; the private select's layered profile underserves the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Cheesecake|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with cheesecake — Virginia small-batch against clean custard. Works; smooth gentle match.",
  "Cheesecake|Bowmore 12": "Bowmore 12 with cheesecake — the custard's clean dairy register doesn't pick up peat. Bowmore's sherry finish actually comes closest of the three (and for guests committed to the pairing, it's the least bad), but the conflict is still there. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Cheesecake|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Braida Montebruna Barbera for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Broccoli Cheddar": "Broccoli Cheddar and the cheesecake -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Cheesecake|Broccolini": "Broccolini and the cheesecake -- bright meets sweet, the contrast stays light. Works; the meal closes cleanly from the side.",
  "Cheesecake|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich: this Islay Scotch's intense peat smoke, medicinal phenol, and bold weight meets the cheesecake's dense, tangy dairy richness. Bold whiskey meets rich chocolate — cross-category depth that plays. Works cleanly.",
  "Cheesecake|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly and the cheesecake -- the entree yields to the dessert, the meal arc reads on-key. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Cheesecake|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with cheesecake — clean custard against BT caramel. Works; gentle match.",
  "Cheesecake|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Cheesecake: custard-dairy dessert meets Buffalo Trace Cream. Excellent; the bourbon-base-caramel-cream frames cheesecake at Kentucky-cream register — caramel-vanilla on dairy, bourbon-drinkers' dessert liqueur.",
  "Cheesecake|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with cheesecake — high-rye spice against clean custard. Works; rye's brightness is a gentle counterpoint.",
  "Cheesecake|Burrata": "Burrata and the cheesecake -- the lighter course hands off to dessert, the meal closes clean. Works; the bookends compose neutrally.",
  "Cheesecake|Butternut Squash": "Butternut Squash and the cheesecake -- sugar on sugar, the close reads heavy with sweetness. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Cheesecake|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cade Cabernet Sauvignon's Howell Mountain Cab character pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Cade Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rested-agave depth sits with the cheesecake, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; the earthy craft character doesn't quite lift the truffle the way polished reposados suggest they might.",
  "Cheesecake|Campari": "Campari with cheesecake — ruby bitter against custard. Strong; the orange notes lift the cheesecake at aperitivo register.",
  "Cheesecake|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the smooth Canadian register carries the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; private-selection wasted on the salad.",
  "Cheesecake|Canadian Club 43 Year": "Canadian Club 43 Year's Canadian whisky character leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the Canadian whisky weight matches the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; own-plus-sourced covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Cheesecake|Captain Morgan Rum": "Cheesecake: custard-dairy dessert meets Captain Morgan. Excellent; a confident headline pairing.",
  "Cheesecake|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the easygoing Canadian character handles the cheesecake, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; AE CS handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Cheesecake|Casa LBV Port": "Late Bottled Vintage Port — Casa LBV, 4-6 year cask, dark cherry, plum, smooth moderate structure, entry-port approachable. The plum lifts cream-cheese density and the smooth structure plays with graham-cracker brown-sugar crust. Peak LBV for cheesecake — approachable port on the water-bath bake. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cheesecake|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character overpowers the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a mezcal. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Casamigos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus — Napa Cab, concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body meets cheesecake's dense cream and the cocoa plays against the tangy richness. Works as iconic Napa Cab for cheesecake — dessert pairing that leverages the Caymus name-recognition for a memorable finish.",
  "Cheesecake|Chambord": "Cheesecake: the cheesecake's dense, tangy dairy richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Cheesecake|Chilean Seabass": "The seabass closes with the cheesecake -- the butter-finished silky fish hands off to dense dairy custard, berry compote brightening the graham-crust base. Works; the dairy depth reads round against the rich main.",
  "Cheesecake|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin with cheesecake — creamy Polish potato against custard. Works; the texture match is natural — creamy vodka with creamy dessert, artisanal finisher.",
  "Cheesecake|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with cheesecake — California American brandy (not cognac), sweeter value workhorse. Excellent; Christian Bros's American sweetness is built for dessert — cheesecake is the featured value-brandy pairing, where the French cognacs aim higher and costlier.",
  "Cheesecake|Château Batailley": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Château Beaucastel": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Château Haut Segottes": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Château Haut Segottes on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Château de Rouillac": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Château de Rouillac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Clam Chowder": "Clam Chowder and the cheesecake -- rich dairy yields to dessert weight, the close composes thickly. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Cheesecake|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's rested-agave body leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the barrel-rested body handles the cheesecake, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; Durango ceremony over-specified for small plate.",
  "Cheesecake|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango with cheesecake — ceremonial extra añejo against custard. Works; Durango presentation slightly over-specified.",
  "Cheesecake|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold with cheesecake — golden ceremonial against custard. Works; ceremony over-specified.",
  "Cheesecake|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's smoky agave character overpowers the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a mezcal. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata's silky Jalisco blanco with ceramic-decanter presentation falls short of the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Clase Azul Plata for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado's ceramic-icon reposado character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rested-agave depth sits with the cheesecake, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; 5-year Ultra over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Cheesecake|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra with cheesecake — 5-year top-tier against custard. Works; Ultra's aged depth is over-specified for dessert simplicity.",
  "Cheesecake|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep with cheesecake — Beam wheated against clean custard. Works; gentle match.",
  "Cheesecake|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Clos Petit Ona Grenache for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's cooked-agave-and-oak register leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the barrel-rested body handles the cheesecake, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; the extended maturation gives the side more than standard reposados bring, though not a standout.",
  "Cheesecake|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth: historic Asti house vermouth (aromatized fortified wine, 16% ABV) — functionally a vermouth not a liqueur, wine-base botanical infusion. Excellent on cheesecake; the vermouth's wine-and-botanical character plays cheesecake cream at aperitivo register — lighter and softer than the high-proof herbal liqueurs.",
  "Cheesecake|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; one of those is the move. Corazon Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rested-agave depth sits with the cheesecake, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Cheesecake|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's añejo-pour depth leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rested-agave depth sits with the cheesecake, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; accessible aged composed with dessert.",
  "Cheesecake|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the cheesecake, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Cheesecake|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the aged-agave weight wraps the cheesecake, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Cheesecake|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Corazón del Sol Malbec lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Corpse Reviver": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- cocktail citrus and custard tang cancel each other — both reach for acid. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Corpse Reviver for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier with cheesecake — drier traditional cognac against custard dessert. Strong; Courvoisier's oak supports the cheesecake, though Hennessy's sweeter approach is the featured cognac-cheesecake pairing.",
  "Cheesecake|Cowboy Ribeye": "The cowboy ribeye closes with the cheesecake -- the marbled char-and-fat hands off to dense dairy custard on graham crust, berry compote lifting the close. Works; the table closes heavy, but the dairy reads round against the rendered fat.",
  "Cheesecake|Crab Cake": "The crab cake and the cheesecake -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust bookends with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Cheesecake|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 with cheesecake — layered Speyside against custard. Strong; Cragganmore's complexity threads the cheesecake with distinctive character; Balvenie 12's vanilla is still the featured match.",
  "Cheesecake|Creamed Spinach": "The creamed spinach and the cheesecake -- the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens hands off cleanly to the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Cheesecake|Creamy Potato": "Creamy Potato and the cheesecake -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Cheesecake|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the Canadian whisky weight matches the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Cheesecake|Cucumber Gimlet": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Cucumber Gimlet for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12 with cheesecake — sherry-finish Highland luxury against custard. Strong; Dalmore's orange-marmalade and dried-fruit thread the cheesecake at Highland-premium register.",
  "Cheesecake|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's Loire Valley Pinot Noir pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Darioush Cabernet Franc for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin's Detroit urban-craft gin clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Detroit City Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City Vodka's hometown craft vodka mis-pairs with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- clean vodka has nothing to add against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a vodka. Avoid; the better calls are above. Detroit City Vodka lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the Highland register carries the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; EC 18 handles dessert density at aged-HH register.",
  "Cheesecake|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the cheesecake, and the oak threads the cream. Works; BIB over-specified for side.",
  "Cheesecake|Disaronno": "★ Disaronno's sweet almond-marzipan character is built for the cheesecake's custard cream — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the nut character deepens the cream. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Cheesecake|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the high-rye spice register matches the cheesecake, and the oak softens the cream. Works; complex profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Cheesecake|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Cheesecake: custard-dairy dessert meets Doctor Bird. Excellent; the high-ester tropical-fruit funk cuts through cheesecake's cream with Jamaican intensity — not subtle, but unforgettable.",
  "Cheesecake|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Domaine Serene Grand Cheval's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Domaine Serene Grand Cheval for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Domaine Tempier Bandol's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Domaine Tempier Bandol for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Domaine du Grand Tinel belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Don Fulano Anejo": "Don Fulano Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the barrel-rested body handles the cheesecake, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; the wine-cask character adds dimension but not standout.",
  "Cheesecake|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Don Fulano Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado's rested-agave body sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the barrel-rested body handles the cheesecake, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Cheesecake|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942 with cheesecake — 1942 against custard. Works; aged depth is refined but not a standout.",
  "Cheesecake|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th's rested-agave body leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the barrel-rested body handles the cheesecake, and the soft-spice note softens the dessert. Works; clean entry match.",
  "Cheesecake|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco's workhorse blanco with clean agave snap falls short of the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Don Julio Blanco belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Don Julio Reposado": "Don Julio Reposado's rested-agave body leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the aged-agave weight wraps the cheesecake, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; accessible match.",
  "Cheesecake|Drambuie": "Drambuie: Scotch whisky-based honey-herbal liqueur from Scotland, 40% ABV, heather-honey and spiced herbs on a scotch base. Excellent on cheesecake; the scotch-honey character threads cheesecake cream at Rusty-Nail register — this is a scotch-drinker's dessert move.",
  "Cheesecake|Dunville's Single Malt": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the cheesecake, and the oak softens the cream. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Cheesecake|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with cheesecake — BT BiB against custard. Avoid; barrel-proof intensity wrong for custard's delicate character.",
  "Cheesecake|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the high-rye spice register matches the cheesecake, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; JD Bonded's backbone threads the cake at Bonded register.",
  "Cheesecake|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with cheesecake — bonded BT against clean custard. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Cheesecake|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with cheesecake — custard clean meets ten years of Buffalo Trace oak. Works; neutral, not elevating.",
  "Cheesecake|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Eastern Kille Dry Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|El Cabo": "El Cabo's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the añejo register carries the cheesecake, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; the 70th's smoothness is an unusual but interesting counterpoint.",
  "Cheesecake|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "El Mayor Extra Anejo with cheesecake — aged El Mayor against custard. Works; accessible aged composed with dessert.",
  "Cheesecake|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the oak-aged tequila body threads the cheesecake, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; extra añejo depth composed with dessert.",
  "Cheesecake|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Elephant Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 with cheesecake — aged HH single-barrel against custard. Works; EC 18's aged character threads the cheesecake at aged-HH register.",
  "Cheesecake|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel with cheesecake — private-barrel against clean custard. Works; curated single-barrel slightly over-specified.",
  "Cheesecake|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel with cheesecake — 94-proof single-barrel against clean custard. Works; oak-spice slightly overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Cheesecake|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with cheesecake — small-batch against clean custard. Works; a gentle composed match.",
  "Cheesecake|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye backbone drives the cheesecake, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the aging is wasted on the salad.",
  "Cheesecake|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Elk Cove Five Mountain for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Elk Cove Pinot Blanc belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee with cheesecake — namesake single-barrel against clean custard. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Cheesecake|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 with cheesecake — rose-ginger BC craft against custard. Works; Empress's rose-notes thread the cheesecake at distinctive register.",
  "Cheesecake|Epoch Ingenuity": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Epoch Ingenuity for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Escargot": "The escargot and the cheesecake -- the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight bookends with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Cheesecake|Espresso Martini": "Tito's, espresso, Grind liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The coffee-and-vanilla profile meets vanilla-bean cheesecake directly; Averna's bitter backbone cuts cream cheese density. Peak coffee-cocktail pour for cheesecake — one of the highest-scoring pairings on the menu.",
  "Cheesecake|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Espresso Old Fashioned's bitters-and-spirit register sits at register with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the spirit-forward register carries the cheesecake, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Cheesecake|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Evening Land Seven Springs on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cheesecake: the cheesecake's dense, tangy dairy richness meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Dark chocolate and spice echo the bold red's fruit, but the tannin fights the sugar unless the dessert is dry-cocoa-bitter. Works when the dessert leans bitter rather than sweet.",
  "Cheesecake|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Far Niente Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Faroe Island Salmon": "The salmon closes with the cheesecake -- the pan-finished oily flesh hands off to dense dairy custard, graham crust and berry compote framing the close. Works; the meal closes round, the dairy reading thick against the rich fish.",
  "Cheesecake|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca with cheesecake — saffron-menthol against custard richness. Strong; Branca's intensity demotes the sweeter amari pairing slightly but still settles as digestif.",
  "Cheesecake|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta with cheesecake — mint Fernet against custard. Strong; cooling mint is a cleaner finish on cheesecake than Branca's saffron would be.",
  "Cheesecake|Filet Mignon": "The filet closes with the cheesecake -- the butter-tender 10oz hands off to dense cream-cheese custard on graham crust, berry compote brightening the close. Works; a heavier close than the steak called for, but it lands cleanly.",
  "Cheesecake|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the liqueur compounds the dessert's sweetness without contrast. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Fireball Whisky on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Fisher Unity Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with cheesecake — 104-proof 6-recipe against clean custard. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Cheesecake|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label with cheesecake — floral-honey bourbon against clean custard. Works; friendly accessible match.",
  "Cheesecake|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with cheesecake — craft bourbon against clean custard. Works; gentle match.",
  "Cheesecake|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Fox & Oden Double Oaked with cheesecake — Detroit Double Oaked against custard. Works; Fox & Oden's intensified caramel-vanilla threads the cheesecake at Double-Oaked register.",
  "Cheesecake|Frangelico": "Frangelico's syrupy-sweet liqueur body meets at register with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the syrupy liqueur weight wraps the cheesecake, and the honey threads the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cheesecake|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Freemark Abbey Bosché belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|French 75": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the French 75 for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|French Onion": "French Onion and the cheesecake -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Cheesecake|Frias Block 5": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Piedmont Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee, gentle sweetness, honey finish. The peach-and-honey profile cuts through dense cream cheese and the bubbles lift the graham-cracker crust. Peak dessert-sparkling for our water-bath cheesecake's density.",
  "Cheesecake|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. G4 Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof's rested-agave body reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the barrel-rested body handles the cheesecake, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; distinctive ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Cheesecake|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the malt-grain blend handles the cheesecake, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive.",
  "Cheesecake|George T. Stagg": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the cask weight has no foil against a delicate dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. George T. Stagg lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12 with cheesecake — apple/pear Speyside against custard. Strong; Fiddich rides the cheesecake with gateway character; Balvenie 12's vanilla-forward profile edges this pairing.",
  "Cheesecake|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14 with cheesecake — new-charred-oak-finished Speyside against custard. Strong; Fiddich 14's baking-spice frames the cheesecake more fully than the 12's apple-pear approach; Balvenie 16 Single Barrel still edges this for vanilla-forward dessert.",
  "Cheesecake|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18 with cheesecake — Oloroso + bourbon cask marriage against custard. Strong; Fiddich 18's dried-fruit frames the cheesecake with aged gravity; Balvenie 16 and Gran Cru 23 are the featured dessert matches.",
  "Cheesecake|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23 with cheesecake — champagne-style Cuvée-cask Speyside against custard dessert. Excellent; Gran Cru's brioche-apple-blossom finish is the featured Speyside-cheesecake pairing in the aged batch — the pastry character threads custard with champagne-cask elegance no other Speyside replicates.",
  "Cheesecake|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12 with cheesecake — Sandend coast Highland against custard. Strong; Glenglassaugh 12's tropical-fruit-and-honey threads the cheesecake at distinctive register.",
  "Cheesecake|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12 with cheesecake — honey-floral Speyside against custard. Strong; Glenlivet rides the cheesecake with pineapple-hint distinction; Balvenie 12's vanilla is still the featured Speyside-cheesecake match.",
  "Cheesecake|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10 with cheesecake — bourbon-cask Highland entry against custard. Works; Glenmorangie 10's honey-vanilla is a soft dessert finisher at entry register.",
  "Cheesecake|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18 with cheesecake — 18yr Highland with Oloroso finish against custard. Strong; Glenmorangie 18's aged-sherry character threads the cheesecake at premium register.",
  "Cheesecake|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "10-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's entry-point, dried apricot, walnut, toffee, gentle spice, smooth finish. The dried apricot lifts cream-cheese density and the walnut plays with graham-cracker brown-sugar crust. Peak Tawny for cheesecake — nutty-sweet match on the water-bath baked dessert. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cheesecake|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "20-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's step-up, concentrated dried fruit, roasted hazelnut, orange peel, silky long finish. The concentrated dried fruit lifts cream-cheese density and the roasted hazelnut plays with graham-cracker brown-sugar crust. Peak step-up Tawny for cheesecake — complexity meets dense water-bath bake. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cheesecake|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "2017 declared-vintage Port — Graham's bottle-aged, dense inky, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violet, powerful structured. The blackcurrant lifts cream-cheese density and the dark chocolate plays with graham-cracker brown-sugar. Peak Vintage Port for cheesecake — declared-vintage structure on the water-bath bake. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cheesecake|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the unaged body brightens the cheesecake, and the agave-citrus thread frames the dessert. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Cheesecake|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier: this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness meets the cheesecake's dense, tangy dairy richness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Works as an indulgence.",
  "Cheesecake|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale with cheesecake — California ocean-conservation gin against custard. Works; Gray Whale is over-specified for dessert — save the ocean-botanicals for seafood.",
  "Cheesecake|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse: 55% ABV, 130+ herbs/botanicals, Carthusian monks since 1605. Excellent on cheesecake; the powerful herbal-complex-bitter character cuts through cheesecake cream like a knife — the dessert-meets-digestif classic at high-proof register.",
  "Cheesecake|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose with cheesecake — soft French wheat against custard dessert. Works; silky vodka as luxury dessert finisher, Russian-tradition-style.",
  "Cheesecake|Grilled Caesar": "The Caesar alongside the cheesecake -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Cheesecake|Gumbo": "The gumbo and the cheesecake -- the gumbo's spiced creole stew bookends with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Cheesecake|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a vodka. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the HDW 100 Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 Year's lightly-peated Hakushu character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the cheesecake, and the oak threads the cream. Works; doesn't drive.",
  "Cheesecake|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel with cheesecake — BT single-barrel against clean custard. Works; complex profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Cheesecake|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hangar 1 Vodka lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Head Fake": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build plays cleanly against the cheesecake's custard cream — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Cheesecake|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with cheesecake — 20yr aged HH against custard. Works; HH 20's aged character threads the cheesecake at collector register.",
  "Cheesecake|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with cheesecake — 100-proof traditional against custard. Works; BIB structure is a touch over-specified for the dessert.",
  "Cheesecake|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with cheesecake — aged premium bourbon against clean custard. Works; 10-year depth overshoots delicacy.",
  "Cheesecake|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Heitz Martha's Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Heitz Trailside Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Flora Adora with cheesecake — Spring-release floral Hendrick's against custard. Works; Flora Adora's 6-floral-botanical threads the cheesecake at distinctive register; Grand Cabaret's black currant is the featured Hendrick's-cheesecake match.",
  "Cheesecake|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks with cheesecake — flagship Scottish gin against custard. Works; Hendrick's cucumber-rose is a soft dessert finisher; Grand Cabaret's black currant is the featured Hendrick's-cheesecake match.",
  "Cheesecake|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Grand Cabaret with cheesecake — Hendrick's black currant + rhubarb cocktail-culture release against custard. Excellent; Grand Cabaret's black-currant-and-rhubarb profile is the featured Hendrick's Family-cheesecake pairing — fruit-currant-with-custard is a distinctive dessert bridge no flagship Hendrick's or floral sibling reaches.",
  "Cheesecake|Hendricks Neptunia": "Neptunia with cheesecake — coastal-release Hendrick's against custard. Works; Neptunia's briny profile is over-specified for dessert; Grand Cabaret's black-currant is the featured Hendrick's-cheesecake match.",
  "Cheesecake|Hendricks Orbium": "Orbium with cheesecake — bitter-tonic Hendrick's against custard. Works; Orbium's wormwood is over-specified for dessert; Grand Cabaret's black-currant is the featured Hendrick's-cheesecake match.",
  "Cheesecake|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy with cheesecake — default-call cognac against custard dessert. Excellent; Hennessy's dried-fruit and vanilla thread the cheesecake with the most-ordered cognac-dessert pairing — textbook French after-dinner finish.",
  "Cheesecake|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB with cheesecake — aged bonded HH against clean custard. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Cheesecake|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Japanese Harmony's blended Japanese harmony sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; boutique character gives the dessert a craft frame.",
  "Cheesecake|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Honey Roasted Carrots and the cheesecake -- two sweets stacked, the close loses dimension. Works; the meal closes cleanly from the side.",
  "Cheesecake|House Wedge": "The wedge alongside the cheesecake -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Cheesecake|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 with cheesecake — 15yr historic-Kentucky against custard. Works; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak threads the cheesecake at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Cheesecake|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Glera from Veneto — dry Prosecco with white peach, green apple aromatics, and a clean citrus finish. The crisp acidity cuts vanilla-bean cheesecake's cream-cheese density and bubbles lift the graham crust. Peak everyday sparkler for cheesecake — bright contrast to richness at approachable price.",
  "Cheesecake|Inhibited": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso in a chocolate-drizzled martini glass. Cream-on-cream stacking works when the guest wants full dessert indulgence; Kahlúa coffee cuts the weight. Strong cocktail route for a rich after-dinner play.",
  "Cheesecake|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Inverroche Gin for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Iron Fish Gin for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon Mezcal Casks with cheesecake — Iron Fish MI mezcal-cask against custard. Works; Ironfish Mezcal threads the cheesecake at Michigan-hometown register.",
  "Cheesecake|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish with cheesecake — A. Smith Bowman port-finish against custard. Strong; Isaac Bowman's port-jammy threads the cheesecake at Virginia-heritage register.",
  "Cheesecake|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards' silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. J Davies Ferrington Vineyards belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon's Diamond Mountain Cab character pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the cheesecake, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; novelty maturation at matched register.",
  "Cheesecake|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the cheesecake, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Cheesecake|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the caramel-spice register matches the cheesecake, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; boutique-sourced wasted on salad.",
  "Cheesecake|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the cheesecake, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Discovery complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Cheesecake|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the malt-grain blend handles the cheesecake, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; wine-cask complexity gives the dessert layered premium character.",
  "Cheesecake|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with cheesecake — master-blender-tribute ocean-finish against custard. Works; Marian McLain L.E. threads the cheesecake at collector register.",
  "Cheesecake|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with cheesecake — sea-aged Jefferson's against custard. Works; Voyage 23's salt-tinged character threads the cheesecake at ocean-finish register.",
  "Cheesecake|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve with cheesecake — blended honey-vanilla against custard. Works; a gentle composed match.",
  "Cheesecake|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with cheesecake — double-barrel oak against clean custard. Works; the Twin Oak's depth slightly overshoots the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Cheesecake|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label with cheesecake — aged Beam against clean custard. Works; oak sits beside the dessert gently.",
  "Cheesecake|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label with cheesecake — entry bourbon against clean custard. Works; accessible gentle match.",
  "Cheesecake|Johnnie Walker Black": "Johnnie Walker Black's Highland-scotch character leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the scotch backbone matches the cheesecake, and the oak softens the cream. Works; AE Triple Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Cheesecake|Johnnie Walker Blue": "Johnnie Walker Blue's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the Highland register carries the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; a gentle composed match at the premium tier.",
  "Cheesecake|Johnnie Walker Red": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the cheesecake, and the oak softens the cream. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Cheesecake|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cheesecake: the cheesecake's dense, tangy dairy richness meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. Cherry fruit echoes dark chocolate, but the elegant red's acidity fights refined sugar. Works for a specific guest — fruit-forward cocoa.",
  "Cheesecake|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Joseph Mellot Sancerre for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Jubilation by Colgin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Cheesecake: custard-dairy dessert meets Jung and Wulff Guyana. Excellent; the burnt-sugar Demerara character with its wood-still terroir meets cheesecake cream at historical-rum register.",
  "Cheesecake|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Cheesecake: custard-dairy dessert meets Jung and Wulff Trinidad. Excellent; the Trinidad column-still polish meets cheesecake cream at refined-medium register — the lighter Jung and Wulff option.",
  "Cheesecake|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet coffee-rum-base depth sits with the cheesecake's custard cream — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the coffee threads the cream. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Cheesecake|Kansas City": "The KC closes with the cheesecake -- the firm savory grain hands off to dense cream-cheese custard, berry compote brightening the graham-crust base. Works; the meal closes substantial, neither course pulling against the other.",
  "Cheesecake|Keenan Chardonnay": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Keenan Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Keenan Mernet": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Keenan Mernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye's rye-spice character leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the cheesecake, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; novelty maturation at matched register.",
  "Cheesecake|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the caramel-spice register matches the cheesecake, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; mid-proof pour is slightly over-specified.",
  "Cheesecake|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the gentle Irish body threads the cheesecake, and the oak threads the cream. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Cheesecake|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One with cheesecake — Dutch wheat against custard. Works; Ketel's citrus threads the cheesecake cleaner than a fully-round vodka does.",
  "Cheesecake|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Knickerbocker Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek with cheesecake — high-rye bourbon against clean custard. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for the dessert's register.",
  "Cheesecake|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year with cheesecake — aged high-rye against clean custard. Works; aged character overshoots delicacy.",
  "Cheesecake|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 with cheesecake — aged Beam single-barrel against custard. Works; KC 15's aged character threads the cheesecake at aged-Beam register.",
  "Cheesecake|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 with cheesecake — 18yr aged Beam KC against custard. Works; KC 18's aged character threads the cheesecake at collector register.",
  "Cheesecake|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with cheesecake — blended bourbon-rye against custard. Works; pour is a touch over-specified for the dessert.",
  "Cheesecake|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the high-rye spice register matches the cheesecake, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; OGD 16's rye-spice meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Cheesecake|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva's aged-agave character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the cheesecake, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; accessible aged sits with richness.",
  "Cheesecake|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo's aged-agave character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the barrel-rested body handles the cheesecake, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Cheesecake|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Añejo with cheesecake — aged Komos against custard. Works; extra añejo depth composed with dessert.",
  "Cheesecake|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado's rested-agave body reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the aged-agave weight wraps the cheesecake, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; clean and refined but not a marquee pairing.",
  "Cheesecake|Lagavulin 8": "Lagavulin 8 with cheesecake — cheesecake's clean dairy-custard register isn't built for peat. The 8-year's brightness can't rescue the pairing; the smoke sits wrong against the custard. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Cheesecake|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Lail Vineyards Blueprint's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Lail Vineyards Blueprint belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Lalo Silver lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Laphroaig 10": "Cheesecake: clean custard delicacy against medicinal peat is a pure mismatch. The dram flattens everything about the dessert. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Cheesecake|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch with cheesecake — wheated caramel against clean custard. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "Cheesecake|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Champagne — clean acid, bubbles, elegant body. The acid cuts through cream cheese density and the bubbles lift the graham crust. Works when guest wants to continue the Champagne through dessert rather than transition to a sweet wine.",
  "Cheesecake|Le Garenne Rosé": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Le Garenne Rosé for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Le Ragnaie Brunello lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Le Volte dell'Ornellaia lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Lemon Lavender Gin Martini for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Les Pallières Raciné": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Les Pallières Raciné lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Liberator Gin": "Liberator with cheesecake — Valentine Distilling Ferndale craft against custard. Works; Liberator's smooth profile threads the cheesecake at hometown register.",
  "Cheesecake|Licor 43": "Cheesecake: the cheesecake's dense, tangy dairy richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Cheesecake|Limoncello": "Cheesecake: the cheesecake's dense, tangy dairy richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cheesecake|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir's Willamette layered Pinot character pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Little Book": "Little Book with cheesecake — Jim Beam Little Book cask-strength against custard. Avoid; barrel-proof intensity is wrong for custard's delicate character.",
  "Cheesecake|Loaded Potato": "Loaded Potato and the cheesecake -- rich cream gives way to sweet, the close lands generously. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Cheesecake|Lobster Mac": "The lobster mac and the cheesecake -- the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence hands off cleanly to the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: cream-and-sugar build the close, the meal finishes generous. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Cheesecake|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year with cheesecake — 20yr complex Highland against custard. Strong; Loch Lomond 20's aged-depth threads the cheesecake at collector register.",
  "Cheesecake|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye spice cuts the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Cheesecake|Long Road Gin": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Long Road Gin for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overpowers the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a mezcal. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII with cheesecake — Grande Champagne luxury icon against custard dessert. Works; cheesecake is lighter than Louis XIII deserves — the Rémy VSOP tier is the appropriate cognac for this dessert. Save the Louis XIII bottle for the protein course.",
  "Cheesecake|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano with cheesecake — floral-anise Basilicata amaro against custard. The lighter profile and anise character give the cheesecake a distinctive post-dessert digestif. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cheesecake|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Lunaria Coste di Moro for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry with cheesecake — benchmark sherried Speyside against custard. Strong; Macallan 12's Oloroso dried-fruit threads the cheesecake with sherry-cask depth.",
  "Cheesecake|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18 with cheesecake — 18yr Oloroso-cask Speyside against custard. Strong; Macallan 18's aged-sherry depth threads the cheesecake at premium register.",
  "Cheesecake|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate with cheesecake — Easter Elchies Estate-oak Macallan against custard. Strong; Macallan Estate's distinctive oak threads the cheesecake at premium register.",
  "Cheesecake|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Macauley Petite Syrah for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Macauley Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Mahon Gin": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Mahon Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark with cheesecake — clean custard meets soft wheated caramel. Works; the match is gentle and approachable.",
  "Cheesecake|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with cheesecake — custard and cellar-aged vanilla meet cleanly. Works; not revelatory but well-matched registers.",
  "Cheesecake|Malibu Rum": "Cheesecake: custard-dairy dessert meets Malibu. Excellent; the coconut liqueur sweetness plays tropical dimension on cheesecake — piña-colada-energy on dessert at liqueur register.",
  "Cheesecake|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye spice cuts the cheesecake, and the oak softens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Cheesecake|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Marc Brédif Chinon's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Marc Brédif Chinon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Margarita": "Margarita's blanco-and-citrus margarita brightness jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- cocktail citrus and custard tang cancel each other — both reach for acid. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Margarita lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Marimar Estate Christina": "Marimar Estate Christina's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Marimar Estate Christina for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Market Fish": "The market fish closes with the cheesecake -- the rotating white-fish hands off to dense cream-cheese custard, graham crust and berry compote framing the close. Works; the dairy weight outsizes the cleaner main, but the meal builds without strain.",
  "Cheesecake|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe: Bohemian-style absinthe from Austria, 60% ABV, wormwood-anise-fennel, traditional louche. Excellent on cheesecake; the wormwood-anise clarity cuts cheesecake cream at absinthe-classic register — high-proof herbal cleaver.",
  "Cheesecake|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Bourbon with cheesecake — Michter's single-barrel 10yr against custard. Works; Michter's 10's aged character threads the cheesecake at prestigious-independent register.",
  "Cheesecake|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; premium rye gives the dessert substantial depth.",
  "Cheesecake|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the dry rye body wraps the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; boutique character doesn't drive.",
  "Cheesecake|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap mis-pairs with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the spirit reads thin against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Mijenta Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo's añejo-pour depth leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the cheesecake, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Cheesecake|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado's rested-agave body leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the añejo register carries the cheesecake, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; 5-year Ultra over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Cheesecake|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam with cheesecake — Sazerac tribute blend against custard. Works; Mister Sam's blended character threads the cheesecake at Seagram's-tribute register.",
  "Cheesecake|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Monkey 47 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the Highland register carries the cheesecake, and the oak threads the cream. Works; distinctive character gives the dessert a unique story-frame.",
  "Cheesecake|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro with cheesecake — Bologna amaro against custard. Vanilla-orange against the cheesecake's richness; the most approachable of the classic amari for dessert. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cheesecake|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum's clean light-rum body with citrus edge finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the bright cane-and-citrus weight carries the cheesecake, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Cheesecake|Muga Reserva": "Muga Reserva's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Muga Reserva lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Mushroom Bisque": "Mushroom Bisque and the cheesecake -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Cheesecake|Mushrooms": "The mushrooms and the cheesecake -- the mushrooms' earthy umami weight hands off cleanly to the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Cheesecake|Myers's Rum": "Cheesecake: custard-dairy dessert meets Myers's. Excellent; the Jamaican dark-rum molasses profile frames the cream at workhorse register — the accessible dark-rum-dessert option.",
  "Cheesecake|Negroni": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — equal parts. The Campari's bitter edge clashes with the cream cheese sweetness and the sour-cream tang. Wrong cocktail for cheesecake — save Negroni for mushrooms, marrow, or as a pre-dinner opener. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Cheesecake|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye spice cuts the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; AE Triple Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Cheesecake|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch with cheesecake — novelty bourbon against clean custard. Works; the unique maturation story adds interest to a gentle dessert match.",
  "Cheesecake|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with cheesecake — MI beer-barrel bourbon against custard. Works; NH Beer Barrel threads the cheesecake at Michigan-hometown register.",
  "Cheesecake|New York Sour": "New York Sour's citrus-lemon build sits with the cheesecake's custard cream — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cheesecake|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Nolets Dry Gin for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Not a Paper Plane": "Not a Paper Plane's stirred whiskey-led build finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the bold-mix depth handles the cheesecake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the call holds at register.",
