  "Cheesecake|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with cheesecake — aged high-rye OGD against custard. Works; OGD 16's rye-spice threads the cheesecake at distinctive register.",
  "Cheesecake|Opus One": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet. The dark fruit plays against cheesecake's cream-cheese richness and the mocha frames the graham-cracker crust. Works as collector Napa Cab blend for cheesecake — prestige red-with-dessert works for guest wanting red continuation.",
  "Cheesecake|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Pablo Sour": "Pablo Sour's citrus-lemon build holds the line with the cheesecake's custard cream — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Cheesecake|Paloma": "Paloma's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Paloma for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye's allocated-icon Pappy rye character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the high-rye spice register matches the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; OGD 16 handles dessert density at distinctive register.",
  "Cheesecake|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage 16th Ed with cheesecake — HH annual cask-strength against custard. Avoid; barrel-proof intensity is wrong for custard's delicate character.",
  "Cheesecake|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rested-agave depth sits with the cheesecake, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Cheesecake|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver's polished silver tequila with agave-forward lift falls short of the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Patron Silver for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet coffee-tequila-base depth sits with the cheesecake's custard cream — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the coffee threads the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cheesecake|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a sparkling. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand with cheesecake — artisanal Grande Champagne against custard dessert. Excellent; Ferrand's floral-honey threads the cheesecake with craft-premium finesse — parallel to Rémy VSOP's elegance, with distinct unblended character.",
  "Cheesecake|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the high-rye spice register matches the cheesecake, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Cheesecake|Pineau des Charentes": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac finish. The honey lifts cream-cheese density and the white grape plays with graham-cracker brown-sugar. Peak Pineau for cheesecake — fortified grape-and-honey match on the water-bath bake. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cheesecake|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Plymouth Gin for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a sparkling. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Pommery Cuvée Louise for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Porterhouse": "The Porterhouse closes with the cheesecake -- the dual-character cut hands off to dense dairy custard, graham crust and berry compote framing the close. Works; the meal closes substantial, the dual cuts carrying the dairy weight cleanly.",
  "Cheesecake|Prime Tartare": "The tartare and the cheesecake -- the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami bookends with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Cheesecake|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|RAEN Royal St Robert": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Rainbow Trout": "The trout closes with the cheesecake -- the delicate pan-finished flesh hands off to dense cream-cheese custard, graham crust and berry compote framing the close. Works; the dairy weight reads thick against the lighter fish, but the meal builds clean.",
  "Cheesecake|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Spanish rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit, clean acid. The rosé fruit plays against the sour-cream tang and the bubbles cut the cream cheese weight. Fun non-obvious pour when guest wants something less sweet than Moscato to finish. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Cheesecake|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Cheesecake|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the cheesecake, and the oak softens the cream. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Cheesecake|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Gatsby with cheesecake — 15yr MGP Gatsby Reserve against custard. Works; Remus 15's rye-spice threads the cheesecake at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Cheesecake|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Rémy VSOP with cheesecake — Fine Champagne refinement against custard dessert. Excellent; Rémy's elegant floral-fruit-vanilla profile threads the cheesecake with more finesse than Hennessy's benchmark or Courvoisier's drier approach.",
  "Cheesecake|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo with cheesecake — Moorish-inspired against custard. Works; distinctive composed with dessert.",
  "Cheesecake|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka with cheesecake — Icelandic glacial vodka against custard. Works; the mineral lift cuts the custard richness distinctively.",
  "Cheesecake|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the caramel-spice register matches the cheesecake, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Cheesecake|Roast Half Chicken": "The chicken closes with the cheesecake -- the herbed roast hands off to dense cream-cheese custard on graham crust, berry compote brightening the close. Works; the meal builds toward sweetness without crowding either course.",
  "Cheesecake|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The chickpea soup alongside the cheesecake -- separate registers share the plate, the contrast settles the build. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Cheesecake|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs' bright sparkling effervescence jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Roku Gin": "Roku Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Roku Gin for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Cheesecake: custard-dairy dessert meets Ron Zacapa. Excellent; the honey-vanilla-solera character threads the cheesecake's creamy register beautifully — premium-sipping-rum-meets-dessert textbook.",
  "Cheesecake|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with cheesecake — aged caramel against clean custard. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Cheesecake|Salt-Cured Halibut": "The halibut closes with the cheesecake -- the salt-cured firm flesh hands off to dense dairy custard, graham crust and berry compote framing the close. Works; the cure meets the dairy weight without strain on the close.",
  "Cheesecake|Sambuca": "Sambuca: Italian anise liqueur (Molinari benchmark), star anise + elderflower + botanicals, traditionally served con la mosca (with 3 coffee beans). Excellent on cheesecake; the sweet-anise character threads custard-cream at Italian-digestif register — sweeter, more dessert-friendly than Mata Hari Absinthe.",
  "Cheesecake|San Simeon Stormwatch": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the San Simeon Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Sauteed Garlic Spinach and the cheesecake -- acid plays against sweetness, the close stays unburdened. Works; the meal closes cleanly from the side.",
  "Cheesecake|Sauternes Glass": "Bordeaux botrytis — apricot, honey, orange marmalade, bright acid. The acidity cuts dense cream cheese cleanly and the honey aligns with the vanilla-bean cream. One of the great dessert wines on the list for a guest who wants peak sophistication alongside cheesecake. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cheesecake|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The absinthe-edge doesn't align with vanilla-bean cheesecake and the rye spice muddies cream cheese. Works only when guest is staying on the cocktail; route dessert-focused guest to Sauternes or Espresso Martini instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye backbone drives the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; EC 18 handles dessert density at aged-HH register.",
  "Cheesecake|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna with cheesecake — New Zealand craft single malt against custard. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir threads the cheesecake at distinctive register.",
  "Cheesecake|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Nebbiolo — tar, dried rose, firm tannin. Barolo obliterates cream cheese and the graham crust gets lost. Never pour with dessert; keep Barolo for the Tomahawk or Porterhouse where the structure earns its place. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Cheesecake|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity leans against the cheesecake's custard cream — the dry white underclubs the dessert sugar, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Cheesecake|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Seafood Tower": "Seafood Tower and the cheesecake -- acid and sugar share the table, neither course pulls heavy. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Cheesecake|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops and the cheesecake -- the savory course resolves into sugar, the meal closes with contrast. Works; the bookends compose neutrally.",
  "Cheesecake|Seasonal Soup": "The seasonal soup and the cheesecake -- the seasonal soup's house seasonal build bookends with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Cheesecake|Seasonal Vegetables": "The seasonal vegetables alongside the cheesecake -- the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; the contrast holds, neither course overshadows the other.",
  "Cheesecake|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Shafer 1.5": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Shafer Hillside Select": "Shafer Hillside Select's Stags Leap allocated Cab power pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Shafer Hillside Select for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Shrimp Bisque": "The shrimp bisque and the cheesecake -- the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup bookends with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Cheesecake|Shrimp Cocktail": "Shrimp Cocktail and the cheesecake -- acid plays against sweetness, the close stays unburdened. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Cheesecake|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap mis-pairs with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the spirit reads thin against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Siete Leguas Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the barrel-rested body handles the cheesecake, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Cheesecake|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak — Alexander Valley Cab, American-oak vanilla, coconut, dill, ripe cassis. The lush body meets cheesecake's dense cream and the vanilla plays against the tangy richness. Works as recognizable Cab for cheesecake — American-oak signature plus cream pairing, though dessert wines peak higher.",
  "Cheesecake|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Simon Family Estate Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Simon Family Estate Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "Spottswoode Lyndenhurst's Napa estate Cab with structured cassis pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Spottswoode Lyndenhurst lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the St Supéry Élu Meritage for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with cheesecake — BT cask-strength against custard. Avoid; barrel-proof intensity wrong for custard's delicate character.",
  "Cheesecake|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit with cheesecake — ultra-premium Russian wheat against custard. Works; the glacial-clean finish is a luxury-register Russian-tradition dessert finisher.",
  "Cheesecake|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp NZ Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Swordfish": "The swordfish closes with the cheesecake -- the firm-fish steak hands off to dense cream-cheese custard, graham crust and berry compote framing the close. Works; the dairy weight settles round against the lemon-finished fish.",
  "Cheesecake|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Tanq 10 Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Tanqueray Gin for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Classic Douro Tawny — Taylor Fladgate (founded 1692), smooth balanced, dried fruit, almond, warm clean finish. The dried fruit lifts cream-cheese density and the almond plays with graham-cracker brown-sugar crust. Peak heritage-Tawny for cheesecake — smooth balance handles the water-bath bake. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cheesecake|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Flagship Vintage Port — Taylor Fladgate bottle, extraordinarily rich, dark fruit, chocolate, tobacco, minutes-long evolving finish. The dark fruit lifts cream-cheese density and the tobacco plays with graham-cracker brown-sugar. Peak flagship-Vintage for cheesecake — Taylor's flagship on the water-bath bake. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cheesecake|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with cheesecake — triple-cask against custard. Works; multi-cask depth composed with dessert.",
  "Cheesecake|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Cheesecake|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|The BG": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build finds neutral with the cheesecake's custard cream — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the honey threads the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Cheesecake|The Happy Wife": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build holds with the cheesecake's custard cream — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Cheesecake|The Manhattan": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye-vermouth body settles into the cheesecake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Cheesecake|The Prisoner Red Blend": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve The Prisoner Red Blend for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk closes with the cheesecake -- the smoky bone-in marbling hands off to dense cream-cheese custard, graham crust holding the dairy weight. Works; the table closes heavy after the heaviest steak, but it carries.",
  "Cheesecake|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye spice cuts the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Cheesecake|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with cheesecake — white-wine-finished Barton bourbon against custard. Excellent; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-butter + custard resonance is distinctive — white-wine-finish for custard is less common and more harmonious than red-wine finishes here.",
  "Cheesecake|Titos Vodka": "Tito's with cheesecake — American vodka against custard dessert. Works; Russian-style clean-vodka-with-cheesecake tradition, Tito's roundness is a soft dessert finisher.",
  "Cheesecake|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; no driver pairing.",
  "Cheesecake|Tomato Basil": "The tomato basil alongside the cheesecake -- the bright register lifts the sweet, the meal closes clean. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Cheesecake|Transfusion": "Transfusion's crisp citrus-ginger build leans against the cheesecake's custard cream — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Cheesecake|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado's aged-agave character leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the cheesecake, and the soft-spice note softens the dessert. Works; distinctive composed with dessert.",
  "Cheesecake|Truffle Fries": "The truffle fries and the cheesecake -- the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight hands off cleanly to the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: sweet contrast lifts the savory. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Cheesecake|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s gentle Irish blend leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the Irish whiskey body sits with the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Cheesecake|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "The tuna closes with the cheesecake -- the sesame-crusted yellowfin hands off to dense dairy custard, graham crust and berry compote framing the close. Works; the dairy weight outsizes the lighter main, but it lands cleanly.",
  "Cheesecake|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "The curry alongside the cheesecake -- separate registers share the plate, the contrast settles the build. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Cheesecake|Venge Family Reserve": "Venge Family Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Venge Family Reserve belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Venge Scout's Honor": "Venge Scout's Honor's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Venge Scout's Honor for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Venge Silencieux": "Venge Silencieux's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Venge Silencieux for the steak course.",
  "Cheesecake|Vesper": "Vesper's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the bright build frames the cheesecake, and the citrus cuts the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Cheesecake|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's classic yellow-label Champagne body clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a sparkling. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Veuve Clicquot Brut lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cheesecake|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. Cognac richness plays against vanilla-bean cream and the herbal complexity adds depth. Works if the guest carries the cocktail through dessert; Sauternes or Espresso Martini peak higher for cheesecake.",
  "Cheesecake|Villon Cognac": "Villon with cheesecake — contemporary cognac against custard dessert. Excellent; Villon's modern profile and soft elderflower edge threads the cheesecake at accessible register.",
  "Cheesecake|Vin Santo": "Tuscan straw-dried dessert wine — honey, dried apricot, walnut, oxidative richness. The nutty intensity meets the graham-cracker-and-brown-sugar crust directly and the honey aligns with the vanilla-bean cream cheese body. One of the more interesting dessert pairings on the list. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cheesecake|Weller 107": "Weller 107 with cheesecake — clean custard meets 107-proof wheated. Works; the heat slightly overshoots the dessert's delicacy, but the caramel helps the match.",
  "Cheesecake|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year with cheesecake — aged oak and vanilla meet the cheesecake's custard. The pairing is composed but doesn't lift either element; Weller Special Reserve does this more cleanly. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Cheesecake|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium with cheesecake — custard clean and restrained meets premium wheated depth. Works; the Millennium's register overshoots the dessert's.",
  "Cheesecake|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve with cheesecake — soft wheated caramel against clean custard. A gentle match; works cleanly without any particular lift. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Cheesecake|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley with cheesecake — BT wheat vodka against custard. Works; Wheatley's cleanness is a soft dessert finisher.",
  "Cheesecake|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye-and-oak weight handles the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; HH 20 is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Cheesecake|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye backbone drives the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Cheesecake|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye spice cuts the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for the dessert's register.",
  "Cheesecake|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye spice cuts the cheesecake, and the oak threads the cream. Works; own-plus-sourced over-specified.",
  "Cheesecake|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye spice cuts the cheesecake, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; annual-release complexity over-specified.",
  "Cheesecake|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the high-rye spice register matches the cheesecake, and the oak softens the cream. Works; the match is gentle and approachable.",
  "Cheesecake|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with cheesecake — craft high-rye against custard. Works; the oak spice is slightly over-specified for the dessert.",
  "Cheesecake|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with cheesecake — small-batch fruit notes against clean custard. Works; the fruit character gives the dessert a subtle counterpoint.",
  "Cheesecake|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with cheesecake — Brown-Forman KY Double Oaked against custard. Works; Woodford Double Oaked's intensified caramel-vanilla threads the cheesecake at KY-benchmark register.",
  "Cheesecake|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the high-rye spice register matches the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Cheesecake|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the cheesecake, and the oak threads the cream. Works; OGD 16 handles dessert density at distinctive register.",
  "Cheesecake|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; 10-year depth overshoots delicacy.",
  "Cheesecake|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the mizunara-cask depth wraps the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Cheesecake|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse: 40% ABV, 130+ herbs/botanicals with honey-saffron notes, sister to Green but gentler and sweeter. Excellent on cheesecake; the honey-herbal character threads the cream at approachable-Carthusian register — where Green cuts, Yellow caresses.",
  "Chilean Seabass|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the 1792 Small Batch on the steak course instead.",
  "Chilean Seabass|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. 1792 Sweet Wheat belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the 1881 Napa Valley for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals reads alongside the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the botanicals lift the savory weight. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Chilean Seabass|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. A Midnight's Winter Dram lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Aberlour 16 on the steak course instead.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's rested-agave body sits alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the barrel-rested body handles the seabass, and the oak threads the plate. Strong; modern cleanness is capable but the starter earns a more character-forward reposado.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the barrel-rested body handles the seabass, and the caramel-agave register brightens the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight aligns with seabass's buttery flesh and the apple-pear lifts the rich texture. Peak accessible Chardonnay for Chilean seabass — California oak on the buttery classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Alexander Valley Cyrus belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Alexander Valley Homestead Red lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Amaro Nonino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert-grade sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs a crisp white instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Angel's Envy for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Angel's Envy Cask Strength for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Angel's Envy Triple Cask for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Aperol on the steak course instead.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Asparagus": "The seabass alongside the asparagus -- the seabass' buttery seabass flesh meets the asparagus' green-vegetal edge cleanly. Strong; the call holds its register at the table against the main.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The seabass alongside the au gratin -- the meal layers rich on rich, the table carries weight cleanly when the registers match. Works; the side sits without pulling focus.",
