  "Chilean Seabass|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Casa LBV Port for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the main fish course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a mezcal. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Casamigos Mezcal lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab against buttery Chilean seabass — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the delicate melt-in-mouth fish. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take seabass with Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, or Chablis.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Chambord on the steak course instead.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Cheesecake": "The seabass closes with the cheesecake -- the butter-finished silky fish hands off to dense dairy custard, berry compote brightening the graham-crust base. Works; the dairy depth reads round against the rich main.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Chocolate Brownie": "The seabass closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the butter-finished silky fish yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Strong; rich-into-rich, the meal closes the way the table came for.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Chocolate Cake": "After the seabass, the chocolate cake -- the butter-rich silky flesh hands off to layered cocoa-and-ganache, the vanilla scoop tying the close. Strong; the indulgent fish earns the layered chocolate dessert at register.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality holds the line with the seabass's buttery flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Christian Bros Brandy for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Château Batailley": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Château Beaucastel": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Château Haut Segottes": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Château de Rouillac": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Château de Rouillac for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Clam Chowder": "The seabass after the chowder -- the silky-flesh seabass composes with the silky chowder bowl. Works; the opener sits without competing.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Clase Azul Anejo on the steak course instead.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango's rested-agave body finds neutral with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the rested-agave depth sits with the seabass, and the soft-spice note softens the plate. Works; distinctive ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold's joven blend with mezcal accent clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Clase Azul Gold on the steak course instead.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- mezcal smoke and the fish's natural oils refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a mezcal. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata's crisp ceramic-agave character lifts the seabass's buttery flesh — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado's ceramic-icon reposado character finds neutral with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the ceramic-icon reposado depth wraps the seabass, and the agave-and-caramel rounds the plate. Works; heavy-caramel-GTS character handles richness at intense register.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra's aged-agave character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- agave and oily fish refuse each other — the fish needs acid, not aged spirit. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Clase Azul Ultra for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Clermont Steep for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Clos Petit Ona Grenache for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's rested-agave body leans against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the seabass, and the cooked-agave note underlines the plate. Works; accessible character doesn't drive.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's vermouth-wine-base sweetness leans against the seabass's buttery flesh — the bitter herb edge cleans the fish. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco's agave-citrus character sits with the seabass's buttery flesh — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the seabass, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the plate. Works; multi-cask over-specified for small plate.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the añejo register carries the seabass, and the soft-spice note softens the plate. Works; extra añejo depth composed with this dish.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg's añejo tequila finished in ex-George-T finds neutral with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the barrel-rested body handles the seabass, and the oak threads the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the oak-aged tequila body threads the seabass, and the caramel-agave register brightens the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Corazón del Sol Malbec on the steak course instead.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Corpse Reviver": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build holds steady against the seabass's buttery flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- cognac and oily fish double up the richness with no foil. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Courvoisier Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Crab Cake": "The seabass and the crab cake across courses -- the silky-flesh seabass plays against the crisp-crusted crab. Strong; the opener carries its register before the main.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Cragganmore 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Creamed Spinach": "Chilean Seabass with the creamed spinach -- the seabass' buttery seabass flesh meets the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Creamy Potato": "Chilean Seabass after the potato soup -- the silky leek-and-potato cream opens with quiet richness, the seabass following with buttery pan-finished flesh that the cream complements on the same plane. Strong; cream into butter reads indulgent.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Creme Brulee": "After the seabass, the brulee -- the miso-touched butter-rich flesh yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the silky main meets the silky finish, the meal arc reads clean.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Willamette Pinot — graceful Oregon Pinot with cherry, spice, medium body, structured. The structured body handles seabass's buttery flesh and the cherry-spice plays against the rich texture. Strong Oregon Pinot for Chilean seabass — Cristom structure on the classic.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Crown Royal lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Cucumber Gimlet": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build reads alongside the seabass's buttery flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Dalmore 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin holds with the seabass's buttery flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Darioush Cabernet Franc lands on the steak course — not here.",
