  "Chilean Seabass|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — delicate Oregon white, stone-fruit, crisp. The crisp acid cuts seabass's buttery richness and the stone-fruit plays against the melt-in-mouth texture. Works as Pinot Blanc for Chilean seabass — Oregon white; Chardonnays peak higher on the buttery fish.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Elmer T. Lee for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals sits with the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Epoch Ingenuity": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Epoch Ingenuity for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Escargot": "Chilean Seabass preceded by the escargot -- the miso-glazed seabass wraps the butter-bath starter. Strong; reliable opener for the main.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Espresso Martini": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Willamette Pinot — savory Eola-Amity Pinot, medium tannin, elegant delicate. The medium tannin meets seabass's buttery flesh and the savory edge plays against the rich texture. Strong Oregon Pinot for Chilean seabass — Evening Land structure on the buttery classic.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Far Niente Chardonnay": "* Far Niente Chardonnay -- Napa Chardonnay, tropical fruit, oak. The bright acid lifts the seabass, and the stone-fruit note underlines the plate. California pivot for guests who want American producers over Loire when pairing with cheese. Gold standard; the call that earns the regular.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet on the steak course instead.",
