  "Chilean Seabass|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's craft high-rye oak spice clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Willett Pot Still Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Woodford Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Woodford Reserve Double Oaked for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Woodford Reserve Rye for the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Woodinville Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character sits alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the Japanese whisky balance frames the seabass, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the plate. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character reads alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the seabass, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the plate. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Chilean Seabass|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bitter herbs on oily fish read metallic — the rich flesh deserves a crisp white. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Yellow Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with the chocolate brownie — 93.7-proof Barton against cocoa. Works; spicy-sweet character meets the brownie cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat with the chocolate brownie — sweet-wheat against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast bring more.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the 1881 Napa Valley for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the 2 James Gin for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the chocolate brownie — rare release against cocoa. Strong; boutique-craft meets the brownie at rare-release register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with the chocolate brownie — double-matured Speyside against cocoa. Excellent; Aberlour 16's sherry-side dried-fruit marries cocoa at balanced-premium register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- aged-agave register fights chocolate sweetness without bridging. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Adictivo Cristalino on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's aged-agave character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the barrel-rested body handles the brownie, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; ceremonial over-specified.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Alexander Valley Chardonnay lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Alexander Valley Cyrus on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Alexander Valley Homestead Red lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino with the chocolate brownie — grappa-based Friulian amaro, elegant and lighter than Averna. Orange peel and botanical complexity against cocoa; the grappa base gives the digestif more structure than sweeter amari bring to dessert. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Ancho Reyes": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Ancho Reyes. Excellent; chile-and-chocolate is a textbook Mexican pairing — Ancho Reyes brings ancho-poblano smoky-sweet-spice to the brownie at 40% register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with the chocolate brownie — flagship Angel's Envy port-finished bourbon, Louisville KY, 86.6 proof, fruity-jammy softer-oak from Portuguese port casks. Excellent; AE's port-derived jammy-fruit marries cocoa at the featured wine-finished-bourbon-chocolate pairing — port-and-chocolate is textbook, and AE brings it at flagship register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa meets annual limited AE port-finish at 117+ proof cask-strength, more intense than flagship. AE CS's port-finish-at-cask-strength is the featured wine-finished-bourbon-chocolate pairing at premium register — port-and-chocolate at higher proof brings more jammy-cocoa intensity than flagship AE. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa meets AE's triple-cask-finished limited release (port + additional casks), multi-cask layered finish. AE Triple Cask's layered multi-cask character is the featured distinctive-wine-finished-chocolate pairing — the additional cask layers add complexity to port-and-chocolate no other AE expression matches. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Aperol": "Aperol with the chocolate brownie — soft orange-rhubarb against cocoa. Works; Aperol is too light to feature vs cocoa where the sipping amari shine.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Asparagus": "Asparagus and the brownie -- acid and sugar share the table, neither course pulls heavy. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes and the brownie -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Averna Amaro": "Averna with the chocolate brownie — Sicilian classic amaro, caramel-and-orange-forward with herbal complexity. Fuller body than Nonino's grappa approach. The caramel-orange notes thread into the brownie's cocoa with the classic Sicilian amaro-dessert character. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Aviation": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Aviation for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Aviation Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Avion 44": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa meets 44-month aged Avion. Extended maturation meets the brownie with premium Avion authority. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Avion Silver lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the cane-and-citrus body lifts the brownie, and the unaged-cane note frames the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Baileys Irish Cream": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Baileys. Excellent; the pairing earns its featured slot.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Baker's": "Baker's with the chocolate brownie — 107-proof small-batch against cocoa. Works; rich vanilla depth meets the brownie with small-batch character.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 American Oak: bourbon-barrel Speyside. Excellent on chocolate brownie; vanilla-honey-sweet Speyside frames cocoa at American-oak register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the chocolate brownie — rum-cask-finished 14yr Speyside against cocoa. Strong; Caribbean Cask's rum-cask-derived brown sugar marries cocoa; Balvenie 21 Portwood's port-cask is the featured Sherried Speyside-chocolate match.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brownie, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; Sinatra Select handles the richness at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood with the chocolate brownie — 21yr port-cask Speyside against cocoa. Excellent; port-and-chocolate is a famous pairing — Portwood's port-derived red-berry fruit is the featured Sherried Speyside-chocolate-brownie match across the cluster.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa meets Bardstown Bourbon Company's wine-cask finish release, French wine cask (Château Laboude), contemporary craft wine-finish approach. Bardstown Château Laboude's French-wine-cask character is the distinctive craft-wine-finished-chocolate pairing — the French-wine provenance distinguishes it from AE's Portuguese port approach. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with the chocolate brownie — Discovery blend against cocoa. Strong; craft-blender complexity meets the brownie at multi-distillery register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with the chocolate brownie — KBS stout-barrel finish against cocoa. The coffee, chocolate, and stout notes from the KBS barrel are built for chocolate desserts; this pairing is the Founders KBS's signature dessert move. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bardstown Foursquare": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa meets Foursquare rum-cask Bardstown. Bardstown Foursquare's rum-cask + cocoa is textbook — tropical-rum + chocolate at Caribbean-finish register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with the chocolate brownie — Fusion blend against cocoa. Strong; own-plus-sourced meets the brownie with Bardstown distillery character.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with the chocolate brownie — Rishi's-picked Bardstown against cocoa. Strong; curated single-barrel meets the brownie with retailer-select character.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with the chocolate brownie — light high-rye and cocoa. Works; Basil Hayden's Toast is the family member that actually lifts this dessert (toast-barrel caramel + cocoa). Standard Basil Hayden's is neutral here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year with the chocolate brownie — aged rye against cocoa. Works; for more interplay with chocolate, Basil Hayden's Toast (in the family) or Weller 107 is the call.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with the chocolate brownie — malted grain against cocoa. Works; for chocolate interplay, Basil Hayden's Toast is the family call.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with the chocolate brownie — Beam's Basil Hayden finished in red wine casks, high-rye mash bill base, 80-proof softer approach. Excellent; BH Red Wine Cask's high-rye + red-wine-finish is the featured rye-forward wine-finished-chocolate pairing — the high-rye base + red-wine fruit adds dimension where AE's wheated-style port-finish doesn't reach.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast with the chocolate brownie — toast-barrel caramel and marshmallow notes against cocoa and fudge. The toast character is built for this dessert; the most remarkable Basil Hayden's for chocolate brownie. Distinct from Weller 107's bigger-body version — lighter, more toasted-sweet. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bee's Knees": "Bee's Knees' citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Bee's Knees on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Beefeater Gin for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Belvedere Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with the chocolate brownie — heritage blend against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with the chocolate brownie — 103-proof richer Blanton's against cocoa. Works; the extra depth gives the brownie more than Blanton's Original does.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with the chocolate brownie — 93-proof single-barrel against cocoa. Works; the balance is slightly underserved by the brownie — reach for Blanton's Gold's richer profile.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's SfB with the chocolate brownie — cask-strength Blanton's against cocoa. Excellent; Blanton's SfB's distinctive character meets cocoa at barrel-proof register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with the chocolate brownie — 10th-Pact milestone against cocoa. Strong; decennial release meets the brownie at milestone-collector register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with the chocolate brownie — Pact 8 annual release against cocoa. Strong; master-blender complexity meets the brownie at annual-Pact register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the chocolate brownie — Pact 9 wine-cask against cocoa. Strong; fruit-cask meets the brownie at annual-Pact register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Bluecoat Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Bodega Noemia A Lisa for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Bombay Dry Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Bombay Sapphire Gin for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow and the brownie -- the entree yields to the dessert, the meal arc reads on-key. Works; the bookends compose neutrally.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bone-In Filet": "The bone-in filet closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the marrow-laced finish yields to dense cocoa fudge, the warm vanilla lifting the close. Strong; bone-depth into chocolate-depth, the meal arc reads thoughtful.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Booker's": "Booker's with the chocolate brownie — uncut/unfiltered Beam against cocoa. Excellent; Booker's 120-130 proof and uncut character is the featured barrel-proof-chocolate pairing — the intensity of both meet at the same register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Botanist Gin": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Botanist Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, rum-infused cherries, orange, bitters. The bourbon's vanilla-caramel meets the brownie's fudge base but the cocktail wasn't built for dessert — the Espresso Old Fashioned is. Works if the guest wants to continue the cocktail; otherwise route to coffee cocktails or tawny.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with chocolate brownie — house-pick wheated against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Weller Millennium is the call.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the chocolate brownie — 90-proof Virginia small-batch against cocoa. Works; smooth character meets the brownie cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bowmore 12": "Bowmore 12 with the chocolate brownie — the sherry cask finish gives the dram a dried-fruit depth that threads into the cocoa. Softer, more balanced than Laphroaig's version; the easier pour for guests less committed to a full peat experience. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Braida Montebruna Barbera for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Broccoli Cheddar": "Broccoli Cheddar and the brownie -- cream-and-sugar build the close, the meal finishes generous. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Broccolini": "The broccolini and the brownie -- the broccolini's tender green stalks hands off cleanly to the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: acid plays against sweetness, the close stays unburdened. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich's progressive unpeated Islay leans against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the malt-and-oak threads the brownie, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the dessert. Works; the spicy-sweet character gives the dessert a Barton frame.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly and the brownie -- savory-into-sweet, the classic close lands intact. Works; the meal closes cleanly from the side.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with the chocolate brownie — flagship oak against cocoa. Works; for more interplay, Weller 107 or Weller Millennium for wheated, or Eagle Rare 10 for non-wheated.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Buffalo Trace Cream. Excellent; bourbon-caramel-cream threads chocolate at deeper register than Baileys — bourbon depth meets cocoa warmth.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with the chocolate brownie — high-rye against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Burrata": "The burrata and the brownie -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy bookends with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Butternut Squash": "Butternut Squash and the brownie -- two sweet closes, the meal piles sugar without contrast. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cade Cabernet Sauvignon's Howell Mountain Cab character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Cade Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila's rested-agave body sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the brownie, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; distinctive ceremony over-specified.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Campari": "Campari with the chocolate brownie — ruby bitter against cocoa. Strong; orange-and-chocolate is the resonance, though Campari is lighter on dessert than the sipping amari.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend leans against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the smooth Canadian register carries the brownie, and the light caramel edge frames the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Canadian Club 43 Year": "Canadian Club 43 Year's Canadian whisky character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the Canadian whisky weight matches the brownie, and the light caramel edge frames the dessert. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's spiced-vanilla character lifts the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the crisp body underclubs the dessert, and the oak frames the cream. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the smooth Canadian register carries the brownie, and the light-grain note brightens the dessert. Works; doesn't drive.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Casa LBV Port": "Late Bottled Vintage Port — Casa LBV, 4-6 year cask, dark cherry, plum, smooth moderate structure, entry-port approachable. The dark cherry wraps around fudge-brownie cocoa and the smooth structure handles whipped-chocolate frosting. Peak LBV for chocolate brownie — approachable port lineup on the dense bake. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's earthen-pit smoke profile sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the espadín-and-smoke body wraps the brownie, and the wood-smoke note underlines the dessert. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab — concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The cocoa-mocha profile meshes directly with chocolate-brownie fudge and the opulent fruit rounds out the dessert. Works as iconic Napa Cab for brownie — Cab-on-chocolate is an established classic the crowd-pleasing Caymus name plays into.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Chambord": "Chambord with a chocolate brownie — the raspberry-chocolate pairing is a dessert menu standard for a reason. Black-raspberry sweetness picks up the brownie's cocoa depth and the acidity balances the sugar. One of the cleanest liqueur-plus-dessert moves on the card. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Chilean Seabass": "The seabass closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the butter-finished silky fish yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Strong; rich-into-rich, the meal closes the way the table came for.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Chopin Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with the chocolate brownie — sweeter American brandy against cocoa. Excellent; Christian Bros's value-sweet profile finds chocolate naturally — the democratic brandy-cocoa pairing when Hennessy feels over-specified.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Château Batailley": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Château Beaucastel": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Château Beaucastel belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Château Gombaude-Guillot on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Château Haut Segottes": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Château de Rouillac": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Château de Rouillac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Clam Chowder": "Clam Chowder and the brownie -- rich dairy yields to dessert weight, the close composes thickly. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Clase Azul Anejo": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Clase Azul Anejo. Excellent; the sherry-butt-finished dried-fruit-butterscotch character threads chocolate at luxury-icon register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango with the chocolate brownie — Durango-edition ceramic luxury against cocoa. The ultra-premium extra añejo meets the brownie with ceremonial Clase Azul authority. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold with the chocolate brownie — 24K gold ceremonial blend against cocoa. The joven-plus-mezcal blend gives the brownie ceremonial Clase Azul authority with distinctive blend character. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's earthen-pit smoke profile reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the smoky agave register handles the brownie, and the earthy backbone threads the dessert. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata's silky Jalisco blanco with ceramic-decanter presentation falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Clase Azul Plata for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado's ceramic-icon reposado character leans against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the brownie, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra with the chocolate brownie — 5-year top-tier Clase Azul against cocoa. The Ultra's aged depth meets the brownie at the absolute ceremonial register; one of the menu's most layered dessert pairings. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep with the chocolate brownie — 90-proof Beam wheated against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Clos Petit Ona Grenache belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's rested-agave body sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the oak-aged tequila body threads the brownie, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; the wine-cask notes don't compete with the dish's sweetness effectively.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Contratto Vermouth": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Contratto Vermouth. Excellent; the Asti vermouth's wine-botanical profile threads chocolate at aperitivo register — lower-proof, softer pairing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light spirit can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a richer pour like an aged rum or tawny. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Corazon Blanco belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado's aged-agave character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the oak-aged tequila body threads the brownie, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; ceremonial depth is composed with the dessert.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Corazon Sazerac": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Corazon Sazerac. Excellent; ex-rye-barrel spice threads chocolate at Expresiones register — rye-spice-cocoa through aged agave.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Corazon Stagg": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Corazon Stagg. Excellent; ex-GTS-barrel caramel-oak-bold-sweetness threads chocolate at intense register — bourbon-character meets cocoa through aged agave.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Corazon Weller": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Corazon Weller. Excellent; wheated-soft-sweet-bourbon threads chocolate at rounded register — softer than Stagg, sweeter than Sazerac — the balanced Expresiones dessert move.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Corazón del Sol Malbec for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Corpse Reviver": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Corpse Reviver for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier with the chocolate brownie — drier oak-forward cognac against cocoa. Excellent; Courvoisier's drier finish lets cocoa lead rather than blending with it — distinct from Hennessy's sweeter-marriage approach.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Cowboy Ribeye": "The cowboy ribeye closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the marbled char-and-fat yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Excellent; bold-into-bold, the meal closes the way the table came for.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Crab Cake": "The crab cake alongside the brownie -- the delicate course resolves into the close, the meal stays unburdened. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brownie, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach and the brownie -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Creamy Potato": "Creamy Potato and the brownie -- rich cream gives way to sweet, the close lands generously. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the brownie, and the easy Canadian register softens the dessert. Works; smooth gentle match.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Cucumber Gimlet": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Cucumber Gimlet for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brownie, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the dessert. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's Loire Valley Pinot Noir flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge lands on the steak course — not here.",
