  "Chocolate Brownie|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with the chocolate brownie — spicy-sweet 94-proof against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rye-and-oak weight handles the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Elk Cove Five Mountain belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Elk Cove Pinot Blanc lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee with the chocolate brownie — namesake single-barrel against cocoa. Works; balance is slightly underserved — Blanton's Gold or E.H. Taylor bring more depth.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Empress 1908 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Epoch Ingenuity": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Epoch Ingenuity lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Escargot": "Escargot and the brownie -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Espresso Martini": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Peak coffee-chocolate pairing — the espresso and coffee liqueur meet the dense fudge brownie's cocoa directly, and Averna's bitterness cuts the chocolate frosting's sweetness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The espresso-chocolate-hazelnut stack meets the dense fudge brownie and cocoa frosting perfectly; brown sugar ties the finish. Peak dessert-cocktail pour — built for chocolate desserts.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Evening Land Seven Springs belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Chocolate Brownie: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Dark chocolate and spice echo the bold red's fruit, but the tannin fights the sugar unless the dessert is dry-cocoa-bitter. Works when the dessert leans bitter rather than sweet.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Far Niente Chardonnay belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Faroe Island Salmon": "The salmon closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the pan-finished Atlantic flesh yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Faust Napa Valley Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca with the chocolate brownie — nuclear menthol-saffron against cocoa. Excellent; cocoa's bitterness tolerates Branca's medicinal depth in a way no other dessert on the menu does.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta with the chocolate brownie — mint-forward Fernet cousin against cocoa. Excellent; mint and chocolate is the classic pairing, and Menta brings it with Branca's backbone but softer entry. The one amaro where this dessert jumps tier.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Filet Mignon": "The filet closes with the brownie -- savory gives way to sweet, the close lands cleanly. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Fireball Whisky": "Chocolate Brownie: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Fisher Unity Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the chocolate brownie — 104-proof premium Four Roses against cocoa. Works; the concentrated character meets the brownie at premium register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label with the chocolate brownie — 80-proof entry against cocoa. Works; for more interplay, Weller 107, Weller Millennium, or Basil Hayden's Toast.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with the chocolate brownie — craft bourbon against cocoa. Works; boutique character meets brownie cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa meets Fox & Oden is a Detroit-based Black-owned whiskey brand with Double Oaked finish (second char intensifies vanilla, toasted oak, heavy caramel). Fox & Oden Double Oaked's intensified-caramel + cocoa is the featured Double-Oaked-chocolate pairing at distinctive-brand register — the second-char caramel amplifies cocoa with Detroit-brand distinction. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Frangelico": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Frangelico. Excellent; the hazelnut-cocoa profile runs directly into chocolate — this is Nutella in liquid form, at Piedmont-liqueur register. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Freemark Abbey Bosché belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|French 75": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the French 75 for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|French Onion": "French Onion and the brownie -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Frias Block 5": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Piedmont Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee, gentle sweetness. The bubbles cut fudge density and peach-honey softens cocoa bitterness. Strong alternative when guest wants sparkling rather than coffee or amaro; lighter register for the brownie.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light spirit. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the G4 Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof's añejo-pour depth leans against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the oak-aged tequila body threads the brownie, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; iconic pour over-specified.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the brownie, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the dessert. Works; IW Harper 15 handles dessert density at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|George T. Stagg": "GTS with the chocolate brownie — BTAC ultra-premium against cocoa. Strong; GTS meets cocoa at collector register; Stagg Jr. delivers this pairing at accessible BT register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12's entry Speyside benchmark reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the malt-and-oak threads the brownie, and the heather brightens the dessert. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14: bourbon-barrel-reserve Speyside. Excellent on chocolate brownie; vanilla-sweet bourbon-finish frames cocoa at Speyside register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Glenfiddich 18": "Chocolate Brownie: warm chocolate meets Glenfiddich 18. Excellent; aged sherry-bourbon-cask Speyside brings dried-fruit-cocoa depth to the brownie at aged-Speyside register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23: Cuvée-finished 23-year Speyside luxury. Excellent on chocolate brownie; champagne-cask depth frames cocoa at Speyside-luxury register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the Highland register carries the brownie, and the heather brightens the dessert. Works; own-plus-sourced covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the scotch backbone matches the brownie, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; the 10-year depth gives the dessert substantial premium frame.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brownie, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; the sweetness is a touch much for the salad's simplicity.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brownie, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; fruit-forward interesting but doesn't drive.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "10-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's entry-point, dried apricot, walnut, toffee, gentle spice, smooth finish. The toffee wraps around fudge-brownie cocoa depth and the walnut plays with whipped-chocolate frosting. Peak Tawny for chocolate brownie — Tawny smooths the cocoa intensity. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "20-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's step-up, concentrated dried fruit, roasted hazelnut, orange peel, silky long finish. The concentrated dried fruit wraps around fudge-brownie cocoa and the roasted hazelnut plays with whipped-chocolate frosting. Peak step-up Tawny for chocolate brownie — complexity handles the cocoa intensity. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "* Graham's 2017 Vintage Port -- vintage port, structured dark-fruit, cellar-worthy. The Sauternes-or-Port body carries the brownie, and the apricot-and-marmalade note underlines the dessert. Serious peak dessert pour. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the silver-spirit register lifts the brownie, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the dessert. Works; refined character is slightly over-specified for the dish.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Grand Marnier": "Chocolate Brownie: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Gray Whale Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Green Chartreuse": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Green Chartreuse. Excellent; the 130-botanical herbal punch cuts through chocolate at high-proof register — Carthusian clarity meets cocoa depth.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Grey Goose Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Grilled Caesar": "The Caesar and the brownie -- the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge bookends with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Gumbo": "The gumbo and the brownie -- the gumbo's spiced creole stew bookends with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the HDW 100 Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 Year's lightly-peated Hakushu character sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the brownie, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the dessert. Works; own-plus-sourced over-specified.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel with the chocolate brownie — 88.9-proof BT single-barrel against cocoa. Works; the complex profile meets the brownie cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Hangar 1 Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Head Fake": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build finds neutral with the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the chocolate brownie — 20yr aged HH against cocoa. Excellent; HH 20's aged leather-tobacco-dried-fruit is the featured aged-HH-chocolate match — 20 years of aging deepens the cocoa resonance.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the chocolate brownie — 100-proof BIB against cocoa. Works; for more cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast. HH BIB is the sipping-bond answer.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with the chocolate brownie — 10-year 100-proof against cocoa. Works; the aged depth meets the brownie at premium register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Heitz Trailside Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Hendricks Flora Adora's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Hendricks Flora Adora for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Hendricks Gin for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Hendricks Grand Cabaret's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Hendricks Grand Cabaret on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Hendricks Neptunia": "Hendricks Neptunia's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Hendricks Neptunia on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Hendricks Orbium": "Hendricks Orbium's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hendricks Orbium lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy with the chocolate brownie — VS/VSOP benchmark against cocoa. Excellent; Hennessy's vanilla-and-oak marries cocoa cleanly; the dried-fruit notes build a bridge to chocolate dessert that doesn't overpower.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB with the chocolate brownie — 10-year bonded against cocoa. Works; the aged BIB depth meets the brownie cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Japanese Harmony's blended Japanese harmony finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the brownie, and the green-fruit edge brightens the dessert. Works; bonded weight overpowers this dish.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Honey Roasted Carrots": "The honey carrots alongside the brownie -- both desserts pull the same lever, the table doubles the sugar. Works; the contrast holds, neither course overshadows the other.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|House Wedge": "The wedge alongside the brownie -- sugar and umami share the table, the contrast carries the meal. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 with the chocolate brownie — BT-distilled 15yr against cocoa. Strong; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak meets cocoa with distinctive cherry-cocoa resonance.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Il Colle Prosecco Superiore for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Inhibited": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. Designed for this pairing — chocolate liqueur and Kahlúa coffee stack with the brownie's cocoa frosting; Bailey's cream softens the density. Peak dessert-cocktail for chocolate.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Inverroche Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Iron Fish Gin for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon Mezcal Casks with the chocolate brownie — northern MI mezcal-cask bourbon against cocoa. Strong; Ironfish Mezcal's smoke meets cocoa at Michigan-hometown register — smoky-agave + chocolate is distinctive.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa meets A. Smith Bowman Distillery (Fredericksburg VA), Sazerac-owned, port-finished Virginia bourbon, BT-adjacent distillery. Isaac Bowman's Virginia-heritage port-finish meets cocoa with BT-adjacent-distillery character — parallel to AE's Louisville port-finish approach, with Virginia-distillery distinction. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards' silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. J Davies Ferrington Vineyards lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon's Diamond Mountain Cab character flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the bourbon body sits with the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; AE CS handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the bourbon depth settles into the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the Twin Oak's depth slightly overshoots the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the bourbon depth settles into the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the gentle Irish body threads the brownie, and the gentle barley note frames the dessert. Works; boutique character gives the dessert a craft frame.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the chocolate brownie — sea-aged L.E. against cocoa. Excellent; Marian McLain L.E.'s salt-sea character meets cocoa at collector register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the chocolate brownie — sea-aged bourbon against cocoa. Strong; Voyage 23's salt-tinged sea-aged character meets cocoa at ocean-finish register — salt + chocolate is textbook pairing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve with the chocolate brownie — blended elegance against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Weller Millennium bring more interplay with chocolate.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the chocolate brownie — Twin Oak complexity against cocoa. Works; for chocolate interplay reach for Weller 107 or Weller Millennium; the VR Twin Oak is better on meatier plates.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label with the chocolate brownie — aged Beam against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label with the chocolate brownie — 80-proof entry against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Johnnie Walker Black": "Johnnie Walker Black's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the malt-and-oak threads the brownie, and the heather brightens the dessert. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Johnnie Walker Blue": "Johnnie Walker Blue's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brownie, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; AE Triple Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Johnnie Walker Red": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the scotch depth settles into the brownie, and the heather brightens the dessert. Works; boutique-blend meets the brownie cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Jordan — Sonoma Cab, cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body meets chocolate-brownie fudge and the cedar-mint plays against the rich dessert. Works as Jordan for brownie — refined Cab-on-chocolate pairing, though Caymus or Opus peak higher on the opulence axis.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Joseph Mellot Sancerre belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Jubilation by Colgin for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana: Demerara-style rum from Guyana's Diamond Distillery, distilled through traditional wooden stills (Port Mourant and Enmore). Excellent on brownie; the heavy-molasses burnt-sugar character with earthy-woody depth threads chocolate at historical-rum register — this is the wooden-still story in a glass.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad: Trinidadian rum from Angostura's column stills, refined medium-character with caramel notes and moderate ester. Excellent on brownie; the cleaner-refined Trinidad character threads chocolate at medium-aged register — the sibling to the Guyana expression, lighter in register and cleaner in profile.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Kahlua": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Kahlua. Excellent; one of the strong calls on the list.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Kansas City": "The KC closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the savory-grain strip yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla scooping over the top. Strong; the bold strip earns the dense brownie close.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Keenan Chardonnay": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Keenan Chardonnay belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Keenan Mernet": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Keenan Mernet for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye with the chocolate brownie — 10-year allocated KO against cocoa. Works; premium depth meets the brownie at collector register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the chocolate brownie — KO Confiscated against cocoa. Strong; craft-blend meets the brownie at KO narrative register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the approachable Irish character carries the brownie, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the dessert. Works; the intensity overpowers this dish.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Ketel One Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Knickerbocker Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek with the chocolate brownie — 100-proof high-rye against cocoa. Works; for more cocoa interplay, reach for Weller 107 (wheated) or Basil Hayden's Toast (toast-barrel).",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year with the chocolate brownie — aged 100-proof against cocoa. Works; the 12-year depth meets the brownie cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 with the chocolate brownie — 15yr Beam KC against cocoa. Strong; KC 15's aged character meets cocoa at aged-Beam register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 with the chocolate brownie — 18yr Beam aged KC against cocoa. Strong; KC 18's deeper aged character meets cocoa at collector register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with the chocolate brownie — blend against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast are better calls.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the dry rye body wraps the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the Boss Hog intensity meets the brownie at unicorn register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Komos Anejo Reserva. Excellent; the wine-cask-Hungarian-oak character threads chocolate at luxury-reserve register — distinctive cask influence on cocoa.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Komos Crystallino. Excellent; filtered-clear añejo threads chocolate at distinctive register — the Komos-reserva-aged profile minus color.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Añejo with the chocolate brownie — aged Komos against cocoa. The extra añejo depth meets the brownie with Riedel-bottle luxury authority. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado's aged-agave character leans against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the añejo register carries the brownie, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Lagavulin 8": "Lagavulin 8 with the chocolate brownie — vibrant peat meets cocoa in a classic Islay dessert move. Less intense than Laphroaig's version, less sweet than Bowmore's sherried take; the 8-year's brightness plays beautifully against dark sweetness without burying it. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Lail Vineyards Blueprint's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Lail Vineyards Blueprint on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Lalo Silver for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Laphroaig 10": "Chocolate Brownie: the warm, espresso-deep cocoa and dense fudge texture meet Laphroaig 10's iodine, tar, and medicinal finish in the most decisive peated-dessert pairing on the menu. The brownie's bitter-sweet finds its match in Laphroaig's medicinal edge. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch with the chocolate brownie — Heaven Hill wheated against cocoa. Works; for more cocoa interplay, reach for Weller 107 or Weller Millennium.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; one of those is the move. Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's classic non-vintage Champagne brightness clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Le Garenne Rosé": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Le Garenne Rosé lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Le Volte dell'Ornellaia belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Lemon Lavender Gin Martini on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Les Pallières Raciné": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Les Pallières Raciné for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Liberator Gin": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Liberator Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Licor 43": "Chocolate Brownie: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Works as an indulgence.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the liqueur compounds the dessert's sweetness without contrast. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Limoncello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir's Willamette layered Pinot character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Little Book": "Little Book with the chocolate brownie — annual blended cask-strength against cocoa. Excellent; Little Book's chapter-series intensity meets cocoa at barrel-proof register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Loaded Potato": "Loaded Potato and the brownie -- cream-and-sugar build the close, the meal finishes generous. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Lobster Mac": "Lobster Mac and the brownie -- cream-and-sugar build the close, the meal finishes generous. Works; the meal closes cleanly from the side.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year's complex 20yr aged Highland sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the scotch depth settles into the brownie, and the malt edge frames the dessert. Works; boutique-sourced doesn't drive.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the chocolate brownie — premium sourced rye against cocoa. Works; boutique-blend meets the brownie cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Long Road Gin": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Long Road Gin for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's earthen-pit smoke profile sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rustic mezcal weight sits with the brownie, and the earthy backbone threads the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII with the chocolate brownie — luxury icon against cocoa. Strong; warm cocoa is dense enough to engage Louis XIII without being overshadowed — the featured dessert for the bottle if the guest wants one. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Lucano Amaro": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa meets Lucano — Basilicata amaro, floral with anise notes. Lighter than Averna, more distinctive than Montenegro. Anise-and-floral gives the brownie a distinctive digestif frame. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Lunaria Coste di Moro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry with the chocolate brownie — 100% Oloroso-cask Speyside against cocoa. Excellent; the sherry-derived raisin-and-spice marries cocoa with textbook dessert-sherry resonance.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18 with the chocolate brownie — premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside against cocoa. Excellent; Macallan 18's deeper sherry-derived raisin-and-spice layers into cocoa with aged-premium depth.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Macallan Estate": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa meets Estate-grown-oak premium Macallan. Macallan Estate's distinctive oak-derived depth layers into cocoa at premium register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Macauley Petite Syrah lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Macauley Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Mahon Gin": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Mahon Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark with the chocolate brownie — iconic wheated against cocoa. Works; for more interplay, reach for Weller 107 or Weller Millennium.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa and fudge meet Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged vanilla. Works; Cellar Aged's elegance underserves the brownie's boldness. Weller 107 or Weller Millennium for more cocoa interplay.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's sweet coconut-tropical character holds the line with the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye with the chocolate brownie — 16-year Michigan rye against cocoa. Works; extended maturation meets the brownie at home-state premium register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Marc Brédif Chinon's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Marc Brédif Chinon for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Margarita": "Margarita's blanco-and-citrus margarita brightness fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Margarita lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Marimar Estate Christina": "Marimar Estate Christina's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Marimar Estate Christina belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Market Fish": "The market fish closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the rotating clean fish yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Works; the meal closes substantial after the lighter main.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Mata Hari. Excellent; the wormwood-anise profile cuts chocolate at Bohemian-absinthe register — high-proof anise on cocoa.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Bourbon with the chocolate brownie — Michter's single-barrel 10yr against cocoa. Excellent; Michter's 10's depth meets cocoa at prestigious-independent register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye with the chocolate brownie — aged Michter's against cocoa. Works; 10-year polish meets the brownie with premium small-batch depth.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the high-rye spice register matches the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the 12-year depth meets the brownie cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light spirit. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Mijenta Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Milagro Anejo": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Milagro Anejo. Excellent; French-oak-agave threads chocolate at refined-craft register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the barrel-rested body handles the brownie, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam with the chocolate brownie — Sazerac Bronfman tribute blend against cocoa. Strong; Mister Sam's aged-blended character meets cocoa at Seagram's-tribute register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Monkey 47 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brownie, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the dessert. Works; Prohibition narrative wasted on salad.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro with the chocolate brownie — Bologna amaro, vanilla-and-orange forward, the crowd-pleasing classic. More approachable than Nonino, sweeter than Averna. The vanilla-orange character makes the brownie a friendly post-dessert digestif. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum's green-agave-and-cane lift leans against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the bright cane-and-citrus weight carries the brownie, and the sugarcane lift underlines the dessert. Works; premium craft composes cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Muga Reserva": "Muga Reserva's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Muga Reserva for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Mushroom Bisque": "Mushroom Bisque and the brownie -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms and the brownie -- sweet contrast lifts the savory. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum: Jamaican dark rum, Diageo-owned, molasses-heavy workhorse. Excellent on brownie; the molasses-forward profile threads chocolate at cocktail-staple register — this is the dark-rum-on-dessert classic without the Zacapa luxury or Doctor Bird funk.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Negroni": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — equal parts, bitter and herbal. The Campari's edge clashes with chocolate frosting sweetness; the cocktail's weight is wrong for dessert. Keep Negroni for mushrooms or marrow where bitter meets savory depth. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character leans against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch with the chocolate brownie — UK-aged against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast are the firmer calls, but Never Say Die's cask character is worth noting.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with the chocolate brownie — Holland MI beer-barrel finish against cocoa. Excellent; NH Beer Barrel's hop-and-beer character meets cocoa distinctively — beer-and-chocolate is textbook (chocolate stouts etc.), and NH Beer Barrel delivers it in bourbon form.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|New York Sour": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets New York Sour. Excellent; bourbon-base with red-wine float brings fruit-meets-cocoa dimension to the brownie at cocktail register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Nolets Dry Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Not a Paper Plane": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build plays cleanly against the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Oban 14": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the malt-driven body wraps the brownie, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; the light body doesn't overshoot the salad.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Oban 18": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the malt-driven body wraps the brownie, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Ocho Anejo": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Ocho Anejo. Excellent; mineral-driven highland character with whiskey-cask aging threads chocolate at craft-vintage register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the chocolate brownie — Old Elk cigar-pairing bourbon against cocoa. Excellent; Old Elk Cigar Cut's distinctive character meets cocoa at cigar-companion register — chocolate and cigars share a palate vocabulary.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer with the chocolate brownie — emmer-wheat wheated bourbon against cocoa. Excellent; wheated bourbon + chocolate is the category-featured dessert pairing, and Old Emmer brings it with ancient-grain craft distinction.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year with the chocolate brownie — wheated BIB against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast are better calls.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year with the chocolate brownie — 9-year wheated against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with the chocolate brownie — aged high-rye OGD against cocoa. Strong; OGD 16's rye-spice meets cocoa at distinctive register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Opus One": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet. The mocha note echoes chocolate-brownie cocoa directly and the dark fruit sweetens against the dessert. Works as collector Napa Cab blend for chocolate brownie — prestige red-with-chocolate works when guest wants it.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Pablo Sour": "Pablo Sour: Bowdie's signature cocktail. Excellent on chocolate brownie; the build-character threads chocolate at cocktail register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Paloma": "Paloma's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Paloma lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye with the chocolate brownie — unicorn Pappy Rye against cocoa. Works; Van Winkle legacy meets the brownie at allocation register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage 16th Ed with the chocolate brownie — Heaven Hill collector cask-strength against cocoa. Excellent; Parker's Heritage 16th's annual-release intensity meets cocoa at collector register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Patron Anejo": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Patron Anejo. Excellent; American-oak vanilla-caramel threads chocolate at premium-gateway añejo register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver's polished silver tequila with agave-forward lift falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light spirit. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Patron Silver for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Patron XO Cafe": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Patron XO Cafe. Excellent; one of the strong calls on the list.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand with the chocolate brownie — unblended cognac against cocoa. Strong; Ferrand's floral-honey marries cocoa cleanly at craft-premium register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the dry rye body wraps the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th handles the richness at annual-release register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Pineau des Charentes": "Pineau des Charentes's sweet char-honey character sits with the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the dessert-wine sweetness threads the dessert. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pio Cesare Barbaresco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Plymouth Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pommery Cuvée Louise belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Porterhouse": "The Porterhouse closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the dual-character cut yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla scooped over. Strong; both cuts and the warm brownie share the close cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Prime Tartare": "The tartare and the brownie -- the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami bookends with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|RAEN Royal St Robert": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Rainbow Trout": "The trout closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the delicate flesh gives way to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Works; the dense close reads against the lighter main, but the meal builds clean.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the gentle Irish body threads the brownie, and the gentle barley note frames the dessert. Works; distinctive character is worth mentioning tableside, even if the pairing isn't marquee.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the Irish whiskey body sits with the brownie, and the gentle barley note frames the dessert. Works; regional craft at matched register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Gatsby with the chocolate brownie — MGP high-rye 15yr against cocoa. Strong; Remus 15's rye-spice + cherry meets cocoa with distinctive rye-cocoa resonance.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Rémy VSOP with the chocolate brownie — Grande Champagne cognac against cocoa. Excellent; Rémy's elegance layers into cocoa with refined character.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo with the chocolate brownie — distinctive-bottle aged tequila against cocoa. The sun-shaped ceramic presentation elevates the dessert pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reyka Vodka lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique with the chocolate brownie — aged retailer-label against cocoa. Strong; boutique-sourced meets the brownie at retailer-label register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Roast Half Chicken": "The chicken closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the jus-finished bird gives way to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Works; the herbed bird and the substantial brownie share the meal cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The chickpea soup alongside the brownie -- the courses pull from different keys but share the table cleanly. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs' bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Roku Gin": "Roku Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Roku Gin for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum: Guatemalan Solera 23, high-altitude Andean aging, premium-sipping rum with honey-vanilla-caramel register. Excellent on brownie; the solera's honey-caramel thread meets warm chocolate at luxury register — this is the sipping-rum-on-dessert move.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with the chocolate brownie — aged Wild Turkey against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Weller Millennium. Russell's is the sipping-bourbon answer here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Salt-Cured Halibut": "The halibut closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the salt-cured flesh gives way to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Works; the cured main and the dense close share weight on the plate.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Sambuca": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Sambuca. Excellent; sweet-anise-elderflower threads chocolate at Italian register — the coffee-bean tradition (con la mosca) already suggests chocolate-coffee-anise harmony.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|San Simeon Stormwatch": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. San Simeon Stormwatch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The garlic spinach and the brownie -- the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens hands off cleanly to the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: acid and sugar share the table, neither course pulls heavy. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes — Sémillon-Sauvignon Blanc with noble rot (botrytis cinerea), apricot, honey, orange marmalade, luscious unctuous, bright acid. The orange marmalade bridges whipped-chocolate frosting, but botrytis honey and fudge cocoa read parallel rather than complementary. Strong Sauternes for chocolate brownie — Vintage Port handles cocoa harder.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The anise-rye profile fights cocoa; the cocktail was never built for dessert. Works only when guest insists on finishing with the Sazerac; Espresso Martini or tawny ports peak dramatically higher for chocolate.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rye-and-oak weight handles the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; refined rye sits with richness at matched register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for the side — save the rare-cask bottle for the protein course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. Dry red Barolo against warm brownie with vanilla ice cream — the cherry-leather can carry chocolate but the firm tannin clashes with sweetness. Works as unusual savory-to-dessert bridge; a dessert wine peaks higher.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity plays cleanly against the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the dry white underclubs the dessert sugar, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Seafood Tower": "The seafood tower alongside the brownie -- the bright register lifts the sweet, the meal closes clean. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops and the brownie -- the entree yields to the dessert, the meal arc reads on-key. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Seasonal Soup": "The seasonal soup alongside the brownie -- the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Seasonal Vegetables": "The seasonal vegetables alongside the brownie -- a clean call, no friction either way. Works; the contrast holds, neither course overshadows the other.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Shafer 1.5": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Shafer Hillside Select": "Shafer Hillside Select's Stags Leap allocated Cab power flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Shafer Hillside Select for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Shrimp Bisque": "The shrimp bisque alongside the brownie -- cream-and-sugar build the close, the meal finishes generous. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Shrimp Cocktail": "The shrimp cocktail alongside the brownie -- bright meets sweet, the contrast stays light. Works; the bookends compose neutrally.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light spirit can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a richer pour like an aged rum or tawny. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Siete Leguas Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rested-agave depth sits with the brownie, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Alexander Valley Cab — American-oak vanilla, coconut, dill, ripe cassis, plum. The lush body meets chocolate-brownie fudge and the vanilla-dill plays against the rich dessert distinctively. Works as Silver Oak for brownie — American-oak signature Cab-on-chocolate, name-recognition sells the moment.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Simon Family Estate Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Simon Family Estate Reserve on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "Spottswoode Lyndenhurst's Napa estate Cab with structured cassis jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Spottswoode Lyndenhurst for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. St Supéry Élu Meritage belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the chocolate brownie — BT cask-strength against cocoa. Excellent; Stagg Jr.'s intensity meets cocoa at barrel-proof register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Stoli Elit Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, green, light. Completely wrong category — green-herbal white against chocolate fudge is a disaster on every axis. Never pair. Stoneleigh belongs with the wedge salad, shrimp cocktail, or Caesar course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Swordfish": "The swordfish closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the meaty firm steak yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Works; the grilled main and the substantial brownie share weight on the plate.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Tanq 10 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Tanqueray Gin for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Classic Douro Tawny — Taylor Fladgate (founded 1692), smooth balanced, dried fruit, almond, warm clean finish. The almond plays with whipped-chocolate frosting and the smooth balance handles fudge-brownie cocoa. Peak heritage-Tawny for chocolate brownie — 1692 Douro on the dense bake. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Flagship Vintage Port — Taylor Fladgate bottle, extraordinarily rich, dark fruit, chocolate, tobacco, minutes-long evolving finish. The chocolate echoes fudge-brownie cocoa directly and the tobacco plays with whipped-chocolate frosting. Peak flagship-Vintage for chocolate brownie — Taylor's celebrated bottle on the dense bake. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with the chocolate brownie — triple-cask aged against cocoa. The sherry and cognac cask influences especially thread through the brownie with dessert-aged character no other extra añejo matches. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|The BG": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build sits alongside the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|The Happy Wife": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build plays cleanly against the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|The Manhattan": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build reads alongside the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|The Prisoner Red Blend": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour The Prisoner Red Blend on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the smoky char-and-fat yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Excellent; the showpiece into the canonical chocolate close, the meal lands at full register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the chocolate brownie — BTAC barrel-proof against cocoa. Works; uncut intensity meets the brownie at allocated register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the chocolate brownie — chardonnay-cask bourbon against cocoa. Strong; TSM Chardonnay Cask's white-wine character meets cocoa at distinctive register; AE's port-finish is the featured wine-finished-chocolate match.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Titos Vodka": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Titos Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the brownie, and the umami-tinged finish frames the dessert. Works; Remus 15 handles dessert density at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Tomato Basil": "Tomato Basil and the brownie -- acid and sugar share the table, neither course pulls heavy. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Transfusion": "Transfusion's crisp citrus-ginger build holds with the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado's añejo-pour depth leans against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the barrel-rested body handles the brownie, and the soft-spice note softens the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries and the brownie -- sugar and umami share the table, the contrast carries the meal. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s gentle Irish blend leans against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the approachable Irish character carries the brownie, and the soft malt rounds the dessert. Works; private-selection wasted on the salad.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "The tuna closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the sesame-crusted seared-rare flesh gives way to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Works; the lighter ocean-clean main into the dense close, the meal builds.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas and the brownie -- the courses pull from different keys but share the table cleanly. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Venge Family Reserve": "Venge Family Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Venge Family Reserve belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Venge Scout's Honor": "Venge Scout's Honor's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Venge Scout's Honor for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Venge Silencieux": "Venge Silencieux's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Venge Silencieux on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Vesper": "Vesper's crisp citrus-juniper build finds neutral with the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's classic yellow-label Champagne body clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Veuve Clicquot Brut belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto vermouth, Bénédictine, two bitters. The cognac richness meets the brownie's fudge base and Bénédictine's herbal depth plays against cocoa, but the cocktail isn't dessert-built. Works if guest stays with the Vieux Carré; Espresso OF peaks higher.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Villon Cognac": "Villon with the chocolate brownie — contemporary cocktail-ready cognac against cocoa. Excellent; Villon's accessible-sweet profile and saffron notes find chocolate naturally — more dessert-forward than Hennessy's benchmark.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Vin Santo": "Tuscan Vin Santo — Trebbiano-Malvasia straw-dried, sealed-barrel years, amber, honey, dried apricot, walnut, nutty oxidative. The walnut plays with whipped-chocolate frosting and the honey wraps fudge-brownie cocoa. Strong Vin Santo for chocolate brownie — Vintage Port's dark-chocolate intensity fits cocoa harder.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Weller 107": "Weller 107 with the chocolate brownie — higher-proof wheat and cocoa in a bigger-body dessert pairing. The heat and caramel depth lift the brownie in a way Weller Special Reserve or Weller 12 can't; the Weller for dessert-and-bourbon guests. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year with the chocolate brownie — oak and caramel against cocoa. The 12-year's refinement underserves the brownie's boldness; reach for Weller 107 or Weller Millennium for more interplay with chocolate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium with the chocolate brownie — deep honeyed oak meets cocoa and fudge. The Millennium's complexity does more with the brownie than Weller 12 can; strong, and a more interesting pairing than the baseline suggests. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Weller Special Reserve": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa and fudge meet Weller Special Reserve's approachable wheat. Works; for more cocoa interplay, reach for Weller 107 or Weller Millennium.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Wheatley Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the chocolate brownie — aged WP rye against cocoa. Works; 10-year depth meets the brownie cleanly at premium rye register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with the chocolate brownie — Sauternes/Madeira/Port-finished rye against cocoa. Works; the Old World fruit gives the brownie layered depth distinct from straight-aged premium ryes.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with the chocolate brownie — 15-year Vermont-oak-finished rye against cocoa. Works; extended maturation meets the brownie with flagship premium depth.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with the chocolate brownie — barrel-proof allocated WP against cocoa. Works; the Boss Hog intensity meets the brownie at unicorn register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with the chocolate brownie — X Commandments allocated WP against cocoa. Works; 10th-edition meets the brownie at unicorn register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the dry rye body wraps the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; aged character overshoots delicacy.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with the chocolate brownie — craft high-rye against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with the chocolate brownie — fruit-forward bourbon against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay reach for Weller 107 or Weller Millennium; Woodford's fruit is a different pairing logic.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with the chocolate brownie — Brown-Forman KY bourbon with toasted-then-charred second barrel, iconic Double-Oaked benchmark. Excellent; Woodford Double Oaked's iconic benchmark intensified-caramel is the featured Double-Oaked-chocolate pairing — the most recognizable Double-Oaked on the menu delivering the textbook bourbon-and-chocolate pairing.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character leans against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rye spice cuts the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Old World complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rye spice cuts the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; AE Triple Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the Japanese whisky balance frames the brownie, and the umami-tinged finish frames the dessert. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for a side — save the bottle for the main course.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the brownie, and the precision rounds the dessert. Works; distinctive character gives the dessert a unique story-frame.",
  "Chocolate Brownie|Yellow Chartreuse": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Yellow Chartreuse. Excellent; honey-herbal profile threads chocolate at 40% register — gentler than Green's 55% herbal punch.",
  "Chocolate Cake|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon depth settles into the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; 10-year depth overshoots delicacy.",
  "Chocolate Cake|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon body sits with the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Remus 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Chocolate Cake|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the 1881 Napa Valley for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Cake|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. 2 James Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Cake|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the malt-and-oak threads the chocolate cake, and the malt edge frames the dessert. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the malt-driven body wraps the chocolate cake, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; gentle match.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- aged tequila has nothing to bridge into dessert. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Adictivo Cristalino for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the barrel-rested body handles the chocolate cake, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a white wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Alexander Valley Chardonnay lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Alexander Valley Cyrus for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Alexander Valley Homestead Red for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino -- orange peel, grappa-base, bitter herb. The bitter herb edge cuts the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; the pairing lands at register without strain.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the digestif body mirrors the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon body sits with the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; milestone-edition over-specified.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the extra proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge plays cleanly against the cake's layered chocolate — the bitter herb edge cuts dessert sweetness, and the orange peel echoes the chocolate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Asparagus": "The asparagus and the chocolate cake -- the asparagus' green-vegetal edge hands off cleanly to the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: bright meets sweet, the contrast stays light. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The au gratin alongside the chocolate cake -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; the contrast holds, neither course overshadows the other.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's caramel-orange sweetness plays cleanly against the cake's layered chocolate — the bitter herb edge cuts dessert sweetness, and the caramel deepens the cocoa. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Aviation": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Aviation on the steak course instead.",
