  "Chocolate Cake|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the Highland register carries the chocolate cake, and the malt edge frames the dessert. Works; annual-release wasted on salad.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the scotch depth settles into the chocolate cake, and the malt edge frames the dessert. Works; the complex character is wasted on the salad.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the chocolate cake, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the malt-driven body wraps the chocolate cake, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; Discovery complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the chocolate cake, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the dessert. Works; Glenmorangie 18 handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "10-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's entry-point, dried apricot, walnut, toffee, gentle spice. The toffee plays with fudgy chocolate-cake crumb and the walnut bridges buttermilk-dairy depth. Strong Tawny for chocolate cake — Vintage Port's structure handles cocoa-intensity harder.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "20-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's step-up, concentrated dried fruit, roasted hazelnut, orange peel, silky long finish. The concentrated dried fruit wraps around fudgy chocolate crumb and the roasted hazelnut plays with buttermilk-dairy depth. Peak step-up Tawny for chocolate cake — twenty-year complexity on cocoa. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "* Graham's 2017 Vintage Port -- vintage port, structured dark-fruit, cellar-worthy. The dessert-wine register matches the chocolate cake, and the noble-rot register brightens the dessert. One of the great pairings on the list. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the chocolate cake, and the green-agave edge brightens the dessert. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Grand Marnier": "Chocolate Cake: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Gray Whale Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's cask-strength herbal-high-proof sweetness sits with the cake's layered chocolate — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a vodka. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Grey Goose Vodka lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Grilled Caesar": "The Caesar and the chocolate cake -- the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge bookends with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Gumbo": "The gumbo alongside the chocolate cake -- the courses pull from different keys but share the table cleanly. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Cake|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the HDW 100 Vodka for the steak course.",
