  "Chocolate Cake|Titos Vodka": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a vodka. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Titos Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the chocolate cake, and the green-fruit edge brightens the dessert. Works; bold proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Tomato Basil": "Tomato Basil and the chocolate cake -- bright meets sweet, the contrast stays light. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Transfusion": "Transfusion's crisp citrus-ginger build holds with the cake's layered chocolate — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rested-agave depth sits with the chocolate cake, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; aged depth is refined but not a standout.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries and the chocolate cake -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; the meal closes cleanly from the side.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s gentle Irish blend reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the chocolate cake, and the soft malt rounds the dessert. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "After the tuna, the chocolate cake -- the seared-rare yellowfin yields to layered cocoa-and-ganache, the vanilla scoop closing the meal. Works; the lighter main hands off to the substantial close, the table builds toward sweetness.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "The curry and the chocolate cake -- the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth bookends with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Venge Family Reserve": "Venge Family Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Venge Family Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Venge Scout's Honor": "Venge Scout's Honor's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Venge Scout's Honor belongs on the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Venge Silencieux": "Venge Silencieux's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Venge Silencieux lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Vesper": "Vesper's crisp citrus-juniper build sits alongside the cake's layered chocolate — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's classic yellow-label Champagne body clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- brut bubbles and chocolate weight refuse each other — needs sweetness, not crisp acid. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a sparkling. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Veuve Clicquot Brut lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Vieux Carré": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build leans against the cake's layered chocolate — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Villon Cognac": "Villon Cognac's rancio-edged cognac weight sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the chocolate cake, and the oak-and-vanilla edge brightens the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Vin Santo": "Tuscan Vin Santo — Trebbiano-Malvasia straw-dried, sealed-barrel years, amber, honey, dried apricot, walnut, nutty oxidative. The walnut plays with fudgy chocolate-cake crumb and the honey plays with buttermilk-dairy depth. Strong Vin Santo for chocolate cake — Vintage Port's structure handles cocoa-on-cocoa harder.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Weller 107": "Weller 107's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon backbone carries the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; AE CS meets the char at premium wine-finish register.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon backbone carries the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon depth settles into the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; light body doesn't overshoot.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Wheatley Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Chocolate Cake|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the dry rye body wraps the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the spicy-sweet character gives the dessert a Barton frame.",
  "Chocolate Cake|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; extended-aged premium wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Chocolate Cake|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rye-and-oak weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Chocolate Cake|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rye-and-oak weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Chocolate Cake|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the high-rye spice register matches the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; distinctive finish over-specified.",
  "Chocolate Cake|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rye spice cuts the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; bold rye slightly over-specified.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the caramel-spice register matches the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the 10-year depth gives the dessert substantial premium frame.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the caramel-spice register matches the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the extra proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon backbone carries the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; complex profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the dry rye body wraps the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; IW Harper 15 handles dessert density at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rye spice cuts the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; OGD 16 frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the chocolate cake, and the precision rounds the dessert. Works; the pour is a touch ambitious for the salad.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the chocolate cake, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the dessert. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Chocolate Cake|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness holds the line with the cake's layered chocolate — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the rounded sweetness meets the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Asparagus": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality stays alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Beignets": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Chopin Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Bone Marrow": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality reads alongside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding, and the oak frames the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Bone-In Filet": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- clean vodka reads thin against the cut's weight — needs a wine or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Chopin Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Broccoli Cheddar": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Broccolini": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality stays alongside the broccolini's tender green — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Brussels and Belly": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality holds with the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Burrata": "Chopin with burrata — Polish potato vodka, single-ingredient, creamy/round mouthfeel. Excellent; a textbook match that delivers.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Butternut Squash": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality sits beside the squash soup's silky sweet — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Carrot Cake": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Chopin Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Cheesecake": "Chopin with cheesecake — creamy Polish potato against custard. Works; the texture match is natural — creamy vodka with creamy dessert, artisanal finisher.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Chilean Seabass": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality holds the line with the seabass's buttery flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Chocolate Brownie": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Chopin Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Chocolate Cake": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a vodka. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Chopin Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Clam Chowder": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Cowboy Ribeye": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Chopin Vodka belongs on another course.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Crab Cake": "Chopin with crab cake — single-ingredient potato vodka against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Chopin's silky texture doesn't overpower crab and the potato mouthfeel is a distinctive frame no wheat vodka offers.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Creamed Spinach": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Creamy Potato": "Chopin Vodka's unflavored crystalline pour sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the unflavored body sits with the potato soup, and the crisp profile carries the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Creme Brulee": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- clean vodka has nothing to add against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Chopin Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Escargot": "Chopin with escargot — silky Polish potato against garlic-butter snails. Strong; the creamy texture sits with the butter, though Belvedere's pepper cuts the dish sharper.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Faroe Island Salmon": "Chopin with the salmon — single-ingredient potato against oily salmon. Strong; Chopin's creaminess rides salmon's richness as a complementary pairing.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Filet Mignon": "Chopin with the filet — silky potato vodka against tenderloin. Works; vodka remains over-specified for steak, but Chopin's softness is a refined option for guests who insist.",
  "Chopin Vodka|French Onion": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Grilled Caesar": "Chopin with the grilled Caesar — creamy Polish potato against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Chopin softens the char where Ketel's citrus lifts it.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Gumbo": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality reads alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Chopin Vodka|House Wedge": "Chopin with the House Wedge — single-ingredient potato against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; the creamy texture sits with the bleu cheese richness at artisanal register.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Kansas City": "Chopin with the KC — silky potato vodka against an 18oz strip. Avoid; vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Loaded Potato": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Lobster Mac": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Market Fish": "Chopin with the market fish — single-ingredient potato vodka against featured fish. Works; Chopin's softness handles fish at a distinctive textural register.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Mocha Creme": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a vodka. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Chopin Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Mushroom Bisque": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality reads alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Mushrooms": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the caramel meets the nut character. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Chopin Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Porterhouse": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Chopin Vodka for another course.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Prime Tartare": "Chopin with the tartare — creamy potato vodka against raw beef. Strong; Chopin's softness rides tartare's minerality, though Belvedere's Polish sibling lifts it with rye-pepper dimension.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Rainbow Trout": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality lifts the trout's delicate flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Roast Half Chicken": "Chopin with the chicken — creamy Polish potato against roasted poultry. Works; Chopin's silk supports the chicken cleanly.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality stays alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality holds steady against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality sits beside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Seafood Tower": "Chopin with the seafood tower — Polish potato vodka against the raw bar. Strong; Chopin frames the tower with creamy character, though Goose's wheat silkiness is the flagship luxury pairing.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Seared Scallops": "Chopin with seared scallops — creamy Polish potato vodka against scallop sweetness. Excellent; the silky texture mirrors scallop richness with a different softness than Goose's wheat-derived silk.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Seasonal Soup": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Seasonal Vegetables": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality leans against the seasonal garden plate — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Shrimp Bisque": "Chopin with shrimp bisque — creamy potato vodka against cream-shrimp soup. Excellent; the pairing earns its featured slot.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Shrimp Cocktail": "Chopin with shrimp cocktail — potato vodka against cold shrimp. Excellent; Chopin's creaminess is a distinctive frame for the classic cold app — softer than Belvedere's rye sibling.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Swordfish": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality holds steady against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Chopin Vodka|The Tomahawk": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Chopin Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Tomato Basil": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality stays alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Truffle Fries": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality plays cleanly against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the caramel meets the nut character. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality holds steady against the tuna's seared rare crust — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Chopin Vodka|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality plays cleanly against the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Asparagus": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- cognac on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a cognac. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Christian Bros Brandy lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Christian Brothers with the au gratin — value brandy against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Christian Bros handles the richness at approachable register.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Beignets": "Christian Brothers with beignets — sweeter American brandy against fried dough and powdered sugar. Excellent; Christian Bros and casual fried dessert is a natural American-pub pairing — the value brandy finds its register here.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Bone Marrow": "Christian Brothers with bone marrow — sweeter American brandy against umami fat. Strong; Christian Bros's sweetness rides the marrow, though Courvoisier's drier oak cuts it sharper.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Bone-In Filet": "Christian Bros Brandy's rancio-edged cognac weight leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the brandy depth wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the caramel rounds the cut. Works; Ferrand's craft character handles the richness.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Broccoli Cheddar": "Christian Bros Brandy's rancio-edged cognac weight reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the brandy depth wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Broccolini": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- cognac on a green vegetable side is wrong direction — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a cognac. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Christian Bros Brandy on the steak course instead.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Brussels and Belly": "Christian Bros Brandy's eau-de-vie body with oak leans against Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the brandy depth wraps the pork-belly plate, and the dried-fruit note underlines the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Burrata": "Christian Brothers with burrata — sweeter American brandy against fresh dairy. Avoid; brandy is too heavy for burrata's cream.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Butternut Squash": "Christian Bros Brandy's rancio-edged cognac weight finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the cognac register matches the squash bisque, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Carrot Cake": "Christian Brothers with carrot cake — California brandy against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Christian Bros's sweetness meets the raisins and spice at value-workhorse register.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Cheesecake": "Christian Brothers with cheesecake — California American brandy (not cognac), sweeter value workhorse. Excellent; Christian Bros's American sweetness is built for dessert — cheesecake is the featured value-brandy pairing, where the French cognacs aim higher and costlier.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Chilean Seabass": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Christian Bros Brandy for the steak course.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Chocolate Brownie": "Christian Brothers with the chocolate brownie — sweeter American brandy against cocoa. Excellent; Christian Bros's value-sweet profile finds chocolate naturally — the democratic brandy-cocoa pairing when Hennessy feels over-specified.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Chocolate Cake": "Christian Bros Brandy's Limousin-oak cognac register leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the chocolate cake, and the oak-and-vanilla edge brightens the dessert. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Clam Chowder": "Christian Bros Brandy's rancio-edged cognac weight leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the brandy depth wraps the chowder, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Cowboy Ribeye": "Christian Bros Brandy's crisp brandy-caramel character holds the line with the ribeye's cap fat and char — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Crab Cake": "Christian Brothers with crab cake — American brandy against delicate crab. Avoid; brandy weight wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Creamed Spinach": "Christian Brothers with creamed spinach — sweeter American brandy against dairy-rich side. Strong; Christian Bros rides the cream at value register.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Creamy Potato": "Christian Bros Brandy's Limousin-oak cognac register leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the brandy depth wraps the potato soup, and the dried-fruit note underlines the bowl. Works; Rémy's elegance handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Creme Brulee": "Christian Brothers with creme brulee — sweeter American brandy against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Excellent; Christian Bros rides the caramel sweetness at accessible register — where Rémy's elegance edges premium-pairing quality.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Escargot": "Christian Bros Brandy's Limousin-oak cognac register leans against the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the brandy depth wraps the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Faroe Island Salmon": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a cognac. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Christian Bros Brandy belongs on the steak course.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Filet Mignon": "Christian Brothers with the filet — California American brandy against tenderloin. Strong; Christian Bros handles the filet at value register, though Hennessy's benchmark and Courvoisier's drier oak both outperform here.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|French Onion": "Christian Bros Brandy's eau-de-vie body with oak reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the brandy depth wraps the French onion, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Grilled Caesar": "Christian Brothers with the grilled Caesar — sweeter brandy against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Christian Bros meets the char at functional register.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Gumbo": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the eau-de-vie body carries the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; Ferrand's craft character handles the richness.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- cognac on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a cognac. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Christian Bros Brandy lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|House Wedge": "Christian Brothers with the House Wedge — American brandy against iceberg and bleu. Works; Christian Bros's oak is functional on the wedge.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Kansas City": "Christian Brothers with the KC — value California brandy against an 18oz strip. Strong; Christian Bros handles the strip at approachable register; Courvoisier's drier oak is still the featured cognac-KC pairing.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Loaded Potato": "Christian Bros Brandy's rancio-edged cognac weight leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the cognac register matches the loaded potato soup, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Lobster Mac": "Christian Brothers with lobster mac — value brandy against pasta-and-lobster richness. Works; Christian Bros is over-specified for the dish vs Hennessy but handles it functionally.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Market Fish": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a cognac. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Christian Bros Brandy for the steak course.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Mocha Creme": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the brandy depth wraps the mocha creme, and the caramel rounds the dessert. Works; Christian Bros meets the char at functional register.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Mushroom Bisque": "Christian Bros Brandy's eau-de-vie body with oak reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the cognac register matches the mushroom bisque, and the caramel softens the umami weight. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Mushrooms": "Christian Brothers with mushrooms — California brandy against earthy umami. Strong; Christian Bros's sweetness threads the mushrooms at workhorse register.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Christian Brothers with the PB brownie — California brandy against peanut butter and cocoa. Excellent; Christian Bros's sweetness meets the dessert density at value-forward register.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Porterhouse": "Christian Bros Brandy's rancio-edged cognac weight reads alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the brandy depth wraps the dual-cut steak, and the caramel rounds the cut. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Prime Tartare": "Christian Bros Brandy's Limousin-oak cognac register sits at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the Limousin-oak weight handles the tartare, and the caramel threads the umami register. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Rainbow Trout": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a cognac. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Christian Bros Brandy on the steak course instead.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Roast Half Chicken": "Christian Bros Brandy's aged-grape brandy depth reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the cognac register matches the roast chicken, and the caramel rounds the plate. Works; Rémy's refinement is over-specified for the casual dessert.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Christian Bros Brandy's rancio-edged cognac weight reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the cognac register matches the chickpea soup, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Christian Bros Brandy on the steak course instead.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- cognac on a green vegetable side is wrong direction — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a cognac. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Christian Bros Brandy for the steak course.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Seafood Tower": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- cognac weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs sparkling or a crisp white. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Christian Bros Brandy for the steak course.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Seared Scallops": "Christian Brothers with seared scallops — California brandy against scallop delicacy. Avoid; brandy overwhelms scallops.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Seasonal Soup": "Christian Bros Brandy's Limousin-oak cognac register sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the brandy depth wraps the seasonal soup, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Seasonal Vegetables": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- cognac on a green vegetable side is wrong direction — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Christian Bros Brandy on the steak course instead.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Shrimp Bisque": "Christian Bros Brandy's rancio-edged cognac weight reads alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the eau-de-vie body carries the shrimp bisque, and the dried-fruit note underlines the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Shrimp Cocktail": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- cognac is wrong-course energy for the table's opener. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Christian Bros Brandy for the steak course.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Swordfish": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- cognac's aged register pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Christian Bros Brandy lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|The Tomahawk": "Christian Bros Brandy's crisp brandy-caramel character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Tomato Basil": "Christian Bros Brandy's eau-de-vie body with oak sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the brandy depth wraps the tomato basil, and the oak-and-vanilla edge brightens the bowl. Works; Ferrand's craft character handles the richness.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Truffle Fries": "Christian Brothers with truffle fries — American brandy against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Christian Bros's sweetness meets the earthy-rich at value register.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- cognac's aged register pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Christian Bros Brandy for the steak course.",
  "Christian Bros Brandy|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Christian Bros Brandy's rancio-edged cognac weight finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the cognac register matches the curry, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Château Batailley|Asparagus": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Château Batailley|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Pauillac 5ème Cru Classé Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the cedar plays against the lactic richness. Strong classified Bordeaux for au gratin — Pauillac structure on the cheesy side.",
  "Château Batailley|Beignets": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Château Batailley on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Batailley|Bone Marrow": "Pauillac 5ème Cru Classé Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the graphite-cedar plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak classified Bordeaux for marrow — Pauillac structure on the rich starter. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Château Batailley|Bone-In Filet": "Château Batailley's powerful blackcurrant body leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Château Batailley|Broccoli Cheddar": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Château Batailley lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Batailley|Broccolini": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Château Batailley lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Batailley|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Château Batailley|Burrata": "Classified Pauillac Bordeaux against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm-tannin graphite-cedar structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Château Batailley|Butternut Squash": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Château Batailley on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Batailley|Carrot Cake": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Château Batailley|Cheesecake": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Château Batailley|Chilean Seabass": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Château Batailley|Chocolate Brownie": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Château Batailley|Chocolate Cake": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Château Batailley on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Batailley|Clam Chowder": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Château Batailley lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Batailley|Cowboy Ribeye": "Pauillac 5ème Cru Classé Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin, full body. The full body and firm tannin handle ribeye's big marbled build and the cedar plays against the char. Strong classified Bordeaux for ribeye — French Pauillac structure on the bold cut.",
  "Château Batailley|Crab Cake": "Classified Pauillac Bordeaux against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — firm-tannin blackcurrant-graphite structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Batailley for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Château Batailley|Creamed Spinach": "Pauillac 5ème Cru Classé Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the cedar plays against the spinach base. Strong classified Bordeaux for creamed spinach — Pauillac structure reframes the rich steakhouse side.",
  "Château Batailley|Creamy Potato": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Château Batailley on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Batailley|Creme Brulee": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Château Batailley|Escargot": "Château Batailley's bold leather-tobacco cassis plays cleanly against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Château Batailley|Faroe Island Salmon": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Château Batailley|Filet Mignon": "Pauillac 5ème Grand Cru Classé left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin, full body. The firm structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the cedar-graphite plays against escargot-butter. Peak classified Bordeaux for filet — 1855-Classification French on the delicate cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Château Batailley|French Onion": "Château Batailley's bold leather-tobacco cassis holds with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Château Batailley|Grilled Caesar": "Pauillac 5ème Cru Classé Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the cedar plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as classified Bordeaux for grilled Caesar — Pauillac structure on the layered salad.",
