  "Château Batailley|Shrimp Cocktail": "Classified Pauillac Bordeaux against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — firm-tannin red and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Château Batailley|Swordfish": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Château Batailley|The Tomahawk": "Château Batailley's bold leather-tobacco cassis holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the Cab structure carries the bone-in cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Château Batailley|Tomato Basil": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Château Batailley|Truffle Fries": "Pauillac 5ème Cru Classé Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts truffle-fry potato fat and the graphite-cedar plays against the truffle-oil umami. Peak classified Bordeaux for truffle fries — French earth layers with the truffle hit. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Château Batailley|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Château Batailley lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Batailley|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Asparagus": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Château Beaucastel lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône — Beaucastel, all 13 permitted varieties, dark fruit, leather, truffle, Provençal herbs, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts gruyere-cream richness and the truffle plays with the cheese. Works for au gratin — pouring Beaucastel on a dairy side underuses the estate's complexity.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Beignets": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Château Beaucastel lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The starter's fat and umami meet the bold red's dark fruit — both land on the palate with real weight. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Bone-In Filet": "Château Beaucastel's bold earth-leather cassis finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the firm tannin reads cleanly against the lean cut, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Broccoli Cheddar": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Château Beaucastel lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Broccolini": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Burrata": "Never pair Beaucastel with burrata — leather, truffle, and full body clash with fresh cream and the Provençal herbs bury tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Butternut Squash": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Carrot Cake": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Château Beaucastel lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Cheesecake": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Chilean Seabass": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Chocolate Brownie": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Château Beaucastel belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Chocolate Cake": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Clam Chowder": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas flattens the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Cowboy Ribeye": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône — Beaucastel, all 13 permitted varieties, dark fruit, leather, truffle, Provençal herbs, full body, medium tannin. The full body matches Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the leather echoes grilled char-depth. Peak bucket-list Châteauneuf for ribeye — one of France's most celebrated estates. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Crab Cake": "Never pair Beaucastel with crab cake — leather, truffle, and Provençal herbs bury delicate lump crab and the full body fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Creamed Spinach": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône — Beaucastel, all 13 permitted varieties, dark fruit, leather, truffle, Provençal herbs, full body, medium tannin. The full body handles dairy-heavy cream and the Provençal herbs lift spinach-iron. Strong bucket-list Châteauneuf for creamed spinach — Beaucastel complexity on a rich side.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Creamy Potato": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Creme Brulee": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Escargot": "Château Beaucastel's bold earth-leather cassis holds steady against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Faroe Island Salmon": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Filet Mignon": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône — Beaucastel, all 13 permitted varieties, dark fruit, leather, truffle, Provençal herbs, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the truffle plays with escargot-butter. Strong bucket-list Châteauneuf for filet — celebrated estate on the delicate cut.",
  "Château Beaucastel|French Onion": "Château Beaucastel's bold earth-leather cassis plays cleanly against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Tannin and char, umami and dark fruit — the bold red and the Caesar punch at the same level. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Gumbo": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Château Beaucastel on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Beaucastel|House Wedge": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône — Beaucastel, all 13 permitted varieties, dark fruit, leather, truffle, Provençal herbs, full body. The leather plays with blue-cheese funk and the Provençal herbs match bacon-fat char. Works for wedge — cold iceberg mutes the bucket-list complexity, so a lighter Chianti lifts higher.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Kansas City": "Kansas City: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Loaded Potato": "Château Beaucastel's bold earth-leather cassis stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Lobster Mac": "Château Beaucastel's bold earth-leather cassis reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Market Fish": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Château Beaucastel belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Mocha Creme": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Château Beaucastel lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Mushroom Bisque": "Château Beaucastel's bold earth-leather cassis stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the side's mushroom richness pulls the bold red's body; tannin and oak settle into the umami without overshooting. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Château Beaucastel belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Prime Tartare": "Château Beaucastel's bold earth-leather cassis sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Rainbow Trout": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Roast Half Chicken": "Château Beaucastel's bold earth-leather cassis leans against the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas jars against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Seafood Tower": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Château Beaucastel on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Beaucastel with seared scallops — truffle and Provençal herbs overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and the medium tannin fights browned-butter. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Seasonal Soup": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Château Beaucastel on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Seasonal Vegetables": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Shrimp Bisque": "Château Beaucastel's bold earth-leather cassis reads alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Beaucastel with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by full body, leather, and truffle, and the bucket-list complexity has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Swordfish": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Château Beaucastel belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Tomato Basil": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas overwhelms the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the bold red's cassis grip carries the mushroom-and-truffle depth — structured weight on the earthy side. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Beaucastel|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Asparagus": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Château Gombaude-Guillot on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Beignets": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Château Gombaude-Guillot lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Bone Marrow": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The velvety Merlot wraps rendered-marrow unctuousness and truffle echoes beefy-bone depth. Works for marrow — plush Bordeaux reads softer than the char-forward wines that peak on the starter.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Bone-In Filet": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The velvety Merlot wraps butter-tender bone-enhanced filet and the truffle plays with bone mineral depth. Strong Pomerol for bone-in filet — plush Bordeaux on the elevated cut.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Broccoli Cheddar": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Château Gombaude-Guillot on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Broccolini": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overshadows the broccolini's tender green stalks -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Château Gombaude-Guillot belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Burrata": "Never pair Château Gombaude-Guillot with burrata — velvety Pomerol's dark plum and mocha obliterate basil-tomato-fresh-cheese delicacy and milky texture. Lead with Il Colle Prosecco, Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc. Gombaude-Guillot is a steak-and-truffle wine.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Butternut Squash": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Carrot Cake": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Cheesecake": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Chilean Seabass": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Chocolate Brownie": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Château Gombaude-Guillot on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Chocolate Cake": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Château Gombaude-Guillot lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Clam Chowder": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Cowboy Ribeye": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The velvety Merlot handles the 26oz bone-in ribeye, though plush Bordeaux reads softer than marbled-fatty ribeye's char demands. Works for ribeye — Silver Oak or Saffredi fits the fat harder.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Crab Cake": "Never pair Château Gombaude-Guillot with crab cake — plush Pomerol Merlot and velvety weight crush lump-crab delicacy and romesco cream. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or French 75. Gombaude-Guillot belongs on the filet course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Creamy Potato": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Château Gombaude-Guillot on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Creme Brulee": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Château Gombaude-Guillot on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Escargot": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's bold coffee-mocha cassis holds steady against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the Cab structure meets the savory depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Faroe Island Salmon": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Filet Mignon": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic estate on Pomerol's clay plateau (Petrus terroir), 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The velvety texture wraps lean-tender filet and the truffle plays with butter-tender richness. Peak Pomerol for filet — plush Merlot on the delicate cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|French Onion": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's bold coffee-mocha cassis holds with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee meets the caramel. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Grilled Caesar": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The truffle plays with parm-char and anchovy-lemon, though plush Merlot reads heavy for the salad. Works for grilled caesar — Chablis or Sancerre fits the greens cleaner.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Gumbo": "Gumbo: the Gumbo's smoky, spiced heartiness with tomato and bold seasoning meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Bold red meets rich, caramelized depth — the wine's dark fruit threads through the savory base. A winning warmth-on-warmth pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit crowds the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Château Gombaude-Guillot belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|House Wedge": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The velvety texture reads heavy against iceberg-and-blue-cheese simplicity, though mocha meets the dressing's bite. Works for wedge — Le Garenne Rosé fits the salad cleaner.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Kansas City": "Strong tier. The Pomerol's truffle and dark plum catch the strip's fat cap and charred edges, while the biodynamic estate's velvety Merlot-Cab Franc mocha finish softens the aggressive sear. Not as tannic as the Napa Cabs — a gentler right-bank read on a classic American cut.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Loaded Potato": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's bold coffee-mocha cassis sits beside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Lobster Mac": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The velvety texture wraps the dairy-rich lobster-cheese-cream build and the mocha plays with the indulgent weight. Peak Pomerol for lobster mac — plush Merlot handles the richest side. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Market Fish": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Château Gombaude-Guillot belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Mocha Creme": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Château Gombaude-Guillot on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Mushroom Bisque": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's bold coffee-mocha cassis plays cleanly against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Mushrooms": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The truffle note echoes mushroom umami directly and velvety Merlot wraps garlic-butter glaze. Works for mushroom side — plush Pomerol on the earthy side where Scavino Barolo peaks harder.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Château Gombaude-Guillot belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Porterhouse": "Strong tier. Two cuts, two reads — the Pomerol's velvety Merlot-Cab Franc handles the filet side beautifully, and its truffle-plum core absorbs the strip's char. Biodynamic Bordeaux softer than a Napa Cab, so it flexes across the porterhouse without dominating either half.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Prime Tartare": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's bold coffee-mocha cassis plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Rainbow Trout": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Château Gombaude-Guillot on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Roast Half Chicken": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's bold coffee-mocha cassis reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overwhelms the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Château Gombaude-Guillot belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overshadows the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Seafood Tower": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Château Gombaude-Guillot belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Château Gombaude-Guillot with seared scallops — plush Pomerol Merlot's dark plum and mocha crush clean-seared shellfish sweetness. Lead with Raventós Cava, Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé. Route Gombaude-Guillot to the filet.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Seasonal Soup": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Château Gombaude-Guillot belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Seasonal Vegetables": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overshadows the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Château Gombaude-Guillot belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Shrimp Bisque": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The velvety Merlot bridges tomato-cream bisque weight and the mocha plays with shellfish depth. Strong Pomerol for bisque — plush Bordeaux handles the rich soup unconventionally.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Château Gombaude-Guillot with shrimp cocktail — plush Pomerol Merlot's velvety weight overshadows cold poached shrimp and cocktail-sauce horseradish. Lead with Veuve Clicquot, Raventós Cava, or French 75. Gombaude-Guillot lives on the red-meat course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Swordfish": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|The Tomahawk": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's bold coffee-mocha cassis holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the concentrated tannin grips the marbled cut. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Tomato Basil": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Truffle Fries": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The truffle in the wine meets the truffle-oil fries directly and velvety Merlot plays with parmesan weight. Strong Pomerol for truffle fries — truffle-on-truffle alignment, French Bordeaux on the side.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Château Gombaude-Guillot belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Gombaude-Guillot|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Château Gombaude-Guillot lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Asparagus": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the clayey earth plays against the lactic richness. Strong right-bank Bordeaux for au gratin — Merlot-led roundness on the cheesy side.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Beignets": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Château Haut Segottes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Bone Marrow": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the truffle plays against the smoky mineral depth. Strong right-bank Bordeaux for marrow — Merlot-led approachability on the rich starter.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Bone-In Filet": "Château Haut Segottes's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Broccoli Cheddar": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Broccolini": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Brussels and Belly": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts pork-belly fat and the truffle plays against charred brussels. Strong right-bank Bordeaux for Brussels and Belly — Merlot-led roundness cuts through the rich starter.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Burrata": "Right-bank Bordeaux against burrata's creamy-delicate center — plum-truffle Merlot overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Butternut Squash": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Carrot Cake": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Château Haut Segottes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Cheesecake": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Château Haut Segottes on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Chilean Seabass": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Chocolate Brownie": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Chocolate Cake": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Clam Chowder": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Château Haut Segottes on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Cowboy Ribeye": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin, medium body. The medium body works against ribeye's big marbled build though soft tannin reads rounder than firm-tannin left-bank on the cut. Strong right-bank Bordeaux for ribeye — approachable French on the bold cut.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Crab Cake": "Right-bank Bordeaux against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — even soft-tannin Merlot-led Grand Cru buries the shellfish's sweet delicacy. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Creamed Spinach": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the clayey earth plays against the spinach base. Peak right-bank Bordeaux for creamed spinach — Merlot-led roundness on the rich side. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Creamy Potato": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Creme Brulee": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Escargot": "Château Haut Segottes's cherry-earth tannin matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Faroe Island Salmon": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Filet Mignon": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — Merlot-dominant, plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin, medium body. The soft tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the truffle plays against escargot-butter. Peak right-bank Bordeaux for filet — Grand Cru Merlot on the delicate cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|French Onion": "Château Haut Segottes's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Grilled Caesar": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the truffle plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as right-bank Bordeaux for grilled Caesar — Merlot-led roundness on the layered salad.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Gumbo": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|House Wedge": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as right-bank Bordeaux for the wedge — Merlot-led roundness; whites and rosés peak higher.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Kansas City": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold strip and the truffle plays against beefy char. Peak right-bank Bordeaux for the strip — Grand Cru Merlot-led on the American steakhouse cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Loaded Potato": "Château Haut Segottes's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Lobster Mac": "Château Haut Segottes's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Market Fish": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Château Haut Segottes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Mocha Creme": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Château Haut Segottes on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Mushroom Bisque": "Château Haut Segottes's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the side's mushroom richness pulls the bold red's body; tannin and oak settle into the umami without overshooting. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Château Haut Segottes on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Porterhouse": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the truffle plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong right-bank Bordeaux for Porterhouse — approachable French on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Prime Tartare": "Château Haut Segottes's cherry-earth tannin matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Rainbow Trout": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Roast Half Chicken": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the Bordeaux body wraps the roast chicken, and the cassis-and-tobacco edge frames the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Château Haut Segottes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Château Haut Segottes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overshadows the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Château Haut Segottes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Seafood Tower": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Seared Scallops": "Right-bank Bordeaux against scallop sweetness — plum-truffle-earth Grand Cru buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Seasonal Soup": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Seasonal Vegetables": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Château Haut Segottes on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Shrimp Bisque": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit finds neutral with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the Bordeaux body wraps the shrimp bisque, and the cassis-and-tobacco edge frames the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Shrimp Cocktail": "Right-bank Bordeaux against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — Merlot-led red and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Swordfish": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Château Haut Segottes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|The Tomahawk": "Château Haut Segottes's cherry-earth tannin sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Tomato Basil": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Château Haut Segottes on the steak course instead.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the mushroom's umami depth meets the bold red's structured tannin; dark fruit threads the truffle-and-earth without crowding. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Château Haut Segottes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château Haut Segottes|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Château Haut Segottes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Asparagus": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Château de Rouillac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Pessac-Léognan left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, tobacco, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the cedar plays against the lactic richness. Strong left-bank Bordeaux for au gratin — French structure on the classic cheesy side.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Beignets": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Château de Rouillac for the steak course.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Bone Marrow": "Pessac-Léognan left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, tobacco, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the cedar plays against the smoky mineral depth. Works as left-bank Bordeaux for marrow — French structure on the rich starter; bolder Napa Cabs peak higher.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Bone-In Filet": "Left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, and tobacco with firm tannin and a structured, old-world profile. The bone-in filet has enough richness from the bone and bold marble for the firmer tannin to grab. A non-Napa alternative worth offering to guests who know Bordeaux. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Broccoli Cheddar": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Château de Rouillac for the steak course.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Broccolini": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Château de Rouillac for the steak course.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Burrata": "Left-bank Bordeaux against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm-tannin blackcurrant-cedar overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Château de Rouillac for the steak course.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Butternut Squash": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Château de Rouillac for the steak course.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Carrot Cake": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Château de Rouillac on the steak course instead.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Cheesecake": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Château de Rouillac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Chilean Seabass": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Château de Rouillac for the steak course.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Chocolate Brownie": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Château de Rouillac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Chocolate Cake": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Château de Rouillac for the steak course.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Clam Chowder": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Château de Rouillac for the steak course.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Cowboy Ribeye": "Pessac-Léognan left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, tobacco, firm tannin, medium body. The firm tannin handles ribeye's big marbled build though medium body reads leaner than full-bodied Napa Cabs on the cut. Strong left-bank Bordeaux for ribeye — French structure on the bold cut.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Crab Cake": "Left-bank Bordeaux against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — firm-tannin blackcurrant-cedar structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Château de Rouillac for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Château de Rouillac|Creamed Spinach": "Pessac-Léognan left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, tobacco, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the cedar-graphite plays against the spinach base. Strong left-bank Bordeaux for creamed spinach — French structure reframes the rich steakhouse side.",
