  "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal|Seasonal Vegetables": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's wood-fire mezcal body leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the smoky agave register handles the seasonal vegetables, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal|Shrimp Bisque": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the wood-fire body meets the shrimp bisque, and the earthy backbone threads the bowl. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal|Shrimp Cocktail": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- mezcal smoke obliterates delicate shellfish — needs sparkling or sancerre. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a mezcal. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal on the steak course instead.",
  "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal|Swordfish": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's smoky agave character doubles down on the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- mezcal smoke and the seared crust double up smoky char without contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a mezcal. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk: 36oz bone-on showpiece meets Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal. Excellent; the ceramic-decanter ceremony matches the dramatic cut — both are occasions, both are plated visually, both are built to be noticed.",
  "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal|Tomato Basil": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a mezcal. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal on the steak course instead.",
  "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal|Truffle Fries": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's bold agave-fruit-smoke character matches the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the agave smoke layers into the rich side, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
