  "Clase Azul Ultra|Grilled Caesar": "Clase Azul Ultra's aged-agave character leans against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the aged-agave weight wraps the grilled Caesar, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Gumbo": "Clase Azul Ultra's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the aged-agave weight wraps the gumbo, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Clase Azul Ultra's barrel-aged tequila weight finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the barrel-rested body handles the honey carrots, and the oak threads the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|House Wedge": "Clase Azul Ultra's rested-agave body finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the aged-agave weight wraps the wedge, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; Ultra's ceremonial depth composed with this dish.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Kansas City": "Clase Azul Ultra with the KC — 5-year top-tier against the strip. Strong; the Ultra's extended aging handles the strip with collector-grade ceremonial authority.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Loaded Potato": "Clase Azul Ultra's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the rested-agave depth sits with the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Lobster Mac": "Clase Azul Ultra's aged-agave character finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the rested-agave depth sits with the lobster mac, and the soft-spice note softens the side. Works; luxury ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Market Fish": "Clase Azul Ultra's aged-agave character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- aged-agave weight buries the delicate flesh — agave register is wrong for fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Clase Azul Ultra for the steak course.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Mocha Creme": "Clase Azul Ultra with mocha creme — 5-year Ultra against coffee-chocolate. Works; top-tier aged depth finds mocha at collector register.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Mushroom Bisque": "Clase Azul Ultra's aged-agave character sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the oak-aged tequila body threads the mushroom bisque, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Mushrooms": "Clase Azul Ultra with the mushrooms — top-tier 5-year Clase Azul against earthy umami. The Ultra's extended maturation layers into mushroom depth at collector-grade register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet top-tier 5-year. Works; Ultra's ceremonial depth composed with dessert.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Porterhouse": "Clase Azul Ultra with the porterhouse — top-tier Clase Azul across strip and filet. Works; 5-year Ultra over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Prime Tartare": "Clase Azul Ultra's ultra-luxury-añejo character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Rainbow Trout": "Clase Azul Ultra's aged-agave character clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Clase Azul Ultra belongs on the steak course.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Roast Half Chicken": "Clase Azul Ultra's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the roast chicken, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the plate. Works; Ultra's ceremonial depth composed with this dish.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Clase Azul Ultra's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak-aged tequila body threads the chickpea soup, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; the bold pour cuts the glaze but is slightly much for the small plate.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Clase Azul Ultra's aged-agave character clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Clase Azul Ultra on the steak course instead.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Clase Azul Ultra's aged-agave character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the garlic spinach, and the cooked-agave note underlines the side. Works; aged depth is refined but not a standout.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Seafood Tower": "Clase Azul Ultra's aged-agave character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- añejo weight has no foil against cold shellfish — needs sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Clase Azul Ultra lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Seared Scallops": "Clase Azul Ultra with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against top-tier ceremonial. Avoid; save the Ultra.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Seasonal Soup": "Clase Azul Ultra's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the añejo register carries the seasonal soup, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Seasonal Vegetables": "Clase Azul Ultra's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rested-agave depth sits with the seasonal vegetables, and the caramel-agave register brightens the side. Works; the bold pour cuts the glaze but is slightly much for the small plate.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Shrimp Bisque": "Clase Azul Ultra's barrel-aged tequila weight finds neutral with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the barrel-rested body handles the shrimp bisque, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Shrimp Cocktail": "Clase Azul Ultra with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against top-tier ceremonial. Avoid; save the Ultra.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Swordfish": "Clase Azul Ultra's aged-agave character fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the cask-aged agave register pulls against the savory sear. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Clase Azul Ultra for the steak course.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|The Tomahawk": "Clase Azul Ultra: ultra-premium 5-year extra añejo, hand-painted platinum-accented decanter. Excellent on the Tomahawk; ceremonial agave meets ceremonial beef at luxury-icon register — save the bottle. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Tomato Basil": "Clase Azul Ultra's añejo-pour depth leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the oak-aged tequila body threads the tomato basil, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; Ultra over-specified.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Truffle Fries": "Clase Azul Ultra with truffle fries — top-tier ceremonial against truffle-parmesan. The 5-year Ultra gives the side collector-grade luxury frame. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Clase Azul Ultra's aged-agave character fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the cask-aged agave register pulls against the savory sear. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; the better calls are above. Clase Azul Ultra belongs on the steak course.",
  "Clase Azul Ultra|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Clase Azul Ultra's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the curry, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; distinctive ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Clermont Steep|Asparagus": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon depth settles into the asparagus, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; the unique maturation story adds interest to a gentle dessert match.",
  "Clermont Steep|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Clermont Steep with au gratin potatoes — Beam wheated against cheese-and-cream. Strong; approachable wheated match.",
  "Clermont Steep|Beignets": "Clermont Steep's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the oak depth lays under the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; IW Harper 15 handles dessert density at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Clermont Steep|Bone Marrow": "Clermont Steep with bone marrow — Beam wheated against umami fat. The wheated profile meets the starter at the approachable register; Jim Beam's answer to the wheated style. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Clermont Steep|Bone-In Filet": "Clermont Steep's crisp caramel-char character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth carries the cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Clermont Steep|Broccoli Cheddar": "Clermont Steep's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Clermont Steep|Broccolini": "Clermont Steep's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon weight handles the broccolini, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; private-selection wasted on the salad.",
  "Clermont Steep|Brussels and Belly": "Clermont Steep with Brussels + Belly — Beam wheated meets maple-glazed pork belly. The wheated profile handles the glaze's sweetness with approachable warmth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Clermont Steep|Burrata": "Clermont Steep with burrata — fresh dairy against Beam wheated. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Clermont Steep|Butternut Squash": "Clermont Steep's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon weight handles the squash bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the pour's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Clermont Steep|Carrot Cake": "Clermont Steep with carrot cake — Jim Beam wheated against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; wheat caramel and cake spice at matched registers.",
  "Clermont Steep|Cheesecake": "Clermont Steep with cheesecake — Beam wheated against clean custard. Works; gentle match.",
  "Clermont Steep|Chilean Seabass": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Clermont Steep for the steak course.",
  "Clermont Steep|Chocolate Brownie": "Clermont Steep with the chocolate brownie — 90-proof Beam wheated against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast.",
  "Clermont Steep|Chocolate Cake": "Clermont Steep's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the brown-spirit warmth threads the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "Clermont Steep|Clam Chowder": "Clermont Steep's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon depth settles into the chowder, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Clermont Steep|Cowboy Ribeye": "Clermont Steep with the Cowboy Ribeye — 90-proof Beam wheated against cap fat. Strong; the wheated profile is slightly underpowered for ribeye scale — Weller 12 or Knob Creek are stronger.",
  "Clermont Steep|Crab Cake": "Clermont Steep with crab cake — Beam wheated against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Clermont Steep|Creamed Spinach": "Clermont Steep with creamed spinach — 90-proof Beam wheated against dairy. Strong; the caramel and wheat honey sit with the cream cleanly.",
  "Clermont Steep|Creamy Potato": "Clermont Steep's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon weight handles the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; boutique character doesn't drive.",
  "Clermont Steep|Creme Brulee": "Clermont Steep's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Clermont Steep|Escargot": "Clermont Steep's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon backbone carries the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; boutique-craft is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Clermont Steep|Faroe Island Salmon": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Clermont Steep lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Clermont Steep|Filet Mignon": "Clermont Steep with the filet — Jim Beam's wheated release from Clermont. 90-proof wheated, distinct from Beam's non-wheated portfolio. Caramel, vanilla, approachable wheated character. The Beam-side answer to the Wellers. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Clermont Steep|French Onion": "Clermont Steep's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the oak depth lays under the French onion, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; BIB structure is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "Clermont Steep|Grilled Caesar": "Clermont Steep with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine against Beam wheated. Works; the wheated profile is a touch gentle for the Caesar.",
  "Clermont Steep|Gumbo": "Clermont Steep's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon backbone carries the gumbo, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Clermont Steep|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Clermont Steep's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Clermont Steep|House Wedge": "Clermont Steep with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against Beam wheated. Works; no driver pairing.",
  "Clermont Steep|Kansas City": "Clermont Steep with the KC — Jim Beam's wheated expression against the strip's marbling. 90-proof wheated with caramel and vanilla; distinct from Jim Beam Black's non-wheated take on this cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Clermont Steep|Loaded Potato": "Clermont Steep's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the brown-spirit warmth threads the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the complex character gives the dessert a refined frame.",
  "Clermont Steep|Lobster Mac": "Clermont Steep's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the brown-spirit warmth threads the lobster mac, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; milestone-release doesn't drive.",
  "Clermont Steep|Market Fish": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Clermont Steep for the steak course.",
  "Clermont Steep|Mocha Creme": "Clermont Steep's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the mocha creme, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; polished 10-year gives the dessert small-batch premium character.",
  "Clermont Steep|Mushroom Bisque": "Clermont Steep's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon body sits with the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the Millennium's register overshoots the dessert's.",
  "Clermont Steep|Mushrooms": "Clermont Steep with the mushrooms — Jim Beam wheated against earthy umami. The wheated softness lets the mushrooms lead; a clean approachable-wheated side pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Clermont Steep|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Clermont Steep's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the oak depth lays under the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Clermont Steep|Porterhouse": "Clermont Steep with the porterhouse — Beam wheated across strip and filet. Strong; handles both cuts cleanly at approachable wheated register.",
  "Clermont Steep|Prime Tartare": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; curated single-barrel sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Clermont Steep|Rainbow Trout": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Clermont Steep lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Clermont Steep|Roast Half Chicken": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; save the BTAC bottle for the protein course — the side doesn't deserve the pour.",
  "Clermont Steep|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Clermont Steep's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the caramel-spice register matches the chickpea soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Clermont Steep|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Clermont Steep belongs on the steak course.",
  "Clermont Steep|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Clermont Steep's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon body sits with the garlic spinach, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Clermont Steep|Seafood Tower": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Clermont Steep for the steak course.",
  "Clermont Steep|Seared Scallops": "Clermont Steep with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against Beam wheated. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Clermont Steep|Seasonal Soup": "Clermont Steep's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon body sits with the seasonal soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Clermont Steep|Seasonal Vegetables": "Clermont Steep's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the brown-spirit warmth threads the seasonal vegetables, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Clermont Steep|Shrimp Bisque": "Clermont Steep with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood against Beam wheated. Works neutrally.",
  "Clermont Steep|Shrimp Cocktail": "Clermont Steep with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against Beam wheated. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Clermont Steep|Swordfish": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Clermont Steep lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Clermont Steep|The Tomahawk": "Clermont Steep's crisp caramel-char character frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Clermont Steep|Tomato Basil": "Clermont Steep's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; pour slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Clermont Steep|Truffle Fries": "Clermont Steep with truffle fries — Beam wheated against truffle-parmesan. The 90-proof caramel-vanilla wheat gives the side a different character than non-wheated Beam expressions. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Clermont Steep|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Clermont Steep lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Clermont Steep|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Clermont Steep's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; boutique character slightly over-specified.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Asparagus": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin sits beside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Beignets": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Clos Petit Ona Grenache on the steak course instead.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Bone Marrow": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin lifts the marrow's umami-fat depth — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Bone-In Filet": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Broccoli Cheddar": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Broccolini": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin sits alongside the broccolini's tender green — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Brussels and Belly": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body. The warm spice plays with pork-belly fat and the garrigue matches brussels-char bitterness. Strong Southern-French for brussels-and-belly — Mediterranean polish on fat-and-char.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Burrata": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body, low tannin. The wild raspberry bridges tomato-basil brightness and the low tannin won't tear against fresh cream. Works for burrata — a Rosé handles the dairy more cleanly.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Butternut Squash": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin finds neutral with the squash soup's silky sweet — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Carrot Cake": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Clos Petit Ona Grenache for the steak course.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Cheesecake": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Clos Petit Ona Grenache for the steak course.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Chilean Seabass": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Clos Petit Ona Grenache for the steak course.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Chocolate Brownie": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Clos Petit Ona Grenache belongs on the steak course.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Chocolate Cake": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Clos Petit Ona Grenache for the steak course.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Clam Chowder": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin sits beside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Cowboy Ribeye": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body, low tannin. Medium body is slightly light for Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in, but the warm spice handles grilled char. Works for ribeye — Grand Tinel's Châteauneuf full-body handles the cut harder.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Crab Cake": "Never pair Clos Petit Ona with crab cake — kirsch and garrigue bury delicate lump crab and the warm spice fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Creamed Spinach": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Creamy Potato": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin sits beside the potato soup's silky body — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Creme Brulee": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Clos Petit Ona Grenache lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Escargot": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Faroe Island Salmon": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body. The medium body matches salmon's oil-richness and the wild raspberry lifts against flesh-sweetness. Peak Southern-French for salmon — preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen for today's build. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Filet Mignon": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body, low tannin. The low tannin matches butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the wild raspberry plays with escargot-butter. Strong Southern-French for filet — Mediterranean lightness on the cut.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|French Onion": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the rye spice cuts the cheese richness. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Grilled Caesar": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body. The garrigue matches anchovy-lemon dressing and the warm spice plays against parm-char. Peak Southern-French for grilled caesar — Mediterranean herb match on the salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Gumbo": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin reads alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|House Wedge": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body. The warm spice plays with bacon-fat and the garrigue matches blue-cheese funk. Peak Southern-French for wedge — Mediterranean lift on the heavy salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Kansas City": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body. The warm spice echoes Kansas City's lean-bold strip char and the garrigue lifts grilled beef. Strong Southern-French for the strip — Mediterranean mid-weight match.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Loaded Potato": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Lobster Mac": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Market Fish": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Clos Petit Ona Grenache belongs on the steak course.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Mocha Creme": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Clos Petit Ona Grenache for the steak course.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Mushroom Bisque": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin plays cleanly against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Mushrooms": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body. The garrigue savory-finish echoes roasted-mushroom glutamate and the warm spice plays with butter. Peak Southern-French for mushrooms — Mediterranean herb-meets-earth match. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Clos Petit Ona Grenache on the steak course instead.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Porterhouse": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the Rhone-blend body wraps the dual-cut steak, and the peppery edge frames the cut. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Prime Tartare": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Rainbow Trout": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Clos Petit Ona Grenache for the steak course.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Roast Half Chicken": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body, low tannin. The garrigue echoes chicken-jus herbaceousness and the warm spice plays with crispy-skin char. Peak Southern-French for the bird — preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen for today's build. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Clos Petit Ona Grenache belongs on the steak course.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body. The garrigue echoes garlic-oil aromatics and the medium body matches spinach-iron. Strong Southern-French for garlic spinach — Mediterranean herb-on-herb harmony.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Seafood Tower": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Clos Petit Ona Grenache on the steak course instead.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Clos Petit Ona with seared scallops — garrigue and warm spice overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and the kirsch fights browned-butter. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Seasonal Soup": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin sits alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Seasonal Vegetables": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin holds with the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Shrimp Bisque": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body. The medium body handles tomato-cream and the wild raspberry lifts shellfish-sweetness. Strong Southern-French for bisque — Mediterranean red-on-shellfish bridge.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Clos Petit Ona with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by kirsch and garrigue, and the warm spice has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Swordfish": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Clos Petit Ona Grenache lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|The Tomahawk": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin plays cleanly against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Tomato Basil": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin leans against the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Truffle Fries": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin reads alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Clos Petit Ona Grenache for the steak course.",
  "Clos Petit Ona Grenache|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin leans against the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Asparagus": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's añejo-pour depth leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the barrel-rested body handles the asparagus, and the soft-spice note softens the side. Works; accessible aged composes with the plate.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the au gratin, and the cooked-agave note underlines the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Beignets": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's reposado aged in uncharred Napa Cabernet wine barrels reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the barrel-rested body handles the beignets, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Bone Marrow": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with bone marrow — Napa Cab-cask reposado against umami fat. Strong; the wine-fruit influence is a distinctive counterpoint.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Bone-In Filet": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's crisp agave-floral character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the añejo weight grips the lean cut, and the juniper sharpens the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Broccoli Cheddar": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the oak-aged tequila body threads the broccoli cheddar, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Broccolini": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the oak-aged tequila body threads the broccolini, and the cooked-agave note underlines the side. Works; benchmark extra añejo is slightly ambitious for the two-cut plate.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Brussels and Belly": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with Brussels + Belly — Cabernet-cask rosé against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; the wine fruit doesn't align cleanly with the maple.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Burrata": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the barrel-rested body handles the burrata, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the starter. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Butternut Squash": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the squash bisque, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; refined but not a standout.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Carrot Cake": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's barrel-aged tequila weight finds neutral with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the aged-agave weight wraps the carrot cake, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Cheesecake": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's cooked-agave-and-oak register leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the barrel-rested body handles the cheesecake, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; the extended maturation gives the side more than standard reposados bring, though not a standout.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Chilean Seabass": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's rested-agave body leans against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the seabass, and the cooked-agave note underlines the plate. Works; accessible character doesn't drive.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Chocolate Brownie": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's rested-agave body sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the oak-aged tequila body threads the brownie, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; the wine-cask notes don't compete with the dish's sweetness effectively.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Chocolate Cake": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the chocolate cake, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; multi-cask complexity over-specified.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Clam Chowder": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's cooked-agave-and-oak register leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the rested-agave depth sits with the chowder, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Cowboy Ribeye": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with the Cowboy Ribeye — Cabernet-cask reposado against cap fat. Strong; the wine-cask character and ribeye fat find each other in a wine-bridge register.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Crab Cake": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with crab cake — Cabernet-cask reposado against lump crab. The wine influence brings berry notes to the crab in a way no standard reposado does; distinctive. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Creamed Spinach": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's cooked-agave-and-oak register leans against the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the barrel-rested body handles the creamed spinach, and the oak threads the side. Works; the bold agave overpowers the dish's restraint.",
  "Codigo 1530 Rosa|Creamy Potato": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's aged-agave character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-aged tequila body threads the potato soup, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; approachable agave cuts the glaze but not marquee.",
