  "Corazon Weller|Roast Half Chicken": "Corazon Weller's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the aged-agave weight wraps the roast chicken, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the plate. Works; multi-cask complexity handles richness at luxury register.",
  "Corazon Weller|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Corazon Weller's aged-agave character sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the rested-agave depth sits with the chickpea soup, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; the bold agave overpowers the dish's restraint.",
  "Corazon Weller|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Corazon Weller's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the rested-agave depth sits with the halibut, and the soft-spice note softens the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Corazon Weller|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Corazon Weller's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the aged-agave weight wraps the garlic spinach, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the side. Works; the 70th's smoothness is an unusual but interesting counterpoint.",
  "Corazon Weller|Seafood Tower": "Corazon Weller's rested-agave body reads alongside the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the añejo register carries the seafood tower, and the soft-spice note softens the starter. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Corazon Weller|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Corazon Weller. Avoid; ex-wheated-bourbon-barrel añejo too heavy for scallops. Reach for a blanco, light rum, or vodka.",
  "Corazon Weller|Seasonal Soup": "Corazon Weller's aged-agave character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the aged-agave weight wraps the seasonal soup, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corazon Weller|Seasonal Vegetables": "Corazon Weller's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the añejo register carries the seasonal vegetables, and the caramel-agave register brightens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Corazon Weller|Shrimp Bisque": "Corazon Weller's rested-agave body reads alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the oak-aged tequila body threads the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corazon Weller|Shrimp Cocktail": "Corazon Weller's aged-agave character overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Corazon Weller lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Corazon Weller|Swordfish": "Corazon Weller's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the barrel-rested body handles the swordfish, and the cooked-agave note underlines the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Corazon Weller|The Tomahawk": "Corazon Weller's agave-char character frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Corazon Weller|Tomato Basil": "Corazon Weller's barrel-aged tequila weight finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the aged-agave weight wraps the tomato basil, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; distinctive ceremony over-specified.",
  "Corazon Weller|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: Parmesan-and-truffle meets Corazon Weller. Excellent; ex-wheated-barrel soft character handles truffle-salt-fat at softer-Expresiones register.",
  "Corazon Weller|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Corazon Weller's aged-agave character leans against the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the rested-agave depth sits with the tuna, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the plate. Works; multi-cask complexity handles richness at luxury register.",
  "Corazon Weller|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Corazon Weller's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the rested-agave depth sits with the curry, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Asparagus": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Mendoza Argentina Malbec — Corazón del Sol, dark plum, blackberry jam, violet, velvety tannin, full body. The velvety tannin handles gruyere-cream weight, but the jam-forward Malbec reads bolder than the side demands. Works for au gratin — Muga's vanilla-leather plays with the cheese harder.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Beignets": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Corazón del Sol Malbec for the steak course.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Bone-In Filet": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's structured dark-fruit weight sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Broccoli Cheddar": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis sits beside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Broccolini": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Burrata": "Never pair Corazón Malbec with burrata — jam-forward plum and violet clash with fresh cream and the full body buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Butternut Squash": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis stays alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the floral notes lift the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Carrot Cake": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Corazón del Sol Malbec for the steak course.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Cheesecake": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Corazón del Sol Malbec lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Chilean Seabass": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Corazón del Sol Malbec on the steak course instead.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Chocolate Brownie": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Corazón del Sol Malbec for the steak course.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Chocolate Cake": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Corazón del Sol Malbec for the steak course.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Clam Chowder": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the floral edge frames the shellfish. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Cowboy Ribeye": "Mendoza Argentina Malbec — Corazón del Sol, dark plum, blackberry jam, violet, velvety tannin, full body, warm finish. The full body matches Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the velvety tannin slices rendered fat. Peak Mendoza for ribeye — plush Malbec built for grilled marbling. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Crab Cake": "Never pair Corazón Malbec with crab cake — jam-forward plum and blackberry bury delicate lump crab and the full body fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Creamy Potato": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis leans against the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the floral notes lift the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Creme Brulee": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Corazón del Sol Malbec lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Escargot": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis plays cleanly against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Faroe Island Salmon": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Corazón del Sol Malbec on the steak course instead.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Filet Mignon": "Mendoza Argentina Malbec — Corazón del Sol, dark plum, blackberry jam, violet, velvety tannin, full body. The velvety tannin matches butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the violet aromatics play with escargot-butter. Strong Mendoza for filet — full body is slightly heavy, but plush-on-tender works.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|French Onion": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Grilled Caesar": "Mendoza Argentina Malbec — Corazón del Sol, dark plum, blackberry jam, violet, velvety tannin, full body. The velvety tannin handles anchovy-depth, but the jam-forward fruit reads heavy against lemon-dressing. Works for grilled caesar — a Kermit Lynch Rhône's garrigue fits the dressing harder.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Gumbo": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis plays cleanly against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis sits beside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|House Wedge": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the structured body wraps the wedge, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; the two sides share the meal without competing.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Kansas City": "Kansas City: the Cab's firm tannin cuts the strip's char and the dark fruit lifts the savory edge — steakhouse symmetry. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Loaded Potato": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis holds with the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the floral notes lift the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Lobster Mac": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis stays alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Market Fish": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Corazón del Sol Malbec lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Mocha Creme": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Corazón del Sol Malbec for the steak course.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Mushroom Bisque": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis holds with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: Cab tannin frames the earthy mushroom-and-truffle depth; dark fruit lifts the umami into the wine's body. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Corazón del Sol Malbec for the steak course.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the Cab's firm tannin cuts the strip's char and the dark fruit lifts the savory edge — steakhouse symmetry. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Prime Tartare": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's structured dark-fruit weight sits at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the concentrated dark-fruit weight handles the tartare, and the iron-mineral plays against the starter. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Rainbow Trout": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Corazón del Sol Malbec for the steak course.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Roast Half Chicken": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis leans against the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Corazón del Sol Malbec belongs on the steak course.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis holds with the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Seafood Tower": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Corazón del Sol Malbec for the steak course.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Corazón Malbec with seared scallops — jam-forward fruit and full body overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and the velvety tannin fights browned-butter sweetness. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Seasonal Soup": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's deep dark-fruit and grippy tannin reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the structured body wraps the seasonal soup, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the close composes without strain.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Seasonal Vegetables": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis stays alongside the seasonal garden plate — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Shrimp Bisque": "Mendoza Argentina Malbec — Corazón del Sol, dark plum, blackberry jam, violet, velvety tannin, full body. The full body handles tomato-cream weight, but the jam-forward fruit reads heavy against shellfish-sweetness. Works for bisque — a Rosé's bridge or Chardonnay's cream fit better.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Corazón Malbec with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by jam-forward full body, and the velvety tannin has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Swordfish": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Corazón del Sol Malbec lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|The Tomahawk": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the concentrated tannin grips the marbled cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Tomato Basil": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright acid — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis frames the herb butter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit anchors the mushroom's umami weight; firm tannin and oak hold their register. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Corazón del Sol Malbec for the steak course.",
  "Corazón del Sol Malbec|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Asparagus": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build plays cleanly against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Beignets": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Corpse Reviver belongs on the steak course.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Bone Marrow": "Warner's gin, Lillet, Pierre Ferrand cognac, lemon, absinthe-rinsed glass. The cocktail's elegance has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat weight. Never pair — keep the Corpse Reviver for starters, route marrow to whiskey cocktails or big Cabs.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Bone-In Filet": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- cocktail citrus reads thin against the cut's structure. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Corpse Reviver lands on another course — not here.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Broccoli Cheddar": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Broccolini": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build leans against the broccolini's tender green — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Brussels and Belly": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build reads alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Burrata": "Warner's gin, Lillet, cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. The cocktail's layered citrus-and-anise profile cuts burrata's cream and cognac plays against basil-tomato depth. Peak starter pairing for guests who want something historically significant with their burrata.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Butternut Squash": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Carrot Cake": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Corpse Reviver on the steak course instead.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Cheesecake": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- cocktail citrus and custard tang cancel each other — both reach for acid. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Corpse Reviver for the steak course.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Chilean Seabass": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build holds steady against the seabass's buttery flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Chocolate Brownie": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Corpse Reviver for the steak course.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Chocolate Cake": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Corpse Reviver on the steak course instead.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Clam Chowder": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Cowboy Ribeye": "Warner's gin, Lillet, cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. Delicate classical cocktail crushed by Cowboy's marbled rendered-fat intensity. Never pair — the Corpse Reviver belongs with starters and raw bar, not the fattiest cut.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Crab Cake": "Warner's gin, Lillet, Pierre Ferrand cognac, lemon, absinthe-rinsed glass. The citrus-and-anise-edge cocktail cuts crab's romesco and cognac adds layered depth against the delicate meat. Peak classical opener pairing for crab cake — both lean into craft and history.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Creamed Spinach": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Creamy Potato": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build plays cleanly against the potato soup's silky body — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Creme Brulee": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Corpse Reviver for the steak course.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Escargot": "Warner's gin, Lillet, cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. The anise-edge nose plays directly against garlic-butter escargot and cognac's richness meets the snails' earth. Peak pre-Prohibition cocktail for a classical starter — textbook pairing from another era. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Faroe Island Salmon": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build plays cleanly against the salmon's rich oily flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Filet Mignon": "Warner's gin, Lillet, cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. The cocktail's classical elegance is wrong for steak — citrus-and-anise have no hook into grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the cocktail; route filet pairing to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan.",
  "Corpse Reviver|French Onion": "Corpse Reviver's shaken-and-bright cocktail register reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bright build frames the French onion, and the citrus cuts the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Grilled Caesar": "Warner's gin, Lillet, Pierre Ferrand cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. The absinthe-and-Lillet herbal complexity echoes our Caesar's anchovy depth; the lemon cleans the palate after each rich, egg-yolk-heavy bite. Pre-Prohibition classic meets classic. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Gumbo": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build sits beside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build sits alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Corpse Reviver|House Wedge": "Warner's gin, Lillet, Pierre Ferrand cognac, lemon, absinthe-rinsed glass. The lemon-and-cognac structure cuts iceberg-and-blue-cheese weight and the anise nose adds complexity. Strong opener pairing — cocktail's layered build matches the wedge's layered components.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Kansas City": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- cocktail citrus reads thin against the cut's structure. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Corpse Reviver for another course.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Loaded Potato": "Corpse Reviver's shaken-and-bright cocktail register sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bright build frames the loaded potato soup, and the citrus cuts the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Lobster Mac": "Corpse Reviver's shaken-and-bright cocktail register sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bright build frames the lobster mac, and the citrus cuts the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Market Fish": "Corpse Reviver's lemon-driven cocktail body sits alongside the market fish's clean white-fish flesh: the bright build frames the market fish, and the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Mocha Creme": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a light cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Corpse Reviver for the steak course.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Mushroom Bisque": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Mushrooms": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build finds neutral with the mushrooms' earthy umami — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Corpse Reviver lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Porterhouse": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Corpse Reviver lands on another course — not here.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this dry, spirit-forward gin cocktail's bitter-herbal complexity. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. Strong — aligned lift.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Rainbow Trout": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build finds register with the trout's delicate flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Roast Half Chicken": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build stays alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the cocktail lift carries the herb-crisped bird, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build lifts the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build leans against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Seafood Tower": "Warner's gin, Lillet, Pierre Ferrand cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. The citrus-and-botanical structure handles the raw-bar spread and the anise-edge adds aromatic depth. Strong tower cocktail — though Champagne and French 75 peak slightly higher for raw shellfish.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Seared Scallops": "Warner's gin, Lillet, Pierre Ferrand cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. The layered botanicals with anise-on-the-nose meet scallops' clean-seared character elegantly; lemon cuts, cognac adds depth. Peak sophisticated cocktail pairing for scallops — history meets delicate seafood.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Seasonal Soup": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Seasonal Vegetables": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build sits alongside the seasonal garden plate — the cocktail lift sits with the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Shrimp Bisque": "Warner's gin, Lillet, Pierre Ferrand cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. The cocktail's lemon cuts bisque's cream-shellfish richness and cognac adds warmth against the soup's weight. Works well — though French 75 or Champagne peak slightly higher for bisque.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Shrimp Cocktail": "Warner's gin, Lillet, Pierre Ferrand cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. The lemon cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the absinthe-edge adds aromatic complexity against cocktail-sauce bite. Peak classical cocktail for a classical starter — sophistication in both glasses.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Swordfish": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build reads alongside the swordfish's meaty flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Corpse Reviver|The Tomahawk": "Classical cocktail against the biggest steak on the menu — complete mismatch. The Corpse Reviver's delicate elegance is obliterated by marbled Tomahawk weight. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Tomato Basil": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build reads alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Truffle Fries": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build leans against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Corpse Reviver's shaken-and-bright cocktail register reads register-aligned with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the high-acid cocktail body sits with the tuna, and the botanical lift underlines the fish. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Corpse Reviver|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build leans against the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Asparagus": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- cognac on a green vegetable side is wrong direction — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Courvoisier Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Courvoisier with the au gratin — drier oak cognac against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Courvoisier's backbone handles the richness.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Beignets": "Courvoisier with beignets — drier traditional cognac against fried dough. Works; Courvoisier's oak is less comfortable than Hennessy for casual dessert.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Bone Marrow": "Courvoisier with bone marrow — drier traditional cognac against umami fat. Excellent; Courvoisier's oak-forward profile cuts marrow harder than Hennessy's dried-fruit rounder approach — the featured cognac-marrow pairing in the batch.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Bone-In Filet": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac finds neutral with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the eau-de-vie body carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak-and-vanilla edge brightens the cut. Works; Rémy's elegance meets the char at functional register.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Broccoli Cheddar": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the cognac register matches the broccoli cheddar, and the dried-fruit note underlines the bowl. Works; Rémy's refinement is over-specified for the casual dessert.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Broccolini": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- cognac on a green vegetable side is wrong direction — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Courvoisier Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Brussels and Belly": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac reads alongside Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the pork-belly plate, and the dried-fruit note underlines the side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Burrata": "Courvoisier with burrata — drier cognac against fresh dairy. Avoid; cognac weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Butternut Squash": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the brandy depth wraps the squash bisque, and the dried-fruit note underlines the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Carrot Cake": "Courvoisier with carrot cake — traditional Jarnac cognac against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Courvoisier's oak threads the spice cleanly at drier register.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Cheesecake": "Courvoisier with cheesecake — drier traditional cognac against custard dessert. Strong; Courvoisier's oak supports the cheesecake, though Hennessy's sweeter approach is the featured cognac-cheesecake pairing.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Chilean Seabass": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- cognac and oily fish double up the richness with no foil. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Courvoisier Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Chocolate Brownie": "Courvoisier with the chocolate brownie — drier oak-forward cognac against cocoa. Excellent; Courvoisier's drier finish lets cocoa lead rather than blending with it — distinct from Hennessy's sweeter-marriage approach.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Chocolate Cake": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the Limousin-oak weight handles the chocolate cake, and the oak-and-vanilla edge brightens the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Clam Chowder": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the cognac register matches the chowder, and the dried-fruit note underlines the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Cowboy Ribeye": "Courvoisier Cognac's crisp cognac-oak character lifts the ribeye's cap fat and char — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Crab Cake": "Courvoisier with crab cake — drier cognac against delicate crab. Avoid; cognac is wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Creamed Spinach": "Courvoisier with creamed spinach — drier oak-forward cognac against dairy-rich side. Strong; Courvoisier's structural oak cuts cream rather than riding it.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Creamy Potato": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the cognac depth wraps the potato soup, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Creme Brulee": "Courvoisier with creme brulee — traditional Jarnac cognac against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; Courvoisier's drier finish contrasts the caramel where Rémy VSOP's elegance harmonizes.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Escargot": "Courvoisier Cognac's crisp cognac-oak character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the barrel-aged depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Faroe Island Salmon": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- cognac and oily fish double up the richness with no foil. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a cognac. Avoid; the better calls are above. Courvoisier Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Filet Mignon": "Courvoisier with the filet — \"Napoleon's cognac,\" drier and oak-forward, traditional Jarnac producer. Excellent; Courvoisier's drier character and pronounced oak frame tenderloin with more structural backbone than Hennessy's rounder approach — the traditional French cognac-steak pairing.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|French Onion": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the brandy depth wraps the French onion, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Grilled Caesar": "Courvoisier with the grilled Caesar — drier cognac against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Courvoisier's oak meets the char at functional register.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Gumbo": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the eau-de-vie body carries the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; Courvoisier's oak is over-specified but functional.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- cognac on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Courvoisier Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|House Wedge": "Courvoisier with the House Wedge — cognac against iceberg and bleu. Works; Courvoisier's oak is over-specified but functional.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Kansas City": "Courvoisier with the KC — drier Napoleon-era cognac against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Courvoisier's drier character and oak backbone are the featured cognac-KC pairing — more structurally aligned with marbling than Hennessy's rounder profile.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Loaded Potato": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the Limousin-oak weight handles the loaded potato soup, and the rancio register softens the bowl. Works; Ferrand's craft character handles the richness.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Lobster Mac": "Courvoisier with lobster mac — Napoleon-era cognac against pasta-and-lobster richness. Strong; Courvoisier's drier oak frames the richness with structural lift.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Market Fish": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- cognac's aged-grape weight buries delicate fish — digestif belongs after the meal. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Courvoisier Cognac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Mocha Creme": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the mocha creme, and the caramel rounds the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Mushroom Bisque": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the Limousin-oak weight handles the mushroom bisque, and the caramel threads the umami register. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Mushrooms": "Courvoisier with mushrooms — drier Napoleon-era cognac against earthy umami. Strong; Courvoisier's oak depth threads the mushrooms with traditional French character.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet drier cognac. Strong; Courvoisier's oak backbone handles the dessert density.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Porterhouse": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the brandy depth wraps the dual-cut steak, and the caramel rounds the cut. Works; Courvoisier's backbone handles the richness.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Prime Tartare": "Courvoisier Cognac's crisp cognac-oak character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the barrel-aged depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Rainbow Trout": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a cognac. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Courvoisier Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Roast Half Chicken": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the brandy depth wraps the roast chicken, and the dried-fruit note underlines the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the brandy depth wraps the chickpea soup, and the oak-and-vanilla edge brightens the bowl. Works; Courvoisier's oak is over-specified but functional.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- cognac's aged-grape weight buries delicate fish — digestif belongs after the meal. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Courvoisier Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- cognac on a green vegetable side is wrong direction — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a cognac. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Courvoisier Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Seafood Tower": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- cognac weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs sparkling or a crisp white. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; the better calls are above. Courvoisier Cognac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Seared Scallops": "Courvoisier with seared scallops — traditional cognac against scallop delicacy. Avoid; cognac overwhelms scallops.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Seasonal Soup": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the eau-de-vie body carries the seasonal soup, and the dried-fruit note underlines the bowl. Works; Rémy's elegance meets the char at functional register.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Seasonal Vegetables": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- cognac on a green vegetable side is wrong direction — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a cognac. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Courvoisier Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Shrimp Bisque": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac reads alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the cognac register matches the shrimp bisque, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; Courvoisier's backbone handles the richness.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Shrimp Cocktail": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- cognac weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs sparkling or a crisp white. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Courvoisier Cognac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Swordfish": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- cognac's aged register pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Courvoisier Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|The Tomahawk": "Courvoisier Cognac's crisp cognac-oak character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Tomato Basil": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the brandy depth wraps the tomato basil, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Truffle Fries": "Courvoisier with truffle fries — drier oak-forward cognac against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Courvoisier's structural backbone frames the earthy-rich side with more cut than Hennessy's rounder approach.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a cognac. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Courvoisier Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Courvoisier Cognac|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the Limousin-oak weight handles the curry, and the dried-fruit note underlines the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with the Cowboy Ribeye — 93.7-proof Barton against cap fat. The spicy-sweet character handles the ribeye with 1792 balance. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat with the Cowboy Ribeye — 94.2-proof sweet-wheat against cap fat. Strong; the extra proof over 90-proof wheated options helps with the ribeye's scale.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|1881 Napa Valley": "Napa Cab — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, smooth medium tannin. The approachable body can meet Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness but the smoother tannin is rounder than rendered fat demands. Strong entry Napa Cab for Cowboy — premium Cabs like Silver Oak or Caymus peak higher for the fattiest ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the 2 James Gin for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the Cowboy Ribeye — rare limited-release against cap fat. The literary-evocative craft character handles the ribeye at boutique-release register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with the cowboy ribeye — 16yr double-matured Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Aberlour 16 frames the ribeye with balanced depth; Balvenie 21 Portwood is the featured ribeye match.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- this expression lands short of the cut's register. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Adictivo Cristalino for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado with the Cowboy Ribeye — near-añejo reposado against cap fat and char. The extended maturation earns this cut where younger reposados fade; Adictivo and G4 High Proof are the two reposados that handle the ribeye. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character falls short of the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- crisp white reads thin against 26+ ounces of cap-fat ribeye. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Alexander Valley Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Sonoma Bordeaux blend — plum, blackberry, mocha, soft tannin, smooth, medium body. The soft structure meets ribeye's big marbled build though body reads leaner than full-bodied Cabs. Strong crowd-pleaser Bordeaux blend for ribeye — accessible Sonoma on the bold cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Amaro Nonino on another course instead.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character flattens the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Ancho Reyes on another course instead.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with the cowboy ribeye — 86.6 proof port-finished against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; AE is under-specified for cap-fat at flagship proof; AE Cask Strength handles this better.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with the cowboy ribeye — 117+ proof AE against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; AE CS is the featured wine-finished-ribeye pairing — cask-strength port-finish is built for cap-fat intensity.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "AE Triple Cask with the cowboy ribeye — multi-cask-finished AE against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; AE Triple Cask frames the ribeye at distinctive register; AE Cask Strength's 117+ proof is better for cap-fat.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Aperol lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Asparagus": "Cowboy Ribeye with the asparagus -- the bright register cleaves the heavy course, the table breathes. Works; the side carries cleanly with the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Cowboy Ribeye with the au gratin -- the 26oz cut handles the rich potato side. Excellent; the side that completes the steak plate.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope — Paso Robles Cab with ripe plum, dark cherry, mocha, warm spice, plush full finish. Paso produces bigger warmer reds than Napa. The plush body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the warm spice meets rendered fat. Peak Paso Cab for Cowboy — maximum richness for the big ribeye. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- aperitivo bitterness fights the headline cut's marbled char — bitter and savory pull apart. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Averna Amaro for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Aviation": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Aviation on another course instead.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Aviation Gin": "Aviation with the cowboy ribeye — Portland craft gin against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Avion 44": "Avion 44 with the Cowboy Ribeye — 44-month aged Avion against cap fat. Strong; extended maturation handles the ribeye with premium Avion authority.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Avion Silver belongs on another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Bacardi Rum lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Baileys Irish Cream for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Baker's": "Baker's with the Cowboy Ribeye — 107-proof against cap fat. The higher proof and bold high-rye character earn the ribeye with Beam small-batch authority. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the cowboy ribeye — first-fill American Oak Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Balvenie's sweet-oak depth frames the ribeye at handcrafted register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the cowboy ribeye — 14yr rum-cask-finished Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Caribbean Cask's tropical-sweetness frames the ribeye; Balvenie 21 Portwood's port-cask richness is the featured ribeye match.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with the cowboy ribeye — hand-picked American Oak against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Balvenie 16 handles the ribeye with single-barrel vanilla depth; Fiddich 18's Oloroso is better for heaviest cuts.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood with the cowboy ribeye — 21yr port-finished Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Portwood is the featured Sherried Speyside-ribeye pairing — the port-cask red-berry richness and 21-year depth are built for fattier cuts where even Macallan 18 only catches up.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with the cowboy ribeye — French-wine-cask Bardstown against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Bardstown Château Laboude frames the ribeye at contemporary-craft register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with the Cowboy Ribeye — 6th Discovery release against cap fat. The multi-distillery complexity gives the ribeye layered craft-blender depth. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with the Cowboy Ribeye — stout-barrel-finished bourbon against cap fat. The KBS coffee-chocolate-stout notes give the ribeye a distinctive beer-barrel-finishing frame. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bardstown Foursquare": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets Foursquare rum-cask Bardstown. Bardstown Foursquare's tropical rum-cask character frames cap-fat with Caribbean-finish distinction. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with the Cowboy Ribeye — Bardstown own-plus-sourced against cap fat. The Fusion blend combines house distillate with sourced whiskey; the ribeye gets layered craft-blender depth with Bardstown's own-distilled character mixed in. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with the Cowboy Ribeye — retailer-pick Bardstown single-barrel against cap fat. The specific-barrel character handles the ribeye with curated single-barrel authority. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with the Cowboy Ribeye — the 80-proof gateway high-rye against the ribeye's cap fat. Underpowered for the cut's weight; capable but Weller 12 or Eagle Rare 10 Year are the premium alternates for this cut. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Cowboy Ribeye: cap fat and char meet aged Basil Hayden's. Aging gives the pour more body, but 80-proof caps the match. Strong; Weller 12 or Eagle Rare 10 Year remain stronger.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — malted character against cap fat. Underpowered for the cut; Weller 12 or Eagle Rare 10 Year remain stronger, but the malted rye offers a distinct flavor proposition. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with the cowboy ribeye — 80-proof BH red-wine finish against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; BH Red Wine Cask is under-specified for cap-fat at 80-proof; AE Cask Strength handles ribeye better.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast with the Cowboy Ribeye — toast finish against the cut's cap fat. Still underpowered for the ribeye's scale, but the toast notes give the pour more to say than standard Basil Hayden's does. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bee's Knees": "Empress Indigo gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup. Delicate floral-citrus cocktail crushed by Cowboy's marbled rendered-fat profile. Never pair — the Bee's Knees belongs with the raw bar or opener course, not with the fattiest steak on the menu.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Beefeater Gin for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Beignets": "After the cowboy ribeye, the beignets -- the bold marbled cut yields to warm choux and powdered sugar, the dipping sauce easing the meal home. Works; the table closes light after the heaviest steak.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Belvedere Vodka belongs on another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with the Cowboy Ribeye — blended heritage against cap fat. Strong; the blend's honey is slightly underpowered for the ribeye's scale — Weller 12 or Knob Creek bring more grip.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with the Cowboy Ribeye — 103-proof against cap fat. The richer Blanton's and extra proof handle the ribeye with more conviction than Original's 93-proof; the stronger single-barrel ribeye call. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with the Cowboy Ribeye — 93-proof single-barrel against cap fat and bold char. The Blanton's caramel-vanilla depth handles the ribeye cleanly; the benchmark single-barrel for this cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's Straight from the Barrel with the cowboy ribeye — export-market cask-strength Blanton's, 130+ proof, distinctive horse-and-jockey stopper design, honey-vanilla-citrus Blanton's character at cask-strength intensity. Excellent; Blanton's SfB's distinctive Blanton's-character-at-full-proof is the featured conversation-piece barrel-proof-ribeye pairing — where the Staggs are BT-traditional, SfB is Blanton's-distinctive with the collector stopper.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with the Cowboy Ribeye — decennial 10th Pact against cap fat. The milestone-release character gives the ribeye collector-level annual-Pact depth. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with the Cowboy Ribeye — Pact 8 blend against cap fat. The Mizunara-finished component gives the ribeye layered master-blender depth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the Cowboy Ribeye — Pact 9 wine-cask blend against cap fat. The fruit-wine character layers over the bourbon blend with master-blender distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat with the cowboy ribeye — Philly patriotic-artisan against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Patagonia Argentina Malbec — Noemia A Lisa, cooler-climate than Mendoza, blackberry, violet, graphite, full body, soft tannin. The full body matches Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the soft tannin won't tear against rendered fat. Peak Patagonian-Malbec for ribeye — silky elegance on the marbled cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Bombay Dry Gin on another course instead.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Bombay Sapphire Gin for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bone Marrow": "Cowboy Ribeye after the bone marrow -- the unctuous marrow opens the meal, the 26oz cap-and-fat richness following with rendered fat and char that share the marrow's beefy language without competing. Strong; both courses speak fat in the same register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Booker's": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets Beam flagship cask-strength bourbon, uncut/unfiltered 120-130 proof, Booker Noe originated the barrel-proof category in 1992. Booker's uncut-unfiltered intensity is the featured barrel-proof-ribeye pairing — the 125+ proof handles 26oz cap-fat with the original-barrel-proof gravitas that started the whole category. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Botanist Gin": "Botanist with the cowboy ribeye — Hebridean craft gin against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace muddled with rum-soaked cherries, orange, bitters over a large cube. The vanilla-caramel backbone and brûléed-orange nose cut the Cowboy's marbled fat; cherry sweetness meets the crust caramelization. Peak spirit-forward pour for the fattiest cut on the menu.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Cowboy Ribeye: the cut's cap fat and char meet the house pick's bigger body from custom staves. Carries the ribeye where standard Maker's fades; Bowdie's signature wheated. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the Cowboy Ribeye — 90-proof Virginia small-batch against cap fat. The smooth Bowman character handles the cut at the everyday small-batch register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bowmore 12": "Cowboy Ribeye: the cut's cap fat and bold char meet Bowmore 12's honeyed peat and sherry notes. The gentlest Islay of the three with this cut — capable and refined. Lagavulin 8 and Laphroaig 10 both hit harder; Bowmore is for guests who want sherry-touched balance with a big cut. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin, medium body. The medium body handles ribeye's big marbled build though low tannin reads softer than firm-tannin Cabs on the cut. Works as benchmark Barbera for ribeye — Italian food-wine alternative on the bold cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin — Mount Veeder mountain Cab, dense and concentrated with dark blackberry, graphite, earth, firm age-worthy tannin. Higher elevation means smaller berries, thicker skins, more structure. The dense body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the graphite meets rendered fat. Peak mountain Cab for Cowboy — serious food-driven structure. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Broccoli Cheddar": "Cowboy Ribeye preceded by the broccoli cheddar -- cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Broccolini": "Cowboy Ribeye with the broccolini -- acid lifts the richness, the meal gets the contrast it needs. Works; the side carries cleanly with the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich -- unpeated Islay, progressive, malt. The unpeated Islay body sits with the cap-fat ribeye, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; composed premium match.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Brussels and Belly": "Cowboy Ribeye with Brussels + Belly -- the 26oz cap-and-fat richness meets the maple-glazed pork belly, the smoke from the grilled cut threading the glaze's sweet edge. Strong; the side mirrors the cut's indulgence note for note.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with the Cowboy Ribeye — 90-proof flagship against cap fat and char. The BT profile earns the ribeye where lighter wheated pours fade. A clean, non-wheated everyday call for this cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Buffalo Trace Cream for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with the Cowboy Ribeye — 90-proof high-rye against cap fat and char. Strong; slightly underpowered for the ribeye's scale — Knob Creek (100-proof) and Willett (94-proof craft) are the higher-impact high-rye calls. Bulleit holds the cut at approachable tier.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Burrata": "Cowboy Ribeye preceded by the burrata -- delicate-into-bold, the meal earns its register lift, the courses arc cleanly. Works; the call holds, the starter primes the cut without strain.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Butternut Squash": "Cowboy Ribeye preceded by the butternut squash -- the entree yields to the dessert, the meal arc reads on-key. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cade — Howell Mountain Cab from one of Napa's first LEED-certified wineries. High-elevation fruit: blackberry, dark plum, iron, volcanic mineral, firm age-worthy tannin. The mountain-fruit body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the iron-mineral meets rendered fat. Peak mountain Cab for Cowboy — compelling story, serious bottle. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila with the Cowboy Ribeye — 80-proof value reposado against cap fat. Strong; underpowered for the cut — G4 High Proof (the premium Camarena sibling) or Adictivo are the ribeye calls.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Campari": "Campari's aperitivo at its most textbook underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Campari for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club with the cowboy ribeye — heritage Canadian against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Works; Canadian Club is lighter than ribeye demands; CC 43 Year handles it better.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Canadian Club 43 Year": "CC 43 Year with the cowboy ribeye — rare 43yr aged Canadian against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; CC 43 Year's aged depth handles the ribeye at collector register — where Crown Royal and CC feel underspecified, the 43 gives the cut proper weight.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Captain Morgan Rum for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing with the cowboy ribeye — single-barrel Canadian against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Works; Caribou Crossing is lighter than ribeye demands; CC 43 Year handles it better.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Carrot Cake": "The cowboy ribeye into the carrot cake -- the rendered cap-fat hands off to spiced cake and cream-cheese frosting, walnut crunch on the close. Works; the bold cut and the spiced cake share weight without crowding.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Casa LBV Port": "Never pair Casa LBV with Cowboy Ribeye — entry-port residual sugar muddles marbled-ribeye char and the smooth structure loses against grilled-beef protein weight. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. The LBV is an after-meal pour.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Casamigos Mezcal": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets Casamigos Mezcal's polished-agave register. Excellent; the accessible smoke kisses the char without bullying it — the mezcal a tequila drinker will actually finish.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "* Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon -- cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. The concentrated dark-fruit weight handles the cap-fat ribeye, and the tannin grips the cut. Peak boutique Napa Cab for ribeye — Simon Family reserve-level option for guests who want less well-known but equally serious.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character flattens the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Chambord for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Cheesecake": "The cowboy ribeye closes with the cheesecake -- the marbled char-and-fat hands off to dense dairy custard on graham crust, berry compote lifting the close. Works; the table closes heavy, but the dairy reads round against the rendered fat.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Chocolate Brownie": "The cowboy ribeye closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the marbled char-and-fat yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Excellent; bold-into-bold, the meal closes the way the table came for.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Chocolate Cake": "After the cowboy ribeye, the chocolate cake -- the rendered cap-fat hands off to layered cocoa-and-ganache, the vanilla scoop tempering both. Excellent; the bold steakhouse close on the bone-in cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Chopin Vodka belongs on another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Bros Brandy's crisp brandy-caramel character holds the line with the ribeye's cap fat and char — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Château Batailley": "Pauillac 5ème Cru Classé Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin, full body. The full body and firm tannin handle ribeye's big marbled build and the cedar plays against the char. Strong classified Bordeaux for ribeye — French Pauillac structure on the bold cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Château Beaucastel": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône — Beaucastel, all 13 permitted varieties, dark fruit, leather, truffle, Provençal herbs, full body, medium tannin. The full body matches Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the leather echoes grilled char-depth. Peak bucket-list Châteauneuf for ribeye — one of France's most celebrated estates. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The velvety Merlot handles the 26oz bone-in ribeye, though plush Bordeaux reads softer than marbled-fatty ribeye's char demands. Works for ribeye — Silver Oak or Saffredi fits the fat harder.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Château Haut Segottes": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin, medium body. The medium body works against ribeye's big marbled build though soft tannin reads rounder than firm-tannin left-bank on the cut. Strong right-bank Bordeaux for ribeye — approachable French on the bold cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Château de Rouillac": "Pessac-Léognan left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, tobacco, firm tannin, medium body. The firm tannin handles ribeye's big marbled build though medium body reads leaner than full-bodied Napa Cabs on the cut. Strong left-bank Bordeaux for ribeye — French structure on the bold cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Clam Chowder": "Cowboy Ribeye into the chowder -- cream rounds the protein, the plate composes thick. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's caramel-butterscotch character finds register with the ribeye's cap fat and char — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango with the Cowboy Ribeye — ceramic-bottle Clase Azul against cap fat. Strong; ceremonial extra añejo covers the ribeye with Durango-artisanship authority.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold with the Cowboy Ribeye — ceremonial joven blend against cap fat. Strong; mezcal-touch blend covers the ribeye with distinctive Clase Azul character.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets Clase Azul Guerrero's luxury-mezcal polish. Excellent; the fruitier-Guerrero character frames the ribeye at ceremonial register — smoke softened, fruit amplified.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata's silky Jalisco blanco with ceramic-decanter presentation underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; one of those is the move. Clase Azul Plata belongs on another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado with the Cowboy Ribeye — ultra-premium reposado against cap fat and char. Strong; Clase Azul's refinement is slightly underserved by the cut's scale, but the ceremony is still the draw.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra with the Cowboy Ribeye — top-tier 5-year against cap fat. Strong; Ultra covers the ribeye with absolute-top Clase Azul authority.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep with the Cowboy Ribeye — 90-proof Beam wheated against cap fat. Strong; the wheated profile is slightly underpowered for ribeye scale — Weller 12 or Knob Creek are stronger.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body, low tannin. Medium body is slightly light for Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in, but the warm spice handles grilled char. Works for ribeye — Grand Tinel's Châteauneuf full-body handles the cut harder.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with the Cowboy Ribeye — Cabernet-cask reposado against cap fat. Strong; the wine-cask character and ribeye fat find each other in a wine-bridge register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's vermouth-wine-base sweetness stays alongside the ribeye's cap fat and char — the bitter herb edge cuts the cap-fat richness. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Corazon Blanco for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado with the Cowboy Ribeye — Sazerac-owned reposado against cap fat. Strong; the highland agave gives the ribeye dimension but 80-proof is the ceiling here; G4 High Proof or Adictivo are stronger.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Corazon Sazerac": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets Corazon Sazerac. Excellent; the ex-rye-barrel spice character frames the fatty cut at crossover register — rye-tequila on ribeye is a conversation starter.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Corazon Stagg": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets Corazon Stagg. Excellent; the ex-GTS-barrel heavy-caramel-oak character frames the fatty cut at crossover-intense register — BT high-proof-bourbon depth through aged agave.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Corazon Weller": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets Corazon Weller. Excellent; the ex-Weller-wheated-barrel soft-sweetness frames the fatty cut at softer-crossover register — gentler character on cap fat than the Stagg intensity.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Mendoza Argentina Malbec — Corazón del Sol, dark plum, blackberry jam, violet, velvety tannin, full body, warm finish. The full body matches Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the velvety tannin slices rendered fat. Peak Mendoza for ribeye — plush Malbec built for grilled marbling. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Corpse Reviver": "Warner's gin, Lillet, cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. Delicate classical cocktail crushed by Cowboy's marbled rendered-fat intensity. Never pair — the Corpse Reviver belongs with starters and raw bar, not the fattiest cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier Cognac's crisp cognac-oak character lifts the ribeye's cap fat and char — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Crab Cake": "The cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness obliterates the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust: the marbled char overshadows the delicate crab cake, and the rendered fat crowds out the sweet shellfish plate. Avoid; the cut is too bold for the delicate starter. Stand the crab cake on its own course -- pair the crab cake with Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin -- and pour Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, or Shafer Hillside Select for the cowboy ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 with the cowboy ribeye — Classic Malt Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Cragganmore's meaty depth and subtle smoke frame the ribeye at distinctive register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Creamed Spinach": "Cowboy Ribeye alongside the creamed spinach -- the 26oz marbled char-and-fat holds with the rich bechamel-laced greens, the cream catching the rendered fat without doubling it. Strong; the classic call for the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Creamy Potato": "Cowboy Ribeye with the potato soup alongside -- two rich courses on the same plate, the table earns its substance. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Creme Brulee": "After the cowboy ribeye, the brulee -- the rendered cap-fat yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries brightening the close. Strong; the heavy steak finds relief in the silky finish.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Willamette Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — graceful low-tannin Pinot falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep Cristom for salmon, chicken, or seafood; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal with the cowboy ribeye — smooth Canadian against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Works; Crown Royal is lighter than ribeye demands; Canadian Club 43 Year handles it better at collector register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Cucumber Gimlet": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda top. Delicate garden-profile cocktail crushed by Cowboy's marbled rendered-fat intensity. Never pair — the Gimlet belongs with raw bar or starters, not the fattiest cut on the menu.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12 with the cowboy ribeye — Matusalem-cask Highland against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Dalmore's sherry-finish luxury handles the ribeye; Dalmore-family or Oban 18 reach higher registers.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Loire red Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — light-body Sancerre falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep Sancerre Rouge for salmon, chicken, or seafood; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Darioush — Silverado Trail Cab from one of Napa's most architecturally striking Persian-inspired estates. Rich opulent: blackcurrant, dark plum, mocha, spice, plush full finish. The opulent body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the mocha-spice meets rendered fat. Peak opulent Napa Cab for Cowboy — estate-story adds occasion. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin's Detroit urban-craft gin underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Detroit City Gin on another course instead.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City Vodka's hometown craft vodka underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Detroit City Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15 with the cowboy ribeye — 15yr aged Dewar's against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Dewar's 15's aged sherry-finish frames the ribeye at premium-blend register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label with the cowboy ribeye — entry blended against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Dewar's White handles the ribeye at approachable register; JW Red's bold-smoke is better for the heavier cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Disaronno for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — craft blended rye against cap fat. Strong; craft character capable but higher-proof ryes (Pikesville 110, Knob Creek 100) bring more grip.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's heavy aged-spirit weight leans against the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the heavy body handles the cap-fat ribeye, and the deep backbone frames the cut. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Domaine Serene Grand Cheval's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift leans against the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the Pinot body brightens the cap-fat ribeye, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the cut. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Provence Bandol AOC Mourvèdre blend — Tempier, dark plum, game, olive, wild herbs, smoky mineral, full body, firm tannin. The firm tannin slices Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the smoky mineral echoes grilled char. Peak cult-Bandol for ribeye — exceptional with Cowboy per the card's own call-out. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Pouilly-Fumé against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — flinty Loire Sauv Blanc crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Pouilly-Fumé for scallops, escargot, crab cake, or the bisque course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The full body matches Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the black pepper echoes grilled char. Peak Châteauneuf for ribeye — reliable GSM on the marbled cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Don Fulano Anejo": "Don Fulano Anejo's agave-apricot character frames the ribeye's cap fat and char — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Don Fulano Blanco for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado with the Cowboy Ribeye — highland refinement against cap fat. Strong; the cut is slightly ambitious for Don Fulano's 80-proof elegance; G4 High Proof or Adictivo bring more grip.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942 with the Cowboy Ribeye — 1942 against cap fat. Strong; iconic extra añejo covers the ribeye with benchmark character.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th with the Cowboy Ribeye — crystalline reposado against cap fat. Strong; the 70th's smoothness is a touch underserved by the ribeye's scale, though more refined than standard Don Julio here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco's workhorse blanco with clean agave snap underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Don Julio Blanco on another course instead.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Don Julio Reposado": "Cowboy Ribeye: cap fat and char meet 80-proof reposado. Strong; cut's weight pushes against Don Julio's balance. G4 High Proof or Adictivo bring more grip.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Drambuie": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Drambuie for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Dunville's Single Malt": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the gentle Irish body threads the cap-fat ribeye, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the cut. Works; bonded weight pushes slightly past the soup.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets BT Bottled-in-Bond at 100 proof (barrel-entry proof), Col. E.H. Taylor Jr. tribute line honoring historic Kentucky bourbon. EHT Full Proof's BiB 100-proof character is the historic-Kentucky-tribute-ribeye pairing — less intensity than 130-proof Staggs, more traditional Kentucky register. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof BIB rye against cap fat. The bonded weight handles the ribeye with authority; the BT-side BIB rye for this cut, alongside Knob Creek Rye's Beam take. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof BIB against cap fat and bold char. The bonded richness earns the ribeye with authority; the BT-side BIB for this cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with the Cowboy Ribeye — ten years of Buffalo Trace aging carries the ribeye's cap fat and char in a way that honors the cut. A premium non-wheated choice; more structure than the Wellers here. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Eastern Kille Dry Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|El Cabo": "El Cabo with the Cowboy Ribeye — craft boutique reposado against cap fat. Strong; the craft character is capable but 80-proof limits the match — G4 High Proof or Adictivo for this cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "El Mayor Extra Anejo with the Cowboy Ribeye — aged El Mayor against cap fat. Strong; accessible premium covers the ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado with the Cowboy Ribeye — 80-proof value reposado against cap fat. Strong; the cut pushes past El Mayor's light oak — G4 High Proof or Adictivo are the reposado ribeye calls.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Elephant Gin for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 with the cowboy ribeye — 18yr HH single-barrel against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; EC 18 frames the ribeye at aged-HH register. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel with the Cowboy Ribeye — private-selection Heaven Hill against cap fat. The house-pick's curated profile gives the ribeye a specifically-chosen match. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel with the Cowboy Ribeye — single-barrel Heaven Hill against cap fat. The concentrated 94-proof single-barrel profile earns the ribeye with Heaven Hill's oak-forward character. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with the Cowboy Ribeye — 94-proof small-batch against cap fat and char. The proof handles the ribeye cleanly; distinct from Heaven Hill BIB (100-proof) — a touch less aggressive, still capable. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 94-proof HH rye against cap fat. The extra proof over 90-proof benchmarks handles the cut where Sazerac Rye and Michter's only work strong; a premium HH rye for the ribeye. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Willamette Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — medium-tannin Pinot still falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep Elk Cove for filet, salmon, or chicken; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — medium-body Oregon white crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Elk Cove for seafood starters, burrata, or the Oregon salmon course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee with the Cowboy Ribeye — BT single-barrel against cap fat. The 90-proof richness handles the ribeye cleanly; a namesake single-barrel for this cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 with the cowboy ribeye — butterfly-pea-flower craft against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Epoch Ingenuity": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, full body, medium tannin. The smoked meat echoes Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in char and the crushed pepper plays with grilled beef. Peak Paso-Rhône for ribeye — Epoch's serious winemaking on the cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Escargot": "The cowboy ribeye after the escargot -- the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight sets up the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; the starter composes before the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Espresso Martini": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness mis-pairs with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Espresso Martini belongs on another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The coffee-and-hazelnut profile cuts through the Cowboy's rendered fat and the bourbon cream softens the cocktail against the ribeye weight. Peak spirit-forward, dessert-leaning option for the fattiest cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, medium tannin. The medium tannin stretches toward ribeye's big marbled build though the body falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand vs. bolder Cabs. Works as savory Pinot for ribeye — serious Willamette for guest wanting Pinot.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Napa Oakville Chardonnay against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — even opulent oak is the wrong axis for rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Far Niente for lobster mac, salmon, scallops, or special-occasion whites.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The graphite cuts marbled-ribeye char and firm tannin handles rendered-fat weight. Peak Super Tuscan for the 26oz bone-in — Maremma Bordeaux-style structure lives for this cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Napa Coombsville Cab — blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the tobacco-chocolate meets rendered fat. Peak value Napa Cab for Cowboy — brooding structure handles fat-and-char with layered complexity at a fair price. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid. The light body is too delicate for Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the high acid clashes with heavy fat. Works for ribeye — Tempier Bandol or Corazón Malbec handles the cut harder.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity leans against the ribeye's cap fat and char — the bitter herb edge cuts the cap-fat richness. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta's bitter-herb aperitivo edge finds neutral with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the herbal cut works the cap-fat ribeye, and the amaro backbone frames the cut. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character flattens the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Fireball Whisky belongs on another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — silky low-tannin Pinot falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep Fisher Unity for salmon, chicken, or seafood; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the Cowboy Ribeye — 104-proof premium Four Roses against cap fat. The concentrated blend handles the ribeye with small-batch authority. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label with the Cowboy Ribeye — 80-proof entry bourbon against the cut's scale. Underpowered; strong as a choice but the ribeye earns a heavier pour. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with the Cowboy Ribeye — craft small-batch against cap fat. The boutique character handles the ribeye at the craft-small-batch register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Fox & Oden Double Oaked with the cowboy ribeye — Double Oaked Fox & Oden against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Fox & Oden's intensified character frames the ribeye at distinctive register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Frangelico for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|French 75": "Inverroche gin, lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top. The cocktail's bubbles and delicacy has no weight to meet the Cowboy's marbled fat. Never pair — route Cowboy to Bowdie's Old Fashioned, Espresso Old Fashioned, or a Napa Cab, and save the 75 for openers.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|French Onion": "Cowboy Ribeye into the French onion -- the marbled char wraps the broiled-cheese opener, and the smoke threads the broth. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Frias Block 5": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, full body, high acid. The full body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the tar plays against beefy char. Strong classic Barolo for ribeye — Vajra's exceptional range on the bold cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Piedmont sweet sparkling against a heavily marbled bone-in ribeye — dessert-level sugar is wildly wrong for rich fatted beef. Never pair. Route Moscato to dessert course (brownies, cheesecake, beignets); take Cowboy Ribeye with big Napa Cabs, Barolo, or Super Tuscans.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the G4 Blanco for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof with the Cowboy Ribeye — 104-proof concentrated reposado against cap fat. The extra proof and bold agave handle the ribeye where 80-proof reposados fade; the reposado pour for ribeye orders. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the approachable Irish character carries the cap-fat ribeye, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the cut. Works; EC 18 handles dessert density at aged-HH register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|George T. Stagg": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets BTAC ultra-premium collector, 130+ proof, 15+ years aged, holy-grail BTAC release. One of the rare pairings where GTS actually earns its pour — 26oz cap-fat is heavy enough that GTS's 15+yr cask-strength gravity gets the meeting it deserves. Reverence, not just dinner. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Ghost Block — Oakville Cab with dense blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak, firm tannin. Oakville's golden strip produces Napa's most consistent Cab. The structured body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the graphite-oak meets rendered fat. Peak Oakville Cab for Cowboy — consistent structure at fair price. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin, bold California. The jammy fruit wraps around Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the cracked pepper echoes grilled char. Peak California-Zin for ribeye — bold Napa on the marbled cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12 with the cowboy ribeye — gateway Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Fiddich's apple/pear handles the ribeye at approachable register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14 with the cowboy ribeye — new-charred-oak-finished Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Fiddich 14 handles the ribeye, though the 18's Oloroso-cask depth is the featured Speyside-ribeye match.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18 with the cowboy ribeye — Oloroso + bourbon cask marriage against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; the sherry-cask fruit-and-spice depth is the featured Speyside-ribeye pairing — Fiddich 18 is built for fattier cuts where the lighter siblings feel under-specified.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23 with the cowboy ribeye — 23yr champagne-cask Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Gran Cru's refinement is over-specified for ribeye — Fiddich 18's Oloroso depth is better suited.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12 with the cowboy ribeye — coastal-tropical Highland against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Glenglassaugh 12's fresh-fruit character frames the ribeye at distinctive coastal-Highland register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12 with the cowboy ribeye — softer Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Glenlivet's honey-floral character frames the ribeye at benchmark register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10 with the cowboy ribeye — bourbon-cask Highland against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Glenmorangie 10's lighter profile frames the ribeye at entry-Highland register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18 with the cowboy ribeye — aged bourbon + Oloroso Highland against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Glenmorangie 18 handles the ribeye with aged depth; Oban 18's maritime + sherry-finish is the featured Highland-ribeye match.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Never pair Graham's 10 Year Tawny with Cowboy Ribeye — sweet oxidative port and grilled-beef char fight each other, the toffee residual sugar muddles marbled-ribeye savory. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. Save the Tawny for after dessert.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Never pair Graham's 20 Year Tawny with Cowboy Ribeye — twenty years of cask oxidation plus grilled-beef char collapses the wine's complexity and the residual sugar muddles marbled-ribeye savory. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. Save the Tawny for after.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Never pair Graham's 2017 Vintage Port with Cowboy Ribeye — declared-vintage structure is built for chocolate, not grilled-beef protein char, and the residual sugar clashes with marbled-ribeye savory. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. Save the Port for dessert.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum's unaged silver-spirit body reads alongside the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the blanco-tequila body matches the cap-fat ribeye, and the unaged-cane note plays against the cut. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Grand Marnier lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale with the cowboy ribeye — California 6-botanical craft against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's cask-strength herbal-high-proof sweetness plays cleanly against the ribeye's cap fat and char — the sweet character meets the cap-fat at digestif register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Grey Goose Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Grilled Caesar": "Cowboy Ribeye into the Caesar -- the bone-in ribeye threads the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Gumbo": "The cowboy ribeye after the gumbo -- the gumbo's spiced creole stew sets the table for the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: spice and substance share the plate, the contrast settles the meal. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the HDW 100 Vodka for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 Year's lightly-peated Hakushu character finds neutral with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the cap-fat ribeye, and the green-fruit edge brightens the cut. Works; own-plus-sourced over-specified.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel with the Cowboy Ribeye — BT single-barrel against cap fat. The 88.9-proof complexity handles the ribeye cleanly; a quieter alternative to Blanton's Original at the same distillery. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Hangar 1 Vodka for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Head Fake": "Head Fake's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- a light citrus cocktail can't carry the marbled char — needs whiskey or red wine weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Head Fake for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the cowboy ribeye — 20yr HH collector against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; HH 20's aged depth frames the ribeye at collector register. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100 proof BIB against the cut's cap fat. The bonded weight handles the ribeye where 90-proof Wellers fade; classic Kentucky BIB for the biggest cuts. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof 10-year against cap fat. The aging depth and proof handle the ribeye; a premium storytelling pour for this cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "Rutherford Heitz Cab — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mint, cedar, refined firm tannin. The balanced body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the mint-cedar meets rendered fat. Strong historic-house Cab for Cowboy — Heitz provenance plus Trailside balance, though bigger Cabs like Silver Oak or Caymus peak higher for fattiest ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Flora Adora with the cowboy ribeye — Spring-release floral Hendrick's against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks with the cowboy ribeye — flagship Hendrick's against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Grand Cabaret with the cowboy ribeye — cocktail-culture Hendrick's against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Hendricks Neptunia": "Neptunia with the cowboy ribeye — Scottish-coast Hendrick's against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Hendricks Orbium": "Orbium with the cowboy ribeye — bitter-tonic Hendrick's against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy Cognac's bold cognac-vanilla character holds steady against the ribeye's cap fat and char — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB with the Cowboy Ribeye — aged 100-proof BIB against cap fat. The 10-year bonded depth handles the ribeye with authority; the premium HH BIB for this cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Japanese Harmony's blended Japanese harmony leans against the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the restrained whisky body threads the cap-fat ribeye, and the precision rounds the cut. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Honey Roasted Carrots": "The cowboy ribeye into the honey carrots -- savory gives way to sweet, the close lands cleanly. Works; safe alongside, but not the headline pick.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|House Wedge": "The cowboy ribeye after the wedge -- the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch sets the table for the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: protein meets umami, the table builds substance. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 with the cowboy ribeye — 15yr BT-distilled IW Harper against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; IW Harper 15 frames the ribeye at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Bright Prosecco against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — Glera's light body is crushed by rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Caymus, Silver Oak, or bold-proof bourbon, and keep Prosecco for pre-steak celebration, the raw bar, or starters.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Inhibited": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle mis-pairs with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Inhibited on another course instead.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche with the cowboy ribeye — Western Cape craft gin against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Iron Fish Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon Mezcal Casks with the cowboy ribeye — Iron Fish MI mezcal-cask finish against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Ironfish Mezcal's smoky-agave character frames cap-fat with distinctive Michigan-farm-to-bottle + mezcal-cask resonance.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish with the cowboy ribeye — A. Smith Bowman port-finish against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Isaac Bowman frames the ribeye at Virginia-heritage register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "Anderson Valley Pinot — dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, refined-firm tannin, medium body. The refined tannin stretches toward ribeye's big marbled build though body falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand vs. bolder Cabs. Works as concentrated Pinot for ribeye — Mendocino structure for Pinot-preferring guest.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "Diamond Mountain Cab — volcanic soils, dark fruit, graphite, mineral backbone, firm tannin. The structured body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the graphite meets rendered fat. Strong mountain Cab for Cowboy — Schramsberg estate complexity, though rounder Cabs like Caymus peak higher for crowd-pleasing ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year with the cowboy ribeye — 12yr aged Tennessee against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; JD 12's aged depth handles the ribeye at premium register; JD Bonded's 100-proof is tied here with more backbone approach.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded with the cowboy ribeye — 100-proof Tennessee against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; JD Bonded's higher proof is the featured JD-ribeye pairing — the extra backbone handles cap-fat intensity where Old No. 7 feels under-specified.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7 with the cowboy ribeye — iconic Tennessee against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Old No. 7 handles the ribeye with iconic character; JD Bonded's 100-proof and Sinatra Select's richer oak are better for the heavier cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "JD Sinatra Select with the cowboy ribeye — Sinatra Barrels Tennessee against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Sinatra Select's extra-oak grooved-barrel character is the featured JD-ribeye pairing — the unique barrel treatment gives it backbone for cap-fat the entry JDs can't match.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness sits alongside the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the approachable Irish character carries the cap-fat ribeye, and the gentle barley note frames the cut. Works; the light body doesn't overshoot the salad.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Russian River Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — smooth low-tannin Pinot falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep Jax Calesa for salmon, chicken, or seafood; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Burgundy Chablis against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — lean unoaked Chard crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Chablis for scallops, escargot, crab cake, or the classical seafood course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the cowboy ribeye — limited-edition sea-aged against 26oz cap-fat. Excellent; Marian McLain L.E.'s premium ocean-finish frames cap-fat at collector register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the cowboy ribeye — sea-aged Jefferson's against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Voyage 23's ocean-motion + salt character frames cap-fat with distinctive ocean-finish register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve with the Cowboy Ribeye — 90-proof blended against cap fat and char. Strong; Jefferson's elegance is slightly underserved by the ribeye's scale — Jefferson's VR Twin Oak or Weller 12 handle this cut more fully.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the Cowboy Ribeye — Twin Oak's double-barrel weight against cap fat and char. Where standard Jefferson's Reserve is underserved here, the VR Twin Oak earns the ribeye. A premium non-wheated alternate to Weller 12 for this cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label with the Cowboy Ribeye — 86-proof aged Beam against cap fat and char. Strong; the aging helps vs. White Label, but Knob Creek or Weller 12 are the better ribeye calls.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label with the Cowboy Ribeye — entry bourbon against cap fat and char. Underpowered; Knob Creek, Heaven Hill BIB, or Weller 12 are the right calls for the ribeye. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Johnnie Walker Black": "JW Black with the cowboy ribeye — 12yr iconic blended JW against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; JW Black's aged balanced-smoke handles the ribeye at premium-blend register — the 12-year depth gives it more dimension than JW Red for heavier cuts.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Johnnie Walker Blue": "JW Blue with the cowboy ribeye — ultra-premium luxury JW against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; JW Blue handles the ribeye with collector-register silkiness, though the bottle is better spent on the filet (the refined cut that matches Blue's silky-smooth character).",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Johnnie Walker Red": "JW Red with the cowboy ribeye — peat-influenced entry JW against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; JW Red's bold-smoke is the featured blended-Scotch-ribeye pairing at entry register — the Islay-malt punch handles cap-fat intensity.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Sonoma Alexander Valley Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body can handle Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness but the fine-grained tannin is gentler than rendered fat demands. Strong elegant Cab for Cowboy — more muscular Cabs like Silver Oak, Caymus, or Frias peak higher for fattiest ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — refined Sauv Blanc crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Sancerre for scallops, crab cake, escargot, salmon, or the seafood course where precision shines.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles ribeye's big marbled build and the dark-chocolate plays against the beefy char. Strong cult Napa for ribeye — Colgin prestige on the bold cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's heavy aged-spirit weight leans against the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the high-proof register matches the cap-fat ribeye, and the dense weight underlines the cut. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's caramel-char character sits with the ribeye's cap fat and char — the light body is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character flattens the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Kahlua lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Keenan Chardonnay": "Keenan Chardonnay against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — balanced mountain white crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Keenan for salmon, chicken, lobster mac, or the seafood-and-cream course where it shines.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Keenan Mernet": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — allocated KO 10-year against cap fat. The premium KY rye character handles the ribeye with allocated authority at the collector tier. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the Cowboy Ribeye — KO Confiscated against cap fat. The Prohibition-era narrative and KO craft-blend character handle the ribeye at storytelling register. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's: limited-release Irish luxury icon. Excellent on Cowboy Ribeye; the sherry-cask depth meets fatty bone-in cap-fat at ceremonial register — save the bottle for the guest who appreciates Irish at allocation level.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The medium body is slightly light for Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in ribeye, but the pepper matches grilled char. Works for ribeye — Grand Tinel's Châteauneuf full-body handles the cut harder.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Ketel One Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Knickerbocker Gin for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof high-rye against cap fat and char. The proof earns the cut where the Wellers' 90-proof fades; Knob is the high-rye workhorse for the biggest cuts on the menu. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year with the Cowboy Ribeye — aged 100-proof high-rye against cap fat. The 12 years of aging and 100-proof weight earn the ribeye with Beam's signature nutty-oak character at premium depth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 with the cowboy ribeye — 15yr Beam KC against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; KC 15 frames the ribeye at aged-Beam register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 with the cowboy ribeye — 18yr Beam limited KC against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; KC 18's aged depth frames the ribeye at collector register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof blend against cap fat and char. The dual mash-bill complexity carries the cut; a distinctive high-impact pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof Beam rye against cap fat. The extra proof and bold rye spice handle the ribeye where Sazerac Rye and Michter's fade; the rye-side answer to Knob Creek 12 Year Bourbon's approach to this cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva's rested-agave body sits alongside the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the barrel-rested body handles the cap-fat ribeye, and the oak threads the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo's rested-agave body meets at register with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the oak-aged tequila body threads the cap-fat ribeye, and the cooked-agave note underlines the cut. Strong; the extra proof cuts through the dairy with more conviction than softer reposados.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Añejo with the Cowboy Ribeye — aged Komos against cap fat. Strong; extra añejo depth covers the ribeye with luxury-brand authority.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado with the Cowboy Ribeye — luxury pink reposado against cap fat. Strong; the wine-cask rosé character is slightly out of register for the ribeye's scale.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Lagavulin 8": "Lagavulin 8 alongside the Cowboy Ribeye — sea-spray and smoke meet the ribeye's cap fat. Reaches for the same register as with the Tomahawk; one for guests who want Islay character with a bigger cut but aren't committed to the longest, heaviest dram on the shelf. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Napa Cab — Lail entry-level, polished blackcurrant, cedar, dark cherry, refined medium tannin, full body. The full body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the cedar plays against beefy char. Strong historically-pedigreed Napa Cab for ribeye — Lail family polish on the bold cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin, full body. The firm tannin handles ribeye's big marbled build and the cassis-graphite plays against beefy char. Peak historically-significant Napa Cab for ribeye — Daniel Cuvée named for Robin Lail's father, Mondavi-Inglenook architect. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Lalo Silver lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Laphroaig 10": "Cowboy Ribeye: the cut's marbling and char meet Laphroaig 10's tar, iodine, and phenolic punch. Medicinal brine against cap fat works at full intensity — the uncompromising Islay pour for the ribeye. Less transcendent than with the Tomahawk (where the scale is bigger) but still fully aligned. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch with the Cowboy Ribeye — 92-proof wheated against cap fat and char. The extra 2 proof over Weller Special Reserve helps here; Larceny handles the ribeye a touch better than the softer wheated pours. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Pinot Noir rosé Champagne against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — the berry-led mousse has no weight to meet rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon and keep the rosé for the opener or raw bar.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's classic non-vintage Champagne brightness underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Le Garenne Rosé": "Le Garenne Rosé against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — light Loire pink crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Le Garenne for burrata, wedge, crab cake, scallops, or seafood tower — summer starters only.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- a light citrus cocktail can't carry the marbled char — needs whiskey or red wine weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Lemon Lavender Gin Martini for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Les Pallières Raciné": "Gigondas Rhône Valley Grenache — Les Pallières Raciné, Brunier-family old-vine, wild red fruit, garrigue, iron, earthy mineral, full body. The full body matches Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the earthy mineral echoes grilled char. Peak Gigondas for ribeye — old-vine intensity on the marbled cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Liberator Gin": "Liberator with the cowboy ribeye — 100% Michigan-grain craft against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Licor 43 for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Limoncello for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, spice, medium body, low tannin. The refined structure underserves the big marbled ribeye — Pinot's low tannin falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Works when guest wants lighter red with ribeye; Cabs peak higher.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Little Book": "Little Book with the cowboy ribeye — Jim Beam's Fred Noe annual blended whiskey series (each \"chapter\" a different artistic blend), Booker Noe's son's creative limited release. Excellent; Little Book's chapter-series craft is the featured conversation-piece barrel-proof-ribeye pairing — every pour is a story about which chapter you're serving.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Loaded Potato": "The cowboy ribeye after the loaded potato -- the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup sets the table for the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: cream rounds the protein, the plate composes thick. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Lobster Mac": "* The cowboy ribeye with the lobster mac -- the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness frames the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence. Gold standard; the side that defines the steak course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year with the cowboy ribeye — 20yr aged Highland against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Loch Lomond 20's aged depth frames the ribeye at collector register; Oban 18's maritime + sherry-finish is the featured Highland-ribeye match.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — premium sourced rye against cap fat. The boutique-blend character handles the ribeye at premium-sourced register. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Long Road Gin": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Long Road Gin for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets Los Vecinos traditional-pit-smoke. Excellent; the artisanal-mezcal character frames the ribeye at craft-authenticity register — real Oaxaca on the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- an icon cognac is best as a separate occasion — pair the steak with a Cab or bourbon, then close with the cognac. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not an icon cognac. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Louis XIII Cognac for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Lucano Amaro for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this medium-bodied red's approachable fruit and food-friendly acidity. The elegant red's medium-light frame gets overrun by the bold, marbled steak — the wine shrinks. A weight mismatch; not the clean pairing. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry with the cowboy ribeye — benchmark sherried Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Macallan 12 frames the ribeye with sherried depth; Macallan 18 or Balvenie 21 Portwood are the featured ribeye pairings.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Macallan 18": "★ Macallan 18's bold sherry-dried-fruit character anchors against the ribeye's cap fat and char — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the dried-fruit notes meet the grilled char. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate with the cowboy ribeye — Estate-grown-oak Macallan against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Macallan Estate's distinctive cask character frames the ribeye; Macallan 18 and Balvenie 21 Portwood are the featured sherried-ribeye matches.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin slices Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the black pepper echoes grilled char. Peak Petite-Sirah for ribeye — powerful Napa on the marbled cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot — bright strawberry, red cherry, light floral, soft tannins, light body. The soft structure falls short of ribeye's big marbled build — Pinot's low tannin can't match the cut's fat-and-char. Works when guest wants accessible lighter red with ribeye; Cabs peak higher.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Macauley Stagecoach — Atlas Peak Cab from one of Napa's most celebrated high-elevation vineyards. Dark blackberry, blueberry, espresso, iron, powerful tannin, exceptional length. The high-elevation structure handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the iron-espresso meets rendered fat. Peak celebrated-vineyard Cab for Cowboy. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Mahon Gin": "Mahón with the cowboy ribeye — Balearic-island craft gin against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark with the Cowboy Ribeye — the cut's cap fat pushes past standard Maker's 90-proof register. Capable but not marquee; reach for Maker's Mark Cellar Aged or Weller 12 for true ribeye alignment. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Cowboy Ribeye: the cut's cap fat and char meet Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's mature depth. Standard Maker's fades against the ribeye; the Cellar Aged sits with it. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Malibu Rum for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 16-year aged Michigan rye against cap fat. Extended maturation handles the ribeye with distinctive Michigan-distillery authority. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Marc Brédif Chinon's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the ribeye's cap fat and char — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Margarita": "Margarita's blanco-and-citrus margarita brightness underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- a light citrus cocktail can't carry the marbled char — needs whiskey or red wine weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Margarita for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Marimar Estate Christina": "Russian River Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — plush low-tannin Pinot falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep Marimar Christina for salmon, chicken, or seafood; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Mata Hari Absinthe for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Bourbon with the cowboy ribeye — Michter's single-barrel 10yr against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Michter's 10 frames the ribeye at prestigious-independent register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 10-year aged Michter's single-barrel against cap fat. The premium aged polish handles the ribeye with Michter's small-batch refinement. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 84.8-proof smooth rye against cap fat. Strong; the lower proof and smooth character are slightly outmatched by the cut — Knob Creek Rye's 100-proof handles the ribeye more fully.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Mijenta Blanco on another course instead.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo's crisp agave-vanilla character sits with the ribeye's cap fat and char — the aged-tequila depth grips the marbled cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado with the Cowboy Ribeye — modern 80-proof reposado against cap fat. Strong; the clean character is underpowered for the cut's scale — G4 High Proof is the high-impact reposado.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam with the cowboy ribeye — Sazerac tribute blended whiskey against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Mister Sam frames the ribeye at Seagram's-tribute register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Mocha Creme": "The cowboy ribeye into the mocha creme -- the marbled char-and-fat hands off to silky coffee-chocolate cream, espresso depth closing the meal. Strong; the bold cut meets the layered close at register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 with the cowboy ribeye — Schwarzwald craft gin against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder with the cowboy ribeye — triple-Speyside-malt blend against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Monkey Shoulder's Speyside-malt fruit meets the ribeye at distinctive register; JW Red's bold-smoke is the featured blended-Scotch-ribeye pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- aperitivo bitterness fights the headline cut's marbled char — bitter and savory pull apart. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Montenegro Amaro for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Mount Gay Rum lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Muga Reserva": "Rioja Alta Reserva Tempranillo-blend — Muga, American-French oak, strawberry, dried cherry, vanilla, leather, medium body. The medium body is slightly light for Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in, but the vanilla-oak handles grilled char. Strong Rioja for ribeye — a refined mid-weight match.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Mushroom Bisque": "Cowboy Ribeye into the mushroom bisque -- umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Mushrooms": "Cowboy Ribeye with the mushrooms -- the 26oz cap-and-fat richness threads the mushrooms' earthy umami weight, char meeting forest-floor in the same register. Strong; the side reads built for the marbled cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's bold molasses-Jamaican character lifts the ribeye's cap fat and char — the caramel-and-oak weight rises with the cap-fat. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Negroni": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari. The bitter-orange backbone cuts the Cowboy's marbled fat and Contratto's herbal depth adds complexity; the stirred structure handles the rendered richness. Strong pairing — though the Bowdie's or Espresso OF peak slightly higher for ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — UK-aged Kentucky rye against cap fat. Strong; distinctive character capable but Kentucky high-proof ryes (Pikesville, Knob Creek Rye, E.H. Taylor Rye) handle the cut with more grip.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch with the Cowboy Ribeye — UK-aged Kentucky against cap fat. Strong; the distinctive character is more about the story than the pairing — Weller 12 or Knob Creek are firmer ribeye calls, but Never Say Die is the conversation pour.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with the cowboy ribeye — Holland MI beer-barrel finish against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; NH Beer Barrel's hop-influenced character frames cap-fat with Michigan-hometown-brewer-heritage distinction.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|New York Sour": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. Works — cocktail-on-steak coherence.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Nickel & Nickel — Oakville prestige Cab with deep cassis, blackberry, graphite, subtle oak, concentrated intensity, age-worthy structured finish. The concentrated body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the graphite meets rendered fat directly. Peak prestige Oakville Cab for Cowboy — terroir specificity for the fatted ribeye. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Nolets Dry Gin for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Not a Paper Plane": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Oban 14": "Oban 14 with the cowboy ribeye — West Highland coastal single malt against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Oban's maritime character frames the ribeye; Oban 18's deeper sherry-finish is the featured Highland-ribeye match.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Oban 18": "Oban 18 with the cowboy ribeye — premium aged coastal Highland against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Oban 18 is the featured Highland-ribeye pairing — the aged maritime + sherry-finish depth is built for fattier cuts where even Glenmorangie 18 catches up but doesn't surpass.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo's rested-agave body matches register with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the añejo register carries the cap-fat ribeye, and the soft-spice note softens the cut. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Stags Leap Cab — dark fruit, sage, cedar, graphite, firm refined tannin. The velvet-glove body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the sage-cedar meets rendered fat. Strong refined Cab for Cowboy — iron-fist-velvet-glove balance, though Silver Oak, Caymus, or Frias peak higher for the fattiest ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the cowboy ribeye — Colorado craft bourbon designed for cigar pairing against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Old Elk Cigar Cut's cigar-companion character frames cap-fat with distinctive purpose-built register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer with the cowboy ribeye — emmer-wheat wheated bourbon against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Old Emmer's ancient-grain softness + wheated depth handles cap-fat intensity at craft register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof wheated bond against cap fat. The bonded proof earns the ribeye where 90-proof wheated pours fade; the wheated BIB answer to this cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof aged wheated against cap fat. The 9-year's depth handles the cut with more grace than Old Fitz 7; the mature wheated-BIB for the biggest cuts. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with the cowboy ribeye — aged high-rye OGD against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; OGD 16's rye-spice frames the ribeye at distinctive register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Opus One": "Opus One — Oakville Mondavi-Rothschild flagship, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The full body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the dried-violet plays against the beefy char. Peak collector Napa Cab blend for ribeye — Bordeaux-style flagship on the American bold cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — rich Oregon Chard crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Acolytes for salmon, chicken, scallops, or the cream-and-bisque course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Pablo Sour": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- a light citrus cocktail can't carry the marbled char — needs whiskey or red wine weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Pablo Sour for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Paloma": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The cocktail's light-bright profile has no weight to meet Cowboy's marbled rendered-fat character. Never pair — route Cowboy to Bowdie's Old Fashioned, Espresso Old Fashioned, or Napa Cab, and keep the Paloma for openers.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — unicorn Van Winkle rye against cap fat. The 13-year Pappy depth gives the ribeye Van Winkle-legacy intensity; collector-pour with serious scarcity. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets Heaven Hill annual limited release, 16th Edition in the Parker's Heritage tribute series, collector-register cask-strength. Parker's Heritage brings HH's heritage collector-register craft to 26oz cap-fat — the annual-release provenance makes every pour a conversation about which edition you're serving. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the barrel-rested body handles the cap-fat ribeye, and the oak threads the cut. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver's polished silver tequila with agave-forward lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Patron Silver for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Patron XO Cafe lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Paul Bara Grand Cru rosé Champagne against the marbled bone-in Cowboy ribeye — grower depth is still rosé weight and crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Paul Bara for starters or the raw bar.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "After the cowboy ribeye, the PB brownie -- the rendered fat gives way to peanut-and-chocolate fudge, the vanilla scoop tempering the close. Strong; the bone-in ribeye earns the substantial dessert.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Rutherford Cab — dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body can handle Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness but the refined tannin is gentler than rendered fat demands. Strong Peju for Cowboy — bigger Cabs like Silver Oak, Caymus, or Frias peak higher for the fattiest ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's cognac-orange-peel character sits with the ribeye's cap fat and char — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "2015 Côte des Blancs prestige Champagne against the marbled bone-in Cowboy ribeye — vintage Chardonnay crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Pierre Gimonnet for starters, seafood tower, or the filet course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 110-proof HH Maryland-style rye against cap fat. The high proof handles the ribeye with authority; the highest-impact rye in the approachable tier for this cut, alongside Knob Creek Rye (100-proof) and E.H. Taylor Rye (100-proof BIB). Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Pineau des Charentes": "Never pair Pineau des Charentes with Cowboy Ribeye — fortified sweet Cognac-blend gets lost against marbled-ribeye char and the honey muddles grilled-beef savory. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. Pineau is a before-meal aperitif or post-dessert pour.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Plymouth Gin for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Aldo Conterno Bussia Monforte d'Alba, rose petal, tar, dried cherry, orange peel, iron minerality, full body, firm tannin. The full body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the tar plays against beefy char. Strong serious-Barolo for ribeye — Aldo Conterno prestige on the bold cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "2004 Pommery Cuvée Louise against the marbled bone-in Cowboy ribeye — aged Champagne crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep the Pommery for starters, seafood tower, or the filet course where it shines.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Prime Tartare": "Cowboy Ribeye after the tartare -- protein and umami share the plate, the build reads weighty and clean. Works; the opener sits cleanly before the steak.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Cab — ripe blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft polished tannin. The lush body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the chocolate-vanilla meets rendered fat. Strong polished Napa Cab for Cowboy — indulgent choice, though Silver Oak, Caymus, or mountain Cabs peak higher for the fattiest ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|RAEN Royal St Robert": "Sea-influenced Sonoma Coast Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — silky Burgundian California Pinot falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep RAEN for salmon, chicken, or seafood; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Raventós Cava rosé against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — light body crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Cava for starters, burrata, or seafood tower — it's the opener, not the main event.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Redbreast 12 Year": "Cowboy Ribeye: fatty bone-in cut meets Redbreast 12 Year. Excellent; single-pot-still Irish character frames cap fat at Redbreast's flagship register — Irish-pot-still depth on the most forgiving steak cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish reads alongside the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the malt-grain blend handles the cap-fat ribeye, and the gentle barley note frames the cut. Works; Caribou Crossing is lighter than ribeye demands; CC 43 Year handles it better.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Gatsby with the cowboy ribeye — MGP high-rye 15yr against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Remus 15's aged rye-spice frames the ribeye at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Remy VSOP Cognac's cognac-fruit character finds register with the ribeye's cap fat and char — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo with the Cowboy Ribeye — Moorish-inspired aged against cap fat. Strong; distinctive-bottle extra añejo covers the ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reyka Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique with the Cowboy Ribeye — retailer-label aged bourbon against cap fat. The boutique-label aged character handles the ribeye at premium-sourced register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The cowboy ribeye after the chickpea soup -- the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth sets the table for the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: spice and substance share the plate, the contrast settles the meal. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit austere Champagne against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — blanc de blancs precision crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Moncuit for starters, seafood tower, or filet course where it shines.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Roku Gin": "Roku Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Roku Gin for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey character frames the ribeye's cap fat and char — the light body is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with the Cowboy Ribeye — aged 90-proof Wild Turkey against cap fat and char. The age gives the pour enough body for the ribeye; a non-wheated alternate to Weller 12 or Eagle Rare 10. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Sambuca": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Sambuca for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|San Simeon Stormwatch": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin, full body. The full body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the mocha plays against beefy char. Strong Paso value Bordeaux blend for ribeye — solid full-body on the bold cut at accessible price.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Santa Barbara Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — rich medium-tannin Pinot still falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep Sanford & Benedict for filet, salmon, or chicken; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The cowboy ribeye with the garlic spinach -- the 26oz cap-fat cut sits with the wilted garlic-buttered greens, the garlic cutting through the rendered fat with a clean edge. Strong; the side holds against the cut without crowding.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Sauternes Glass": "Never pair Sauternes Glass with Cowboy Ribeye — botrytis honey and orange marmalade muddle marbled-ribeye char and luscious unctuous clashes with grilled-beef savory. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. Sauternes is a finishing pour.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The rye's spice cuts the Cowboy's marbled fat and the absinthe-edge nose adds complexity to the char; Peychaud's floral-cherry softens the weight. Strong spirit-forward pour for the fattiest cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — BT's rye against cap fat. Strong; the 90-proof spicy-sweet rye handles the cut well but Knob Creek Rye's 100-proof or E.H. Taylor Rye bring more grip for the ribeye's scale.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the cowboy ribeye — New Zealand single malt against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Fortuna's NZ terroir frames the ribeye at distinctive register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid, full body. The full body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the leather plays against beefy char. Strong King-of-Italian for ribeye — Scavino prestige on the bold cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — light off-dry German white crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Riesling for bisque, crab cake, scallops, salmon, or burrata.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin, full body. The full body meets ribeye's big marbled build though medium tannin reads softer than firm-tannin Napa Cabs on the cut. Works as by-the-glass Cab for ribeye — accessible value when guest wants Cab at the price point.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Seafood Tower": "Cowboy Ribeye after the seafood tower -- the iced shellfish opens with bright cold-brine, the 26oz marbled char-and-fat following with rendered richness that the cold opener primed the palate to receive. Strong; the contrast carries the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Seared Scallops": "The cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness overwhelms the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness: the rendered fat overshadows the scallops, and the marbled char crowds out the seared-rare scallop plate. Avoid; the courses can't share a meal cleanly. Pair the scallops with Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre on a different visit; pour Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, or Shafer Hillside Select alongside the cowboy ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Seasonal Soup": "Cowboy Ribeye with the seasonal soup alongside -- a quiet call, the meal carries without strain. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Seasonal Vegetables": "Cowboy Ribeye with the seasonal vegetables -- a clean call, no friction either way. Works; the side carries cleanly with the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Tuscany Brunello DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Sesti biodynamic, cherry, dried rose, sandalwood, high acid, firm tannin. The firm tannin meets Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in ribeye and the high acid lifts the rendered fat. Peak biodynamic-Brunello for the ribeye — Sesti's elegance polishes bold char. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Shafer 1.5": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level named for magnum format, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin, smooth full body. The plush body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the mocha plays against beefy char. Peak Stags Leap Cab for ribeye — bridge-to-Hillside-Select on the bold cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Shafer Hillside Select": "Stags Leap collector's Cab — Hillside Select 32-month French oak, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violet, graphite, dense yet silky, firm tannin. The dense silky structure handles ribeye's big marbled build and the dark chocolate plays against beefy char. Peak collector's Napa Cab for ribeye — Hillside Select extraordinary length on the bold cut. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Shrimp Bisque": "The cowboy ribeye after the shrimp bisque -- the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup sets the table for the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: two rich courses on the same plate, the table earns its substance. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Shrimp Cocktail": "Cowboy Ribeye after the shrimp cocktail -- the chilled shrimp's citrus-horseradish snap cleans the palate, the 26oz cap-and-fat cut following with rendered char-and-fat richness. Strong; the bright opener primes the indulgent cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Siete Leguas Blanco for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado with the Cowboy Ribeye — tahona-crushed reposado against cap fat. Strong; the rustic craft character is more at home than polished reposados here, though G4 High Proof brings more grip.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak — Alexander Valley Sonoma Cab aged exclusively in American oak, giving distinctive vanilla, coconut, dill alongside ripe cassis, plum, soft polished tannin. The American-oak lushness handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness directly. Peak recognizable Cab for Cowboy — guests who know Cabernet know Silver Oak. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Boutique Napa family estate Cab — dark plum, cassis, toasted oak, long refined finish, firm tannin, full body. The firm tannin handles ribeye's big marbled build and the toasted oak plays against beefy char. Peak boutique Napa Cab for ribeye — Simon Family reserve-level option for guests who want less well-known but equally serious. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "St. Helena Napa Cab — Spottswoode organic family estate, plum, dark cherry, violet, silky medium tannin, full body. The full body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the violet plays against beefy char. Strong organic Napa Cab for ribeye — Spottswoode family story on the bold cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab — blackberry, cassis, mocha, firm tannin. The firm structure handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the mocha meets rendered fat. Strong by-the-glass Cab for Cowboy — classic Napa character, though bigger Cabs like Silver Oak or Caymus peak higher for the fattest ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "Napa Sauvignon Blanc against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — Napa Sauv Blanc crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep St Supéry for crab cake, scallops, shrimp cocktail, wedge, or the seafood course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin, full body. The full body and firm tannin handle ribeye's big marbled build and the espresso plays against the char. Strong estate Meritage for ribeye — age-integrated Napa on the bold cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the cowboy ribeye — BT non-age-statement cask-strength, premium batch, the \"little brother to GTS\" at accessible BT register. Excellent; Stagg Jr.'s cask-strength handles 26oz cap-fat with BT premium-barrel-proof character — the way to get GTS-adjacent intensity without holy-grail allocation.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Stoli Elit Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Stoneleigh Marlborough Sauv Blanc against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — zesty NZ Sauv Blanc crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Stoneleigh for wedge, burrata, crab cake, or seafood tower.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Tanq 10 Gin for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Tanqueray Gin for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Never pair Taylor Fladgate Tawny with Cowboy Ribeye — 1692 heritage Tawny's residual sugar muddles marbled-ribeye char and the smooth balance loses against grilled-beef protein weight. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. Save the Tawny for after.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Never pair Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle with Cowboy Ribeye — flagship vintage structure is built for chocolate, not grilled-beef protein char, and residual sugar clashes with marbled-ribeye savory. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. The Vintage Bottle belongs at dessert.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with the Cowboy Ribeye — triple-cask aged against cap fat. Strong; multi-cask depth covers the ribeye with González's legendary craft authority.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Cab — rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth lingering finish, polished. The crowd-pleaser body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the mocha meets rendered fat. Peak mid-tier Napa Cab for Cowboy — polished concentration frames fat-and-char without the prestige-bottle price. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|The BG": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. The whiskey's barrel depth and the steak's flame-grilled fat run at the same intensity — a classic steakhouse move. A headliner steakhouse cocktail. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|The Happy Wife": "The Happy Wife's gin-or-tequila cocktail lift sits alongside the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the bright build frames the cap-fat ribeye, and the citrus cuts the cut. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|The Manhattan": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The Manhattan's bourbon depth stands against the Cowboy's marbled fat but the lighter vanilla-syrup softening doesn't quite match the Bowdie's Old Fashioned or Espresso Old Fashioned's weight here. Strong alternative spirit pour.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|The Prisoner Red Blend": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The full body matches Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the smooth tannin won't tear against rendered fat. Peak Prisoner for ribeye — reliable familiar sell on the marbled cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — BTAC barrel-proof against cap fat. The uncut unfiltered Sazerac intensity handles the ribeye with BT-allocated authority; an annual-release marquee pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the cowboy ribeye — Barton chardonnay-cask against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Works; TSM Chardonnay Cask is under-specified for cap-fat — white-wine finish is lighter than red; AE Cask Strength handles ribeye better.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Titos Vodka": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Titos Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character leans against the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the Japanese whisky balance frames the cap-fat ribeye, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the cut. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Tomato Basil": "The cowboy ribeye after the tomato basil -- the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid sets the table for the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the bright register cleaves the heavy course, the table breathes. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Transfusion": "Transfusion's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- a light citrus cocktail can't carry the marbled char — needs whiskey or red wine weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Transfusion lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado with the Cowboy Ribeye — cedar-wood-aged against cap fat. Strong; the cedar gives the ribeye a unique frame.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Truffle Fries": "Cowboy Ribeye alongside the truffle fries -- the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness meets the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight: protein and umami share the plate, the build reads weighty and clean. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s gentle Irish blend sits alongside the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the Irish whiskey body sits with the cap-fat ribeye, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the cut. Works; richer profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Venge Family Reserve": "Kirk Venge flagship Oakville Cab — concentrated blackcurrant, dark chocolate, vanilla, seamless polished finish, soft tannin, full body. The full body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the vanilla-chocolate plays against beefy char. Peak Oakville flagship for ribeye — step above Silencieux in power and presence. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Venge Scout's Honor": "Napa Zin-led blend — Kirk Venge's Scout's Honor, blackberry, boysenberry, black pepper, vanilla, full body, generous finish. The black pepper matches marbled-ribeye char and the ripe Zin fruit wraps around grilled-beef fat. Peak for the 26oz bone-in — bold Californian fruit handles ribeye intensity. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Venge Silencieux": "Kirk Venge single-vineyard Napa — dark cherry, cassis, chocolate, floral violet, silky soft tannin, full body, long focused finish. The silky structure handles ribeye's big marbled build and the violet plays against beefy char. Peak winemaker's Napa Cab for ribeye — Venge precision on the bold cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Vesper": "Vesper's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- a light citrus cocktail can't carry the marbled char — needs whiskey or red wine weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Vesper for another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — iconic yellow label crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Veuve for starters, seafood tower, or celebration pours before the steak course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. The rye-cognac split and Bénédictine's herbal depth meet the Cowboy's marbled fat well; complexity layers with crust character. Strong alternative to Cab-forward or Old Fashioned options for the fatty cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Villon Cognac": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge leans against the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the brandy depth wraps the cap-fat ribeye, and the caramel rounds the cut. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Vin Santo": "Never pair Vin Santo with Cowboy Ribeye — Tuscan oxidative dessert wine's honey residual sugar muddles marbled-ribeye char and the walnut clashes with grilled-beef savory. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. Vin Santo is a finishing pour.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Weller 107": "Weller 107 with the Cowboy Ribeye — 107 proof earns the ribeye where Weller Special Reserve falls short. The cap fat meets the pour's bigger body head-on; this is the strongest Weller call for this cut (Weller 12 is the alternate refined pour). Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year with the Cowboy Ribeye — twelve years of aging delivers enough oak-softened depth to carry the ribeye's cap fat and bold char. Richer and more aligned than Weller Special Reserve here; where the younger Wellers are solid, Weller 12 shines. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium with the Cowboy Ribeye — ultra-premium wheated meets ultra-premium cut. The Millennium's deep oak carries the ribeye's cap fat and char with composure; a marquee pairing when the bill isn't the concern. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve with the Cowboy Ribeye — the cut's cap fat pushes slightly past the 90-proof Weller's register. Capable, but the ribeye earns Weller 12 or Weller 107 for true alignment. Strong as a choice; not the standout.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Wheatley Vodka belongs on another course.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof 10-year single-barrel against cap fat. The aged WP depth and cut's marbling find each other at the premium rye register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with the Cowboy Ribeye — fruit-cask-finished 12-year against cap fat. The Sauternes/Madeira/Port influence gives the ribeye layered fruit-and-oak complexity unique to Old World finishing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with the Cowboy Ribeye — 15-year Vermont-oak-finished rye against cap fat. The extended maturation and Vermont finish earn the ribeye with premium flagship authority. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with the Cowboy Ribeye — barrel-proof Boss Hog against cap fat. The allocated ultra-premium WP intensity handles the ribeye with unicorn-level authority; pour for the table that asked for WP's best. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with the Cowboy Ribeye — 10th-edition Boss Hog against cap fat. The X Commandments annual character gives the ribeye collector-bottle authority; the specific-year Boss Hog for ribeye. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof WP against cap fat. Strong; the Vermont-brand accessible tier is capable but Knob Creek Rye, E.H. Taylor Rye, or Pikesville Rye bring more grip for the ribeye.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with the Cowboy Ribeye — craft high-rye against cap fat and char. The 94-proof handles the ribeye; distinct from Knob Creek's bigger-distillery 100-proof take — more craft, more oak spice. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with the Cowboy Ribeye — small-batch fruit-forward against cap fat and char. Strong; Woodford's elegance is slightly underserved by the ribeye's scale — BT or Weller 12 are firmer calls for max-impact cuts.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with the cowboy ribeye — Brown-Forman Double Oaked against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Woodford Double Oaked's intensified character frames the ribeye at KY-benchmark register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 90.4-proof small-batch against cap fat. Strong; the elegance is slightly ambitious for the ribeye's scale — Knob Creek Rye or E.H. Taylor Rye bring more grip.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — Washington State small-batch against cap fat. Strong; PNW craft capable but higher-proof Kentucky ryes bring more grip for this cut.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the restrained whisky body threads the cap-fat ribeye, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the cut. Works; Caribbean Cask frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the cap-fat ribeye, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the cut. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Cowboy Ribeye|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for another course.",
  "Crab Cake|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with crab cake — Barton small-batch against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat with crab cake — sweet-wheat against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|1881 Napa Valley": "Entry Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — mocha-tannin body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep 1881 for the steak course where its Napa step-up character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|2 James Gin": "2 James with crab cake — Detroit distillery local craft gin, American interpretation of London Dry. Excellent; 2 James is the hometown pairing — Michigan craft gin with delicate Michigan-adjacent seafood, the local-proud choice on the menu.",
  "Crab Cake|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with crab cake — rare craft release against delicate crab. Avoid; save the rare bottle.",
  "Crab Cake|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with crab cake — double-matured Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Adictivo Cristalino": "Crab Cake: lump crab meets Adictivo Cristalino — filtered cristalino, clear liquid carrying aged character (reposado/añejo filtered to blanco appearance). Distinctive in the blanco cluster; technically aged but presents clear. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado with crab cake — long-aged reposado against lump crab. The extended-maturation oak gives the pairing more weight than everyday reposados bring to delicate seafood. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced. The oak rounds the pairing against crab's sweet meat and the apple-pear fruit plays cleanly against romesco. Strong accessible Chardonnay for crab cake — approachable California weight on the classic starter.",
  "Crab Cake|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Sonoma Meritage against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — full-body cassis-cedar-chocolate structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Cyrus for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Sonoma Bordeaux blend against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — plum-blackberry-mocha structure buries the shellfish's sweet delicacy. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Homestead Red for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino with crab cake — grappa-based amaro against delicate crab. Avoid; amari aren't built for seafood.",
  "Crab Cake|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character smothers the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sugar buries the delicate shellfish without any acid to balance. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Ancho Reyes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crab Cake|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with crab cake — port-finished Louisville bourbon against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with crab cake — cask-strength port-finish against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "AE Triple Cask with crab cake — multi-cask AE against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Aperol": "Aperol with crab cake — softest amaro against delicate crab. Works; this is the one amaro in the family gentle enough to sit alongside crab — not a featured match, but it doesn't overpower.",
  "Crab Cake|Asparagus": "Crab Cake alongside the asparagus -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust meets the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: both light, the meal stays composed. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Crab Cake|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The crab cake and the au gratin -- delicate gives way to rich, the table earns its weight. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Crab Cake|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Bold Paso Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — plush warm-spice body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Austin Hope for the steak course where its Paso warmth shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Averna Amaro": "Averna with crab cake — Sicilian amaro against delicate crab. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Crab Cake|Aviation": "Aviation gin with Luxardo maraschino, crème de violette, ginger syrup. The cocktail's floral-cherry profile plays against crab's sweet-delicate meat and the ginger adds warmth against romesco. Peak cocktail for crab cake when guest wants visual drama alongside the pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Aviation Gin": "Crab Cake: delicate lump crab meets Portland OR American craft, soft juniper with lavender, cardamom, and orange peel. Aviation's smooth-balanced profile is the crowd-pleasing gin-crab pairing — where Bluecoat citruses and Empress floralizes, Aviation just delivers the expected with softer American character. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Avion 44": "Avion 44 with crab cake — aged Avion against delicate crab. Avoid; save the premium aged Avion.",
  "Crab Cake|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver with crab cake — 80-proof Jalisco-highlands blanco, filtered and clean. Global distribution; approachable and craft-adjacent. The clean filtered agave lifts the crab at the globally-familiar register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|Bacardi Rum": "Crab Cake: delicate lump crab meets Bacardi Rum. Excellent; the ultra-clean cocktail-workhorse register threads lump crab without stepping on it — this is exactly what Bacardi was built for.",
  "Crab Cake|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Baileys Irish Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crab Cake|Baker's": "Baker's with crab cake — 107-proof small-batch against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with crab cake — Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with crab cake — Caribbean-rum-cask Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with crab cake — collector-register Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood.",
  "Crab Cake|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood with crab cake — ultra-premium port-cask Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood — save the collector bottle.",
  "Crab Cake|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with crab cake — Bardstown French-wine finish against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with crab cake — craft-blender against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with crab cake — stout-barrel bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare with crab cake — tropical rum-cask Bardstown against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with crab cake — Fusion blend against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with crab cake — retailer-pick Bardstown against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with crab cake — even light high-rye overshoots crab delicacy. Avoid; the pour's spice doesn't find the crab's sweetness. Reach for a white wine or a gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year with crab cake — aged bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; even refined Basil Hayden's overshoots the starter.",
  "Crab Cake|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with crab cake — malted rye against crab delicacy. Avoid; even distinctive character doesn't rescue the register mismatch.",
  "Crab Cake|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with crab cake — high-rye BH red-wine finish against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast with crab cake — toasted caramel against delicate crab is a mismatch. Avoid; choose a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Bee's Knees": "Empress Indigo gin (butterfly-pea blue-grey), lemon, honey lavender syrup. The honey softens the lemon's bite and meets crab's sweet meat directly; lavender adds floral complexity to the romesco. Peak opener cocktail for crab cake — lightness with visual interest in the glass.",
  "Crab Cake|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater with crab cake — 40% London Dry, 9 botanicals, 24-hour steep. Excellent; Beefeater's balanced juniper-citrus is the democratic workhorse for crab — where Tanq lifts with proof and Plymouth softens, Beefeater just works reliably at approachable register.",
  "Crab Cake|Beignets": "The crab cake and the beignets -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust bookends with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Crab Cake|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere with crab cake — Polish rye against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Belvedere's subtle pepper adds dimension to the crab that softer vodkas miss.",
  "Crab Cake|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with crab cake — blended bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with crab cake — 103-proof richer Blanton's against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with crab cake — premium single-barrel against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's SfB with crab cake — cask-strength Blanton's against delicate lump crab. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with crab cake — decennial milestone Pact against delicate crab. Avoid; save the 10th-Pact collector bottle.",
  "Crab Cake|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with crab cake — Pact 8 annual-release against delicate crab. Avoid; save the Blood Oath allocation.",
  "Crab Cake|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with crab cake — Pact 9 wine-cask against delicate crab. Avoid; save the Blood Oath bottle.",
  "Crab Cake|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat with crab cake — Philadelphia American dry gin with 4 organic citrus peels (lemon, orange, and more). Excellent; Bluecoat's citrus-forward patriotic-artisan profile frames delicate crab with bright American-citrus character — more lifting than Aviation's lavender softness.",
  "Crab Cake|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Never pair Noemia with crab cake — blackberry, graphite, and full body bury delicate lump crab and the silky tannin can't lift the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry with crab cake — 8-botanical London Dry against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Bombay Dry's juniper-forward character frames crab with angular precision.",
  "Crab Cake|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire with crab cake — vapor-infused floral gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Sapphire's softness is the gentlest approach to crab in the London Dry batch — less aggression than Tanq, more floral than Beefeater.",
  "Crab Cake|Bone-In Filet": "The bone-in filet after the crab cake -- the panko-and-lump crab opens with delicate sweetness, the 14oz bone-enhanced cut following with marrow-touched butter that doesn't crowd the opener's memory. Strong; refined opener into bone-deepened cut.",
  "Crab Cake|Booker's": "Booker's with crab cake — 120-130 proof Beam against delicate lump crab. Avoid; barrel-proof intensity obliterates crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Botanist Gin": "Botanist with crab cake — Bruichladdich Islay 31-botanical craft gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Botanist's wildcrafted herbal complexity lifts crab with Hebridean character no classic London Dry can replicate.",
  "Crab Cake|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, bitters. Spirit-forward bourbon overwhelms the crab's sweet-delicate meat and the cocktail's weight doesn't meet the light romesco. Never pair — crab cake belongs with Sauvignon Blanc, Champagne, or a lighter cocktail.",
  "Crab Cake|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with crab cake — house-pick depth against delicate crab. Avoid; the pour's specificity doesn't rescue the register mismatch.",
  "Crab Cake|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with crab cake — Virginia small-batch against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Bowmore 12": "Crab Cake: the delicate crab cake gets overpowered by any peat, Bowmore's gentle profile included. Sherry-soft doesn't rescue it. Avoid across all three Islays for delicate seafood.",
  "Crab Cake|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Italian food-wine Barbera against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Braida's high-acid juicy-cherry profile could theoretically match seafood, but dark cherry and blackberry still overpower the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Braida for red meat and pasta.",
  "Crab Cake|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Mount Veeder mountain Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm-tannin graphite-earth crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Brandlin for the steak course where its Mount Veeder food-driven character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Broccoli Cheddar": "The crab cake preceded by the broccoli cheddar -- restrained yields to cream, the table arcs cleanly. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "Crab Cake|Broccolini": "Crab Cake alongside the broccolini -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust meets the broccolini's tender green stalks: two restrained registers, the table reads air across the courses. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Crab Cake|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich: this Islay Scotch's intense peat smoke, medicinal phenol, and bold weight meets this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness. The whiskey sits heavy against a starter; the match depends on whether the plate has fat to scrub. Works as a parallel pour.",
  "Crab Cake|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with crab cake — flagship bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with crab cake — high-rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Butternut Squash": "Crab Cake and the butternut squash together -- the lighter course hands off to dessert, the meal closes clean. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Crab Cake|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Howell Mountain Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm-tannin iron-mineral crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Cade for the steak course where its LEED-estate Howell Mountain character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila with crab cake — entry reposado against lump crab. The approachable agave lifts the crab at the value tier. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Campari": "Campari with crab cake — ruby bitter against delicate crab. Avoid; even the aperitivo bitter is too much for crab.",
  "Crab Cake|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club with crab cake — traditional Canadian against delicate lump crab. Works; Canadian Club's grain-forward easy-drinking character sits with crab at heritage register; not featured.",
  "Crab Cake|Canadian Club 43 Year": "CC 43 Year with crab cake — 43yr ultra-premium Canadian against delicate lump crab. Works; save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Crab Cake|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's spiced-vanilla character frames the lump crab's delicate sweetness — the crisp body lifts the shellfish without overwhelming, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Crab Cake|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing with crab cake — Sazerac single-barrel Canadian against delicate lump crab. Works; Caribou Crossing's craft character sits with crab at single-barrel register; not featured.",
  "Crab Cake|Carrot Cake": "The crab cake alongside the carrot cake -- restrained meets sugar, the close composes light. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Crab Cake|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Casa LBV Port on the steak course instead.",
  "Crab Cake|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- mezcal smoke obliterates delicate shellfish — needs sparkling or sancerre. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a mezcal. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Casamigos Mezcal on the steak course instead.",
  "Crab Cake|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — opulent cocoa-mocha crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep the iconic Napa label for the steak course where its Rutherford character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Chambord lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crab Cake|Cheesecake": "The crab cake and the cheesecake -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust bookends with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Crab Cake|Chilean Seabass": "The seabass and the crab cake across courses -- the silky-flesh seabass plays against the crisp-crusted crab. Strong; the opener carries its register before the main.",
  "Crab Cake|Chocolate Brownie": "The crab cake alongside the brownie -- the delicate course resolves into the close, the meal stays unburdened. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Crab Cake|Chocolate Cake": "Crab Cake and the chocolate cake -- restrained meets sugar, the close composes light. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Crab Cake|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin with crab cake — single-ingredient potato vodka against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Chopin's silky texture doesn't overpower crab and the potato mouthfeel is a distinctive frame no wheat vodka offers.",
  "Crab Cake|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with crab cake — American brandy against delicate crab. Avoid; brandy weight wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Château Batailley": "Classified Pauillac Bordeaux against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — firm-tannin blackcurrant-graphite structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Batailley for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Château Beaucastel": "Never pair Beaucastel with crab cake — leather, truffle, and Provençal herbs bury delicate lump crab and the full body fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Never pair Château Gombaude-Guillot with crab cake — plush Pomerol Merlot and velvety weight crush lump-crab delicacy and romesco cream. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or French 75. Gombaude-Guillot belongs on the filet course.",
  "Crab Cake|Château Haut Segottes": "Right-bank Bordeaux against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — even soft-tannin Merlot-led Grand Cru buries the shellfish's sweet delicacy. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Château de Rouillac": "Left-bank Bordeaux against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — firm-tannin blackcurrant-cedar structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Château de Rouillac for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Clam Chowder": "The crab cake and the chowder -- both shellfish-forward, the table doubles up on shellfish. Avoid; pick one or the other. Pair the crab cake with Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin; pair the chowder with Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis.",
  "Crab Cake|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- añejo weight has no foil against cold shellfish — needs sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Clase Azul Anejo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crab Cake|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango with crab cake — ceremonial Durango against delicate crab. Avoid; save the ceremony.",
  "Crab Cake|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold with crab cake — golden-ceremonial against delicate crab. Avoid; save the ceremony.",
  "Crab Cake|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- mezcal smoke obliterates delicate shellfish — needs sparkling or sancerre. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a mezcal. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata with crab cake — ultra-premium ceremonial blanco in hand-painted ceramic. The luxury smooth agave lifts the lump crab with the same ceremony the reposado brings. A premium blanco-seafood call; the presentation is as much the pairing as the flavor. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado with crab cake — ceramic-bottle luxury against lump crab. The smooth agave lifts the crab cleanly; a premium tequila-seafood ceremony. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra with crab cake — 5-year top-tier against delicate crab. Avoid; save the Ultra — collector-grade bottle deserves ceremony-level pairings.",
  "Crab Cake|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep with crab cake — Beam wheated against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Never pair Clos Petit Ona with crab cake — kirsch and garrigue bury delicate lump crab and the warm spice fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with crab cake — Cabernet-cask reposado against lump crab. The wine influence brings berry notes to the crab in a way no standard reposado does; distinctive. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- amaro bitterness on cold shellfish flattens the delicate brine — needs sparkling instead. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Contratto Vermouth for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Corazon Blanco": "Crab Cake: lump crab meets Corazon Blanco — Sazerac's tequila brand, single-estate highland agave, 80-proof. Distinct from Don Julio Blanco's benchmark polish — more agave-forward highland character. Sister-pour to Corazon Reposado. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado with crab cake — single-estate agave against lump crab. The highland-grown agave brightens the crab distinctly from standard reposados; a character-forward seafood pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's aged-agave character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- añejo weight has no foil against cold shellfish — needs sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Corazon Sazerac for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg's añejo tequila finished in ex-George-T overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Corazon Stagg for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's aged-agave character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Corazon Weller belongs on the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Never pair Corazón Malbec with crab cake — jam-forward plum and blackberry bury delicate lump crab and the full body fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|Corpse Reviver": "Warner's gin, Lillet, Pierre Ferrand cognac, lemon, absinthe-rinsed glass. The citrus-and-anise-edge cocktail cuts crab's romesco and cognac adds layered depth against the delicate meat. Peak classical opener pairing for crab cake — both lean into craft and history.",
  "Crab Cake|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier with crab cake — drier cognac against delicate crab. Avoid; cognac is wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Cowboy Ribeye": "The cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness obliterates the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust: the marbled char overshadows the delicate crab cake, and the rendered fat crowds out the sweet shellfish plate. Avoid; the cut is too bold for the delicate starter. Stand the crab cake on its own course -- pair the crab cake with Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin -- and pour Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, or Shafer Hillside Select for the cowboy ribeye.",
  "Crab Cake|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 with crab cake — Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Creamed Spinach": "Crab Cake alongside the creamed spinach -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust meets the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: restrained yields to cream, the table arcs cleanly. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Crab Cake|Creamy Potato": "Crab Cake and the potato soup together -- the delicate plate yields to rich, the meal builds. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Crab Cake|Creme Brulee": "Crab Cake and the brulee -- the lighter course hands off to dessert, the meal closes clean. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Crab Cake|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral backbone, low tannin. The low tannin leaves room for crab's sweet meat and the minerality plays against romesco. Peak Oregon Pinot for crab cake — benchmark red that works where heavier reds would bury the shellfish. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal with crab cake — iconic smooth Canadian against delicate lump crab. Works; Crown Royal is gentle enough to sit with crab; not featured but possible at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Crab Cake|Cucumber Gimlet": "Hendrick's muddled with cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, topped with soda. The cucumber-forward build brings freshness against the rich crab cake and tempers the jalapeño heat in our romesco. Cooling cocktail that doesn't fight. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12 with crab cake — Highland luxury sherry-finish against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body. The chalky acid cuts romesco and the dried-herbs play against crab's sweet meat. Peak Loire red for crab cake — unconventional French Pinot where heavier reds would bury the shellfish. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Rare Napa CF against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Darioush's medium-tannin violet-herb structure buries the shellfish even though it's lighter than Cab Sauvignon. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Darioush for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Opulent Silverado Trail Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — plush mocha-spice crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Darioush for the steak course where its Persian-inspired plush character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin with crab cake — Detroit urban craft gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Detroit City's juniper-forward profile frames crab with hometown-Detroit distinction — the local-pride pick alongside 2 James in the Detroit gin program.",
  "Crab Cake|Detroit City Vodka": "Crab Cake: delicate lump crab meets Detroit City Distillery, Michigan-craft hometown vodka. Detroit City is the local-pride pairing for delicate seafood — parallel to 2 James Gin in the gin category, the Michigan answer when a guest wants something local and story-driven. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15 with crab cake — 15yr aged Dewar's against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label with crab cake — entry blended Scotch against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Disaronno belongs on the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with crab cake — craft blended rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum on the steak course instead.",
  "Crab Cake|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Willamette flagship Pinot Blend — dark cherry, Bing cherry, toasted spice, forest floor, silky soft tannin. The soft tannin doesn't fight crab's sweet meat and the forest-floor plays against romesco. Strong flagship Willamette Pinot for crab cake — layered Oregon red on shellfish.",
  "Crab Cake|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Never pair Tempier with crab cake — game, olive, and smoky mineral bury delicate lump crab and firm tannin fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — Sauv Blanc with gun-flint minerality, grapefruit, lime zest. The smoky-mineral backbone cuts crab's romesco and the citrus plays against the sweet meat cleanly. Peak serious Sauv Blanc for crab cake — more body than NZ expressions, more depth than Sancerre. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Crab Cake|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Never pair Grand Tinel with crab cake — garrigue, black pepper, and full body bury delicate lump crab and the herbal earth fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|Don Fulano Anejo": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Don Fulano Anejo on the steak course instead.",
  "Crab Cake|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco with crab cake — premium highland blanco, 80 proof, refined craft. The Jalisco-highland agave gives the crab a cleaner lift than Don Julio's benchmark; distinct sibling to Don Fulano Reposado in the blanco register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado with crab cake — premium highland agave against lump crab. The refinement lifts the crab with more elegance than standard Don Julio; a sophisticated reposado-seafood pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942's icon extra-añejo character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- añejo weight has no foil against cold shellfish — needs sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Don Julio 1942 for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th with crab cake — the crystalline reposado's filtered clarity against lump crab. More delicate than standard Don Julio here; the 70th lets the crab fully lead. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco with crab cake — the benchmark premium blanco. 80-proof, fresh agave with citrus and clean minerality. The bright agave lifts the lump crab's sweetness; the industry-standard reference for blanco-seafood pairings at Bowdie's. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Don Julio Reposado": "Don Julio Reposado with crab cake — the reposado's bright agave and light oak lift the lump crab's sweetness. Tequila's signature seafood pairing at the everyday reposado tier; Don Julio is the benchmark. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Drambuie": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Drambuie for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Dunville's Single Malt": "Dunville's Single Malt with crab cake — Irish single malt against delicate lump crab. Works; Irish whiskey's category advantage lets Dunville's sit with crab at distinctive register.",
  "Crab Cake|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with crab cake — BT Bottled-in-Bond against delicate lump crab. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye with crab cake — 100-proof BIB against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with crab cake — 100-proof BIB against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with crab cake — aged bourbon weight against delicate crab. Avoid; reach for a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Eastern Kille with crab cake — Grand Rapids classic London Dry against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Eastern Kille's juniper-forward character lifts crab with Grand Rapids hometown pride — the local-pride pick parallel to Long Road in the GR program.",
  "Crab Cake|El Cabo": "El Cabo with crab cake — craft small-batch reposado against lump crab. The agave-forward profile lifts the crab cleanly; a boutique interpretation of reposado-seafood. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "El Mayor Extra Anejo with crab cake — aged El Mayor against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado with crab cake — value reposado against lump crab. The light-oak agave lifts the crab at the everyday register; tequila-seafood pairing at the value tier. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin with crab cake — German craft distillery, African botanicals (baobab, buchu, lion's tail, devil's claw, African wormwood). Excellent; Elephant's distinctive African-botanical profile frames delicate crab with a story no London Dry tells — the conservation-charity gin with distinctive character, a conversation-starter pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 with crab cake — HH 18yr against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel with crab cake — house-pick single-barrel against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel with crab cake — single-barrel Heaven Hill against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with crab cake — 94-proof small-batch against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with crab cake — HH rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — cherry, violet, clove, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets crab's sweet meat without overwhelming it and the violet plays against romesco. Peak step-up Willamette Pinot for crab cake — structured Oregon red on the shellfish starter. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Willamette Oregon Pinot Blanc — soft pear, white peach, delicate floral lift, medium body, refreshing acidity. The lighter-than-Chard weight cuts crab's romesco without overpowering and the pear-peach fruit plays cleanly against the sweet meat. Peak unoaked white for crab cake — lighter than Chard, more interesting than Sauv Blanc.",
  "Crab Cake|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee with crab cake — smooth single-barrel against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 with crab cake — Victoria BC floral-citrus craft against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Empress's rose-and-citrus-peel profile frames crab with Pacific-Northwest elegance and visual distinction.",
  "Crab Cake|Epoch Ingenuity": "Never pair Epoch with crab cake — smoked meat, olive, and crushed pepper bury delicate lump crab and the full body fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|Espresso Martini": "Tito's, espresso, Grind liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Coffee-forward dessert cocktail overwhelms crab's sweet-delicate meat; espresso has no hook into seafood. Never pair — crab cake belongs with French 75, Sauvignon Blanc, or Champagne, not coffee cocktails.",
  "Crab Cake|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Coffee-hazelnut weight overwhelms crab's sweet-delicate meat; the cocktail was built for dessert, not starters. Never pair — route crab cake guest to Sauvignon Blanc, Champagne, or a lighter opener cocktail.",
  "Crab Cake|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone. The medium tannin meets crab's sweet meat and the crushed-stone minerality plays against romesco. Peak savory Pinot for crab cake — Eola-Amity red where heavier reds would bury the shellfish. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Far Niente Oakville Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — opulent chocolate-espresso crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Far Niente for the steak course where its luxurious Oakville character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Far Niente — Napa Oakville Chardonnay with buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush texture. The opulent oak cuts crab's romesco and the hazelnut complements the sweet meat directly. Strong special-occasion Chardonnay for crab cake — Napa opulence on the classic starter.",
  "Crab Cake|Faroe Island Salmon": "Faroe Island Salmon with the crab cake as the lead-in -- the Atlantic salmon plate holds beside the sweet shellfish plate. Works; the opener composes at neutral register.",
  "Crab Cake|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Never pair Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi with crab cake — Super Tuscan firm-tannin and blackcurrant weight bulldoze lump-crab delicacy and romesco cream. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or French 75 cocktail. Saffredi belongs on the red-meat course.",
  "Crab Cake|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Brooding Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm-tannin tobacco-chocolate body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Faust for the steak course where its Coombsville structure shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Never pair Fento Mencía with crab cake — slate minerality and high acid are stylistically close to white wine but the red-cherry tannin still dulls lump crab delicacy. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for cleaner shellfish pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca with crab cake — nuclear bitter against delicate crab. Avoid; Branca obliterates seafood.",
  "Crab Cake|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta with crab cake — mint Fernet against delicate crab. Avoid; Fernet character still overwhelms seafood.",
  "Crab Cake|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon after the crab cake -- the panko-and-lump crab opens with delicate sweetness, the 10oz tenderloin following with restrained beef butter. Strong; both courses lean refined, the cut closes the line cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Fireball Whisky belongs on the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky soft tannin. The soft tannin doesn't fight crab's sweet meat and the cranberry acid cuts romesco. Peak by-the-glass Pinot for crab cake — unconventional red that works where heavier reds would bury the shellfish. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with crab cake — 104-proof premium against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label with crab cake — entry bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with crab cake — craft small-batch against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Fox & Oden Double Oaked with crab cake — Double Oaked Fox & Oden against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Rutherford Bosché Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — firm-tannin blackcurrant-graphite structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Freemark Abbey Bosché for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|French 75": "Inverroche gin shaken with lemon and vanilla syrup, topped with Prosecco. The bubbles and citrus reset the palate after every rich bite; vanilla softens the jalapeño heat. Celebratory pour when the guest wants a cocktail with the cake. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|French Onion": "Crab Cake and the French onion together -- restrained gives way to umami, the meal earns its weight. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Crab Cake|Frias Block 5": "Spring Mountain single-block Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm iron-herbal body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Frias Block 5 for the steak course where its Spring Mountain terroir shines.",
  "Crab Cake|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "Classic Piedmont Barolo against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Vajra's firm-tannin tar-rose structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Vajra for aged beef, filet, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "The natural sweetness of Dungeness or blue crab has an almost honeyed quality that the Moscato's peach and apricot tones lock onto directly, while the wine's carbonation lifts the fried crust off the palate so it doesn't accumulate. The result is that the crab's delicate sweetness dominates rather than the breading. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Crab Cake|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco with crab cake — 80-proof from Felipe Camarena's G4 distillery. Craft, agave-forward, distillery uses rainwater in production. Distinct from G4 High Proof (104-proof reposado) by both proof and category; G4 Blanco is the standard-proof sibling. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof with crab cake — 104-proof against delicate crab. Strong; the extra proof is slightly too assertive for the crab's delicacy — Don Julio, Don Julio 70th, or Clase Azul are better crab calls.",
  "Crab Cake|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue with crab cake — western Ireland Sligo-river craft Irish against delicate lump crab. Strong; Garavogue's distinctive-regional craft character is the whisky-curious crab pairing — Irish whiskey's triple-distilled softness + Garavogue's craft profile is gentler than the iconic Irish entries.",
  "Crab Cake|George T. Stagg": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power overshadows the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. George T. Stagg lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crab Cake|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Oakville Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm-tannin graphite-oak body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Ghost Block for the steak course where its Napa golden-strip character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Never pair Ghost Block with crab cake — jammy fruit, cracked pepper, and full body bury delicate lump crab and the medium tannin fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12 with crab cake — Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch is wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14 with crab cake — aged Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18 with crab cake — premium aged Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23 with crab cake — rare-release 23yr Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood — save the Gran Cru bottle.",
  "Crab Cake|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12 with crab cake — Sandend-coast Highland against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12 with crab cake — Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10 with crab cake — Highland bourbon-cask against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18 with crab cake — aged Highland against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Graham's 20 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Graham's 2017 Vintage Port's structured vintage port with dark-fruit weight overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Graham's 2017 Vintage Port for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum with crab cake — triple-distilled premium Patron in glass crystal. Smooth, concentrated agave; distinct from standard Patron Silver by triple distillation and ultra-premium positioning. The top-tier Patron for lump crab; a glass-bottle luxury pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character smothers the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sugar buries the delicate shellfish without any acid to balance. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Grand Marnier lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crab Cake|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale with crab cake — California gin with 6 California-sourced ocean-and-coast botanicals: Mendocino kombu (sea kelp), Temecula limes, Big Sur juniper, Central Valley almonds, Sonoma mint. Excellent; Gray Whale's ocean-conservation-mission with actual sea-kelp botanical is the thematic-perfect craft-gin-crab pairing — ocean gin meets ocean seafood with story-driven precision.",
  "Crab Cake|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Green Chartreuse lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crab Cake|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose with crab cake — French wheat against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Goose's softness doesn't overpower crab and adds polish the workhorses miss.",
  "Crab Cake|Grilled Caesar": "The crab cake with the Caesar -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust meets the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the lighter plate hands off to the savory, the table arcs clean. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Crab Cake|Gumbo": "The crab cake with the gumbo -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust meets the gumbo's spiced creole stew: spice plays against the delicate, the contrast holds. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Crab Cake|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Savory Sonoma Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm-tannin cedar-herb body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Gundlach Bundschu for the steak course where its Rhinefarm restrained character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 with crab cake — higher-backbone craft vodka against delicate lump crab. Excellent; HDW 100's proof lifts the crab with more character than standard vodka while staying clean enough not to overwhelm.",
  "Crab Cake|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 with crab cake — Forest-Distillery Japanese single malt against delicate lump crab. Works; Hakushu's lightly-peated character is at the edge of crab-possible Japanese whiskies — not featured but distinctive.",
  "Crab Cake|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel with crab cake — BT single-barrel against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 with crab cake — California blended grain+grape vodka against delicate lump crab. Excellent; the Viognier grape component gives Hangar 1 a floral lift that adds dimension to crab the pure-grain vodkas don't reach.",
  "Crab Cake|Head Fake": "Wheatley vodka, limoncello, triple sec, lemon, simple syrup. The rounded limoncello citrus cuts crab's romesco and the sugar rim plays against the sweet meat; clean Wheatley base doesn't fight. Strong bright-cocktail pairing for crab cake — sophisticated lemon-drop structure.",
  "Crab Cake|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with crab cake — HH 20yr against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with crab cake — 100-proof BIB against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with crab cake — aged 100-proof against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "Iconic Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — Martha's Vineyard's eucalyptus-and-cedar intensity crushes the romesco-and-crab delicacy. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne; save Heitz for the steak course where its legendary Rutherford character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "Rutherford Heitz Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm refined mint-cedar body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Heitz Trailside for the steak course where its historic-house character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Flora Adora with crab cake — lavish-floral Hendrick's Spring release against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Flora Adora's 6-floral-botanical profile is the featured Hendrick's Family-crab pairing — floral lifts crab harder than flagship Hendrick's cucumber-rose approach can.",
  "Crab Cake|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks with crab cake — flagship Scottish gin against delicate lump crab. Strong; Hendrick's cucumber-rose supports crab at crowd-pleasing register; Flora Adora's lavish floral lifts crab harder.",
  "Crab Cake|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Grand Cabaret with crab cake — absinthe-style fruit Hendrick's against delicate lump crab. Strong; Grand Cabaret's black-currant supports crab at distinctive register; Flora Adora's lavish floral lifts crab harder.",
  "Crab Cake|Hendricks Neptunia": "Neptunia with crab cake — coastal-release Hendrick's (bladderwrack + Ayrshire thyme) against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Neptunia's maritime profile frames crab with ocean-to-ocean thematic resonance — the most Hendrick's-appropriate crab pairing across the family.",
  "Crab Cake|Hendricks Orbium": "Orbium with crab cake — quinine-wormwood Hendrick's against delicate lump crab. Strong; Orbium's bitter-tonic supports crab with distinctive contrast; Flora Adora's lavish floral lifts crab harder.",
  "Crab Cake|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy with crab cake — ubiquitous cognac against delicate crab. Avoid; cognac weight wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB with crab cake — 10-year 100-proof BIB against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Harmony with crab cake — refined Japanese blend against delicate lump crab. Works; Japanese whisky's delicate category + Hibiki's refinement let it sit with crab at premium register.",
  "Crab Cake|Honey Roasted Carrots": "The crab cake and the honey carrots -- the delicate course resolves into the close, the meal stays unburdened. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Crab Cake|House Wedge": "Crab Cake and the wedge together -- the delicate course primes the savory, the table builds register. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Crab Cake|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 with crab cake — 15yr historic-Kentucky against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Veneto Glera — bright floral Prosecco with white peach, green apple, and clean citrus finish. The light-bodied effervescence cuts the romesco and lifts crab's sweet meat without overpowering. Peak affordable pour for crab cake — the Prosecco-and-shellfish classic at accessible price.",
  "Crab Cake|Inhibited": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. Dessert-position cream-and-coffee cocktail obliterates crab's sweet-delicate meat on every axis. Never pair — crab cake belongs with light gin cocktails, French 75, or Sauvignon Blanc.",
  "Crab Cake|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche with crab cake — South African fynbos gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; the Cape-floral botanicals add distinctive dimension to crab no European craft gin offers.",
  "Crab Cake|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish with crab cake — Thompsonville farm-distilled gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Iron Fish's farm-to-bottle character adds dimension to crab the urban-craft siblings don't reach.",
  "Crab Cake|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon Mezcal Casks with crab cake — Iron Fish MI mezcal-cask against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish with crab cake — Virginia port-finished bourbon against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "Anderson Valley Pinot — dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, refined tannin. The medium tannin meets crab's sweet meat and the mocha plays against romesco. Strong Anderson Valley Pinot for crab cake — concentrated red on the shellfish starter.",
  "Crab Cake|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "Diamond Mountain Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm-tannin graphite-mineral crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep J. Davies for the steak course where its volcanic mountain character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year with crab cake — aged Tennessee against delicate lump crab. Avoid; Tennessee wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded with crab cake — 100-proof Tennessee against delicate lump crab. Avoid; Tennessee is wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7 with crab cake — iconic Tennessee against delicate lump crab. Avoid; Tennessee whiskey is wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "JD Sinatra Select with crab cake — Sinatra-tribute Tennessee against delicate lump crab. Avoid; Tennessee wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson with crab cake — smooth triple-distilled Irish against delicate lump crab. Works; Jameson is one of the few whiskies light enough to sit alongside crab without overwhelming — Irish whiskey's triple-distilled softness is the category advantage. Not featured (vodka or gin still lead), but Jameson is the whisky-curious pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, low tannin, smooth. The soft tannin doesn't fight crab's sweet meat and the pomegranate plays against romesco. Peak food-friendly Pinot for crab cake — reliable red that works where heavier reds would bury shellfish. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Burgundy Chablis — unoaked Chard with flinty chalky minerality, lemon, green apple, lean precise. The flint-chalk cuts crab's romesco and the lemon-apple plays against the sweet meat cleanly. Peak textbook unoaked white for crab cake — precision meets classic starter at benchmark quality.",
  "Crab Cake|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with crab cake — sea-aged bourbon against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for seafood even with salt-character resonance. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve with crab cake — blended bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with crab cake — Twin Oak depth against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine, save the VR Twin Oak.",
  "Crab Cake|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label with crab cake — aged Beam against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label with crab cake — entry bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Johnnie Walker Black": "JW Black with crab cake — iconic 12yr blended Scotch against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Johnnie Walker Blue": "Johnnie Walker Blue's Highland-scotch character overshadows the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Johnnie Walker Blue lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crab Cake|Johnnie Walker Red": "JW Red with crab cake — smoky entry JW against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Jordan Alexander Valley Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — elegant cedar-mint body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Jordan for the steak course where its refined Sonoma balance shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre — Loire Sauv Blanc with white grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension-filled finish. The refined mineral backbone cuts romesco and the herbs play against crab's sweet meat cleanly. Peak benchmark Sauv Blanc for crab cake — Loire precision on the classic starter. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|Jubilation by Colgin": "Cult Napa Cab blend against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Jubilation's firm-tannin Colgin structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Jubilation for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Jung and Wulff Guyana on the steak course instead.",
  "Crab Cake|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Jung and Wulff Trinidad for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Kahlua for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Kansas City": "Kansas City after the crab cake -- the panko-and-lump crab opens with delicate sweetness, the 18oz lean-bold strip following with savory grain that the crab's brightness preceded cleanly. Strong; refined opener primes the strip.",
  "Crab Cake|Keenan Chardonnay": "Keenan Chardonnay — Spring Mountain Napa with green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak. The bright acid cuts crab's romesco and the lemon curd plays against the sweet meat cleanly; subtle oak adds depth without overpowering. Strong mountain Chardonnay for crab cake — balance meets classic starter.",
  "Crab Cake|Keenan Mernet": "Spring Mountain Napa Mernet against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — mountain-grown Merlot-Cab structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Keenan Mernet for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye with crab cake — allocated premium KO against delicate crab. Avoid; save the Kentucky Owl bottle.",
  "Crab Cake|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with crab cake — KO Confiscated against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with crab cake — luxury Irish crossover against delicate lump crab. Works; even with Irish whiskey's category advantage, Kentucky Owl is over-specified for crab — save the collector bottle.",
  "Crab Cake|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Never pair Kermit Lynch Rhône with crab cake — wild herbs and pepper overwhelm delicate lump crab and the garrigue bury the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One with crab cake — Dutch wheat against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Ketel's citrus-clean finish brightens the crab at a precise register.",
  "Crab Cake|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker with crab cake — hop-influenced Holland MI gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Knickerbocker's brewer's-heritage character adds distinctive herbal-hop dimension to crab.",
  "Crab Cake|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek with crab cake — 100-proof high-rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year with crab cake — 12-year aged Beam against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 with crab cake — 15yr Beam KC against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 with crab cake — 18yr Beam aged KC against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with crab cake — blended 100-proof against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye with crab cake — 100-proof bold rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva's aged-agave character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Komos Anejo Reserva for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo's aged-agave character smothers the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- aged-agave register crowds the delicate shellfish frame. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Komos Crystallino Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Añejo with crab cake — Riedel-luxury aged against delicate crab. Avoid; save the Komos bottle.",
  "Crab Cake|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado with crab cake — wine-cask-finished pink reposado against lump crab. The rosé character and strawberry threads lift the crab with wine-driven elegance; a distinctive seafood pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Lagavulin 8": "Lagavulin 8 with the crab cake — sea-spray and peat steamroll the crab's sweetness. The delicate lump meat can't carry the dram's intensity, even the 8-year's. Seafood-wide avoid for all three Islays; pour a gin or a lighter tequila here instead. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Crab Cake|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Historically-pedigreed Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Blueprint's refined medium-tannin structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Blueprint for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Historically-significant Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Daniel Cuvée's firm-tannin full-body structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Daniel Cuvée for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Lalo Silver": "Crab Cake: lump crab meets Lalo Silver — craft blanco by Eduardo 'Lalo' González, descendant of Don Julio González family. Additive-free, clean highland agave; newer craft brand with direct Don Julio family lineage. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Laphroaig 10": "Laphroaig 10 with crab cake — medicinal iodine against delicate crab is a complete mismatch. The dram incinerates the starter. Hard avoid; Laphroaig is the wrong lane for anything this delicate. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Crab Cake|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch with crab cake — wheated bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; choose a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Tours-sur-Marne Pinot Noir rosé Champagne — benchmark rosé with red berry aromas, silky mousse, long elegant finish. The berry fruit cuts crab's sweet romesco and bubbles lift the cake's pan-fry. Peak rosé Champagne for crab cake — sophisticated starter pour at benchmark quality. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid structure cuts romesco and lifts crab's sweet meat cleanly. Peak Champagne for crab cake — refined acidity frames the sweet shellfish perfectly. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Crab Cake|Le Garenne Rosé": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — pale salmon with strawberry, watermelon, floral, crisp dry finish. The fresh-fruit body cuts crab's romesco and the berry-floral plays against the sweet meat. Peak summer rosé for crab cake — approachable pink on the classic starter, seasonal lightness.",
  "Crab Cake|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Italy's-most-prestigious Brunello against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Le Ragnaie's firm-tannin tobacco-leather structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Le Ragnaie for aged beef — reserve for guests who know what they're ordering.",
  "Crab Cake|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Tuscan Merlot-driven blend against crab cake's delicate shellfish — Le Volte's medium-full dark-fruit structure is too weighty for the romesco-and-crab starter. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne; save Le Volte for heartier Italian plates and red-meat courses.",
  "Crab Cake|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The lemon cuts crab's romesco and honey-lavender adds floral lift against the sweet meat; Prosecco adds effervescence. Peak floral cocktail pairing for crab cake — approachable even for gin-hesitant guests.",
  "Crab Cake|Les Pallières Raciné": "Never pair Raciné with crab cake — garrigue, iron, and earthy mineral bury delicate lump crab and the full body fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|Liberator Gin": "Liberator with crab cake — Valentine Distilling, Ferndale Michigan, 100% Michigan grain hometown craft. Excellent; Liberator is the Michigan-hometown craft-gin pairing for crab — sibling to Long Road, Eastern Kille, 2 James, Detroit City, and Iron Fish in the Michigan gin program, the Valentine Distilling answer.",
  "Crab Cake|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Licor 43 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crab Cake|Limoncello": "Crab Cake: this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Liqueur sweetness or herbal complexity against a starter — wrong register typically. Works as a digestif move.",
  "Crab Cake|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, spice, refined structure, low tannin. The soft tannin doesn't fight crab's sweet meat and the forest-floor earth plays against romesco. Peak by-the-glass Pinot for crab cake — unconventional red that works where heavier reds would bury the shellfish. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Little Book": "Little Book with crab cake — Fred Noe cask-strength against delicate lump crab. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Loaded Potato": "The crab cake with the loaded potato -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust meets the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the lighter course primes the richer, the meal carries register lift. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Crab Cake|Lobster Mac": "The crab cake and the lobster mac share shellfish as the primary character. Avoid; the meal doubles down without contrast. Route the crab cake with Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin or the lobster mac with Cowboy Ribeye, Far Niente Chardonnay, or The Tomahawk instead.",
  "Crab Cake|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year with crab cake — 20yr Highland against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with crab cake — premium sourced rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Long Road Gin": "Long Road with crab cake — Grand Rapids ultra-local gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Long Road's estate-pride Michigan-botanical profile frames crab with Grand Rapids distinction.",
  "Crab Cake|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- mezcal smoke obliterates delicate shellfish — needs sparkling or sancerre. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a mezcal. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- a luxury cognac is wrong moment for the opener — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an icon cognac. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano with crab cake — Basilicata amaro against delicate crab. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Crab Cake|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Rustic Abruzzo Montepulciano against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Lunaria's earthy medium-tannin grip buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Lunaria for filet, strip, or rustic meat courses.",
  "Crab Cake|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry with crab cake — sherried Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18 with crab cake — premium aged sherried Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate with crab cake — distinctive Estate-oak Macallan against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Never pair Macauley with crab cake — inky color, dense tannin, and black pepper bury delicate lump crab and the full body fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot — bright strawberry, red cherry, light floral, soft tannins, low tannin. The soft tannin doesn't fight crab's sweet meat and the strawberry plays against romesco. Strong accessible Pinot for crab cake — mid-tier red where heavier reds would bury shellfish.",
  "Crab Cake|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Atlas Peak Stagecoach Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — powerful-tannin iron-espresso crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Macauley Stagecoach for the steak course where its celebrated-vineyard character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Mahon Gin": "Mahón with crab cake — Xoriguer Mahón Spanish Mediterranean gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Mahón's sharp Mediterranean juniper lifts crab with Balearic-island character.",
  "Crab Cake|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark with the crab cake — soft wheated sweetness against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp wine instead.",
  "Crab Cake|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with crab cake — the Cellar Aged's depth overwhelms crab delicacy. Avoid; choose a crisp wine instead.",
  "Crab Cake|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's green-agave-and-cane lift meets at register with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust: the cane-and-citrus body lifts the delicate crab cake, and the Caribbean-rum register underlines the starter. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Crab Cake|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character smothers the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Loire Cab Franc — Chinon light body, raspberry, red cherry, bell pepper, forest floor, supple low tannin, bright high acid. The bright acid handles crab cake's delicate sweet meat and the bell pepper plays against romesco. Works as unusual Loire CF for crab cake — food-wine acid reframes the shellfish, though whites peak higher.",
  "Crab Cake|Margarita": "Corazon Blanco, lemon, lime, orange, Cointreau — salted rim. The triple-citrus build cuts crab's romesco and the blanco's clean agave profile meets the sweet meat directly; Cointreau smooths the finish. Peak classic cocktail for crab cake when guest wants citrus-forward agave.",
  "Crab Cake|Marimar Estate Christina": "Russian River Green Valley Pinot — ripe strawberry, cherry compote, warm spice, silky, low tannin. The soft tannin doesn't fight crab's sweet meat and the cherry-compote plays against romesco. Peak plush Pinot for crab cake — Russian River opulence where heavier reds would bury shellfish. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|Market Fish": "The market fish and the crab cake across courses -- the kitchen-driven fish meets the delicate crab cake. Strong; the opener carries its register before the main.",
  "Crab Cake|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Mata Hari Absinthe on the steak course instead.",
  "Crab Cake|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Bourbon with crab cake — Michter's 10yr single-barrel against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye with crab cake — aged Michter's against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye with crab cake — smooth rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco with crab cake — premium craft brand, Jalisco highlands, additive-free. Maestra Tequilera Ana Maria Romero's craft blanco; distinct from the big brands by additive-free philosophy and female-led production story. The clean craft agave lifts the crab with sustainability-focused craft character. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo's aged-agave character smothers the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- aged-agave register crowds the delicate shellfish frame. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Milagro Anejo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crab Cake|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado with crab cake — modern-style reposado against lump crab. The triple-distillation cleanness lifts the crab with modern simplicity; a clean contemporary reposado-seafood pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam with crab cake — Sazerac Seagram's-tribute against delicate lump crab. Avoid; blended whiskey wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Mocha Creme": "The crab cake and the mocha creme -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust bookends with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Crab Cake|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 with crab cake — Black Forest 47-botanical craft against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Monkey 47's forest-herbal character adds Schwarzwald dimension to crab no urban-craft gin brings.",
  "Crab Cake|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder with crab cake — Speyside-malt blend against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro with crab cake — Bologna amaro against delicate crab. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Crab Cake|Mount Gay Rum": "Crab Cake: delicate lump crab meets Mount Gay. Excellent; the Barbados-rum character with slight molasses warmth frames lump crab at craft-heritage register — more character than the neutral pour.",
  "Crab Cake|Muga Reserva": "Never pair Muga with crab cake — oak-driven vanilla and leather bury delicate lump crab and the savory finish fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|Mushroom Bisque": "Crab Cake alongside the mushroom bisque -- restrained gives way to umami, the meal earns its weight. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "Crab Cake|Mushrooms": "The crab cake and the mushrooms -- restrained gives way to umami, the meal earns its weight. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Crab Cake|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Myers's Rum lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crab Cake|Negroni": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari. The bitter-orange profile is too aggressive for crab's sweet-delicate meat and the cocktail's structure crushes the romesco. Never pair — route crab cake to French 75, Bee's Knees, or Sauvignon Blanc, not a bitter red cocktail.",
  "Crab Cake|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye with crab cake — novelty UK-aged rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch with crab cake — novelty bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with crab cake — Michigan beer-barrel bourbon against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|New York Sour": "Crab Cake: this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey's oak and caramel are only going to land if the starter can carry weight — check the plate. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Prestige Oakville Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — concentrated graphite-cassis body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Nickel & Nickel for the steak course where its terroir-specific Oakville concentration shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolet's with crab cake — Nolet-family fruit-forward gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Nolet's rose-peach-raspberry frames crab with contemporary character British London Drys don't offer.",
  "Crab Cake|Not a Paper Plane": "Bulleit bourbon, Nonino Quintessential, Aperol, Angostura. Spirit-forward bourbon-and-amaro weight overwhelms crab's sweet-delicate meat; the cocktail has no hook into the light romesco. Never pair — crab cake belongs with Sauv Blanc, French 75, or gin cocktails.",
  "Crab Cake|Oban 14": "Oban 14 with crab cake — maritime Highland against delicate crab. Avoid; even the coastal-terroir doesn't save this — Scotch overwhelms crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Oban 18": "Oban 18 with crab cake — premium aged Highland against delicate crab. Avoid; even the coastal-terroir doesn't save this — Scotch overwhelms crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character smothers the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- aged-agave register crowds the delicate shellfish frame. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Ocho Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Refined Stags Leap Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — velvet-glove sage-cedar body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Odette for the steak course where its iron-fist-velvet-glove character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with crab cake — cigar-paired bourbon against delicate lump crab. Avoid; cigar-paired bourbon is the opposite of seafood-friendly. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer with crab cake — emmer-wheat wheated bourbon against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon is wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year with crab cake — wheated BIB against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year with crab cake — aged wheated BIB against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with crab cake — 16yr OGD against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Opus One": "Collector Napa Cab blend against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Opus One's firm-tannin Bordeaux blend buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Opus One for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Willamette Trappist Chardonnay — rich pear, butterscotch, toasted oak, layered. The rich oak weight cuts crab's romesco and the butterscotch plays against the sweet meat directly. Strong Oregon provenance Chardonnay for crab cake — monastery-made opulence on the classic starter.",
  "Crab Cake|Pablo Sour": "Crab Cake: this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness meets this bright tequila cocktail's citrus lift and agave backbone. Light cocktail and delicate starter share the same register — fresh opener. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Paloma": "Reposado tequila, fresh grapefruit, vanilla syrup — three ingredients, no tricks. The grapefruit's bite cuts crab's romesco and the vanilla softens against sweet crab meat; reposado's light oak adds structure. Peak easy-drinking cocktail for crab cake when guest wants tequila rather than gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye's allocated-icon Pappy rye character crowds out the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage 16th Ed with crab cake — HH collector cask-strength against delicate lump crab. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Patron Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver with crab cake — 80-proof iconic blanco, globally the most recognized brand. Clean citrus-forward agave against lump crab; less distinctive than Don Julio Blanco's benchmark character, more globally familiar. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Patron XO Cafe for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Bouzy Grand Cru Pinot Noir grower Champagne — structured, ripe raspberry, red currant, minerally finish. The serious Burgundian backbone frames crab's sweet meat and cuts romesco cleanly. Peak rosé Champagne for crab cake — more substance than standard pink bubbles. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|Peanut Butter Brownie": "The crab cake alongside the PB brownie -- delicate gives way to sweet, the close lands light. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Crab Cake|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Elegant Rutherford Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — refined dusty-cocoa body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Peju for the steak course where its family-estate Rutherford character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand with crab cake — artisanal cognac against delicate crab. Avoid; cognac weight wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "2015 Côte des Blancs grower Champagne — prestige cuvée, layered citrus zest, toasted brioche, white flowers, creamy texture. The vintage depth cuts romesco and brioche complements crab's pan-fry. Peak grower Champagne for crab cake — both technical and indulgent in the right measure. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye with crab cake — 110-proof bold rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Pineau des Charentes": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The honey bridges romesco richness and the white grape lifts lump-crab delicacy. Works for crab cake — a Sancerre's mineral edge reads cleaner on the delicate shellfish.",
  "Crab Cake|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Approachable-Barolo-sibling Barbaresco against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Pio Cesare's firm-tannin tar structure buries the shellfish despite elegant profile. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Pio Cesare for aged beef, filet, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth with crab cake — softer English gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Plymouth's earthier juniper is gentle enough to frame crab without overpowering — Tanq lifts harder, Plymouth complements.",
  "Crab Cake|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Serious-Barolo Aldo Conterno against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — decade-built firm tannin and iron-minerality structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Aldo Conterno for aged beef — one of the most serious bottles deserves it.",
  "Crab Cake|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "2004 Pommery Cuvée Louise — aged 20-year Champagne from Reims. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich palate. The aged depth cuts romesco and brioche complements crab's pan-fry; almond plays against sweet meat. Peak occasion Champagne for crab cake — rare indulgence. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|Porterhouse": "The porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust: the dual strip-and-filet overshadows the delicate crab cake, and the bold beefy character crowds out the sweet shellfish plate. Avoid; the courses can't share a meal cleanly. Pair the crab cake with Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin on a different visit; pour Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel alongside the porterhouse.",
  "Crab Cake|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Polished Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — lush chocolate-vanilla crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Quilt for the steak course where its indulgent Napa character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|RAEN Royal St Robert": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, crushed rock, silky soft tannin. The soft tannin doesn't fight crab's sweet meat and the sea-bred minerality plays against romesco. Peak Burgundian California Pinot for crab cake — elegant red on the shellfish starter. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|Rainbow Trout": "The trout after the crab cake -- the herb-finished trout holds with the lemon-finished crab plate. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Crab Cake|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Raventós de NIT Cava rosé — Catalan blend with wild strawberry, raspberry, mineral spine, and saline finish. The mineral-berry profile cuts romesco and the saline complements crab's sweet meat. Peak Cava for crab cake — sophisticated food-friendly pink at accessible price. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 with crab cake — premium pot still Irish against delicate lump crab. Works; Redbreast 12 is richer than Jameson so less gentle with crab, but Irish whiskey's triple-distilled-pot-still softness still keeps it in the whisky-curious crab territory.",
  "Crab Cake|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 with crab cake — aged premium pot still Irish against delicate lump crab. Works; Redbreast 21's aged-oily character is rich for crab but still within Irish whiskey's category advantage; save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Crab Cake|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Gatsby with crab cake — MGP 15yr Gatsby Reserve against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Rémy VSOP with crab cake — Fine Champagne cognac against delicate crab. Avoid; even refined cognac is wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo with crab cake — distinctive-bottle aged against delicate crab. Avoid; save the sun-bottle ceremony.",
  "Crab Cake|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka with crab cake — Icelandic glacial vodka against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Reyka's mineral character echoes crab's marine origin at a distinctive register.",
  "Crab Cake|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique with crab cake — retailer-label against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Roast Half Chicken": "Roast Half Chicken with the crab cake as the lead-in -- the herb-butter chicken carries into the panko-and-lump crab cake. Strong; the starter sets the table for the chicken.",
  "Crab Cake|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Crab Cake and the chickpea soup together -- heat lifts the restrained, the table earns the warmer note. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Crab Cake|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Le Mesnil Grand Cru Chardonnay Champagne — chalky minerality, lemon curd, green apple, almost no dosage. The pure austere structure cuts romesco and the lemon curd plays against crab's sweet meat directly. Peak sommelier's Champagne for crab cake — precision-on-precision pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|Roku Gin": "Roku with crab cake — Japanese Suntory gin with sakura + yuzu + sansho against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Roku's sansho-pepper lift and yuzu-citrus frame crab with Japanese-seafood-precision — parallel to Inverroche's fynbos-floral and Botanist's Islay-foraged approaches as terroir-distinctive craft gin for seafood.",
  "Crab Cake|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey premium-sipping rum overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Ron Zacapa Rum belongs on the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with crab cake — aged bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Salt-Cured Halibut": "The halibut preceded by the crab cake -- the firm-flesh fish meets quietly the panko-and-lump crab cake. Works; safe opener, but not the headline pick.",
  "Crab Cake|Sambuca": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|San Simeon Stormwatch": "Paso Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — full-body mocha-cherry structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Stormwatch for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sta. Rita Hills Santa Barbara Pinot — rich, layered, dark cherry, earth, dried herbs, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets crab's sweet meat and the dried-herbs play against romesco. Peak legendary-vineyard Pinot for crab cake — Santa Barbara depth on the shellfish starter. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Crab Cake and the garlic spinach -- bright and delicate share air, the meal carries lift without weight. Works; both items hold their register, no clash.",
  "Crab Cake|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sauternes Glass belongs on the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Sazerac": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- the cocktail's weight is forward for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Sazerac for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye with crab cake — iconic rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna with crab cake — NZ single malt against delicate lump crab. Avoid; single malt weight wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Scavino Barolo": "King-of-Italian Barolo against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Scavino's powerful firm-tannin tar-leather structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Scavino for aged beef — the bottle demands the meat course.",
  "Crab Cake|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads — Rheingau Riesling with apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry fruit cuts crab's romesco and the apricot-peach plays against the sweet meat; lively acid cuts the pan-fry. Peak German Riesling for crab cake — fruit-and-acid match, teaching-wine provenance. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "By-the-glass California Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — full-body medium-tannin structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Lost Chapters for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Seared Scallops": "The crab cake and the scallops share shellfish as the primary character. Avoid; the meal doubles down without contrast. Route the crab cake with Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin or the scallops with Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre instead.",
  "Crab Cake|Seasonal Soup": "The crab cake with the seasonal soup -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust meets the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: a clean call, no friction either way. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Crab Cake|Seasonal Vegetables": "Crab Cake alongside the seasonal vegetables -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust meets the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: a clean call, no friction either way. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Crab Cake|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Never pair Sesti with crab cake — firm tannin, high acid, and sandalwood earth bury delicate lump crab and the savory-rich profile fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|Shafer 1.5": "Stags Leap Shafer Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — 1.5's opulent plush-tannin structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Shafer 1.5 for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Shafer Hillside Select": "Stag's Leap flagship Cabernet — structured, powerful, built for beef. Tannin plus delicate crab equals mismatch; the wine eats the dish. Reserve the bottle for the Tomahawk or Porterhouse where it can actually meet real weight. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Crab Cake|Shrimp Bisque": "The crab cake alongside the shrimp bisque -- both bring shellfish as the headline. Avoid; one is enough. Pair the crab cake with Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin; pair the shrimp bisque with Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay.",
  "Crab Cake|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco with crab cake — traditional tahona-crushed blanco, family-distillery craft. Rustic, earthier agave than Don Julio's polished benchmark; the traditional character gives the crab more texture than globally-marketed blancos bring. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado with crab cake — traditional agave's earthier character against lump crab. The tahona-crushed craft gives the pairing more texture than Don Julio's balance brings; distinctive. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Crab Cake|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak Alexander Valley Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — American-oak vanilla-dill crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Silver Oak for the steak course where its recognizable American-oak signature shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Boutique Napa estate Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Estate Reserve's firm-tannin full-body structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Estate Reserve for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "Organic Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Lyndenhurst's full-body plum-violet structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Lyndenhurst for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm-tannin full body crushes romesco-and-crab and the mocha clashes with sweet meat. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep St Supéry for the steak course where its Napa character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "Napa Sauvignon Blanc from Dollarhide Ranch — lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, clean lively finish. Rounder than New Zealand. The lively acid cuts crab's romesco and the lemon-gooseberry plays against the sweet meat cleanly. Peak Napa Sauv Blanc for crab cake — bridges freshness and body well. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "Napa Meritage against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — firm-tannin five-varietal structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Élu for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with crab cake — BT cask-strength against delicate lump crab. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit with crab cake — quadruple-filtered Russian wheat against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Elit's glacial-clean profile showcases crab's delicacy without adding character the way a rye or potato vodka would.",
  "Crab Cake|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, fresh-cut grass, zesty acidity. The zesty acid cuts crab's romesco and the tropical-citrus plays against the sweet meat cleanly. Peak accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for crab cake — reliable bright by-the-glass anchor on the classic starter. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|Swordfish": "The swordfish after the crab cake -- the meaty steak matches the sweet shellfish plate. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Crab Cake|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 with crab cake — small-batch premium Tanq against delicate lump crab. Excellent; the chamomile-softened citrus frames crab with refinement flagship Tanq's juniper-heavy profile doesn't bring.",
  "Crab Cake|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray with crab cake — 47.3% juniper-forward London Dry, quadruple-distilled British benchmark. Excellent; Tanq's higher proof and juniper intensity lifts delicate crab with pine-and-citrus precision where lower-proof gins just sit. The default Tanq-seafood pairing. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Crab Cake|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with crab cake — triple-cask aged against delicate crab. Avoid; save the González legendary bottle.",
  "Crab Cake|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Polished Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — smooth mocha body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Teeter Totter for the steak course where its polished mid-tier Napa character shines.",
  "Crab Cake|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tuscan Chianti Classico against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Arceno's medium-tannin cherry-earth structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Arceno for Italian table pairings, filet, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|The BG": "The BG's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- this cocktail's register doesn't lift the opener — try a sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. The BG belongs on the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|The Happy Wife": "Tito's, orange juice, ginger syrup — stirred. The orange juice cuts crab's romesco and ginger's subtle spice plays against the sweet meat; Tito's clean vodka keeps it uncomplicated. Peak easy-drinking cocktail for crab cake — approachable choice that doesn't disappoint.",
  "Crab Cake|The Manhattan": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Whiskey cocktail weight overwhelms the crab's sweet-delicate meat; the Italian vermouth and vanilla syrup have no hook into the light romesco. Never pair — crab cake belongs with Sauvignon Blanc or Champagne, not the Manhattan.",
  "Crab Cake|The Prisoner Red Blend": "Never pair Prisoner with crab cake — layered dark fruit and full body bury delicate lump crab and the integrated tannin fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Crab Cake|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust: the marbled char overshadows the sweet shellfish plate, and the smoky bone-in weight crowds out the delicate crab cake. Avoid; the courses can't share a meal cleanly. Pair the crab cake with Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin on a different visit; pour Opus One, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or George T. Stagg alongside the Tomahawk.",
  "Crab Cake|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with crab cake — BTAC allocated against delicate crab. Avoid; save the Antique Collection bottle.",
  "Crab Cake|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with crab cake — chardonnay-cask bourbon against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood, even with white-wine finish. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Titos Vodka": "Tito's with crab cake — American corn-based workhorse against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Tito's round/slightly-sweet character doesn't overpower crab the way a rye-based vodka like Belvedere can. The default call for a reason here.",
  "Crab Cake|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory with crab cake — entry Japanese blend against delicate lump crab. Works; Japanese whisky's delicate category lets Toki sit with crab where most whiskies can't — not featured (vodka/gin still lead) but distinctively possible.",
  "Crab Cake|Tomato Basil": "The crab cake preceded by the tomato basil -- both light, the meal stays composed. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "Crab Cake|Transfusion": "Hangar 1 vodka, muddled black grapes, lime, ginger beer. The grape-and-lime profile cuts crab's romesco and ginger beer's dry effervescence lifts the pairing. Peak refreshing cocktail for crab cake — more complex than it looks, matches the crab's delicate sweetness.",
  "Crab Cake|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado with crab cake — cedar-aged reposado against lump crab. Strong; the cedar character competes with the crab's delicacy — standard reposados lift the crab more cleanly.",
  "Crab Cake|Truffle Fries": "Crab Cake and the truffle fries -- restrained gives way to umami, the meal earns its weight. Works; both items hold their register, no clash.",
  "Crab Cake|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W. with crab cake — traditional triple-distilled Irish against delicate lump crab. Works; Tullamore's heritage character sits alongside crab at traditional-Irish register; vodka or gin still lead for the featured crab pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna preceded by the crab cake -- the seared-rare flesh sits with the panko-and-lump crab cake. Works; the call holds, neither soars nor fights.",
  "Crab Cake|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Crab Cake alongside the curry -- the jumbo-lump cake carries into the coconut-curry main. Strong; the openers compose cleanly together.",
  "Crab Cake|Venge Family Reserve": "Oakville flagship Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Family Reserve's full-body blackcurrant-chocolate structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Family Reserve for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Venge Scout's Honor": "Never pair Venge Scout's Honor with crab cake — the Napa Zin-led blend's full body and ripe blackberry fruit bulldoze lump-crab delicacy and romesco cream. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or French 75 cocktail. Scout's Honor belongs on the red-meat course.",
  "Crab Cake|Venge Silencieux": "Winemaker's Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Silencieux's full-body cassis-chocolate structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Silencieux for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Crab Cake|Vesper": "Nolet's, Ketel One, Lillet — stirred. The Lillet's slight citrus bitterness lifts crab's romesco and Nolet's rose-peach profile adds aromatic depth against the delicate meat. Peak martini pairing for crab cake — elegance meets elegance.",
  "Crab Cake|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut — iconic yellow label, Reims Champagne. Ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse, lively acidity. The creamy body cuts romesco and the biscuit complements crab's pan-fry. Peak reliable Champagne for crab cake — crowd-pleaser meets classic starter beautifully. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Crab Cake|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto vermouth, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. Layered spirit-forward weight crushes crab's sweet-delicate meat. Never pair — crab cake belongs with Sauvignon Blanc, Champagne, or a lighter opener, not a complex whiskey-cognac cocktail.",
  "Crab Cake|Villon Cognac": "Villon with crab cake — modern cognac spirit against delicate crab. Avoid; cognac weight wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Vin Santo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crab Cake|Weller 107": "Weller 107 with the crab cake — 107 proof's heat against crab delicacy is a clean miss. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year with the crab cake — aged bourbon's weight overwhelms the crab's delicacy. Oak and the cake's gentleness don't meet. Reach for a crisp wine or a gin-based cocktail here. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Crab Cake|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium with crab cake — premium bourbon against delicate crab. The Millennium deserves a better pairing; avoid, reach for the right white wine. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Crab Cake|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve with the crab cake — soft wheated sweetness against delicate crab. Avoid; the lump meat can't find the Weller's profile. A crisp wine or a gin is the better call.",
  "Crab Cake|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley with crab cake — Buffalo Trace's wheat vodka, 10x-distilled, house-made. Excellent; Wheatley's clean-wheat profile is value-premium character — a BT distillery product built for exactly this kind of delicate seafood pairing.",
  "Crab Cake|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with crab cake — aged WP rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with crab cake — wine-cask WP against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with crab cake — WP flagship against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with crab cake — allocated barrel-proof WP against delicate crab. Avoid; never pour Boss Hog with something this delicate — save the bottle.",
  "Crab Cake|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with crab cake — 10th-edition allocated WP against delicate crab. Avoid; save the X Commandments bottle.",
  "Crab Cake|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year with crab cake — WP 100-proof against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with crab cake — craft high-rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with crab cake — small-batch against delicate crab. Avoid; choose a white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with crab cake — iconic Double-Oaked against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Crab Cake|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with crab cake — elegant small-batch rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with crab cake — Washington craft rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Crab Cake|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 with crab cake — flagship Japanese single malt against delicate lump crab. Works; Japanese whisky's delicate category lets Yamazaki sit with crab at flagship register; not featured but distinctive.",
  "Crab Cake|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 with crab cake — aged flagship Japanese single malt against delicate lump crab. Works; save the Yamazaki 18 collector bottle for the protein course — Yamazaki 12 is the whisky-curious crab choice at lower register.",
  "Crab Cake|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- amaro bitterness on cold shellfish flattens the delicate brine — needs sparkling instead. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Yellow Chartreuse on the steak course instead.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Asparagus": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the malt-and-oak threads the asparagus, and the heather brightens the side. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a soup; save the rare bottle.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Cragganmore 12 with the au gratin — distinctive Speyside against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Strong; Cragganmore's layered complexity handles the richness at Classic Malt register.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Beignets": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the scotch depth settles into the beignets, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the dessert. Works; allocated character doesn't drive.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Bone Marrow": "Cragganmore 12 with bone marrow — Classic Malt Speyside against umami fat. Works; Cragganmore's subtle smoke meets marrow better than the lighter siblings, but Fernet Branca is still the featured marrow pairing.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Bone-In Filet": "Cragganmore 12 with the bone-in filet — Classic Malt Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Cragganmore's meaty depth and subtle smoke find the cut's marrow-adjacent richness with distinctive character.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Broccoli Cheddar": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the layered malt wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for a side — save the bottle for the main course.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Broccolini": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the malt-driven body wraps the broccolini, and the honey rounds the side. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Brussels and Belly": "Cragganmore 12 with Brussels + Belly — Diageo Classic Malt against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; Cragganmore's subtle smoke finds the glaze with distinctive character.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Burrata": "Cragganmore 12 with burrata — Classic Malt Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Butternut Squash": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the layered malt wraps the squash bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Voyage 23 handles the richness at ocean-finish register.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Carrot Cake": "Cragganmore 12 with carrot cake — layered Speyside against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Cragganmore's complexity threads the cake's spice with Classic Malt distinction.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Cheesecake": "Cragganmore 12 with cheesecake — layered Speyside against custard. Strong; Cragganmore's complexity threads the cheesecake with distinctive character; Balvenie 12's vanilla is still the featured match.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Chilean Seabass": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Cragganmore 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Chocolate Brownie": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brownie, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Chocolate Cake": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the scotch backbone matches the chocolate cake, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th handles the richness at annual-release register.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Clam Chowder": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the Highland register carries the chowder, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; distinctive character gives the dessert a unique story-frame.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cragganmore 12 with the cowboy ribeye — Classic Malt Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Cragganmore's meaty depth and subtle smoke frame the ribeye at distinctive register.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Crab Cake": "Cragganmore 12 with crab cake — Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Creamed Spinach": "Cragganmore 12 with creamed spinach — Diageo Classic Malt against dairy-rich side. Strong; Cragganmore's subtle smoke cuts the cream more than honey-Glenlivet rides it.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Creamy Potato": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the scotch backbone matches the potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; Caribbean Cask rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Creme Brulee": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch depth settles into the brulee, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; AE Triple Cask's layered character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Escargot": "Cragganmore 12 -- Speyside complexity, malt, fruit. The heather-and-honey weight sits with the buttered escargot, and the honey rounds the starter. Strong; the distinctive maturation handles both cuts with character-story behind it; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Faroe Island Salmon": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Cragganmore 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Filet Mignon": "Cragganmore 12 with the filet — Diageo Classic Malt, lighter layered Speyside with subtle smoke hint and meaty depth. Excellent; Cragganmore's distinctive layered complexity and subtle smoke frame tenderloin with more dimension than Fiddich's gateway or Glenlivet's benchmark — the thinking-person's Speyside-filet pairing.",
  "Cragganmore 12|French Onion": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the scotch depth settles into the French onion, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; own-plus-sourced over-specified.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Grilled Caesar": "Cragganmore 12 with the grilled Caesar — Classic Malt Speyside against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Cragganmore's subtle smoke hint is the featured Speyside-Caesar pairing — the smoke-meets-char resonance no other Batch 1 Speyside can claim.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Gumbo": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the scotch backbone matches the gumbo, and the smoke threads the bowl. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Highland register carries the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; boutique over-specified.",
  "Cragganmore 12|House Wedge": "Cragganmore 12 with the House Wedge — layered Speyside against iceberg and bleu. Works; Cragganmore's complexity is over-specified for the wedge.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Kansas City": "Cragganmore 12 with the KC — distinctive Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Cragganmore handles the strip with subtle smoke character; Speyside 18 or Balvenie 16 are the featured Speyside-KC choices.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Loaded Potato": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the malt-driven body wraps the loaded potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; bonded weight pushes slightly past the soup.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Lobster Mac": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the lobster mac, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Market Fish": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Cragganmore 12 for the steak course.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Mocha Creme": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the malt-driven body wraps the mocha creme, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; Old World complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Mushroom Bisque": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the Highland register carries the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the umami. Works; light body doesn't overshoot.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Mushrooms": "Cragganmore 12 with mushrooms — layered Speyside against earthy umami. Excellent; Cragganmore's subtle smoke and herbal complexity are the featured Speyside-mushroom pairing — more dimensional than Fiddich's apple or Balvenie's vanilla approach.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet distinctive Speyside. Works; Cragganmore handles the density with Classic Malt complexity; Balvenie 12's vanilla is still the featured match.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Porterhouse": "Cragganmore 12 with the porterhouse — layered Speyside against strip-and-filet. Strong; Cragganmore's complexity frames the two-cut plate with Classic Malt character.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Prime Tartare": "Cragganmore 12 -- Speyside complexity, malt, fruit. The scotch backbone matches the tartare, and the oak depth meets the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Rainbow Trout": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Cragganmore 12 for the steak course.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Roast Half Chicken": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the scotch depth settles into the roast chicken, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the plate. Works; pour is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the scotch depth settles into the chickpea soup, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; Gran Cru's brioche meets the glaze at refined register.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Cragganmore 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the scotch depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; Discovery complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Seafood Tower": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Cragganmore 12 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Seared Scallops": "Cragganmore 12 with seared scallops — layered Speyside against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Seasonal Soup": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the malt-and-oak threads the seasonal soup, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; X Commandments story wasted on salad.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Seasonal Vegetables": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the malt-driven body wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the honey rounds the side. Works; BIB structure is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Shrimp Bisque": "Cragganmore 12 with shrimp bisque — Classic Malt Speyside against cream-shrimp soup. Works; Cragganmore's subtle smoke cuts the bisque at distinctive register.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Shrimp Cocktail": "Cragganmore 12 with shrimp cocktail — distinctive Speyside against cold shrimp. Avoid; Scotch wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Swordfish": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Cragganmore 12 on the steak course instead.",
  "Cragganmore 12|The Tomahawk": "Cragganmore 12's bold char-floral character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Tomato Basil": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the Highland register carries the tomato basil, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Truffle Fries": "Cragganmore 12 with truffle fries — Diageo Classic Malt against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Cragganmore's layered complexity and smoke-hint meet the earthy-rich with distinctive depth.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Cragganmore 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cragganmore 12|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the Highland register carries the curry, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; the sweetness is a touch much for the salad's simplicity.",
  "Creamed Spinach|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with creamed spinach — Barton small-batch against dairy. Strong; spicy-sweet character sits with richness at balanced register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat with creamed spinach — sweet-wheat against dairy. Strong; distinctive wheat-sweetness sits with the cream cleanly.",
  "Creamed Spinach|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 — Napa Cab with dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, smooth tannin. The approachable body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the mocha plays against the earthy sauce. Peak entry Napa Cab for creamed spinach — step-up from glass that handles the classic steakhouse side confidently. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Creamed Spinach|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with creamed spinach — rare limited-release against dairy. Strong; boutique-craft sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with creamed spinach — double-matured Speyside against dairy-rich side. Works; Aberlour 16's bourbon-side vanilla rides the cream.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the rested-agave depth sits with the creamed spinach, and the caramel-agave register brightens the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's cooked-agave-and-oak register leans against the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the creamed spinach, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Creamed Spinach: structured red meets dairy-driven side: the bold tannin scrubs the cream, the dark fruit lifts the indulgence into balance. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Sonoma Bordeaux blend — plum, blackberry, mocha, soft tannin, smooth. The soft tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the mocha plays against the spinach base. Works as crowd-pleaser Bordeaux blend for creamed spinach — Sonoma softness on the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Amaro Nonino on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with creamed spinach — port-finished bourbon against dairy-rich side. Works; AE's softer-oak rides the cream at wine-finish register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with creamed spinach — cask-strength AE against dairy-rich side. Works; AE CS's cask-strength character rides the cream at premium wine-finish register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "AE Triple Cask with creamed spinach — multi-cask AE against dairy-rich side. Works; AE Triple Cask's layered character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Aperol lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Aviation": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Avion 44": "Avion 44 with creamed spinach — 44-month aged against dairy. Strong; extended maturation sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's clean silver register sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the blanco-tequila body matches the creamed spinach, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the bright cane-and-citrus weight carries the creamed spinach, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Baker's": "Baker's with creamed spinach — 107-proof small-batch against dairy. Strong; premium proof sits with richness at bold register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with creamed spinach — handcrafted American Oak Speyside against dairy-rich side. Strong; Balvenie's vanilla rides the cream at bourbon-cask register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with creamed spinach — tropical-sweet rum-cask Speyside against dairy-rich side. Works; Caribbean Cask rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with creamed spinach — First Fill American Oak against dairy-rich side. Strong; Balvenie 16's vanilla rides the cream with collector-register depth.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood with creamed spinach — ultra-premium 21yr port-cask Speyside against dairy-rich side. Works; Portwood is over-specified for a side — save for main course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with creamed spinach — Bardstown French-wine finish against dairy-rich side. Works; Bardstown Château Laboude's French-wine character rides the cream at contemporary-craft register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with creamed spinach — sourced-blend against dairy. Strong; multi-distillery complexity sits with richness at craft-blender register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with creamed spinach — stout-barrel against dairy. Strong; KBS character sits with richness at distinctive-finish register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare with creamed spinach — Bardstown rum-cask finish against dairy-rich side. Strong; Bardstown Foursquare's tropical character rides the cream at Caribbean-finish register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with creamed spinach — Fusion blend against dairy. Strong; own-plus-sourced sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with creamed spinach — retailer-picked against dairy. Strong; curated single-barrel sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with creamed spinach — rye spice against dairy richness. Cleaner and brighter than the Wellers' caramel-wheat take on this side; the 80-proof delicacy lets the cream lead. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year with creamed spinach — aged rye spice against dairy richness. The 10-year adds oak depth to Basil Hayden's brighter-than-wheated profile; strong. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with creamed spinach — bready malted grain against dairy richness. The bread-and-dairy pairing logic is built-in; malted rye's grain character threads through the cream in a way no other Basil Hayden's reaches here. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with creamed spinach — Beam BH red-wine finish against dairy-rich side. Works; BH Red Wine Cask's softer-proof character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast with creamed spinach — toast and caramel against dairy. The toasted finish softens the side's richness with marshmallow warmth; distinct from wheated-caramel takes. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bee's Knees": "Bee's Knees's crisp butter-citrus build plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the honey threads the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater Gin's London-Dry-juniper botanicals sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Beignets": "The creamed spinach alongside the beignets -- rich cream gives way to sweet, the close lands generously. Works; the contrast holds, neither course overshadows the other.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with creamed spinach — blended bourbon's honey against dairy. Strong; a composed heritage-blend match.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with creamed spinach — 103-proof richer Blanton's against dairy. Strong; extra proof adds dimension at the premium register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with creamed spinach — single-barrel caramel-vanilla against dairy richness. Strong; the premium character sits with the side at matched register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's SfB with creamed spinach — export cask-strength Blanton's against dairy-rich side. Strong; Blanton's SfB cuts the cream at barrel-proof register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with creamed spinach — milestone Pact 10 against dairy. Strong; decennial-release complexity sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with creamed spinach — Pact 8 blend against dairy. Strong; master-blender complexity sits with richness at annual-release register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with creamed spinach — Pact 9 blend against dairy. Strong; wine-cask complexity sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Patagonia Argentina Malbec — Noemia A Lisa, cooler-climate than Mendoza, blackberry, violet, graphite, full body, soft tannin. The full body handles dairy-heavy cream and the soft tannin won't tear against spinach-iron. Strong Patagonian-Malbec for creamed spinach — silky polish on the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bone Marrow": "The bone marrow opens the meal, the creamed spinach with the entree -- the marrow-and-gremolata plate sits cleanly with the creamed spinach. Strong; both items.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bone-In Filet": "The bone-in filet with the creamed spinach -- the 14oz bone-deepened butter holds with the rich bechamel-laced greens, the cream complementing the marrow-touched richness. Strong; the call composes without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Booker's": "Booker's with creamed spinach — uncut/unfiltered Beam against dairy-rich side. Strong; Booker's cask-strength cuts the cream at barrel-proof register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Botanist Gin": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, bitters. The cocktail's vanilla-caramel softens against the creamed spinach's dairy weight; bourbon stands with the side without fighting it. Works — though the spinach peaks better against the Cab pour guests usually already have.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with creamed spinach — the house selection's deeper caramel against dairy. Strong; the private-select character adds dimension beyond standard Maker's.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with creamed spinach — Virginia-aged small-batch against dairy. Strong; smooth character sits with richness at matched register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bowmore 12": "Creamed Spinach: dairy-rich side meets Bowmore 12's sherry depth. The honey threads into the cream; the gentler peat doesn't flatten the side the way Lagavulin or Laphroaig do. Works cleanly but won't move a guest.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin. The high acid cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the blackberry plays against the spinach base. Strong benchmark Barbera for creamed spinach — food-wine acidity reframes the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Creamed Spinach: the structured red's grip carries the rich side's dairy load; oak-driven body absorbs the cream and tannin clears the finish. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Broccoli Cheddar": "The broccoli cheddar and the creamed spinach on the same meal -- cream stacked on cream, the plate reads dense across the meal. Works; sequence or share, the build holds.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich's progressive unpeated Islay finds neutral with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the scotch backbone matches the creamed spinach, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; blended character doesn't drive.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels + Belly and the creamed spinach across the plate -- the sweet-savory build holds beside the rich greens. Works; safe call when the table doubles up on sides.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with creamed spinach — BT's oak and vanilla against dairy richness. Strong; the non-wheated profile gives the side a different frame than Maker's or Larceny.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with creamed spinach — high-rye spice against dairy. Strong; rye's brightness gives the side a distinctive counterpoint to wheated caramel takes.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Burrata": "The burrata and the creamed spinach -- the lighter course primes the richer, the meal carries register lift. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Butternut Squash": "Butternut Squash and the creamed spinach -- the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream meets the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: cream-and-sugar build the close, the meal finishes generous. Works; no conflict however the table orders them.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Creamed Spinach: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila's rested-agave body finds neutral with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the aged-agave weight wraps the creamed spinach, and the soft-spice note softens the side. Works; the pour is a touch over-specified for the side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Campari": "Campari's aperitivo at its most textbook clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Campari for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club with creamed spinach — traditional Canadian blend against dairy-rich side. Strong; Canadian Club's grain-forward character rides the cream at heritage register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Canadian Club 43 Year": "CC 43 Year with creamed spinach — ultra-premium aged Canadian against dairy-rich side. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- light spirit needs the cut, not the supporting plate. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a light spirit. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Captain Morgan Rum for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing with creamed spinach — single-barrel Canadian against dairy-rich side. Strong; Caribou Crossing's craft character rides the cream at single-barrel register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Carrot Cake": "The creamed spinach alongside the carrot cake -- rich cream gives way to sweet, the close lands generously. Works; the contrast holds, neither course overshadows the other.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Casa LBV Port lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight finds neutral with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the smoky agave register handles the creamed spinach, and the earthy backbone threads the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus — Napa Cab with concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. The opulent body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the cocoa plays against the earthy sauce. Peak iconic Napa Cab for creamed spinach — crowd-pleasing opulence on the classic steakhouse side. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Cheesecake": "The creamed spinach and the cheesecake -- the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens hands off cleanly to the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Chilean Seabass": "Chilean Seabass with the creamed spinach -- the seabass' buttery seabass flesh meets the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Chocolate Brownie": "Creamed Spinach and the brownie -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Chocolate Cake": "Creamed Spinach and the chocolate cake -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; the meal closes cleanly from the side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin Vodka's bold char-cream neutrality sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with creamed spinach — sweeter American brandy against dairy-rich side. Strong; Christian Bros rides the cream at value register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Château Batailley": "Pauillac 5ème Cru Classé Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the cedar plays against the spinach base. Strong classified Bordeaux for creamed spinach — Pauillac structure reframes the rich steakhouse side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Château Beaucastel": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône — Beaucastel, all 13 permitted varieties, dark fruit, leather, truffle, Provençal herbs, full body, medium tannin. The full body handles dairy-heavy cream and the Provençal herbs lift spinach-iron. Strong bucket-list Châteauneuf for creamed spinach — Beaucastel complexity on a rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Château Haut Segottes": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the clayey earth plays against the spinach base. Peak right-bank Bordeaux for creamed spinach — Merlot-led roundness on the rich side. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Château de Rouillac": "Pessac-Léognan left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, tobacco, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the cedar-graphite plays against the spinach base. Strong left-bank Bordeaux for creamed spinach — French structure reframes the rich steakhouse side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Clam Chowder": "The chowder and the creamed spinach -- cream stacked on cream, the plate reads dense across the meal. Works; sequence them or share the table, the call holds either way.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's caramel-butterscotch character frames the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the añejo weight overshoots the light side, and the caramel pairs into the cream. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango with creamed spinach — ceremonial against dairy. Strong; Durango ceremony sits with richness at ultra-premium register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold with creamed spinach — joven blend against dairy. Strong; ceremonial presentation sits with richness at ultra-premium register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's earthen-pit smoke profile leans against the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the wood-fire body meets the creamed spinach, and the campfire register frames the side. Works; a measured close to the meal.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata's silky Jalisco blanco with ceramic-decanter presentation leans against the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the creamed spinach, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado's ceramic-icon reposado character finds neutral with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the rested-agave depth sits with the creamed spinach, and the cooked-agave note underlines the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra with creamed spinach — top-tier ceremonial against dairy. Strong; 5-year Ultra sits with richness at collector-grade register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep with creamed spinach — 90-proof Beam wheated against dairy. Strong; the caramel and wheat honey sit with the cream cleanly.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's cooked-agave-and-oak register leans against the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the barrel-rested body handles the creamed spinach, and the oak threads the side. Works; the bold agave overpowers the dish's restraint.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's vermouth-wine-base sweetness leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the bitter herb edge sits with the light side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the unaged body brightens the creamed spinach, and the unaged-cane note plays against the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado's aged-agave character sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the rested-agave depth sits with the creamed spinach, and the cooked-agave note underlines the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's agave-pepper character holds the line with the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the añejo weight overshoots the light side, and the oak rounds the cream. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg's rested-agave body leans against the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the creamed spinach, and the caramel-agave register brightens the side. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's agave-char character holds the line with the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the añejo weight overshoots the light side, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Corpse Reviver": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier with creamed spinach — drier oak-forward cognac against dairy-rich side. Strong; Courvoisier's structural oak cuts cream rather than riding it.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye alongside the creamed spinach -- the 26oz marbled char-and-fat holds with the rich bechamel-laced greens, the cream catching the rendered fat without doubling it. Strong; the classic call for the cut.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Crab Cake": "Crab Cake alongside the creamed spinach -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust meets the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: restrained yields to cream, the table arcs cleanly. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 with creamed spinach — Diageo Classic Malt against dairy-rich side. Strong; Cragganmore's subtle smoke cuts the cream more than honey-Glenlivet rides it.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Creamy Potato": "The potato soup and the creamed spinach -- the meal doubles the cream, neither course gets to play off the other. Works; sequence them or share the table, the call holds either way.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Creme Brulee": "Creamed Spinach and the brulee -- rich cream gives way to sweet, the close lands generously. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal with creamed spinach — iconic Canadian against dairy-rich side. Strong; Crown Royal's smoothness rides the cream at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Cucumber Gimlet": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12 with creamed spinach — Matusalem-cask Highland against dairy-rich side. Strong; Dalmore's slightly-oily sherry-finish rides the cream at luxury register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the savory herb plays against the spinach base. Strong rare-Napa CF for creamed spinach — Darioush aromatic elegance reframes the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Darioush — Silverado Trail Cab with blackcurrant, dark plum, mocha, spice. The opulent body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the mocha-spice plays against the earthy sauce. Strong opulent Napa Cab for creamed spinach — Persian-inspired estate story on the classic steakhouse side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin's bold char-juniper botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City Vodka's crisp grain-juniper neutrality stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15 with creamed spinach — aged sherry-finish Dewar's against dairy-rich side. Strong; Dewar's 15's aged character rides the cream at premium register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label with creamed spinach — entry blended Scotch against dairy-rich side. Strong; Dewar's White rides the cream at approachable register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Disaronno belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with creamed spinach — craft rye against dairy. Strong; boutique character sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's deep-bodied spirit register reads alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the heavy body handles the creamed spinach, and the deep backbone frames the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Domaine Serene Grand Cheval's char-cherry tannin holds with the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Creamed Spinach: the structured red's grip carries the rich side's dairy load; oak-driven body absorbs the cream and tannin clears the finish. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The full body handles dairy-heavy cream and the herbal earth lifts spinach-iron. Strong Châteauneuf for creamed spinach — GSM polish on the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Don Fulano Anejo": "Don Fulano Anejo's agave-apricot character lifts the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the añejo weight overshoots the light side, and the oak frames the cream. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift reads alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the unaged body brightens the creamed spinach, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado's rested-agave body sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the aged-agave weight wraps the creamed spinach, and the soft-spice note softens the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942 with creamed spinach — benchmark extra añejo against dairy. Strong; aged depth sits with richness at iconic register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th's aged-agave character sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the aged-agave weight wraps the creamed spinach, and the oak threads the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco's workhorse blanco with clean agave snap reads alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the unaged body brightens the creamed spinach, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Don Julio Reposado": "Don Julio Reposado's aged-agave character leans against the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the aged-agave weight wraps the creamed spinach, and the caramel-agave register brightens the side. Works; the 70th's clarity is too delicate to drive the pairing.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Drambuie": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Drambuie lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Dunville's Single Malt": "Dunville's Single Malt with creamed spinach — Northern Ireland single malt against dairy-rich side. Works; Dunville's Echlinville character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with creamed spinach — Col. E.H. Taylor Jr. tribute against dairy-rich side. Strong; EHT Full Proof cuts the cream at historic Kentucky register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye with creamed spinach — 100-proof BIB against dairy. Strong; bonded structure sits with richness at premium register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with creamed spinach — bonded 100-proof against dairy. Strong; BIB structure sits with the richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with creamed spinach — aged oak against dairy richness. Strong; the 10-year's structure sits beside the side without being sweet the way wheated bourbons are.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|El Cabo": "El Cabo's añejo-pour depth leans against the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the oak-aged tequila body threads the creamed spinach, and the oak threads the side. Works; covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Creamed Spinach|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "El Mayor Extra Anejo with creamed spinach — aged El Mayor against dairy. Strong; accessible aged sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado's aged-agave character finds neutral with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the oak-aged tequila body threads the creamed spinach, and the soft-spice note softens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin's crisp char-herbal botanicals sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 with creamed spinach — HH aged single-barrel against dairy-rich side. Strong; EC 18's aged character rides the cream at aged-HH register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel with creamed spinach — house-pick single-barrel against dairy. Strong; curated oak-spice sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel with creamed spinach — single-barrel Heaven Hill against dairy. Strong; oak-spice sits with richness at the premium register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with creamed spinach — 94-proof small-batch against dairy. Strong; oak-spice sits with the richness at the same register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with creamed spinach — 94-proof HH against dairy. Strong; the rye spice sits with richness at HH register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's apple-char acidity stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee with creamed spinach — namesake single-barrel against dairy. Strong; the smooth character at matched register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Epoch Ingenuity": "Creamed Spinach: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Escargot": "Escargot alongside the creamed spinach -- the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight meets the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: cream meets umami, the table goes deep. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Espresso Martini": "Tito's, espresso, Grind liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The cocktail's coffee-and-amaro depth sits alongside creamed spinach's dairy weight; Averna's bitter edge cuts the cream. Strong pairing when guest carries the cocktail through the entrée with the spinach.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The cocktail's coffee-hazelnut cream profile meets the side's dairy richness without fighting it; brown sugar bridges to the parmesan crust. Strong pairing when guest carries the cocktail through the entrée.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone. The crushed-stone minerality cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the smoked-meat plays against the spinach base. Strong savory Pinot for creamed spinach — Eola-Amity complexity reframes the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Far Niente — Oakville Cab, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, espresso, violet, smooth powerful. The opulent body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the chocolate-espresso plays against the earthy sauce. Strong luxurious Oakville Cab for creamed spinach — Far Niente opulence on the classic steakhouse side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Far Niente — Napa Oakville Chardonnay, buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush texture. The opulent oak-butter aligns with the creamed spinach's dairy weight and the hazelnut adds depth against the rich side. Works well as Chardonnay for creamed spinach — cream-on-cream, oak-on-butter. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Faroe Island Salmon": "The salmon into the creamed spinach -- two rich courses on the same plate, the table earns its substance. Works; safe alongside, but not the headline pick.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The firm tannin cuts béchamel cream weight and the graphite plays with garlic-dairy depth. Strong Super Tuscan for creamed spinach — Bordeaux-style structure on the indulgent side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Agustin Huneeus's elegant Napa Cab — moderate tannin, fruit-forward, polished finish. Handles the creamed spinach well, but the bigger Cabs (Caymus, Far Niente) peak higher against the roux's weight and parmesan depth. Acceptable mid-tier Cab pour. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the bitter herb edge sits with the light side, and the bitter cuts the fat. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta's aperitivo herb-and-bitter cut sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the herbal cut works the creamed spinach, and the amaro backbone frames the side. Works; too light for dessert density at this level.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon alongside the creamed spinach -- the 10oz lean cut sits with the bechamel-laced greens, the cream matching the filet's natural butter without crowding it. Strong; reliable, season-after-season order.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the Pinot body brightens the creamed spinach, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with creamed spinach — 104-proof 6-recipe against dairy. Strong; premium proof sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label with creamed spinach — 80-proof floral-honey against dairy. Strong; the light body sits beside the side cleanly without overshadowing.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with creamed spinach — craft small-batch against dairy. Strong; boutique character sits with richness at matched register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Fox & Oden Double Oaked with creamed spinach — Double Oaked against dairy-rich side. Strong; Fox & Oden's caramel-oak rides the cream at Double-Oaked register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Frangelico lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Creamed Spinach|French 75": "French 75's citrus-earth build plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Creamed Spinach|French Onion": "The French onion alongside the creamed spinach -- dairy plays against umami, the table reads layered. Works; the table can build them in order or together.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Frias Block 5": "Creamed Spinach: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, high acid. The high acid cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the rose petal plays against the spinach base. Strong classic Barolo for creamed spinach — Vajra's food-demanding acid on the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Piedmont sweet sparkling — peach, honey, floral. The sweetness fights the parmesan-garlic roux and the spinach's vegetal bite on every axis imaginable. Route Moscato to dessert where sugar makes sense — never pour on a savory cream side. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Creamed Spinach|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift finds neutral with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the unaged blanco body cuts the creamed spinach, and the agave-citrus thread frames the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Creamed Spinach|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the rested-agave depth sits with the creamed spinach, and the soft-spice note softens the side. Works; Riedel-luxury over-specified.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue with creamed spinach — craft Irish against dairy-rich side. Works; Garavogue's distinctive character rides the cream at craft-Irish register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|George T. Stagg": "GTS with creamed spinach — BTAC holy-grail against dairy-rich side. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The full body handles dairy-heavy cream and the cracked pepper cuts spinach-iron. Strong California-Zin for creamed spinach — bold Napa on the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12 with creamed spinach — ex-bourbon-cask Speyside against dairy-rich side. Strong; Fiddich's honey-apple rides the cream at gateway register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14 with creamed spinach — new-charred-oak Speyside against dairy-rich side. Strong; Fiddich 14's baking-spice rides the cream with more backbone than the 12.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18 with creamed spinach — Oloroso sherry-cask Speyside against dairy-rich side. Strong; Fiddich 18's nutmeg-and-spice rides the cream with aged character.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23 with creamed spinach — 23yr Cuvée-cask Speyside against dairy-rich side. Strong; Gran Cru's pastry-brioche rides the cream at rare-release register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12 with creamed spinach — coastal Highland against dairy-rich side. Strong; Glenglassaugh 12's tropical-fruit character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Glenlivet 12": "Creamed Spinach: dairy-rich side meets \"The Glenlivet house style.\" Strong; Glenlivet's honey-floral rides the cream at benchmark register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10 with creamed spinach — Highland bourbon-cask against dairy-rich side. Strong; Glenmorangie 10's honey-floral rides the cream at entry-Highland register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18 with creamed spinach — 18yr bourbon + sherry-finish Highland against dairy-rich side. Strong; Glenmorangie 18's aged character rides the cream at premium register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Graham's 20 Year Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Graham's 2017 Vintage Port's structured vintage port with dark-fruit weight doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Graham's 2017 Vintage Port for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum's unaged silver-spirit body sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the blanco-tequila body matches the creamed spinach, and the bright blanco register underlines the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Grand Marnier belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's cask-strength herbal-high-proof sweetness sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose Vodka's crisp French-wheat-soft neutrality plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar and the creamed spinach -- the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge meets the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: cream rounds the savory, the meal earns its depth. Works; no conflict however the table orders them.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Gumbo": "The gumbo and the creamed spinach -- heat plays against dairy, the meal earns the cooler register. Works; sequence them or share the table, the call holds either way.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Creamed Spinach: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Creamed Spinach|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 with creamed spinach — Japanese Alps single malt against dairy-rich side. Works; Hakushu's crisp-green character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel with creamed spinach — BT single-barrel against dairy. Strong; complex character at matched register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 Vodka's crisp char-floral neutrality sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the floral notes lift the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Head Fake": "Head Fake's lemon-driven cocktail body finds neutral with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the bright build frames the creamed spinach, and the citrus cuts the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with creamed spinach — HH 20yr aged against dairy-rich side. Strong; HH 20's aged character rides the cream at collector-HH register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with creamed spinach — 100-proof traditional bourbon against dairy. Strong; the BIB structure sits beside the side with character distinct from wheated softness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with creamed spinach — aged Dylan bourbon against dairy. Strong; premium aged depth sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "The fat in the cream binds to Martha's Vineyard's tannins at the molecular level, smoothing the wine's firm structure into something richer and more supple than it presents on its own. That softening effect lets the wine's dark cherry and cedar come forward against the savory, dairy-sweet backdrop of the spinach, turning a side dish into a legitimate vehicle for one of Napa's most iconic Cabs. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "Creamed Spinach: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks Gin's crisp cucumber-rose botanicals reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the floral notes lift the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Hendricks Grand Cabaret's bold anise-char botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the anise threads the herb edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Hendricks Neptunia": "Hendricks Neptunia's crisp anise-brine botanicals stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Hendricks Orbium": "Hendricks Orbium's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy with creamed spinach — VS/VSOP against dairy-rich side. Strong; Hennessy's vanilla rides the cream at benchmark register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB with creamed spinach — aged bonded HH against dairy. Strong; BIB structure and 10-year depth sit with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Harmony with creamed spinach — premium Suntory blend against dairy-rich side. Works; Hibiki's refined character rides the cream at premium register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|House Wedge": "The wedge and the creamed spinach -- cream and umami share the plate, the build reads dense and clean. Works; sequence them or share the table, the call holds either way.",
  "Creamed Spinach|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 with creamed spinach — historic-Kentucky 15yr against dairy-rich side. Strong; IW Harper 15's aged character rides the cream at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Inhibited": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche Gin's crisp earth-floral botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish Gin's cask-strength citrus-earth botanicals holds with the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the honey threads the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon Mezcal Casks with creamed spinach — northern MI mezcal-cask bourbon against dairy-rich side. Strong; Ironfish Mezcal's smoky character rides the cream at Michigan-hometown register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish with creamed spinach — Virginia port-finished bourbon against dairy-rich side. Works; Isaac Bowman's softer-oak rides the cream at Virginia-heritage register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards's cherry-coffee tannin leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "J. Davies — Diamond Mountain Cab with dark fruit, graphite, mineral, firm tannin. The structured body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the graphite-mineral plays against the earthy sauce. Peak mountain Cab for creamed spinach — Schramsberg provenance and volcanic-soil complexity on the classic side. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year with creamed spinach — 12yr aged Tennessee against dairy-rich side. Strong; JD 12's aged depth rides the cream at premium register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded with creamed spinach — 100-proof Tennessee against dairy-rich side. Strong; JD Bonded's backbone cuts the cream at Bonded-Act register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7 with creamed spinach — iconic Tennessee against dairy-rich side. Strong; Old No. 7's vanilla-caramel rides the cream at iconic register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "JD Sinatra Select with creamed spinach — Sinatra-tribute Tennessee against dairy-rich side. Strong; Sinatra Select's extra oak rides the cream at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson with creamed spinach — iconic Irish against dairy-rich side. Works; Jameson's smoothness rides the cream at entry Irish register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis's crisp apple-char acidity stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with creamed spinach — master-blender-tribute sea-aged against dairy-rich side. Strong; Marian McLain L.E. rides the cream at collector register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with creamed spinach — sea-aged Jefferson's against dairy-rich side. Strong; Voyage 23's salt-ocean character rides the cream at ocean-finish register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve with creamed spinach — blended honey-vanilla against dairy. Strong; a composed match at the register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with creamed spinach — double-barrel depth against dairy. Strong; Twin Oak's complexity is slightly over-ambitious for the side, but the pairing is refined.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label with creamed spinach — aged Beam against dairy. Strong; oak and vanilla sit with the cream at matched registers.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label with creamed spinach — entry bourbon against dairy. Strong; accessible everyday match.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Johnnie Walker Black": "JW Black with creamed spinach — iconic 12yr JW against dairy-rich side. Strong; JW Black's balanced character cuts the cream at premium-blend register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Johnnie Walker Blue": "JW Blue with creamed spinach — 60+yr rare-cask JW against dairy-rich side. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for a side — save the bottle for the main course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Johnnie Walker Red": "JW Red with creamed spinach — 80-proof smoky JW against dairy-rich side. Strong; JW Red's bold character cuts the cream with distinctive peat-influenced smoke.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Alexander Valley Cabernet — soft tannin. Works beautifully with our creamed spinach: the Cab's dark fruit cuts through the parmesan-and-heavy-cream sauce, and the soft tannin doesn't fight the roasted garlic. Peak Cab pour for this side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the espresso plays against the spinach base. Strong cult Napa for creamed spinach — Colgin prestige reframes the rich steakhouse side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's heavy aged-spirit weight reads alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the layered spirit body sits with the creamed spinach, and the deep backbone frames the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's high-proof pour register reads alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the dense-spirit weight carries the creamed spinach, and the deep backbone frames the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Kahlua lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Kansas City": "Kansas City alongside the creamed spinach -- the 18oz strip's clean savory grain sits with the bechamel-laced greens, cream balancing the strip's leaner edge. Strong; the side anchors the cut without crowding it.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Keenan Chardonnay": "Creamed Spinach: this rich, creamy side's dairy-driven indulgence meets this rich, oak-aged Chardonnay's butter, stone fruit, and full-bodied weight. Opulent Chardonnay's butter and oak mirror the side's dairy richness — same-register match. Works — a capable side pour. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Keenan Mernet": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye with creamed spinach — premium KO against dairy. Strong; allocated character sits with richness at collector register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with creamed spinach — KO Confiscated against dairy. Strong; craft-blend sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with creamed spinach — $150+ luxury Irish crossover against dairy-rich side. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The medium body handles dairy-heavy cream and the wild herbs lift spinach-iron. Strong Southern-French for creamed spinach — a gentle Rhône counterpoint to the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek with creamed spinach — 100-proof nutty high-rye against dairy. Strong; the nutty oak gives the side a dimension the Wellers' wheated caramel can't.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year with creamed spinach — aged nutty Beam against dairy. Strong; oak-aged depth sits with richness at premium register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 with creamed spinach — Beam aged single-barrel against dairy-rich side. Strong; KC 15's aged character rides the cream at aged-Beam register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 with creamed spinach — 18yr Beam KC against dairy-rich side. Strong; KC 18's aged character rides the cream at collector register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with creamed spinach — blended 100-proof against dairy. Strong; rye brightness and bourbon depth add dimension.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye with creamed spinach — 100-proof rye against dairy. Strong; bold rye sits with richness at high-impact register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva's rested-agave body leans against the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the barrel-rested body handles the creamed spinach, and the oak threads the side. Strong; multi-cask complexity sits with richness at collector-craft register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo's crisp char-floral character holds steady against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the añejo weight overshoots the light side, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Añejo with creamed spinach — Riedel-luxury aged against dairy. Strong; aged Komos sits with richness at luxury register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado's añejo-pour depth leans against the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the aged-agave weight wraps the creamed spinach, and the cooked-agave note underlines the side. Works; luxury ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Lagavulin 8": "Creamed Spinach: the dairy-forward side meets Lagavulin 8's balanced peat. The peat sits beside the cream without quite meshing; the 8-year fares better than Laphroaig's medicinal weight here, but this isn't the Islay's strongest register. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Napa Cab — Lail entry-level, polished blackcurrant, cedar, dark cherry, refined medium tannin. The refined tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the cedar plays against the spinach base. Strong historically-pedigreed Napa Cab for creamed spinach — Lail family polish reframes the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the cassis plays against the spinach base. Strong historically-significant Napa Cab for creamed spinach — Daniel Cuvée structure reframes the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver's green-agave-and-cane lift sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the blanco-tequila body matches the creamed spinach, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Laphroaig 10": "Laphroaig 10 with creamed spinach — medicinal peat crushes the dairy-forward side. Capable only; the side can't carry the dram's weight. Reach for Bowmore 12 if pairing peated Islay with this side is the goal. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch with creamed spinach — wheated caramel-honey against dairy richness. Matched registers; a straightforward, friendly pairing. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Champagne — clean acid, bubbles, elegant body. Far too light for our creamed spinach's roux-based richness; the Champagne disappears under the parmesan-and-heavy-cream sauce. Keep Laurent with lighter starters — tartare, tower, scallops, shrimp cocktail. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Le Garenne Rosé": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Creamed Spinach: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Creamed Spinach: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Les Pallières Raciné": "Creamed Spinach: the structured red's grip carries the rich side's dairy load; oak-driven body absorbs the cream and tannin clears the finish. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Liberator Gin": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Licor 43 for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Limoncello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, whisper of spice. The acid cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the forest-floor earth plays against the spinach base. Strong by-the-glass Pinot for creamed spinach — Oregon elegance reframes the rich steakhouse side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Little Book": "Little Book with creamed spinach — Jim Beam Little Book against dairy-rich side. Strong; Little Book's cask-strength cuts the cream at Fred-Noe-artistic register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Loaded Potato": "The loaded potato alongside the creamed spinach -- two cream-rounded courses share the meal -- the weight piles on. Works; the table can build them in order or together.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year with creamed spinach — complex 20yr aged Highland against dairy-rich side. Strong; Loch Lomond 20's varied-still character rides the cream at collector register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with creamed spinach — sourced premium rye against dairy. Strong; boutique-blend sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Long Road Gin": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's wood-fire mezcal body leans against the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the earthen-pit smoke carries the creamed spinach, and the mineral smoke plays against the side. Works; the call holds at neutral register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII with creamed spinach — luxury icon against dairy-rich side. Works; creamed spinach is a side, not the centerpiece — Louis XIII's aged depth is wasted on the accompaniment. Save the bottle for the filet or marrow course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Creamed Spinach: this rich, creamy side's dairy-driven indulgence meets this medium-bodied red's approachable fruit and food-friendly acidity. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. Strong — the wine refreshes the side's richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry with creamed spinach — Oloroso-cask Speyside against dairy-rich side. Works; the sherry-cask character rides the cream at benchmark register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18 with creamed spinach — premium aged sherried Speyside against dairy-rich side. Works; Macallan 18's 18yr character rides the cream at premium register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate with creamed spinach — Easter Elchies Estate-oak Macallan against dairy-rich side. Works; Macallan Estate rides the cream at premium register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds with the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Creamed Spinach: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Mahon Gin": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark with creamed spinach — iconic wheated caramel-vanilla against dairy. A reliable, approachable pairing; the side and the pour at the same register. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with creamed spinach — the bourbon's honeyed caramel depth sits beside the dairy. More refined than standard Maker's here; the aging gives the pour something to contribute. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- light spirit needs the cut, not the supporting plate. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Malibu Rum on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye with creamed spinach — aged Michigan rye against dairy. Strong; extended-maturation character sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Marc Brédif Chinon's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Margarita": "Corazon Blanco, lemon, lime, orange juice, Cointreau, salt rim. The triple-citrus acid clashes with the parmesan-roux cream and escargot-butter garlic; wrong on every axis. Route the Margarita to chicken, tartare, or lighter appetizer courses instead. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Marimar Estate Christina": "Marimar Estate Christina's cherry-earth tannin leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Market Fish": "Market Fish with the creamed spinach -- the market fish's clean white-fish flesh meets the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the delicate plate yields to rich, the meal builds. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Mata Hari Absinthe for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Bourbon with creamed spinach — Michter's allocated 10yr against dairy-rich side. Strong; Michter's 10's single-barrel character rides the cream at prestigious-independent register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye with creamed spinach — aged premium Michter's against dairy. Strong; polished aged character sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye with creamed spinach — smooth small-batch rye against dairy. Strong; Michter's smooth character sits with the side's richness with refined polish.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the creamed spinach, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo's crisp agave-vanilla character holds steady against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the añejo weight overshoots the light side, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado's aged-agave character leans against the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the oak-aged tequila body threads the creamed spinach, and the oak threads the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam with creamed spinach — Mister Sam tribute against dairy-rich side. Strong; Mister Sam's blended character rides the cream at Seagram's-tribute register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Mocha Creme": "The creamed spinach and the mocha creme -- the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens hands off cleanly to the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder with creamed spinach — Glenfiddich-Balvenie blend against dairy-rich side. Strong; Monkey Shoulder's Speyside-malt character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Montenegro Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum's Caribbean-rum register with bright sugarcane sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the unaged body brightens the creamed spinach, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Muga Reserva": "Rioja Alta Reserva Tempranillo-blend — Muga, American-French oak, strawberry, dried cherry, vanilla, leather, medium body. The vanilla-oak wraps around dairy-heavy cream and the medium body handles spinach-iron. Strong Rioja for creamed spinach — a gentle traditional pairing.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Mushroom Bisque": "Mushroom Bisque and the creamed spinach -- the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream meets the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: dairy plays against umami, the table reads layered. Works; no conflict however the table orders them.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's dense-spirit weight finds neutral with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the dense-spirit weight carries the creamed spinach, and the heavy-pour register grips the side. Works; a measured close to the meal.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Negroni": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — stirred. The bitter-herbal profile cuts the side's dairy weight cleanly and Campari's orange plays against parmesan. Strong pairing — the Negroni's structure handles cream where sweeter cocktails would compound the weight.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye with creamed spinach — UK-aged rye against dairy. Strong; distinctive character sits with richness at novelty register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch with creamed spinach — UK-matured bourbon against dairy. Strong; distinctive cask character adds dimension standard bourbons don't bring.",
  "Creamed Spinach|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with creamed spinach — beer-barrel-finished bourbon against dairy-rich side. Works; NH Beer Barrel's hop-character rides the cream at Michigan-hometown register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|New York Sour": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register crowds the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the New York Sour on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Creamed Spinach: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Not a Paper Plane": "Bulleit bourbon, Nonino amaro, Aperol, Angostura. The bourbon-and-amaro depth sits alongside creamed spinach's dairy weight; Nonino's bitterness cuts the cream and Aperol brightens against parmesan. Strong pairing — a sophisticated cocktail for a classic side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Oban 14": "Oban 14 with creamed spinach — West Highland coastal against dairy-rich side. Strong; Oban's sea-salt and honey ride the cream with maritime resonance.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Oban 18": "Oban 18 with creamed spinach — premium aged coastal Highland against dairy-rich side. Strong; Oban 18's aged character and sherry-finish ride the cream at premium maritime register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo's agave-char character finds register with the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the añejo weight overshoots the light side, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Creamed Spinach: the structured red's grip carries the rich side's dairy load; oak-driven body absorbs the cream and tannin clears the finish. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with creamed spinach — Old Elk Colorado craft against dairy-rich side. Strong; Old Elk Cigar Cut's cigar-paired character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer with creamed spinach — emmer-wheat wheated bourbon against dairy-rich side. Strong; Old Emmer's wheated softness rides the cream at craft register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year with creamed spinach — bonded wheat against dairy. Strong; the wheat honey threads through cream with BIB's structural backbone.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year with creamed spinach — 9-year wheated against dairy. Strong; the mature honey adds refinement to the BIB bones.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with creamed spinach — 16yr OGD high-rye against dairy-rich side. Strong; OGD 16's rye-spice cuts the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Opus One": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the mocha plays against the spinach base. Strong collector Napa blend for creamed spinach — prestige wine reframes the rich steakhouse side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Pablo Sour": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- cocktail acid on dairy side cuts the cream wrong — texture reads off. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Paloma": "Reposado tequila, vanilla syrup, fresh grapefruit. The grapefruit's bitterness fights our creamed spinach's parmesan-roux richness; the cocktail has the wrong job here. Save Paloma for chicken, salmon, or lighter work. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye with creamed spinach — Pappy Rye against dairy. Strong; Van Winkle legacy sits with richness at unicorn register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage 16th Ed with creamed spinach — Heaven Hill annual against dairy-rich side. Strong; Parker's Heritage 16th's cask-strength cuts the cream at collector register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's crisp oak-agave character lifts the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the añejo weight overshoots the light side, and the oak rounds the cream. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver's polished silver tequila with agave-forward lift finds neutral with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the creamed spinach, and the bright blanco register underlines the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Patron XO Cafe on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Peanut Butter Brownie": "The creamed spinach alongside the PB brownie -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; the contrast holds, neither course overshadows the other.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Peju — Rutherford Cab, dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the cocoa plays against the earthy sauce. Peak elegant Napa Cab for creamed spinach — family-estate Rutherford character on the classic steakhouse side. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand with creamed spinach — artisanal cognac against dairy-rich side. Strong; Ferrand's unblended character rides the cream at craft register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye with creamed spinach — 110-proof against dairy. Strong; bold rye sits with richness at high-impact register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Pineau des Charentes": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pineau des Charentes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Creamed Spinach: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Porterhouse": "* Porterhouse alongside the creamed spinach -- the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character carries cleanly into the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens. Gold standard; the unskippable match for this combo.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Prime Tartare": "The tartare and the creamed spinach -- cream rounds the savory, the meal earns its depth. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt — Napa Cab with blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft tannin. The lush body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the chocolate plays against the earthy sauce. Peak polished Napa Cab for creamed spinach — crowd-pleasing lushness on the classic steakhouse side. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|RAEN Royal St Robert": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Rainbow Trout": "Rainbow Trout with the creamed spinach -- the trout's delicate flesh meets the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: delicate gives way to rich, the table earns its weight. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 with creamed spinach — pot still Irish against dairy-rich side. Works; Redbreast 12's rich-oily character rides the cream at premium-Irish register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 with creamed spinach — aged pot still Irish against dairy-rich side. Works; Redbreast 21 is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Gatsby with creamed spinach — 15yr MGP Gatsby Reserve against dairy-rich side. Strong; Remus 15's rye-spice cuts the cream at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Rémy VSOP with creamed spinach — Fine Champagne cognac against dairy-rich side. Strong; Rémy's elegance rides the cream at premium register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo with creamed spinach — sun-shaped-bottle aged against dairy. Strong; distinctive ceremony sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality finds neutral with the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique with creamed spinach — retailer-label against dairy. Strong; aged-sourced sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Roast Half Chicken": "Roast Half Chicken with the creamed spinach -- the herb-butter chicken matches the rich greens. Strong; reliable side for the chicken.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The chickpea soup and the creamed spinach on the same meal -- heat plays against dairy, the meal earns the cooler register. Works; sequence or share, the build holds.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs's crisp apple-char acidity reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Roku Gin": "Roku Gin's crisp floral-juniper botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey premium-sipping rum sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the high-proof register matches the creamed spinach, and the heavy-pour register grips the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with creamed spinach — aged bourbon depth against dairy. Strong; the 10-year oak gives the side backbone distinct from wheated approaches.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Salt-Cured Halibut": "The halibut alongside the creamed spinach -- restrained yields to cream, the table arcs cleanly. Works; the side sits without pulling focus.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Sambuca": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Sambuca on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamed Spinach|San Simeon Stormwatch": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the mocha plays against the spinach base. Peak Paso value Bordeaux blend for creamed spinach — solid full-body reframes the rich side. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sauternes Glass lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The rye's spice and anise-edge cut the side's dairy weight cleanly; Peychaud's floral note adds complexity against creamed greens. Strong cocktail pour alongside the classic spinach — rye handles cream well.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye with creamed spinach — 90-proof rye against dairy. Strong; the spicy-sweet character sits with the side's richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna with creamed spinach — NZ craft single malt against dairy-rich side. Strong; Fortuna's NZ terroir rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the dried rose plays against the spinach base. Strong King-of-Italian for creamed spinach — Scavino prestige reframes the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity finds neutral with the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the dark chocolate plays against the spinach base. Strong by-the-glass Cab for creamed spinach — accessible California reframes the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Seafood Tower": "Seafood Tower and the creamed spinach -- acid plays against richness, the contrast holds across the courses. Works; both items hold their register, no clash.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops and the creamed spinach -- cream and protein share the meal, the build reads full. Works; both items hold their register, no clash.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Seasonal Soup": "The seasonal soup alongside the creamed spinach -- the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; the table can build them in order or together.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Creamed Spinach: structured red meets dairy-driven side: the bold tannin scrubs the cream, the dark fruit lifts the indulgence into balance. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Shafer 1.5": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Shafer Hillside Select": "Stags Leap collector's Cab — Hillside Select 32-month French oak, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violet, graphite, dense yet silky, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the violet plays against the spinach base. Strong collector's Napa Cab for creamed spinach — Hillside Select poise reframes the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Shrimp Bisque": "The shrimp bisque alongside the creamed spinach -- the meal doubles the cream, neither course gets to play off the other. Works; the table can build them in order or together.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Shrimp Cocktail": "The shrimp cocktail and the creamed spinach -- the bright register cleaves the heavy course, the table breathes. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body reads alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the unaged body brightens the creamed spinach, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado's aged-agave character finds neutral with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the añejo register carries the creamed spinach, and the cooked-agave note underlines the side. Works; ceremonial over-specified.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak — Alexander Valley Cab, American-oak vanilla, coconut, dill, ripe cassis, plum. The lush body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the vanilla-dill plays against the earthy sauce. Peak recognizable Cab for creamed spinach — American-oak signature on the classic steakhouse side. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Boutique Napa family estate Cab — dark plum, cassis, toasted oak, long refined finish, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the toasted oak plays against the spinach base. Strong boutique Napa Cab for creamed spinach — Simon Family estate structure reframes the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "St. Helena Napa Cab — Spottswoode organic family estate, plum, dark cherry, violet, silky medium tannin. The silky tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the violet plays against the spinach base. Peak organic Napa Cab for creamed spinach — Spottswoode estate polish reframes the rich side. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry — Napa Cab with blackberry, cassis, mocha, firm tannin. The full body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the mocha plays against the earthy sauce. Peak by-the-glass Cab for creamed spinach — Rutherford character frames the classic steakhouse side at accessible price. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the espresso plays against the spinach base. Strong estate Meritage for creamed spinach — Napa structure reframes the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with creamed spinach — BT cask-strength against dairy-rich side. Strong; Stagg Jr.'s cask-strength cuts the cream at barrel-proof register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, green, light-bodied. The weight class is wrong for a parmesan-roux creamed spinach; the wine vanishes underneath the heavy cream. Keep Stoneleigh for wedge or shrimp cocktail service where it has a defined job. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Swordfish": "Swordfish alongside the creamed spinach -- the meal layers rich on rich, the table carries weight cleanly when the registers match. Works; the side carries cleanly with the swordfish.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with creamed spinach — triple-cask aged against dairy. Strong; multi-cask complexity sits with richness at collector-craft register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter — Napa Cab, rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth finish. The polished body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the mocha plays against the earthy sauce. Strong mid-tier Napa Cab for creamed spinach — crowd-pleaser concentration on the classic steakhouse side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Creamed Spinach: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|The BG": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the honey threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamed Spinach|The Happy Wife": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamed Spinach|The Manhattan": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The bourbon depth sits alongside the side's dairy weight without dominating; vanilla syrup softens the contrast against cream-and-parmesan. Strong pairing when guest carries the Manhattan through the entrée course with the spinach.",
  "Creamed Spinach|The Prisoner Red Blend": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The full body handles dairy-heavy cream and the smooth tannin won't tear against spinach-iron. Strong Prisoner for creamed spinach — Napa lush on the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk with the creamed spinach -- the 36oz showpiece's theatrical bone-in marbling meets the rich bechamel-laced greens, the cream catching the rendered fat the long-bone delivers. Strong; the side anchors the spectacle.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with creamed spinach — BTAC barrel-proof against dairy. Strong; uncut intensity sits with richness at allocated register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with creamed spinach — white-wine-finished Barton bourbon against dairy-rich side. Works; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-butter rides the cream with distinctive resonance.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Titos Vodka": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory with creamed spinach — Suntory entry blend against dairy-rich side. Works; Toki's light-floral character rides the cream at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Tomato Basil": "The tomato basil and the creamed spinach -- bright-acid breaks the weight, the plate finds its lift. Works; sequence them or share the table, the call holds either way.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Transfusion": "Transfusion's crisp citrus-ginger build stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the añejo register carries the creamed spinach, and the caramel-agave register brightens the side. Works; multi-cask complexity handles richness at luxury register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W. with creamed spinach — heritage Irish against dairy-rich side. Works; Tullamore's drier character rides the cream at traditional register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "The tuna into the creamed spinach -- delicate gives way to rich, the table earns its weight. Works; safe alongside, but not the headline pick.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas and the creamed spinach -- the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth meets the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: spice cuts cream cleanly, the contrast holds. Works; no conflict however the table orders them.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Venge Family Reserve": "Kirk Venge flagship Oakville Cab — concentrated blackcurrant, dark chocolate, vanilla, seamless polished finish, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the dark chocolate plays against the spinach base. Strong Oakville flagship for creamed spinach — Venge opulence reframes the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Venge Scout's Honor": "Napa Zin-led blend — Kirk Venge's Scout's Honor, blackberry, boysenberry, black pepper, vanilla, full body, generous. The vanilla plays with béchamel cream and the black pepper cuts garlic-dairy weight. Strong Zin-led for creamed spinach — full body handles the indulgent side without clashing.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Venge Silencieux": "Kirk Venge single-vineyard Napa — dark cherry, cassis, chocolate, floral violet, silky soft tannin. The silky tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the chocolate plays against the spinach base. Strong winemaker's Napa Cab for creamed spinach — Venge precision reframes the rich side.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Vesper": "Vesper's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift reads alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the bright build frames the creamed spinach, and the citrus cuts the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the apple notes lift the custard. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. The cocktail's layered herbal-spirit complexity sits alongside the side's dairy weight; Bénédictine's herbal note plays against creamed greens. Strong cocktail pour alongside the classic spinach preparation.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Villon Cognac": "Villon with creamed spinach — contemporary cognac against dairy-rich side. Strong; Villon's modern profile rides the cream.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Vin Santo on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Weller 107": "Weller 107 with creamed spinach — the pour's heat against the dairy's richness. More assertive than Weller Special Reserve; the 107-proof kick gives the side dimension without burying it. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year with creamed spinach — the 12-year's aged oak and wheated sweetness thread into the dairy's richness. More refined than Weller Special Reserve here; the oak gives the side a backbone the younger Wellers don't. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium with creamed spinach — Millennium-level depth meets a dairy side. The bourbon overreaches slightly for the side's register; strong but not the most deserving pour for the Millennium's scarcity. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve with creamed spinach — soft caramel-vanilla against dairy richness. Meets the side at its level; wheated honey threads into the cream without the oak depth of Weller 12. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Creamed Spinach|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with creamed spinach — aged 100-proof single-barrel against dairy. Strong; Vermont-finished character sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with creamed spinach — fruit-cask WP against dairy. Strong; wine-cask character sits with richness at premium complexity register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with creamed spinach — extended-aged WP flagship against dairy. Strong; Vermont-oak depth sits with richness at flagship register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with creamed spinach — barrel-proof WP against dairy. Strong; the unicorn WP sits with richness at allocated register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with creamed spinach — allocated 10th-edition WP against dairy. Strong; collector intensity at allocated register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year with creamed spinach — 100-proof WP against dairy. Strong; Vermont-brand rye sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with creamed spinach — craft high-rye against dairy richness. Strong; the oak spice distinct from wheated caramel takes.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with creamed spinach — small-batch fruit and floral against dairy. Strong; the non-wheated profile with fruit notes gives the side a different dimension than caramel-wheat does.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with creamed spinach — iconic Double-Oaked against dairy-rich side. Strong; Woodford Double Oaked's caramel-oak rides the cream at KY-benchmark register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with creamed spinach — 90.4-proof small-batch against dairy. Strong; the Woodford elegance sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with creamed spinach — Washington craft rye against dairy. Strong; PNW regional character sits with richness.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 with creamed spinach — Suntory flagship against dairy-rich side. Works; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak character rides the cream at flagship register.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 with creamed spinach — aged 18yr Yamazaki against dairy-rich side. Works; Yamazaki 18 is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Creamed Spinach|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Yellow Chartreuse on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamy Potato|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the caramel-spice register matches the potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamy Potato|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; light body doesn't overshoot.",
  "Creamy Potato|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's bold cherry-coffee cassis finds neutral with the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamy Potato|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals sits beside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamy Potato|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the Highland register carries the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Creamy Potato|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the malt-driven body wraps the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Creamy Potato|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the potato soup, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamy Potato|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the aged-agave weight wraps the potato soup, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; distinctive ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Creamy Potato|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight aligns with the soup's cream-potato richness and the apple-pear lifts the dairy base. Strong accessible Chardonnay for creamy potato — California oak on the classic.",
  "Creamy Potato|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis leans against the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis cuts the cheese richness. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamy Potato|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamy Potato|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Amaro Nonino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creamy Potato|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Creamy Potato|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Old Fitz 9 brings more depth here.",
  "Creamy Potato|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon body sits with the potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "Creamy Potato|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon backbone carries the potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Creamy Potato|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Creamy Potato|Asparagus": "The potato soup alongside the asparagus -- the bright cuts the rich cleanly. Works; the table can build them in order or together.",
  "Creamy Potato|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The potato soup and the au gratin -- both potato-forward, the table doubles up on potato. Avoid; pick one or the other. Pair the potato soup with Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis; pair the au gratin with Bone-In Filet, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Keenan Chardonnay.",
  "Creamy Potato|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamy Potato|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge fights the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- bitter herbs cut the bisque's round cream without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Averna Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creamy Potato|Aviation": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build sits alongside the potato soup's silky body — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Creamy Potato|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals stays alongside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamy Potato|Avion 44": "Avion 44's añejo-pour depth leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-aged tequila body threads the potato soup, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; clean character doesn't drive the pairing.",
  "Creamy Potato|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the unaged body brightens the potato soup, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bright cane-and-citrus weight carries the potato soup, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamy Potato|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Baileys Irish Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamy Potato|Baker's": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamy Potato|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the Highland register carries the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Creamy Potato|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the malt-and-oak threads the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Creamy Potato|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's First Fill American Oak finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the scotch depth settles into the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Creamy Potato|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood's aged Port-finished Speyside reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the malt-driven body wraps the potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; accessible gentle match.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon body sits with the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon backbone carries the potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; save the BTAC bottle for the protein course — the side doesn't deserve the pour.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; stout-barrel story wasted on salad.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon backbone carries the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; richer profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon weight handles the potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the 10-year depth gives the dessert substantial premium frame.",
  "Creamy Potato|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon body sits with the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for the side — save the rare-cask bottle for the protein course.",
  "Creamy Potato|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the caramel-spice register matches the potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamy Potato|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye's rye-spice character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the high-rye spice register matches the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; multi-distillery complexity doesn't drive.",
  "Creamy Potato|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the caramel-spice register matches the potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Creamy Potato|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon backbone carries the potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; milestone-collector wasted on salad.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bee's Knees": "Bee's Knees's crisp butter-citrus build plays cleanly against the potato soup's silky body — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamy Potato|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater Gin's London-Dry-juniper botanicals leans against the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; capable alongside. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamy Potato|Beignets": "Creamy Potato and the beignets -- rich cream gives way to sweet, the close lands generously. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Creamy Potato|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality stays alongside the potato soup's silky body — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamy Potato|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the caramel-spice register matches the potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Creamy Potato|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the caramel-spice register matches the potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamy Potato|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Creamy Potato|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's Straight from the Barrel's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "Creamy Potato|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "Creamy Potato|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon body sits with the potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Creamy Potato|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon backbone carries the potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat Gin's char-citrus botanicals reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the floral notes lift the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry Gin's crisp botanical-juniper botanicals leans against the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's crisp London-Dry-citrus botanicals sits beside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bone Marrow": "The bone marrow with the potato soup -- the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness meets the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the meal layers rich on rich, the table carries weight cleanly when the registers match. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bone-In Filet": "Bone-In Filet with the potato soup alongside -- cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Creamy Potato|Booker's": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon body sits with the potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Creamy Potato|Botanist Gin": "Botanist Gin's crisp Islay-gentle-botanical botanicals sits alongside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace Old Fashioned — bourbon vanilla-caramel with cherries, orange, bitters — against a cream-based potato soup. Spirit-weight whiskey is the wrong axis for a silky dairy starter. Never layer. Route the cocktail to fatted steaks or marrow; take creamy potato with Chardonnay, Pinot Blanc, or Chablis.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak depth lays under the potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the match is gentle and approachable.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; the toast notes don't really interplay with the soup but don't clash.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bowmore 12": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the malt-and-oak threads the potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive but adds interest.",
  "Creamy Potato|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the potato soup, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creamy Potato|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamy Potato|Broccolini": "The potato soup and the broccolini -- acid lifts the richness, the meal gets the contrast it needs. Works; sequence them or share the table, the call holds either way.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich's progressive unpeated Islay sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the scotch depth settles into the potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; extended-aged home-state pour wasted on salad.",
  "Creamy Potato|Brussels and Belly": "The potato soup alongside Brussels + Belly -- cream and protein share the meal, the build reads full. Works; the table can build them in order or together.",
  "Creamy Potato|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon body sits with the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; AE Triple Cask's layered character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creamy Potato|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Creamy Potato|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the unique maturation story adds interest to a gentle dessert match.",
  "Creamy Potato|Burrata": "The burrata preceded by the potato soup -- delicate gives way to rich, the table earns its weight. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "Creamy Potato|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cade Cabernet Sauvignon's Howell Mountain Cab character clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Cade Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamy Potato|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila's cooked-agave-and-oak register leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-aged tequila body threads the potato soup, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creamy Potato|Campari": "Campari's aperitivo at its most textbook clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Campari for the steak course.",
  "Creamy Potato|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the easygoing Canadian character handles the potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creamy Potato|Canadian Club 43 Year": "Canadian Club 43 Year's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the Canadian whisky weight matches the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the richer pour finds more with the dessert than Original brings.",
  "Creamy Potato|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the light spirit doesn't add to the soup-salad course — neutral but wrong. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Captain Morgan Rum lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creamy Potato|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the smooth Canadian register carries the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; richer profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Creamy Potato|Carrot Cake": "Creamy Potato and the carrot cake -- cream-and-sugar build the close, the meal finishes generous. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Creamy Potato|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Casa LBV Port on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamy Potato|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- mezcal smoke fights the soup's cream — smoke has no bridge into dairy delicacy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a mezcal. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Casamigos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Creamy Potato|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab against a cream-and-potato soup — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the silky dairy base. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take creamy potato with Chardonnay, Pinot Blanc, or Chablis.",
  "Creamy Potato|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Creamy Potato|Cheesecake": "Creamy Potato and the cheesecake -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Creamy Potato|Chilean Seabass": "Chilean Seabass after the potato soup -- the silky leek-and-potato cream opens with quiet richness, the seabass following with buttery pan-finished flesh that the cream complements on the same plane. Strong; cream into butter reads indulgent.",
  "Creamy Potato|Chocolate Brownie": "Creamy Potato and the brownie -- rich cream gives way to sweet, the close lands generously. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Creamy Potato|Chocolate Cake": "The potato soup alongside the chocolate cake -- cream-and-sugar build the close, the meal finishes generous. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Creamy Potato|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin Vodka's unflavored crystalline pour sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the unflavored body sits with the potato soup, and the crisp profile carries the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamy Potato|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Bros Brandy's Limousin-oak cognac register leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the brandy depth wraps the potato soup, and the dried-fruit note underlines the bowl. Works; Rémy's elegance handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Creamy Potato|Château Batailley": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Château Batailley on the steak course instead.",
  "Creamy Potato|Château Beaucastel": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Creamy Potato|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Château Gombaude-Guillot on the steak course instead.",
