  "Creamy Potato|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality leans against the potato soup's silky body — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creamy Potato|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the dry rye body wraps the potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creamy Potato|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the high-rye spice register matches the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for a side — save the bottle for the main course.",
  "Creamy Potato|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rye-and-oak weight handles the potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; stout-barrel story wasted on salad.",
  "Creamy Potato|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Creamy Potato|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the high-rye spice register matches the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; wine-cask over-specified.",
  "Creamy Potato|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rye backbone drives the potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Creamy Potato|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Creamy Potato|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the oak frames the cream. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for this dish's register.",
  "Creamy Potato|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the pairing is pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Creamy Potato|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rye backbone drives the potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; pour slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Creamy Potato|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rye spice cuts the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creamy Potato|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Creamy Potato|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the restrained whisky body threads the potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "Creamy Potato|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Yellow Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Creme Brulee|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the brulee, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Creme Brulee|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the 1881 Napa Valley for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. 2 James Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch backbone matches the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; regional craft at matched register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with creme brulee — double-matured 16yr Speyside against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; Aberlour 16's balance supports the brulee; Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-caramel is the featured brulee match.",
  "Creme Brulee|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- añejo weight and custard tang refuse each other. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Adictivo Cristalino on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's rested-agave body finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the añejo register carries the brulee, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creme Brulee|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Alexander Valley Chardonnay on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Alexander Valley Cyrus for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Alexander Valley Homestead Red for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino with creme brulee — grappa-based amaro against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. The orange peel and botanical complexity echo the brulee's caramel; refined digestif pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Ancho Reyes": "Creme Brulee: caramelized custard meets Ancho Reyes. Excellent; the smoky-sweet-chile character plays with burnt-sugar-cream at Mexican-liqueur register — the chile-dessert classic.",
  "Creme Brulee|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with creme brulee — port-finished Louisville bourbon against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; AE's port-fruit finds the caramel cleanly at wine-finish register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with creme brulee — cask-strength port-finish against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; AE CS's port-fruit finds the caramel at premium register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Creme Brulee: vanilla custard and caramelized sugar meet triple-cask-finished AE. Strong; AE Triple Cask's multi-cask layers find the caramel with distinctive complexity.",
  "Creme Brulee|Aperol": "Aperol with creme brulee — soft aperitivo against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; the soft orange finds the caramel at a lower intensity than Campari brings.",
  "Creme Brulee|Asparagus": "Asparagus and the brulee -- bright meets sweet, the contrast stays light. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Creme Brulee|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The au gratin and the brulee -- the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust hands off cleanly to the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: cream-and-sugar build the close, the meal finishes generous. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Creme Brulee|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Averna Amaro": "Averna with creme brulee — Sicilian caramel-orange amaro against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. The amaro's caramel notes echo the brulee's burnt sugar; classic pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Aviation": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Aviation for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Aviation Gin for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Avion 44": "Avion 44 with creme brulee — 44-month aged Avion against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; aged Avion's maturation depth mirrors the custard's richness.",
  "Creme Brulee|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the spirit reads thin against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Avion Silver lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the light-rum body cuts the brulee, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the dessert. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream: the original cream liqueur (Ireland, 1974), Irish whiskey + Irish cream base, 17% ABV, vanilla-cocoa-cream profile. Excellent on creme brulee; the classic-cream-liqueur character threads the burnt-sugar custard at dessert-time register — the most universally-recognized cream liqueur dessert pairing.",
  "Creme Brulee|Baker's": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the 10-year depth gives the dessert substantial premium frame.",
  "Creme Brulee|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creme Brulee|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with creme brulee — rum-cask-finished Speyside against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Excellent; caramel-meets-caramel is the textbook match — Caribbean Cask's rum-cask-derived caramel is the featured Sherried Speyside-brulee pairing.",
  "Creme Brulee|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the Highland register carries the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; gentle match.",
  "Creme Brulee|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood with creme brulee — 21yr port-finished Speyside against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Works; Portwood handles the brulee but Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-caramel is the featured brulee match.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with creme brulee — Bardstown Bourbon Co. French-wine finish against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; Bardstown Château Laboude's French-wine-fruit finds the caramel at contemporary-craft register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; friendly accessible match.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon weight handles the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; complexity wasted on salad.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon weight handles the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; a composed but not remarkable match.",
  "Creme Brulee|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon backbone carries the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; flagship depth gives the dessert premium aged character.",
  "Creme Brulee|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; smooth character is slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Creme Brulee|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye's rye-spice character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye spice cuts the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with creme brulee — BH high-rye red-wine finish against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; BH Red Wine Cask's red-wine-fruit + rye-spice meets the caramel at distinctive register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the caramel-spice register matches the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bee's Knees": "Bee's Knees' citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Bee's Knees for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Beefeater Gin for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Beignets": "The beignets alongside the brulee -- the fried choux holds against the vanilla-and-cream brulee. Works; the dessert split holds at neutral register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Belvedere Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon backbone carries the brulee, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the distinctive character is underused by the salad's simplicity.",
  "Creme Brulee|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon backbone carries the brulee, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the pour's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Creme Brulee|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the caramel-spice register matches the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive.",
  "Creme Brulee|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's Straight from the Barrel's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; bonded weight slightly over-specified.",
  "Creme Brulee|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the caramel-spice register matches the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; Boss Hog's allocation story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Creme Brulee|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon weight handles the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; milestone-collector wasted on salad.",
  "Creme Brulee|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the intensity overpowers this dish.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Bluecoat Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Bodega Noemia A Lisa on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Bombay Dry Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Bombay Sapphire Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow and the brulee -- savory gives way to sweet, the close lands cleanly. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bone-In Filet": "After the bone-in filet, the brulee -- the marrow-edged finish gives way to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the elevated cut into the steakhouse classic.",
  "Creme Brulee|Booker's": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon backbone carries the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the spicy-sweet character gives the dessert a Barton frame.",
  "Creme Brulee|Botanist Gin": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Botanist Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, Angostura over a large cube. Bourbon weight fights vanilla custard's delicacy; the cocktail belongs with marrow or Tomahawk. Route guest to Espresso Martini or Baileys for a dessert-appropriate cocktail. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon weight handles the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; light body doesn't overshoot.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bowmore 12": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch backbone matches the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; bold intensity slightly over-specified.",
  "Creme Brulee|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Braida Montebruna Barbera for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Broccoli Cheddar": "The broccoli cheddar alongside the brulee -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Creme Brulee|Broccolini": "Broccolini and the brulee -- acid plays against sweetness, the close stays unburdened. Works; the meal closes cleanly from the side.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich's progressive unpeated Islay leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the malt-driven body wraps the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creme Brulee|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels + Belly alongside the brulee -- the savory course resolves into sugar, the meal closes with contrast. Works; the contrast holds, neither course overshadows the other.",
  "Creme Brulee|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon weight handles the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Creme Brulee|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream: bourbon-base cream liqueur from Buffalo Trace Distillery (Kentucky), vanilla-caramel-cream with bourbon depth. Excellent on creme brulee; the bourbon-caramel-cream character amplifies burnt-sugar-custard at bourbon-cream register — richer register than Baileys' Irish-whiskey base.",
  "Creme Brulee|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon weight handles the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "Creme Brulee|Burrata": "The burrata and the brulee -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy bookends with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Creme Brulee|Butternut Squash": "The butternut squash alongside the brulee -- both desserts pull the same lever, the table doubles the sugar. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Creme Brulee|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cade Cabernet Sauvignon's Howell Mountain Cab character jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Cade Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the aged-agave weight wraps the brulee, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; the wine-cask character adds dimension but not standout.",
  "Creme Brulee|Campari": "Campari with creme brulee — bitter orange-rhubarb against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; the bitter-orange finds the caramel with aperitivo character.",
  "Creme Brulee|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the Canadian whisky weight matches the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; polished 10-year gives the dessert small-batch premium character.",
  "Creme Brulee|Canadian Club 43 Year": "Canadian Club 43 Year's Canadian whisky character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the Canadian whisky weight matches the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; annual-release wasted on salad.",
  "Creme Brulee|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's green-agave-and-cane lift rests at register with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the cane-and-citrus body lifts the brulee, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the dessert. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Creme Brulee|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the blended whisky body wraps the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|Casa LBV Port": "Late Bottled Vintage Port — Casa LBV, 4-6 year cask, dark cherry, plum, smooth moderate structure, entry-port approachable. The plum plays with torched-sugar crust caramelization and the smooth structure won't overwhelm vanilla-custard delicacy. Peak LBV for creme brulee — approachable port on the classic match. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Creme Brulee|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the wood-fire body meets the brulee, and the wood-smoke note underlines the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creme Brulee|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus — cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. The Cab's structure destroys vanilla custard; tannin and cream don't meet on any axis. Peak Cab belongs with the steak course, not the crème brûlée. Route guest to Moscato, Vin Santo, or Sauternes. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|Chambord": "Chambord: this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness meets this delicate custard's creamy, vanilla sweetness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Creme Brulee|Chilean Seabass": "After the seabass, the brulee -- the miso-touched butter-rich flesh yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the silky main meets the silky finish, the meal arc reads clean.",
  "Creme Brulee|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- clean vodka has nothing to add against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Chopin Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with creme brulee — sweeter American brandy against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Excellent; Christian Bros rides the caramel sweetness at accessible register — where Rémy's elegance edges premium-pairing quality.",
  "Creme Brulee|Château Batailley": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Château Beaucastel": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Château Gombaude-Guillot on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Château Haut Segottes": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Château de Rouillac": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Château de Rouillac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Clam Chowder": "Clam Chowder and the brulee -- rich cream gives way to sweet, the close lands generously. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Creme Brulee|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the barrel-rested body handles the brulee, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango with creme brulee — ceremonial extra añejo against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; Durango's aged depth mirrors the custard, ceremonial presentation elevates.",
  "Creme Brulee|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold with creme brulee — golden ceremonial blend against vanilla custard. Strong; the blend's complexity plus ceremonial presentation elevate the dessert.",
  "Creme Brulee|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's wood-fire mezcal body sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the smoky agave register handles the brulee, and the mineral smoke plays against the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata's silky Jalisco blanco with ceramic-decanter presentation falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light spirit. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Clase Azul Plata belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado's ceramic-icon reposado character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rested-agave depth sits with the brulee, and the soft-spice note softens the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creme Brulee|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra with creme brulee — 5-year top-tier Clase Azul against vanilla custard. Strong; the Ultra's aged depth mirrors the custard's richness at the absolute ceremonial register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Clos Petit Ona Grenache lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the aged-agave weight wraps the brulee, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; iconic extra añejo is composed with the dessert.",
  "Creme Brulee|Contratto Vermouth": "Creme Brulee: caramelized custard meets Contratto Vermouth. Excellent; the wine-based aromatized character plays burnt-sugar-cream at 16% ABV aperitivo register — softer dessert pairing than Chartreuse or absinthe.",
  "Creme Brulee|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light spirit. Avoid; one of those is the move. Corazon Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado's rested-agave body leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the aged-agave weight wraps the brulee, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; accessible aged composed with dessert.",
  "Creme Brulee|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the aged-agave weight wraps the brulee, and the soft-spice note softens the dessert. Works; accessible aged composed with dessert.",
  "Creme Brulee|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak-aged tequila body threads the brulee, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; the bold pour cuts the glaze but is slightly much for the small plate.",
  "Creme Brulee|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the barrel-rested body handles the brulee, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creme Brulee|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Corazón del Sol Malbec lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Corpse Reviver": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Corpse Reviver for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier with creme brulee — traditional Jarnac cognac against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; Courvoisier's drier finish contrasts the caramel where Rémy VSOP's elegance harmonizes.",
  "Creme Brulee|Cowboy Ribeye": "After the cowboy ribeye, the brulee -- the rendered cap-fat yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries brightening the close. Strong; the heavy steak finds relief in the silky finish.",
  "Creme Brulee|Crab Cake": "Crab Cake and the brulee -- the lighter course hands off to dessert, the meal closes clean. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Creme Brulee|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch depth settles into the brulee, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; AE Triple Cask's layered character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach and the brulee -- rich cream gives way to sweet, the close lands generously. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Creme Brulee|Creamy Potato": "Creamy Potato and the brulee -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Creme Brulee|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the easygoing Canadian character handles the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the toast notes don't really interplay with the soup but don't clash.",
  "Creme Brulee|Cucumber Gimlet": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- cocktail citrus and custard tang cancel each other — both reach for acid. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Cucumber Gimlet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the Highland register carries the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the pour's refinement is underused by the salad.",
  "Creme Brulee|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's Loire Valley Pinot Noir jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Darioush Cabernet Franc on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin's Detroit urban-craft gin jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Detroit City Gin for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City Vodka's hometown craft vodka mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- clean vodka has nothing to add against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Detroit City Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the malt-and-oak threads the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Creme Brulee|Disaronno": "Disaronno: Italian amaretto (bitter-almond/apricot-pit flavor) from Saronno, 28% ABV, iconic square bottle. Excellent on creme brulee; the vanilla-marzipan-apricot-pit character amplifies burnt-sugar-custard at amaretto-classic register — almond-on-caramel, with more punch than Frangelico.",
  "Creme Brulee|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's heavy aged-spirit weight finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rich pour wraps the brulee, and the heavy-pour register grips the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Creme Brulee|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Domaine Serene Grand Cheval's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Domaine Serene Grand Cheval for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Domaine Tempier Bandol's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Domaine Tempier Bandol on the steak course instead.",
