  "Creme Brulee|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Faust Napa Valley Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca with creme brulee — saffron-menthol against vanilla custard. Works; Branca's power demotes this vs the sweeter classic amari, but the caramel-bitter bridge still functions.",
  "Creme Brulee|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta with creme brulee — mint bitter against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; the mint lift reads cleanly through the caramel.",
  "Creme Brulee|Filet Mignon": "After the filet, the brulee -- the lean restraint yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the steakhouse classic close on the lighter cut.",
  "Creme Brulee|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the liqueur compounds the dessert's sweetness without contrast. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Fireball Whisky belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Fisher Unity Pinot Noir lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the caramel-spice register matches the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; Prohibition narrative wasted on salad.",
  "Creme Brulee|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the caramel-spice register matches the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the premium pour is wasted on the salad's simplicity.",
  "Creme Brulee|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Creme Brulee: vanilla custard and caramelized sugar meet Detroit-brand Double Oaked. Fox & Oden's second-char caramel amplifies the brulee caramel — the featured Double-Oaked-brulee match at distinctive register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Creme Brulee|Frangelico": "Frangelico: Italian hazelnut liqueur from Piedmont (the hazelnut heartland), 20% ABV, monk-shaped bottle nodding to its Franciscan friar origins. Excellent on creme brulee; the hazelnut-vanilla-cocoa profile threads burnt-sugar-custard at iconic-Italian-digestif register — hazelnut-on-caramel classic.",
  "Creme Brulee|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Freemark Abbey Bosché for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|French 75": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. French 75 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|French Onion": "French Onion and the brulee -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Creme Brulee|Frias Block 5": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Frias Block 5 on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Piedmont Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee, gentle effervescence, honeyed finish. The peach and honey echo the crème brûlée's vanilla custard, and the bubbles cut through the cream weight. Peak dessert-sparkling pairing for the most approachable dessert on the menu.",
  "Creme Brulee|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. G4 Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rested-agave depth sits with the brulee, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creme Brulee|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the approachable Irish character carries the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the toast notes don't really interplay with the soup but don't clash.",
  "Creme Brulee|George T. Stagg": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the distinctive character is underused by the salad's simplicity.",
  "Creme Brulee|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12's entry Speyside benchmark reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the malt-driven body wraps the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; AE Triple Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14's Highland-scotch character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; premium proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Creme Brulee|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18's aged Speyside with sherry finish finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch backbone matches the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; oak slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Creme Brulee|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch backbone matches the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the aging is wasted on the salad.",
  "Creme Brulee|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; light body doesn't overshoot.",
  "Creme Brulee|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the malt-and-oak threads the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the boldness pushes slightly past what the soup asks for.",
  "Creme Brulee|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the malt-and-oak threads the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the richer pour finds more with the dessert than Original brings.",
  "Creme Brulee|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the malt-and-oak threads the brulee, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Creme Brulee|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "10-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's entry-point, dried apricot, walnut, toffee, gentle spice, smooth finish. The toffee echoes the torched-sugar crust directly and the walnut plays with vanilla-custard depth. Peak Tawny for creme brulee — caramelized-on-caramelized textbook match. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Creme Brulee|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "20-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's step-up, concentrated dried fruit, roasted hazelnut, orange peel, silky long finish. The roasted hazelnut echoes torched-sugar crust caramelization and the orange peel lifts vanilla-custard. Peak step-up Tawny for creme brulee — twenty years of cask evolution on the classic match. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Creme Brulee|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "2017 declared-vintage Port — Graham's bottle-aged, dense inky, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violet, powerful structured. The blackcurrant plays with torched-sugar crust, but the dense inky structure reads heavy against vanilla-custard delicacy. Strong Vintage Port for creme brulee — Tawny's toffee fits the caramel harder.",
  "Creme Brulee|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum's green-agave-and-cane lift reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the blanco-tequila body matches the brulee, and the bright blanco register underlines the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Creme Brulee|Grand Marnier": "Creme Brulee: this delicate custard's creamy, vanilla sweetness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Creme Brulee|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Gray Whale Gin for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Green Chartreuse": "Creme Brulee: caramelized custard meets Green Chartreuse. Excellent; the herbal-complex digestif contrasts burnt-sugar-cream with bracing 55% clarity — one of the greatest dessert-pairings on the menu.",
  "Creme Brulee|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Grey Goose Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar and the brulee -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Creme Brulee|Gumbo": "Gumbo and the brulee -- different registers compose without clash. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Creme Brulee|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the HDW 100 Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 Year's lightly-peated Hakushu character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the lightly-peated Hakushu meets the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; boutique character gives the dessert a craft frame.",
  "Creme Brulee|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 Vodka's crystalline neutrality mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- clean vodka has nothing to add against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Hangar 1 Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Head Fake": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creme Brulee|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creme Brulee|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Hendricks Flora Adora's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Hendricks Flora Adora on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Hendricks Gin for the steak course.",
