  "Creme Brulee|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy with creme brulee — default-call cognac against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; Hennessy's dried-fruit-vanilla finds the caramel cleanly; Rémy VSOP's elegance still edges this pairing.",
  "Creme Brulee|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Vermont-brand character doesn't drive.",
  "Creme Brulee|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Japanese Harmony's blended Japanese harmony finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "Creme Brulee|Honey Roasted Carrots": "The honey carrots alongside the brulee -- two sweet closes, the meal piles sugar without contrast. Works; the contrast holds, neither course overshadows the other.",
  "Creme Brulee|House Wedge": "The wedge alongside the brulee -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Creme Brulee|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the caramel-spice register matches the brulee, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; bold character slightly over-specified for the soup.",
  "Creme Brulee|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's bright sparkling effervescence jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Il Colle Prosecco Superiore on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Inhibited": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso in a chocolate-drizzled martini glass. Coffee-and-cream cocktail alongside vanilla custard — rich stacked on rich, but the espresso bitterness keeps it from cloying. Dessert-course-as-cocktail paired with actual dessert. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Creme Brulee|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Inverroche Gin for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Iron Fish Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; AE Triple Cask's layered character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish with creme brulee — Sazerac-owned port-finish against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; Isaac Bowman's port-fruit finds the caramel at Virginia-heritage register.",
  "Creme Brulee|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards' silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. J Davies Ferrington Vineyards belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon's Diamond Mountain Cab character pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon backbone carries the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Creme Brulee|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon weight handles the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Portwood is over-specified for a side — save for main course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; grain notes don't really engage the soup but don't clash.",
