  "Creme Brulee|Johnnie Walker Black": "Johnnie Walker Black's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch backbone matches the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|Johnnie Walker Blue": "Johnnie Walker Blue's Highland-scotch character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch depth settles into the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for the side — save the rare-cask bottle for the protein course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Johnnie Walker Red": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Creme Brulee: this delicate custard's creamy, vanilla sweetness meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. Lighter red against dessert — fruit meets sugar without enough weight to carry. Works for a fruit-led dessert.",
  "Creme Brulee|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Joseph Mellot Sancerre on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Jubilation by Colgin for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's deep-bodied spirit register reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the high-proof register matches the brulee, and the layered body brightens the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creme Brulee|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's high-proof pour register leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the layered spirit body sits with the brulee, and the layered body brightens the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Creme Brulee|Kahlua": "Kahlua's herbal-and-sugar liqueur register reads register-aligned with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the sweet-liqueur register carries the brulee, and the honey threads the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Creme Brulee|Kansas City": "After the KC, the brulee -- the lean-bold strip yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creme Brulee|Keenan Chardonnay": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Keenan Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Keenan Mernet": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Keenan Mernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye-and-oak weight handles the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the distinctive character is underused by the salad's simplicity.",
  "Creme Brulee|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the pour's brightness is a gentle counterpoint to the dessert's richness.",
  "Creme Brulee|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the gentle Irish body threads the brulee, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; accessible gentle match.",
  "Creme Brulee|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Ketel One Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Knickerbocker Gin for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the bourbon-and-side match holds — neither soars nor fights.",
  "Creme Brulee|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 Year's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "Creme Brulee|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Creme Brulee|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye-and-oak weight handles the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Creme Brulee|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the high-rye spice register matches the brulee, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the private select's layered profile underserves the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Creme Brulee|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva's aged-agave character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the añejo register carries the brulee, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creme Brulee|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo's aged-agave character jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- añejo weight and custard tang refuse each other. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Komos Crystallino Anejo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Añejo with creme brulee — aged Komos against vanilla custard. Strong; extra añejo depth mirrors the custard's richness with Riedel presentation.",
  "Creme Brulee|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado's rested-agave body leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rested-agave depth sits with the brulee, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; luxury ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Creme Brulee|Lagavulin 8": "Lagavulin 8's younger-Islay peat character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the malt-and-oak threads the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; Discovery complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Creme Brulee|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Lail Vineyards Blueprint's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Lail Vineyards Blueprint on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the spirit reads thin against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light spirit. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Lalo Silver lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Laphroaig 10": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch backbone matches the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive but adds interest.",
  "Creme Brulee|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a sparkling. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Champagne — clean acid, bubbles, elegant body. Not the peak dessert pour, but the acid cuts cream well and the bubbles handle the torched sugar. Works when the guest wants to continue with Champagne rather than transition to dessert wine.",
  "Creme Brulee|Le Garenne Rosé": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Le Garenne Rosé lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Le Ragnaie Brunello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Le Volte dell'Ornellaia on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Lemon Lavender Gin Martini on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Les Pallières Raciné": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Les Pallières Raciné for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Liberator Gin": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Liberator Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Licor 43": "Creme Brulee: this delicate custard's creamy, vanilla sweetness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Creme Brulee|Limoncello": "Creme Brulee: this delicate custard's creamy, vanilla sweetness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Creme Brulee|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir's Willamette layered Pinot character jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Little Book": "Little Book's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; AE Triple Cask's layered character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Loaded Potato": "The loaded potato and the brulee -- the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup bookends with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Creme Brulee|Lobster Mac": "The lobster mac and the brulee -- the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence hands off cleanly to the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: rich dairy yields to dessert weight, the close composes thickly. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Creme Brulee|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch backbone matches the brulee, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Creme Brulee|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye backbone drives the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for the side — save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Long Road Gin": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Long Road Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rustic mezcal weight sits with the brulee, and the green-agave edge brightens the dessert. Works; the two desserts share the close without crowding.",
  "Creme Brulee|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII with creme brulee — 40-100 year cognac against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Works; brulee is a Rémy VSOP-level pairing — Louis XIII's complexity is wasted on caramel-sugar the Fine Champagne-level sibling handles perfectly. Save the bottle.",
  "Creme Brulee|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano with creme brulee — floral-anise amaro against vanilla custard. The anise notes thread through the brulee's vanilla distinctively. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Creme Brulee|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Lunaria Coste di Moro for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry with creme brulee — Oloroso-cask Speyside against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; Macallan 12's sherry-derived spice finds the caramel cleanly.",
  "Creme Brulee|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18 with creme brulee — premium aged 18yr Speyside against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; Macallan 18's aged sherry-derived spice finds the caramel at collector register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Macallan Estate": "Creme Brulee: vanilla custard and caramelized sugar meet distinctive Estate-oak Macallan. Strong; Macallan Estate's terroir-distinctive depth finds the caramel at premium register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Macauley Petite Syrah for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Macauley Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Mahon Gin": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Mahon Gin on the steak course instead.",
