  "Creme Brulee|Market Fish": "After the market fish, the brulee -- the kitchen pick yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the lighter main meets the silky-classic finish, the meal arc lands clean.",
  "Creme Brulee|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Creme Brulee: caramelized custard meets Mata Hari Absinthe. Excellent; wormwood-anise-fennel character cuts burnt-sugar-cream at 60% register — sharpest of the four herbal-dessert pairings.",
  "Creme Brulee|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Year Bourbon's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the caramel-spice register matches the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; Voyage 23 frames the cut at ocean-finish register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye backbone drives the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; EHT Full Proof is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Creme Brulee|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Mijenta Blanco belongs on the steak course.",
