  "Creme Brulee|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year's Heaven Hill's wheated heritage bond sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive.",
  "Creme Brulee|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th handles the richness at annual-release register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; boutique-craft doesn't drive.",
  "Creme Brulee|Opus One": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet. The dark fruit plays against creme-brulee's vanilla-custard cream and the mocha frames the caramelized crust. Works as collector Napa Cab blend for creme brulee — prestige red-with-dessert for guest wanting red continuation.",
  "Creme Brulee|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Pablo Sour": "Pablo Sour's citrus-lemon build matches the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Creme Brulee|Paloma": "Paloma's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Paloma for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye's allocated-icon Pappy rye character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the high-rye spice register matches the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; craft-blend over-specified.",
  "Creme Brulee|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; IW Harper 15 handles dessert density at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rested-agave depth sits with the brulee, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; clean and composed pairing.",
  "Creme Brulee|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver's polished silver tequila with agave-forward lift mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the spirit reads thin against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Patron Silver belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet coffee-tequila-base depth holds steady against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the coffee threads the cream. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Creme Brulee|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a sparkling. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand with creme brulee — unblended vintage-style cognac against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Excellent; Ferrand's floral-honey finds the caramel with artisanal elegance — alongside Rémy VSOP's Fine Champagne approach in the featured-pairing register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye-and-oak weight handles the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the toast adds a warmth the dessert can accept.",
  "Creme Brulee|Pineau des Charentes": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac finish, aperitif-or-dessert. The honey wraps the torched-sugar crust directly and the warm nutty Cognac plays with vanilla-custard. Peak Pineau for creme brulee — honey-on-caramel match. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Creme Brulee|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Plymouth Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Pommery Cuvée Louise for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Porterhouse": "After the Porterhouse, the brulee -- the dual strip-and-tender yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the table-share steak meets the steakhouse classic close.",
  "Creme Brulee|Prime Tartare": "The tartare alongside the brulee -- sweet contrast lifts the savory. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Creme Brulee|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|RAEN Royal St Robert": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the RAEN Royal St Robert on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Rainbow Trout": "After the trout, the brulee -- the lemon-bright delicate flesh yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the lighter main earns the silky-classic finish at register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Spanish rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit character, clean acid. Bubbles and fruit cut through vanilla custard cleanly; the rosé lifts the dessert lighter than a Champagne would. Fun non-obvious sparkling finisher when the guest wants to keep it dry-ish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creme Brulee|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the pour's delicacy doesn't overpower the soup, but doesn't particularly elevate it either.",
  "Creme Brulee|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the brulee, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; smooth character is slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Creme Brulee|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; wine-cask complexity gives the dessert layered premium character.",
  "Creme Brulee|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Rémy VSOP with creme brulee — Fine Champagne elegance against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Excellent; Rémy's refined profile is the featured cognac-brulee match — floral notes find the caramel with grace Courvoisier can't replicate.",
  "Creme Brulee|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo with creme brulee — sun-bottle aged against vanilla custard. Strong; distinctive-packaging aged depth mirrors the custard with ceremonial presentation.",
  "Creme Brulee|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka Vodka's crystalline neutrality mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- clean vodka has nothing to add against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reyka Vodka lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Roast Half Chicken": "After the chicken, the brulee -- the jus-finished bird yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the steakhouse-classic close on the lighter main.",
  "Creme Brulee|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The chickpea soup alongside the brulee -- separate registers share the plate, the contrast settles the build. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Creme Brulee|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs' bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a sparkling. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Roku Gin": "Roku Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Roku Gin for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum -- Solera 23, Guatemalan, honey-vanilla-caramel. The Solera honey wraps the brulee, and the caramel rounds the dessert. Excellent; the herbal-complex digestif contrasts burnt-sugar-cream with bracing 55% clarity — one of the greatest dessert-pairings on the menu. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon backbone carries the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; stout-barrel story wasted on salad.",
  "Creme Brulee|Salt-Cured Halibut": "After the halibut, the brulee -- the cured firm flesh yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the salt-cured depth meets the silky-classic finish at register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Sambuca": "Creme Brulee: caramelized custard meets Sambuca. Excellent; the sweet star-anise-elderflower profile meets burnt-sugar-cream at Italian-digestif register — the sweeter anise pairing on dessert.",
  "Creme Brulee|San Simeon Stormwatch": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the San Simeon Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The garlic spinach and the brulee -- the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens hands off cleanly to the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bright register lifts the sweet, the meal closes clean. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Creme Brulee|Sauternes Glass": "Bordeaux botrytis wine — Sémillon and Sauvignon Blanc with noble rot. Apricot, honey, orange marmalade, bright acid keeping it from cloying. The honey-apricot notes align with vanilla custard; the acid cuts the cream. One of the great pairings on the list. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Creme Brulee|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The anise-rye profile fights vanilla custard; the minimal-build cocktail isn't built for dessert. Works if guest carries the Sazerac through the finish; Moscato and Vin Santo peak far higher on brûlée.",
  "Creme Brulee|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the dry rye body wraps the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creme Brulee|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the toast adds a warmth the dessert can accept.",
  "Creme Brulee|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. Dry red Barolo against crème brûlée's custard-caramelized-sugar — high acid handles the cream but firm tannin clashes with the vanilla sweetness. Works as unusual bridge; a dessert wine peaks higher.",
  "Creme Brulee|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the dry white underclubs the dessert sugar, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Seafood Tower": "Seafood Tower and the brulee -- acid and sugar share the table, neither course pulls heavy. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Creme Brulee|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops and the brulee -- protein hands off to dessert, the close builds without conflict. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Creme Brulee|Seasonal Soup": "The seasonal soup alongside the brulee -- a quiet call, the meal carries without strain. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Creme Brulee|Seasonal Vegetables": "The seasonal vegetables and the brulee -- the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate hands off cleanly to the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Creme Brulee|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Shafer 1.5": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Shafer 1.5 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Shafer Hillside Select": "Shafer Hillside Select's Stags Leap allocated Cab power flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Shafer Hillside Select lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Shrimp Bisque": "Shrimp Bisque and the brulee -- the cream rounds into the sweet close, the meal lands rich and full. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Creme Brulee|Shrimp Cocktail": "The shrimp cocktail alongside the brulee -- acid and sugar share the table, neither course pulls heavy. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Creme Brulee|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the spirit reads thin against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Siete Leguas Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the brulee, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Alexander Valley Cab — American-oak vanilla, coconut, dill, ripe cassis. The lush body can handle crème brûlée's custard-and-burnt-sugar, though Cab-with-custard stretches the pairing axis. Works as Silver Oak for crème brûlée — American-oak signature, though dessert wines peak higher for the custard.",
  "Creme Brulee|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Simon Family Estate Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Simon Family Estate Reserve on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "Spottswoode Lyndenhurst's Napa estate Cab with structured cassis jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Spottswoode Lyndenhurst on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the St Supéry Élu Meritage for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creme Brulee|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Stoli Elit Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp NZ Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Swordfish": "After the swordfish, the brulee -- the meaty grilled fish yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the lemon-bright main and the silky finish read clean across the courses.",
  "Creme Brulee|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Tanq 10 Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Tanqueray Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Classic Douro Tawny — Taylor Fladgate (founded 1692), smooth balanced, dried fruit, almond, warm clean finish. The almond echoes the torched-sugar crust's toast and the dried fruit plays with vanilla-custard. Peak heritage-Tawny for creme brulee — 1692 lineage on the caramelized classic. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Creme Brulee|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Flagship Vintage Port — Taylor Fladgate bottle, extraordinarily rich, dark fruit, chocolate, tobacco, minutes-long evolving finish. The dark fruit plays with torched-sugar crust, but extraordinarily rich structure overshadows vanilla-custard delicacy. Strong flagship-Vintage for creme brulee — Tawny's toffee fits the caramel harder.",
  "Creme Brulee|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with creme brulee — multi-cask aged tequila against vanilla custard. Strong; triple-cask depth (especially sherry and cognac notes) mirrors the custard with distinctive multi-cask character.",
  "Creme Brulee|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|The BG": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|The Happy Wife": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build holds with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|The Manhattan": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the oak frames the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|The Prisoner Red Blend": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour The Prisoner Red Blend on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|The Tomahawk": "After the Tomahawk, the brulee -- the 36oz showpiece yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries brightening the close. Strong; the theatrical steak earns the steakhouse classic close.",
  "Creme Brulee|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye spice cuts the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; curated single-barrel slightly over-specified.",
  "Creme Brulee|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with creme brulee — Barton Distilling chardonnay-cask against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Excellent; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-butter + caramel is the featured wine-finished-brulee pairing — the chardonnay-cask apple + vanilla resonance reads distinctively.",
  "Creme Brulee|Titos Vodka": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- clean vodka has nothing to add against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Titos Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the entry Suntory body sits with the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Tomato Basil": "Tomato Basil and the brulee -- the bright register lifts the sweet, the meal closes clean. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Creme Brulee|Transfusion": "Transfusion's crisp citrus-ginger build stays alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creme Brulee|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the añejo register carries the brulee, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; 1942 aged depth is a touch over-specified for the small plate.",
  "Creme Brulee|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries and the brulee -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Creme Brulee|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s gentle Irish blend leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the Irish whiskey body sits with the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th handles the richness at annual-release register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "After the tuna, the brulee -- the seared-rare flesh hands off to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the clean ocean main meets the silky-classic finish, the meal arc lands intact.",
  "Creme Brulee|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "The curry and the brulee -- the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth bookends with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Creme Brulee|Venge Family Reserve": "Venge Family Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Venge Family Reserve belongs on the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Venge Scout's Honor": "Venge Scout's Honor's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Venge Scout's Honor on the steak course instead.",
  "Creme Brulee|Venge Silencieux": "Venge Silencieux's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Venge Silencieux for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Vesper": "Vesper's crisp citrus-juniper build sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creme Brulee|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's classic yellow-label Champagne body clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a sparkling. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Veuve Clicquot Brut for the steak course.",
  "Creme Brulee|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The cocktail's cognac-and-rye split can meet the torched-sugar crust but the Bénédictine's herbal edge doesn't fully align with vanilla custard. Works rather than peaks — Moscato and Vin Santo shine brighter for brûlée.",
  "Creme Brulee|Villon Cognac": "Villon with creme brulee — saffron/elderflower Courvoisier-sibling against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Excellent; Villon's infusion lifts the caramel with floral-spice distinction.",
  "Creme Brulee|Vin Santo": "* Vin Santo -- Tuscan dessert wine, dried-fruit and honey, biscotti pour. The Sauternes-or-Port body carries the brulee, and the honey threads the cream. Serious peak dessert pour. Gold standard; the call that earns the regular.",
  "Creme Brulee|Weller 107": "Weller 107's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Creme Brulee|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; own-plus-sourced over-specified.",
  "Creme Brulee|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Creme Brulee|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Creme Brulee|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Wheatley Vodka lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Creme Brulee|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye-and-oak weight handles the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "Creme Brulee|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye backbone drives the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Creme Brulee|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye backbone drives the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Creme Brulee|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye backbone drives the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; multi-distillery complexity doesn't drive.",
  "Creme Brulee|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the dry rye body wraps the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Creme Brulee|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the high-rye spice register matches the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Creme Brulee|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon weight handles the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Creme Brulee|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon weight handles the brulee, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Creme Brulee|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with creme brulee — iconic Double-Oaked against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Excellent; Woodford Double Oaked's toasted-then-charred second-barrel caramel is the featured Double-Oaked-brulee pairing — the most recognizable Double-Oaked delivering the textbook bourbon-brulee match.",
  "Creme Brulee|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the high-rye spice register matches the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; KBS distinctiveness is slightly over-specified for the two-cut plate.",
  "Creme Brulee|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the restrained whisky body threads the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Creme Brulee|Yellow Chartreuse": "Creme Brulee: caramelized custard meets Yellow Chartreuse. Excellent; the honey-saffron character plays burnt-sugar-cream at lower-proof register — sweeter Chartreuse pairing than Green, more accessible to a guest easing into herbal liqueurs.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Asparagus": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Beignets": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Bone Marrow": "Willamette Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — graceful Oregon Pinot lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Cristom for the chicken, salmon, or seafood course.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Bone-In Filet": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée -- Willamette Pinot, red cherry, silky tannins. The silky red-fruit body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cherry-fruit frames the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Broccoli Cheddar": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin holds with the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Broccolini": "Willamette Pinot — graceful Oregon Pinot with cherry, spice, medium body, structured. The structured body handles broccolini's char weight and the spice plays against the vegetal edge. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Broccolini but below the wine's peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Brussels and Belly": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance reads alongside Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the silky red-fruit body wraps the pork-belly plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Burrata": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral, low tannin. The low tannin meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the raspberry plays against basil-tomato. Works as benchmark Pinot for burrata — accessible red; whites and rosés peak higher.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Butternut Squash": "Willamette Pinot — graceful Oregon Pinot with cherry, spice, medium body, structured. The structured body handles butternut's cream weight and the spice mirrors nutmeg-cinnamon warmth directly. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Butternut Squash but below the wine's peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Carrot Cake": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée on the steak course instead.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Cheesecake": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Chilean Seabass": "Willamette Pinot — graceful Oregon Pinot with cherry, spice, medium body, structured. The structured body handles seabass's buttery flesh and the cherry-spice plays against the rich texture. Strong Oregon Pinot for Chilean seabass — Cristom structure on the classic.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Chocolate Brownie": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Chocolate Cake": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Clam Chowder": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin holds with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Cowboy Ribeye": "Willamette Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — graceful low-tannin Pinot falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep Cristom for salmon, chicken, or seafood; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Crab Cake": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral backbone, low tannin. The low tannin leaves room for crab's sweet meat and the minerality plays against romesco. Peak Oregon Pinot for crab cake — benchmark red that works where heavier reds would bury the shellfish. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Creamed Spinach": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Creamy Potato": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the potato soup's silky body — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Creme Brulee": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Escargot": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin holds the line with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Faroe Island Salmon": "Willamette Pinot from the Eola-Amity Hills — bright cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral backbone. Benchmark Oregon producer. A peak Pinot match for the Faroe salmon's richness; elegant without being delicate, handles any seasonal sauce. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Filet Mignon": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral backbone, graceful. The elegant body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the forest-floor echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak benchmark Pinot for filet — Willamette terroir frames the delicate cut. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|French Onion": "Willamette Pinot — graceful Oregon Pinot with cherry, spice, medium body, structured. The structured medium body handles French Onion's long-cooked beef broth and the spice-cherry frames the caramelized onion sweetness. Peaks from the dish's side; on this wine's card it's strong — below peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins. Confident pour when a guest anchors on French Onion. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Grilled Caesar": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral backbone. The high acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the forest-floor plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong benchmark Pinot for grilled Caesar — Willamette elegance on the layered salad.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Gumbo": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin holds with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Willamette Pinot — graceful Oregon Pinot with cherry, spice, medium body, structured. The structured body handles carrots' honey-glazed weight and the spice plays against the roasted char. Peaks from the dish's side; on this wine's card it's strong — below peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins. Confident pour when a guest anchors on Honey Roasted Carrots. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|House Wedge": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral, high acid. The acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the raspberry plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak benchmark Pinot for the wedge — Willamette elegance reframes the classic steakhouse salad. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Kansas City": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral, medium body. The graceful structure works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though low tannin falls short of bolder Cabs. Works as benchmark Pinot for the strip — guest-preference call over classic Cab.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Loaded Potato": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Lobster Mac": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Market Fish": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Mocha Creme": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Mushroom Bisque": "Willamette Pinot — graceful Oregon Pinot with cherry, spice, medium body, structured. The structured medium body handles the bisque's cream weight and the cherry-spice plays against the mushroom earth. Strong Oregon Pinot for the bisque — Cristom's Eola-Amity structure on umami-and-cream.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Mushrooms": "* Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée -- Willamette Pinot, red cherry, silky tannins. The Pinot finesse threads the mushrooms, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the side. Peak Southern-French for the bird — preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen for today's build. Gold standard; the call that earns the regular.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée on the steak course instead.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Porterhouse": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin matches the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's silky frame sits alongside the starter without weight-fighting — the fruit threads through the plate. Strong — aligned register.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Rainbow Trout": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée on the steak course instead.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Roast Half Chicken": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral backbone. The high acid plays against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus structure; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak benchmark Oregon Pinot for chicken across most seasonal builds. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Willamette Pinot — graceful Oregon Pinot with cherry, spice, medium body, structured. The structured body handles the soup's roasted-pepper weight and the spice plays against the smoky chickpea base. Peaks from the dish's side; on this wine's card it's strong — below peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins. Confident pour when a guest anchors on Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral. The acid cuts the sauté butter and the forest-floor plays against garlic without clashing with spinach earth. Works as benchmark Pinot for the sauté side — Willamette elegance rounds the pairing.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Seafood Tower": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Seared Scallops": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral, low tannin. The soft tannin leaves room for scallop sweetness and the minerality plays against caramelized-exterior char. Strong Willamette Pinot for scallops — benchmark Oregon red on the delicate shellfish.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Seasonal Soup": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house build — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Seasonal Vegetables": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Shrimp Bisque": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral, high acid. The acid cuts bisque's cream weight and the forest-floor plays against shellfish umami and sherry. Strong Oregon Pinot for shrimp bisque — benchmark red against the rich shellfish soup.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Shrimp Cocktail": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's Willamette Pinot elegance overshadows the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée on the steak course instead.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Swordfish": "Willamette Pinot — graceful Oregon Pinot with cherry, spice, medium body, structured. The structured body handles swordfish's meaty flesh and the spice plays against the grilled char. Strong Oregon Pinot for swordfish — Cristom structure on the grilled classic.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|The Tomahawk": "Willamette Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — graceful low-tannin Oregon Pinot has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Cristom for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Tomato Basil": "Willamette Pinot — graceful Oregon Pinot with cherry, spice, medium body, structured. The structured body handles tomato's acidity and the spice plays against basil's aromatic edge. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Tomato Basil but below the wine's peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Truffle Fries": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna: this lean, seared tuna's sesame-sweet crust and rare-pink center meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's cherry-bright, low-tannin frame threads cleanly through the fish — classic Burgundy logic. A headliner fish pair. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Willamette Pinot — graceful Oregon Pinot with cherry, spice, medium body, structured. The structured body handles the curry's coconut-milk weight and the spice mirrors the curry's warm aromatics. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas but below the wine's peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins.",
  "Crown Royal|Asparagus": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the Canadian-whisky body sits with the asparagus, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Crown Royal|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Crown Royal with the au gratin — iconic Canadian against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Crown Royal's smoothness handles the richness at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Crown Royal|Beignets": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the blended whisky body wraps the beignets, and the gentle rye thread underlines the dessert. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Crown Royal|Bone Marrow": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness sits alongside the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the rich marrow, and the easy Canadian register softens the starter. Works; the intensity overpowers this dish; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Crown Royal|Bone-In Filet": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the Canadian-whisky body sits with the bone-enhanced filet, and the caramel rounds the cut. Works; craft-blend covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Crown Royal|Broccoli Cheddar": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the smooth Canadian register carries the broccoli cheddar, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the complex character gives the dessert a refined frame.",
  "Crown Royal|Broccolini": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the smooth Canadian register carries the broccolini, and the light caramel edge frames the side. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for the side — save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Crown Royal|Brussels and Belly": "Crown Royal with Brussels + Belly — smooth Canadian icon against maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; Crown Royal's vanilla-honey meets the glaze at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Crown Royal|Burrata": "Crown Royal with burrata — iconic smooth Canadian against fresh dairy. Avoid; Crown Royal is too light-vanilla-sweet to match burrata.",
  "Crown Royal|Butternut Squash": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the Canadian whisky weight matches the squash bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Crown Royal|Carrot Cake": "Crown Royal with carrot cake — vanilla-honey Canadian against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; Crown Royal's vanilla-honey meets the cake at crowd-pleasing register; Canadian Club 43 Year's aged depth edges this dessert.",
  "Crown Royal|Cheesecake": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the Canadian whisky weight matches the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Crown Royal|Chilean Seabass": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Crown Royal lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crown Royal|Chocolate Brownie": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the brownie, and the easy Canadian register softens the dessert. Works; smooth gentle match.",
  "Crown Royal|Chocolate Cake": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the Canadian whisky weight matches the chocolate cake, and the light caramel edge frames the dessert. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Crown Royal|Clam Chowder": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the blended whisky body wraps the chowder, and the oak softens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Crown Royal|Cowboy Ribeye": "Crown Royal with the cowboy ribeye — smooth Canadian against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Works; Crown Royal is lighter than ribeye demands; Canadian Club 43 Year handles it better at collector register.",
  "Crown Royal|Crab Cake": "Crown Royal with crab cake — iconic smooth Canadian against delicate lump crab. Works; Crown Royal is gentle enough to sit with crab; not featured but possible at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Crown Royal|Creamed Spinach": "Crown Royal with creamed spinach — iconic Canadian against dairy-rich side. Strong; Crown Royal's smoothness rides the cream at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Crown Royal|Creamy Potato": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the smooth Canadian register carries the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Crown Royal|Creme Brulee": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the easygoing Canadian character handles the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the toast notes don't really interplay with the soup but don't clash.",
  "Crown Royal|Escargot": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness leans against the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the blended whisky body wraps the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Crown Royal|Faroe Island Salmon": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Crown Royal on the steak course instead.",
  "Crown Royal|Filet Mignon": "Crown Royal with the filet — iconic Canadian blend against tenderloin. Excellent; Crown Royal's smooth-hand-selected character frames tenderloin at crowd-pleasing Canadian register — the democratic Canadian-filet pairing everyone recognizes.",
  "Crown Royal|French Onion": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the Canadian whisky weight matches the French onion, and the soft profile rounds the bowl. Works; aged premium slightly over-specified.",
  "Crown Royal|Grilled Caesar": "Crown Royal with the grilled Caesar — iconic smooth Canadian against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Crown Royal meets the char at crowd-pleasing register; Canadian Club's grain-forward heritage is the featured Canadian-Caesar match.",
  "Crown Royal|Gumbo": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the smooth Canadian register carries the gumbo, and the light-grain note brightens the bowl. Works; premium single-barrel wasted on the salad.",
  "Crown Royal|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Canadian whisky weight matches the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; EHT Full Proof handles the richness at historic Kentucky register.",
  "Crown Royal|House Wedge": "Crown Royal with the House Wedge — iconic smooth Canadian against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Crown Royal's vanilla-honey character threads the wedge with crowd-pleasing Canadian distinction.",
  "Crown Royal|Kansas City": "Crown Royal with the KC — iconic Canadian blend against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Crown Royal's vanilla-honey-fruit character handles strip marbling at crowd-pleasing Canadian register.",
  "Crown Royal|Loaded Potato": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the loaded potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a soup; save the rare bottle.",
  "Crown Royal|Lobster Mac": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the smooth Canadian register carries the lobster mac, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the sweetness is a touch much for the salad's simplicity.",
  "Crown Royal|Market Fish": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Crown Royal for the steak course.",
  "Crown Royal|Mocha Creme": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the blended whisky body wraps the mocha creme, and the oak softens the cream. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Crown Royal|Mushroom Bisque": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the blended whisky body wraps the mushroom bisque, and the oak balances the umami register. Works; the intensity overpowers this dish.",
  "Crown Royal|Mushrooms": "Crown Royal with mushrooms — Gimli Manitoba iconic Canadian against earthy umami. Excellent; Crown Royal's smooth-hand-selected character threads mushroom earthiness at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Crown Royal|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the smooth Canadian register carries the PB brownie, and the light-grain note brightens the dessert. Works; Caribbean Cask frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Crown Royal|Porterhouse": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness reads alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the easygoing Canadian character handles the dual-cut steak, and the easy Canadian register softens the cut. Works; distinctive character gives the dessert a unique story-frame.",
  "Crown Royal|Prime Tartare": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness reads alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the blended whisky body wraps the tartare, and the oak balances the umami register. Works; the private select's layered profile underserves the dessert's simplicity; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Crown Royal|Rainbow Trout": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Crown Royal belongs on the steak course.",
  "Crown Royal|Roast Half Chicken": "Crown Royal with the chicken — iconic Canadian blended whisky, Gimli Manitoba, purple velvet bag, vanilla-honey-fruit smooth crowd-pleaser. Excellent; Crown Royal is the featured Canadian-chicken pairing — the iconic smooth character frames roasted poultry with crowd-pleasing gravitas, the Canadian whisky everyone recognizes.",
  "Crown Royal|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the smooth Canadian register carries the chickpea soup, and the gentle rye thread underlines the bowl. Works; Caribbean Cask frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Crown Royal|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Crown Royal for the steak course.",
  "Crown Royal|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the garlic spinach, and the light caramel edge frames the side. Works; allocated character doesn't drive.",
  "Crown Royal|Seafood Tower": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Crown Royal for the steak course.",
  "Crown Royal|Seared Scallops": "Crown Royal with seared scallops — iconic Canadian against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Crown Royal's vanilla-sweetness is wrong for scallops.",
  "Crown Royal|Seasonal Soup": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the smooth Canadian register carries the seasonal soup, and the soft profile rounds the bowl. Works; the pour's brightness is a gentle counterpoint to the dessert's richness.",
  "Crown Royal|Seasonal Vegetables": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the Canadian whisky weight matches the seasonal vegetables, and the easy Canadian register softens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Crown Royal|Shrimp Bisque": "Crown Royal with shrimp bisque — smooth Canadian against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Crown Royal's vanilla-honey rides the bisque at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Crown Royal|Shrimp Cocktail": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness drowns out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; one of those is the move. Crown Royal belongs on the steak course.",
  "Crown Royal|Swordfish": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Crown Royal on the steak course instead.",
  "Crown Royal|The Tomahawk": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness sits alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the 36oz showpiece, and the light caramel edge frames the cut. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Crown Royal|Tomato Basil": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the Canadian whisky weight matches the tomato basil, and the soft profile rounds the bowl. Works; the pour is a touch ambitious for the salad.",
  "Crown Royal|Truffle Fries": "Crown Royal with truffle fries — iconic Canadian blend against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Crown Royal's smooth character meets the earthy-rich at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Crown Royal|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Crown Royal lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Crown Royal|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the blended whisky body wraps the curry, and the soft profile rounds the bowl. Works; extended-aged premium wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Asparagus": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda. The garden-fresh profile meshes with asparagus's vegetal character directly and the citrus cuts the char. Works as gin cocktail for asparagus — garden-to-plate route; Sauv Blanc and Sancerre peak higher on sulfur notes.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Beignets": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Cucumber Gimlet for the steak course.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Bone Marrow": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda. Garden-fresh cocktail has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat richness — the Gimlet is built for raw bar, not bone dishes. Never pair — route marrow to whiskey cocktails or big reds.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Bone-In Filet": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Cucumber Gimlet lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Broccoli Cheddar": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Broccolini": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda. The garden-fresh profile meshes with broccolini's vegetal character directly and the citrus cuts the char. Works as gin cocktail for broccolini — garden-to-plate route; wines peak higher as dedicated pairings.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Brussels and Belly": "Hendrick's muddled with cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, topped with soda. Cucumber-light cocktail for a pork-belly-forward side — the weights mismatch. Keep the Gimlet for lighter seafood; lean into Negroni or bourbon cocktails here. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Burrata": "Hendrick's gin, cucumber slices, lime, ginger syrup, soda top. Fresh cucumber against burrata's creamy-basil delicacy is a direct match; lime brightness cuts the cream and ginger adds subtle warmth. Peak starter pairing — both lean into garden-fresh, clean profiles. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Butternut Squash": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build sits beside the squash soup's silky sweet — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Carrot Cake": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Cucumber Gimlet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Cheesecake": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Cucumber Gimlet for the steak course.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Chilean Seabass": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build reads alongside the seabass's buttery flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Chocolate Brownie": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Cucumber Gimlet for the steak course.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Chocolate Cake": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a light cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Cucumber Gimlet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Clam Chowder": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build stays alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Cowboy Ribeye": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda top. Delicate garden-profile cocktail crushed by Cowboy's marbled rendered-fat intensity. Never pair — the Gimlet belongs with raw bar or starters, not the fattiest cut on the menu.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Crab Cake": "Hendrick's muddled with cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, topped with soda. The cucumber-forward build brings freshness against the rich crab cake and tempers the jalapeño heat in our romesco. Cooling cocktail that doesn't fight. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Creamed Spinach": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Creamy Potato": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Creme Brulee": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- cocktail citrus and custard tang cancel each other — both reach for acid. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Cucumber Gimlet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Escargot": "Hendrick's gin, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda top. The fresh cucumber-and-lime structure cuts garlic-butter escargot cleanly and ginger adds aromatic warmth against the snails' earth. Strong opener cocktail when guest wants something garden-fresh before heavier starters.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Faroe Island Salmon": "Hendrick's muddled with cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, topped with soda. Cool cocktail that lets the Faroe speak; the cucumber and ginger don't fight whatever seasonal prep shows up. Clean cocktail route for the salmon. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Filet Mignon": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda top. The cocktail's garden-fresh profile is wrong for any steak — cucumber and soda have no hook into grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the cocktail; route filet to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|French Onion": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda — garden-fresh cocktail has no structural match for French Onion's rich cheese-and-broth depth. Never pair. Route the Gimlet to raw bar or lighter starters; take French Onion with Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, or a structured red.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Grilled Caesar": "Hendrick's gin, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda. The cucumber-and-lime freshness cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and ginger adds warmth against grilled-romaine char. Works as unconventional opener alongside a charred salad when guest wants something cooling.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Gumbo": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build sits beside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build reads alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|House Wedge": "Hendrick's muddled with cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, topped with soda. The cooling cocktail softens the blue cheese dressing's weight and the cucumber plays against the iceberg's crunch. Refreshing cocktail route for the salad course. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Kansas City": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Cucumber Gimlet lands on another course — not here.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Loaded Potato": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda — garden-fresh cocktail has no structural match for loaded potato's cheddar-bacon-sour-cream depth. Never pair. Route the Gimlet to raw bar or lighter starters; take loaded potato with Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, or Champagne.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Lobster Mac": "Cucumber Gimlet: this gin cocktail's citrus brightness and botanical lift meets this rich, creamy side's dairy-driven indulgence. Citrus cocktail against rich, dairy-heavy side — acid cuts fat but brightness overreaches. Works as a contrast pour.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Market Fish": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build plays cleanly against the fish's clean flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Mocha Creme": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a light cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Cucumber Gimlet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Mushroom Bisque": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Mushrooms": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Cucumber Gimlet for the steak course.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Porterhouse": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Cucumber Gimlet belongs on another course.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this gin cocktail's citrus brightness and botanical lift. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. Strong — aligned lift.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Rainbow Trout": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda. The garden-fresh profile lifts trout's delicate freshwater character and the citrus plays against the flaky flesh. Works as gin cocktail for rainbow trout — garden-to-stream route; wines peak higher as dedicated pairings.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Roast Half Chicken": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the cocktail lift carries the herb-crisped bird, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build plays cleanly against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda. The ginger and cucumber echo Asian-soy-glaze character and the citrus lifts halibut's cured flesh. Works as gin cocktail for salt-cured halibut — fusion-adjacent route; wines peak higher as dedicated pairings.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Hendrick's muddled with cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, topped with soda. Clean cocktail that doesn't fight the escargot butter's garlic weight; the cucumber and ginger keep the palate fresh for the lightest sharing side. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Seafood Tower": "Hendrick's muddled with cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda top. The cocktail's garden-fresh profile handles the raw-bar spread — oysters, shrimp, crab legs — well; soda adds lift. Strong tower pairing when guest wants a non-bubbly option alongside the shellfish.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Seared Scallops": "Hendrick's muddled with cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda. The clean cocktail profile meets scallops' clean-seared sweet character; cucumber-and-ginger adds aromatic depth without crushing the delicacy. Peak refreshing cocktail for scallops when guest wants something lighter than Champagne.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Seasonal Soup": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build stays alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Seasonal Vegetables": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build leans against the seasonal garden plate — the cocktail lift sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Shrimp Bisque": "Hendrick's muddled with cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda top. The lime-and-ginger structure cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and cucumber adds freshness against the soup's weight. Strong pairing when guest wants cocktail rather than white wine alongside bisque.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Shrimp Cocktail": "Hendrick's gin, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda top. Fresh cucumber and lime cut chilled shrimp's brine and the ginger syrup softens cocktail-sauce horseradish bite. Peak starter pairing — cocktail's garden-fresh structure meets the shrimp's clean sweetness. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Swordfish": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift leans against the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the bright build frames the swordfish, and the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|The Tomahawk": "Garden-fresh gin cocktail against the biggest steak on the menu — zero structural overlap. The Cucumber Gimlet has no weight to handle marbled tomahawk fat. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or heavy bourbon cocktails.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Tomato Basil": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build leans against the tomato basil's bright acid — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Truffle Fries": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build holds with the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda. The ginger and cucumber echo Asian-inspired honey-sambal salad and the citrus lifts rare-seared tuna's lean flesh. Works as gin cocktail for tuxedo tuna — fusion-adjacent cocktail route; wines peak higher as dedicated pairings.",
  "Cucumber Gimlet|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Cucumber Gimlet's crisp citrus-ginger build sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Dalmore 12|Asparagus": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the malt-and-oak threads the asparagus, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; KBS distinctiveness is slightly over-specified for the two-cut plate.",
  "Dalmore 12|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Dalmore 12 with the au gratin — Highland luxury against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Dalmore's sherry-finish depth handles the richness at luxury register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Beignets": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the scotch depth settles into the beignets, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the dessert. Works; save the bottle for the protein course — a soup is wrong for a $200 pour.",
  "Dalmore 12|Bone Marrow": "Dalmore 12 with bone marrow — Highland luxury sherry-finish against umami fat. Strong; Dalmore's sherry-finish depth sits alongside marrow with chocolate-orange character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Dalmore 12|Bone-In Filet": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the sherry-cask depth wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the orange-and-chocolate plays against the cut. Works; extended-aged home-state pour wasted on salad.",
  "Dalmore 12|Broccoli Cheddar": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the broccoli cheddar, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the complex character gives the dessert a refined frame.",
  "Dalmore 12|Broccolini": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the scotch backbone matches the broccolini, and the honey rounds the side. Works; the sweetness is a touch much for the salad's simplicity.",
  "Dalmore 12|Brussels and Belly": "Dalmore 12 with Brussels + Belly — sherry-finish Highland against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; Dalmore's Matusalem-cask dried-fruit meets the glaze at Highland-luxury register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Burrata": "Dalmore 12 with burrata — Highland luxury sherry-finish against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Dalmore 12|Butternut Squash": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the malt-driven body wraps the squash bisque, and the oak softens the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Dalmore 12|Carrot Cake": "Dalmore 12 with carrot cake — sherry-finish Highland against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Dalmore's Matusalem-sherry dried-fruit and orange-marmalade marry the cake's raisins and spice — the featured Highland Scotch-carrot-cake pairing, parallel to Macallan 12's sherried-Speyside approach.",
  "Dalmore 12|Cheesecake": "Dalmore 12 with cheesecake — sherry-finish Highland luxury against custard. Strong; Dalmore's orange-marmalade and dried-fruit thread the cheesecake at Highland-premium register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Chilean Seabass": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Dalmore 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Dalmore 12|Chocolate Brownie": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brownie, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the dessert. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "Dalmore 12|Chocolate Cake": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the chocolate cake, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "Dalmore 12|Clam Chowder": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the sherry-cask depth wraps the chowder, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th handles the richness at annual-release register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Cowboy Ribeye": "Dalmore 12 with the cowboy ribeye — Matusalem-cask Highland against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Dalmore's sherry-finish luxury handles the ribeye; Dalmore-family or Oban 18 reach higher registers.",
  "Dalmore 12|Crab Cake": "Dalmore 12 with crab cake — Highland luxury sherry-finish against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Dalmore 12|Creamed Spinach": "Dalmore 12 with creamed spinach — Matusalem-cask Highland against dairy-rich side. Strong; Dalmore's slightly-oily sherry-finish rides the cream at luxury register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Creamy Potato": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the scotch backbone matches the potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Creme Brulee": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the Highland register carries the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the pour's refinement is underused by the salad.",
  "Dalmore 12|Escargot": "Dalmore 12's crisp chocolate-citrus character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Dalmore 12|Faroe Island Salmon": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Dalmore 12 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Dalmore 12|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets Highland luxury single malt with Matusalem sherry-cask finish, orange-marmalade and chocolate notes, slightly oily premium character. Dalmore's sherry-finish luxury profile frames tenderloin with Highland-premium gravitas — where Glenmorangie 10 is honey-floral-bourbon and Oban 14 is maritime-coastal, Dalmore is sherry-finished luxury. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Dalmore 12|French Onion": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the sherry-cask depth wraps the French onion, and the orange-and-chocolate plays against the bowl. Works; blend character covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Dalmore 12|Grilled Caesar": "Dalmore 12 with the grilled Caesar — Highland luxury sherry-finish against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Dalmore's orange-chocolate character meets the char with luxurious Highland distinction.",
  "Dalmore 12|Gumbo": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the scotch backbone matches the gumbo, and the smoke threads the bowl. Works; single-barrel depth gives the dessert more than Small Batch.",
  "Dalmore 12|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the scotch depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; Prohibition narrative wasted on salad.",
  "Dalmore 12|House Wedge": "Dalmore 12 with the House Wedge — Highland luxury against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Dalmore's sherry-finish depth threads the wedge at premium Highland register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Kansas City": "Dalmore 12 with the KC — Matusalem sherry-cask Highland against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Dalmore's sherry-finish dried-fruit-and-chocolate is the featured Highland-KC pairing — the sherry-cask weight is built for strip marbling where Glenmorangie 10's lighter bourbon-cask honey feels under-specified.",
  "Dalmore 12|Loaded Potato": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the malt-driven body wraps the loaded potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Dalmore 12|Lobster Mac": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the malt-driven body wraps the lobster mac, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the sherry-cask character rides the cream at benchmark register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Market Fish": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character flattens the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Dalmore 12 for the steak course.",
  "Dalmore 12|Mocha Creme": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the mocha creme, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; private-selection wasted on the salad.",
  "Dalmore 12|Mushroom Bisque": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the Highland register carries the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the umami. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Mushrooms": "Dalmore 12 with mushrooms — Highland luxury sherry-finish against earthy umami. Excellent; Dalmore's orange-chocolate-dried-fruit character threads mushroom earthiness with sherry-finish depth.",
  "Dalmore 12|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the Highland register carries the PB brownie, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the dessert. Works; extended maturation meets the brownie at home-state premium register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Porterhouse": "Dalmore 12 with the porterhouse — sherry-finish Highland luxury against strip-and-filet. Strong; Dalmore's Matusalem-cask depth frames the two-cut plate at Highland-premium register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Prime Tartare": "Dalmore 12 -- sherry-cask Highland, orange, chocolate. The sherry-cask depth wraps the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; AE Triple Cask's layered-cask character threads mushrooms at distinctive register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Dalmore 12|Rainbow Trout": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Dalmore 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Dalmore 12|Roast Half Chicken": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the Highland register carries the roast chicken, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Dalmore 12|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the scotch depth settles into the chickpea soup, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; pour slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Dalmore 12|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Dalmore 12 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Dalmore 12|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the scotch depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; JD Bonded's backbone handles the richness at Bonded register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Seafood Tower": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Dalmore 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Dalmore 12|Seared Scallops": "Dalmore 12 with seared scallops — Matusalem-cask Highland against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Dalmore 12|Seasonal Soup": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the malt-and-oak threads the seasonal soup, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; bonded weight pushes slightly past the soup.",
  "Dalmore 12|Seasonal Vegetables": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the malt-driven body wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the honey rounds the side. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Dalmore 12|Shrimp Bisque": "Dalmore 12 with shrimp bisque — Matusalem-cask Highland against cream-shrimp soup. Works; Dalmore's sherry-finish rides the bisque at luxury register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Shrimp Cocktail": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character overshadows the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Dalmore 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Dalmore 12|Swordfish": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Dalmore 12 for the steak course.",
  "Dalmore 12|The Tomahawk": "Dalmore 12's crisp chocolate-citrus character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Dalmore 12|Tomato Basil": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the scotch depth settles into the tomato basil, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Dalmore 12|Truffle Fries": "Dalmore 12 with truffle fries — Matusalem-cask Highland luxury against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Dalmore's slightly-oily sherry-finish character meets the earthy-rich with luxurious depth.",
  "Dalmore 12|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Dalmore 12 for the steak course.",
  "Dalmore 12|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the scotch depth settles into the curry, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Asparagus": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin sits beside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Beignets": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's Loire Valley Pinot Noir fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge belongs on the steak course.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Bone Marrow": "Loire red Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — light-body Sancerre lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Sancerre Rouge for the chicken, salmon, or seafood course.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Bone-In Filet": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Broccoli Cheddar": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin leans against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Broccolini": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin sits alongside the broccolini's tender green — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Brussels and Belly": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin leans against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Burrata": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body. The chalk-minerality meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the dried-herbs play against basil-tomato. Strong Loire red for burrata — unconventional French Pinot on the delicate starter.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Butternut Squash": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin leans against the squash soup's silky sweet — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Carrot Cake": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's Loire Valley Pinot Noir clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge belongs on the steak course.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Cheesecake": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's Loire Valley Pinot Noir pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge for the steak course.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Chilean Seabass": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin holds with the seabass's buttery flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Chocolate Brownie": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's Loire Valley Pinot Noir flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Chocolate Cake": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's Loire Valley Pinot Noir fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge for the steak course.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Clam Chowder": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Cowboy Ribeye": "Loire red Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — light-body Sancerre falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep Sancerre Rouge for salmon, chicken, or seafood; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Crab Cake": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body. The chalky acid cuts romesco and the dried-herbs play against crab's sweet meat. Peak Loire red for crab cake — unconventional French Pinot where heavier reds would bury the shellfish. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Creamed Spinach": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Creamy Potato": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Creme Brulee": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's Loire Valley Pinot Noir jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Escargot": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body, high acid. The chalk-minerality cuts escargot's garlic-parsley butter and the dried-herbs echo the Burgundy-classic preparation. Peak Loire red for escargot — French Pinot on the French classic. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Faroe Island Salmon": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body, high acid. The chalk-minerality plays against salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak French red Pinot for salmon — distinct Loire style across most preparations. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Filet Mignon": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body, low tannin. The light body handles butter-tender filet's lean sweetness though the low tannin underserves the cut vs. classic Napa Cab. Works as Loire red for filet — French Pinot for guest wanting lighter red.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|French Onion": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge: Loire Valley Pinot Noir. Excellent on French Onion; earth-fruit-light-red frames beef-broth-and-gruyere at Loire register — the French answer to the French classic. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Grilled Caesar": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, high acid. The chalk-acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the dried-herbs play against grilled-romaine char. Strong Loire red for grilled Caesar — French Pinot reframes the layered salad.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Gumbo": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin reads alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|House Wedge": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, high acid. The chalk-acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dried-herbs play against blue cheese dressing. Peak Loire red for the wedge — French Pinot reframes the classic steakhouse salad. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Kansas City": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin finds register with the strip's lean char — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Loaded Potato": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin sits alongside the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Lobster Mac": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin stays alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Market Fish": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the fish's clean flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Mocha Creme": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's Loire Valley Pinot Noir jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge for the steak course.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Mushroom Bisque": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin holds with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Mushrooms": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs. The dried-herbs echo roasted-mushroom earth and the chalk-minerality plays against glutamate depth. Works as Loire red for mushrooms — French Pinot on the earthy side; Willamette Pinots peak higher for mushroom depth.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's Loire Valley Pinot Noir fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge belongs on the steak course.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Porterhouse": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin sits with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Prime Tartare": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body, low tannin. The low tannin leaves room for raw-beef delicacy and the chalk-acid plays against egg yolk and capers. Strong Loire red for tartare — hidden-gem French Pinot on the raw-beef classic.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Rainbow Trout": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the trout's delicate flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Roast Half Chicken": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body. The dried-herbs play against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong Loire Pinot for chicken across most seasonal preparations.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs. The chalk-acid cuts the sauté butter and the dried-herbs play against garlic without clashing with spinach earth. Works as Loire red for the sauté side — French Pinot rounds the pairing.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Seafood Tower": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's Loire Valley Pinot Noir crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge for the steak course.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Seared Scallops": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body. The chalk-minerality plays against scallop sweetness and the dried-herbs frame caramelized-exterior char. Peak Loire red for scallops — distinct French Pinot on the delicate shellfish. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Seasonal Soup": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin sits alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Seasonal Vegetables": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin finds neutral with the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Shrimp Bisque": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, high acid. The chalk-acid cuts bisque's cream weight and the dried-herbs play against shellfish umami and sherry. Strong Loire red for shrimp bisque — hidden-gem French Pinot against the rich soup.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Shrimp Cocktail": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's Loire Valley Pinot Noir crowds out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge for the steak course.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Swordfish": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin sits beside the swordfish's meaty flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|The Tomahawk": "Loire red Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — light-body low-tannin Sancerre has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold reds; keep Sancerre Rouge for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Tomato Basil": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin stays alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Truffle Fries": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin sits beside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin reads alongside the tuna's seared rare crust — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Asparagus": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Darioush Cabernet Franc on the steak course instead.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the savory herb plays against the lactic richness. Works as rare-Napa CF for au gratin — Darioush aromatic precision on the cheesy side.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Beignets": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Darioush Cabernet Franc for the steak course.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Bone Marrow": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the savory herb plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak rare-Napa CF for marrow — Darioush Persian-influenced elegance on the rich starter. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Bone-In Filet": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's cherry-floral tannin holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Broccoli Cheddar": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper flattens the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Darioush Cabernet Franc on the steak course instead.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Broccolini": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Darioush Cabernet Franc on the steak course instead.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Burrata": "Rare Napa CF against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Darioush's medium-tannin violet-herb structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Darioush for the steak course where the Persian-estate story earns the bottle.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Butternut Squash": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Darioush Cabernet Franc for the steak course.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Carrot Cake": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Darioush Cabernet Franc lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Cheesecake": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Darioush Cabernet Franc for the steak course.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Chilean Seabass": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Darioush Cabernet Franc lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Chocolate Brownie": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Darioush Cabernet Franc lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Chocolate Cake": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Darioush Cabernet Franc belongs on the steak course.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Clam Chowder": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper flattens the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Darioush Cabernet Franc for the steak course.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Crab Cake": "Rare Napa CF against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Darioush's medium-tannin violet-herb structure buries the shellfish even though it's lighter than Cab Sauvignon. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Darioush for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Creamed Spinach": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the savory herb plays against the spinach base. Strong rare-Napa CF for creamed spinach — Darioush aromatic elegance reframes the rich side.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Creamy Potato": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Darioush Cabernet Franc belongs on the steak course.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Creme Brulee": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Darioush Cabernet Franc on the steak course instead.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Escargot": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's cherry-floral tannin holds steady against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Faroe Island Salmon": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Darioush Cabernet Franc for the steak course.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Filet Mignon": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin, medium body. The elegant medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the violet plays against escargot-butter. Peak rare-Napa CF for filet — Persian-influenced estate story worth telling on the delicate cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|French Onion": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's cherry-floral tannin plays cleanly against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Grilled Caesar": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the savory herb plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong rare-Napa CF for grilled Caesar — Darioush aromatic elegance on the layered salad.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Gumbo": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Darioush Cabernet Franc on the steak course instead.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Darioush Cabernet Franc for the steak course.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|House Wedge": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as rare-Napa CF for the wedge — Darioush aromatic elegance reframes the classic salad.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Kansas City": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin. The elegant medium tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the savory herb plays against beefy char. Peak rare-Napa CF for the strip — Darioush Persian-influenced estate on the American cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Darioush Cabernet Franc|Loaded Potato": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's cherry-floral tannin finds neutral with the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
