  "Disaronno|Asparagus": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Beignets": "Disaronno's digestif-pour sweetness sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the sweet-liqueur register carries the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Disaronno|Bone Marrow": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character flattens the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Disaronno on the steak course instead.",
  "Disaronno|Bone-In Filet": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Disaronno lands on another course — not here.",
  "Disaronno|Broccoli Cheddar": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Broccolini": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Brussels and Belly": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character doubles down on Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- sweet liqueur and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no contrast left. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Disaronno on the steak course instead.",
  "Disaronno|Burrata": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Disaronno on the steak course instead.",
  "Disaronno|Butternut Squash": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Disaronno lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Disaronno|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Disaronno. Strong; amaretto-marzipan-almond plays cake spice at Italian register — almond-cake pairing classic. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Disaronno|Cheesecake": "★ Disaronno's sweet almond-marzipan character is built for the cheesecake's custard cream — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the nut character deepens the cream. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Disaronno|Chilean Seabass": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Disaronno. Excellent; a confident headline pairing.",
  "Disaronno|Chocolate Cake": "Disaronno's after-dinner liqueur weight sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the digestif body mirrors the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Disaronno|Clam Chowder": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Cowboy Ribeye": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Disaronno for another course.",
  "Disaronno|Crab Cake": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Disaronno belongs on the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Creamed Spinach": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Disaronno belongs on the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Creamy Potato": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Creme Brulee": "Disaronno: Italian amaretto (bitter-almond/apricot-pit flavor) from Saronno, 28% ABV, iconic square bottle. Excellent on creme brulee; the vanilla-marzipan-apricot-pit character amplifies burnt-sugar-custard at amaretto-classic register — almond-on-caramel, with more punch than Frangelico.",
  "Disaronno|Escargot": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Faroe Island Salmon": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Filet Mignon": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the liqueur's sugar buries the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Disaronno belongs on another course.",
  "Disaronno|French Onion": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Disaronno lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Disaronno|Grilled Caesar": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Gumbo": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Disaronno belongs on the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sweet liqueur and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no contrast left. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|House Wedge": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Disaronno belongs on the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Kansas City": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Disaronno for another course.",
  "Disaronno|Loaded Potato": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Disaronno lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Disaronno|Lobster Mac": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Disaronno on the steak course instead.",
  "Disaronno|Market Fish": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- sugar on delicate fish is wrong direction — the plate needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Disaronno belongs on the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Mocha Creme": "Mocha Creme: coffee-chocolate dessert meets Disaronno. Excellent; the marzipan-vanilla-almond character plays coffee-chocolate at amaretto-register — amaretto-in-coffee is an Italian dessert standard.",
  "Disaronno|Mushroom Bisque": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Disaronno lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Disaronno|Mushrooms": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character fights the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- the liqueur's sweet register pulls against the mushroom's umami without bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Peanut Butter Brownie": "★ Disaronno's sweet almond-marzipan character rises to meet the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the nut character carries the chocolate. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Disaronno|Porterhouse": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Disaronno belongs on another course.",
  "Disaronno|Prime Tartare": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character flattens the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Disaronno lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Disaronno|Rainbow Trout": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Roast Half Chicken": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character buries the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- sugar smothers the chicken's savory frame — wait for dessert. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Disaronno on the steak course instead.",
  "Disaronno|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Seafood Tower": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Disaronno belongs on the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Disaronno. Avoid; amaretto weight wrong for scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Disaronno|Seasonal Soup": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Seasonal Vegetables": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Shrimp Bisque": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Disaronno lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Disaronno|Shrimp Cocktail": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character smothers the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sugar buries the delicate shellfish without any acid to balance. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Disaronno on the steak course instead.",
  "Disaronno|Swordfish": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Disaronno belongs on the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|The Tomahawk": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character flattens the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Disaronno lands on another course — not here.",
  "Disaronno|Tomato Basil": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Truffle Fries": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Disaronno|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Disaronno lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Asparagus": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the asparagus, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the side. Works; mid-proof pour is slightly over-specified.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with au gratin potatoes — craft rye against cheese-and-cream. Works; boutique at matched register.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Beignets": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the rye-and-oak weight handles the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Bone Marrow": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with bone marrow — craft sourced rye against umami fat. The boutique-blend character meets marrow with craft distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Bone-In Filet": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's herbal-spice character frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey character holds register with the tenderloin, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Broccoli Cheddar": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the dry rye body wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Broccolini": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the rye spice cuts the broccolini, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; BIB slightly over-specified.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Brussels and Belly": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with Brussels + Belly — craft blended rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; boutique character cuts the glaze with craft distinctiveness.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Burrata": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against boutique craft rye. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Butternut Squash": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the rye spice cuts the squash bisque, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the pour's toasted warmth threads through the char without overpowering.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Carrot Cake": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with carrot cake — craft sourced rye against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; craft rye spice and cake spice find each other.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Cheesecake": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the high-rye spice register matches the cheesecake, and the oak softens the cream. Works; complex profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Chilean Seabass": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye for the steak course.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Chocolate Brownie": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rye backbone drives the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; boutique character doesn't drive.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Chocolate Cake": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rye spice cuts the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; accessible gentle match.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Clam Chowder": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the rye-and-oak weight handles the chowder, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; annual-release complexity over-specified.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Cowboy Ribeye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — craft blended rye against cap fat. Strong; craft character capable but higher-proof ryes (Pikesville 110, Knob Creek 100) bring more grip.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Crab Cake": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with crab cake — craft blended rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Creamed Spinach": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with creamed spinach — craft rye against dairy. Strong; boutique character sits with richness.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Creamy Potato": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the dry rye body wraps the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; EHT Full Proof handles the richness at historic Kentucky register.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Creme Brulee": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Escargot": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's herbal-spice character lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Faroe Island Salmon": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye for the steak course.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Filet Mignon": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the filet — craft blended rye against the 10oz cut. The boutique-blender character gives the filet a craft voice distinct from distillery-specific ryes. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|French Onion": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the high-rye spice register matches the French onion, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the bowl. Works; allocated character doesn't drive.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Grilled Caesar": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the grilled Caesar — craft blended rye against charred romaine. The boutique character earns the Caesar with craft-story distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Gumbo": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the dry rye body wraps the gumbo, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; IW Harper 15 handles dessert density at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rye spice cuts the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; AE's fruity-jammy character engages the bleu at wine-finish register.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|House Wedge": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the House Wedge — craft sourced rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; boutique character cuts the bleu distinctively.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Kansas City": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the KC — craft sourced rye, Pacific Northwest boutique brand. Blended from multiple sources with craft finishing. Distinct from Kentucky ryes (Sazerac, Michter's, HH ryes) by craft-blender approach; a boutique-craft rye for strip orders. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Loaded Potato": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the rye backbone drives the loaded potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Lobster Mac": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the dry rye body wraps the lobster mac, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the complex character is wasted on the salad.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Market Fish": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Mocha Creme": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye backbone drives the mocha creme, and the oak softens the cream. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Mushroom Bisque": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the mushroom bisque, and the oak depth meets the umami. Works; the story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Mushrooms": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the mushrooms — craft sourced rye against earthy umami. Strong; boutique character layers into mushroom depth distinctively.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Porterhouse": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the porterhouse — craft blended rye across strip and filet. Strong; boutique character covers both cuts.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Prime Tartare": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the tartare — Pacific Northwest craft brand against raw beef. The craft-blender character is a distinctive counterpoint. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Rainbow Trout": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye for the steak course.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the rye-and-oak weight handles the roast chicken, and the rye-grain bite cuts the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the high-rye spice register matches the chickpea soup, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; spice over-specified for side's character.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye-and-oak weight handles the garlic spinach, and the rye-grain bite cuts the side. Works; bonded weight pushes slightly past the soup.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Seafood Tower": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Seared Scallops": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against craft rye. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Seasonal Soup": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the rye-and-oak weight handles the seasonal soup, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; the pour's refinement is underused by the salad.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye spice cuts the seasonal vegetables, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with shrimp bisque — craft blended rye against cream-based seafood. Works; boutique character doesn't drive.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against craft rye. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Swordfish": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye for the steak course.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|The Tomahawk": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the rye-and-oak weight handles the 36oz showpiece, and the dry-spice register brightens the cut. Strong; boutique character covers both cuts.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Tomato Basil": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the tomato basil, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the bowl. Works; bonded weight a touch ambitious for the dessert.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Truffle Fries": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with truffle fries — craft sourced against truffle-parmesan. The blended craft character gives the side a boutique frame. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the rye-and-oak weight handles the curry, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the bowl. Works; EHT Full Proof handles the richness at historic Kentucky register.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Asparagus": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's rich layered-spirit body leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the rich pour wraps the asparagus, and the deep backbone frames the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's high-proof pour register leans against the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the rich pour wraps the au gratin, and the layered body brightens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Beignets": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's rich layered-spirit body leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the rich pour wraps the beignets, and the heavy-pour register grips the dessert. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets Doctor Bird Jamaica. Excellent; the intense Jamaican hogo stands up to marrow's umami depth at high-ester register — the rum equivalent of a peated scotch on this plate.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Bone-In Filet": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's bold Jamaican-funk character matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Broccoli Cheddar": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's dense-spirit weight finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the dense-spirit weight carries the broccoli cheddar, and the rich character softens the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Broccolini": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's dense-spirit weight sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the rich pour wraps the broccolini, and the rich character softens the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Brussels and Belly": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's heavy aged-spirit weight sits alongside Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the layered spirit body sits with the pork-belly plate, and the rich character softens the side. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Burrata": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum for the steak course.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Butternut Squash": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's deep-bodied spirit register finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the dense-spirit weight carries the squash bisque, and the rich character softens the bowl. Works; a measured close to the meal.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Doctor Bird Jamaica. Excellent; the Jamaican pot-still funk brings tropical-fruit dimension to the carrot-cake spice register — most character-forward rum-dessert on the menu.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Cheesecake": "Cheesecake: custard-dairy dessert meets Doctor Bird. Excellent; the high-ester tropical-fruit funk cuts through cheesecake's cream with Jamaican intensity — not subtle, but unforgettable.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Chilean Seabass": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- heavy spirit weight buries the main protein — needs a wine or lighter spirit. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum for the steak course.",
  "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum|Chocolate Brownie": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum: pot-still Jamaican rum with intense hogo funk and high-ester tropical-fruit overdrive. Excellent on brownie; the Jamaican funk meets warm chocolate with tropical-fruit intensity — Doctor Bird is the dark-rum answer for bourbon drinkers wanting something wild.",
