  "Domaine Tempier Bandol|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Tempier with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by game, olive, and firm tannin, and the cult-producer complexity has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Domaine Tempier Bandol|Swordfish": "Domaine Tempier Bandol's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Domaine Tempier Bandol belongs on the steak course.",
  "Domaine Tempier Bandol|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Domaine Tempier Bandol|Tomato Basil": "Domaine Tempier Bandol's bold earth-mineral cassis leans against the tomato basil's bright acid — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Domaine Tempier Bandol|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the mushroom's umami depth meets the bold red's structured tannin; dark fruit threads the truffle-and-earth without crowding. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Domaine Tempier Bandol|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Domaine Tempier Bandol's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Domaine Tempier Bandol lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Domaine Tempier Bandol|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Provence Bandol Mourvèdre blend — dark plum, game, olive, wild herbs, smoky mineral, firm tannin. The wild herbs play against curry's aromatic spice and the firm tannin handles the coconut-milk weight. Strong Bandol for the curry — Provence structure on the spiced classic.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Asparagus": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — serious gun-flint structure, citrus, mineral. The gun-flint structure handles asparagus's sulfur notes and the citrus lift plays against the vegetal char. Peak Loire Sauv Blanc for asparagus — Pouilly-Fumé precision on the wine-tricky vegetable. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity stays alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Beignets": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé on the steak course instead.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Bone Marrow": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé against marrow's rendered-fat richness — even serious gun-flint structure is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Pouilly-Fumé for scallops, escargot, crab cake, or seafood tower.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Bone-In Filet": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé on another course instead.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Broccoli Cheddar": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Broccolini": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — serious gun-flint structure, citrus, mineral. The flinty mineral plays against broccolini's vegetal char and the citrus lift cuts the tender-stem pepper. Peak Loire Sauv Blanc for broccolini — gun-flint structure on the charred-vegetable side. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Brussels and Belly": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity reads alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Burrata": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — Sauv Blanc with gun-flint minerality, grapefruit, lime zest. The serious body can handle burrata's cream but the smoky-flint profile is stronger than the delicate salad needs. Works as unconventional pairing; Sancerre or St Supéry peak higher for burrata.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Butternut Squash": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity stays alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Carrot Cake": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé for the steak course.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Cheesecake": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé for the steak course.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Chilean Seabass": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity holds the line with the seabass's buttery flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Chocolate Brownie": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé for the steak course.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Chocolate Cake": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé for the steak course.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Clam Chowder": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — serious gun-flint structure, citrus, mineral. The flinty mineral plays against the chowder's clam brine and the citrus lift cuts the cream base. Strong Loire Sauv Blanc for clam chowder — gun-flint structure on the shellfish classic.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Cowboy Ribeye": "Pouilly-Fumé against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — flinty Loire Sauv Blanc crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Pouilly-Fumé for scallops, escargot, crab cake, or the bisque course.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Crab Cake": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — Sauv Blanc with gun-flint minerality, grapefruit, lime zest. The smoky-mineral backbone cuts crab's romesco and the citrus plays against the sweet meat cleanly. Peak serious Sauv Blanc for crab cake — more body than NZ expressions, more depth than Sancerre. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Creamed Spinach": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Creamy Potato": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — serious gun-flint structure, citrus, mineral. The flinty mineral plays against the soup's potato base and the high acid cuts the smooth cream. Works as Loire Sauv Blanc for creamy potato — gun-flint lift on the classic; Chardonnays peak higher.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Creme Brulee": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé for the steak course.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Escargot": "Pouilly-Fumé's flinty Loire Sauvignon Blanc — a smokier profile than Sancerre. Handles the escargot butter's herb-and-garlic complexity and hooks into the absinthe-finish's subtle anise note. The smoke character gives it an edge over cleaner Loire whites here. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Faroe Island Salmon": "Pouilly-Fumé's flinty Loire Sauvignon Blanc — gunflint smoke character hooks into the seared salmon exterior. More depth than Sancerre, tighter acid than Chablis. Good non-obvious white pour when the kitchen's running a grill-or-plancha prep on the Faroe. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Filet Mignon": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — serious Sauv Blanc with gun-flint, grapefruit, lime, fuller body. The body can handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the flint plays against escargot-butter toppers unconventionally. Works as serious-white-with-steak; classic reds peak higher for filet.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|French Onion": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cheese richness. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Grilled Caesar": "Pouilly-Fumé's flinty Loire Sauvignon Blanc — smoke hooks into the grill char on the romaine. More depth than Sancerre; the acid cuts our egg-yolk-emulsified dressing cleanly. Non-obvious white with a real flavor hook into the dish's char. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Gumbo": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity reads alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the crisp acidity sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|House Wedge": "Pouilly-Fumé — Loire Sauv Blanc with gun-flint minerality, grapefruit, lime zest. The serious body cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the smoky mineral plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong serious Sauv Blanc for the wedge — Loire sense-of-place on the steakhouse classic.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Kansas City": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé belongs on another course.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Loaded Potato": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity finds neutral with the loaded potato's richness — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Lobster Mac": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Market Fish": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity holds the line with the fish's clean flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Mocha Creme": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé for the steak course.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Mushroom Bisque": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Mushrooms": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Porterhouse": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- crisp white reads thin against 26+ ounces of cap-fat ribeye. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé for another course.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Prime Tartare": "Pouilly-Fumé — Loire Sauv Blanc, gun-flint minerality, grapefruit, lime. The smoky-mineral backbone and fuller body meet raw prime beef's iron richness and the citrus cuts through the seasoned tartare. Strong serious Sauv Blanc for tartare — sense-of-place wine on the sophisticated starter.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Rainbow Trout": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — serious gun-flint structure, citrus, mineral. The flinty mineral plays against trout's clean freshwater character and the citrus lift cuts the flaky flesh. Peak Loire Sauv Blanc for rainbow trout — gun-flint structure on the delicate classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Roast Half Chicken": "Pouilly-Fumé — Loire Sauv Blanc with gun-flint minerality, grapefruit, lime. The serious body handles roasted chicken; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as sophisticated Sauv Blanc for chicken across most seasonal accompaniments.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — serious gun-flint structure, citrus, mineral. The flinty mineral plays against halibut's salt-cure and the citrus lift cuts the soy glaze. Strong Loire Sauv Blanc for salt-cured halibut — gun-flint structure on the cured classic.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Pouilly-Fumé — Loire Sauv Blanc with gun-flint minerality, grapefruit, lime. The smoky-mineral backbone plays against the spinach's earth and the citrus cuts the sauté butter directly. Strong serious Sauv Blanc for the spinach side — flint-and-earth from the same tradition.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Seafood Tower": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the tower's chilled shellfish — the crisp acidity lifts the shellfish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Seared Scallops": "Loire Valley Sauvignon Blanc from Pouilly-Fumé — gun-flint smoky minerality beneath grapefruit and lime zest. Fuller and more serious than New Zealand Sauv Blanc. The flinty minerality frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the citrus cuts the browned edge. Peak serious Sauv Blanc for scallops. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Seasonal Soup": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity stays alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Seasonal Vegetables": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the seasonal garden plate — the crisp acidity sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Shrimp Bisque": "Pouilly-Fumé — Loire Sauv Blanc, gun-flint minerality, grapefruit, lime zest, fuller body than NZ. The smoky mineral cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and the citrus lifts the butter-roux weight. Peak serious Sauv Blanc for bisque — flint meets cream in a classical match. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Shrimp Cocktail": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — Sauv Blanc with gun-flint smoky minerality, grapefruit, lime zest. The serious-bodied flinty structure cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the citrus plays against cocktail-sauce bite. Strong Sauv Blanc for shrimp cocktail — more depth than NZ, more serious than Sancerre.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Swordfish": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|The Tomahawk": "Serious Loire Sauv Blanc against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even flinty Pouilly-Fumé structure can't meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Pouilly-Fumé for scallops, crab cake, bisque, or escargot.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Tomato Basil": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — serious gun-flint structure, citrus, mineral. The flinty mineral cuts tomato's acidity and the citrus lift plays against basil's aromatic edge. Peak Loire Sauv Blanc for tomato basil — gun-flint structure on the fresh-herb classic. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Truffle Fries": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's cool-climate white acidity leans against the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight: the bright acid lifts the truffle fries, and the citrus cuts the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity sits with the tuna's seared rare crust — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's citrus-mineral acidity leans against the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Asparagus": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's powerful blackcurrant body reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the structured body wraps the asparagus, and the cassis frames the side. Works; the opener sits without competing.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts gruyere-cream richness and the full body handles dairy. Works for au gratin — Muga's vanilla-leather fits the cheese harder.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Beignets": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Domaine du Grand Tinel belongs on the steak course.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Bone Marrow": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts roasted-marrow unctuousness and the black pepper plays with toasted-bread char. Peak Châteauneuf for bone marrow — GSM balances the richness. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Bone-In Filet": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the Cab structure frames the tenderloin, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Broccoli Cheddar": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Broccolini": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis sits alongside the broccolini's tender green — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; safe call, no friction.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Burrata": "Never pair Grand Tinel with burrata — garrigue, black pepper, and full body clash with fresh cream and the herbal earth buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Butternut Squash": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Carrot Cake": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Domaine du Grand Tinel lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Cheesecake": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Domaine du Grand Tinel belongs on the steak course.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Chilean Seabass": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Domaine du Grand Tinel for the steak course.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Chocolate Brownie": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Domaine du Grand Tinel for the steak course.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Chocolate Cake": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Domaine du Grand Tinel for the steak course.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Clam Chowder": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Cowboy Ribeye": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The full body matches Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the black pepper echoes grilled char. Peak Châteauneuf for ribeye — reliable GSM on the marbled cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Crab Cake": "Never pair Grand Tinel with crab cake — garrigue, black pepper, and full body bury delicate lump crab and the herbal earth fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Creamed Spinach": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The full body handles dairy-heavy cream and the herbal earth lifts spinach-iron. Strong Châteauneuf for creamed spinach — GSM polish on the rich side.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Creamy Potato": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis plays cleanly against the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Creme Brulee": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Domaine du Grand Tinel belongs on the steak course.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Escargot": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Faroe Island Salmon": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Domaine du Grand Tinel lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Filet Mignon": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the garrigue plays with escargot-butter. Strong Châteauneuf for filet — full body is bold but the cut handles it.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|French Onion": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the rye spice cuts the cheese richness. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Grilled Caesar": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body. The garrigue matches anchovy-lemon dressing and the black pepper cuts parm-char. Strong Châteauneuf for grilled caesar — Rhône herb hook on the salad.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Gumbo": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The garrigue-herb meets gumbo's bay-and-file aromatics directly and the full body handles the stew's weight. Peak CdP for gumbo — Rhône herbal structure on the Cajun classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis sits beside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; capable alongside. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|House Wedge": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body. The garrigue plays with blue-cheese funk and the black pepper matches bacon-fat char. Works for wedge — cold iceberg mutes the Châteauneuf complexity, so a lighter Chianti lifts higher.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Kansas City": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The black pepper echoes Kansas City's lean-bold strip char and the garrigue plays with grilled beef. Peak Châteauneuf for the strip — GSM full-body on the American cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Loaded Potato": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis finds neutral with the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Lobster Mac": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis sits beside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the opulent body matches the indulgent side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Market Fish": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Domaine du Grand Tinel belongs on the steak course.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Mocha Creme": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Domaine du Grand Tinel for the steak course.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Mushroom Bisque": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's powerful blackcurrant body leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the structured body wraps the mushroom bisque, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; the dessert closes without crowding.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the side's mushroom richness pulls the bold red's body; tannin and oak settle into the umami without overshooting. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Domaine du Grand Tinel on the steak course instead.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: strip-and-filet two-cut plate meets Domaine du Grand Tinel. Excellent; the Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône blend's earth-and-fruit balance handles both the strip's char and the filet's refinement at a single register. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Prime Tartare": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Rainbow Trout": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Domaine du Grand Tinel on the steak course instead.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Roast Half Chicken": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the pepper amplifies the spice. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Domaine du Grand Tinel on the steak course instead.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis reads alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Seafood Tower": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Domaine du Grand Tinel for the steak course.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Grand Tinel with seared scallops — black pepper and garrigue overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and full body fights browned-butter sweetness. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Seasonal Soup": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis leans against the seasonal soup's house build — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Seasonal Vegetables": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis finds neutral with the seasonal garden plate — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Shrimp Bisque": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis plays cleanly against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Grand Tinel with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by full body, black pepper, and garrigue, and the herbal earth has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Swordfish": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Domaine du Grand Tinel belongs on the steak course.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|The Tomahawk": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The full body handles Tomahawk's massive 36oz bone-in and the black pepper plays with smoky char. Strong Châteauneuf for Tomahawk — Scavino Barolo edges higher on the grand cut.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Tomato Basil": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright acid — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the bold red's cassis grip carries the mushroom-and-truffle depth — structured weight on the earthy side. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Domaine du Grand Tinel for the steak course.",
  "Domaine du Grand Tinel|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the pepper amplifies the spice. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Asparagus": "Don Fulano Anejo's rested-agave body finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the barrel-rested body handles the asparagus, and the oak threads the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: cheese-and-cream potatoes meet Don Fulano. Works; extended-aged complexity handles richness at Los-Altos register.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Beignets": "Don Fulano Anejo's rested-agave body leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the aged-agave weight wraps the beignets, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; clean but not a standout.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Bone Marrow": "Don Fulano Anejo's agave-apricot character finds register with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel softens the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Bone-In Filet": "Don Fulano Anejo's agave-apricot character sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the añejo weight grips the lean cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Broccoli Cheddar": "Don Fulano Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the oak-aged tequila body threads the broccoli cheddar, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Broccolini": "Don Fulano Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the oak-aged tequila body threads the broccolini, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed meets Don Fulano. Excellent; the extended-aged French-oak complexity rides the maple glaze at sophisticated-Los-Altos register.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Burrata": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character fights the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- agave and fresh dairy refuse each other texturally. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Don Fulano Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Butternut Squash": "Don Fulano Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the squash bisque, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Don Fulano. Strong; 3-year French-oak vanilla-spice amplifies cake spice at terroir-forward register.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Cheesecake": "Don Fulano Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the barrel-rested body handles the cheesecake, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; the wine-cask character adds dimension but not standout.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Chilean Seabass": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- agave and oily fish refuse each other — the fish needs acid, not aged spirit. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Don Fulano Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Don Fulano. Excellent; 3-year French-oak depth threads chocolate at sophisticated-aged register — longer aging, more chocolate depth.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Chocolate Cake": "Don Fulano Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the oak-aged tequila body threads the chocolate cake, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Clam Chowder": "Don Fulano Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the barrel-rested body handles the chowder, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Cowboy Ribeye": "Don Fulano Anejo's agave-apricot character frames the ribeye's cap fat and char — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Crab Cake": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Don Fulano Anejo on the steak course instead.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Creamed Spinach": "Don Fulano Anejo's agave-apricot character lifts the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the añejo weight overshoots the light side, and the oak frames the cream. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Creamy Potato": "Don Fulano Anejo's rested-agave body leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the añejo register carries the potato soup, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Creme Brulee": "Don Fulano Anejo's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rested-agave depth sits with the brulee, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Escargot": "Don Fulano Anejo's agave-apricot character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Faroe Island Salmon": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Don Fulano Anejo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: tender lean cut meets Don Fulano. Excellent; the Los Altos estate-terroir with extended French-oak aging frames filet at sophisticated register — more complex than Patron or Milagro.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|French Onion": "Don Fulano Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the aged-agave weight wraps the French onion, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; luxury ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Grilled Caesar": "Don Fulano Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the oak-aged tequila body threads the grilled Caesar, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; the wine-cask character adds dimension but not standout.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Gumbo": "Don Fulano Anejo's rested-agave body sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the gumbo, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Don Fulano Anejo's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the barrel-rested body handles the honey carrots, and the soft-spice note softens the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|House Wedge": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the oak-aged tequila body threads the wedge, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; accessible match.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Kansas City": "Don Fulano Anejo: Los Altos highland tequila, 100% estate-grown Blue Weber, 3 years in French oak — extended aging for añejo category. Excellent on KC strip; the sophisticated terroir-forward Los Altos character with 3-year French-oak complexity frames the lean cut at extended-aged register — this is the añejo for guests who want depth, not workhorse.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Loaded Potato": "Don Fulano Anejo's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the loaded potato soup, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Lobster Mac": "Don Fulano Anejo's rested-agave body sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak-aged tequila body threads the lobster mac, and the soft-spice note softens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Market Fish": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Don Fulano Anejo on the steak course instead.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Mocha Creme": "Don Fulano Anejo's rested-agave body reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the añejo register carries the mocha creme, and the soft-spice note softens the dessert. Works; ceremonial over-specified.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Mushroom Bisque": "Don Fulano Anejo's rested-agave body reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the barrel-rested body handles the mushroom bisque, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: earthy umami meets Don Fulano. Excellent; the pairing earns its featured slot.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Don Fulano Anejo's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the aged-agave weight wraps the PB brownie, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Porterhouse": "Don Fulano Anejo's agave-apricot character sits with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the aged-tequila depth grips the marbled cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Prime Tartare": "Don Fulano Anejo's rested-agave body rests at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the añejo register carries the tartare, and the oak threads the starter. Strong; multi-cask complexity sits with richness at collector-craft register.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Rainbow Trout": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- aged-agave weight buries the delicate flesh — agave register is wrong for fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Don Fulano Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Roast Half Chicken": "Don Fulano Anejo's rested-agave body sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the rested-agave depth sits with the roast chicken, and the caramel-agave register brightens the plate. Works; approachable agave cuts the glaze but not marquee.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Don Fulano Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the rested-agave depth sits with the chickpea soup, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- aged-agave weight buries the delicate flesh — agave register is wrong for fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Don Fulano Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Don Fulano Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the aged-agave weight wraps the garlic spinach, and the soft-spice note softens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Seafood Tower": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Don Fulano Anejo on the steak course instead.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Don Fulano. Avoid; aged-agave heavy for scallops. Reach for a blanco, light rum, or vodka.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Seasonal Soup": "Don Fulano Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the barrel-rested body handles the seasonal soup, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Seasonal Vegetables": "Don Fulano Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the añejo register carries the seasonal vegetables, and the soft-spice note softens the side. Works; Durango ceremony over-specified for small plate.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Shrimp Bisque": "Don Fulano Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register leans against the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the añejo register carries the shrimp bisque, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Shrimp Cocktail": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character smothers the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- aged-agave register crowds the delicate shellfish frame. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Don Fulano Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Swordfish": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the cask-aged agave register pulls against the savory sear. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; the better calls are above. Don Fulano Anejo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|The Tomahawk": "Don Fulano Anejo's agave-apricot character finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Tomato Basil": "Don Fulano Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the rested-agave depth sits with the tomato basil, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; 1942 aged depth is a touch over-specified for the small plate.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: Parmesan-and-truffle meets Don Fulano. Excellent; the 3-year French-oak complexity handles truffle-salt-fat at extended-aged terroir register.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the cask-aged agave register pulls against the savory sear. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Don Fulano Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Don Fulano Anejo|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Don Fulano Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the barrel-rested body handles the curry, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; brightness can cut the glaze but wheated bourbons sit better with this dish.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Asparagus": "Don Fulano Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the unaged body brightens the asparagus, and the unaged-cane note plays against the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Don Fulano Blanco's clean silver register reads alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the blanco-tequila body matches the au gratin, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Beignets": "Don Fulano Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a light spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Don Fulano Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Bone Marrow": "Don Fulano Blanco with bone marrow — refined craft against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms blanco at any register.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Bone-In Filet": "Don Fulano Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the silver-spirit register lifts the bone-enhanced filet, and the unaged-cane note plays against the cut. Works; blanco isn't the natural filet call even at premium craft register.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Broccoli Cheddar": "Don Fulano Blanco's clean silver register sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the unaged body brightens the broccoli cheddar, and the agave-citrus thread frames the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Broccolini": "Don Fulano Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the broccolini, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; refined craft composes cleanly.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Brussels and Belly": "Don Fulano Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge reads alongside Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the blanco-tequila body matches the pork-belly plate, and the bright blanco register underlines the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Burrata": "Don Fulano Blanco with burrata — highland craft blanco against fresh mozzarella. The clean refined agave lifts the burrata with premium craft elegance. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Butternut Squash": "Don Fulano Blanco's clean silver register reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the squash bisque, and the agave-citrus thread frames the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Carrot Cake": "Don Fulano Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a light spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Don Fulano Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Cheesecake": "Don Fulano Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Don Fulano Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Chilean Seabass": "Don Fulano Blanco's crisp agave-herbal character holds steady against the seabass's buttery flesh — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Chocolate Brownie": "Don Fulano Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light spirit can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a richer pour like an aged rum or tawny. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light spirit. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Don Fulano Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Chocolate Cake": "Don Fulano Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- light spirit can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a richer pour like an aged rum or tawny. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a light spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Don Fulano Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Clam Chowder": "Don Fulano Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the chowder, and the bright blanco register underlines the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Cowboy Ribeye": "Don Fulano Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Don Fulano Blanco for another course.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Crab Cake": "Don Fulano Blanco with crab cake — premium highland blanco, 80 proof, refined craft. The Jalisco-highland agave gives the crab a cleaner lift than Don Julio's benchmark; distinct sibling to Don Fulano Reposado in the blanco register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Creamed Spinach": "Don Fulano Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift reads alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the unaged body brightens the creamed spinach, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Creamy Potato": "Don Fulano Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the unaged body brightens the potato soup, and the green-agave edge brightens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Creme Brulee": "Don Fulano Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the spirit reads thin against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light spirit. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Don Fulano Blanco on the steak course instead.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Escargot": "Don Fulano Blanco with escargot — highland craft against garlic-butter snails. Strong; refined agave cuts the garlic-butter with craft elegance.",
  "Don Fulano Blanco|Faroe Island Salmon": "Don Fulano Blanco with the salmon — premium highland blanco against salmon's richness. The refined craft agave frames the fish elegantly; arguably the best blanco-salmon match alongside Don Julio Blanco and Mijenta. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
