  "Don Julio Reposado|Seasonal Vegetables": "Don Julio Reposado's cooked-agave-and-oak register leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the seasonal vegetables, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Don Julio Reposado|Shrimp Bisque": "Don Julio Reposado with shrimp bisque — reposado's agave against cream-based seafood. Strong; the brightness cuts through the dairy while the oak sits with the richness.",
  "Don Julio Reposado|Shrimp Cocktail": "Shrimp Cocktail: cold shrimp and cocktail sauce meet Don Julio Reposado's citrus-forward agave. Brightness threads through the cocktail sauce's horseradish. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Don Julio Reposado|Swordfish": "Don Julio Reposado's aged-agave character leans against the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the rested-agave depth sits with the swordfish, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the plate. Works; multi-cask over-specified for small plate.",
  "Don Julio Reposado|The Tomahawk": "Don Julio Reposado with the Tomahawk — 80-proof reposado against the bone-in ribeye's scale. Avoid; the cut overwhelms any reposado. Bourbon or scotch territory.",
  "Don Julio Reposado|Tomato Basil": "Don Julio Reposado's cooked-agave-and-oak register leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the barrel-rested body handles the tomato basil, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Don Julio Reposado|Truffle Fries": "Don Julio Reposado with truffle fries — reposado against truffle-parmesan. Works; the oak sits beside the truffle's earth, but this isn't the reposado's strongest match.",
  "Don Julio Reposado|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Don Julio Reposado's añejo-pour depth leans against the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the barrel-rested body handles the tuna, and the cooked-agave note underlines the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Don Julio Reposado|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Don Julio Reposado's aged-agave character finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the rested-agave depth sits with the curry, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; wheated-soft character handles richness at gentler-Expresiones register.",
  "Drambuie|Asparagus": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Drambuie for the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Drambuie on the steak course instead.",
  "Drambuie|Beignets": "Beignets: warm sugar-dusted pastry meets Drambuie. Excellent; the heather-honey liqueur plays with sugar-dust at scotch-honey register — unique pairing through the honey thread.",
  "Drambuie|Bone Marrow": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Drambuie belongs on the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Bone-In Filet": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Drambuie for another course.",
  "Drambuie|Broccoli Cheddar": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Drambuie on the steak course instead.",
  "Drambuie|Broccolini": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Drambuie on the steak course instead.",
  "Drambuie|Brussels and Belly": "Drambuie's sweet honey-scotch-herbal character lifts the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Drambuie|Burrata": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Drambuie for the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Butternut Squash": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Drambuie for the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Drambuie. Works; heather-honey-and-spiced-herbs amplify the cake's spice at scotch-liqueur register.",
  "Drambuie|Cheesecake": "Drambuie: Scotch whisky-based honey-herbal liqueur from Scotland, 40% ABV, heather-honey and spiced herbs on a scotch base. Excellent on cheesecake; the scotch-honey character threads cheesecake cream at Rusty-Nail register — this is a scotch-drinker's dessert move.",
  "Drambuie|Chilean Seabass": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Drambuie lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Drambuie|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Drambuie. Excellent; the Scotch-whisky base with heather-honey threads chocolate at Scottish-digestif register — honey-whisky on cocoa.",
  "Drambuie|Chocolate Cake": "Drambuie's herbal-and-sugar liqueur register sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the digestif body mirrors the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Drambuie|Clam Chowder": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Drambuie belongs on the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Cowboy Ribeye": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Drambuie for another course.",
  "Drambuie|Crab Cake": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Drambuie for the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Creamed Spinach": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Drambuie lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Drambuie|Creamy Potato": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Drambuie on the steak course instead.",
  "Drambuie|Creme Brulee": "Creme Brulee: caramelized custard meets Drambuie. Excellent; the pairing earns its featured slot.",
  "Drambuie|Escargot": "Escargot: garlic-herb butter meets Drambuie. Excellent; the spiced-herbal profile of Drambuie engages the garlic-herb butter at scotch-base register — a herbal-spice counterpoint unique to this liqueur.",
  "Drambuie|Faroe Island Salmon": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Drambuie on the steak course instead.",
  "Drambuie|Filet Mignon": "Drambuie's syrupy-sweet liqueur body finds neutral with the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the after-dinner pour body sits with the lean buttery filet, and the herbal-sugar edge threads the cut. Works; the side sits without pulling focus.",
  "Drambuie|French Onion": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Drambuie for the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: char-romaine with anchovy-parmesan meets Drambuie. Excellent; the scotch-whisky base brings smoke-adjacent depth to char-and-brine at honey-herbal register.",
  "Drambuie|Gumbo": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Drambuie lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Drambuie|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Drambuie for the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|House Wedge": "Drambuie's sweet honey-scotch-herbal character lifts the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the honey threads the cream. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Drambuie|Kansas City": "Drambuie's after-dinner liqueur weight leans against the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the digestif body mirrors the strip steak, and the digestif sweetness softens the cut. Works; the close holds, neither soars nor fights.",
  "Drambuie|Loaded Potato": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Drambuie belongs on the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Lobster Mac": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Drambuie lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Drambuie|Market Fish": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Drambuie belongs on the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Mocha Creme": "Drambuie's sweet honey-scotch-herbal character holds steady against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the honey threads the cream. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Drambuie|Mushroom Bisque": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Drambuie for the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Mushrooms": "Drambuie's sweet honey-scotch-herbal character frames the mushrooms' earthy umami — the sweet character sits at digestif register. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Drambuie|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Drambuie's sweet honey-scotch-herbal character holds the line with the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Drambuie|Porterhouse": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Drambuie belongs on another course.",
  "Drambuie|Prime Tartare": "Drambuie's herbal-and-sugar liqueur register leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the herbal-sweet weight matches the tartare, and the caramel underlines the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Drambuie|Rainbow Trout": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Drambuie belongs on the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Roast Half Chicken": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Drambuie lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Drambuie|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Drambuie belongs on the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Drambuie for the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Drambuie on the steak course instead.",
  "Drambuie|Seafood Tower": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sugar buries the delicate shellfish without any acid to balance. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Drambuie lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Drambuie|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Drambuie. Avoid; scotch-whisky-honey liqueur wrong for scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Drambuie|Seasonal Soup": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Drambuie for the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Seasonal Vegetables": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Drambuie lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Drambuie|Shrimp Bisque": "Drambuie's sweet honey-scotch-herbal character holds steady against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the honey threads the cream. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Drambuie|Shrimp Cocktail": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Drambuie lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Drambuie|Swordfish": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Drambuie lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Drambuie|The Tomahawk": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character flattens the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Drambuie on another course instead.",
  "Drambuie|Tomato Basil": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Drambuie on the steak course instead.",
  "Drambuie|Truffle Fries": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Drambuie for the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- sugar dulls the crust's clean char instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Drambuie belongs on the steak course.",
  "Drambuie|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Drambuie for the steak course.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Asparagus": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the asparagus, and the gentle barley note frames the side. Works; AE Triple Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the au gratin, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; Remus 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Beignets": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the Irish whiskey body sits with the beignets, and the soft malt rounds the dessert. Works; BIB slightly over-specified.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets Dunville's. Excellent; revived Belfast Irish single-malt brings craft-heritage character to the marrow plate at Irish-single-malt register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Bone-In Filet": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the malt-grain blend handles the bone-enhanced filet, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the cut. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Broccoli Cheddar": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the broccoli cheddar, and the oak softens the cream. Works; Caribbean Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Broccolini": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the approachable Irish character carries the broccolini, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the side. Works; bold character slightly over-specified for the soup.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Brussels and Belly": "Dunville's Single Malt with Brussels + Belly — Belfast Irish single malt against maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; Dunville's character meets the glaze at Northern Ireland register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Burrata": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bourbon weight buries the milky cheese plate's delicate frame. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Dunville's Single Malt for the steak course.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Butternut Squash": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the Irish whiskey body sits with the squash bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Carrot Cake": "Dunville's Single Malt with carrot cake — Belfast Irish single malt against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; Dunville's character threads the cake at distinctive Irish-single-malt register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Cheesecake": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the cheesecake, and the oak softens the cream. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Chilean Seabass": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Dunville's Single Malt for the steak course.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Chocolate Brownie": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the brownie, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the dessert. Works; aged premium slightly over-specified.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Chocolate Cake": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the malt-grain blend handles the chocolate cake, and the grain register softens the dessert. Works; curated character over-specified.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Clam Chowder": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the Irish whiskey body sits with the chowder, and the oak softens the cream. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Cowboy Ribeye": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the gentle Irish body threads the cap-fat ribeye, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the cut. Works; bonded weight pushes slightly past the soup.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Crab Cake": "Dunville's Single Malt with crab cake — Irish single malt against delicate lump crab. Works; Irish whiskey's category advantage lets Dunville's sit with crab at distinctive register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Creamed Spinach": "Dunville's Single Malt with creamed spinach — Northern Ireland single malt against dairy-rich side. Works; Dunville's Echlinville character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Creamy Potato": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; multi-distillery complexity doesn't drive.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Creme Brulee": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the Irish whiskey body sits with the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Escargot": "Dunville's Single Malt's crisp char-malt character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Faroe Island Salmon": "Faroe Island Salmon: oily salmon meets Belfast Irish single malt. Dunville's Northern Ireland character + Irish-single-malt refinement is a distinctive Irish-salmon pairing — Garavogue brings western-Ireland craft, Dunville's brings northern-Ireland single-malt polish. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Filet Mignon": "Dunville's Single Malt with the filet — Echlinville Distillery Belfast single malt against tenderloin. Excellent; Dunville's is the Irish-single-malt filet pairing — where Redbreast 12 brings pot still richness, Dunville's brings single-malt refinement at Northern Ireland register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|French Onion": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the gentle Irish body threads the French onion, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the bowl. Works; single-barrel depth gives the dessert more than Small Batch.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: charred romaine and anchovy meet Echlinville Irish single malt. Strong; Dunville's meets the char at distinctive Irish-single-malt register; Tullamore's drier blend still leads the entry-Irish-Caesar pairing.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Gumbo": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the Irish whiskey body sits with the gumbo, and the soft malt rounds the bowl. Works; OGD 16 frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|House Wedge": "Dunville's Single Malt with the House Wedge — Belfast Irish single malt against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Dunville's Northern Ireland character threads the wedge at distinctive register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Kansas City": "Dunville's Single Malt with the KC — Irish single malt against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Dunville's Belfast Irish-single-malt character handles strip marbling at distinctive register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Loaded Potato": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the approachable Irish character carries the loaded potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; regional craft at matched register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Lobster Mac": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the approachable Irish character carries the lobster mac, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for a side — save the bottle for the main course.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Market Fish": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Dunville's Single Malt for the steak course.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Mocha Creme": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the approachable Irish character carries the mocha creme, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the Millennium's register overshoots the dessert's.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Mushroom Bisque": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the gentle Irish body threads the mushroom bisque, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Works; sweet character distinctive but not driving.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Mushrooms": "Dunville's Single Malt with mushrooms — Northern Ireland single malt against earthy umami. Excellent; Dunville's Echlinville character threads mushroom earthiness at distinctive Irish-single-malt register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the Irish whiskey body sits with the PB brownie, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the dessert. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Porterhouse": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the approachable Irish character carries the dual-cut steak, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the cut. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Prime Tartare": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness reads register-aligned with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the tartare, and the oak threads the umami plate. Strong; Fusion complexity is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character for this cut; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Rainbow Trout": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Dunville's Single Malt for the steak course.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Roast Half Chicken": "Dunville's Single Malt with the chicken — Northern Ireland single malt from Echlinville Distillery (Belfast), a genuine Irish single malt (reclassified from American Single Malt / Tennessee — Dunville's is Belfast-based). Excellent; Dunville's Irish-single-malt character frames chicken with Northern Ireland provenance — distinctive among the Irish program, where Redbreast is pot still, Jameson is blended, and Dunville's is single malt.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the chickpea soup, and the soft malt rounds the bowl. Works; save the bottle for the protein course — a soup is wrong for a $200 pour.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Dunville's Single Malt on the steak course instead.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the gentle Irish body threads the garlic spinach, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the side. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Seafood Tower": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Dunville's Single Malt for the steak course.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Seared Scallops": "Dunville's Single Malt with seared scallops — Belfast Irish single malt against scallop delicacy. Works; Dunville's is gentle enough for scallops within Irish whiskey's category advantage.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Seasonal Soup": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the Irish whiskey body sits with the seasonal soup, and the gentle barley note frames the bowl. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Seasonal Vegetables": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the approachable Irish character carries the seasonal vegetables, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the side. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Shrimp Bisque": "Dunville's Single Malt with shrimp bisque — Northern Ireland single malt against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Dunville's Echlinville character rides the bisque at distinctive register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Shrimp Cocktail": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness overshadows the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Dunville's Single Malt on the steak course instead.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Swordfish": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Dunville's Single Malt for the steak course.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|The Tomahawk": "Dunville's Single Malt with the Tomahawk — Irish single malt against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; Irish whiskey is too light for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Tomato Basil": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the tomato basil, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the bowl. Works; boutique over-specified.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Truffle Fries": "Dunville's Single Malt with truffle fries — Belfast Irish single malt against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Dunville's meets the earthy-rich at distinctive register.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Dunville's Single Malt for the steak course.",
  "Dunville's Single Malt|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the Irish whiskey body sits with the curry, and the soft malt rounds the bowl. Works; the boldness pushes slightly past what the soup asks for.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Asparagus": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sweet-oak depth has nothing to bridge into green vegetable freshness. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the E.H. Taylor Full Proof on the steak course instead.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Au Gratin Potatoes": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the au gratin — BT Bottled-in-Bond against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; EHT Full Proof handles the richness at historic Kentucky register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Beignets": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the caramel-spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Bone Marrow": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with bone marrow — BT BiB 100-proof against umami fat. Excellent; EHT Full Proof engages marrow at historic Kentucky register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Bone-In Filet": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's cask-strength oak-spice character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Broccoli Cheddar": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; bold proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Broccolini": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the bourbon's register is wrong for a clean vegetable side — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. E.H. Taylor Full Proof belongs on the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Brussels and Belly": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with Brussels + Belly — Col. E.H. Taylor Jr. tribute against maple-glazed pork belly. Excellent; EHT Full Proof meets the glaze at historic Kentucky register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Burrata": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with burrata — Col. E.H. Taylor Jr. tribute against fresh dairy. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates burrata.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Butternut Squash": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon weight handles the squash bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Portwood is over-specified for sides.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Carrot Cake": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with carrot cake — BT BiB against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; EHT Full Proof meets the cake at historic Kentucky register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Cheesecake": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with cheesecake — BT BiB against custard. Avoid; barrel-proof intensity wrong for custard's delicate character.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Chilean Seabass": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the E.H. Taylor Full Proof for the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Chocolate Brownie": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the chocolate brownie — BT BiB 100-proof against cocoa. Excellent; EHT Full Proof meets cocoa at historic Kentucky-bourbon register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Chocolate Cake": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Clam Chowder": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the oak depth lays under the chowder, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the toast notes don't really interplay with the soup but don't clash.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets BT Bottled-in-Bond at 100 proof (barrel-entry proof), Col. E.H. Taylor Jr. tribute line honoring historic Kentucky bourbon. EHT Full Proof's BiB 100-proof character is the historic-Kentucky-tribute-ribeye pairing — less intensity than 130-proof Staggs, more traditional Kentucky register. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Crab Cake": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with crab cake — BT Bottled-in-Bond against delicate lump crab. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Creamed Spinach": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with creamed spinach — Col. E.H. Taylor Jr. tribute against dairy-rich side. Strong; EHT Full Proof cuts the cream at historic Kentucky register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Creamy Potato": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; bold intensity slightly over-specified.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Creme Brulee": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Escargot": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's cask-strength oak-spice character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Faroe Island Salmon": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the salmon — BT Bottled-in-Bond against oily salmon. Avoid; barrel-proof bourbon too intense for fish.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Filet Mignon": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the filet — BT BiB 100-proof against tenderloin. Works; EHT Full Proof is a more balanced option for filet than cask-strength BT siblings — not over-specified but barrel-proof category still prefers the ribeye.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|French Onion": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; the pour's assertiveness is a bit much for the soup but doesn't clash.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Grilled Caesar": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the grilled Caesar — Col. E.H. Taylor Jr. tribute against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; EHT Full Proof is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Gumbo": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the gumbo, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; the single-barrel depth is over-specified for this dish.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Honey Roasted Carrots": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth doubles down on the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sweet-oak and a sweet glaze double the sugar register with no contrast. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the E.H. Taylor Full Proof for the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|House Wedge": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the wedge, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Kansas City": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the KC — Col. E.H. Taylor Jr. tribute BiB against an 18oz strip. Excellent; EHT Full Proof's 100-proof Bottled-in-Bond handles strip marbling with historic Kentucky register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Loaded Potato": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the brown-spirit warmth threads the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; BIB structure is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Lobster Mac": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the lobster mac, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; bold proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Market Fish": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the E.H. Taylor Full Proof on the steak course instead.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Mocha Creme": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mocha creme, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the sweetness is a touch much for the salad's simplicity.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Mushroom Bisque": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for this dish's register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Mushrooms": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with mushrooms — Col. E.H. Taylor Jr. tribute against earthy umami. Strong; EHT Full Proof threads mushrooms at historic Kentucky-bourbon register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet BT Bottled-in-Bond 100-proof. Strong; EHT Full Proof handles dessert density at historic Kentucky register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Porterhouse": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the porterhouse — BT Bottled-in-Bond against strip-and-filet. Strong; EHT Full Proof frames the two-cut plate at historic Kentucky register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Prime Tartare": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's cask-strength oak-spice character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Rainbow Trout": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the E.H. Taylor Full Proof for the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Roast Half Chicken": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the chicken — Col. E.H. Taylor Jr. tribute against roasted poultry. Avoid; barrel-proof is over-specified for chicken.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak depth lays under the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Salt-Cured Halibut": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the E.H. Taylor Full Proof for the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overshadows the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- sweet-oak depth has nothing to bridge into green vegetable freshness. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. E.H. Taylor Full Proof lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Seafood Tower": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the E.H. Taylor Full Proof for the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Seared Scallops": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with seared scallops — BT BiB against scallop delicacy. Avoid; barrel-proof wrong for scallops.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Seasonal Soup": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Seasonal Vegetables": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the side reads small under oak-and-vanilla density. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the E.H. Taylor Full Proof on the steak course instead.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Shrimp Bisque": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Shrimp Cocktail": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with shrimp cocktail — BT BiB against cold shrimp. Avoid; barrel-proof wrong for cold seafood.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Swordfish": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the E.H. Taylor Full Proof for the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|The Tomahawk": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the Tomahawk — BT BiB 100-proof against 32oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; EHT Full Proof handles the Tomahawk with historic Kentucky-bourbon register — where Staggs bring 130-proof intensity, EHT brings 100-proof BiB tradition.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Tomato Basil": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon body sits with the tomato basil, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; allocated character doesn't drive.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Truffle Fries": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with truffle fries — BT BiB against truffle-parmesan. Strong; EHT Full Proof meets the earthy-rich at historic Kentucky register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the E.H. Taylor Full Proof on the steak course instead.",
  "E.H. Taylor Full Proof|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon body sits with the curry, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; the toast adds a warmth the dessert can accept.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Asparagus": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the high-rye spice register matches the asparagus, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; Gran Cru's brioche meets the glaze at refined register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Au Gratin Potatoes": "E.H. Taylor Rye with au gratin potatoes — bonded BT rye against cheese-and-cream. Works; BIB over-specified for side.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Beignets": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; EC 18 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Bone Marrow": "E.H. Taylor Rye with bone marrow — 100-proof BIB BT rye against umami fat. The bonded weight and aging meet marrow's intensity with premium authority; the BT-side premium BIB rye for marrow. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Bone-In Filet": "E.H. Taylor Rye's bold pepper-spice character frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey character holds register with the tenderloin, and the agave-pepper carries the char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Broccoli Cheddar": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the dry rye body wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Broccolini": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the rye backbone drives the broccolini, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the side. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Brussels and Belly": "E.H. Taylor Rye with Brussels + Belly — 100-proof BIB rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; bonded weight cuts the glaze with BIB authority.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Burrata": "E.H. Taylor Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against bonded BT rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Butternut Squash": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the rye spice cuts the squash bisque, and the oak softens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Carrot Cake": "E.H. Taylor Rye with carrot cake — 100-proof BIB rye against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; the bonded rye spice and cake spice find each other at premium register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Cheesecake": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the high-rye spice register matches the cheesecake, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; JD Bonded's backbone threads the cake at Bonded register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Chilean Seabass": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. E.H. Taylor Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Chocolate Brownie": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the dry rye body wraps the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; 10-year depth overshoots delicacy.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Chocolate Cake": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rye spice cuts the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the single-barrel depth is slightly over-specified for the dessert.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Clam Chowder": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the rye-and-oak weight handles the chowder, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Cowboy Ribeye": "E.H. Taylor Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof BIB rye against cap fat. The bonded weight handles the ribeye with authority; the BT-side BIB rye for this cut, alongside Knob Creek Rye's Beam take. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Crab Cake": "E.H. Taylor Rye with crab cake — 100-proof BIB against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Creamed Spinach": "E.H. Taylor Rye with creamed spinach — 100-proof BIB against dairy. Strong; bonded structure sits with richness at premium register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Creamy Potato": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the dry rye body wraps the potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; bold proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Creme Brulee": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; boutique character doesn't drive.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Escargot": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character sits at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the dry rye body wraps the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; the call holds its register at the table against the cut; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Faroe Island Salmon": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. E.H. Taylor Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Filet Mignon": "E.H. Taylor Rye with the filet — 100-proof BIB rye against the 10oz cut. The bonded aged character gives the filet a premium rye frame; distinct from non-bonded ryes by weight and historical significance. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|French Onion": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the high-rye spice register matches the French onion, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; narrative-bourbon doesn't drive.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Grilled Caesar": "E.H. Taylor Rye with the grilled Caesar — BT's bonded rye against charred romaine. The 100-proof bonded character earns the Caesar with premium BIB rye authority. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Gumbo": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the dry rye body wraps the gumbo, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Honey Roasted Carrots": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the high-rye spice register matches the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; aged premium slightly over-specified.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|House Wedge": "E.H. Taylor Rye with the House Wedge — bonded BT rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; BIB weight cuts the bleu with premium authority.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Kansas City": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets E.H. Taylor Rye — BT's 100-proof BIB rye, honoring Edmund Haynes Taylor Jr. Premium aged BIB rye; sister to E.H. Taylor Small Batch Bourbon. Bonded weight gives more grip than 90-proof Sazerac Rye. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Loaded Potato": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the rye backbone drives the loaded potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; EHT Full Proof handles the richness at historic Kentucky register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Lobster Mac": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the rye spice cuts the lobster mac, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; aging wasted on the salad.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Market Fish": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the E.H. Taylor Rye for the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Mocha Creme": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye backbone drives the mocha creme, and the oak softens the cream. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Mushroom Bisque": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the high-rye spice register matches the mushroom bisque, and the oak depth meets the umami. Works; novelty maturation at matched register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Mushrooms": "E.H. Taylor Rye with the mushrooms — bonded BT rye against earthy umami. Strong; BIB depth layers into mushroom character at premium register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye-and-oak weight handles the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; neutral companion.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Porterhouse": "E.H. Taylor Rye with the porterhouse — 100-proof BIB across strip and filet. Strong; bonded weight leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Prime Tartare": "E.H. Taylor Rye with the tartare — premium BIB BT rye against raw beef. The bonded aged character is a premium counterpoint to tartare at the BIB register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Rainbow Trout": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the E.H. Taylor Rye for the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the rye-and-oak weight handles the roast chicken, and the rye-pepper note underlines the plate. Works; collector-bottle doesn't drive.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the rye-and-oak weight handles the chickpea soup, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the bowl. Works; the intensity overpowers this dish.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the E.H. Taylor Rye for the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the high-rye spice register matches the garlic spinach, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; the richer pour finds more with the dessert than Original brings.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Seafood Tower": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the E.H. Taylor Rye for the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Seared Scallops": "E.H. Taylor Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 100-proof BIB. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Seasonal Soup": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the rye spice cuts the seasonal soup, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; AE meets the char at wine-finish register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye backbone drives the seasonal vegetables, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; the bourbon-and-side match holds — neither soars nor fights.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "E.H. Taylor Rye with shrimp bisque — 100-proof BIB against cream-based seafood. Works; bonded weight slightly over-specified.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "E.H. Taylor Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against 100-proof BIB rye. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Swordfish": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. E.H. Taylor Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|The Tomahawk": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character meets at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the rye-and-oak weight handles the 36oz showpiece, and the rye-grain bite cuts the cut. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Tomato Basil": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the tomato basil, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; boutique-sourced wasted on salad.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Truffle Fries": "E.H. Taylor Rye with truffle fries — 100-proof BIB rye against truffle-parmesan. The bonded weight gives the side real grip at premium BT register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. E.H. Taylor Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "E.H. Taylor Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the high-rye spice register matches the curry, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Asparagus": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the caramel-spice register matches the asparagus, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; neutral companion.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Au Gratin Potatoes": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with au gratin potatoes — 100-proof BIB against cheese-and-cream. Strong; bonded weight at matched premium register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Beignets": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon depth settles into the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Bone Marrow": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with bone marrow — 100-proof BIB against umami fat. The bonded weight and rich oak meet the marrow's intensity head-on; the premium BIB marrow pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Bone-In Filet": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak-and-spice register settles the cut. Strong; clean match at the register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Broccoli Cheddar": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon backbone carries the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Broccolini": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon depth settles into the broccolini, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; X Commandments story wasted on salad.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Brussels and Belly": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with Brussels + Belly — bonded 100-proof meets maple-glazed pork belly. The oak-forward richness cuts the glaze with authority. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Burrata": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with burrata — fresh dairy against bonded 100-proof. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Butternut Squash": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon weight handles the squash bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the unique maturation story adds interest to a gentle dessert match.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Carrot Cake": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with carrot cake — bonded BT against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; BIB slightly over-specified.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Cheesecake": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with cheesecake — bonded BT against clean custard. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Chilean Seabass": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the E.H. Taylor Small Batch on the steak course instead.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Chocolate Brownie": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the chocolate brownie — 100-proof BIB against cocoa. Works; bonded depth meets the brownie cleanly.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Chocolate Cake": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the brown-spirit warmth threads the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; AE CS meets the char at premium wine-finish register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Clam Chowder": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon depth settles into the chowder, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; AE's softer-oak rides the cream at wine-finish register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Cowboy Ribeye": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof BIB against cap fat and bold char. The bonded richness earns the ribeye with authority; the BT-side BIB for this cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Crab Cake": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with crab cake — 100-proof BIB against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Creamed Spinach": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with creamed spinach — bonded 100-proof against dairy. Strong; BIB structure sits with the richness.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Creamy Potato": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Creme Brulee": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the Twin Oak's depth slightly overshoots the dessert's simplicity.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Escargot": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's caramel-honey character holds the line with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Faroe Island Salmon": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the E.H. Taylor Small Batch on the steak course instead.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Filet Mignon": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the filet — Buffalo Trace's bottled-in-bond honoring Edmund Haynes Taylor Jr., the father of modern bourbon regulation. 100-proof BIB, rich caramel and vanilla, oak-forward. Distinct from Blanton's single-barrel approach — E.H. Taylor is the BT premium BIB, heavier and more oak-driven. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|French Onion": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the oak depth lays under the French onion, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side; save the rare bottle for the main.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Grilled Caesar": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the grilled Caesar — bonded BT against charred romaine. Strong; BIB earns the Caesar where 90-proof single-barrels only work.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Gumbo": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon backbone carries the gumbo, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; aged premium slightly over-specified.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Honey Roasted Carrots": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; the pour's sweetness meets the dessert without pushing past.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|House Wedge": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the House Wedge — 100-proof BIB against iceberg and bleu. Works; bonded weight overpowers the salad.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Kansas City": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the KC — 100-proof BIB against the strip's marbling. The bonded weight and rich oak carry the cut where 90-proof BT pours can't; a premium BIB strip call. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Loaded Potato": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; the pour's toasted warmth threads through the char without overpowering.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Lobster Mac": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the brown-spirit warmth threads the lobster mac, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; own-plus-sourced over-specified.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Market Fish": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. E.H. Taylor Small Batch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Mocha Creme": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon weight handles the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the aging is wasted on the salad.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Mushroom Bisque": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the oak depth lays under the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Mushrooms": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the mushrooms — bonded BT against earthy umami. The rich oak depth layers into mushroom character at the premium BIB register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Peanut Butter Brownie": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the PB brownie — 100-proof BIB against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Porterhouse": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the porterhouse — 100-proof BIB across strip and filet. Strong; bonded weight leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Prime Tartare": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's caramel-honey character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Rainbow Trout": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. E.H. Taylor Small Batch belongs on the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Roast Half Chicken": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon body sits with the roast chicken, and the oak frames the plate. Works; the bourbon-and-dessert match settles as a soft alongside.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the caramel-spice register matches the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Salt-Cured Halibut": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the E.H. Taylor Small Batch on the steak course instead.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon body sits with the garlic spinach, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; annual-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Seafood Tower": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the E.H. Taylor Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Seared Scallops": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 100-proof BIB. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Seasonal Soup": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon body sits with the seasonal soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; Remus 15 handles dessert density at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Seasonal Vegetables": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon backbone carries the seasonal vegetables, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; boutique character slightly over-specified.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Shrimp Bisque": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the bourbon weight handles the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Shrimp Cocktail": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against 100-proof BIB. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Swordfish": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. E.H. Taylor Small Batch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|The Tomahawk": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the Tomahawk — 100-proof BIB against bone-in ribeye. Strong; the bonded weight handles the cut's scale where 90-proof single-barrels fade — E.H. Taylor and Blanton's Gold are the two bigger-impact BT calls for the Tomahawk.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Tomato Basil": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; bonded weight pushes slightly past the soup.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Truffle Fries": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with truffle fries — 100-proof BIB against truffle-parmesan. The rich caramel and oak give the side more grip than 90-proof BT pours bring. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the E.H. Taylor Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "E.H. Taylor Small Batch|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Asparagus": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon backbone carries the asparagus, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; boutique-craft doesn't drive.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: cheese and cream richness meet Eagle Rare 10 Year's refined oak. Strong; balance against richness.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Beignets": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon weight handles the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: the rich umami fat meets Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged oak and vanilla. Ten years of barrel influence carries marrow without being heavy; refined rather than brawling. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Bone-In Filet": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth supports the lean cut without overpowering, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Broccoli Cheddar": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the oak depth lays under the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Broccolini": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the aged-oak depth threads the broccolini, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet Eagle Rare 10 Year's oak and spice. Structured enough to frame the dish's sweetness without being outsweetened. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Burrata": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with burrata — fresh dairy can't carry Buffalo Trace's aged oak. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Butternut Squash": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak depth lays under the squash bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Carrot Cake": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with carrot cake — aged oak and vanilla meet cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; a confident match across the table. Non-wheated age does different things with dessert.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Cheesecake": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with cheesecake — custard clean meets ten years of Buffalo Trace oak. Works; neutral, not elevating.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Chilean Seabass": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Eagle Rare 10 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa and fudge meet Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged oak and vanilla. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Chocolate Cake": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the caramel-spice register matches the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Clam Chowder": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the aged-oak depth threads the chowder, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cream. Works; richer profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Cowboy Ribeye": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with the Cowboy Ribeye — ten years of Buffalo Trace aging carries the ribeye's cap fat and char in a way that honors the cut. A premium non-wheated choice; more structure than the Wellers here. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Crab Cake": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with crab cake — aged bourbon weight against delicate crab. Avoid; reach for a crisp white wine.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Creamed Spinach": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with creamed spinach — aged oak against dairy richness. Strong; the 10-year's structure sits beside the side without being sweet the way wheated bourbons are.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Creamy Potato": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the light body doesn't overshoot the salad.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Creme Brulee": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Escargot": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak character holds steady against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Faroe Island Salmon": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Eagle Rare 10 Year for the steak course.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: the 10oz center-cut's clean restraint meets Eagle Rare 10 Year's balanced oak and ten years of Buffalo Trace aging. Distinct from the wheated Wellers and Maker's here — more oak spice, less honey. The non-wheated premium call. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|French Onion": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the aged-oak depth threads the French onion, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; boutique-blend depth gives composed dessert character.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Grilled Caesar": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy meet ten years of balanced oak. The Caesar earns the 10-year's refinement; strong. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Gumbo": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the caramel-spice register matches the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; AE meets the char at wine-finish register.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for the side — save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|House Wedge": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with House Wedge — iceberg simplicity against aged oak is a mismatch of ambition. Works only as a separate sip.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Kansas City": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with the KC — the strip's char meets ten years of Buffalo Trace barrel influence. Different from the Wellers here: more oak spice, less wheat sweetness. The non-wheated premium aged pour for strip orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Loaded Potato": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon body sits with the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Lobster Mac": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the caramel-spice register matches the lobster mac, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the premium pour is wasted on the salad's simplicity.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Market Fish": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Eagle Rare 10 Year for the steak course.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Mocha Creme": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon depth settles into the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; boutique at matched register.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Mushroom Bisque": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon backbone carries the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Mushrooms": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with the mushrooms — aged complexity meets umami depth. The 10-year's balance lets the mushrooms lead; distinct from wheated takes on this side — more oak-driven, less honey. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon weight handles the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Porterhouse": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with the porterhouse — the 10-year's oak and balance handle both strip and filet sides cleanly. The versatile premium call for this two-cut plate; Weller Millennium is the wheated alternate. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Prime Tartare": "Eagle Rare 10 Year -- BTAC adjacent, aged oak, caramel. The caramel-spice register matches the tartare, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Strong; aged-sourced sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Rainbow Trout": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Eagle Rare 10 Year for the steak course.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Roast Half Chicken": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon backbone carries the roast chicken, and the rounded sweetness brightens the plate. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the chickpea soup, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Eagle Rare 10 Year for the steak course.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the brown-spirit warmth threads the garlic spinach, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; multi-distillery complexity doesn't drive.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Seafood Tower": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Eagle Rare 10 Year for the steak course.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Seared Scallops": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with seared scallops — the 10-year's oak buries scallop delicacy. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Seasonal Soup": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the seasonal soup, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Seasonal Vegetables": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal vegetables, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Shrimp Bisque": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood meets aged oak. Works; the 10-year holds without lifting.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Shrimp Cocktail": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with shrimp cocktail — cold poached shrimp meets aged oak. Avoid; separate courses.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Swordfish": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Eagle Rare 10 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|The Tomahawk": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Tomato Basil": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon weight handles the tomato basil, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; Fortuna handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Truffle Fries": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with truffle fries — Buffalo Trace's aged elegance against truffle-parmesan earthiness. The 10-year's oak gives truffle the frame it needs; clean, not sweet the way wheated bourbons are here. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Eagle Rare 10 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Eagle Rare 10 Year|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the curry, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Asparagus": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Beignets": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Eastern Kille Dry Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Bone Marrow": "Eastern Kille with bone marrow — classic London Dry against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Eastern Kille Dry Gin for another course.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Broccolini": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals stays alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Burrata": "Burrata: fresh dairy meets Eastern Kille Distilling, Grand Rapids, classic London Dry style, small-batch family-operated. Eastern Kille's juniper-citrus classic character frames burrata with Grand Rapids hometown distinction — the Grand Rapids-pride gin pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Butternut Squash": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Carrot Cake": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- juniper-citrus and warm cake spices pull in opposite directions. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Eastern Kille Dry Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Cheesecake": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Eastern Kille Dry Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals lifts the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Eastern Kille Dry Gin for the steak course.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Eastern Kille Dry Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Clam Chowder": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Eastern Kille Dry Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Crab Cake": "Eastern Kille with crab cake — Grand Rapids classic London Dry against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Eastern Kille's juniper-forward character lifts crab with Grand Rapids hometown pride — the local-pride pick parallel to Long Road in the GR program.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Creamy Potato": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Creme Brulee": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Eastern Kille Dry Gin for the steak course.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Escargot": "Eastern Kille with escargot — classic London Dry Grand Rapids craft against garlic-butter snails. Excellent; Eastern Kille's juniper-forward classic profile is the featured Local MI Gin-escargot pairing — small-batch family-operated character that cuts butter like the British classics do.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Eastern Kille with the salmon — Grand Rapids classic juniper against oily salmon. Strong; Eastern Kille's London Dry style frames the fish at GR-craft register; Iron Fish's northern-Michigan farm profile is still the distinctive Local MI Gin-salmon match.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Filet Mignon": "Eastern Kille with the filet — classic London Dry GR craft against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|French Onion": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Eastern Kille with the grilled Caesar — classic London Dry against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Eastern Kille's juniper meets the char at GR-craft register; Knickerbocker's hop-influenced profile is the featured Local MI Gin-Caesar match.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Gumbo": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals sits beside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals plays cleanly against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|House Wedge": "Eastern Kille with the House Wedge — classic GR London Dry against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Eastern Kille threads the wedge with juniper-citrus hometown distinction.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Kansas City": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Eastern Kille Dry Gin for another course.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Loaded Potato": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals leans against the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Lobster Mac": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals sits beside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Market Fish": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals lifts the fish's clean flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Mocha Creme": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Eastern Kille Dry Gin for the steak course.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals sits alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Mushrooms": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals sits beside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Eastern Kille Dry Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Porterhouse": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Eastern Kille Dry Gin for another course.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Prime Tartare": "Eastern Kille with the tartare — Grand Rapids classic London Dry against raw beef. Strong; Eastern Kille's juniper supports tartare at GR-craft register.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals matches the trout's delicate flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Eastern Kille with the chicken — Grand Rapids family craft against roasted poultry. Works; Eastern Kille supports chicken at hometown register.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals stays alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals holds steady against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Eastern Kille Dry Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's crisp char-citrus botanicals plays cleanly against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
