  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Market Fish": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Elijah Craig Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Mocha Creme": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the caramel-spice register matches the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Mushroom Bisque": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cream. Works; boutique-sourced wasted on salad.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Mushrooms": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with the mushrooms — 94-proof small-batch against earthy umami. Solid, everyday-premium side pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the brown-spirit warmth threads the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th handles the richness at annual-release register.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Porterhouse": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with the porterhouse — small-batch character across strip and filet. Strong; covers both cuts cleanly.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Prime Tartare": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's caramel-char character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Rainbow Trout": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Elijah Craig Small Batch belongs on the steak course.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Roast Half Chicken": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the roast chicken, and the caramel rounds the plate. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon weight handles the chickpea soup, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Elijah Craig Small Batch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the garlic spinach, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; the story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Seafood Tower": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Elijah Craig Small Batch on the steak course instead.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Seared Scallops": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against small-batch. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Seasonal Soup": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon backbone carries the seasonal soup, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; milestone-release doesn't drive.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Seasonal Vegetables": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the oak frames the side. Works; the pour's refinement is underused by the salad.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Shrimp Bisque": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood against small-batch. Works neutrally.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Shrimp Cocktail": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against small-batch. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Swordfish": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Elijah Craig Small Batch belongs on the steak course.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|The Tomahawk": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's caramel-char character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Tomato Basil": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon backbone carries the tomato basil, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the complex profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Truffle Fries": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with truffle fries — spicy-sweet small-batch against truffle-parmesan. The oak spice gives the side a different vocabulary than Heaven Hill BIB's straight bond-structure brings. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Elijah Craig Small Batch belongs on the steak course.",
  "Elijah Craig Small Batch|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon backbone carries the curry, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; premium KO story wasted on salad.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Asparagus": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the rye spice cuts the asparagus, and the rye-pepper note underlines the side. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with au gratin potatoes — HH rye against cheese-and-cream. Works; spice over-specified for side's character.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Beignets": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the rye backbone drives the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Bone Marrow": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with bone marrow — 94-proof HH rye against umami fat. The rye spice and HH character meet marrow's intensity cleanly at the premium HH register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Bone-In Filet": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the rye-and-oak weight handles the bone-enhanced filet, and the dry-spice register brightens the cut. Strong; BH Red Wine Cask's rye + red-wine character threads mushrooms at distinctive register.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Broccoli Cheddar": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the broccoli cheddar, and the oak softens the cream. Works; complex profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Broccolini": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the high-rye spice register matches the broccolini, and the rye-grain bite cuts the side. Works; bold proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Brussels and Belly": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with Brussels + Belly — 94-proof HH rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; HH rye spice cuts the glaze distinctly.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Burrata": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against HH rye. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Butternut Squash": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the high-rye spice register matches the squash bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Carrot Cake": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with carrot cake — 94-proof HH rye against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; rye spice and cake spice find each other.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Cheesecake": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye backbone drives the cheesecake, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the aging is wasted on the salad.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Chilean Seabass": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Elijah Craig Straight Rye for the steak course.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Chocolate Brownie": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rye-and-oak weight handles the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Chocolate Cake": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Clam Chowder": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the rye backbone drives the chowder, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Cowboy Ribeye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 94-proof HH rye against cap fat. The extra proof over 90-proof benchmarks handles the cut where Sazerac Rye and Michter's only work strong; a premium HH rye for the ribeye. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Crab Cake": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with crab cake — HH rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Creamed Spinach": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with creamed spinach — 94-proof HH against dairy. Strong; the rye spice sits with richness at HH register.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Creamy Potato": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rye-and-oak weight handles the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; BIB structure is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Creme Brulee": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye backbone drives the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; high proof over-specified for the soup.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Escargot": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's crisp char-spice character holds steady against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Faroe Island Salmon": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Elijah Craig Straight Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Filet Mignon": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the filet — HH 94-proof rye against the 10oz cut. The Heaven Hill rye spice frames the filet distinctly from BT's Sazerac Rye or Beam's Knob Creek Rye; a distinctive HH-side rye for filet. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|French Onion": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the dry rye body wraps the French onion, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; Vermont-brand character doesn't drive.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Grilled Caesar": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the grilled Caesar — Heaven Hill rye against charred romaine. The HH spicy-sweet character earns the Caesar at premium HH register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Gumbo": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the high-rye spice register matches the gumbo, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; boutique character doesn't drive.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rye backbone drives the honey carrots, and the oak threads the caramelized sugar. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|House Wedge": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the House Wedge — HH rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; the 94-proof cuts the bleu with HH rye character.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Kansas City": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the KC — Heaven Hill's 94-proof rye, sister to the Elijah Craig bourbon line. The HH spicy-sweet rye character gives the strip more grip than Michter's 84.8-proof smoothness; a premium Heaven Hill rye for strip orders. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Loaded Potato": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the high-rye spice register matches the loaded potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Lobster Mac": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the high-rye spice register matches the lobster mac, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Market Fish": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Elijah Craig Straight Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Mocha Creme": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the mocha creme, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Mushroom Bisque": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the rye backbone drives the mushroom bisque, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Works; accessible gentle match.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Mushrooms": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the mushrooms — HH 94-proof against earthy umami. Strong; the rye spice layers into mushroom depth with HH character.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye spice cuts the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the aged depth meets the brownie at premium register.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Porterhouse": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the porterhouse — 94-proof HH across strip and filet. Strong; HH character covers both cuts at premium register.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Prime Tartare": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the tartare — HH straight rye against raw beef. The 94-proof spicy-sweet character is a distinctive counterpoint distinct from BT or Beam ryes. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Rainbow Trout": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Elijah Craig Straight Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the rye spice cuts the roast chicken, and the rye edge frames the plate. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the dry rye body wraps the chickpea soup, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Elijah Craig Straight Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye spice cuts the garlic spinach, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Seafood Tower": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Elijah Craig Straight Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Seared Scallops": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 94-proof rye. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Seasonal Soup": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the rye spice cuts the seasonal soup, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the seasonal vegetables, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; grain notes don't really engage the soup but don't clash.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with shrimp bisque — 94-proof HH against cream-based seafood. Works; HH rye interesting but doesn't drive.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against HH rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Swordfish": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Elijah Craig Straight Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|The Tomahawk": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the rye backbone drives the 36oz showpiece, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the cut. Strong; the 10-year polish covers both cuts with small-batch refinement.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Tomato Basil": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the dry rye body wraps the tomato basil, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; gentle match.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Truffle Fries": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with truffle fries — 94-proof HH rye against truffle-parmesan. The rye spice cuts the richness with HH authority. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Elijah Craig Straight Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Elijah Craig Straight Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the rye backbone drives the curry, and the rye-grain bite cuts the bowl. Works; premium KO story wasted on salad.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Asparagus": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin holds with the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Beignets": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Elk Cove Five Mountain for the steak course.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Bone Marrow": "Willamette Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — medium-tannin Oregon Pinot still lacks the grip needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Elk Cove for the filet, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Bone-In Filet": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the Pinot body brightens the bone-enhanced filet, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the cut. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Broccoli Cheddar": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Broccolini": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin holds with the broccolini's tender green — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Brussels and Belly": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin stays alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Burrata": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — cherry, violet, clove, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the violet plays against basil-tomato. Works as step-up Pinot for burrata — structured Willamette red; whites and rosés peak higher.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Butternut Squash": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Carrot Cake": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Elk Cove Five Mountain on the steak course instead.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Cheesecake": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Elk Cove Five Mountain for the steak course.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Chilean Seabass": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Elk Cove Five Mountain on the steak course instead.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Chocolate Brownie": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Elk Cove Five Mountain belongs on the steak course.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Chocolate Cake": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Elk Cove Five Mountain for the steak course.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Clam Chowder": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Cowboy Ribeye": "Willamette Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — medium-tannin Pinot still falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep Elk Cove for filet, salmon, or chicken; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Crab Cake": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — cherry, violet, clove, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets crab's sweet meat without overwhelming it and the violet plays against romesco. Peak step-up Willamette Pinot for crab cake — structured Oregon red on the shellfish starter. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
