  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin leans against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|House Wedge": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — cherry, violet, clove, structured backbone. The acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the clove-spice plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak step-up Willamette Pinot for the wedge — age-worthy Oregon red reframes the classic steakhouse salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Kansas City": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — cherry, violet, clove, medium tannin, medium body. The structured body works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though medium tannin still falls short of bolder Cabs. Works as step-up Pinot for the strip — serious Willamette for guest wanting Pinot over Cab.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Loaded Potato": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Lobster Mac": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin sits beside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Market Fish": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Elk Cove Five Mountain lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Mocha Creme": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Elk Cove Five Mountain belongs on the steak course.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Mushroom Bisque": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: this earthy, umami-packed side's mushroom-and-truffle depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. Strong — the wine refreshes the side's richness.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Elk Cove Five Mountain for the steak course.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Porterhouse": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin matches the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's silky frame sits alongside the starter without weight-fighting — the fruit threads through the plate. Strong — aligned register.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Rainbow Trout": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Elk Cove Five Mountain lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Roast Half Chicken": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — cherry, violet, clove, structured backbone. The clove-spice plays against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak step-up Willamette Pinot for chicken across most preparations. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Elk Cove Five Mountain for the steak course.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — cherry, violet, clove, structured. The acid cuts the sauté butter and the clove-spice plays against garlic without clashing with spinach earth. Works as step-up Pinot for the sauté side — Willamette structure rounds the pairing.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Seafood Tower": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Elk Cove Five Mountain for the steak course.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Seared Scallops": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — cherry, violet, clove, medium tannin. The medium tannin leaves room for scallop sweetness and the violet-clove plays against caramelized-exterior char. Strong step-up Pinot for scallops — structured Willamette on the delicate shellfish.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Seasonal Soup": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin stays alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Seasonal Vegetables": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin stays alongside the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Shrimp Bisque": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — cherry, violet, clove, structured. The acid cuts bisque's cream weight and the clove-spice plays against shellfish umami and sherry. Strong step-up Pinot for shrimp bisque — structured Willamette against the rich soup.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Shrimp Cocktail": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overshadows the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Elk Cove Five Mountain on the steak course instead.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Swordfish": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Elk Cove Five Mountain lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|The Tomahawk": "Willamette Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — medium-tannin Oregon Pinot still has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Elk Cove for the filet, salmon, or chicken course.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Tomato Basil": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin leans against the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Truffle Fries": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin leans against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Elk Cove Five Mountain for the steak course.",
  "Elk Cove Five Mountain|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Asparagus": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — delicate Oregon white, stone-fruit, crisp. The crisp acid handles asparagus's sulfur notes and the stone-fruit plays against the vegetal char. Strong Pinot Blanc for asparagus — Oregon white on the wine-tricky vegetable.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's apple-char acidity stays alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Beignets": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Elk Cove Pinot Blanc for the steak course.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Bone Marrow": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's apple-char acidity reads alongside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Bone-In Filet": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's crisp white-wine character underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Elk Cove Pinot Blanc on another course instead.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Broccoli Cheddar": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — delicate Oregon white, stone-fruit, crisp. The crisp acid cuts the soup's cheddar-cream weight and the stone-fruit plays against broccoli's vegetal edge. Strong Pinot Blanc for broccoli cheddar — Oregon white on the classic.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Broccolini": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — delicate Oregon white, stone-fruit, crisp. The crisp acid cuts broccolini's char weight and the stone-fruit plays against the vegetal edge. Strong Pinot Blanc for broccolini — Oregon white on the charred classic.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Brussels and Belly": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's apple-char acidity plays cleanly against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge frames the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Burrata": "Oregon Pinot Blanc — pear and apple, clean acid. Fresh fruit against the creamy burrata and whatever seasonal fruit component the kitchen adds today. Reliable mid-tier white that always works with fresh cheese and fruit preparations. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Butternut Squash": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — delicate Oregon white, stone-fruit, crisp. The stone-fruit plays against butternut's caramelized-squash sweetness and the crisp acid cuts the cream base. Works as Pinot Blanc for butternut squash — Oregon white on the autumn classic.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Carrot Cake": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Elk Cove Pinot Blanc for the steak course.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Cheesecake": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Elk Cove Pinot Blanc belongs on the steak course.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Chilean Seabass": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — delicate Oregon white, stone-fruit, crisp. The crisp acid cuts seabass's buttery richness and the stone-fruit plays against the melt-in-mouth texture. Works as Pinot Blanc for Chilean seabass — Oregon white; Chardonnays peak higher on the buttery fish.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Chocolate Brownie": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Elk Cove Pinot Blanc lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Chocolate Cake": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's crisp white-wine character falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a white wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Elk Cove Pinot Blanc for the steak course.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Clam Chowder": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — delicate Oregon white, stone-fruit, crisp. The crisp acid cuts the chowder's cream weight and the clean profile plays against the clam brine. Peak Pinot Blanc for clam chowder — Oregon white on the shellfish-cream classic. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Cowboy Ribeye": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — medium-body Oregon white crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Elk Cove for seafood starters, burrata, or the Oregon salmon course.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Crab Cake": "Willamette Oregon Pinot Blanc — soft pear, white peach, delicate floral lift, medium body, refreshing acidity. The lighter-than-Chard weight cuts crab's romesco without overpowering and the pear-peach fruit plays cleanly against the sweet meat. Peak unoaked white for crab cake — lighter than Chard, more interesting than Sauv Blanc.",
