  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — delicate Oregon white, stone-fruit, crisp. The crisp acid cuts the soup's tomato-pepper base and the stone-fruit plays against roasted-pepper sweetness. Strong Pinot Blanc for the soup — Oregon white on the smoky classic.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — delicate Oregon white, stone-fruit, crisp. The crisp acid lifts halibut's cured flesh and the stone-fruit plays against sweet soy glaze. Peak Pinot Blanc for salt-cured halibut — Oregon white on the glazed classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc — Willamette, pear, peach, floral, medium body. The refreshing acid cuts the sauté butter and the floral lift plays against garlic without clashing with spinach's earth. Works as unoaked white for the sauté side — lighter cut than Chardonnay for a delicate side.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Seafood Tower": "Willamette Oregon Pinot Blanc — soft pear, white peach, floral, refreshing acidity. The medium-body freshness resets palate between oysters, shrimp, and crab and the fruit plays against brine across the platter. Strong unoaked white for the seafood tower — more character than Sauv Blanc.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Seared Scallops": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc — Willamette Oregon, soft pear, white peach, floral, refreshing acidity. The refreshing acid frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the pear-peach fruit adds lift against the pan-sear without overpowering. Peak medium-body white for scallops — sophisticated alternative to Chard.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Seasonal Soup": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc: this crisp white's citrus-driven acidity and mineral edge meets this rich, creamy soup's dairy-driven warmth. The wine's acidity cuts the cream, but its weight doesn't carry the soup. Strong if the soup needs brightness.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Seasonal Vegetables": "Oregon Pinot Blanc — pear, apple, clean acid. Lightweight backup for seasonal vegetable preparations; always works, rarely peaks. Ask the kitchen what's on the plate today; the prep determines if this is the call. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Shrimp Bisque": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc — Willamette with soft pear, white peach, floral lift, refreshing acidity. The refreshing acid cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and the pear-peach fruit adds lift against butter-roux weight. Peak medium-body white for bisque — lighter cut than Chard, more fruit than Chablis.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Shrimp Cocktail": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — soft pear, white peach, delicate floral, refreshing acidity. The refreshing acid cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the pear-peach fruit plays against cocktail-sauce bite. Peak medium-body white for shrimp cocktail — lighter than Chard, rounder than Sauv Blanc.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Swordfish": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's apple-char acidity holds the line with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|The Tomahawk": "Willamette Pinot Blanc against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — delicate pear-peach-floral has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Elk Cove for crab cake, scallops, escargot, or the bisque.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Tomato Basil": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — delicate Oregon white, stone-fruit, crisp. The crisp acid meets tomato's acidity and the stone-fruit plays against basil's aromatic edge. Works as Pinot Blanc for tomato basil — Oregon white on the fresh classic.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Truffle Fries": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's apple-char acidity plays cleanly against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — delicate Oregon white, stone-fruit, crisp. The crisp acid cuts sesame-crust weight and the stone-fruit plays against honey sambal's sweet-spicy lift. Peak Pinot Blanc for tuxedo tuna — Oregon white on the crusted rare-seared classic. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — delicate Oregon white, stone-fruit, crisp. The crisp acid cuts the curry's coconut-milk base and the stone-fruit tames the turmeric-cumin heat. Strong Pinot Blanc for the curry — Oregon white on the spiced classic.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Asparagus": "Elmer T. Lee's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon body sits with the asparagus, and the oak frames the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Elmer T. Lee with au gratin potatoes — 90-proof single-barrel against cheese-and-cream. Strong; composed namesake match.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Beignets": "Elmer T. Lee's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon depth settles into the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; premium KO story wasted on salad.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Bone Marrow": "Elmer T. Lee with bone marrow — smooth single-barrel against umami fat. Strong; namesake balance sits with marrow cleanly.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Bone-In Filet": "Elmer T. Lee -- Buffalo Trace single-barrel, 90 proof. The bourbon depth settles into the bone-enhanced filet, and the vanilla softens the cut. Strong; AE is under-specified for cap-fat at flagship proof; AE Cask Strength handles this better.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Broccoli Cheddar": "Elmer T. Lee's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon weight handles the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the complex character gives the dessert a refined frame.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Broccolini": "Elmer T. Lee's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon depth settles into the broccolini, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Brussels and Belly": "Elmer T. Lee with Brussels + Belly — Buffalo Trace single-barrel meets maple-glazed pork belly. The smooth balance handles the dish's sweetness cleanly. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Burrata": "Elmer T. Lee with burrata — fresh dairy against single-barrel. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Butternut Squash": "Elmer T. Lee's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon backbone carries the squash bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Carrot Cake": "Elmer T. Lee with carrot cake — smooth single-barrel against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Cheesecake": "Elmer T. Lee with cheesecake — namesake single-barrel against clean custard. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Chilean Seabass": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Elmer T. Lee for the steak course.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Chocolate Brownie": "Elmer T. Lee with the chocolate brownie — namesake single-barrel against cocoa. Works; balance is slightly underserved — Blanton's Gold or E.H. Taylor bring more depth.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Chocolate Cake": "Elmer T. Lee's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon backbone carries the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the complex character gives the dessert a refined frame.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Clam Chowder": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon body sits with the chowder, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; boutique-sourced doesn't drive.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Cowboy Ribeye": "Elmer T. Lee with the Cowboy Ribeye — BT single-barrel against cap fat. The 90-proof richness handles the ribeye cleanly; a namesake single-barrel for this cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Crab Cake": "Elmer T. Lee with crab cake — smooth single-barrel against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Creamed Spinach": "Elmer T. Lee with creamed spinach — namesake single-barrel against dairy. Strong; the smooth character at matched register.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Creamy Potato": "Elmer T. Lee's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon weight handles the potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; JD 12 handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Creme Brulee": "Elmer T. Lee's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; accessible gentle match.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Escargot": "Elmer T. Lee's botanical-honey character holds the line with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Faroe Island Salmon": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Elmer T. Lee for the steak course.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Filet Mignon": "Elmer T. Lee with the filet — Buffalo Trace single-barrel, 90 proof, named for the legendary master distiller who championed the single-barrel approach. Rich, smooth, balanced; a namesake pour that frames the filet with BT's mash-forward character — distinct from Blanton's iconic horse-topper branding. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|French Onion": "Elmer T. Lee's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the caramel-spice register matches the French onion, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Grilled Caesar": "Elmer T. Lee with the grilled Caesar — namesake single-barrel against charred romaine. Works; the smooth character doesn't drive the Caesar.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Gumbo": "Elmer T. Lee's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the brown-spirit warmth threads the gumbo, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; the richer pour finds more with the dessert than Original brings.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Elmer T. Lee's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon body sits with the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; curated single-barrel covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|House Wedge": "Elmer T. Lee with the House Wedge — smooth single-barrel against iceberg and bleu. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Kansas City": "Elmer T. Lee with the KC — 90-proof namesake single-barrel against the strip's marbling. The smooth BT character carries the strip at refined single-barrel register; a namesake pour for guests who know the history. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Loaded Potato": "Elmer T. Lee's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon weight handles the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the blend's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Lobster Mac": "Elmer T. Lee's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the brown-spirit warmth threads the lobster mac, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; mid-proof pour is slightly over-specified.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Market Fish": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Elmer T. Lee belongs on the steak course.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Mocha Creme": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the 10-year holds without lifting.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Mushroom Bisque": "Elmer T. Lee's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Mushrooms": "Elmer T. Lee with the mushrooms — smooth namesake single-barrel against earthy umami. The BT balance layers cleanly into mushroom depth. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Elmer T. Lee with the PB brownie — smooth single-barrel against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Porterhouse": "Elmer T. Lee with the porterhouse — namesake single-barrel across strip and filet. Strong; the 90-proof balance covers both cuts cleanly.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Prime Tartare": "Elmer T. Lee's botanical-honey character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Rainbow Trout": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Elmer T. Lee for the steak course.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Roast Half Chicken": "Elmer T. Lee's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Elmer T. Lee's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak depth lays under the chickpea soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; Remus 15 handles dessert density at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Elmer T. Lee for the steak course.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Elmer T. Lee's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the caramel-spice register matches the garlic spinach, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Seafood Tower": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Elmer T. Lee for the steak course.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Seared Scallops": "Elmer T. Lee with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against namesake single-barrel. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Seasonal Soup": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; craft-blend covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Seasonal Vegetables": "Elmer T. Lee's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the brown-spirit warmth threads the seasonal vegetables, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Shrimp Bisque": "Elmer T. Lee's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the bourbon backbone carries the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side; save the rare bottle for the main.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Shrimp Cocktail": "Elmer T. Lee with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against namesake single-barrel. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Swordfish": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Elmer T. Lee lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|The Tomahawk": "Elmer T. Lee with the Tomahawk — 90-proof single-barrel against bone-in ribeye. Strong; scale pushes past Elmer T's balance — E.H. Taylor or Blanton's Gold are bigger Tomahawk calls.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Tomato Basil": "Elmer T. Lee's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the oak depth lays under the tomato basil, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; save the BTAC bottle for the protein course — the side doesn't deserve the pour.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Truffle Fries": "Elmer T. Lee with truffle fries — namesake single-barrel against truffle-parmesan. The smooth character gives the side a refined frame at the premium single-barrel register. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Elmer T. Lee belongs on the steak course.",
  "Elmer T. Lee|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the oak depth lays under the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; premium single-barrel wasted on the salad.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Asparagus": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals stays alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Beignets": "Empress 1908 with beignets — rose-ginger-fennel Victoria craft against fried dough. Excellent; Empress's rose-and-floral profile is the featured Smooth/Citrus Craft Gin-beignets pairing — rose-with-sugar is a distinctive dessert bridge no other Smooth/Citrus sibling brings.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Bone Marrow": "Empress 1908 with bone marrow — rose-and-floral BC gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Empress 1908 Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Empress 1908 Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Broccolini": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals stays alongside the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals leans against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Burrata": "Empress 1908 with burrata — Victoria BC color-changing gin (butterfly pea flower + rose + 8 botanicals). Excellent; Empress's rose-and-floral profile is the featured Smooth/Citrus Craft Gin-burrata pairing — rose-with-cream is classically beautiful, and the indigo-to-pink color change makes this a visual conversation-starter at the table.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Butternut Squash": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals leans against the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Carrot Cake": "Empress 1908 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Empress 1908 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Cheesecake": "Empress 1908 with cheesecake — rose-ginger BC craft against custard. Works; Empress's rose-notes thread the cheesecake at distinctive register.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals sits with the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Empress 1908 Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Empress 1908 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Empress 1908 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Empress 1908 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Clam Chowder": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Empress 1908 with the cowboy ribeye — butterfly-pea-flower craft against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Crab Cake": "Empress 1908 with crab cake — Victoria BC floral-citrus craft against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Empress's rose-and-citrus-peel profile frames crab with Pacific-Northwest elegance and visual distinction.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Creamy Potato": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals sits alongside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Creme Brulee": "Empress 1908 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Empress 1908 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Escargot": "Empress 1908 with escargot — floral-citrus Victoria craft against garlic-butter snails. Works; Empress's rose-profile is over-specified for escargot — Mahón's Mediterranean juniper cuts butter sharper.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Empress 1908 with the salmon — color-changing BC gin against oily salmon. Excellent; Empress's rose-ginger-fennel profile lifts the fish with Pacific-Northwest-adjacent terroir distinction and visual-distinctive presentation.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Filet Mignon": "Empress 1908 with the filet — BC floral-citrus gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|French Onion": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Empress 1908 with the grilled Caesar — color-changing craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Empress's floral-citrus meets the char softly; Bluecoat's 4-citrus and Tanq 10's citrus are the featured craft-Caesar matches.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Gumbo": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals sits alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|House Wedge": "Empress 1908 with the House Wedge — Victoria butterfly-pea-flower gin against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Empress's rose-citrus threads the wedge with BC-craft distinction.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Kansas City": "Empress 1908 Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Empress 1908 Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Loaded Potato": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Lobster Mac": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Market Fish": "Empress 1908 Gin's juniper-and-citrus lift meets at register with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh: the bright botanical edge handles the market fish, and the dry gin lift plays against the plate. Strong; Orbium's bitter-tonic profile cuts the fish's richness with distinctive contrast.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Mocha Creme": "Empress 1908 Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Empress 1908 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals sits alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Mushrooms": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals sits beside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Empress 1908 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Empress 1908 Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Porterhouse": "Empress 1908 Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Empress 1908 Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Prime Tartare": "Empress 1908 with the tartare — rose-and-citrus BC craft against raw beef. Strong; Empress's floral profile supports tartare at distinctive register; Monkey 47's 47-botanical complexity is the featured craft-gin-tartare match.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Empress 1908 Gin's herbal-botanical edge leans against the trout's delicate flesh: the floral edge frames the trout, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Neptunia's briny character engages the bleu with distinctive maritime profile.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Empress 1908 with the chicken — rose-ginger-fennel Victoria craft against roasted poultry. Strong; Empress's floral-citrus supports chicken at distinctive register.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals holds the line with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals reads alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Seafood Tower": "Empress 1908 with the seafood tower — BC color-changing gin against the raw bar. Strong; Empress's floral-citrus profile lifts the tower with visual-distinctive character; Gray Whale's California ocean-botanicals is still the featured craft-tower match.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Seared Scallops": "Empress 1908 with seared scallops — BC butterfly-pea-flower gin against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Empress's floral-citrus profile mirrors scallop sweetness with distinctive BC-craft character.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals plays cleanly against the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Empress 1908 with shrimp bisque — color-changing BC craft against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Empress's floral-citrus rides the bisque at distinctive register.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Empress 1908 with shrimp cocktail — color-changing floral-citrus gin against cold shrimp. Excellent; Empress's rose-and-citrus profile elevates the classic cold app with visual-distinctive character the other Smooth/Citrus siblings don't offer.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Swordfish": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals lifts the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|The Tomahawk": "Empress 1908 with the Tomahawk — color-changing craft against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Tomato Basil": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals sits alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Truffle Fries": "Empress 1908 Gin's herbal-botanical edge finds neutral with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight: the floral edge frames the truffle fries, and the juniper threads the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals holds the line with the tuna's seared rare crust — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Empress 1908 Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Empress 1908 Gin's bold anise-butter botanicals sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Asparagus": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis sits alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts gruyere-cream richness and the full body handles dairy. Works for au gratin — Muga's vanilla-leather fits the cheese harder.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Beignets": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Epoch Ingenuity for the steak course.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Bone-In Filet": "Epoch Ingenuity's structured dark-fruit weight leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; the call reads at register without overshooting.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Broccoli Cheddar": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis leans against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Broccolini": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis plays cleanly against the broccolini's tender green — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Brussels and Belly": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts pork-belly fat and the smoked meat echoes brussels-char bitterness. Strong Paso-Rhône for brussels-and-belly — Epoch smoke-on-char match.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Burrata": "Never pair Epoch with burrata — smoked meat, olive, and crushed pepper clash with fresh cream and the full body buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Butternut Squash": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis leans against the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Carrot Cake": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Epoch Ingenuity belongs on the steak course.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Cheesecake": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Epoch Ingenuity for the steak course.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Chilean Seabass": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Epoch Ingenuity for the steak course.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Chocolate Brownie": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Epoch Ingenuity lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Chocolate Cake": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Epoch Ingenuity belongs on the steak course.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Clam Chowder": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the smoke wraps the briny shellfish. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Cowboy Ribeye": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, full body, medium tannin. The smoked meat echoes Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in char and the crushed pepper plays with grilled beef. Peak Paso-Rhône for ribeye — Epoch's serious winemaking on the cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Crab Cake": "Never pair Epoch with crab cake — smoked meat, olive, and crushed pepper bury delicate lump crab and the full body fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Creamy Potato": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis finds neutral with the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Creme Brulee": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Epoch Ingenuity belongs on the steak course.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Escargot": "Epoch Ingenuity's structured dark-fruit weight reads register-aligned with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the powerful body carries the buttered escargot, and the espresso-cocoa note rounds the starter. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Faroe Island Salmon": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Epoch Ingenuity belongs on the steak course.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Filet Mignon": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, full body, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the olive plays with escargot-butter. Strong Paso-Rhône for filet — Epoch savory on the delicate cut.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|French Onion": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Grilled Caesar": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, full body. The olive-savory matches anchovy-depth and the crushed pepper cuts parm-char. Works for grilled caesar — Epoch's full body reads heavy, a Rhône Villages lifts lighter.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Gumbo": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis holds with the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|House Wedge": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, full body. The olive matches blue-cheese funk and the crushed pepper plays with bacon-fat. Works for wedge — iceberg-cold mutes Epoch's savory depth, a lighter Chianti lifts higher.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Kansas City": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, full body, medium tannin. The crushed pepper echoes Kansas City's lean-bold strip char and the smoked meat plays with grilled beef. Peak Paso-Rhône for the strip — European-inspired winemaking. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Loaded Potato": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis leans against the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Lobster Mac": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis plays cleanly against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Market Fish": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Epoch Ingenuity on the steak course instead.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Mocha Creme": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Epoch Ingenuity belongs on the steak course.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Mushroom Bisque": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis leans against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the side's mushroom richness pulls the bold red's body; tannin and oak settle into the umami without overshooting. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Epoch Ingenuity on the steak course instead.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Porterhouse": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis finds register with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the opulent body meets the cap-fat. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Prime Tartare": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Rainbow Trout": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Epoch Ingenuity for the steak course.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Roast Half Chicken": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the smoke meets the char. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the pepper amplifies the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Epoch Ingenuity belongs on the steak course.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis reads alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Seafood Tower": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Epoch Ingenuity for the steak course.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Epoch with seared scallops — smoked meat and crushed pepper overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and the full body fights browned-butter. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Seasonal Soup": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis leans against the seasonal soup's house build — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Seasonal Vegetables": "Epoch Ingenuity's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the structured body wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the cassis frames the side. Works; the dessert sits cleanly against the meal's register.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Shrimp Bisque": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis plays cleanly against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the smoke wraps the briny shellfish. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Epoch with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by smoked meat, olive, and full body, and the crushed pepper has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Swordfish": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Epoch Ingenuity for the steak course.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|The Tomahawk": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, full body, medium tannin. The full body handles Tomahawk's massive 36oz bone-in and the smoked meat plays with char. Strong Paso-Rhône for Tomahawk — a firmer-tannin Cab edges higher on the grand cut.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Tomato Basil": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright acid — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the spice meets the herb butter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the side's mushroom richness pulls the bold red's body; tannin and oak settle into the umami without overshooting. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Epoch Ingenuity lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Epoch Ingenuity|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis stays alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the pepper amplifies the spice. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Escargot|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Escargot|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the caramel-spice register matches the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; aging gives a solid match; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Escargot|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's bold cherry-coffee cassis plays cleanly against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Escargot|2 James Gin": "2 James with escargot — Michigan craft gin against garlic-butter snails. Excellent; 2 James's American juniper cuts butter at local-craft register — less traditional than British counterparts, but distinctive.",
  "Escargot|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character rests at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the malt-and-oak threads the buttered escargot, and the heather brightens the starter. Strong; Fortuna frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Escargot|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 -- double-matured Speyside, bourbon-plus-sherry, balanced. The bourbon-plus-sherry depth wraps the buttered escargot, and the balanced sweetness frames the starter. Strong; the curated single-barrel is capable but single-distillery aged bourbons bring more traditional focused character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Escargot|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino with escargot — filtered aged against garlic-butter snails. Strong; cristalino depth cuts the garlic-butter with craft-theatrical distinctiveness.",
  "Escargot|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's crisp agave-caramel character lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Escargot|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay — Sonoma with ripe apple, pear, vanilla oak, smooth texture. The oak-vanilla weight cuts escargot's garlic-parsley butter and the apple-pear fruit lifts the snail richness. Strong California Chardonnay for escargot — accessible pairing on the French classic.",
  "Escargot|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' concentrated dark-fruit and tannin leans against the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the structured body wraps the buttered escargot, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Escargot|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Escargot|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness doubles down on the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- aperitivo herbs and a herb-bright opener compound the bitter register — no contrast. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Amaro Nonino on the steak course instead.",
  "Escargot|Ancho Reyes": "Escargot: garlic-herb butter meets Ancho Reyes. Excellent; the chile-heat brings unusual dimension to garlic-herb butter at Mexican-liqueur register — unexpected but earns its place through spice.",
  "Escargot|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's crisp vanilla character finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Escargot|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight meets at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon depth settles into the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; accessible everyday match; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Escargot|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's spice-vanilla character holds steady against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Escargot|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge overwhelms the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Aperol on the steak course instead.",
  "Escargot|Asparagus": "Escargot alongside the asparagus -- the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight meets the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: acid lifts the umami, the meal carries lift without losing depth. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Escargot|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The escargot and the au gratin -- cream meets umami, the table goes deep. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Escargot|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-coffee cassis holds the line with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Escargot|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Escargot|Aviation": "Escargot: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this gin cocktail's citrus brightness and botanical lift. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Escargot|Aviation Gin": "Aviation with escargot — smooth Portland craft against garlic-butter snails. Works; Aviation's soft juniper meets butter at crowd-pleasing register; Mahón and Eastern Kille's sharper juniper cut it harder.",
  "Escargot|Avion 44": "Avion 44's chocolate-cocoa character holds steady against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Escargot|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver with escargot — filtered blanco against garlic-butter snails. Strong; clean highland cuts the garlic-butter at the global register.",
  "Escargot|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character mis-pairs with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- this light spirit's register is wrong for the starter — try a different pour. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a light spirit. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Bacardi Rum for the steak course.",
  "Escargot|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character buries the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- sweet liqueur smothers the herb-bright opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Baileys Irish Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Escargot|Baker's": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon depth settles into the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; collector intensity at allocated register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Escargot|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 American Oak -- Speyside, bourbon cask, vanilla, honey. The bourbon-cask vanilla settles into the buttered escargot, and the honey rounds the starter. Strong; the 10-year oak gives the side backbone distinct from wheated approaches; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
