  "Escargot|Truffle Fries": "The escargot and the truffle fries -- savory-on-savory, the table reads dense and earnest. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Escargot|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W. -- Irish blend, triple-distilled, gentle. The gentle Irish body sits with the buttered escargot, and the soft malt rounds the starter. Strong; Tullamore's drier character engages the bleu at traditional register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Escargot|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna with the escargot as the lead-in -- the sesame crust holds with the herb-bright snail plate. Strong; the starter sets the table for the tuna.",
  "Escargot|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "The escargot with the curry -- the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight meets the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth. Strong; the opening courses share the table at full register.",
  "Escargot|Venge Family Reserve": "Venge Family Reserve's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Escargot|Venge Scout's Honor": "Venge Scout's Honor's bold pepper-vanilla cassis plays cleanly against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Escargot|Venge Silencieux": "Venge Silencieux's bold cherry-chocolate cassis lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Escargot|Vesper": "Nolet's, Ketel One, Lillet. The martini's cool dryness cuts garlic-butter escargot cleanly and Lillet adds citrus brightness against the snails' earthy profile. Strong opener cocktail — classical elegance on both sides of the pairing.",
  "Escargot|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Yellow label Veuve — iconic Reims Champagne with ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse. The biscuit echoes escargot's buttered-bread French tradition directly and the creamy body cuts garlic-parsley. Strong recognizable Champagne for escargot — everyone's favorite meets the French classic.",
  "Escargot|Vieux Carré": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey-build depth cuts the umami fat, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Escargot|Villon Cognac": "Villon Cognac's Limousin-oak cognac register sits at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the eau-de-vie body carries the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; Ferrand's craft character frames the richness with unblended polish.",
  "Escargot|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Escargot|Weller 107": "Weller 107's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; the polished aged character is a refined premium counterpoint distinct from WP's Vermont-finished approach; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Escargot|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year's wheated-vanilla character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Escargot|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon backbone carries the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; extra proof adds dimension at the premium register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Escargot|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve's caramel-honey character lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Escargot|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley with escargot — BT's house-made wheat against garlic-butter snails. Strong; Wheatley's cleanness sits with butter; Belvedere's pepper still cuts sharper.",
  "Escargot|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's pepper-spice character holds the line with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Escargot|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character leans against the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the high-rye spice register matches the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; Vermont-brand rye sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Escargot|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character rests at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; polished aged character sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Escargot|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's cask-strength juniper-oak character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Escargot|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments's cask-strength spice character finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Escargot|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's crisp spice character matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Escargot|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's crisp caramel-spice character frames the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Escargot|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon body sits with the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; multi-distillery complexity sits with richness at craft-blender register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Escargot|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's bold caramel-char character finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Escargot|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's floral-spice character lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Escargot|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the rye spice cuts the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; extended-aged Michigan character is a distinctive counterpoint with home-state-pride story; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Escargot|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Japanese-sherry-touched character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the sherry depth threads the umami fat. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Escargot|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's bold Mizunara-oak-Japanese character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Escargot|Yellow Chartreuse": "Escargot: garlic-herb butter meets Yellow Chartreuse. Excellent; the 130-botanical profile ties to herb butter at honey-sweetened register — softer than Green, still hits the French-classic mark.",
  "Espresso Martini|Asparagus": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness crowds the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Beignets": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The espresso bitterness cuts the beignet's sweet powdered sugar and the vanilla aligns. Strong cocktail route when guest wants coffee alongside a lighter dessert.",
  "Espresso Martini|Bone Marrow": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The espresso cuts marrow's rendered fat and Averna's bitter herbal backbone meets the bone's savory depth. Strong unconventional pairing — coffee cocktail with marrow works for guests wanting different.",
  "Espresso Martini|Bone-In Filet": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Espresso Martini for another course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Broccoli Cheddar": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness flattens the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the soup's cream instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Espresso Martini lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Espresso Martini|Broccolini": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness overshadows the broccolini's tender green stalks -- whiskey-cocktail register crowds the green vegetable plate. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Brussels and Belly": "Tito's, espresso, Grind liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The coffee and Averna's bitter edge cut pork-belly fat and vanilla syrup plays against the belly's glaze. Strong unconventional pairing for the sweet-savory belly build when guest wants coffee-forward.",
  "Espresso Martini|Burrata": "Tito's, espresso, Grind liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Coffee-forward cocktail crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile; espresso has no hook into basil-tomato-cheese. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco, French 75, or the light gin cocktails.",
  "Espresso Martini|Butternut Squash": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness flattens the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the soup's cream instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Carrot Cake": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The Averna's bitter herbal edge meets carrot-cake warm spice and the vanilla echoes the bean-paste frosting. Peak cocktail route when guest wants coffee with a spiced dessert. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Espresso Martini|Cheesecake": "Tito's, espresso, Grind liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The coffee-and-vanilla profile meets vanilla-bean cheesecake directly; Averna's bitter backbone cuts cream cheese density. Peak coffee-cocktail pour for cheesecake — one of the highest-scoring pairings on the menu.",
  "Espresso Martini|Chilean Seabass": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Chocolate Brownie": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Peak coffee-chocolate pairing — the espresso and coffee liqueur meet the dense fudge brownie's cocoa directly, and Averna's bitterness cuts the chocolate frosting's sweetness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Espresso Martini|Chocolate Cake": "Tito's, espresso, Grind liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Coffee-and-cocoa alignment — the espresso base and Averna's bitter herbal backbone meet chocolate cake's cocoa depth directly. Peak dessert-cocktail pour when the cake returns to the menu; currently unavailable. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Espresso Martini|Clam Chowder": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness flattens the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the soup's cream instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Espresso Martini lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Espresso Martini|Cowboy Ribeye": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness mis-pairs with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Espresso Martini belongs on another course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Crab Cake": "Tito's, espresso, Grind liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Coffee-forward dessert cocktail overwhelms crab's sweet-delicate meat; espresso has no hook into seafood. Never pair — crab cake belongs with French 75, Sauvignon Blanc, or Champagne, not coffee cocktails.",
  "Espresso Martini|Creamed Spinach": "Tito's, espresso, Grind liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The cocktail's coffee-and-amaro depth sits alongside creamed spinach's dairy weight; Averna's bitter edge cuts the cream. Strong pairing when guest carries the cocktail through the entrée with the spinach.",
  "Espresso Martini|Creamy Potato": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Creme Brulee": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The Averna's bitter herbal backbone cuts through the crème brûlée's cream and the vanilla-on-vanilla axis aligns. Strong cocktail route for guests who want coffee with dessert.",
  "Espresso Martini|Escargot": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness overshadows the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the herb-bright opener. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Faroe Island Salmon": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- whiskey cocktail and oily fish refuse each other — needs acid, not bitters. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Espresso Martini lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Espresso Martini|Filet Mignon": "Tito's, espresso, Grind liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Dessert-forward coffee cocktail with butter-tender lean filet — weight mismatch on both sides. Works only when guest stays on the cocktail through the entrée; route filet to Cab or Manhattan for peak pairing.",
  "Espresso Martini|French Onion": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- bitter-and-bourbon cocktail and savory broth refuse each other. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Grilled Caesar": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness overwhelms the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- whiskey-cocktail weight has no business on cold lettuce. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Gumbo": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- bitter-and-bourbon cocktail and savory broth refuse each other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Espresso Martini on the steak course instead.",
  "Espresso Martini|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness doubles down on the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- bourbon-cocktail sugar and glaze sugar compound without lift. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Espresso Martini lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Espresso Martini|House Wedge": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness overwhelms the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- whiskey-cocktail weight has no business on cold lettuce. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Espresso Martini belongs on the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Kansas City": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The cocktail's coffee-and-amaro weight handles the KC's lean-bold strip but the drink is dessert-positioned; works if guest insists on coffee cocktail with steak. Route KC to Cab or Manhattan for peak. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Espresso Martini|Loaded Potato": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Espresso Martini on the steak course instead.",
  "Espresso Martini|Lobster Mac": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness doubles down on the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- cocktail whiskey and dairy side meet without contrast — both heavy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Espresso Martini on the steak course instead.",
  "Espresso Martini|Market Fish": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Espresso Martini lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Espresso Martini|Mocha Creme": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Coffee-on-coffee alignment — the cocktail's espresso and the Mocha Crème's espresso-pod build share a direct axis. Peak coffee-cocktail pour when the dessert returns; currently unavailable. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Espresso Martini|Mushroom Bisque": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness flattens the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the soup's cream instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Espresso Martini lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Espresso Martini|Mushrooms": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness crowds the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The coffee-and-vanilla profile meets the peanut butter frosting's richness directly; Averna's bitter edge cuts the peanut butter weight. Peak coffee-cocktail pour for the PB brownie.",
  "Espresso Martini|Porterhouse": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness mis-pairs with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Espresso Martini on another course instead.",
  "Espresso Martini|Prime Tartare": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness underclubs the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- the cocktail is capable but the meat starter could carry a bigger pour. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Rainbow Trout": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- whiskey-forward cocktail weight buries delicate fish — the cocktail belongs on red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Espresso Martini on the steak course instead.",
  "Espresso Martini|Roast Half Chicken": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness overwhelms the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- whiskey-cocktail weight buries the chicken's mild frame. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Espresso Martini belongs on the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the bitters-and-bourbon register has no foil against gentle flesh. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Espresso Martini belongs on the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Seafood Tower": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Coffee-forward cocktail against raw shellfish spread is incoherent on every axis. Never pair — the tower demands Champagne, French 75, or light gin cocktails, never an espresso-based dessert cocktail.",
  "Espresso Martini|Seared Scallops": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Coffee-and-amaro weight annihilates scallops' delicate sweet-seared profile on every axis. Never pair — scallops demand crisp whites, French 75, or Vesper, not an espresso-based dessert cocktail.",
  "Espresso Martini|Seasonal Soup": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Seasonal Vegetables": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness overshadows the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- whiskey-cocktail register crowds the green vegetable plate. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Espresso Martini belongs on the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Shrimp Bisque": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness flattens the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the soup's cream instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Shrimp Cocktail": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- whiskey-cocktail weight is wrong for cold shellfish — the opener needs lift, not bitters. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Swordfish": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- cocktail bitterness pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|The Tomahawk": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness mis-pairs with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Espresso Martini for another course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Tomato Basil": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- bitter-and-bourbon cocktail and savory broth refuse each other. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Truffle Fries": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The coffee meets truffle oil's earth directly and Averna's bitter backbone cuts parmesan weight. Strong cocktail-side pairing when the fries carry a heavier cocktail program.",
  "Espresso Martini|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- cocktail bitterness pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Martini|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Asparagus": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Espresso Old Fashioned on the steak course instead.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register doubles down on the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- cocktail whiskey and dairy side meet without contrast — both heavy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Espresso Old Fashioned belongs on the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Beignets": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Brown sugar meets the fried-choux-exterior caramelization and hazelnut bridges the powdered sugar. Works — though the beignet's lightness peaks higher with Moscato or Prosecco than a heavy coffee cocktail.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Bone Marrow": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The espresso cuts marrow's fat weight directly and Frangelico's hazelnut adds aromatic depth against roasted bone savor. Peak cocktail for marrow when guest wants the coffee-chocolate register alongside.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Bone-In Filet": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Espresso Old Fashioned for another course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Broccoli Cheddar": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Broccolini": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Brussels and Belly": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Brown sugar meets the belly's glaze directly; espresso cuts the rendered pork fat and Frangelico bridges the brussels char. Peak cocktail for the sweet-savory belly build.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Burrata": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Dessert cocktail weight crushes the burrata's creamy-delicate profile; coffee has no hook into basil-tomato. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco or a crisp white opener, not a coffee cocktail.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Butternut Squash": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Carrot Cake": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The brown sugar ties into the cake's build; Frangelico's hazelnut meets the warm-spice profile; espresso anchors. Strong cocktail route for guests wanting spirit-forward with spiced dessert.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Cheesecake": "Espresso Old Fashioned's bitters-and-spirit register sits at register with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the spirit-forward register carries the cheesecake, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Chilean Seabass": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Chocolate Brownie": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The espresso-chocolate-hazelnut stack meets the dense fudge brownie and cocoa frosting perfectly; brown sugar ties the finish. Peak dessert-cocktail pour — built for chocolate desserts.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Chocolate Cake": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Coffee-forward bourbon cocktail with Frangelico hazelnut — meets the chocolate cake's cocoa density directly; brown sugar ties to the build. Peak spirit-forward pour when cake returns. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Clam Chowder": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Espresso Old Fashioned on the steak course instead.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Cowboy Ribeye": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The coffee-and-hazelnut profile cuts through the Cowboy's rendered fat and the bourbon cream softens the cocktail against the ribeye weight. Peak spirit-forward, dessert-leaning option for the fattiest cut.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Crab Cake": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Coffee-hazelnut weight overwhelms crab's sweet-delicate meat; the cocktail was built for dessert, not starters. Never pair — route crab cake guest to Sauvignon Blanc, Champagne, or a lighter opener cocktail.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Creamed Spinach": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The cocktail's coffee-hazelnut cream profile meets the side's dairy richness without fighting it; brown sugar bridges to the parmesan crust. Strong pairing when guest carries the cocktail through the entrée.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Creamy Potato": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Creme Brulee": "Espresso Old Fashioned's sweet coffee-cream build finds register with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the nut character deepens the cream. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Escargot": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register overshadows the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the herb-bright opener. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Espresso Old Fashioned belongs on the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Faroe Island Salmon": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- whiskey cocktail and oily fish refuse each other — needs acid, not bitters. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Filet Mignon": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The cocktail is heavier than the lean filet strictly needs but coffee and hazelnut complement buttery beef and brown sugar softens the profile. Strong when guest wants a rich spirit pour with filet.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|French Onion": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Espresso Old Fashioned on the steak course instead.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Grilled Caesar": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- whiskey-cocktail weight has no business on cold lettuce. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Espresso Old Fashioned lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Gumbo": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register doubles down on the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- bourbon-cocktail sugar and glaze sugar compound without lift. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Espresso Old Fashioned belongs on the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|House Wedge": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Dessert-forward cocktail weight is wrong for the light iceberg wedge, but the bourbon-cream structure doesn't entirely clash with bacon and blue cheese. Works when guest leads with the cocktail before switching to wine.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Kansas City": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The coffee weight handles the KC's lean-bold beef; brown sugar meets the char and Frangelico adds aromatic depth. Strong dessert-forward spirit option with the most approachable bold cut.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Loaded Potato": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register flattens the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the soup's cream instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Lobster Mac": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Coffee bitterness fights our truffle roux's nuance; the cocktail's weight buries the lobster. Reserve for the marrow or steak course. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Market Fish": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- whiskey-forward cocktail weight buries delicate fish — the cocktail belongs on red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Espresso Old Fashioned belongs on the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Mocha Creme": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The coffee-chocolate-hazelnut stack aligns with Mocha Crème's espresso-pod-and-cocoa build directly — designed-together pairing. Strong dessert-cocktail pour when the dessert returns; currently unavailable.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Mushroom Bisque": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register flattens the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the soup's cream instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Espresso Old Fashioned lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Mushrooms": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register crowds the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Hazelnut via Frangelico and brown sugar meet peanut butter directly; coffee anchors the pairing against the fudge brownie base. Peak spirit-forward dessert cocktail route. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Porterhouse": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register mis-pairs with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Espresso Old Fashioned for another course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Prime Tartare": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar syrup, Angostura. The coffee bitterness frames the worcestershire's umami and the Bourbon Cream rounds it. Heavier than our Old Fashioned — position as the dessert-leaning cocktail pairing. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Rainbow Trout": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the bitters-and-bourbon register has no foil against gentle flesh. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Espresso Old Fashioned lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Roast Half Chicken": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- bitter-and-bourbon cocktail and savory broth refuse each other. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Espresso Old Fashioned belongs on the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the bitters-and-bourbon register has no foil against gentle flesh. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Espresso Old Fashioned belongs on the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The heavy coffee cocktail overpowers the lightest sharing side on every axis. Save for Brussels & Belly, marrow, or dessert-course pairings. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Seafood Tower": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Raw shellfish-and-oyster display against a dessert-weight coffee cocktail is incoherent on every axis. Never pair — the tower demands Champagne, crisp whites, or oyster-friendly bubbles, never a heavy coffee-bourbon pour.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Seared Scallops": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Dessert-weight cocktail annihilates the scallops' delicate sweetness; coffee and hazelnut have no hook into the clean seared profile. Never pair — scallops demand bright whites, not heavy coffee cocktails.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Seasonal Soup": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Espresso Old Fashioned lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Seasonal Vegetables": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Shrimp Bisque": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Coffee-hazelnut profile against cream-shellfish soup is unusual but not clashing; the bourbon cream softens the contrast. Works for cocktail-first guests — though white wine or Champagne peaks higher for bisque.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Shrimp Cocktail": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the cocktail's weight is forward for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Swordfish": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- cocktail bitterness pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|The Tomahawk": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The coffee-forward cocktail and Frangelico's hazelnut warmth meet the Tomahawk's char and marbled fat; brown sugar ties into the crust. Peak cocktail for guests wanting dessert-leaning spirit with the biggest cut.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Tomato Basil": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- bitter-and-bourbon cocktail and savory broth refuse each other. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Truffle Fries": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The coffee bitterness and Frangelico's nuttiness meet truffle's earthiness well; heavier dessert-adjacent cocktail route when the guest's already committed to a big-weight pairing. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Espresso Old Fashioned|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Asparagus": "Evening Land Seven Springs's cherry-earth tannin leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Evening Land Seven Springs's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Beignets": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Evening Land Seven Springs for the steak course.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Bone Marrow": "Willamette Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — savory Eola-Amity Pinot's medium tannin still falls short of the render's demand. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Evening Land for the filet, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Bone-In Filet": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone, medium tannin. The medium tannin handles bone-in filet's intensified beefy edge and the smoked-meat plays against the bone-deepened cut. Peak serious Willamette Pinot for bone-in filet — structured enough for the elevated filet. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Broccoli Cheddar": "Evening Land Seven Springs's cherry-earth tannin finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Broccolini": "Willamette Pinot — savory Eola-Amity Pinot, medium tannin, elegant delicate. The savory edge plays against broccolini's vegetal char and the medium tannin lifts the tender stem. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Broccolini but below the wine's peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. Works — clean if not dominant.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Burrata": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- red structure on burrata curdles the cream's clean delicacy. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Evening Land Seven Springs lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Butternut Squash": "Willamette Pinot — savory Eola-Amity Pinot, medium tannin, elegant delicate. The savory edge plays against butternut's caramelized sweetness and the medium tannin lifts the cream base. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Butternut Squash but below the wine's peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Carrot Cake": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Evening Land Seven Springs belongs on the steak course.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Cheesecake": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Evening Land Seven Springs on the steak course instead.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Chilean Seabass": "Willamette Pinot — savory Eola-Amity Pinot, medium tannin, elegant delicate. The medium tannin meets seabass's buttery flesh and the savory edge plays against the rich texture. Strong Oregon Pinot for Chilean seabass — Evening Land structure on the buttery classic.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Chocolate Brownie": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Evening Land Seven Springs belongs on the steak course.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Chocolate Cake": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Evening Land Seven Springs lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Clam Chowder": "Evening Land Seven Springs's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the smoke wraps the briny shellfish. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Cowboy Ribeye": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, medium tannin. The medium tannin stretches toward ribeye's big marbled build though the body falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand vs. bolder Cabs. Works as savory Pinot for ribeye — serious Willamette for guest wanting Pinot.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Crab Cake": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone. The medium tannin meets crab's sweet meat and the crushed-stone minerality plays against romesco. Peak savory Pinot for crab cake — Eola-Amity red where heavier reds would bury the shellfish. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Creamed Spinach": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone. The crushed-stone minerality cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the smoked-meat plays against the spinach base. Strong savory Pinot for creamed spinach — Eola-Amity complexity reframes the rich side.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Creamy Potato": "Evening Land Seven Springs's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the potato soup's silky body — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Creme Brulee": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Evening Land Seven Springs for the steak course.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Escargot": "Evening Land Seven Springs's cherry-earth tannin lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the medium-body red meets the savory plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Faroe Island Salmon": "Eola-Amity Hills Willamette Pinot — darker and more savory than the Avni. Black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone. For guests who want a serious Pinot; the smoked-meat note hooks into a seared Faroe prep beautifully. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Filet Mignon": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — darker, savory, black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone, medium tannin. The smoked-meat character plays against butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the crushed-stone minerality echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak savory Willamette Pinot for filet — age-worthy Oregon serious red. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|French Onion": "Willamette Pinot — savory Eola-Amity Pinot, medium tannin, elegant delicate. The medium tannin meets French Onion's caramelized onion depth and the savory edge plays against the beef-broth umami. Peaks from the dish's side; on this wine's card it's strong — below peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins. Confident pour when a guest anchors on French Onion. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Grilled Caesar": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone. The medium tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the smoked-meat plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong savory Pinot for grilled Caesar — Willamette depth on the layered salad.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Gumbo": "Evening Land Seven Springs's cherry-earth tannin holds with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Willamette Pinot — savory Eola-Amity Pinot, medium tannin, elegant delicate. The savory edge plays against carrots' honey-glaze sweetness and the medium tannin meets the roasted char. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Honey Roasted Carrots but below the wine's peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|House Wedge": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone. The smoked-meat plays against iceberg-and-bacon and the dried-rose plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak savory Pinot for the wedge — Eola-Amity complexity reframes the classic steakhouse salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Kansas City": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, medium tannin. The medium tannin handles Kansas City's lean-bold strip and the smoked-meat plays against beefy char. Strong savory Willamette Pinot for the strip — structured enough for the marbled cut.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Loaded Potato": "Evening Land Seven Springs's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Lobster Mac": "Evening Land Seven Springs's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Market Fish": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Evening Land Seven Springs on the steak course instead.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Mocha Creme": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Evening Land Seven Springs belongs on the steak course.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Mushroom Bisque": "Willamette Pinot — savory Eola-Amity Pinot, medium tannin, elegant delicate. The savory edge plays against the bisque's mushroom umami and the medium tannin lifts the cream base. Works as elegant Oregon Pinot for the bisque — delicate Seven Springs character on the earthy soup.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Mushrooms": "Evening Land Seven Springs: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this earthy, umami-packed side's mushroom-and-truffle depth. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. Works — clean if not dominant.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Evening Land Seven Springs for the steak course.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Porterhouse": "Evening Land Seven Springs's cherry-earth tannin holds the line with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Prime Tartare": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone, medium body. The smoked-meat character plays against raw-beef delicacy and the crushed-stone minerality frames egg yolk and capers. Peak savory Pinot for tartare — Eola-Amity complexity on the raw-beef classic. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Rainbow Trout": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Evening Land Seven Springs on the steak course instead.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Roast Half Chicken": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone. The smoked-meat plays against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong savory Pinot for chicken across most seasonal builds.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Willamette Pinot — savory Eola-Amity Pinot, medium tannin, elegant delicate. The savory edge plays against the soup's smoky chickpea base and the medium tannin meets roasted-pepper sweetness. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea but below the wine's peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Evening Land Seven Springs for the steak course.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Evening Land Seven Springs: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this light, green vegetable's clean herbal edge. The elegant red's earthy edge meets light green or sweet root — cherry and earth hold the pairing together. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Seafood Tower": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Evening Land Seven Springs for the steak course.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Seared Scallops": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone, medium tannin. The smoked-meat plays against scallop sweetness and the crushed-stone frames caramelized-exterior char. Peak savory Pinot for scallops — Willamette depth on the delicate shellfish. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Seasonal Soup": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the silky body carries the seasonal soup, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; too light for dessert density at this level.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Seasonal Vegetables": "Evening Land Seven Springs's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Shrimp Bisque": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone. The crushed-stone minerality cuts bisque's cream weight and the smoked-meat plays against shellfish umami and sherry. Peak savory Pinot for shrimp bisque — Willamette complexity against the rich soup. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Shrimp Cocktail": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance overshadows the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Evening Land Seven Springs for the steak course.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Swordfish": "Willamette Pinot — savory Eola-Amity Pinot, medium tannin, elegant delicate. The medium tannin meets swordfish's meaty flesh and the savory edge plays against the grilled char. Peak Oregon Pinot for swordfish — Evening Land structure on the grilled classic. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|The Tomahawk": "Willamette Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — savory Eola-Amity Pinot has some grip but not enough for the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Evening Land for filet, salmon, or tartare.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Tomato Basil": "Evening Land Seven Springs's cherry-earth tannin finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Truffle Fries": "Evening Land Seven Springs: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this earthy, umami-packed side's mushroom-and-truffle depth. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. Works — clean if not dominant.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Willamette Pinot — savory Eola-Amity Pinot, medium tannin, elegant delicate. The savory edge plays against tuna's rare-seared meatiness and the medium tannin handles honey sambal's sweet-spicy edge. Strong Oregon Pinot for tuxedo tuna — Evening Land elegance on the crusted classic.",
  "Evening Land Seven Springs|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Willamette Pinot — savory Eola-Amity Pinot, medium tannin, elegant delicate. The savory edge plays against the curry's chickpea-vegetable base and the medium tannin lifts the coconut-milk weight. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas but below the wine's peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Asparagus": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Oakville luxurious Cab — blackcurrant, dark chocolate, espresso, violet, smooth powerful. The opulent body cuts au gratin's cheese-and-cream weight and the chocolate-espresso plays against the golden crust. Works as luxurious Oakville Cab for au gratin — Far Niente opulence on the decadent side.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Beignets": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The starter's fat and umami meet the bold red's dark fruit — both land on the palate with real weight. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone-In Filet": "Oakville luxurious Cab — blackcurrant, dark chocolate, espresso, violet, smooth powerful, long finish. The opulent body meets bone-in filet's added depth and the chocolate-espresso frames the steak-flavor directly. Peak luxurious Cab for bone-in filet — Far Niente's long finish aligns with the premium tenderloin cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccoli Cheddar": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccolini": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Burrata": "Far Niente Oakville Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — opulent chocolate-espresso body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackcurrant-violet. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Far Niente for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Butternut Squash": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: the carrot cake's warm-spice, cream-cheese indulgence meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Dark chocolate and spice echo the bold red's fruit, but the tannin fights the sugar unless the dessert is dry-cocoa-bitter. Works when the dessert leans bitter rather than sweet.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Cheesecake": "Cheesecake: the cheesecake's dense, tangy dairy richness meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Dark chocolate and spice echo the bold red's fruit, but the tannin fights the sugar unless the dessert is dry-cocoa-bitter. Works when the dessert leans bitter rather than sweet.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Chilean Seabass": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Dark chocolate and spice echo the bold red's fruit, but the tannin fights the sugar unless the dessert is dry-cocoa-bitter. Works when the dessert leans bitter rather than sweet.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Cake": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Clam Chowder": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Crab Cake": "Far Niente Oakville Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — opulent chocolate-espresso crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Far Niente for the steak course where its luxurious Oakville character shines.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamed Spinach": "Far Niente — Oakville Cab, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, espresso, violet, smooth powerful. The opulent body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the chocolate-espresso plays against the earthy sauce. Strong luxurious Oakville Cab for creamed spinach — Far Niente opulence on the classic steakhouse side.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamy Potato": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Creme Brulee": "Creme Brulee: this delicate custard's creamy, vanilla sweetness meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Creamy vanilla or airy beignets get run over by bold-red tannin. Works as an unconventional pour — set expectations.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Escargot": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Faroe Island Salmon": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Filet Mignon": "Far Niente — Oakville Cab, lush luxurious with blackcurrant, dark chocolate, espresso, violet, smooth powerful structure, extraordinarily long finish. The opulent body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the chocolate-espresso echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak luxurious Oakville Cab for filet — pair with the best cuts. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|French Onion": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the estate Cab body matches the French onion, and the mocha edge plays against the bowl. Works; the dessert sits cleanly against the meal's register.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Grilled Caesar": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis sits beside the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the opulent body overshoots the leaf-and-acid plate, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Gumbo": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|House Wedge": "Oakville luxurious Cab — blackcurrant, dark chocolate, espresso, violet, smooth powerful. The opulent body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the chocolate-espresso plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as luxurious Oakville Cab for the wedge — Far Niente red choice, though whites peak higher and the wine deserves a bigger course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Kansas City": "Kansas City: the strip's marbled-and-char richness finds the bold red's grip; cassis carries the savory weight cleanly. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Loaded Potato": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Lobster Mac": "Lobster Mac: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Market Fish": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Mocha Creme": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushroom Bisque": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushrooms": "Far Niente — Oakville Cab with blackcurrant, dark chocolate, espresso, violet, smooth powerful. The opulent body meshes with mushroom umami and the chocolate-espresso frames the sauté earth. Strong luxurious Oakville Cab for mushrooms — Far Niente opulence on the earthy side with extraordinary long finish.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Porterhouse": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Prime Tartare": "Luxurious Oakville Cab against prime tartare's raw-iron richness — opulent chocolate-espresso crushes the delicate seasoned beef. Never pair. Route tartare to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Far Niente for the steak course where its Oakville luxurious character peaks.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Rainbow Trout": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Roast Half Chicken": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis leans against the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character jars against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Seafood Tower": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Seared Scallops": "Luxurious Oakville Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — opulent chocolate-espresso obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Far Niente for the steak course where its best-cuts character peaks.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Soup": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character overwhelms the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Vegetables": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Bisque": "Far Niente — Oakville Cab, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, espresso, violet, smooth powerful. The opulent body can meet the bisque's cream-shellfish richness but Cab-with-shellfish-soup stretches the axis. Works as Far Niente for bisque — Chardonnay, Champagne, or Riesling peak higher on cream-soup.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Cocktail": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character crowds out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Swordfish": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Tomato Basil": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Truffle Fries": "Oakville luxurious Cab — blackcurrant, dark chocolate, espresso, violet, smooth powerful. The opulent body frames truffle-fry earth and the chocolate-espresso cuts the crisp oil-and-salt. Peak luxurious Oakville Cab for truffle fries — Far Niente's long finish meets the decadent side with extraordinary depth. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Asparagus": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity stays alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Au Gratin Potatoes": "* Far Niente Chardonnay -- Napa Chardonnay, tropical fruit, oak. The cool-climate register carries the au gratin, and the chalky minerality plays against the side. Peak pour. Gold standard; the call that earns the regular.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Beignets": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Far Niente Chardonnay belongs on the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Bone Marrow": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity sits beside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Bone-In Filet": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Far Niente Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Broccoli Cheddar": "Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush. The opulent oak aligns with the soup's cheddar-cream weight and the hazelnut plays against the broccoli vegetal base. Peak special-occasion Chardonnay for broccoli cheddar — Napa opulence on the classic. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Broccolini": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Brussels and Belly": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity holds with the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Burrata": "Far Niente — Oakville Chardonnay with buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush texture. The opulent oak is heavier than burrata needs — the lush weight threatens to crush the delicate cheese, though the hazelnut does play against pistachio. Strong with restraint — lighter Chards peak higher.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Butternut Squash": "Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush. The opulent oak aligns with butternut's cream-sweet weight and the hazelnut plays against nutmeg-cinnamon warmth. Strong special-occasion Chardonnay for butternut squash — Napa opulence on the autumn classic.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Carrot Cake": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Far Niente Chardonnay belongs on the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Cheesecake": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Far Niente Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Chilean Seabass": "* Far Niente Chardonnay -- Napa Chardonnay, tropical fruit, oak. The bright acid lifts the seabass, and the stone-fruit note underlines the plate. California pivot for guests who want American producers over Loire when pairing with cheese. Gold standard; the call that earns the regular.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Chocolate Brownie": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Far Niente Chardonnay belongs on the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Chocolate Cake": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Far Niente Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Clam Chowder": "Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush. The opulent oak aligns with the chowder's cream-and-clam weight and the hazelnut plays against potato and bacon bits. Peak special-occasion Chardonnay for clam chowder — Napa opulence on the New England classic. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Cowboy Ribeye": "Napa Oakville Chardonnay against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — even opulent oak is the wrong axis for rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Far Niente for lobster mac, salmon, scallops, or special-occasion whites.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Crab Cake": "Far Niente — Napa Oakville Chardonnay with buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush texture. The opulent oak cuts crab's romesco and the hazelnut complements the sweet meat directly. Strong special-occasion Chardonnay for crab cake — Napa opulence on the classic starter.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Creamed Spinach": "Far Niente — Napa Oakville Chardonnay, buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush texture. The opulent oak-butter aligns with the creamed spinach's dairy weight and the hazelnut adds depth against the rich side. Works well as Chardonnay for creamed spinach — cream-on-cream, oak-on-butter. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Creamy Potato": "Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush. The opulent oak aligns with the soup's cream-and-potato weight and the hazelnut plays against the smooth dairy base. Peak special-occasion Chardonnay for creamy potato — Napa opulence on cream-on-cream. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Creme Brulee": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Far Niente Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Escargot": "Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush velvety. The opulent oak-butter weight cuts escargot's garlic-parsley and the hazelnut plays against the snail's rich texture. Strong special-occasion Chardonnay for escargot — Napa weight on the French classic.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Faroe Island Salmon": "Napa Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush velvety texture, long indulgent finish. The lush oak weight handles salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen. Peak special-occasion Chardonnay for salmon — Napa opulence across most seasonal builds. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Filet Mignon": "Far Niente — Oakville Chardonnay, buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush velvety texture. The opulent oak weight handles butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the hazelnut echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong Chardonnay-with-steak when guest wants Napa white over Cab.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|French Onion": "Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush. The opulent oak aligns with French Onion's cheese-and-broth weight and the hazelnut plays against the caramelized crust. Strong special-occasion Chardonnay for French Onion — Napa opulence on the hearty classic.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Grilled Caesar": "Napa Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush. The opulent oak cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the hazelnut plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong special-occasion Chardonnay for grilled Caesar — Napa weight on the layered classic salad.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Gumbo": "Oakville Chardonnay against a dark-roux Cajun stew — opulent oak-butter has no hook into gumbo's smoky-spicy depth. Never pair. Route Far Niente to salmon, lobster mac, scallops, or the bisque course; take gumbo with Rioja, CdP, Super Tuscan, or Zinfandel.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity sits alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the nut character meets the caramel. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|House Wedge": "Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush velvety. The opulent oak weight cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the hazelnut plays against blue cheese. Works as a special-occasion Chardonnay for the wedge — though the wine deserves a more substantial course.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Kansas City": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Far Niente Chardonnay on another course instead.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Loaded Potato": "Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush. The opulent oak aligns with the soup's cheddar-bacon-cream weight and the hazelnut plays against the loaded toppings. Peaks from the dish's side; on this wine's card it's strong — below peaks with shellfish, Market Fish, and chicken. Confident pour when a guest anchors on Loaded Potato. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Lobster Mac": "Far Niente Chardonnay -- Napa Chardonnay, tropical fruit, oak. The cool-climate register carries the lobster mac, and the green-apple edge brightens the side. Excellent; a confident headline pairing. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Market Fish": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity plays cleanly against the fish's clean flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Mocha Creme": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Far Niente Chardonnay lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Mushroom Bisque": "Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush. The opulent oak aligns with the bisque's cream-and-mushroom weight and the hazelnut echoes roasted-mushroom earth directly. Peak special-occasion Chardonnay for the bisque — Napa opulence on umami-on-cream. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Mushrooms": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Far Niente Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Porterhouse": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Far Niente Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Prime Tartare": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity leans against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Rainbow Trout": "Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush. The opulent oak is heavier than delicate trout demands — Napa lushness overwhelms freshwater character. Works as special-occasion Chardonnay for rainbow trout; leaner whites like Chablis or Sauv Blanc peak higher on the delicate classic.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Roast Half Chicken": "Napa Chardonnay — full oak treatment, tropical fruit, rounder body. Stronger match than Keenan when the kitchen's running a richer chicken preparation; the wine's oak meets the roasted-skin caramelization directly and the body handles herb accompaniments. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Salt-Cured Halibut: this delicate seafood's mild, flaky flesh meets this rich, oak-aged Chardonnay's butter, stone fruit, and full-bodied weight. Oaky Chardonnay's butter and stone fruit lock into rich fish — texture matching texture. A headliner white pour for rich fish. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Seafood Tower": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity finds register with the tower's chilled shellfish — the crisp acidity lifts the shellfish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Seared Scallops": "Napa Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, velvety texture. The lush oak-butter echoes the scallops' pan-butter directly and the hazelnut adds depth against the pan-sear. Peak special-occasion Chardonnay for scallops — indulgence meets precision. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Seasonal Soup": "Far Niente Chardonnay: Napa flagship oak-cream-apple Chardonnay. Excellent on seasonal soup; the oak-cream character handles most seasonal cream-based soups at premium register. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Seasonal Vegetables": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the Chardonnay weight matches the seasonal vegetables, and the tropical fruit lifts the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Shrimp Bisque": "Far Niente Chardonnay -- Napa Chardonnay, tropical fruit, oak. The crisp white-wine body matches the shrimp bisque, and the stone-fruit note underlines the bowl. Excellent; a textbook match that delivers. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Shrimp Cocktail": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity frames the shrimp's chilled sweetness — the crisp acidity lifts the shellfish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Swordfish": "Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush. The opulent oak aligns with swordfish's meaty firm flesh and the hazelnut plays against the grilled char. Peak special-occasion Chardonnay for swordfish — Napa opulence on the grilled classic. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|The Tomahawk": "Far Niente opulent Chardonnay against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even Napa weight is the wrong axis for Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Far Niente for scallops, lobster mac, salmon, or the chicken course.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Tomato Basil": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity stays alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Truffle Fries": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity matches the tuna's seared rare crust — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Far Niente Chardonnay|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity plays cleanly against the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the 1792 Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the 1792 Sweet Wheat for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the 1881 Napa Valley for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|2 James Gin": "2 James with the salmon — Detroit distillery gin against oily salmon. Strong; 2 James's juniper lifts the fish at local-craft register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the A Midnight's Winter Dram for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Aberlour 16 for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino with the salmon — filtered aged against salmon's richness. Strong; aged-but-clear frames the fish with distinctive craft character.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado with the salmon — near-añejo reposado against salmon richness. Strong; the aged depth frames the salmon with more gravitas than younger reposados.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight handles salmon's oil-rich flesh and the apple-pear fruit plays against the flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak Chardonnay for salmon across most seasonal builds. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Alexander Valley Cyrus for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Alexander Valley Homestead Red belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bitter herbs on oily fish read metallic — the rich flesh deserves a crisp white. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Ancho Reyes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with the salmon — flagship AE against oily salmon. Avoid; port-finished bourbon wrong for fish.",
