  "Faroe Island Salmon|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Blood Oath Pact 8 on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Blood Oath Pact 9 for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat with the salmon — Philadelphia citrus-forward craft against oily salmon. Excellent; Bluecoat's 4-citrus profile is the featured Smooth/Citrus Craft Gin-salmon pairing — citrus-and-fish is textbook, Bluecoat brings it with American-artisan distinction.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Bodega Noemia A Lisa on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry with the salmon — 8-botanical London Dry against oily salmon. Strong; Bombay Dry's juniper lifts the fish with traditional-gin character.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire with the salmon — vapor-infused 10-botanical against oily salmon. Strong; Sapphire's softer character frames the fish without the aggression of flagship London Drys.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Bone Marrow": "The salmon after the bone marrow -- the pan-finished salmon frames the beefy unctuous opener. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Booker's": "Booker's with the salmon — Beam 120-130 proof against oily salmon. Avoid; barrel-proof bourbon is too intense for fish.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Botanist Gin": "Botanist with the salmon — Bruichladdich Islay botanical gin against oily salmon. Strong; the Hebridean-coastal botanicals find resonance with Faroe Island fish — coastal gin meets coastal fish thematically.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace muddled with our rum-soaked cherries, orange, and Angostura over a large cube. Bourbon weight buries the Faroe's delicate flavor and fat. Reserve this cocktail for the steak or marrow course. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Bowman Brothers Small Batch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Bowmore 12": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Bowmore 12 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin, medium body. The high acid and low tannin play against salmon's rich omega-three oils; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong benchmark Barbera for salmon — food-wine acid handles fish across most seasonal preparations.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Broccoli Cheddar": "Faroe Island Salmon after the broccoli cheddar -- the cheddar-and-broccoli cream opens with rich-savory weight, the salmon following with oily flesh that meets the cream without crowding. Strong; both courses honor richness in their own register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Broccolini": "The salmon alongside the broccolini -- the salmon's rich oily flesh meets the broccolini's tender green stalks cleanly. Strong; the call holds its register at the table against the main.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich with the salmon — clean barley notes and soft citrus against the salmon's richness. Without the peat, the pairing keeps the fish centered. One of the best non-peated Islay pairings on the card. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Brussels and Belly": "Faroe Island Salmon with Brussels + Belly -- the buttery salmon sits cleanly with the sweet-savory build. Strong; reliable side for the salmon.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Buffalo Trace belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Bulleit Bourbon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Burrata": "The salmon and the burrata across courses -- the oil-rich fish anchors against the heirloom burrata plate. Strong; the opener carries its register before the main.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Butternut Squash": "Faroe Island Salmon after the butternut squash -- the silky sweet squash cream opens with autumnal warmth, the salmon following with rich oily flesh that the squash's sweetness echoes without doubling. Strong; sweet cream sets the table for oily fish.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cade Cabernet Sauvignon's Howell Mountain Cab character jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Cade Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila with the salmon — value reposado against salmon richness. Strong; accessible agave frames the salmon at entry tier.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Campari": "Campari's aperitivo at its most textbook clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Campari for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Canadian Club for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Canadian Club 43 Year": "Canadian Club 43 Year's Canadian whisky character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Canadian Club 43 Year for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's clean silver register reads alongside the salmon's rich oily flesh: the cane-and-citrus body lifts the salmon, and the rum-and-lime thread softens the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Caribou Crossing on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Carrot Cake": "The salmon into the carrot cake -- the pan-finished Atlantic flesh hands off to spiced cake and cream-cheese frosting, walnut crunch on the close. Works; the rich fish and the spice cake share the meal at separate registers.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Casa LBV Port for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- mezcal smoke and the fish's natural oils refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a mezcal. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Casamigos Mezcal belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon's cocoa-mocha velvety-tannin opulence clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert-grade sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs a crisp white instead. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Cheesecake": "The salmon closes with the cheesecake -- the pan-finished oily flesh hands off to dense dairy custard, graham crust and berry compote framing the close. Works; the meal closes round, the dairy reading thick against the rich fish.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Chocolate Brownie": "The salmon closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the pan-finished Atlantic flesh yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Chocolate Cake": "After the salmon, the chocolate cake -- the rich oily flesh hands off to layered cocoa-and-ganache, the vanilla scoop softening the close. Works; the oily fish softens before the chocolate weight, the meal builds toward the close.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin with the salmon — single-ingredient potato against oily salmon. Strong; Chopin's creaminess rides salmon's richness as a complementary pairing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Bros Brandy's barrel-aged cognac character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a cognac. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Christian Bros Brandy belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Château Batailley": "Château Batailley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Château Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Château Beaucastel": "Château Beaucastel's old-world Châteauneuf-du-Pape gravitas clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Château Beaucastel for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Château Gombaude-Guillot for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Château Haut Segottes": "Château Haut Segottes' Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Château Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Château de Rouillac": "Château de Rouillac's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Château de Rouillac for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Clam Chowder": "Faroe Island Salmon into the chowder -- the pan-finished salmon sits with the chowder. Works; safe opener, but not the headline pick.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Clase Azul Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango's aged-agave character sits alongside the salmon's rich oily flesh: the aged-agave weight wraps the salmon, and the soft-spice note softens the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold's joven blend with mezcal accent clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- agave and oily fish refuse each other — the fish needs acid, not aged spirit. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Clase Azul Gold lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the main fish course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a mezcal. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata with the salmon — ultra-premium blanco against salmon's richness. Strong; smooth agave frames the fish with ceremony.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado with the salmon — ceramic-bottle reposado against salmon richness. The smoothness of Clase Azul is a refined frame for the fish; a premium tequila-salmon pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra's aged-agave character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- agave and oily fish refuse each other — the fish needs acid, not aged spirit. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Clase Azul Ultra for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Clermont Steep lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body. The medium body matches salmon's oil-richness and the wild raspberry lifts against flesh-sweetness. Peak Southern-French for salmon — preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen for today's build. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with the salmon — Napa Cabernet-aged reposado against salmon richness. The red-wine fruit influence gives the salmon a wine-logic frame; arguably the best Rosa-reposado salmon pairing because of the Napa influence. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's vermouth-wine-base sweetness leans against the salmon's rich oily flesh — the bitter herb edge cleans the fish. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco with the salmon — single-estate highland against salmon's richness. Strong; the agave-forward character frames the fish at single-estate register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado with the salmon — Sazerac reposado against salmon richness. Strong; highland agave's brightness frames the salmon differently than oak-forward reposados.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's barrel-aged tequila weight finds neutral with the salmon's rich oily flesh: the oak-aged tequila body threads the salmon, and the oak threads the plate. Works; craft character doesn't compete with dish density.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg's rested-agave body finds neutral with the salmon's rich oily flesh: the aged-agave weight wraps the salmon, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the plate. Works; ceremonial pour over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the salmon's rich oily flesh: the añejo register carries the salmon, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Corazón del Sol Malbec on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Corpse Reviver": "Corpse Reviver's anise-citrus build plays cleanly against the salmon's rich oily flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- cognac and oily fish double up the richness with no foil. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a cognac. Avoid; the better calls are above. Courvoisier Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Crab Cake": "Faroe Island Salmon with the crab cake as the lead-in -- the Atlantic salmon plate holds beside the sweet shellfish plate. Works; the opener composes at neutral register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Cragganmore 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Creamed Spinach": "The salmon into the creamed spinach -- two rich courses on the same plate, the table earns its substance. Works; safe alongside, but not the headline pick.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Creamy Potato": "Faroe Island Salmon after the potato soup -- the silky leek-and-potato cream opens with quiet richness, the salmon following with rich oily flesh that meets the cream on the same plane. Strong; cream into oil reads indulgent without overlap.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Creme Brulee": "After the salmon, the brulee -- the oily-rich Faroe yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the rich fish meets the silky dessert, the close lands clean.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Willamette Pinot from the Eola-Amity Hills — bright cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral backbone. Benchmark Oregon producer. A peak Pinot match for the Faroe salmon's richness; elegant without being delicate, handles any seasonal sauce. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Crown Royal on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Cucumber Gimlet": "Hendrick's muddled with cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, topped with soda. Cool cocktail that lets the Faroe speak; the cucumber and ginger don't fight whatever seasonal prep shows up. Clean cocktail route for the salmon. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Dalmore 12 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body, high acid. The chalk-minerality plays against salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak French red Pinot for salmon — distinct Loire style across most preparations. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Darioush Cabernet Franc for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin with the salmon — Detroit City Distillery against oily salmon. Strong; Detroit City's juniper frames the fish at hometown register; Iron Fish's northern-Michigan farm character is the distinctive salmon match.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City with the salmon — local Michigan vodka against oily salmon. Strong; Detroit City frames the fish with hometown craft character.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15's 15yr aged Highland blend overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Dewar's 15 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label's Highland blend with Aberfeldy-malt core swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Dewar's White Label for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- heavy spirit weight buries the main protein — needs a wine or lighter spirit. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Willamette flagship Pinot Blend — dark cherry, Bing cherry, toasted spice, forest floor, silky layered. The layered structure plays against salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak flagship Willamette Pinot for salmon across most seasonal preparations. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Domaine Tempier Bandol's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Domaine Tempier Bandol lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Pouilly-Fumé's flinty Loire Sauvignon Blanc — gunflint smoke character hooks into the seared salmon exterior. More depth than Sancerre, tighter acid than Chablis. Good non-obvious white pour when the kitchen's running a grill-or-plancha prep on the Faroe. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Domaine du Grand Tinel lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Don Fulano Anejo": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Don Fulano Anejo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco with the salmon — premium highland blanco against salmon's richness. The refined craft agave frames the fish elegantly; arguably the best blanco-salmon match alongside Don Julio Blanco and Mijenta. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado with the salmon — premium highland agave against salmon richness. The refined vanilla-honey frames the salmon elegantly; arguably the best reposado-salmon match alongside Don Julio 70th. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942's icon extra-añejo character leans against the salmon's rich oily flesh: the rested-agave depth sits with the salmon, and the soft-spice note softens the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th with the salmon — crystalline filtered reposado against salmon's richness. The 70th's clean smoothness lifts the salmon in a way standard Don Julio Reposado doesn't quite reach. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco with the salmon — iconic blanco against salmon's richness. The fresh agave and citrus lift the fish without competing; the benchmark blanco-salmon pairing. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Don Julio Reposado": "Don Julio Reposado with the Faroe Island Salmon — reposado agave against salmon richness. Strong; the reposado's brightness frames the salmon cleanly.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Drambuie": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Drambuie on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Dunville's Single Malt": "Faroe Island Salmon: oily salmon meets Belfast Irish single malt. Dunville's Northern Ireland character + Irish-single-malt refinement is a distinctive Irish-salmon pairing — Garavogue brings western-Ireland craft, Dunville's brings northern-Ireland single-malt polish. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the salmon — BT Bottled-in-Bond against oily salmon. Avoid; barrel-proof bourbon too intense for fish.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. E.H. Taylor Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the E.H. Taylor Small Batch on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak elegance clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Eagle Rare 10 Year for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Eastern Kille with the salmon — Grand Rapids classic juniper against oily salmon. Strong; Eastern Kille's London Dry style frames the fish at GR-craft register; Iron Fish's northern-Michigan farm profile is still the distinctive Local MI Gin-salmon match.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|El Cabo": "El Cabo with the salmon — craft small-batch reposado against salmon richness. Strong; agave brightness frames the salmon at the boutique register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "El Mayor Extra Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- agave and oily fish refuse each other — the fish needs acid, not aged spirit. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the El Mayor Extra Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado with the salmon — value reposado against salmon richness. Strong; agave-forward profile frames the salmon accessibly — Don Julio 70th or Don Fulano for refined takes.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin with the salmon — German-distilled African-botanical gin against oily salmon. Strong; Elephant's distinctive profile frames the fish at contemporary register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Elijah Craig 18 Year for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Elijah Craig Private Barrel for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Elijah Craig Single Barrel for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Elijah Craig Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Elijah Craig Straight Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Faroe Island Salmon: this rich, fatty seafood's buttery flesh and delicate-yet-substantial texture meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's cherry-bright, low-tannin frame threads cleanly through the fish — classic Burgundy logic. A headliner fish pair. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc — Willamette, pear, peach, floral, medium body. The refreshing acid cuts salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong Oregon white for salmon across most seasonal builds — regional pairing done thoughtfully.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Elmer T. Lee for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 with the salmon — color-changing BC gin against oily salmon. Excellent; Empress's rose-ginger-fennel profile lifts the fish with Pacific-Northwest-adjacent terroir distinction and visual-distinctive presentation.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Epoch Ingenuity": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Epoch Ingenuity belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Escargot": "Faroe Island Salmon with the escargot as the lead-in -- the rich oily flesh anchors against the butter-bath starter. Strong; the starter sets the table for the salmon.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Espresso Martini": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- whiskey cocktail and oily fish refuse each other — needs acid, not bitters. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Espresso Martini lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- whiskey cocktail and oily fish refuse each other — needs acid, not bitters. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Espresso Old Fashioned for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Eola-Amity Hills Willamette Pinot — darker and more savory than the Avni. Black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone. For guests who want a serious Pinot; the smoked-meat note hooks into a seared Faroe prep beautifully. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Napa Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush velvety texture, long indulgent finish. The lush oak weight handles salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen. Peak special-occasion Chardonnay for salmon — Napa opulence across most seasonal builds. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid, Burgundian lift. The high acid cuts salmon's oil-richness and slate minerality echoes coastal brine. Peak Galician-Mencía for salmon — preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen for today's build. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity finds neutral with the salmon's rich oily flesh — the bitter herb edge cleans the fish, and the bitter cuts the fat. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta's crisp mint-menthol edge plays cleanly against the salmon's rich oily flesh — the bitter herb edge cleans the fish, and the bitter cuts the fat. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Filet Mignon": "The filet's lean buttery tenderness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh: the buttery beef overwhelms the oily-rich fish, and the lean cut crowds out the salmon. Avoid; the two protein courses pull in different directions. Order one or the other -- pour Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year for the filet, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain for the salmon.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot — bright cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky tannin. Rounder and riper than the Willamette Pinots; handles salmon well for guests who want California over Oregon. Not the peak, but solid. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Four Roses Small Batch Select lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Four Roses Yellow Label for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Fox & Oden lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Fox & Oden Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Fox & Oden Double Oaked belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert-grade sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs a crisp white instead. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Freemark Abbey Bosché lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|French 75": "French 75's citrus-earth build matches the salmon's rich oily flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|French Onion": "Faroe Island Salmon with the French onion alongside -- the oil-rich fish rests against the broiled-cheese opener. Works; the side-salad call holds before the main.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Frias Block 5": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- this specific sparkling's register is wrong for the main — try a different brut or vintage. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a sparkling. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco with the salmon — craft 80-proof against salmon's richness. Strong; agave-forward character frames the fish at the craft register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof with the salmon — 104-proof agave against salmon richness. Strong; the bold pour meets the salmon's weight where softer reposados only work.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue with the salmon — Sligo-river-named craft Irish whiskey, western Ireland distinctive small-batch character. Excellent; Garavogue's craft-Irish profile is the featured Irish-salmon pairing — the western-Ireland provenance frames Faroe Island fish with a distinctive north-Atlantic story that Jameson's iconic-entry can't tell as specifically.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|George T. Stagg": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the George T. Stagg on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Ghost Block Zinfandel on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12's entry Speyside benchmark overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Glenfiddich 12 on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14's Highland-scotch character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Glenfiddich 14 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18's aged Speyside with sherry finish smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Glenfiddich 18 lands on the steak course — not here.",
