  "Faroe Island Salmon|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Glenmorangie 10 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Glenmorangie 18 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- honeyed wine and oily fish double up richness with no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Graham's 20 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Graham's 2017 Vintage Port's structured vintage port with dark-fruit weight clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Graham's 2017 Vintage Port on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum with the salmon — triple-distilled Patron against salmon's richness. Strong; the refined character frames the fish at the premium register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Grand Marnier on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale with the salmon — California ocean-conservation gin against oily salmon. Excellent; Gray Whale's kombu-and-ocean-botanical profile is a thematic-perfect fish pairing — ocean gin meets ocean fish.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Green Chartreuse on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose with the salmon — Cognac-region wheat vodka against oily salmon. Strong; Goose's softness frames the fish with polish without fighting its richness.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Grilled Caesar": "Faroe Island Salmon after the Caesar -- the charred-romaine and anchovy edge opens with savory-bright lift, the salmon following with rich oily flesh that the anchovy preceded in the same register. Strong; anchovy into oily fish reads composed.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Gumbo": "Faroe Island Salmon into the gumbo -- the buttery salmon holds beside the spiced creole bowl. Works; safe opener, but not the headline pick.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 with the salmon — 100-proof craft vodka against oily salmon. Strong; HDW 100's extra ABV frames the fish's richness with herbal lift.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 with the salmon — Japanese single malt with subtle peat against oily salmon. Excellent; Hakushu's crisp-green + subtle-smoke character frames fish — the pine-forest notes marry salmon's oiliness with Forest-Distillery distinction.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 with the salmon — Alameda blended vodka against oily salmon. Strong; Hangar 1's floral lift frames the fish with California-craft character.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Head Fake": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build plays cleanly against the salmon's rich oily flesh — the sweet character meets the fish at digestif register, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Heaven Hill 20 Year belongs on the steak course.",
