  "Faroe Island Salmon|Johnnie Walker Red": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Johnnie Walker Red on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre — Loire Sauv Blanc, grapefruit, chalk, herbs, tension-filled. The refined mineral acid cuts salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen. Peak benchmark Sauv Blanc for salmon across most seasonal builds — French precision meets oil-rich fish. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Jubilation by Colgin on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- heavy spirit weight buries the main protein — needs a wine or lighter spirit. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Jung and Wulff Guyana on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- heavy spirit weight buries the main protein — needs a wine or lighter spirit. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Jung and Wulff Trinidad belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Kahlua for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Keenan Chardonnay": "Napa Chardonnay with apple and lemon, softer oak than Far Niente. Same function, lighter footprint — handles the Faroe fat without the full oak weight. Pivot when the guest wants Napa Chard but not the fullest version. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Keenan Mernet": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Keenan Mernet for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Kentucky Owl Confiscated on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with the salmon — Kentucky Owl's Irish-American crossover against oily salmon. Excellent; Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's bourbon-cask-finished Irish frames the fish with luxury-crossover distinction.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The medium body handles salmon's oil-richness and the red cherry lifts against flesh-sweetness. Peak Southern-French for salmon — preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen for today's build. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One with the salmon — Dutch wheat against oily salmon. Strong; Ketel's citrus brightens the fish where softer vodkas just accompany.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker with the salmon — brewer's-heritage gin against oily salmon. Strong; Knickerbocker's hop-botanical lift frames the fish at distinctive register; Iron Fish's farm profile is still the featured Local MI Gin-salmon match.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Knob Creek for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year's aged 12 years at 100 proof overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Knob Creek 12 Year for the steak course.",
