  "Faroe Island Salmon|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Knob Creek 18 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Knob Creek Rye for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva's aged-agave character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Komos Anejo Reserva lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo's crisp char-floral character lifts the salmon's rich oily flesh — the añejo weight overshoots the fish, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- agave and oily fish refuse each other — the fish needs acid, not aged spirit. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Komos Extra Anejo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado with the salmon — wine-cask-finished reposado against salmon richness. The pink character and strawberry notes frame the salmon with rosé-wine logic; the rosa-reposado salmon pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Lagavulin 8": "Lagavulin 8's younger-Islay peat character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Lagavulin 8 on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Lail Vineyards Blueprint's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Lail Vineyards Blueprint for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver with the salmon — DJ-family-descendant craft against salmon's richness. Strong; the clean additive-free character frames the fish at craft heritage register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Laphroaig 10": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Laphroaig 10 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch's Heaven Hill wheated caramel clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Larceny Small Batch belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Pinot Noir Champagne rosé — silky mousse, red berry aromas, long elegant finish. The structure and body handle salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works well as rosé Champagne across most seasonal salmon preparations.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne — green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, clean long finish. The vibrant acidity cuts salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works across most seasonal salmon builds as elegant Champagne pour.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Le Garenne Rosé": "Provence rosé — strawberry, citrus, clean. Works against the salmon but doesn't peak; the Chardonnays and Pinots do more against the Faroe's fat. Default pour for a guest committed to rosé through the main.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Le Volte dell'Ornellaia for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build holds the line with the salmon's rich oily flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Les Pallières Raciné": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Les Pallières Raciné for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Liberator Gin": "Liberator with the salmon — 100% Michigan-grain craft gin against oily salmon. Strong; Liberator frames the fish at hometown-craft register; Iron Fish's northern-Michigan farm profile is the distinctive MI-gin-salmon match.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Licor 43 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Limoncello lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, low tannin. The classic salmon-and-Pinot move; the low tannin won't clash with the Faroe's fat and the forest-floor savory depth meets the salmon's richness. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Little Book": "Little Book with the salmon — Beam Little Book cask-strength against oily salmon. Avoid; barrel-proof bourbon too intense for fish.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Loaded Potato": "Faroe Island Salmon into the loaded potato -- the oil-rich fish holds against the indulgent loaded soup. Works; safe opener, but not the headline pick.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Lobster Mac": "Faroe Island Salmon with the lobster mac -- the salmon's rich oily flesh meets the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year's complex 20yr aged Highland swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Loch Lomond 20 Year belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Lock Stock & Barrel Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Long Road Gin": "Long Road with the salmon — GR Michigan-botanical gin against oily salmon. Strong; Long Road's estate-pride frames the fish at hometown register; Iron Fish's northern-Michigan farm profile is still the distinctive Local MI Gin-salmon match.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the main fish course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a mezcal. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- an icon cognac belongs at the end of the meal as a special-occasion pour — not the main course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an icon cognac. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bitter herbs on oily fish read metallic — the rich flesh deserves a crisp white. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Lucano Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Lunaria Coste di Moro for the steak course.",
