  "Faroe Island Salmon|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Macallan 18 for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate's estate Speyside character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Macallan Estate for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Macauley Petite Syrah lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Faroe Island Salmon: this rich, fatty seafood's buttery flesh and delicate-yet-substantial texture meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's cherry-bright, low-tannin frame threads cleanly through the fish — classic Burgundy logic. A headliner fish pair. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Mahon Gin": "Mahón with the salmon — Balearic-island Mediterranean gin against oily salmon. Strong; Mahón's sharp juniper cuts the fish with Spanish-Mediterranean character.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Maker's Mark for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Maker's Mark Cellar Aged for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's unaged unaged-cane body leans against the salmon's rich oily flesh: the bright cane-and-citrus weight carries the salmon, and the cane-and-citrus thread frames the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Loire Cab Franc — Chinon light body, raspberry, red cherry, bell pepper, forest floor, supple low tannin, bright high acid. The bright acid plays against salmon's rich omega-three oils; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak food wine for salmon — lighter-than-Bordeaux CF handles fish across seasonal preparations.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Margarita": "Corazon Blanco, lemon, lime, orange, Cointreau. The triple-citrus can meet salmon's fat content well, though preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen. Works as a bright cocktail option alongside the salmon, especially for guests wanting lighter than Pinot Noir.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Marimar Estate Christina": "Russian River Green Valley Pinot — plush, ripe strawberry, cherry compote, warm spice, silky, rich. The opulent structure plays against salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak plush Pinot for salmon — Russian River opulence across most preparations. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Mata Hari Absinthe for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Year Bourbon's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Michter's 10 Year Bourbon for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye's rye-spice character smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Michter's 10 Year Rye for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Michter's US*1 Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco with the salmon — additive-free craft against salmon's richness. Strong; the premium-craft blanco frames the fish at the Maestra-Tequilera register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- agave and oily fish refuse each other — the fish needs acid, not aged spirit. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Milagro Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado with the salmon — triple-distilled reposado against salmon richness. Strong; the modern cleanness frames the salmon at the accessible tier — Don Julio 70th for the refined take.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Mocha Creme": "The salmon into the mocha creme -- the rich oily flesh hands off to silky coffee-chocolate cream, espresso lifting the close. Strong; the substantial fish earns the layered cream dessert at register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 with the salmon — Black Forest 47-botanical gin against oily salmon. Strong; Monkey 47's forest-herbal frames the fish at Schwarzwald-premium register; Inverroche's fynbos-floral is still the featured craft-salmon match.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Monkey Shoulder belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bitter herbs on oily fish read metallic — the rich flesh deserves a crisp white. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Montenegro Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum's crisp Barbados-molasses character holds the line with the salmon's rich oily flesh — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Muga Reserva": "Rioja Alta Reserva Tempranillo-blend — Muga, American-French oak, strawberry, dried cherry, vanilla, leather. The medium body handles salmon's oil-richness, though the oak-vanilla is slightly heavy for flesh-sweetness. Works for salmon — preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen for today's build.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Mushroom Bisque": "Faroe Island Salmon preceded by the mushroom bisque -- the pan-finished salmon reads alongside the mushroom bisque. Works; the call holds at neutral register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Mushrooms": "The salmon alongside the mushrooms -- the salmon's rich oily flesh meets the mushrooms' earthy umami weight cleanly. Strong; the pairing lands at register without strain against the main.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- heavy spirit weight buries the main protein — needs a wine or lighter spirit. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Myers's Rum belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Negroni": "Negroni's manhattan-or-old-fashioned register sits alongside the salmon's rich oily flesh: the bold-mix depth handles the salmon, and the bitters edge frames the plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Never Say Die Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Never Say Die Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|New York Sour": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- whiskey cocktail and oily fish refuse each other — needs acid, not bitters. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolet's with the salmon — floral-contemporary Dutch gin against oily salmon. Strong; Nolet's rose-peach lifts the fish with contemporary distinction.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Not a Paper Plane": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build lifts the salmon's rich oily flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Oban 14": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Oban 14 on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Oban 18": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Oban 18 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Ocho Anejo on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Odette Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Old Elk Cigar Cut lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Old Emmer on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year's Heaven Hill's wheated heritage bond clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Old Fitzgerald 7 Year for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year's aged wheated BIB's mature honey swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Old Fitzgerald 9 Year for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's 16yr OGD high-rye bourbon overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Old Grand-Dad 16 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Opus One": "Opus One: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets this rich, fatty seafood's buttery flesh and delicate-yet-substantial texture. The bold red's tannin shreds the fish's delicate flake, and the dark fruit clashes with briny sweetness. Textbook avoid — this is the classic red-meat-only territory. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Willamette Chardonnay made by Trappist monks — rich layered with ripe pear, butterscotch, toasted oak. Mention the provenance — it's a conversation starter. The rich oak weight handles salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen. Peak Oregon Chardonnay for salmon across most seasonal builds. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Pablo Sour": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla syrup, egg white. The lemon-and-vanilla with silky foam can meet the salmon's fat well; preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen. Works as a lighter cocktail option — though the Pablo's texture peaks higher on starters than on mains.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Paloma": "Reposado tequila, fresh grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The bright grapefruit and reposado's clean agave profile can meet the salmon's fat content well; preparation changes seasonally, so check with kitchen. Works as a lighter cocktail alternative to Pinot Noir or rosé for salmon.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye's allocated-icon Pappy rye character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage 16th Ed with the salmon — HH cask-strength against oily salmon. Avoid; barrel-proof bourbon too intense for fish.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Patron Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver with the salmon — iconic blanco against salmon's richness. The citrus-forward character frames the salmon at the everyday accessible tier. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Patron XO Cafe on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Bouzy Grand Cru Pinot Noir grower Champagne — structured rosé with raspberry, red currant, and mineral finish. The Burgundian weight handles salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as serious rosé Champagne for salmon across most seasonal builds.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "After the salmon, the PB brownie -- the oily-rich fish gives way to peanut-and-chocolate fudge, vanilla ice cream tempering the close. Works; the heavy close after a heavy main, both courses share register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a cognac. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Pierre Ferrand Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "Pierre Gimonnet 2015 Special Club — prestige grower Champagne with citrus, brioche, white flowers, creamy texture. The vintage depth handles salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong sophisticated Champagne across most seasonal salmon builds.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pikesville Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Pineau des Charentes": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Pineau des Charentes on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth with the salmon — single-distillery protected gin against oily salmon. Strong; Plymouth's earthier juniper frames the fish softly.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "2004 Pommery Cuvée Louise — 20-year vintage from Reims. Almond, apricot, honey, brioche. The aged complexity handles salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong occasion Champagne for salmon across most seasonal preparations.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Prime Tartare": "Faroe Island Salmon with the tartare as the lead-in -- the buttery salmon carries into the cool tartare opener. Strong; the starter sets the table for the salmon.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|RAEN Royal St Robert": "Faroe Island Salmon: this rich, fatty seafood's buttery flesh and delicate-yet-substantial texture meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's cherry-bright, low-tannin frame threads cleanly through the fish — classic Burgundy logic. A headliner fish pair. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit character, bright acid. The rosé fruit plays against a seared salmon and the bubbles cut the Faroe's fat. Non-obvious sparkling pivot when Champagne isn't the move. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 with the salmon — pot still Irish against oily salmon. Excellent; one of the strong calls on the list.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 with the salmon — aged single pot still Irish against oily salmon. Excellent; one of the strong calls on the list.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Remy VSOP Cognac's VSOP fine-champagne cognac clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- cognac and oily fish double up the richness with no foil. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a cognac. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Remy VSOP Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Rey Sol Anejo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka with the salmon — lava-filtered Icelandic vodka against Faroe Island salmon. Excellent; the Nordic-adjacent pairing is thematic — glacial vodka meets North Atlantic fish at a register no other vodka on the menu touches.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Rishi's Old Antique for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Faroe Island Salmon after the chickpea soup -- the roasted-pepper warmth opens with smoky-sweet lift, the salmon following with rich oily flesh the soup's char preceded in echoing register. Strong; warm opener primes the warm main.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Le Mesnil Grand Cru blanc de blancs — chalky minerality, lemon curd, green apple, no dosage. The austere precision cuts salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong sommelier Champagne for salmon across most seasonal builds.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Roku Gin": "Roku with the salmon — Japanese gin with yuzu + sakura + sansho against oily salmon. Excellent; Roku's yuzu-and-sakura profile is classic sashimi territory — the thematic-perfect Japanese-gin-salmon pairing, where yuzu lifts fish like the Japanese classics teach.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey premium-sipping rum overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- heavy spirit weight buries the main protein — needs a wine or lighter spirit. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Ron Zacapa Rum for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year's aged Wild Turkey oak clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Russell's Reserve 10 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Sambuca": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|San Simeon Stormwatch": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. San Simeon Stormwatch belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sta. Rita Hills Santa Barbara Pinot — rich, layered, dark cherry, earth, dried herbs, finesse. The layered body plays against salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak legendary-vineyard Pinot for salmon — Santa Barbara depth across seasonal preparations. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The salmon alongside the garlic spinach -- the salmon's rich oily flesh meets the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens cleanly. Strong; the pairing lands at register without strain against the main.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
