  "Faroe Island Salmon|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon's American-oak Cab character fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Simon Family Estate Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Simon Family Estate Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "Spottswoode Lyndenhurst's Napa estate Cab with structured cassis clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Spottswoode Lyndenhurst for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "Napa Sauvignon Blanc — lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, clean lively. The lively acid cuts salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as Napa Sauv Blanc for salmon across most seasonal builds — rounder body than NZ, still bright.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the St Supéry Élu Meritage for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the salmon — BT cask-strength against oily salmon. Avoid; barrel-proof bourbon too intense for fish.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit with the salmon — glacial-clean Russian wheat against oily salmon. Strong; Elit's purity frames the fish with ultra-premium polish.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty acidity. The zesty acid cuts salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for salmon across most seasonal builds — by-the-glass reliability.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 with the salmon — whole-citrus premium Tanq against oily salmon. Strong; the grapefruit-orange lift frames salmon's richness with martini-gin precision.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray with the salmon — 47.3% juniper icon against oily salmon. Strong; Tanq's botanical spine lifts the fish's richness with British precision.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- honeyed wine and oily fish double up richness with no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the salmon's rich oily flesh: the oak-aged tequila body threads the salmon, and the soft-spice note softens the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|The BG": "The BG's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- light cocktail register is wrong for a main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a light cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. The BG lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|The Happy Wife": "Tito's, orange juice, ginger syrup — stirred. The orange-and-ginger profile can meet salmon's fat well, though preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen. Strong approachable cocktail option alongside the salmon for guests wanting easy-drinking.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|The Manhattan": "Maker's Mark private barrel with Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Dark, sweet, heavy cocktail that overwhelms the Faroe's clean rich flavor. Save Manhattan for the beef course or the marrow. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|The Prisoner Red Blend": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. The Prisoner Red Blend lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the salmon — chardonnay-cask bourbon against oily salmon. Avoid; even the white-wine finish doesn't rescue bourbon-and-fish.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Titos Vodka": "Tito's with the salmon — Texas corn against oily salmon. Strong; Tito's slightly-sweet character doesn't fight salmon's richness — clean default pairing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory with the salmon — entry Japanese blend against oily salmon. Excellent; Toki's light-floral character is gentle enough for fish — Japanese whisky's delicate-elegant-balanced category lets Toki sit with salmon where heavier whiskies overwhelm, parallel to Irish whiskey's category advantage.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Tomato Basil": "Faroe Island Salmon after the tomato basil -- the bright tomato-basil acid cleans the palate, the salmon following with rich oily flesh that the soup's brightness preceded with intent. Strong; acid primes the oil-rich main.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Transfusion": "Hangar 1 vodka muddled with black grapes and lime, topped with ginger beer. Grape fruit and ginger-beer effervescence keep the drink light enough for Faroe salmon; the fruit adds dimension without competing. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado with the salmon — cedar-aged reposado against salmon richness. Strong; cedar and salmon work (cedar plank salmon echoes here) but the pairing is a novelty more than a standout.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Truffle Fries": "Faroe Island Salmon alongside the truffle fries -- protein meets umami, the table builds substance. Works; the side carries cleanly with the salmon.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W. with the salmon — traditional heritage triple-distilled Irish against oily salmon. Excellent; Tullamore's drier character frames the fish with heritage-Irish distinction.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Venge Family Reserve": "Venge Family Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Venge Family Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Venge Scout's Honor": "Never pair Venge Scout's Honor with salmon — preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen for today's build. Full-bodied Zin-led Napa red crushes salmon fat and black-pepper weight fights delicate flesh. Lead with Lingua Franca Pinot or Domaine Serene. Scout's Honor is a steak wine.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Venge Silencieux": "Venge Silencieux's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Venge Silencieux for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Vesper": "Vesper's crisp citrus-juniper build holds steady against the salmon's rich oily flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Yellow label Veuve — Reims Champagne with apple, peach, biscuit, creamy mousse. The body cuts salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as iconic Champagne for salmon course across most seasonal builds — crowd-pleasing default.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Vieux Carré": "Vieux Carré's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Vieux Carré for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Villon Cognac": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a cognac. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Villon Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Vin Santo on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Weller 107": "Weller 107's bigger-body wheated buries scallop sweetness overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Weller 107 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Weller 12 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Weller Millennium belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Weller Special Reserve on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley with the salmon — Buffalo Trace wheat vodka against oily salmon. Strong; Wheatley's clean profile frames the fish at value register without fighting richness.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the WhistlePig 12 Year Old World for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the WhistlePig 15 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's craft high-rye oak spice smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Willett Pot Still Reserve lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Woodford Reserve on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Woodford Reserve Double Oaked belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Woodford Reserve Rye for the steak course.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Woodinville Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 with the salmon — Suntory flagship Japanese single malt against oily salmon. Excellent; Yamazaki 12's honey-fruit character and Mizunara-oak finish frame fish with Japanese-single-malt elegance — the category advantage + Yamazaki refinement.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 with the salmon — aged Yamazaki against oily salmon. Strong; Yamazaki 18's aged Mizunara character frames fish at collector register; Yamazaki 12's lighter honey-fruit is still the featured Yamazaki-salmon match, where 18 is over-specified.",
  "Faroe Island Salmon|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Yellow Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Asparagus": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis plays cleanly against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The firm tannin cuts manchego-cream weight but reads heavier than the gratin strictly demands. Works for au gratin — Cabernet fits the dense dairy harder.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Beignets": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi for the steak course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Bone Marrow": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. Graphite echoes beefy-bone mineral depth and tobacco plays with rendered-marrow unctuousness. Strong Super Tuscan for marrow — Maremma structure on the rich starter.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Bone-In Filet": "Strong tier. Bone-in concentration gives the Saffredi's firm tannin and graphite a target. Maremma Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot wraps the marrow-edged cut with blackcurrant and tobacco, while Mediterranean herbs dovetail the flame-grilled char. Italian structure where the Napa Cabs lean ripe.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Broccoli Cheddar": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Broccolini": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis leans against the broccolini's tender green — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Burrata": "Never pair Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi with burrata — Maremma firm-tannin blackcurrant obliterates basil-tomato-fresh-cheese delicacy and milky texture. Lead with Il Colle Prosecco, Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc. Saffredi is a Tomahawk wine.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Butternut Squash": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's deep dark-fruit and grippy tannin sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the structured body wraps the squash bisque, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the call holds at neutral register.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Carrot Cake": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi for the steak course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Cheesecake": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi on the steak course instead.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Chilean Seabass": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Chocolate Brownie": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi for the steak course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Chocolate Cake": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi for the steak course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Clam Chowder": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Cowboy Ribeye": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The graphite cuts marbled-ribeye char and firm tannin handles rendered-fat weight. Peak Super Tuscan for the 26oz bone-in — Maremma Bordeaux-style structure lives for this cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Crab Cake": "Never pair Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi with crab cake — Super Tuscan firm-tannin and blackcurrant weight bulldoze lump-crab delicacy and romesco cream. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or French 75 cocktail. Saffredi belongs on the red-meat course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Creamed Spinach": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The firm tannin cuts béchamel cream weight and the graphite plays with garlic-dairy depth. Strong Super Tuscan for creamed spinach — Bordeaux-style structure on the indulgent side.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Creamy Potato": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis sits beside the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Creme Brulee": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi on the steak course instead.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Escargot": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis holds the line with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Faroe Island Salmon": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi on the steak course instead.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Filet Mignon": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The firm tannin overshadows butter-tender delicate filet and the blackcurrant reads dense against lean flesh. Strong Super Tuscan for filet — Caymus or Gombaude-Guillot fits the cut harder.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|French Onion": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Grilled Caesar": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis sits alongside the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the opulent body overshoots the leaf-and-acid plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Gumbo": "Super Tuscan — Maremma Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The firm tannin cuts gumbo's dark-roux fat and the herbs play against Cajun bay-and-file spice. Peak Super Tuscan for gumbo — Mediterranean herbal structure meets the stew's complexity. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis leans against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|House Wedge": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis sits alongside the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the opulent body overshoots the leaf-and-acid plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Kansas City": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi from the Maremma coast, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The firm tannin handles lean-bold strip beef and the graphite plays with firm-savory grain. Peak Super Tuscan for KC — Bordeaux-style intensity on the well-marbled cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Loaded Potato": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Lobster Mac": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The firm tannin reads heavier than lobster-cheese-cream delicacy demands but Mediterranean herbs play with the indulgent build. Works for lobster mac — Chardonnay fits the dairy cleaner.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Market Fish": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi for the steak course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Mocha Creme": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi for the steak course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Mushroom Bisque": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis sits alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Mushrooms": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The Mediterranean herbs play with garlic-butter mushroom glaze and the graphite echoes earthy umami. Strong Super Tuscan for mushroom side — Maremma herbal lift on the funky richness.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi on the steak course instead.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Porterhouse": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi from the Maremma coast, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The firm tannin handles dual-texture strip-and-tender and the tobacco plays with the mixed profile. Peak Super Tuscan for Porterhouse — Maremma structure bridges both cuts. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The starter's fat and umami meet the bold red's dark fruit — both land on the palate with real weight. Works, though neither flavor is showcased.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Rainbow Trout": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi for the steak course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Roast Half Chicken": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis stays alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis sits beside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi for the steak course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis stays alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Seafood Tower": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi for the steak course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi with seared scallops — Super Tuscan firm tannin and tobacco crush clean-seared shellfish and the graphite fights scallop sweetness. Lead with Raventós Cava, Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé. Route Saffredi to ribeye.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Seasonal Soup": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Seasonal Vegetables": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis leans against the seasonal garden plate — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Shrimp Bisque": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's dense blackberry-and-cassis tannin leans against the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the structured body wraps the shrimp bisque, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; the close holds, neither soars nor fights.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi with shrimp cocktail — Super Tuscan firm-tannin overshadows cold poached shrimp and tobacco-graphite clashes with cocktail-sauce horseradish. Lead with Veuve Clicquot, Raventós Cava, or French 75. Saffredi lives on the red-meat course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Swordfish": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi for the steak course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|The Tomahawk": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The tobacco plays with the 36oz bone-in's smoky-char and firm tannin handles the grand cut's weight. Strong Super Tuscan for Tomahawk — Bordeaux-style Italian on the theatrical presentation.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Tomato Basil": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis plays cleanly against the tomato basil's bright acid — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Truffle Fries": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The graphite plays with truffle-oil earth and the tobacco cuts parmesan weight. Works for truffle fries — Saffredi's firm tannin reads heavy; Scavino or Caymus fits lighter.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi for the steak course.",
  "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Asparagus": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Agustin Huneeus's elegant Napa Cab. Moderate tannin, fruit-forward. Works with the gratin but the bigger Cabs (Caymus, Far Niente) peak higher against the 90-minute-baked richness. Fine for a mid-tier Cab guest.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Beignets": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Bone Marrow": "Faust — Napa Cab with blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the tobacco-chocolate frames the bone depth. Peak value Napa Cab for marrow — layered brooding complexity at a price that makes the starter pairing easy. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Bone-In Filet": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge. The dark-fruit frame supports the filet, but the tannin rides harder than the lean profile demands. A serviceable option; not the cleanest bold-red + filet alignment. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Broccoli Cheddar": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth flattens the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Faust Napa Valley Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Broccolini": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Burrata": "Brooding Napa Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm-tannin tobacco-chocolate crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackcurrant-blueberry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Faust for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Butternut Squash": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Carrot Cake": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Cheesecake": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Chilean Seabass": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Chocolate Brownie": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Faust Napa Valley Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Chocolate Cake": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Faust Napa Valley Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Clam Chowder": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Faust Napa Valley Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Cowboy Ribeye": "Napa Coombsville Cab — blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the tobacco-chocolate meets rendered fat. Peak value Napa Cab for Cowboy — brooding structure handles fat-and-char with layered complexity at a fair price. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Crab Cake": "Brooding Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm-tannin tobacco-chocolate body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Faust for the steak course where its Coombsville structure shines.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Creamed Spinach": "Agustin Huneeus's elegant Napa Cab — moderate tannin, fruit-forward, polished finish. Handles the creamed spinach well, but the bigger Cabs (Caymus, Far Niente) peak higher against the roux's weight and parmesan depth. Acceptable mid-tier Cab pour. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Creamy Potato": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Creme Brulee": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Faust Napa Valley Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Escargot": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds the line with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the concentrated body grips the savory plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Faroe Island Salmon": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Filet Mignon": "Napa Coombsville Cab — blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the tobacco-chocolate echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong value Napa Cab for filet — brooding complexity offers more depth than entry Cabs, though lighter-structure Cabs peak higher for filet's delicacy.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|French Onion": "Napa Coombsville Cab — blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body handles French Onion's cheese-and-broth weight but firm tobacco-chocolate reads bolder than the classic demands. Works as value Napa Cab for French Onion — acceptable red pour; Pinots and Chardonnays peak higher.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Grilled Caesar": "Napa Coombsville Cab — blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the tobacco plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as value Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — brooding complexity on the classic layered salad.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Gumbo": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth overwhelms the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|House Wedge": "Faust — Napa Cab, blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the tobacco plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as value Napa Cab for the wedge — unconventional red choice with brooding depth, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Kansas City": "Faust — Napa Coombsville-and-Oak-Knoll Cab with dark blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin, layered finish. The structured body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the tobacco plays against the beefy char. Peak value Napa Cab for Kansas City — structure plus layered complexity at this price tier. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Loaded Potato": "Napa Coombsville Cab — blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body handles the soup's bacon-cheddar weight but firm tannin reads bolder than the classic demands. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Loaded Potato but below the wine's peaks with the Tomahawk, Porterhouse, and Bone-In Filet.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Lobster Mac": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the opulent body matches the indulgent side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Market Fish": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Mocha Creme": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Faust Napa Valley Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Mushroom Bisque": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Mushrooms": "Agustin Huneeus's elegant Napa Cab — moderate tannin, fruit-forward, polished finish. Works with mushrooms, but the bigger Cabs (Caymus, Far Niente, Opus) peak higher against the umami depth. Acceptable mid-tier Cab pour when price sensitivity matters.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Porterhouse": "Faust — Napa Cab, blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the layered complexity plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong value Napa Cab for Porterhouse — brooding depth on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Prime Tartare": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis reads alongside the tartare's raw beef and brine — the concentrated body grips the savory plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Rainbow Trout": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Faust Napa Valley Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Roast Half Chicken": "Agustin Huneeus's project — elegant Napa Cabernet, moderate tannin. Same function as Jordan but with more Napa character. Works if the guest insists on Cab with the chicken; the Pinots peak higher here.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth overwhelms the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Faust Napa Valley Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Faust Napa Valley Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Seafood Tower": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Faust Napa Valley Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Seared Scallops": "Faust Napa Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — brooding tobacco-chocolate obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Faust for the steak course where its value Napa structure peaks.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Seasonal Soup": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Seasonal Vegetables": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Shrimp Bisque": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Shrimp Cocktail": "Faust Napa Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — brooding tobacco-chocolate is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Faust for the steak course where its brooding Napa character belongs.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Swordfish": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|The Tomahawk": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the velvety body settles into the flame-grilled char, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Tomato Basil": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the bold red's cassis grip carries the mushroom-and-truffle depth — structured weight on the earthy side. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Faust Napa Valley Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Asparagus": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Beignets": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Bone Marrow": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Bone-In Filet": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Broccoli Cheddar": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Broccolini": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Brussels and Belly": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid. The high acid cuts pork-belly fat and the red-cherry lifts against brussels-char. Strong Galician-Mencía for brussels-and-belly — bright-acid contrast on the fat-and-char side.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Burrata": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid. The high acid cuts fresh cream and the red-cherry bridges tomato-basil brightness. Works for burrata — a Rosé or Pinot Grigio handles the dairy more cleanly, but Mencía's lightness keeps it fresh.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Butternut Squash": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Carrot Cake": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Cheesecake": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Chilean Seabass": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Chocolate Brownie": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Chocolate Cake": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Clam Chowder": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Cowboy Ribeye": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid. The light body is too delicate for Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the high acid clashes with heavy fat. Works for ribeye — Tempier Bandol or Corazón Malbec handles the cut harder.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Crab Cake": "Never pair Fento Mencía with crab cake — slate minerality and high acid are stylistically close to white wine but the red-cherry tannin still dulls lump crab delicacy. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for cleaner shellfish pairing.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Creamed Spinach": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Creamy Potato": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the potato soup's silky body — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Creme Brulee": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía on the steak course instead.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Escargot": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid, Burgundian lift. The high acid cuts garlic-butter richness and the slate plays with parsley-herb. Strong Galician-Mencía for escargot — Burgundian sensibility on a French classic.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Faroe Island Salmon": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid, Burgundian lift. The high acid cuts salmon's oil-richness and slate minerality echoes coastal brine. Peak Galician-Mencía for salmon — preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen for today's build. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Filet Mignon": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid, Burgundian lift. The light body matches butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the red-cherry plays with escargot-butter. Strong Galician-Mencía for filet — Burgundian-style sensibility on the delicate cut.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|French Onion": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Grilled Caesar": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid. The high acid matches anchovy-lemon dressing and the slate plays with charred-romaine smoke. Peak Galician-Mencía for grilled caesar — bright-acid Burgundian-style match on the salad. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Gumbo": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Tempranillo body wraps the honey carrots, and the vanilla-leather edge frames the side. Works; the courses share the table without crowding.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|House Wedge": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid, Burgundian lift. The light body won't mute cold iceberg and the high acid cuts blue-cheese funk. Peak Galician-Mencía for wedge — bright-acid Mencía freshens the heavy salad. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Kansas City": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid. The high acid cuts Kansas City's lean-bold strip char, though light body is slightly delicate for the cut. Strong Galician-Mencía for the strip — bright-acid foil to marbled beef.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Loaded Potato": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Lobster Mac": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Market Fish": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Mocha Creme": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Mushroom Bisque": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Mushrooms": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid, Burgundian lift. The slate minerality echoes roasted-mushroom earth and the fresh red-cherry lifts against butter. Peak Galician-Mencía for mushrooms — mineral-meets-earth match. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Porterhouse": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin sits with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Prime Tartare": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the Tempranillo body wraps the tartare, and the vanilla-leather edge frames the starter. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Rainbow Trout": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Roast Half Chicken": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento Ollo de Sapo, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid, Burgundian lift. The light body won't overwhelm poultry and slate minerality plays with chicken-jus depth. Peak Galician-Mencía for the bird — preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen for today's build. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid, Burgundian lift. The high acid matches garlic-oil aromatics and the slate plays with spinach-iron. Strong Galician-Mencía for garlic spinach — mineral-meets-herbal match.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Seafood Tower": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Fento Mencía with seared scallops — fresh red cherry and high acid still fight the delicate sweet shellfish and browned-butter richness. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Seasonal Soup": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Seasonal Vegetables": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Shrimp Bisque": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid. The high acid cuts tomato-cream and the slate plays with shellfish-coastal notes. Works for bisque — a red-on-shellfish needs careful sell, but Mencía's lightness threads the needle.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Shrimp Cocktail": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth crowds out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Swordfish": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|The Tomahawk": "Never pair Fento Mencía with Tomahawk — light body and high acid get bulldozed by the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and smoky char, leaving the wine tasting thin. Lead with Scavino Barolo's firm tannin or Tempier Bandol's cult-producer density instead.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Tomato Basil": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Truffle Fries": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Fernet Branca|Asparagus": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity plays cleanly against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the bitter herb edge sits with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fernet Branca|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the bitter herb edge cuts the rich side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fernet Branca|Beignets": "Fernet Branca with beignets — 40% menthol bitter against fried dough. Works; Branca is too intense to feature with casual sugared dessert but can land as digestif.",
  "Fernet Branca|Bone Marrow": "Fernet Branca with bone marrow — nuclear menthol-saffron bitter against umami fat. Excellent; the 27-herb digestif cuts marrow richness harder than any sipping amaro in the family, and the bartender's-handshake reputation is earned exactly here. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Fernet Branca|Bone-In Filet": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity sits beside the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bitter herb edge cleans the cut, and the bitter cleans the char. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fernet Branca|Broccoli Cheddar": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cream — the bitter herb edge frames the light soup, and the bitter cuts the fat. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Fernet Branca|Broccolini": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity sits alongside the broccolini's tender green — the bitter herb edge sits with the light side. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Fernet Branca|Brussels and Belly": "Fernet Branca with Brussels + Belly — nuclear bitter against maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; Branca cuts the glaze's sweetness harder than sipping amari and makes the side read savory.",
  "Fernet Branca|Burrata": "Fernet Branca with burrata — 27-herb bitter against fresh dairy. Avoid; Branca is too aggressive for burrata's cream.",
  "Fernet Branca|Butternut Squash": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity finds neutral with the squash soup's silky sweet — the bitter herb edge frames the light soup, and the bitter cuts the cream richness. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Fernet Branca|Carrot Cake": "Fernet Branca with carrot cake — 27-herb bitter against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; Branca finds the spice notes but the cream cheese softens less than with Averna or Montenegro.",
  "Fernet Branca|Cheesecake": "Fernet Branca with cheesecake — saffron-menthol against custard richness. Strong; Branca's intensity demotes the sweeter amari pairing slightly but still settles as digestif.",
  "Fernet Branca|Chilean Seabass": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity reads alongside the seabass's buttery flesh — the bitter herb edge cleans the fish. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Fernet Branca|Chocolate Brownie": "Fernet Branca with the chocolate brownie — nuclear menthol-saffron against cocoa. Excellent; cocoa's bitterness tolerates Branca's medicinal depth in a way no other dessert on the menu does.",
