  "Fernet Menta|Seasonal Soup": "Fernet Menta's crisp mint-menthol edge plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the bitter herb edge frames the light soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Fernet Menta|Seasonal Vegetables": "Fernet Menta's crisp mint-menthol edge leans against the seasonal garden plate — the bitter herb edge sits with the light side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Fernet Menta|Shrimp Bisque": "Fernet Menta's crisp mint-menthol edge leans against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the bitter herb edge frames the hearty soup. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Fernet Menta|Shrimp Cocktail": "Fernet Menta's crisp mint-menthol edge plays cleanly against the shrimp's chilled sweetness — the bitter herb edge overpowers the shellfish. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Fernet Menta|Swordfish": "Fernet Menta's aperitivo herb-and-bitter cut leans against the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the herbal cut works the swordfish, and the amaro backbone frames the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Fernet Menta|The Tomahawk": "Fernet Menta's bittersweet rhubarb-and-orange edge leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the herbal cut works the 36oz showpiece, and the amaro backbone frames the cut. Works; the side sits without pulling focus.",
  "Fernet Menta|Tomato Basil": "Fernet Menta's crisp mint-menthol edge sits alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the bitter herb edge frames the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fernet Menta|Truffle Fries": "Fernet Menta with truffle fries — cooling mint against truffle-parmesan. Works; mint is a refreshing counterpoint to the earthy side.",
  "Fernet Menta|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Fernet Menta's crisp mint-menthol edge holds with the tuna's seared rare crust — the bitter herb edge cleans the fish, and the bitter cuts the fat. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Fernet Menta|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Fernet Menta's crisp mint-menthol edge holds with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the bitter herb edge frames the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Filet Mignon|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with the filet — Barton 1792's flagship small-batch. 93.7 proof, spicy-sweet, balanced; high-rye-forward character distinct from Beam or BT. A balanced mid-proof small-batch call for filet. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat with the filet — Barton's wheated release from the 1792 distillery. 94.2-proof wheated bourbon with distinctive sweet-wheat character. Different from the Wellers (Buffalo Trace) or Maker's — 1792's sweet-wheat profile is its own voice. A distinctive wheated pour for filet. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|1881 Napa Valley": "Napa Cab — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, smooth medium tannin, clean finish. The approachable body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the cherry-currant plays against escargot-butter toppers. Peak entry Napa Cab for filet — reliable Rutherford-style character for guests building confidence in the bottle pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|2 James Gin": "2 James with the filet — Detroit local gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified; 2 James is the hometown case.",
  "Filet Mignon|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the filet — rare limited-release against the 10oz cut. Strong; boutique-craft is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character.",
  "Filet Mignon|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with the filet — 16yr Speyside double-matured in ex-bourbon and Oloroso sherry casks, balanced premium approachable-16yr character. Excellent; Aberlour 16's double-matured balance frames tenderloin with both vanilla-bourbon and dried-fruit-sherry notes — the most balanced Sherried Speyside-filet option, where Macallans go pure-sherry and Caribbean Cask goes pure-rum.",
  "Filet Mignon|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino with the filet — filtered aged tequila against the 10oz cut. Strong; the cristalino's aged-but-clear depth gives the filet more than standard blancos can — where Don Julio Blanco works at best, Adictivo Cristalino earns Strong.",
  "Filet Mignon|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado with the filet — extended-maturation reposado against the 10oz cut. The near-añejo depth gives the filet a richer frame than standard reposados; a premium extended-aged call. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay — Sonoma with ripe apple, pear, vanilla oak. The oak body can handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the vanilla echoes escargot-butter toppers. Works as accessible white-with-steak when guest wants Chardonnay over Cab; classic reds peak higher for filet.",
  "Filet Mignon|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Meritage — cassis, plum, cedar, dark chocolate, medium tannin, full body. The medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the cedar plays against escargot-butter. Strong Sonoma Meritage for filet — accessible Meritage on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Sonoma Bordeaux blend — plum, blackberry, mocha, soft tannin, smooth, medium body. The soft tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the plum plays against escargot-butter. Peak crowd-pleaser Bordeaux blend for filet — accessible Sonoma red on the delicate cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- amaro bitterness flattens the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Amaro Nonino for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness plays cleanly against the filet's tender lean cut — the sweet character meets the cut at digestif register. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Filet Mignon|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with the filet — port-finished Louisville bourbon against tenderloin. Excellent; AE's port-derived softer-oak frames tenderloin with wine-finish distinction — the flagship port-finish-filet pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with the filet — 117+ proof cask-strength port-finished against tenderloin. Excellent; AE CS's cask-strength port-finish frames tenderloin at premium wine-finish register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "AE Triple Cask with the filet — triple-cask-finished AE against tenderloin. Excellent; AE Triple Cask's multi-cask layered character frames tenderloin with distinctive triple-finish gravitas.",
  "Filet Mignon|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- amaro bitterness flattens the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Aperol for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Asparagus": "Filet Mignon with the asparagus -- the lean 10oz cut meets the asparagus's tender green-vegetal lift, the spear cutting the filet's butter without competing for weight. Strong; the most refined side on the strong-tier list.",
  "Filet Mignon|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The filet with the au gratin -- cream and protein share the meal, the build reads full. Works; the side sits without pulling focus.",
  "Filet Mignon|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope — Paso Cab with ripe plum, dark cherry, mocha, warm spice, plush. The bold body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the warm-spice-mocha echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong Paso Cab for filet — bigger than filet usually needs, though guest-choice when maximum warmth is the ask.",
  "Filet Mignon|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Averna Amaro belongs on another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Aviation": "Aviation gin, Luxardo, crème de violette, ginger syrup. The cocktail's floral delicacy is wrong for any steak — cherry-and-violet profile has no hook into grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the cocktail; route filet to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan.",
  "Filet Mignon|Aviation Gin": "Aviation with the filet — soft American craft against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Avion 44": "Avion 44 with the filet — Avion's 44-month aged extra añejo. Sister to Avion Silver at the top of the Avion line; the 44 months of aging deliver deep oak and extended-maturation depth. A premium aged Avion for filet orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver with the filet — 80-proof Jalisco-highlands against the 10oz cut. Works; blanco isn't the natural filet call.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character underclubs the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a light spirit. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Bacardi Rum for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the liqueur's sugar buries the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Baileys Irish Cream for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Baker's": "Baker's with the filet — Jim Beam's 107-proof 7-year small-batch premium. Rich vanilla, bold high-rye character, named for Baker Beam. Distinct from Knob Creek 12 (12-year aged) — Baker's is younger but higher-proof, more immediate intensity. Premium Beam-side for filet. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the filet — first-fill American Oak bourbon-cask only, vanilla-forward, handcrafted Speyside. Excellent; Balvenie's vanilla-forward American Oak character frames tenderloin with the richest sweet-oak depth in the batch — where Fiddich is apple-crisp and Glenlivet is honey-soft, Balvenie is vanilla-caramel.",
  "Filet Mignon|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the filet — 14yr Speyside finished in Caribbean rum casks, tropical-sweetness with vanilla-caramel-brown-sugar layers. Excellent; Caribbean Cask's rum-cask-derived sweetness frames tenderloin with distinctive tropical character — different from Macallan's dried-fruit-Oloroso approach and Balvenie 16's pure American Oak vanilla.",
  "Filet Mignon|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets First Fill American Oak, hand-picked single barrel (fewer than 300 bottles per barrel), deeper vanilla than the 12. Balvenie 16's single-barrel depth and American-Oak vanilla are the collector-register Speyside-filet pairing — where Balvenie 12 Am Oak is handcrafted, the 16 is hand-selected. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood with the filet — 21yr Speyside finished in port casks, red-berry-fruit and port-richness, ultra-premium collector-register. Excellent; port-and-beef is a classical marriage — Portwood's port-derived red-berry fruit frames tenderloin with the most premium Sherried Speyside-filet pairing in the cluster.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with the filet — Bardstown Bourbon Co. French-wine-cask finish against tenderloin. Excellent; Bardstown Château Laboude's French-wine character frames tenderloin with contemporary-craft wine-finish distinction.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with the filet — multi-distillery Discovery against the 10oz cut. Strong; the craft-blender complexity is capable but single-distillery aged bourbons (Weller 12, E.H. Taylor, Blanton's) bring more focused character.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with the filet — stout-barrel-finished against the 10oz cut. Strong; KBS's beer-barrel distinctiveness is a conversation-starter but single-distillery aged bourbons (Weller 12, Blanton's) bring more traditional filet character.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare with the filet — Bardstown Foursquare rum-cask against tenderloin. Strong; Bardstown Foursquare's tropical rum-cask character frames tenderloin at Caribbean-finish register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with the filet — Bardstown own-plus-sourced against the 10oz cut. Strong; Fusion complexity is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character for this cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with the filet — retailer-pick Bardstown against the 10oz cut. Strong; the curated single-barrel is capable but single-distillery aged bourbons bring more traditional focused character.",
  "Filet Mignon|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with the filet — the 80-proof gateway high-rye. Light body and rye-pepper brightness give the filet a pairing that lets the cut lead. Distinct from the wheated Wellers and Maker's — more spice, less caramel softness. The light-rye call for filet. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Filet Mignon: the 10oz center-cut meets Basil Hayden's 10 Year's aged 80-proof depth. More refined than standard Basil Hayden's; the aged high-rye pour for filet. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with the filet — malted rye mash gives this pour a bready, distinctive grain character that nothing else on the menu brings. 80-proof light body frames the filet with a grain vocabulary standard Basil Hayden's doesn't have. A distinctive, unexpected filet pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with the filet — BH high-rye with red-wine finish against tenderloin. Excellent; BH Red Wine Cask's rye-forward + red-wine-fruit frames tenderloin with distinctive wine-finish character.",
  "Filet Mignon|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Filet Mignon: the 10oz center-cut meets Basil Hayden's Toast's toasted-barrel caramel and marshmallow notes over the 80-proof base. Distinct from standard Basil Hayden's — more caramel, less bare rye. A light filet pour with signature character. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bee's Knees": "Empress Indigo gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup. The cocktail's delicate three-ingredient build has no structural match for grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the cocktail through the entrée; route filet pairing to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan.",
  "Filet Mignon|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater with the filet — 40% London Dry against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified; Beefeater's balance is a functional match when guests insist.",
  "Filet Mignon|Beignets": "After the filet, the beignets -- the restrained tenderloin gives way to warm choux and powdered sugar, the dipping sauce tempering the heat. Works; the table closes light after a measured main.",
  "Filet Mignon|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere with the filet — aromatic Polish rye against tenderloin. Works; vodka still over-specified, but Belvedere's rye character is the least wrong vodka for a steak cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with the filet — Diageo's Stitzel-Weller revival brand. Blended bourbon with some old-stock Stitzel-Weller heritage whiskey in the blend. Honey, vanilla, caramel; the bottle's five keys reference the Stitzel-Weller distillery heritage. A storytelling pour for the filet. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with the filet — the 103-proof export-market Blanton's. Richer, bolder than Blanton's Original; the extra 10 proof points and deeper concentration give the filet a more assertive single-barrel frame. Premium-plus single-barrel for guests who want more depth than Original brings. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with the filet — the benchmark premium single-barrel from Buffalo Trace. 93-proof, iconic horse-topper bottle. Caramel, vanilla, honey; balanced enough to frame the filet's clean beef without overpowering. The marquee single-barrel pour for filet orders. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's SfB with the filet — cask-strength Blanton's against tenderloin. Works; Blanton's SfB's intensity is over-specified for filet — save for the ribeye/Tomahawk.",
  "Filet Mignon|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with the filet — decennial milestone Pact against the 10oz cut. Strong; the 10th-Pact collector-edition is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused filet character.",
  "Filet Mignon|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with the filet — Pact 8 blend against the 10oz cut. Strong; the annual-release complexity is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character.",
  "Filet Mignon|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the filet — Pact 9 wine-cask blend against the 10oz cut. Strong; annual-release capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat with the filet — Philadelphia citrus-forward gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Patagonia Argentina Malbec — Noemia A Lisa, cooler-climate than Mendoza, blackberry, violet, graphite, full body, soft tannin, silky elegance. The soft tannin matches butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the violet plays with escargot-butter. Strong Patagonian-Malbec for filet — Mendoza's jam-forward lift reads heavier here.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry with the filet — original-recipe gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified; Bombay Dry's juniper is the traditional case for guests who insist.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire with the filet — 10-botanical softer London Dry against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified; Sapphire's softness is a functional case.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bone Marrow": "Filet Mignon after the bone marrow -- the marrow's beefy unctuous richness adds the dimension the 10oz lean cut leaves room for, the opener anchoring what the filet keeps clean. Strong; the marrow primes the palate the filet finishes.",
  "Filet Mignon|Booker's": "Booker's with the filet — 120-130 proof Beam against tenderloin. Works; Booker's intensity is over-specified for filet's finesse — save the barrel-proof for the ribeye/Tomahawk.",
  "Filet Mignon|Botanist Gin": "Botanist with the filet — Islay foraged-botanical gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Filet Mignon: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with the filet — the house's chosen stave profile brings distinct character to the standard Maker's expression. Richer oak, deeper caramel, tailored to what Bowdie's wanted. A house-signature pour for filet; the Maker's expression designed specifically for this menu. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the filet — A. Smith Bowman's Virginia-aged small-batch, Sazerac-owned. 90-proof, smooth, distinctively Virginia-matured (the distillery ages in a climate distinct from Kentucky). A non-Kentucky small-batch voice for the filet. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bowmore 12": "Bowmore 12 with the filet — the gentlest of the Islays, with honey and orchard fruit from the sherry finish, is the one peated scotch that actually lifts the filet cleanly. The other two (Lagavulin 8, Laphroaig 10) overpower the cut's subtlety; Bowmore brings character without crushing it. This is the Islay pour for filet orders. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin, medium body. The high acid and low tannin meet butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the dark cherry plays against escargot-butter. Strong benchmark Barbera for filet — Italian food-wine on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Mount Veeder mountain Cab — dark blackberry, graphite, earth, firm age-worthy tannin. The dense body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the graphite-earth echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong mountain Cab for filet — Mount Veeder structure, though lighter Cabs like Jordan peak higher on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Broccoli Cheddar": "Filet Mignon into the broccoli cheddar -- the meal layers rich on rich, the table carries weight cleanly when the registers match. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "Filet Mignon|Broccolini": "Filet Mignon with the broccolini -- the bright cuts the rich cleanly. Works; the side carries cleanly with the cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich Classic Laddie with the filet — unpeated Islay is a revelation for guests who assume all Islay smokes. The barley sweetness and orchard fruit frame the filet cleanly. Pour when a guest wants something distinctive but doesn't want the peat. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Brussels and Belly": "Filet Mignon with Brussels + Belly -- the 10oz tenderloin's lean butter meets the maple-glazed pork belly over charred sprouts, the glaze lending the richness the lean cut leaves room for. Strong; the side rounds out the filet's clean register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with the filet — the flagship of the distillery, 90 proof, non-wheated mash #1. Caramel, vanilla, and clean oak give the filet a frame distinct from the Wellers and Maker's (no wheat softness). The workhorse premium bourbon — always a safe, well-composed call. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the liqueur's sugar buries the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Buffalo Trace Cream lands on another course — not here.",
  "Filet Mignon|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with the filet — 90-proof high-rye with distinctive bold spice. The rye-forward character gives the filet a different vocabulary than the wheated pours or the BT flagship; distinct from Knob Creek's bigger-body 100-proof — more accessible, still spice-driven. The high-rye filet pour at the approachable register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Burrata": "Filet Mignon after the burrata -- fresh basil-tomato-cream opens the meal, the 10oz lean cut following with restrained beef butter that pairs to the cream's milky note. Strong; refined opener into refined cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Butternut Squash": "Filet Mignon with the butternut squash alongside -- savory gives way to sweet, the close lands cleanly. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Howell Mountain Cab — blackberry, dark plum, iron, volcanic mineral, firm age-worthy tannin. The structured body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the iron-mineral echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong mountain Cab for filet — Cade's LEED-certified Howell Mountain character, though lighter Cabs peak higher on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila with the filet — value Camarena-family reposado against the 10oz cut. The approachable character meets the filet at the entry register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Campari": "Campari's aperitivo at its most textbook overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- amaro bitterness flattens the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Campari for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club with the filet — 1858 Hiram Walker heritage Canadian against tenderloin. Excellent; Canadian Club's grain-forward traditional character frames tenderloin at heritage register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Canadian Club 43 Year": "CC 43 Year with the filet — rare aged Canadian Club (43 years), ultra-premium Canadian, collector-register. Excellent; CC 43 Year's rare aged character is the featured Canadian-filet pairing at collector register — 43 years of aging is unusual for Canadian whisky, and the depth frames tenderloin with distinctive aged-Canadian gravitas.",
  "Filet Mignon|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Captain Morgan Rum on another course instead.",
  "Filet Mignon|Caribou Crossing": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets Sazerac-owned single-barrel Canadian whisky (distinctive — most Canadian is blended), hand-selected character in smaller bottle format. Caribou Crossing's single-barrel distinction is the featured Canadian-filet pairing at craft register — where Crown Royal is iconic-smooth and CC is heritage-grain, Caribou Crossing is single-barrel-distinctive. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Carrot Cake": "The filet into the carrot cake -- the butter-tender 10oz yields to spiced cake and cream-cheese frosting, walnut adding crunch on the close. Works; the spice-and-frosting weight outsizes the steak, but the table builds cleanly across courses.",
  "Filet Mignon|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- dessert wine is post-meal — wrong register for the steak course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Casa LBV Port belongs on another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight sits alongside the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the earthen-pit smoke carries the lean buttery filet, and the green-agave edge brightens the cut. Works; the close composes without strain.",
  "Filet Mignon|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus — Napa Rutherford Cab, concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the cocoa-mocha echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak iconic Napa Cab for filet — the name, the opulence, the long warming finish all line up for the classic cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Chambord lands on another course — not here.",
  "Filet Mignon|Cheesecake": "The filet closes with the cheesecake -- the butter-tender 10oz hands off to dense cream-cheese custard on graham crust, berry compote brightening the close. Works; a heavier close than the steak called for, but it lands cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Chocolate Brownie": "The filet closes with the brownie -- savory gives way to sweet, the close lands cleanly. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Filet Mignon|Chocolate Cake": "After the filet, the chocolate cake -- the savory course resolves into sugar, the meal closes with contrast. Works; the heavy close after a heavy main, both courses share register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin with the filet — silky potato vodka against tenderloin. Works; vodka remains over-specified for steak, but Chopin's softness is a refined option for guests who insist.",
  "Filet Mignon|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with the filet — California American brandy against tenderloin. Strong; Christian Bros handles the filet at value register, though Hennessy's benchmark and Courvoisier's drier oak both outperform here.",
  "Filet Mignon|Château Batailley": "Pauillac 5ème Grand Cru Classé left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin, full body. The firm structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the cedar-graphite plays against escargot-butter. Peak classified Bordeaux for filet — 1855-Classification French on the delicate cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Château Beaucastel": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône — Beaucastel, all 13 permitted varieties, dark fruit, leather, truffle, Provençal herbs, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the truffle plays with escargot-butter. Strong bucket-list Châteauneuf for filet — celebrated estate on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic estate on Pomerol's clay plateau (Petrus terroir), 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The velvety texture wraps lean-tender filet and the truffle plays with butter-tender richness. Peak Pomerol for filet — plush Merlot on the delicate cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Château Haut Segottes": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — Merlot-dominant, plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin, medium body. The soft tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the truffle plays against escargot-butter. Peak right-bank Bordeaux for filet — Grand Cru Merlot on the delicate cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Château de Rouillac": "Pessac-Léognan left-bank Bordeaux — classic blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, tobacco finish, firm tannin, medium body. The firm structure handles butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the cedar-graphite plays against escargot-butter. Peak left-bank Bordeaux for filet — classified French classic for the delicate cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Clam Chowder": "Filet Mignon into the chowder -- cream and protein share the meal, the build reads full. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "Filet Mignon|Clase Azul Anejo": "Filet Mignon: tender lean cut meets Clase Azul Anejo. Excellent; the 25-month American-oak + sherry-butt character with its caramel-butterscotch depth frames filet at ultra-premium register — the ceramic-decanter añejo on the most refined cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango with the filet — Clase Azul's Durango edition featuring hand-painted ceramic bottle with Durango artisanship. Ultra-premium ceremonial extra añejo; distinct from Clase Azul Reposado's blue-ceramic approach — Durango showcases Mexican regional craftsmanship. Ceremonial filet pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold with the filet — Clase Azul's distinctive joven blend: blanco, reposado, and añejo combined with a touch of mezcal, with 24K gold flakes suspended in the bottle. Ceremonial presentation is tableside theater; the golden-ceramic is the draw as much as the spirit. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's smoky agave character reads alongside the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the espadín-and-smoke body wraps the lean buttery filet, and the campfire register frames the cut. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Filet Mignon|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata with the filet — luxury blanco against the 10oz cut. Works; the ceremony is a statement but blanco isn't the natural filet call.",
  "Filet Mignon|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado with the filet — ultra-premium hand-painted ceramic against the 10oz cut. The Clase Azul smoothness gives the filet a ceremonial frame. This isn't everyday — pour when the table wants the experience and the presentation. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra with the filet — 5-year extra añejo, the top-tier Clase Azul with platinum/gold accents on hand-painted ceramic. Above Durango and Gold in the Clase Azul hierarchy; the collector-grade ceremonial pour. For guests who ask for the ultimate Clase Azul; the Ultra justifies its tier with actual 5-year aging. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep with the filet — Jim Beam's wheated release from Clermont. 90-proof wheated, distinct from Beam's non-wheated portfolio. Caramel, vanilla, approachable wheated character. The Beam-side answer to the Wellers. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body, low tannin. The low tannin matches butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the wild raspberry plays with escargot-butter. Strong Southern-French for filet — Mediterranean lightness on the cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with the filet — Napa Cab-cask-aged reposado against the 10oz cut. The red-wine fruit influence gives the filet a wine-and-tequila bridge distinct from anything else on the menu. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's bittersweet rhubarb-and-orange edge leans against the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the herbal cut works the lean buttery filet, and the amaro backbone frames the cut. Works; the sweeter amaro style doubles the dish's sweetness slightly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco with the filet — single-estate blanco against the 10oz cut. Works; blanco isn't the natural filet call.",
  "Filet Mignon|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado with the filet — single-estate Sazerac-owned reposado. The highland-agave character gives the filet a different frame than Don Julio's oak-forward style; a distinctive everyday reposado for the cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's cooked-agave-and-oak register leans against the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the oak-aged tequila body threads the lean buttery filet, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the cut. Strong; the boutique character gives dimension but the starter earns a heavier pour.",
  "Filet Mignon|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg's añejo tequila finished in ex-George-T sits alongside the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the barrel-rested body handles the lean buttery filet, and the oak threads the cut. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Filet Mignon|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's agave-char character finds register with the filet's tender lean cut — the añejo weight grips the lean cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Mendoza Argentina Malbec — Corazón del Sol, dark plum, blackberry jam, violet, velvety tannin, full body. The velvety tannin matches butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the violet aromatics play with escargot-butter. Strong Mendoza for filet — full body is slightly heavy, but plush-on-tender works.",
  "Filet Mignon|Corpse Reviver": "Warner's gin, Lillet, cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. The cocktail's classical elegance is wrong for steak — citrus-and-anise have no hook into grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the cocktail; route filet pairing to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan.",
  "Filet Mignon|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier with the filet — \"Napoleon's cognac,\" drier and oak-forward, traditional Jarnac producer. Excellent; Courvoisier's drier character and pronounced oak frame tenderloin with more structural backbone than Hennessy's rounder approach — the traditional French cognac-steak pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Crab Cake": "Filet Mignon after the crab cake -- the panko-and-lump crab opens with delicate sweetness, the 10oz tenderloin following with restrained beef butter. Strong; both courses lean refined, the cut closes the line cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 with the filet — Diageo Classic Malt, lighter layered Speyside with subtle smoke hint and meaty depth. Excellent; Cragganmore's distinctive layered complexity and subtle smoke frame tenderloin with more dimension than Fiddich's gateway or Glenlivet's benchmark — the thinking-person's Speyside-filet pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Creamed Spinach": "Filet Mignon alongside the creamed spinach -- the 10oz lean cut sits with the bechamel-laced greens, the cream matching the filet's natural butter without crowding it. Strong; reliable, season-after-season order.",
  "Filet Mignon|Creamy Potato": "Filet Mignon into the potato soup -- two rich courses on the same plate, the table earns its substance. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "Filet Mignon|Creme Brulee": "After the filet, the brulee -- the lean restraint yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the steakhouse classic close on the lighter cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral backbone, graceful. The elegant body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the forest-floor echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak benchmark Pinot for filet — Willamette terroir frames the delicate cut. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Filet Mignon|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal with the filet — iconic Canadian blend against tenderloin. Excellent; Crown Royal's smooth-hand-selected character frames tenderloin at crowd-pleasing Canadian register — the democratic Canadian-filet pairing everyone recognizes.",
  "Filet Mignon|Cucumber Gimlet": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda top. The cocktail's garden-fresh profile is wrong for any steak — cucumber and soda have no hook into grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the cocktail; route filet to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan.",
  "Filet Mignon|Dalmore 12": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets Highland luxury single malt with Matusalem sherry-cask finish, orange-marmalade and chocolate notes, slightly oily premium character. Dalmore's sherry-finish luxury profile frames tenderloin with Highland-premium gravitas — where Glenmorangie 10 is honey-floral-bourbon and Oban 14 is maritime-coastal, Dalmore is sherry-finished luxury. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body, low tannin. The light body handles butter-tender filet's lean sweetness though the low tannin underserves the cut vs. classic Napa Cab. Works as Loire red for filet — French Pinot for guest wanting lighter red.",
  "Filet Mignon|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin, medium body. The elegant medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the violet plays against escargot-butter. Peak rare-Napa CF for filet — Persian-influenced estate story worth telling on the delicate cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silverado Trail Cab — blackcurrant, dark plum, mocha, spice, rich plush. The opulent body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the mocha-spice echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong opulent Napa Cab for filet — Darioush Persian-inspired story, though leaner Cabs like Jordan peak higher on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin with the filet — Detroit craft against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City with the filet — Detroit City Distillery against tenderloin. Works; vodka over-specified for filet; Detroit City's hometown character is a functional case.",
  "Filet Mignon|Dewar's 15": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets aged sherry-finish Highland blend. Dewar's 15's sherry-cask finish layers depth beyond the White Label's honey-vanilla entry — the upgraded Dewar's-filet pairing for guests who know the brand and want the aged expression. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label with the filet — Highland blend with Aberfeldy-malt core against tenderloin. Excellent; Dewar's White is the approachable blended-Scotch-filet benchmark — softer than JW Red's smoke, less distinct than Monkey Shoulder's Speyside-malt-fruit.",
  "Filet Mignon|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the liqueur's sugar buries the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Disaronno belongs on another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the filet — craft blended rye against the 10oz cut. The boutique-blender character gives the filet a craft voice distinct from distillery-specific ryes. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's high-proof pour register sits at register with the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the heavy body handles the lean buttery filet, and the dense weight underlines the cut. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Filet Mignon|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Willamette flagship Pinot Blend — Dundee & Eola-Amity, dark cherry, Bing cherry, toasted spice, forest floor, silky, layered finish. The silky layered body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the forest-floor echoes escargot-butter. Peak flagship Willamette Pinot for filet — Domaine Serene elegance. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Provence Bandol AOC Mourvèdre blend — Tempier, dark plum, game, olive, wild herbs, smoky mineral, full body, firm tannin. The firm tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the olive plays with escargot-butter. Strong cult-Bandol for filet — full body is bold but structure threads the needle.",
  "Filet Mignon|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Loire Pouilly-Fumé — serious Sauv Blanc with gun-flint, grapefruit, lime, fuller body. The body can handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the flint plays against escargot-butter toppers unconventionally. Works as serious-white-with-steak; classic reds peak higher for filet.",
  "Filet Mignon|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the garrigue plays with escargot-butter. Strong Châteauneuf for filet — full body is bold but the cut handles it.",
  "Filet Mignon|Don Fulano Anejo": "Filet Mignon: tender lean cut meets Don Fulano. Excellent; the Los Altos estate-terroir with extended French-oak aging frames filet at sophisticated register — more complex than Patron or Milagro.",
  "Filet Mignon|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco with the filet — refined highland blanco against the 10oz cut. Works; blanco isn't the natural filet call even at premium craft register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado with the filet — premium highland reposado against the 10oz cut. The vanilla-honey-oak refinement gives the filet a cleaner frame than everyday reposados; a refined reposado call. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942 with the filet — the benchmark extra añejo. Iconic tall-elongated bottle, aged 2.5+ years. Vanilla, caramel, and deep oak against the filet's clean restraint; 1942 is the reference standard for extra añejo with the cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th with the filet — crystalline filtered reposado against the 10oz cut. The 70th's smoothness gives the filet a cleaner frame than standard Don Julio's oak-forward profile; a distinctive premium pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco with the filet — iconic 80-proof against the 10oz cut. Works; the blanco's brightness is an unusual counterpoint to the beef, but bourbon and reposado are the natural calls.",
  "Filet Mignon|Don Julio Reposado": "Filet Mignon: the 10oz cut meets Don Julio Reposado's 8-month-aged balance. Vanilla and light oak frame the filet without overpowering; the everyday reposado call. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Drambuie": "Drambuie's syrupy-sweet liqueur body finds neutral with the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the after-dinner pour body sits with the lean buttery filet, and the herbal-sugar edge threads the cut. Works; the side sits without pulling focus.",
  "Filet Mignon|Dunville's Single Malt": "Dunville's Single Malt with the filet — Echlinville Distillery Belfast single malt against tenderloin. Excellent; Dunville's is the Irish-single-malt filet pairing — where Redbreast 12 brings pot still richness, Dunville's brings single-malt refinement at Northern Ireland register.",
  "Filet Mignon|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the filet — BT BiB 100-proof against tenderloin. Works; EHT Full Proof is a more balanced option for filet than cask-strength BT siblings — not over-specified but barrel-proof category still prefers the ribeye.",
  "Filet Mignon|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye with the filet — 100-proof BIB rye against the 10oz cut. The bonded aged character gives the filet a premium rye frame; distinct from non-bonded ryes by weight and historical significance. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the filet — Buffalo Trace's bottled-in-bond honoring Edmund Haynes Taylor Jr., the father of modern bourbon regulation. 100-proof BIB, rich caramel and vanilla, oak-forward. Distinct from Blanton's single-barrel approach — E.H. Taylor is the BT premium BIB, heavier and more oak-driven. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Filet Mignon: the 10oz center-cut's clean restraint meets Eagle Rare 10 Year's balanced oak and ten years of Buffalo Trace aging. Distinct from the wheated Wellers and Maker's here — more oak spice, less honey. The non-wheated premium call. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Eastern Kille with the filet — classic London Dry GR craft against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|El Cabo": "El Cabo with the filet — craft boutique reposado against the 10oz cut. The small-batch character gives the filet a craft-spirit frame different from the benchmark Don Julios. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "Filet Mignon: the 10oz cut meets El Mayor Extra Anejo — El Mayor's aged extra añejo, value-adjacent premium of the aged-tequila tier. Sister to El Mayor Reposado; accessible premium-aged tequila. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado with the filet — value reposado against the 10oz cut. The agave-forward profile meets the filet cleanly at the accessible tier; a friendly everyday reposado call. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin with the filet — African-botanical gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak; Elephant's distinctive character is a contemporary case.",
  "Filet Mignon|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 with the filet — Heaven Hill aged single-barrel, 18-year premium with distinctive aged HH character (leather, tobacco, dried fruit). Excellent; EC 18's refined 18yr depth frames tenderloin with aged-HH-single-barrel gravitas — the polished sibling to Elijah Craig Small Batch at collector register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel with the filet — Heaven Hill's house-picked single-barrel. Each barrel varies; the private-selection approach gives the filet a pour specifically chosen for this room. Distinct from Elijah Craig Single Barrel (general release) — private barrel is the curated pick. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Filet Mignon: the 10oz cut meets Elijah Craig Single Barrel — HH's 94-proof single-barrel. More concentrated than Small Batch; single-barrel approach brings caramel-vanilla-oak-spice depth that Small Batch doesn't reach. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with the filet — Heaven Hill's 94-proof small-batch, named for the 'Father of Bourbon.' Spicy-sweet caramel and vanilla with moderate oak; a middle-ground pour between the Wellers' wheat and BT's flagship. Distinct from Heaven Hill BIB (100-proof) — softer, more small-batch character. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the filet — HH 94-proof rye against the 10oz cut. The Heaven Hill rye spice frames the filet distinctly from BT's Sazerac Rye or Beam's Knob Creek Rye; a distinctive HH-side rye for filet. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — five-AVA blend, cherry, violet, clove, structured age-worthy backbone, medium tannin. The structure meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the violet-clove plays against escargot-butter. Peak step-up Willamette Pinot for filet — serious Oregon red on the delicate cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc — Willamette with pear, peach, floral, medium body. The medium body can handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness unconventionally; fruit plays against escargot-butter toppers. Works as light-white-with-steak; classic reds peak higher for filet's deeper beef character.",
  "Filet Mignon|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee with the filet — Buffalo Trace single-barrel, 90 proof, named for the legendary master distiller who championed the single-barrel approach. Rich, smooth, balanced; a namesake pour that frames the filet with BT's mash-forward character — distinct from Blanton's iconic horse-topper branding. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 with the filet — BC floral-citrus gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Epoch Ingenuity": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, full body, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the olive plays with escargot-butter. Strong Paso-Rhône for filet — Epoch savory on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Escargot": "Filet Mignon after the escargot -- the herb-butter snail plate opens with garlic-and-parsley lift, the 10oz tenderloin meeting it with the same butter register, scaled down. Strong; both courses speak the same gentle language.",
  "Filet Mignon|Espresso Martini": "Tito's, espresso, Grind liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Dessert-forward coffee cocktail with butter-tender lean filet — weight mismatch on both sides. Works only when guest stays on the cocktail through the entrée; route filet to Cab or Manhattan for peak pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The cocktail is heavier than the lean filet strictly needs but coffee and hazelnut complement buttery beef and brown sugar softens the profile. Strong when guest wants a rich spirit pour with filet.",
  "Filet Mignon|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — darker, savory, black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone, medium tannin. The smoked-meat character plays against butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the crushed-stone minerality echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak savory Willamette Pinot for filet — age-worthy Oregon serious red. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Far Niente — Oakville Cab, lush luxurious with blackcurrant, dark chocolate, espresso, violet, smooth powerful structure, extraordinarily long finish. The opulent body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the chocolate-espresso echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak luxurious Oakville Cab for filet — pair with the best cuts. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Far Niente — Oakville Chardonnay, buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush velvety texture. The opulent oak weight handles butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the hazelnut echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong Chardonnay-with-steak when guest wants Napa white over Cab.",
  "Filet Mignon|Faroe Island Salmon": "The filet's lean buttery tenderness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh: the buttery beef overwhelms the oily-rich fish, and the lean cut crowds out the salmon. Avoid; the two protein courses pull in different directions. Order one or the other -- pour Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year for the filet, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain for the salmon.",
  "Filet Mignon|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The firm tannin overshadows butter-tender delicate filet and the blackcurrant reads dense against lean flesh. Strong Super Tuscan for filet — Caymus or Gombaude-Guillot fits the cut harder.",
  "Filet Mignon|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Napa Coombsville Cab — blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the tobacco-chocolate echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong value Napa Cab for filet — brooding complexity offers more depth than entry Cabs, though lighter-structure Cabs peak higher for filet's delicacy.",
  "Filet Mignon|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid, Burgundian lift. The light body matches butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the red-cherry plays with escargot-butter. Strong Galician-Mencía for filet — Burgundian-style sensibility on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity holds with the filet's tender lean cut — the bitter herb edge cleans the cut, and the bitter cleans the char. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Filet Mignon|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta's crisp mint-menthol edge finds neutral with the filet's tender lean cut — the bitter herb edge cleans the cut. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Filet Mignon|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Fireball Whisky on another course instead.",
  "Filet Mignon|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky tannins, medium body. The silky tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate texture and the cherry-spice plays against escargot-butter. Peak by-the-glass Pinot for filet — silky California red for the lean cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the filet — higher-end Four Roses using 6 of their 10 recipes, 104 proof non-chill-filtered. More concentrated than Yellow Label's entry-blend; the higher proof and select recipes give the filet a premium Four Roses frame. Distinct from Yellow Label by both proof and recipe selection. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label with the filet — entry-level 80-proof bourbon against the 10oz cut. Capable but underpowered; Weller Special Reserve or Maker's Mark are the stronger everyday wheated calls, Buffalo Trace is the non-wheated alternate. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Filet Mignon|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with the filet — craft small-batch bourbon, a newer boutique brand with small-production character. Distinct from the big-brand small batches (Knob Creek 12, Baker's) by scale alone; a craft-spirit voice for the filet with regional-distillery gravitas. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets Detroit-brand Double Oaked bourbon. Fox & Oden's Double Oaked intensified character frames tenderloin with distinctive Detroit-brand register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Frangelico on another course instead.",
  "Filet Mignon|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Rutherford Cab — Bosché Vineyard since 1970, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, firm tannin, full body, age-worthy. The firm structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the tobacco plays against escargot-butter. Peak Bosché Vineyard Cab for filet — 1970-legacy structure on the delicate cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|French 75": "Inverroche gin, lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top. The cocktail's delicacy is wrong for any steak — bubbles have no job with grilled beef. Works only if guest insists on finishing the cocktail into the entrée; route beef pairing to Cab, Pinot, or whiskey cocktail.",
  "Filet Mignon|French Onion": "Filet Mignon into the French onion -- umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "Filet Mignon|Frias Block 5": "Spring Mountain Cab — Frias Block 5, blackberry, dark plum, iron, savory herbal, firm tannin. The structured body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the iron-herbal echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong single-block mountain Cab for filet — Spring Mountain structure, though lighter Cabs peak higher on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, classic tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, full body, high acid. The firm tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the rose petal plays against escargot-butter. Peak classic Barolo for filet — G.D. Vajra exceptional across range on the delicate cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Sweet, effervescent Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, and lychee. A dessert wine, not a steak wine. Fine as a pour to finish the meal; wrong alongside the filet. Redirect if the guest asks. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Filet Mignon|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco with the filet — craft 80-proof against the 10oz cut. Works; blanco isn't the natural filet call.",
  "Filet Mignon|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof with the filet — 104-proof against the 10oz cut. The concentrated agave is slightly ambitious for the filet's restraint; capable but Don Fulano or Don Julio 70th sit with the cut more cleanly. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue with the filet — Sligo-river craft Irish against tenderloin. Excellent; Garavogue's distinctive regional character frames tenderloin with craft-Irish story-telling.",
  "Filet Mignon|George T. Stagg": "GTS with the filet — 130+ proof BTAC against tenderloin. Works; GTS's intensity is over-specified for filet's finesse — save the collector bottle for the Tomahawk/Cowboy Ribeye where it actually earns its pour.",
  "Filet Mignon|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Oakville Cab — blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak, firm tannin. The structured body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the graphite-oak echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong Oakville Cab for filet — Napa's golden-strip character, though lighter Cabs like Jordan peak higher on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the cracked pepper plays with escargot-butter. Strong California-Zin for filet — jammy full body reads heavy but the cut handles it.",
  "Filet Mignon|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12 with the filet — entry Speyside benchmark, ex-bourbon-cask, apple/pear/honey gateway profile. Excellent; Fiddich 12 is the democratic Speyside-filet pairing — the 40% ABV green-apple character lifts tenderloin where heavier Scotches would compete. The bottle guests reach for by name.",
  "Filet Mignon|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14 with the filet — Bourbon Barrel Reserve, finished in new charred oak for richer vanilla and baking spice depth beyond the 12. Excellent; Fiddich 14's intensified vanilla-spice character frames tenderloin with more oak backbone than the 12's gateway apple/pear — the upgrade pour for the guest who knows Fiddich 12 and wants the next level.",
  "Filet Mignon|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18 with the filet — Oloroso sherry + bourbon cask marriage against tenderloin. Excellent; Fiddich 18's dried-fruit-and-spice depth frames the filet with aged Speyside gravitas — the premium Speyside-filet experience.",
  "Filet Mignon|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23 with the filet — 23 years matured, finished in French Cuvée (champagne) oak casks for distinctive brioche, pastry, and apple-blossom notes. Excellent; Gran Cru's champagne-style finish is unlike any other Speyside in the cluster — its elegant brioche-pastry character frames tenderloin as the thinking-drinker's premium Speyside-filet pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12 with the filet — Sandend coast Highland single malt, recently reopened distillery with coastal-tropical character (tropical fruit + honey). Excellent; Glenglassaugh 12's tropical-coastal profile is distinctive against tenderloin — where Oban is maritime-smoky, Glenglassaugh is coastal-tropical; where Dalmore is sherry-luxury, Glenglassaugh is fresh-fruit-forward.",
  "Filet Mignon|Glenlivet 12": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets \"The Glenlivet house style,\" honey-floral Speyside benchmark, softer entry than Glenfiddich 12. Glenlivet's honey-floral character frames tenderloin with more softness and pineapple-hint than Fiddich's greener apple/pear — the benchmark pick for guests who want Speyside without the crisp green-fruit edge. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10 with the filet — Highland entry benchmark, tallest stills in Scotland, bourbon-cask matured with honeysuckle-peach-vanilla honey-floral profile. Excellent; Glenmorangie 10's delicate-refined bourbon-cask approach frames tenderloin with Highland-floral lightness — the democratic Highland-filet pairing, where Dalmore brings sherry-finish and Oban brings coastal.",
  "Filet Mignon|Glenmorangie 18": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets 18yr Highland, bourbon-cask-matured with 3-year Oloroso sherry finish, layered dried-fruit depth beyond the 10. Glenmorangie 18's aged + sherry-finished character frames tenderloin with Highland-premium depth — where Glenmorangie 10 is honey-floral-bourbon entry, the 18 adds Oloroso dried-fruit complexity that engages richer cuts. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet wine smothers the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Graham's 10 Year Tawny on another course instead.",
  "Filet Mignon|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet wine smothers the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Graham's 20 Year Tawny for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Graham's 2017 Vintage Port's structured vintage port with dark-fruit weight overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet wine smothers the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Graham's 2017 Vintage Port for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum with the filet — ultra-premium Patron against the 10oz cut. Works; blanco isn't the natural filet call even at premium tier.",
  "Filet Mignon|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the liqueur's sugar buries the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Grand Marnier belongs on another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale with the filet — California ocean-botanical gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's cask-strength herbal-high-proof sweetness sits alongside the filet's tender lean cut — the sweet character meets the cut at digestif register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Filet Mignon|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose with the filet — luxury French wheat against tenderloin. Works; vodka remains over-specified for steak, but Goose's polish is the softest case.",
  "Filet Mignon|Grilled Caesar": "The filet with the Caesar alongside -- the 10oz tenderloin matches the charred-and-anchovy salad. Strong; the opener carries its register against the cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Gumbo": "Filet Mignon with the gumbo alongside -- heat lifts the rich course, the table earns the warmer note. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Sonoma Cab — blackberry, cedar, dried herb, firm tannin, restrained classic style. The savory body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the cedar-herb plays against escargot-butter toppers. Strong savory Sonoma Cab for filet — historic restrained character offers a different frame from Napa Cabs.",
  "Filet Mignon|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 with the filet — 100-proof craft vodka against tenderloin. Works; vodka over-specified; HDW 100's extra proof is the most credible craft-vodka-steak case.",
  "Filet Mignon|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 with the filet — Suntory single malt from the \"Forest Distillery\" in the Japanese Alps, lightly peated with crisp-green and pine-forest notes. Excellent; Hakushu's Forest-Distillery character frames tenderloin with Japanese-single-malt distinction — the subtle peat + forest notes are unlike anything Scotland makes.",
  "Filet Mignon|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel with the filet — Buffalo Trace single-barrel using the same mash bill as Blanton's. 88.9 proof, rich-complex; a BT single-barrel that's quieter in the market than Blanton's but carries the same distillery DNA. A distinct BT single-barrel filet pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 with the filet — California craft blended vodka against tenderloin. Works; vodka over-specified; Hangar 1's floral character is a refined case.",
  "Filet Mignon|Head Fake": "Wheatley vodka, limoncello, triple sec, lemon, simple syrup. The cocktail's sweet-citrus profile is wrong for any steak — limoncello has no hook into grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the cocktail; route filet pairing to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan for peak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the filet — HH 20yr collector limited release, deeper aged HH character beyond EC 18. Excellent; HH 20's collector-register depth is the featured aged-HH-filet pairing — where EC 18 brings aged-HH polish, the 20 closes with HH's deepest aged expression.",
  "Filet Mignon|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the filet — 100 proof, traditional BIB. Heaven Hill's workhorse bond — caramel, vanilla, and sturdy oak at the classic BIB strength. Distinct from the Wellers' wheated softness and BT's flagship cleanliness; the traditional-BIB approach to the filet. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with the filet — Bob Dylan's bourbon brand. 100-proof 10-year Tennessee-sourced, decorative bottle featuring Dylan's ironwork art. A story-driven pour — the 10-year aging frames the filet with aged depth while the bottle theatrics carry tableside conversation. A celebrity-brand premium bourbon with substance. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "The filet's mild myoglobin flavor and butter-basted tenderness give Martha's Vineyard's bold tannin structure room to dominate the pairing — the wine essentially seasons the steak the way a sauce would. What you get is the full aromatic expression of the Cab, mint and cedar and dark fruit, framed by the clean, neutral canvas of the filet without any competing fat or char. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Filet Mignon|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "Heitz Trailside — Rutherford Cab from one of Napa's great historic houses. More approachable style than legendary Martha's: dark cherry, blackcurrant, mint, cedar, firm but refined tannin. The body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the mint-cedar echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak historic-house Cab for filet. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Flora Adora with the filet — Spring-release floral Hendrick's against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks with the filet — flagship cucumber-rose gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Grand Cabaret with the filet — fruit-forward Hendrick's against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Hendricks Neptunia": "Neptunia with the filet — coastal-release Hendrick's against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Hendricks Orbium": "Orbium with the filet — bitter-tonic Hendrick's against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy with the filet — ubiquitous VS/VSOP cognac, dried-fruit-and-vanilla benchmark. Excellent; Hennessy is the default call for a reason — its eaux-de-vie blend delivers enough oak and dried-fruit weight to frame tenderloin without dominating it, the most democratic cognac-and-steak pairing on the menu.",
  "Filet Mignon|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB with the filet — Heaven Hill's 10-year bottled-in-bond single-barrel. 100-proof, aged depth, historic brand. A premium aged-BIB alternate to Elijah Craig Single Barrel; the 10-year aging gives the filet a richer Heaven Hill frame. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets premium Suntory blend, 5+ cask types, refined-elegant iconic Japanese-blend premium. Hibiki's refined-elegant character frames tenderloin with premium Japanese-blend gravitas — where Toki is crowd-pleasing entry, Hibiki is refined-premium, and the harmony of 5+ cask types is the featured Japanese-blend-filet match. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Filet Mignon with the honey carrots -- savory gives way to sweet, the close lands cleanly. Works; the side carries cleanly with the cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|House Wedge": "Filet Mignon with the wedge alongside -- protein meets umami, the table builds substance. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 with the filet — historic Kentucky brand, Diageo-owned, BT-distilled 15yr limited release, distinctive cherry-oak aged character. Excellent; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak 15yr character frames tenderloin with distinctive aged-KY-heritage gravitas — a BT-distilled bourbon wearing a historic Kentucky name.",
  "Filet Mignon|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Glera from Veneto — light dry Prosecco with white peach and green apple. Bubbles work with lean buttery filet, but the light body has limited weight to meet grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on Prosecco; Cab or Pinot peak higher for filet's butter-tender profile.",
  "Filet Mignon|Inhibited": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the cocktail's weight crowds the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Inhibited belongs on another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche with the filet — South African fynbos-botanical gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish with the filet — farm-to-bottle northern-Michigan gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon Mezcal Casks with the filet — northern MI farm-to-bottle mezcal-cask bourbon against tenderloin. Strong; Ironfish Mezcal's smoky character frames tenderloin at Michigan-hometown register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish with the filet — Virginia port-finished bourbon against tenderloin. Excellent; Isaac Bowman's port-finish character frames tenderloin with Virginia-heritage distinction.",
  "Filet Mignon|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "Anderson Valley Pinot — concentrated-cool-climate style, dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, firm-but-refined tannin, long finish. The refined structure meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the mocha plays against escargot-butter. Peak Anderson Valley Pinot for filet — concentrated Mendocino red on the delicate cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "J. Davies — Diamond Mountain Cab from the Schramsberg estate with volcanic-soil intensity, dark fruit, graphite, mineral backbone, firm tannin, real aging potential. The structured body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the graphite plays against escargot-butter toppers. Peak mountain Cab for filet — Schramsberg provenance hook. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year with the filet — aged Tennessee against tenderloin. Excellent; JD 12's aged depth frames tenderloin at premium Tennessee register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded with the filet — 100-proof Bottled-in-Bond Tennessee against tenderloin. Excellent; JD Bonded's extra backbone frames tenderloin with Bonded-Act gravitas — where Old No. 7 is iconic-entry, Bonded is premium-robust.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7 with the filet — iconic Tennessee whiskey against tenderloin. Excellent; Old No. 7's Lincoln County Process + charcoal-mellowing frames tenderloin with crowd-pleasing Tennessee character — the democratic Tennessee-filet pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "JD Sinatra Select with the filet — Frank Sinatra tribute Tennessee, \"Sinatra Barrels\" with special grooves that expose whiskey to extra oak surface, richer-oakier unique expression. Excellent; Sinatra Select's grooved-barrel character frames tenderloin with Sinatra-tribute gravitas — a premium JD that tells a story at the table.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson with the filet — iconic smooth Irish against tenderloin. Excellent; Jameson's crowd-pleasing character frames tenderloin at entry Irish register — the democratic Irish-filet pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, balanced acidity, smooth finish, medium body. The smooth structure meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the subtle earth plays against escargot-butter toppers. Peak food-friendly Pinot for filet — reliable Russian River on the delicate cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Grossot Chablis — unoaked Burgundy Chard, flint, chalk, lemon, green apple. The lean acidity can handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness unconventionally; mineral echoes escargot-butter toppers. Works as classical white-with-steak; Cab, Pinot, or vintage Champagne peak higher for filet.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the filet — master-blender tribute sea-aged against tenderloin. Strong; Marian McLain L.E. frames tenderloin at collector register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the filet — ship-aged Jefferson's Voyage #23 against tenderloin. Strong; Voyage 23's sea-aged character frames tenderloin at ocean-finish register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve with the filet — the blended elegance of Jefferson's signature profile. 90-proof honey, vanilla, and smooth oak frame the filet without aggressive character. Distinct from the Wellers' wheated softness or Buffalo Trace's straight oak — blending gives it a polished middle-ground. A refined everyday-premium call for filet. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the filet — the Very Rare line's double-barrel finish through two custom-toasted oak barrels. Deeper vanilla, more complex oak, more sophistication than standard Jefferson's Reserve. A premium non-wheated call for filet; distinct from Weller 12 (wheated) or Eagle Rare 10 (aged Buffalo Trace). Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label with the filet — 86-proof aged Beam against the 10oz cut. Strong; more body than White Label brings, but still underpowered vs. Weller Special Reserve, Maker's Mark, or Buffalo Trace for the filet.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label with the filet — 80-proof entry bourbon against the 10oz cut. Capable but the cut earns better; Weller Special Reserve, Maker's Mark, or Buffalo Trace are stronger everyday calls. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Filet Mignon|Johnnie Walker Black": "JW Black with the filet — 12yr balanced JW against tenderloin. Excellent; JW Black is the iconic blended-Scotch-filet pairing — the balanced smoke + malt + sherry character is the benchmark everyone recognizes, for good reason.",
  "Filet Mignon|Johnnie Walker Blue": "JW Blue with the filet — ultra-premium luxury JW, hand-selected rare casks including 60+yr old stocks, ~$200/pour silky-complex collector icon. Excellent; JW Blue earns its pour on tenderloin — the ultra-premium silky-smooth character is one of the three pairings on the menu where the bottle actually gets what it deserves. Reverence, not just dinner.",
  "Filet Mignon|Johnnie Walker Red": "JW Red with the filet — bold-fiery 80-proof blended JW against tenderloin. Excellent; JW Red's smoke-forward profile frames tenderloin with iconic-blended character — polarizing but definitive.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Jordan — Alexander Valley Sonoma Cab with cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine-grained tannin, impeccable balance. The elegant body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the cedar-mint echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak elegant Cab for filet — Jordan has been producing at this level for decades, balanced match on the classic cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre — Loire benchmark Sauv Blanc, grapefruit, chalk, herbs. The refined body can handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness unconventionally and the herbs play against escargot-butter toppers. Works as Sauv-Blanc-with-steak; Cab, Pinot, or vintage Champagne peak higher for filet.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin, age-worthy. The structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the violets-espresso play against escargot-butter. Peak cult Napa for filet — Colgin prestige on the delicate cut with proper reverence. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's deep-bodied spirit register leans against the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the heavy body handles the lean buttery filet, and the rich character softens the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Filet Mignon|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's high-proof pour register sits alongside the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the heavy body handles the lean buttery filet, and the deep backbone frames the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Filet Mignon|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Kahlua on another course instead.",
  "Filet Mignon|Keenan Chardonnay": "Filet Mignon: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this rich, oak-aged Chardonnay's butter, stone fruit, and full-bodied weight. Rich Chardonnay weight approaches steak territory, but the absence of tannin and the oak sweetness clash with the char. Strong — the interaction lands despite the axis challenge.",
  "Filet Mignon|Keenan Mernet": "Spring Mountain Napa Merlot-Cab — 'Mernet' portmanteau (Merlot + Cabernet), mountain-grown structure, plush dark cherry, plum, medium tannin, full body. The plush body meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the Merlot roundness plays against escargot-butter. Peak mountain Merlot-Cab for filet — worth explaining Mernet to curious guests. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye with the filet — allocated premium KO against the 10oz cut. The 10-year depth earns the filet at excellent; the allocated KO for guests who want distinctive-brand premium KY rye. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the filet — KO Confiscated against the 10oz cut. Strong; the narrative-bourbon is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character.",
  "Filet Mignon|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets Kentucky Owl's luxury Irish crossover (~$150+ limited edition), a blend of Irish whiskies aged in bourbon casks (American-style twist on Irish). Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's is one of the few pairings where this collector bottle earns its pour — the American-bourbon-cask-finished Irish character brings distinctive depth to tenderloin no traditional Irish replicates. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The medium body matches butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the wild herbs play against escargot-butter. Strong Southern-French for filet — a lighter-touch Rhône pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One with the filet — pot-and-column Dutch wheat against tenderloin. Works; vodka over-specified for filet; Ketel's precision doesn't clash.",
  "Filet Mignon|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker with the filet — hop-influenced Holland MI gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek with the filet — 100 proof, high-rye, nutty, full-bodied. The extra proof and nutty-oak character give the filet a pour that stands beside it with weight the Wellers' 90-proof softness can't. Distinct from wheated — more spice, more proof, more nutty oak. The Beam-side premium for filet. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year with the filet — aged 12 years at 100 proof. Beam's premium Knob Creek expression; the extra aging gives the nutty-oak character more depth than standard Knob Creek brings. A premium aged Beam-side call for filet orders. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 with the filet — Beam Suntory's aged Knob Creek at 100 proof single-barrel, deeper aged KC character. Excellent; KC 15's aged-single-barrel depth frames tenderloin with Beam-family aged polish — where entry KC is 9-year-benchmark, the 15 brings collector-register aging.",
  "Filet Mignon|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 with the filet — Beam's 18yr KC limited release, deeper aged collector-register. Excellent; KC 18 is the featured aged-Beam-KC-filet pairing — where KC 15 opens aged-Beam at collector register, the 18 closes with additional aging depth.",
  "Filet Mignon|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Filet Mignon: the 10oz center-cut meets Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye — a blend of bourbon and rye at 100 proof. Unique within the Beam lineup; spicy-sweet complexity neither pure bourbon nor pure rye delivers. Layered conversation-starter pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye with the filet — 100-proof Beam rye against the 10oz cut. The bold character gives the filet more rye grip than benchmark ryes bring; a premium-impact Beam rye for filet orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Filet Mignon: tender lean cut meets Komos Anejo Reserva. Excellent; the French-oak + wine-cask finishes bring distinctive complexity to filet at luxury-reserve register — Komos is the craftsman-curated alternative to Clase Azul.",
  "Filet Mignon|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Filet Mignon: tender lean cut meets Komos Crystallino. Excellent; the filtered-clear añejo (color-removed-flavor-retained) frames filet at bridge-luxury register — distinctive category move on a refined plate.",
  "Filet Mignon|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Añejo with the filet — Komos's aged expression in Riedel-designed crystalline bottle. Luxury-brand packaging; the extra añejo maturation adds depth beyond Komos Rosa Reposado's lighter profile. The bottle presentation is part of the pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado with the filet — wine-cask-finished reposado against the 10oz cut. The rosé character and strawberry notes give the filet a different frame than standard reposados bring; a luxury distinctive pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Lagavulin 8": "Filet Mignon: the restraint and clean beef purity of the 10oz center-cut meets Lagavulin 8's vibrant peat. Capable but not ideal — the 8-year still has too much smoke for the filet's subtlety. Pour Bowmore 12 if a guest wants a peated Islay that actually lifts this cut. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Filet Mignon|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Napa Cab — Robin Lail (Mondavi/Inglenook family) entry-level bottling, polished blackcurrant, cedar, dark cherry, refined medium tannin, balanced. The refined structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the cedar plays against escargot-butter. Peak historically-pedigreed Napa Cab for filet — Lail family story on the delicate cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin, full body. The firm tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the dried violet plays against escargot-butter. Peak historically-significant Napa Cab for filet — Lail family pedigree, Mondavi-Inglenook architect's legacy. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver with the filet — family-heritage craft blanco against the 10oz cut. Works; blanco isn't the natural filet call.",
  "Filet Mignon|Laphroaig 10": "Filet Mignon: the restraint of the 10oz center-cut meets Laphroaig 10's medicinal intensity. The peat and iodine overpower the filet's subtlety. Capable only; reach for Bowmore 12 instead when a guest asks for peat with a filet. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Filet Mignon|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch with the filet — Heaven Hill's wheated answer to the Weller family. 92 proof and caramel-honey-vanilla; a touch more body than Weller Special Reserve, a different distillery voice than Maker's. The approachable wheated alternate for filet, from the Heaven Hill side of the menu. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Pinot Noir Champagne rosé — silky structured mousse, red berry complexity, long elegant finish. The body and berry frame handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness unconventionally, and bubbles lift the sear. Strong alternative when guest wants Champagne with their steak instead of classic Cab.",
  "Filet Mignon|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, brioche, vibrant acidity, clean long finish. The body and structure handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness unconventionally; bubbles lift the sear. Strong Champagne-with-steak alternative when guest wants precision over classic Cab weight.",
  "Filet Mignon|Le Garenne Rosé": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — strawberry, watermelon, floral, crisp dry. The light body has limited weight to meet grilled beef; fresh-fruit plays against escargot-butter toppers. Works as light-rosé-with-steak when guest wants summer pink; Cab, Pinot, or grower rosés peak higher for filet.",
  "Filet Mignon|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Tuscany Brunello di Montalcino DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Le Ragnaie, five years minimum aging, dried cherry, tobacco, leather, rose petal, full body, firm tannin, high acid, age-worthy. The firm tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the tobacco plays against escargot-butter. Peak Italy's-most-prestigious for filet — Brunello on the delicate cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Filet Mignon: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The dark-fruit frame supports the filet, but the tannin rides harder than the lean profile demands. Pour when the guest wants the bold-red experience. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The cocktail's floral-bright profile is wrong for steak — lemon and lavender have no hook into grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the cocktail; route filet pairing to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan.",
  "Filet Mignon|Les Pallières Raciné": "Gigondas Rhône Valley Grenache — Les Pallières Raciné, Brunier-family old-vine, wild red fruit, garrigue, iron, earthy mineral, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the garrigue plays with escargot-butter. Strong Gigondas for filet — full body is bold but the old-vine structure threads.",
  "Filet Mignon|Liberator Gin": "Liberator with the filet — Valentine Distilling Michigan craft against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the liqueur's sugar buries the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Licor 43 on another course instead.",
  "Filet Mignon|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Limoncello for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, whisper of spice, refined structure, medium body, low tannin. The elegant body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the forest-floor note plays against escargot-butter toppers. Peak by-the-glass Oregon Pinot for filet — accessible serious Pinot for the delicate cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Little Book": "Little Book with the filet — Beam Fred Noe annual cask-strength against tenderloin. Works; Little Book's intensity is over-specified for filet — save the chapter-series bottle for the ribeye/Tomahawk.",
  "Filet Mignon|Loaded Potato": "Filet Mignon with the loaded potato alongside -- cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Lobster Mac": "The filet alongside the lobster mac -- the filet's lean buttery tenderness frames the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence. Excellent; the pairing earns its featured slot.",
  "Filet Mignon|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year with the filet — 20yr Highland single malt, distinctive for varied still types (traditional pot + straight-neck) giving complex aged character. Excellent; Loch Lomond 20's complex-aged profile frames tenderloin with distinctive depth — 20 years of aging gives it gravitas the 12-18yr siblings don't quite reach.",
  "Filet Mignon|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the filet — premium sourced rye against the 10oz cut. Strong; the boutique blend is capable but WP 15 Year, Michter's 10 Year, or the allocated ryes bring more single-distillery distinctiveness.",
  "Filet Mignon|Long Road Gin": "Long Road with the filet — ultra-local Grand Rapids gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight reads alongside the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the earthen-pit smoke carries the lean buttery filet, and the earthy backbone threads the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Filet Mignon|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII with the filet — Rémy Martin luxury icon, 1,200+ eaux-de-vie aged 40-100 years, Grande Champagne only, Baccarat crystal decanter (~$4,000/bottle). Excellent; this is one of exactly three pairings on the menu where Louis XIII actually earns its pour — tenderloin's quiet finesse is the only steak that doesn't compete with the bottle's 40-100 year complexity. Reverence, not just dinner.",
  "Filet Mignon|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Lucano Amaro belongs on another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Abruzzo Montepulciano d'Abruzzo — Lunaria earthy grippy, dark cherry, dried herbs, rustic medium body, medium tannin. The rustic structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the dried herbs play against escargot-butter. Peak Italian-approachable red for filet — great value on the delicate cut, Italian alternative to Cab. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets 100% Oloroso sherry-cask-matured 12yr Speyside, the entry sherried-Speyside benchmark. Macallan 12's dried-fruit-and-spice character frames tenderloin with sherry-cask depth — where Glenfiddich 12's bourbon-cask leans apple/pear and Balvenie 12 leans vanilla, Macallan 12 leans raisin-and-spice. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18 with the filet — premium aged 18yr 100% Oloroso sherry-cask Speyside, collector-register. Excellent; Macallan 18's deeper aging and layered dried-fruit-spice depth frame tenderloin with the most complete sherried-Speyside-filet experience in the cluster — where the 12 is benchmark, the 18 is featured.",
  "Filet Mignon|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate with the filet — distinctive premium Macallan expression featuring Easter Elchies Estate-grown oak, distillery-estate-story-driven. Excellent; Macallan Estate's terroir-specific estate-oak character frames tenderloin with a story the other Macallans can't tell — estate-grown oak is the distinction, where Macallan 12 is Oloroso-benchmark and Macallan 18 is aged-Oloroso.",
  "Filet Mignon|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the black pepper plays with escargot-butter. Strong Petite-Sirah for filet — the firm tannin runs hot but the cut handles it.",
  "Filet Mignon|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot — bright strawberry, red cherry, light floral, soft tannins, light body. The soft structure meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the red cherry plays against escargot-butter. Peak accessible Pinot for filet — Macauley light body on the delicate cut at mid-tier price. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Atlas Peak Cab — Stagecoach, dark blackberry, blueberry, espresso, iron, powerful tannin. The high-elevation body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the iron-espresso echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong celebrated-vineyard Cab for filet — Stagecoach structure, though lighter Cabs peak higher on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Mahon Gin": "Mahón with the filet — Balearic-island juniper-heavy gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark with the filet — the iconic red-wax 90-proof wheated. Soft caramel and vanilla frame the filet's clean beef with the approachable warmth that made Maker's Maker's. Distinct from the Weller profile — rounder, less spicy, more 'home' wheated. The gateway wheated pour alongside Weller Special Reserve. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with the filet — extended maturation gives standard Maker's a richer oak backbone and deeper caramel. The filet's restraint meets a more complex wheated pour than regular Maker's. The alternate premium wheated for filet when Weller 12 isn't the call. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the spirit's register doesn't carry even the lean cut's structure. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Malibu Rum for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye with the filet — 16-year aged Michigan single-barrel against the 10oz cut. Extended maturation earns the filet at excellent; the Michigan-heritage rye for filet orders — with home-state pride for local guests. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Loire Cab Franc — Chinon light body, raspberry, red cherry, bell pepper, forest floor, supple low tannin, bright high acid. The bright acid and supple tannin meet butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the bell pepper plays against escargot-butter. Strong Loire CF for filet — unusual lighter pairing on the delicate cut, talking point for guests.",
  "Filet Mignon|Margarita": "Corazon Blanco, lemon, lime, orange, Cointreau — salted rim. The cocktail's triple-citrus profile is wrong for steak — bright citrus has no hook into grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the Margarita; route filet pairing to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan for peak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Marimar Estate Christina": "Russian River Green Valley Pinot — plush, ripe strawberry, cherry compote, warm spice, silky, rich, smooth. The opulent body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the cherry-compote plays against escargot-butter. Peak plush California Pinot for filet — opulent Russian River style for the delicate cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe's gentian-driven cut reads alongside the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the herbal cut works the lean buttery filet, and the amaro backbone frames the cut. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Filet Mignon|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets Michter's flagship single-barrel 10yr bourbon, allocated/sought-after prestigious independent. Michter's 10 Bourbon's single-barrel 10yr depth frames tenderloin with allocated-prestigious-independent gravitas — the independent-bourbon answer to the aged super-premium category. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye with the filet — aged premium Michter's against the 10oz cut. The 10-year polish earns the filet where Michter's US*1 Rye (non-aged) only works strong; the premium Michter's for filet orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the filet — Michter's small-batch smooth rye against the 10oz cut. Distinct from Sazerac Rye by texture — smoother, more polished; a refined rye for filet orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco with the filet — craft premium blanco against the 10oz cut. Works; blanco isn't the natural filet call.",
  "Filet Mignon|Milagro Anejo": "Filet Mignon: tender lean cut meets Milagro Anejo. Excellent; the triple-distilled French-oak profile frames filet at refined-accessible register — cleaner finish than Patron's American-oak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado with the filet — triple-distilled modern reposado against the 10oz cut. The clean agave character frames the filet at the accessible modern register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam with the filet — Sazerac's Mister Sam Bronfman tribute, blended American whiskey including Seagram's-era stocks, limited-release premium blend. Excellent; Mister Sam's blended-Seagram's-era character frames tenderloin with collector-register Seagram's nostalgia — where Beam family brings Beam aged, Mister Sam brings Seagram's heritage preserved in blend form.",
  "Filet Mignon|Mocha Creme": "The filet into the mocha creme -- the butter-tender 10oz hands off to silky coffee-chocolate cream, espresso depth closing the meal cleanly. Strong; the lighter steak meets the layered dessert at register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 with the filet — 47-botanical German gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder with the filet — triple-Speyside-malt blend (Glenfiddich + Balvenie + Kininvie), 40% ABV, cocktail-friendly rich-fruit-and-vanilla. Excellent; Monkey Shoulder's Speyside-malt derived profile frames tenderloin with fruit-and-vanilla character that no other blended Scotch brings — it's literally a blend of the single malts we've already individualized.",
  "Filet Mignon|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Montenegro Amaro on another course instead.",
  "Filet Mignon|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the spirit's register doesn't carry even the lean cut's structure. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a light spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Mount Gay Rum lands on another course — not here.",
  "Filet Mignon|Muga Reserva": "Rioja Alta Reserva Tempranillo-blend — Muga, American-French oak, strawberry, dried cherry, vanilla, leather, medium body. The medium body matches butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the vanilla-oak plays against escargot-butter. Strong Rioja for filet — approachable traditional-house pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Mushroom Bisque": "Filet Mignon preceded by the mushroom bisque -- the buttery beef meets cleanly the truffle-edged bisque. Excellent; the soup-or-salad frames the steak course cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Mushrooms": "* The filet with the mushrooms -- the filet's lean buttery tenderness frames the mushrooms' earthy umami weight. Gold standard; the side that defines the steak course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's bold molasses-Jamaican character matches the filet's tender lean cut — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Filet Mignon|Negroni": "Gin, Contratto, Campari — equal parts, stirred. The cocktail's bitter-orange and herbal complexity can meet butter-tender filet, though heavier steaks suit the Negroni better. Strong alternative to Cab or Manhattan when guest wants bitter structure with filet.",
  "Filet Mignon|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye with the filet — UK-aged Kentucky rye against the 10oz cut. The Yorkshire cask influence gives the filet a singular character; a conversation pour with real substance behind the story. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch with the filet — Kentucky bourbon aged in Yorkshire, UK. Distinctive cask influence from maritime climate; a novelty release with a genuinely unique maturation story. A character-driven conversation pour for the filet; the story is the draw. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with the filet — Michigan beer-barrel bourbon against tenderloin. Strong; NH Beer Barrel's hop-influenced character frames tenderloin at Michigan-hometown register.",
  "Filet Mignon|New York Sour": "Filet Mignon: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. A headliner steakhouse cocktail. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Oakville Cab — deep cassis, blackberry, graphite, subtle oak, concentrated structure. The body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the graphite-cassis echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong prestige Cab for filet — Nickel & Nickel terroir specificity, though lighter Cabs like Jordan peak higher on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolet's with the filet — contemporary Dutch gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified; Nolet's fruit-floral is a distinctive case.",
  "Filet Mignon|Not a Paper Plane": "Bulleit bourbon, Nonino amaro, Aperol, Angostura — stirred, spirit-forward. The bourbon depth meets butter-tender filet and Nonino's clean herbal bitterness cuts the beef's richness; Aperol's orange edge adds aromatic lift. Peak amaro-forward cocktail for filet — rewards attention.",
  "Filet Mignon|Oban 14": "Oban 14 with the filet — West Highland tiny-distillery single malt, coastal-influenced with sea-salt and honey, dried fruit + subtle smoke hint. Excellent; Oban's maritime-Highland character frames tenderloin with coastal terroir — where Glenmorangie 10 is honey-floral-inland and Dalmore is sherry-luxury, Oban is maritime-Highland.",
  "Filet Mignon|Oban 18": "Oban 18 with the filet — premium aged West Highland Oban, 18yr with sherry-cask finish, deeper maritime-plus-sherry character. Excellent; Oban 18's aged maritime + sherry-finish combination is distinctive Highland-premium — where Oban 14 brings coastal-with-subtle-smoke, the 18 adds sherry-finish depth for the tenderloin.",
  "Filet Mignon|Ocho Anejo": "Filet Mignon: tender lean cut meets Ocho Anejo. Excellent; the field-specific terroir with ex-whiskey-cask aging frames filet at vintage-craft register — where Patron is the recognizable pour, Ocho is the nerd's pick.",
  "Filet Mignon|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Odette — Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality. Dark fruit, sage, cedar, graphite, firm but remarkably refined tannin. The body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the sage-cedar echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak refined Cab for filet — Stags Leap signature balance on the classic tenderloin cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the filet — Colorado distillery cigar-paired bourbon against tenderloin. Strong; Old Elk Cigar Cut frames tenderloin at distinctive cigar-companion register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Old Emmer": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets emmer-wheat wheated bourbon. Old Emmer's ancient-grain wheated character frames tenderloin with distinctive craft-bourbon register — parallel to Weller 12 and Basil Hayden's approaches, but using an ancient wheat variant. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year with the filet — Heaven Hill's wheated heritage bond. 100-proof BIB, 7 years aged, wheated mash. Distinct from Larceny's 92-proof wheated and from Wellers' similar wheated profile — more bonded weight. The wheated BIB for filet orders. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year with the filet — the older Old Fitz, 100-proof BIB, 9 years aged. Deeper oak and more mature honey than the 7-year. A premium wheated-BIB alternative to Weller 12 at a slightly different register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with the filet — Old Grand-Dad 16yr, Beam Suntory's aged OGD with high-rye mash bill for rye-forward spice character. Excellent; OGD 16's aged rye-forward spice is the distinctive aged-bourbon-filet pairing — where HH-family brings dried-fruit-aged character, OGD brings aged-rye-spice distinction.",
  "Filet Mignon|Opus One": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin, impeccably balanced. The balanced structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the mocha plays against escargot-butter. Peak collector Napa blend for filet — sell the Mondavi-Rothschild story on the delicate cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes — Willamette Trappist Chard with pear, butterscotch, oak, rich layered. The rich oak weight handles butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the butterscotch echoes escargot-butter toppers. Works as opulent Chardonnay-with-steak; classic reds peak higher for filet.",
  "Filet Mignon|Pablo Sour": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla syrup, egg white. The cocktail's silky texture and sour profile is wrong for steak — foam has no hook into grilled beef. Strong only when guest leads with the Pablo and stays on it; route filet pairing to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan.",
  "Filet Mignon|Paloma": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The cocktail's three-ingredient lightness is wrong for steak — grapefruit has no hook into grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the Paloma through the entrée; route filet to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan for peak pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye with the filet — unicorn Van Winkle against the 10oz cut. The 13-year Pappy Rye earns the filet at excellent; the Van Winkle-legacy rye for filet orders — extreme scarcity means this is once-a-table territory. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage 16th Ed with the filet — HH annual limited cask-strength against tenderloin. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th is over-specified for filet — save the collector bottle for the ribeye/Tomahawk.",
  "Filet Mignon|Patron Anejo": "Filet Mignon: tender lean cut meets Patron Anejo. Excellent; the American-oak vanilla-caramel profile frames filet at accessible-premium register — Patron is the universal-recognition añejo on the most refined cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver with the filet — 80-proof iconic Patron against the 10oz cut. Works; blanco isn't the natural filet call.",
  "Filet Mignon|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the liqueur's sugar buries the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Patron XO Cafe for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Grower Champagne from Bouzy Grand Cru — Pinot Noir rosé, structured and serious with ripe raspberry, red currant, and mineral finish. The Burgundian weight meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness directly and stands up to the steak. Peak rosé Champagne-with-steak choice. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "After the filet, the PB brownie -- the lean tenderloin yields to peanut-and-chocolate fudge, the vanilla scoop tempering the close. Works; the peanut richness reads heavier than the filet but the warm pairing settles cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab — dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, balanced acidity, refined tannin. The elegant body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the Rutherford-dust-plus-cocoa echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak elegant Napa Cab for filet — family-estate restraint on the classic tenderloin cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand with the filet — artisanal Grande Champagne 1er Cru cognac, unblended vintage-style, traditional methods. Excellent; Ferrand's craft-premium floral-honey character frames tenderloin with artisanal polish — the bartender-favorite cognac-steak pairing where Hennessy is democratic and Rémy is elegant-premium.",
  "Filet Mignon|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "2015 Côte des Blancs grower Champagne — layered Chardonnay with citrus zest, toasted brioche, white flowers, creamy refined texture. The vintage depth and body handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the brioche echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak prestige Champagne-with-steak pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye with the filet — 110-proof HH Maryland-style rye against the 10oz cut. The high proof gives the filet bold rye grip distinct from 90-proof benchmarks; for guests who want high-impact rye. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Pineau des Charentes": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the liqueur's sugar buries the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Pineau des Charentes for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Piedmont Barbaresco DOCG Nebbiolo — Pio Cesare Barolo's approachable sibling, floral aromatics, dried cherry, tar, firm-but-elegant tannins, full body, high acid. The elegant firm tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the floral aromatics play against escargot-butter. Peak approachable-Barolo-sibling for filet — earlier-drinking Nebbiolo on the delicate cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth with the filet — softer Plymouth-style against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified; Plymouth's softness is the gentlest case.",
  "Filet Mignon|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Aldo Conterno Bussia Monforte d'Alba, rose petal, tar, dried cherry, orange peel, iron minerality, full body, firm tannin, high acid, age-worthy. The firm tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the orange peel plays against escargot-butter. Peak serious-Barolo for filet — one of Barolo's greatest names on the delicate cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery 2004 Cuvée Louise — 20-year-old Reims Champagne. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich. The aged complexity and body handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness; honey-brioche complements escargot-butter toppers directly. Peak occasion Champagne-with-steak — treat it accordingly. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Prime Tartare": "Filet Mignon after the tartare -- raw beef into flame-grilled beef, both lean cuts, the tartare's caper-and-shallot brightness priming the palate for the 10oz tenderloin's restraint. Strong; the opener sets the table for the cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Cab — ripe blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft tannin, indulgent finish. The polished tannin meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the chocolate-vanilla echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak lush Napa Cab for filet — crowd-pleasing by-the-bottle anchor on the classic cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|RAEN Royal St Robert": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced Burgundian freshness, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, crushed rock, silky, long mineral. The silky structure meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the crushed-rock echoes escargot-butter. Peak Burgundian-style California Pinot for filet — one of California's finest. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Filet Mignon: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this sparkling rosé's strawberry-and-brioche charm with crisp acidity. Bubbles scrub fat well, but sparkling lacks the dark-fruit weight and tannin for steak. Strong — the interaction lands despite the axis challenge.",
  "Filet Mignon|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 with the filet — premium single pot still Irish against tenderloin. Excellent; Redbreast 12's distinctive pot still character frames tenderloin with rich-spicy-oily gravitas the blended Irish expressions can't match — this is what Irish whiskey becomes when you strip away the grain component.",
  "Filet Mignon|Redbreast 21 Year": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets aged 21-year single pot still Irish, deeper sherry-cask depth, collector-register. Redbreast 21 is the featured aged-Irish-filet pairing — 21 years of aging adds depth to the pot still character where Redbreast 12 reaches, the 21 closes with collector-grade gravitas. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets MGP's Ross & Squibb Distillery, George Remus \"King of Bootleggers\" tribute, 15yr Gatsby-era release, high-rye MGP mash bill with rye-spice + cherry character. Remus 15 Gatsby's high-rye aged-MGP character frames tenderloin with Prohibition-era tribute storytelling — where HH-family brings dried-fruit-aged, Remus brings high-rye-aged-MGP spice. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Rémy VSOP with the filet — Rémy Martin Very Superior Old Pale, Fine Champagne blend (Grande + Petite Champagne crus only), elegant premium. Excellent; Rémy's refined character lifts tenderloin with Grande Champagne elegance — more nuanced than Hennessy's democratic approach, more polished than Courvoisier's drier oak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo with the filet — distinctive Moorish-inspired sun-shaped ceramic bottle. The bottle presentation is genuinely unique on the menu; aged extra añejo character with collectible-packaging draw. Pour for guests who want the distinct bottle experience. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka with the filet — lava-filtered Icelandic against tenderloin. Works; vodka over-specified; Reyka's mineral adds a distinctive register if a guest insists.",
  "Filet Mignon|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique with the filet — retailer-label aged bourbon against the 10oz cut. Strong; boutique-label is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character.",
  "Filet Mignon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The filet after the chickpea soup -- the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth sets the table for the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the spiced register breaks the weight, the table reads round. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Le Mesnil Grand Cru blanc de blancs — chalky minerality, lemon curd, green apple, no dosage. The austere precision handles butter-tender filet's lean sweetness unconventionally; the chalk-mineral echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong Champagne-with-steak when guest wants the sommelier's bottle over classic Cab.",
  "Filet Mignon|Roku Gin": "Roku with the filet — Suntory Japanese gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Filet Mignon|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum -- Solera 23, Guatemalan, honey-vanilla-caramel. The dense-spirit weight carries the lean buttery filet, and the heavy-pour register grips the cut. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Filet Mignon|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with the filet — Wild Turkey's aged sipping bourbon. 90 proof, 10 years old, classic Kentucky caramel-vanilla-oak profile. Distinct from BT's flagship cleanliness — more Wild Turkey character, more oak richness from aging. A non-wheated premium alternate for filet. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Sambuca": "Sambuca's syrupy-sweet liqueur body leans against the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the syrupy liqueur weight wraps the lean buttery filet, and the digestif sweetness softens the cut. Works; the two sides share the meal without competing.",
  "Filet Mignon|San Simeon Stormwatch": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin, full body. The medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the mocha plays against escargot-butter. Strong Paso value Bordeaux blend for filet — solid full-body on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sta. Rita Hills Santa Barbara Pinot — legendary 1971 vineyard, rich, layered, dark cherry, earth, dried herbs, finesse, medium tannin. The layered structure meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the dried-herbs play against escargot-butter. Peak legendary-vineyard Pinot for filet — California Pinot history on the cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Filet Mignon with the garlic spinach -- the 10oz tenderloin meets the wilted garlic-buttered greens cleanly, the garlic anchoring the cut's mild beefy register. Strong; lighter than creamed, just as composed.",
  "Filet Mignon|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet wine smothers the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Sauternes Glass for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The anise-rye profile and Peychaud's floral-cherry note play against butter-tender filet elegantly; the spirit-forward structure meets lean beef without crushing delicacy. Peak spirit-forward classic for the most tender cut — historical pairing weight.",
  "Filet Mignon|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye with the filet — BT's iconic rye against the 10oz cut. The 90-proof spicy-sweet rye frames the filet's clean beef with the bright pepper-and-floral character that makes rye distinct from bourbon; the benchmark rye pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the filet — New Zealand single malt (reclassified from American Single Malt / Tennessee — Scapegrace is a New Zealand craft distillery). Excellent; Fortuna's distinctive NZ-single-malt terroir frames tenderloin with southern-hemisphere character no other spirit on the menu can claim — the first NZ whisky on the reference.",
  "Filet Mignon|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid, full body. The high acid cuts butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the dried rose plays against escargot-butter. Peak King-of-Italian for filet — top Barolo producer on the delicate cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's off-dry Rheingau Riesling with lime-petrol edge mis-pairs with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- this white's register is wrong for the filet — a softer red would bridge better. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Schloss Vollrads Riesling for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin, smooth full body. The smooth medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the dark chocolate plays against escargot-butter. Strong by-the-glass Cab for filet — accessible California value on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Seafood Tower": "Filet Mignon after the seafood tower -- the iced shellfish lineup cleans the palate, the 10oz lean cut following with quiet beef butter. Strong; the cold opener resets the table for the cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Seasonal Soup": "Filet Mignon preceded by the seasonal soup -- a clean call, no friction either way. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
  "Filet Mignon|Seasonal Vegetables": "Filet Mignon with the seasonal vegetables -- the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; the side carries cleanly with the cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Tuscany Brunello DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Sesti biodynamic, cherry, dried rose, sandalwood, high acid, firm tannin, elegant. The elegance matches butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the sandalwood plays against escargot-butter. Peak biodynamic-Brunello for filet — a refined Montalcino anchor on the delicate cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Shafer 1.5": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin. The plush tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the vanilla-mocha plays against escargot-butter. Strong Stags Leap Cab for filet — Shafer opulence on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Shafer Hillside Select": "Stags Leap collector's Cab — Hillside Select 32-month French oak, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violet, graphite, dense yet silky, firm tannin, full body. The silky structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the violet plays against escargot-butter. Strong collector's Napa Cab for filet — Hillside Select poise on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Shrimp Bisque": "Filet Mignon after the shrimp bisque -- the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup primes the palate for the filet's lean buttery tenderness. Strong; the soup-or-salad sets the table without crowding the cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Shrimp Cocktail": "Filet Mignon after the shrimp cocktail -- the chilled shrimp and citrus-horseradish bite refreshes the palate, the 10oz tenderloin following with clean grilled butter. Strong; the bright opener primes the lean cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco with the filet — traditional craft blanco against the 10oz cut. Works; blanco isn't the natural filet call.",
  "Filet Mignon|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado with the filet — tahona-crushed traditional character against the 10oz cut. The rustic agave gives the filet a different vocabulary than polished reposados; a craft-forward call for the cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak — Alexander Valley Cab with American-oak vanilla, coconut, dill, ripe cassis, plum, soft tannin. The lush body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the vanilla-dill echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong recognizable Cab for filet — American-oak lushness, though leaner Cabs peak higher on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Boutique Napa family estate Cab — dark plum, cassis, toasted oak, long refined finish, firm tannin, full body. The firm tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the toasted oak plays against escargot-butter. Strong boutique Napa Cab for filet — Simon Family estate-grown pedigree on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "St. Helena Napa Cab — Spottswoode certified-organic family estate second label, plum, dark cherry, violet, silky medium tannin, full body. The silky structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the violet plays against escargot-butter. Peak organic Napa Cab for filet — estate story on the delicate cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab — blackberry, cassis, mocha, firm tannin, full body. The firm structure meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the cassis plays against escargot-butter toppers cleanly. Peak by-the-glass Cab for filet — classic Rutherford Cab character at an accessible price. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character mis-pairs with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- this white's register is wrong for the filet — a softer red would bridge better. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — five-varietal estate flagship, blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin, full body, 5-year bottle age integration. The integrated structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the espresso plays against escargot-butter. Peak estate Meritage for filet — age-integrated Napa. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the filet — BT cask-strength against tenderloin. Works; Stagg Jr.'s intensity is over-specified for filet — save for the ribeye/Tomahawk.",
  "Filet Mignon|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit with the filet — quadruple-filtered Russian wheat against tenderloin. Works; vodka over-specified for steak, but Elit's top-shelf purity is the most elegant vodka case.",
  "Filet Mignon|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, and fresh-cut grass with zesty acid. Bright and light, but the filet's buttery richness washes the wine out and the wine doesn't have the body to hold red meat. Redirect to a red. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Filet Mignon|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 with the filet — premium martini gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak; Tanq 10's refinement is the most elegant gin case.",
  "Filet Mignon|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray with the filet — high-proof juniper against tenderloin. Works; gin is over-specified for steak; Tanq's proof at least gives it a fighting chance.",
  "Filet Mignon|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- dessert wine is post-meal — wrong register for the steak course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet wine smothers the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle belongs on another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with the filet — Germán González's extra añejo aged in three different casks: Scotch, sherry, and cognac. One of the most distinctive multi-cask tequilas in the world; González is a legendary tequila maker. The cask-influence layers create a frame no other extra añejo matches. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter — Napa Cab with rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth lingering finish. The polished body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the mocha echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong mid-tier Napa Cab for filet — crowd-pleaser balance, though leaner-style Cabs like Jordan peak similarly on the classic cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tuscany Chianti Classico DOCG Sangiovese — Arceno vibrant cherry, dried herbs, tomato leaf, savory earth, medium body, medium tannin, high acid. The high acid and medium tannin meet butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the dried herbs play against escargot-butter. Peak Tuscan Sangiovese for filet — Italian table wine on the delicate cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|The BG": "Filet Mignon: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. The whiskey's barrel depth and the steak's flame-grilled fat run at the same intensity — a classic steakhouse move. Works — cocktail-on-steak coherence.",
  "Filet Mignon|The Happy Wife": "Tito's, orange juice, ginger syrup — stirred. The simple cocktail profile is wrong for any steak — orange juice and ginger have no hook into grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the cocktail; route filet pairing to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan.",
  "Filet Mignon|The Manhattan": "Maker's Mark private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The vanilla softens what would be a drier Manhattan, making it meet butter-tender filet without overwhelming; Contratto's herbal-sweet depth plays against lean beef. Peak whiskey cocktail for the most delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|The Prisoner Red Blend": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The smooth tannin matches butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the layered fruit plays with escargot-butter. Strong Prisoner for filet — full body reads heavy but the smoothness threads.",
  "Filet Mignon|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the filet — BTAC uncut against the 10oz cut. The Antique Collection Sazerac earns the filet at excellent; the BT-allocated Sazerac when the benchmark isn't enough. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the filet — Barton Distilling (Sazerac) bourbon finished in chardonnay casks — a white-wine finish, DISTINCTIVE from the port/red-wine crowd. Excellent; TSM Chardonnay Cask's white-wine-finish character is the distinctive-white-wine-finished-bourbon-filet pairing — where every other wine-finish in this cluster brings red/port jammy-fruit, TSM brings chardonnay's crisp-apple-butter notes for a completely different wine-bourbon story.",
  "Filet Mignon|Titos Vodka": "Tito's with the filet — American corn against tenderloin. Works; vodka is over-specified for filet, but Tito's roundness won't clash — a functional backup if a guest insists.",
  "Filet Mignon|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets entry Suntory blend (Hakushu + Yamazaki + Chita grain), light-floral, highball-friendly. Toki's crowd-pleasing Japanese-entry character frames tenderloin with Suntory-blended elegance — the democratic Japanese-whisky-filet pairing, where Hibiki's refinement goes premium and Hakushu's peat goes distinctive. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Tomato Basil": "The filet after the tomato basil -- the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid sets the table for the filet's lean buttery tenderness: acid plays against richness, the contrast holds across the courses. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Transfusion": "Hangar 1 vodka, muddled black grapes, lime, ginger beer top. The cocktail's fruit-led sessionable profile is wrong for any steak — ginger beer has no hook into grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the cocktail; route filet to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan.",
  "Filet Mignon|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado with the filet — cedar-wood-aged reposado against the 10oz cut. The pine and cedar notes give the filet a singular frame; a curiosity pour for adventurous guests. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Truffle Fries": "The filet into the truffle fries -- the filet's lean buttery tenderness meets the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight. Excellent; reliable headline side for the steak course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W. with the filet — traditional heritage Irish against tenderloin. Excellent; Tullamore's drier triple-distilled character frames tenderloin at heritage-Irish register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Venge Family Reserve": "Kirk Venge flagship Oakville Cab — concentrated blackcurrant, dark chocolate, vanilla, seamless polished finish, soft tannin, full body. The soft tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the vanilla-chocolate plays against escargot-butter. Strong Oakville flagship for filet — Venge craftsman opulence on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Venge Scout's Honor": "Napa Zin-led blend — Kirk Venge's Scout's Honor, blackberry, boysenberry, black pepper, vanilla, full body, generous. The ripe fruit and full body read heavier than lean-tender filet demands — the butter-tender cut calls for Caymus or Pinot. Works if guest is already on the Zin blend — route filet-specific pairing elsewhere.",
  "Filet Mignon|Venge Silencieux": "Kirk Venge single-vineyard Napa — dark cherry, cassis, chocolate, floral violet, silky soft tannin. The silky tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the violet-chocolate plays against escargot-butter. Strong winemaker's Napa Cab for filet — Venge precision on the delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Vesper": "Nolet's, Ketel One, Lillet — equal parts, stirred. The martini's elegance is wrong for any steak — clean cocktail structure has no hook into grilled beef. Works if guest stays on the martini; route filet pairing to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan.",
  "Filet Mignon|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut — yellow label Champagne, Pinot Noir-dominant. Ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse, lively acidity. The body handles butter-tender filet's lean sweetness unconventionally; biscuit echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong Champagne-with-steak when guest wants iconic bubbles alongside filet.",
  "Filet Mignon|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye and Pierre Ferrand cognac split, Contratto vermouth, Bénédictine, Angostura and Peychaud's bitters. The rye-cognac split gives both spice and richness; Bénédictine's herbal depth plays against butter-tender filet. Peak sophisticated cocktail for the most delicate cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|Villon Cognac": "Villon with the filet — Courvoisier's modern spirit aimed at younger drinkers, Cognac with saffron + elderflower \"Voyage\" infusion. Excellent; Villon's contemporary accessibility and light spice frame tenderloin at a register Hennessy's benchmark doesn't reach — the younger-guest cognac-steak pairing for guests new to the category.",
  "Filet Mignon|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet wine smothers the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Vin Santo belongs on another course.",
  "Filet Mignon|Weller 107": "Weller 107 with the filet — the higher-proof Weller. The extra 17 proof points give this pour heat and body the Special Reserve doesn't have; frames the filet with more muscle. The Weller for guests who want wheated but also want a hit of alcohol warmth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Weller 12 Year": "Filet Mignon: the 10oz center-cut's clean beef purity and restraint meet Weller 12 Year's aged wheated oak, honey, and caramel. The 12-year's depth frames the filet without overpowering; the marquee wheated pour for this cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium with the filet — a special-occasion pour for a restrained cut. The Millennium release's polished deep oak, honey, and vanilla frame the filet in a way that's unmistakably premium. The top-tier wheated pour for filet when a guest wants the absolute best. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|Weller Special Reserve": "Filet Mignon: the 10oz center-cut's clean beef purity meets Weller Special Reserve's 90-proof caramel-vanilla warmth. The gateway wheated pour for filet; less refined than Weller 12 but perfectly poised for the cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley with the filet — Buffalo Trace wheat vodka against tenderloin. Works; vodka over-specified for steak, but Wheatley's BT pedigree is a clean functional match.",
  "Filet Mignon|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the filet — aged 100-proof WP single-barrel against the 10oz cut. Strong; the premium rye depth frames the filet well but WP 15 Year or Michter's 10 Year bring more refined aged character for this cut.",
  "Filet Mignon|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with the filet — fruit-cask-finished 12-year against the 10oz cut. Strong; Old World fruit character gives the filet layered complexity but WP 15 Year's extended maturation earns excellent here.",
  "Filet Mignon|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with the filet — extended-aged 15-year flagship against the 10oz cut. The Vermont-oak-finished depth earns the filet where WP 10 Year only works strong; the WP flagship for filet orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with the filet — ultra-premium WP allocated against the 10oz cut. The Boss Hog's barrel-proof depth earns the filet at excellent where WP 10 Year only works strong; the unicorn WP filet pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with the filet — X Commandments 10th-edition against the 10oz cut. The allocated ultra-premium WP earns the filet at excellent; the specific-vintage Boss Hog for filet. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Filet Mignon|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year with the filet — 100-proof 6-year WP rye against the 10oz cut. The Vermont-brand approach gives the filet a Canadian-rye-sourced character distinct from Kentucky rye profiles. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with the filet — Willett's signature craft pour, distinctive pot-still-shaped bottle. 94-proof high-rye with caramel, vanilla, and oak spice. Distinct from the Beam-side high-ryes (Knob Creek, Bulleit) — more craft-small-batch character. A distinctive filet pour with bottle theatrics tableside. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with the filet — Kentucky's small-batch classic. 90.4 proof with fruit-forward and floral notes layered over clean caramel. Distinct from Buffalo Trace's straight-line oak — more interplay, more fruit vocabulary. A refined non-wheated call for filet orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with the filet — Brown-Forman KY Double Oaked against tenderloin. Excellent; Woodford Double Oaked's iconic intensified character frames tenderloin with KY-benchmark distinction.",
  "Filet Mignon|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the filet — elegant small-batch rye against the 10oz cut. The fruit-forward character gives the filet a distinctive rye frame; more refined than Sazerac Rye's benchmark character. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with the filet — Washington State small-batch rye against the 10oz cut. The PNW craft character gives the filet a non-Kentucky rye voice; distinctive regional-craft pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Filet Mignon|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 with the filet — Suntory's flagship single malt from Japan's first single malt distillery (opened 1923 by Shinjiro Torii), Mizunara-oak-signature honey-fruit with sandalwood hint. Excellent; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak character frames tenderloin with Japanese-single-malt gravitas — the distillery that started Japanese whisky making its featured filet pairing.",
  "Filet Mignon|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year -- aged Suntory, mizunara cask, silky. The Mizunara-influenced register sits with the lean buttery filet, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the cut. Excellent; JD Bonded's backbone cuts the glaze harder than Old No. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Filet Mignon|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness sits alongside the filet's tender lean cut — the sweet character meets the cut at digestif register, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Asparagus": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Fireball Whisky belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Beignets": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the liqueur compounds the dessert's sweetness without contrast. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Bone Marrow": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character flattens the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Bone-In Filet": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Fireball Whisky belongs on another course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Broccoli Cheddar": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Broccolini": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Brussels and Belly": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character doubles down on Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- sweet liqueur and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no contrast left. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Burrata": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Butternut Squash": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Carrot Cake": "Fireball with carrot cake — cinnamon on cinnamon is the whole play. The cream cheese frosting absorbs the whisky heat and the spice registers line up cleanly. This is the Fireball pairing that actually earns its place on the dessert list. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Cheesecake": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the liqueur compounds the dessert's sweetness without contrast. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Fireball Whisky on the steak course instead.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Chilean Seabass": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert-grade sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs a crisp white instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Fireball Whisky on the steak course instead.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Chocolate Cake": "Fireball Whisky: this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness meets this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Works as an indulgence.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Clam Chowder": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Fireball Whisky belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Cowboy Ribeye": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character flattens the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Fireball Whisky belongs on another course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Crab Cake": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Fireball Whisky belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Creamed Spinach": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Creamy Potato": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Creme Brulee": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the liqueur compounds the dessert's sweetness without contrast. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Fireball Whisky belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Escargot": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character buries the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- sweet liqueur smothers the herb-bright opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Fireball Whisky lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Faroe Island Salmon": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Filet Mignon": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Fireball Whisky on another course instead.",
  "Fireball Whisky|French Onion": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Fireball Whisky belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Grilled Caesar": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character jars against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Fireball Whisky on the steak course instead.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Gumbo": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Fireball Whisky belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sweet liqueur and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no contrast left. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|House Wedge": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Kansas City": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Fireball Whisky for another course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Loaded Potato": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Lobster Mac": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Market Fish": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- sugar on delicate fish is wrong direction — the plate needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Mocha Creme": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the liqueur compounds the dessert's sweetness without contrast. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Fireball Whisky on the steak course instead.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Mushroom Bisque": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Mushrooms": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character fights the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- the liqueur's sweet register pulls against the mushroom's umami without bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Works as an indulgence.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Porterhouse": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Fireball Whisky lands on another course — not here.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Prime Tartare": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Fireball Whisky lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Rainbow Trout": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- sugar on delicate fish is wrong direction — the plate needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Fireball Whisky lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Roast Half Chicken": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Fireball Whisky lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Seafood Tower": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Seared Scallops": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character smothers the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- sugar buries the delicate shellfish without any acid to balance. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Fireball Whisky lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Seasonal Soup": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Seasonal Vegetables": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Fireball Whisky on the steak course instead.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Shrimp Bisque": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Shrimp Cocktail": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Fireball Whisky lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Swordfish": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Fireball Whisky on the steak course instead.",
  "Fireball Whisky|The Tomahawk": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Fireball Whisky belongs on another course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Tomato Basil": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Truffle Fries": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Fireball Whisky lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fireball Whisky|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Asparagus": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Beignets": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Fisher Unity Pinot Noir belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Bone Marrow": "Sonoma Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — silky Pinot lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Fisher Unity for the chicken, salmon, or seafood course.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Bone-In Filet": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Broccoli Cheddar": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Broccolini": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the broccolini's tender green — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Brussels and Belly": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Burrata": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The silky tannin meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the cherry plays against basil-tomato. Works as by-the-glass Pinot for burrata — accessible red for the delicate starter; whites and rosés peak higher.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Butternut Squash": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Carrot Cake": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Fisher Unity Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Cheesecake": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Fisher Unity Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Chilean Seabass": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The cranberry acid lifts seabass's buttery flesh and the baking spice plays against the rich texture. Works as by-the-glass Pinot for Chilean seabass — California silk on the buttery classic.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Chocolate Brownie": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Fisher Unity Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Chocolate Cake": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Fisher Unity Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Clam Chowder": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Cowboy Ribeye": "Sonoma Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — silky low-tannin Pinot falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep Fisher Unity for salmon, chicken, or seafood; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Crab Cake": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky soft tannin. The soft tannin doesn't fight crab's sweet meat and the cranberry acid cuts romesco. Peak by-the-glass Pinot for crab cake — unconventional red that works where heavier reds would bury the shellfish. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Creamed Spinach": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift sits alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the Pinot body brightens the creamed spinach, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Creamy Potato": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the potato soup's silky body — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Creme Brulee": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Fisher Unity Pinot Noir lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Escargot": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift reads alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the Pinot body brightens the buttered escargot, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the starter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Faroe Island Salmon": "Sonoma Pinot — bright cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky tannin. Rounder and riper than the Willamette Pinots; handles salmon well for guests who want California over Oregon. Not the peak, but solid. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Filet Mignon": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky tannins, medium body. The silky tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate texture and the cherry-spice plays against escargot-butter. Peak by-the-glass Pinot for filet — silky California red for the lean cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|French Onion": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The cranberry acid cuts French Onion's gruyère-crust richness and the baking spice plays against the caramelized onion. Peaks from the dish's side; on this wine's card it's strong — below peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins. Confident pour when a guest anchors on French Onion. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Grilled Caesar": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The cranberry acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the spice plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong by-the-glass Pinot for grilled Caesar — California silk reframes the layered salad.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Gumbo": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The cranberry acid cuts carrots' honey-glaze sweetness and the baking spice plays against the roasted char. Works as by-the-glass Pinot for honey-roasted carrots — California silk on the sweet-root classic.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|House Wedge": "Sonoma Pinot — riper fruit, rounder than the Willamette. Works with our lardons but the wedge is heavy for Pinot overall. The Avni or a rosé does more; keep Fisher for the steak or marrow.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Kansas City": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky tannins, medium body. The silky structure works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though the low tannin falls short of bolder Cabs' char-requirement. Works as by-the-glass Pinot for the strip — guest-preference call over classic Cab.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Loaded Potato": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The cranberry acid cuts the soup's cheddar-bacon richness and the baking spice plays against scallion toppings. Works as by-the-glass Pinot for loaded potato — California silk on the indulgent classic.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Lobster Mac": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The acid cuts lobster-mac's cream-cheese richness and the cherry plays against the dense pasta. Works as by-the-glass Pinot for the lobster mac side — unconventional red pick, accessible Chard peaks higher.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Market Fish": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin holds with the fish's clean flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the spice meets the herb butter. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Mocha Creme": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Fisher Unity Pinot Noir lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Mushroom Bisque": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Mushrooms": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Fisher Unity Pinot Noir on the steak course instead.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Porterhouse": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin matches the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Prime Tartare": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky tannins. The soft tannin leaves room for raw-beef delicacy and the cherry-cranberry plays against egg yolk and capers. Strong by-the-glass Pinot for tartare — California silk on the raw-beef classic.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Rainbow Trout": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the trout's delicate flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the spice meets the herb butter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Roast Half Chicken": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky tannins. The spice note echoes roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak California Pinot for chicken — silky red across most seasonal preparations. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The cranberry acid cuts the soup's tomato-pepper base and the baking spice plays against smoky chickpea. Works as by-the-glass Pinot for the soup — California silk on the Mediterranean classic.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin leans against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky tannins. The acid cuts the sauté butter and the cherry-spice plays against garlic without clashing with spinach earth. Strong by-the-glass Pinot for the sauté side — California silk rounds the pairing.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Seafood Tower": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Fisher Unity Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Seared Scallops": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The soft tannin leaves room for scallop sweetness and the cherry-spice plays against caramelized-exterior char. Strong by-the-glass Pinot for scallops — California silk handles the delicate shellfish.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Seasonal Soup": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Seasonal Vegetables": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Shrimp Bisque": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The acid cuts bisque's cream weight and the cherry-spice plays against shellfish umami and sherry. Strong by-the-glass Pinot for shrimp bisque — California silk against the rich soup.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Shrimp Cocktail": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overshadows the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Fisher Unity Pinot Noir on the steak course instead.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Swordfish": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The cranberry acid lifts swordfish's meaty flesh and the baking spice plays against the grilled char. Strong by-the-glass Pinot for swordfish — California silk on the grilled classic.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|The Tomahawk": "Sonoma Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — silky California Pinot has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Fisher Unity for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Tomato Basil": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin holds with the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Truffle Fries": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The cranberry acid cuts sesame-crust weight and the baking spice plays against honey sambal. Strong by-the-glass Pinot for tuxedo tuna — California silk on the rare-seared classic.",
  "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Asparagus": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon depth settles into the asparagus, and the oak frames the side. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with au gratin potatoes — 104-proof premium Four Roses against cheese-and-cream. Strong; concentrated character at premium register.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Beignets": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the oak depth lays under the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Bone Marrow": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with bone marrow — 104-proof premium Four Roses against umami fat. The concentrated blend meets the marrow with premium small-batch weight. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Bone-In Filet": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads register-aligned with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the brown-spirit warmth threads the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak-and-spice register settles the cut. Strong; collector intensity at allocated register.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Broccoli Cheddar": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon weight handles the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Broccolini": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon depth settles into the broccolini, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; AE's fruity-jammy character engages the bleu at wine-finish register.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Brussels and Belly": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with Brussels + Belly — 104-proof premium 4R meets maple-glazed pork belly. The concentrated blend cuts the glaze with premium small-batch authority. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Burrata": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with burrata — fresh dairy against 104-proof premium. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Butternut Squash": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon backbone carries the squash bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the pairing is pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Carrot Cake": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with carrot cake — 104-proof premium 4R against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; concentrated blend slightly over-specified.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Cheesecake": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with cheesecake — 104-proof 6-recipe against clean custard. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Chilean Seabass": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Four Roses Small Batch Select for the steak course.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Chocolate Brownie": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the chocolate brownie — 104-proof premium Four Roses against cocoa. Works; the concentrated character meets the brownie at premium register.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Chocolate Cake": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; bonded weight a touch ambitious for the dessert.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Clam Chowder": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the caramel-spice register matches the chowder, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Cowboy Ribeye": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the Cowboy Ribeye — 104-proof premium Four Roses against cap fat. The concentrated blend handles the ribeye with small-batch authority. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Crab Cake": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with crab cake — 104-proof premium against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Creamed Spinach": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with creamed spinach — 104-proof 6-recipe against dairy. Strong; premium proof sits with richness.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Creamy Potato": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Creme Brulee": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the caramel-spice register matches the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Escargot": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's floral-rose character finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Faroe Island Salmon": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Four Roses Small Batch Select lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Filet Mignon": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the filet — higher-end Four Roses using 6 of their 10 recipes, 104 proof non-chill-filtered. More concentrated than Yellow Label's entry-blend; the higher proof and select recipes give the filet a premium Four Roses frame. Distinct from Yellow Label by both proof and recipe selection. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|French Onion": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the oak depth lays under the French onion, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; 100-proof complexity sits beside the soup.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Grilled Caesar": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the grilled Caesar — premium 4R against charred romaine. Strong; 104-proof earns the Caesar with small-batch character.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Gumbo": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; boutique-sourced wasted on salad.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the caramel-spice register matches the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|House Wedge": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the House Wedge — 104-proof 6-recipe against iceberg and bleu. Works; premium proof overpowers the salad.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Kansas City": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the KC — 104-proof 6-recipe blend against the strip's marbling. The extra proof handles the cut where Yellow Label's 80-proof fades; premium Four Roses for strip orders. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Loaded Potato": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive but adds interest.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Lobster Mac": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the lobster mac, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; neutral, not elevating.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Market Fish": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Four Roses Small Batch Select for the steak course.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Mocha Creme": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mocha creme, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; HH 20 is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Mushroom Bisque": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the cream. Works; doesn't drive.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Mushrooms": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the mushrooms — 104-proof 6-recipe against earthy umami. The concentrated premium blend layers into mushroom depth with Four Roses floral-complex character. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the PB brownie — 104-proof premium against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; the premium Four Roses character gives the dessert small-batch depth Yellow Label can't bring.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Porterhouse": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the porterhouse — 104-proof premium across strip and filet. Strong; extra proof leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Prime Tartare": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's floral-rose character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Rainbow Trout": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Four Roses Small Batch Select for the steak course.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Roast Half Chicken": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the oak depth lays under the roast chicken, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the chickpea soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Four Roses Small Batch Select for the steak course.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon body sits with the garlic spinach, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; novelty maturation at matched register.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Seafood Tower": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Four Roses Small Batch Select belongs on the steak course.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Seared Scallops": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 104-proof. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Seasonal Soup": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the oak depth lays under the seasonal soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; doesn't drive.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Seasonal Vegetables": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; gentle match at matched registers.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Shrimp Bisque": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the bourbon depth settles into the shrimp bisque, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; bonded weight pushes past the soup's register slightly.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Shrimp Cocktail": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against 104-proof. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Swordfish": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Four Roses Small Batch Select on the steak course instead.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk: bone-in ribeye meets 104-proof 6-recipe blend. Extra proof earns the cut where Four Roses Yellow Label can't reach; Small Batch Select and Baker's are premium-small-batch Tomahawk calls. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Tomato Basil": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the caramel-spice register matches the tomato basil, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; Portwood is over-specified for a side — save for main course.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Truffle Fries": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with truffle fries — premium 104-proof against truffle-parmesan. The extra proof and recipe-select complexity give the side real grip. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Four Roses Small Batch Select lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Four Roses Small Batch Select|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the caramel-spice register matches the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the unique maturation story adds interest to a gentle dessert match.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Asparagus": "Four Roses Yellow Label's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the brown-spirit warmth threads the asparagus, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; the Millennium's register overshoots the dessert's.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Four Roses Yellow Label with au gratin potatoes — entry-level bourbon against cheese-and-cream. Strong; accessible everyday match.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Beignets": "Four Roses Yellow Label's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon backbone carries the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the boldness pushes slightly past what the soup asks for.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Bone Marrow": "Four Roses Yellow Label with bone marrow — entry-level 80-proof against umami fat. Outmatched by the starter; Weller 12 or Buffalo Trace carry the marrow more fully. Capable everyday choice. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Bone-In Filet": "Four Roses Yellow Label's crisp caramel-floral character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Broccoli Cheddar": "Four Roses Yellow Label's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon depth settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the oak frames the cream. Works; premium proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Broccolini": "Four Roses Yellow Label's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon body sits with the broccolini, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; retailer-pick wasted on salad.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Brussels and Belly": "Four Roses Yellow Label with Brussels + Belly — 80-proof floral-honey against maple-glazed pork belly. The light body doesn't compete; a friendly, approachable small-plate pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Burrata": "Four Roses Yellow Label with burrata — fresh dairy against entry bourbon. Avoid; register mismatch.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Butternut Squash": "Four Roses Yellow Label's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon depth settles into the squash bisque, and the oak frames the cream. Works; the Millennium's register overshoots the dessert's.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Carrot Cake": "Four Roses Yellow Label with carrot cake — floral-and-honey entry bourbon against cream cheese and cinnamon. The floral notes pick up the cake's raisins lightly; a friendly dessert pairing within the entry tier. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
