  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Creme Brulee": "Four Roses Yellow Label's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; Prohibition narrative wasted on salad.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Escargot": "Four Roses Yellow Label's crisp caramel-floral character matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Faroe Island Salmon": "Four Roses Yellow Label's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Four Roses Yellow Label for the steak course.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|Filet Mignon": "Four Roses Yellow Label with the filet — entry-level 80-proof bourbon against the 10oz cut. Capable but underpowered; Weller Special Reserve or Maker's Mark are the stronger everyday wheated calls, Buffalo Trace is the non-wheated alternate. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Four Roses Yellow Label|French Onion": "Four Roses Yellow Label's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the French onion, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
