  "Fox & Oden|Escargot": "Fox & Oden's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon weight handles the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; the curated single-barrel is capable but single-distillery aged bourbons bring more traditional focused character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Fox & Oden|Faroe Island Salmon": "Fox & Oden's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Fox & Oden lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Fox & Oden|Filet Mignon": "Fox & Oden with the filet — craft small-batch bourbon, a newer boutique brand with small-production character. Distinct from the big-brand small batches (Knob Creek 12, Baker's) by scale alone; a craft-spirit voice for the filet with regional-distillery gravitas. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Fox & Oden|French Onion": "Fox & Oden's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the oak depth lays under the French onion, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; accessible gentle match.",
  "Fox & Oden|Grilled Caesar": "Fox & Oden with the grilled Caesar — craft bourbon against charred romaine. Works; boutique character doesn't drive the Caesar.",
  "Fox & Oden|Gumbo": "Fox & Oden's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon weight handles the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; premium KO story wasted on salad.",
  "Fox & Oden|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Fox & Oden's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the caramel-spice register matches the honey carrots, and the oak threads the caramelized sugar. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Fox & Oden|House Wedge": "Fox & Oden with the House Wedge — craft small-batch against iceberg and bleu. Works; boutique character slightly over-specified.",
  "Fox & Oden|Kansas City": "Fox & Oden with the KC — craft boutique bourbon against the strip's marbling. The small-production character gives the strip a craft-spirit frame distinct from big-brand pours. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Fox & Oden|Loaded Potato": "Fox & Oden's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon weight handles the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; HH 20 is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Fox & Oden|Lobster Mac": "Fox & Oden's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon depth settles into the lobster mac, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Fox & Oden|Market Fish": "Fox & Oden's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Fox & Oden for the steak course.",
  "Fox & Oden|Mocha Creme": "Fox & Oden's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon backbone carries the mocha creme, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; annual-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Fox & Oden|Mushroom Bisque": "Fox & Oden's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon body sits with the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; aged character overshoots delicacy.",
  "Fox & Oden|Mushrooms": "Fox & Oden with the mushrooms — craft bourbon against earthy umami. The small-production character layers into mushroom depth distinctly. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Fox & Oden|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Fox & Oden with the PB brownie — craft small-batch against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; boutique character gives the dessert a craft frame.",
  "Fox & Oden|Porterhouse": "Fox & Oden with the porterhouse — craft bourbon across strip and filet. Strong; the boutique character covers both cuts at craft-small-batch register.",
  "Fox & Oden|Prime Tartare": "Fox & Oden's crisp caramel-vanilla character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Fox & Oden|Rainbow Trout": "Fox & Oden's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Fox & Oden for the steak course.",
  "Fox & Oden|Roast Half Chicken": "Fox & Oden's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon body sits with the roast chicken, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the plate. Works; milestone-release doesn't drive.",
  "Fox & Oden|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Fox & Oden's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the chickpea soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Fox & Oden|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Fox & Oden's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Fox & Oden belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fox & Oden|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Fox & Oden's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the oak depth lays under the garlic spinach, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Fox & Oden|Seafood Tower": "Fox & Oden's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Fox & Oden belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fox & Oden|Seared Scallops": "Fox & Oden with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against craft bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Fox & Oden|Seasonal Soup": "Fox & Oden's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the oak depth lays under the seasonal soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; AE Triple Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Fox & Oden|Seasonal Vegetables": "Fox & Oden's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; Sinatra Select handles the richness at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Fox & Oden|Shrimp Bisque": "Fox & Oden's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the bourbon backbone carries the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; narrative-bourbon doesn't drive.",
  "Fox & Oden|Shrimp Cocktail": "Fox & Oden with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against craft bourbon. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Fox & Oden|Swordfish": "Fox & Oden's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Fox & Oden for the steak course.",
  "Fox & Oden|The Tomahawk": "Fox & Oden with the Tomahawk — craft small-batch against bone-in ribeye. Strong; the boutique character is capable but Baker's, Four Roses Small Batch Select, or E.H. Taylor bring more Tomahawk grip.",
  "Fox & Oden|Tomato Basil": "Fox & Oden's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the caramel-spice register matches the tomato basil, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the pairing is pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Fox & Oden|Truffle Fries": "Fox & Oden with truffle fries — boutique small-batch against truffle-parmesan. The craft character gives the side a regional-distillery frame. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Fox & Oden|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Fox & Oden's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Fox & Oden belongs on the steak course.",
  "Fox & Oden|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Fox & Oden's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a soup — save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Frangelico|Asparagus": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Frangelico on the steak course instead.",
  "Frangelico|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Frangelico belongs on the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Beignets": "Frangelico's sweet hazelnut-cocoa character holds steady against the beignet's warm sugar-dusted pastry — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Frangelico|Bone Marrow": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Bone-In Filet": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Frangelico for another course.",
  "Frangelico|Broccoli Cheddar": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register overwhelms the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Frangelico on the steak course instead.",
  "Frangelico|Broccolini": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Brussels and Belly": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Frangelico lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Frangelico|Burrata": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register doubles down on the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur and fresh dairy compound the richness without contrast — heavy too early. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Frangelico on the steak course instead.",
  "Frangelico|Butternut Squash": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Frangelico. Strong; hazelnut-vanilla-cocoa plays cake spice at Italian-liqueur register.",
  "Frangelico|Cheesecake": "Frangelico's syrupy-sweet liqueur body meets at register with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the syrupy liqueur weight wraps the cheesecake, and the honey threads the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Frangelico|Chilean Seabass": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Frangelico. Excellent; the hazelnut-cocoa profile runs directly into chocolate — this is Nutella in liquid form, at Piedmont-liqueur register. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Frangelico|Chocolate Cake": "Frangelico's sweet hazelnut-cocoa character holds the line with the cake's layered chocolate — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the cocoa underlines the chocolate. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Frangelico|Clam Chowder": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register doubles down on the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Frangelico lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Frangelico|Cowboy Ribeye": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Frangelico for another course.",
  "Frangelico|Crab Cake": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Creamed Spinach": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Frangelico lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Frangelico|Creamy Potato": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Creme Brulee": "Frangelico: Italian hazelnut liqueur from Piedmont (the hazelnut heartland), 20% ABV, monk-shaped bottle nodding to its Franciscan friar origins. Excellent on creme brulee; the hazelnut-vanilla-cocoa profile threads burnt-sugar-custard at iconic-Italian-digestif register — hazelnut-on-caramel classic.",
  "Frangelico|Escargot": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register buries the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- sweet liqueur smothers the herb-bright opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Faroe Island Salmon": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert-grade sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs a crisp white instead. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Filet Mignon": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Frangelico on another course instead.",
  "Frangelico|French Onion": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Frangelico on the steak course instead.",
  "Frangelico|Grilled Caesar": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register jars against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Gumbo": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Frangelico belongs on the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register doubles down on the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sweet liqueur and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no contrast left. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Frangelico on the steak course instead.",
  "Frangelico|House Wedge": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Frangelico belongs on the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Kansas City": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Frangelico for another course.",
  "Frangelico|Loaded Potato": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Lobster Mac": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Frangelico on the steak course instead.",
  "Frangelico|Market Fish": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Mocha Creme": "Mocha Creme: coffee-chocolate dessert meets Frangelico. Excellent; hazelnut-coffee-chocolate is a textbook Italian pairing — Frangelico brings Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Frangelico|Mushroom Bisque": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register overwhelms the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Mushrooms": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Frangelico lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Frangelico|Peanut Butter Brownie": "★ Frangelico's sweet hazelnut-cocoa character anchors against the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the cocoa underlines the chocolate. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Frangelico|Porterhouse": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Frangelico for another course.",
  "Frangelico|Prime Tartare": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register flattens the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Rainbow Trout": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Frangelico belongs on the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Roast Half Chicken": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register buries the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- sugar smothers the chicken's savory frame — wait for dessert. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- sugar on delicate fish is wrong direction — the plate needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Seafood Tower": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Frangelico on the steak course instead.",
  "Frangelico|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Frangelico. Avoid; hazelnut-liqueur wrong for scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Frangelico|Seasonal Soup": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Frangelico on the steak course instead.",
  "Frangelico|Seasonal Vegetables": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Frangelico belongs on the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Shrimp Bisque": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Frangelico lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Frangelico|Shrimp Cocktail": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Swordfish": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sugar dulls the crust's clean char instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Frangelico lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Frangelico|The Tomahawk": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Frangelico lands on another course — not here.",
  "Frangelico|Tomato Basil": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Frangelico belongs on the steak course.",
  "Frangelico|Truffle Fries": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register flattens the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- sugar on starch is dessert territory — wrong course for the supporting plate. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Frangelico lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Frangelico|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Frangelico on the steak course instead.",
  "Frangelico|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Asparagus": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's bold juniper-leather cassis leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Rutherford Cab — Bosché Vineyard since 1970, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the tobacco plays against the lactic richness. Works as Bosché Vineyard Cab for au gratin — 1970-legacy Rutherford on the cheesy side.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Beignets": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Freemark Abbey Bosché on the steak course instead.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Bone Marrow": "Rutherford Cab — Bosché Vineyard since 1970, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the graphite-tobacco plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak Bosché Vineyard Cab for marrow — 1970-legacy Rutherford on the rich starter. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Bone-In Filet": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's dense blackberry-and-cassis tannin leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; the pairing lands at register without strain.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Broccoli Cheddar": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's bold juniper-leather cassis sits beside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Broccolini": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's bold juniper-leather cassis sits beside the broccolini's tender green — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Burrata": "Rutherford Bosché Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm-tannin graphite-tobacco structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Freemark Abbey Bosché for the steak course.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Butternut Squash": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's bold juniper-leather cassis sits beside the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Carrot Cake": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Freemark Abbey Bosché on the steak course instead.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Cheesecake": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Freemark Abbey Bosché belongs on the steak course.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Chilean Seabass": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Freemark Abbey Bosché lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Chocolate Brownie": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Freemark Abbey Bosché belongs on the steak course.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Chocolate Cake": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Freemark Abbey Bosché on the steak course instead.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Clam Chowder": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's structured dark-fruit weight sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the structured body wraps the chowder, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Crab Cake": "Rutherford Bosché Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — firm-tannin blackcurrant-graphite structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Freemark Abbey Bosché for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Creamy Potato": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's bold juniper-leather cassis finds neutral with the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis cuts the cheese richness. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Freemark Abbey Bosché|Creme Brulee": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Freemark Abbey Bosché for the steak course.",
