  "French 75|Broccolini": "French 75's citrus-earth build sits beside the broccolini's tender green — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "French 75|Brussels and Belly": "French 75's citrus-earth build plays cleanly against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "French 75|Burrata": "Inverroche gin shaken with lemon and vanilla syrup, topped with Prosecco. Bubbles, vanilla, and lemon against the burrata's cream and the seasonal fruit on the plate. Refined cocktail route for a light starter. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "French 75|Butternut Squash": "French 75's citrus-earth build sits alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "French 75|Carrot Cake": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the French 75 for the steak course.",
  "French 75|Cheesecake": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the French 75 for the steak course.",
  "French 75|Chilean Seabass": "French 75 -- gin, lemon, Champagne top, celebratory cocktail, effervescent build. The bubble-and-citrus build brightens the seabass, and the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "French 75|Chocolate Brownie": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the French 75 for the steak course.",
  "French 75|Chocolate Cake": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the French 75 on the steak course instead.",
  "French 75|Clam Chowder": "French 75's citrus-earth build stays alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "French 75|Cowboy Ribeye": "Inverroche gin, lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top. The cocktail's bubbles and delicacy has no weight to meet the Cowboy's marbled fat. Never pair — route Cowboy to Bowdie's Old Fashioned, Espresso Old Fashioned, or a Napa Cab, and save the 75 for openers.",
  "French 75|Crab Cake": "Inverroche gin shaken with lemon and vanilla syrup, topped with Prosecco. The bubbles and citrus reset the palate after every rich bite; vanilla softens the jalapeño heat. Celebratory pour when the guest wants a cocktail with the cake. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "French 75|Creamed Spinach": "French 75's citrus-earth build plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
