  "French 75|Creme Brulee": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. French 75 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "French 75|Escargot": "Inverroche gin shaken with lemon and vanilla syrup, topped with Prosecco. Bubbles and lemon against our escargot butter's garlic weight; the vanilla rounds the edges. Ceremonial cocktail route for a French classic — acid and sparkle do the work. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "French 75|Faroe Island Salmon": "French 75's citrus-earth build matches the salmon's rich oily flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "French 75|Filet Mignon": "Inverroche gin, lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top. The cocktail's delicacy is wrong for any steak — bubbles have no job with grilled beef. Works only if guest insists on finishing the cocktail into the entrée; route beef pairing to Cab, Pinot, or whiskey cocktail.",
  "French 75|French Onion": "French 75's citrus-earth build holds with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "French 75|Grilled Caesar": "Inverroche gin, lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top. Citrus and bubbles cut anchovy-parmesan dressing and the fynbos gin plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong unconventional pairing — the cocktail's brightness handles the charred-salad profile well.",
