  "French 75|Lobster Mac": "French 75's citrus-earth build finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the oak frames the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "French 75|Market Fish": "French 75's citrus-earth build reads alongside the fish's clean flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "French 75|Mocha Creme": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a light cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the French 75 for the steak course.",
  "French 75|Mushroom Bisque": "French 75's citrus-earth build holds with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "French 75|Mushrooms": "French 75's citrus-earth build holds with the mushrooms' earthy umami — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the caramel meets the nut character. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "French 75|Peanut Butter Brownie": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the French 75 on the steak course instead.",
  "French 75|Porterhouse": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the French 75 for another course.",
  "French 75|Prime Tartare": "Inverroche gin, lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top. Bubbles and citrus cut the raw prime beef's iron richness; the fynbos gin adds aromatic layering to the tartare seasoning. Strong opener cocktail — unconventional for tartare but lightness makes room for the course.",
  "French 75|Rainbow Trout": "French 75's citrus-earth build holds the line with the trout's delicate flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "French 75|Roast Half Chicken": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bubble-and-citrus build brightens the roast chicken, and the gin botanicals frame the plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "French 75|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "French 75's citrus-earth build sits beside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "French 75|Salt-Cured Halibut": "French 75's citrus-earth build plays cleanly against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "French 75|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Inverroche gin shaken with lemon and vanilla syrup, topped with Prosecco. The bubbles and lemon against the escargot butter's garlic weight; light cocktail for the lightest side. Not the peak, but doesn't fight. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "French 75|Seafood Tower": "Inverroche gin, lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top. The Prosecco's bubbles and citrus-led gin are built for raw shellfish — oysters, shrimp, crab legs. Vanilla syrup softens what would be a sharper build. Peak cocktail for the tower's celebratory, raw-bar-centric indulgence.",
  "French 75|Seared Scallops": "Inverroche gin, lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top. The citrus-and-Prosecco profile meets scallops' clean sweet-seared character directly and the vanilla-softened gin doesn't crush the delicacy. Peak cocktail alternative to Champagne for scallops — shares the bubbles axis. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "French 75|Seasonal Soup": "French 75's citrus-earth build reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "French 75|Seasonal Vegetables": "French 75's citrus-earth build reads alongside the seasonal garden plate — the cocktail lift sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "French 75|Shrimp Bisque": "Inverroche gin, lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top. The bubbles-and-citrus cut the bisque's cream-and-shellfish richness and fynbos gin adds aromatic lift. Works as a bright opener alongside the bisque — though Sauvignon Blanc or Champagne peak slightly higher.",
  "French 75|Shrimp Cocktail": "Inverroche gin with lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top in a flute. Citrus and bubbles cut the chilled shrimp's sweet brine and lift the cocktail sauce's horseradish edge. Peak classic pairing — bubbles-with-shrimp is textbook and the gin adds aromatic depth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "French 75|Swordfish": "French 75's citrus-earth build finds register with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "French 75|The Tomahawk": "Champagne-topped gin cocktail in a flute against the biggest steak on the menu — zero overlap. The cocktail's delicacy is annihilated by the Tomahawk's marbled weight. Never pair — Tomahawk demands Cab, Barolo, or a spirit-forward bourbon cocktail.",
  "French 75|Tomato Basil": "French 75's citrus-earth build leans against the tomato basil's bright acid — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "French 75|Truffle Fries": "Inverroche gin shaken with lemon and vanilla syrup, topped with Prosecco. Bubbles and lemon against truffle earth feels wrong; the cocktail's brightness fights the fry sauce. Keep for shellfish starters. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "French 75|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "French 75 -- gin, lemon, Champagne top, celebratory cocktail, effervescent build. The bubble-and-citrus build brightens the tuna, and the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "French 75|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bubble-and-citrus build brightens the curry, and the gin botanicals frame the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "French Onion|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the rounded sweetness softens the bowl. Works; richer profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "French Onion|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the French onion, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "French Onion|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's bold cherry-coffee cassis holds with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee meets the caramel. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "French Onion|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "French Onion|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the malt-driven body wraps the French onion, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "French Onion|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the malt-and-oak threads the French onion, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; Fortuna handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "French Onion|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the aged-agave weight wraps the French onion, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; the 70th's clarity gets overwhelmed by the dish's density.",
  "French Onion|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's aged-agave character reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the French onion, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; the wine influence is unusual here but doesn't quite drive.",
  "French Onion|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "French Onion|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "French Onion|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the silky body carries the French onion, and the cherry-pepper note frames the bowl. Works; a measured close to the meal.",
  "French Onion|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness jars against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "French Onion|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "French Onion|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; KBS distinctiveness is slightly over-specified for the two-cut plate.",
  "French Onion|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon backbone carries the French onion, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; the pour's toasted warmth threads through the char without overpowering.",
