  "French Onion|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the French onion, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; boutique-craft doesn't drive.",
  "French Onion|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the French onion, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; Discovery complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "French Onion|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the oak depth lays under the French onion, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "French Onion|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the French onion, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; boutique at matched register.",
  "French Onion|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the rye-and-oak weight handles the French onion, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "French Onion|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the French onion, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the bowl. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "French Onion|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the restrained whisky body threads the French onion, and the precision rounds the bowl. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "French Onion|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Yellow Chartreuse on the steak course instead.",
  "Frias Block 5|Asparagus": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Frias Block 5 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Frias Block 5|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Frias Block 5|Beignets": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Frias Block 5|Bone-In Filet": "Frias Block 5: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge. The dark-fruit frame supports the filet, but the tannin rides harder than the lean profile demands. A strong match — the tannin and filet tenderness align cleanly. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Frias Block 5|Broccoli Cheddar": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Frias Block 5 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Broccolini": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the broccolini's tender green stalks -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Frias Block 5|Burrata": "Spring Mountain Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm iron-herbal body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackberry-plum. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Butternut Squash": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Carrot Cake": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Cheesecake": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Chilean Seabass": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Chocolate Brownie": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Chocolate Cake": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Frias Block 5 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Clam Chowder": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Frias Block 5 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Frias Block 5|Crab Cake": "Spring Mountain single-block Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm iron-herbal body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Frias Block 5 for the steak course where its Spring Mountain terroir shines.",
  "Frias Block 5|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Frias Block 5|Creamy Potato": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Creme Brulee": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Frias Block 5 on the steak course instead.",
  "Frias Block 5|Escargot": "Frias Block 5's bold herbal-umami cassis finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Frias Block 5|Faroe Island Salmon": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Filet Mignon": "Spring Mountain Cab — Frias Block 5, blackberry, dark plum, iron, savory herbal, firm tannin. The structured body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the iron-herbal echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong single-block mountain Cab for filet — Spring Mountain structure, though lighter Cabs peak higher on the delicate cut.",
  "Frias Block 5|French Onion": "Frias Block 5's bold herbal-umami cassis stays alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Frias Block 5|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The Caesar's anchovy-parmesan depth gives the bold red's tannin something real to work against. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Frias Block 5|Gumbo": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Frias Block 5 on the steak course instead.",
  "Frias Block 5|House Wedge": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "Frias Block 5|Kansas City": "Kansas City: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Frias Block 5|Loaded Potato": "Frias Block 5's bold herbal-umami cassis stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Frias Block 5|Lobster Mac": "Frias Block 5's bold herbal-umami cassis holds with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Frias Block 5|Market Fish": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Mocha Creme": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Frias Block 5 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Frias Block 5|Mushroom Bisque": "Frias Block 5's bold herbal-umami cassis leans against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Frias Block 5|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the side's mushroom richness pulls the bold red's body; tannin and oak settle into the umami without overshooting. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Frias Block 5|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Frias Block 5|Prime Tartare": "Frias Block 5's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin meets at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the concentrated dark-fruit weight handles the tartare, and the iron-mineral plays against the starter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Frias Block 5|Rainbow Trout": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Roast Half Chicken": "Frias Block 5's bold herbal-umami cassis reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Frias Block 5|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Frias Block 5 on the steak course instead.",
  "Frias Block 5|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Frias Block 5 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Seafood Tower": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Seared Scallops": "Frias Block 5 Spring Mountain Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — firm iron-herbal obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Frias for the steak course where its single-block character peaks.",
  "Frias Block 5|Seasonal Soup": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Seasonal Vegetables": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Frias Block 5 on the steak course instead.",
  "Frias Block 5|Shrimp Bisque": "Frias Block 5's bold herbal-umami cassis holds with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Frias Block 5|Shrimp Cocktail": "Frias Block 5 Spring Mountain Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — firm iron-herbal is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Frias for the steak course where its single-block character belongs.",
  "Frias Block 5|Swordfish": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Frias Block 5 on the steak course instead.",
  "Frias Block 5|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Frias Block 5|Tomato Basil": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Frias Block 5 on the steak course instead.",
  "Frias Block 5|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the mushroom's umami depth meets the bold red's structured tannin; dark fruit threads the truffle-and-earth without crowding. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Frias Block 5|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Frias Block 5|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Frias Block 5 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Asparagus": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo on the steak course instead.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, high acid. The high acid cuts gruyère-cream weight and the tar plays against the lactic richness. Strong classic Barolo for au gratin — Vajra prestige on the cheesy side.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Beignets": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Bone Marrow": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, high acid. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the tar plays against the smoky mineral depth. Works as classic Barolo for marrow — Vajra prestige on the rich starter.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Bone-In Filet": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's structured dark-fruit weight rests at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the dark-fruit body sits with the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; the pairing carries its register cleanly against the cut.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Broccoli Cheddar": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Broccolini": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Brussels and Belly": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, high acid. The firm tannin cuts pork-belly fat and the tar plays against charred brussels. Strong classic Barolo for Brussels and Belly — Vajra's exceptional range on the rich starter.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Burrata": "Classic Piedmont Barolo against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Vajra's firm-tannin tar-rose structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Vajra for aged beef courses.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Butternut Squash": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip flattens the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Carrot Cake": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Cheesecake": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Chilean Seabass": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo on the steak course instead.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Chocolate Brownie": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Chocolate Cake": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Clam Chowder": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip flattens the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Cowboy Ribeye": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, full body, high acid. The full body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the tar plays against beefy char. Strong classic Barolo for ribeye — Vajra's exceptional range on the bold cut.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Crab Cake": "Classic Piedmont Barolo against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Vajra's firm-tannin tar-rose structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Vajra for aged beef, filet, or the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Creamed Spinach": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, high acid. The high acid cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the rose petal plays against the spinach base. Strong classic Barolo for creamed spinach — Vajra's food-demanding acid on the rich side.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Creamy Potato": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Creme Brulee": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Escargot": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's powerful blackcurrant body reads register-aligned with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the powerful body carries the buttered escargot, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; the pairing carries its register cleanly against the cut.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Faroe Island Salmon": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Filet Mignon": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, classic tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, full body, high acid. The firm tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the rose petal plays against escargot-butter. Peak classic Barolo for filet — G.D. Vajra exceptional across range on the delicate cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|French Onion": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's sweet char-cherry tannin sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Grilled Caesar": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, high acid. The high acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the rose petal plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak classic Barolo for grilled Caesar — Vajra's food-demanding structure on the layered salad. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Gumbo": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Honey Roasted Carrots": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|House Wedge": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, high acid. The high acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dried cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as classic Barolo for the wedge — Vajra reframes the salad, though whites peak higher.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Kansas City": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, full body, high acid. The firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the tar plays against beefy char. Peak classic Barolo for the strip — Vajra's range includes Moscato on our Bubbles list. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Loaded Potato": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's sweet char-cherry tannin plays cleanly against the loaded potato's richness — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Lobster Mac": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's structured dark-fruit weight sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the structured body wraps the lobster mac, and the cassis frames the side. Works; the two sides share the meal without competing.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Market Fish": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Mocha Creme": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Mushroom Bisque": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's sweet char-cherry tannin stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Mushrooms": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, full body, high acid. The tar echoes roasted-mushroom glutamate and the dried cherry plays against the earth depth. Peak classic Barolo for mushrooms — earth-on-earth Barolo match. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Peanut Butter Brownie": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Porterhouse": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, full body, high acid. The firm tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the rose petal plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong classic Barolo for Porterhouse — Vajra exceptional on the dual-cut.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Prime Tartare": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's powerful blackcurrant body sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the structured body wraps the tartare, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Rainbow Trout": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Roast Half Chicken": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, high acid. The high acid plays against chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as classic Barolo for chicken across most seasonal preparations.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Salt-Cured Halibut": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Seafood Tower": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Seared Scallops": "Classic Piedmont Barolo against scallop sweetness — Vajra's firm-tannin tar-rose structure buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Vajra for aged beef where the classic Barolo character earns the bottle.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Seasonal Soup": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Seasonal Vegetables": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Shrimp Bisque": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's sweet char-cherry tannin finds neutral with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Shrimp Cocktail": "Classic Piedmont Barolo against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — firm-tannin Vajra and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Vajra for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Swordfish": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|The Tomahawk": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's sweet char-cherry tannin matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the sweet character meets the cap-fat at digestif register. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Tomato Basil": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo on the steak course instead.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Truffle Fries": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, high acid. The firm tannin cuts truffle-fry potato fat and the tar plays against the truffle-oil umami. Peak classic Barolo for truffle fries — tar-truffle echo on the umami hit. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Asparagus": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sparkling is opener territory — the side reads small under its lift. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a sparkling. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Piedmont sweet sparkling — peach, honey, floral. The sweetness is catastrophic against a 180°C 90-minute-baked manchego cream gratin; the dish has too much savory weight and cream body. Route Moscato to dessert where sugar makes sense. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Beignets": "Piedmont Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee, gentle effervescence. The bubbles mirror the choux steam-risen airiness and the peach-honey profile lifts powdered-sugar sweetness. Peak sparkling-dessert pairing; both beignet and Moscato share lightness as their defining trait. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Bone Marrow": "Piedmont sweet sparkling against roasted bone marrow — sweet-dessert bubbles against rich umami fat is the wrong axis for a savory starter. Never pair. Route Moscato to dessert; take bone marrow with structured reds like Scavino, Muga, or Opus One.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Bone-In Filet": "Sweet, effervescent — peach blossom, apricot, lychee. This is a dessert wine. Against a bone-in filet it reads sugary and thin. Fine to pour at the end; don't pair it with the course. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Broccoli Cheddar": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- this sparkling's acidity is wrong direction for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti on the steak course instead.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Broccolini": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- sparkling is opener territory — the side reads small under its lift. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Brussels and Belly": "Piedmont sweet sparkling — peach, honey, floral. The sweetness fights the brussels' bitterness and the cumin-paprika lardon rub; wrong wine on every axis. Route Moscato to dessert where it belongs. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Burrata": "Piedmont sweet sparkling — peach, honey, floral, low alcohol. The sweetness and fruit can work with the seasonal fruit on the plate, but it's dessert-adjacent. Position carefully — best for a table sharing the burrata after cocktails. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Butternut Squash": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- this sparkling's acidity is wrong direction for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Carrot Cake": "The Moscato's stone-fruit and honey notes echo the cake's brown sugar and warm spice, while the effervescence lifts through the dense cream cheese frosting so it reads as airy rather than thick. That interplay makes the spice linger longer on the finish than it would with a still wine. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Cheesecake": "Piedmont Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee, gentle sweetness, honey finish. The peach-and-honey profile cuts through dense cream cheese and the bubbles lift the graham-cracker crust. Peak dessert-sparkling for our water-bath cheesecake's density.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Chilean Seabass": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- this specific sparkling's register is wrong for the main — try a different brut or vintage. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a sparkling. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Chocolate Brownie": "Piedmont Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee, gentle sweetness. The bubbles cut fudge density and peach-honey softens cocoa bitterness. Strong alternative when guest wants sparkling rather than coffee or amaro; lighter register for the brownie.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Chocolate Cake": "Piedmont Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee, gentle sweetness. Bubbles cut cocoa density and peach softens bitter chocolate. Strong alternative when guest wants sparkling rather than coffee — but the cake is currently unavailable.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Clam Chowder": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- this sparkling's acidity is wrong direction for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a sparkling. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Cowboy Ribeye": "Piedmont sweet sparkling against a heavily marbled bone-in ribeye — dessert-level sugar is wildly wrong for rich fatted beef. Never pair. Route Moscato to dessert course (brownies, cheesecake, beignets); take Cowboy Ribeye with big Napa Cabs, Barolo, or Super Tuscans.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Crab Cake": "The natural sweetness of Dungeness or blue crab has an almost honeyed quality that the Moscato's peach and apricot tones lock onto directly, while the wine's carbonation lifts the fried crust off the palate so it doesn't accumulate. The result is that the crab's delicate sweetness dominates rather than the breading. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Creamed Spinach": "Piedmont sweet sparkling — peach, honey, floral. The sweetness fights the parmesan-garlic roux and the spinach's vegetal bite on every axis imaginable. Route Moscato to dessert where sugar makes sense — never pour on a savory cream side. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Creamy Potato": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- this sparkling's acidity is wrong direction for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a sparkling. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Creme Brulee": "Piedmont Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee, gentle effervescence, honeyed finish. The peach and honey echo the crème brûlée's vanilla custard, and the bubbles cut through the cream weight. Peak dessert-sparkling pairing for the most approachable dessert on the menu.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Escargot": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- this specific sparkling fights the starter — a different brut or rosé would carry it. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti on the steak course instead.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Faroe Island Salmon": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- this specific sparkling's register is wrong for the main — try a different brut or vintage. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a sparkling. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti on the steak course instead.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Filet Mignon": "Sweet, effervescent Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, and lychee. A dessert wine, not a steak wine. Fine as a pour to finish the meal; wrong alongside the filet. Redirect if the guest asks. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|French Onion": "Piedmont sweet sparkling against French Onion's long-cooked savory beef broth and gruyère crust — dessert-level sugar is the wrong axis for the hearty umami classic. Never pair. Route Moscato to dessert and take French Onion with Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, or a structured red.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Grilled Caesar": "Piedmont sweet sparkling — peach, honey, floral, low alcohol. The sweetness fights everything about the Caesar: anchovy, parmesan, cayenne, grilled char. Don't pour here; route Moscato to dessert where the sweetness belongs. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Gumbo": "Piedmont sweet sparkling against a dark-roux Cajun stew with andouille and seafood — dessert-sugar is wildly wrong for the savory-spicy classic. Never pair. Route Moscato to dessert and take gumbo with Rioja, CdP, Super Tuscan, or Zinfandel.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Honey Roasted Carrots": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sparkling is opener territory — the side reads small under its lift. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|House Wedge": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets the Moscato's peach-and-honey sweetness with delicate effervescence. Moscato's sweetness on a savory plate — wrong register. Works as a dessert transition with a savory bridge.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Kansas City": "Sweet, aromatic, effervescent Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee with a honeyed finish. This is a dessert pour, not a steak pour. The KC's bold, firm beef reads flat against the Moscato's sweetness. Serve at the end of the meal, not with the course. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Loaded Potato": "Piedmont sweet sparkling against a loaded baked-potato soup with bacon, cheddar, scallion — dessert-sugar is the wrong axis for the hearty loaded bowl. Never pair. Route Moscato to dessert and take loaded potato with Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, or Champagne.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Lobster Mac": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- sparkling is opener territory — the side reads small under its lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a sparkling. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Market Fish": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- this specific sparkling's register is wrong for the main — try a different brut or vintage. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Mocha Creme": "Piedmont Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee, gentle sweetness. Bubbles cut the mocha crème's cream-and-cocoa density. Strong alternative when guest wants sparkling rather than coffee; currently unavailable on the menu.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Mushroom Bisque": "Piedmont sweet sparkling against mushroom bisque's savory-umami cream base — dessert-sugar is the wrong axis for a mushroom soup. Never pair. Route Moscato to dessert and take the bisque with Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, or Chablis.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Mushrooms": "Piedmont sweet sparkling — peach, honey, floral. Sweetness fights every umami aspect of the mushrooms; the earthy depth gets fought by every note in the wine. Route Moscato to dessert. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Piedmont Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee, gentle sweetness, effervescent. The bubbles cut peanut butter density and peach softens the fudge. Strong sparkling alternative when guest wants lighter than coffee or amaro. Flag peanut allergies before pouring.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Porterhouse": "Sweet, effervescent Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee with a honeyed finish. A dessert wine, not a steak wine. The porterhouse's 40oz of marbled beef flattens the aromatics and drowns the delicate sweetness. Save for the end of the meal, not alongside the course. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Prime Tartare": "This is a contrast pairing — the raw beef's iron-rich umami and sharp shallot are deliberately offset by the wine's soft sweetness and bubbles, which act as a palate cleanser between each bite rather than a flavor echo. The tartare's bold, mineral edge becomes more pronounced and defined because the Moscato keeps the fat from coating the tongue. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Rainbow Trout": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the trout's delicate flesh -- this specific sparkling's register is wrong for the main — try a different brut or vintage. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Roast Half Chicken": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- this specific sparkling's register is wrong for the main — try a different brut or vintage. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a sparkling. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- this sparkling's acidity is wrong direction for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Salt-Cured Halibut": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- this specific sparkling's register is wrong for the main — try a different brut or vintage. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti on the steak course instead.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- sparkling is opener territory — the side reads small under its lift. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a sparkling. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Seafood Tower": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- this specific sparkling fights the starter — a different brut or rosé would carry it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a sparkling. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Seared Scallops": "The Maillard crust on a scallop produces caramelized, slightly sweet compounds that echo the Moscato's stone fruit character, while the wine's acidity keeps the natural oceanic brine from turning cloying. Together they read as a single continuous sweetness — one savory, one fruity — with a clean, bright finish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Seasonal Soup": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- this sparkling's acidity is wrong direction for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Seasonal Vegetables": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- sparkling is opener territory — the side reads small under its lift. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Shrimp Bisque": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- this sparkling's acidity is wrong direction for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a sparkling. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti on the steak course instead.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Shrimp Cocktail": "Shrimp Cocktail: this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness meets the Moscato's peach-and-honey sweetness with delicate effervescence. Moscato's sweetness on a savory plate — wrong register. Strong when the dish plays sweet — not the default.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Swordfish": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- this specific sparkling's register is wrong for the main — try a different brut or vintage. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a sparkling. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti belongs on the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|The Tomahawk": "Piedmont sweet sparkling against Bowdie's 35-oz theatrical ribeye — dessert-level sugar and low alcohol have no weight to meet the smoky-beefy signature cut. Never pair. Route Moscato to dessert course; take the Tomahawk with Caymus, Opus One, or Scavino Barolo.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Tomato Basil": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- this sparkling's acidity is wrong direction for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti belongs on the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Truffle Fries": "Piedmont sweet sparkling — peach, honey, floral. Sweet wine against truffle and garlic-Dijon fry sauce is wrong on every axis. Route Moscato to dessert; it has no business on fries. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- this specific sparkling's register is wrong for the main — try a different brut or vintage. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti's peach-and-honey frizzante sweetness mis-pairs with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- this sparkling's acidity is wrong direction for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a sparkling. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti for the steak course.",
  "G4 Blanco|Asparagus": "G4 Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the unaged body brightens the asparagus, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "G4 Blanco|Au Gratin Potatoes": "G4 Blanco's clean silver register sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the blanco-tequila body matches the au gratin, and the agave-citrus thread frames the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "G4 Blanco|Beignets": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a light spirit. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. G4 Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G4 Blanco|Bone Marrow": "G4 Blanco with bone marrow — craft blanco against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms blanco.",
  "G4 Blanco|Bone-In Filet": "G4 Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift finds neutral with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the blanco-tequila body matches the bone-enhanced filet, and the bright blanco register underlines the cut. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "G4 Blanco|Broccoli Cheddar": "G4 Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the silver-tequila register lifts the broccoli cheddar, and the bright blanco register underlines the bowl. Works; ceremonial pairing depends on preparation.",
  "G4 Blanco|Broccolini": "G4 Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the unaged body brightens the broccolini, and the bright blanco register underlines the side. Works; premium craft composes cleanly.",
  "G4 Blanco|Brussels and Belly": "G4 Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge leans against Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the pork-belly plate, and the bright blanco register underlines the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "G4 Blanco|Burrata": "G4 Blanco with burrata — 80-proof craft blanco against fresh mozzarella. The agave-forward Camarena character lifts the burrata with craft distinctiveness. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "G4 Blanco|Butternut Squash": "G4 Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the unaged body brightens the squash bisque, and the bright blanco register underlines the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "G4 Blanco|Carrot Cake": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the G4 Blanco for the steak course.",
  "G4 Blanco|Cheesecake": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. G4 Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G4 Blanco|Chilean Seabass": "G4 Blanco's crisp agave-mineral character holds the line with the seabass's buttery flesh — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "G4 Blanco|Chocolate Brownie": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light spirit. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the G4 Blanco for the steak course.",
  "G4 Blanco|Chocolate Cake": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a light spirit. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the G4 Blanco for the steak course.",
  "G4 Blanco|Clam Chowder": "G4 Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the silver-tequila register lifts the chowder, and the green-agave edge brightens the bowl. Works; refined craft composes cleanly.",
  "G4 Blanco|Cowboy Ribeye": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the G4 Blanco for another course.",
  "G4 Blanco|Crab Cake": "G4 Blanco with crab cake — 80-proof from Felipe Camarena's G4 distillery. Craft, agave-forward, distillery uses rainwater in production. Distinct from G4 High Proof (104-proof reposado) by both proof and category; G4 Blanco is the standard-proof sibling. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "G4 Blanco|Creamed Spinach": "G4 Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift finds neutral with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the unaged blanco body cuts the creamed spinach, and the agave-citrus thread frames the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "G4 Blanco|Creamy Potato": "G4 Blanco's clean silver register reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the potato soup, and the bright blanco register underlines the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "G4 Blanco|Creme Brulee": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. G4 Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G4 Blanco|Escargot": "G4 Blanco with escargot — Camarena craft against garlic-butter snails. Strong; agave-forward cuts the garlic-butter with craft distinctiveness.",
  "G4 Blanco|Faroe Island Salmon": "G4 Blanco with the salmon — craft 80-proof against salmon's richness. Strong; agave-forward character frames the fish at the craft register.",
  "G4 Blanco|Filet Mignon": "G4 Blanco with the filet — craft 80-proof against the 10oz cut. Works; blanco isn't the natural filet call.",
  "G4 Blanco|French Onion": "G4 Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the unaged body brightens the French onion, and the bright blanco register underlines the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "G4 Blanco|Grilled Caesar": "G4 Blanco with the grilled Caesar — Felipe Camarena's craft blanco against charred romaine. Strong; the agave-forward character earns the Caesar at craft register.",
  "G4 Blanco|Gumbo": "G4 Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the silver-tequila register lifts the gumbo, and the agave-citrus thread frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "G4 Blanco|Honey Roasted Carrots": "G4 Blanco's clean silver register sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the unaged body brightens the honey carrots, and the agave-citrus thread frames the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "G4 Blanco|House Wedge": "G4 Blanco with the House Wedge — craft 80-proof against iceberg and bleu. The agave-forward character cuts the bleu at the craft blanco register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "G4 Blanco|Kansas City": "G4 Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift leans against the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the silver-spirit register lifts the strip steak, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "G4 Blanco|Loaded Potato": "G4 Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the loaded potato soup, and the unaged-cane note plays against the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "G4 Blanco|Lobster Mac": "G4 Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the blanco-tequila body matches the lobster mac, and the unaged-cane note plays against the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "G4 Blanco|Market Fish": "G4 Blanco with the market fish — craft blanco against catch of the day. Works; craft agave lifts the fish depending on preparation.",
  "G4 Blanco|Mocha Creme": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- light spirit can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a richer pour like an aged rum or tawny. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the G4 Blanco for the steak course.",
  "G4 Blanco|Mushroom Bisque": "G4 Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the silver-spirit register lifts the mushroom bisque, and the bright blanco register underlines the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "G4 Blanco|Mushrooms": "G4 Blanco's clean silver register leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami weight: the blanco-tequila body matches the mushrooms, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "G4 Blanco|Peanut Butter Brownie": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a light spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. G4 Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "G4 Blanco|Porterhouse": "G4 Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the unaged body brightens the dual-cut steak, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "G4 Blanco|Prime Tartare": "G4 Blanco with the tartare — Felipe Camarena's craft blanco against raw beef. The agave-forward character is a distinctive counterpoint to tartare. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "G4 Blanco|Rainbow Trout": "G4 Blanco's crisp agave-mineral character matches the trout's delicate flesh — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "G4 Blanco|Roast Half Chicken": "G4 Blanco with the roast half chicken — Camarena craft against roasted chicken. Works; agave-forward composes cleanly.",
  "G4 Blanco|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "G4 Blanco's clean silver register leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the silver-spirit register lifts the chickpea soup, and the bright blanco register underlines the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "G4 Blanco|Salt-Cured Halibut": "G4 Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register reads register-aligned with the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the blanco-tequila body matches the halibut, and the unaged-cane note plays against the plate. Strong; clean additive-free cuts the garlic-butter at craft register.",
  "G4 Blanco|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "G4 Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the unaged blanco body cuts the garlic spinach, and the bright blanco register underlines the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "G4 Blanco|Seafood Tower": "G4 Blanco with the seafood tower — craft 80-proof across raw oysters, shrimp, lobster. Strong; Camarena-family craft provides a distinctive through-line.",
  "G4 Blanco|Seared Scallops": "G4 Blanco with seared scallops — Felipe Camarena's craft blanco against caramelized scallop. The agave-forward character meets the sear with G4 distillery distinctiveness. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "G4 Blanco|Seasonal Soup": "G4 Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the blanco-tequila body matches the seasonal soup, and the agave-citrus thread frames the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "G4 Blanco|Seasonal Vegetables": "G4 Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the silver-spirit register lifts the seasonal vegetables, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "G4 Blanco|Shrimp Bisque": "G4 Blanco with shrimp bisque — craft blanco against cream-based seafood. Strong; agave-forward character sits with the bisque at craft register.",
  "G4 Blanco|Shrimp Cocktail": "G4 Blanco with shrimp cocktail — 80-proof G4 against cold shrimp. The craft agave cuts through cocktail sauce with Camarena-family character. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "G4 Blanco|Swordfish": "G4 Blanco's crisp agave-mineral character finds register with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "G4 Blanco|The Tomahawk": "G4 Blanco with the Tomahawk — 80-proof craft blanco against bone-in ribeye. Avoid; bourbon or scotch territory.",
  "G4 Blanco|Tomato Basil": "G4 Blanco's clean silver register leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the unaged body brightens the tomato basil, and the bright blanco register underlines the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "G4 Blanco|Truffle Fries": "G4 Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge leans against the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the truffle fries, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "G4 Blanco|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "G4 Blanco's crisp agave-mineral character sits with the tuna's seared rare crust — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "G4 Blanco|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "G4 Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the curry, and the unaged-cane note plays against the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
