  "Garavogue Irish Whiskey|Seasonal Vegetables": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the side. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Garavogue Irish Whiskey|Shrimp Bisque": "Garavogue with shrimp bisque — craft Irish against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Garavogue's small-batch character rides the bisque at craft-Irish register.",
  "Garavogue Irish Whiskey|Shrimp Cocktail": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness crowds out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Garavogue Irish Whiskey on the steak course instead.",
  "Garavogue Irish Whiskey|Swordfish": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Garavogue Irish Whiskey lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Garavogue Irish Whiskey|The Tomahawk": "Garavogue with the Tomahawk — craft Irish against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; Irish whiskey is too light for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Garavogue Irish Whiskey|Tomato Basil": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the malt-grain blend handles the tomato basil, and the soft malt rounds the bowl. Works; retailer-pick wasted on salad.",
  "Garavogue Irish Whiskey|Truffle Fries": "Garavogue with truffle fries — Sligo-river craft Irish against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Garavogue's craft character meets the earthy-rich at distinctive register.",
  "Garavogue Irish Whiskey|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Garavogue Irish Whiskey lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Garavogue Irish Whiskey|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the malt-grain blend handles the curry, and the grain register softens the bowl. Works; boutique-sourced doesn't drive.",
  "George T. Stagg|Asparagus": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power smothers the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the side reads small under oak-and-vanilla density. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the George T. Stagg on the steak course instead.",
  "George T. Stagg|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: cheese-and-cream potatoes meet BTAC collector. Works; save the BTAC bottle for the protein course — the side doesn't deserve the pour.",
  "George T. Stagg|Beignets": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon depth settles into the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Caribbean Cask frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "George T. Stagg|Bone Marrow": "GTS with bone marrow — BTAC holy-grail collector against umami fat. Excellent; GTS's 15+yr cask-strength is one of the few spirits on the menu that meets marrow as an equal — the featured BTAC-marrow pairing at collector register. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "George T. Stagg|Bone-In Filet": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength barrel-proof-leather character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "George T. Stagg|Broccoli Cheddar": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "George T. Stagg|Broccolini": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the spirit needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the George T. Stagg for the steak course.",
  "George T. Stagg|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet 130+ proof BTAC. Strong; GTS meets the glaze at collector register, though Stagg Jr. handles this pairing more economically.",
  "George T. Stagg|Burrata": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bourbon weight buries the milky cheese plate's delicate frame. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the George T. Stagg for the steak course.",
  "George T. Stagg|Butternut Squash": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the squash bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; high proof over-specified.",
  "George T. Stagg|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet BTAC. Works; GTS is over-specified for dessert — save the collector bottle for the ribeye/Tomahawk.",
  "George T. Stagg|Cheesecake": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the cask weight has no foil against a delicate dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. George T. Stagg lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "George T. Stagg|Chilean Seabass": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the George T. Stagg for the steak course.",
  "George T. Stagg|Chocolate Brownie": "GTS with the chocolate brownie — BTAC ultra-premium against cocoa. Strong; GTS meets cocoa at collector register; Stagg Jr. delivers this pairing at accessible BT register.",
  "George T. Stagg|Chocolate Cake": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the brown-spirit warmth threads the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; bold intensity slightly over-specified.",
  "George T. Stagg|Clam Chowder": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon body sits with the chowder, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for a side — save the bottle for the main course.",
  "George T. Stagg|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets BTAC ultra-premium collector, 130+ proof, 15+ years aged, holy-grail BTAC release. One of the rare pairings where GTS actually earns its pour — 26oz cap-fat is heavy enough that GTS's 15+yr cask-strength gravity gets the meeting it deserves. Reverence, not just dinner. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "George T. Stagg|Crab Cake": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power overshadows the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. George T. Stagg lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "George T. Stagg|Creamed Spinach": "GTS with creamed spinach — BTAC holy-grail against dairy-rich side. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "George T. Stagg|Creamy Potato": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "George T. Stagg|Creme Brulee": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the distinctive character is underused by the salad's simplicity.",
  "George T. Stagg|Escargot": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength barrel-proof-leather character finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "George T. Stagg|Faroe Island Salmon": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the George T. Stagg on the steak course instead.",
  "George T. Stagg|Filet Mignon": "GTS with the filet — 130+ proof BTAC against tenderloin. Works; GTS's intensity is over-specified for filet's finesse — save the collector bottle for the Tomahawk/Cowboy Ribeye where it actually earns its pour.",
  "George T. Stagg|French Onion": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the caramel-spice register matches the French onion, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "George T. Stagg|Grilled Caesar": "GTS with the grilled Caesar — BTAC 15+yr against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; GTS is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "George T. Stagg|Gumbo": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the brown-spirit warmth threads the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; the bourbon's character is distinctive but the pairing isn't a standout.",
  "George T. Stagg|Honey Roasted Carrots": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power smothers the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- the side reads small under oak-and-vanilla density. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. George T. Stagg lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "George T. Stagg|House Wedge": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the brown-spirit warmth threads the wedge, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the intensity overpowers the salad.",
  "George T. Stagg|Kansas City": "GTS with the KC — BTAC 15+yr cask-strength against an 18oz strip. Excellent; GTS's collector-register cask-strength handles strip marbling with ultra-premium gravitas — where Stagg Jr. brings BT cask-strength, GTS closes with BTAC aging and holy-grail collector status.",
  "George T. Stagg|Loaded Potato": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; HH 20 frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "George T. Stagg|Lobster Mac": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the brown-spirit warmth threads the lobster mac, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "George T. Stagg|Market Fish": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the George T. Stagg for the steak course.",
  "George T. Stagg|Mocha Creme": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; Voyage 23 handles the richness at ocean-finish register.",
  "George T. Stagg|Mushroom Bisque": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the caramel-spice register matches the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "George T. Stagg|Mushrooms": "GTS with mushrooms — BTAC 15+yr cask-strength against earthy umami. Strong; GTS threads mushrooms at collector-BTAC register.",
  "George T. Stagg|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet BTAC collector. Works; GTS is over-specified for dessert — save the collector bottle for the ribeye/Tomahawk.",
  "George T. Stagg|Porterhouse": "GTS with the porterhouse — BTAC holy-grail against strip-and-filet. Strong; GTS frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "George T. Stagg|Prime Tartare": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength barrel-proof-leather character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "George T. Stagg|Rainbow Trout": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the George T. Stagg for the steak course.",
  "George T. Stagg|Roast Half Chicken": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power swallows the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the George T. Stagg on the steak course instead.",
  "George T. Stagg|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak depth lays under the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; private-selection wasted on the salad.",
  "George T. Stagg|Salt-Cured Halibut": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the George T. Stagg for the steak course.",
  "George T. Stagg|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power smothers the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side reads small under oak-and-vanilla density. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. George T. Stagg lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "George T. Stagg|Seafood Tower": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the George T. Stagg for the steak course.",
  "George T. Stagg|Seared Scallops": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power smothers the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the George T. Stagg for the steak course.",
  "George T. Stagg|Seasonal Soup": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "George T. Stagg|Seasonal Vegetables": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the spirit needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the George T. Stagg for the steak course.",
  "George T. Stagg|Shrimp Bisque": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power leans against the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the brown-spirit warmth threads the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "George T. Stagg|Shrimp Cocktail": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power crowds out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the George T. Stagg on the steak course instead.",
  "George T. Stagg|Swordfish": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the George T. Stagg for the steak course.",
  "George T. Stagg|The Tomahawk": "★ George T. Stagg's cask-strength barrel-proof-leather character is the lock for the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat. Gold standard; recommend without hesitation.",
  "George T. Stagg|Tomato Basil": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the BTAC cask-strength drives the tomato basil, and the concentrated oak underlines the bowl. Works; BH Red Wine Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "George T. Stagg|Truffle Fries": "GTS with truffle fries — BTAC ultra-premium against truffle-parmesan. Strong; GTS meets the earthy-rich at collector register; consider whether the side deserves a BTAC pour.",
  "George T. Stagg|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the George T. Stagg for the steak course.",
  "George T. Stagg|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon body sits with the curry, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; AE meets the char at wine-finish register.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Asparagus": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Oakville Cab — blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak, firm tannin. The dense body cuts au gratin's cheese-and-cream weight and the graphite-oak plays against the golden crust. Works as Oakville Cab for au gratin — Napa golden-strip structure on the decadent side.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Beignets": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone Marrow": "Oakville Cabernet — firm structure, classic Napa fruit profile. Cuts the marrow fat with the dust-and-dark-fruit character Oakville is known for. Good mid-tier Cab when the guest wants serious without Caymus-level weight. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone-In Filet": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-oak cassis reads alongside the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the Cab structure frames the tenderloin, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccoli Cheddar": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccolini": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Brussels and Belly": "Oakville Cab — blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak, firm tannin. The dense body handles pork-belly fat and the graphite-oak plays against charred brussels. Strong Oakville Cab for Brussels and Belly — Napa's golden-strip structure cuts the rich starter.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Burrata": "Oakville Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm graphite-oak body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackcurrant-cherry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Ghost Block for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Butternut Squash": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Carrot Cake": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Cheesecake": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Chilean Seabass": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Brownie": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Cake": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Clam Chowder": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Cowboy Ribeye": "Ghost Block — Oakville Cab with dense blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak, firm tannin. Oakville's golden strip produces Napa's most consistent Cab. The structured body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the graphite-oak meets rendered fat. Peak Oakville Cab for Cowboy — consistent structure at fair price. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Crab Cake": "Oakville Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm-tannin graphite-oak body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Ghost Block for the steak course where its Napa golden-strip character shines.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamy Potato": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Creme Brulee": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Escargot": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-oak cassis reads alongside the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Faroe Island Salmon": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Filet Mignon": "Oakville Cab — blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak, firm tannin. The structured body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the graphite-oak echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong Oakville Cab for filet — Napa's golden-strip character, though lighter Cabs like Jordan peak higher on the delicate cut.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|French Onion": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-oak cassis holds with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Grilled Caesar": "Ghost Block — Oakville Cab with blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak. The dense body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the graphite-oak plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as Oakville Cab for grilled Caesar — Napa's golden-strip structure on the classic layered salad.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Gumbo": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|House Wedge": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Kansas City": "Oakville Cab — blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak, firm tannin. The dense structured body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the graphite-oak plays against the beefy char. Peak Oakville Cab for Kansas City — Napa's most consistent strip-producing AVA delivers on the classic cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Loaded Potato": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-oak cassis sits beside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Lobster Mac": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-oak cassis sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the opulent body matches the indulgent side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Market Fish": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Mocha Creme": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushroom Bisque": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-oak cassis plays cleanly against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushrooms": "Oakville Cab — blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak, firm tannin. The graphite-oak meshes with mushroom umami and the dense structure frames the sauté earth. Strong Oakville Cab for mushrooms — Napa's golden-strip complexity on the earthy side.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Porterhouse": "Ghost Block — Oakville Cab, blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak, firm tannin. The dense body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the graphite-oak plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong Oakville Cab for Porterhouse — Napa's golden-strip structure on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Prime Tartare": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-oak cassis frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Rainbow Trout": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Roast Half Chicken": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-oak cassis finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Seafood Tower": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Seared Scallops": "Ghost Block Oakville Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — firm graphite-oak obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Ghost Block for the steak course where its Oakville character peaks.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Soup": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Vegetables": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Bisque": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's dense blackberry-and-cassis tannin sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the structured body wraps the shrimp bisque, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Cocktail": "Ghost Block Oakville Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — firm graphite-oak is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Ghost Block for the steak course where its Napa-strip structure belongs.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Swordfish": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|The Tomahawk": "Ghost Block — Oakville Cab with dense blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak, firm tannin. The structured body meets Tomahawk fat and char and the graphite-oak frames the theatrical bone-in cut. Peak Oakville Cab for Tomahawk — Napa's golden strip structure stands up to the biggest marbled steak. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Tomato Basil": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Truffle Fries": "Ghost Block — Oakville Cab with dense blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak. The structured body frames truffle-fry earth and the graphite-oak cuts the crisp oil-and-salt. Peak Oakville Cab for truffle fries — Napa's golden-strip structure meets the decadent side. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Asparagus": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Ghost Block Zinfandel on the steak course instead.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts gruyere-cream richness and the full body handles dairy. Works for au gratin — a Chardonnay's cream-on-cream fits softer.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Beignets": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Ghost Block Zinfandel on the steak course instead.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Bone Marrow": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts roasted-marrow unctuousness and the cracked pepper echoes toasted-bread char. Peak California-Zin for bone marrow — Napa bold-on-rich harmony. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Bone-In Filet": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's bold pepper-spice cassis finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the velvety body lifts the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Broccoli Cheddar": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Broccolini": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Brussels and Belly": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The cracked pepper cuts pork-belly fat and the jammy fruit plays with brussels-char bitterness. Peak California-Zin for brussels-and-belly — bold Napa handles fat-and-char. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Burrata": "Never pair Ghost Block with burrata — jammy fruit, cracked pepper, and full body clash with fresh cream and the preserves bury tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Butternut Squash": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift flattens the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Carrot Cake": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Cheesecake": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Chilean Seabass": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Chocolate Brownie": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Chocolate Cake": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Clam Chowder": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Cowboy Ribeye": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin, bold California. The jammy fruit wraps around Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the cracked pepper echoes grilled char. Peak California-Zin for ribeye — bold Napa on the marbled cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Crab Cake": "Never pair Ghost Block with crab cake — jammy fruit, cracked pepper, and full body bury delicate lump crab and the medium tannin fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Creamed Spinach": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The full body handles dairy-heavy cream and the cracked pepper cuts spinach-iron. Strong California-Zin for creamed spinach — bold Napa on the rich side.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Creamy Potato": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Creme Brulee": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Escargot": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's bold pepper-spice cassis holds steady against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Faroe Island Salmon": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Ghost Block Zinfandel on the steak course instead.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Filet Mignon": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the cracked pepper plays with escargot-butter. Strong California-Zin for filet — jammy full body reads heavy but the cut handles it.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|French Onion": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's bold pepper-spice cassis finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Grilled Caesar": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body. The cracked pepper plays with anchovy-lemon dressing, but the jammy fruit reads heavy for the salad's lightness. Works for grilled caesar — a Rhône garrigue fits the anchovy harder.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Gumbo": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The cracked pepper meets gumbo's Cajun spice directly and the jammy fruit plays against the dark-roux weight. Strong Zinfandel for gumbo — pepper-on-spice on the stew classic.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Ghost Block Zinfandel lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|House Wedge": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The cracked pepper plays with bacon-fat char and the medium tannin holds against blue-cheese funk. Works for wedge — cold iceberg mutes Ghost Block's bold profile, so lighter reds lift higher.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Kansas City": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The cracked pepper echoes Kansas City's lean-bold strip char and the jammy fruit plays with grilled beef. Peak California-Zin for the strip — bold Napa on the American cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Loaded Potato": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's bold pepper-spice cassis holds with the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Lobster Mac": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's bold pepper-spice cassis finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Ghost Block Zinfandel|Market Fish": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Ghost Block Zinfandel lands on the steak course — not here.",
