  "Glenlivet 12|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Glenlivet 12 with the au gratin — softer Speyside against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Strong; Glenlivet handles the richness at benchmark register.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Beignets": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the malt-and-oak threads the beignets, and the malt edge frames the dessert. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Bone Marrow": "Glenlivet 12 with bone marrow — softer Speyside against umami fat. Works; Glenlivet is too light to cut marrow the way Fernet Branca does — Fernet is the featured marrow pairing.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Bone-In Filet": "Glenlivet 12 with the bone-in filet — honey-floral Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Glenlivet's softer profile frames the cut's marrow-adjacent depth at benchmark register.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Broccoli Cheddar": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the malt-driven body wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; Boss Hog's allocation story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Broccolini": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the scotch backbone matches the broccolini, and the smoke threads the side. Works; Portwood is over-specified for sides.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Brussels and Belly": "Glenlivet 12 with Brussels + Belly — honey-pineapple Speyside against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; Glenlivet's honey meets the glaze at benchmark register.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Burrata": "Glenlivet 12 with burrata — softer Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Butternut Squash": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the scotch backbone matches the squash bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; gentle match at matched registers.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Carrot Cake": "Glenlivet 12 with carrot cake — honey-pineapple Speyside against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Glenlivet's tropical-honey notes meet the cake's spice with benchmark finesse.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Cheesecake": "Glenlivet 12 with cheesecake — honey-floral Speyside against custard. Strong; Glenlivet rides the cheesecake with pineapple-hint distinction; Balvenie 12's vanilla is still the featured Speyside-cheesecake match.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Chilean Seabass": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Glenlivet 12 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Chocolate Brownie": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the scotch backbone matches the brownie, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; the 10-year depth gives the dessert substantial premium frame.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Chocolate Cake": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the chocolate cake, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Clam Chowder": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the Highland register carries the chowder, and the oak softens the cream. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Cowboy Ribeye": "Glenlivet 12 with the cowboy ribeye — softer Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Glenlivet's honey-floral character frames the ribeye at benchmark register.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Crab Cake": "Glenlivet 12 with crab cake — Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: dairy-rich side meets \"The Glenlivet house style.\" Strong; Glenlivet's honey-floral rides the cream at benchmark register.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Creamy Potato": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Remus 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Creme Brulee": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the malt-and-oak threads the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the boldness pushes slightly past what the soup asks for.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Escargot": "Glenlivet 12 -- entry Speyside, pear, honey. The bourbon-cask pear lifts the buttered escargot, and the honey rounds the starter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Faroe Island Salmon": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Glenlivet 12 for the steak course.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets \"The Glenlivet house style,\" honey-floral Speyside benchmark, softer entry than Glenfiddich 12. Glenlivet's honey-floral character frames tenderloin with more softness and pineapple-hint than Fiddich's greener apple/pear — the benchmark pick for guests who want Speyside without the crisp green-fruit edge. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Glenlivet 12|French Onion": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the scotch depth settles into the French onion, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; distinctive character is worth mentioning tableside, even if the pairing isn't marquee.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Grilled Caesar": "Glenlivet 12 with the grilled Caesar — softer Speyside against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Glenlivet's honey-floral meets the char with benchmark softness — where Fiddich is crisp-apple, Glenlivet is honey-soft.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Gumbo": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the gumbo, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Highland register carries the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "Glenlivet 12|House Wedge": "Glenlivet 12 with the House Wedge — honey-floral Speyside against iceberg and bleu. Works; Glenlivet's softness is a functional wedge pairing.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Kansas City": "Glenlivet 12 with the KC — honey-floral Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Glenlivet handles the strip at benchmark register; Speyside 18 or Balvenie 16 are the featured Speyside-KC choices.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Loaded Potato": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the malt-driven body wraps the loaded potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Lobster Mac": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the lobster mac, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Market Fish": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Glenlivet 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Mocha Creme": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the mocha creme, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Mushroom Bisque": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the scotch depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the oak threads the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Mushrooms": "Glenlivet 12 with mushrooms — honey-floral Speyside against earthy umami. Excellent; Glenlivet's softer character marries mushroom earthiness with the pineapple-hint providing distinctive lift.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet softer Speyside. Works; Glenlivet handles the density at benchmark register; Balvenie 12's vanilla is the better match.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Porterhouse": "Glenlivet 12 with the porterhouse — honey-pineapple Speyside against strip-and-filet. Strong; Glenlivet's softer profile frames the two-cut plate with benchmark elegance.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Prime Tartare": "Glenlivet 12's crisp floral-honey character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Rainbow Trout": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Glenlivet 12 for the steak course.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Roast Half Chicken": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the scotch depth settles into the roast chicken, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the plate. Works; the unique maturation story adds interest to a gentle dessert match.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the malt-and-oak threads the chickpea soup, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Glenlivet 12 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the malt-and-oak threads the garlic spinach, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Seafood Tower": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Glenlivet 12 for the steak course.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Seared Scallops": "Glenlivet 12 with seared scallops — honey-floral Speyside against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Seasonal Soup": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the malt-and-oak threads the seasonal soup, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; distinctive finish over-specified.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Seasonal Vegetables": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the scotch backbone matches the seasonal vegetables, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the side. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Shrimp Bisque": "Shrimp Bisque: cream-shrimp soup meets \"The Glenlivet house style.\" Works; Glenlivet rides the bisque at benchmark register.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Shrimp Cocktail": "Glenlivet 12 with shrimp cocktail — benchmark Speyside against cold shrimp. Avoid; Scotch wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Swordfish": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Glenlivet 12 for the steak course.",
  "Glenlivet 12|The Tomahawk": "Glenlivet 12 -- entry Speyside, pear, honey. The bourbon-cask pear lifts the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; premium aged depth sits with richness.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Tomato Basil": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the malt-and-oak threads the tomato basil, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; boutique character slightly over-specified.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Truffle Fries": "Glenlivet 12 with truffle fries — \"The Glenlivet house style\" against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Glenlivet's honey-floral frames the earthy-rich with benchmark elegance.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Glenlivet 12 on the steak course instead.",
  "Glenlivet 12|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Glenlivet 12's entry Speyside character finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the malt-and-oak threads the curry, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Asparagus": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the Highland register carries the asparagus, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; Caribbean Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Glenmorangie 10 with the au gratin — tallest-stills Highland against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Glenmorangie 10's vanilla-honey handles the richness at entry-Highland register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Beignets": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the malt-and-oak threads the beignets, and the heather brightens the dessert. Works; the richer pour finds more with the dessert than Original brings.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Bone Marrow": "Glenmorangie 10 with bone marrow — tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask against umami fat. Strong; Glenmorangie 10's honeysuckle-vanilla sits alongside marrow at entry-Highland register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Bone-In Filet": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the scotch backbone matches the bone-enhanced filet, and the smoke threads the cut. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Broccoli Cheddar": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the broccoli cheddar, and the oak softens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Broccolini": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the broccolini, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the side. Works; the match is gentle and approachable.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Brussels and Belly": "Glenmorangie 10 with Brussels + Belly — honey-floral Highland against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; Glenmorangie 10's honeysuckle meets the glaze at entry-Highland register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Burrata": "Glenmorangie 10 with burrata — Highland bourbon-cask against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Butternut Squash": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the squash bisque, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the complex character gives the dessert a refined frame.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Carrot Cake": "Glenmorangie 10 with carrot cake — Highland bourbon-cask entry against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; Glenmorangie 10's vanilla-honey meets the cake at entry register; Dalmore 12's sherry-finish is the featured Highland-carrot-cake match.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Cheesecake": "Glenmorangie 10 with cheesecake — bourbon-cask Highland entry against custard. Works; Glenmorangie 10's honey-vanilla is a soft dessert finisher at entry register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Chilean Seabass": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Glenmorangie 10 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Chocolate Brownie": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brownie, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; the sweetness is a touch much for the salad's simplicity.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Chocolate Cake": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the malt-driven body wraps the chocolate cake, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; Discovery complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Clam Chowder": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the scotch depth settles into the chowder, and the oak softens the cream. Works; the pour's brightness is a gentle counterpoint to the dessert's richness.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Cowboy Ribeye": "Glenmorangie 10 with the cowboy ribeye — bourbon-cask Highland against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Glenmorangie 10's lighter profile frames the ribeye at entry-Highland register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Crab Cake": "Glenmorangie 10 with crab cake — Highland bourbon-cask against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Creamed Spinach": "Glenmorangie 10 with creamed spinach — Highland bourbon-cask against dairy-rich side. Strong; Glenmorangie 10's honey-floral rides the cream at entry-Highland register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Creamy Potato": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; EHT Full Proof handles the richness at historic Kentucky register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Creme Brulee": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the malt-and-oak threads the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the richer pour finds more with the dessert than Original brings.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Escargot": "Glenmorangie 10's crisp Highland-orange-peel character finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Faroe Island Salmon": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Glenmorangie 10 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Filet Mignon": "Glenmorangie 10 with the filet — Highland entry benchmark, tallest stills in Scotland, bourbon-cask matured with honeysuckle-peach-vanilla honey-floral profile. Excellent; Glenmorangie 10's delicate-refined bourbon-cask approach frames tenderloin with Highland-floral lightness — the democratic Highland-filet pairing, where Dalmore brings sherry-finish and Oban brings coastal.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|French Onion": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the Highland register carries the French onion, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; aged premium slightly over-specified.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Grilled Caesar": "Glenmorangie 10 with the grilled Caesar — honey-floral Highland bourbon-cask against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Glenmorangie 10's honeysuckle-peach-vanilla is the featured Highland-Caesar pairing — the bourbon-cask floral-vanilla lifts char-and-anchovy where Dalmore's sherry is over-specified.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Gumbo": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the gumbo, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Highland register carries the honey carrots, and the oak threads the caramelized sugar. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|House Wedge": "Glenmorangie 10 with the House Wedge — honey-floral Highland against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Glenmorangie 10's honeysuckle-peach threads the wedge at entry-Highland register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Kansas City": "Glenmorangie 10 with the KC — tallest-stills Highland entry against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Glenmorangie 10's bourbon-cask vanilla-and-honey handles the strip at entry-Highland register; Dalmore's sherry-finish is the featured Highland-KC match.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Loaded Potato": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon-cask vanilla settles into the loaded potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; EHT Full Proof is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Lobster Mac": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the malt-driven body wraps the lobster mac, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Market Fish": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Glenmorangie 10 for the steak course.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Mocha Creme": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the scotch backbone matches the mocha creme, and the oak softens the cream. Works; KBS distinctiveness is slightly over-specified for the two-cut plate.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Mushroom Bisque": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the scotch depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Works; high proof over-specified for the soup.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Mushrooms": "Glenmorangie 10 with mushrooms — Highland bourbon-cask entry against earthy umami. Excellent; Glenmorangie 10's honeysuckle-peach profile threads mushroom earthiness with light Highland character.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon-cask vanilla settles into the PB brownie, and the honey-floral lifts the dessert. Works; bonded weight pushes past the soup's register slightly.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Porterhouse": "Glenmorangie 10 with the porterhouse — honey-floral Highland against strip-and-filet. Strong; Glenmorangie 10's vanilla-peach character frames the two-cut plate at entry register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Prime Tartare": "Glenmorangie 10 -- Highland entry, tallest stills, honeysuckle-vanilla. The bourbon-cask vanilla settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; clean match at the register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Rainbow Trout": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Glenmorangie 10 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Roast Half Chicken": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon-cask vanilla settles into the roast chicken, and the honey-floral lifts the plate. Works; the story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the scotch depth settles into the chickpea soup, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Glenmorangie 10 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the Highland register carries the garlic spinach, and the heather brightens the side. Works; Sinatra Select handles the richness at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Seafood Tower": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Glenmorangie 10 on the steak course instead.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Seared Scallops": "Glenmorangie 10 with seared scallops — honey-floral Highland against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Seasonal Soup": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the scotch depth settles into the seasonal soup, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Seasonal Vegetables": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the scotch backbone matches the seasonal vegetables, and the smoke threads the side. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for a side — save the bottle for the main course.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Shrimp Bisque": "Glenmorangie 10 with shrimp bisque — bourbon-cask Highland against cream-shrimp soup. Works; Glenmorangie 10's honey-floral rides the bisque at entry-Highland register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Shrimp Cocktail": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character overshadows the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Glenmorangie 10 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Swordfish": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Glenmorangie 10 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|The Tomahawk": "Glenmorangie 10 -- Highland entry, tallest stills, honeysuckle-vanilla. The scotch depth settles into the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the 103-proof sits with the richness at the bolder register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Tomato Basil": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the malt-and-oak threads the tomato basil, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; Voyage 23 handles the richness at ocean-finish register.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Truffle Fries": "Glenmorangie 10 with truffle fries — tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Glenmorangie 10's honeysuckle-peach-vanilla profile is the featured Highland-truffle pairing — the bourbon-cask vanilla marries truffle-parmesan earthiness cleanly.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Glenmorangie 10 on the steak course instead.",
  "Glenmorangie 10|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the Highland register carries the curry, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; pour slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Asparagus": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the malt-and-oak threads the asparagus, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Glenmorangie 18 with the au gratin — 18yr bourbon + sherry-finish Highland against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Glenmorangie 18 handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Beignets": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the Highland register carries the beignets, and the malt edge frames the dessert. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Bone Marrow": "Glenmorangie 18 with bone marrow — aged Highland with Oloroso finish against umami fat. Strong; Glenmorangie 18's Oloroso-cask depth sits alongside marrow at premium register; Oban 14's maritime character still leads the Highland-marrow pairings.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Bone-In Filet": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the malt-driven body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the smoke threads the cut. Works; aging wasted on the salad.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Broccoli Cheddar": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the malt-driven body wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the oak threads the cream. Works; IW Harper 15 handles dessert density at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Broccolini": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the broccolini, and the honey rounds the side. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for a side — save the bottle for the main course.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet aged Glenmorangie with Oloroso finish. Works; Glenmorangie 18's Oloroso dried-fruit meets the glaze at premium register.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Burrata": "Glenmorangie 18 with burrata — aged bourbon + Oloroso Highland against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Butternut Squash": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the scotch backbone matches the squash bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Caribbean Cask rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Carrot Cake": "Glenmorangie 18 with carrot cake — 18yr Highland with Oloroso sherry finish against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Glenmorangie 18's Oloroso-cask dried-fruit and spice marry the cake's raisins and cinnamon — a featured aged-Highland carrot-cake pairing alongside Dalmore 12's sherry-finish approach.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Cheesecake": "Glenmorangie 18 with cheesecake — 18yr Highland with Oloroso finish against custard. Strong; Glenmorangie 18's aged-sherry character threads the cheesecake at premium register.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Chilean Seabass": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Glenmorangie 18 for the steak course.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Chocolate Brownie": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brownie, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; fruit-forward interesting but doesn't drive.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Chocolate Cake": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the chocolate cake, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the dessert. Works; Glenmorangie 18 handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Clam Chowder": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the scotch depth settles into the chowder, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Cowboy Ribeye": "Glenmorangie 18 with the cowboy ribeye — aged bourbon + Oloroso Highland against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Glenmorangie 18 handles the ribeye with aged depth; Oban 18's maritime + sherry-finish is the featured Highland-ribeye match.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Crab Cake": "Glenmorangie 18 with crab cake — aged Highland against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Creamed Spinach": "Glenmorangie 18 with creamed spinach — 18yr bourbon + sherry-finish Highland against dairy-rich side. Strong; Glenmorangie 18's aged character rides the cream at premium register.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Creamy Potato": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the scotch backbone matches the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; complex profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Creme Brulee": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the malt-and-oak threads the brulee, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Escargot": "Glenmorangie 18 -- aged Highland, Oloroso finish, dried-fruit depth. The Oloroso-cask depth wraps the buttered escargot, and the aged-Highland weight frames the starter. Strong; multi-distillery complexity sits with richness at craft-blender register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Faroe Island Salmon": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Glenmorangie 18 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets 18yr Highland, bourbon-cask-matured with 3-year Oloroso sherry finish, layered dried-fruit depth beyond the 10. Glenmorangie 18's aged + sherry-finished character frames tenderloin with Highland-premium depth — where Glenmorangie 10 is honey-floral-bourbon entry, the 18 adds Oloroso dried-fruit complexity that engages richer cuts. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|French Onion": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the Highland register carries the French onion, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Grilled Caesar": "Glenmorangie 18 with the grilled Caesar — aged Glenmorangie with Oloroso-finish against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Glenmorangie 18's deeper character meets the char with aged-premium Highland distinction.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Gumbo": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the malt-driven body wraps the gumbo, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; boutique-sourced doesn't drive.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the scotch depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|House Wedge": "House Wedge: iceberg and bleu meet aged Glenmorangie. Strong; Glenmorangie 18's aged depth threads the wedge at premium register.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Kansas City": "Glenmorangie 18 with the KC — 18yr bourbon + Oloroso-finish Highland against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Glenmorangie 18's aged + sherry-finish gives it the backbone the 10 reaches for but can't close — the featured Highland-KC match in the aged batch, parallel to Dalmore 12's sherry-finish approach.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Loaded Potato": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the malt-driven body wraps the loaded potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; boutique character gives the dessert a craft frame.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Lobster Mac": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the malt-driven body wraps the lobster mac, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the toast adds a warmth the dessert can accept.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Market Fish": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Glenmorangie 18 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Mocha Creme": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the mocha creme, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Mushroom Bisque": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the malt-and-oak threads the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the umami. Works; accessible gentle match.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Mushrooms": "Glenmorangie 18 with mushrooms — aged bourbon + Oloroso Highland against earthy umami. Excellent; Glenmorangie 18's layered character threads mushroom earthiness with aged-premium depth.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the malt-and-oak threads the PB brownie, and the heather brightens the dessert. Works; milestone-collector wasted on salad.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Porterhouse": "Glenmorangie 18 with the porterhouse — 18yr Glenmorangie with Oloroso finish against strip-and-filet. Strong; Glenmorangie 18's aged depth frames the two-cut plate at premium register.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Prime Tartare": "Glenmorangie 18 -- aged Highland, Oloroso finish, dried-fruit depth. The Oloroso-cask depth wraps the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the fruit-forward character cuts the bleu with small-batch refinement; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Rainbow Trout": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Glenmorangie 18 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Roast Half Chicken": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the Highland register carries the roast chicken, and the malt edge frames the plate. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the Highland register carries the chickpea soup, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Glenmorangie 18 on the steak course instead.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the scotch depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; neutral, not elevating.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Seafood Tower": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Glenmorangie 18 for the steak course.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Seared Scallops": "Glenmorangie 18 with seared scallops — 18yr Highland against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Seasonal Soup": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the malt-and-oak threads the seasonal soup, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Seasonal Vegetables": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the seasonal vegetables, and the honey rounds the side. Works; pour is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Shrimp Bisque": "Glenmorangie 18 with shrimp bisque — aged Highland against cream-shrimp soup. Works; Glenmorangie 18 rides the bisque at premium register.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Shrimp Cocktail": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish crowds out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Glenmorangie 18 for the steak course.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Swordfish": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Glenmorangie 18 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|The Tomahawk": "Glenmorangie 18's extra-mature-Highland character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Tomato Basil": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish reads alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the malt-and-oak threads the tomato basil, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; GTS is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Truffle Fries": "Glenmorangie 18 with truffle fries — 18yr Glenmorangie against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Glenmorangie 18's aged bourbon-plus-sherry depth meets the earthy-rich at premium register; the 10's lighter approach is entry-level, the 18 closes the deal.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Glenmorangie 18 for the steak course.",
  "Glenmorangie 18|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the Highland register carries the curry, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; the complex profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Asparagus": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Beignets": "10-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's entry-point, dried apricot, walnut, toffee, gentle spice. The toffee clings to hot choux-fried dough and the gentle spice lifts powdered-sugar simplicity. Strong Tawny for beignets — Taylor Fladgate's cleaner fruit sits neater on the light airy bite.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Bone Marrow": "Never pair Graham's 10 Year Tawny with bone marrow — sweet oxidative port drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and the toffee sugar clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée for marrow. Tawny belongs at dessert, not the start of the meal.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Bone-In Filet": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for another course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Broccoli Cheddar": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Broccolini": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Brussels and Belly": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Burrata": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Graham's 10 Year Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Butternut Squash": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness doubles down on the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Carrot Cake": "10-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's entry-point, dried apricot, walnut, toffee, gentle spice, smooth finish. The gentle spice echoes allspice-and-cinnamon directly and the toffee plays with cream-cheese frosting's vanilla-bean paste. Peak Tawny for carrot cake — spice-on-spice harmony. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Cheesecake": "10-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's entry-point, dried apricot, walnut, toffee, gentle spice, smooth finish. The dried apricot lifts cream-cheese density and the walnut plays with graham-cracker brown-sugar crust. Peak Tawny for cheesecake — nutty-sweet match on the water-bath baked dessert. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Chilean Seabass": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Chocolate Brownie": "10-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's entry-point, dried apricot, walnut, toffee, gentle spice, smooth finish. The toffee wraps around fudge-brownie cocoa depth and the walnut plays with whipped-chocolate frosting. Peak Tawny for chocolate brownie — Tawny smooths the cocoa intensity. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Chocolate Cake": "10-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's entry-point, dried apricot, walnut, toffee, gentle spice. The toffee plays with fudgy chocolate-cake crumb and the walnut bridges buttermilk-dairy depth. Strong Tawny for chocolate cake — Vintage Port's structure handles cocoa-intensity harder.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Clam Chowder": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Cowboy Ribeye": "Never pair Graham's 10 Year Tawny with Cowboy Ribeye — sweet oxidative port and grilled-beef char fight each other, the toffee residual sugar muddles marbled-ribeye savory. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. Save the Tawny for after dessert.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Crab Cake": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Creamed Spinach": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Creamy Potato": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Creme Brulee": "10-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's entry-point, dried apricot, walnut, toffee, gentle spice, smooth finish. The toffee echoes the torched-sugar crust directly and the walnut plays with vanilla-custard depth. Peak Tawny for creme brulee — caramelized-on-caramelized textbook match. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Escargot": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Graham's 10 Year Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Faroe Island Salmon": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Filet Mignon": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet wine smothers the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Graham's 10 Year Tawny on another course instead.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|French Onion": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Grilled Caesar": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness jars against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- sweet wine on cold lettuce refuses the salad's clean register. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Graham's 10 Year Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Gumbo": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- honeyed sugar on savory broth jars — wrong direction for the course. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|House Wedge": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Graham's 10 Year Tawny lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Kansas City": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Graham's 10 Year Tawny lands on another course — not here.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Loaded Potato": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Lobster Mac": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness doubles down on the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Graham's 10 Year Tawny lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Market Fish": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the wine's sweetness buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Mocha Creme": "10-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's entry-point, dried apricot, walnut, toffee, gentle spice. The toffee plays with espresso-yolk custard depth and the walnut echoes coffee-roast bitterness. Strong Tawny for mocha creme — Vintage Port's chocolate intensity edges higher on the espresso pour.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Mushroom Bisque": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Mushrooms": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Peanut Butter Brownie": "10-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's entry-point, dried apricot, walnut, toffee, gentle spice. The walnut echoes peanut-butter-frosting nuttiness and the toffee bridges fudge-brownie brown-sugar depth. Peak Tawny for peanut butter brownie — nut-and-caramel match on the dense base. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Porterhouse": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- dessert wine on prime cut is wrong-course entirely — save it for after. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Graham's 10 Year Tawny lands on another course — not here.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Prime Tartare": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Rainbow Trout": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the wine's sweetness buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Roast Half Chicken": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness buries the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- dessert wine on chicken is wrong course — sugar smothers the herbed-skin frame. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Graham's 10 Year Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Graham's 10 Year Tawny lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- dessert wine on delicate fish is wrong-course energy — needs a dry white, not honeyed sugar. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- sweet wine on green vegetable is wrong direction — needs crisp white. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Seafood Tower": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Seared Scallops": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Seasonal Soup": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Seasonal Vegetables": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Shrimp Bisque": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness doubles down on the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Shrimp Cocktail": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Swordfish": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|The Tomahawk": "Never pair Graham's 10 Year Tawny with Tomahawk — sweet oxidative port buries the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and the residual sugar clashes with smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. Tawny is a finishing pour, not a steak wine.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Tomato Basil": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Truffle Fries": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Graham's 10 Year Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 10 Year Tawny|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Graham's 10 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Asparagus": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sweet wine on green vegetable is wrong direction — needs crisp white. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Graham's 20 Year Tawny lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Graham's 20 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Beignets": "20-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's step-up, concentrated dried fruit, roasted hazelnut, orange peel, silky finish. The silky finish clings to hot choux-fried dough and the orange peel lifts powdered-sugar. Strong step-up Tawny for beignets — twenty-year complexity is over-spent on the light airy bite.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Bone Marrow": "Never pair Graham's 20 Year Tawny with bone marrow — sweet oxidative tawny drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and twenty-year concentration flattens beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. Tawny belongs at dessert.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Bone-In Filet": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Graham's 20 Year Tawny for another course.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Broccoli Cheddar": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth doubles down on the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Graham's 20 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Broccolini": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Graham's 20 Year Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Brussels and Belly": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth doubles down on Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- sweet wine and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no foil. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Graham's 20 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Burrata": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Graham's 20 Year Tawny lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Butternut Squash": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth doubles down on the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Graham's 20 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Carrot Cake": "20-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's step-up, concentrated dried fruit, roasted hazelnut, orange peel, silky long finish. The orange peel echoes allspice-cinnamon-brightness and the roasted hazelnut plays with cream-cheese frosting. Peak step-up Tawny for carrot cake — twenty-year depth on the spiced bake. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Cheesecake": "20-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's step-up, concentrated dried fruit, roasted hazelnut, orange peel, silky long finish. The concentrated dried fruit lifts cream-cheese density and the roasted hazelnut plays with graham-cracker brown-sugar crust. Peak step-up Tawny for cheesecake — complexity meets dense water-bath bake. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Chilean Seabass": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Graham's 20 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Chocolate Brownie": "20-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's step-up, concentrated dried fruit, roasted hazelnut, orange peel, silky long finish. The concentrated dried fruit wraps around fudge-brownie cocoa and the roasted hazelnut plays with whipped-chocolate frosting. Peak step-up Tawny for chocolate brownie — complexity handles the cocoa intensity. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Chocolate Cake": "20-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's step-up, concentrated dried fruit, roasted hazelnut, orange peel, silky long finish. The concentrated dried fruit wraps around fudgy chocolate crumb and the roasted hazelnut plays with buttermilk-dairy depth. Peak step-up Tawny for chocolate cake — twenty-year complexity on cocoa. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Clam Chowder": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth doubles down on the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Graham's 20 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Cowboy Ribeye": "Never pair Graham's 20 Year Tawny with Cowboy Ribeye — twenty years of cask oxidation plus grilled-beef char collapses the wine's complexity and the residual sugar muddles marbled-ribeye savory. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. Save the Tawny for after.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Crab Cake": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Graham's 20 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Creamed Spinach": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Graham's 20 Year Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Creamy Potato": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth doubles down on the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Graham's 20 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Graham's 20 Year Tawny|Creme Brulee": "20-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's step-up, concentrated dried fruit, roasted hazelnut, orange peel, silky long finish. The roasted hazelnut echoes torched-sugar crust caramelization and the orange peel lifts vanilla-custard. Peak step-up Tawny for creme brulee — twenty years of cask evolution on the classic match. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
