  "Gran Patron Platinum|Mushrooms": "Gran Patron Platinum's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register reads alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami weight: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the mushrooms, and the agave-citrus thread frames the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Gran Patron Platinum's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the PB brownie, and the unaged-cane note plays against the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Porterhouse": "Gran Patron Platinum's agave-citrus character lifts the porterhouse's two-cut character — the light body is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Prime Tartare": "Gran Patron Platinum with the tartare — triple-distilled Patron against raw beef. The smooth concentrated agave is a refined counterpoint at the ultra-premium tier. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Rainbow Trout": "Gran Patron Platinum's green-agave-and-cane lift sits alongside the trout's delicate flesh: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the trout, and the unaged-cane note plays against the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Roast Half Chicken": "Gran Patron Platinum with the roast half chicken — triple-distilled Patron against roasted chicken. Works; refined character is slightly over-specified for the dish.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Gran Patron Platinum's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the unaged body brightens the chickpea soup, and the bright blanco register underlines the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Gran Patron Platinum's unaged silver-spirit body leans against the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the blanco-tequila body matches the halibut, and the bright blanco register underlines the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Gran Patron Platinum's unaged silver-spirit body reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the silver-spirit register lifts the garlic spinach, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Seafood Tower": "Gran Patron Platinum with the seafood tower — ultra-premium Patron across raw oysters, shrimp, and lobster. The triple-distilled smoothness provides a refined through-line for the whole tower. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Seared Scallops": "Gran Patron Platinum with seared scallops — triple-distilled premium Patron against caramelized scallop. The smooth concentrated agave frames the sear at the ultra-premium register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Seasonal Soup": "Gran Patron Platinum's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the seasonal soup, and the green-agave edge brightens the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Seasonal Vegetables": "Gran Patron Platinum's green-agave-and-cane lift reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the blanco-tequila body matches the seasonal vegetables, and the unaged-cane note plays against the side. Works; premium craft composes cleanly.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Shrimp Bisque": "Gran Patron Platinum with shrimp bisque — premium Patron against cream-based seafood. Strong; triple-distilled smoothness sits with the bisque's richness.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Shrimp Cocktail": "Gran Patron Platinum with shrimp cocktail — premium Patron against cold shrimp. The triple-distilled smoothness threads through cocktail sauce with luxury refinement. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Swordfish": "Gran Patron Platinum's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register sits alongside the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the silver-spirit register lifts the swordfish, and the unaged-cane note plays against the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|The Tomahawk": "Gran Patron Platinum's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Gran Patron Platinum lands on another course — not here.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Tomato Basil": "Gran Patron Platinum's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the unaged body brightens the tomato basil, and the green-agave edge brightens the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Truffle Fries": "Gran Patron Platinum's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register finds neutral with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the truffle fries, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Gran Patron Platinum's unaged silver-spirit body sits alongside the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the blanco-tequila body matches the tuna, and the green-agave edge brightens the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Gran Patron Platinum|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Gran Patron Platinum's green-agave-and-cane lift finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the blanco-tequila body matches the curry, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Grand Marnier|Asparagus": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Grand Marnier lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Grand Marnier|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Beignets": "Grand Marnier alongside beignets — orange peel and cognac warmth against warm fried dough and powdered sugar. A New Orleans-register pairing that sells itself; the citrus cuts the fat and the cognac depth anchors the sweetness. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Grand Marnier|Bone Marrow": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Grand Marnier belongs on the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Bone-In Filet": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Grand Marnier lands on another course — not here.",
  "Grand Marnier|Broccoli Cheddar": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Broccolini": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Brussels and Belly": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character doubles down on Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- sweet liqueur and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no contrast left. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Burrata": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur and fresh dairy compound the richness without contrast — heavy too early. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Grand Marnier belongs on the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Butternut Squash": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Grand Marnier lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Grand Marnier|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: the carrot cake's warm-spice, cream-cheese indulgence meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Grand Marnier|Cheesecake": "Grand Marnier: this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness meets the cheesecake's dense, tangy dairy richness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Works as an indulgence.",
  "Grand Marnier|Chilean Seabass": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Grand Marnier|Chocolate Cake": "Chocolate Cake: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grand Marnier|Clam Chowder": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Grand Marnier on the steak course instead.",
  "Grand Marnier|Cowboy Ribeye": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Grand Marnier lands on another course — not here.",
  "Grand Marnier|Crab Cake": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character smothers the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sugar buries the delicate shellfish without any acid to balance. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Grand Marnier lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Grand Marnier|Creamed Spinach": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Grand Marnier belongs on the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Creamy Potato": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Creme Brulee": "Creme Brulee: this delicate custard's creamy, vanilla sweetness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Grand Marnier|Escargot": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character buries the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- sweet liqueur smothers the herb-bright opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Faroe Island Salmon": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Grand Marnier on the steak course instead.",
  "Grand Marnier|Filet Mignon": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the liqueur's sugar buries the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Grand Marnier belongs on another course.",
  "Grand Marnier|French Onion": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Grilled Caesar": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Grand Marnier belongs on the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Gumbo": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Grand Marnier on the steak course instead.",
  "Grand Marnier|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|House Wedge": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Grand Marnier on the steak course instead.",
  "Grand Marnier|Kansas City": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Grand Marnier belongs on another course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Loaded Potato": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Lobster Mac": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Grand Marnier lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Grand Marnier|Market Fish": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Mocha Creme": "Mocha Creme: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grand Marnier|Mushroom Bisque": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Mushrooms": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Works as an indulgence.",
  "Grand Marnier|Porterhouse": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Grand Marnier on another course instead.",
  "Grand Marnier|Prime Tartare": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character flattens the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Grand Marnier lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Grand Marnier|Rainbow Trout": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- sugar on delicate fish is wrong direction — the plate needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Roast Half Chicken": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Grand Marnier lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Grand Marnier|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Seafood Tower": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Seared Scallops": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Seasonal Soup": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Seasonal Vegetables": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Shrimp Bisque": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Grand Marnier belongs on the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Shrimp Cocktail": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Grand Marnier lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Grand Marnier|Swordfish": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|The Tomahawk": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Grand Marnier for another course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Tomato Basil": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Truffle Fries": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character flattens the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- sugar on starch is dessert territory — wrong course for the supporting plate. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Grand Marnier for the steak course.",
  "Grand Marnier|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Grand Marnier on the steak course instead.",
  "Grand Marnier|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Grand Marnier lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Asparagus": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals sits alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Beignets": "Gray Whale with beignets — California 6-botanical craft against fried dough. Works; Gray Whale is over-specified for casual dessert; Empress 1908's rose and Aviation's soft lavender are better dessert matches.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Bone Marrow": "Gray Whale with bone marrow — ocean-botanical California gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Gray Whale Gin for another course.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals sits beside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Broccolini": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals sits alongside the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals reads alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Burrata": "Gray Whale with burrata — California 6-botanical craft (Sonoma mint + Temecula limes + more) against fresh dairy. Excellent; Gray Whale's mint-and-citrus California terroir brightens cream at ocean-conservation register.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Butternut Squash": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals reads alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Carrot Cake": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Gray Whale Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Cheesecake": "Gray Whale with cheesecake — California ocean-conservation gin against custard. Works; Gray Whale is over-specified for dessert — save the ocean-botanicals for seafood.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals matches the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Gray Whale Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Gray Whale Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Clam Chowder": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Gray Whale with the cowboy ribeye — California 6-botanical craft against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Crab Cake": "Gray Whale with crab cake — California gin with 6 California-sourced ocean-and-coast botanicals: Mendocino kombu (sea kelp), Temecula limes, Big Sur juniper, Central Valley almonds, Sonoma mint. Excellent; Gray Whale's ocean-conservation-mission with actual sea-kelp botanical is the thematic-perfect craft-gin-crab pairing — ocean gin meets ocean seafood with story-driven precision.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Creamy Potato": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals leans against the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Creme Brulee": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Gray Whale Gin for the steak course.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Escargot": "Gray Whale with escargot — Mendocino-kombu California craft against garlic-butter snails. Strong; Gray Whale's kombu-umami cuts butter with thematic-distinct character — more complex than Aviation's soft juniper, less classic than Mahón's Mediterranean.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Gray Whale with the salmon — California ocean-conservation gin against oily salmon. Excellent; Gray Whale's kombu-and-ocean-botanical profile is a thematic-perfect fish pairing — ocean gin meets ocean fish.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Filet Mignon": "Gray Whale with the filet — California ocean-botanical gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|French Onion": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Gray Whale with the grilled Caesar — California 6-botanical craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Gray Whale's California terroir meets the char; Bluecoat's 4-citrus cuts sharper.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Gumbo": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals plays cleanly against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals sits beside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|House Wedge": "Gray Whale with the House Wedge — California 6-botanical craft against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Gray Whale's California terroir meets the bleu at ocean-conservation register.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Kansas City": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Gray Whale Gin for another course.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Loaded Potato": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Lobster Mac": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals sits beside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Market Fish": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals lifts the fish's clean flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Mocha Creme": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Gray Whale Gin for the steak course.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Mushrooms": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Gray Whale Gin for the steak course.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Porterhouse": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Gray Whale Gin for another course.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Prime Tartare": "Gray Whale with the tartare — California ocean-botanical gin against raw beef. Strong; Gray Whale's kombu-umami-adjacent profile supports tartare's minerality; Monkey 47's 47-botanical complexity is still the featured craft-gin-tartare match.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Gray Whale Gin's coriander-and-juniper register leans against the trout's delicate flesh: the floral edge frames the trout, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Sapphire's softness meets the bleu at accessible register.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Gray Whale with the chicken — California 6-botanical craft against roasted poultry. Strong; Gray Whale's California-terroir profile supports chicken at ocean-conservation register.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals plays cleanly against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals finds register with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals sits alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Seafood Tower": "Gray Whale with the seafood tower — California ocean-botanical gin with Mendocino kombu against the raw bar. Excellent; Gray Whale is the featured Smooth/Citrus Craft Gin-tower pairing — the kombu-and-California-botanical profile is the most thematically right craft gin for the raw bar, parallel to Botanist's Islay approach for Botanical-Forward.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Seared Scallops": "Gray Whale with seared scallops — California ocean-conservation gin against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Gray Whale's Mendocino-kombu-and-Big-Sur-juniper frames scallops with Pacific-coast-specific terroir.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals stays alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Gray Whale Gin's bold citrus-herbal botanicals sits beside the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Gray Whale with shrimp bisque — California ocean-botanical gin against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Gray Whale's kombu-adjacent profile cuts the bisque with thematic resonance — ocean gin, seafood soup.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Gray Whale with shrimp cocktail — ocean-botanical California gin against cold shrimp. Excellent; Gray Whale's 6-California-botanical profile elevates the classic cold app with ocean-conservation-mission distinction.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|Swordfish": "Gray Whale Gin's herbal-botanical edge meets at register with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the juniper-led botanicals carry the swordfish, and the coriander-and-cardamom edge frames the plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Gray Whale Gin|The Tomahawk": "Gray Whale with the Tomahawk — California ocean-craft against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
