  "Grey Goose Vodka|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Grey Goose Vodka's crisp French-wheat-soft neutrality matches the tuna's seared rare crust — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Grey Goose Vodka|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Grey Goose Vodka's crisp French-wheat-soft neutrality reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Grilled Caesar|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with the grilled Caesar — spicy-sweet Barton against charred romaine. Works; the high-rye character doesn't quite drive the Caesar at 93.7 proof.",
  "Grilled Caesar|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine against sweet-wheat. Works; the bourbon's character is distinctive but the pairing isn't a standout.",
  "Grilled Caesar|1881 Napa Valley": "Napa Cab — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, smooth medium tannin. The approachable body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mocha plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as entry Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — unconventional step-up pour on the classic layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|2 James Gin": "2 James with the grilled Caesar — Michigan local craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; 2 James meets the char with American-juniper character; Tanq 10's citrus is still the sharper gin-Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the grilled Caesar — rare release against charred romaine. Works; boutique-craft doesn't drive.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance sits alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the bourbon-plus-sherry depth wraps the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino with the grilled Caesar — filtered cristalino against charred romaine. Strong; the aged-but-clear character earns the Caesar with distinctive craft depth.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado with the grilled Caesar — long-aged reposado against charred romaine. Strong; the rich oak earns the Caesar where standard reposados only work.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, vanilla oak, smooth balanced. The oak weight handles anchovy-parmesan dressing and the apple-pear plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as accessible California Chard for grilled Caesar — approachable weight on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Meritage — cassis, plum, cedar, dark chocolate, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the cedar plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as Sonoma Meritage for grilled Caesar — accessible Meritage on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Sonoma Bordeaux blend — plum, blackberry, mocha, soft tannin, smooth. The soft tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mocha plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak crowd-pleaser Bordeaux blend for grilled Caesar — accessible Sonoma on the layered salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino with the grilled Caesar — Friulian amaro against charred romaine. Strong; botanical complexity complements the Caesar's anchovy-and-char flavors.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Ancho Reyes": "Grilled Caesar: char-romaine with anchovy-parmesan meets Ancho Reyes. Excellent; the smoky-sweet-chile character doubles with grill-char at Mexican-liqueur register — smoke meets smoke, heat meets char.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with the grilled Caesar — port-finished Louisville against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; AE meets the char at wine-finish register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with the grilled Caesar — 117+ proof AE against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; AE CS meets the char at premium wine-finish register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "AE Triple Cask with the grilled Caesar — multi-cask AE against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; AE Triple Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Aperol": "Aperol with the grilled Caesar — soft aperitivo against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Aperol's orange-rhubarb lifts the Caesar at a softer register than Campari — spritz-friendly, brunch-adjacent match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Asparagus": "The Caesar and the asparagus on the same meal -- the bright cuts the savory weight. Works; sequence or share, the build holds.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The Caesar and the au gratin -- both cheese-forward, the table doubles up on cheese. Avoid; pick one or the other. Pair the Caesar with Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo; pair the au gratin with Bone-In Filet, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Keenan Chardonnay.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope — Paso Cab, ripe plum, dark cherry, mocha, warm spice. The bold body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the warm-spice plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as Paso Cab for grilled Caesar — warm-climate richness on the classic layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Averna Amaro": "Averna with the grilled Caesar — fuller-body amaro against charred romaine. Strong; caramel-orange finds the Caesar's anchovy register cleanly.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Aviation": "Aviation gin, Luxardo maraschino, crème de violette, ginger syrup. The floral-cherry profile can handle grilled-romaine char and ginger softens anchovy-parmesan dressing. Works as unconventional cocktail pairing — Aviation brings lightness where heavier options would crush the salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Aviation Gin": "Aviation with the grilled Caesar — Portland lavender-cardamom against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Aviation meets the char at balanced register; Bluecoat's 4-citrus cuts char sharper, Tanq 10 still leads.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Avion 44": "Avion 44's rested-agave body reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the oak-aged tequila body threads the grilled Caesar, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver with the grilled Caesar — globally-familiar blanco against charred romaine. Strong; clean agave finds the Caesar at the everyday tier.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character frames the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the crisp body matches the leaf-and-acid plate, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character jars against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Baileys Irish Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Baker's": "Baker's with the grilled Caesar — 107-proof small-batch against charred romaine. Strong; the bold proof earns the Caesar with small-batch authority.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the grilled Caesar — handcrafted Speyside against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Balvenie's vanilla-oak meets the char with sweet-oak depth, though Fiddich's crisp-apple and Glenlivet's honey-floral are more traditional Speyside-Caesar approaches.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the malt-and-oak threads the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with the grilled Caesar — hand-picked American Oak against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Balvenie 16's vanilla-depth meets the char; Cragganmore 12's subtle smoke leads the Speyside-Caesar pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood's aged Port-finished Speyside finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the malt-driven body wraps the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Works; milestone-edition over-specified.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with the grilled Caesar — Bardstown French-wine finish against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Bardstown Château Laboude meets the char at contemporary-craft register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with the grilled Caesar — Discovery blend against charred romaine. Works; multi-distillery complexity doesn't drive.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with the grilled Caesar — stout-barrel against charred romaine. Works; KBS character doesn't drive the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare with the grilled Caesar — Foursquare rum-cask against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Bardstown Foursquare meets the char at Caribbean-finish register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with the grilled Caesar — Fusion blend against charred romaine. Works; doesn't drive.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with the grilled Caesar — Rishi's-picked Bardstown against charred romaine. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with the grilled Caesar — rye pepper meets charred romaine and anchovy. Works; the light body lets the Caesar lead, distinct from the Wellers' wheated-and-heavy take.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year with the grilled Caesar — aged rye spice against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; the 10-year earns a bit more of the Caesar than standard Basil Hayden's but not as much as Weller 12.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with the grilled Caesar — malted rye against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; grain notes pick up the char but the Caesar's weight still pushes against 80-proof light-body.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with the grilled Caesar — high-rye BH red-wine finish against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; BH Red Wine Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast with the grilled Caesar — toast and marshmallow against charred romaine. Works; the pour's toasted warmth threads through the char without overpowering.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bee's Knees": "Empress Indigo gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup. The honey's sweetness balances anchovy-parmesan dressing and the lemon cuts the dressing's salinity; lavender plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong unconventional pairing — cocktail's simplicity handles the salad's layered flavors.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater with the grilled Caesar — classic 40% against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Beefeater's balance meets the char cleanly.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Beignets": "Grilled Caesar and the beignets -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere with the grilled Caesar — rye vodka against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Belvedere's character engages the char; Ketel's citrus still wins this pairing for vodka.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine against blended bourbon. Works; a composed but not remarkable match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with the grilled Caesar — 103-proof Blanton's against charred romaine. Strong; the extra proof earns this Caesar where Original only works.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with the grilled Caesar — 93-proof single-barrel against charred romaine. Works; the premium pour doesn't quite drive the Caesar — Blanton's Gold or E.H. Taylor earn it more fully.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Grilled Caesar: charred romaine and anchovy meet export cask-strength Blanton's. Works; Blanton's SfB is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with the grilled Caesar — 10th-Pact against charred romaine. Works; milestone-release doesn't drive.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with the grilled Caesar — Pact 8 against charred romaine. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the grilled Caesar — Pact 9 wine-cask against charred romaine. Works; fruit-cask doesn't drive.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat with the grilled Caesar — 4-organic-citrus American dry gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Bluecoat's citrus-forward profile is the featured Smooth/Citrus Craft Gin-Caesar pairing — parallel to Tanq 10's citrus approach from London Dry, the patriotic-artisan equivalent.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Tannin and char, umami and dark fruit — the bold red and the Caesar punch at the same level. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry with the grilled Caesar — angular juniper against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Bombay Dry's pine cuts the char at traditional register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire with the grilled Caesar — softer floral against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Sapphire meets the char gently; Tanq 10's citrus is still the sharper gin-Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bone Marrow": "The bone marrow with the Caesar -- the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness meets the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the savory course rounds the protein, the meal reads layered. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bone-In Filet": "The bone-in filet preceded by the Caesar -- the elevated filet meets the parmesan-Caesar plate. Strong; the opener composes cleanly before the cut.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Booker's": "Booker's with the grilled Caesar — uncut Beam against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Booker's 125+ proof is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Botanist Gin": "Botanist with the grilled Caesar — 22-foraged-botanical Islay craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Botanist's wildcrafted herbs meet the char at Hebridean register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned's bold caramel-char build leans against the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the whiskey-build weight is misaligned with the leaf-and-acid plate, and the oak frames the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with the grilled Caesar — the house pick's character earns this salad where standard Maker's only works. Strong; the private select's depth gives the Caesar something specific.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the grilled Caesar — Virginia-aged small-batch against charred romaine. Works; the smooth 90-proof doesn't quite drive the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bowmore 12": "Grilled Caesar: charred romaine and anchovy dressing meet Bowmore 12's honeyed sherry finish. The softer peat lets the Caesar lead; this is the Caesar pairing for guests who aren't sure how much smoke they want. The most versatile Islay-Caesar pour of the three. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin. The high acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the dark cherry plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak benchmark Barbera for grilled Caesar — food-wine acidity reframes the layered salad. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin — Mount Veeder Cab with dark blackberry, graphite, earth, firm tannin. The dense body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the graphite-earth plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as mountain Cab for grilled Caesar — Mount Veeder structure on the classic layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Broccoli Cheddar": "The broccoli cheddar and the Caesar -- both cheese-forward, the table doubles up on cheese. Avoid; pick one or the other. Pair the broccoli cheddar with Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay; pair the Caesar with Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Broccolini": "The Caesar alongside the broccolini -- acid lifts the umami, the meal carries lift without losing depth. Works; the table can build them in order or together.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich: this Islay Scotch's intense peat smoke, medicinal phenol, and bold weight meets the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing. Bold whiskey against the Caesar's char and umami — weight matches, but the flavors compete. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Brussels and Belly": "The Caesar and Brussels + Belly on the same meal -- umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; sequence or share, the build holds.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with the grilled Caesar — flagship oak against charred romaine and anchovy. BT's balance earns the Caesar in a way softer wheated pours can't; strong. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character jars against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Buffalo Trace Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with the grilled Caesar — high-rye spice against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; the rye earns this Caesar in a way Wellers' wheated softness doesn't.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Burrata": "The burrata alongside the Caesar -- both bring cheese as the headline. Avoid; one is enough. Pair the burrata with Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc; pair the Caesar with Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Butternut Squash": "Butternut Squash and the Caesar together -- soup-and-salad open the meal. Works; both courses share the opening without crowding.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's structure meets the Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold dressing — weight on weight. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila with the grilled Caesar — value reposado against charred romaine. Strong; approachable agave finds the Caesar at the everyday tier.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Campari": "Campari with the grilled Caesar — ruby bitter orange-rhubarb against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Campari is aperitivo at its most textbook, and the bitter-orange lifts the Caesar's anchovy-char in a way the Fernets can't reach. The aperitivo amaro finds its clearest app.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club with the grilled Caesar — grain-forward traditional Canadian against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Canadian Club's grain-forward heritage character is the featured Canadian-Caesar pairing — the grain-forward profile cuts char-and-anchovy more distinctively than Crown Royal's smooth-vanilla approach.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Canadian Club 43 Year": "CC 43 Year with the grilled Caesar — rare aged Canadian against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; CC 43 Year's aged character meets the char at collector register; CC's entry grain-forward is still the featured Canadian-Caesar at lower register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's green-agave-and-cane lift sits alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the silver-rum body cuts the grilled Caesar, and the unaged-cane note plays against the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing with the grilled Caesar — Sazerac-owned single-barrel Canadian against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Caribou Crossing's hand-selected character meets the char at craft register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Carrot Cake": "The Caesar alongside the carrot cake -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness jars against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- sweet wine on cold lettuce refuses the salad's clean register. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Casa LBV Port for the steak course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's bold agave-soft-smoke character finds register with the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the agave smoke meets the plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus — Napa Cab, concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the cocoa plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as iconic Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — unconventional red pairing, though the name recognition sells the moment.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Cheesecake": "The Caesar alongside the cheesecake -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Chilean Seabass": "Chilean Seabass after the Caesar -- the charred-romaine and anchovy edge opens with savory-bright lift, the seabass following with buttery pan-finished flesh the anchovy primed cleanly. Strong; bright opener into rich fish reads classic.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Chocolate Brownie": "The Caesar and the brownie -- the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge bookends with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Chocolate Cake": "The Caesar and the chocolate cake -- the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge bookends with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin with the grilled Caesar — creamy Polish potato against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Chopin softens the char where Ketel's citrus lifts it.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with the grilled Caesar — sweeter brandy against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Christian Bros meets the char at functional register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Château Batailley": "Pauillac 5ème Cru Classé Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the cedar plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as classified Bordeaux for grilled Caesar — Pauillac structure on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Château Beaucastel": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Tannin and char, umami and dark fruit — the bold red and the Caesar punch at the same level. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The truffle plays with parm-char and anchovy-lemon, though plush Merlot reads heavy for the salad. Works for grilled caesar — Chablis or Sancerre fits the greens cleaner.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Château Haut Segottes": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the truffle plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as right-bank Bordeaux for grilled Caesar — Merlot-led roundness on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Château de Rouillac": "Pessac-Léognan left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, tobacco, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the cedar plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak left-bank Bordeaux for grilled Caesar — structured French red on the layered salad. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Clam Chowder": "The chowder alongside the Caesar -- the clam-and-potato body reads alongside the parmesan-Caesar plate. Works; the opening pair holds at table register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the añejo register carries the grilled Caesar, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the rested-agave depth sits with the grilled Caesar, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the rested-agave depth sits with the grilled Caesar, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; the ceremony earns the dish a different register than standard reposado brings.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register meets at register with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the earthen-pit smoke carries the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata with the grilled Caesar — ceramic-bottle luxury against charred romaine. Strong; the ceremony earns the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado with the grilled Caesar — ceramic-bottle reposado against charred romaine. Strong; the pairing isn't the draw, the presentation is.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra's aged-agave character leans against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the aged-agave weight wraps the grilled Caesar, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine against Beam wheated. Works; the wheated profile is a touch gentle for the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body. The garrigue matches anchovy-lemon dressing and the warm spice plays against parm-char. Peak Southern-French for grilled caesar — Mediterranean herb match on the salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with the grilled Caesar — Napa Cab-cask rosé against charred romaine. Strong; the wine-cask character earns the Caesar distinctively.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Contratto Vermouth": "Grilled Caesar: char-romaine with anchovy-parmesan meets Contratto Vermouth. Excellent; the aromatized-wine profile meets char-and-brine at 16% aperitivo register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco with the grilled Caesar — Sazerac blanco against charred romaine. Strong; highland-agave earns the Caesar at single-estate register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado with the grilled Caesar — single-estate reposado against charred romaine. Strong; agave brightness and Caesar anchovy find each other.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the grilled Caesar, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg's aged-agave character sits alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the aged-agave weight wraps the grilled Caesar, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; the wine-cask character adds interest but isn't a standout.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the añejo register carries the grilled Caesar, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Mendoza Argentina Malbec — Corazón del Sol, dark plum, blackberry jam, violet, velvety tannin, full body. The velvety tannin handles anchovy-depth, but the jam-forward fruit reads heavy against lemon-dressing. Works for grilled caesar — a Kermit Lynch Rhône's garrigue fits the dressing harder.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Corpse Reviver": "Warner's gin, Lillet, Pierre Ferrand cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. The absinthe-and-Lillet herbal complexity echoes our Caesar's anchovy depth; the lemon cleans the palate after each rich, egg-yolk-heavy bite. Pre-Prohibition classic meets classic. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier with the grilled Caesar — drier cognac against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Courvoisier's oak meets the char at functional register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye into the Caesar -- the bone-in ribeye threads the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Crab Cake": "The crab cake with the Caesar -- the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust meets the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the lighter plate hands off to the savory, the table arcs clean. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 with the grilled Caesar — Classic Malt Speyside against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Cragganmore's subtle smoke hint is the featured Speyside-Caesar pairing — the smoke-meets-char resonance no other Batch 1 Speyside can claim.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Creamed Spinach": "Grilled Caesar and the creamed spinach -- the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge meets the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: cream rounds the savory, the meal earns its depth. Works; no conflict however the table orders them.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Creamy Potato": "Creamy Potato and the Caesar together -- soup-and-salad open the meal. Works; both courses share the opening without crowding.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Creme Brulee": "Grilled Caesar and the brulee -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral backbone. The high acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the forest-floor plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong benchmark Pinot for grilled Caesar — Willamette elegance on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal with the grilled Caesar — iconic smooth Canadian against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Crown Royal meets the char at crowd-pleasing register; Canadian Club's grain-forward heritage is the featured Canadian-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Cucumber Gimlet": "Hendrick's gin, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda. The cucumber-and-lime freshness cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and ginger adds warmth against grilled-romaine char. Works as unconventional opener alongside a charred salad when guest wants something cooling.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12 with the grilled Caesar — Highland luxury sherry-finish against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Dalmore's orange-chocolate character meets the char with luxurious Highland distinction.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, high acid. The chalk-acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the dried-herbs play against grilled-romaine char. Strong Loire red for grilled Caesar — French Pinot reframes the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the savory herb plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong rare-Napa CF for grilled Caesar — Darioush aromatic elegance on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Darioush — Silverado Trail Cab with blackcurrant, dark plum, mocha, spice. The opulent body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mocha-spice plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as opulent Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — Persian-inspired estate story on the classic layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin with the grilled Caesar — urban craft juniper against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Detroit City meets the char at hometown register; Knickerbocker's hop-influenced profile is the distinctive Local MI Gin-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City with the grilled Caesar — Michigan craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Detroit City meets the char with hometown character; Hangar 1's floral-grape lift is the sharper craft-Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15 with the grilled Caesar — aged Highland blend with sherry-finish against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Dewar's 15's aged character meets the char with premium-blend resonance.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label with the grilled Caesar — approachable blended Scotch against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Dewar's White meets the char at benchmark register; JW Red's smoke is more distinctive here.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the grilled Caesar — craft blended rye against charred romaine. The boutique character earns the Caesar with craft-story distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's high-proof pour register leans against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the layered spirit body sits with the grilled Caesar, and the deep backbone frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Willamette flagship Pinot Blend — dark cherry, Bing cherry, toasted spice, forest floor, silky. The acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the toasted-spice plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong flagship Willamette Pinot for grilled Caesar — Domaine Serene layering on the salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Provence Bandol AOC Mourvèdre blend — Tempier, dark plum, game, olive, wild herbs, smoky mineral, full body. The olive-savory matches anchovy-depth and the wild herbs play with charred-romaine. Strong cult-Bandol for grilled caesar — Tempier Provençal hook on the salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Pouilly-Fumé's flinty Loire Sauvignon Blanc — smoke hooks into the grill char on the romaine. More depth than Sancerre; the acid cuts our egg-yolk-emulsified dressing cleanly. Non-obvious white with a real flavor hook into the dish's char. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body. The garrigue matches anchovy-lemon dressing and the black pepper cuts parm-char. Strong Châteauneuf for grilled caesar — Rhône herb hook on the salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Don Fulano Anejo": "Don Fulano Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the oak-aged tequila body threads the grilled Caesar, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; the wine-cask character adds dimension but not standout.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco with the grilled Caesar — highland refined blanco against charred romaine. Strong; the craft character earns the Caesar at the premium register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado with the grilled Caesar — refined highland reposado against charred romaine. Strong; honey-oak gives the Caesar a refined frame.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942's icon extra-añejo character finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the rested-agave depth sits with the grilled Caesar, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; aged depth is refined but not a standout.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th with the grilled Caesar — filtered reposado against charred romaine. Strong; the 70th's clarity is an elegant frame for the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco with the grilled Caesar — benchmark blanco against charred romaine and anchovy. The agave brightness and the anchovy salinity find each other; an industry-standard pairing. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Don Julio Reposado": "Don Julio Reposado with the grilled Caesar — reposado brightness against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; the agave's lift gives the Caesar dimension bourbon can't.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Drambuie": "Grilled Caesar: char-romaine with anchovy-parmesan meets Drambuie. Excellent; the scotch-whisky base brings smoke-adjacent depth to char-and-brine at honey-herbal register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Dunville's Single Malt": "Grilled Caesar: charred romaine and anchovy meet Echlinville Irish single malt. Strong; Dunville's meets the char at distinctive Irish-single-malt register; Tullamore's drier blend still leads the entry-Irish-Caesar pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the grilled Caesar — Col. E.H. Taylor Jr. tribute against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; EHT Full Proof is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye with the grilled Caesar — BT's bonded rye against charred romaine. The 100-proof bonded character earns the Caesar with premium BIB rye authority. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the grilled Caesar — bonded BT against charred romaine. Strong; BIB earns the Caesar where 90-proof single-barrels only work.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy meet ten years of balanced oak. The Caesar earns the 10-year's refinement; strong. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Eastern Kille with the grilled Caesar — classic London Dry against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Eastern Kille's juniper meets the char at GR-craft register; Knickerbocker's hop-influenced profile is the featured Local MI Gin-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|El Cabo": "El Cabo with the grilled Caesar — boutique reposado against charred romaine. Strong; the craft character earns the Caesar distinctively.",
  "Grilled Caesar|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "El Mayor Extra Anejo's aged-agave character finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the rested-agave depth sits with the grilled Caesar, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; Durango presentation slightly over-specified.",
  "Grilled Caesar|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado with the grilled Caesar — value reposado against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; the agave brightness finds the Caesar at the everyday tier.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin with the grilled Caesar — craft African-botanical gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Elephant's herbal profile meets the char; Tanq 10's citrus is still the sharper gin-Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 with the grilled Caesar — 18yr HH single-barrel against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; EC 18 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel with the grilled Caesar — house-pick single-barrel against charred romaine. Works; curated character doesn't quite drive the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel with the grilled Caesar — Heaven Hill single-barrel against charred romaine. Strong; the 94-proof single-barrel character earns the Caesar where Elijah Craig Small Batch only works.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with the grilled Caesar — 94-proof small-batch against charred romaine. Strong; the oak spice earns the Caesar where softer Wellers only work.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the grilled Caesar — Heaven Hill rye against charred romaine. The HH spicy-sweet character earns the Caesar at premium HH register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — cherry, violet, clove. The structured acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the clove-spice plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong step-up Pinot for grilled Caesar — Chehalem Mountains depth on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — soft pear, white peach, floral, medium body. The refreshing acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the fruit plays against grilled-romaine char without overpowering. Strong unoaked white for grilled Caesar — sophisticated alternative to the usual Chardonnay pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee with the grilled Caesar — namesake single-barrel against charred romaine. Works; the smooth character doesn't drive the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 with the grilled Caesar — color-changing craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Empress's floral-citrus meets the char softly; Bluecoat's 4-citrus and Tanq 10's citrus are the featured craft-Caesar matches.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Epoch Ingenuity": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, full body. The olive-savory matches anchovy-depth and the crushed pepper cuts parm-char. Works for grilled caesar — Epoch's full body reads heavy, a Rhône Villages lifts lighter.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Escargot": "Escargot and the Caesar together -- savory-on-savory, the table reads dense and earnest. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Espresso Martini": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness overwhelms the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- whiskey-cocktail weight has no business on cold lettuce. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- whiskey-cocktail weight has no business on cold lettuce. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Espresso Old Fashioned lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone. The medium tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the smoked-meat plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong savory Pinot for grilled Caesar — Willamette depth on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis sits beside the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the opulent body overshoots the leaf-and-acid plate, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Napa Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush. The opulent oak cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the hazelnut plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong special-occasion Chardonnay for grilled Caesar — Napa weight on the layered classic salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Faroe Island Salmon": "Faroe Island Salmon after the Caesar -- the charred-romaine and anchovy edge opens with savory-bright lift, the salmon following with rich oily flesh that the anchovy preceded in the same register. Strong; anchovy into oily fish reads composed.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis sits alongside the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the opulent body overshoots the leaf-and-acid plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Napa Coombsville Cab — blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the tobacco plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as value Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — brooding complexity on the classic layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid. The high acid matches anchovy-lemon dressing and the slate plays with charred-romaine smoke. Peak Galician-Mencía for grilled caesar — bright-acid Burgundian-style match on the salad. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca with the grilled Caesar — menthol-saffron against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Branca's savory herbal lean finds the anchovy-and-char more than the sweeter amari do.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta with the grilled Caesar — cooling mint bitter against charred romaine. Strong; mint bridges the char-and-anchovy with a fresh lift.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Filet Mignon": "The filet with the Caesar alongside -- the 10oz tenderloin matches the charred-and-anchovy salad. Strong; the opener carries its register against the cut.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character jars against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Fireball Whisky on the steak course instead.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky. The cranberry acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the spice plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong by-the-glass Pinot for grilled Caesar — California silk reframes the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the grilled Caesar — premium 4R against charred romaine. Strong; 104-proof earns the Caesar with small-batch character.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine against entry bourbon. Works; light body doesn't compete with the Caesar's weight.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with the grilled Caesar — craft bourbon against charred romaine. Works; boutique character doesn't drive the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Fox & Oden Double Oaked's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the caramel-spice register matches the grilled Caesar, and the vanilla rounds the flame-grilled char. Works; aging wasted on the salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register jars against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Rutherford Cab — Bosché Vineyard since 1970, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the tobacco plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as Bosché Vineyard Cab for grilled Caesar — 1970-legacy Rutherford on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|French 75": "Inverroche gin, lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top. Citrus and bubbles cut anchovy-parmesan dressing and the fynbos gin plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong unconventional pairing — the cocktail's brightness handles the charred-salad profile well.",
  "Grilled Caesar|French Onion": "The French onion and the Caesar share cheese as the primary character. Avoid; the meal doubles down without contrast. Route the French onion with Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée or the Caesar with Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo instead.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Frias Block 5": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The Caesar's anchovy-parmesan depth gives the bold red's tannin something real to work against. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Grilled Caesar|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, high acid. The high acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the rose petal plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak classic Barolo for grilled Caesar — Vajra's food-demanding structure on the layered salad. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Grilled Caesar|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Piedmont sweet sparkling — peach, honey, floral, low alcohol. The sweetness fights everything about the Caesar: anchovy, parmesan, cayenne, grilled char. Don't pour here; route Moscato to dessert where the sweetness belongs. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Grilled Caesar|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco with the grilled Caesar — Felipe Camarena's craft blanco against charred romaine. Strong; the agave-forward character earns the Caesar at craft register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof with the grilled Caesar — 104-proof against charred romaine and anchovy. The bold agave matches the Caesar's weight; the high-proof reposado Caesar pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue with the grilled Caesar — western Ireland craft Irish against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Garavogue meets the char with craft-Irish character; Tullamore's drier heritage still leads the entry-Irish-Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|George T. Stagg": "GTS with the grilled Caesar — BTAC 15+yr against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; GTS is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Ghost Block — Oakville Cab with blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak. The dense body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the graphite-oak plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as Oakville Cab for grilled Caesar — Napa's golden-strip structure on the classic layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body. The cracked pepper plays with anchovy-lemon dressing, but the jammy fruit reads heavy for the salad's lightness. Works for grilled caesar — a Rhône garrigue fits the anchovy harder.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12 with the grilled Caesar — entry Speyside against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Fiddich's apple/pear lightness meets the char cleanly — the gateway-Speyside Caesar pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14 with the grilled Caesar — Bourbon Barrel Reserve against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Fiddich 14's charred-oak finish meets the char with resonance the 12's apple doesn't quite reach; Cragganmore 12's subtle smoke still leads this pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18 with the grilled Caesar — sherry-cask Speyside against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Fiddich 18's depth meets the char at premium register; Cragganmore 12's subtle smoke still leads the Speyside-Caesar pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23 with the grilled Caesar — champagne-style 23yr Speyside against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Gran Cru's brioche-pastry meets the char at refined register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12 with the grilled Caesar — coastal-tropical Highland against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Glenglassaugh 12's fresh-fruit-and-honey profile meets the char with distinctive Sandend character.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12 with the grilled Caesar — softer Speyside against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Glenlivet's honey-floral meets the char with benchmark softness — where Fiddich is crisp-apple, Glenlivet is honey-soft.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10 with the grilled Caesar — honey-floral Highland bourbon-cask against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Glenmorangie 10's honeysuckle-peach-vanilla is the featured Highland-Caesar pairing — the bourbon-cask floral-vanilla lifts char-and-anchovy where Dalmore's sherry is over-specified.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18 with the grilled Caesar — aged Glenmorangie with Oloroso-finish against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Glenmorangie 18's deeper character meets the char with aged-premium Highland distinction.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness jars against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- sweet wine on cold lettuce refuses the salad's clean register. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Graham's 10 Year Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Graham's 20 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Graham's 2017 Vintage Port's structured vintage port with dark-fruit weight clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Graham's 2017 Vintage Port for the steak course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum with the grilled Caesar — premium Patron against charred romaine. Strong; refined agave earns the Caesar at ultra-premium tier.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Grand Marnier belongs on the steak course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale with the grilled Caesar — California 6-botanical craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Gray Whale's California terroir meets the char; Bluecoat's 4-citrus cuts sharper.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Green Chartreuse": "Grilled Caesar: flame-kissed romaine with anchovy-parmesan meets Green Chartreuse. Excellent; the herbal-complex clarity cuts char-and-brine at high-proof register. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose with the grilled Caesar — soft wheat vodka against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Goose tempers the char, though Ketel One's citrus-floral finish is the sharper vodka-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Gumbo": "Grilled Caesar and the gumbo together -- soup-and-salad open the meal. Works; both courses share the opening without crowding.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The Caesar's anchovy-parmesan depth gives the bold red's tannin something real to work against. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Grilled Caesar|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 with the grilled Caesar — higher-proof craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; HDW 100 cuts the char more aggressively than the 80-proof siblings; Hangar 1's floral-grape is still the sharpest craft-Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 with the grilled Caesar — lightly-peated Japanese single malt against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Hakushu's subtle peat + crisp-green meet the char with Forest-Distillery distinction — the featured Japanese-whisky-Caesar pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel with the grilled Caesar — Blanton's-mash BT against charred romaine. Works; the pour is slightly too restrained to drive the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Grilled Caesar: charred romaine and anchovy meet blended grain+grape California craft. Hangar 1's grape-component floral lift is the featured craft-vodka-Caesar pairing — where flagship Ketel One (Classic Vodka) is the benchmark, Hangar 1 brings the craft floral answer. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Head Fake": "Wheatley vodka, limoncello, triple sec, lemon, simple syrup. The lemon balances anchovy-parmesan dressing and limoncello adds Italian-citrus depth against grilled-romaine char. Works as a bright unconventional cocktail pairing — sweetness and char meet on the rim.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the grilled Caesar — HH 20yr against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; HH 20 is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against 100-proof BIB. Works; the bonded weight is slightly ambitious for the Caesar but the char absorbs some of it.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with the grilled Caesar — aged 100-proof against charred romaine. Strong; 10-year depth earns the Caesar with premium bonded weight.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the structured body wraps the grilled Caesar, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "Heitz Trailside — Rutherford Cab with dark cherry, blackcurrant, mint, cedar. The balanced body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mint-cedar plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as historic-house Cab for grilled Caesar — Heitz provenance on the classic layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Flora Adora with the grilled Caesar — lavish-floral Hendrick's against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Flora Adora's floral-garden meets the char softly; Orbium's wormwood-bitter is the featured Hendrick's-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks with the grilled Caesar — flagship low-juniper gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Hendrick's perfumed character meets the char softly; Orbium's wormwood-bitter is the featured Hendrick's-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Grand Cabaret with the grilled Caesar — absinthe-style Hendrick's against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Grand Cabaret's fruit-forward meets the char softly; Orbium's wormwood-bitter is the featured Hendrick's-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Hendricks Neptunia": "Neptunia with the grilled Caesar — bladderwrack-Ayrshire Hendrick's against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Neptunia's maritime-briny meets the anchovy with distinctive resonance; Orbium's wormwood-bitter is the featured Hendrick's-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Hendricks Orbium": "Orbium with the grilled Caesar — quinine-wormwood Hendrick's against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Orbium's bitter-tonic profile is the featured Hendrick's Family-Caesar pairing — wormwood-meets-char with distinctive bitter resonance the floral siblings can't replicate.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy with the grilled Caesar — cognac against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Hennessy's vanilla-oak meets the char without featuring.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB with the grilled Caesar — aged bonded HH against charred romaine. Strong; the 10-year BIB earns the Caesar with BIB weight and age.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Harmony with the grilled Caesar — refined Japanese blend against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Hibiki meets the char at premium register; Hakushu's lightly-peated is the featured Japanese-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Honey Roasted Carrots": "The Caesar and the honey carrots -- sugar and umami share the table, the contrast carries the meal. Works; sequence them or share the table, the call holds either way.",
  "Grilled Caesar|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 with the grilled Caesar — 15yr BT-distilled against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Veneto Glera — bright Prosecco with white peach, green apple, and crisp citrus. The light effervescence can handle anchovy-parmesan dressing, but Prosecco lacks the body to meet grilled-romaine char fully. Works as unconventional opener — Champagne peaks higher for grilled Caesar's layered weight.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Inhibited": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the bold-mix depth handles the grilled Caesar, and the bitters edge frames the bowl. Works; the dessert closes without crowding.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche with the grilled Caesar — Cape-floral gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Inverroche's fynbos lifts the char at terroir register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish with the grilled Caesar — estate-botanical gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Iron Fish meets the char at farm-forward register; Knickerbocker's hop-influenced profile is the featured Local MI Gin-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon Mezcal Casks with the grilled Caesar — Iron Fish MI mezcal-cask against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Ironfish Mezcal's smoke meets char at Michigan-hometown register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish with the grilled Caesar — Virginia port-finish against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Isaac Bowman meets the char at Virginia-heritage register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "Anderson Valley Pinot — dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, refined tannin. The medium tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mocha plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong Anderson Valley Pinot for grilled Caesar — concentrated Mendocino on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "Diamond Mountain Cab — dark fruit, graphite, mineral, firm tannin. The structured body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the graphite-mineral plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as mountain Cab for grilled Caesar — Schramsberg provenance and volcanic complexity on the classic layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year with the grilled Caesar — aged Tennessee against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; JD 12's aged vanilla-caramel meets the char at premium register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded with the grilled Caesar — 100-proof Tennessee against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; JD Bonded's backbone meets the char at Bonded register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7 with the grilled Caesar — charcoal-mellowed Tennessee against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Old No. 7's vanilla-caramel meets the char at iconic register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "JD Sinatra Select with the grilled Caesar — Sinatra-tribute Tennessee against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Sinatra Select's extra oak meets the char at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson with the grilled Caesar — iconic smooth Irish against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Jameson's softness meets the char at entry register; Tullamore D.E.W.'s drier heritage is the featured entry-Irish-Caesar pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, balanced acidity. The acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the subtle earth plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong food-friendly Pinot for grilled Caesar — reliable Russian River on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Burgundy Chablis — unoaked Chard with flinty chalky minerality, lemon, green apple. The lean acidity cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the flint-chalk plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong classical Chablis for the Caesar — unoaked precision on the layered classic salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the grilled Caesar — master-blender tribute against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Marian McLain L.E. meets the char at collector register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the oak depth lays under the grilled Caesar, and the vanilla brightens the grilled char-edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against blended bourbon. Works; Jefferson's VR Twin Oak earns more of this Caesar than the standard Reserve does.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the grilled Caesar — double-barrel depth against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; the Twin Oak earns this Caesar where standard Jefferson's only works.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine against aged Beam. Works; the aging helps the Caesar match vs. White Label.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine against entry Beam. Works; light body doesn't compete with the Caesar's weight.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Johnnie Walker Black": "JW Black with the grilled Caesar — iconic 12yr JW against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; JW Black's balanced smoke meets the char with premium-blend resonance — the 12-year aging mellows JW Red's bold-fiery approach into a more refined smoke.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Johnnie Walker Blue": "JW Blue with the grilled Caesar — ultra-premium rare-cask JW against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; JW Blue's luxury silky-smoke meets the char at collector register; JW Black handles this pairing more economically.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Johnnie Walker Red": "JW Red with the grilled Caesar — bold-smoke entry JW against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; JW Red's peat-influence is the featured blended-Scotch-Caesar pairing — smoke-meets-char with Islay-malt resonance, distinct from the Highland honey-floral approach.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing. Cherry-driven red with the Caesar's umami and char — a classic cross-category salad pair. Strong — aligned register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre — Loire Sauv Blanc with grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension finish. The refined mineral cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the herbs play against grilled-romaine char. Strong benchmark Sauv Blanc for grilled Caesar — French precision on the layered American classic.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation by Colgin's bold char-chocolate cassis sits beside the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the opulent body overshoots the leaf-and-acid plate, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's high-proof pour register reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the rich pour wraps the grilled Caesar, and the heavy-pour register grips the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's high-proof pour register finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the dense-spirit weight carries the grilled Caesar, and the rich character softens the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character jars against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Kahlua for the steak course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Kansas City": "Kansas City into the Caesar -- the lean-bold strip carries into the anchovy-Caesar plate. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Keenan Chardonnay": "Keenan Chardonnay — Spring Mountain with green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acid. The bright acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the subtle oak plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong mountain Chardonnay for grilled Caesar — balance reframes the classic layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Keenan Mernet": "Spring Mountain Napa Mernet — Merlot-Cab, mountain structure, plush dark cherry, plum, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the plum plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong mountain Merlot-Cab for grilled Caesar — Keenan story on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye with the grilled Caesar — premium KO against charred romaine. Works; allocated character doesn't drive.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the grilled Caesar — KO Confiscated against charred romaine. Works; narrative-bourbon doesn't drive.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with the grilled Caesar — luxury crossover Irish against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Kentucky Owl's bourbon-cask Irish meets the char at collector register; Redbreast 12's pot still spice is still the featured Irish-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The wild herbs match anchovy-lemon dressing and the pepper cuts through parm. Peak Southern-French for grilled caesar — Kermit Lynch food-wine handles the salad beautifully. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One with the grilled Caesar — pot-and-column Dutch wheat against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Ketel's citrus-floral finish is the distinctive vodka-Caesar match — it lifts the char rather than rounding it (Tito's) or softening it (Goose).",
  "Grilled Caesar|Knickerbocker Gin": "Grilled Caesar: charred romaine and anchovy meet hop-influenced Holland MI gin. Knickerbocker's hop-botanical character is the featured Local MI Gin-Caesar pairing — where the others treat this as Works, Knickerbocker's brewer-heritage character is built for char-and-bitter resonance like its parent New Holland beers.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek with the grilled Caesar — nutty high-rye against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; the 100-proof earns the Caesar more than Weller Special Reserve or Buffalo Trace bring.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year with the grilled Caesar — aged nutty-oak Beam against charred romaine. Strong; the 12-year depth earns the Caesar with aged character.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 with the grilled Caesar — 15yr Beam KC against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; KC 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 with the grilled Caesar — 18yr Beam KC against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; KC 18 is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with the grilled Caesar — blended bourbon-rye against charred romaine and anchovy. The rye brightness and bourbon depth together earn this Caesar in a way no single-mash bourbon can. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye with the grilled Caesar — 100-proof bold rye against charred romaine. The Beam intensity and anchovy find each other with premium grip. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the barrel-rested body handles the grilled Caesar, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo's aged-agave character sits alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the rested-agave depth sits with the grilled Caesar, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Anejo's aged-agave character reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the barrel-rested body handles the grilled Caesar, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; clean agave cuts the glaze but isn't a standout.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado with the grilled Caesar — luxury pink reposado against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; the wine-cask strawberry notes give the Caesar a different frame than standard reposados.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Lagavulin 8": "Grilled Caesar: the charred romaine and anchovy-forward dressing meet Lagavulin 8's bright peat and sea-spray brine. The char echoes the smoke, the anchovy picks up the iodine. The lightest Islay of the three — the Caesar pairing for guests who want peat without a medicinal wall. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Napa Cab — Lail entry-level, polished blackcurrant, cedar, dark cherry, refined medium tannin. The refined tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the cedar plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as historically-pedigreed Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — Lail family polish on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the graphite plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as historically-significant Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — Daniel Cuvée structure on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver with the grilled Caesar — Lalo González craft against charred romaine. Strong; the clean additive-free character earns the Caesar at craft heritage register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Laphroaig 10": "Laphroaig 10 with the grilled Caesar — the medicinal intensity risks overrunning the Caesar's finesse. Capable, but Lagavulin 8 or Bowmore 12 are the better Islay calls here. Pour Laphroaig only if the guest has specifically asked. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against Heaven Hill wheated. Works; Larceny's honey notes earn a bit more of the Caesar than Weller Special Reserve brings.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Pinot Noir Champagne rosé — benchmark silky mousse, red berry notes, long elegant finish. The bubbles cut anchovy-parmesan dressing and the structure meets grilled-romaine char; strawberry adds lift against the heavy salad. Strong rosé Champagne pour for grilled Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — green apple, lemon zest, brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid structure cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the brioche plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong precision Champagne for the Caesar — classical match for the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Le Garenne Rosé": "Le Garenne Rosé — Loire Pinot Noir with strawberry, watermelon, floral, crisp dry. The fresh-fruit acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the berry-floral plays against grilled-romaine char without overpowering. Strong summer rosé for grilled Caesar — seasonal lightness on the layered classic.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Tuscany Brunello di Montalcino DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Le Ragnaie, five years minimum aging, dried cherry, tobacco, leather, rose petal, firm tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the rose petal plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as Italy's-most-prestigious for grilled Caesar — Brunello reframes the salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Tannin and char, umami and dark fruit — the bold red and the Caesar punch at the same level. Strong — aligned intensity.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The honey-lavender profile plays against anchovy-parmesan dressing and lemon cuts the saltiness; Prosecco adds lift against grilled-romaine char. Works as unconventional floral pairing with a charred salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Les Pallières Raciné": "Gigondas Rhône Valley Grenache — Les Pallières Raciné, Brunier-family old-vine, wild red fruit, garrigue, iron, earthy mineral, full body. The garrigue matches anchovy-lemon dressing and the iron plays against parm-char. Strong Gigondas for grilled caesar — Rhône herb hook on the salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Liberator Gin": "Liberator with the grilled Caesar — 100% Michigan-grain craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Liberator meets the char at hometown register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Licor 43 on the steak course instead.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, spice, medium body. The acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the forest-floor plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as by-the-glass Pinot for grilled Caesar — unconventional red choice for the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Little Book": "Little Book with the grilled Caesar — Fred Noe annual against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Little Book is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Loaded Potato": "Grilled Caesar and the loaded potato together -- soup-and-salad open the meal. Works; both courses share the opening without crowding.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Lobster Mac": "The Caesar and the lobster mac -- cheese-on-cheese, the meal goes redundant. Avoid; the table reads heavy without payoff. Suggest Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo for the Caesar, or Cowboy Ribeye, Far Niente Chardonnay, or The Tomahawk for the lobster mac.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year with the grilled Caesar — 20yr aged varied-still Highland against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Loch Lomond 20's complex-aged character meets the char at collector-Highland register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the grilled Caesar — sourced rye against charred romaine. Works; blended character doesn't drive.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Long Road Gin": "Long Road with the grilled Caesar — Grand Rapids estate gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Long Road meets the char at hometown register; Knickerbocker's hop-influenced profile is the featured Local MI Gin-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register leans against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the wood-fire body meets the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII with the grilled Caesar — 40-100 year aged cognac against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; the Caesar is an appetizer-register dish — Louis XIII belongs with the main course or standalone. Save the bottle.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano with the grilled Caesar — floral-anise amaro against charred romaine. Strong; anise character finds the Caesar's anchovy distinctively.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Abruzzo Montepulciano d'Abruzzo — Lunaria earthy grippy, dark cherry, dried herbs, rustic medium body, medium tannin. The rustic grip cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the dark cherry plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak Italian-approachable red for grilled Caesar — rustic Italian reframes the layered salad. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character leans against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the scotch backbone matches the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Works; boutique-blend wasted on salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the scotch depth settles into the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Works; BIB slightly over-specified.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate's estate Speyside character leans against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the scotch backbone matches the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Works; gentle match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The black pepper plays with anchovy-depth, but the dense tannin reads heavy for the salad. Works for grilled caesar — a Rhône-style red fits anchovy softer.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. Cherry-driven red with the Caesar's umami and char — a classic cross-category salad pair. Strong — aligned register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Macauley Stagecoach — Atlas Peak Cab with dark blackberry, blueberry, espresso, iron. The high-elevation body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the iron-espresso plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as celebrated-vineyard Cab for grilled Caesar — Stagecoach structure on the classic layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Mahon Gin": "Mahón with the grilled Caesar — Mediterranean PDO juniper against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Mahón's sharp Mediterranean character meets the char at Spanish register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against Maker's 90-proof softness. Works; Maker's Cellar Aged or Weller 12 earn this Caesar more fully.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy meet the Cellar Aged's deep vanilla. The Cellar Aged earns this salad where standard Maker's can't; strong. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's unaged-cane register sits alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the cane-and-citrus body lifts the grilled Caesar, and the unaged-cane register underlines the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye with the grilled Caesar — 16-year aged Michigan against charred romaine. Works; extended maturation doesn't drive the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Loire Cab Franc — Chinon light body, raspberry, red cherry, bell pepper, forest floor, supple low tannin, bright high acid. The bright acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the bell pepper plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak Loire CF for grilled Caesar — food-wine acidity reframes the layered salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Margarita": "Corazon Blanco, lemon, lime, orange, Cointreau — salted rim. The triple-citrus plays against anchovy-parmesan dressing and the salt rim bridges the grilled-romaine char. Strong unconventional pairing — the Margarita's brightness holds up to the salad's layered smoke-and-salt.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Marimar Estate Christina": "Russian River Green Valley Pinot — ripe strawberry, cherry compote, warm spice, silky. The acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the warm-spice plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong plush Pinot for grilled Caesar — Russian River opulence on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Market Fish": "Market Fish after the Caesar -- the charred-romaine and anchovy edge opens with savory-bright lift, the kitchen-driven fish following with white-fish flesh the anchovy primes cleanly. Strong; the bright opener carries into the fish course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Grilled Caesar: char-romaine with anchovy-parmesan meets Mata Hari. Excellent; the anise-wormwood clarity cuts char-and-brine at high-proof absinthe register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Bourbon with the grilled Caesar — Michter's allocated 10yr against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Michter's 10 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye with the grilled Caesar — 10-year Michter's against charred romaine. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the grilled Caesar — smooth pot-still rye against charred romaine and anchovy. The Michter's smooth character earns the Caesar at the refined small-batch register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco with the grilled Caesar — premium craft against charred romaine. Strong; the additive-free clean character earns the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the aged-agave weight wraps the grilled Caesar, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado with the grilled Caesar — modern reposado against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; clean agave finds the Caesar at the contemporary everyday tier.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Grilled Caesar: charred romaine and anchovy meet Mister Sam Bronfman tribute. Works; Mister Sam is over-specified for an app; save the tribute bottle for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Mocha Creme": "The Caesar alongside the mocha creme -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 with the grilled Caesar — Schwarzwald forest-herbal gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Monkey 47's complexity meets the char at premium register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder with the grilled Caesar — Speyside-malt blend against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Monkey Shoulder's rich-fruit-and-vanilla meets the char with Speyside-malt character — softer than JW Red's smoke, more fruit-forward than Dewar's honey.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro with the grilled Caesar — Bologna amaro against charred romaine. Strong; vanilla-orange finds the Caesar at approachable register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum's green-agave-and-cane lift matches register with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the unaged-cane body sits with the grilled Caesar, and the unaged-cane note plays against the bowl. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Muga Reserva": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Tannin and char, umami and dark fruit — the bold red and the Caesar punch at the same level. A confident Caesar pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Mushroom Bisque": "Grilled Caesar and the mushroom bisque together -- soup-and-salad open the meal. Works; both courses share the opening without crowding.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Mushrooms": "Grilled Caesar and the mushrooms -- the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge meets the mushrooms' earthy umami weight: both pull from the umami register, the contrast sits flat. Works; no conflict however the table orders them.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's dense-spirit weight finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the layered spirit body sits with the grilled Caesar, and the deep backbone frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Negroni": "Gin, Contratto, Campari — stirred, orange peel. The bitter-orange profile cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and Contratto's herbal complexity plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak bitter cocktail for the grilled salad — structural match through the Negroni's edge.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye with the grilled Caesar — UK-matured rye against charred romaine. The distinctive maturation gives the Caesar a craft-story frame. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine against UK-aged bourbon. Works; distinctive character is worth mentioning tableside, even if the pairing isn't marquee.",
  "Grilled Caesar|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with the grilled Caesar — MI beer-barrel finish against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; NH Beer Barrel's hop-bitter meets char at Michigan-hometown register (parallel to sibling Knickerbocker Gin's hop-Caesar).",
  "Grilled Caesar|New York Sour": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Bold whiskey against the Caesar's char and umami — weight matches, but the flavors compete. Strong — both land heavy.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Tannin and char, umami and dark fruit — the bold red and the Caesar punch at the same level. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolet's with the grilled Caesar — Dutch floral gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Nolet's rose-peach meets the char softly; Tanq 10's citrus is still the sharper gin-Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Not a Paper Plane": "Bulleit bourbon, Nonino amaro, Aperol, Angostura — stirred. The bourbon-and-amaro handles anchovy-parmesan dressing directly and Aperol's bitter orange plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak bitter cocktail for the salad — the Nonino-and-Aperol herbal structure matches the layered flavors.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Oban 14": "Oban 14 with the grilled Caesar — maritime Highland single malt against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Oban's subtle smoke hint and sea-salt lift the char-and-anchovy with coastal-Highland resonance — distinct from Glenmorangie's bourbon-cask lift.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Oban 18": "Oban 18 with the grilled Caesar — aged maritime Highland with sherry-finish against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Oban 18's aged coastal + sherry-finish meets the char with deeper resonance than Oban 14's entry approach.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the barrel-rested body handles the grilled Caesar, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; the pour is a touch over-specified for the side.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's structure meets the Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold dressing — weight on weight. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the grilled Caesar — Old Elk Colorado craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Old Elk Cigar Cut meets the char at cigar-companion register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer with the grilled Caesar — wheated bourbon against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Old Emmer meets the char at craft-wheated register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine against wheated bond. Works; Old Fitz 9 brings more depth here.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against 9-year wheated. Strong; the aging earns the Caesar where Old Fitz 7 only works.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with the grilled Caesar — 16yr OGD against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Opus One": "Mondavi-Rothschild's $950 prestige Cabernet — the dressing's anchovy and egg yolk flatten the structured tannin. Never pour Opus on the Caesar; the wine loses. Reserve for the Tomahawk or Porterhouse. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes — Willamette Trappist Chard, pear, butterscotch, oak, rich. The rich oak cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the butterscotch plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong opulent Chardonnay for grilled Caesar — monastery provenance adds conversation to the pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Pablo Sour": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla, egg white. The lemon cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the foam adds textural contrast against grilled-romaine char; vanilla softens the edges. Strong unconventional pairing — the Pablo's silkiness holds up to the salad's layered profile.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Paloma": "Reposado tequila, fresh grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The grapefruit's bite balances anchovy-parmesan dressing and vanilla softens grilled-romaine char. Strong unconventional pairing — the Paloma's brightness holds its own against the salad's layered smoke-and-salt profile.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye with the grilled Caesar — 13-year Pappy against charred romaine. Works; Van Winkle legacy doesn't drive the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage 16th Ed with the grilled Caesar — HH annual against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the añejo register carries the grilled Caesar, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver with the grilled Caesar — Patron against charred romaine. Strong; the citrus and anchovy find each other at the everyday globally-familiar register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character jars against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Patron XO Cafe lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Bouzy Grand Cru rosé Champagne — structured Pinot Noir with raspberry, red currant, mineral finish. The Burgundian body can handle anchovy-parmesan and the mineral finish meets grilled-romaine char. Works as a serious rosé alternative to the LP rosé or LP Le Cuvée for Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Grilled Caesar and the PB brownie -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab — dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the cocoa-dust plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as elegant Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — family-estate refinement on the layered classic salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand with the grilled Caesar — Grande Champagne 1er Cru against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Ferrand's floral-honey meets the char at functional register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "2015 Côte des Blancs Chardonnay Champagne — layered citrus zest, toasted brioche, and white flowers. The creamy texture meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the brioche plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong prestige pairing for grilled Caesar — vintage Champagne elevates the classic salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye with the grilled Caesar — 110-proof bold rye against charred romaine and anchovy. The high proof earns the Caesar with Maryland-style authority. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Pineau des Charentes": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The honey bridges anchovy-lemon dressing and the warm nutty Cognac plays with parm-char. Works for grilled caesar — a crisp white handles the salad cleaner.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Piedmont Barbaresco DOCG Nebbiolo — Pio Cesare Barolo's approachable sibling, floral aromatics, dried cherry, tar, firm-but-elegant tannins, high acid. The high acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the floral aromatics play against grilled-romaine char. Strong approachable-Barolo-sibling for grilled Caesar — Barbaresco elegance on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth with the grilled Caesar — softer English gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Plymouth softens the char where the London Drys cut it.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Tannin and char, umami and dark fruit — the bold red and the Caesar punch at the same level. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "2004 Pommery Cuvée Louise — aged Champagne from Reims. Almond, apricot, honey, brioche. The aged depth meets anchovy-parmesan and the brioche plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as occasion Champagne for Caesar — though this vintage belongs with more substantial courses.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Porterhouse": "The porterhouse preceded by the Caesar -- the bone-in T-bone plays against the parmesan-Caesar plate. Strong; the opener composes cleanly before the cut.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Prime Tartare": "The tartare preceded by the Caesar -- both pull from the umami register, the contrast sits flat. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Cab — blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft tannin. The lush body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the chocolate plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as polished Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — unconventional indulgent red for the classic layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|RAEN Royal St Robert": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. Cherry-driven red with the Caesar's umami and char — a classic cross-category salad pair. Strong — aligned register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Rainbow Trout": "Rainbow Trout after the Caesar -- the charred-romaine and anchovy edge opens with savory-bright lift, the trout following with delicate pan-finished flesh that the anchovy primed cleanly. Strong; bright opener into delicate fish reads classic.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit character. Bubbles cut the dressing's egg-yolk weight, and the rosé fruit survives against our cayenne croutons' heat. Clean sparkling route for a rich salad course — does real work here. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 with the grilled Caesar — single pot still Irish against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Redbreast 12's pot still spice is the featured Irish-Caesar pairing — the distinctive spice component lifts char-and-anchovy where Tullamore's drier blend rounds it.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 with the grilled Caesar — aged 21yr pot still Irish against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Redbreast 21's aged pot still spice meets the char with collector-register resonance.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Gatsby with the grilled Caesar — MGP 15yr against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Remus 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Rémy VSOP with the grilled Caesar — Fine Champagne cognac against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Rémy's elegance meets the char at functional register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo's rested-agave body finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the barrel-rested body handles the grilled Caesar, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka with the grilled Caesar — Icelandic vodka against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Reyka's mineral-glacial character meets the char cleanly; Ketel's citrus is still the sharper vodka-Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique with the grilled Caesar — aged retailer-label against charred romaine. Works; boutique-sourced doesn't drive.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Roast Half Chicken": "Roast Half Chicken after the Caesar -- the charred-romaine and anchovy edge opens with savory-bright lift, the chicken following with herbed crisp-skin roast the anchovy preceded in matching savory register. Strong; the bright opener primes the herbed bird.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Grilled Caesar and the chickpea soup together -- soup-and-salad open the meal. Works; both courses share the opening without crowding.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs — Le Mesnil Grand Cru Chardonnay with chalk, lemon curd, green apple, no dosage. The austere precision cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mineral plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong sommelier Champagne for grilled Caesar — technical pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Roku Gin": "Roku Gin's crisp floral-juniper botanicals stays alongside the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the botanical lift matches the leaf-and-acid plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey premium-sipping rum reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the high-proof register matches the grilled Caesar, and the dense weight underlines the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against aged bourbon. Works; the 10-year earns a bit more of the Caesar than younger non-wheated pours bring.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Salt-Cured Halibut after the Caesar -- the charred-romaine and anchovy edge opens with savory-bright lift, the halibut following with firm salt-cured flesh the anchovy preceded in the same brine-saline register. Strong; salt into salt reads coherent.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Sambuca": "Grilled Caesar: char-romaine with anchovy-parmesan meets Sambuca. Excellent; the sweet-anise-elderflower character meets char-and-brine at Italian register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|San Simeon Stormwatch": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mocha plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong Paso value Bordeaux blend for grilled Caesar — solid full-body on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sta. Rita Hills Santa Barbara Pinot — rich, layered, dark cherry, earth, dried herbs. The medium tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the dried-herbs play against grilled-romaine char. Strong legendary-vineyard Pinot for grilled Caesar — Santa Barbara depth on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The Caesar alongside the garlic spinach -- acid breaks the umami stack, the table earns the brighter note. Works; the table can build them in order or together.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sauternes Glass belongs on the steak course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The anise-edge nose plays against the grilled romaine's smoke directly and the rye's spice handles the anchovy-parmesan dressing. Strong unconventional pairing — the Sazerac's herbal edge meets the salad's char nicely.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye with the grilled Caesar — iconic rye against charred romaine and anchovy. The rye spice and anchovy salinity are a classic match; the benchmark rye-Caesar pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the grilled Caesar — NZ single malt against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Fortuna's distinctive NZ-whisky character meets the char at terroir-driven register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the dried rose plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak King-of-Italian for grilled Caesar — Scavino's food-demanding structure on the layered salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads — Rheingau Riesling, apricot, peach, petrol, off-dry. The off-dry fruit cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the acid plays against grilled-romaine char; petrol adds intrigue. Works as German Riesling for grilled Caesar — conversation-starter choice when guest wants something unexpected.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin, smooth. The medium tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the dark chocolate plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak by-the-glass Cab for grilled Caesar — accessible value on the layered salad. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Seafood Tower": "The seafood tower with the Caesar -- the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup meets the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: acid breaks the umami stack, the table earns the brighter note. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Seared Scallops": "The scallops with the Caesar -- the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness meets the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge. Strong; the opening courses share the table at full register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Seasonal Soup": "Grilled Caesar and the seasonal soup together -- soup-and-salad open the meal. Works; both courses share the opening without crowding.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Seasonal Vegetables": "The Caesar and the seasonal vegetables on the same meal -- the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; sequence or share, the build holds.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's structure meets the Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold dressing — weight on weight. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Shafer 1.5": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin. The plush tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mocha plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as Stags Leap Cab for grilled Caesar — Shafer opulence on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Shafer Hillside Select": "Shafer Hillside Select's bold cassis-tobacco tannin sits alongside the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the opulent body overshoots the leaf-and-acid plate, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Shrimp Bisque": "The Caesar alongside the shrimp bisque -- the garlic-bright Caesar meets quietly the sherry-bisque bowl. Works; the opening pair holds at table register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Shrimp Cocktail": "Shrimp Cocktail alongside the Caesar -- acid breaks the umami stack, the table earns the brighter note. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco with the grilled Caesar — tahona-crushed against charred romaine. Strong; earthy agave matches the Caesar's weight.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado with the grilled Caesar — tahona-crushed traditional against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; the earthy agave matches the Caesar's weight.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cassis-oak tannin sits alongside the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the opulent body overshoots the leaf-and-acid plate, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Boutique Napa family estate Cab — dark plum, cassis, toasted oak, long refined finish, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the toasted oak plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as boutique Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — Simon Family estate structure on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "St. Helena Napa Cab — Spottswoode organic family estate, plum, dark cherry, violet, silky medium tannin. The silky tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the violet plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as organic Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — Spottswoode family story on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab — blackberry, cassis, mocha, firm tannin. The full body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mocha-tannin plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong by-the-glass Cab for grilled Caesar — a red pairing that works on the layered classic salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "Napa Dollarhide Sauvignon Blanc — lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, clean lively. The lively acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the lemon-grapefruit plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong Napa Sauv Blanc for grilled Caesar — rounded body meets the layered salad, brightness keeps it bright.",
  "Grilled Caesar|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the espresso plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as estate Meritage for grilled Caesar — Napa structure on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the grilled Caesar — BT cask-strength against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Stagg Jr. is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit with the grilled Caesar — ultra-premium Russian wheat against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Elit's clarity supports the Caesar, though Ketel's citrus-floral is the sharper vodka-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Stoneleigh — Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc, passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty. The zesty acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the tropical-citrus plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for grilled Caesar — reliable by-the-glass pour on the layered classic.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Swordfish": "Swordfish after the Caesar -- the charred-romaine and anchovy edge opens with savory-bright lift, the swordfish following with meaty pan-finished steak the anchovy preceded in the same savory register. Strong; the bright opener primes the meaty main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 with the grilled Caesar — whole-citrus premium gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Tanq 10's citrus is the distinctive gin-Caesar pairing — where flagship Tanq cuts with juniper, Tanq 10 lifts with fresh citrus that reads like the lemon wedge.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray with the grilled Caesar — juniper-forward Tanq against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Tanq's proof cuts the char, though the Caesar leans toward vodkas (Ketel) cleaner. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle on the steak course instead.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the grilled Caesar, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; clean character doesn't drive the pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter — Napa Cab, rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth polished finish. The body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mocha plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as mid-tier Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — crowd-pleaser concentration on the classic layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tuscany Chianti Classico DOCG Sangiovese — Arceno vibrant cherry, dried herbs, tomato leaf, savory earth, medium body, medium tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the tomato leaf plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak Tuscan Sangiovese for grilled Caesar — Italian table wine on the layered salad. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|The BG": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Bold whiskey against the Caesar's char and umami — weight matches, but the flavors compete. Strong — both land heavy.",
  "Grilled Caesar|The Happy Wife": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this gin cocktail's citrus brightness and botanical lift. Bright, light cocktail with the Caesar's bold char — the drink reads thin against the umami. Works if the cocktail carries enough body.",
  "Grilled Caesar|The Manhattan": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build leans against the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the whiskey-build weight is misaligned with the leaf-and-acid plate, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|The Prisoner Red Blend": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The layered fruit plays with anchovy-depth, but the full body reads heavy for the salad. Works for grilled caesar — a Rhône garrigue fits anchovy harder.",
  "Grilled Caesar|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk into the Caesar -- the marbled char anchors against the anchovy-Caesar plate, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the grilled Caesar — uncut BT rye against charred romaine. Works; collector-bottle doesn't drive.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the grilled Caesar — chardonnay-cask bourbon against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; TSM Chardonnay Cask meets the char with distinctive white-wine-finish character.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Titos Vodka": "Tito's with the grilled Caesar — American vodka against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Tito's rounds the char-and-anchovy, though Ketel One's citrus lifts it cleaner.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory with the grilled Caesar — Suntory entry blend against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Toki meets the char at entry register; Hakushu's lightly-peated character is the featured Japanese-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Tomato Basil": "The Caesar alongside the tomato basil -- the grilled-Caesar dressing meets quietly the roasted-tomato bisque. Works; the opening pair holds at table register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Transfusion": "Hangar 1, muddled black grapes, lime, ginger beer. The tart grape and lime balance anchovy-parmesan dressing and ginger beer's dry finish cleans against grilled-romaine char. Strong pairing — the Transfusion's fruit-led refreshment holds up to the salad's layered profile.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado with the grilled Caesar — cedar-wood-aged against charred romaine and anchovy. The cedar and char meet at a distinctive register; a standout unconventional pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Truffle Fries": "The Caesar alongside the truffle fries -- both bring cheese as the headline. Avoid; one is enough. Pair the Caesar with Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo; pair the truffle fries with The Tomahawk, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Grilled Caesar: charred romaine and anchovy meet slightly drier Irish blend. Tullamore's drier-classic character is the featured entry-Irish-Caesar pairing — where Jameson's smoothness rounds the char, Tullamore's drier character cuts it more sharply at heritage register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna after the Caesar -- the charred-romaine and anchovy edge opens with savory-bright lift, the tuna following with seared-rare flesh and sesame crust the anchovy primed cleanly. Strong; the savory opener sets up the seared main.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Grilled Caesar with the curry -- the grilled-Caesar dressing wraps the spiced vegetable plate, and the spice lifts the green plate. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Venge Family Reserve": "Kirk Venge flagship Oakville Cab — concentrated blackcurrant, dark chocolate, vanilla, seamless polished finish, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the dark chocolate plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as Oakville flagship for grilled Caesar — Venge opulence on the layered salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Venge Scout's Honor": "Napa Zin-led blend — Kirk Venge's Scout's Honor, blackberry, boysenberry, black pepper, vanilla, full body, generous. The full body reads heavy against anchovy-lemon dressing, though black pepper meets parm-char bite. Works for grilled caesar — Chablis or Sancerre fits the salad cleaner.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Venge Silencieux": "Venge Silencieux's bold cherry-chocolate cassis reads alongside the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the opulent body overshoots the leaf-and-acid plate, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Vesper": "Nolet's gin, Ketel One vodka, Lillet. The martini's clean dryness cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and Lillet's citrus plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as unconventional pairing — cocktail handles salad's layered profile without overwhelming.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Yellow label Veuve — Reims Pinot Noir-dominant Champagne. Ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse. The lively acidity cuts anchovy-parmesan and the biscuit plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong iconic Champagne for the Caesar — recognizable crowd-pleaser elevates the salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. The cocktail's herbal complexity and cognac depth can stand alongside grilled romaine's smoke; Bénédictine plays against anchovy-parmesan dressing. Works when the guest wants a spirit-forward opener alongside a charred salad.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Villon Cognac": "Villon with the grilled Caesar — saffron/elderflower cognac against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Villon's infusion meets the char at functional register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Vin Santo on the steak course instead.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Weller 107": "Weller 107 with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine meets higher-proof heat. Works; the pour's boldness pushes slightly past what the Caesar needs, but the char absorbs some intensity.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year with the grilled Caesar — anchovy salinity and charred romaine meet aged honeyed wheat. The 12-year's depth saves this pairing from being too polite; Weller Special Reserve and Weller 107 can't quite earn this Caesar the same way. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium with the grilled Caesar — the Millennium's aged honey and oak against charred romaine and anchovy. The Caesar actually meets this pour in a way House Wedge doesn't; strong but ambitious. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against 90-proof Weller. The pairing is capable but the gateway Weller doesn't have the depth Weller 12 brings to this Caesar. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley with the grilled Caesar — BT wheat vodka against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Wheatley supports the Caesar cleanly; Ketel's citrus is still the sharper vodka-Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the grilled Caesar — 100-proof aged rye against charred romaine. Works; aged character doesn't quite drive the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with the grilled Caesar — fruit-cask WP against charred romaine. Works; Old World fruit doesn't drive the Caesar's savory register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with the grilled Caesar — 15-year WP against charred romaine. Works; flagship aged character doesn't quite drive the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with the grilled Caesar — allocated WP against charred romaine. Works; collector-bottle character doesn't drive the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with the grilled Caesar — X Commandments against charred romaine. Works; 10th-edition doesn't drive the Caesar.",
  "Grilled Caesar|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year with the grilled Caesar — 100-proof WP rye against charred romaine. The Vermont-brand character earns the Caesar with distinctive rye voice. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with the grilled Caesar — craft oak-spice against charred romaine. Strong; the distinctive character earns the Caesar in a way the everyday bourbons don't.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against fruit-forward bourbon. Works; Woodford's complexity earns the Caesar a bit more than most everyday bourbons.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the bourbon weight handles the grilled Caesar, and the vanilla underlines the rendered char. Works; the private select's layered profile underserves the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the grilled Caesar — balanced small-batch rye against charred romaine. The fruit-floral character earns the Caesar with small-batch elegance. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with the grilled Caesar — Pacific Northwest craft against charred romaine. The PNW character earns the Caesar with regional distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 with the grilled Caesar — Suntory flagship against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Yamazaki 12's honey-fruit character meets the char at flagship register; Hakushu's lightly-peated is the featured Japanese-whisky-Caesar match.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 with the grilled Caesar — aged flagship Japanese single malt against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Yamazaki 18's aged character meets the char at collector register.",
  "Grilled Caesar|Yellow Chartreuse": "Grilled Caesar: char-romaine with anchovy-parmesan meets Yellow Chartreuse. Excellent; the honey-herbal character meets char-and-brine at gentler-Carthusian register.",
  "Gumbo|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon depth settles into the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Gumbo|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon body sits with the gumbo, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Gumbo|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's bold cherry-coffee cassis stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Gumbo|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gumbo|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the Highland register carries the gumbo, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; boutique-sourced wasted on salad.",
  "Gumbo|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the malt-driven body wraps the gumbo, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; the richer pour finds more with the dessert than Original brings.",
  "Gumbo|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-aged tequila body threads the gumbo, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Gumbo|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the aged-agave weight wraps the gumbo, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Gumbo|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Gumbo|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Gumbo|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin sits beside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Gumbo|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Gumbo|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Ancho Reyes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Gumbo|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gumbo|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon body sits with the gumbo, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; Vermont-brand character doesn't drive.",
  "Gumbo|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon depth settles into the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; AE's fruity-jammy character engages the bleu at wine-finish register.",
  "Gumbo|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Aperol belongs on the steak course.",
  "Gumbo|Asparagus": "The gumbo and the asparagus on the same meal -- acid plays against warmth, the meal carries the cooler note through. Works; sequence or share, the build holds.",
  "Gumbo|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The gumbo alongside the au gratin -- the spiced course breaks the cream, the table reads balanced. Works; the table can build them in order or together.",
  "Gumbo|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Gumbo|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Gumbo|Aviation": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build sits alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Gumbo|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the orange meets the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Gumbo|Avion 44": "Avion 44's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-aged tequila body threads the gumbo, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; multi-cask complexity composed with this dish at distinctive register.",
  "Gumbo|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's unaged silver-spirit body leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the unaged body brightens the gumbo, and the green-agave edge brightens the bowl. Works; clean composes cleanly.",
  "Gumbo|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the unaged body brightens the gumbo, and the unaged-cane note frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Gumbo|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Baileys Irish Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Gumbo|Baker's": "Baker's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon backbone carries the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; annual-release complexity over-specified.",
  "Gumbo|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the scotch depth settles into the gumbo, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Gumbo|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the malt-and-oak threads the gumbo, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "Gumbo|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the scotch backbone matches the gumbo, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; the boldness pushes slightly past what the soup asks for.",
  "Gumbo|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood's aged Port-finished Speyside sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the gumbo, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive but adds interest.",
  "Gumbo|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon body sits with the gumbo, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Gumbo|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon weight handles the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; craft-blend covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Gumbo|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the caramel-spice register matches the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Gumbo|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the gumbo, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Gumbo|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the caramel-spice register matches the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; own-plus-sourced covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Gumbo|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon backbone carries the gumbo, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; oak slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Gumbo|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak depth lays under the gumbo, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Gumbo|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon depth settles into the gumbo, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; annual-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Gumbo|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the high-rye spice register matches the gumbo, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; bold rye slightly over-specified.",
  "Gumbo|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon depth settles into the gumbo, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Gumbo|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon weight handles the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; a composed but not remarkable match.",
  "Gumbo|Bee's Knees": "Bee's Knees's crisp butter-citrus build leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the honey balances the spice. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Gumbo|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater Gin's London-Dry-juniper botanicals stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Gumbo|Beignets": "Gumbo and the beignets -- two registers in contrast, the meal earns its shape. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Gumbo|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere Vodka's bold Polish-rye-clean neutrality finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Gumbo|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon depth settles into the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; BH Red Wine Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
