  "Gumbo|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Gumbo|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the rye backbone drives the gumbo, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Gumbo|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the rye spice cuts the gumbo, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; the match is gentle and approachable.",
  "Gumbo|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the gumbo, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Gumbo|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the rye-and-oak weight handles the gumbo, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Gumbo|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the gumbo, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; curated single-barrel covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Gumbo|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the dry rye body wraps the gumbo, and the rye-grain bite cuts the bowl. Works; HH 20 frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Gumbo|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the caramel-spice register matches the gumbo, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; the oak spice is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Gumbo|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the caramel-spice register matches the gumbo, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Gumbo|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon depth settles into the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Gumbo|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the rye spice cuts the gumbo, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; private-selection wasted on the salad.",
  "Gumbo|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the dry rye body wraps the gumbo, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; milestone-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Gumbo|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the Japanese-whisky precision frames the gumbo, and the silky body rounds the bowl. Works; the 10-year holds without lifting.",
  "Gumbo|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the gumbo, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the bowl. Works; premium proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Gumbo|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Yellow Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Asparagus": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Beignets": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Bone Marrow": "Gundlach Bundschu — Sonoma Cab, blackberry, cedar, dried herb, firm tannin. The savory body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the cedar-herb frames the bone depth. Strong savory Sonoma Cab for marrow — restrained Rhinefarm character offers a different register than Napa lushness.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Bone-In Filet": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's bold earth-umami cassis matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the firm tannin reads cleanly against the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Broccoli Cheddar": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Broccolini": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Burrata": "Savory Sonoma Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm-tannin cedar-herb crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackberry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Gundlach Bundschu for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Butternut Squash": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Carrot Cake": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Cheesecake": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Chilean Seabass": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Chocolate Brownie": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Chocolate Cake": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Clam Chowder": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Crab Cake": "Savory Sonoma Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm-tannin cedar-herb body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Gundlach Bundschu for the steak course where its Rhinefarm restrained character shines.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Creamy Potato": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Creme Brulee": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Escargot": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's bold earth-umami cassis frames the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Faroe Island Salmon": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Filet Mignon": "Sonoma Cab — blackberry, cedar, dried herb, firm tannin, restrained classic style. The savory body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the cedar-herb plays against escargot-butter toppers. Strong savory Sonoma Cab for filet — historic restrained character offers a different frame from Napa Cabs.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|French Onion": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's bold earth-umami cassis plays cleanly against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The Caesar's anchovy-parmesan depth gives the bold red's tannin something real to work against. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Gumbo": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|House Wedge": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Kansas City": "Kansas City: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Loaded Potato": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's bold earth-umami cassis stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis cuts the cheese richness. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Lobster Mac": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's bold earth-umami cassis holds with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the opulent body matches the indulgent side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Market Fish": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Mocha Creme": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Mushroom Bisque": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's bold earth-umami cassis stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the bold red's cassis grip carries the mushroom-and-truffle depth — structured weight on the earthy side. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Prime Tartare": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's bold earth-umami cassis holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Rainbow Trout": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Roast Half Chicken": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's bold earth-umami cassis sits beside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Seafood Tower": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Seared Scallops": "Sonoma Rhinefarm Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — savory cedar-herb obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Gundlach Bundschu for the steak course where its savory Sonoma character peaks.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Seasonal Soup": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Seasonal Vegetables": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Shrimp Bisque": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's bold earth-umami cassis reads alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis frames the herb butter. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Shrimp Cocktail": "Gundlach Bundschu Sonoma Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — savory cedar-herb is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save the Rhinefarm Cab for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Swordfish": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|The Tomahawk": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured dark-fruit weight meets at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the dark-fruit body sits with the 36oz showpiece, and the blackberry edge underlines the cut. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Tomato Basil": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the bold red's cassis grip carries the mushroom-and-truffle depth — structured weight on the earthy side. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Asparagus": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality stays alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Au Gratin Potatoes": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality plays cleanly against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Beignets": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the HDW 100 Vodka for the steak course.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Bone Marrow": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality sits beside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Bone-In Filet": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the HDW 100 Vodka for another course.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Broccoli Cheddar": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Broccolini": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality stays alongside the broccolini's tender green — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Brussels and Belly": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality sits alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the herbs frame the grilled char. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Burrata": "HDW 100 with burrata — higher-proof craft vodka against fresh dairy. Strong; HDW 100's backbone cuts cream rather than riding it; Chopin-style creamy match it is not, but the proof gives it distinctive character.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Butternut Squash": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Carrot Cake": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. HDW 100 Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Cheesecake": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a vodka. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the HDW 100 Vodka for the steak course.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Chilean Seabass": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality finds register with the seabass's buttery flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Chocolate Brownie": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the HDW 100 Vodka for the steak course.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Chocolate Cake": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the HDW 100 Vodka for the steak course.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Clam Chowder": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Cowboy Ribeye": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the HDW 100 Vodka for another course.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Crab Cake": "HDW 100 with crab cake — higher-backbone craft vodka against delicate lump crab. Excellent; HDW 100's proof lifts the crab with more character than standard vodka while staying clean enough not to overwhelm.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Creamed Spinach": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Creamy Potato": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality plays cleanly against the potato soup's silky body — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Creme Brulee": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the HDW 100 Vodka for the steak course.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Escargot": "HDW 100 with escargot — 100-proof craft vodka against garlic-butter snails. Excellent; HDW 100's extra ABV cuts butter harder than Detroit City or Hangar 1 can — the featured craft-vodka-escargot pairing.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Faroe Island Salmon": "HDW 100 with the salmon — 100-proof craft vodka against oily salmon. Strong; HDW 100's extra ABV frames the fish's richness with herbal lift.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Filet Mignon": "HDW 100 with the filet — 100-proof craft vodka against tenderloin. Works; vodka over-specified; HDW 100's extra proof is the most credible craft-vodka-steak case.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|French Onion": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality stays alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Grilled Caesar": "HDW 100 with the grilled Caesar — higher-proof craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; HDW 100 cuts the char more aggressively than the 80-proof siblings; Hangar 1's floral-grape is still the sharpest craft-Caesar.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Gumbo": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality sits beside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "HDW 100 Vodka|Honey Roasted Carrots": "HDW 100 Vodka's char-herbal neutrality stays alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
