  "Head Fake|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build plays cleanly against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Head Fake|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build matches the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the sweet character meets the fish at digestif register, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Head Fake|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build sits beside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Head Fake|Seafood Tower": "Wheatley vodka, limoncello, triple sec, lemon, simple syrup. The citrus-forward profile handles the raw-bar spread and Wheatley's clean finish doesn't crush oysters or shrimp. Strong tower cocktail when guest wants sweet-and-bright rather than Champagne or French 75.",
  "Head Fake|Seared Scallops": "Wheatley vodka, limoncello, triple sec, lemon, simple syrup. The lemon-and-citrus profile meets scallops' clean-seared sweetness and Wheatley's wheat-based clean finish doesn't crush the delicacy. Peak bright-and-sweet cocktail for scallops when guest wants citrus over martini.",
  "Head Fake|Seasonal Soup": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house build — the sweet character sits at digestif register. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Head Fake|Seasonal Vegetables": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build sits beside the seasonal garden plate — the sweet character sits at digestif register. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Head Fake|Shrimp Bisque": "Wheatley vodka, limoncello, triple sec, lemon, simple syrup. The limoncello-and-lemon cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and the sugar rim adds contrast against the soup's weight. Strong bright-cocktail pairing for bisque when guest wants citrus-forward.",
  "Head Fake|Shrimp Cocktail": "Wheatley vodka, limoncello, triple sec, lemon, simple syrup — sugar rim. The Italian-citrus limoncello rounds the lemon and cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine; the sugar rim plays against cocktail-sauce. Peak bright-citrus starter cocktail for shrimp cocktail — approachable lemon drop done right.",
  "Head Fake|Swordfish": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build reads alongside the swordfish's meaty flesh — the sweet character meets the fish at digestif register, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Head Fake|The Tomahawk": "Sweet-and-citrus lemon-drop cocktail against the biggest steak on the menu — zero structural overlap. Head Fake's delicacy is crushed by marbled Tomahawk weight. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "Head Fake|Tomato Basil": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build sits beside the tomato basil's bright acid — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Head Fake|Truffle Fries": "Wheatley vodka, limoncello, triple sec, lemon, simple syrup, sugar rim — a lemon drop, essentially. Sweet citrus against truffle fries with garlic-Dijon fry sauce is a bad axis; never pair. Route to dessert. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Head Fake|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build reads alongside the tuna's seared rare crust — the sweet character meets the fish at digestif register, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Head Fake|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Head Fake's gin-or-tequila cocktail lift finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bright build frames the curry, and the citrus cuts the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Asparagus": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the caramel-spice register matches the asparagus, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; distinctive character gives the dessert a unique story-frame.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the au gratin — HH 20yr against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Strong; HH 20's 20-year depth handles the richness at collector register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Beignets": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon body sits with the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; too light for dessert density at this level.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Bone Marrow": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with bone marrow — HH 20yr collector against umami fat. Excellent; HH 20's 20-year-aged depth engages marrow at collector register — the featured aged-HH marrow pairing.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Bone-In Filet": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the bone-in filet — HH 20yr collector against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; HH 20's aged-HH character frames the cut at collector-HH register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Broccoli Cheddar": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon depth settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side; save the rare bottle for the main.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Broccolini": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon weight handles the broccolini, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Brussels and Belly": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with Brussels + Belly — 20yr HH against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Burrata": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with burrata — HH 20yr collector against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Butternut Squash": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the squash bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Carrot Cake": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with carrot cake — HH 20yr collector against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; HH 20's aged dried-fruit threads the cake at collector register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Cheesecake": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with cheesecake — 20yr aged HH against custard. Works; HH 20's aged character threads the cheesecake at collector register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Chilean Seabass": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Heaven Hill 20 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Chocolate Brownie": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the chocolate brownie — 20yr aged HH against cocoa. Excellent; HH 20's aged leather-tobacco-dried-fruit is the featured aged-HH-chocolate match — 20 years of aging deepens the cocoa resonance.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Chocolate Cake": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the brown-spirit warmth threads the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; stout-barrel story wasted on salad.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Clam Chowder": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon depth settles into the chowder, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Cowboy Ribeye": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the cowboy ribeye — 20yr HH collector against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; HH 20's aged depth frames the ribeye at collector register. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Crab Cake": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with crab cake — HH 20yr against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Creamed Spinach": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with creamed spinach — HH 20yr aged against dairy-rich side. Strong; HH 20's aged character rides the cream at collector-HH register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Creamy Potato": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon weight handles the potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; blended character doesn't drive.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Creme Brulee": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Escargot": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's bold aged-oak character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Faroe Island Salmon": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Heaven Hill 20 Year belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Filet Mignon": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the filet — HH 20yr collector limited release, deeper aged HH character beyond EC 18. Excellent; HH 20's collector-register depth is the featured aged-HH-filet pairing — where EC 18 brings aged-HH polish, the 20 closes with HH's deepest aged expression.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|French Onion": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the oak depth lays under the French onion, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; Discovery complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Grilled Caesar": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the grilled Caesar — HH 20yr against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; HH 20 is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Gumbo": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon backbone carries the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; pour is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|House Wedge": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the brown-spirit warmth threads the wedge, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; the pour's delicacy doesn't overpower the soup, but doesn't particularly elevate it either.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Kansas City": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the KC — HH 20yr collector against an 18oz strip. Strong; HH 20 is the featured aged-HH-KC pairing — the 20-year depth handles marbling at collector register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Loaded Potato": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the brown-spirit warmth threads the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; extended-aged premium wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Lobster Mac": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the brown-spirit warmth threads the lobster mac, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a soup; save the rare bottle.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Market Fish": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Heaven Hill 20 Year for the steak course.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Mocha Creme": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Mushroom Bisque": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the oak depth lays under the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the intensity overpowers this dish.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Mushrooms": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with mushrooms — HH 20yr aged against earthy umami. Excellent; HH 20's aged-HH depth threads mushroom earthiness at collector register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the PB brownie — HH 20yr against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Porterhouse": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the porterhouse — HH 20yr against strip-and-filet. Works; HH 20 frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Prime Tartare": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's bold aged-oak character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Rainbow Trout": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Heaven Hill 20 Year for the steak course.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Roast Half Chicken": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon body sits with the roast chicken, and the oak frames the plate. Works; boutique character slightly over-specified.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the caramel-spice register matches the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; Remus 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Heaven Hill 20 Year for the steak course.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon body sits with the garlic spinach, and the oak frames the side. Works; boutique-craft doesn't drive.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Seafood Tower": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Heaven Hill 20 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Seared Scallops": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with seared scallops — HH 20yr aged against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Seasonal Soup": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon body sits with the seasonal soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Seasonal Vegetables": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon backbone carries the seasonal vegetables, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Shrimp Bisque": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the toast notes don't really interplay with the soup but don't clash.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Shrimp Cocktail": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with shrimp cocktail — HH 20yr against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Swordfish": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Heaven Hill 20 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|The Tomahawk": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the Tomahawk — HH 20yr collector against 32oz cap-fat. Works; HH 20's aged character is under-specified for Tomahawk intensity; reach for a barrel-proof bourbon.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Tomato Basil": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Truffle Fries": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with truffle fries — HH 20yr aged against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; HH 20's 20-year depth meets the earthy-rich at collector register.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Heaven Hill 20 Year for the steak course.",
  "Heaven Hill 20 Year|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Asparagus": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon depth settles into the asparagus, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with au gratin potatoes — 100-proof BIB against cheese-and-cream. Strong; classic match with sturdy bonded character.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Beignets": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; too light for dessert density at this level.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Bone Marrow": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with bone marrow — 100-proof bond against umami fat. The BIB weight meets the marrow's intensity cleanly; classic Kentucky approach, distinct from the Wellers' wheated take. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Bone-In Filet": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's bold caramel-rye-spice character matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth carries the cut, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Broccoli Cheddar": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon depth settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; distinctive character is worth mentioning tableside, even if the pairing isn't marquee.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Broccolini": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon body sits with the broccolini, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; Remus 15 frames the two-cut plate at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Brussels and Belly": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with Brussels + Belly — 100-proof bond meets maple-glazed pork belly. BIB's structure handles the dish's sweetness with more grip than Wellers' wheated softness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Burrata": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with burrata — fresh dairy against 100-proof BIB. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Butternut Squash": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the caramel-spice register matches the squash bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for this dish's register.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Carrot Cake": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with carrot cake — 100-proof traditional against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; the BIB oak and vanilla frame the cake cleanly, distinct from wheated-cake-spice pairings.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Cheesecake": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with cheesecake — 100-proof traditional against custard. Works; BIB structure is a touch over-specified for the dessert.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Chilean Seabass": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Chocolate Brownie": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the chocolate brownie — 100-proof BIB against cocoa. Works; for more cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Basil Hayden's Toast. HH BIB is the sipping-bond answer.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Chocolate Cake": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the oak depth lays under the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the sweetness is a touch much for the salad's simplicity.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Clam Chowder": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon depth settles into the chowder, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; Caribbean Cask rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Cowboy Ribeye": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100 proof BIB against the cut's cap fat. The bonded weight handles the ribeye where 90-proof Wellers fade; classic Kentucky BIB for the biggest cuts. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Crab Cake": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with crab cake — 100-proof BIB against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Creamed Spinach": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with creamed spinach — 100-proof traditional bourbon against dairy. Strong; the BIB structure sits beside the side with character distinct from wheated softness.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Creamy Potato": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Creme Brulee": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Escargot": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's bold caramel-rye-spice character lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Faroe Island Salmon": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond for the steak course.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Filet Mignon": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the filet — 100 proof, traditional BIB. Heaven Hill's workhorse bond — caramel, vanilla, and sturdy oak at the classic BIB strength. Distinct from the Wellers' wheated softness and BT's flagship cleanliness; the traditional-BIB approach to the filet. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|French Onion": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Grilled Caesar": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against 100-proof BIB. Works; the bonded weight is slightly ambitious for the Caesar but the char absorbs some of it.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Gumbo": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon depth settles into the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon backbone carries the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; complexity wasted on salad.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|House Wedge": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against 100-proof BIB. Works as a separate sip; the bonded weight overpowers the salad.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Kansas City": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the KC — 100-proof BIB against the strip's marbling. The bonded proof gives the pour weight the 90-proof Wellers don't quite reach; a sturdy, dependable strip pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Loaded Potato": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the private select's layered profile underserves the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Lobster Mac": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon body sits with the lobster mac, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Market Fish": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond for the steak course.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Mocha Creme": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon body sits with the mocha creme, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Mushroom Bisque": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; single-barrel depth gives the dessert more than Small Batch.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Mushrooms": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the mushrooms — 100-proof BIB against earthy umami. The bonded weight meets the side's depth; a traditional Kentucky side pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon weight handles the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; bonded weight pushes past the soup's register slightly.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Porterhouse": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the porterhouse — 100-proof across strip and filet sides. Strong; the BIB weight leans toward the strip side, the filet side tolerates it.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Prime Tartare": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's bold caramel-rye-spice character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Rainbow Trout": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond for the steak course.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Roast Half Chicken": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon backbone carries the roast chicken, and the rounded sweetness brightens the plate. Works; Vermont-brand character doesn't drive.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the chickpea soup, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; a composed but not remarkable match.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond for the steak course.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon backbone carries the garlic spinach, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Seafood Tower": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Seared Scallops": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against bonded weight. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Seasonal Soup": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon weight handles the seasonal soup, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; boutique covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Seasonal Vegetables": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon body sits with the seasonal vegetables, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Shrimp Bisque": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood against 100-proof BIB. Works; bonded weight pushes past the soup's register slightly.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Shrimp Cocktail": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against 100-proof bond. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Swordfish": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond for the steak course.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|The Tomahawk": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's bold caramel-rye-spice character lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the caramel-and-oak weight rises with the cap-fat, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Tomato Basil": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon backbone carries the tomato basil, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; annual-release complexity over-specified.",
  "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond|Truffle Fries": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with truffle fries — 100-proof traditional against truffle-parmesan. The BIB's straight oak and caramel give the side a clean, sturdy frame; no pretense, just classic Kentucky. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
