  "Heaven's Door 10 Year|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Heaven's Door 10 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon body sits with the garlic spinach, and the oak frames the side. Works; curated single-barrel slightly over-specified.",
  "Heaven's Door 10 Year|Seafood Tower": "Heaven's Door 10 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Heaven's Door 10 Year for the steak course.",
  "Heaven's Door 10 Year|Seared Scallops": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against aged premium. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Heaven's Door 10 Year|Seasonal Soup": "Heaven's Door 10 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; doesn't drive.",
  "Heaven's Door 10 Year|Seasonal Vegetables": "Heaven's Door 10 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon backbone carries the seasonal vegetables, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; extended-aged premium wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Heaven's Door 10 Year|Shrimp Bisque": "Heaven's Door 10 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; distinctive finish over-specified.",
  "Heaven's Door 10 Year|Shrimp Cocktail": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against aged Dylan bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Heaven's Door 10 Year|Swordfish": "Heaven's Door 10 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Heaven's Door 10 Year for the steak course.",
  "Heaven's Door 10 Year|The Tomahawk": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with the Tomahawk — 100-proof 10-year against bone-in ribeye. Strong; the aged bonded weight handles the cut — Henry McKenna 10 BIB, E.H. Taylor, or Baker's bring similar authority with different distillery stories.",
  "Heaven's Door 10 Year|Tomato Basil": "Heaven's Door 10 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; distinctive character is worth mentioning tableside, even if the pairing isn't marquee.",
  "Heaven's Door 10 Year|Truffle Fries": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with truffle fries — 100-proof 10-year against truffle-parmesan. The aging depth gives the side grip; a storytelling premium pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Heaven's Door 10 Year|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Heaven's Door 10 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Heaven's Door 10 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Heaven's Door 10 Year|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Heaven's Door 10 Year's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; aged character overshoots delicacy.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Asparagus": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Heitz Martha's Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: the structured red's grip carries the rich side's dairy load; oak-driven body absorbs the cream and tannin clears the finish. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Beignets": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Heitz Martha's Vineyard on the steak course instead.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Richness from the plate and structure from the wine share the same register — a savory weight-on-weight setup. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Bone-In Filet": "Bone-In Filet: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The dark-fruit frame supports the filet, but the tannin rides harder than the lean profile demands. A strong match — the tannin and filet tenderness align cleanly. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Broccoli Cheddar": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Heitz Martha's Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Broccolini": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the broccolini's tender green stalks -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Heitz Martha's Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Burrata": "Iconic Napa Cab against fresh burrata — Martha's Vineyard's tannin and mint-cedar intensity annihilates the creamy-fresh dairy starter. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Champagne, or crisp Italian whites; save Heitz for the beef course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Butternut Squash": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Heitz Martha's Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Carrot Cake": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Cheesecake": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Heitz Martha's Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Chilean Seabass": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Chocolate Brownie": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Chocolate Cake": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Clam Chowder": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Heitz Martha's Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Crab Cake": "Iconic Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — Martha's Vineyard's eucalyptus-and-cedar intensity crushes the romesco-and-crab delicacy. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne; save Heitz for the steak course where its legendary Rutherford character shines.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Creamed Spinach": "The fat in the cream binds to Martha's Vineyard's tannins at the molecular level, smoothing the wine's firm structure into something richer and more supple than it presents on its own. That softening effect lets the wine's dark cherry and cedar come forward against the savory, dairy-sweet backdrop of the spinach, turning a side dish into a legitimate vehicle for one of Napa's most iconic Cabs. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Creamy Potato": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Heitz Martha's Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Creme Brulee": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Escargot": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's bold eucalyptus-herbal cassis finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Faroe Island Salmon": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Filet Mignon": "The filet's mild myoglobin flavor and butter-basted tenderness give Martha's Vineyard's bold tannin structure room to dominate the pairing — the wine essentially seasons the steak the way a sauce would. What you get is the full aromatic expression of the Cab, mint and cedar and dark fruit, framed by the clean, neutral canvas of the filet without any competing fat or char. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|French Onion": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's bold eucalyptus-herbal cassis holds with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Grilled Caesar": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the structured body wraps the grilled Caesar, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Gumbo": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Heitz Martha's Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Heitz Martha's Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|House Wedge": "Napa icon from Oakville's Martha's Vineyard, decades-aged reputation. Blue cheese is a specific wine-killer — this bottle deserves the Tomahawk or Porterhouse. Never pour on a wedge; the guest and the wine both lose. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Kansas City": "Kansas City: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Loaded Potato": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's bold eucalyptus-herbal cassis leans against the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Lobster Mac": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's bold eucalyptus-herbal cassis leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Market Fish": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Mocha Creme": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Mushroom Bisque": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's bold eucalyptus-herbal cassis reads alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the bold red's cassis grip carries the mushroom-and-truffle depth — structured weight on the earthy side. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Heitz Martha's Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Prime Tartare": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth. The starter's fat and umami meet the bold red's dark fruit — both land on the palate with real weight. Not the sharpest pairing. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Rainbow Trout": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Heitz Martha's Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Roast Half Chicken": "Napa icon from Oakville's Martha's Vineyard — the $600+ prestige Cabernet. The tannin absolutely crushes roasted chicken; the wine deserves the steak course. Never pour on the chicken — steer guest firmly toward the Tomahawk or Porterhouse. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Heitz Martha's Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Heitz Martha's Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Seafood Tower": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Seared Scallops": "Iconic Napa Cab against seared scallops' clean caramelized shellfish — Heitz's bold menthol-cedar signature obliterates the delicate sweet sear. Never pair. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc; save Martha's Vineyard for the Tomahawk or Porterhouse.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Seasonal Soup": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Heitz Martha's Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Seasonal Vegetables": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Shrimp Bisque": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's bold eucalyptus-herbal cassis plays cleanly against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Shrimp Cocktail": "Iconic Napa Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — Martha's Vineyard's concentrated dark fruit and cedar are wildly wrong for a cold shellfish classic. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis; save the legendary Heitz for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Swordfish": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|The Tomahawk": "The wine's signature eucalyptus and cedar cut through the thick intramuscular fat of the tomahawk the way fresh herb does in a compound butter, providing aromatic contrast to the beef's Maillard-rich crust. On the palate, the fat coats and softens the tannins while the wine's menthol lift keeps each bite from feeling heavy, turning a powerful steak into something more elegant. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Tomato Basil": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Heitz Martha's Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit anchors the mushroom's umami weight; firm tannin and oak hold their register. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Heitz Martha's Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Martha's Vineyard|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Heitz Martha's Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Asparagus": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Heitz Trailside Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Beignets": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Heitz Trailside Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Bone Marrow": "Heitz Trailside — Rutherford Cab with dark cherry, blackcurrant, mint, cedar, refined tannin. The body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the mint-cedar frames the bone depth. Peak historic-house Cab for marrow — Heitz provenance plus Trailside approachable balance on the rich starter. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Bone-In Filet": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's bold cherry-mint cassis reads alongside the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the concentrated body carries the strip, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Broccoli Cheddar": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Broccolini": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Burrata": "Rutherford Heitz Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — refined mint-cedar body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with dark cherry-blackcurrant. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Heitz Trailside for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Butternut Squash": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Carrot Cake": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Heitz Trailside Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Cheesecake": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Heitz Trailside Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Chilean Seabass": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Chocolate Brownie": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Heitz Trailside Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Chocolate Cake": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Heitz Trailside Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Clam Chowder": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Cowboy Ribeye": "Rutherford Heitz Cab — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mint, cedar, refined firm tannin. The balanced body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the mint-cedar meets rendered fat. Strong historic-house Cab for Cowboy — Heitz provenance plus Trailside balance, though bigger Cabs like Silver Oak or Caymus peak higher for fattiest ribeye.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Crab Cake": "Rutherford Heitz Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — firm refined mint-cedar body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Heitz Trailside for the steak course where its historic-house character shines.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Creamy Potato": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Heitz Trailside Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Creme Brulee": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Escargot": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's bold cherry-mint cassis plays cleanly against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Faroe Island Salmon": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Filet Mignon": "Heitz Trailside — Rutherford Cab from one of Napa's great historic houses. More approachable style than legendary Martha's: dark cherry, blackcurrant, mint, cedar, firm but refined tannin. The body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the mint-cedar echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak historic-house Cab for filet. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|French Onion": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's bold cherry-mint cassis finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Grilled Caesar": "Heitz Trailside — Rutherford Cab with dark cherry, blackcurrant, mint, cedar. The balanced body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mint-cedar plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as historic-house Cab for grilled Caesar — Heitz provenance on the classic layered salad.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Gumbo": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|House Wedge": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Kansas City": "Kansas City: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Loaded Potato": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's bold cherry-mint cassis sits beside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Lobster Mac": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's bold cherry-mint cassis finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Market Fish": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Mocha Creme": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Heitz Trailside Vineyard on the steak course instead.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Mushroom Bisque": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's bold cherry-mint cassis stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: Cab tannin frames the earthy mushroom-and-truffle depth; dark fruit lifts the umami into the wine's body. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Heitz Trailside Vineyard on the steak course instead.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Prime Tartare": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's bold cherry-mint cassis plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Rainbow Trout": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Heitz Trailside Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Roast Half Chicken": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's bold cherry-mint cassis holds with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character jars against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Heitz Trailside Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character overshadows the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Heitz Trailside Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Seafood Tower": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Seared Scallops": "Heitz Trailside Rutherford Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — refined mint-cedar obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Heitz Trailside for the steak course where its historic-house Rutherford character peaks.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Seasonal Soup": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Heitz Trailside Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Seasonal Vegetables": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character overshadows the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Heitz Trailside Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Shrimp Bisque": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the icon-Cab body handles the shrimp bisque, and the graphite-and-mint plays against the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Shrimp Cocktail": "Heitz Trailside Rutherford Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — refined mint-cedar is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Heitz Trailside for steak where its historic-house character belongs.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Swordfish": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Heitz Trailside Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Tomato Basil": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character overwhelms the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: Cab tannin frames the earthy mushroom-and-truffle depth; dark fruit lifts the umami into the wine's body. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Heitz Trailside Vineyard|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's Napa-icon Cab character overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Heitz Trailside Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Asparagus": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals sits alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey threads the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Beignets": "Flora Adora with beignets — Hendrick's lavish-floral Spring release against fried dough. Excellent; Flora Adora's honeysuckle-chamomile-elderflower is a distinctive floral-with-sugar bridge more dimensional than flagship Hendrick's.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Bone Marrow": "Flora Adora with bone marrow — floral-garden Hendrick's against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Bone-In Filet": "Hendricks Flora Adora's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Hendricks Flora Adora for another course.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Broccoli Cheddar": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Broccolini": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Brussels and Belly": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals plays cleanly against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Burrata": "Flora Adora with burrata — Hendrick's Spring release, \"lavish floral garden\" profile (honeysuckle, chamomile, primrose, elderflower, hibiscus, meadowsweet). Excellent; a confident headline pairing.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Butternut Squash": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Carrot Cake": "Hendricks Flora Adora's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hendricks Flora Adora lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Cheesecake": "Flora Adora with cheesecake — Spring-release floral Hendrick's against custard. Works; Flora Adora's 6-floral-botanical threads the cheesecake at distinctive register; Grand Cabaret's black currant is the featured Hendrick's-cheesecake match.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Chilean Seabass": "Hendricks Flora Adora's juniper-and-citrus lift rests at register with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the floral edge frames the seabass, and the dry gin lift plays against the plate. Strong; the gin option when the gold call is spoken for.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Chocolate Brownie": "Hendricks Flora Adora's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Hendricks Flora Adora for the steak course.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Chocolate Cake": "Hendricks Flora Adora's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hendricks Flora Adora lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Clam Chowder": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals sits beside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey threads the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Cowboy Ribeye": "Flora Adora with the cowboy ribeye — Spring-release floral Hendrick's against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Crab Cake": "Flora Adora with crab cake — lavish-floral Hendrick's Spring release against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Flora Adora's 6-floral-botanical profile is the featured Hendrick's Family-crab pairing — floral lifts crab harder than flagship Hendrick's cucumber-rose approach can.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Creamed Spinach": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Creamy Potato": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals sits alongside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey threads the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Creme Brulee": "Hendricks Flora Adora's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Hendricks Flora Adora on the steak course instead.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Escargot": "Flora Adora with escargot — lavish-floral Hendrick's against garlic-butter snails. Works; Flora Adora's floral profile is over-specified for escargot.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Faroe Island Salmon": "Flora Adora with the salmon — Spring-release floral Hendrick's against oily salmon. Excellent; Flora Adora's honeysuckle-elderflower-chamomile profile lifts the fish with lavish-floral distinction beyond flagship Hendrick's.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Filet Mignon": "Flora Adora with the filet — Spring-release floral Hendrick's against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|French Onion": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the rounded sweetness threads the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Grilled Caesar": "Flora Adora with the grilled Caesar — lavish-floral Hendrick's against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Flora Adora's floral-garden meets the char softly; Orbium's wormwood-bitter is the featured Hendrick's-Caesar match.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Gumbo": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey balances the spice. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals sits alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|House Wedge": "Flora Adora with the House Wedge — Spring-release floral Hendrick's against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Flora Adora's lavish-garden threads the wedge with distinctive floral character.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Kansas City": "Hendricks Flora Adora's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Hendricks Flora Adora on another course instead.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Loaded Potato": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals sits alongside the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Lobster Mac": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Market Fish": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals lifts the fish's clean flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Mocha Creme": "Hendricks Flora Adora's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Hendricks Flora Adora for the steak course.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Mushroom Bisque": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey threads the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Mushrooms": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals stays alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Hendricks Flora Adora's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hendricks Flora Adora belongs on the steak course.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Porterhouse": "Hendricks Flora Adora's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Hendricks Flora Adora for another course.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Prime Tartare": "Flora Adora with the tartare — lavish-floral Hendrick's against raw beef. Strong; Flora Adora's floral-garden profile supports tartare at distinctive register; Orbium's quinine-wormwood bitter lifts it sharper.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Rainbow Trout": "Hendricks Flora Adora's herbal-botanical edge matches register with the trout's delicate flesh: the gin lift cuts the trout, and the dry gin lift plays against the plate. Strong; gin on the trout reads cleanly at the table.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Roast Half Chicken": "Flora Adora with the chicken — Spring-release floral Hendrick's against roasted poultry. Works; Flora Adora's lavish-floral supports chicken at distinctive register.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey balances the spice. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals sits with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Hendricks Flora Adora|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Hendricks Flora Adora's crisp char-elderflower botanicals reads alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
