  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Vieux Carré": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build plays cleanly against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Villon Cognac": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- cognac on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a cognac. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Villon Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sweet wine and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no foil. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Weller 107": "Weller 107's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon body sits with the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; the bourbon-and-side match holds — neither soars nor fights.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the oak depth lays under the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak threads the caramelized sugar. Works; a gentle composed match at the premium tier.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the wheated softness matches the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality leans against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; craft-blend covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the dry rye body wraps the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; pour is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rye spice cuts the honey carrots, and the oak threads the caramelized sugar. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side; save the rare bottle for the main.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the dry rye body wraps the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; the 10-year holds without lifting.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the dry rye body wraps the honey carrots, and the oak threads the caramelized sugar. Works; the pour's toasted warmth threads through the char without overpowering.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the high-rye spice register matches the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; the premium pour is wasted on the salad's simplicity.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; single-barrel depth gives the dessert more than Small Batch.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon weight handles the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; private-selection wasted on the salad.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rye spice cuts the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; AE Triple Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rye-and-oak weight handles the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Japanese whisky balance frames the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; Marian McLain L.E.",
  "Honey Roasted Carrots|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- aperitivo bitterness and the glaze's sugar refuse each other — bitter wins, dessert reads cloying. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with the House Wedge — Barton 1792 against iceberg and bleu. Works; mid-proof pour is slightly over-specified.",
  "House Wedge|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against sweet-wheat. Works; the sweetness is a touch much for the salad's simplicity.",
  "House Wedge|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 — Napa Cab, dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, smooth tannin. The approachable body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the mocha plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as entry Napa Cab for the wedge — unconventional red choice, though whites or rosés peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|2 James Gin": "2 James with the House Wedge — Michigan local gin against iceberg and bleu. Strong; 2 James's juniper engages the bleu with American-craft distinction.",
  "House Wedge|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the House Wedge — rare limited-release against iceberg and bleu. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the scotch backbone matches the wedge, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; Prohibition narrative wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino with the House Wedge — filtered aged against iceberg and bleu. The distinctive cristalino character cuts the bleu with aged depth blanco can't normally deliver. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado with the House Wedge — extended-aged reposado against iceberg and bleu. The rich oak gives the salad a depth beyond what Don Julio or Corazon bring. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "House Wedge|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak body cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the apple-pear fruit plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong accessible pairing for the wedge — California Chard reframes the classic steakhouse salad.",
  "House Wedge|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Sonoma Bordeaux blend — plum, blackberry, mocha, soft tannin, smooth. The soft tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the plum plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong crowd-pleaser Bordeaux blend for the wedge — Sonoma accessibility reframes the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino with the House Wedge — elegant amaro against iceberg and bleu. Works; the botanical complexity is a post-salad digestif option.",
  "House Wedge|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness holds steady against the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the agave smoke frames the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "House Wedge|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with the House Wedge — port-finished bourbon against iceberg and bleu. Works; AE's fruity-jammy character engages the bleu at wine-finish register.",
  "House Wedge|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with the House Wedge — cask-strength port-finish against iceberg and bleu. Works; AE CS's cask-strength character engages the bleu at premium register.",
  "House Wedge|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "AE Triple Cask with the House Wedge — AE triple-cask against iceberg and bleu. Works; AE Triple Cask's layered character engages the bleu at distinctive register.",
  "House Wedge|Aperol": "Aperol with the House Wedge — soft orange-rhubarb at 11% ABV against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; the lightest, most approachable amari pairing on the menu — Aperol's gentle bitter complements the wedge without overpowering it, where Campari's 24% edge reads heavier.",
  "House Wedge|Asparagus": "The wedge and the asparagus on the same meal -- the bright register pulls against the savory, the contrast lands. Works; sequence or share, the build holds.",
  "House Wedge|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The wedge and the au gratin -- both cheese-forward, the table doubles up on cheese. Avoid; pick one or the other. Pair the wedge with St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé; pair the au gratin with Bone-In Filet, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Keenan Chardonnay.",
  "House Wedge|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope — Paso Cab, ripe plum, dark cherry, mocha, warm spice. The plush body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the warm-spice plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as Paso Cab for the wedge — unconventional red with warmth, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Averna Amaro": "Averna with the House Wedge — Sicilian amaro against iceberg and bleu. Works; caramel-orange is a post-salad digestif option.",
  "House Wedge|Aviation": "Aviation gin, Luxardo, crème de violette, ginger syrup. The cocktail's floral-and-cherry profile cuts iceberg-and-blue-cheese dressing and the ginger softens bacon's smoke. Strong opener pairing — wedge's layered flavors meet the Aviation's botanical complexity.",
  "House Wedge|Aviation Gin": "Aviation with the House Wedge — lavender-cardamom American craft against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Aviation's soft juniper threads the wedge at balanced register.",
  "House Wedge|Avion 44": "Avion 44's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the oak-aged tequila body threads the wedge, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "House Wedge|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver with the House Wedge — globally-distributed blanco against iceberg and bleu. The clean filtered character cuts the bleu at the approachable register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|Bacardi Rum": "House Wedge: iceberg with bacon and bleu meets Bacardi Rum. Excellent; the clean-light character cuts the bleu richness at cocktail-workhorse register.",
  "House Wedge|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|Baker's": "Baker's with the House Wedge — bold 107-proof against iceberg and bleu. Works; the intensity overpowers the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the House Wedge — handcrafted Speyside against iceberg and bleu. Works; Balvenie's sweet-oak is over-specified for the wedge but functional.",
  "House Wedge|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the malt-and-oak threads the wedge, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for a side — save the bottle for the main course.",
  "House Wedge|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with the House Wedge — hand-picked American Oak against iceberg and bleu. Works; Balvenie 16 is over-specified for the wedge — save for the filet or dessert course.",
  "House Wedge|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood's aged Port-finished Speyside leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the wedge, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "House Wedge|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with the House Wedge — French-wine-cask Bardstown against iceberg and bleu. Works; Bardstown Château Laboude's French-wine-fruit engages the bleu at contemporary-craft register.",
  "House Wedge|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with the House Wedge — craft-blender against iceberg and bleu. Works; Discovery complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "House Wedge|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with the House Wedge — KBS-finished against iceberg and bleu. Works; stout-barrel story wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the wedge, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "House Wedge|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with the House Wedge — Fusion blend against iceberg and bleu. Works; complexity wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with the House Wedge — curated Bardstown against iceberg and bleu. Works; retailer-pick wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with the House Wedge — light-bodied rye against iceberg and bleu. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "House Wedge|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year with the House Wedge — aged high-rye against iceberg and bleu. Works; the pour's refinement is underused by the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with the House Wedge — malted bread notes against iceberg and bleu. Works; the grain character adds something standard Basil Hayden's can't, but the salad's simplicity still caps the match.",
  "House Wedge|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with the House Wedge — Beam BH red-wine finish against iceberg and bleu. Works; BH Red Wine Cask's red-wine-fruit engages the bleu at distinctive register.",
  "House Wedge|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast with the House Wedge — toast notes against iceberg and bleu. Works; the toast gives the salad a little more than standard Basil Hayden's would.",
  "House Wedge|Bee's Knees": "Empress Indigo gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup. The lemon cuts iceberg-and-bacon crunch and honey-lavender plays against blue cheese's funk; the blue-grey hue adds visual interest. Strong opener cocktail — keeps palate fresh for the steak course ahead.",
  "House Wedge|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater with the House Wedge — classic 40% London Dry against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Beefeater's balance is a democratic wedge pairing — neither too sharp nor too sweet.",
  "House Wedge|Beignets": "House Wedge and the beignets -- sugar and umami share the table, the contrast carries the meal. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "House Wedge|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere with the House Wedge — Polish rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; the rye pepper engages the bleu sharpness differently than wheat vodkas do.",
  "House Wedge|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against heritage blend. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with the House Wedge — richer single-barrel against iceberg and bleu. Works; the extra proof overpowers the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with the House Wedge — iconic single-barrel against iceberg and bleu. Works; the premium pour is wasted on the salad's simplicity.",
  "House Wedge|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's Straight from the Barrel's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the brown-spirit warmth threads the wedge, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; craft-blend over-specified.",
  "House Wedge|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with the House Wedge — 10th-Pact against iceberg and bleu. Works; milestone-collector wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with the House Wedge — Pact 8 against iceberg and bleu. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the House Wedge — Pact 9 wine-cask against iceberg and bleu. Works; annual-release wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat with the House Wedge — Philly patriotic-artisan gin against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Bluecoat's citrus cuts the bleu with American-artisan distinction.",
  "House Wedge|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Patagonia Argentina Malbec — Noemia A Lisa, cooler-climate than Mendoza, blackberry, violet, graphite, full body, soft tannin. The soft tannin holds against blue-cheese funk, but the elegant style gets muted by cold iceberg. Works for wedge — a lighter Mencía or Chianti lifts higher.",
  "House Wedge|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry with the House Wedge — original-recipe London Dry against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Bombay Dry's juniper engages the bleu with traditional character.",
  "House Wedge|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire with the House Wedge — vapor-infused floral gin against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Sapphire's softness meets the bleu at accessible register.",
  "House Wedge|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow and the wedge together -- protein meets umami, the table builds substance. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "House Wedge|Bone-In Filet": "Bone-In Filet with the wedge alongside -- protein and umami share the plate, the build reads weighty and clean. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Booker's": "Booker's' cask-strength bourbon depth finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon body sits with the wedge, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; neutral, not elevating.",
  "House Wedge|Botanist Gin": "Botanist with the House Wedge — 31-botanical Islay craft against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Botanist's wildcrafted complexity meets the bleu with Hebridean distinction.",
  "House Wedge|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, bitters. Cocktail weight is heavier than the iceberg-and-blue-cheese wedge strictly needs, but the vanilla depth plays with bacon and the cherry brightens the dressing. Works as an opener when guest leads with whiskey.",
  "House Wedge|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against house-pick wheat. Works as a separate sip; the private select's depth is a waste on the wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the House Wedge — Virginia small-batch against iceberg and bleu. Works; smooth character is slightly over-specified for the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Bowmore 12": "House Wedge: iceberg with bleu dressing meets Bowmore 12's honey-tinged peat. The honey notes offer something the dressing can pick up, but the peat still overwhelms the crisp base. Works as a side-sip, not a driver pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin. The high acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong benchmark Barbera for the wedge — food-wine acidity reframes the classic salad.",
  "House Wedge|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin — Mount Veeder Cab, dark blackberry, graphite, earth, firm tannin. The dense body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the graphite plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as mountain Cab for the wedge — Mount Veeder food-driven red, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Broccoli Cheddar": "The broccoli cheddar alongside the wedge -- both bring cheese as the headline. Avoid; one is enough. Pair the broccoli cheddar with Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay; pair the wedge with St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé.",
  "House Wedge|Broccolini": "The wedge alongside the broccolini -- the bright cuts the savory weight. Works; the table can build them in order or together.",
  "House Wedge|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich: this Islay Scotch's intense peat smoke, medicinal phenol, and bold weight meets the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing. Heavy whiskey on a crisp wedge — the drink steamrolls the salad. Strong — the interaction lands despite the axis challenge.",
  "House Wedge|Brussels and Belly": "The wedge alongside Brussels + Belly -- protein meets umami, the table builds substance. Works; the table can build them in order or together.",
  "House Wedge|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against flagship bourbon. Works; no driver pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Buffalo Trace Cream lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "House Wedge|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against high-rye bourbon. Works; the spice is underused by the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Burrata": "The burrata alongside the wedge -- both bring cheese as the headline. Avoid; one is enough. Pair the burrata with Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc; pair the wedge with St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé.",
  "House Wedge|Butternut Squash": "The butternut squash alongside the wedge -- the silky cream holds beside the iceberg-and-bleu salad. Works; the opening pair holds at table register.",
  "House Wedge|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila with the House Wedge — Camarena-family value reposado against iceberg and bleu. The approachable agave cuts the bleu at the entry tier. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Campari": "Campari with the House Wedge — 24% bitter orange-rhubarb against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; this is aperitivo country — Campari's ruby bitter was built for crisp greens and blue cheese bite, where the sipping amari would feel heavy.",
  "House Wedge|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club with the House Wedge — traditional heritage Canadian against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Canadian Club's grain-forward character threads the wedge at heritage register.",
  "House Wedge|Canadian Club 43 Year": "CC 43 Year with the House Wedge — aged collector Canadian against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; CC 43 Year's 43yr character engages the bleu at collector register.",
  "House Wedge|Captain Morgan Rum": "House Wedge: iceberg with bacon and bleu meets Captain Morgan. Excellent; the molasses-spice character cuts bacon-and-bleu at approachable register — the spiced rum handles the salty-rich wedge cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing with the House Wedge — distinctive single-barrel Canadian against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Caribou Crossing's craft character threads the wedge at single-barrel register.",
  "House Wedge|Carrot Cake": "The wedge and the carrot cake -- the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch bookends with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "House Wedge|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sweet wine on cold lettuce refuses the salad's clean register. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Casa LBV Port for the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's earthen-pit smoke profile reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the earthen-pit smoke carries the wedge, and the wood-smoke note underlines the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "House Wedge|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cassis-cocoa tannin leans against the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the opulent body overshoots the leaf-and-acid plate, and the cassis frames the herb butter. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "House Wedge|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Chambord on the steak course instead.",
  "House Wedge|Cheesecake": "The wedge alongside the cheesecake -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "House Wedge|Chilean Seabass": "Chilean Seabass after the wedge -- the iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch refreshes the palate, the seabass following with buttery pan-finished flesh that the cold opener gave room for. Strong; the salad cleans the table for the rich main.",
  "House Wedge|Chocolate Brownie": "The wedge alongside the brownie -- sugar and umami share the table, the contrast carries the meal. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "House Wedge|Chocolate Cake": "The wedge alongside the chocolate cake -- sweet contrast lifts the savory. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "House Wedge|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin with the House Wedge — single-ingredient potato against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; the creamy texture sits with the bleu cheese richness at artisanal register.",
  "House Wedge|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with the House Wedge — American brandy against iceberg and bleu. Works; Christian Bros's oak is functional on the wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Château Batailley": "Pauillac 5ème Cru Classé Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the graphite plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as classified Bordeaux for the wedge — Pauillac structure reframes the classic salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Château Beaucastel": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône — Beaucastel, all 13 permitted varieties, dark fruit, leather, truffle, Provençal herbs, full body. The leather plays with blue-cheese funk and the Provençal herbs match bacon-fat char. Works for wedge — cold iceberg mutes the bucket-list complexity, so a lighter Chianti lifts higher.",
  "House Wedge|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Pomerol Merlot-Cab Franc — Château Gombaude-Guillot, biodynamic Pomerol, 2016, dark plum, truffle, mocha, velvety finish. The velvety texture reads heavy against iceberg-and-blue-cheese simplicity, though mocha meets the dressing's bite. Works for wedge — Le Garenne Rosé fits the salad cleaner.",
  "House Wedge|Château Haut Segottes": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as right-bank Bordeaux for the wedge — Merlot-led roundness; whites and rosés peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Château de Rouillac": "Pessac-Léognan left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, tobacco, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the graphite plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as left-bank Bordeaux for the wedge — French structure reframes the classic salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Clam Chowder": "Clam Chowder and the wedge together -- soup-and-salad open the meal. Works; both courses share the opening without crowding.",
  "House Wedge|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the oak-aged tequila body threads the wedge, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "House Wedge|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango's rested-agave body finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the aged-agave weight wraps the wedge, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "House Wedge|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold's aged-agave character reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the aged-agave weight wraps the wedge, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; accessible aged composed with this dish.",
  "House Wedge|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's smoky agave character sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the wood-fire body meets the wedge, and the mineral smoke plays against the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "House Wedge|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata with the House Wedge — ceremonial ceramic blanco against iceberg and bleu. The premium presentation is the pairing story as much as the flavor. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "House Wedge|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado with the House Wedge — ultra-premium reposado against iceberg and bleu. The ceramic-bottle theatrics are the draw as much as the pairing; the Clase Azul experience with a salad. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra's rested-agave body finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the aged-agave weight wraps the wedge, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; Ultra's ceremonial depth composed with this dish.",
  "House Wedge|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against Beam wheated. Works; no driver pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body. The warm spice plays with bacon-fat and the garrigue matches blue-cheese funk. Peak Southern-French for wedge — Mediterranean lift on the heavy salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with the House Wedge — Cabernet-cask reposado against iceberg and bleu. The wine-cask fruit cuts the bleu with berry-driven logic. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's aperitivo herb-and-bitter cut rests at register with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the rhubarb-and-orange aperitivo carries the wedge, and the bittersweet thread cuts the bowl. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "House Wedge|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco with the House Wedge — Sazerac-family blanco against iceberg and bleu. The highland-agave character cuts the bleu distinctly from Don Julio's polish. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado with the House Wedge — single-estate reposado against iceberg and bleu. The agave-forward profile cuts the bleu differently than Don Julio's oakier take. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the wedge, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; the call holds at neutral register.",
  "House Wedge|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg's rested-agave body leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the rested-agave depth sits with the wedge, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; Durango presentation slightly over-specified.",
  "House Wedge|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's aged-agave character finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the oak-aged tequila body threads the wedge, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; distinctive ceremony over-specified.",
  "House Wedge|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the structured body wraps the wedge, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; the two sides share the meal without competing.",
  "House Wedge|Corpse Reviver": "Warner's gin, Lillet, Pierre Ferrand cognac, lemon, absinthe-rinsed glass. The lemon-and-cognac structure cuts iceberg-and-blue-cheese weight and the anise nose adds complexity. Strong opener pairing — cocktail's layered build matches the wedge's layered components.",
  "House Wedge|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier with the House Wedge — cognac against iceberg and bleu. Works; Courvoisier's oak is over-specified but functional.",
  "House Wedge|Cowboy Ribeye": "The cowboy ribeye after the wedge -- the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch sets the table for the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: protein meets umami, the table builds substance. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "House Wedge|Crab Cake": "Crab Cake and the wedge together -- the delicate course primes the savory, the table builds register. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "House Wedge|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 with the House Wedge — layered Speyside against iceberg and bleu. Works; Cragganmore's complexity is over-specified for the wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Creamed Spinach": "The wedge and the creamed spinach -- cream and umami share the plate, the build reads dense and clean. Works; sequence them or share the table, the call holds either way.",
  "House Wedge|Creamy Potato": "The potato soup alongside the wedge -- the smooth potato base reads alongside the classic wedge. Works; the opening pair holds at table register.",
  "House Wedge|Creme Brulee": "The wedge alongside the brulee -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "House Wedge|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral, high acid. The acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the raspberry plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak benchmark Pinot for the wedge — Willamette elegance reframes the classic steakhouse salad. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "House Wedge|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal with the House Wedge — iconic smooth Canadian against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Crown Royal's vanilla-honey character threads the wedge with crowd-pleasing Canadian distinction.",
  "House Wedge|Cucumber Gimlet": "Hendrick's muddled with cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, topped with soda. The cooling cocktail softens the blue cheese dressing's weight and the cucumber plays against the iceberg's crunch. Refreshing cocktail route for the salad course. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12 with the House Wedge — Highland luxury against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Dalmore's sherry-finish depth threads the wedge at premium Highland register.",
  "House Wedge|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, high acid. The chalk-acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dried-herbs play against blue cheese dressing. Peak Loire red for the wedge — French Pinot reframes the classic steakhouse salad. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as rare-Napa CF for the wedge — Darioush aromatic elegance reframes the classic salad.",
  "House Wedge|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Darioush — Silverado Trail Cab, blackcurrant, dark plum, mocha, spice. The opulent body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the mocha-spice plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as opulent Napa Cab for the wedge — Persian-inspired estate story red, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin with the House Wedge — Detroit urban craft against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Detroit City threads the wedge with hometown pride.",
  "House Wedge|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City with the House Wedge — local Michigan vodka against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Detroit City threads the wedge with hometown character — the story-driven wedge pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15 with the House Wedge — sherry-finish Highland blend against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Dewar's 15's aged + sherry-finish threads the wedge with premium-blend distinction.",
  "House Wedge|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label with the House Wedge — entry blended Scotch against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Dewar's White threads the wedge with honey-vanilla crowd-pleasing character.",
  "House Wedge|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Disaronno belongs on the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the House Wedge — craft sourced rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; boutique character cuts the bleu distinctively.",
  "House Wedge|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's dense-spirit weight sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the heavy body handles the wedge, and the heavy-pour register grips the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "House Wedge|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Willamette flagship Pinot Blend — dark cherry, Bing cherry, toasted spice, forest floor, silky. The acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the toasted-spice plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak flagship Willamette Pinot for the wedge — Domaine Serene elegance reframes the salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Provence Bandol AOC Mourvèdre blend — Tempier, dark plum, game, olive, wild herbs, smoky mineral, firm tannin. The game plays with blue-cheese funk and the smoky mineral matches bacon-char. Works for wedge — cold iceberg mutes Tempier's cult-producer complexity, so a lighter Chianti lifts higher.",
  "House Wedge|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Pouilly-Fumé — Loire Sauv Blanc with gun-flint minerality, grapefruit, lime zest. The serious body cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the smoky mineral plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong serious Sauv Blanc for the wedge — Loire sense-of-place on the steakhouse classic.",
  "House Wedge|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body. The garrigue plays with blue-cheese funk and the black pepper matches bacon-fat char. Works for wedge — cold iceberg mutes the Châteauneuf complexity, so a lighter Chianti lifts higher.",
  "House Wedge|Don Fulano Anejo": "Don Fulano Anejo's aged-agave character sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the oak-aged tequila body threads the wedge, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; accessible match.",
  "House Wedge|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco with the House Wedge — highland-craft blanco against iceberg and bleu. The refined 80-proof gives the salad a craft-register frame distinct from the globally-marketed pours. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado with the House Wedge — premium highland reposado against iceberg and bleu. The refinement gives the salad more than everyday reposados bring. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942's icon extra-añejo character leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the aged-agave weight wraps the wedge, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; accessible character doesn't drive.",
  "House Wedge|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th with the House Wedge — crystalline reposado against iceberg and bleu. Smoother than standard Don Julio; the 70th lets the bleu lead without fighting it. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco with the House Wedge — iconic 80-proof against iceberg and bleu. The clean, bright agave cuts the bleu's richness the way no bourbon can; the benchmark blanco for salad orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Don Julio Reposado": "Don Julio Reposado with the House Wedge — 8-month-aged agave against iceberg and bleu. The reposado's brightness cuts the bleu's richness while the oak adds warmth; the everyday reposado with the wedge. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Drambuie": "Drambuie's sweet honey-scotch-herbal character lifts the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the honey threads the cream. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "House Wedge|Dunville's Single Malt": "Dunville's Single Malt with the House Wedge — Belfast Irish single malt against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Dunville's Northern Ireland character threads the wedge at distinctive register.",
  "House Wedge|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the wedge, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "House Wedge|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye with the House Wedge — bonded BT rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; BIB weight cuts the bleu with premium authority.",
  "House Wedge|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the House Wedge — 100-proof BIB against iceberg and bleu. Works; bonded weight overpowers the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with House Wedge — iceberg simplicity against aged oak is a mismatch of ambition. Works only as a separate sip.",
  "House Wedge|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Eastern Kille with the House Wedge — classic GR London Dry against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Eastern Kille threads the wedge with juniper-citrus hometown distinction.",
  "House Wedge|El Cabo": "El Cabo with the House Wedge — boutique reposado against iceberg and bleu. The craft character gives the salad a different frame than big-brand reposados. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "House Wedge|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "El Mayor Extra Anejo's aged-agave character sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the aged-agave weight wraps the wedge, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; Durango presentation slightly over-specified.",
  "House Wedge|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado with the House Wedge — value agave-forward reposado against iceberg and bleu. The brightness cuts the bleu at the accessible register. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "House Wedge|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin with the House Wedge — African craft gin against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Elephant's contemporary botanicals engage the bleu with distinctive character.",
  "House Wedge|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the brown-spirit warmth threads the wedge, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; collector-bottle depth gives ultra-premium character.",
  "House Wedge|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel with the House Wedge — curated single-barrel against iceberg and bleu. Works; private-selection wasted on the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel with the House Wedge — single-barrel Heaven Hill against iceberg and bleu. Works; premium single-barrel wasted on the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against 94-proof small-batch. Works; the pour is a touch ambitious for the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the House Wedge — HH rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; the 94-proof cuts the bleu with HH rye character.",
  "House Wedge|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — cherry, violet, clove, structured backbone. The acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the clove-spice plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak step-up Willamette Pinot for the wedge — age-worthy Oregon red reframes the classic steakhouse salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc — Willamette with pear, peach, floral, refreshing acidity. The refreshing acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the fruit plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong unoaked white for the wedge — lighter touch than Chard, fuller body than Sauv Blanc.",
  "House Wedge|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee with the House Wedge — smooth single-barrel against iceberg and bleu. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 with the House Wedge — Victoria butterfly-pea-flower gin against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Empress's rose-citrus threads the wedge with BC-craft distinction.",
  "House Wedge|Epoch Ingenuity": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, full body. The olive matches blue-cheese funk and the crushed pepper plays with bacon-fat. Works for wedge — iceberg-cold mutes Epoch's savory depth, a lighter Chianti lifts higher.",
  "House Wedge|Escargot": "The escargot preceded by the wedge -- savory-on-savory, the table reads dense and earnest. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "House Wedge|Espresso Martini": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness overwhelms the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- whiskey-cocktail weight has no business on cold lettuce. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Espresso Martini belongs on the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Dessert-forward cocktail weight is wrong for the light iceberg wedge, but the bourbon-cream structure doesn't entirely clash with bacon and blue cheese. Works when guest leads with the cocktail before switching to wine.",
  "House Wedge|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone. The smoked-meat plays against iceberg-and-bacon and the dried-rose plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak savory Pinot for the wedge — Eola-Amity complexity reframes the classic steakhouse salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Oakville luxurious Cab — blackcurrant, dark chocolate, espresso, violet, smooth powerful. The opulent body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the chocolate-espresso plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as luxurious Oakville Cab for the wedge — Far Niente red choice, though whites peak higher and the wine deserves a bigger course.",
  "House Wedge|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Oakville Chardonnay — buttered citrus, toasted hazelnut, crème brûlée, lush velvety. The opulent oak weight cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the hazelnut plays against blue cheese. Works as a special-occasion Chardonnay for the wedge — though the wine deserves a more substantial course.",
  "House Wedge|Faroe Island Salmon": "Faroe Island Salmon after the wedge -- the iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch refreshes the palate, the salmon following with rich oily flesh that the cold opener cleared room for. Strong; the salad's bite primes the rich fish course.",
  "House Wedge|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's bold herbal-leather cassis sits alongside the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the opulent body overshoots the leaf-and-acid plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "House Wedge|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Faust — Napa Cab, blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the tobacco plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as value Napa Cab for the wedge — unconventional red choice with brooding depth, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid, Burgundian lift. The light body won't mute cold iceberg and the high acid cuts blue-cheese funk. Peak Galician-Mencía for wedge — bright-acid Mencía freshens the heavy salad. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca with the House Wedge — nuclear bitter against iceberg and bleu. Works; post-salad digestif option, but Branca's intensity is over-specified for a light wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta with the House Wedge — mint Fernet against iceberg and bleu. Works; mint as post-salad digestif, still over-specified for the wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon with the wedge alongside -- protein meets umami, the table builds substance. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Fireball Whisky for the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot — riper fruit, rounder than the Willamette. Works with our lardons but the wedge is heavy for Pinot overall. The Avni or a rosé does more; keep Fisher for the steak or marrow.",
  "House Wedge|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the House Wedge — 104-proof 6-recipe against iceberg and bleu. Works; premium proof overpowers the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against 80-proof entry. Works; the light body doesn't overshoot the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with the House Wedge — craft small-batch against iceberg and bleu. Works; boutique character slightly over-specified.",
  "House Wedge|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Fox & Oden Double Oaked's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon depth settles into the wedge, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; premium proof overpowers the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Frangelico belongs on the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Rutherford Cab — Bosché Vineyard since 1970, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the tobacco plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as Bosché Vineyard Cab for the wedge — 1970-legacy Rutherford reframes the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|French 75": "Inverroche gin, lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top. The citrus-and-bubbles lift cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the vanilla softens against blue cheese dressing. Strong opener pairing — keeps the palate fresh before the steak course arrives.",
  "House Wedge|French Onion": "The French onion and the wedge share cheese as the primary character. Avoid; the meal doubles down without contrast. Route the French onion with Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée or the wedge with St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé instead.",
  "House Wedge|Frias Block 5": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "House Wedge|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, high acid. The high acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dried cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as classic Barolo for the wedge — Vajra reframes the salad, though whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets the Moscato's peach-and-honey sweetness with delicate effervescence. Moscato's sweetness on a savory plate — wrong register. Works as a dessert transition with a savory bridge.",
  "House Wedge|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco with the House Wedge — craft 80-proof against iceberg and bleu. The agave-forward character cuts the bleu at the craft blanco register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof with the House Wedge — bold concentrated reposado against iceberg and bleu. The agave intensity cuts the bleu with conviction; a high-impact reposado salad pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue with the House Wedge — Sligo-named craft Irish against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Garavogue's distinctive character threads the wedge with craft-Irish distinction.",
  "House Wedge|George T. Stagg": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the brown-spirit warmth threads the wedge, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the intensity overpowers the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The cracked pepper plays with bacon-fat char and the medium tannin holds against blue-cheese funk. Works for wedge — cold iceberg mutes Ghost Block's bold profile, so lighter reds lift higher.",
  "House Wedge|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12 with the House Wedge — entry Speyside against iceberg and bleu. Works; Fiddich's light profile is a functional wedge pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14 with the House Wedge — Bourbon Barrel Reserve against iceberg and bleu. Works; Fiddich 14's depth is over-specified for the wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18 with the House Wedge — Small Batch Reserve against iceberg and bleu. Works; Fiddich 18 is over-specified for the wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23 with the House Wedge — French Cuvée-cask Speyside against iceberg and bleu. Works; Gran Cru's rare-release character is wasted on the wedge — save the pour for filet or dessert.",
  "House Wedge|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12 with the House Wedge — recently-reopened Sandend coast against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Glenglassaugh 12's tropical-coastal threads the wedge with distinctive character.",
  "House Wedge|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12 with the House Wedge — honey-floral Speyside against iceberg and bleu. Works; Glenlivet's softness is a functional wedge pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10 with the House Wedge — honey-floral Highland against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Glenmorangie 10's honeysuckle-peach threads the wedge at entry-Highland register.",
  "House Wedge|Glenmorangie 18": "House Wedge: iceberg and bleu meet aged Glenmorangie. Strong; Glenmorangie 18's aged depth threads the wedge at premium register.",
  "House Wedge|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Graham's 10 Year Tawny lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "House Wedge|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Graham's 20 Year Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "House Wedge|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Graham's 2017 Vintage Port's structured vintage port with dark-fruit weight clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Graham's 2017 Vintage Port belongs on the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum with the House Wedge — ultra-premium Patron against iceberg and bleu. The smooth concentrated agave cuts the bleu at the premium-Patron register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Grand Marnier on the steak course instead.",
  "House Wedge|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale with the House Wedge — California 6-botanical craft against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Gray Whale's California terroir meets the bleu at ocean-conservation register.",
  "House Wedge|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's cask-strength herbal-high-proof sweetness holds steady against the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "House Wedge|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose with the House Wedge — soft French wheat against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Goose frames the wedge with polish Tito's doesn't bring.",
  "House Wedge|Gumbo": "The gumbo alongside the wedge -- the dark roux sits cleanly beside the cool wedge plate, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; the opening pair holds at table register.",
  "House Wedge|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "House Wedge|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 with the House Wedge — 100-proof craft against iceberg and bleu. Strong; HDW 100's extra backbone engages the bleu more aggressively than standard craft vodkas.",
  "House Wedge|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 with the House Wedge — Japanese Alps single malt against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Hakushu's subtle-peat character engages the bleu at Forest-Distillery register.",
  "House Wedge|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel with the House Wedge — BT single-barrel against iceberg and bleu. Works; the complex character is wasted on the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 with the House Wedge — California craft against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Hangar 1's floral notes meet the bleu with California-craft distinction.",
  "House Wedge|Head Fake": "Wheatley vodka, limoncello, triple sec, lemon, simple syrup — sugar rim. The lemon-and-citrus profile cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the limoncello rounds against blue cheese. Strong bright opener for the wedge — keeps palate fresh before the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the brown-spirit warmth threads the wedge, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; the pour's delicacy doesn't overpower the soup, but doesn't particularly elevate it either.",
  "House Wedge|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against 100-proof BIB. Works as a separate sip; the bonded weight overpowers the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with the House Wedge — premium aged bourbon against iceberg and bleu. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "Napa icon from Oakville's Martha's Vineyard, decades-aged reputation. Blue cheese is a specific wine-killer — this bottle deserves the Tomahawk or Porterhouse. Never pour on a wedge; the guest and the wine both lose. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "House Wedge|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Flora Adora with the House Wedge — Spring-release floral Hendrick's against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Flora Adora's lavish-garden threads the wedge with distinctive floral character.",
  "House Wedge|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks with the House Wedge — flagship perfumed gin against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Hendrick's cucumber-rose threads the wedge at crowd-pleasing register; Orbium's bitter-tonic cuts bleu sharper.",
  "House Wedge|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Grand Cabaret with the House Wedge — absinthe-style Hendrick's against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Grand Cabaret's fruit-forward threads the wedge with stage-show character.",
  "House Wedge|Hendricks Neptunia": "Neptunia with the House Wedge — coastal-release Hendrick's against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Neptunia's briny character engages the bleu with distinctive maritime profile.",
  "House Wedge|Hendricks Orbium": "Orbium with the House Wedge — Hendrick's \"spherical\" release: quinine + wormwood + blue lotus, bitter-tonic-gin hybrid. Excellent; Orbium's quinine-wormwood bitter is the featured Hendrick's Family-wedge pairing — the bitter-tonic character cuts bleu sharper than any floral or cucumber-rose sibling can, engaging the wedge's pungency with distinctive contrast.",
  "House Wedge|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy with the House Wedge — cognac against iceberg and bleu. Works; Hennessy's oak is over-specified for the wedge but functional.",
  "House Wedge|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB with the House Wedge — 100-proof 10-year BIB against iceberg and bleu. Works; bonded weight overpowers the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Harmony with the House Wedge — refined premium Japanese blend against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Hibiki's elegant character threads the wedge at premium-blend register.",
  "House Wedge|Honey Roasted Carrots": "House Wedge and the honey carrots -- the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch meets the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; no conflict however the table orders them.",
  "House Wedge|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon depth settles into the wedge, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; extended-aged premium wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "House Wedge|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Glera from Veneto — bright dry Prosecco with white peach, green apple, and clean citrus. The crisp finish cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the effervescence plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong pairing for the wedge — light bubbles reset the palate between each forkful.",
  "House Wedge|Inhibited": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build stays alongside the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the cocktail lift matches the leaf-and-acid plate, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "House Wedge|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche with the House Wedge — South African fynbos gin against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Inverroche's Cape-floral meets the bleu with terroir-specific character.",
  "House Wedge|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish with the House Wedge — northern-Michigan farm-distilled gin against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Iron Fish's estate-botanical profile meets the bleu with farm-forward distinction.",
  "House Wedge|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon backbone carries the wedge, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; Old World complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "House Wedge|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish with the House Wedge — A. Smith Bowman port-finish against iceberg and bleu. Works; Isaac Bowman's port-fruit engages the bleu at Virginia-heritage register.",
  "House Wedge|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "Anderson Valley Pinot — dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, refined tannin. The acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the mocha plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong Anderson Valley Pinot for the wedge — concentrated Mendocino reframes the classic steakhouse salad.",
  "House Wedge|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "J. Davies — Diamond Mountain Cab, dark fruit, graphite, mineral, firm tannin. The structured body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the graphite plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as mountain Cab for the wedge — Schramsberg provenance red choice, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year with the House Wedge — aged Tennessee against iceberg and bleu. Strong; JD 12's aged character engages the bleu at premium register.",
  "House Wedge|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded with the House Wedge — 100-proof Tennessee against iceberg and bleu. Strong; JD Bonded's backbone engages the bleu at Bonded-Act register.",
  "House Wedge|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7 with the House Wedge — iconic Tennessee against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Old No. 7 threads the wedge at iconic Tennessee register.",
  "House Wedge|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "JD Sinatra Select with the House Wedge — Sinatra Barrels Tennessee against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Sinatra Select's grooved-barrel character engages the bleu at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "House Wedge|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson with the House Wedge — entry Irish against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Jameson's smooth character threads the wedge with crowd-pleasing Irish distinction.",
  "House Wedge|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, balanced acidity. The acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the pomegranate plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak food-friendly Pinot for the wedge — reliable Russian River reframes the classic steakhouse salad. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "House Wedge|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Grossot Chablis — Burgundy unoaked Chardonnay, flint, chalk, lemon, green apple. The lean precise acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the mineral plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong Chablis for the wedge — French precision reframes the classic steakhouse salad.",
  "House Wedge|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon depth settles into the wedge, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; AE Triple Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "House Wedge|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the oak depth lays under the wedge, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; AE CS handles the richness at premium register.",
  "House Wedge|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against blended elegance. Works; the pour's refinement is underused by the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against Twin Oak depth. Works only as a separate sip; the VR Twin Oak's rarity is wasted on the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against aged Beam. Works; oak slightly over-specified for the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against 80-proof entry. Works; light body doesn't overshoot.",
  "House Wedge|Johnnie Walker Black": "JW Black with the House Wedge — 12yr balanced JW against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; JW Black's balanced smoke-and-sherry character engages the bleu at premium-blend register.",
  "House Wedge|Johnnie Walker Blue": "JW Blue with the House Wedge — ultra-premium JW against iceberg and bleu. Works; the wedge is far too casual for JW Blue — save the rare-cask bottle for something worthy of its register.",
  "House Wedge|Johnnie Walker Red": "JW Red with the House Wedge — 80-proof smoky JW against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; JW Red's bold character engages the bleu with distinctive smoke-driven bite.",
  "House Wedge|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Jordan — Alexander Valley Cab, cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the cedar-mint plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as elegant Cab for the wedge — refined red choice, though whites or rosés peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Loire Sancerre — benchmark Sauv Blanc with grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension finish. The refined mineral cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the herbs play against blue cheese dressing. Strong Sancerre for the wedge — Loire precision reframes the American steakhouse classic.",
  "House Wedge|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark chocolate plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as cult Napa for the wedge — Pritchard Hill structure on the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's heavy aged-spirit weight reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the heavy body handles the wedge, and the deep backbone frames the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "House Wedge|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's heavy aged-spirit weight leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the heavy body handles the wedge, and the rich character softens the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "House Wedge|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Kahlua belongs on the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|Kansas City": "Kansas City into the wedge -- umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "House Wedge|Keenan Chardonnay": "Spring Mountain Chardonnay — green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acidity. The bright acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the subtle oak plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as mountain Chardonnay for the wedge — though the wine's versatility shines higher on salmon, chicken, or the bisque.",
  "House Wedge|Keenan Mernet": "Spring Mountain Napa Mernet — Merlot-Cab, mountain structure, plush dark cherry, plum, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the plum plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as mountain Merlot-Cab for the wedge — Keenan story reframes the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye with the House Wedge — allocated KO against iceberg and bleu. Works; premium KO story wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the House Wedge — KO Confiscated against iceberg and bleu. Works; Prohibition narrative wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with the House Wedge — luxury Irish crossover against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Kentucky Owl engages the bleu at collector register; consider whether the wedge deserves a $150+ pour.",
  "House Wedge|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The garrigue plays with blue-cheese funk and the pepper matches bacon-fat char. Strong Southern-French for wedge — medium body handles iceberg-cold without muting.",
  "House Wedge|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One with the House Wedge — Dutch wheat against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; the citrus finish brightens the wedge with precision.",
  "House Wedge|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker with the House Wedge — hop-influenced gin against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Knickerbocker's brewer's-heritage bitter-hop meets bleu with distinctive character (demoted slightly from other Local MI Gins' Excellent tier because the hop-bitter adds tension the classic juniper profiles avoid).",
  "House Wedge|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against 100 proof. Works only as a separate sip; the Knob's boldness overpowers the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year with the House Wedge — premium aged Beam against iceberg and bleu. Works; aging wasted on the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon backbone carries the wedge, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; the extra proof overpowers the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon depth settles into the wedge, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "House Wedge|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against blended 100-proof. Works; the blend's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye with the House Wedge — 100-proof Beam rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; bold rye cuts the bleu with extra-proof intensity.",
  "House Wedge|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva's aged-agave character finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the aged-agave weight wraps the wedge, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; extended-aged complexity handles richness at Los-Altos register.",
  "House Wedge|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo's aged-agave character leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the rested-agave depth sits with the wedge, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; multi-cask over-specified for small plate.",
  "House Wedge|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Anejo's rested-agave body leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the rested-agave depth sits with the wedge, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "House Wedge|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado with the House Wedge — luxury pink reposado against iceberg and bleu. The wine-cask character and bleu pick each other up in unexpected ways; distinctive. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Lagavulin 8": "Lagavulin 8 with the House Wedge — the peat overpowers the crisp iceberg and bleu dressing more than it lifts them. Capable only; the spirit steamrolls the salad. Pour as an aperitif alongside rather than as a match. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "House Wedge|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Napa Cab — Lail entry-level, polished blackcurrant, cedar, dark cherry, refined medium tannin. The refined tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the cedar plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as historically-pedigreed Napa Cab for the wedge — Lail family polish reframes the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the cassis plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as historically-significant Napa Cab for the wedge — Daniel Cuvée pedigree reframes the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver with the House Wedge — DJ-family-descendant craft against iceberg and bleu. The clean additive-free character cuts the bleu at the family-heritage craft register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Laphroaig 10": "Laphroaig 10 with the House Wedge — the wedge's crisp iceberg and bleu dressing are completely overwhelmed by Laphroaig's intensity. Capable only as a separate drink alongside; not a match. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "House Wedge|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against wheated honey. Works as a before-course sip; not a driving pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Tours-sur-Marne Pinot Noir rosé — benchmark Champagne with strawberry, raspberry, silky mousse, long finish. The red berry cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the structure meets blue cheese dressing without crumpling. Strong elegant pairing for wedge — sophisticated opener that reframes the classic.",
  "House Wedge|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne — green apple, lemon zest, brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the structure meets blue cheese dressing. Strong elegant pairing for the wedge — refined Champagne reframes the classic steakhouse salad.",
  "House Wedge|Le Garenne Rosé": "Le Garenne Rosé — Loire Pinot Noir with strawberry, watermelon, light floral, dry crisp. The fresh-fruit acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the floral lift plays against blue cheese dressing without clashing. Peak summer rosé for the wedge — seasonal lightness reframes the classic.",
  "House Wedge|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Tuscany Brunello di Montalcino DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Le Ragnaie, five years minimum aging, dried cherry, tobacco, leather, rose petal, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the leather plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as Italy's-most-prestigious for the wedge — Brunello deserves aged beef.",
  "House Wedge|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The lemon-and-honey profile cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and lavender plays against blue cheese; Prosecco adds lift. Strong opener pairing — approachable floral martini for a classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Les Pallières Raciné": "Gigondas Rhône Valley Grenache — Les Pallières Raciné, Brunier-family old-vine, wild red fruit, garrigue, iron, earthy mineral, full body. The garrigue plays with blue-cheese funk and the iron matches bacon-fat char. Works for wedge — cold iceberg mutes Raciné's old-vine depth, so a lighter Chianti lifts higher.",
  "House Wedge|Liberator Gin": "Liberator with the House Wedge — Michigan 100%-grain craft against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Liberator threads the wedge with Ferndale-craft character.",
  "House Wedge|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Licor 43 for the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Limoncello on the steak course instead.",
  "House Wedge|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, spice, refined structure. The low tannin and high acid cut iceberg-and-bacon and the dark cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak by-the-glass Pinot for the wedge — Oregon elegance reframes the classic steakhouse salad. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Little Book": "Little Book's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the brown-spirit warmth threads the wedge, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; boutique covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "House Wedge|Loaded Potato": "House Wedge and the loaded potato together -- soup-and-salad open the meal. Works; both courses share the opening without crowding.",
  "House Wedge|Lobster Mac": "The wedge and the lobster mac share cheese as the primary character. Avoid; the meal doubles down without contrast. Route the wedge with St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé or the lobster mac with Cowboy Ribeye, Far Niente Chardonnay, or The Tomahawk instead.",
  "House Wedge|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year with the House Wedge — 20yr aged Highland against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Loch Lomond 20's complex depth engages the bleu at collector register.",
  "House Wedge|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the House Wedge — sourced premium rye against iceberg and bleu. Works; boutique-blend wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Long Road Gin": "Long Road with the House Wedge — ultra-local GR gin against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Long Road's Michigan-botanical character threads the wedge with hometown pride.",
  "House Wedge|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's earthen-pit smoke profile finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the espadín-and-smoke body wraps the wedge, and the wood-smoke note underlines the bowl. Works; the dessert sits cleanly against the meal's register.",
  "House Wedge|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII with the House Wedge — luxury icon against iceberg and bleu. Works; the wedge is far too casual for Louis XIII — save the bottle for something worthy of its register.",
  "House Wedge|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano with the House Wedge — Basilicata amaro against iceberg and bleu. Works; floral-anise is a distinctive post-salad digestif.",
  "House Wedge|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Abruzzo Montepulciano d'Abruzzo — Lunaria earthy grippy, dark cherry, dried herbs, rustic medium body, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as Italian-approachable red for the wedge — rustic value reframes the classic salad.",
  "House Wedge|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the scotch backbone matches the wedge, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; bonded weight slightly over-specified.",
  "House Wedge|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the scotch depth settles into the wedge, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; milestone-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "House Wedge|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate's estate Speyside character sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the malt-driven body wraps the wedge, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; private-selection wasted on the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin holds against blue-cheese funk and the black pepper plays with bacon-fat char. Works for wedge — iceberg-cold mutes Macauley's power, so a lighter red lifts higher.",
  "House Wedge|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot — bright strawberry, red cherry, light floral, soft tannins. The light acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the strawberry plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong accessible Pinot for the wedge — Macauley mid-tier reframes the classic steakhouse salad.",
  "House Wedge|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Mahon Gin": "Mahón with the House Wedge — Xoriguer Mahón against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Mahón's sharp juniper engages the bleu with Mediterranean character.",
  "House Wedge|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against iconic wheated. Works as a before-course sip; no driver pairing.",
  "House Wedge|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with the House Wedge — iceberg's crispness and bleu can't carry the Cellar Aged's depth. Works only as a separate sip.",
  "House Wedge|Malibu Rum": "House Wedge: iceberg with bacon and bleu meets Malibu. Excellent; the coconut-sweet liqueur character cuts through bacon-and-bleu with tropical contrast — unexpected but earns its place at liqueur register.",
  "House Wedge|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye with the House Wedge — 16-year Michigan against iceberg and bleu. Works; extended-aged home-state pour wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Loire Cab Franc — Chinon light body, raspberry, red cherry, bell pepper, forest floor, supple low tannin, bright high acid. The bright acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the red cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak Loire CF for the wedge — food-wine acidity reframes the classic salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|Margarita": "Corazon Blanco, lemon, lime, orange, Cointreau — salted rim. The triple-citrus and salt profile cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and plays against blue cheese's funk; Cointreau smooths the edges. Peak Margarita pairing for the wedge — classic and approachable together. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "House Wedge|Marimar Estate Christina": "Russian River Green Valley Pinot — ripe strawberry, cherry compote, warm spice, silky. The acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the cherry-compote plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak plush Pinot for the wedge — Russian River opulence reframes the classic steakhouse salad. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "House Wedge|Market Fish": "Market Fish after the wedge -- the iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch refreshes the palate, the kitchen-driven fish following with clean flesh that the cold opener primed. Strong; the salad cleans the table for the rotating main.",
  "House Wedge|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe's sweet wormwood-anise character holds the line with the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the anise threads the herb edge. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "House Wedge|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Year Bourbon's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon weight handles the wedge, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for this dish's register.",
  "House Wedge|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye with the House Wedge — aged Michter's against iceberg and bleu. Works; premium aged Michter's wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "House Wedge|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the House Wedge — smooth rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; the Michter's polish cuts the bleu with refined rye character.",
  "House Wedge|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco with the House Wedge — premium craft blanco against iceberg and bleu. The clean highland agave cuts the bleu at the additive-free premium register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo's aged-agave character reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the aged-agave weight wraps the wedge, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; approachable agave is a gentle counterpoint.",
  "House Wedge|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado with the House Wedge — modern reposado against iceberg and bleu. The clean agave character cuts the bleu with contemporary simplicity. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "House Wedge|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the caramel-spice register matches the wedge, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; Marian McLain L.E.",
  "House Wedge|Mocha Creme": "House Wedge and the mocha creme -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "House Wedge|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 with the House Wedge — 47-botanical Black Forest against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Monkey 47's complexity meets the bleu with Schwarzwald depth.",
  "House Wedge|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder with the House Wedge — triple-Speyside-malt blend against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Monkey Shoulder's Speyside-derived fruit threads the wedge with distinctive blended-malt character.",
  "House Wedge|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro with the House Wedge — approachable amaro against iceberg and bleu. Works; vanilla-orange is a friendly post-salad option.",
  "House Wedge|Mount Gay Rum": "House Wedge: iceberg with bacon and bleu meets Mount Gay. Excellent; the heritage-rum character with slight warmth meets bacon-and-bleu at craft register.",
  "House Wedge|Muga Reserva": "Rioja Alta Reserva Tempranillo-blend — Muga, American-French oak, strawberry, dried cherry, vanilla, leather. The leather matches blue-cheese funk and the vanilla-oak plays with bacon-fat char. Works for wedge — cold iceberg mutes Muga's oak complexity, so a lighter Chianti reads cleaner.",
  "House Wedge|Mushroom Bisque": "House Wedge and the mushroom bisque together -- soup-and-salad open the meal. Works; both courses share the opening without crowding.",
  "House Wedge|Mushrooms": "House Wedge and the mushrooms -- the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch meets the mushrooms' earthy umami weight: the meal stacks savory weight, the courses share register without lift. Works; no conflict however the table orders them.",
  "House Wedge|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's dense-spirit weight finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the rich pour wraps the wedge, and the dense weight underlines the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "House Wedge|Negroni": "Gin, Contratto, Campari. The bitter-orange and herbal complexity cut iceberg-and-bacon weight and Campari's edge plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as an opener for bitter-cocktail fans — Negroni's structure meets the wedge's layered components.",
  "House Wedge|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye with the House Wedge — novelty UK-matured rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; distinctive cask character cuts the bleu with Yorkshire-influence voice.",
  "House Wedge|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against novelty bourbon. Works; the story is wasted on the salad.",
  "House Wedge|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon backbone carries the wedge, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; annual-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "House Wedge|New York Sour": "Jameson, lemon, simple, egg white, Daou Cab float. The sour profile cuts iceberg's cold crunch and blue-cheese richness; the Cab float adds visual interest and red-wine weight against the wedge. Peak opener cocktail when guest wants a showpiece alongside a classic salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Nickel & Nickel — Oakville Cab, deep cassis, blackberry, graphite, subtle oak. The concentrated body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the graphite plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as prestige Oakville Cab for the wedge — terroir-specific red, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolet's with the House Wedge — floral-contemporary Dutch gin against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Nolet's fruit-forward botanicals meet bleu sharpness with contemporary distinction — the unexpected wedge pairing that actually works.",
  "House Wedge|Not a Paper Plane": "Bulleit bourbon, Nonino Quintessential, Aperol, Angostura — stirred. The bourbon-and-amaro structure can handle iceberg-and-blue-cheese weight and Aperol's bitter orange plays against the dressing. Works as an amaro-forward opener — heavier than most wedge cocktails but holds up.",
  "House Wedge|Oban 14": "Oban 14 with the House Wedge — coastal Highland against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Oban's maritime character engages the bleu with distinctive coastal register.",
  "House Wedge|Oban 18": "Oban 18 with the House Wedge — 18yr aged Oban against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Oban 18's aged coastal depth engages the bleu at premium register.",
  "House Wedge|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the barrel-rested body handles the wedge, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "House Wedge|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Odette — Stags Leap Cab, dark fruit, sage, cedar, graphite, refined tannin. The velvet-glove body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the sage-cedar plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as refined Stags Leap Cab for the wedge — iron-fist-velvet-glove red, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon weight handles the wedge, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; AE CS handles the richness at premium register.",
  "House Wedge|Old Emmer": "House Wedge: iceberg and bleu meet emmer-wheat wheated bourbon. Works; Old Emmer's wheated character threads the wedge at craft register.",
  "House Wedge|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against wheated BIB. Works; pour slightly over-specified for the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against aged wheated BIB. Works; the aging is wasted on the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the brown-spirit warmth threads the wedge, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Opus One": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark fruit plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as collector Napa blend for the wedge — prestige wine reframes the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Willamette Trappist Chardonnay — rich pear, butterscotch, toasted oak. The rich oak weight cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the butterscotch plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong provenance Chardonnay for the wedge — opulent weight reframes the classic steakhouse salad, mention the monastery.",
  "House Wedge|Pablo Sour": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla, egg white. The foam adds textural contrast against iceberg's crunch and lemon cuts bacon-and-blue-cheese dressing; vanilla softens the edges. Peak textural opener for the wedge — 'the most texturally interesting cocktail on the tequila side' shows well.",
  "House Wedge|Paloma": "Reposado tequila, fresh grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The grapefruit's bitter-bright cuts iceberg-and-blue-cheese weight and vanilla softens against bacon; reposado handles the wedge's layered components. Peak agave opener for the classic wedge — both deliver clean structure.",
  "House Wedge|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye with the House Wedge — Van Winkle unicorn against iceberg and bleu. Works; Pappy's allocation story is wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the brown-spirit warmth threads the wedge, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; Marian McLain L.E.",
  "House Wedge|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's rested-agave body sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the oak-aged tequila body threads the wedge, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; the earthy craft character doesn't quite lift the truffle the way polished reposados suggest they might.",
  "House Wedge|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver with the House Wedge — globally-marketed blanco against iceberg and bleu. The clean citrus cuts the bleu at the everyday accessible register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Patron XO Cafe on the steak course instead.",
  "House Wedge|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Bouzy Grand Cru grower rosé Champagne — structured ripe raspberry and red currant with mineral finish. The Burgundian body and fine bubbles cut iceberg-and-bacon weight and the red-fruit frames blue cheese dressing. Strong elegant pairing for the wedge — grower Champagne depth on the classic.",
  "House Wedge|Peanut Butter Brownie": "House Wedge and the PB brownie -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "House Wedge|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Peju — Rutherford Cab, dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the cocoa plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as elegant Napa Cab for the wedge — Rutherford-dust red choice, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand with the House Wedge — craft-premium cognac against iceberg and bleu. Works; Ferrand's artisanal character is over-specified for the wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "2015 Côte des Blancs prestige cuvée — layered citrus, toasted brioche, white flowers, creamy refined. The vintage body and brioche cut iceberg-and-bacon and the creamy texture complements blue cheese. Strong elegant grower Champagne for the wedge — prestige-level pairing on the classic.",
  "House Wedge|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye with the House Wedge — 110-proof HH against iceberg and bleu. Strong; high-proof rye cuts the bleu with Maryland-style intensity.",
  "House Wedge|Pineau des Charentes": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The honey bridges blue-cheese funk and the warm nutty Cognac plays with bacon-fat char. Strong Pineau for wedge — aperitif-mode honey-on-funk match.",
  "House Wedge|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Piedmont Barbaresco DOCG Nebbiolo — Pio Cesare Barolo's approachable sibling, floral aromatics, dried cherry, tar, firm-but-elegant tannins, high acid. The high acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dried cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as approachable-Barolo-sibling for the wedge — Barbaresco elegance reframes the classic salad.",
  "House Wedge|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth with the House Wedge — softer Plymouth-style against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Plymouth's sweet notes match the bleu sharpness with textural harmony the drier London Drys miss.",
  "House Wedge|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Aldo Conterno Bussia Monforte d'Alba, rose petal, tar, dried cherry, orange peel, iron minerality, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the orange peel plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as serious-Barolo for the wedge — this bottle deserves aged beef.",
  "House Wedge|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery Cuvée Louise 2004 — 20-year aged Champagne. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich. The aged depth cuts iceberg-and-bacon and honey plays against blue cheese. Strong occasion pairing for wedge — treat the wine accordingly when opening for special moments.",
  "House Wedge|Porterhouse": "The porterhouse after the wedge -- the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch sets the table for the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: protein meets umami, the table builds substance. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "House Wedge|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare alongside the wedge -- both pull from the umami register, the contrast sits flat. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "House Wedge|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt — Napa Cab, blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla. The soft tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the chocolate plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as polished Napa Cab for the wedge — indulgent red choice, though whites or rosés peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|RAEN Royal St Robert": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, crushed rock, high acid. The high acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the crushed-rock plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak Burgundian-style California Pinot for the wedge — one of California's finest reframes the salad. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "House Wedge|Rainbow Trout": "Rainbow Trout after the wedge -- the iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch refreshes the palate, the trout following with delicate pan-finished flesh that the cold opener gave clean room for. Strong; the salad cleans for the gentle main.",
  "House Wedge|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit character, bright acid. The bubbles cut the blue cheese dressing weight and the rosé fruit plays against the brown-sugar-and-paprika lardon seasoning. Fun, non-obvious sparkling route for the wedge. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 with the House Wedge — pot still Irish against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Redbreast 12's rich-spicy-oily character engages the bleu at premium-Irish register.",
  "House Wedge|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 with the House Wedge — 21-year aged pot still Irish against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Redbreast 21's aged depth engages the bleu at collector register.",
  "House Wedge|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon backbone carries the wedge, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "House Wedge|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Rémy VSOP with the House Wedge — premium cognac against iceberg and bleu. Works; Rémy's refinement is over-specified for the wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the añejo register carries the wedge, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "House Wedge|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka with the House Wedge — small-batch Icelandic against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; the mineral character brightens the wedge with distinctive lift.",
  "House Wedge|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique with the House Wedge — Rishi's retailer-label against iceberg and bleu. Works; boutique-sourced wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Roast Half Chicken": "Roast Half Chicken after the wedge -- the iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch refreshes the palate, the chicken following with herbed crisp-skin roast that the cold opener primed cleanly. Strong; the salad cleans for the warm bird.",
  "House Wedge|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The wedge alongside the chickpea soup -- the bleu-and-bacon plate sits cleanly beside the warm chickpea bowl, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; the opening pair holds at table register.",
  "House Wedge|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs — Le Mesnil Grand Cru blanc de blancs. Chalky minerality, lemon curd, green apple, no dosage. The austere acidity cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the chalk-mineral plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong sommelier Champagne for the wedge — precision reframes the classic.",
  "House Wedge|Roku Gin": "Roku with the House Wedge — Japanese sakura-yuzu-tea-sansho gin against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Roku's sansho-pepper engages the bleu with distinctive Japanese character.",
  "House Wedge|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey premium-sipping rum finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the heavy body handles the wedge, and the rich character softens the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "House Wedge|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against aged bourbon. Works; the Wild Turkey depth is underused by the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Salt-Cured Halibut after the wedge -- the iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch refreshes the palate, the halibut following with firm salt-cured flesh that the cold opener primed cleanly. Strong; the salad's bite carries into the cured main.",
  "House Wedge|Sambuca": "Sambuca's sweet anise-elderflower character lifts the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the anise threads the herb edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "House Wedge|San Simeon Stormwatch": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as Paso value Bordeaux blend for the wedge — solid full-body reframes the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sta. Rita Hills Santa Barbara Pinot — rich, layered, dark cherry, earth, dried herbs. The acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dried-herbs play against blue cheese dressing. Strong legendary-vineyard Pinot for the wedge — Santa Barbara finesse on the classic salad.",
  "House Wedge|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The wedge and the garlic spinach -- acid breaks the umami stack, the table earns the brighter note. Works; sequence them or share the table, the call holds either way.",
  "House Wedge|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Sauternes Glass lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "House Wedge|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. Spirit-forward cocktail is heavier than the iceberg wedge strictly demands, but Peychaud's floral-cherry note plays against bacon-and-blue-cheese. Works as an opener when guest leads with the classic.",
  "House Wedge|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye with the House Wedge — BT rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; the rye's bright pepper cuts the bleu's richness differently than bourbon.",
  "House Wedge|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the House Wedge — New Zealand single malt against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Fortuna's distinctive NZ character engages the bleu at terroir-driven register.",
  "House Wedge|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the leather plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as King-of-Italian for the wedge — Scavino reframes the salad, though this bottle deserves the meat course.",
  "House Wedge|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Rheingau Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry fruit cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the acid plays against blue cheese dressing; petrol adds intrigue. Strong German Riesling for the wedge — conversation-starter wine reframes the classic steakhouse salad.",
  "House Wedge|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin, smooth. The medium tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark chocolate plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak by-the-glass Cab for the wedge — accessible California value reframes the classic salad. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "House Wedge|Seafood Tower": "Seafood Tower and the wedge together -- acid lifts the umami, the meal carries lift without losing depth. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "House Wedge|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops alongside the wedge -- the caramelized sear plays against the cool wedge plate. Strong; the openers compose cleanly together.",
  "House Wedge|Seasonal Soup": "House Wedge and the seasonal soup together -- soup-and-salad open the meal. Works; both courses share the opening without crowding.",
  "House Wedge|Seasonal Vegetables": "The wedge and the seasonal vegetables -- a quiet call, the meal carries without strain. Works; sequence them or share the table, the call holds either way.",
  "House Wedge|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Tuscany Brunello DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Sesti biodynamic, cherry, dried rose, sandalwood, firm tannin. The firm tannin holds against blue-cheese funk and the dried-rose lift plays with bacon-fat. Works for wedge — cold iceberg dulls Sesti's aromatics, so a lighter Chianti lifts higher.",
  "House Wedge|Shafer 1.5": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin. The plush tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the vanilla plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as Stags Leap Cab for the wedge — Shafer opulence reframes the classic salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Shafer Hillside Select": "Stags Leap collector's Cab — Hillside Select 32-month French oak, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violet, graphite, dense yet silky, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the violet plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as collector's Napa Cab for the wedge — Hillside Select reframes the classic salad, but this bottle deserves the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|Shrimp Bisque": "The wedge alongside the shrimp bisque -- the iceberg wedge holds against the silky shrimp cream. Works; the opening pair holds at table register.",
  "House Wedge|Shrimp Cocktail": "The shrimp cocktail with the wedge -- the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish meets the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bright register pulls against the savory, the contrast lands. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "House Wedge|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco with the House Wedge — tahona-crushed traditional against iceberg and bleu. The earthier agave cuts the bleu with craft distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado with the House Wedge — tahona-crushed traditional against iceberg and bleu. The earthier agave cuts the bleu distinctly; craft reposado for salad orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak — Alexander Valley Cab, American-oak vanilla, coconut, dill. The lush body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the vanilla plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as recognizable Cab for the wedge — American-oak signature red, though whites or rosés peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Boutique Napa family estate Cab — dark plum, cassis, toasted oak, long refined finish, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the toasted oak plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as boutique Napa Cab for the wedge — Simon Family estate polish reframes the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "St. Helena Napa Cab — Spottswoode organic family estate, plum, dark cherry, violet, silky medium tannin. The silky tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the plum plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as organic Napa Cab for the wedge — Spottswoode polish reframes the classic salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry — Napa Cab, blackberry, cassis, mocha, full body. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the mocha plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as by-the-glass Cab for the wedge — unconventional red choice, though whites or rosés peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry — Napa Sauv Blanc with lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, clean lively. The lively acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the lemon-gooseberry plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak Napa Sauv Blanc for the wedge — rounded body reframes the classic, brightness keeps it fresh. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the espresso plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as estate Meritage for the wedge — Napa structure reframes the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon depth settles into the wedge, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; a gentle composed match at the premium tier.",
  "House Wedge|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit with the House Wedge — ultra-premium Russian wheat against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Elit's clarity frames the wedge at top-shelf register with maximal precision.",
  "House Wedge|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — classic NZ with passionfruit, grapefruit, fresh-cut grass, zesty acidity. The zesty acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the passionfruit-grapefruit plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak by-the-glass Sauv Blanc for the wedge — reliable bright crowd-pleaser on the classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "House Wedge|Swordfish": "Swordfish after the wedge -- the iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch refreshes the palate, the swordfish following with meaty pan-finished steak that the cold opener primed cleanly. Strong; the salad cleans for the dense fish course.",
  "House Wedge|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 with the House Wedge — premium whole-citrus against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Tanq 10's citrus is a refined wedge pairing without Plymouth's sweetness.",
  "House Wedge|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray with the House Wedge — juniper icon against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Tanq's botanicals cut the bleu where Plymouth's softness matches it differently.",
  "House Wedge|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sweet wine on cold lettuce refuses the salad's clean register. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle on the steak course instead.",
  "House Wedge|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the añejo register carries the wedge, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "House Wedge|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter — Napa Cab, rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth finish. The polished body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the mocha plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as mid-tier Napa Cab for the wedge — unconventional crowd-pleaser red, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tuscany Chianti Classico DOCG Sangiovese — Arceno vibrant cherry, dried herbs, tomato leaf, savory earth, medium body, medium tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the vibrant cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as Tuscan Sangiovese for the wedge — Italian table wine reframes the classic salad.",
  "House Wedge|The BG": "Traveller whiskey, honey lavender syrup, whole grapefruit squeeze, black walnut bitters, ginger beer. The grapefruit and ginger cut iceberg-and-bacon weight and honey lavender softens against blue cheese; black walnut adds complexity. Strong sessionable whiskey opener for the wedge.",
  "House Wedge|The Happy Wife": "Tito's, orange juice, ginger syrup — stirred. The orange juice cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and ginger's warmth plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong opener cocktail for the wedge — uncomplicated screwdriver-plus-ginger meets classic wedge structure.",
  "House Wedge|The Manhattan": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Cocktail weight is heavier than the iceberg-bacon-blue-cheese wedge strictly wants, but the vanilla softens enough for the pairing to hold. Works as an opener when guest leads with a spirit-forward classic.",
  "House Wedge|The Prisoner Red Blend": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The smooth tannin holds against blue-cheese funk and the layered fruit plays with bacon-fat. Works for wedge — cold iceberg mutes the lushness, a lighter Chianti lifts higher.",
  "House Wedge|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk into the wedge -- the 36oz showpiece matches the classic wedge. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "House Wedge|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the House Wedge — BTAC allocated against iceberg and bleu. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the House Wedge — Barton chardonnay-cask against iceberg and bleu. Works; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-butter engages the bleu with distinctive white-wine character.",
  "House Wedge|Titos Vodka": "Tito's with the House Wedge — Texas corn vodka against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Tito's American roundness threads the wedge cleanly — this is the most democratic vodka-wedge pairing on the menu, comfortable for any guest.",
  "House Wedge|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory with the House Wedge — light-floral Japanese entry blend against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Toki's delicate character threads the wedge at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "House Wedge|Tomato Basil": "The wedge alongside the tomato basil -- the bleu dressing reads beside the herb-bright soup. Works; the opening pair holds at table register.",
  "House Wedge|Transfusion": "Hangar 1 vodka muddled with black grapes and lime, ginger beer top. The tart grape and lime cut iceberg-and-bacon weight and the ginger beer's dry finish plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak tall-sessionable cocktail for the wedge — fruit-led freshness meets classic salad.",
  "House Wedge|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado with the House Wedge — cedar-aged reposado against iceberg and bleu. The distinctive pine notes cut the bleu with a character no other reposado brings. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "House Wedge|Truffle Fries": "The wedge and the truffle fries -- both cheese-forward, the table doubles up on cheese. Avoid; pick one or the other. Pair the wedge with St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé; pair the truffle fries with The Tomahawk, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka.",
  "House Wedge|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W. with the House Wedge — heritage triple-distilled Irish against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Tullamore's drier character engages the bleu at traditional register.",
  "House Wedge|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna after the wedge -- the iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch refreshes the palate, the tuna following with seared-rare flesh and sesame crust that the cold opener primed. Strong; cold salad into seared-rare main reads composed.",
  "House Wedge|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "House Wedge with the curry -- the cool crunch frames the spiced vegetable plate, and the spice lifts the green plate. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "House Wedge|Venge Family Reserve": "Kirk Venge flagship Oakville Cab — concentrated blackcurrant, dark chocolate, vanilla, seamless polished finish, soft tannin. The soft tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark chocolate plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as Oakville flagship for the wedge — Venge opulence reframes the classic salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Venge Scout's Honor": "Napa Zin-led blend — Kirk Venge's Scout's Honor, blackberry, boysenberry, black pepper, vanilla, full body, generous finish. The full body overshadows iceberg-and-blue-cheese simplicity, though black pepper meets the dressing's bite. Works for wedge — Le Garenne Rosé or Sauv Blanc reads cleaner on the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Venge Silencieux": "Kirk Venge single-vineyard Napa — dark cherry, cassis, chocolate, floral violet, silky soft tannin. The silky tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the chocolate plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as winemaker's Napa Cab for the wedge — Venge precision reframes the classic salad; whites peak higher.",
  "House Wedge|Vesper": "Nolet's gin, Ketel One vodka, Lillet — stirred cold. The cocktail's clean structure cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and Lillet's citrus-bitter plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong opener pairing — the wedge's layered components meet the martini's composure.",
  "House Wedge|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut — Reims Champagne, ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse, lively acidity. The creamy body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the biscuit plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong crowd-pleasing Champagne for the wedge — celebration pour on the classic salad.",
  "House Wedge|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto vermouth, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. The cocktail's complexity is heavier than the iceberg wedge demands, but Bénédictine's herbal depth plays off the blue cheese. Works as an opener when guest leads with a layered classic.",
  "House Wedge|Villon Cognac": "Villon with the House Wedge — modern cognac against iceberg and bleu. Works; Villon's infusion is over-specified for the wedge.",
  "House Wedge|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Vin Santo belongs on the steak course.",
  "House Wedge|Weller 107": "Weller 107 with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against 107 proof. Works only as a separate sip; the pour's heat overpowers the crisp salad.",
  "House Wedge|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year with the House Wedge — iceberg's crispness and bleu's tang meet aged wheated oak. The pour overreaches for the salad's simplicity; works as a before-course sip, not a driving pairing. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "House Wedge|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium with the House Wedge — the wedge's simplicity can't carry the Millennium's depth. Works only as a separate sip; a waste of the pour.",
  "House Wedge|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve with the House Wedge — iceberg's crispness and bleu's tang against soft wheated sweetness. The Weller works but doesn't lift the salad; a before-course sip more than a driver pairing. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "House Wedge|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley with the House Wedge — Buffalo Trace wheat vodka against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Wheatley's clean-wheat finish is the democratic pick for guests who like BT — the wedge works comfortably here.",
  "House Wedge|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the House Wedge — aged WP single-barrel against iceberg and bleu. Works; premium aged rye wasted on the salad's simplicity.",
  "House Wedge|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with the House Wedge — wine-cask 12-year against iceberg and bleu. Works; Old World complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "House Wedge|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with the House Wedge — WP flagship against iceberg and bleu. Works; extended-aged premium wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "House Wedge|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with the House Wedge — unicorn WP against iceberg and bleu. Works; Boss Hog's allocation story is wasted on the salad.",
  "House Wedge|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with the House Wedge — 10th-edition allocated WP against iceberg and bleu. Works; X Commandments story wasted on salad.",
  "House Wedge|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year with the House Wedge — accessible WP against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Canadian-sourced rye cuts the bleu distinctively.",
  "House Wedge|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against craft high-rye. Works; the distinctive character is underused by the salad's simplicity.",
  "House Wedge|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with the House Wedge — small-batch against iceberg and bleu. Works; the pour's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "House Wedge|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the brown-spirit warmth threads the wedge, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "House Wedge|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the House Wedge — elegant rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; the fruit-forward character cuts the bleu with small-batch refinement.",
  "House Wedge|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with the House Wedge — Pacific Northwest craft against iceberg and bleu. Strong; regional craft cuts the bleu with PNW distinctiveness.",
  "House Wedge|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 with the House Wedge — Japan's first single malt distillery against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak character threads the wedge at flagship register.",
  "House Wedge|Yamazaki 18 Year": "House Wedge: iceberg and bleu meet 18yr aged Yamazaki. Strong; Yamazaki 18's aged Mizunara-oak engages the bleu at collector register.",
  "House Wedge|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness reads alongside the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the honey threads the cream. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Asparagus": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon depth settles into the asparagus, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Au Gratin Potatoes": "I.W. Harper 15 with the au gratin — BT-distilled 15yr against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Excellent; IW Harper 15's aged cherry-oak handles the richness at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Beignets": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the oak depth lays under the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; collector-bottle depth gives ultra-premium character.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Bone Marrow": "I.W. Harper 15 with bone marrow — 15yr historic-Kentucky bourbon against umami fat. Excellent; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak-aged character engages marrow at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Bone-In Filet": "I.W. Harper 15 with the bone-in filet — 15yr historic-Kentucky brand against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak character frames the cut at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Broccoli Cheddar": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon weight handles the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Broccolini": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the brown-spirit warmth threads the broccolini, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Brussels and Belly": "I.W. Harper 15 with Brussels + Belly — historic-Kentucky 15yr against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak meets the glaze at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Burrata": "I.W. Harper 15 with burrata — 15yr IW Harper against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Butternut Squash": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the squash bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; Caribbean Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Carrot Cake": "I.W. Harper 15 with carrot cake — 15yr historic-KY against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; IW Harper 15's aged cherry-oak threads the cake at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Cheesecake": "I.W. Harper 15 with cheesecake — 15yr historic-Kentucky against custard. Works; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak threads the cheesecake at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Chilean Seabass": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the I.W. Harper 15 Year for the steak course.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Chocolate Brownie": "I.W. Harper 15 with the chocolate brownie — BT-distilled 15yr against cocoa. Strong; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak meets cocoa with distinctive cherry-cocoa resonance.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Chocolate Cake": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Clam Chowder": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the caramel-spice register matches the chowder, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Cowboy Ribeye": "I.W. Harper 15 with the cowboy ribeye — 15yr BT-distilled IW Harper against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; IW Harper 15 frames the ribeye at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Crab Cake": "I.W. Harper 15 with crab cake — 15yr historic-Kentucky against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Creamed Spinach": "I.W. Harper 15 with creamed spinach — historic-Kentucky 15yr against dairy-rich side. Strong; IW Harper 15's aged character rides the cream at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Creamy Potato": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Creme Brulee": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the caramel-spice register matches the brulee, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; bold character slightly over-specified for the soup.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Escargot": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register rests at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon weight handles the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; aged premium at matched register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Faroe Island Salmon": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. I.W. Harper 15 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Filet Mignon": "I.W. Harper 15 with the filet — historic Kentucky brand, Diageo-owned, BT-distilled 15yr limited release, distinctive cherry-oak aged character. Excellent; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak 15yr character frames tenderloin with distinctive aged-KY-heritage gravitas — a BT-distilled bourbon wearing a historic Kentucky name.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|French Onion": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the oak depth lays under the French onion, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; JD Bonded's backbone handles the richness at Bonded register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Grilled Caesar": "I.W. Harper 15 with the grilled Caesar — 15yr BT-distilled against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Gumbo": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the gumbo, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; the intensity overpowers this dish.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Honey Roasted Carrots": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; smooth gentle match.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|House Wedge": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon depth settles into the wedge, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; extended-aged premium wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Kansas City": "I.W. Harper 15 with the KC — historic-Kentucky 15yr against an 18oz strip. Strong; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak handles the strip at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Loaded Potato": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon weight handles the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Lobster Mac": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the lobster mac, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; annual-release wasted on salad.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Market Fish": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the I.W. Harper 15 Year for the steak course.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Mocha Creme": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; boutique character slightly over-specified.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Mushroom Bisque": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Mushrooms": "I.W. Harper 15 with mushrooms — BT-distilled 15yr against earthy umami. Excellent; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak character threads mushroom earthiness at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet BT-distilled 15yr. Works; IW Harper 15 handles dessert density at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Porterhouse": "I.W. Harper 15 with the porterhouse — 15yr BT-distilled against strip-and-filet. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Prime Tartare": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's crisp char-cherry character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Rainbow Trout": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. I.W. Harper 15 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Roast Half Chicken": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the oak depth lays under the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; boutique-blend depth gives composed dessert character.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive but adds interest.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Salt-Cured Halibut": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the I.W. Harper 15 Year for the steak course.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon body sits with the garlic spinach, and the oak frames the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Seafood Tower": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. I.W. Harper 15 Year belongs on the steak course.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Seared Scallops": "I.W. Harper 15 with seared scallops — BT-distilled 15yr against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Seasonal Soup": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the oak depth lays under the seasonal soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Seasonal Vegetables": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon weight handles the seasonal vegetables, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Shrimp Bisque": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the bourbon depth settles into the shrimp bisque, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Shrimp Cocktail": "I.W. Harper 15 with shrimp cocktail — historic-Kentucky 15yr against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Swordfish": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the I.W. Harper 15 Year for the steak course.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|The Tomahawk": "I.W. Harper 15 with the Tomahawk — BT-distilled 15yr against 32oz cap-fat. Works; IW Harper 15's aged character is under-specified for Tomahawk intensity; reach for a barrel-proof bourbon.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Tomato Basil": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the caramel-spice register matches the tomato basil, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; no driver pairing.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Truffle Fries": "I.W. Harper 15 with truffle fries — historic-KY 15yr against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak depth meets the earthy-rich at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the I.W. Harper 15 Year for the steak course.",
  "I.W. Harper 15 Year|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the caramel-spice register matches the curry, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; oak-spice slightly overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Asparagus": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity finds neutral with the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Beignets": "Veneto Glera — floral Prosecco with white peach, green apple, and crisp citrus finish. The bright acidity cuts beignets' fried-dough weight and the effervescence plays against powdered sugar and berry jam. Peak approachable bubbles for beignets — sweet-tart contrast without the Champagne price tag.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Bone Marrow": "Light Glera Prosecco against marrow's rendered-fat richness — bubbles crumple under the weight and no structural hook exists. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned, and keep Prosecco for starters, seafood tower, or dessert service.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Bone-In Filet": "White peach, green apple, crisp bubbles — a lighter opener or celebration wine. Against the bone-in filet the bubbles flatten and the wine loses its footing. Serve before the course, not with it. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Broccoli Cheddar": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity sits beside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Broccolini": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity sits beside the broccolini's tender green — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; safe call, no friction.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Brussels and Belly": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity reads alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Burrata": "Glera from Veneto — bright Prosecco with white peach, green apple, and crisp refreshing bubbles carrying a clean citrus finish. The light floral profile meets burrata's creamy-delicate center without crushing basil-tomato, and the bubbles refresh between bites. Peak approachable opener for burrata.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Butternut Squash": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity stays alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Carrot Cake": "Veneto Glera, Conegliano Valdobbiadene DOCG — floral, white peach, green apple, clean citrus. The Prosecco's lift cuts cream-cheese frosting without adding sweetness; works when the table wants bubbles rather than a dessert wine. Serviceable finisher. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Cheesecake": "Glera from Veneto — dry Prosecco with white peach, green apple aromatics, and a clean citrus finish. The crisp acidity cuts vanilla-bean cheesecake's cream-cheese density and bubbles lift the graham crust. Peak everyday sparkler for cheesecake — bright contrast to richness at approachable price.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Chilean Seabass": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity reads alongside the seabass's buttery flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Chocolate Brownie": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Il Colle Prosecco Superiore for the steak course.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Chocolate Cake": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- brut bubbles and chocolate weight refuse each other — needs sweetness, not crisp acid. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a sparkling. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Il Colle Prosecco Superiore for the steak course.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Clam Chowder": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity holds with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Cowboy Ribeye": "Bright Prosecco against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — Glera's light body is crushed by rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Caymus, Silver Oak, or bold-proof bourbon, and keep Prosecco for pre-steak celebration, the raw bar, or starters.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Crab Cake": "Veneto Glera — bright floral Prosecco with white peach, green apple, and clean citrus finish. The light-bodied effervescence cuts the romesco and lifts crab's sweet meat without overpowering. Peak affordable pour for crab cake — the Prosecco-and-shellfish classic at accessible price.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Creamed Spinach": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Creamy Potato": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity stays alongside the potato soup's silky body — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Creme Brulee": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's bright sparkling effervescence jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Il Colle Prosecco Superiore on the steak course instead.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Escargot": "Veneto Glera — dry Prosecco with white peach, green apple, and crisp citrus finish. The bright acidity cuts escargot's garlic-parsley butter weight and the light effervescence lifts the snail's richness. Strong approachable pour — though Champagne peaks higher for the escargot course.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Faroe Island Salmon": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity reads alongside the salmon's rich oily flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Filet Mignon": "Glera from Veneto — light dry Prosecco with white peach and green apple. Bubbles work with lean buttery filet, but the light body has limited weight to meet grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on Prosecco; Cab or Pinot peak higher for filet's butter-tender profile.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|French Onion": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Grilled Caesar": "Veneto Glera — bright Prosecco with white peach, green apple, and crisp citrus. The light effervescence can handle anchovy-parmesan dressing, but Prosecco lacks the body to meet grilled-romaine char fully. Works as unconventional opener — Champagne peaks higher for grilled Caesar's layered weight.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Gumbo": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity holds with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity sits beside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|House Wedge": "Glera from Veneto — bright dry Prosecco with white peach, green apple, and clean citrus. The crisp finish cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the effervescence plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong pairing for the wedge — light bubbles reset the palate between each forkful.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Kansas City": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Il Colle Prosecco Superiore for another course.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Loaded Potato": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity sits beside the loaded potato's richness — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Lobster Mac": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity plays cleanly against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Market Fish": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity lifts the fish's clean flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Mocha Creme": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- brut bubbles and chocolate weight refuse each other — needs sweetness, not crisp acid. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a sparkling. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Il Colle Prosecco Superiore on the steak course instead.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Mushroom Bisque": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Mushrooms": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Il Colle Prosecco Superiore lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Porterhouse": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Il Colle Prosecco Superiore for another course.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this versatile sparkling's bright acidity, toasty lees character, and cleansing bubbles. Sparkling's bubbles and acid scrub the starter's fat while the bright frame lifts the plate. Strong — palate-cleansing start.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Rainbow Trout": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity lifts the trout's delicate flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Roast Half Chicken": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity holds with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the mousse-and-citrus lifts the herb-crisped bird, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity sits with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity sits alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Seafood Tower": "Glera Prosecco from Veneto — bright floral with white peach, green apple, clean citrus finish. The light crisp bubbles meet the tower's mixed shellfish and bright brine cleanly, refreshing between oysters, shrimp, and crab. Strong approachable seafood tower pour — value-forward without sacrificing freshness.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Seared Scallops": "Veneto Glera — floral Prosecco with white peach, green apple, crisp bubbles, and clean citrus finish. The light-bodied effervescence meets scallops' clean-seared sweetness without overpowering. Strong starter pour for scallops — though Champagne and high-acid whites peak higher on this pairing.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Seasonal Soup": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity sits beside the seasonal soup's house build — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Seasonal Vegetables": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity sits alongside the seasonal garden plate — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Shrimp Bisque": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity sits beside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Shrimp Cocktail": "Glera from Veneto — bright and floral with white peach, green apple, and a clean citrus finish. The crisp bubbles cut chilled shrimp's sweet brine and play cleanly against cocktail-sauce horseradish bite. Peak starter pour — Prosecco-and-shrimp classic done right. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Swordfish": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity finds register with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|The Tomahawk": "Light floral Prosecco against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — zero structural overlap. Glera has no weight to meet rendered Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward bourbon, and save the Prosecco for openers or brunch service.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Tomato Basil": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity stays alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Truffle Fries": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity sits alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity plays cleanly against the tuna's seared rare crust — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's crisp apple-citrus acidity finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Inhibited|Asparagus": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build plays cleanly against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the cocktail lift sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Inhibited|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. The cocktail's cream-and-coffee weight meets the gratin's cheesy dairy richness; Kahlúa's bitter edge cuts through. Strong unconventional pairing — both dishes share indulgent richness as defining trait.",
  "Inhibited|Beignets": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. Heavy coffee-cream cocktail against light choux-based beignets — the cocktail's weight is higher than the beignets strictly want, but Bailey's cream softens the contrast. Peak if guest wants indulgent over elegant. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Inhibited|Bone Marrow": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build plays cleanly against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the cocktail lift refreshes the starter, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Inhibited|Bone-In Filet": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Inhibited for another course.",
  "Inhibited|Broccoli Cheddar": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bold-mix depth handles the broccoli cheddar, and the bitters edge frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Inhibited|Broccolini": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green — the cocktail lift sits with the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Inhibited|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this coffee-driven cocktail's bitter roast, rich texture, and dessert-adjacent sweetness. Coffee cocktail against a savory plate — wrong register entirely. Works only as a transition pour.",
  "Inhibited|Burrata": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. Dessert cocktail crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile; chocolate has no hook into basil-tomato. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco, French 75, or light gin cocktails.",
  "Inhibited|Butternut Squash": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build reads alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Inhibited|Carrot Cake": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. Rich coffee-cream cocktail — the Bailey's cream mirrors the cream-cheese frosting and the warm-spice notes in the coffee roast echo the cake's cinnamon. Strong cocktail route.",
  "Inhibited|Cheesecake": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso in a chocolate-drizzled martini glass. Cream-on-cream stacking works when the guest wants full dessert indulgence; Kahlúa coffee cuts the weight. Strong cocktail route for a rich after-dinner play.",
  "Inhibited|Chilean Seabass": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build holds the line with the seabass's buttery flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Inhibited|Chocolate Brownie": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. Designed for this pairing — chocolate liqueur and Kahlúa coffee stack with the brownie's cocoa frosting; Bailey's cream softens the density. Peak dessert-cocktail for chocolate.",
  "Inhibited|Chocolate Cake": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso — chocolate-drizzled martini glass. Designed for chocolate desserts; chocolate liqueur stacks with cocoa, Kahlúa's coffee meets the cake's frosting, Bailey's softens the weight. Peak dessert cocktail when the cake returns; currently unavailable. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Inhibited|Clam Chowder": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Inhibited|Cowboy Ribeye": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle mis-pairs with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Inhibited on another course instead.",
  "Inhibited|Crab Cake": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. Dessert-position cream-and-coffee cocktail obliterates crab's sweet-delicate meat on every axis. Never pair — crab cake belongs with light gin cocktails, French 75, or Sauvignon Blanc.",
  "Inhibited|Creamed Spinach": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Inhibited|Creamy Potato": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build leans against the potato soup's silky body — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Inhibited|Creme Brulee": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso in a chocolate-drizzled martini glass. Coffee-and-cream cocktail alongside vanilla custard — rich stacked on rich, but the espresso bitterness keeps it from cloying. Dessert-course-as-cocktail paired with actual dessert. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Inhibited|Escargot": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build leans against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the cocktail lift refreshes the starter. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Inhibited|Faroe Island Salmon": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build reads alongside the salmon's rich oily flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Inhibited|Filet Mignon": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the cocktail's weight crowds the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Inhibited belongs on another course.",
  "Inhibited|French Onion": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Inhibited|Grilled Caesar": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the bold-mix depth handles the grilled Caesar, and the bitters edge frames the bowl. Works; the dessert closes without crowding.",
  "Inhibited|Gumbo": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Inhibited|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build leans against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Inhibited|House Wedge": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build stays alongside the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the cocktail lift matches the leaf-and-acid plate, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Inhibited|Kansas City": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Inhibited for another course.",
  "Inhibited|Loaded Potato": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build holds with the loaded potato's richness — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Inhibited|Lobster Mac": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. Cream-on-cream with the lobster mac's dairy richness; Bailey's softens the cheese weight and espresso cuts through. Strong unconventional pairing when guest wants indulgent cocktail alongside the most indulgent side.",
  "Inhibited|Market Fish": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build holds the line with the fish's clean flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Inhibited|Mocha Creme": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. Designed for coffee-chocolate desserts — chocolate liqueur and Kahlúa meet the mocha crème's cocoa-and-espresso axis directly. Peak cocktail pour once the dessert returns. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Inhibited|Mushroom Bisque": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Inhibited|Mushrooms": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Inhibited|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. Peanut-butter-and-chocolate classic with a coffee-cream cocktail — the chocolate liqueur echoes the fudge base and Bailey's softens the dense peanut butter frosting. Peak cocktail pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Inhibited|Porterhouse": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle mis-pairs with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Inhibited for another course.",
  "Inhibited|Prime Tartare": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build finds neutral with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the cocktail lift refreshes the starter. Works; capable alongside. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Inhibited|Rainbow Trout": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build matches the trout's delicate flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Inhibited|Roast Half Chicken": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build holds with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the cocktail lift carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Inhibited|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Inhibited|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build holds steady against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Inhibited|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build leans against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Inhibited|Seafood Tower": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. Dessert coffee-cream cocktail against raw shellfish spread is incoherent on every axis. Never pair — the tower demands Champagne, French 75, or light gin cocktails, never a chocolate martini.",
  "Inhibited|Seared Scallops": "Tito's, Kahlúa, Bailey's, chocolate liqueur, espresso, chocolate-drizzled glass. Dessert cocktail crushes scallops' delicate sweet-seared profile; chocolate and coffee have no hook into clean seafood. Never pair — scallops demand crisp whites or French 75, not rich coffee cocktails.",
  "Inhibited|Seasonal Soup": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build stays alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Inhibited|Seasonal Vegetables": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build sits alongside the seasonal garden plate — the cocktail lift sits with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Inhibited|Shrimp Bisque": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build plays cleanly against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Inhibited|Shrimp Cocktail": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build sits with the shrimp's chilled sweetness — the cocktail lift carries the shellfish without weight. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Inhibited|Swordfish": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build holds steady against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Inhibited|The Tomahawk": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle mis-pairs with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Inhibited belongs on another course.",
  "Inhibited|Tomato Basil": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build plays cleanly against the tomato basil's bright acid — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Inhibited|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: this earthy, umami-packed side's mushroom-and-truffle depth meets this coffee-driven cocktail's bitter roast, rich texture, and dessert-adjacent sweetness. Coffee cocktail against a savory plate — wrong register entirely. Strong — the interaction lands despite the axis challenge.",
  "Inhibited|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build sits with the tuna's seared rare crust — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Inhibited|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Asparagus": "Inverroche Gin's crisp earth-floral botanicals sits beside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Inverroche Gin's crisp earth-floral botanicals leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Beignets": "Inverroche with beignets — South African fynbos gin against fried dough. Works; the floral-herbal profile is over-specified for casual dessert.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Bone Marrow": "Inverroche with bone marrow — fynbos-floral South African gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Inverroche Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Inverroche Gin for another course.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Inverroche Gin's crisp earth-floral botanicals stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Broccolini": "Inverroche Gin's crisp earth-floral botanicals reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Inverroche Gin's crisp earth-floral botanicals reads alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs frame the grilled char. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Burrata": "Inverroche with burrata — South African gin built on fynbos (Western Cape native flora), distinctive floral-herbal terroir. Excellent; Inverroche's fynbos-floral profile is the featured craft-gin-burrata pairing in the cluster — the Cape-floral character meets cream with a register no other gin on the menu can touch.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Butternut Squash": "Inverroche Gin's crisp earth-floral botanicals stays alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Carrot Cake": "Inverroche Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Inverroche Gin for the steak course.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Cheesecake": "Inverroche Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Inverroche Gin for the steak course.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Inverroche Gin's crisp earth-floral botanicals finds register with the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Inverroche Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Inverroche Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Inverroche Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Inverroche Gin for the steak course.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Clam Chowder": "Inverroche Gin's crisp earth-floral botanicals leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Inverroche with the cowboy ribeye — Western Cape craft gin against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Inverroche Gin|Crab Cake": "Inverroche with crab cake — South African fynbos gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; the Cape-floral botanicals add distinctive dimension to crab no European craft gin offers.",
