  "Inverroche Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Inverroche Gin's crisp earth-floral botanicals sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Asparagus": "Iron Fish Gin's cask-strength citrus-earth botanicals plays cleanly against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Iron Fish Gin's cask-strength citrus-earth botanicals plays cleanly against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Beignets": "Iron Fish Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- gin's herbal edge has no foil against warm pastry sugar — sharp meets sweet, neither wins. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Iron Fish Gin for the steak course.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Bone Marrow": "Iron Fish with bone marrow — Thompsonville estate gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Iron Fish Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Iron Fish Gin on another course instead.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Iron Fish Gin's cask-strength citrus-earth botanicals plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Broccolini": "Iron Fish Gin's cask-strength citrus-earth botanicals plays cleanly against the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Iron Fish Gin's cask-strength citrus-earth botanicals reads alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Burrata": "Iron Fish with burrata — Iron Fish Distillery, Thompsonville (Benzie County) northern Michigan, farm-to-bottle with estate-grown botanicals and Michigan honey. Excellent; Iron Fish's farm-forward character frames burrata with northern-Michigan-pride distinction — the farm-to-bottle gin pairing no other Local MI gin replicates.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Butternut Squash": "Iron Fish Gin's cask-strength citrus-earth botanicals plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey threads the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Carrot Cake": "Iron Fish Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- juniper-citrus and warm cake spices pull in opposite directions. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Iron Fish Gin for the steak course.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Cheesecake": "Iron Fish Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Iron Fish Gin for the steak course.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Iron Fish Gin's cask-strength citrus-earth botanicals lifts the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Iron Fish Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Iron Fish Gin for the steak course.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Iron Fish Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Iron Fish Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Clam Chowder": "Iron Fish Gin's cask-strength citrus-earth botanicals plays cleanly against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Iron Fish Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Iron Fish Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Iron Fish Gin belongs on another course.",
