  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Gumbo": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards' silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the Pinot body brightens the gumbo, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Honey Roasted Carrots": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards's cherry-coffee tannin sits beside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the coffee meets the caramel. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|House Wedge": "Anderson Valley Pinot — dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, refined tannin. The acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the mocha plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong Anderson Valley Pinot for the wedge — concentrated Mendocino reframes the classic steakhouse salad.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Kansas City": "Anderson Valley Pinot — dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, firm-but-refined tannin, long finish. The refined structure handles Kansas City's lean-bold strip and the mocha plays against beefy char. Peak Anderson Valley Pinot for the strip — concentrated Mendocino red structured enough for the marbled cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Loaded Potato": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards's cherry-coffee tannin sits alongside the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Lobster Mac": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards's cherry-coffee tannin reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Market Fish": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards' silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the J Davies Ferrington Vineyards on the steak course instead.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Mocha Creme": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards' silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. J Davies Ferrington Vineyards belongs on the steak course.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Mushroom Bisque": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards's cherry-coffee tannin stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Mushrooms": "Anderson Valley Pinot — dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, refined tannin. The mocha-earth echoes roasted-mushroom glutamate and the blackberry plays against the earth depth. Peak Anderson Valley Pinot for mushrooms — concentrated Mendocino on the classic Pinot-and-mushroom pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Peanut Butter Brownie": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards' silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. J Davies Ferrington Vineyards lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Porterhouse": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards's cherry-coffee tannin reads alongside the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Prime Tartare": "Anderson Valley Pinot — dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, refined tannin. The refined tannin stretches beyond most Pinots for tartare — the concentration can slightly bury raw-beef delicacy vs. lighter Pinots. Works as Anderson Valley Pinot for tartare — Mendocino depth on the raw-beef classic.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Rainbow Trout": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards' silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. J Davies Ferrington Vineyards lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Roast Half Chicken": "Anderson Valley Pinot — dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, refined tannin, long finish. The refined structure plays against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak Anderson Valley Pinot for chicken across most seasonal builds. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards's cherry-coffee tannin finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Salt-Cured Halibut": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards' silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. J Davies Ferrington Vineyards lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards's cherry-coffee tannin leans against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Seafood Tower": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards' silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the J Davies Ferrington Vineyards for the steak course.",
  "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards|Seared Scallops": "Anderson Valley Pinot — dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, refined tannin. The refined tannin leaves room for scallop sweetness and the mocha plays against caramelized-exterior char. Strong Anderson Valley Pinot for scallops — concentrated Mendocino on the delicate shellfish.",
