  "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select|Rainbow Trout": "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select for the steak course.",
  "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select|Roast Half Chicken": "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon weight handles the roast chicken, and the oak-and-spice register settles the plate. Works; the story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the chickpea soup, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon weight handles the garlic spinach, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; Portwood is over-specified for a side — save for main course.",
  "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select|Seafood Tower": "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select for the steak course.",
