  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Rainbow Trout": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Jameson Irish Whiskey for the steak course.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Roast Half Chicken": "Jameson with the chicken — iconic entry Irish whiskey, triple-distilled (grain + pot still + single malt), smooth sherry+bourbon-cask blend. Excellent; Jameson's smooth-approachable character is the featured Irish-chicken pairing — the triple-distilled softness complements roasted poultry at crowd-pleasing register, the most democratic Irish-and-chicken on the menu.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the chickpea soup, and the gentle barley note frames the bowl. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for this dish's register.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Jameson Irish Whiskey for the steak course.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the garlic spinach, and the soft malt rounds the side. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Seafood Tower": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Jameson Irish Whiskey for the steak course.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Seared Scallops": "Jameson with seared scallops — iconic entry Irish against scallop delicacy. Works; Jameson's smooth triple-distilled character is gentle enough to sit alongside scallops where heavier whiskies can't; not featured but the softest whisky option.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Seasonal Soup": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the seasonal soup, and the grain register softens the bowl. Works; Remus 15 handles dessert density at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Seasonal Vegetables": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the triple-distilled softness sits with the seasonal vegetables, and the malt rounds the side. Works; the spicy-sweet character gives the dessert a Barton frame.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Shrimp Bisque": "Jameson with shrimp bisque — iconic entry Irish against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Jameson's smooth sherry+bourbon-cask character rides the bisque at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Shrimp Cocktail": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness overshadows the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Jameson Irish Whiskey on the steak course instead.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Swordfish": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Jameson Irish Whiskey on the steak course instead.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|The Tomahawk": "Jameson with the Tomahawk — smooth Irish against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; Irish whiskey is too light for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Tomato Basil": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the tomato basil, and the gentle barley note frames the bowl. Works; boutique-blend wasted on salad.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Truffle Fries": "Jameson with truffle fries — triple-distilled Irish against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Jameson's smooth character meets the earthy-rich at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Jameson Irish Whiskey lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Jameson Irish Whiskey|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the approachable Irish character carries the curry, and the gentle barley note frames the bowl. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for this dish's register.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Asparagus": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin finds neutral with the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Beignets": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Bone Marrow": "Russian River Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — smooth Pinot lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Jax Calesa for the chicken, salmon, or seafood course.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Bone-In Filet": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Broccoli Cheddar": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin holds with the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Broccolini": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the Pinot body brightens the broccolini, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Brussels and Belly": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Burrata": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, smooth, low tannin. The soft tannin meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the pomegranate plays against basil-tomato. Works as food-friendly Pinot for burrata — reliable red; whites and rosés peak higher.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Butternut Squash": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin holds with the squash soup's silky sweet — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Carrot Cake": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Cheesecake": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir on the steak course instead.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Chilean Seabass": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin finds neutral with the seabass's buttery flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Chocolate Brownie": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Chocolate Cake": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Clam Chowder": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Cowboy Ribeye": "Russian River Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — smooth low-tannin Pinot falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep Jax Calesa for salmon, chicken, or seafood; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Crab Cake": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, low tannin, smooth. The soft tannin doesn't fight crab's sweet meat and the pomegranate plays against romesco. Peak food-friendly Pinot for crab cake — reliable red that works where heavier reds would bury shellfish. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Creamed Spinach": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Creamy Potato": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the Pinot body brightens the potato soup, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; a measured close to the meal.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Creme Brulee": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Escargot": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin finds neutral with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Faroe Island Salmon": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, balanced acidity, smooth. The food-friendly body plays against salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak reliable Pinot for salmon across most seasonal preparations. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Filet Mignon": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, balanced acidity, smooth finish, medium body. The smooth structure meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the subtle earth plays against escargot-butter toppers. Peak food-friendly Pinot for filet — reliable Russian River on the delicate cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|French Onion": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Grilled Caesar": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, balanced acidity. The acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the subtle earth plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong food-friendly Pinot for grilled Caesar — reliable Russian River on the layered salad.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Gumbo": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|House Wedge": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, balanced acidity. The acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the pomegranate plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak food-friendly Pinot for the wedge — reliable Russian River reframes the classic steakhouse salad. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Kansas City": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, smooth, medium body. The smooth structure works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though the low tannin falls short of bolder Cabs' char-requirement. Works as food-friendly Pinot for the strip — reliable lighter red pick.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Loaded Potato": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin holds with the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Lobster Mac": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Market Fish": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the fish's clean flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Mocha Creme": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Mushroom Bisque": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: this earthy, umami-packed side's mushroom-and-truffle depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. Strong — the wine refreshes the side's richness.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Porterhouse": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin frames the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's silky frame sits alongside the starter without weight-fighting — the fruit threads through the plate. Strong — aligned register.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Rainbow Trout": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the trout's delicate flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Roast Half Chicken": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, balanced acidity. The smooth structure plays against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak food-friendly Pinot for chicken across most seasonal builds. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin holds with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, balanced acidity. The acid cuts the sauté butter and the subtle earth plays against garlic without clashing with spinach earth. Works as food-friendly Pinot for the sauté side — reliable Russian River rounds the pairing.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Seafood Tower": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Seared Scallops": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, smooth, low tannin. The soft tannin leaves room for scallop sweetness and the subtle earth plays against caramelized-exterior char. Strong food-friendly Pinot for scallops — reliable pairing on the delicate shellfish.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Seasonal Soup": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin holds with the seasonal soup's house build — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Seasonal Vegetables": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin leans against the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Shrimp Bisque": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, balanced acidity. The acid cuts bisque's cream weight and the subtle earth plays against shellfish umami and sherry. Strong food-friendly Pinot for shrimp bisque — reliable Russian River against the rich soup.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Shrimp Cocktail": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Swordfish": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the swordfish's meaty flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|The Tomahawk": "Russian River Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — smooth low-tannin Pinot has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Jax Calesa for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Tomato Basil": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Truffle Fries": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the tuna's seared rare crust — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the Pinot body brightens the curry, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Asparagus": "Burgundy Chablis — lean flint-chalk precision, green apple, citrus. The flinty mineral handles asparagus's sulfur notes and the high acid cuts the vegetal char. Peak Chablis for asparagus — Burgundian precision on the wine-tricky vegetable. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis's crisp apple-char acidity reads alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Beignets": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Bone Marrow": "Grossot Chablis against marrow's rendered-fat richness — lean flint-chalk precision is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Chablis for scallops, escargot, crab cake, or the seafood tower course.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Bone-In Filet": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis for another course.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Broccoli Cheddar": "Burgundy Chablis — lean flint-chalk precision, green apple, citrus. The high acid cuts the soup's cheddar-cream weight and the flinty mineral plays against the broccoli vegetal edge. Strong Chablis for broccoli cheddar — Burgundian mineral on the classic.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Broccolini": "Burgundy Chablis — lean flint-chalk precision, green apple, citrus. The flinty mineral plays against broccolini's vegetal char and the high acid lifts the tender stem. Peak Chablis for broccolini — Burgundian precision on the charred-vegetable side. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Brussels and Belly": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis's crisp apple-char acidity reads alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; capable alongside. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Burrata": "Grossot Chablis — unoaked Burgundy Chard, flint, chalk, lemon, green apple. The lean acidity cuts burrata's cream but the mineral-heavy profile can feel austere for the delicate Italian salad. Works as precision pairing; Sancerre, St Supéry, or Elk Cove peak higher for burrata's basil-tomato register.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Butternut Squash": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis's crisp apple-char acidity plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Carrot Cake": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis for the steak course.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Cheesecake": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis for the steak course.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Chilean Seabass": "Burgundy Chablis — lean flint-chalk precision, green apple, citrus. The flinty mineral lifts seabass's buttery flesh and the high acid cuts the rich texture. Strong Chablis for Chilean seabass — Burgundian precision on the buttery classic.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Chocolate Brownie": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis on the steak course instead.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Chocolate Cake": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a white wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis for the steak course.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Clam Chowder": "Burgundy Chablis — lean flint-chalk precision, green apple, citrus. The flinty mineral aligns directly with the chowder's clam brine and the high acid cuts the cream base. Peak Chablis for clam chowder — textbook Burgundian-shellfish pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Cowboy Ribeye": "Burgundy Chablis against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — lean unoaked Chard crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Chablis for scallops, escargot, crab cake, or the classical seafood course.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Crab Cake": "Burgundy Chablis — unoaked Chard with flinty chalky minerality, lemon, green apple, lean precise. The flint-chalk cuts crab's romesco and the lemon-apple plays against the sweet meat cleanly. Peak textbook unoaked white for crab cake — precision meets classic starter at benchmark quality.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Creamed Spinach": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis's crisp apple-char acidity stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Creamy Potato": "Burgundy Chablis — lean flint-chalk precision, green apple, citrus. The high acid cuts the soup's smooth cream weight and the flinty mineral plays against the potato's earthy base. Peak Chablis for creamy potato — Burgundian mineral on the rich soup. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Creme Brulee": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis for the steak course.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Escargot": "Grossot Chablis — unoaked Burgundy Chardonnay, flinty chalky minerality, lemon, green apple, lean precise. The flint-chalk cuts escargot's garlic-parsley butter and the lemon plays against the French classic directly. Peak classic Chablis for escargot — both from the same terroir tradition. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Faroe Island Salmon": "Burgundy Chablis — unoaked Chard, flint, chalk, lemon, green apple, lean precise. The clean acidity cuts salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak unoaked Chardonnay for salmon across most seasonal builds — classical French pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Filet Mignon": "Grossot Chablis — unoaked Burgundy Chard, flint, chalk, lemon, green apple. The lean acidity can handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness unconventionally; mineral echoes escargot-butter toppers. Works as classical white-with-steak; Cab, Pinot, or vintage Champagne peak higher for filet.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|French Onion": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the Chablis flint frames the French onion, and the citrus cuts the bowl. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Grilled Caesar": "Burgundy Chablis — unoaked Chard with flinty chalky minerality, lemon, green apple. The lean acidity cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the flint-chalk plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong classical Chablis for the Caesar — unoaked precision on the layered classic salad.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Gumbo": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis's crisp apple-char acidity stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis's crisp apple-char acidity stays alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|House Wedge": "Grossot Chablis — Burgundy unoaked Chardonnay, flint, chalk, lemon, green apple. The lean precise acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the mineral plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong Chablis for the wedge — French precision reframes the classic steakhouse salad.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Kansas City": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis for another course.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Loaded Potato": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis's crisp apple-char acidity holds with the loaded potato's richness — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Lobster Mac": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis: this crisp white's citrus-driven acidity and mineral edge meets this rich, creamy side's dairy-driven indulgence. Crisp white acidity cuts dairy richness — palate-cleansing counterpoint. Strong — the acid refreshes the fat.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Market Fish": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis: this crisp white's citrus-driven acidity and mineral edge meets this delicate seafood's mild, flaky flesh. Crisp acidity and mineral edge mirror fish's own brininess — clean, classic alignment. Strong — textbook match. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Mocha Creme": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a white wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Mushroom Bisque": "Burgundy Chablis — lean flint-chalk precision, green apple, citrus. The flinty mineral plays against the bisque's mushroom umami and the high acid cuts the silky cream. Peak Chablis for mushroom bisque — Burgundian mineral on earth-and-cream. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Mushrooms": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis's crisp apple-char acidity leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Porterhouse": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- white wine can't stand up to the marbled char — the cut earns a structured red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis belongs on another course.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Prime Tartare": "Grossot Chablis — Burgundy unoaked Chard with flint-chalk minerality, lemon, green apple. The lean acidity cuts raw prime beef's iron richness and the flint-mineral adds dimension against the seasoned tartare. Peak unoaked Chardonnay for tartare — precision on precision. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Rainbow Trout": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis -- Burgundy Chablis, flint, lemon. The citrus-and-stone-fruit weight sits with the trout, and the chalky minerality plays against the plate. Excellent; the starter sets up the cut at full register. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Roast Half Chicken": "Burgundy Chablis — unoaked Chard with flint, chalk, lemon, green apple. The lean precise body handles roasted chicken; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works well as classical Chablis for chicken across most seasonal accompaniments — French-on-French.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Burgundy Chablis — lean flint-chalk precision, green apple, citrus. The high acid cuts the soup's tomato-pepper base and the flinty mineral plays against the chickpea earth. Strong Chablis for the soup — Burgundian mineral on the smoky classic.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Burgundy Chablis — lean flint-chalk precision, green apple, citrus. The flinty mineral lifts halibut's firm flesh and the high acid cuts through the sweet soy glaze. Peak Chablis for salt-cured halibut — Burgundian precision on the cured classic. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Grossot Chablis — flint, chalk, lemon, green apple, lean precise. The mineral cuts the sauté butter and the lemon plays against garlic without clashing with spinach's earth. Strong unoaked Chardonnay for the spinach side — Burgundy classicism on a simple but well-made sauce.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Seafood Tower": "Burgundy Chablis — unoaked Chard with flint, chalk, lemon, green apple, lean precise. The clean mineral acidity meshes with brine across the tower and resets palate between oysters, shrimp, and crab. Strong classical Chablis for the seafood tower — textbook unoaked Chardonnay pairing.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Seared Scallops": "Burgundy unoaked Chardonnay — flinty, chalky minerality with bright lemon and green apple. Lean and precise. The flint-chalk frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the lemon cuts the browned edge. Peak textbook Chablis for scallops — unoaked precision on the French classic. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Seasonal Soup": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis: this crisp white's citrus-driven acidity and mineral edge meets this rich, creamy soup's dairy-driven warmth. The wine's acidity cuts the cream, but its weight doesn't carry the soup. Strong if the soup needs brightness.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Seasonal Vegetables": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis: this crisp white's citrus-driven acidity and mineral edge meets this light, green vegetable's clean herbal edge. Bright acidity lifts green vegetables; mineral lines up with the clean vegetable bite. Strong — refreshing and clean.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Shrimp Bisque": "Grossot Chablis — Burgundy unoaked Chardonnay, flint, chalk, lemon, green apple. The lean precise acid cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and the flint-chalk adds dimension against the butter-roux. Peak classical Chablis for bisque — French-on-French at benchmark quality. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Shrimp Cocktail": "Burgundy Chablis — unoaked Chard with flint, chalk, lemon, green apple. The lean precise acid cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the flint-chalk plays against cocktail-sauce bite. Strong classical unoaked white for shrimp cocktail — French precision on the classic starter.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Swordfish": "Burgundy Chablis — lean flint-chalk precision, green apple, citrus. The flinty mineral lifts swordfish's meaty flesh but lean structure reads lighter than the grilled steak-of-the-sea demands. Works as Chablis for swordfish — acceptable Burgundian white; Chardonnays and Pinots peak higher on the meaty cut.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|The Tomahawk": "Grossot Chablis against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — austere unoaked precision has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Chablis for scallops, escargot, crab cake, bisque, or seafood tower.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Tomato Basil": "Burgundy Chablis — lean flint-chalk precision, green apple, citrus. The flinty mineral cuts tomato's acidity and the lean structure plays against the basil's aromatic lift. Peak Chablis for tomato basil — Burgundian mineral on the fresh-herb classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Truffle Fries": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis's crisp apple-char acidity plays cleanly against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Burgundy Chablis — lean flint-chalk precision, green apple, citrus. The flinty mineral lifts rare-seared tuna's lean flesh and the high acid cuts sesame-crust weight. Peak Chablis for tuxedo tuna — Burgundian precision on the crusted classic. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis's crisp apple-char acidity holds with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Asparagus": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon body sits with the asparagus, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; stout-barrel story wasted on salad.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the au gratin — sea-aged L.E. against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Marian McLain L.E. handles the richness at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Beignets": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the caramel-spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the sweetness is a touch much for the salad's simplicity.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Bone Marrow": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with bone marrow — Jefferson's Ocean L.E. against umami fat. Excellent; Marian McLain L.E.'s salt-sea character engages marrow at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Bone-In Filet": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the bone-in filet — sea-aged L.E. against tenderloin-with-bone. Works; Marian McLain L.E. frames the cut at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Broccoli Cheddar": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; extended-aged home-state pour wasted on salad.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Broccolini": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the brown-spirit warmth threads the broccolini, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; Remus 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Brussels and Belly": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with Brussels + Belly — limited-edition ocean-finish against maple-glazed pork belly. Excellent; Marian McLain L.E. meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Burrata": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with burrata — limited-edition ocean-finish against fresh dairy. Avoid; save the collector bottle.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Butternut Squash": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the squash bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Carrot Cake": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with carrot cake — limited-edition sea-aged against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; Marian McLain L.E. threads the cake at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Cheesecake": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with cheesecake — master-blender-tribute ocean-finish against custard. Works; Marian McLain L.E. threads the cheesecake at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Chilean Seabass": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Chocolate Brownie": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the chocolate brownie — sea-aged L.E. against cocoa. Excellent; Marian McLain L.E.'s salt-sea character meets cocoa at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Chocolate Cake": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the premium pour is wasted on the salad's simplicity.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Clam Chowder": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon depth settles into the chowder, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; regional craft at matched register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Cowboy Ribeye": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the cowboy ribeye — limited-edition sea-aged against 26oz cap-fat. Excellent; Marian McLain L.E.'s premium ocean-finish frames cap-fat at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Crab Cake": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Creamed Spinach": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with creamed spinach — master-blender-tribute sea-aged against dairy-rich side. Strong; Marian McLain L.E. rides the cream at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Creamy Potato": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s soft-oak bourbon body leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; mid-proof pour is slightly over-specified.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Creme Brulee": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; milestone-release doesn't drive.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Escargot": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon depth settles into the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; the Twin Oak finish gives the side more dimension than standard Jefferson's brings; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Faroe Island Salmon": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Filet Mignon": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the filet — master-blender tribute sea-aged against tenderloin. Strong; Marian McLain L.E. frames tenderloin at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|French Onion": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; rich vanilla and high-proof give the dessert bolder match than standard Beam brings.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Grilled Caesar": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the grilled Caesar — master-blender tribute against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Marian McLain L.E. meets the char at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Gumbo": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon depth settles into the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the oak depth lays under the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|House Wedge": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon depth settles into the wedge, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; AE Triple Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Kansas City": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets Jefferson's Ocean limited edition honoring master blender Marian McLain Kaminski, sea-aged premium. Marian McLain L.E. brings the sea-aged character of Voyage 23 at limited-edition register — collector-grade ocean-finish + strip marbling. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Loaded Potato": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; pour slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Lobster Mac": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the lobster mac, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Market Fish": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Mocha Creme": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mocha creme, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Mushroom Bisque": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; complexity wasted on salad.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Mushrooms": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with mushrooms — sea-aged L.E. against earthy umami. Strong; Marian McLain L.E. threads mushrooms at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the PB brownie — limited-edition ocean-finish against peanut butter and cocoa. Strong; Marian McLain L.E. handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Porterhouse": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the porterhouse — limited-edition ocean-finish against strip-and-filet. Works; Marian McLain L.E. frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Prime Tartare": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s spice-and-caramel bourbon depth matches register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon weight handles the tartare, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Strong; GTS meets the earthy-rich at collector register; consider whether the side deserves a BTAC pour; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Rainbow Trout": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. belongs on the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Roast Half Chicken": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon body sits with the chickpea soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; regional craft at matched register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the oak frames the side. Works; smooth character is slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Seafood Tower": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Seared Scallops": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with seared scallops — master-blender tribute against scallop delicacy. Avoid; save the collector bottle.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Seasonal Soup": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Seasonal Vegetables": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Shrimp Bisque": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with shrimp bisque — master-blender tribute against cream-shrimp soup. Avoid; save the collector bottle.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Shrimp Cocktail": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with shrimp cocktail — sea-aged L.E. against cold shrimp. Avoid; save the collector bottle.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Swordfish": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|The Tomahawk": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the Tomahawk — limited-edition ocean-finish against 32oz cap-fat. Strong; Marian McLain L.E. handles the Tomahawk at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Tomato Basil": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon body sits with the tomato basil, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; allocated character doesn't drive.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Truffle Fries": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with truffle fries — master-blender-tribute sea-aged against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Marian McLain L.E.'s limited-edition character meets the earthy-rich at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. belongs on the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon body sits with the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; HH 20 is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Asparagus": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the brown-spirit warmth threads the asparagus, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; bonded weight pushes slightly past the soup.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the au gratin — sea-aged Jefferson's against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Voyage 23 handles the richness at ocean-finish register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Beignets": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the brown-spirit warmth threads the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; save the bottle for the protein course — a soup is wrong for a $200 pour.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Bone Marrow": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with bone marrow — ship-aged Jefferson's against umami fat. Excellent; Voyage 23's salt-tinged sea-aged character engages marrow with distinctive ocean-finish resonance — salt + marrow is textbook.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Bone-In Filet": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the bone-in filet — ship-aged Jefferson's against tenderloin-with-bone. Works; Voyage 23 frames the cut at ocean-finish register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Broccoli Cheddar": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the caramel-spice register matches the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; smooth character is slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Broccolini": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon depth settles into the broccolini, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Brussels and Belly": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with Brussels + Belly — sea-aged bourbon against maple-glazed pork belly. Excellent; Voyage 23's ocean-motion character meets the glaze with distinctive ocean-finish story.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Burrata": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with burrata — sea-aged Jefferson's against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Butternut Squash": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon depth settles into the squash bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Carrot Cake": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with carrot cake — Voyage #23 sea-aged against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; Voyage 23 threads the cake at ocean-finish register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Cheesecake": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with cheesecake — sea-aged Jefferson's against custard. Works; Voyage 23's salt-tinged character threads the cheesecake at ocean-finish register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Chilean Seabass": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 on the steak course instead.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Chocolate Brownie": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the chocolate brownie — sea-aged bourbon against cocoa. Strong; Voyage 23's salt-tinged sea-aged character meets cocoa at ocean-finish register — salt + chocolate is textbook pairing.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Chocolate Cake": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the oak depth lays under the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; narrative-bourbon doesn't drive.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Clam Chowder": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon depth settles into the chowder, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Cowboy Ribeye": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the cowboy ribeye — sea-aged Jefferson's against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Voyage 23's ocean-motion + salt character frames cap-fat with distinctive ocean-finish register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Crab Cake": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with crab cake — sea-aged bourbon against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for seafood even with salt-character resonance. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Creamed Spinach": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with creamed spinach — sea-aged Jefferson's against dairy-rich side. Strong; Voyage 23's salt-ocean character rides the cream at ocean-finish register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Creamy Potato": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon body sits with the potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; no driver pairing.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Creme Brulee": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the brulee, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Escargot": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the caramel-spice register matches the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; aged BIB at matched premium register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Faroe Island Salmon": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 on the steak course instead.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Filet Mignon": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the filet — ship-aged Jefferson's Voyage #23 against tenderloin. Strong; Voyage 23's sea-aged character frames tenderloin at ocean-finish register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|French Onion": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Grilled Caesar": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the oak depth lays under the grilled Caesar, and the vanilla brightens the grilled char-edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Gumbo": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak depth lays under the gumbo, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon weight handles the honey carrots, and the oak threads the caramelized sugar. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|House Wedge": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the oak depth lays under the wedge, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; AE CS handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Kansas City": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the KC — sea-aged bourbon, specific Voyage #23, distinctive ocean-motion + salt-tinged character from ship-based maturation. Excellent; Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's sea-aged character is the featured ocean-finish-bourbon-KC pairing — the ocean-motion + salt character cuts strip marbling with a distinctive story no other bourbon on the menu can tell.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Loaded Potato": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the caramel-spice register matches the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Lobster Mac": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak depth lays under the lobster mac, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Market Fish": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 on the steak course instead.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Mocha Creme": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon body sits with the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; Caribbean Cask rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Mushroom Bisque": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Mushrooms": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with mushrooms — sea-aged Jefferson's against earthy umami. Excellent; Voyage 23's salt + ocean-character threads mushrooms with distinctive ocean-finish resonance.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the PB brownie — ship-aged Jefferson's against peanut butter and cocoa. Strong; Voyage 23's sea-aged character handles dessert density at ocean-finish register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Porterhouse": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the porterhouse — Voyage #23 against strip-and-filet. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Prime Tartare": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the oak depth lays under the tartare, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Strong; uncut intensity sits with richness at allocated register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Rainbow Trout": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Roast Half Chicken": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon weight handles the roast chicken, and the caramel rounds the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon backbone carries the chickpea soup, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; Gran Cru's brioche meets the glaze at refined register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the garlic spinach, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Seafood Tower": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 on the steak course instead.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Seared Scallops": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with seared scallops — Voyage #23 against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops even with ocean-finish.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Seasonal Soup": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon backbone carries the seasonal soup, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; milestone-collector wasted on salad.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Seasonal Vegetables": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon body sits with the seasonal vegetables, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; BH Red Wine Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Shrimp Bisque": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with shrimp bisque — sea-aged bourbon against cream-shrimp soup. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for soup despite ocean-theme.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Shrimp Cocktail": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with shrimp cocktail — ship-aged bourbon against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Swordfish": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|The Tomahawk": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the Tomahawk — Voyage #23 sea-aged against 32oz cap-fat. Strong; Voyage 23 handles the Tomahawk at ocean-finish register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Tomato Basil": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the tomato basil, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Truffle Fries": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with truffle fries — Voyage #23 sea-aged against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Voyage 23's salt-tinged character meets the earthy-rich with distinctive ocean-finish resonance.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon weight handles the curry, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; a composed but not remarkable match.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Asparagus": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon body sits with the asparagus, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; Old Fitz 9 brings more depth here.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with au gratin potatoes — premium oak against cheese-and-cream. Strong; the Twin Oak finish gives the side more dimension than standard Jefferson's brings.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Beignets": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the caramel-spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; blend character covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Bone Marrow": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with bone marrow — double-barrel oak and deep vanilla against umami fat. The Twin Oak finish gives the pour the weight standard Jefferson's doesn't quite reach for marrow. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Bone-In Filet": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's bold double-oak-vanilla character holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth supports the lean cut without overpowering, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Broccoli Cheddar": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the bourbon-and-soup pairing settles as a quiet partner.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Broccolini": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon backbone carries the broccolini, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; the story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Brussels and Belly": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with Brussels + Belly — Twin Oak depth against maple-glazed pork belly. The complex oak frames the dish's sweetness without the doubling of wheated pours; sophisticated. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Burrata": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with burrata — fresh dairy against Twin Oak depth. Avoid; save the pour.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Butternut Squash": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon weight handles the squash bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; single-barrel depth gives the dessert more than Small Batch.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Carrot Cake": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with carrot cake — Twin Oak complexity against cream cheese and cinnamon. The deeper oak and vanilla frame the cake's spice; a premium non-wheated dessert pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Cheesecake": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with cheesecake — double-barrel oak against clean custard. Works; the Twin Oak's depth slightly overshoots the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Chilean Seabass": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak belongs on the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Chocolate Brownie": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the chocolate brownie — Twin Oak complexity against cocoa. Works; for chocolate interplay reach for Weller 107 or Weller Millennium; the VR Twin Oak is better on meatier plates.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Chocolate Cake": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Clam Chowder": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon body sits with the chowder, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Cowboy Ribeye": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the Cowboy Ribeye — Twin Oak's double-barrel weight against cap fat and char. Where standard Jefferson's Reserve is underserved here, the VR Twin Oak earns the ribeye. A premium non-wheated alternate to Weller 12 for this cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Crab Cake": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with crab cake — Twin Oak depth against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a white wine, save the VR Twin Oak.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Creamed Spinach": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with creamed spinach — double-barrel depth against dairy. Strong; Twin Oak's complexity is slightly over-ambitious for the side, but the pairing is refined.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Creamy Potato": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon backbone carries the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Creme Brulee": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Escargot": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's bold double-oak-vanilla character holds steady against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Faroe Island Salmon": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Filet Mignon": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the filet — the Very Rare line's double-barrel finish through two custom-toasted oak barrels. Deeper vanilla, more complex oak, more sophistication than standard Jefferson's Reserve. A premium non-wheated call for filet; distinct from Weller 12 (wheated) or Eagle Rare 10 (aged Buffalo Trace). Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|French Onion": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Grilled Caesar": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the grilled Caesar — double-barrel depth against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; the Twin Oak earns this Caesar where standard Jefferson's only works.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Gumbo": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; the pour's delicacy doesn't overpower the soup, but doesn't particularly elevate it either.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the oak depth lays under the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; gentle match at matched registers.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|House Wedge": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against Twin Oak depth. Works only as a separate sip; the VR Twin Oak's rarity is wasted on the salad.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Kansas City": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the KC — Twin Oak's double-barrel depth against the strip's marbling. The complex oak gives the cut more dimension than standard Jefferson's; a premium strip pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Loaded Potato": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon backbone carries the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; aged premium slightly over-specified.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Lobster Mac": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon weight handles the lobster mac, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; not revelatory but well-matched registers.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Market Fish": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Mocha Creme": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mocha creme, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; Voyage 23 handles the richness at ocean-finish register.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Mushroom Bisque": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; multi-distillery complexity doesn't drive.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Mushrooms": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the mushrooms — double-barrel complexity against earthy umami. The depth layers into mushroom character distinctively; premium side pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the caramel-spice register matches the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; multi-distillery complexity doesn't drive.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Porterhouse": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the porterhouse — double-barrel finish handles both strip and filet sides cleanly. The Twin Oak depth covers the plate in a way standard Jefferson's Reserve can't. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Prime Tartare": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; oak-aged depth sits with richness at premium register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Rainbow Trout": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Roast Half Chicken": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the oak frames the plate. Works; boutique character slightly over-specified.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak depth lays under the chickpea soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Seafood Tower": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak on the steak course instead.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Seared Scallops": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against double-barrel oak. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Seasonal Soup": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Seasonal Vegetables": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon weight handles the seasonal vegetables, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; GTS is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Shrimp Bisque": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood against double-barrel. Works; the VR Twin Oak is over-specified for the soup's register.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Shrimp Cocktail": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against double-barrel. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Swordfish": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|The Tomahawk": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's bold double-oak-vanilla character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Tomato Basil": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the oak depth lays under the tomato basil, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; single-barrel depth gives the dessert more than Small Batch.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Truffle Fries": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with truffle fries — complex oak-finished bourbon against truffle-parmesan. The Twin Oak depth echoes the truffle's gravitas; a refined premium side pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the oak depth lays under the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; premium proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Asparagus": "Jefferson's Reserve's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the asparagus, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Jefferson's Reserve with au gratin potatoes — blended elegance against cheese-and-cream. Strong; sophisticated everyday match.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Beignets": "Jefferson's Reserve's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Bone Marrow": "Jefferson's Reserve with bone marrow — blended honey and vanilla against umami fat. The blend's smoothness meets marrow without the sweet-on-fat pattern of wheated takes; a refined starter pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Bone-In Filet": "Jefferson's Reserve's caramel-honey character matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth supports the lean cut without overpowering, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Broccoli Cheddar": "Jefferson's Reserve's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon depth settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; blended character doesn't drive.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Broccolini": "Jefferson's Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon body sits with the broccolini, and the oak frames the side. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Brussels and Belly": "Jefferson's Reserve with Brussels + Belly — blended bourbon meets maple-glazed pork belly. The elegance handles the dish's sweetness without the Wellers' wheat-on-maple doubling; a different logic for the same plate. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Burrata": "Jefferson's Reserve with burrata — fresh dairy against blended bourbon. Avoid; register mismatch.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Butternut Squash": "Jefferson's Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the caramel-spice register matches the squash bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Carrot Cake": "Jefferson's Reserve with carrot cake — blended honey and vanilla against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; Jefferson's VR Twin Oak brings deeper oak for this dessert when the call is richness.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Cheesecake": "Jefferson's Reserve with cheesecake — blended honey-vanilla against custard. Works; a gentle composed match.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Chilean Seabass": "Jefferson's Reserve's blended honey and vanilla clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Jefferson's Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Chocolate Brownie": "Jefferson's Reserve with the chocolate brownie — blended elegance against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay, Weller 107 or Weller Millennium bring more interplay with chocolate.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Chocolate Cake": "Jefferson's Reserve's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon body sits with the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; no driver pairing.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Clam Chowder": "Jefferson's Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon backbone carries the chowder, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; rich vanilla and high-proof give the dessert bolder match than standard Beam brings.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Cowboy Ribeye": "Jefferson's Reserve with the Cowboy Ribeye — 90-proof blended against cap fat and char. Strong; Jefferson's elegance is slightly underserved by the ribeye's scale — Jefferson's VR Twin Oak or Weller 12 handle this cut more fully.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Crab Cake": "Jefferson's Reserve with crab cake — blended bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Creamed Spinach": "Jefferson's Reserve with creamed spinach — blended honey-vanilla against dairy. Strong; a composed match at the register.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Creamy Potato": "Jefferson's Reserve's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon body sits with the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for this dish's register.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Creme Brulee": "Jefferson's Reserve's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Escargot": "Jefferson's Reserve's caramel-honey character matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Faroe Island Salmon": "Jefferson's Reserve's blended honey and vanilla smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Jefferson's Reserve belongs on the steak course.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Filet Mignon": "Jefferson's Reserve with the filet — the blended elegance of Jefferson's signature profile. 90-proof honey, vanilla, and smooth oak frame the filet without aggressive character. Distinct from the Wellers' wheated softness or Buffalo Trace's straight oak — blending gives it a polished middle-ground. A refined everyday-premium call for filet. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|French Onion": "Jefferson's Reserve's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the French onion, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Grilled Caesar": "Jefferson's Reserve with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against blended bourbon. Works; Jefferson's VR Twin Oak earns more of this Caesar than the standard Reserve does.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Gumbo": "Jefferson's Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon body sits with the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the bourbon's character is distinctive but the pairing isn't a standout.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Jefferson's Reserve's blended honey and vanilla reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|House Wedge": "Jefferson's Reserve with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against blended elegance. Works; the pour's refinement is underused by the salad.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Kansas City": "Jefferson's Reserve with the KC — blended bourbon's elegance against the strip's marbling. The smoothness carries the char without competing; a sophisticated strip pour distinct from BT's cleaner flagship approach. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Loaded Potato": "Jefferson's Reserve's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; light body doesn't overshoot.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Lobster Mac": "Jefferson's Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak depth lays under the lobster mac, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; smooth character is slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Market Fish": "Jefferson's Reserve's blended honey and vanilla smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Jefferson's Reserve on the steak course instead.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Mocha Creme": "Jefferson's Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon body sits with the mocha creme, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Mushroom Bisque": "Jefferson's Reserve's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; own-plus-sourced covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Mushrooms": "Jefferson's Reserve with the mushrooms — blended honey-vanilla against earthy umami. The smoothness gives mushroom depth an elegant counterpoint; a sophisticated everyday side pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Jefferson's Reserve|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Jefferson's Reserve's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pour is a touch ambitious for the salad.",
