  "Johnnie Walker Red|Creme Brulee": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Escargot": "Johnnie Walker Red's bold peat-smoke character matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Faroe Island Salmon": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Johnnie Walker Red on the steak course instead.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Filet Mignon": "JW Red with the filet — bold-fiery 80-proof blended JW against tenderloin. Excellent; JW Red's smoke-forward profile frames tenderloin with iconic-blended character — polarizing but definitive.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|French Onion": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the malt-driven body wraps the French onion, and the smoke threads the bowl. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Grilled Caesar": "JW Red with the grilled Caesar — bold-smoke entry JW against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; JW Red's peat-influence is the featured blended-Scotch-Caesar pairing — smoke-meets-char with Islay-malt resonance, distinct from the Highland honey-floral approach.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Gumbo": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the malt-and-oak threads the gumbo, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; a composed but not remarkable match.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the malt-driven body wraps the honey carrots, and the oak threads the caramelized sugar. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|House Wedge": "JW Red with the House Wedge — 80-proof smoky JW against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; JW Red's bold character engages the bleu with distinctive smoke-driven bite.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Kansas City": "JW Red with the KC — 80-proof bold-fiery entry Johnnie Walker with peat-influenced Islay-malt components. Excellent; JW Red's bold-smoke character is the distinctive blended-Scotch-KC pairing — the Islay-malt peat influence handles strip marbling with smoke + intensity Dewar's honey-vanilla can't match.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Loaded Potato": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the malt-and-oak threads the loaded potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Lobster Mac": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the scotch depth settles into the lobster mac, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Market Fish": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Johnnie Walker Red for the steak course.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Mocha Creme": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the malt-and-oak threads the mocha creme, and the oak softens the cream. Works; annual-release complexity over-specified.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Mushroom Bisque": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the scotch backbone matches the mushroom bisque, and the oak balances the umami register. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Mushrooms": "JW Red with mushrooms — peat-influenced entry JW against earthy umami. Excellent; JW Red's smoke marries mushroom earthiness with distinctive Islay-malt character — smoke + earth is the featured JW Red-mushroom pairing.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the scotch backbone matches the PB brownie, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; mid-proof pour is slightly over-specified.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Porterhouse": "JW Red with the porterhouse — 80-proof bold JW against strip-and-filet. Strong; JW Red's smoke-forward frames the two-cut plate with distinctive character.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Prime Tartare": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the scotch depth settles into the tartare, and the oak depth meets the umami. Strong; own-plus-sourced sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Rainbow Trout": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Johnnie Walker Red for the steak course.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Roast Half Chicken": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the roast chicken, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the plate. Works; boutique character gives the dessert a craft frame.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the chickpea soup, and the smoke threads the bowl. Works; wine-cask over-specified.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Johnnie Walker Red for the steak course.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the garlic spinach, and the honey rounds the side. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Seafood Tower": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Johnnie Walker Red for the steak course.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Seared Scallops": "JW Red with seared scallops — peat-influenced JW against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Seasonal Soup": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the malt-driven body wraps the seasonal soup, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Seasonal Vegetables": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the Highland register carries the seasonal vegetables, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; bonded weight pushes slightly past the soup.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Shrimp Bisque": "JW Red with shrimp bisque — 80-proof smoky JW against cream-shrimp soup. Works; JW Red's smoke is distinctive against the bisque but over-specified.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Shrimp Cocktail": "JW Red with shrimp cocktail — bold-smoke entry JW against cold shrimp. Avoid; Scotch wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Swordfish": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Johnnie Walker Red lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|The Tomahawk": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the scotch depth settles into the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; collector intensity at allocated register.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Tomato Basil": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character reads alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the scotch backbone matches the tomato basil, and the smoke threads the bowl. Works; save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Truffle Fries": "JW Red with truffle fries — peat-influenced entry JW against truffle-parmesan. Strong; JW Red's smoke meets the earthy-rich with distinctive Islay-malt character.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Johnnie Walker Red for the steak course.",
  "Johnnie Walker Red|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the scotch depth settles into the curry, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; own-plus-sourced over-specified.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Asparagus": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this rich, creamy side's dairy-driven indulgence. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. A bright-meets-rich headliner. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Beignets": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone Marrow": "Sonoma Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin, elegant balance. The refined body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness but the fine-grained tannin is gentler than the bone-depth demands. Works as Jordan for marrow — firmer Cabs like Silver Oak or Frias peak higher for the rich starter.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone-In Filet": "Alexander Valley Sonoma Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine-grained tannin. The elegant body meets bone-in filet's added depth and the cedar-mint frames the steak-flavor directly. Peak elegant Cab for bone-in filet — Jordan's decades of consistency and balance align perfectly with the premium tenderloin cut. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccoli Cheddar": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance flattens the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccolini": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance overshadows the broccolini's tender green stalks -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Brussels and Belly": "Jordan — Sonoma Cab with cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body handles pork-belly fat and the cedar-mint plays against charred brussels. Strong elegant Cab for Brussels and Belly — refined balance cuts the rich starter without overpowering.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Burrata": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance fights the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- tannin and fresh dairy curdle texture-wise — the milky plate reads sour. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Butternut Squash": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: the carrot cake's warm-spice, cream-cheese indulgence meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. Cherry fruit echoes dark chocolate, but the elegant red's acidity fights refined sugar. Works for a specific guest — fruit-forward cocoa.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Cheesecake": "Cheesecake: the cheesecake's dense, tangy dairy richness meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. Cherry fruit echoes dark chocolate, but the elegant red's acidity fights refined sugar. Works for a specific guest — fruit-forward cocoa.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Chilean Seabass": "Sonoma Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin, elegant balance. The fine tannin meets seabass's buttery flesh and the cedar plays against the rich texture. Works as approachable Cab for Chilean seabass — Jordan's fine tannin makes it the fish-friendliest Cab; Chardonnays and Pinots peak higher.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Brownie": "Jordan — Sonoma Cab, cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body meets chocolate-brownie fudge and the cedar-mint plays against the rich dessert. Works as Jordan for brownie — refined Cab-on-chocolate pairing, though Caymus or Opus peak higher on the opulence axis.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Cake": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Clam Chowder": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance flattens the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Cowboy Ribeye": "Sonoma Alexander Valley Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body can handle Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness but the fine-grained tannin is gentler than rendered fat demands. Strong elegant Cab for Cowboy — more muscular Cabs like Silver Oak, Caymus, or Frias peak higher for fattiest ribeye.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Crab Cake": "Jordan Alexander Valley Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — elegant cedar-mint body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Jordan for the steak course where its refined Sonoma balance shines.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamed Spinach": "Alexander Valley Cabernet — soft tannin. Works beautifully with our creamed spinach: the Cab's dark fruit cuts through the parmesan-and-heavy-cream sauce, and the soft tannin doesn't fight the roasted garlic. Peak Cab pour for this side.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamy Potato": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Creme Brulee": "Creme Brulee: this delicate custard's creamy, vanilla sweetness meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. Lighter red against dessert — fruit meets sugar without enough weight to carry. Works for a fruit-led dessert.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Escargot": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cassis-cedar tannin matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the concentrated body grips the savory plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Faroe Island Salmon": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Filet Mignon": "Jordan — Alexander Valley Sonoma Cab with cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine-grained tannin, impeccable balance. The elegant body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the cedar-mint echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak elegant Cab for filet — Jordan has been producing at this level for decades, balanced match on the classic cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|French Onion": "Sonoma Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin, elegant balance. The elegant tannin cuts French Onion's gruyère-crust weight and the cedar frames the caramelized onion depth. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for French Onion but below the wine's peaks with the Tomahawk, Porterhouse, and Bone-In Filet.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Grilled Caesar": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing. Cherry-driven red with the Caesar's umami and char — a classic cross-category salad pair. Strong — aligned register.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Gumbo": "Sonoma Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin, elegant balance. The fine tannin cuts gumbo's roux fat and the cedar plays against bay-and-file aromatics. Works as approachable Cab for gumbo — elegant red; Rhônes, Riojas, and Super Tuscans peak higher on the stew's herbal-spice complexity.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Sonoma Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin, elegant balance. The fine tannin cuts carrots' honey-glaze sweetness and the cedar plays against the roasted char. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Honey Roasted Carrots but below the wine's peaks with the Tomahawk, Porterhouse, and Bone-In Filet.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|House Wedge": "Jordan — Alexander Valley Cab, cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the cedar-mint plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as elegant Cab for the wedge — refined red choice, though whites or rosés peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Kansas City": "Jordan — Alexander Valley Cab with cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the cedar-mint plays against the beefy char. Peak elegant Cab for Kansas City — trusted name, decades of consistency, balanced structure on the classic strip. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Loaded Potato": "Sonoma Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin, elegant balance. The elegant tannin cuts the soup's cheddar-cream weight and the cedar plays against bacon and scallion toppings. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Loaded Potato but below the wine's peaks with the Tomahawk, Porterhouse, and Bone-In Filet.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Lobster Mac": "Alexander Valley Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body meets lobster-mac's cream-cheese richness and the cedar plays against the dense pasta. Strong Jordan for lobster mac — refined balance handles the indulgent side, though opulent Chardonnay often peaks higher.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Market Fish": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Mocha Creme": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushroom Bisque": "Sonoma Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin, elegant balance. The cedar plays against the bisque's mushroom earth but the fine tannin reads firmer than a cream soup needs. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Mushroom Bisque but below the wine's peaks with the Tomahawk, Porterhouse, and Bone-In Filet.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushrooms": "Alexander Valley Sonoma Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body meshes with mushroom umami and the cedar-mint frames the sauté earth. Works as Jordan for mushrooms — refined balance on the earthy side, though bolder Cabs match the earth-on-earth axis more directly.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Porterhouse": "Jordan — Alexander Valley Cab, cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the cedar-mint plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong elegant Cab for Porterhouse — Jordan balance on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Prime Tartare": "Elegant Alexander Valley Cab against prime tartare's raw-iron richness — fine-tannin cedar-mint crushes the delicate seasoned beef. Never pair. Route tartare to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Jordan for the steak course where its refined balance peaks.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Rainbow Trout": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Roast Half Chicken": "Alexander Valley Cabernet — soft tannin. Works with roasted chicken's richer preparations when the guest wants Cab with a non-steak main. The Alexander Valley style is friendlier to chicken than a structured Napa Cab.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance overwhelms the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Alexander Valley Cabernet — soft tannin. Works if the guest is committed to red through the main and side, but the Sauvignon Blancs and Pinots peak higher against the lighter spinach. Acceptable not-peak option.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Seafood Tower": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Seared Scallops": "Elegant Sonoma Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — fine-tannin cedar-mint obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Jordan for the steak course where its refined Alexander Valley balance peaks.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Soup": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance overwhelms the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Vegetables": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Bisque": "Alexander Valley Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body can meet the bisque's cream-shellfish richness but Cab-with-shellfish-soup stretches the pairing axis. Strong Jordan for bisque — Chardonnay, Champagne, or Riesling peak higher on cream-soup, though Jordan's balance handles it.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Cocktail": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Swordfish": "Sonoma Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin, elegant balance. The fine tannin meets swordfish's meaty flesh and the cedar plays against the grilled char — the fish handles red more than most. Works as approachable Cab for swordfish — Pinots peak higher but Jordan's fine tannin makes this the most fish-friendly Cab.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|The Tomahawk": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon -- black cherry, cedar, silky tannins. The silky tannins wrap the 36oz showpiece, and the cherry-fruit frames the cut. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Tomato Basil": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance overwhelms the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Truffle Fries": "Sonoma Cab — cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin, impeccable balance. The elegant body frames truffle-fry earth and the cedar cuts the crisp oil-and-salt. Peak elegant Cab for truffle fries — Jordan's refined character meets the decadent side with balance. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Asparagus": "Loire Sancerre — refined tension-filled mineral, citrus, flint. The flinty citrus tension handles asparagus's notorious sulfur notes and the grassy mineral plays against the vegetal char. Peak Sancerre for asparagus — Loire precision on the one wine-tricky vegetable. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Beignets": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a white wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Joseph Mellot Sancerre lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Bone Marrow": "Loire Sancerre against marrow's rendered-fat richness — refined tension-filled mineral is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Sancerre for scallops, crab cake, tartare, escargot, or seafood tower.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Bone-In Filet": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Joseph Mellot Sancerre lands on another course — not here.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Broccoli Cheddar": "Loire Sancerre — refined tension-filled mineral, citrus, flint. The flinty citrus cuts the soup's cheddar-cream weight and the mineral plays against broccoli vegetal base. Works as Sancerre for broccoli cheddar — Loire precision on the classic; Chardonnays peak higher on the cheese.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Broccolini": "Loire Sancerre — refined tension-filled mineral, citrus, flint. The flinty citrus lifts broccolini's tender stem and the mineral plays against the vegetal char. Strong Sancerre for broccolini — Loire precision on the charred-vegetable side.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Brussels and Belly": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity stays alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Burrata": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre — Loire Sauv Blanc, grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension. The fresh-herb profile plays directly against burrata's basil and the chalk minerality cuts through the cream without crushing. Strong benchmark Sauv Blanc for burrata — herb-on-herb match done precisely.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Butternut Squash": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity leans against the squash soup's silky sweet — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Carrot Cake": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Joseph Mellot Sancerre for the steak course.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Cheesecake": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Joseph Mellot Sancerre for the steak course.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Chilean Seabass": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the seabass's buttery flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Chocolate Brownie": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Joseph Mellot Sancerre belongs on the steak course.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Chocolate Cake": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Joseph Mellot Sancerre for the steak course.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Clam Chowder": "Loire Sancerre — refined tension-filled mineral, citrus, flint. The flinty citrus tension cuts the chowder's cream weight and the mineral plays against clam brine. Works as Sancerre for clam chowder — Loire precision on the classic; Chardonnays peak higher on the cream-forward base.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Cowboy Ribeye": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — refined Sauv Blanc crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Sancerre for scallops, crab cake, escargot, salmon, or the seafood course where precision shines.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Crab Cake": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre — Loire Sauv Blanc with white grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension-filled finish. The refined mineral backbone cuts romesco and the herbs play against crab's sweet meat cleanly. Peak benchmark Sauv Blanc for crab cake — Loire precision on the classic starter. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Creamed Spinach": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Creamy Potato": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the potato soup's silky body — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Creme Brulee": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Joseph Mellot Sancerre on the steak course instead.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Escargot": "Loire Sancerre — benchmark Sauv Blanc with grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension finish. The crisp refined mineral cuts escargot's garlic-parsley butter and the herbs complement the French classic directly. Peak Sancerre for escargot — both wine and dish from the same Loire tradition. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Faroe Island Salmon": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre — Loire Sauv Blanc, grapefruit, chalk, herbs, tension-filled. The refined mineral acid cuts salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen. Peak benchmark Sauv Blanc for salmon across most seasonal builds — French precision meets oil-rich fish. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Filet Mignon": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre — Loire benchmark Sauv Blanc, grapefruit, chalk, herbs. The refined body can handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness unconventionally and the herbs play against escargot-butter toppers. Works as Sauv-Blanc-with-steak; Cab, Pinot, or vintage Champagne peak higher for filet.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|French Onion": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cheese richness. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Grilled Caesar": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre — Loire Sauv Blanc with grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension finish. The refined mineral cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the herbs play against grilled-romaine char. Strong benchmark Sauv Blanc for grilled Caesar — French precision on the layered American classic.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Gumbo": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity reads alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the crisp acidity sits with the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|House Wedge": "Loire Sancerre — benchmark Sauv Blanc with grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension finish. The refined mineral cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the herbs play against blue cheese dressing. Strong Sancerre for the wedge — Loire precision reframes the American steakhouse classic.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Kansas City": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Joseph Mellot Sancerre for another course.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Loaded Potato": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the loaded potato's richness — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Lobster Mac": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Market Fish": "* Joseph Mellot Sancerre -- Loire Sauvignon Blanc, flint, citrus. The cool-climate register carries the market fish, and the chalky minerality plays against the plate. Works — a capable side pour. Gold standard; a confident headline pour.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Mocha Creme": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Joseph Mellot Sancerre for the steak course.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Mushroom Bisque": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity reads alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Mushrooms": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity sits alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Joseph Mellot Sancerre lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Porterhouse": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- crisp white reads thin against 26+ ounces of cap-fat ribeye. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Joseph Mellot Sancerre for another course.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Prime Tartare": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre — Loire Sauv Blanc benchmark, grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension finish. The refined mineral backbone meets raw prime beef's iron richness and the herbs-and-chalk frame the seasoned tartare. Peak Sancerre for tartare — precision-on-precision French pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Rainbow Trout": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre -- Loire Sauvignon Blanc, flint, citrus. The cool-climate register carries the trout, and the stone-fruit note underlines the plate. Excellent; the starter sets up the cut at full register. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Roast Half Chicken": "Loire Sancerre — crisp refined Sauv Blanc with grapefruit, chalk, herbs, tension. The refined body handles roasted chicken; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works well as benchmark Sancerre for chicken across most seasonal accompaniments — French classical pairing.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Loire Sancerre — refined tension-filled mineral, citrus, flint. The flinty citrus tension lifts halibut's firm flesh and the mineral plays against the salt-cure. Strong Sancerre for salt-cured halibut — Loire precision on the cured classic.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre — Loire Sauv Blanc with grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs. The mineral and herb profile plays against the sauté butter-garlic and echoes spinach's earth cleanly. Works as Sancerre for the sauté side — Loire precision on a simple but well-made sauce.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Seafood Tower": "Loire Sancerre — benchmark Sauv Blanc with grapefruit, chalk, herbs, tension finish. The tension-filled mineral meshes with brine across oysters, shrimp, crab, and lobster and resets palate between tower elements. Strong benchmark Sauv Blanc for the tower — Loire precision for the whole shellfish platter.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Seared Scallops": "Loire Sancerre — benchmark Sauv Blanc, crisp and refined with white grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension-filled finish. The tension-filled mineral backbone frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the herbs play against the pan-sear. Peak Sancerre for scallops — textbook French-on-French pairing. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Seasonal Soup": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Seasonal Vegetables": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity stays alongside the seasonal garden plate — the crisp acidity sits with the light side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Shrimp Bisque": "Loire Sancerre — crisp refined Sauv Blanc, grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension finish. The tension-filled acid cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and the herbs play against butter-roux. Strong benchmark Sauv Blanc for bisque — Loire precision meets French cream-soup tradition.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Shrimp Cocktail": "Loire Sancerre — crisp refined Sauv Blanc, white grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension finish. The tension-filled structure cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the grapefruit-herb plays against cocktail-sauce bite. Peak benchmark Sauv Blanc for shrimp cocktail — precision pairing, sense-of-place wine. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Swordfish": "Loire Sancerre — refined tension-filled mineral, citrus, flint. The flinty citrus lifts swordfish's meaty flesh but Loire precision reads lighter than the grilled cut demands. Works as Sancerre for swordfish — acceptable Loire white; Chardonnays and Pinots peak higher on the meaty fish.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|The Tomahawk": "Loire Sancerre against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even benchmark Sauv Blanc tension has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Sancerre for scallops, tartare, crab cake, escargot, or salmon.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Tomato Basil": "Loire Sancerre — refined tension-filled mineral, citrus, flint. The flinty citrus tension cuts tomato's acidity and the mineral lift plays against basil's aromatic edge. Strong Sancerre for tomato basil — Loire precision on the fresh classic.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Truffle Fries": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's crisp citrus-mineral acidity stays alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Loire Sancerre — refined tension-filled mineral, citrus, flint. The flinty citrus lifts tuna's lean flesh and the mineral plays against sesame-crust. Works as Sancerre for tuxedo tuna — Loire precision on the crusted classic; Pinot Noir peaks higher on the sesame-honey combo.",
  "Joseph Mellot Sancerre|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Loire Sancerre — refined tension-filled mineral, citrus, flint. The flinty citrus tension cuts the curry's coconut-milk weight and the mineral plays against chickpea earth. Works as Sancerre for the curry — Loire precision on the spiced classic; Riesling peaks higher on the heat.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Asparagus": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Jubilation by Colgin for the steak course.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the espresso plays against the lactic richness. Works as cult Napa for au gratin — Colgin prestige on the cheesy side.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Beignets": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Jubilation by Colgin for the steak course.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Bone Marrow": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the espresso-chocolate plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak cult Napa for marrow — Colgin prestige on the rich starter with reverence. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Bone-In Filet": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles bone-in filet's intensified beefy edge and the espresso plays against the bone-deepened cut. Peak cult Napa for bone-in filet — Pritchard Hill structure for the elevated cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Jubilation by Colgin for the steak course.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Broccolini": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Jubilation by Colgin for the steak course.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Brussels and Belly": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts pork-belly fat and the dark chocolate plays against charred brussels. Works as cult Napa for Brussels and Belly — Colgin prestige cuts through the rich starter.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Burrata": "Cult Napa Cab blend against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Jubilation's firm-tannin Pritchard-Hill structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Jubilation for the steak course.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Butternut Squash": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Jubilation by Colgin on the steak course instead.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Carrot Cake": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Jubilation by Colgin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Cheesecake": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Jubilation by Colgin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Chilean Seabass": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Jubilation by Colgin for the steak course.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Chocolate Brownie": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Jubilation by Colgin for the steak course.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Chocolate Cake": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Jubilation by Colgin for the steak course.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Clam Chowder": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Jubilation by Colgin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles ribeye's big marbled build and the dark-chocolate plays against the beefy char. Strong cult Napa for ribeye — Colgin prestige on the bold cut.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Crab Cake": "Cult Napa Cab blend against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Jubilation's firm-tannin Colgin structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Jubilation for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Creamed Spinach": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the espresso plays against the spinach base. Strong cult Napa for creamed spinach — Colgin prestige reframes the rich steakhouse side.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Creamy Potato": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Jubilation by Colgin for the steak course.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Creme Brulee": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Jubilation by Colgin for the steak course.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Escargot": "Jubilation by Colgin's firm cassis-and-cocoa tannin sits at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bold Cab register matches the buttered escargot, and the iron-mineral plays against the starter. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Jubilation by Colgin on the steak course instead.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|Filet Mignon": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin, age-worthy. The structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the violets-espresso play against escargot-butter. Peak cult Napa for filet — Colgin prestige on the delicate cut with proper reverence. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Jubilation by Colgin|French Onion": "Jubilation by Colgin's bold char-chocolate cassis finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; the call holds at register.",
