  "Kahlua|Lobster Mac": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Kahlua for the steak course.",
  "Kahlua|Market Fish": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- sugar on delicate fish is wrong direction — the plate needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Kahlua for the steak course.",
  "Kahlua|Mocha Creme": "Kahlua: Mexican rum-base coffee liqueur (since 1936, Diageo-owned), Arabica-coffee-vanilla-caramel at 20% ABV. Excellent on mocha creme; the iconic sweet-coffee-rum profile threads coffee-chocolate at workhorse register — Kahlua-on-mocha is textbook. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Kahlua|Mushroom Bisque": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Kahlua for the steak course.",
  "Kahlua|Mushrooms": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character fights the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- the liqueur's sweet register pulls against the mushroom's umami without bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Kahlua on the steak course instead.",
  "Kahlua|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut-chocolate stack meets Kahlua. Excellent; the sweet-coffee-rum profile wraps peanut-chocolate at iconic register — coffee-and-peanut-butter dessert classic.",
  "Kahlua|Porterhouse": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Kahlua lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kahlua|Prime Tartare": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Kahlua lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kahlua|Rainbow Trout": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Kahlua for the steak course.",
  "Kahlua|Roast Half Chicken": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character buries the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- sugar smothers the chicken's savory frame — wait for dessert. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Kahlua lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kahlua|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Kahlua for the steak course.",
  "Kahlua|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Kahlua on the steak course instead.",
  "Kahlua|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Kahlua on the steak course instead.",
  "Kahlua|Seafood Tower": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sugar buries the delicate shellfish without any acid to balance. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Kahlua lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kahlua|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Kahlua. Avoid; coffee-liqueur weight wrong for scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Kahlua|Seasonal Soup": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Kahlua for the steak course.",
  "Kahlua|Seasonal Vegetables": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Kahlua belongs on the steak course.",
  "Kahlua|Shrimp Bisque": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Kahlua for the steak course.",
  "Kahlua|Shrimp Cocktail": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Kahlua for the steak course.",
  "Kahlua|Swordfish": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Kahlua belongs on the steak course.",
  "Kahlua|The Tomahawk": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Kahlua for another course.",
  "Kahlua|Tomato Basil": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Kahlua belongs on the steak course.",
  "Kahlua|Truffle Fries": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Kahlua for the steak course.",
  "Kahlua|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Kahlua belongs on the steak course.",
  "Kahlua|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Kahlua on the steak course instead.",
  "Kansas City|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with the KC — Barton 1792 small-batch against the strip's marbling. The spicy-sweet high-rye handles the cut at the balanced mid-proof register; distinctly 1792's voice. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat with the KC — Barton's sweet-wheat expression against the strip's marbling. The 94.2 proof gives the pour more body than 90-proof wheated options; a distinctive wheated strip call. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley — entry-point Napa Cab with dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, smooth medium tannin. The approachable body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the mocha plays against the beefy char. Peak entry Napa Cab for Kansas City — reliable step-up from by-the-glass for guests ready to commit. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. 2 James Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets A Midnight's Winter Dram — rare limited-release craft bourbon. Evocative literary name; boutique-brand distinctiveness. The strip gets a rare-release craft frame that stands out for bottle-story tableside. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with the KC — double-matured 16yr Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Aberlour 16 handles the strip with balanced character; Macallan 18 and Balvenie 21 Portwood are the featured Sherried Speyside-KC matches.",
  "Kansas City|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's rested-agave body sits alongside the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the oak-aged tequila body threads the strip steak, and the oak threads the cut. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Kansas City|Adictivo Reposado": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets Adictivo Reposado — long-aged reposado pushing into near-añejo territory. Rich oak, caramel, concentrated; extended maturation gives the strip a fuller-bodied frame than everyday 8-month-aged pours. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Alexander Valley Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Kansas City: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Sonoma Bordeaux blend — plum, blackberry, mocha, soft tannin, smooth finish, medium body. The soft tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold strip and the mocha plays against the beefy char. Peak crowd-pleaser Bordeaux blend for the strip — accessible Sonoma red on the American cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino with the KC — elegant amaro as post-steak digestif. Strong; the grappa-base and orange peel settle the meal after the strip.",
  "Kansas City|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness finds neutral with the strip's lean char — the sweet character meets the cut at digestif register. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Kansas City|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with the KC — flagship port-finished bourbon against an 18oz strip. Strong; AE handles the strip at port-finish register; AE Cask Strength's 117+ proof is the upgrade for marbling.",
  "Kansas City|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with the KC — 117+ proof port-finish against an 18oz strip. Excellent; AE CS's cask-strength port-finish is the featured wine-finished-bourbon-KC pairing — the cask-strength proof handles marbling where flagship AE feels under-specified.",
  "Kansas City|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "AE Triple Cask with the KC — triple-cask AE against an 18oz strip. Strong; AE Triple Cask handles the strip with layered-finish character; AE Cask Strength is the featured wine-finished-KC match.",
  "Kansas City|Aperol": "Aperol with the KC — brunch-register bitter against an 18oz strip. Avoid; Aperol has no business closing a heavy steak. Redirect to Fernet Branca.",
  "Kansas City|Asparagus": "The KC into the asparagus -- acid lifts the richness, the meal gets the contrast it needs. Works; safe alongside, but not the headline pick.",
  "Kansas City|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The KC into the au gratin -- the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain meets the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust. Excellent; reliable headline side for the steak course.",
  "Kansas City|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Paso Robles Cab — ripe plum, dark cherry, mocha, warm spice, plush finish. The bold body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the warm spice-mocha plays against the beefy char. Peak Paso Cab for Kansas City — warmer-climate richness frames the strip with a different register than Napa. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Averna Amaro": "Averna with the KC — Sicilian classic as post-steak digestif. Strong; caramel-orange settles the meal.",
  "Kansas City|Aviation": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- cocktail citrus reads thin against the cut's structure. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Aviation belongs on another course.",
  "Kansas City|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Aviation Gin on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Avion 44": "Avion 44 with the KC — 44-month aged Avion against the strip. Strong; Avion 44 covers the strip with premium extended-aged character.",
  "Kansas City|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register leans against the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the blanco-tequila body matches the strip steak, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Kansas City|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- light spirit reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Bacardi Rum belongs on another course.",
  "Kansas City|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Baileys Irish Cream on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Baker's": "Baker's with the KC — 107-proof 7-year Beam premium against the strip's marbling. The extra proof handles the cut with Beam small-batch intensity. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the KC — vanilla-forward Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Balvenie handles the strip with handcrafted American Oak depth; Speyside 18 or Balvenie 16 (next batch) are the featured Speyside-KC choices.",
  "Kansas City|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the KC — Caribbean-rum-cask-finished Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Caribbean Cask handles the strip with rum-cask-tropical character; Macallan 18 or Balvenie 21 Portwood are the featured Sherried Speyside-KC matches.",
  "Kansas City|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with the KC — First Fill American Oak against an 18oz strip. Strong; Balvenie 16's depth handles the strip at collector register; Fiddich 18's Oloroso depth is still the featured Speyside-KC match.",
  "Kansas City|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets 21yr port-cask Speyside. Portwood's port-derived richness is tied with Macallan 18 as the featured Sherried Speyside-KC pairing — where Macallan 18 brings Oloroso dried-fruit, Portwood brings port red-berry. Both are built for strip marbling. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with the KC — Bardstown Bourbon Co. French-wine finish against an 18oz strip. Strong; Bardstown Château Laboude handles the strip at contemporary-craft register; AE Cask Strength is the featured wine-finished-KC match.",
  "Kansas City|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with the KC — Bardstown Bourbon Co.'s Discovery series, 6th release. Multi-distillery sourced blend; Bardstown's curatorial approach to bourbon-sourcing tells a blend story tableside. The complex blended character gives the strip a craft-blender voice distinct from single-distillery bourbons. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with the KC — Bardstown's collab release finished in Founders Brewing's KBS (Kentucky Breakfast Stout) barrels. Coffee, chocolate, and stout-beer notes atop bourbon; a genuinely distinctive stout-barrel-finished bourbon that nothing else on the menu replicates. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Bardstown Foursquare": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets Bardstown Bourbon Co. finished in Foursquare rum casks (Barbados), distinctive Caribbean-rum-cask character. Bardstown Foursquare's tropical rum-cask character is the featured Caribbean-finish-bourbon-KC pairing — rum-cask brown-sugar + tropical-fruit handles strip marbling with a Barbados-Kentucky story the straight bourbons can't tell. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with the KC — Bardstown's Fusion series combining their own distilled bourbon with sourced whiskey. 7th release. Distinct from Discovery (pure-sourced): Fusion is Bardstown's own-plus-sourced blend, showcasing their in-house distillate. The strip gets a distillery-proud-plus-sourced craft frame. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with the KC — Bardstown Bourbon Co. single-barrel selected by Rishi's. Retailer-pick barrel; the specific character is what Rishi's chose. Distinct from Bardstown's Discovery (sourced-blend) or Fusion (own-plus-sourced) — Rishi's is a single-barrel pick. The strip gets a curated specific-barrel frame. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with the KC — the strip's marbling pushes slightly past Basil Hayden's 80-proof light register. Capable; the rye spice gives the strip a different dimension than the Wellers, but the pour doesn't carry the cut's full weight. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Kansas City|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year with the KC — the aged body finally gives Basil Hayden's the weight to match the strip's marbling. Where standard Basil Hayden's is underpowered here, the 10-year earns it. The high-rye alternate to Weller 12 for strip pairings. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with the KC — malted grain character against the strip's marbling. 80-proof still caps the weight, but the malted rye brings a grain-forward dimension no other Basil Hayden's has for this cut. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Kansas City|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with the KC — BH red-wine finish against an 18oz strip. Strong; BH Red Wine Cask handles the strip with rye-spice + red-wine character; AE Cask Strength is the featured wine-finished-KC match.",
  "Kansas City|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast with the KC — toast-barrel character against the strip's marbling. The 80-proof light body still pushes against the strip's weight, but the toast finish adds dimension standard Basil Hayden's doesn't have. Strong.",
  "Kansas City|Bee's Knees": "Bee's Knees' citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- cocktail citrus reads thin against the cut's structure. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Bee's Knees for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Beefeater Gin for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Beignets": "After the KC, the beignets -- the lean-bold strip yields to warm fried choux and powdered sugar, the dipping sauce closing the meal at register. Works; the table closes light after the bold main.",
  "Kansas City|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere with the KC — Polish rye against an 18oz strip. Avoid; even rye-character vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon or rye.",
  "Kansas City|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with the KC — blended bourbon with Stitzel-Weller heritage against the strip's marbling. The honey-forward blend carries the char; a heritage-story pour for everyday premium orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with the KC — 103-proof export Blanton's against the strip's marbling. The richer profile and extra proof carry the strip where Original is balanced; a bolder single-barrel strip call. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with the KC — iconic 93-proof single-barrel against the strip's marbling. The barrel character gives the strip more depth than standard Buffalo Trace's flagship; a premium single-barrel strip pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's SfB with the KC — cask-strength Blanton's against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Blanton's SfB handles strip marbling with distinctive Blanton's-character at barrel-proof register.",
  "Kansas City|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with the KC — the decennial 10th Pact, 2024 milestone release from master blender John Rempe. A milestone collector edition within the Blood Oath series; 10 years of annual releases culminate here. The strip gets the milestone-Pact blender-signature frame. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with the KC — master blender John Rempe's 8th annual limited Blood Oath, from Lux Row Distillers. Three bourbons blended with a Mizunara oak-finished component. The Pact 8 Japanese oak character gives the strip a distinct blender-signature frame; 2022 release. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the KC — master blender John Rempe's 9th annual Blood Oath, 2023 release from Lux Row. Three bourbons blended with a wine-cask finish component. The Pact 9 wine-cask character gives the strip a layered annual-release frame distinct from Pact 8's Mizunara approach. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Bluecoat Gin for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Kansas City: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Bombay Dry Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Kansas City|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Bombay Sapphire Gin on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Bone Marrow": "Kansas City after the bone marrow -- the marrow's beefy unctuous depth opens the meal, the 18oz strip's savory grain following with clean lean character that the marrow primes without overlap. Strong; both courses honor beef in different keys.",
  "Kansas City|Booker's": "Booker's with the KC — uncut/unfiltered Beam cask-strength against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Booker's 120-130 proof handles strip marbling with the original-barrel-proof bourbon that defined the category.",
  "Kansas City|Botanist Gin": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Botanist Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Kansas City: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "Kansas City|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with the KC — the house-picked stave profile gives the strip a pour made for this room. More depth than standard Maker's, more specific than Maker's Mark Cellar Aged; Bowdie's answer to 'what should I drink with my strip.'. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets Virginia-aged 90-proof small-batch. Smooth Bowman character carries the cut at small-batch register; Sazerac-family alternate to BT's flagship voice. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Bowmore 12": "Kansas City: the strip's char and marbling meet Bowmore 12's sherry-finished honeyed peat. The gentlest Islay with the strip — the gentler peat favors the beef over the dram. Pour when a guest wants the strip to lead the conversation. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Kansas City|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Piedmont Monferrato Barbera d'Asti — Braida benchmark, juicy dark cherry, blackberry, high acid, low tannin, medium body. The juicy acid meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the dark cherry plays against beefy char. Peak benchmark Barbera for the strip — Italian alternative to Barolo on the American cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin — Mount Veeder Cab with dark blackberry, graphite, earth, firm tannin. The dense mountain-fruit body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the graphite-earth plays against the beefy char. Peak mountain Cab for Kansas City — Mount Veeder structure on the classic strip. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Broccoli Cheddar": "Kansas City preceded by the broccoli cheddar -- cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
  "Kansas City|Broccolini": "The KC into the broccolini -- acid plays against richness, the contrast holds across the courses. Works; safe alongside, but not the headline pick.",
  "Kansas City|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich: this Islay Scotch's intense peat smoke, medicinal phenol, and bold weight meets this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char. The whiskey's barrel depth and the steak's flame-grilled fat run at the same intensity — a classic steakhouse move. Works — cocktail-on-steak coherence.",
  "Kansas City|Brussels and Belly": "* The KC with Brussels + Belly -- the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain frames Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts. Gold standard; the side that defines the steak course.",
  "Kansas City|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with the KC — BT flagship against the strip's char. The 90-proof balance handles the marbling cleanly; distinct from wheated pours, more oak-spice and less honey. A versatile everyday premium call. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Buffalo Trace Cream lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with the KC — bold high-rye spice against the strip's marbling. The 90-proof rye-forward profile cuts the char differently than wheated — more spice, more grip. A distinctive strip pour at the approachable tier. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Burrata": "Kansas City after the burrata -- fresh basil-tomato-cream opens with milky delicacy, the 18oz lean-bold strip following with clean savory grain that the cream cleaned the palate for. Strong; the dairy opener lifts the strip's middle weight.",
  "Kansas City|Butternut Squash": "Kansas City preceded by the butternut squash -- savory-into-sweet, the classic close lands intact. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
  "Kansas City|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Kansas City: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila with the KC — Camarena family's value brand. 80-proof, approachable, everyday entry reposado. Distinct from G4 High Proof (their premium): Camarena is the accessible entry call for strip orders. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Campari": "Campari with the KC — aperitivo bitter against an 18oz strip. Avoid; Campari is meant to open the meal, not close a heavy steak. Save for before dinner or redirect to Fernet Branca post-strip.",
  "Kansas City|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club with the KC — traditional grain-forward Canadian against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Canadian Club's heritage character handles strip marbling at traditional register.",
  "Kansas City|Canadian Club 43 Year": "CC 43 Year with the KC — 43yr aged ultra-premium Canadian against an 18oz strip. Excellent; CC 43 Year's aged depth handles strip marbling at collector register — where CC handles it at heritage register, the 43 closes with rare-aged gravity.",
  "Kansas City|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light spirit. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Captain Morgan Rum for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing with the KC — single-barrel Canadian against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Caribou Crossing's hand-selected single-barrel character handles strip marbling at craft register.",
  "Kansas City|Carrot Cake": "The KC into the carrot cake -- the savory grain hands off to spiced cake and cream-cheese frosting, walnut crunch on the close. Works; the spice and the strip find each other across the meal arc.",
  "Kansas City|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- dessert wine is post-meal — wrong register for the steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Casa LBV Port belongs on another course.",
  "Kansas City|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's earthen-pit smoke profile sits alongside the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the earthen-pit smoke carries the strip steak, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Kansas City|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab — concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. The opulent body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the cocoa frames the beefy char. Peak Napa Cab for Kansas City — Caymus is the recognizable pour that confirms a good choice for guests who know the label. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Chambord for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Cheesecake": "The KC closes with the cheesecake -- the firm savory grain hands off to dense cream-cheese custard, berry compote brightening the graham-crust base. Works; the meal closes substantial, neither course pulling against the other.",
  "Kansas City|Chocolate Brownie": "The KC closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the savory-grain strip yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla scooping over the top. Strong; the bold strip earns the dense brownie close.",
  "Kansas City|Chocolate Cake": "After the KC, the chocolate cake -- the lean-bold strip hands off to layered cocoa-and-ganache, vanilla ice cream lifting the close. Strong; the firm-grain steak meets the classic chocolate close at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin with the KC — silky potato vodka against an 18oz strip. Avoid; vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Kansas City|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with the KC — value California brandy against an 18oz strip. Strong; Christian Bros handles the strip at approachable register; Courvoisier's drier oak is still the featured cognac-KC pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Château Batailley": "Pauillac 5ème Cru Classé left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the graphite plays against the beefy char. Peak classified Bordeaux for the strip — 1855 French prestige on the American steakhouse cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Château Beaucastel": "Kansas City: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Strong tier. The Pomerol's truffle and dark plum catch the strip's fat cap and charred edges, while the biodynamic estate's velvety Merlot-Cab Franc mocha finish softens the aggressive sear. Not as tannic as the Napa Cabs — a gentler right-bank read on a classic American cut.",
  "Kansas City|Château Haut Segottes": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold strip and the truffle plays against beefy char. Peak right-bank Bordeaux for the strip — Grand Cru Merlot-led on the American steakhouse cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Château de Rouillac": "Pessac-Léognan left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, tobacco, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the graphite plays against the beefy char. Peak left-bank Bordeaux for the strip — French classic on the American steakhouse cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Clam Chowder": "The KC after the chowder -- the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base sets the table for the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: two rich courses on the same plate, the table earns its substance. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Kansas City|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo: ultra-premium Los Altos tequila, 25 months American oak + 2 months sherry butts, hand-painted ceramic decanter by Mexican artisans. Excellent on KC strip; the caramel-dried-fruit-maple-butterscotch character frames the lean cut at luxury-icon register — save the bottle for the guest who wants ceremony with their añejo.",
  "Kansas City|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango with the KC — Durango ceremonial against the strip. Strong; Clase Azul Durango handles the strip with ceremonial presentation.",
  "Kansas City|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold with the KC — golden-ceramic blend against the strip. Strong; ceremonial blend covers the strip with Clase Azul Gold theater.",
  "Kansas City|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's bold agave-fruit-smoke character finds register with the strip's lean char — the agave smoke frames the lean cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata's silky Jalisco blanco with ceramic-decanter presentation finds neutral with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the silver-spirit register lifts the strip steak, and the green-agave edge brightens the cut. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Kansas City|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado with the KC — ultra-premium in a hand-painted ceramic decanter. 8-month-aged, smooth agave. The bottle's artistry is part of the pairing experience; tableside presentation is the whole point. A ceremonial strip pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra with the KC — 5-year top-tier against the strip. Strong; the Ultra's extended aging handles the strip with collector-grade ceremonial authority.",
  "Kansas City|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep with the KC — Jim Beam's wheated expression against the strip's marbling. 90-proof wheated with caramel and vanilla; distinct from Jim Beam Black's non-wheated take on this cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Roussillon Southern France Grenache — Clos Petit Ona, wild raspberry, kirsch, garrigue, warm spice, medium body. The warm spice echoes Kansas City's lean-bold strip char and the garrigue lifts grilled beef. Strong Southern-French for the strip — Mediterranean mid-weight match.",
  "Kansas City|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with the KC — reposado aged in uncharred Napa Cabernet wine barrels. Pink hue from Cabernet-cask influence; red-wine fruit meets agave. Distinct from Komos Rosa's French-oak approach — Codigo leans more directly into the Napa Cab character. A wine-country strip pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's aperitivo herb-and-bitter cut reads alongside the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the herbal cut works the strip steak, and the amaro backbone frames the cut. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Kansas City|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge reads alongside the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the unaged body brightens the strip steak, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; the close holds, neither soars nor fights.",
  "Kansas City|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado with the KC — Sazerac's tequila brand, single-estate highland agave. Balanced, vanilla-and-oak; distinct from Don Julio's benchmark profile — Corazon has more agave-forward highland character. A single-estate reposado call for the strip. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac: añejo tequila finished in ex-Sazerac-Rye barrels (Buffalo Trace program) — rye-spice-meets-aged-agave crossover. Excellent on KC strip; the rye-whiskey-cask character brings spice-backbone to agave on the lean cut at Expresiones register — this is the tequila for the Sazerac Rye drinker.",
  "Kansas City|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg: añejo tequila finished in ex-George-T.-Stagg barrels (uncut unfiltered barrel-proof bourbon casks) — the most intense Expresiones expression. Excellent on KC strip; heavy-caramel-oak-bold-sweetness from the GTS barrels frames the lean cut at Expresiones register — tequila for the GTS drinker, literally.",
  "Kansas City|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller: añejo tequila finished in ex-Weller wheated-bourbon barrels (12-year Weller) — the softer, gentler Expresiones expression. Excellent on KC strip; wheated-soft-sweet-bourbon character frames the lean cut at rounded-Expresiones register — tequila for the Weller drinker, or the guest who finds Stagg too intense.",
  "Kansas City|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Kansas City: the Cab's firm tannin cuts the strip's char and the dark fruit lifts the savory edge — steakhouse symmetry. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Corpse Reviver": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- cocktail citrus reads thin against the cut's structure. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Corpse Reviver for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier with the KC — drier Napoleon-era cognac against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Courvoisier's drier character and oak backbone are the featured cognac-KC pairing — more structurally aligned with marbling than Hennessy's rounder profile.",
  "Kansas City|Crab Cake": "Kansas City after the crab cake -- the panko-and-lump crab opens with delicate sweetness, the 18oz lean-bold strip following with savory grain that the crab's brightness preceded cleanly. Strong; refined opener primes the strip.",
  "Kansas City|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 with the KC — distinctive Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Cragganmore handles the strip with subtle smoke character; Speyside 18 or Balvenie 16 are the featured Speyside-KC choices.",
  "Kansas City|Creamed Spinach": "Kansas City alongside the creamed spinach -- the 18oz strip's clean savory grain sits with the bechamel-laced greens, cream balancing the strip's leaner edge. Strong; the side anchors the cut without crowding it.",
  "Kansas City|Creamy Potato": "Kansas City preceded by the potato soup -- cream rounds the protein, the plate composes thick. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
  "Kansas City|Creme Brulee": "After the KC, the brulee -- the lean-bold strip yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Kansas City|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral, medium body. The graceful structure works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though low tannin falls short of bolder Cabs. Works as benchmark Pinot for the strip — guest-preference call over classic Cab.",
  "Kansas City|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal with the KC — iconic Canadian blend against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Crown Royal's vanilla-honey-fruit character handles strip marbling at crowd-pleasing Canadian register.",
  "Kansas City|Cucumber Gimlet": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Cucumber Gimlet lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12 with the KC — Matusalem sherry-cask Highland against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Dalmore's sherry-finish dried-fruit-and-chocolate is the featured Highland-KC pairing — the sherry-cask weight is built for strip marbling where Glenmorangie 10's lighter bourbon-cask honey feels under-specified.",
  "Kansas City|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin finds register with the strip's lean char — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Kansas City|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin. The elegant medium tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the savory herb plays against beefy char. Peak rare-Napa CF for the strip — Darioush Persian-influenced estate on the American cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Darioush — Silverado Trail Cab with blackcurrant, dark plum, mocha, spice, rich plush. The opulent body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the mocha-spice plays against the beefy char. Peak opulent Napa Cab for Kansas City — Persian-inspired estate story on the classic strip. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin's Detroit urban-craft gin underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Detroit City Gin on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City with the KC — craft Michigan vodka against an 18oz strip. Avoid; vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Kansas City|Dewar's 15": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets 15yr aged Dewar's with sherry-cask finish, richer than White Label with Highland-blend-premium character. Dewar's 15's aged-with-sherry-finish depth is the upgraded blended-Scotch-KC pairing — where White Label's honey-vanilla handles the strip at entry register, the 15 closes the deal with sherry-cask weight. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label with the KC — entry blended Scotch with Aberfeldy-malt core, honey-vanilla Highland-blend approachable crowd-pleaser. Excellent; Dewar's White is the democratic blended-Scotch-KC pairing — honey-vanilla handles strip marbling at entry register, where Monkey Shoulder goes Speyside-malt-fruit and JW Red goes smoky-bold.",
  "Kansas City|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Disaronno for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the KC — craft sourced rye, Pacific Northwest boutique brand. Blended from multiple sources with craft finishing. Distinct from Kentucky ryes (Sazerac, Michter's, HH ryes) by craft-blender approach; a boutique-craft rye for strip orders. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's bold Jamaican-funk character lifts the strip's lean char — the bourbon depth carries the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Kansas City|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Willamette flagship Pinot Blend — dark cherry, Bing cherry, toasted spice, forest floor, silky, medium body. The layered body works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though low tannin falls short of bolder Cabs. Works as flagship Willamette Pinot for the strip — Domaine Serene elegance for Pinot-preferring guest.",
  "Kansas City|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Kansas City: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé belongs on another course.",
  "Kansas City|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The black pepper echoes Kansas City's lean-bold strip char and the garrigue plays with grilled beef. Peak Châteauneuf for the strip — GSM full-body on the American cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Don Fulano Anejo": "Don Fulano Anejo: Los Altos highland tequila, 100% estate-grown Blue Weber, 3 years in French oak — extended aging for añejo category. Excellent on KC strip; the sophisticated terroir-forward Los Altos character with 3-year French-oak complexity frames the lean cut at extended-aged register — this is the añejo for guests who want depth, not workhorse.",
  "Kansas City|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco's clean silver register sits alongside the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the unaged body brightens the strip steak, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the cut. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Kansas City|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado with the KC — premium highland reposado from Tamaulipas-adjacent Jalisco highlands. Refined vanilla-honey-oak; distinct from Don Julio's benchmark balance — Don Fulano is more elegant, more highland-refined. A sophisticated strip pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942 with the KC — benchmark extra añejo against the strip. Strong; the aged 1942 handles the strip cleanly but not as marquee as with filet — Clase Azul Ultra brings more top-tier character for strip.",
  "Kansas City|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th with the KC — the crystalline expression. Charcoal-filtered reposado so it's clear like a blanco but carries 8-month barrel character. The strip's marbling meets the 70th's uncommon smoothness; a distinctive premium reposado for strip orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco's workhorse blanco with clean agave snap leans against the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the strip steak, and the green-agave edge brightens the cut. Works; the side call holds at neutral register.",
  "Kansas City|Don Julio Reposado": "Don Julio Reposado with the KC — the benchmark everyday reposado. 80-proof, 8 months in American oak; balanced agave with vanilla and caramel. The strip's marbling meets the reposado's oak cleanly; the iconic pour that sets the bar other reposados measure against. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Drambuie": "Drambuie's after-dinner liqueur weight leans against the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the digestif body mirrors the strip steak, and the digestif sweetness softens the cut. Works; the close holds, neither soars nor fights.",
  "Kansas City|Dunville's Single Malt": "Dunville's Single Malt with the KC — Irish single malt against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Dunville's Belfast Irish-single-malt character handles strip marbling at distinctive register.",
  "Kansas City|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the KC — Col. E.H. Taylor Jr. tribute BiB against an 18oz strip. Excellent; EHT Full Proof's 100-proof Bottled-in-Bond handles strip marbling with historic Kentucky register.",
  "Kansas City|E.H. Taylor Rye": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets E.H. Taylor Rye — BT's 100-proof BIB rye, honoring Edmund Haynes Taylor Jr. Premium aged BIB rye; sister to E.H. Taylor Small Batch Bourbon. Bonded weight gives more grip than 90-proof Sazerac Rye. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the KC — 100-proof BIB against the strip's marbling. The bonded weight and rich oak carry the cut where 90-proof BT pours can't; a premium BIB strip call. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with the KC — the strip's char meets ten years of Buffalo Trace barrel influence. Different from the Wellers here: more oak spice, less wheat sweetness. The non-wheated premium aged pour for strip orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Eastern Kille Dry Gin for another course.",
  "Kansas City|El Cabo": "El Cabo with the KC — craft small-batch reposado, agave-forward character. Boutique production gives the pour distinctive craft voice; distinct from the big-brand reposados (Don Julio, Maker's etc.) by production scale alone. A craft-tequila strip call. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "El Mayor Extra Anejo with the KC — accessible aged El Mayor against the strip. Strong; value-adjacent premium covers the strip at accessible register.",
  "Kansas City|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado with the KC — value 80-proof reposado, agave-forward with light oak. Approachable everyday pour; less refined than Don Julio's benchmark, less distinctive than Siete Leguas' craft — El Mayor is the dependable value call for strip orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Elephant Gin on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 with the KC — HH 18yr single-barrel against an 18oz strip. Strong; EC 18 handles the strip at aged-HH register; HH 20yr is the aged-HH-KC upgrade.",
  "Kansas City|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel with the KC — house-picked Heaven Hill single-barrel against the strip's marbling. The specifically-selected barrel gives the strip a curated pour; narrate the story tableside. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel with the KC — single-barrel Heaven Hill against the strip's marbling. The 94-proof single-barrel depth handles the strip where Small Batch is solid but less intense; the premium Elijah Craig for strip orders. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets 94-proof small-batch. Small-batch depth gives the strip a solid frame; Heaven Hill's everyday-premium. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the KC — Heaven Hill's 94-proof rye, sister to the Elijah Craig bourbon line. The HH spicy-sweet rye character gives the strip more grip than Michter's 84.8-proof smoothness; a premium Heaven Hill rye for strip orders. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Chehalem Mountains Willamette Pinot — cherry, violet, clove, medium tannin, medium body. The structured body works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though medium tannin still falls short of bolder Cabs. Works as step-up Pinot for the strip — serious Willamette for guest wanting Pinot over Cab.",
  "Kansas City|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's crisp white-wine character underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Elk Cove Pinot Blanc for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee with the KC — 90-proof namesake single-barrel against the strip's marbling. The smooth BT character carries the strip at refined single-barrel register; a namesake pour for guests who know the history. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Empress 1908 Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Epoch Ingenuity": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, full body, medium tannin. The crushed pepper echoes Kansas City's lean-bold strip char and the smoked meat plays with grilled beef. Peak Paso-Rhône for the strip — European-inspired winemaking. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Escargot": "Kansas City after the escargot -- protein and umami share the plate, the build reads weighty and clean. Works; the opener sits cleanly before the steak.",
  "Kansas City|Espresso Martini": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The cocktail's coffee-and-amaro weight handles the KC's lean-bold strip but the drink is dessert-positioned; works if guest insists on coffee cocktail with steak. Route KC to Cab or Manhattan for peak. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Kansas City|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The coffee weight handles the KC's lean-bold beef; brown sugar meets the char and Frangelico adds aromatic depth. Strong dessert-forward spirit option with the most approachable bold cut.",
  "Kansas City|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, medium tannin. The medium tannin handles Kansas City's lean-bold strip and the smoked-meat plays against beefy char. Strong savory Willamette Pinot for the strip — structured enough for the marbled cut.",
  "Kansas City|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Kansas City: the strip's marbled-and-char richness finds the bold red's grip; cassis carries the savory weight cleanly. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Kansas City|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Far Niente Chardonnay on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi from the Maremma coast, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The firm tannin handles lean-bold strip beef and the graphite plays with firm-savory grain. Peak Super Tuscan for KC — Bordeaux-style intensity on the well-marbled cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Faust — Napa Coombsville-and-Oak-Knoll Cab with dark blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin, layered finish. The structured body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the tobacco plays against the beefy char. Peak value Napa Cab for Kansas City — structure plus layered complexity at this price tier. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Galicia Ribeira Sacra Mencía — Fento, fresh red cherry, slate minerality, light body, high acid. The high acid cuts Kansas City's lean-bold strip char, though light body is slightly delicate for the cut. Strong Galician-Mencía for the strip — bright-acid foil to marbled beef.",
  "Kansas City|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca with the KC — 40% ABV menthol-saffron bitter as post-strip digestif. Excellent; Branca's intensity is the amaro built to cut marbling after a heavy steak, where the classic amari would feel underpowered.",
  "Kansas City|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta with the KC — mint-forward Fernet as post-strip digestif. Excellent; Menta's cooling character settles the palate after the strip with a brighter finish than Branca.",
  "Kansas City|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Fireball Whisky for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky tannins, medium body. The silky structure works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though the low tannin falls short of bolder Cabs' char-requirement. Works as by-the-glass Pinot for the strip — guest-preference call over classic Cab.",
  "Kansas City|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the KC — 104-proof 6-recipe blend against the strip's marbling. The extra proof handles the cut where Yellow Label's 80-proof fades; premium Four Roses for strip orders. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label with the KC — 80-proof entry bourbon against the strip's marbling. The light body can't quite carry the cut; strong as an option but not a marquee pairing. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Kansas City|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with the KC — craft boutique bourbon against the strip's marbling. The small-production character gives the strip a craft-spirit frame distinct from big-brand pours. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets Double Oaked Fox & Oden. Fox & Oden's intensified-caramel character handles strip marbling at Double-Oaked register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Frangelico for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Rutherford Cab — Bosché Vineyard since 1970, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the tobacco plays against beefy char. Peak Bosché Vineyard Cab for the strip — 1970-legacy Rutherford structure on the American cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|French 75": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the French 75 on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|French Onion": "Kansas City into the French onion -- the bone-in strip threads the beef-broth bowl. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Kansas City|Frias Block 5": "Kansas City: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, full body, high acid. The firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the tar plays against beefy char. Peak classic Barolo for the strip — Vajra's range includes Moscato on our Bubbles list. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Sweet, aromatic, effervescent Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee with a honeyed finish. This is a dessert pour, not a steak pour. The KC's bold, firm beef reads flat against the Moscato's sweetness. Serve at the end of the meal, not with the course. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Kansas City|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift leans against the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the silver-spirit register lifts the strip steak, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Kansas City|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof with the KC — 104-proof reposado from Felipe Camarena's G4 distillery. Bold, concentrated, agave-forward; the extra proof gives the pour real grip the 80-proof reposados don't touch. Distinct from the everyday reposado tier — this is the high-impact call for strip orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue with the KC — craft Irish against an 18oz strip. Strong; Garavogue handles the strip with craft-Irish character at distinctive register.",
  "Kansas City|George T. Stagg": "GTS with the KC — BTAC 15+yr cask-strength against an 18oz strip. Excellent; GTS's collector-register cask-strength handles strip marbling with ultra-premium gravitas — where Stagg Jr. brings BT cask-strength, GTS closes with BTAC aging and holy-grail collector status.",
  "Kansas City|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Oakville Cab — blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak, firm tannin. The dense structured body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the graphite-oak plays against the beefy char. Peak Oakville Cab for Kansas City — Napa's most consistent strip-producing AVA delivers on the classic cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The cracked pepper echoes Kansas City's lean-bold strip char and the jammy fruit plays with grilled beef. Peak California-Zin for the strip — bold Napa on the American cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12 with the KC — entry Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Fiddich handles the strip with gateway character, though the Speyside 18 or Balvenie 16 (next batch) are the distinctive Speyside-KC choices.",
  "Kansas City|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14 with the KC — Bourbon Barrel Reserve against an 18oz strip. Excellent; the intensified vanilla and charred-oak spice give Fiddich 14 enough backbone for the strip's marbling where the 12 feels under-specified — the entry-aged Speyside-KC match.",
  "Kansas City|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18 with the KC — Small Batch Reserve, Oloroso sherry cask + bourbon cask marriage, dried fruit + cinnamon + nutmeg + oak-spice depth. Excellent; Fiddich 18's Oloroso dried-fruit weight is the featured Speyside-KC pairing in the batch — the aged sherry-cask character is built to frame strip marbling where the 12's apple/pear and 14's vanilla can't.",
  "Kansas City|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23 with the KC — Cuvée-cask 23yr Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Gran Cru handles the strip with refined 23yr character, though Fiddich 18's Oloroso dried-fruit is the featured Speyside-KC match — Gran Cru's elegance is better spent on the filet.",
  "Kansas City|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12 with the KC — Sandend-coast reopened distillery against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Glenglassaugh 12's tropical-fruit character handles the strip with distinctive coastal-Highland lift.",
  "Kansas City|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12 with the KC — honey-floral Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Glenlivet handles the strip at benchmark register; Speyside 18 or Balvenie 16 are the featured Speyside-KC choices.",
  "Kansas City|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10 with the KC — tallest-stills Highland entry against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Glenmorangie 10's bourbon-cask vanilla-and-honey handles the strip at entry-Highland register; Dalmore's sherry-finish is the featured Highland-KC match.",
  "Kansas City|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18 with the KC — 18yr bourbon + Oloroso-finish Highland against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Glenmorangie 18's aged + sherry-finish gives it the backbone the 10 reaches for but can't close — the featured Highland-KC match in the aged batch, parallel to Dalmore 12's sherry-finish approach.",
  "Kansas City|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Graham's 10 Year Tawny lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Graham's 20 Year Tawny lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Graham's 2017 Vintage Port's structured vintage port with dark-fruit weight clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Graham's 2017 Vintage Port for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum's agave-citrus character holds the line with the strip's lean char — the light body is outweighed by the cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Kansas City|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Grand Marnier belongs on another course.",
  "Kansas City|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Gray Whale Gin for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's cask-strength herbal-high-proof sweetness stays alongside the strip's lean char — the sweet character meets the cut at digestif register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Kansas City|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose with the KC — French wheat against an 18oz strip. Avoid; even luxury vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Kansas City|Grilled Caesar": "Kansas City into the Caesar -- the lean-bold strip carries into the anchovy-Caesar plate. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Kansas City|Gumbo": "Kansas City into the gumbo -- the spiced register breaks the weight, the table reads round. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "Kansas City|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Kansas City: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 with the KC — 100-proof craft vodka against an 18oz strip. Avoid; even higher-proof vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Kansas City|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 with the KC — 12yr Japanese Alps single malt against an 18oz strip. Strong; Hakushu handles the strip with Forest-Distillery depth; Yamazaki's Mizunara-oak is the featured Japanese-KC pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel with the KC — BT single-barrel against the strip's marbling. The same mash as Blanton's but at 88.9 proof; carries the strip with restrained single-barrel character. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 with the KC — California craft against an 18oz strip. Avoid; vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Kansas City|Head Fake": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build reads alongside the strip's lean char — the sweet character meets the cut at digestif register, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Kansas City|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the KC — HH 20yr collector against an 18oz strip. Strong; HH 20 is the featured aged-HH-KC pairing — the 20-year depth handles marbling at collector register.",
  "Kansas City|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the KC — 100-proof BIB against the strip's marbling. The bonded proof gives the pour weight the 90-proof Wellers don't quite reach; a sturdy, dependable strip pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with the KC — 100-proof 10-year Dylan-brand bourbon against the strip's marbling. The aging and bonded proof deliver the cut; the bottle story sells the pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "Kansas City: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "Kansas City: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Hendricks Flora Adora's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Hendricks Flora Adora on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Hendricks Gin for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Hendricks Grand Cabaret's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hendricks Grand Cabaret lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Hendricks Neptunia": "Hendricks Neptunia's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hendricks Neptunia lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Hendricks Orbium": "Hendricks Orbium's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hendricks Orbium lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy with the KC — ubiquitous cognac against an 18oz strip. Strong; Hennessy handles the strip with benchmark character, though Courvoisier's drier oak cuts marbling sharper.",
  "Kansas City|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB with the KC — 100-proof 10-year bonded single-barrel against the strip's marbling. The bonded weight and aging carry the cut at the premium HH BIB register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Harmony with the KC — premium Suntory blend against an 18oz strip. Strong; Hibiki's refined character handles the strip at premium register; Yamazaki's Mizunara-oak is the featured Japanese-KC pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Honey Roasted Carrots": "The KC with the honey carrots -- protein hands off to dessert, the close builds without conflict. Works; the side sits without pulling focus.",
  "Kansas City|House Wedge": "Kansas City into the wedge -- umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "Kansas City|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 with the KC — historic-Kentucky 15yr against an 18oz strip. Strong; IW Harper 15's cherry-oak handles the strip at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Kansas City|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Il Colle Prosecco Superiore for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Inhibited": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Inhibited for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Inverroche Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Iron Fish Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets Iron Fish Distillery (Thompsonville MI, northern Michigan, farm-to-bottle) bourbon finished in mezcal casks, sibling to Iron Fish Gin already on the menu. Ironfish Mezcal Cask's smoky-agave-finish + Michigan-farm-to-bottle character is the featured Michigan-hometown-exotic-finish-KC pairing — mezcal-cask smoke + strip marbling is a distinctive pairing no other bourbon on the menu offers. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish with the KC — Sazerac-owned Virginia port-finish against an 18oz strip. Strong; Isaac Bowman handles the strip at Virginia-heritage register; AE Cask Strength's 117+ proof is the featured wine-finished-KC match.",
  "Kansas City|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "Anderson Valley Pinot — dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, firm-but-refined tannin, long finish. The refined structure handles Kansas City's lean-bold strip and the mocha plays against beefy char. Peak Anderson Valley Pinot for the strip — concentrated Mendocino red structured enough for the marbled cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "Diamond Mountain Cab — Schramsberg estate, volcanic soils, dark fruit, graphite, mineral, firm tannin. The structured body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the graphite-mineral plays against the beefy char. Peak mountain Cab for Kansas City — Schramsberg provenance story plus high-elevation concentration on the strip. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year with the KC — aged Tennessee whiskey, 12yr premium tier, deeper vanilla-caramel depth. Excellent; JD 12's aged depth is the premium JD-KC pairing — where Old No. 7 is iconic-entry and Bonded brings 100-proof backbone, the 12 Year adds aged Tennessee gravitas.",
  "Kansas City|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded with the KC — Bottled-in-Bond Jack Daniel's, 100-proof, more robust than Old No. 7. Excellent; JD Bonded's 100-proof backbone is the featured JD-KC upgrade — the extra proof handles strip marbling more structurally than Old No. 7's standard charcoal-mellowed approach.",
  "Kansas City|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7 with the KC — iconic Tennessee whiskey, Lincoln County Process charcoal-mellowed, square-bottle vanilla-caramel the-call Tennessee. Excellent; Old No. 7 is the featured Tennessee-KC pairing — the charcoal-mellowed vanilla-caramel frames strip marbling with iconic Tennessee gravitas, the most-called Tennessee-steak pairing on the menu.",
  "Kansas City|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "JD Sinatra Select with the KC — Sinatra-tribute Tennessee against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Sinatra Select's grooved-barrel oak depth handles strip marbling at premium Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Kansas City|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson with the KC — triple-distilled iconic Irish against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Jameson handles the strip at crowd-pleasing register — lighter than Scotch but with enough sherry+bourbon-cask depth for marbling.",
  "Kansas City|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Russian River Pinot — red cherry, pomegranate, subtle earth, smooth, medium body. The smooth structure works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though the low tannin falls short of bolder Cabs' char-requirement. Works as food-friendly Pinot for the strip — reliable lighter red pick.",
  "Kansas City|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets Jefferson's Ocean limited edition honoring master blender Marian McLain Kaminski, sea-aged premium. Marian McLain L.E. brings the sea-aged character of Voyage 23 at limited-edition register — collector-grade ocean-finish + strip marbling. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the KC — sea-aged bourbon, specific Voyage #23, distinctive ocean-motion + salt-tinged character from ship-based maturation. Excellent; Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's sea-aged character is the featured ocean-finish-bourbon-KC pairing — the ocean-motion + salt character cuts strip marbling with a distinctive story no other bourbon on the menu can tell.",
  "Kansas City|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve with the KC — blended bourbon's elegance against the strip's marbling. The smoothness carries the char without competing; a sophisticated strip pour distinct from BT's cleaner flagship approach. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the KC — Twin Oak's double-barrel depth against the strip's marbling. The complex oak gives the cut more dimension than standard Jefferson's; a premium strip pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label with the KC — aged Jim Beam, 8 years in the barrel, 86 proof. The aging gives the pour more weight than White Label and enough oak to carry the strip. Distinct from White Label (too light for the strip); the aged Beam for everyday strip orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label with the KC — entry 80-proof against the strip's marbling. Strong as a choice; underpowered for a premium steak's scale.",
  "Kansas City|Johnnie Walker Black": "JW Black with the KC — iconic 12yr blended Scotch, balanced smoke + malt + sherry, benchmark premium blend. Excellent; JW Black is the archetypal premium-blended-Scotch-KC pairing — the 12-year aging and balanced-smoke-and-sherry character handle strip marbling with gravitas JW Red can only gesture at.",
  "Kansas City|Johnnie Walker Blue": "JW Blue with the KC — ultra-premium luxury blended Scotch against an 18oz strip. Excellent; JW Blue's hand-selected rare-cask silkiness handles strip marbling at luxury-register — where JW Black is iconic-premium and JW Red is entry-smoke, Blue is the collector experience on the biggest steak cut.",
  "Kansas City|Johnnie Walker Red": "JW Red with the KC — 80-proof bold-fiery entry Johnnie Walker with peat-influenced Islay-malt components. Excellent; JW Red's bold-smoke character is the distinctive blended-Scotch-KC pairing — the Islay-malt peat influence handles strip marbling with smoke + intensity Dewar's honey-vanilla can't match.",
  "Kansas City|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Jordan — Alexander Valley Cab with cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the cedar-mint plays against the beefy char. Peak elegant Cab for Kansas City — trusted name, decades of consistency, balanced structure on the classic strip. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Kansas City|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Joseph Mellot Sancerre for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the espresso plays against the beefy char. Strong cult Napa for the strip — Pritchard Hill on the American steakhouse cut.",
  "Kansas City|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's bold char-earth character matches the strip's lean char — the bourbon depth carries the cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Kansas City|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's dense-spirit weight leans against the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the heavy body handles the strip steak, and the deep backbone frames the cut. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Kahlua on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Keenan Chardonnay": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Keenan Chardonnay lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Keenan Mernet": "Kansas City: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye with the KC — KO allocated premium against the strip. The 10-year Kentucky Owl character gives the strip a distinctively-branded premium rye frame. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the KC — Kentucky Owl's 'Confiscated' release, named for the Prohibition-era story of Charles Mortimer Dedman's bourbon confiscated during Prohibition. KO's mainstream offering; the Confiscated label brings the brand's storytelling-narrative tableside. Distinct from KO 10 Year Rye by being a bourbon and more available. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with the KC — luxury American-distiller Irish crossover against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's bourbon-cask-finished Irish character handles strip marbling with a collector-bottle distinction — the American-Irish hybrid is the featured pairing Redbreast 21's aged pot still can't quite replicate thematically.",
  "Kansas City|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The pepper note echoes Kansas City's lean-bold beef char and the wild herbs lift against grilled strip. Peak Southern-French for the strip — a mid-weight garrigue match. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One with the KC — Dutch wheat against an 18oz strip. Avoid; vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Kansas City|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Knickerbocker Gin for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek with the KC — 100-proof high-rye against the strip's marbling. Nutty oak and bold bourbon handle the char cleanly; distinct from wheated approaches — more grip, more heat, more character. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year with the KC — 12-year aged high-rye at 100 proof against the strip's marbling. The aging adds oak depth to standard Knob Creek's nutty character; a premium aged-Beam strip pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 with the KC — aged Beam Knob Creek against an 18oz strip. Strong; KC 15 handles the strip at aged-Beam register; KC 18 is the aged-Beam-KC upgrade.",
  "Kansas City|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 with the KC — 18yr aged Beam Knob Creek against an 18oz strip. Excellent; KC 18 is the featured aged-Beam-KC-16oz-strip pairing — the aged depth handles marbling at collector register.",
  "Kansas City|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with the KC — the blend's layered spicy-sweet against the strip's marbling. The 100-proof depth from both mash bills handles the char cleanly; a distinctive strip pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye with the KC — Beam's 100-proof bold rye, sibling to Knob Creek Bourbon. The extra proof and bold rye spice carry the strip where softer ryes (Sazerac, Michter's) only hit strong; the high-impact rye for strip orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva: ultra-premium Los Altos tequila, 12-14 months in French oak + Hungarian + ex-Moscow wine barrels — wine-cask-finish distinctive among luxury añejos. Excellent on KC strip; the wine-cask-finished character frames the lean cut at ceremonial-luxury register — save the bottle for the guest who wants the wine-cask-distinctive añejo expression.",
  "Kansas City|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo: charcoal-filtered to remove color while retaining flavor complexity — the filtered-clear crystallino expression of Komos's luxury añejo. Excellent on KC strip; the añejo-depth-with-blanco-presentation character frames the lean cut at trendy-luxury register — save the bottle for the guest who wants añejo flavor without the oak color.",
  "Kansas City|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Añejo with the KC — luxury aged Komos against the strip. Strong; Komos extra añejo handles the strip with Riedel-brand ceremony.",
  "Kansas City|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado with the KC — luxury brand, wine-cask-finished (pink hue from French oak + wine cask). Strawberry, cherry, light oak atop the reposado base. Riedel-designed bottle is part of the presentation; a luxury pour that brings theatrics to the strip. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Lagavulin 8": "Kansas City: the strip's firm marbling and savory char meet Lagavulin 8's balanced peat and sea-spray brine. The 8-year carries the strip cleanly; less intense than Laphroaig with the same cut, more assertive than Bowmore. The middle-ground Islay pour for strip orders. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Kansas City|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Napa Cab — Lail entry-level, polished blackcurrant, cedar, dark cherry, refined medium tannin. The refined tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the cedar plays against beefy char. Peak historically-pedigreed Napa Cab for the strip — Robin Lail's Mondavi-Inglenook pedigree on the American cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the graphite plays against beefy char. Strong historically-significant Napa Cab for the strip — Daniel Cuvée pedigree on the American cut.",
  "Kansas City|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver's clean silver register reads alongside the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the blanco-tequila body matches the strip steak, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Kansas City|Laphroaig 10": "Kansas City: the strip's char and marbling meet Laphroaig 10's medicinal weight. Strip marbling takes a harder peated pour than the filet does; Laphroaig's intensity gives the char somewhere to go. Less transcendent than with the Tomahawk (the scale is smaller) but fully aligned. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Kansas City|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch with the KC — 92-proof wheated against the strip's marbling. More body than Weller Special Reserve, more honey-forward than Maker's. Heaven Hill's everyday wheated for strip orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's classic non-vintage Champagne brightness underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut belongs on another course.",
  "Kansas City|Le Garenne Rosé": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity sits alongside the strip's lean char — the white-wine body is outweighed by the cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Kansas City|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Tuscany Brunello di Montalcino DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Le Ragnaie, five years minimum aging, dried cherry, tobacco, leather, rose petal, full body, firm tannin, high acid. The firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the leather plays against beefy char. Peak Italy's-most-prestigious for the strip — Brunello on the American cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Kansas City: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- cocktail citrus reads thin against the cut's structure. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Lemon Lavender Gin Martini lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Les Pallières Raciné": "Kansas City: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Liberator Gin": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Liberator Gin for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Licor 43 for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Limoncello lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, whisper of spice. The medium body works against Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip though the low tannin falls short of the char-requirement of bolder Cabs. Strong by-the-glass Pinot for a guest wanting lighter red with the strip.",
  "Kansas City|Little Book": "Little Book with the KC — Jim Beam Little Book series against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Little Book's cask-strength annual-blend handles strip marbling at Fred-Noe-artistic register.",
  "Kansas City|Loaded Potato": "The KC after the loaded potato -- the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup sets the table for the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: cream and protein share the meal, the build reads full. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Kansas City|Lobster Mac": "Kansas City alongside the lobster mac -- the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain carries cleanly into the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence. Excellent; the headline side pairing for the KC.",
  "Kansas City|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year with the KC — 20yr complex-aged Highland against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Loch Lomond 20's 20-year aging and varied-still complexity handle strip marbling at collector register.",
  "Kansas City|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the KC — premium sourced blend against the strip. The boutique-brand character gives the strip a premium-sourced rye frame distinct from distillery-specific ryes. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Long Road Gin": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Long Road Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's agave-pit-smoke character sits with the strip's lean char — the agave smoke frames the lean cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Kansas City|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII with the KC — luxury-icon Grande Champagne against an 18oz strip. Strong; the KC is heavy enough not to waste the bottle, though its marbling-forward character is more aligned with Courvoisier's drier oak than Louis XIII's 40-100 year complexity.",
  "Kansas City|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano with the KC — Basilicata amaro as post-steak digestif. Strong; floral-anise settles the meal with distinctive character.",
  "Kansas City|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Abruzzo Montepulciano d'Abruzzo — Lunaria earthy grippy, dark cherry, dried herbs, rustic medium body, medium tannin. The rustic grip meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the dried herbs play against beefy char. Peak Italian-approachable red for the strip — great value on the American cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry with the KC — Oloroso Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Macallan 12's sherry-cask depth handles the strip, though Macallan 18's deeper aging is the featured Sherried Speyside-KC match.",
  "Kansas City|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18 with the KC — premium 18yr Oloroso Speyside against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Macallan 18's deeper dried-fruit-and-wood-spice is the featured Sherried Speyside-KC pairing — the 18yr aging gives it the backbone the 12 reaches for but can't quite close.",
  "Kansas City|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate with the KC — distinctive Estate-oak Macallan against an 18oz strip. Strong; Macallan Estate handles the strip with distinctive character; Macallan 18's aged-Oloroso is still the featured Sherried Speyside-KC match.",
  "Kansas City|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The black pepper echoes Kansas City's lean-bold strip char and the dense tannin handles grilled beef. Peak Petite-Sirah for the strip — powerful Napa on the American cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot — bright strawberry, red cherry, light floral, soft tannins, light body. The soft structure works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though the low tannin falls short of bolder Cabs' char-requirement. Peak accessible Pinot for the strip when guest wants lighter red pick. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Kansas City: the strip's marbled-and-char richness finds the bold red's grip; cassis carries the savory weight cleanly. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Mahon Gin": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Mahon Gin on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark with the KC — iconic wheated softness meets the strip's marbling. 90-proof warmth carries the cut without competing with the char; the crowd-pleasing strip pour. Maker's Mark Cellar Aged is richer, this is the everyday version. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with the KC — cellar-aging's deeper wax-and-vanilla meets the strip's char. Richer than standard Maker's Mark with the strip; the pour shows its age in the way it carries the cut's marbling. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Malibu Rum for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye with the KC — 16-year Michigan single-barrel against the strip. The home-state 16-year aged character gives the strip distinctive Michigan-rye depth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Loire Cab Franc — Chinon light body, raspberry, red cherry, bell pepper, forest floor, supple low tannin, bright high acid. The light body handles Kansas City's lean-bold strip though low tannin reads softer than full-bodied Cabs on the cut. Works as unusual Loire CF for the strip — talking point for guests exploring lighter reds.",
  "Kansas City|Margarita": "Margarita's crisp agave-brine build sits beside the strip's lean char — the cocktail is outweighed by the cut, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Kansas City|Marimar Estate Christina": "Russian River Green Valley Pinot — ripe strawberry, cherry compote, warm spice, silky, medium body. The opulent body works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though the low tannin falls short of bolder Cabs. Works as plush Pinot for the strip — Russian River richness over classic Cab.",
  "Kansas City|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe's bitter-herb aperitivo edge sits alongside the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the amaro-bitter register cuts the strip steak, and the amaro backbone frames the cut. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Kansas City|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Bourbon with the KC — Michter's allocated 10yr against an 18oz strip. Strong; Michter's 10 handles the strip at prestigious-independent register.",
  "Kansas City|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye with the KC — aged premium Michter's against the strip. The 10-year polish gives the strip a smooth-premium rye frame distinct from WP 10 Year's Vermont-finished character. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the KC — small-batch smooth rye, 84.8 proof, copper pot-still distilled. Michter's signature approach gives the rye a smoother texture than benchmark Sazerac Rye; smooth rye spice against the strip's marbling. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco's clean silver register sits alongside the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the strip steak, and the bright blanco register underlines the cut. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Kansas City|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo: Highland Jalisco 100% Blue Weber, triple-distilled, 14+ months French oak — softer and cleaner than Patron. Excellent on KC strip; the French-oak-aged refined character frames the lean cut at accessible-craft register — Milagro is the softer-finishing accessible craft answer to Patron.",
  "Kansas City|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado with the KC — modern-style triple-distilled reposado. Clean agave with vanilla; distinct from traditional reposados (Siete Leguas) by production approach — Milagro is the modern clean pour. A contemporary everyday strip call. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam with the KC — Mister Sam Bronfman tribute against an 18oz strip. Strong; Mister Sam handles the strip at Seagram's-tribute register.",
  "Kansas City|Mocha Creme": "The KC into the mocha creme -- the savory-grain strip hands off to silky coffee-chocolate cream, the espresso edge closing the meal cleanly. Works; the lighter dessert reads delicate against the bold main, but the contrast holds.",
  "Kansas City|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Monkey 47 Gin on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder with the KC — Speyside-malt blend against an 18oz strip. Strong; Monkey Shoulder handles the strip with Speyside-malt fruit-and-vanilla character; JW Red's bold-smoke is still the featured blended-Scotch-KC pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro with the KC — approachable amaro as post-steak digestif. Strong; the Bologna classic settles the meal with vanilla-orange ease.",
  "Kansas City|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- light spirit reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Mount Gay Rum lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Muga Reserva": "Rioja Alta Reserva Tempranillo-blend — Muga, American-French oak, strawberry, dried cherry, vanilla, leather, savory-earthy. The vanilla-oak wraps around Kansas City's lean-bold strip char and the leather echoes grilled-beef depth. Peak Rioja for the strip — oak-driven Tempranillo match. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Mushroom Bisque": "Kansas City with the mushroom bisque alongside -- protein and umami share the plate, the build reads weighty and clean. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Mushrooms": "Kansas City alongside the mushrooms -- the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain meets the mushrooms' earthy umami weight: protein and umami share the plate, the build reads weighty and clean. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Kansas City|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's bold molasses-Jamaican character frames the strip's lean char — the bourbon depth carries the cut. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Kansas City|Negroni": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari. The Negroni's bitter-herbal structure handles the KC's lean-bold strip and Campari's orange adds aromatic lift against the char. Peak bitter cocktail option for the most approachable bold cut — structural match.",
  "Kansas City|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye with the KC — Kentucky rye aged in Yorkshire, UK. Distinctive maritime-climate maturation; sibling to Never Say Die Bourbon, same UK-aging novelty. The story is the draw; Yorkshire cask influence gives the strip a genuinely unique frame no other rye on the list brings. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch with the KC — UK-matured Kentucky bourbon against the strip's marbling. The Yorkshire maturation gives the pour distinctive cask influence; a novelty strip pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with the KC — Holland MI bourbon finished in beer barrels, hometown Michigan brewer-heritage distillery (sibling to Knickerbocker Gin already on the menu). Excellent; NH Beer Barrel's hop-influenced beer-barrel finish is the featured Michigan-hometown-exotic-finish-KC pairing — beer-barrel character cuts strip marbling with distinctive brewer-heritage register.",
  "Kansas City|New York Sour": "Kansas City: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. A headliner steakhouse cocktail. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Nickel & Nickel — Oakville Cab with deep cassis, blackberry, graphite, subtle oak. The concentrated body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the graphite-cassis plays against the beefy char. Peak prestige Oakville Cab for Kansas City — terroir-specific bottling on the classic strip. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Nolets Dry Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Kansas City|Not a Paper Plane": "Kansas City: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. A headliner steakhouse cocktail. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Oban 14": "Oban 14 with the KC — coastal West Highland against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Oban's subtle smoke and sea-salt frame strip marbling with maritime character.",
  "Kansas City|Oban 18": "Oban 18 with the KC — 18yr maritime Highland with sherry-finish against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Oban 18's aged maritime + sherry-cask is distinctive against strip marbling — the coastal-sherry combination no Speyside or other Highland replicates.",
  "Kansas City|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo: Los Altos Arandas single-estate vintage-dated tequila from Carlos Camarena — each bottle field-specific, transparent sourcing, 1 year in ex-American-whiskey casks. Excellent on KC strip; the mineral-driven highland character with whiskey-cask aging frames the lean cut at craft-transparency register — Ocho is the connoisseur añejo.",
  "Kansas City|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Kansas City: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the KC — Old Elk Distillery (Colorado Fort Collins) bourbon designed specifically for cigar pairing, distinctive cigar-companion character. Excellent; Old Elk Cigar Cut's cigar-designed character is the featured distinctive-finish-bourbon-KC pairing — purpose-built to accompany rich flavors, which makes strip marbling its native territory.",
  "Kansas City|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer with the KC — wheated bourbon made from emmer wheat (ancient grain), reclassified from American Single Malt / Tennessee (it's a wheated bourbon, not a single malt). Excellent; Old Emmer's emmer-wheat-derived softness is the distinctive wheated-bourbon-KC pairing — where Weller and Maker's use modern soft red winter wheat, Old Emmer uses the ancient-grain variant for distinctive craft character.",
  "Kansas City|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year with the KC — 100-proof wheated bond against the strip's marbling. The bonded weight from Heaven Hill's wheated heritage carries the cut cleanly; distinct from Heaven Hill BIB (non-wheated) — honey-softer, still 100-proof. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year with the KC — aged wheated bond against the strip's marbling. The extra 2 years over Old Fitz 7 give the pour more oak depth; a sophisticated aged-wheated-BIB strip call. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with the KC — 16yr OGD high-rye bourbon against an 18oz strip. Excellent; OGD 16's aged rye-forward spice is the featured rye-forward-aged-bourbon-KC pairing — the distinctive spice cuts marbling where dried-fruit-aged bourbons round it.",
  "Kansas City|Opus One": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Kansas City's lean-bold strip and the mocha plays against the beefy char. Strong collector Napa blend for the strip — Mondavi-Rothschild on the American steakhouse cut.",
  "Kansas City|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Pablo Sour": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla syrup, egg white. The cocktail's sour structure is wrong for the KC's lean-beefy strip — foam and lemon have no hook into char. Works only when guest stays on the cocktail; route KC to Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned.",
  "Kansas City|Paloma": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build plays cleanly against the strip's lean char — the cocktail is outweighed by the cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Kansas City|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye with the KC — Van Winkle Family Reserve against the strip. The 13-year unicorn gives the strip Pappy-legacy depth; the absolute top-shelf rye for strip when it's the table's special occasion. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Kansas City|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage 16th Ed with the KC — Heaven Hill collector annual against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Parker's Heritage 16th's cask-strength handles strip marbling at HH-annual-release register.",
  "Kansas City|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo: Jalisco 100% Blue Weber, 12+ months American oak — the most-distributed premium añejo globally, the accessible gateway to aged tequila. Excellent on KC strip; vanilla-caramel-cooked-agave frames the lean cut at premium-workhorse register — the iconic-bottle pour on the steak guest every room knows.",
  "Kansas City|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver's polished silver tequila with agave-forward lift reads alongside the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the blanco-tequila body matches the strip steak, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Kansas City|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Patron XO Cafe on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Peanut Butter Brownie": "After the KC, the PB brownie -- the lean-bold strip gives way to peanut-and-chocolate fudge, the warm vanilla tempering the heat. Strong; both have weight on the plate, the meal closes substantial.",
  "Kansas City|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Peju — family-owned Rutherford Cab with dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, balanced acidity, refined tannin. Rutherford's famous \"dust\" character gives a distinctive mid-palate. The elegant body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the cocoa plays against the beefy char. Peak elegant Napa Cab for Kansas City. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand with the KC — artisanal cognac against an 18oz strip. Strong; Ferrand handles the strip with craft-premium character, though Courvoisier's drier oak remains the featured cognac-KC pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye with the KC — Heaven Hill's 110-proof 6-year Maryland-style rye. The highest-proof rye in the approachable tier; bold rye spice with serious intensity. Distinct from HH's Elijah Craig Straight Rye (94 proof) by 16 extra proof points and Maryland-style character. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Pineau des Charentes": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pineau des Charentes lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "* Pio Cesare Barbaresco's powerful blackcurrant body meets head-on the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the dark-fruit body sits with the strip steak, and the cassis frames the cut. Peak California-Zin for the strip — bold Napa on the American cut. Gold standard; a confident headline pour.",
  "Kansas City|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Plymouth Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Kansas City|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Aldo Conterno Bussia Monforte d'Alba, rose petal, tar, dried cherry, orange peel, iron minerality, full body, firm tannin. The firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the iron minerality plays against beefy char. Peak serious-Barolo for the strip — Aldo Conterno prestige on the American cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pommery Cuvée Louise belongs on another course.",
  "Kansas City|Prime Tartare": "The KC preceded by the tartare -- the savory course rounds the protein, the meal reads layered. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding the cut.",
  "Kansas City|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt — Napa Cab with ripe blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft polished tannin. The lush body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the vanilla-chocolate plays against the beefy char. Peak polished Napa Cab for Kansas City — indulgent anchor without committing to a prestige bottle. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|RAEN Royal St Robert": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, silky, medium body. The silky structure works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though low tannin falls short of bolder Cabs. Works as Burgundian California Pinot for the strip — elegant red when guest prefers Pinot.",
  "Kansas City|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 with the KC — single pot still Irish whiskey (Ireland's distinctive style — mix of malted + unmalted barley), sherry + bourbon cask, rich-spicy-oily mouthfeel. Excellent; Redbreast 12's pot still oiliness and sherry-cask depth are the featured Irish-KC pairing — the pot still distillation creates a richer body than blended Irish can offer, giving it the backbone for strip marbling.",
  "Kansas City|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 with the KC — 21-year aged pot still Irish against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Redbreast 21's aged sherry-cask + pot still depth is the featured premium-Irish-KC match at collector register.",
  "Kansas City|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Gatsby with the KC — MGP 15yr high-rye Gatsby Reserve against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Remus 15's aged rye-spice is the featured MGP-aged-KC pairing — where OGD 16's high-rye Beam takes this, Remus brings MGP's own Prohibition-tribute approach.",
  "Kansas City|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Rémy VSOP with the KC — Fine Champagne cognac against an 18oz strip. Strong; Rémy's elegance handles the strip at refined register, though Courvoisier's drier oak is the featured cognac-KC match.",
  "Kansas City|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo with the KC — sun-bottle aged against the strip. Strong; distinctive packaging ceremony covers the strip.",
  "Kansas City|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka with the KC — Icelandic vodka against an 18oz strip. Avoid; vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Kansas City|Rishi's Old Antique": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets Rishi's Old Antique — Rishi's retailer-label aged sourced bourbon. Premium sourced under Rishi's boutique banner; distinct from Rishi's-picked single-barrel Bardstown by being a label release rather than specific-cask selection. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The KC after the chickpea soup -- the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth sets the table for the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the spiced register breaks the weight, the table reads round. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Kansas City|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs' bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Roku Gin": "Roku Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Roku Gin on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey character sits with the strip's lean char — the light body is outweighed by the cut, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Kansas City|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with the KC — Wild Turkey's 10-year aged profile against the strip's marbling. The age gives the pour enough oak depth to carry the char cleanly; distinct from Eagle Rare 10's Buffalo Trace-style elegance — more classic Kentucky vocabulary. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Sambuca": "Sambuca's after-dinner liqueur weight sits alongside the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the digestif body mirrors the strip steak, and the soft-spice register brightens the cut. Works; the dessert closes without crowding.",
  "Kansas City|San Simeon Stormwatch": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin, full body, elegant finish. The full body handles Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the mocha plays against beefy char. Peak Paso value Bordeaux blend for the strip — solid full-body on the American steakhouse cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sta. Rita Hills Santa Barbara Pinot — rich, layered, dark cherry, earth, dried herbs, medium tannin. The rich body works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though medium tannin falls short of bolder Cabs. Works as legendary Pinot for the strip — Santa Barbara layered depth for Pinot-preferring guest.",
  "Kansas City|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Kansas City with the garlic spinach -- the 18oz strip meets the wilted garlic-buttered greens, garlic accenting the strip's beefy register cleanly. Strong; lighter than the creamed pairing, just as honest.",
  "Kansas City|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- dessert wine is post-meal — wrong register for the steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sauternes Glass lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Sazerac": "Kansas City: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Oak, caramel, and spirit heat stand up to the beef's render; the caramelized sweetness mirrors the crust. A headliner steakhouse cocktail. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye with the KC — the iconic rye cocktail benchmark, 90 proof, Buffalo Trace. Spicy-sweet with floral-vanilla notes; the namesake of the Sazerac cocktail. The rye spice finds the strip's char in a way smoother brown spirits can't; the industry-standard rye for strip orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the KC — NZ single malt against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Fortuna's New Zealand craft-distillery character handles strip marbling at distinctive-terroir register.",
  "Kansas City|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid, full body. The powerful firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the leather plays against beefy char. Peak King-of-Italian for the strip — Scavino prestige on the American cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Off-dry Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol with a lively acid backbone keeping the residual sweetness in check. Still, the light body can't meet a bone-in strip like the KC. Redirect to a red — the wine is a gateway Riesling, but not a steak pairing. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Kansas City|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "California Cab by-the-glass value — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin, smooth full finish. The smooth structure meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the dark chocolate plays against beefy char. Peak by-the-glass Cab for the strip — accessible California value on the American cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Seafood Tower": "Kansas City after the seafood tower -- the iced shellfish opens with cold-brine refresh, the 18oz strip following with clean savory grain that the tower's cool primed without crowding. Strong; the contrast in register reads composed.",
  "Kansas City|Seasonal Soup": "Kansas City with the seasonal soup alongside -- the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Seasonal Vegetables": "Kansas City alongside the seasonal vegetables -- the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain meets the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Kansas City|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Tuscany Brunello DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Sesti biodynamic, cherry, dried rose, sandalwood, high acid, firm tannin. The firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the high acid lifts beefy char. Strong Brunello for the strip — Sesti's elegance polishes the American cut.",
  "Kansas City|Shafer 1.5": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin. The plush structure meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the mocha plays against beefy char. Peak Stags Leap Cab for the strip — Shafer opulence on the American cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Shafer Hillside Select": "Stags Leap collector's Cab — Hillside Select 32-month French oak, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violet, graphite, dense yet silky, firm tannin. The firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the graphite plays against beefy char. Strong collector's Napa Cab for the strip — Hillside Select on the American cut.",
  "Kansas City|Shrimp Bisque": "The KC after the shrimp bisque -- the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup sets the table for the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: cream rounds the protein, the plate composes thick. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Kansas City|Shrimp Cocktail": "Kansas City after the shrimp cocktail -- the chilled shrimp's citrus-horseradish bite cleans the palate, the 18oz strip's clean savory grain following with restrained char. Strong; the bright opener primes the strip's middle register.",
  "Kansas City|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge finds neutral with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the silver-spirit register lifts the strip steak, and the bright blanco register underlines the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Kansas City|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado with the KC — traditional tahona-crushed agave, family-distillery craft. Rustic, earthier character than Don Julio's polished benchmark; the strip's marbling meets the traditional agave with real grip. Old-school tequila for guests who want character. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Alexander Valley Cab — American-oak vanilla, coconut, dill, ripe cassis, plum, soft tannin. The lush American-oak body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the vanilla-dill plays against the beefy char. Peak recognizable Cab for Kansas City — Silver Oak's distinctive American-oak signature on the classic strip. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Boutique Napa family estate Cab — dark plum, cassis, toasted oak, long refined finish, firm tannin. The firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the cassis plays against beefy char. Peak boutique Napa Cab for the strip — Simon Family estate-grown pedigree on the American cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "St. Helena Napa Cab — Spottswoode organic family estate, plum, dark cherry, violet, silky medium tannin. The silky tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the violet plays against beefy char. Peak organic Napa Cab for the strip — Spottswoode family story on the American cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry — Napa Rutherford Cab with blackberry, cassis, mocha, firm tannin. The full body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the firm tannin frames the beefy char. Peak by-the-glass Napa Cab for Kansas City — accessible price point for a classic steak-and-Cab pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin, full body. The firm tannin handles Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the espresso plays against beefy char. Peak estate Meritage for the strip — age-integrated Napa Bordeaux-style on the American cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the KC — BT premium cask-strength against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Stagg Jr.'s cask-strength handles strip marbling at BT premium register.",
  "Kansas City|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit with the KC — Russian wheat against an 18oz strip. Avoid; even ultra-premium vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Kansas City|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, fresh-cut grass with zesty acid. Light-bodied white can't match the KC's beefy, bone-in profile. The acid cuts through the steak but leaves the palate without grounding. Redirect to a red for the course. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Kansas City|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Tanq 10 Gin on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Tanqueray Gin on another course instead.",
  "Kansas City|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- dessert wine is post-meal — wrong register for the steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for another course.",
  "Kansas City|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with the KC — multi-cask aged against the strip. Strong; triple-cask complexity covers the strip with distinctive multi-cask character.",
  "Kansas City|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter — polished Napa Cab with rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth lingering finish, approachable concentration. The polished body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the mocha plays against the beefy char. Peak mid-tier Napa Cab for Kansas City — crowd-pleaser balance without committing to a prestige bottle. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Kansas City: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|The BG": "Kansas City: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. A headliner steakhouse cocktail. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|The Happy Wife": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build reads alongside the strip's lean char — the cocktail is outweighed by the cut, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Kansas City|The Manhattan": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The cocktail's bourbon depth and herbal-sweet balance stands against the KC's lean-bold strip; vanilla syrup plays against the char. Peak Manhattan pairing for the most approachable bold cut — weight-matches well. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|The Prisoner Red Blend": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The integrated tannin wraps around Kansas City's lean-bold strip char and the layered dark fruit plays with grilled beef. Peak Prisoner for the strip — one of the country's most recognized labels. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the KC — BTAC uncut against the strip. The Handy's barrel-proof Sazerac character gives the strip BT's top-tier rye intensity; the allocated answer to Sazerac Rye's benchmark. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the KC — Barton white-wine-finished bourbon against an 18oz strip. Strong; TSM Chardonnay Cask handles the strip with distinctive chardonnay character; AE Cask Strength is the featured wine-finished-KC match.",
  "Kansas City|Titos Vodka": "Tito's with the KC — corn vodka against an 18oz strip. Avoid; vodka has no way to match strip marbling intensity. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Kansas City|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory with the KC — entry Suntory blend against an 18oz strip. Strong; Toki handles the strip at crowd-pleasing entry register; Yamazaki's Mizunara-oak is the featured Japanese-KC pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Tomato Basil": "The KC after the tomato basil -- the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid sets the table for the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the bright cuts the rich cleanly. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Kansas City|Transfusion": "Transfusion's crisp citrus-ginger build leans against the strip's lean char — the cocktail is outweighed by the cut, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Kansas City|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado with the KC — aged in cedar-wood barrels. Distinctive cedar and pine notes atop agave; a singular character pour with nothing else on the menu quite like it. The cedar gives the strip's char a forest-floor counterpoint. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Truffle Fries": "Kansas City with the truffle fries -- the 18oz lean-bold strip's savory grain meets the parmesan-and-truffle fry weight, classic steakhouse plate, both sides hold their weight. Strong; a confident order, no second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W. with the KC — drier traditional Irish blend against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Tullamore's drier heritage handles the strip — slightly more structural than Jameson's smoother approach.",
  "Kansas City|Venge Family Reserve": "Kirk Venge flagship Oakville Cab — concentrated blackcurrant, dark chocolate, vanilla, seamless polished finish, soft tannin, full body. The polished structure meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the dark chocolate plays against beefy char. Peak Oakville flagship for the strip — Venge craftsman pedigree on the American cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Venge Scout's Honor": "Napa Zin-led blend — Kirk Venge's Scout's Honor, blackberry, boysenberry, black pepper, vanilla, full body, generous finish. The ripe Zin fruit plays with lean-bold strip beef and black pepper cuts firm-savory grain. Strong Zin-led for KC — Californian fruit-forward weight handles the well-marbled cut.",
  "Kansas City|Venge Silencieux": "Kirk Venge single-vineyard Napa — dark cherry, cassis, chocolate, floral violet, silky soft tannin. The silky structure meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the chocolate plays against beefy char. Peak winemaker's Napa Cab for the strip — Venge precision on the American cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Vesper": "Vesper's crisp citrus-juniper build sits beside the strip's lean char — the cocktail is outweighed by the cut, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Kansas City|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's classic yellow-label Champagne body underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Veuve Clicquot Brut lands on another course — not here.",
  "Kansas City|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. The rye's spice cuts the KC's lean-beefy strip while cognac's richness meets the char; Bénédictine's herbal complexity adds depth. Peak Vieux Carré pairing with the most approachable bold cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Villon Cognac": "Villon with the KC — contemporary cognac spirit against an 18oz strip. Strong; Villon handles the strip at approachable register; Courvoisier's drier oak is still the featured cognac-KC pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Vin Santo belongs on another course.",
  "Kansas City|Weller 107": "Weller 107 with the KC — the higher proof carries the strip's marbling in ways Weller Special Reserve can't quite reach. More viscous, more caramel-forward, more heat. The bolder wheated call for strip orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year with the KC — the strip's marbling and char meet twelve years of oak-softened wheat sweetness. Richer than Weller Special Reserve, more refined than Weller 107. The sipping bourbon for guests who want depth without heat on a premium strip. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Weller Millennium": "* Weller Millennium's grain-and-oak bourbon register runs straight into the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the strip steak, and the vanilla balances the char-and-fat. Layered conversation-starter pour. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Kansas City|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve with the KC — the strip's marbling meets Weller's entry-level caramel and honey. Not as rich as Weller 12, not as bold as Weller 107; the approachable strip pour when a guest wants wheated character without committing to the premium bottles. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley with the KC — Buffalo Trace's wheat vodka against an 18oz strip. Avoid; even the BT pedigree can't make vodka match strip marbling. Reach for the BT bourbon side of the house.",
  "Kansas City|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the KC — aged 10-year single-barrel rye against the strip's marbling. The WP-core character gives the strip a premium single-barrel rye frame distinct from approachable tier Kentucky ryes. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with the KC — 12-year fruit-cask-finished rye against the strip. The multi-cask complexity layers fruit-wine influence over aged rye; distinctive within the premium rye tier. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with the KC — premium 15-year WP flagship against the strip. Extended maturation gives the strip a deeply aged rye frame; the top-tier WP for strip orders. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with the KC — allocated annual WP against the strip. The Boss Hog IX barrel-proof character gives the strip WP's top-tier aged-rye intensity; the absolute premium WP for strip orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with the KC — X Commandments edition against the strip. The 10th-Boss-Hog barrel-proof annual gives the strip collector authority; vintage-year allocation story tableside. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kansas City|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year — WP's accessible line, 6-year 100-proof, Canadian-sourced, Vermont brand. Approachable WP entry distinct from higher-tier bottlings. Vermont-finished character distinct from Kentucky ryes. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with the KC — craft high-rye against the strip's marbling. The 94-proof oak spice gives the strip a different dimension than the bigger-distillery bourbons; a signature-character strip pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with the KC — small-batch fruit-forward bourbon meets the strip's marbling. The floral and fruit notes give the strip a dimension BT's straight oak doesn't bring; a sophisticated everyday call. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kansas City|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with the KC — iconic Double-Oaked benchmark against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Woodford Double Oaked's intensified-caramel is the featured iconic-Double-Oaked-KC pairing — the benchmark everyone recognizes.",
  "Kansas City|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the KC — 90.4-proof small-batch balanced rye, fruit-forward and elegant. Sister to Woodford Reserve Bourbon; carries the distillery's fruit-floral complexity into rye territory. Distinct from Sazerac Rye's benchmark spicy-sweet — Woodford is more fruit-forward, more elegant. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with the KC — Washington State craft rye from Woodinville Whiskey Co. Pacific Northwest small-batch using local grain; distinct from Kentucky, Heaven Hill, or BT ryes by state of origin alone. A regional-craft rye for strip orders. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kansas City|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 with the KC — flagship Suntory single malt against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak depth is the featured Japanese-whisky-KC pairing — the Mizunara oak is Japan's answer to European oak traditions, and it frames strip marbling with distinctive character.",
  "Kansas City|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets 18yr aged Suntory single malt. Yamazaki 18's deeper Mizunara-oak and sherry-cask dried-fruit handle strip marbling at collector register — the premium Japanese-KC match where Yamazaki 12 opens the lane and the 18 completes it. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kansas City|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness sits alongside the strip's lean char — the sweet character meets the cut at digestif register, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Asparagus": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Keenan Chardonnay — Spring Mountain Napa, green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acid. The bright acid cuts the au gratin's cheese-and-cream weight and the subtle oak complements the golden crust. Works well as balanced Chardonnay for au gratin — Napa structure on the indulgent side. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Beignets": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Keenan Chardonnay on the steak course instead.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Bone Marrow": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity stays alongside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Bone-In Filet": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Keenan Chardonnay on another course instead.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Broccoli Cheddar": "Keenan Chardonnay -- Spring Mountain Chardonnay, oaked Napa, tropical-fruit and toast. The cool-climate register carries the broccoli cheddar, and the green-apple edge brightens the bowl. Excellent; a textbook match that delivers. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Broccolini": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Brussels and Belly": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Burrata": "Keenan Chardonnay — Spring Mountain, green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak. The bright acid cuts burrata's cream and the subtle oak meets the delicate cheese without crushing; lemon curd plays against basil-tomato. Strong balanced Chardonnay for burrata — mountain structure without overpowering the starter.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Butternut Squash": "Spring Mountain Napa Chardonnay — green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acid. The bright acid cuts the soup's caramelized-squash sweetness and the subtle oak plays against nutmeg-cream warmth. Peak balanced Chardonnay for butternut squash — Napa structure on the autumn classic. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Carrot Cake": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Keenan Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Cheesecake": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Keenan Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Chilean Seabass": "Spring Mountain Napa Chardonnay — green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acid. The bright acid lifts seabass's buttery rich flesh and the subtle oak plays against the melt-in-mouth texture. Peak balanced Chardonnay for Chilean seabass — Napa structure on the rich classic. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Chocolate Brownie": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Keenan Chardonnay belongs on the steak course.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Chocolate Cake": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Keenan Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Clam Chowder": "Spring Mountain Napa Chardonnay — green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acid. The bright acid cuts the chowder's cream weight and the lemon curd lifts the clam brine. Peak balanced Chardonnay for clam chowder — Napa structure on the creamy classic. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Cowboy Ribeye": "Keenan Chardonnay against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — balanced mountain white crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Keenan for salmon, chicken, lobster mac, or the seafood-and-cream course where it shines.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Crab Cake": "Keenan Chardonnay — Spring Mountain Napa with green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak. The bright acid cuts crab's romesco and the lemon curd plays against the sweet meat cleanly; subtle oak adds depth without overpowering. Strong mountain Chardonnay for crab cake — balance meets classic starter.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: this rich, creamy side's dairy-driven indulgence meets this rich, oak-aged Chardonnay's butter, stone fruit, and full-bodied weight. Opulent Chardonnay's butter and oak mirror the side's dairy richness — same-register match. Works — a capable side pour. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Creamy Potato": "Spring Mountain Napa Chardonnay — green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acid. The bright acid cuts the soup's buttery cream weight and the subtle oak plays against the potato base. Peak balanced Chardonnay for creamy potato — Napa structure on the rich classic. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Creme Brulee": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Keenan Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Escargot": "Spring Mountain Chardonnay — green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acidity. The bright acid cuts escargot's garlic-parsley butter and the lemon curd plays against the snail richness; subtle oak adds French elegance. Strong mountain Chardonnay for escargot — balance on the French classic.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Faroe Island Salmon": "Napa Chardonnay with apple and lemon, softer oak than Far Niente. Same function, lighter footprint — handles the Faroe fat without the full oak weight. Pivot when the guest wants Napa Chard but not the fullest version. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this rich, oak-aged Chardonnay's butter, stone fruit, and full-bodied weight. Rich Chardonnay weight approaches steak territory, but the absence of tannin and the oak sweetness clash with the char. Strong — the interaction lands despite the axis challenge.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|French Onion": "Keenan Chardonnay — Spring Mountain Napa, green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acid. The bright acid cuts French Onion's gruyère-cream weight and the subtle oak plays against the caramelized broth. Strong balanced Chardonnay for French Onion — Napa structure reframes the classic as a white pairing.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Grilled Caesar": "Keenan Chardonnay — Spring Mountain with green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acid. The bright acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the subtle oak plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong mountain Chardonnay for grilled Caesar — balance reframes the classic layered salad.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Gumbo": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity sits beside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Spring Mountain Napa Chardonnay — green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acid. The bright acid cuts carrots' honey-glaze sweetness and the subtle oak plays against roasted-root char. Strong balanced Chardonnay for honey-roasted carrots — Napa structure on the sweet-root classic.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|House Wedge": "Spring Mountain Chardonnay — green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acidity. The bright acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the subtle oak plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as mountain Chardonnay for the wedge — though the wine's versatility shines higher on salmon, chicken, or the bisque.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Kansas City": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Keenan Chardonnay lands on another course — not here.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Loaded Potato": "Spring Mountain Napa Chardonnay — green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acid. The bright acid cuts the soup's cream-and-cheddar weight and the subtle oak plays against bacon and scallion toppings. Peak balanced Chardonnay for loaded potato — Napa structure on the indulgent bowl. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Lobster Mac": "Spring Mountain Napa Chardonnay — green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acidity. The bright acid cuts lobster-mac's cream-cheese richness and the subtle oak adds depth without crushing the lobster's delicate sweetness. Peak balanced Chardonnay for lobster mac — mountain structure meets indulgent side. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Market Fish": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the fish's clean flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Mocha Creme": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Keenan Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Mushroom Bisque": "Spring Mountain Napa Chardonnay — green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acid. The bright acid cuts the bisque's silky cream weight and the subtle oak plays against the mushroom's earthy depth. Peak balanced Chardonnay for mushroom bisque — Napa structure lifts the umami base. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Mushrooms": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Keenan Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Porterhouse": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- white wine can't stand up to the marbled char — the cut earns a structured red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Keenan Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Prime Tartare": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity finds neutral with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Rainbow Trout": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity holds steady against the trout's delicate flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Roast Half Chicken": "Keenan Chardonnay — Spring Mountain Napa with green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acidity. The versatile acid-oak balance handles roasted chicken; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak Napa Chardonnay for chicken across most seasonal accompaniments. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Salt-Cured Halibut: this delicate seafood's mild, flaky flesh meets this rich, oak-aged Chardonnay's butter, stone fruit, and full-bodied weight. Oaky Chardonnay's butter and stone fruit lock into rich fish — texture matching texture. A headliner white pour for rich fish. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Keenan Chardonnay: Spring Mountain oak-forward Napa Chardonnay. Excellent on sauteed garlic spinach; oak-butter matches the garlic-butter greens at Napa register. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Seafood Tower": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity holds steady against the tower's chilled shellfish — the crisp acidity lifts the shellfish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Seared Scallops": "Napa Chardonnay with apple and lemon, softer oak presence than Far Niente. Same function, lighter footprint — less oak means the scallop's caramelized exterior gets room. Pitch when the guest wants Napa Chard but not the fullest version. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Seasonal Soup": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Seasonal Vegetables": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the seasonal garden plate — the crisp acidity sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Shrimp Bisque": "Keenan Chardonnay — Spring Mountain Napa, green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acid. The bright acid cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and the subtle oak complements the butter-roux without adding too much weight. Peak mountain Chardonnay for bisque — balance meets cream-soup classic. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Shrimp Cocktail": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity finds register with the shrimp's chilled sweetness — the crisp acidity lifts the shellfish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Swordfish": "Spring Mountain Napa Chardonnay — green apple, lemon curd, subtle oak, bright acid. The bright acid lifts swordfish's meaty flesh and the subtle oak plays against the grilled char. Peak balanced Chardonnay for swordfish — Napa structure on the grilled classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|The Tomahawk": "Spring Mountain Chardonnay against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even mountain structure can't meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Keenan for salmon, chicken, lobster mac, scallops, or the bisque course.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Tomato Basil": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the tomato basil's bright acid — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Truffle Fries": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity holds with the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity lifts the tuna's seared rare crust — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Keenan Chardonnay|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity plays cleanly against the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Asparagus": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Spring Mountain Napa Mernet — Merlot-Cab, mountain structure, plush dark cherry, plum, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the mountain-earth plays against the lactic richness. Works as mountain Merlot-Cab for au gratin — Keenan on the cheesy side.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Beignets": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Keenan Mernet for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Bone-In Filet": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the Cab structure frames the tenderloin, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Broccoli Cheddar": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Broccolini": "Keenan Mernet's structured dark-fruit weight finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the structured body wraps the broccolini, and the cassis frames the side. Works; a measured close to the meal.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Burrata": "Spring Mountain Napa Mernet against burrata's creamy-delicate center — mountain-grown Merlot-Cab structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Keenan Mernet for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Butternut Squash": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis reads alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Carrot Cake": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Keenan Mernet for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Cheesecake": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Keenan Mernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Chilean Seabass": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Keenan Mernet for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Chocolate Brownie": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Keenan Mernet for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Chocolate Cake": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Keenan Mernet for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Clam Chowder": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis plays cleanly against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Crab Cake": "Spring Mountain Napa Mernet against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — mountain-grown Merlot-Cab structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Keenan Mernet for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Creamy Potato": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis sits beside the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Creme Brulee": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Keenan Mernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Escargot": "Keenan Mernet's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the structured body wraps the buttered escargot, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Faroe Island Salmon": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Keenan Mernet for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Filet Mignon": "Spring Mountain Napa Merlot-Cab — 'Mernet' portmanteau (Merlot + Cabernet), mountain-grown structure, plush dark cherry, plum, medium tannin, full body. The plush body meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the Merlot roundness plays against escargot-butter. Peak mountain Merlot-Cab for filet — worth explaining Mernet to curious guests. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Keenan Mernet|French Onion": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis plays cleanly against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Grilled Caesar": "Spring Mountain Napa Mernet — Merlot-Cab, mountain structure, plush dark cherry, plum, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the plum plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong mountain Merlot-Cab for grilled Caesar — Keenan story on the layered salad.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Gumbo": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis holds with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis stays alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Keenan Mernet|House Wedge": "Spring Mountain Napa Mernet — Merlot-Cab, mountain structure, plush dark cherry, plum, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the plum plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as mountain Merlot-Cab for the wedge — Keenan story reframes the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Kansas City": "Kansas City: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Loaded Potato": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Lobster Mac": "Keenan Mernet's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the structured body wraps the lobster mac, and the cassis frames the side. Works; the courses share the table without crowding.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Market Fish": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Keenan Mernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Mocha Creme": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Keenan Mernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Mushroom Bisque": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis sits alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit anchors the mushroom's umami weight; firm tannin and oak hold their register. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Keenan Mernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Prime Tartare": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Rainbow Trout": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Keenan Mernet for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Roast Half Chicken": "Spring Mountain Napa Mernet — Merlot-Cab, mountain structure, plush dark cherry, plum, medium tannin. The plush body plays against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as mountain Merlot-Cab for chicken — Keenan story on the bird course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Keenan Mernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis plays cleanly against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Seafood Tower": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Keenan Mernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Seared Scallops": "Spring Mountain Napa Mernet against scallop sweetness — mountain-grown Merlot-Cab structure buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Keenan Mernet for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Seasonal Soup": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis stays alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Seasonal Vegetables": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis reads alongside the seasonal garden plate — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Shrimp Bisque": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis sits alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Shrimp Cocktail": "Spring Mountain Napa Mernet against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — mountain-grown Merlot-Cab and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Keenan Mernet for the steak course.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Swordfish": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Keenan Mernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Keenan Mernet|The Tomahawk": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the Cab structure carries the bone-in cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Tomato Basil": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis reads alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit anchors the mushroom's umami weight; firm tannin and oak hold their register. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Keenan Mernet's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Keenan Mernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Keenan Mernet|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye|Asparagus": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the asparagus, and the rye-grain bite cuts the side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the dry rye body wraps the au gratin, and the oak threads the cream. Works; aging wasted on the salad.",
  "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye|Beignets": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye's rye-spice character leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the rye-and-oak weight handles the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
