  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with au gratin potatoes — KO Confiscated against cheese-and-cream. Works; craft-blend over-specified.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Beignets": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon depth settles into the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Bone Marrow": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with bone marrow — KO Confiscated against umami fat. The narrative-bourbon character meets marrow with KO mainstream authority. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Bone-In Filet": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the bourbon weight handles the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak-and-spice register settles the cut. Strong; uncut intensity sits with richness at allocated register.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Broccoli Cheddar": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon backbone carries the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; boutique-blend depth gives composed dessert character.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Broccolini": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the brown-spirit warmth threads the broccolini, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Brussels and Belly": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with Brussels + Belly — KO Confiscated meets maple-glazed pork belly. The storytelling-brand character cuts the glaze with KO distinctiveness. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Burrata": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with burrata — fresh dairy against KO Confiscated. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Butternut Squash": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the squash bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; aged character overshoots delicacy.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Carrot Cake": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with carrot cake — KO Confiscated against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; craft-blend finds cake spice with KO narrative character.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Cheesecake": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the caramel-spice register matches the cheesecake, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; mid-proof pour is slightly over-specified.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Chilean Seabass": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Kentucky Owl Confiscated for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Chocolate Brownie": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the chocolate brownie — KO Confiscated against cocoa. Strong; craft-blend meets the brownie at KO narrative register.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Chocolate Cake": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; gentle match.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Clam Chowder": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon body sits with the chowder, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; OGD 16 frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Cowboy Ribeye": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the Cowboy Ribeye — KO Confiscated against cap fat. The Prohibition-era narrative and KO craft-blend character handle the ribeye at storytelling register. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Crab Cake": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with crab cake — KO Confiscated against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Creamed Spinach": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with creamed spinach — KO Confiscated against dairy. Strong; craft-blend sits with richness.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Creamy Potato": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon backbone carries the potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; boutique at matched register.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Creme Brulee": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the pour's brightness is a gentle counterpoint to the dessert's richness.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Escargot": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon backbone carries the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; EHT Full Proof frames the two-cut plate at historic Kentucky register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Faroe Island Salmon": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Kentucky Owl Confiscated on the steak course instead.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Filet Mignon": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the filet — KO Confiscated against the 10oz cut. Strong; the narrative-bourbon is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|French Onion": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Grilled Caesar": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the grilled Caesar — KO Confiscated against charred romaine. Works; narrative-bourbon doesn't drive.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Gumbo": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the gumbo, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon body sits with the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; KBS distinctiveness is slightly over-specified for the two-cut plate.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|House Wedge": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the House Wedge — KO Confiscated against iceberg and bleu. Works; Prohibition narrative wasted on salad.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Kansas City": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the KC — Kentucky Owl's 'Confiscated' release, named for the Prohibition-era story of Charles Mortimer Dedman's bourbon confiscated during Prohibition. KO's mainstream offering; the Confiscated label brings the brand's storytelling-narrative tableside. Distinct from KO 10 Year Rye by being a bourbon and more available. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Loaded Potato": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Lobster Mac": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the lobster mac, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Market Fish": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Kentucky Owl Confiscated for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Mocha Creme": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon backbone carries the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the complex character is wasted on the salad.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Mushroom Bisque": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Mushrooms": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the mushrooms — KO Confiscated against earthy umami. Strong; craft-blend character layers into mushroom depth with KO narrative distinctiveness.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the PB brownie — KO Confiscated against peanut butter and cocoa. The craft-blend narrative character gives the dessert KO storytelling depth. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Porterhouse": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the porterhouse — KO Confiscated across strip and filet. Works; craft-blend covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Prime Tartare": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's crisp spice-vanilla character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the caramel meets the nut character. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Rainbow Trout": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Kentucky Owl Confiscated for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Roast Half Chicken": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak depth lays under the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the 10-year holds without lifting.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Kentucky Owl Confiscated lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; pour is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Seafood Tower": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Kentucky Owl Confiscated lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Seared Scallops": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against KO Confiscated. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Seasonal Soup": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Seasonal Vegetables": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon weight handles the seasonal vegetables, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; pour is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Shrimp Bisque": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the bourbon weight handles the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Shrimp Cocktail": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against KO Confiscated. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Swordfish": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Kentucky Owl Confiscated for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|The Tomahawk": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the Tomahawk — KO Confiscated against bone-in ribeye. Strong; craft-blend narrative covers the cut with KO authority.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Tomato Basil": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; high proof over-specified for the soup.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Truffle Fries": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with truffle fries — KO Confiscated against truffle-parmesan. The narrative-bourbon gives the side a KO storytelling frame. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Kentucky Owl Confiscated lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kentucky Owl Confiscated|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon body sits with the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Asparagus": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the bourbon's register is wrong for a clean vegetable side — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the approachable Irish character carries the au gratin, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; EHT Full Proof is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Beignets": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the gentle Irish body threads the beignets, and the soft malt rounds the dessert. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Bone Marrow": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's: Irish-luxury-icon limited release. Excellent on bone marrow; sherry-cask Irish depth handles umami-fat at allocation-luxury register — save the bottle.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Bone-In Filet": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's crisp Irish-sherry-cask character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the dried-fruit notes meet the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Broccoli Cheddar": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift, not oak-and-vanilla depth. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's on the steak course instead.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Broccolini": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the bourbon's register is wrong for a clean vegetable side — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's belongs on the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet Kentucky Owl luxury Irish. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for the side — save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Burrata": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness crowds out the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet-oak register curdles next to fresh dairy without acid to balance. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Butternut Squash": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift, not oak-and-vanilla depth. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's belongs on the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Carrot Cake": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with carrot cake — luxury Irish against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for dessert — Redbreast 21's aged sherry-cask is the featured Irish-carrot-cake match.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Cheesecake": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the gentle Irish body threads the cheesecake, and the oak threads the cream. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Chilean Seabass": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Chocolate Brownie": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the approachable Irish character carries the brownie, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the dessert. Works; the intensity overpowers this dish.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Chocolate Cake": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the malt-grain blend handles the chocolate cake, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the dessert. Works; bold intensity slightly over-specified.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Clam Chowder": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the soup-salad course needs lift, not oak-and-vanilla depth. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Cowboy Ribeye": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's: limited-release Irish luxury icon. Excellent on Cowboy Ribeye; the sherry-cask depth meets fatty bone-in cap-fat at ceremonial register — save the bottle for the guest who appreciates Irish at allocation level.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Crab Cake": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with crab cake — luxury Irish crossover against delicate lump crab. Works; even with Irish whiskey's category advantage, Kentucky Owl is over-specified for crab — save the collector bottle.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Creamed Spinach": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with creamed spinach — $150+ luxury Irish crossover against dairy-rich side. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Creamy Potato": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift, not oak-and-vanilla depth. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Creme Brulee": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the gentle Irish body threads the brulee, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; accessible gentle match.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Escargot": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the Irish whiskey body sits with the buttered escargot, and the soft malt rounds the starter. Strong; accessible everyday match; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Faroe Island Salmon": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with the salmon — Kentucky Owl's Irish-American crossover against oily salmon. Excellent; Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's bourbon-cask-finished Irish frames the fish with luxury-crossover distinction.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets Kentucky Owl's luxury Irish crossover (~$150+ limited edition), a blend of Irish whiskies aged in bourbon casks (American-style twist on Irish). Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's is one of the few pairings where this collector bottle earns its pour — the American-bourbon-cask-finished Irish character brings distinctive depth to tenderloin no traditional Irish replicates. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|French Onion": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the French onion, and the grain register softens the bowl. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Grilled Caesar": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with the grilled Caesar — luxury crossover Irish against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Kentucky Owl's bourbon-cask Irish meets the char at collector register; Redbreast 12's pot still spice is still the featured Irish-Caesar match.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Gumbo": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness crowds the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the bourbon has no acid to balance the broth's savory base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's belongs on the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness crowds the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- the spirit needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|House Wedge": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with the House Wedge — luxury Irish crossover against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Kentucky Owl engages the bleu at collector register; consider whether the wedge deserves a $150+ pour.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Kansas City": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with the KC — luxury American-distiller Irish crossover against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's bourbon-cask-finished Irish character handles strip marbling with a collector-bottle distinction — the American-Irish hybrid is the featured pairing Redbreast 21's aged pot still can't quite replicate thematically.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Loaded Potato": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the malt-grain blend handles the loaded potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; Boss Hog's allocation story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Lobster Mac": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the malt-grain blend handles the lobster mac, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the complex character is wasted on the salad.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Market Fish": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Mocha Creme": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the mocha creme, and the oak threads the cream. Works; complexity wasted on salad.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Mushroom Bisque": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the gentle Irish body threads the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the umami. Works; the pour's toasted warmth threads through the char without overpowering.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Mushrooms": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with mushrooms — luxury American-Irish crossover against earthy umami. Strong; Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's distinctive bourbon-cask Irish character threads mushrooms at collector register.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the PB brownie, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the dessert. Works; aged character overshoots delicacy.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Porterhouse": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the malt-grain blend handles the dual-cut steak, and the soft malt rounds the cut. Strong; composed namesake match.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Prime Tartare": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the Irish whiskey body sits with the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; boutique-craft covers the cut with rare-release character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Rainbow Trout": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Roast Half Chicken": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with the chicken — luxury Irish crossover against roasted poultry. Excellent; Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's bourbon-cask-finished Irish frames chicken at collector register — the American-twist distinction earns the pour.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness crowds the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the bourbon has no acid to balance the broth's savory base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's on the steak course instead.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the bourbon's register is wrong for a clean vegetable side — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's on the steak course instead.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Seafood Tower": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's on the steak course instead.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Seared Scallops": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with seared scallops — luxury American-Irish crossover against scallop delicacy. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for scallops — save the collector bottle.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Seasonal Soup": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- spirit warmth and the broth's salt-and-spice frame refuse each other. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Seasonal Vegetables": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the bourbon's register is wrong for a clean vegetable side — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Shrimp Bisque": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with shrimp bisque — luxury crossover Irish against cream-shrimp soup. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a soup — save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Shrimp Cocktail": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness fights the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Swordfish": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|The Tomahawk": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with the Tomahawk — luxury Irish crossover against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; even luxury Irish is too light for this cut. Save the collector bottle — reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Tomato Basil": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness crowds the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the bourbon has no acid to balance the broth's savory base. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Truffle Fries": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with truffle fries — Kentucky Owl luxury Irish against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Kentucky Owl's distinctive bourbon-cask Irish character meets the earthy-rich at collector register.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's for the steak course.",
  "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's Irish whiskey smoothness clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- spirit warmth and the broth's salt-and-spice frame refuse each other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Asparagus": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the Rhone-blend body wraps the asparagus, and the peppery edge frames the side. Works; the side-salad call holds.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The pepper cuts gruyere-cream richness and the medium body handles dairy weight. Works for au gratin — Muga Reserva's vanilla-leather plays with the cheese harder.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Beignets": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Bone Marrow": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register leans against the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness: the Rhone-blend body wraps the rich marrow, and the peppery edge frames the starter. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Bone-In Filet": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Broccoli Cheddar": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the Rhone-blend body wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the peppery edge frames the bowl. Works; the close holds, neither soars nor fights.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Broccolini": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the broccolini's tender green — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Brussels and Belly": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The pepper cuts pork-belly fat and the garrigue echoes brussels-char. Strong Southern-French for brussels-and-belly — Rhône savory polish on a fat-and-char side.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Burrata": "Never pair Kermit Lynch Rhône with burrata — garrigue and pepper clash with fresh cream and the wild herbs fight tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Butternut Squash": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the Rhone-blend body wraps the squash bisque, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Carrot Cake": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône for the steak course.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Cheesecake": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Chilean Seabass": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône for the steak course.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Chocolate Brownie": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône for the steak course.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Chocolate Cake": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône for the steak course.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Clam Chowder": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Cowboy Ribeye": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The medium body is slightly light for Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in ribeye, but the pepper matches grilled char. Works for ribeye — Grand Tinel's Châteauneuf full-body handles the cut harder.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Crab Cake": "Never pair Kermit Lynch Rhône with crab cake — wild herbs and pepper overwhelm delicate lump crab and the garrigue bury the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Creamed Spinach": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The medium body handles dairy-heavy cream and the wild herbs lift spinach-iron. Strong Southern-French for creamed spinach — a gentle Rhône counterpoint to the rich side.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Creamy Potato": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the potato soup's silky body — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Creme Brulee": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Escargot": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Faroe Island Salmon": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The medium body handles salmon's oil-richness and the red cherry lifts against flesh-sweetness. Peak Southern-French for salmon — preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen for today's build. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Filet Mignon": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The medium body matches butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the wild herbs play against escargot-butter. Strong Southern-French for filet — a lighter-touch Rhône pairing.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|French Onion": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the rye spice cuts the cheese richness. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Grilled Caesar": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The wild herbs match anchovy-lemon dressing and the pepper cuts through parm. Peak Southern-French for grilled caesar — Kermit Lynch food-wine handles the salad beautifully. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Gumbo": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the pepper amplifies the spice. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin finds neutral with the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|House Wedge": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The garrigue plays with blue-cheese funk and the pepper matches bacon-fat char. Strong Southern-French for wedge — medium body handles iceberg-cold without muting.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Kansas City": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The pepper note echoes Kansas City's lean-bold beef char and the wild herbs lift against grilled strip. Peak Southern-French for the strip — a mid-weight garrigue match. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Loaded Potato": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Lobster Mac": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Market Fish": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône belongs on the steak course.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Mocha Creme": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Mushroom Bisque": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Mushrooms": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The garrigue savory-finish echoes roasted-mushroom glutamate and the pepper note plays with butter. Peak Southern-French for mushrooms — Rhône garrigue-on-earth match. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône for the steak course.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Porterhouse": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. Medium body is slightly light for Porterhouse's dual-cut heft, but wild herbs play against grilled beef. Works for Porterhouse — Tempier Bandol or Scavino Barolo handles the cut harder.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Prime Tartare": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Rainbow Trout": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône for the steak course.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Roast Half Chicken": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch selection, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body, food-friendly. The wild herbs echo chicken-jus herbaceousness and medium body won't overwhelm poultry. Peak Southern-French for the bird — preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen for today's build. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the pepper amplifies the spice. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône on the steak course instead.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. The wild herbs echo garlic-oil aromatics and the medium body matches spinach-iron. Strong Southern-French for garlic spinach — Rhône herb-on-herb harmony.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Seafood Tower": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône belongs on the steak course.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Kermit Lynch Rhône with seared scallops — pepper and garrigue overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and the medium body clashes with browned-butter. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Seasonal Soup": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Seasonal Vegetables": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Shrimp Bisque": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin stays alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Kermit Lynch Rhône with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by pepper and garrigue, and the red-fruit has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Swordfish": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône on the steak course instead.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|The Tomahawk": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Tomato Basil": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Truffle Fries": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône on the steak course instead.",
  "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin holds with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the pepper amplifies the spice. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Asparagus": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality sits beside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Beignets": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a vodka. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Ketel One Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Bone Marrow": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality finds neutral with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Bone-In Filet": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- clean vodka reads thin against the cut's weight — needs a wine or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Ketel One Vodka for another course.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Broccoli Cheddar": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Broccolini": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality plays cleanly against the broccolini's tender green — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Brussels and Belly": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality plays cleanly against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Burrata": "Ketel One with burrata — citrus-clean Dutch wheat against fresh dairy. Excellent; Ketel's finish adds lift to the cream where softer vodkas just sit.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Butternut Squash": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Carrot Cake": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Ketel One Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Cheesecake": "Ketel One with cheesecake — Dutch wheat against custard. Works; Ketel's citrus threads the cheesecake cleaner than a fully-round vodka does.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Chilean Seabass": "Ketel One Vodka's clean cold-distilled body matches register with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the neutral-spirit weight handles the seabass, and the silky neutrality lifts the plate. Strong; Ketel handles the tower cleanly, though Goose's luxury softness wins the flagship pairing.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Chocolate Brownie": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Ketel One Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Chocolate Cake": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Ketel One Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Clam Chowder": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Cowboy Ribeye": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Ketel One Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Crab Cake": "Ketel One with crab cake — Dutch wheat against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Ketel's citrus-clean finish brightens the crab at a precise register.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Creamed Spinach": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Creamy Potato": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Creme Brulee": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Ketel One Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Escargot": "Ketel One with escargot — crisp Dutch wheat against garlic-butter snails. Strong; Ketel's citrus lifts the butter notes at precise register.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Faroe Island Salmon": "Ketel One with the salmon — Dutch wheat against oily salmon. Strong; Ketel's citrus brightens the fish where softer vodkas just accompany.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Filet Mignon": "Ketel One with the filet — pot-and-column Dutch wheat against tenderloin. Works; vodka over-specified for filet; Ketel's precision doesn't clash.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|French Onion": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality stays alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Grilled Caesar": "Ketel One with the grilled Caesar — pot-and-column Dutch wheat against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Ketel's citrus-floral finish is the distinctive vodka-Caesar match — it lifts the char rather than rounding it (Tito's) or softening it (Goose).",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Gumbo": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality reads alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality plays cleanly against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|House Wedge": "Ketel One with the House Wedge — Dutch wheat against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; the citrus finish brightens the wedge with precision.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Kansas City": "Ketel One with the KC — Dutch wheat against an 18oz strip. Avoid; vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Loaded Potato": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Lobster Mac": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Market Fish": "Ketel One with the market fish — crisp Nolet wheat against featured fish. Works; Ketel's citrus handles most fish preparations cleanly.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Mocha Creme": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a vodka. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Ketel One Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Mushroom Bisque": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality plays cleanly against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Mushrooms": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality reads alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Ketel One Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Porterhouse": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Ketel One Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Prime Tartare": "Ketel One with the tartare — Dutch wheat against raw beef. Strong; Ketel's citrus is a cleaner lift than Tito's round neutrality, though Belvedere's rye pepper still features.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Rainbow Trout": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality matches the trout's delicate flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Roast Half Chicken": "Ketel One with the chicken — clean Dutch wheat against roasted poultry. Works; Ketel's citrus is a brighter chicken companion than Tito's neutrality.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality stays alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Ketel One Vodka's neutral vodka register leans against the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the unflavored body sits with the halibut, and the crisp profile carries the plate. Strong; Ketel handles the tower cleanly, though Goose's luxury softness wins the flagship pairing.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality reads alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Seafood Tower": "Ketel One with the seafood tower — pot-and-column Dutch wheat against the raw bar. Strong; Ketel handles the tower cleanly, though Goose's luxury softness wins the flagship pairing.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Seared Scallops": "Ketel One with seared scallops — clean Nolet-family wheat against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Ketel's crispness frames the scallop cleanly — neither rounded nor silky, just precise.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Seasonal Soup": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Seasonal Vegetables": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality stays alongside the seasonal garden plate — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Shrimp Bisque": "Ketel One with shrimp bisque — clean Dutch wheat against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Ketel's citrus cuts the bisque's richness rather than riding it.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Shrimp Cocktail": "Ketel One with shrimp cocktail — Dutch wheat from the Nolet family distillery. Excellent; Ketel's clean citrus-floral finish lifts cold shrimp with Dutch-bar precision — where Tito's rounds and Goose softens, Ketel crisps.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Swordfish": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality holds steady against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|The Tomahawk": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Ketel One Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Tomato Basil": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality reads alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Truffle Fries": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality plays cleanly against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality holds steady against the tuna's seared rare crust — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Ketel One Vodka|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Ketel One Vodka's Dutch-wheat-crisp neutrality leans against the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Asparagus": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals sits alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Beignets": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- gin's herbal edge has no foil against warm pastry sugar — sharp meets sweet, neither wins. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Knickerbocker Gin for the steak course.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Bone Marrow": "Knickerbocker with bone marrow — brewer's-heritage gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Knickerbocker Gin for another course.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Broccolini": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals leans against the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Burrata": "Knickerbocker with burrata — New Holland Artisan Spirits (Holland, MI), hop-influenced gin with brewer's-heritage character. Excellent; Knickerbocker's hop-botanical profile complements burrata with a register no other Local MI Gin touches — brewer's heritage meets cream.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Butternut Squash": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Carrot Cake": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- juniper-citrus and warm cake spices pull in opposite directions. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Knickerbocker Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Cheesecake": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Knickerbocker Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Knickerbocker Gin's gin lift with floral-and-pepper sits at register with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the bright botanical edge handles the seabass, and the floral-and-herb register brightens the plate. Strong; Knickerbocker's brewer's-heritage character is a story-driven tower pairing.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Knickerbocker Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Knickerbocker Gin for the steak course.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Clam Chowder": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Knickerbocker Gin for another course.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Crab Cake": "Knickerbocker with crab cake — hop-influenced Holland MI gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Knickerbocker's brewer's-heritage character adds distinctive herbal-hop dimension to crab.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Creamy Potato": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals sits beside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Creme Brulee": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Knickerbocker Gin for the steak course.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Escargot": "Knickerbocker with escargot — hop-influenced gin against garlic-butter snails. Strong; Knickerbocker's bitter-hop notes meet butter distinctively; Eastern Kille's classic London Dry is still the featured Local MI Gin-escargot match.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Knickerbocker with the salmon — brewer's-heritage gin against oily salmon. Strong; Knickerbocker's hop-botanical lift frames the fish at distinctive register; Iron Fish's farm profile is still the featured Local MI Gin-salmon match.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Filet Mignon": "Knickerbocker with the filet — hop-influenced Holland MI gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|French Onion": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: charred romaine and anchovy meet hop-influenced Holland MI gin. Knickerbocker's hop-botanical character is the featured Local MI Gin-Caesar pairing — where the others treat this as Works, Knickerbocker's brewer-heritage character is built for char-and-bitter resonance like its parent New Holland beers.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Gumbo": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals sits beside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|House Wedge": "Knickerbocker with the House Wedge — hop-influenced gin against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Knickerbocker's brewer's-heritage bitter-hop meets bleu with distinctive character (demoted slightly from other Local MI Gins' Excellent tier because the hop-bitter adds tension the classic juniper profiles avoid).",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Kansas City": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Knickerbocker Gin for another course.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Loaded Potato": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Lobster Mac": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Market Fish": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals sits with the fish's clean flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Mocha Creme": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Knickerbocker Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals reads alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Mushrooms": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals sits alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Knickerbocker Gin for the steak course.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Porterhouse": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Knickerbocker Gin for another course.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Prime Tartare": "Knickerbocker with the tartare — brewer's-heritage Holland MI gin against raw beef. Strong; Knickerbocker's hop-botanical character supports tartare with distinctive lift.",
  "Knickerbocker Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Knickerbocker Gin's bold citrus-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the trout's delicate flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
