  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Market Fish": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye for the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Mocha Creme": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the dry rye body wraps the mocha creme, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; Voyage 23 frames the cut at ocean-finish register.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Mushroom Bisque": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the rye-and-oak weight handles the mushroom bisque, and the oak threads the umami plate. Works; Portwood is over-specified for sides.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Mushrooms": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with the mushrooms — blended 100-proof against earthy umami. The rye brightness in the blend cuts mushroom depth differently than pure bourbon does. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye-and-oak weight handles the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Porterhouse": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with the porterhouse — blend covers both strip and filet sides. Strong; the bourbon-rye layering handles both cuts with distinctive character.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Prime Tartare": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the rye spice cuts the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; Fusion complexity is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character for this cut; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Rainbow Trout": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the roast chicken, and the rye edge frames the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the rye spice cuts the chickpea soup, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; annual-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye spice cuts the garlic spinach, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Seafood Tower": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye for the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Seared Scallops": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against blended depth. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Seasonal Soup": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the rye spice cuts the seasonal soup, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; richer profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye spice cuts the seasonal vegetables, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the side. Works; AE CS meets the char at premium wine-finish register.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood against blended bourbon-rye. Works; 100-proof complexity sits beside the soup.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against blend. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Swordfish": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|The Tomahawk": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's bold spice character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Tomato Basil": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the high-rye spice register matches the tomato basil, and the rye-grain bite cuts the bowl. Works; premium KO story wasted on salad.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Truffle Fries": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with truffle fries — the blend's spicy-sweet against truffle-parmesan. A distinctive side pairing that shows what the bourbon-rye blend does with earth. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye for the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the rye-and-oak weight handles the curry, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Asparagus": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the asparagus, and the rye-pepper note underlines the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Knob Creek Rye with au gratin potatoes — 100-proof rye against cheese-and-cream. Works; bold rye slightly over-specified.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Beignets": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the rye-and-oak weight handles the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Bone Marrow": "Knob Creek Rye with bone marrow — 100-proof bold rye against umami fat. The high-proof Beam rye meets marrow's intensity with authority; one of the strongest rye-marrow pairings. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Bone-In Filet": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the rye spice cuts the bone-enhanced filet, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the cut. Strong; GTS threads mushrooms at collector-BTAC register.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Broccoli Cheddar": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the rye spice cuts the broccoli cheddar, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; a gentle composed match at the premium tier.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Broccolini": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the rye backbone drives the broccolini, and the rye-grain bite cuts the side. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Brussels and Belly": "Knob Creek Rye with Brussels + Belly — 100-proof Beam rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; bold rye cuts the glaze with extra-proof authority.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Burrata": "Knob Creek Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against 100-proof bold rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Butternut Squash": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the dry rye body wraps the squash bisque, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Carrot Cake": "Knob Creek Rye with carrot cake — 100-proof Beam rye against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; bold rye spice and cake spice find each other.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Cheesecake": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the high-rye spice register matches the cheesecake, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; OGD 16's rye-spice meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Chilean Seabass": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Knob Creek Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Chocolate Brownie": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the dry rye body wraps the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the Boss Hog intensity meets the brownie at unicorn register.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Chocolate Cake": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rye backbone drives the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; KBS distinctiveness is slightly over-specified for the two-cut plate.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Clam Chowder": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the high-rye spice register matches the chowder, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the pour's assertiveness is a bit much for the soup but doesn't clash.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Cowboy Ribeye": "Knob Creek Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof Beam rye against cap fat. The extra proof and bold rye spice handle the ribeye where Sazerac Rye and Michter's fade; the rye-side answer to Knob Creek 12 Year Bourbon's approach to this cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Crab Cake": "Knob Creek Rye with crab cake — 100-proof bold rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Creamed Spinach": "Knob Creek Rye with creamed spinach — 100-proof rye against dairy. Strong; bold rye sits with richness at high-impact register.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Creamy Potato": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rye backbone drives the potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Creme Brulee": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the high-rye spice register matches the brulee, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the private select's layered profile underserves the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Escargot": "Knob Creek Rye's bold caramel-citrus character holds the line with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Faroe Island Salmon": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Knob Creek Rye for the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Filet Mignon": "Knob Creek Rye with the filet — 100-proof Beam rye against the 10oz cut. The bold character gives the filet more rye grip than benchmark ryes bring; a premium-impact Beam rye for filet orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|French Onion": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the French onion, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Grilled Caesar": "Knob Creek Rye with the grilled Caesar — 100-proof bold rye against charred romaine. The Beam intensity and anchovy find each other with premium grip. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Gumbo": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the dry rye body wraps the gumbo, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; collector-bottle doesn't drive.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rye-and-oak weight handles the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; Voyage 23 frames the cut at ocean-finish register.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|House Wedge": "Knob Creek Rye with the House Wedge — 100-proof Beam rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; bold rye cuts the bleu with extra-proof intensity.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Kansas City": "Knob Creek Rye with the KC — Beam's 100-proof bold rye, sibling to Knob Creek Bourbon. The extra proof and bold rye spice carry the strip where softer ryes (Sazerac, Michter's) only hit strong; the high-impact rye for strip orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Loaded Potato": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the rye spice cuts the loaded potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Lobster Mac": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the dry rye body wraps the lobster mac, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; 100-proof complexity sits beside the soup.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Market Fish": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Knob Creek Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Mocha Creme": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye backbone drives the mocha creme, and the oak threads the cream. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Mushroom Bisque": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the high-rye spice register matches the mushroom bisque, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Works; the bourbon-and-side match reads as a controlled companion.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Mushrooms": "Knob Creek Rye with the mushrooms — 100-proof rye against earthy umami. Strong; bold rye layers into mushroom depth with Beam authority.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; no driver pairing.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Porterhouse": "Knob Creek Rye with the porterhouse — 100-proof bold rye across strip and filet. Strong; Beam intensity leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Prime Tartare": "Knob Creek Rye with the tartare — 100-proof Beam rye against raw beef. The bold rye character is a premium counterpoint to tartare; more intense than Sazerac Rye's benchmark. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Rainbow Trout": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Knob Creek Rye for the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the rye-and-oak weight handles the roast chicken, and the rye-pepper note underlines the plate. Works; curated single-barrel slightly over-specified.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the rye-and-oak weight handles the chickpea soup, and the rye-grain bite cuts the bowl. Works; wine-cask complexity gives the dessert layered premium character.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Knob Creek Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the high-rye spice register matches the garlic spinach, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; high proof over-specified for the soup.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Seafood Tower": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Knob Creek Rye for the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Seared Scallops": "Knob Creek Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 100-proof rye. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Seasonal Soup": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the rye spice cuts the seasonal soup, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye spice cuts the seasonal vegetables, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "Knob Creek Rye with shrimp bisque — 100-proof rye against cream-based seafood. Works; bold character slightly over-specified for the soup.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "Knob Creek Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against 100-proof Beam rye. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Swordfish": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Knob Creek Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|The Tomahawk": "Knob Creek Rye -- Beam's 100-proof bold rye, sibling to Knob Creek Bourbon. The rye spice cuts the 36oz showpiece, and the rye edge frames the cut. Strong; premium proof sits with richness at bold register.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Tomato Basil": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the tomato basil, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Truffle Fries": "Knob Creek Rye with truffle fries — 100-proof Beam rye against truffle-parmesan. The bold rye cuts through the richness with Beam authority. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Knob Creek Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Knob Creek Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the high-rye spice register matches the curry, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; Old Fitz 9 brings more depth here.",
  "Knob Creek|Asparagus": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the 100-proof depth handles the asparagus, and the vanilla rounds the side. Works; Boss Hog's allocation story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Knob Creek|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Knob Creek with au gratin potatoes — bold high-rye against cheese-and-cream. Strong; the 100-proof handles the side's richness cleanly.",
  "Knob Creek|Beignets": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon weight handles the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Knob Creek|Bone Marrow": "Knob Creek with bone marrow — 100-proof nutty bourbon against umami fat. The extra proof and high-rye body carry the marrow with real weight; a bolder call than the Wellers' wheated softness brings. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Knob Creek|Bone-In Filet": "Knob Creek's bold caramel-spice character frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth supports the lean cut without overpowering, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Knob Creek|Broccoli Cheddar": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon depth settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the light body doesn't overshoot the salad.",
  "Knob Creek|Broccolini": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the oak depth lays under the broccolini, and the oak frames the side. Works; retailer-pick wasted on salad.",
  "Knob Creek|Brussels and Belly": "Knob Creek with Brussels + Belly — 100-proof high-rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. The rye spice cuts the glaze's sweetness in a way wheated can't; a bolder small-plate pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Knob Creek|Burrata": "Knob Creek with burrata — fresh dairy against high-rye 100 proof. Avoid; register mismatch, hard.",
  "Knob Creek|Butternut Squash": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the caramel-spice register matches the squash bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; complexity wasted on salad.",
  "Knob Creek|Carrot Cake": "Knob Creek with carrot cake — 100-proof high-rye spice against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; the rye spice and cake spice find each other in a way wheated caramel can't.",
  "Knob Creek|Cheesecake": "Knob Creek with cheesecake — high-rye bourbon against clean custard. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for the dessert's register.",
  "Knob Creek|Chilean Seabass": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Knob Creek for the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek|Chocolate Brownie": "Knob Creek with the chocolate brownie — 100-proof high-rye against cocoa. Works; for more cocoa interplay, reach for Weller 107 (wheated) or Basil Hayden's Toast (toast-barrel).",
  "Knob Creek|Chocolate Cake": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the oak depth lays under the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Knob Creek|Clam Chowder": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the brown-spirit warmth threads the chowder, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the toast notes don't really interplay with the soup but don't clash.",
  "Knob Creek|Cowboy Ribeye": "Knob Creek with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof high-rye against cap fat and char. The proof earns the cut where the Wellers' 90-proof fades; Knob is the high-rye workhorse for the biggest cuts on the menu. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Knob Creek|Crab Cake": "Knob Creek with crab cake — 100-proof high-rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Knob Creek|Creamed Spinach": "Knob Creek with creamed spinach — 100-proof nutty high-rye against dairy. Strong; the nutty oak gives the side a dimension the Wellers' wheated caramel can't.",
  "Knob Creek|Creamy Potato": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon body sits with the potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; grain notes don't really engage the soup but don't clash.",
  "Knob Creek|Creme Brulee": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the bourbon-and-side match holds — neither soars nor fights.",
  "Knob Creek|Escargot": "Knob Creek's bold caramel-spice character frames the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Knob Creek|Faroe Island Salmon": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Knob Creek for the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek|Filet Mignon": "Knob Creek with the filet — 100 proof, high-rye, nutty, full-bodied. The extra proof and nutty-oak character give the filet a pour that stands beside it with weight the Wellers' 90-proof softness can't. Distinct from wheated — more spice, more proof, more nutty oak. The Beam-side premium for filet. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Knob Creek|French Onion": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the French onion, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Knob Creek|Grilled Caesar": "Knob Creek with the grilled Caesar — nutty high-rye against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; the 100-proof earns the Caesar more than Weller Special Reserve or Buffalo Trace bring.",
  "Knob Creek|Gumbo": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon body sits with the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; annual-release complexity over-specified.",
  "Knob Creek|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the brown-spirit warmth threads the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Knob Creek|House Wedge": "Knob Creek with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against 100 proof. Works only as a separate sip; the Knob's boldness overpowers the salad.",
  "Knob Creek|Kansas City": "Knob Creek with the KC — 100-proof high-rye against the strip's marbling. Nutty oak and bold bourbon handle the char cleanly; distinct from wheated approaches — more grip, more heat, more character. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Knob Creek|Loaded Potato": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Knob Creek|Lobster Mac": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon body sits with the lobster mac, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the complex profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Knob Creek|Market Fish": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Knob Creek for the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek|Mocha Creme": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon body sits with the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Knob Creek|Mushroom Bisque": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Knob Creek|Mushrooms": "Knob Creek with the mushrooms — 100-proof high-rye and nutty depth against earthy umami. The boldness meets the side's weight; a full-bodied side pairing distinct from wheated takes. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Knob Creek|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; gentle match.",
  "Knob Creek|Porterhouse": "Knob Creek with the porterhouse — 100-proof high-rye across strip and filet. The strip side takes the boldness beautifully; the filet side is a touch over-matched. Strong; the Wellers are lighter for filet, Knob is the better strip-side call.",
  "Knob Creek|Prime Tartare": "Knob Creek -- 100-proof, oak, vanilla. The oak depth lays under the tartare, and the oak threads the umami plate. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Knob Creek|Rainbow Trout": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Knob Creek lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Knob Creek|Roast Half Chicken": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon backbone carries the roast chicken, and the caramel rounds the plate. Works; blend character covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Knob Creek|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon weight handles the chickpea soup, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; not revelatory but well-matched registers.",
  "Knob Creek|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Knob Creek for the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon backbone carries the garlic spinach, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; the bourbon's character is distinctive but the pairing isn't a standout.",
  "Knob Creek|Seafood Tower": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Knob Creek for the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek|Seared Scallops": "Knob Creek with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 100-proof bourbon. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Knob Creek|Seasonal Soup": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon weight handles the seasonal soup, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Knob Creek|Seasonal Vegetables": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the oak depth lays under the seasonal vegetables, and the oak frames the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Knob Creek|Shrimp Bisque": "Knob Creek with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood against 100-proof high-rye. Works; the boldness pushes slightly past what the soup asks for.",
  "Knob Creek|Shrimp Cocktail": "Knob Creek with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against bold high-rye. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Knob Creek|Swordfish": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Knob Creek for the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek|The Tomahawk": "Knob Creek's bold caramel-spice character finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Knob Creek|Tomato Basil": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon backbone carries the tomato basil, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for the side — save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Knob Creek|Truffle Fries": "Knob Creek with truffle fries — nutty oak and high-rye spice against truffle-parmesan. Knob's character gives the side more grip than wheated pours bring; a bolder everyday-premium side pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Knob Creek|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Knob Creek belongs on the steak course.",
  "Knob Creek|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Knob Creek's oak-and-vanilla 100-proof depth leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon backbone carries the curry, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; BH Red Wine Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Komos Anejo Reserva|Asparagus": "Komos Anejo Reserva's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak-aged tequila body threads the asparagus, and the oak threads the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Komos Anejo Reserva|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: cheese-and-cream potatoes meet Komos Anejo Reserva. Works; multi-cask complexity handles richness at luxury register.",
  "Komos Anejo Reserva|Beignets": "Komos Anejo Reserva's rested-agave body sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the añejo register carries the beignets, and the soft-spice note softens the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Komos Anejo Reserva|Bone Marrow": "Komos Anejo Reserva's wine-finish-caramel character frames the marrow's umami-fat depth — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Komos Anejo Reserva|Bone-In Filet": "Komos Anejo Reserva's wine-finish-caramel character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-tequila depth carries the cut, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Komos Anejo Reserva|Broccoli Cheddar": "Komos Anejo Reserva's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the aged-agave weight wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; accessible match.",
  "Komos Anejo Reserva|Broccolini": "Komos Anejo Reserva's aged-agave character finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the aged-agave weight wraps the broccolini, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the side. Works; extra añejo depth composed with this dish.",
  "Komos Anejo Reserva|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed meets Komos Anejo Reserva. Excellent; the wine-cask-distinctive character rides the maple glaze at luxury register — wine-cask depth amplifies glaze.",
  "Komos Anejo Reserva|Burrata": "Komos Anejo Reserva's aged-agave character overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Komos Anejo Reserva belongs on the steak course.",
  "Komos Anejo Reserva|Butternut Squash": "Komos Anejo Reserva's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the aged-agave weight wraps the squash bisque, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; approachable agave is a gentle counterpoint.",
  "Komos Anejo Reserva|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Komos Anejo Reserva. Strong; wine-cask-finished French-oak amplifies cake spice at luxury register.",
