  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Chilean Seabass": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée on the steak course instead.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Chocolate Brownie": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Chocolate Cake": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée belongs on the steak course.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Clam Chowder": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's bold floral-oak cassis finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Cowboy Ribeye": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin, full body. The firm tannin handles ribeye's big marbled build and the cassis-graphite plays against beefy char. Peak historically-significant Napa Cab for ribeye — Daniel Cuvée named for Robin Lail's father, Mondavi-Inglenook architect. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Crab Cake": "Historically-significant Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Daniel Cuvée's firm-tannin full-body structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Daniel Cuvée for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Creamed Spinach": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the cassis plays against the spinach base. Strong historically-significant Napa Cab for creamed spinach — Daniel Cuvée structure reframes the rich side.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Creamy Potato": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's bold floral-oak cassis holds with the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Creme Brulee": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée on the steak course instead.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Escargot": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's bold floral-oak cassis frames the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the concentrated body grips the savory plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Faroe Island Salmon": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Filet Mignon": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin, full body. The firm tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the dried violet plays against escargot-butter. Peak historically-significant Napa Cab for filet — Lail family pedigree, Mondavi-Inglenook architect's legacy. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|French Onion": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's bold floral-oak cassis leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Grilled Caesar": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the graphite plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as historically-significant Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — Daniel Cuvée structure on the layered salad.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Gumbo": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's bold floral-oak cassis holds with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's bold floral-oak cassis finds neutral with the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|House Wedge": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the cassis plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as historically-significant Napa Cab for the wedge — Daniel Cuvée pedigree reframes the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Kansas City": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the graphite plays against beefy char. Strong historically-significant Napa Cab for the strip — Daniel Cuvée pedigree on the American cut.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Loaded Potato": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's firm cassis-and-cocoa tannin reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the structured body wraps the loaded potato soup, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Lobster Mac": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's dense blackberry-and-cassis tannin leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the structured body wraps the lobster mac, and the cassis frames the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Market Fish": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Mocha Creme": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Mushroom Bisque": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's bold floral-oak cassis sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Mushrooms": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin. The graphite-earth echoes roasted-mushroom glutamate and the dried violet plays against the earth depth. Strong historically-significant Napa Cab for mushrooms — Daniel Cuvée structure on the earthy side.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée on the steak course instead.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Porterhouse": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the cassis plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong historically-significant Napa Cab for Porterhouse — Daniel Cuvée pedigree on the dual-cut classic.",
