  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Roast Half Chicken": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured dark-fruit weight finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the structured body wraps the roast chicken, and the cassis frames the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's bold floral-oak cassis reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the caramel rounds the earthy plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's bold floral-oak cassis plays cleanly against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Seafood Tower": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée for the steak course.",
  "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée|Seared Scallops": "Historically-significant Napa Cab against scallop sweetness — Daniel Cuvée's firm-tannin graphite-cassis structure buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Daniel Cuvée for the steak course.",
