  "Lalo Silver|Swordfish": "Lalo Silver's crisp agave-citrus character matches the swordfish's meaty flesh — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Lalo Silver|The Tomahawk": "Lalo Silver with the Tomahawk — craft blanco against bone-in ribeye. Avoid; bourbon or scotch territory.",
  "Lalo Silver|Tomato Basil": "Lalo Silver's green-agave-and-cane lift reads alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the blanco-tequila body matches the tomato basil, and the unaged-cane note plays against the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Lalo Silver|Truffle Fries": "Lalo Silver's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register leans against the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight: the silver-spirit register lifts the truffle fries, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Lalo Silver|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Lalo Silver's crisp agave-citrus character matches the tuna's seared rare crust — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Lalo Silver|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Lalo Silver's green-agave-and-cane lift leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the blanco-tequila body matches the curry, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Asparagus": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the asparagus, and the honey rounds the side. Works; the private select's layered profile underserves the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat finds neutral with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the malt-and-oak threads the au gratin, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; aging wasted on the salad.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Beignets": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the scotch backbone matches the beignets, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; the pour's assertiveness is a bit much for the soup but doesn't clash.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Bone Marrow": "Laphroaig 10 with bone marrow — medicinal iodine, tar, and band-aid TCP meet the marrow's fat in a pairing that's either heaven or a hard no. Nothing subtle about it. Pour for guests who know what they're asking for; the most decisive peated-Islay-and-fat pairing on the menu. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Bone-In Filet": "Laphroaig 10's bold peat-iodine character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Broccoli Cheddar": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the malt-and-oak threads the broccoli cheddar, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Broccolini": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the Highland register carries the broccolini, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Brussels and Belly": "Laphroaig 10 with Brussels + Belly — the medicinal edge slices through the pork belly's maple-glazed fat like nothing else on the spirit list. Not for every guest; for the right guest, unforgettable. The other two Islays are more approachable; Laphroaig is the statement. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Burrata": "Laphroaig 10 with burrata — fresh dairy cream and basil oil get annihilated by Laphroaig's tar and band-aid TCP. Hard avoid; don't let a guest try this. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Butternut Squash": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the scotch depth settles into the squash bisque, and the oak threads the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Carrot Cake": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat finds neutral with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the malt-driven body wraps the carrot cake, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; curated single-barrel covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Cheesecake": "Cheesecake: clean custard delicacy against medicinal peat is a pure mismatch. The dram flattens everything about the dessert. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Chilean Seabass": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Laphroaig 10 on the steak course instead.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: the warm, espresso-deep cocoa and dense fudge texture meet Laphroaig 10's iodine, tar, and medicinal finish in the most decisive peated-dessert pairing on the menu. The brownie's bitter-sweet finds its match in Laphroaig's medicinal edge. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Chocolate Cake": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the Highland register carries the chocolate cake, and the malt edge frames the dessert. Works; sweet character distinctive but not driving.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Clam Chowder": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the chowder, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Portwood is over-specified for a side — save for main course.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: the cut's marbling and char meet Laphroaig 10's tar, iodine, and phenolic punch. Medicinal brine against cap fat works at full intensity — the uncompromising Islay pour for the ribeye. Less transcendent than with the Tomahawk (where the scale is bigger) but still fully aligned. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Crab Cake": "Laphroaig 10 with crab cake — medicinal iodine against delicate crab is a complete mismatch. The dram incinerates the starter. Hard avoid; Laphroaig is the wrong lane for anything this delicate. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Creamed Spinach": "Laphroaig 10 with creamed spinach — medicinal peat crushes the dairy-forward side. Capable only; the side can't carry the dram's weight. Reach for Bowmore 12 if pairing peated Islay with this side is the goal. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Creamy Potato": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the malt-and-oak threads the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Creme Brulee": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch backbone matches the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Escargot": "Laphroaig 10's bold peat-iodine character lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Faroe Island Salmon": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Laphroaig 10 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: the restraint of the 10oz center-cut meets Laphroaig 10's medicinal intensity. The peat and iodine overpower the filet's subtlety. Capable only; reach for Bowmore 12 instead when a guest asks for peat with a filet. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Laphroaig 10|French Onion": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the scotch backbone matches the French onion, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; the pour's refinement is underused by the salad.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Grilled Caesar": "Laphroaig 10 with the grilled Caesar — the medicinal intensity risks overrunning the Caesar's finesse. Capable, but Lagavulin 8 or Bowmore 12 are the better Islay calls here. Pour Laphroaig only if the guest has specifically asked. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Gumbo": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the malt-and-oak threads the gumbo, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; pour is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the malt-driven body wraps the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "Laphroaig 10|House Wedge": "Laphroaig 10 with the House Wedge — the wedge's crisp iceberg and bleu dressing are completely overwhelmed by Laphroaig's intensity. Capable only as a separate drink alongside; not a match. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Kansas City": "Kansas City: the strip's char and marbling meet Laphroaig 10's medicinal weight. Strip marbling takes a harder peated pour than the filet does; Laphroaig's intensity gives the char somewhere to go. Less transcendent than with the Tomahawk (the scale is smaller) but fully aligned. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Loaded Potato": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the Highland register carries the loaded potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Lobster Mac": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the scotch depth settles into the lobster mac, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; milestone-release doesn't drive.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Market Fish": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Laphroaig 10 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Mocha Creme": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the Highland register carries the mocha creme, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; doesn't drive.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Mushroom Bisque": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the malt-driven body wraps the mushroom bisque, and the oak threads the umami plate. Works; smooth character is slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Mushrooms": "Laphroaig 10 with the mushrooms — iodine and tar against earthy umami. The mushrooms have enough body to stand up to Laphroaig's intensity where lighter sides don't. Strong but aggressive; less versatile than Lagavulin 8 or Bowmore 12 here.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the PB brownie, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; novelty maturation at matched register.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Porterhouse": "Laphroaig 10 with the porterhouse — the strip side takes Laphroaig's medicinal punch head-on; the filet side tolerates less but holds. A bold pour for the cut; the dram is never going to recede into the background, and that's the point. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Prime Tartare": "Laphroaig 10 with the tartare — the tartare's delicacy can't carry Laphroaig's full phenolic weight. Capable only; the medicinal edge overwhelms the mineral beef depth. Pour Lagavulin 8 for the best Islay-tartare match. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Rainbow Trout": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Laphroaig 10 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Roast Half Chicken": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the roast chicken, and the honey rounds the plate. Works; Fortuna handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the scotch backbone matches the chickpea soup, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; save the BTAC bottle for the protein course — the side doesn't deserve the pour.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Laphroaig 10 for the steak course.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the garlic spinach, and the smoke threads the side. Works; high proof over-specified for the soup.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Seafood Tower": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Laphroaig 10 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Seared Scallops": "Laphroaig 10 with scallops — seared seafood's clean caramelized sweetness cannot survive Laphroaig's phenolic wall. Absolute miss; avoid. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Seasonal Soup": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the scotch backbone matches the seasonal soup, and the smoke threads the bowl. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Seasonal Vegetables": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the scotch depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; Vermont-brand character doesn't drive.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Shrimp Bisque": "Shrimp Bisque: the cream-base bisque meets Laphroaig 10's medicinal iodine. Too much; the dram's intensity flattens the soup's nuance. Capable only. For Islay-bisque pairings, Lagavulin 8 is balanced, Bowmore 12 is refined. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Shrimp Cocktail": "Laphroaig 10 with shrimp cocktail — iodine-on-iodine isn't the echo it sounds like; it's just too much. The shrimp gets erased. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Swordfish": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Laphroaig 10 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Laphroaig 10|The Tomahawk": "Laphroaig 10 alongside the Tomahawk — the full weight of Laphroaig's phenolic punch matches the full weight of the cut. Seaweed and iodine cut through marbling in a way no other peated Islay touches. This is the Islay marquee with max-impact beef. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Tomato Basil": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the tomato basil, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; bonded weight pushes past the soup's register slightly.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Truffle Fries": "Laphroaig 10 with truffle fries — medicinal peat against truffle-parmesan fat. Capable, but Laphroaig's intensity competes with the truffle rather than complementing it. Lagavulin 8 or Bowmore 12 are better Islay calls for this side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Laphroaig 10 for the steak course.",
  "Laphroaig 10|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Laphroaig 10's classic Islay peat sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the malt-driven body wraps the curry, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; no driver pairing.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Asparagus": "Larceny Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak depth lays under the asparagus, and the oak frames the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Larceny Small Batch with au gratin potatoes — 92-proof wheated against cheese-and-cream. Solid, composed match at the register. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Beignets": "Larceny Small Batch's Heaven Hill wheated caramel sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon depth settles into the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Bone Marrow": "Larceny Small Batch with bone marrow — Heaven Hill wheated caramel against the marrow's umami fat. Capable and approachable; Weller 12 is richer, Larceny is the friendly alternate. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Bone-In Filet": "Larceny Small Batch's bold caramel-honey character sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth supports the lean cut without overpowering, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Broccoli Cheddar": "Larceny Small Batch's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon weight handles the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; oak slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Broccolini": "Larceny Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon weight handles the broccolini, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet Larceny's 92-proof wheated. Clean everyday pairing; approachable without being underwhelming. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Burrata": "Larceny Small Batch with burrata — fresh dairy against wheated caramel. Avoid; register mismatch.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Butternut Squash": "Larceny Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon backbone carries the squash bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; milestone-edition over-specified.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Carrot Cake": "Larceny Small Batch with carrot cake — wheated honey against cream cheese frosting and cinnamon. Heaven Hill's honey-forward profile finds the cake's sweetness cleanly; a friendly dessert pairing. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Cheesecake": "Larceny Small Batch with cheesecake — wheated caramel against clean custard. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Chilean Seabass": "Larceny Small Batch's Heaven Hill wheated caramel clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Larceny Small Batch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Chocolate Brownie": "Larceny Small Batch with the chocolate brownie — Heaven Hill wheated against cocoa. Works; for more cocoa interplay, reach for Weller 107 or Weller Millennium.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Chocolate Cake": "Larceny Small Batch's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon backbone carries the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Clam Chowder": "Larceny Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon body sits with the chowder, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Cowboy Ribeye": "Larceny Small Batch with the Cowboy Ribeye — 92-proof wheated against cap fat and char. The extra 2 proof over Weller Special Reserve helps here; Larceny handles the ribeye a touch better than the softer wheated pours. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Crab Cake": "Larceny Small Batch with crab cake — wheated bourbon against delicate crab. Avoid; choose a crisp white wine.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Creamed Spinach": "Larceny Small Batch with creamed spinach — wheated caramel-honey against dairy richness. Matched registers; a straightforward, friendly pairing. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Creamy Potato": "Larceny Small Batch's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the bourbon-and-dessert match settles as a soft alongside.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Creme Brulee": "Larceny Small Batch's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive but adds interest.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Escargot": "Larceny Small Batch's bold caramel-honey character holds steady against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Faroe Island Salmon": "Larceny Small Batch's Heaven Hill wheated caramel clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Larceny Small Batch belongs on the steak course.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Filet Mignon": "Larceny Small Batch with the filet — Heaven Hill's wheated answer to the Weller family. 92 proof and caramel-honey-vanilla; a touch more body than Weller Special Reserve, a different distillery voice than Maker's. The approachable wheated alternate for filet, from the Heaven Hill side of the menu. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|French Onion": "Larceny Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the caramel-spice register matches the French onion, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; sweet character distinctive but not driving.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Grilled Caesar": "Larceny Small Batch with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against Heaven Hill wheated. Works; Larceny's honey notes earn a bit more of the Caesar than Weller Special Reserve brings.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Gumbo": "Larceny Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon backbone carries the gumbo, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Larceny Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon body sits with the honey carrots, and the oak threads the caramelized sugar. Works; the complex character is wasted on the salad.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|House Wedge": "Larceny Small Batch with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against wheated honey. Works as a before-course sip; not a driving pairing.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Kansas City": "Larceny Small Batch with the KC — 92-proof wheated against the strip's marbling. More body than Weller Special Reserve, more honey-forward than Maker's. Heaven Hill's everyday wheated for strip orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Loaded Potato": "Larceny Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Lobster Mac": "Larceny Small Batch's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon backbone carries the lobster mac, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Market Fish": "Larceny Small Batch's Heaven Hill wheated caramel overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Larceny Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Mocha Creme": "Larceny Small Batch's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon weight handles the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Mushroom Bisque": "Larceny Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the caramel-spice register matches the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th handles the richness at annual-release register.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Mushrooms": "Larceny Small Batch with the mushrooms — Heaven Hill wheated against earthy umami. The honey notes work with mushroom depth differently than the Wellers' oak-forward wheat does. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Larceny Small Batch's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon depth settles into the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; X Commandments story wasted on salad.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Porterhouse": "Larceny Small Batch with the porterhouse — wheated honey across both strip and filet sides. A versatile everyday pour; Heaven Hill's alternate to Weller Special Reserve for this plate. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Prime Tartare": "Larceny Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character sits at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon backbone carries the tartare, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Strong; smooth character covers both cuts cleanly; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Rainbow Trout": "Larceny Small Batch's Heaven Hill wheated caramel smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Larceny Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Roast Half Chicken": "Larceny Small Batch's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the caramel-spice register matches the roast chicken, and the oak frames the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Larceny Small Batch's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon body sits with the chickpea soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; light body doesn't overshoot.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Larceny Small Batch's Heaven Hill wheated caramel swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Larceny Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Larceny Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the caramel-spice register matches the garlic spinach, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; aged character overshoots delicacy.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Seafood Tower": "Larceny Small Batch's Heaven Hill wheated caramel fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Larceny Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Seared Scallops": "Larceny Small Batch with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against wheated. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Seasonal Soup": "Larceny Small Batch's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; own-plus-sourced covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Seasonal Vegetables": "Larceny Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the brown-spirit warmth threads the seasonal vegetables, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; bold proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Shrimp Bisque": "Larceny Small Batch with shrimp bisque — wheated honey against cream-based seafood. Works neutrally; doesn't drive.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Shrimp Cocktail": "Larceny Small Batch with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against wheated honey. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Swordfish": "Larceny Small Batch's Heaven Hill wheated caramel swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Larceny Small Batch on the steak course instead.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|The Tomahawk": "Larceny Small Batch's bold caramel-honey character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon character meets the rendered fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Tomato Basil": "Larceny Small Batch's Heaven Hill wheated caramel finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a soup; save the rare bottle.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Truffle Fries": "Larceny Small Batch with truffle fries — wheated caramel-honey against truffle-parmesan. The 92-proof body gives the fries more pour-weight than Maker's Mark brings; a clean, balanced side pairing. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Larceny Small Batch's Heaven Hill wheated caramel flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Larceny Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Larceny Small Batch|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Larceny Small Batch's Heaven Hill wheated caramel sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; AE CS's cask-strength character engages the bleu at premium register.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Asparagus": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity sits alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity reads alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Beignets": "Tours-sur-Marne Pinot Noir rosé Champagne — silky mousse, red berry, long finish. The strawberry-raspberry complements beignets' berry-jam filling directly and bubbles cut fried-dough weight. Strong Champagne-and-beignets pairing — the dessert mirrors the berry profile of the rosé.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Bone Marrow": "Pinot Noir rosé Champagne against marrow's rendered-fat richness — the silky strawberry profile is crushed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned and save the rosé for the raw bar or tartare opener.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Bone-In Filet": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé for another course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Broccoli Cheddar": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cream — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Broccolini": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity sits alongside the broccolini's tender green — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Brussels and Belly": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity plays cleanly against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Burrata": "Pinot Noir Champagne rosé — silky mousse, red berry aromas, long elegant finish. The strawberry-raspberry cuts burrata's cream and plays cleanly against basil-tomato-pistachio. Peak rosé Champagne for burrata — the berry-fruit framework meets the salad's delicate build beautifully. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Butternut Squash": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Carrot Cake": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé on the steak course instead.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Cheesecake": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé belongs on the steak course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Chilean Seabass": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity reads alongside the seabass's buttery flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Chocolate Brownie": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; one of those is the move. Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Chocolate Cake": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- brut bubbles and chocolate weight refuse each other — needs sweetness, not crisp acid. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé for the steak course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Clam Chowder": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the fine bubbles lift the chowder, and the citrus frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Cowboy Ribeye": "Pinot Noir rosé Champagne against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — the berry-led mousse has no weight to meet rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon and keep the rosé for the opener or raw bar.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Crab Cake": "Tours-sur-Marne Pinot Noir rosé Champagne — benchmark rosé with red berry aromas, silky mousse, long elegant finish. The berry fruit cuts crab's sweet romesco and bubbles lift the cake's pan-fry. Peak rosé Champagne for crab cake — sophisticated starter pour at benchmark quality. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Creamed Spinach": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Creamy Potato": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the fine bubbles lift the potato soup, and the citrus frames the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Creme Brulee": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a sparkling. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé belongs on the steak course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Escargot": "Escargot: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this sparkling rosé's strawberry-and-brioche charm with crisp acidity. Sparkling's bubbles and acid scrub the starter's fat while the bright frame lifts the plate. Strong — palate-cleansing start.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Faroe Island Salmon": "Pinot Noir Champagne rosé — silky mousse, red berry aromas, long elegant finish. The structure and body handle salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works well as rosé Champagne across most seasonal salmon preparations.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Filet Mignon": "Pinot Noir Champagne rosé — silky structured mousse, red berry complexity, long elegant finish. The body and berry frame handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness unconventionally, and bubbles lift the sear. Strong alternative when guest wants Champagne with their steak instead of classic Cab.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|French Onion": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity stays alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Grilled Caesar": "Pinot Noir Champagne rosé — benchmark silky mousse, red berry notes, long elegant finish. The bubbles cut anchovy-parmesan dressing and the structure meets grilled-romaine char; strawberry adds lift against the heavy salad. Strong rosé Champagne pour for grilled Caesar.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Gumbo": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity sits beside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|House Wedge": "Tours-sur-Marne Pinot Noir rosé — benchmark Champagne with strawberry, raspberry, silky mousse, long finish. The red berry cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the structure meets blue cheese dressing without crumpling. Strong elegant pairing for wedge — sophisticated opener that reframes the classic.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Kansas City": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé lands on another course — not here.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Loaded Potato": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity sits alongside the loaded potato's richness — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Lobster Mac": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Market Fish": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence sits at register with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh: the fine bubbles lift the market fish, and the apple-pear edge plays against the plate. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Mocha Creme": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé on the steak course instead.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Mushroom Bisque": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity plays cleanly against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Mushrooms": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé for the steak course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Porterhouse": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves a structured red. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé for another course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Prime Tartare": "Pinot Noir Champagne rosé from Tours-sur-Marne — benchmark rosé with silky structured mousse, strawberry, raspberry, fresh cherry, and long elegant finish. The red-berry complexity meets raw prime beef's iron richness directly and the silky mousse holds through each bite. Peak rosé Champagne for tartare. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Rainbow Trout": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity matches the trout's delicate flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Roast Half Chicken": "Pinot Noir rosé Champagne from Tours-sur-Marne — silky mousse, red berry, elegant finish. The body and berry frame can handle roasted chicken; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as sophisticated Champagne pairing for the chicken course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's fine-bubble Champagne register leans against the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the fine bubbles lift the halibut, and the citrus frames the plate. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity leans against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Seafood Tower": "Pinot Noir Champagne rosé — silky structured mousse with strawberry, raspberry, and fresh cherry. The fine bubbles and red-berry complexity reset the palate between oysters, shrimp, and crab and elevate the whole shellfish platter. Peak sophisticated rosé Champagne for the seafood tower. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Seared Scallops": "Tours-sur-Marne Pinot Noir rosé Champagne — benchmark rosé, silky mousse, red berry notes, long elegant finish. The strawberry-raspberry complexity frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the fine bubbles refresh the browned edge. Peak rosé Champagne for scallops — elegance-on-elegance pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Seasonal Soup": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity sits beside the seasonal soup's house build — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Seasonal Vegetables": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity reads alongside the seasonal garden plate — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Shrimp Bisque": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity stays alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Shrimp Cocktail": "Pinot Noir Champagne rosé from Tours-sur-Marne — silky mousse with strawberry, raspberry, and cherry. The red-berry profile cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the long elegant finish plays against cocktail-sauce bite. Peak rosé Champagne for shrimp cocktail — benchmark starter pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Swordfish": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity plays cleanly against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|The Tomahawk": "Silky Pinot Noir rosé Champagne against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — the Tomahawk's rendered fat and char crushes the rosé's delicate strawberry-raspberry structure. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo and save the rosé for starters.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Tomato Basil": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the fine bubbles lift the tomato basil, and the citrus frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Truffle Fries": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity plays cleanly against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity finds register with the tuna's seared rare crust — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's crisp berry-cherry acidity finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; safe call, no friction.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Asparagus": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts through asparagus's sulfur edge and the brioche plays against the vegetal char. Works as Champagne for asparagus — bubbles on the wine-tricky vegetable; Sauv Blancs and Sancerre peak higher on sulfur notes.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut: this versatile sparkling's bright acidity, toasty lees character, and cleansing bubbles meets this rich, creamy side's dairy-driven indulgence. Acid and bubbles scrub the side's fat while brightness keeps the palate clear. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Beignets": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts beignets' fried-dough weight and the brioche complements the pastry directly. Strong elegant Champagne-with-dessert pairing — bubbles reset the palate at the sweet course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Bone Marrow": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne against marrow's rendered-fat richness — precise acidity is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned and keep LP Le Cuvée for the seafood tower, tartare, or filet opener.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Bone-In Filet": "Bone-In Filet: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this versatile sparkling's bright acidity, toasty lees character, and cleansing bubbles. Bubbles scrub fat well, but sparkling lacks the dark-fruit weight and tannin for steak. Strong — the interaction lands despite the axis challenge.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Broccoli Cheddar": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts the soup's cheddar-cream weight and the brioche plays against the broccoli base. Strong Champagne for broccoli cheddar — bubbles on the classic.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Broccolini": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's citrus-mineral acidity leans against the broccolini's tender green — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge frames the green plate. Works; capable alongside. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Brussels and Belly": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut: this versatile sparkling's bright acidity, toasty lees character, and cleansing bubbles meets the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts. Acid and bubbles scrub the side's fat while brightness keeps the palate clear. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Burrata": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne — green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid structure cuts burrata's cream and plays cleanly against basil-tomato; brioche echoes pistachio crunch. Peak precise Champagne for burrata — classical starter pour at benchmark quality. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Butternut Squash": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts butternut's cream-sweet weight and the brioche plays against caramelized squash. Strong Champagne for butternut squash — bubbles on the autumn classic.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Carrot Cake": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's classic non-vintage Champagne brightness clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Cheesecake": "Champagne — clean acid, bubbles, elegant body. The acid cuts through cream cheese density and the bubbles lift the graham crust. Works when guest wants to continue the Champagne through dessert rather than transition to a sweet wine.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Chilean Seabass": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts seabass's buttery richness and the brioche plays against the melt-in-mouth texture. Strong Champagne for Chilean seabass — bubbles reset the palate between bites.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Chocolate Brownie": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's classic non-vintage Champagne brightness clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut for the steak course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Chocolate Cake": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's classic non-vintage Champagne brightness clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut for the steak course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Clam Chowder": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts the chowder's cream weight and the brioche plays against potato-bacon base. Strong Champagne for clam chowder — bubbles on the creamy shellfish classic.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Cowboy Ribeye": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's classic non-vintage Champagne brightness underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut lands on another course — not here.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Crab Cake": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid structure cuts romesco and lifts crab's sweet meat cleanly. Peak Champagne for crab cake — refined acidity frames the sweet shellfish perfectly. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Creamed Spinach": "Champagne — clean acid, bubbles, elegant body. Far too light for our creamed spinach's roux-based richness; the Champagne disappears under the parmesan-and-heavy-cream sauce. Keep Laurent with lighter starters — tartare, tower, scallops, shrimp cocktail. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Creamy Potato": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts the soup's cream weight and the brioche plays against the potato base. Strong Champagne for creamy potato — bubbles reset the palate between spoons.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Creme Brulee": "Champagne — clean acid, bubbles, elegant body. Not the peak dessert pour, but the acid cuts cream well and the bubbles handle the torched sugar. Works when the guest wants to continue with Champagne rather than transition to dessert wine.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Escargot": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise refinement with green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche. The vibrant acidity cuts escargot's garlic-parsley butter weight and the brioche complements the French classic. Peak Champagne for escargot — both wine and dish from the same tradition. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Faroe Island Salmon": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne — green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, clean long finish. The vibrant acidity cuts salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works across most seasonal salmon builds as elegant Champagne pour.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Filet Mignon": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, brioche, vibrant acidity, clean long finish. The body and structure handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness unconventionally; bubbles lift the sear. Strong Champagne-with-steak alternative when guest wants precision over classic Cab weight.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|French Onion": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts French Onion's gruyère crust and the brioche plays against the caramelized onion. Works as Champagne-palate-cleanser for French Onion — elegant bubbles reset the rich classic; still wines peak higher as dedicated pairings.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Grilled Caesar": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — green apple, lemon zest, brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid structure cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the brioche plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong precision Champagne for the Caesar — classical match for the layered salad.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Gumbo": "Elegant Champagne against a dark-roux Cajun stew — precise citrus-brioche has no hook into gumbo's smoky spice-and-sausage weight. Never pair. Route Laurent Perrier to tartare, seafood tower, or crab cake; take gumbo with Rioja, CdP, Super Tuscan, or Zinfandel.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's citrus-mineral acidity stays alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|House Wedge": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne — green apple, lemon zest, brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the structure meets blue cheese dressing. Strong elegant pairing for the wedge — refined Champagne reframes the classic steakhouse salad.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Kansas City": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's classic non-vintage Champagne brightness underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut belongs on another course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Loaded Potato": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts the soup's cream-cheddar weight and the brioche plays against the loaded toppings. Strong Champagne for loaded potato — bubbles reset the palate between spoons.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Lobster Mac": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut: this versatile sparkling's bright acidity, toasty lees character, and cleansing bubbles meets this rich, creamy side's dairy-driven indulgence. Acid and bubbles scrub the side's fat while brightness keeps the palate clear. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Market Fish": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut: this versatile sparkling's bright acidity, toasty lees character, and cleansing bubbles meets this delicate seafood's mild, flaky flesh. Bubbles and acidity scrub rich fish fat while toasty lees add texture without weight. Strong — bubbles lift the protein.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Mocha Creme": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's classic non-vintage Champagne brightness clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- brut bubbles and chocolate weight refuse each other — needs sweetness, not crisp acid. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a sparkling. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut for the steak course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Mushroom Bisque": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts the bisque's cream weight and the brioche plays against the mushroom earth. Strong Champagne for mushroom bisque — bubbles reset the palate between spoons.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Mushrooms": "Champagne — clean acid, bubbles, elegant body. Mushroom umami calls for bourbon-or-Cab-level weight; the Champagne has no business at this depth. The wine disappears underneath the earthy side. Keep Laurent for lighter starters where it shines. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's classic non-vintage Champagne brightness clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut on the steak course instead.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Porterhouse": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's classic non-vintage Champagne brightness underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut for another course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Prime Tartare": "Champagne acid cuts through the cured egg yolk's richness and the raw filet's fat, while the bubbles reset between bites. A signature Bowdie's starter deserves a prestige sparkling — position as the elegant choice from the opening pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Rainbow Trout": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts through delicate trout without overwhelming and the brioche plays against the flaky flesh. Strong Champagne for rainbow trout — bubbles reset the palate between bites.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Roast Half Chicken": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise lemon zest, green apple, brioche, vibrant acidity. The structure can handle roasted chicken; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as sophisticated Champagne pairing for chicken across most seasonal accompaniments.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's citrus-mineral acidity holds with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts through the sweet soy glaze and the brioche plays against cured halibut. Strong Champagne for salt-cured halibut — bubbles reset the palate between bites.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's citrus-mineral acidity leans against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Seafood Tower": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise and refined with green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, and clean long finish. The vibrant acidity resets the palate between oysters, shrimp, crab, and lobster — sommelier's choice for the whole platter. Peak seafood tower Champagne. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Seared Scallops": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple and lemon zest, whisper of brioche, clean long finish. The vibrant acidity frames scallops' sweet-seared profile and the mineral precision cuts the browned edge. Peak Champagne for scallops — elegance-on-elegance pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Seasonal Soup": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's citrus-mineral acidity finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house build — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Seasonal Vegetables": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's citrus-mineral acidity sits alongside the seasonal garden plate — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Shrimp Bisque": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne — green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid precision cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and the brioche note complements butter-roux weight. Peak Champagne pairing for shrimp bisque — cleanliness meets indulgence course-perfectly. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Shrimp Cocktail": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne — green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid structure cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and plays precisely against cocktail-sauce bite. Peak Champagne for shrimp cocktail — classical pairing at benchmark quality. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Swordfish": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's citrus-mineral acidity finds register with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|The Tomahawk": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — precise lemon-apple-brioche has no weight to meet rendered Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo and save LP Le Cuvée for the opener or tartare course.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Tomato Basil": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts tomato's acidity and the lemon zest plays against basil's aromatic lift. Works as Champagne for tomato basil — bubbles on the fresh classic; Sauv Blancs peak higher.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Truffle Fries": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut: this versatile sparkling's bright acidity, toasty lees character, and cleansing bubbles meets this earthy, umami-packed side's mushroom-and-truffle depth. Acid and bubbles scrub the side's fat while brightness keeps the palate clear. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tours-sur-Marne Chardonnay-blend Champagne — precise green apple, lemon zest, whisper of brioche, vibrant acidity. The high-acid finish cuts sesame-crust weight and the brioche plays against honey sambal sweetness. Strong Champagne for tuxedo tuna — bubbles reset the palate between bites.",
  "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's citrus-mineral acidity sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Asparagus": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — delicate fresh-fruit, strawberry, crisp. The crisp body handles asparagus's sulfur notes and the fresh fruit plays against the vegetal char. Strong Loire rosé for asparagus — summer pairing on the wine-tricky vegetable.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Beignets": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Le Garenne Rosé for the steak course.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Bone Marrow": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé against marrow's rendered-fat richness — delicate fresh-fruit has no weight to meet bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Le Garenne for burrata, wedge, crab cake, scallops, or summer starter service.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Bone-In Filet": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity plays cleanly against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the white-wine body is outweighed by the cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Broccoli Cheddar": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — delicate fresh-fruit, strawberry, crisp. The crisp body cuts the soup's cheddar-cream weight and the fresh fruit plays against broccoli's vegetal edge. Works as Loire rosé for broccoli cheddar — summer pairing on the classic.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Broccolini": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — delicate fresh-fruit, strawberry, crisp. The crisp body lifts broccolini's tender stem and the fresh fruit plays against the vegetal char. On this wine's card Broccolini is strong — veg sides are rosé's crossover territory and Le Garenne's identity peaks here, even when leaner whites like Chablis or Sancerre peak higher on Broccolini's own card. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Brussels and Belly": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity leans against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Burrata": "Provence rosé — strawberry, citrus, clean. Rosé with cheese-and-fruit is textbook; the strawberry note echoes whatever seasonal fruit the kitchen has on today, and the acid cuts the burrata cream. Peak pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Butternut Squash": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — delicate fresh-fruit, strawberry, crisp. The fresh fruit plays against butternut's caramelized-squash sweetness and the crisp body cuts the cream base. Works as Loire rosé for butternut squash — summer-into-fall bridge on the classic.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Carrot Cake": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Le Garenne Rosé for the steak course.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Cheesecake": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Le Garenne Rosé for the steak course.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Chilean Seabass": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — delicate fresh-fruit, strawberry, crisp. The crisp body lifts seabass's buttery flesh and the fresh fruit plays against the rich texture. Works as Loire rosé for Chilean seabass — summer pairing on the buttery classic.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Chocolate Brownie": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Le Garenne Rosé lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Chocolate Cake": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Le Garenne Rosé for the steak course.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Clam Chowder": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — delicate fresh-fruit, strawberry, crisp. The crisp body cuts the chowder's cream weight and the fresh fruit plays against the clam brine. Works as summer rosé for clam chowder — Loire delicacy on the shellfish classic.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Cowboy Ribeye": "Le Garenne Rosé against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — light Loire pink crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Le Garenne for burrata, wedge, crab cake, scallops, or seafood tower — summer starters only.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Crab Cake": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — pale salmon with strawberry, watermelon, floral, crisp dry finish. The fresh-fruit body cuts crab's romesco and the berry-floral plays against the sweet meat. Peak summer rosé for crab cake — approachable pink on the classic starter, seasonal lightness.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Creamed Spinach": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Creamy Potato": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — delicate fresh-fruit, strawberry, crisp. The crisp acid cuts the soup's cream weight and the fresh fruit plays against the potato base. Works as summer rosé for creamy potato — Loire delicacy on the rich classic.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Creme Brulee": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Le Garenne Rosé lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Escargot": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — strawberry, watermelon, floral, crisp dry. The fresh-fruit acid cuts escargot's garlic-parsley butter and the berry-floral plays against the snail richness. Strong summer rosé for escargot — Loire-on-Loire lightness, though Sancerre and grower rosés peak higher.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Faroe Island Salmon": "Provence rosé — strawberry, citrus, clean. Works against the salmon but doesn't peak; the Chardonnays and Pinots do more against the Faroe's fat. Default pour for a guest committed to rosé through the main.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Filet Mignon": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — strawberry, watermelon, floral, crisp dry. The light body has limited weight to meet grilled beef; fresh-fruit plays against escargot-butter toppers. Works as light-rosé-with-steak when guest wants summer pink; Cab, Pinot, or grower rosés peak higher for filet.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|French Onion": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Grilled Caesar": "Le Garenne Rosé — Loire Pinot Noir with strawberry, watermelon, floral, crisp dry. The fresh-fruit acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the berry-floral plays against grilled-romaine char without overpowering. Strong summer rosé for grilled Caesar — seasonal lightness on the layered classic.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Gumbo": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — delicate fresh-fruit, strawberry, crisp. The fresh fruit plays against carrots' caramelized honey sweetness and the crisp body lifts the roasted char. Peak Loire rosé for honey-roasted carrots — summer pairing on the sweet-root classic. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|House Wedge": "Le Garenne Rosé — Loire Pinot Noir with strawberry, watermelon, light floral, dry crisp. The fresh-fruit acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the floral lift plays against blue cheese dressing without clashing. Peak summer rosé for the wedge — seasonal lightness reframes the classic.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Kansas City": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity sits alongside the strip's lean char — the white-wine body is outweighed by the cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Loaded Potato": "Le Garenne Rosé's acid-driven white register leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bright acid lifts the loaded potato soup, and the citrus cuts the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Lobster Mac": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Market Fish": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity matches the fish's clean flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Mocha Creme": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp white-wine character falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a white wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Le Garenne Rosé belongs on the steak course.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Mushroom Bisque": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity leans against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Mushrooms": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp white-wine character clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Le Garenne Rosé lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Porterhouse": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity leans against the porterhouse's two-cut character — the white-wine body is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this dry Provence-style rosé's red-fruit freshness and light weight. Crisp or off-dry white's acidity lifts the starter — classic opening-course move. Strong — clean lift.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Rainbow Trout": "Rainbow Trout: this delicate seafood's mild, flaky flesh meets this dry Provence-style rosé's red-fruit freshness and light weight. Crisp acidity and mineral edge mirror fish's own brininess — clean, classic alignment. Strong — textbook match.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Roast Half Chicken": "Le Garenne Rosé — Loire Pinot Noir with strawberry, watermelon, floral, crisp dry. The fresh-fruit body handles roasted chicken; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works well as summer rosé for chicken across most seasonal accompaniments — Loire-on-France lightness.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — delicate fresh-fruit, strawberry, crisp. The fresh fruit plays against roasted-pepper sweetness and the crisp body lifts the chickpea earth. Peak Loire rosé for the soup — summer pairing on the smoky-sweet classic. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Salt-Cured Halibut: this delicate seafood's mild, flaky flesh meets this dry Provence-style rosé's red-fruit freshness and light weight. Crisp acidity and mineral edge mirror fish's own brininess — clean, classic alignment. Works cleanly.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Loire rosé — Pinot Noir with strawberry, watermelon, floral, crisp dry. The fresh-fruit acid cuts the sauté butter and the berry-floral plays against garlic without clashing with spinach's earth. Works as summer rosé for the sauté side — seasonal lightness on a simple side.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Seafood Tower": "Loire rosé — Pinot Noir with strawberry, watermelon, floral, crisp dry. The fresh-fruit acid resets palate between oysters, shrimp, crab, and lobster and the berry-floral plays against brine across the platter. Strong summer rosé for the seafood tower — seasonal lightness for mixed shellfish.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Seared Scallops": "Le Garenne Rosé — Loire Pinot Noir with strawberry, watermelon, light floral, crisp dry. The fresh-fruit acid frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the delicate berry adds lift against the pan-sear. Peak summer rosé for scallops — seasonal lightness meets sweet shellfish.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Seasonal Soup": "Le Garenne Rosé: this dry Provence-style rosé's red-fruit freshness and light weight meets this rich, creamy soup's dairy-driven warmth. The wine's acidity cuts the cream, but its weight doesn't carry the soup. Strong if the soup needs brightness.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Seasonal Vegetables": "Le Garenne Rosé: this dry Provence-style rosé's red-fruit freshness and light weight meets this light, green vegetable's clean herbal edge. Bright acidity lifts green vegetables; mineral lines up with the clean vegetable bite. Strong — refreshing and clean.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Shrimp Bisque": "Provence rosé — strawberry, citrus, clean. Works against the bisque but doesn't peak; the Chardonnays and Chablis do more against the coconut milk and the smoked paprika finish. Fine default pour when the guest isn't moving off rosé.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Shrimp Cocktail": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — pale salmon, strawberry, watermelon, floral, crisp dry. The fresh-fruit finish cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the berry-floral plays against cocktail-sauce bite. Peak summer rosé for shrimp cocktail — seasonal lightness on the classic chilled starter.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Swordfish": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — delicate fresh-fruit, strawberry, crisp. The crisp body lifts swordfish's meaty flesh and the fresh fruit plays against the grilled char. Strong Loire rosé for swordfish — summer pairing on the grilled classic.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|The Tomahawk": "Light Loire rosé against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — delicate strawberry-watermelon-floral has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Le Garenne for burrata, wedge, crab cake, scallops, or shrimp cocktail.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Tomato Basil": "Tomato Basil: this bright, vegetal soup's acidic herbal lift meets this dry Provence-style rosé's red-fruit freshness and light weight. Crisp, vegetal wine mirrors the soup's bright herbal lift. A same-register headliner. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Truffle Fries": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity reads alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Loire Pinot Noir rosé — delicate fresh-fruit, strawberry, crisp. The fresh fruit plays against honey sambal's sweet-spicy edge and the crisp body lifts rare-seared tuna. Works as Loire rosé for tuxedo tuna — summer pairing on the crusted classic.",
  "Le Garenne Rosé|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas: this spiced vegetable curry's warm, complex aromatic lift meets this dry Provence-style rosé's red-fruit freshness and light weight. Crisp, vegetal wine mirrors the soup's bright herbal lift. A same-register headliner. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Asparagus": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Le Ragnaie Brunello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: structured red meets dairy-driven side: the bold tannin scrubs the cream, the dark fruit lifts the indulgence into balance. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Beignets": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Bone Marrow": "Tuscany Brunello di Montalcino DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Le Ragnaie, five years minimum aging, dried cherry, tobacco, leather, rose petal, full body, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the leather plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak Italy's-most-prestigious for marrow — Brunello on the rich starter. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Bone-In Filet": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's powerful blackcurrant body leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Broccoli Cheddar": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overwhelms the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Broccolini": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Burrata": "Italy's-most-prestigious Brunello against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Le Ragnaie's firm-tannin tobacco-leather weight overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Le Ragnaie for aged beef courses.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Butternut Squash": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Le Ragnaie Brunello on the steak course instead.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Carrot Cake": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Cheesecake": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Le Ragnaie Brunello lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Chilean Seabass": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Le Ragnaie Brunello on the steak course instead.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Chocolate Brownie": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Chocolate Cake": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Le Ragnaie Brunello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Clam Chowder": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Le Ragnaie Brunello on the steak course instead.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Crab Cake": "Italy's-most-prestigious Brunello against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Le Ragnaie's firm-tannin tobacco-leather structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Le Ragnaie for aged beef — reserve for guests who know what they're ordering.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Creamy Potato": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Le Ragnaie Brunello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Creme Brulee": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Le Ragnaie Brunello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Escargot": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's powerful blackcurrant body sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the structured body wraps the buttered escargot, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; the pairing lands at register without strain.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Faroe Island Salmon": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Filet Mignon": "Tuscany Brunello di Montalcino DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Le Ragnaie, five years minimum aging, dried cherry, tobacco, leather, rose petal, full body, firm tannin, high acid, age-worthy. The firm tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the tobacco plays against escargot-butter. Peak Italy's-most-prestigious for filet — Brunello on the delicate cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|French Onion": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's bold cherry-earth cassis sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Grilled Caesar": "Tuscany Brunello di Montalcino DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Le Ragnaie, five years minimum aging, dried cherry, tobacco, leather, rose petal, firm tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the rose petal plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as Italy's-most-prestigious for grilled Caesar — Brunello reframes the salad.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Gumbo": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|House Wedge": "Tuscany Brunello di Montalcino DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Le Ragnaie, five years minimum aging, dried cherry, tobacco, leather, rose petal, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the leather plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as Italy's-most-prestigious for the wedge — Brunello deserves aged beef.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Kansas City": "Tuscany Brunello di Montalcino DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Le Ragnaie, five years minimum aging, dried cherry, tobacco, leather, rose petal, full body, firm tannin, high acid. The firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the leather plays against beefy char. Peak Italy's-most-prestigious for the strip — Brunello on the American cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Loaded Potato": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the structured body wraps the loaded potato soup, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the side sits without pulling focus.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Lobster Mac": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's bold cherry-earth cassis finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the juniper carries the shellfish. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Market Fish": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Le Ragnaie Brunello on the steak course instead.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Mocha Creme": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Le Ragnaie Brunello on the steak course instead.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Mushroom Bisque": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's bold cherry-earth cassis leans against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the mushroom's umami depth meets the bold red's structured tannin; dark fruit threads the truffle-and-earth without crowding. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Le Ragnaie Brunello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Prime Tartare": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's bold cherry-earth cassis holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Rainbow Trout": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Roast Half Chicken": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's bold cherry-earth cassis stays alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the juniper sharpens the char. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overwhelms the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Le Ragnaie Brunello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Le Ragnaie Brunello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Seafood Tower": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Seared Scallops": "Italy's-most-prestigious Brunello against scallop sweetness — Le Ragnaie's firm-tannin tobacco-leather structure buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Le Ragnaie for Bone-In Filet, Tomahawk, or Porterhouse.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Seasonal Soup": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overwhelms the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Seasonal Vegetables": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overshadows the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Le Ragnaie Brunello lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Shrimp Bisque": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's bold cherry-earth cassis leans against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the juniper carries the shellfish. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Shrimp Cocktail": "Italy's-most-prestigious Brunello against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — firm-tannin Le Ragnaie and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Le Ragnaie for aged beef.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Swordfish": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Tomato Basil": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Le Ragnaie Brunello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the side's mushroom richness pulls the bold red's body; tannin and oak settle into the umami without overshooting. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Le Ragnaie Brunello for the steak course.",
  "Le Ragnaie Brunello|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Le Ragnaie Brunello on the steak course instead.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Asparagus": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Beignets": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Le Volte dell'Ornellaia belongs on the steak course.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Richness from the plate and structure from the wine share the same register — a savory weight-on-weight setup. Works, though neither flavor is showcased.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Bone-In Filet": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Broccoli Cheddar": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin leans against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Broccolini": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin stays alongside the broccolini's tender green — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Burrata": "Tuscan Merlot-driven blend against fresh burrata — Le Volte's structure and dark-fruit weight crush the creamy-fresh dairy starter. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Champagne, or crisp Italian whites; save Le Volte for heavier Italian-leaning courses.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Butternut Squash": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin leans against the squash soup's silky sweet — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Carrot Cake": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Le Volte dell'Ornellaia on the steak course instead.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Cheesecake": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Le Volte dell'Ornellaia lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Chilean Seabass": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Le Volte dell'Ornellaia lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Chocolate Brownie": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Le Volte dell'Ornellaia belongs on the steak course.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Chocolate Cake": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Le Volte dell'Ornellaia for the steak course.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Clam Chowder": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the chowder, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the side sits without pulling focus.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Crab Cake": "Tuscan Merlot-driven blend against crab cake's delicate shellfish — Le Volte's medium-full dark-fruit structure is too weighty for the romesco-and-crab starter. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne; save Le Volte for heartier Italian plates and red-meat courses.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Creamy Potato": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the potato soup, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Creme Brulee": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Le Volte dell'Ornellaia on the steak course instead.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Escargot": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin reads alongside the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Faroe Island Salmon": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Le Volte dell'Ornellaia for the steak course.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The dark-fruit frame supports the filet, but the tannin rides harder than the lean profile demands. Pour when the guest wants the bold-red experience. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|French Onion": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin stays alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the coffee meets the caramel. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Tannin and char, umami and dark fruit — the bold red and the Caesar punch at the same level. Strong — aligned intensity.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Gumbo": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin sits alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin finds neutral with the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the coffee meets the caramel. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|House Wedge": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Kansas City": "Kansas City: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Loaded Potato": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Lobster Mac": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Market Fish": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Le Volte dell'Ornellaia on the steak course instead.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Mocha Creme": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Le Volte dell'Ornellaia lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Mushroom Bisque": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin holds with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the mushroom's umami depth meets the bold red's structured tannin; dark fruit threads the truffle-and-earth without crowding. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Le Volte dell'Ornellaia for the steak course.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Prime Tartare": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Rainbow Trout": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Le Volte dell'Ornellaia belongs on the steak course.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Roast Half Chicken": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the medium-body red supports the herb-crisped bird, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the chickpea soup, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Le Volte dell'Ornellaia on the steak course instead.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin sits alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Seafood Tower": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Le Volte dell'Ornellaia belongs on the steak course.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Seared Scallops": "Tuscan Merlot-driven blend against seared scallops' clean sweet sear — Le Volte's dark-fruit and Italian tannin are wrong-footed against delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc; save Le Volte for the steak course or hearty pasta.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Seasonal Soup": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin stays alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Seasonal Vegetables": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin sits beside the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Shrimp Bisque": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin leans against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Shrimp Cocktail": "Tuscan Merlot-driven blend against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — Le Volte's medium-full red structure is the wrong axis for cold shellfish. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis; save Le Volte for the steak course.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Swordfish": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Le Volte dell'Ornellaia for the steak course.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|The Tomahawk": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Tomato Basil": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the tomato basil, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the mushroom's umami depth meets the bold red's structured tannin; dark fruit threads the truffle-and-earth without crowding. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Le Volte dell'Ornellaia on the steak course instead.",
  "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Asparagus": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build sits alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the honey threads the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Beignets": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Lemon Lavender Gin Martini lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Bone Marrow": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The cocktail's floral lightness has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat weight — lavender-and-honey don't cut bone depth. Never pair — keep the martini for starters and raw bar.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Bone-In Filet": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- cocktail citrus reads thin against the cut's structure. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Lemon Lavender Gin Martini belongs on another course.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Broccoli Cheddar": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build sits beside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the honey threads the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Broccolini": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build stays alongside the broccolini's tender green — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Brussels and Belly": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build holds with the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Burrata": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. Plymouth's softer juniper profile meets burrata's creamy-basil delicacy and the honey-lavender adds floral complexity; the Prosecco float lifts the finish. Peak floral-martini pairing for burrata — both elegant and aromatic.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Butternut Squash": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build sits beside the squash soup's silky sweet — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the honey threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Carrot Cake": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Lemon Lavender Gin Martini for the steak course.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Cheesecake": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Lemon Lavender Gin Martini for the steak course.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Chilean Seabass": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build holds steady against the seabass's buttery flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Chocolate Brownie": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Lemon Lavender Gin Martini on the steak course instead.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Chocolate Cake": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Lemon Lavender Gin Martini for the steak course.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Clam Chowder": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the honey threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Cowboy Ribeye": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- a light citrus cocktail can't carry the marbled char — needs whiskey or red wine weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Lemon Lavender Gin Martini for another course.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Crab Cake": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The lemon cuts crab's romesco and honey-lavender adds floral lift against the sweet meat; Prosecco adds effervescence. Peak floral cocktail pairing for crab cake — approachable even for gin-hesitant guests.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Creamed Spinach": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Creamy Potato": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build sits alongside the potato soup's silky body — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Creme Brulee": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Lemon Lavender Gin Martini on the steak course instead.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Escargot": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The honey's sweetness softens garlic-butter escargot and lavender's floral note lifts the snails' earth; Prosecco adds effervescent lift. Peak aromatic pairing for escargot with approachable gin profile.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Faroe Island Salmon": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build holds the line with the salmon's rich oily flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Filet Mignon": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The cocktail's floral-bright profile is wrong for steak — lemon and lavender have no hook into grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the cocktail; route filet pairing to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|French Onion": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the rounded sweetness threads the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Grilled Caesar": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The honey-lavender profile plays against anchovy-parmesan dressing and lemon cuts the saltiness; Prosecco adds lift against grilled-romaine char. Works as unconventional floral pairing with a charred salad.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Gumbo": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the honey balances the spice. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build reads alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|House Wedge": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The lemon-and-honey profile cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and lavender plays against blue cheese; Prosecco adds lift. Strong opener pairing — approachable floral martini for a classic wedge.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Kansas City": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- cocktail citrus reads thin against the cut's structure. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Lemon Lavender Gin Martini lands on another course — not here.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Loaded Potato": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the honey threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Lobster Mac": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the honey threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Market Fish": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build holds the line with the fish's clean flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Mocha Creme": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Lemon Lavender Gin Martini for the steak course.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Mushroom Bisque": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's shaken-and-bright cocktail register reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bright build frames the mushroom bisque, and the citrus cuts the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Mushrooms": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build sits alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Lemon Lavender Gin Martini lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Porterhouse": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Lemon Lavender Gin Martini for another course.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this gin cocktail's citrus brightness and botanical lift. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. Strong — aligned lift.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Rainbow Trout": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build lifts the trout's delicate flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Roast Half Chicken": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the cocktail lift carries the herb-crisped bird, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build stays alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the honey balances the spice. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build holds steady against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build leans against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the honey threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Seafood Tower": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The floral-bright profile handles raw-bar spread and Prosecco's lift matches the tower's celebratory mood. Strong cocktail alternative to Champagne for the tower — especially for gin-curious guests.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Seared Scallops": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The cocktail's floral-bright profile meets scallops' clean-seared sweetness directly; honey softens and Prosecco adds lift. Peak floral-martini pairing for scallops — sophistication with approachability.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Seasonal Soup": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Seasonal Vegetables": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build reads alongside the seasonal garden plate — the cocktail lift sits with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Shrimp Bisque": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The lemon-and-lavender cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and Prosecco adds effervescence against the soup's weight. Strong pairing — floral martini handles bisque where heavier cocktails would fight.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Shrimp Cocktail": "Plymouth gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup, Prosecco float. The lemon-and-honey cuts chilled shrimp's brine and lavender adds aromatic complexity against cocktail-sauce. Peak cocktail pairing for shrimp cocktail — shares the bright, light profile with the starter.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Swordfish": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build finds register with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|The Tomahawk": "Floral Plymouth-gin martini against the biggest steak on the menu — complete mismatch. The cocktail's delicacy is crushed by marbled Tomahawk weight. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Tomato Basil": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build stays alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Truffle Fries": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift finds neutral with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight: the bright build frames the truffle fries, and the citrus cuts the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build sits with the tuna's seared rare crust — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the honey balances the spice. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Asparagus": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis holds with the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Gigondas Rhône Valley Grenache — Les Pallières Raciné, Brunier-family old-vine, wild red fruit, garrigue, iron, earthy mineral, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts gruyere-cream richness and the full body handles dairy. Works for au gratin — Muga's vanilla-leather fits the cheese harder.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Beignets": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Les Pallières Raciné lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Bone Marrow": "Gigondas Rhône Valley Grenache — Les Pallières Raciné, Brunier-family old-vine, wild red fruit, garrigue, iron, earthy mineral, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts roasted-marrow unctuousness and the iron echoes beefy-bone depth. Peak Gigondas for bone marrow — old-vine structure balances the richness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Bone-In Filet": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the velvety body lifts the lean cut. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Broccoli Cheddar": "Les Pallières Raciné's deep dark-fruit and grippy tannin finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the structured body wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; the close composes without strain.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Broccolini": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Brussels and Belly": "Gigondas Rhône Valley Grenache — Les Pallières Raciné, Brunier-family old-vine, wild red fruit, garrigue, iron, earthy mineral, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts pork-belly fat and the garrigue matches brussels-char. Strong Gigondas for brussels-and-belly — Rhône polish on fat-and-char.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Burrata": "Never pair Raciné with burrata — garrigue, iron, and earthy mineral clash with fresh cream and the old-vine depth buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Butternut Squash": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis holds with the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Carrot Cake": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Les Pallières Raciné for the steak course.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Cheesecake": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Les Pallières Raciné lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Chilean Seabass": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Les Pallières Raciné for the steak course.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Chocolate Brownie": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Les Pallières Raciné for the steak course.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Chocolate Cake": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Les Pallières Raciné for the steak course.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Clam Chowder": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Cowboy Ribeye": "Gigondas Rhône Valley Grenache — Les Pallières Raciné, Brunier-family old-vine, wild red fruit, garrigue, iron, earthy mineral, full body. The full body matches Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the earthy mineral echoes grilled char. Peak Gigondas for ribeye — old-vine intensity on the marbled cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Crab Cake": "Never pair Raciné with crab cake — garrigue, iron, and earthy mineral bury delicate lump crab and the full body fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the structured red's grip carries the rich side's dairy load; oak-driven body absorbs the cream and tannin clears the finish. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Creamy Potato": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Creme Brulee": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Les Pallières Raciné for the steak course.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Escargot": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Faroe Island Salmon": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Les Pallières Raciné for the steak course.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Filet Mignon": "Gigondas Rhône Valley Grenache — Les Pallières Raciné, Brunier-family old-vine, wild red fruit, garrigue, iron, earthy mineral, medium tannin. The medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the garrigue plays with escargot-butter. Strong Gigondas for filet — full body is bold but the old-vine structure threads.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|French Onion": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the rye spice cuts the cheese richness. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Grilled Caesar": "Gigondas Rhône Valley Grenache — Les Pallières Raciné, Brunier-family old-vine, wild red fruit, garrigue, iron, earthy mineral, full body. The garrigue matches anchovy-lemon dressing and the iron plays against parm-char. Strong Gigondas for grilled caesar — Rhône herb hook on the salad.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Gumbo": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis finds neutral with the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|House Wedge": "Gigondas Rhône Valley Grenache — Les Pallières Raciné, Brunier-family old-vine, wild red fruit, garrigue, iron, earthy mineral, full body. The garrigue plays with blue-cheese funk and the iron matches bacon-fat char. Works for wedge — cold iceberg mutes Raciné's old-vine depth, so a lighter Chianti lifts higher.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Kansas City": "Kansas City: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Loaded Potato": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis sits alongside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Lobster Mac": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the opulent body matches the indulgent side, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Market Fish": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Les Pallières Raciné on the steak course instead.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Mocha Creme": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Les Pallières Raciné on the steak course instead.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Mushroom Bisque": "Les Pallières Raciné's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the structured body wraps the mushroom bisque, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; the close composes without strain.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Mushrooms": "Gigondas Rhône Valley Grenache — Les Pallières Raciné, Brunier-family old-vine, wild red fruit, garrigue, iron, earthy mineral, full body. The earthy mineral echoes roasted-mushroom glutamate and the iron plays with butter-depth. Peak Gigondas for mushrooms — Rhône earth-on-earth match. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Les Pallières Raciné lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the Cab's firm tannin cuts the strip's char and the dark fruit lifts the savory edge — steakhouse symmetry. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Prime Tartare": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Rainbow Trout": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Les Pallières Raciné belongs on the steak course.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Roast Half Chicken": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis holds with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Les Pallières Raciné on the steak course instead.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis holds with the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Seafood Tower": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Les Pallières Raciné belongs on the steak course.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Raciné with seared scallops — garrigue, iron, and earthy mineral overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and the medium tannin fights browned-butter. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Seasonal Soup": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis sits alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Seasonal Vegetables": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis reads alongside the seasonal garden plate — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Shrimp Bisque": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis sits beside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Raciné with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by garrigue, iron, and full body, and the old-vine complexity has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Swordfish": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Les Pallières Raciné belongs on the steak course.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|The Tomahawk": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the opulent body meets the cap-fat, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Tomato Basil": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright acid — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the spice meets the herb butter. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the bold red's cassis grip carries the mushroom-and-truffle depth — structured weight on the earthy side. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Les Pallières Raciné for the steak course.",
  "Les Pallières Raciné|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis stays alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Liberator Gin|Asparagus": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Liberator Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Liberator Gin|Beignets": "Liberator with beignets — Valentine Distilling Michigan craft against fried dough. Excellent; Liberator's smooth profile is a soft dessert finisher at hometown register.",
  "Liberator Gin|Bone Marrow": "Liberator with bone marrow — Ferndale MI craft gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Liberator Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Liberator Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Liberator Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Liberator Gin|Broccolini": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Liberator Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals sits beside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Liberator Gin|Burrata": "Liberator with burrata — Valentine Distilling Ferndale MI craft against fresh dairy. Excellent; Liberator's smooth approachable profile complements cream at hometown register.",
  "Liberator Gin|Butternut Squash": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Liberator Gin|Carrot Cake": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Liberator Gin for the steak course.",
  "Liberator Gin|Cheesecake": "Liberator with cheesecake — Valentine Distilling Ferndale craft against custard. Works; Liberator's smooth profile threads the cheesecake at hometown register.",
  "Liberator Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals lifts the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Liberator Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Liberator Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Liberator Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Liberator Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Liberator Gin|Clam Chowder": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Liberator Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Liberator with the cowboy ribeye — 100% Michigan-grain craft against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Liberator Gin|Crab Cake": "Liberator with crab cake — Valentine Distilling, Ferndale Michigan, 100% Michigan grain hometown craft. Excellent; Liberator is the Michigan-hometown craft-gin pairing for crab — sibling to Long Road, Eastern Kille, 2 James, Detroit City, and Iron Fish in the Michigan gin program, the Valentine Distilling answer.",
  "Liberator Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Liberator Gin|Creamy Potato": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Liberator Gin|Creme Brulee": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Liberator Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Liberator Gin|Escargot": "Liberator with escargot — Michigan 100%-grain craft against garlic-butter snails. Works; Liberator meets butter at hometown register; Mahón's Mediterranean juniper and Eastern Kille's classic London Dry cut it harder.",
  "Liberator Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Liberator with the salmon — 100% Michigan-grain craft gin against oily salmon. Strong; Liberator frames the fish at hometown-craft register; Iron Fish's northern-Michigan farm profile is the distinctive MI-gin-salmon match.",
  "Liberator Gin|Filet Mignon": "Liberator with the filet — Valentine Distilling Michigan craft against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Liberator Gin|French Onion": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Liberator Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Liberator with the grilled Caesar — 100% Michigan-grain craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Liberator meets the char at hometown register.",
  "Liberator Gin|Gumbo": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Liberator Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals sits alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Liberator Gin|House Wedge": "Liberator with the House Wedge — Michigan 100%-grain craft against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Liberator threads the wedge with Ferndale-craft character.",
  "Liberator Gin|Kansas City": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Liberator Gin for another course.",
  "Liberator Gin|Loaded Potato": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals sits alongside the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Liberator Gin|Lobster Mac": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Liberator Gin|Market Fish": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals lifts the fish's clean flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Liberator Gin|Mocha Creme": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Liberator Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Liberator Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Liberator Gin|Mushrooms": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Liberator Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Liberator Gin for the steak course.",
  "Liberator Gin|Porterhouse": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Liberator Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Liberator Gin|Prime Tartare": "Liberator with the tartare — Valentine Distilling Michigan craft against raw beef. Strong; Liberator supports tartare at hometown register.",
  "Liberator Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals sits with the trout's delicate flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Liberator Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Liberator with the chicken — Ferndale Valentine craft against roasted poultry. Strong; Liberator supports chicken at Michigan-craft register.",
  "Liberator Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals sits beside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Liberator Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Liberator Gin's gin lift with floral-and-pepper sits at register with the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the juniper-led botanicals carry the halibut, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; the gin option when the gold call is spoken for.",
  "Liberator Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals sits alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Liberator Gin|Seafood Tower": "Liberator with the seafood tower — Ferndale Valentine craft against the raw bar. Strong; Liberator is a Michigan-pride tower pairing; Gray Whale's California ocean-botanicals is still the featured craft-tower match.",
  "Liberator Gin|Seared Scallops": "Liberator with seared scallops — 100% Michigan-grain craft gin against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Liberator's smooth hometown profile frames scallops at Michigan-pride register.",
  "Liberator Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals sits alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Liberator Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Liberator Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Liberator with shrimp bisque — 100% Michigan-grain craft against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Liberator's smooth profile rides the bisque at hometown register.",
  "Liberator Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Liberator with shrimp cocktail — Valentine Distilling Michigan craft against cold shrimp. Excellent; Liberator delivers the classic cold app with Ferndale-hometown-craft character.",
  "Liberator Gin|Swordfish": "Liberator Gin's citrus-juniper botanicals holds steady against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
