  "Limoncello|Butternut Squash": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: the carrot cake's warm-spice, cream-cheese indulgence meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Limoncello|Cheesecake": "Cheesecake: the cheesecake's dense, tangy dairy richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Limoncello|Chilean Seabass": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert-grade sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs a crisp white instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Limoncello lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Limoncello|Chocolate Brownie": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the liqueur compounds the dessert's sweetness without contrast. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Limoncello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Chocolate Cake": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- two sweet registers without contrast — neither course lifts the other. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Limoncello on the steak course instead.",
  "Limoncello|Clam Chowder": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Limoncello on the steak course instead.",
  "Limoncello|Cowboy Ribeye": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Limoncello for another course.",
  "Limoncello|Crab Cake": "Crab Cake: this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Liqueur sweetness or herbal complexity against a starter — wrong register typically. Works as a digestif move.",
  "Limoncello|Creamed Spinach": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Limoncello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Creamy Potato": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Limoncello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Creme Brulee": "Creme Brulee: this delicate custard's creamy, vanilla sweetness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Limoncello|Escargot": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character buries the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- sweet liqueur smothers the herb-bright opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Limoncello on the steak course instead.",
  "Limoncello|Faroe Island Salmon": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Limoncello lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Limoncello|Filet Mignon": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Limoncello for another course.",
  "Limoncello|French Onion": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Limoncello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Grilled Caesar": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Gumbo": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Limoncello belongs on the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sweet liqueur and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no contrast left. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|House Wedge": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character jars against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- sugar on cold lettuce reads like dressing gone wrong — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Limoncello on the steak course instead.",
  "Limoncello|Kansas City": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Limoncello lands on another course — not here.",
  "Limoncello|Loaded Potato": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Lobster Mac": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Limoncello on the steak course instead.",
  "Limoncello|Market Fish": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Mocha Creme": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the liqueur compounds the dessert's sweetness without contrast. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Mushroom Bisque": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Mushrooms": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character fights the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- the liqueur's sweet register pulls against the mushroom's umami without bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- two sweet registers without contrast — neither course lifts the other. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Limoncello lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Limoncello|Porterhouse": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Limoncello on another course instead.",
  "Limoncello|Prime Tartare": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Rainbow Trout": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Limoncello on the steak course instead.",
  "Limoncello|Roast Half Chicken": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Limoncello lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Limoncello|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Limoncello lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Limoncello|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Seafood Tower": "Seafood Tower: this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Liqueur sweetness or herbal complexity against a starter — wrong register typically. Works as a digestif move.",
  "Limoncello|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Liqueur sweetness or herbal complexity against a starter — wrong register typically. Works as a digestif move.",
  "Limoncello|Seasonal Soup": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Seasonal Vegetables": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Shrimp Bisque": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Shrimp Cocktail": "Shrimp Cocktail: this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Liqueur sweetness or herbal complexity against a starter — wrong register typically. Works as a digestif move.",
  "Limoncello|Swordfish": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Limoncello on the steak course instead.",
  "Limoncello|The Tomahawk": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Limoncello belongs on another course.",
  "Limoncello|Tomato Basil": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Limoncello lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Limoncello|Truffle Fries": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character flattens the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- sugar on starch is dessert territory — wrong course for the supporting plate. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- sugar dulls the crust's clean char instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Limoncello|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Asparagus": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts asparagus's tender spears and the forest-floor earth plays against the vegetal char — the rare red that handles asparagus without clashing. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Asparagus but below the wine's peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this rich, creamy side's dairy-driven indulgence. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. Works — clean if not dominant.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Beignets": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir's Willamette layered Pinot character flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir on the steak course instead.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Bone Marrow": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. Bone marrow is pure collagen fat needing tannin to cut — Pinot's low tannin can't clear the palate. Works only when guest specifically wants Pinot with marrow; Cabs and bold reds peak higher for this rich starter.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Bone-In Filet": "Dark cherry, forest floor, low tannin — elegant Willamette Pinot. Doesn't match the bone-in's richness the way a Cab would, but the savory depth and light structure make it a clean, non-obvious pour. Good for a Pinot-committed guest. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Broccoli Cheddar": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts the soup's cheddar weight and the forest-floor earth plays against broccoli vegetal base. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Broccoli Cheddar but below the wine's peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Broccolini": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts broccolini's tender-stem structure and the forest-floor earth plays against the vegetal char. Peaks from the dish's side; on this wine's card it's strong — below peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins. Confident pour when a guest anchors on Broccolini. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Brussels and Belly": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, spice, medium body. The acid cuts pork-belly fat and the forest-floor plays against charred brussels. Strong by-the-glass Oregon Pinot for Brussels and Belly — elegant counterpoint to the rich starter.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Burrata": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, low tannin. Red wine with burrata is unusual but the low-tannin profile doesn't fight the creamy cheese or the delicate fruit. Good pivot for committed Pinot drinkers. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Butternut Squash": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts the soup's cream weight and the forest-floor earth plays against caramelized-squash sweetness. Peaks from the dish's side; on this wine's card it's strong — below peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins. Confident pour when a guest anchors on Butternut Squash. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Carrot Cake": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir's Willamette layered Pinot character clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Cheesecake": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir's Willamette layered Pinot character pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Chilean Seabass": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts seabass's buttery flesh and the forest-floor earth plays against the rich texture. Peak Oregon Pinot for Chilean seabass — refined red on the buttery classic. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Chocolate Brownie": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir's Willamette layered Pinot character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Chocolate Cake": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir's Willamette layered Pinot character fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Clam Chowder": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts the chowder's cream weight and the forest-floor earth plays against bacon-potato base. Works as Oregon Pinot for clam chowder — unconventional red on the shellfish-cream classic.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Cowboy Ribeye": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, spice, medium body, low tannin. The refined structure underserves the big marbled ribeye — Pinot's low tannin falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Works when guest wants lighter red with ribeye; Cabs peak higher.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Crab Cake": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, spice, refined structure, low tannin. The soft tannin doesn't fight crab's sweet meat and the forest-floor earth plays against romesco. Peak by-the-glass Pinot for crab cake — unconventional red that works where heavier reds would bury the shellfish. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Creamed Spinach": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, whisper of spice. The acid cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the forest-floor earth plays against the spinach base. Strong by-the-glass Pinot for creamed spinach — Oregon elegance reframes the rich steakhouse side.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Creamy Potato": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts the soup's cream-potato weight and the forest-floor earth plays against the dairy base. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Creamy Potato but below the wine's peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Creme Brulee": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir's Willamette layered Pinot character jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir belongs on the steak course.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Escargot": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth. Elegant red's cherry and silk mirror the starter's register — light-on-light or bright-on-rich depending on the plate. Works cleanly.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Faroe Island Salmon": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, low tannin. The classic salmon-and-Pinot move; the low tannin won't clash with the Faroe's fat and the forest-floor savory depth meets the salmon's richness. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Filet Mignon": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, whisper of spice, refined structure, medium body, low tannin. The elegant body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the forest-floor note plays against escargot-butter toppers. Peak by-the-glass Oregon Pinot for filet — accessible serious Pinot for the delicate cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|French Onion": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts French Onion's caramelized beef-broth weight and the forest-floor earth plays against the sweet onion depth. Peak by-the-glass Pinot for French Onion — Avni's refined Willamette character meets the classic without crushing the gruyère crust. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Grilled Caesar": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, spice, medium body. The acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the forest-floor plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as by-the-glass Pinot for grilled Caesar — unconventional red choice for the layered salad.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Gumbo": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts the carrots' caramelized honey sweetness and the forest-floor earth plays against roasted-root depth. Peaks from the dish's side; on this wine's card it's strong — below peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins. Confident pour when a guest anchors on Honey Roasted Carrots. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|House Wedge": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, spice, refined structure. The low tannin and high acid cut iceberg-and-bacon and the dark cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak by-the-glass Pinot for the wedge — Oregon elegance reframes the classic steakhouse salad. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Kansas City": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, whisper of spice. The medium body works against Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip though the low tannin falls short of the char-requirement of bolder Cabs. Strong by-the-glass Pinot for a guest wanting lighter red with the strip.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Loaded Potato": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts the soup's bacon-cheddar weight and the forest-floor earth plays against the loaded toppings. Peaks from the dish's side; on this wine's card it's strong — below peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins. Confident pour when a guest anchors on Loaded Potato. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Lobster Mac": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, low tannin. The forest floor meets truffle earthiness directly; low tannin keeps the lobster's delicacy intact and doesn't bully the cream. Non-obvious red route for the mac on a mixed table. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Market Fish": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this delicate seafood's mild, flaky flesh. The elegant red's cherry-bright, low-tannin frame threads cleanly through the fish — classic Burgundy logic. A strong cross-category match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Mocha Creme": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir's Willamette layered Pinot character fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Mushroom Bisque": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The forest-floor earth meshes directly with mushroom umami and the silky body lifts the cream base. Peak Oregon Pinot for the bisque — earth-on-earth pairing, refined Willamette character on the umami classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Mushrooms": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this earthy, umami-packed side's mushroom-and-truffle depth. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. Strong — the wine refreshes the side's richness.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir's Willamette layered Pinot character flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir belongs on the steak course.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Porterhouse": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir -- Willamette Pinot, red plum, forest floor. The layered Pinot body matches the dual-cut steak, and the forest-floor edge frames the cut. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Prime Tartare": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, whisper of spice, medium body. The low tannin leaves room for raw-beef delicacy and the cherry-forest-floor plays against egg yolk and capers. Peak by-the-glass Pinot for tartare — textbook red-with-raw-beef without overwhelming the dish. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Rainbow Trout": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts trout's delicate flesh but even the lightest red reads heavier than freshwater fish demands. Works as Oregon Pinot for rainbow trout — acceptable red; crisp whites peak higher on the delicate classic.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Roast Half Chicken": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, spice, medium body. The refined structure plays against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong by-the-glass Oregon Pinot for chicken — elegant red across most seasonal preparations.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts the soup's roasted-pepper sweetness and the forest-floor earth plays against chickpea's earthy base. Peaks from the dish's side; on this wine's card it's strong — below peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins. Confident pour when a guest anchors on Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts halibut's firm flesh and the cherry plays against sweet soy glaze. Strong Oregon Pinot for salt-cured halibut — unconventional red on the cured classic.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this light, green vegetable's clean herbal edge. The elegant red's earthy edge meets light green or sweet root — cherry and earth hold the pairing together. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Seafood Tower": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir's Willamette layered Pinot character crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir on the steak course instead.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Seared Scallops": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, spice, medium body, low tannin. The soft tannin leaves room for scallop sweetness and the cherry-spice plays against caramelized-exterior char. Peak by-the-glass Pinot for scallops — textbook delicate-red-with-sweet-shellfish pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Seasonal Soup": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this rich, creamy soup's dairy-driven warmth. The elegant red's cherry and earth anchor into rich soup depth — acidity cuts the cream. Strong — bright fruit lifts the weight.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Seasonal Vegetables": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this light, green vegetable's clean herbal edge. The elegant red's earthy edge meets light green or sweet root — cherry and earth hold the pairing together. A strong vegetable-friendly red choice. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Shrimp Bisque": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, spice, refined structure. The acid cuts bisque's cream weight and the forest-floor earth plays against shellfish umami and sherry. Peak by-the-glass Oregon Pinot for shrimp bisque — elegant red against the rich shellfish soup. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Shrimp Cocktail": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness. Elegant red's cherry and silk mirror the starter's register — light-on-light or bright-on-rich depending on the plate. Works cleanly.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Swordfish": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts swordfish's meaty firm flesh and the forest-floor earth plays against the grilled char. Peak Oregon Pinot for swordfish — the fish-friendliest red on the grilled classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|The Tomahawk": "Willamette Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — low-tannin Oregon Pinot has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds with structure; keep Lingua Franca for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Tomato Basil": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk handles tomato's acidity and the cherry plays against basil's aromatic lift. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Tomato Basil but below the wine's peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Truffle Fries": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this earthy, umami-packed side's mushroom-and-truffle depth. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. Works — clean if not dominant.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts rare-seared tuna's lean flesh and the cherry plays against sesame-crust and honey sambal. Peak Oregon Pinot for tuxedo tuna — refined red on the rare-seared classic. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts the curry's coconut-milk weight and the forest-floor earth plays against chickpea depth. Peaks from the dish's side; on this wine's card it's strong — below peaks with duck, lamb, and mushroom-sauced proteins. Confident pour when a guest anchors on Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Little Book|Asparagus": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sweet-oak depth has nothing to bridge into green vegetable freshness. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Little Book for the steak course.",
  "Little Book|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Little Book with the au gratin — Beam blended cask-strength against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Little Book handles the richness at chapter-series register.",
  "Little Book|Beignets": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon depth settles into the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "Little Book|Bone Marrow": "Little Book with bone marrow — Beam Fred Noe annual against umami fat. Excellent; Little Book cuts marrow at barrel-proof register with chapter-series distinction.",
  "Little Book|Bone-In Filet": "Little Book's cask-strength oak character sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth carries the cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Little Book|Broccoli Cheddar": "Little Book's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the single-barrel depth is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Little Book|Broccolini": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the spirit needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Little Book lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Little Book|Brussels and Belly": "Little Book with Brussels + Belly — Jim Beam Fred Noe blended cask-strength against maple-glazed pork belly. Excellent; Little Book meets the glaze with chapter-series intensity.",
  "Little Book|Burrata": "Little Book with burrata — Jim Beam Little Book against fresh dairy. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates burrata.",
  "Little Book|Butternut Squash": "Little Book's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the squash bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "Little Book|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Jim Beam Little Book. Works; Little Book meets the cake at chapter-series register.",
  "Little Book|Cheesecake": "Little Book with cheesecake — Jim Beam Little Book cask-strength against custard. Avoid; barrel-proof intensity is wrong for custard's delicate character.",
  "Little Book|Chilean Seabass": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Little Book for the steak course.",
  "Little Book|Chocolate Brownie": "Little Book with the chocolate brownie — annual blended cask-strength against cocoa. Excellent; Little Book's chapter-series intensity meets cocoa at barrel-proof register.",
  "Little Book|Chocolate Cake": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the brown-spirit warmth threads the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Little Book|Clam Chowder": "Little Book's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the oak depth lays under the chowder, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; distinctive finish over-specified.",
  "Little Book|Cowboy Ribeye": "Little Book with the cowboy ribeye — Jim Beam's Fred Noe annual blended whiskey series (each \"chapter\" a different artistic blend), Booker Noe's son's creative limited release. Excellent; Little Book's chapter-series craft is the featured conversation-piece barrel-proof-ribeye pairing — every pour is a story about which chapter you're serving.",
  "Little Book|Crab Cake": "Little Book with crab cake — Fred Noe cask-strength against delicate lump crab. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Little Book|Creamed Spinach": "Little Book with creamed spinach — Jim Beam Little Book against dairy-rich side. Strong; Little Book's cask-strength cuts the cream at Fred-Noe-artistic register.",
  "Little Book|Creamy Potato": "Little Book's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Little Book|Creme Brulee": "Little Book's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; AE Triple Cask's layered character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Little Book|Escargot": "Little Book's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon depth settles into the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; extended-maturation character sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Little Book|Faroe Island Salmon": "Little Book with the salmon — Beam Little Book cask-strength against oily salmon. Avoid; barrel-proof bourbon too intense for fish.",
  "Little Book|Filet Mignon": "Little Book with the filet — Beam Fred Noe annual cask-strength against tenderloin. Works; Little Book's intensity is over-specified for filet — save the chapter-series bottle for the ribeye/Tomahawk.",
  "Little Book|French Onion": "Little Book's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the caramel-spice register matches the French onion, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; HH 20 is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Little Book|Grilled Caesar": "Little Book with the grilled Caesar — Fred Noe annual against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Little Book is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Little Book|Gumbo": "Little Book's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the brown-spirit warmth threads the gumbo, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; boutique-sourced doesn't drive.",
  "Little Book|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- bourbon weight buries the glaze's sweetness instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Little Book belongs on the steak course.",
  "Little Book|House Wedge": "Little Book's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the brown-spirit warmth threads the wedge, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; boutique covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Little Book|Kansas City": "Little Book with the KC — Jim Beam Little Book series against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Little Book's cask-strength annual-blend handles strip marbling at Fred-Noe-artistic register.",
  "Little Book|Loaded Potato": "Little Book's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; Voyage 23 frames the cut at ocean-finish register.",
  "Little Book|Lobster Mac": "Little Book's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon backbone carries the lobster mac, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; boutique character slightly over-specified.",
  "Little Book|Market Fish": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Little Book for the steak course.",
  "Little Book|Mocha Creme": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon weight handles the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; bold proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Little Book|Mushroom Bisque": "Little Book's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the caramel-spice register matches the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; blend character covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Little Book|Mushrooms": "Little Book with mushrooms — Jim Beam Little Book annual against earthy umami. Strong; Little Book's cask-strength character threads mushrooms at Fred-Noe-artistic register.",
  "Little Book|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet Fred Noe annual. Strong; Little Book's cask-strength handles dessert density at chapter-series register.",
  "Little Book|Porterhouse": "Little Book with the porterhouse — Beam Fred Noe annual against strip-and-filet. Strong; Little Book frames the two-cut plate at chapter-series register.",
  "Little Book|Prime Tartare": "Little Book's cask-strength oak character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Little Book|Rainbow Trout": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Little Book belongs on the steak course.",
  "Little Book|Roast Half Chicken": "Little Book with the chicken — Fred Noe annual against roasted poultry. Avoid; barrel-proof is over-specified for chicken.",
  "Little Book|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon body sits with the chickpea soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; novelty maturation at matched register.",
  "Little Book|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Little Book for the steak course.",
  "Little Book|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side reads small under oak-and-vanilla density. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Little Book for the steak course.",
  "Little Book|Seafood Tower": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Little Book for the steak course.",
  "Little Book|Seared Scallops": "Little Book with seared scallops — Beam blended cask-strength against scallop delicacy. Avoid; barrel-proof wrong for scallops.",
  "Little Book|Seasonal Soup": "Little Book's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Little Book|Seasonal Vegetables": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overshadows the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- sweet-oak depth has nothing to bridge into green vegetable freshness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Little Book for the steak course.",
  "Little Book|Shrimp Bisque": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the brown-spirit warmth threads the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; JD 12 handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Little Book|Shrimp Cocktail": "Little Book with shrimp cocktail — Fred Noe annual against cold shrimp. Avoid; barrel-proof wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Little Book|Swordfish": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Little Book belongs on the steak course.",
  "Little Book|The Tomahawk": "Little Book with the Tomahawk — Fred Noe annual blended whiskey against 32oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Little Book's cask-strength blended character handles Tomahawk intensity with Fred-Noe-artistic-blend distinction.",
  "Little Book|Tomato Basil": "Little Book's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon body sits with the tomato basil, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Little Book|Truffle Fries": "Little Book with truffle fries — Beam blended cask-strength against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Little Book meets the earthy-rich at chapter-series register.",
  "Little Book|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Little Book for the steak course.",
  "Little Book|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Little Book's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon body sits with the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the intensity overpowers this dish.",
  "Loaded Potato|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the oak frames the cream. Works; wine-cask over-specified.",
  "Loaded Potato|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the caramel-spice register matches the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Loaded Potato|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's bold cherry-coffee cassis leans against the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Loaded Potato|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals leans against the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Loaded Potato|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the scotch depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Loaded Potato|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the scotch backbone matches the loaded potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the blend's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Loaded Potato|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's rested-agave body sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Loaded Potato|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the aged-agave weight wraps the loaded potato soup, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; accessible aged composed with this dish.",
  "Loaded Potato|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight aligns with the soup's cheddar-bacon richness and the apple-pear lifts the loaded base. Strong accessible Chardonnay for loaded potato — California oak on the classic.",
  "Loaded Potato|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis finds neutral with the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis cuts the cheese richness. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Loaded Potato|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin sits beside the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Loaded Potato|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Loaded Potato|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Ancho Reyes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Loaded Potato|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side; save the rare bottle for the main.",
  "Loaded Potato|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; a gentle composed match.",
  "Loaded Potato|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon backbone carries the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the match is gentle and approachable.",
