  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Mushrooms": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the mushrooms — premium sourced rye against earthy umami. Strong; boutique-blend character layers into mushroom depth.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the PB brownie — sourced premium rye against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; boutique-blend depth gives composed dessert character.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Porterhouse": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the porterhouse — sourced boutique across strip and filet. Strong; blended character covers both cuts at premium-sourced register.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Prime Tartare": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the tartare — premium sourced rye against raw beef. Strong; the boutique-blend is a capable counterpoint to tartare.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Rainbow Trout": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Lock Stock & Barrel Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the rye spice cuts the roast chicken, and the rye edge frames the plate. Works; a gentle composed match.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the dry rye body wraps the chickpea soup, and the rye-grain bite cuts the bowl. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th handles the richness at annual-release register.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Lock Stock & Barrel Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye backbone drives the garlic spinach, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the side. Works; the sweetness is a touch much for the salad's simplicity.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Seafood Tower": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Lock Stock & Barrel Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Seared Scallops": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against sourced rye. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Seasonal Soup": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the rye backbone drives the seasonal soup, and the rye-grain bite cuts the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the seasonal vegetables, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the side. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the rye-and-oak weight handles the shrimp bisque, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; bold intensity slightly over-specified.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against premium sourced. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Swordfish": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Lock Stock & Barrel Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|The Tomahawk": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the Tomahawk — sourced rye, often MGP-blended at premium age. Boutique brand sourcing premium liquid; distinct from distillery-specific ryes by blender approach. The Tomahawk gets a premium-blended rye frame. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Tomato Basil": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the rye spice cuts the tomato basil, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Truffle Fries": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with truffle fries — premium sourced against truffle-parmesan. The boutique-blend character gives the side a premium-sourced frame. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Lock Stock & Barrel Rye for the steak course.",
  "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye's rye-spice character leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the rye spice cuts the curry, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the bowl. Works; Remus 15 handles dessert density at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Long Road Gin|Asparagus": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals sits alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Long Road Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Long Road Gin|Beignets": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- gin's herbal edge has no foil against warm pastry sugar — sharp meets sweet, neither wins. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Long Road Gin for the steak course.",
  "Long Road Gin|Bone Marrow": "Long Road with bone marrow — Grand Rapids Michigan-botanical against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Long Road Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Long Road Gin for another course.",
  "Long Road Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Long Road Gin|Broccolini": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals leans against the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Long Road Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals reads alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Long Road Gin|Burrata": "Long Road with burrata — Long Road Distillers, Grand Rapids, ultra-local with Michigan botanicals and estate pride. Excellent; Long Road is Grand Rapids-proud gin pairing — parallel to Eastern Kille as the GR-craft tandem, complementing burrata with hometown register.",
  "Long Road Gin|Butternut Squash": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Long Road Gin|Carrot Cake": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Long Road Gin for the steak course.",
  "Long Road Gin|Cheesecake": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Long Road Gin for the steak course.",
  "Long Road Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals lifts the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Long Road Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Long Road Gin for the steak course.",
  "Long Road Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Long Road Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Long Road Gin|Clam Chowder": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Long Road Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Long Road Gin for another course.",
  "Long Road Gin|Crab Cake": "Long Road with crab cake — Grand Rapids ultra-local gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Long Road's estate-pride Michigan-botanical profile frames crab with Grand Rapids distinction.",
  "Long Road Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Long Road Gin|Creamy Potato": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals sits alongside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Long Road Gin|Creme Brulee": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Long Road Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Long Road Gin|Escargot": "Long Road with escargot — GR craft Michigan-botanical gin against garlic-butter snails. Strong; Long Road meets butter at hometown register; Eastern Kille's classic London Dry cuts it sharper.",
  "Long Road Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Long Road with the salmon — GR Michigan-botanical gin against oily salmon. Strong; Long Road's estate-pride frames the fish at hometown register; Iron Fish's northern-Michigan farm profile is still the distinctive Local MI Gin-salmon match.",
  "Long Road Gin|Filet Mignon": "Long Road with the filet — ultra-local Grand Rapids gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Long Road Gin|French Onion": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Long Road Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Long Road with the grilled Caesar — Grand Rapids estate gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Long Road meets the char at hometown register; Knickerbocker's hop-influenced profile is the featured Local MI Gin-Caesar match.",
  "Long Road Gin|Gumbo": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Long Road Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals leans against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Long Road Gin|House Wedge": "Long Road with the House Wedge — ultra-local GR gin against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Long Road's Michigan-botanical character threads the wedge with hometown pride.",
  "Long Road Gin|Kansas City": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Long Road Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Long Road Gin|Loaded Potato": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals sits alongside the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Long Road Gin|Lobster Mac": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals holds with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Long Road Gin|Market Fish": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals finds register with the fish's clean flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Long Road Gin|Mocha Creme": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Long Road Gin for the steak course.",
  "Long Road Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals reads alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Long Road Gin|Mushrooms": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals sits alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Long Road Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Long Road Gin for the steak course.",
  "Long Road Gin|Porterhouse": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Long Road Gin on another course instead.",
  "Long Road Gin|Prime Tartare": "Long Road with the tartare — Grand Rapids estate-pride gin against raw beef. Strong; Long Road's Michigan-botanical character supports tartare at local register.",
  "Long Road Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals lifts the trout's delicate flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Long Road Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Long Road with the chicken — GR Michigan-botanical craft against roasted poultry. Works; Long Road supports chicken at hometown register.",
  "Long Road Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Long Road Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals sits with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Long Road Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Long Road Gin|Seafood Tower": "Long Road with the seafood tower — ultra-local Grand Rapids gin against the raw bar. Strong; Long Road is a GR-pride story-driven tower pairing.",
  "Long Road Gin|Seared Scallops": "Long Road with seared scallops — Grand Rapids estate gin against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Long Road's Michigan-botanical profile frames scallops at hometown register.",
  "Long Road Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Long Road Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals sits beside the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Long Road Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Long Road with shrimp bisque — Grand Rapids estate-pride gin against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Long Road's Michigan-botanical character rides the bisque at hometown register.",
  "Long Road Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Long Road with shrimp cocktail — GR craft Michigan-botanical gin against cold shrimp. Excellent; Long Road delivers the classic cold app with Grand Rapids-pride hometown character.",
  "Long Road Gin|Swordfish": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Long Road Gin|The Tomahawk": "Long Road with the Tomahawk — GR gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Long Road Gin|Tomato Basil": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals reads alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Long Road Gin|Truffle Fries": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals stays alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Long Road Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift sits alongside the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the floral edge frames the tuna, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Sapphire's softness meets the bleu at accessible register.",
  "Long Road Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Long Road Gin's crisp citrus-juniper botanicals sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Asparagus": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the smoky agave register handles the asparagus, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; the opener sits without competing.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's earthen-pit smoke profile finds neutral with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the espadín-and-smoke body wraps the au gratin, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; too light for dessert density at this level.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Beignets": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's wood-fire mezcal body reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the earthen-pit smoke carries the beignets, and the campfire register frames the dessert. Works; the side-salad call holds.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Bone Marrow": "Los Vecinos Mezcal: 'The Neighbors' — Oaxacan artisanal pit-roasted expressions from multiple mezcaleros. Excellent on marrow; the authentic-traditional-smoke meets umami fat at craft register — the mezcal the purist reaches for.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Bone-In Filet": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the wood-fire body meets the bone-enhanced filet, and the earthy backbone threads the cut. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Broccoli Cheddar": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a mezcal. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Los Vecinos Mezcal belongs on the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Broccolini": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the wood-fire body meets the broccolini, and the earthy backbone threads the side. Works; the side call holds at neutral register.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet Los Vecinos. Excellent; the traditional-smoke rides the glaze and belly at craft-mezcal register — smoke meets smoke-adjacent belly fat.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Burrata": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- smoke and fresh dairy refuse each other — texture reads broken. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a mezcal. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Butternut Squash": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- mezcal smoke fights the soup's cream — smoke has no bridge into dairy delicacy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a mezcal. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Los Vecinos Mezcal on the steak course instead.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Carrot Cake": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's wood-fire mezcal body reads alongside the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the rustic mezcal weight sits with the carrot cake, and the mineral smoke plays against the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Cheesecake": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overpowers the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a mezcal. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Chilean Seabass": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- mezcal smoke and the fish's natural oils refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a mezcal. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Chocolate Brownie": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's earthen-pit smoke profile sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rustic mezcal weight sits with the brownie, and the earthy backbone threads the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Chocolate Cake": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's wood-fire mezcal body finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rustic mezcal weight sits with the chocolate cake, and the mineral smoke plays against the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Clam Chowder": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- mezcal smoke fights the soup's cream — smoke has no bridge into dairy delicacy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a mezcal. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Los Vecinos Mezcal on the steak course instead.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: 26oz bone-in cap-fat meets Los Vecinos traditional-pit-smoke. Excellent; the artisanal-mezcal character frames the ribeye at craft-authenticity register — real Oaxaca on the cut.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Crab Cake": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- mezcal smoke obliterates delicate shellfish — needs sparkling or sancerre. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a mezcal. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Creamed Spinach": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's wood-fire mezcal body leans against the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the earthen-pit smoke carries the creamed spinach, and the mineral smoke plays against the side. Works; the call holds at neutral register.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Creamy Potato": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a mezcal. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Los Vecinos Mezcal on the steak course instead.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Creme Brulee": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rustic mezcal weight sits with the brulee, and the green-agave edge brightens the dessert. Works; the two desserts share the close without crowding.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Escargot": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character leans against the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the wood-fire body meets the buttered escargot, and the earthy backbone threads the starter. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Faroe Island Salmon": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the main fish course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a mezcal. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Filet Mignon": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight reads alongside the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the earthen-pit smoke carries the lean buttery filet, and the earthy backbone threads the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|French Onion": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the rustic mezcal weight sits with the French onion, and the mineral smoke plays against the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Grilled Caesar": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register leans against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the wood-fire body meets the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Gumbo": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a mezcal. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the earthen-pit smoke carries the honey carrots, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|House Wedge": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's earthen-pit smoke profile finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the espadín-and-smoke body wraps the wedge, and the wood-smoke note underlines the bowl. Works; the dessert sits cleanly against the meal's register.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Kansas City": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's agave-pit-smoke character sits with the strip's lean char — the agave smoke frames the lean cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Loaded Potato": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the wood-fire body meets the loaded potato soup, and the earthy backbone threads the bowl. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Lobster Mac": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the wood-fire body meets the lobster mac, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Market Fish": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- mezcal smoke buries the delicate flesh — the fish has no foil against agave + smoke. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a mezcal. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Mocha Creme": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the espadín-and-smoke body wraps the mocha creme, and the wood-smoke note underlines the dessert. Works; the opener sits without competing.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Mushroom Bisque": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the espadín-and-smoke body wraps the mushroom bisque, and the green-agave edge brightens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Mushrooms": "* Los Vecinos Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight runs straight into the mushrooms' earthy umami weight: the espadín-and-smoke body wraps the mushrooms, and the earthy backbone threads the side. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's wood-fire mezcal body reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rustic mezcal weight sits with the PB brownie, and the earthy backbone threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Porterhouse": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's wood-fire mezcal body sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the rustic mezcal weight sits with the dual-cut steak, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: hand-cut raw beef meets Los Vecinos Mezcal. Excellent; the authentic-pit-roast smoke frames the tartare at artisanal register — the most intense of the three mezcals against the raw beef.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Rainbow Trout": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- mezcal smoke buries the delicate flesh — the fish has no foil against agave + smoke. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a mezcal. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Roast Half Chicken": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the espadín-and-smoke body wraps the roast chicken, and the green-agave edge brightens the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a mezcal. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the main fish course. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a mezcal. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Los Vecinos Mezcal on the steak course instead.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's wood-fire mezcal body sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rustic mezcal weight sits with the garlic spinach, and the earthy backbone threads the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Seafood Tower": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- mezcal smoke is too forward for the table's opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a mezcal. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Los Vecinos Mezcal on the steak course instead.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Los Vecinos. Avoid; traditional smoke wrong for scallops. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Seasonal Soup": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the soup-salad course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a mezcal. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Seasonal Vegetables": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's rustic mezcal weight reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rustic mezcal weight sits with the seasonal vegetables, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; the close composes without strain.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Shrimp Bisque": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register leans against the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the smoky agave register handles the shrimp bisque, and the green-agave edge brightens the bowl. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Shrimp Cocktail": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- mezcal smoke obliterates delicate shellfish — needs sparkling or sancerre. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a mezcal. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Swordfish": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the main fish course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a mezcal. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Los Vecinos Mezcal on the steak course instead.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk: 36oz bone-on showpiece meets Los Vecinos. Excellent; the traditional-smoke-forward character works the dramatic cut at craft register — authentic smoke on the most dramatic cut we plate.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Tomato Basil": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- mezcal's smoke crowds the broth's clean register without lifting it. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a mezcal. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Los Vecinos Mezcal belongs on the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Truffle Fries": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's agave-pit-smoke character holds the line with the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the agave smoke layers into the rich side, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character doubles down on the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- mezcal smoke and the seared crust double up smoky char without contrast. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a mezcal. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Los Vecinos Mezcal|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- mezcal's smoke crowds the broth's clean register without lifting it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a mezcal. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Los Vecinos Mezcal for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Asparagus": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- a luxury cognac belongs alone after the meal, not alongside a side. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an icon cognac. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Louis XIII with the au gratin — Grande Champagne luxury icon against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; the side is not the place to pour Louis XIII — the bottle demands main-course or sipping-alone attention.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Beignets": "Louis XIII with beignets — 1,200-eaux-de-vie cognac against casual fried dough. Works; save the Louis XIII for a thoughtful dessert like carrot cake or chocolate brownie — beignets are a Hennessy-register pairing.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Bone Marrow": "Louis XIII with bone marrow — 40-100 year aged Grande Champagne against umami fat. Excellent; the marrow's deep umami is the rare flavor intense enough to meet Louis XIII without the bottle overpowering or being overpowered. The benchmark cognac-marrow experience at legendary register.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Bone-In Filet": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- an icon cognac is best as a separate occasion — pair the steak with a Cab or bourbon, then close with the cognac. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not an icon cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Louis XIII Cognac for another course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Broccoli Cheddar": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- an icon cognac on a soup-salad course is wrong moment — its register demands the close, not the open. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an icon cognac. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Broccolini": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- a luxury cognac belongs alone after the meal, not alongside a side. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an icon cognac. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Louis XIII Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Brussels and Belly": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- a luxury cognac belongs alone after the meal, not alongside a side. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not an icon cognac. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Burrata": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- a luxury cognac is wrong moment for the opener — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an icon cognac. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Louis XIII Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Butternut Squash": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- an icon cognac on a soup-salad course is wrong moment — its register demands the close, not the open. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an icon cognac. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Louis XIII Cognac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Carrot Cake": "Louis XIII with carrot cake — 40-100 year aged cognac against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; the cake's raisins and spice have enough depth to share space with Louis XIII without wasting the pour — the thoughtful dessert pairing for the bottle.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Cheesecake": "Louis XIII with cheesecake — Grande Champagne luxury icon against custard dessert. Works; cheesecake is lighter than Louis XIII deserves — the Rémy VSOP tier is the appropriate cognac for this dessert. Save the Louis XIII bottle for the protein course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Chilean Seabass": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- an icon cognac belongs at the end of the meal as a special-occasion pour — not the main course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an icon cognac. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Louis XIII Cognac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Chocolate Brownie": "Louis XIII with the chocolate brownie — luxury icon against cocoa. Strong; warm cocoa is dense enough to engage Louis XIII without being overshadowed — the featured dessert for the bottle if the guest wants one. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Chocolate Cake": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the prestige-blend weight carries the chocolate cake, and the caramel rounds the dessert. Works; the side is not the place to pour Louis XIII — the bottle demands main-course or sipping-alone attention.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Clam Chowder": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- an icon cognac on a soup-salad course is wrong moment — its register demands the close, not the open. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an icon cognac. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Louis XIII Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Cowboy Ribeye": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- an icon cognac is best as a separate occasion — pair the steak with a Cab or bourbon, then close with the cognac. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not an icon cognac. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Louis XIII Cognac for another course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Crab Cake": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- a luxury cognac is wrong moment for the opener — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an icon cognac. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Creamed Spinach": "Louis XIII with creamed spinach — luxury icon against dairy-rich side. Works; creamed spinach is a side, not the centerpiece — Louis XIII's aged depth is wasted on the accompaniment. Save the bottle for the filet or marrow course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Creamy Potato": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- an icon cognac on a soup-salad course is wrong moment — its register demands the close, not the open. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an icon cognac. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Louis XIII Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Creme Brulee": "Louis XIII with creme brulee — 40-100 year cognac against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Works; brulee is a Rémy VSOP-level pairing — Louis XIII's complexity is wasted on caramel-sugar the Fine Champagne-level sibling handles perfectly. Save the bottle.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Escargot": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- a luxury cognac is wrong moment for the opener — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not an icon cognac. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Louis XIII Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Faroe Island Salmon": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- an icon cognac belongs at the end of the meal as a special-occasion pour — not the main course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an icon cognac. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Filet Mignon": "Louis XIII with the filet — Rémy Martin luxury icon, 1,200+ eaux-de-vie aged 40-100 years, Grande Champagne only, Baccarat crystal decanter (~$4,000/bottle). Excellent; this is one of exactly three pairings on the menu where Louis XIII actually earns its pour — tenderloin's quiet finesse is the only steak that doesn't compete with the bottle's 40-100 year complexity. Reverence, not just dinner.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|French Onion": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- an icon cognac on a soup-salad course is wrong moment — its register demands the close, not the open. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an icon cognac. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Louis XIII Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Grilled Caesar": "Louis XIII with the grilled Caesar — 40-100 year aged cognac against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; the Caesar is an appetizer-register dish — Louis XIII belongs with the main course or standalone. Save the bottle.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Gumbo": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- an icon cognac on a soup-salad course is wrong moment — its register demands the close, not the open. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an icon cognac. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- a luxury cognac belongs alone after the meal, not alongside a side. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an icon cognac. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|House Wedge": "Louis XIII with the House Wedge — luxury icon against iceberg and bleu. Works; the wedge is far too casual for Louis XIII — save the bottle for something worthy of its register.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Kansas City": "Louis XIII with the KC — luxury-icon Grande Champagne against an 18oz strip. Strong; the KC is heavy enough not to waste the bottle, though its marbling-forward character is more aligned with Courvoisier's drier oak than Louis XIII's 40-100 year complexity.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Loaded Potato": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- an icon cognac on a soup-salad course is wrong moment — its register demands the close, not the open. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an icon cognac. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Louis XIII Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Lobster Mac": "Louis XIII with lobster mac — 1,200-eaux-de-vie cognac against pasta-and-lobster. Works; the rich dish is over-specified for a bottle meant to be savored alone — save the Louis XIII.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Market Fish": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- an icon cognac belongs at the end of the meal as a special-occasion pour — not the main course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an icon cognac. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Louis XIII Cognac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Mocha Creme": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the icon-cognac register elevates the mocha creme, and the polished oak softens the dessert. Works; the side is not the place to pour Louis XIII — the bottle demands main-course or sipping-alone attention.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Mushroom Bisque": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- an icon cognac on a soup-salad course is wrong moment — its register demands the close, not the open. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an icon cognac. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Louis XIII Cognac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Mushrooms": "Louis XIII with mushrooms — 1,200-eaux-de-vie cognac against earthy umami. Strong; the mushrooms' earth-depth engages Louis XIII's aged complexity at reverent register.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet Baccarat-decanter cognac. Works; the PB's fatty density competes with Louis XIII's finesse — a Pierre Ferrand or Hennessy is the right cognac here. Save the bottle.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Porterhouse": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- an icon cognac is best as a separate occasion — pair the steak with a Cab or bourbon, then close with the cognac. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not an icon cognac. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Louis XIII Cognac for another course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Prime Tartare": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- a luxury cognac is wrong moment for the opener — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not an icon cognac. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Rainbow Trout": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the trout's delicate flesh -- an icon cognac belongs at the end of the meal as a special-occasion pour — not the main course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an icon cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Louis XIII Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Roast Half Chicken": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- an icon cognac belongs at the end of the meal as a special-occasion pour — not the main course. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an icon cognac. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Louis XIII Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- an icon cognac on a soup-salad course is wrong moment — its register demands the close, not the open. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an icon cognac. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Louis XIII Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- an icon cognac belongs at the end of the meal as a special-occasion pour — not the main course. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an icon cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Louis XIII Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- a luxury cognac belongs alone after the meal, not alongside a side. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an icon cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Seafood Tower": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- a luxury cognac is wrong moment for the opener — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an icon cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Louis XIII Cognac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Seared Scallops": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- a luxury cognac is wrong moment for the opener — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not an icon cognac. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Seasonal Soup": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- an icon cognac on a soup-salad course is wrong moment — its register demands the close, not the open. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an icon cognac. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Seasonal Vegetables": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- a luxury cognac belongs alone after the meal, not alongside a side. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an icon cognac. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Shrimp Bisque": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- an icon cognac on a soup-salad course is wrong moment — its register demands the close, not the open. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an icon cognac. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Louis XIII Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Shrimp Cocktail": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- a luxury cognac is wrong moment for the opener — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an icon cognac. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Louis XIII Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Swordfish": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- an icon cognac belongs at the end of the meal as a special-occasion pour — not the main course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an icon cognac. Avoid; one of those is the move. Louis XIII Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|The Tomahawk": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- an icon cognac is best as a separate occasion — pair the steak with a Cab or bourbon, then close with the cognac. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not an icon cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Louis XIII Cognac for another course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Tomato Basil": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- an icon cognac on a soup-salad course is wrong moment — its register demands the close, not the open. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an icon cognac. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Louis XIII Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Truffle Fries": "Louis XIII with truffle fries — 1,200-eaux-de-vie Baccarat-decanter cognac against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; earthy truffle depth is tier-appropriate for a bottle that demands earthy reverence — the only side on the menu that can share the table with Louis XIII without feeling casual by comparison.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- an icon cognac belongs at the end of the meal as a special-occasion pour — not the main course. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an icon cognac. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Louis XIII Cognac|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- an icon cognac on a soup-salad course is wrong moment — its register demands the close, not the open. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an icon cognac. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Louis XIII Cognac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Asparagus": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge doubles down on the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Lucano Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Beignets": "Lucano with beignets — floral-anise amaro against fried dough. Works; the anise character gives the casual dessert distinctive character.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Bone Marrow": "Lucano with bone marrow — Basilicata amaro against umami fat. Strong; floral-anise cuts through marrow distinctively.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Bone-In Filet": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- bitter botanicals on the cut fight the savory char without payoff. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Lucano Amaro lands on another course — not here.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Broccoli Cheddar": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Broccolini": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Brussels and Belly": "Lucano with Brussels + Belly — floral-anise amaro against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; the anise character cuts the glaze distinctively.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Burrata": "Lucano with burrata — Basilicata amaro against fresh dairy. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Butternut Squash": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Lucano Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Carrot Cake": "Lucano with carrot cake — Basilicata floral-anise amaro against cream cheese and cinnamon. The floral character and cake spice find each other distinctively. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Cheesecake": "Lucano with cheesecake — floral-anise Basilicata amaro against custard. The lighter profile and anise character give the cheesecake a distinctive post-dessert digestif. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Chilean Seabass": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bitter herbs on oily fish read metallic — the rich flesh deserves a crisp white. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Lucano Amaro on the steak course instead.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa meets Lucano — Basilicata amaro, floral with anise notes. Lighter than Averna, more distinctive than Montenegro. Anise-and-floral gives the brownie a distinctive digestif frame. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Chocolate Cake": "Lucano Amaro's crisp floral-anise sweetness matches the cake's layered chocolate — the bitter herb edge cuts dessert sweetness, and the anise threads the herb edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Clam Chowder": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Lucano Amaro on the steak course instead.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Cowboy Ribeye": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Lucano Amaro for another course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Crab Cake": "Lucano with crab cake — Basilicata amaro against delicate crab. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Creamed Spinach": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Creamy Potato": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Creme Brulee": "Lucano with creme brulee — floral-anise amaro against vanilla custard. The anise notes thread through the brulee's vanilla distinctively. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Escargot": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge doubles down on the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- aperitivo herbs and a herb-bright opener compound the bitter register — no contrast. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Lucano Amaro on the steak course instead.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Faroe Island Salmon": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bitter herbs on oily fish read metallic — the rich flesh deserves a crisp white. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Lucano Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Filet Mignon": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Lucano Amaro belongs on another course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|French Onion": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Grilled Caesar": "Lucano with the grilled Caesar — floral-anise amaro against charred romaine. Strong; anise character finds the Caesar's anchovy distinctively.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Gumbo": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Lucano Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lucano Amaro|House Wedge": "Lucano with the House Wedge — Basilicata amaro against iceberg and bleu. Works; floral-anise is a distinctive post-salad digestif.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Kansas City": "Lucano with the KC — Basilicata amaro as post-steak digestif. Strong; floral-anise settles the meal with distinctive character.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Loaded Potato": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Lucano Amaro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Lobster Mac": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Market Fish": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- aperitivo bitterness fights gentle flesh without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Mocha Creme": "Lucano Amaro's crisp floral-anise sweetness matches the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the bitter herb edge cuts dessert sweetness, and the anise underlines the cream. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Mushroom Bisque": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge fights the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- bitter herbs cut the bisque's round cream without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Mushrooms": "Lucano with the mushrooms — Basilicata amaro against earthy umami. Strong; floral-anise layers into mushroom depth distinctively.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet Basilicata floral-anise amaro. Anise notes find the dessert distinctively. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Porterhouse": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Lucano Amaro for another course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Prime Tartare": "Lucano with the tartare — floral-anise amaro against raw beef. Strong; the anise notes are a distinctive counterpoint to tartare.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Rainbow Trout": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Roast Half Chicken": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Lucano Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Lucano Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge doubles down on the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Seafood Tower": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Lucano Amaro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Seared Scallops": "Lucano with seared scallops — floral-anise amaro against scallop delicacy. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Seasonal Soup": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Lucano Amaro on the steak course instead.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Seasonal Vegetables": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge doubles down on the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Lucano Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Shrimp Bisque": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Lucano Amaro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Shrimp Cocktail": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- amaro bitterness on cold shellfish flattens the delicate brine — needs sparkling instead. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Swordfish": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bitter botanicals fight the savory sear — bitter against char without resolution. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Lucano Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lucano Amaro|The Tomahawk": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- aperitivo bitterness fights the headline cut's marbled char — bitter and savory pull apart. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Lucano Amaro lands on another course — not here.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Tomato Basil": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Lucano Amaro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Truffle Fries": "Lucano with truffle fries — Basilicata amaro against truffle-parmesan. Works; floral-anise is a distinctive counterpoint.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bitter botanicals fight the savory sear — bitter against char without resolution. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Lucano Amaro on the steak course instead.",
  "Lucano Amaro|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Lucano Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Asparagus": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Abruzzo Montepulciano d'Abruzzo — Lunaria earthy grippy, dark cherry, dried herbs, rustic medium body, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the dried herbs play against the lactic richness. Strong Italian-approachable red for au gratin — rustic character on the cheesy side.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Beignets": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Lunaria Coste di Moro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Bone Marrow": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin lifts the marrow's umami-fat depth — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Bone-In Filet": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's spice-driven red elegance leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the silky body carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the cherry-pepper note frames the cut. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Broccoli Cheddar": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Broccolini": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin plays cleanly against the broccolini's tender green — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this medium-bodied red's approachable fruit and food-friendly acidity. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. Strong — the wine refreshes the side's richness.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Burrata": "Rustic Abruzzo Montepulciano against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Lunaria's earthy grip overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Lunaria for the meat course where rustic grip earns the bottle.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Butternut Squash": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Carrot Cake": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Lunaria Coste di Moro on the steak course instead.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Cheesecake": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Lunaria Coste di Moro for the steak course.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Chilean Seabass": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Lunaria Coste di Moro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Chocolate Brownie": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Lunaria Coste di Moro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Chocolate Cake": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Lunaria Coste di Moro on the steak course instead.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Clam Chowder": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin holds with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this medium-bodied red's approachable fruit and food-friendly acidity. The elegant red's medium-light frame gets overrun by the bold, marbled steak — the wine shrinks. A weight mismatch; not the clean pairing. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Crab Cake": "Rustic Abruzzo Montepulciano against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Lunaria's earthy medium-tannin grip buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Lunaria for filet, strip, or rustic meat courses.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: this rich, creamy side's dairy-driven indulgence meets this medium-bodied red's approachable fruit and food-friendly acidity. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. Strong — the wine refreshes the side's richness.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Creamy Potato": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin leans against the potato soup's silky body — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Creme Brulee": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Lunaria Coste di Moro for the steak course.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Escargot": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Faroe Island Salmon": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Lunaria Coste di Moro for the steak course.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Filet Mignon": "Abruzzo Montepulciano d'Abruzzo — Lunaria earthy grippy, dark cherry, dried herbs, rustic medium body, medium tannin. The rustic structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the dried herbs play against escargot-butter. Peak Italian-approachable red for filet — great value on the delicate cut, Italian alternative to Cab. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|French Onion": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin holds with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Grilled Caesar": "Abruzzo Montepulciano d'Abruzzo — Lunaria earthy grippy, dark cherry, dried herbs, rustic medium body, medium tannin. The rustic grip cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the dark cherry plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak Italian-approachable red for grilled Caesar — rustic Italian reframes the layered salad. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Gumbo": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's silky red-fruit lift leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the silky body carries the honey carrots, and the cherry-pepper note frames the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|House Wedge": "Abruzzo Montepulciano d'Abruzzo — Lunaria earthy grippy, dark cherry, dried herbs, rustic medium body, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as Italian-approachable red for the wedge — rustic value reframes the classic salad.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Kansas City": "Abruzzo Montepulciano d'Abruzzo — Lunaria earthy grippy, dark cherry, dried herbs, rustic medium body, medium tannin. The rustic grip meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the dried herbs play against beefy char. Peak Italian-approachable red for the strip — great value on the American cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Loaded Potato": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin finds neutral with the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Lobster Mac": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin holds with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Market Fish": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Lunaria Coste di Moro lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Mocha Creme": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Lunaria Coste di Moro for the steak course.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Mushroom Bisque": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's spice-driven red elegance leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the silky body carries the mushroom bisque, and the cherry-pepper note frames the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: this earthy, umami-packed side's mushroom-and-truffle depth meets this medium-bodied red's approachable fruit and food-friendly acidity. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. A bright-meets-rich headliner. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Lunaria Coste di Moro for the steak course.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this medium-bodied red's approachable fruit and food-friendly acidity. The elegant red's medium-light frame gets overrun by the bold, marbled steak — the wine shrinks. Works only if the guest prizes restraint over match.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Prime Tartare": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Rainbow Trout": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Lunaria Coste di Moro for the steak course.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Roast Half Chicken": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin leans against the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the medium-body red supports the herb-crisped bird, and the smoke meets the char. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Lunaria Coste di Moro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin reads alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Seafood Tower": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Lunaria Coste di Moro belongs on the steak course.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Seared Scallops": "Rustic Abruzzo Montepulciano against scallop sweetness — Lunaria's earthy grip buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Lunaria for the steak course or rustic Italian pairings.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Seasonal Soup": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Seasonal Vegetables": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin holds with the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Shrimp Bisque": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin holds with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Shrimp Cocktail": "Rustic Abruzzo Montepulciano against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — earthy Lunaria and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Lunaria for rustic meat courses.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Swordfish": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Lunaria Coste di Moro on the steak course instead.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|The Tomahawk": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's silky red-fruit lift leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the silky body carries the 36oz showpiece, and the cherry-pepper note frames the cut. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Tomato Basil": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin sits beside the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Truffle Fries": "Abruzzo Montepulciano d'Abruzzo — Lunaria earthy grippy, dark cherry, dried herbs, rustic medium body, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts truffle-fry potato fat and the dried herbs play against the truffle-oil umami. Peak Italian-approachable red for truffle fries — rustic Italian character on the umami hit. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Lunaria Coste di Moro for the steak course.",
  "Lunaria Coste di Moro|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; capable alongside. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Asparagus": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the sherry-cask depth wraps the asparagus, and the dried-fruit-and-spice frames the side. Works; wine-cask complexity gives the dessert layered premium character.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Macallan 12 Sherry with the au gratin — entry-sherried Speyside against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Macallan 12's sherry depth handles the richness.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Beignets": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the sherry-cask depth wraps the beignets, and the dried-fruit-and-spice frames the dessert. Works; richer profile is slightly over-specified for the dessert.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Bone Marrow": "Macallan 12 Sherry with bone marrow — Oloroso-cask Speyside against umami fat. Strong; Macallan 12's sherry-cask depth frames the marrow where the bourbon-cask Speysides just sit — but Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Bone-In Filet": "Macallan 12 Sherry with the bone-in filet — 100% Oloroso Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Macallan 12's sherried-dried-fruit complements the cut's marrow-adjacent richness with benchmark depth.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Broccoli Cheddar": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the malt-driven body wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Broccolini": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the scotch backbone matches the broccolini, and the honey rounds the side. Works; gentle match at matched registers.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Brussels and Belly": "Macallan 12 Sherry with Brussels + Belly — sherried Speyside against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; Macallan 12's dried-fruit meets the glaze at benchmark register.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Burrata": "Macallan 12 Sherry with burrata — benchmark sherried Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Butternut Squash": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the squash bisque, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; curated character over-specified.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Carrot Cake": "Macallan 12 Sherry with carrot cake — 100% Oloroso-cask against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; sherry-cask raisin-and-spice marries the cake's raisins and cinnamon naturally — textbook sherried-Speyside dessert-matching.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Cheesecake": "Macallan 12 Sherry with cheesecake — benchmark sherried Speyside against custard. Strong; Macallan 12's Oloroso dried-fruit threads the cheesecake with sherry-cask depth.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Chilean Seabass": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Macallan 12 Sherry for the steak course.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Chocolate Brownie": "Macallan 12 Sherry with the chocolate brownie — 100% Oloroso-cask Speyside against cocoa. Excellent; the sherry-derived raisin-and-spice marries cocoa with textbook dessert-sherry resonance.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Chocolate Cake": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the malt-driven body wraps the chocolate cake, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the dessert. Works; boutique over-specified.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Clam Chowder": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the Highland register carries the chowder, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Cowboy Ribeye": "Macallan 12 Sherry with the cowboy ribeye — benchmark sherried Speyside against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Macallan 12 frames the ribeye with sherried depth; Macallan 18 or Balvenie 21 Portwood are the featured ribeye pairings.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Crab Cake": "Macallan 12 Sherry with crab cake — sherried Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Creamed Spinach": "Macallan 12 Sherry with creamed spinach — Oloroso-cask Speyside against dairy-rich side. Works; the sherry-cask character rides the cream at benchmark register.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Creamy Potato": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the scotch backbone matches the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the single-barrel depth is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Creme Brulee": "Macallan 12 Sherry with creme brulee — Oloroso-cask Speyside against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; Macallan 12's sherry-derived spice finds the caramel cleanly.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Escargot": "Macallan 12 Sherry -- Speyside, sherry-cask, dried fruit, spice. The sherry-cask depth wraps the buttered escargot, and the dried-fruit-and-spice frames the starter. Strong; the 90-proof balance covers both cuts cleanly; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Faroe Island Salmon": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Macallan 12 Sherry lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets 100% Oloroso sherry-cask-matured 12yr Speyside, the entry sherried-Speyside benchmark. Macallan 12's dried-fruit-and-spice character frames tenderloin with sherry-cask depth — where Glenfiddich 12's bourbon-cask leans apple/pear and Balvenie 12 leans vanilla, Macallan 12 leans raisin-and-spice. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|French Onion": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the scotch depth settles into the French onion, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Grilled Caesar": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character leans against the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the scotch backbone matches the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Works; boutique-blend wasted on salad.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Gumbo": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the gumbo, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; KBS distinctiveness is slightly over-specified for the two-cut plate.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Highland register carries the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; wine-cask complexity gives the dessert layered premium character.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|House Wedge": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the scotch backbone matches the wedge, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; bonded weight slightly over-specified.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Kansas City": "Macallan 12 Sherry with the KC — Oloroso Speyside against an 18oz strip. Strong; Macallan 12's sherry-cask depth handles the strip, though Macallan 18's deeper aging is the featured Sherried Speyside-KC match.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Loaded Potato": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the malt-driven body wraps the loaded potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; distinctive finish over-specified.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Lobster Mac": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the scotch backbone matches the lobster mac, and the oak softens the cream. Works; OGD 16 frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Market Fish": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character flattens the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Macallan 12 Sherry lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Mocha Creme": "Macallan 12 Sherry with mocha creme — Oloroso-cask Speyside against coffee-chocolate dessert. Works; Macallan 12's sherried-raisin threads the mocha at benchmark register.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Mushroom Bisque": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the malt-and-oak threads the mushroom bisque, and the oak balances the umami register. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Mushrooms": "Macallan 12 Sherry with mushrooms — Oloroso-matured Speyside against earthy umami. Excellent; the sherry-cask dried-fruit threads the mushrooms with baseline sherried-Speyside character.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Macallan 12 Sherry with the PB brownie — 100% Oloroso Speyside against peanut butter and cocoa. Strong; Macallan 12's sherry-cask handles the dessert density with raisin-and-spice character.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Porterhouse": "Macallan 12 Sherry with the porterhouse — benchmark sherried Speyside against strip-and-filet. Works; Macallan 12 frames the two-cut plate at entry-sherried register.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Prime Tartare": "Macallan 12 Sherry -- Speyside, sherry-cask, dried fruit, spice. The scotch backbone matches the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; distinctive maturation story cuts the glaze with UK-cask voice; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Rainbow Trout": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Macallan 12 Sherry for the steak course.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Roast Half Chicken": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the scotch depth settles into the roast chicken, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the plate. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the scotch depth settles into the chickpea soup, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; the pour's refinement is underused by the salad.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Macallan 12 Sherry lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the Highland register carries the garlic spinach, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; distinctive finish over-specified.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Seafood Tower": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Macallan 12 Sherry for the steak course.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Seared Scallops": "Macallan 12 Sherry with seared scallops — Oloroso-cask Speyside against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Seasonal Soup": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the malt-and-oak threads the seasonal soup, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Seasonal Vegetables": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the scotch backbone matches the seasonal vegetables, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the side. Works; distinctive character gives the dessert a unique story-frame.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Shrimp Bisque": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character leans against the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the scotch backbone matches the shrimp bisque, and the oak softens the cream. Works; the pour's delicacy doesn't overpower the soup, but doesn't particularly elevate it either.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Shrimp Cocktail": "Macallan 12 Sherry with shrimp cocktail — sherried Speyside against cold shrimp. Avoid; Scotch wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Swordfish": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Macallan 12 Sherry lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|The Tomahawk": "Macallan 12 Sherry -- Speyside, sherry-cask, dried fruit, spice. The malt-and-oak threads the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; covers both cuts cleanly.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Tomato Basil": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the Highland register carries the tomato basil, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; Macallan 12's dried-fruit meets the glaze at benchmark register.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Truffle Fries": "Macallan 12 Sherry with truffle fries — benchmark sherried-Speyside against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Macallan 12's Oloroso dried-fruit meets the earthy-rich with sherry-cask complexity.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Macallan 12 Sherry for the steak course.",
  "Macallan 12 Sherry|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Macallan 12 Sherry's classic Sherry-Cask Speyside character reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the malt-and-oak threads the curry, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "Macallan 18|Asparagus": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the scotch backbone matches the asparagus, and the smoke threads the side. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Macallan 18|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Macallan 18 with the au gratin — 18yr Oloroso-cask Speyside against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Macallan 18's aged sherry handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Macallan 18|Beignets": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the scotch backbone matches the beignets, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; Remus 15 handles dessert density at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Macallan 18|Bone Marrow": "Macallan 18 with bone marrow — 18yr premium Oloroso Speyside against umami fat. Strong; Macallan 18's aged-sherry depth frames marrow at collector register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Macallan 18|Bone-In Filet": "Macallan 18 with the bone-in filet — 18yr premium Oloroso Speyside against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Macallan 18's deeper sherry-cask depth frames the cut's marrow-adjacent richness with aged-premium gravitas.",
  "Macallan 18|Broccoli Cheddar": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the Highland register carries the broccoli cheddar, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the single-barrel depth is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Macallan 18|Broccolini": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the scotch depth settles into the broccolini, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Macallan 18|Brussels and Belly": "Macallan 18 with Brussels + Belly — 18yr aged sherried Speyside against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; Macallan 18's dried-fruit meets the glaze at premium register.",
  "Macallan 18|Burrata": "Macallan 18 with burrata — premium aged sherried Speyside against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Macallan 18|Butternut Squash": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the malt-and-oak threads the squash bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macallan 18|Carrot Cake": "Macallan 18 with carrot cake — 18yr Oloroso-cask Speyside against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Macallan 18's aged sherry-cask dried-fruit marries the cake's raisins and spice with deeper-than-12 gravitas.",
  "Macallan 18|Cheesecake": "Macallan 18 with cheesecake — 18yr Oloroso-cask Speyside against custard. Strong; Macallan 18's aged-sherry depth threads the cheesecake at premium register.",
  "Macallan 18|Chilean Seabass": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Macallan 18 for the steak course.",
  "Macallan 18|Chocolate Brownie": "Macallan 18 with the chocolate brownie — premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside against cocoa. Excellent; Macallan 18's deeper sherry-derived raisin-and-spice layers into cocoa with aged-premium depth.",
  "Macallan 18|Chocolate Cake": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the scotch depth settles into the chocolate cake, and the heather brightens the dessert. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Macallan 18|Clam Chowder": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the malt-driven body wraps the chowder, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; AE CS's cask-strength character rides the cream at premium wine-finish register.",
  "Macallan 18|Cowboy Ribeye": "★ Macallan 18's bold sherry-dried-fruit character anchors against the ribeye's cap fat and char — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the dried-fruit notes meet the grilled char. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Macallan 18|Crab Cake": "Macallan 18 with crab cake — premium aged sherried Speyside against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Macallan 18|Creamed Spinach": "Macallan 18 with creamed spinach — premium aged sherried Speyside against dairy-rich side. Works; Macallan 18's 18yr character rides the cream at premium register.",
  "Macallan 18|Creamy Potato": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the Highland register carries the potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "Macallan 18|Creme Brulee": "Macallan 18 with creme brulee — premium aged 18yr Speyside against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Strong; Macallan 18's aged sherry-derived spice finds the caramel at collector register.",
  "Macallan 18|Escargot": "Macallan 18's bold sherry-dried-fruit character holds the line with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the sherry depth threads the umami fat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Macallan 18|Faroe Island Salmon": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Macallan 18 for the steak course.",
  "Macallan 18|Filet Mignon": "Macallan 18 with the filet — premium aged 18yr 100% Oloroso sherry-cask Speyside, collector-register. Excellent; Macallan 18's deeper aging and layered dried-fruit-spice depth frame tenderloin with the most complete sherried-Speyside-filet experience in the cluster — where the 12 is benchmark, the 18 is featured.",
  "Macallan 18|French Onion": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the malt-driven body wraps the French onion, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the bowl. Works; a composed but not remarkable match.",
  "Macallan 18|Grilled Caesar": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the scotch depth settles into the grilled Caesar, and the smoke meets the char. Works; BIB slightly over-specified.",
  "Macallan 18|Gumbo": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the Highland register carries the gumbo, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macallan 18|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the scotch backbone matches the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; high proof over-specified.",
  "Macallan 18|House Wedge": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the scotch depth settles into the wedge, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; milestone-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Macallan 18|Kansas City": "Macallan 18 with the KC — premium 18yr Oloroso Speyside against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Macallan 18's deeper dried-fruit-and-wood-spice is the featured Sherried Speyside-KC pairing — the 18yr aging gives it the backbone the 12 reaches for but can't quite close.",
  "Macallan 18|Loaded Potato": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the Highland register carries the loaded potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Macallan 18|Lobster Mac": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the scotch depth settles into the lobster mac, and the oak softens the cream. Works; Voyage 23 handles the richness at ocean-finish register.",
  "Macallan 18|Market Fish": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Macallan 18 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Macallan 18|Mocha Creme": "Macallan 18 with mocha creme — premium aged Oloroso Speyside against coffee-chocolate dessert. Works; Macallan 18's aged-sherry depth threads the mocha at collector register.",
  "Macallan 18|Mushroom Bisque": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the aged Oloroso depth wraps the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the umami. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macallan 18|Mushrooms": "Macallan 18 with mushrooms — premium aged Oloroso Speyside against earthy umami. Strong; Macallan 18's 18yr depth threads the mushrooms with aged sherry-cask character.",
