  "Macallan Estate|Seasonal Soup": "Macallan Estate's estate Speyside character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the malt-and-oak threads the seasonal soup, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Macallan Estate|Seasonal Vegetables": "Macallan Estate's estate Speyside character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the malt-driven body wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the honey rounds the side. Works; the pour's toasted warmth threads through the char without overpowering.",
  "Macallan Estate|Shrimp Bisque": "Macallan Estate's estate Speyside character finds neutral with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the estate depth wraps the shrimp bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Macallan Estate|Shrimp Cocktail": "Macallan Estate with shrimp cocktail — distinctive Estate-oak Macallan against cold shrimp. Avoid; Scotch wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Macallan Estate|Swordfish": "Macallan Estate's estate Speyside character overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Macallan Estate for the steak course.",
  "Macallan Estate|The Tomahawk": "Macallan Estate -- estate Speyside, sherry-cask, oak. The estate depth wraps the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; BIB weight cuts the bleu with premium authority.",
  "Macallan Estate|Tomato Basil": "Macallan Estate's estate Speyside character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the malt-and-oak threads the tomato basil, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; IW Harper 15 handles dessert density at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Macallan Estate|Truffle Fries": "Macallan Estate with truffle fries — Estate-oak premium Macallan against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Macallan Estate's distinctive oak character meets the earthy-rich with distillery-estate-terroir resonance.",
  "Macallan Estate|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Macallan Estate's estate Speyside character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Macallan Estate belongs on the steak course.",
  "Macallan Estate|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Macallan Estate's estate Speyside character sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the malt-and-oak threads the curry, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; pour is a touch over-specified for this dish.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Asparagus": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds with the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin cuts gruyere-cream richness but runs aggressive on a dairy side. Works for au gratin — Muga's softer oak-vanilla fits the cheese gentler.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Beignets": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Macauley Petite Syrah for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Bone Marrow": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin cuts roasted-marrow unctuousness and the dark chocolate echoes toasted-bread char. Peak Petite-Sirah for bone marrow — powerful Napa on the richness. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Bone-In Filet": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the Cab structure frames the tenderloin, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Broccoli Cheddar": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds with the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Broccolini": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis sits beside the broccolini's tender green — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the spice lifts the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Brussels and Belly": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin slices pork-belly fat and the black pepper matches brussels-char bitterness. Strong Petite-Sirah for brussels-and-belly — powerful Napa on fat-and-char.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Burrata": "Never pair Macauley with burrata — inky color, dense tannin, and black pepper clash with fresh cream and the full body buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Butternut Squash": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds with the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Carrot Cake": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Macauley Petite Syrah belongs on the steak course.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Cheesecake": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Macauley Petite Syrah for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Chilean Seabass": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Macauley Petite Syrah for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Chocolate Brownie": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Macauley Petite Syrah lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Chocolate Cake": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Macauley Petite Syrah belongs on the steak course.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Clam Chowder": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis sits beside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Cowboy Ribeye": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin slices Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the black pepper echoes grilled char. Peak Petite-Sirah for ribeye — powerful Napa on the marbled cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Crab Cake": "Never pair Macauley with crab cake — inky color, dense tannin, and black pepper bury delicate lump crab and the full body fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Creamed Spinach": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds with the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Creamy Potato": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis leans against the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis cuts the cheese richness. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Creme Brulee": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Macauley Petite Syrah for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Escargot": "Macauley Petite Syrah's powerful blackcurrant body sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the structured body wraps the buttered escargot, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Faroe Island Salmon": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Macauley Petite Syrah lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Filet Mignon": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the black pepper plays with escargot-butter. Strong Petite-Sirah for filet — the firm tannin runs hot but the cut handles it.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|French Onion": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis stays alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Grilled Caesar": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The black pepper plays with anchovy-depth, but the dense tannin reads heavy for the salad. Works for grilled caesar — a Rhône-style red fits anchovy softer.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Gumbo": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis leans against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|House Wedge": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin holds against blue-cheese funk and the black pepper plays with bacon-fat char. Works for wedge — iceberg-cold mutes Macauley's power, so a lighter red lifts higher.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Kansas City": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The black pepper echoes Kansas City's lean-bold strip char and the dense tannin handles grilled beef. Peak Petite-Sirah for the strip — powerful Napa on the American cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Loaded Potato": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis finds neutral with the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis cuts the cheese richness. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Lobster Mac": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Market Fish": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Macauley Petite Syrah for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Mocha Creme": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Macauley Petite Syrah for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Mushroom Bisque": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Mushrooms": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The black pepper plays with roasted-mushroom butter, though the dense tannin reads aggressive for earth-glutamate. Strong Petite-Sirah for mushrooms — a Rhône garrigue fits softer.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Macauley Petite Syrah for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Porterhouse": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin handles Porterhouse's dual-cut heft and the black pepper works across both filet and strip. Strong Petite-Sirah — exceptional with Porterhouse per the card's call-out.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Prime Tartare": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Rainbow Trout": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Macauley Petite Syrah for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Roast Half Chicken": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the pepper amplifies the spice. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Macauley Petite Syrah for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds with the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Seafood Tower": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Macauley Petite Syrah for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Macauley with seared scallops — dense tannin and black pepper overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and the full body fights browned-butter sweetness. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Seasonal Soup": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Seasonal Vegetables": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis stays alongside the seasonal garden plate — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Shrimp Bisque": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis frames the herb butter. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Macauley with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by inky dense tannin and full body, and the black pepper has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Swordfish": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Macauley Petite Syrah on the steak course instead.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|The Tomahawk": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin handles Tomahawk's massive 36oz bone-in and the black pepper plays with smoky char. Peak Petite-Sirah — exceptional with Tomahawk per the card's call-out. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Tomato Basil": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis leans against the tomato basil's bright acid — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis frames the herb butter. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Truffle Fries": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The black pepper cuts parm-richness and the dense tannin handles truffle-oil weight. Strong Petite-Sirah for truffle fries — powerful Napa on the cut.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Macauley Petite Syrah lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Macauley Petite Syrah|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the pepper amplifies the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Asparagus": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the Pinot body brightens the asparagus, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the side. Works; a measured close to the meal.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Beignets": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Macauley Pinot Noir on the steak course instead.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Bone Marrow": "Sonoma Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — light-body soft-tannin Pinot lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Macauley for the chicken, salmon, or seafood course.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Bone-In Filet": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin plays cleanly against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Broccoli Cheddar": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin sits beside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Broccolini": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. Strong — the wine refreshes the side's richness.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Burrata": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- red structure on burrata curdles the cream's clean delicacy. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Macauley Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Butternut Squash": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin sits beside the squash soup's silky sweet — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Carrot Cake": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Macauley Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Cheesecake": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Macauley Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Chilean Seabass": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift reads alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the Pinot body brightens the seabass, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Chocolate Brownie": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Macauley Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Chocolate Cake": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Macauley Pinot Noir on the steak course instead.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Clam Chowder": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Cowboy Ribeye": "Sonoma Pinot — bright strawberry, red cherry, light floral, soft tannins, light body. The soft structure falls short of ribeye's big marbled build — Pinot's low tannin can't match the cut's fat-and-char. Works when guest wants accessible lighter red with ribeye; Cabs peak higher.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Crab Cake": "Sonoma Pinot — bright strawberry, red cherry, light floral, soft tannins, low tannin. The soft tannin doesn't fight crab's sweet meat and the strawberry plays against romesco. Strong accessible Pinot for crab cake — mid-tier red where heavier reds would bury shellfish.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Creamed Spinach": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Creamy Potato": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin sits beside the potato soup's silky body — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Creme Brulee": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Macauley Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Escargot": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin stays alongside the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Faroe Island Salmon": "Faroe Island Salmon: this rich, fatty seafood's buttery flesh and delicate-yet-substantial texture meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's cherry-bright, low-tannin frame threads cleanly through the fish — classic Burgundy logic. A headliner fish pair. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Filet Mignon": "Sonoma Pinot — bright strawberry, red cherry, light floral, soft tannins, light body. The soft structure meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the red cherry plays against escargot-butter. Peak accessible Pinot for filet — Macauley light body on the delicate cut at mid-tier price. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|French Onion": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. Cherry-driven red with the Caesar's umami and char — a classic cross-category salad pair. Strong — aligned register.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Gumbo": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin sits beside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin sits alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|House Wedge": "Sonoma Pinot — bright strawberry, red cherry, light floral, soft tannins. The light acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the strawberry plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong accessible Pinot for the wedge — Macauley mid-tier reframes the classic steakhouse salad.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Kansas City": "Sonoma Pinot — bright strawberry, red cherry, light floral, soft tannins, light body. The soft structure works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though the low tannin falls short of bolder Cabs' char-requirement. Peak accessible Pinot for the strip when guest wants lighter red pick. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Loaded Potato": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Lobster Mac": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin stays alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Market Fish": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin leans against the fish's clean flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Mocha Creme": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Macauley Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Mushroom Bisque": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: this earthy, umami-packed side's mushroom-and-truffle depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. A bright-meets-rich headliner. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Macauley Pinot Noir lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Porterhouse": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin holds steady against the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's silky frame sits alongside the starter without weight-fighting — the fruit threads through the plate. Works cleanly.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Rainbow Trout": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin holds with the trout's delicate flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Roast Half Chicken": "Sonoma Pinot — bright strawberry, red cherry, light floral, soft tannins. The light body plays against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak accessible Pinot for chicken across most seasonal preparations. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin sits alongside the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin sits beside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Seafood Tower": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Macauley Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Seared Scallops": "Sonoma Pinot — bright strawberry, red cherry, light floral, soft tannins, light body. The soft tannin leaves room for scallop sweetness and the red cherry plays against caramelized-exterior char. Strong accessible Pinot for scallops — Macauley on the delicate shellfish.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Seasonal Soup": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the Pinot body brightens the seasonal soup, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Seasonal Vegetables": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin holds with the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Shrimp Bisque": "Sonoma Pinot — bright strawberry, red cherry, light floral, soft tannins. The light acid cuts bisque's cream weight and the strawberry plays against shellfish umami and sherry. Works as accessible Pinot for shrimp bisque — mid-tier Macauley against the rich soup.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Shrimp Cocktail": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overshadows the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Macauley Pinot Noir belongs on the steak course.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Swordfish": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin plays cleanly against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|The Tomahawk": "Sonoma Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — light-body soft-tannin Pinot has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Macauley for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Tomato Basil": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin plays cleanly against the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Truffle Fries": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin leans against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin sits beside the tuna's seared rare crust — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Macauley Pinot Noir|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the Pinot body brightens the curry, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Asparagus": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: structured red meets dairy-driven side: the bold tannin scrubs the cream, the dark fruit lifts the indulgence into balance. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Beignets": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The starter's fat and umami meet the bold red's dark fruit — both land on the palate with real weight. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Bone-In Filet": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured dark-fruit weight leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Broccoli Cheddar": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Broccolini": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis leans against the broccolini's tender green — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Burrata": "Atlas Peak Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — powerful iron-espresso body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with blackberry-blueberry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Macauley Stagecoach for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Butternut Squash": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis reads alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Carrot Cake": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Cheesecake": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Chilean Seabass": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Chocolate Brownie": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Chocolate Cake": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Clam Chowder": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Cowboy Ribeye": "Macauley Stagecoach — Atlas Peak Cab from one of Napa's most celebrated high-elevation vineyards. Dark blackberry, blueberry, espresso, iron, powerful tannin, exceptional length. The high-elevation structure handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the iron-espresso meets rendered fat. Peak celebrated-vineyard Cab for Cowboy. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Crab Cake": "Atlas Peak Stagecoach Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — powerful-tannin iron-espresso crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Macauley Stagecoach for the steak course where its celebrated-vineyard character shines.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Creamy Potato": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis leans against the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Creme Brulee": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Escargot": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Faroe Island Salmon": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Filet Mignon": "Atlas Peak Cab — Stagecoach, dark blackberry, blueberry, espresso, iron, powerful tannin. The high-elevation body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the iron-espresso echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong celebrated-vineyard Cab for filet — Stagecoach structure, though lighter Cabs peak higher on the delicate cut.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|French Onion": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee meets the caramel. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Grilled Caesar": "Macauley Stagecoach — Atlas Peak Cab with dark blackberry, blueberry, espresso, iron. The high-elevation body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the iron-espresso plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as celebrated-vineyard Cab for grilled Caesar — Stagecoach structure on the classic layered salad.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Gumbo": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis sits beside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the coffee meets the caramel. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|House Wedge": "House Wedge: the House Wedge's crisp iceberg against creamy bleu dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The wedge's crisp iceberg and creamy dressing sit light against the bold red's dense tannin — the wine overwhelms the salad. Works only as a second-thought pairing.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Kansas City": "Kansas City: the strip's marbled-and-char richness finds the bold red's grip; cassis carries the savory weight cleanly. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Loaded Potato": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis finds neutral with the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Lobster Mac": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Market Fish": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Mocha Creme": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Mushroom Bisque": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the side's mushroom richness pulls the bold red's body; tannin and oak settle into the umami without overshooting. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Prime Tartare": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Rainbow Trout": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard on the steak course instead.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Roast Half Chicken": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis holds with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis holds with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard on the steak course instead.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis plays cleanly against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Seafood Tower": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Seared Scallops": "Macauley Stagecoach Atlas Peak Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — powerful iron-espresso obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Macauley for the steak course where its Stagecoach character peaks.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Seasonal Soup": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Seasonal Vegetables": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis sits alongside the seasonal garden plate — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Shrimp Bisque": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis holds with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Shrimp Cocktail": "Macauley Stagecoach Atlas Peak Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — powerful iron-espresso is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Macauley for steak where its celebrated-vineyard structure belongs.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Swordfish": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard for the steak course.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|The Tomahawk": "Atlas Peak Cab — Stagecoach Vineyard, dark blackberry, blueberry, espresso, iron, powerful tannin. The structured body meets Tomahawk fat and char and the iron-espresso frames the theatrical bone-in cut. Peak celebrated-vineyard Cab for Tomahawk — Stagecoach's extreme diurnal shift gives structure to meet the biggest marbled steak. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Tomato Basil": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright acid — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis frames the herb butter. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the bold red's cassis grip carries the mushroom-and-truffle depth — structured weight on the earthy side. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard belongs on the steak course.",
  "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Mahon Gin|Asparagus": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Mahon Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Mahon Gin|Beignets": "Mahón with beignets — Xoriguer Mahón against fried dough. Works; Mahón's sharp Mediterranean juniper is over-specified for casual dessert.",
  "Mahon Gin|Bone Marrow": "Mahón with bone marrow — PDO Menorcan juniper gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Mahon Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Mahon Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Mahon Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Mahon Gin|Broccolini": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals leans against the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Mahon Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals sits beside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs frame the grilled char. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Mahon Gin|Burrata": "Mahón with burrata — PDO juniper-heavy Spanish gin against fresh dairy. Strong; Mahón's sharp juniper contrasts cream more than the complex-layered siblings; Inverroche's fynbos-floral is the featured craft-gin-burrata match.",
  "Mahon Gin|Butternut Squash": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals sits alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Mahon Gin|Carrot Cake": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Mahon Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Mahon Gin|Cheesecake": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Mahon Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Mahon Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift leans against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the floral edge frames the seabass, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Neptunia's briny character engages the bleu with distinctive maritime profile.",
  "Mahon Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Mahon Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Mahon Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Mahon Gin for the steak course.",
  "Mahon Gin|Clam Chowder": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Mahon Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Mahón with the cowboy ribeye — Balearic-island craft gin against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Mahon Gin|Crab Cake": "Mahón with crab cake — Xoriguer Mahón Spanish Mediterranean gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Mahón's sharp Mediterranean juniper lifts crab with Balearic-island character.",
  "Mahon Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Mahon Gin|Creamy Potato": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals sits alongside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Mahon Gin|Creme Brulee": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Mahon Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Mahon Gin|Escargot": "Escargot: garlic-butter snails meet PDO juniper-heavy Mediterranean gin. Mahón's Spanish dry-juniper profile cuts butter with Mediterranean sharpness — the featured Botanical-Forward Craft Gin escargot pairing, parallel to Tanqueray's flagship juniper approach from the London Dry cluster. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Mahon Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Mahón with the salmon — Balearic-island Mediterranean gin against oily salmon. Strong; Mahón's sharp juniper cuts the fish with Spanish-Mediterranean character.",
  "Mahon Gin|Filet Mignon": "Mahón with the filet — Balearic-island juniper-heavy gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Mahon Gin|French Onion": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Mahon Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Mahón with the grilled Caesar — Mediterranean PDO juniper against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Mahón's sharp Mediterranean character meets the char at Spanish register.",
  "Mahon Gin|Gumbo": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Mahon Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals stays alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Mahon Gin|House Wedge": "Mahón with the House Wedge — Xoriguer Mahón against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Mahón's sharp juniper engages the bleu with Mediterranean character.",
  "Mahon Gin|Kansas City": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Mahon Gin on another course instead.",
  "Mahon Gin|Loaded Potato": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals sits alongside the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Mahon Gin|Lobster Mac": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Mahon Gin|Market Fish": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals finds register with the fish's clean flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Mahon Gin|Mocha Creme": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Mahon Gin for the steak course.",
  "Mahon Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Mahon Gin|Mushrooms": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Mahon Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Mahon Gin for the steak course.",
  "Mahon Gin|Porterhouse": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Mahon Gin on another course instead.",
  "Mahon Gin|Prime Tartare": "Mahón with the tartare — PDO Menorcan juniper-heavy gin against raw beef. Excellent; Mahón's sharp Mediterranean juniper lifts tartare's minerality with Spanish-dry character distinct from Monkey 47's complex-layered or Botanist's Hebridean approaches.",
  "Mahon Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals sits with the trout's delicate flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Mahon Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Mahón with the chicken — Menorcan PDO gin against roasted poultry. Works; Mahón's Mediterranean juniper supports chicken at Spanish-craft register.",
  "Mahon Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the caramel rounds the earthy plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Mahon Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals holds the line with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Mahon Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals sits beside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Mahon Gin|Seafood Tower": "Mahón with the seafood tower — Xoriguer Mahón, Menorca (Balearic Islands), PDO-protected juniper-heavy Mediterranean gin. Excellent; Mahón's Mediterranean juniper character frames the raw bar with Spanish-island terroir — built for seafood the way its maker's island location would suggest.",
  "Mahon Gin|Seared Scallops": "Mahón with seared scallops — Menorcan PDO juniper-heavy Mediterranean gin against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Mahón's Spanish-island terroir frames scallops with Mediterranean sharpness.",
  "Mahon Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals sits alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Mahon Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals sits alongside the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Mahon Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Mahón with shrimp bisque — PDO Menorcan gin against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Mahón's Mediterranean juniper cuts the bisque with Spanish distinction.",
  "Mahon Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Mahón with shrimp cocktail — Menorcan Mediterranean gin against cold shrimp. Excellent; Mahón's sharp Mediterranean character elevates the classic cold app with Spanish-island distinction.",
  "Mahon Gin|Swordfish": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals reads alongside the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Mahon Gin|The Tomahawk": "Mahón with the Tomahawk — Spanish Mediterranean gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Mahon Gin|Tomato Basil": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals sits alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Mahon Gin|Truffle Fries": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals stays alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Mahon Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the tuna's seared rare crust — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs frame the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Mahon Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Mahon Gin's bold herbal-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Asparagus": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak depth lays under the asparagus, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with au gratin potatoes — cheese and cream meet the Cellar Aged's mature caramel. Strong; the side and the pour meet at the same register.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Beignets": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the caramel-spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; flagship depth gives the dessert premium aged character.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: the rich umami fat meets Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's cellar-aged depth. Standard Maker's is pleasant but thin here; the Cellar Aged earns the marrow. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Bone-In Filet": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's bold wheated-cellar-aged character finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the rounded sweetness meets the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Broccoli Cheddar": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the 10-year holds without lifting.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Broccolini": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon backbone carries the broccolini, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Brussels and Belly": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with Brussels + Belly — the bourbon's deeper wax-caramel meets maple glaze and pork belly fat. More considered than standard Maker's here; the Cellar Aged earns the small plate. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Burrata": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with burrata — fresh dairy and cellar-aged depth don't bridge. Avoid; the cream can't accommodate the pour.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Butternut Squash": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon weight handles the squash bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; sweet character distinctive but not driving.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Carrot Cake": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with carrot cake — deeper caramel and vanilla meet cream cheese frosting and cinnamon. A dessert pairing that shows the bourbon's maturation; balance over sweet-on-sweet. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Cheesecake": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with cheesecake — custard and cellar-aged vanilla meet cleanly. Works; not revelatory but well-matched registers.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Chilean Seabass": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Maker's Mark Cellar Aged for the steak course.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa and fudge meet Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged vanilla. Works; Cellar Aged's elegance underserves the brownie's boldness. Weller 107 or Weller Millennium for more cocoa interplay.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Chocolate Cake": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Clam Chowder": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon body sits with the chowder, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; boutique character slightly over-specified.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: the cut's cap fat and char meet Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's mature depth. Standard Maker's fades against the ribeye; the Cellar Aged sits with it. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Crab Cake": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with crab cake — the Cellar Aged's depth overwhelms crab delicacy. Avoid; choose a crisp wine instead.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Creamed Spinach": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with creamed spinach — the bourbon's honeyed caramel depth sits beside the dairy. More refined than standard Maker's here; the aging gives the pour something to contribute. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Creamy Potato": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Vermont-brand character doesn't drive.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Creme Brulee": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Remus 15 handles dessert density at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Escargot": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's bold wheated-cellar-aged character finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the rounded sweetness meets the herb butter. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Faroe Island Salmon": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Maker's Mark Cellar Aged for the steak course.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Filet Mignon": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with the filet — extended maturation gives standard Maker's a richer oak backbone and deeper caramel. The filet's restraint meets a more complex wheated pour than regular Maker's. The alternate premium wheated for filet when Weller 12 isn't the call. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|French Onion": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Grilled Caesar": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy meet the Cellar Aged's deep vanilla. The Cellar Aged earns this salad where standard Maker's can't; strong. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Gumbo": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon weight handles the gumbo, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; blend character covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon body sits with the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; curated character over-specified.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|House Wedge": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with the House Wedge — iceberg's crispness and bleu can't carry the Cellar Aged's depth. Works only as a separate sip.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Kansas City": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with the KC — cellar-aging's deeper wax-and-vanilla meets the strip's char. Richer than standard Maker's Mark with the strip; the pour shows its age in the way it carries the cut's marbling. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Loaded Potato": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon backbone carries the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; sweet character distinctive but not driving.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Lobster Mac": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the lobster mac, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Market Fish": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Maker's Mark Cellar Aged for the steak course.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Mocha Creme": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Mushroom Bisque": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; milestone-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Mushrooms": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with the mushrooms — aged caramel and vanilla against umami. The cellar-aged depth complements mushroom weight; a sophisticated Maker's interpretation of this side. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the caramel-spice register matches the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the cut's strip-and-filet split meets Maker's Mark Cellar Aged. The strip side takes the depth; the filet side holds. More appropriate than standard Maker's for this plate; Weller 12 is the alternate premium call. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Prime Tartare": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's bold wheated-cellar-aged character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Rainbow Trout": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Maker's Mark Cellar Aged for the steak course.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Roast Half Chicken": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon body sits with the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; extended-aged home-state pour wasted on salad.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Maker's Mark Cellar Aged for the steak course.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the cellar-aged depth wraps the garlic spinach, and the oak frames the side. Works; Voyage 23 handles the richness at ocean-finish register.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Seafood Tower": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Maker's Mark Cellar Aged for the steak course.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Seared Scallops": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with seared scallops — aged oak against scallop caramelized sweetness is a mismatch. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Seasonal Soup": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; curated single-barrel covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Seasonal Vegetables": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Shrimp Bisque": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood meets cellar-aged caramel. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Shrimp Cocktail": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp and aged bourbon don't find each other. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Swordfish": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Maker's Mark Cellar Aged lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|The Tomahawk": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's bold wheated-cellar-aged character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon character meets the rendered fat. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Tomato Basil": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth reads alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon body sits with the tomato basil, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; the Millennium's register overshoots the dessert's.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Truffle Fries": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged with truffle fries — deeper caramel and vanilla meet truffle earth. The extended maturation gives the pour the richness to echo the truffle; standard Maker's is just sweet here, Cellar Aged is resonant. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Maker's Mark Cellar Aged for the steak course.",
  "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon body sits with the curry, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Maker's Mark|Asparagus": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the caramel-spice register matches the asparagus, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; richer profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Maker's Mark|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Maker's Mark with au gratin potatoes — cheese and cream meet Maker's 90-proof softness. Cleanly matched; not the most interesting pairing but perfectly solid. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Maker's Mark|Beignets": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the oak depth lays under the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; bonded weight a touch ambitious for the dessert.",
  "Maker's Mark|Bone Marrow": "Maker's Mark with bone marrow — soft caramel and vanilla against umami fat. Approachable and well-matched; Maker's Mark Cellar Aged brings more weight, but standard Maker's hits the mark cleanly for everyday pours. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Maker's Mark|Bone-In Filet": "Maker's Mark -- wheated, red-rope, caramel. The oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak-and-spice register settles the cut. Strong; smooth character covers both cuts cleanly.",
  "Maker's Mark|Broccoli Cheddar": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the wheated softness matches the broccoli cheddar, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; Marian McLain L.E.",
  "Maker's Mark|Broccolini": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon weight handles the broccolini, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; the single-barrel depth is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Maker's Mark|Brussels and Belly": "Maker's Mark with Brussels + Belly — iconic wheated sweetness meets maple-glazed pork belly. A clean, crowd-pleasing match; the gateway Maker's alongside Weller Special Reserve. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Maker's Mark|Burrata": "Maker's Mark with burrata — fresh dairy against wheated caramel. Avoid; register mismatch.",
  "Maker's Mark|Butternut Squash": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the squash bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the boldness pushes slightly past what the soup asks for.",
  "Maker's Mark|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Maker's soft caramel-vanilla. Composed pairing; Maker's Mark Cellar Aged adds depth but this is clean. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Maker's Mark|Cheesecake": "Maker's Mark with cheesecake — clean custard meets soft wheated caramel. Works; the match is gentle and approachable.",
  "Maker's Mark|Chilean Seabass": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Maker's Mark on the steak course instead.",
  "Maker's Mark|Chocolate Brownie": "Maker's Mark with the chocolate brownie — iconic wheated against cocoa. Works; for more interplay, reach for Weller 107 or Weller Millennium.",
  "Maker's Mark|Chocolate Cake": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the brown-spirit warmth threads the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Maker's Mark|Clam Chowder": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon depth settles into the chowder, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Maker's Mark|Cowboy Ribeye": "Maker's Mark with the Cowboy Ribeye — the cut's cap fat pushes past standard Maker's 90-proof register. Capable but not marquee; reach for Maker's Mark Cellar Aged or Weller 12 for true ribeye alignment. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Maker's Mark|Crab Cake": "Maker's Mark with the crab cake — soft wheated sweetness against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp wine instead.",
  "Maker's Mark|Creamed Spinach": "Maker's Mark with creamed spinach — iconic wheated caramel-vanilla against dairy. A reliable, approachable pairing; the side and the pour at the same register. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Maker's Mark|Creamy Potato": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Maker's Mark|Creme Brulee": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Maker's Mark|Escargot": "Maker's Mark -- wheated, red-rope, caramel. The wheated softness matches the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; composed namesake match; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Maker's Mark|Faroe Island Salmon": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Maker's Mark for the steak course.",
  "Maker's Mark|Filet Mignon": "Maker's Mark with the filet — the iconic red-wax 90-proof wheated. Soft caramel and vanilla frame the filet's clean beef with the approachable warmth that made Maker's Maker's. Distinct from the Weller profile — rounder, less spicy, more 'home' wheated. The gateway wheated pour alongside Weller Special Reserve. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Maker's Mark|French Onion": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon body sits with the French onion, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; grain notes don't really engage the soup but don't clash.",
  "Maker's Mark|Grilled Caesar": "Maker's Mark with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against Maker's 90-proof softness. Works; Maker's Cellar Aged or Weller 12 earn this Caesar more fully.",
  "Maker's Mark|Gumbo": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon backbone carries the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Maker's Mark|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; boutique character doesn't drive.",
  "Maker's Mark|House Wedge": "Maker's Mark with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against iconic wheated. Works as a before-course sip; no driver pairing.",
  "Maker's Mark|Kansas City": "Maker's Mark with the KC — iconic wheated softness meets the strip's marbling. 90-proof warmth carries the cut without competing with the char; the crowd-pleasing strip pour. Maker's Mark Cellar Aged is richer, this is the everyday version. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Maker's Mark|Loaded Potato": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the brown-spirit warmth threads the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; distinctive character is worth mentioning tableside, even if the pairing isn't marquee.",
  "Maker's Mark|Lobster Mac": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon backbone carries the lobster mac, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; boutique-sourced doesn't drive.",
  "Maker's Mark|Market Fish": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Maker's Mark for the steak course.",
  "Maker's Mark|Mocha Creme": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the mocha creme, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the distinctive character is underused by the salad's simplicity.",
  "Maker's Mark|Mushroom Bisque": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the oak depth lays under the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Maker's Mark|Mushrooms": "Maker's Mark with the mushrooms — soft caramel and vanilla against earthy umami. The most approachable wheated with this side; less refined than Weller 12 but perfectly composed at the register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Maker's Mark|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon body sits with the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Portwood is over-specified for sides.",
  "Maker's Mark|Porterhouse": "Maker's Mark with the porterhouse — soft wheated against the strip-and-filet split. Works cleanly across both sides without standing out; Maker's Cellar Aged and Weller 12 are the stronger calls.",
  "Maker's Mark|Prime Tartare": "Maker's Mark -- wheated, red-rope, caramel. The bourbon backbone carries the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; balance against richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Maker's Mark|Rainbow Trout": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Maker's Mark on the steak course instead.",
  "Maker's Mark|Roast Half Chicken": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon body sits with the roast chicken, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the plate. Works; AE Triple Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Maker's Mark|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the caramel-spice register matches the chickpea soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; AE CS handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Maker's Mark|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Maker's Mark for the steak course.",
  "Maker's Mark|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon body sits with the garlic spinach, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; Vermont-brand character doesn't drive.",
  "Maker's Mark|Seafood Tower": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Maker's Mark for the steak course.",
  "Maker's Mark|Seared Scallops": "Maker's Mark with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against iconic wheated. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Maker's Mark|Seasonal Soup": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon body sits with the seasonal soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Maker's Mark|Seasonal Vegetables": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon backbone carries the seasonal vegetables, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; bonded weight pushes past the soup's register slightly.",
  "Maker's Mark|Shrimp Bisque": "Maker's Mark with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood meets 90-proof wheated. Works neutrally; pour doesn't drive.",
  "Maker's Mark|Shrimp Cocktail": "Maker's Mark with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against 90-proof wheated. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Maker's Mark|Swordfish": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Maker's Mark belongs on the steak course.",
  "Maker's Mark|The Tomahawk": "Maker's Mark -- wheated, red-rope, caramel. The oak depth lays under the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla underlines the rendered char. Strong; composed premium match.",
  "Maker's Mark|Tomato Basil": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Maker's Mark|Truffle Fries": "Maker's Mark with truffle fries — the iconic red-wax wheated against truffle-parmesan. The 90-proof softness lets truffle lead; a guest-friendly pairing that doesn't make demands. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Maker's Mark|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Maker's Mark for the steak course.",
  "Maker's Mark|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; OGD 16 frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Malibu Rum|Asparagus": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- light spirit needs the cut, not the supporting plate. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a light spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Malibu Rum lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Malibu Rum|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Malibu Rum's clean silver register sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the unaged body brightens the au gratin, and the light-rum lift plays against the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Malibu Rum|Beignets": "Malibu Rum: coconut-flavored rum liqueur from Barbados, 21% ABV, sweet-tropical register. Excellent on beignets; the coconut-and-sweet character wraps the warm fried pastry in tropical-dessert mode — beach-vibe dessert pairing, lowest-alcohol option on the menu.",
  "Malibu Rum|Bone Marrow": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- light spirit reads thin against the rich meat opener. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a light spirit. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Malibu Rum belongs on the steak course.",
  "Malibu Rum|Bone-In Filet": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Malibu Rum for another course.",
  "Malibu Rum|Broccoli Cheddar": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the light spirit doesn't add to the soup-salad course — neutral but wrong. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Malibu Rum for the steak course.",
  "Malibu Rum|Broccolini": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- light spirit needs the cut, not the supporting plate. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Malibu Rum for the steak course.",
  "Malibu Rum|Brussels and Belly": "Malibu Rum's green-agave-and-cane lift finds neutral with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the light-rum body matches the pork-belly plate, and the bright cane edge brightens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Malibu Rum|Burrata": "Malibu Rum's unaged unaged-cane body leans against the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the unaged body brightens the burrata, and the cane register underlines the starter. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Malibu Rum|Butternut Squash": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the light spirit doesn't add to the soup-salad course — neutral but wrong. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a light spirit. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Malibu Rum for the steak course.",
  "Malibu Rum|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Malibu. Excellent; the coconut sweetness adds tropical dimension to carrot-cake spice at sweet-flavored register.",
  "Malibu Rum|Cheesecake": "Cheesecake: custard-dairy dessert meets Malibu. Excellent; the coconut liqueur sweetness plays tropical dimension on cheesecake — piña-colada-energy on dessert at liqueur register.",
  "Malibu Rum|Chilean Seabass": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- light spirit reads thin against a main protein — needs a fuller pour. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Malibu Rum for the steak course.",
  "Malibu Rum|Chocolate Brownie": "Malibu Rum's sweet coconut-tropical character holds the line with the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Malibu Rum|Chocolate Cake": "Malibu Rum's sweet coconut-tropical character lifts the cake's layered chocolate — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Malibu Rum|Clam Chowder": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the light spirit doesn't add to the soup-salad course — neutral but wrong. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a light spirit. Avoid; one of those is the move. Malibu Rum belongs on the steak course.",
  "Malibu Rum|Cowboy Ribeye": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Malibu Rum for another course.",
  "Malibu Rum|Crab Cake": "Malibu Rum's green-agave-and-cane lift meets at register with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust: the cane-and-citrus body lifts the delicate crab cake, and the Caribbean-rum register underlines the starter. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Malibu Rum|Creamed Spinach": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- light spirit needs the cut, not the supporting plate. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Malibu Rum on the steak course instead.",
  "Malibu Rum|Creamy Potato": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the light spirit doesn't add to the soup-salad course — neutral but wrong. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a light spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Malibu Rum for the steak course.",
  "Malibu Rum|Creme Brulee": "Malibu Rum's clean silver register sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the silver-rum body cuts the brulee, and the bright sugarcane edge underlines the dessert. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Malibu Rum|Escargot": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- this light spirit's register is wrong for the starter — try a different pour. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a light spirit. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Malibu Rum lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Malibu Rum|Faroe Island Salmon": "Malibu Rum's unaged unaged-cane body leans against the salmon's rich oily flesh: the bright cane-and-citrus weight carries the salmon, and the cane-and-citrus thread frames the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Malibu Rum|Filet Mignon": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the spirit's register doesn't carry even the lean cut's structure. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Malibu Rum for another course.",
  "Malibu Rum|French Onion": "Malibu Rum's green-agave-and-cane lift sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the unaged-cane body sits with the French onion, and the unaged-cane note plays against the bowl. Works; refined craft composes cleanly.",
  "Malibu Rum|Grilled Caesar": "Malibu Rum's unaged-cane register sits alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the cane-and-citrus body lifts the grilled Caesar, and the unaged-cane register underlines the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Malibu Rum|Gumbo": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the light spirit doesn't add to the soup-salad course — neutral but wrong. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Malibu Rum for the steak course.",
  "Malibu Rum|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- light spirit needs the cut, not the supporting plate. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a light spirit. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Malibu Rum on the steak course instead.",
  "Malibu Rum|House Wedge": "House Wedge: iceberg with bacon and bleu meets Malibu. Excellent; the coconut-sweet liqueur character cuts through bacon-and-bleu with tropical contrast — unexpected but earns its place at liqueur register.",
  "Malibu Rum|Kansas City": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a light spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Malibu Rum for another course.",
  "Malibu Rum|Loaded Potato": "Malibu Rum's unaged unaged-cane body sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the light-rum body cuts the loaded potato soup, and the cane-and-citrus thread frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Malibu Rum|Lobster Mac": "Malibu Rum's unaged silver-rum body sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the unaged-cane register matches the lobster mac, and the unaged-cane note plays against the side. Works; clean additive-free lifts the dish depending on preparation.",
  "Malibu Rum|Market Fish": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- light spirit reads thin against a main protein — needs a fuller pour. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a light spirit. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Malibu Rum on the steak course instead.",
  "Malibu Rum|Mocha Creme": "Malibu Rum's unaged cane-and-citrus body meets at register with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the light-rum body cuts the mocha creme, and the bright sugarcane edge underlines the dessert. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
