  "Mata Hari Absinthe|Creme Brulee": "Creme Brulee: caramelized custard meets Mata Hari Absinthe. Excellent; wormwood-anise-fennel character cuts burnt-sugar-cream at 60% register — sharpest of the four herbal-dessert pairings.",
  "Mata Hari Absinthe|Escargot": "Escargot: garlic-herb butter meets Mata Hari Absinthe. Excellent; the wormwood-anise-fennel ties directly to French herbal traditions — absinthe was built for this plate at 60% register. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Mata Hari Absinthe|Faroe Island Salmon": "Mata Hari Absinthe's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Mata Hari Absinthe for the steak course.",
  "Mata Hari Absinthe|Filet Mignon": "Mata Hari Absinthe's gentian-driven cut reads alongside the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the herbal cut works the lean buttery filet, and the amaro backbone frames the cut. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Mata Hari Absinthe|French Onion": "Mata Hari Absinthe's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Mata Hari Absinthe on the steak course instead.",
  "Mata Hari Absinthe|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: char-romaine with anchovy-parmesan meets Mata Hari. Excellent; the anise-wormwood clarity cuts char-and-brine at high-proof absinthe register.",
