  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Escargot": "Michter's US*1 Rye's crisp spice character holds steady against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Faroe Island Salmon": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Michter's US*1 Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Filet Mignon": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the filet — Michter's small-batch smooth rye against the 10oz cut. Distinct from Sazerac Rye by texture — smoother, more polished; a refined rye for filet orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|French Onion": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the rye backbone drives the French onion, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Grilled Caesar": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the grilled Caesar — smooth pot-still rye against charred romaine and anchovy. The Michter's smooth character earns the Caesar at the refined small-batch register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Gumbo": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the gumbo, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; Voyage 23 handles the richness at ocean-finish register.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the dry rye body wraps the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; AE meets the char at wine-finish register.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|House Wedge": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the House Wedge — smooth rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; the Michter's polish cuts the bleu with refined rye character.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Kansas City": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the KC — small-batch smooth rye, 84.8 proof, copper pot-still distilled. Michter's signature approach gives the rye a smoother texture than benchmark Sazerac Rye; smooth rye spice against the strip's marbling. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Loaded Potato": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the rye-and-oak weight handles the loaded potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Lobster Mac": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the rye-and-oak weight handles the lobster mac, and the oak softens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Market Fish": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Michter's US*1 Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Mocha Creme": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the high-rye spice register matches the mocha creme, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; EC 18 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Mushroom Bisque": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the dry rye body wraps the mushroom bisque, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Mushrooms": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the mushrooms — smooth rye against earthy umami. Strong; the Michter's polish layers into mushroom depth distinctly.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye backbone drives the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; boutique character slightly over-specified.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Porterhouse": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the porterhouse — smooth small-batch rye across strip and filet. Strong; covers both cuts with Michter's refined polish.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Prime Tartare": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the tartare — smooth small-batch rye against raw beef. The Michter's polish gives tartare a refined rye counterpoint. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Rainbow Trout": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Michter's US*1 Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the rye backbone drives the roast chicken, and the rye-pepper note underlines the plate. Works; bonded weight overpowers this dish.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the dry rye body wraps the chickpea soup, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the bowl. Works; the rye's bright character is slightly at odds with the side's richness.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Michter's US*1 Rye for the steak course.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye spice cuts the garlic spinach, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Seafood Tower": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Michter's US*1 Rye for the steak course.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Seared Scallops": "Michter's US*1 Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 84.8-proof rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Seasonal Soup": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the rye backbone drives the seasonal soup, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; milestone-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye spice cuts the seasonal vegetables, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; collector-bottle doesn't drive.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "Michter's US*1 Rye with shrimp bisque — smooth pot-still rye against cream-based seafood. Works; the Michter's polish is interesting but doesn't drive.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "Michter's US*1 Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against Michter's rye. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Swordfish": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Michter's US*1 Rye for the steak course.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|The Tomahawk": "Michter's US*1 Rye's crisp spice character finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Tomato Basil": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the rye spice cuts the tomato basil, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; craft-blend over-specified.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Truffle Fries": "Michter's US*1 Rye with truffle fries — smooth small-batch rye against truffle-parmesan. The Michter's polish gives the side a refined frame. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Michter's US*1 Rye for the steak course.",
  "Michter's US*1 Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the rye spice cuts the curry, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Mijenta Blanco|Asparagus": "Mijenta Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the silver-tequila register lifts the asparagus, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Mijenta Blanco|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Mijenta Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body reads alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the unaged body brightens the au gratin, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Mijenta Blanco|Beignets": "Mijenta Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a light spirit. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Mijenta Blanco on the steak course instead.",
  "Mijenta Blanco|Bone Marrow": "Mijenta Blanco with bone marrow — additive-free craft against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms blanco.",
  "Mijenta Blanco|Bone-In Filet": "Mijenta Blanco's clean silver register reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the blanco-tequila body matches the bone-enhanced filet, and the bright blanco register underlines the cut. Works; a measured close to the meal.",
  "Mijenta Blanco|Broccoli Cheddar": "Mijenta Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the unaged body brightens the broccoli cheddar, and the unaged-cane note plays against the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Mijenta Blanco|Broccolini": "Mijenta Blanco's clean silver register finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the broccolini, and the agave-citrus thread frames the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Mijenta Blanco|Brussels and Belly": "Mijenta Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge reads alongside Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the pork-belly plate, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; clean composes cleanly.",
  "Mijenta Blanco|Burrata": "Mijenta Blanco with burrata — Maestra-Tequilera craft against fresh mozzarella. The clean additive-free agave lifts the burrata with premium craft integrity. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Mijenta Blanco|Butternut Squash": "Mijenta Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the unaged body brightens the squash bisque, and the unaged-cane note plays against the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Mijenta Blanco|Carrot Cake": "Mijenta Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Mijenta Blanco belongs on the steak course.",
  "Mijenta Blanco|Cheesecake": "Mijenta Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap mis-pairs with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the spirit reads thin against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Mijenta Blanco for the steak course.",
