  "Mocha Creme|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Piedmont Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee, gentle sweetness. Bubbles cut the mocha crème's cream-and-cocoa density. Strong alternative when guest wants sparkling rather than coffee; currently unavailable on the menu.",
  "Mocha Creme|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- light spirit can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a richer pour like an aged rum or tawny. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the G4 Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Mocha Creme|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof's rested-agave body finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the mocha creme, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; distinctive-bottle over-specified.",
  "Mocha Creme|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the mocha creme, and the oak threads the cream. Works; grain notes don't really engage the soup but don't clash.",
  "Mocha Creme|George T. Stagg": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; Voyage 23 handles the richness at ocean-finish register.",
