  "Mocha Creme|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Sauteed Garlic Spinach and the mocha creme -- acid plays against sweetness, the close stays unburdened. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Mocha Creme|Sauternes Glass": "Bordeaux botrytis — apricot, honey, orange marmalade, bright acid. The acid cuts mocha crème's cream-and-cocoa density. Works alongside — though crème brûlée peaks higher on Sauternes since the torched sugar meets the honey more directly.",
  "Mocha Creme|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, and absinthe-rinsed glass. The spirit-forward anise-rye profile collides with cream-and-coffee custard — herbal bitterness overrides cocoa softness. Never pair — save Sazerac for tartare, marrow, or pre-dinner spirit service. Mocha Crème is currently off-menu.",
  "Mocha Creme|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the mocha creme, and the oak softens the cream. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Mocha Creme|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mocha creme, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Mocha Creme|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Nebbiolo — tar, dried rose, firm tannin. Barolo's structure crushes a cream-based custard; tannin-meets-dessert is incoherent. Never pair — save Barolo for Tomahawk or Porterhouse where structure earns its place.",
  "Mocha Creme|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the dry white underclubs the dessert sugar, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Mocha Creme|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters on the steak course instead.",
  "Mocha Creme|Seafood Tower": "The seafood tower alongside the mocha creme -- the bright register lifts the sweet, the meal closes clean. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Mocha Creme|Seared Scallops": "The scallops alongside the mocha creme -- the entree yields to the dessert, the meal arc reads on-key. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Mocha Creme|Seasonal Soup": "Seasonal Soup and the mocha creme -- the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Mocha Creme|Seasonal Vegetables": "The seasonal vegetables alongside the mocha creme -- a clean call, no friction either way. Works; the contrast holds, neither course overshadows the other.",
  "Mocha Creme|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Mocha Creme|Shafer 1.5": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Shafer 1.5 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Mocha Creme|Shafer Hillside Select": "Shafer Hillside Select's Stags Leap allocated Cab power flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Shafer Hillside Select on the steak course instead.",
  "Mocha Creme|Shrimp Bisque": "The shrimp bisque alongside the mocha creme -- cream-and-sugar build the close, the meal finishes generous. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Mocha Creme|Shrimp Cocktail": "The shrimp cocktail and the mocha creme -- the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish bookends with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Mocha Creme|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- light spirit can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a richer pour like an aged rum or tawny. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a light spirit. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Siete Leguas Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Mocha Creme|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado's añejo-pour depth leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rested-agave depth sits with the mocha creme, and the soft-spice note softens the dessert. Works; Riedel-luxury over-specified.",
  "Mocha Creme|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon's American-oak Cab character fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Mocha Creme|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Simon Family Estate Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Simon Family Estate Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Mocha Creme|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "Spottswoode Lyndenhurst's Napa estate Cab with structured cassis fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Spottswoode Lyndenhurst for the steak course.",
  "Mocha Creme|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Mocha Creme|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Mocha Creme|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the St Supéry Élu Meritage on the steak course instead.",
  "Mocha Creme|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon backbone carries the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Mocha Creme|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a vodka. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Stoli Elit Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Mocha Creme|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, green, herbaceous, light-bodied with grapefruit bite. Wrong category completely; green-herbal white against cream-cocoa-espresso custard is incoherent. Never pair. Stoneleigh belongs with the wedge salad or lighter starters, not desserts.",
  "Mocha Creme|Swordfish": "The swordfish into the mocha creme -- the meaty steak hands off to silky coffee-chocolate cream, espresso closing the meal. Works; the grilled fish meets the layered close at register.",
  "Mocha Creme|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Tanq 10 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Mocha Creme|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Tanqueray Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Mocha Creme|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Classic Douro Tawny — Taylor Fladgate (founded 1692), smooth balanced, dried fruit, almond, warm clean finish. The almond echoes espresso-roast bitterness and the warm finish plays with yolk-cream custard. Strong heritage-Tawny for mocha creme — Vintage Port's chocolate intensity edges higher on espresso.",
  "Mocha Creme|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Flagship Vintage Port — Taylor Fladgate bottle, extraordinarily rich, dark fruit, chocolate, tobacco, minutes-long evolving finish. The chocolate and tobacco echo espresso-roast depth and the extraordinarily rich profile handles yolk-cream custard. Peak flagship-Vintage for mocha creme — espresso-on-port intensity. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Mocha Creme|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with mocha creme — triple-cask aged against coffee-chocolate. Works; cognac and sherry cask notes find mocha at distinctive register.",
  "Mocha Creme|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Mocha Creme|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico for the steak course.",
  "Mocha Creme|The BG": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the honey threads the cream. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Mocha Creme|The Happy Wife": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build plays cleanly against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Mocha Creme|The Manhattan": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Mocha Creme|The Prisoner Red Blend": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour The Prisoner Red Blend on the steak course instead.",
  "Mocha Creme|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk into the mocha creme -- the smoky bone-in marbling hands off to silky coffee-chocolate cream, espresso depth closing the table. Strong; the theatrical cut meets the layered dessert.",
  "Mocha Creme|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the mocha creme, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for the side — save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Mocha Creme|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with mocha creme — chardonnay-cask bourbon against coffee-chocolate dessert. Strong; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-butter threads mocha at distinctive register.",
  "Mocha Creme|Titos Vodka": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Titos Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Mocha Creme|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the restrained whisky body threads the mocha creme, and the oak softens the cream. Works; boutique character doesn't drive.",
  "Mocha Creme|Tomato Basil": "Tomato Basil and the mocha creme -- acid plays against sweetness, the close stays unburdened. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Mocha Creme|Transfusion": "Transfusion's crisp citrus-ginger build reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Mocha Creme|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the aged-agave weight wraps the mocha creme, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; single-estate character handles richness at craft register.",
  "Mocha Creme|Truffle Fries": "The truffle fries alongside the mocha creme -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; the contrast holds, neither course overshadows the other.",
  "Mocha Creme|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s gentle Irish blend leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the approachable Irish character carries the mocha creme, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; Sinatra Select handles the richness at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Mocha Creme|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "The tuna into the mocha creme -- the sesame crust yields to silky coffee-chocolate cream, espresso closing the meal. Strong; the seared-rare main and the layered cream close share the register cleanly.",
  "Mocha Creme|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "The curry and the mocha creme -- the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth bookends with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Mocha Creme|Venge Family Reserve": "Venge Family Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Venge Family Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Mocha Creme|Venge Scout's Honor": "Venge Scout's Honor's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Venge Scout's Honor lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Mocha Creme|Venge Silencieux": "Venge Silencieux's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Venge Silencieux for the steak course.",
  "Mocha Creme|Vesper": "Vesper's crisp citrus-juniper build holds with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Mocha Creme|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's classic yellow-label Champagne body clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- brut bubbles and chocolate weight refuse each other — needs sweetness, not crisp acid. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Veuve Clicquot Brut for the steak course.",
  "Mocha Creme|Vieux Carré": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build holds with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Mocha Creme|Villon Cognac": "Villon Cognac's eau-de-vie body with oak reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the Limousin-oak weight handles the mocha creme, and the dried-fruit note underlines the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Mocha Creme|Vin Santo": "Tuscan Vin Santo — Trebbiano-Malvasia straw-dried, sealed-barrel years, amber, honey, dried apricot, walnut, nutty oxidative. The walnut echoes espresso-roast bitterness and the honey plays with yolk-cream custard. Strong Vin Santo for mocha creme — Vintage Port's chocolate intensity edges higher on espresso.",
  "Mocha Creme|Weller 107": "Weller 107's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; bold intensity slightly over-specified.",
  "Mocha Creme|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon backbone carries the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; 10-year depth overshoots delicacy.",
  "Mocha Creme|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon weight handles the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the blend's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Mocha Creme|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the caramel-spice register matches the mocha creme, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Mocha Creme|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Wheatley Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Mocha Creme|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the dry rye body wraps the mocha creme, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; premium single-barrel wasted on the salad.",
  "Mocha Creme|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the high-rye spice register matches the mocha creme, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the aging is wasted on the salad.",
  "Mocha Creme|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye-and-oak weight handles the mocha creme, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Mocha Creme|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye-and-oak weight handles the mocha creme, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Mocha Creme|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the high-rye spice register matches the mocha creme, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "Mocha Creme|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye spice cuts the mocha creme, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; IW Harper 15 handles dessert density at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Mocha Creme|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon depth settles into the mocha creme, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; HH 20 frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Mocha Creme|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon depth settles into the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Mocha Creme|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with mocha creme — iconic Double-Oaked against coffee-chocolate dessert. Works; Woodford Double Oaked's second-char caramel threads mocha at KY-benchmark register.",
  "Mocha Creme|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye spice cuts the mocha creme, and the oak softens the cream. Works; flagship depth gives the dessert premium aged character.",
  "Mocha Creme|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye spice cuts the mocha creme, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; blended character doesn't drive.",
  "Mocha Creme|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the mocha creme, and the oak threads the cream. Works; polished 10-year gives the dessert small-batch premium character.",
  "Mocha Creme|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the mocha creme, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; gentle match at matched registers.",
  "Mocha Creme|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the honey threads the cream. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Asparagus": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals plays cleanly against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals sits alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Beignets": "Monkey 47 with beignets — Black Forest premium craft against fried dough. Works; Monkey 47 is over-specified for casual dessert — save the complex botanicals for the seafood or tartare.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Bone Marrow": "Monkey 47 with bone marrow — 47-botanical German gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Monkey 47 Gin on another course instead.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Broccolini": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals leans against the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals sits beside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Burrata": "Monkey 47 with burrata — lingonberry-featuring 47-botanical German gin against fresh dairy. Excellent; Monkey 47's lingonberry-and-forest-herbal complements cream with distinctive Schwarzwald character — tied with Inverroche's fynbos-floral as the featured craft-gin-burrata match.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Butternut Squash": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Carrot Cake": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Monkey 47 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Cheesecake": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Monkey 47 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals reads alongside the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Monkey 47 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Monkey 47 Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Clam Chowder": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Monkey 47 with the cowboy ribeye — Schwarzwald craft gin against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Crab Cake": "Monkey 47 with crab cake — Black Forest 47-botanical craft against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Monkey 47's forest-herbal character adds Schwarzwald dimension to crab no urban-craft gin brings.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Creamy Potato": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals sits beside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Creme Brulee": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Monkey 47 Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Escargot": "Monkey 47 with escargot — 47-botanical Black Forest gin against garlic-butter snails. Excellent; Monkey 47's complex herbal-forest profile cuts butter with Schwarzwald terroir — richer-layered than Botanist's Islay approach.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Monkey 47 with the salmon — Black Forest 47-botanical gin against oily salmon. Strong; Monkey 47's forest-herbal frames the fish at Schwarzwald-premium register; Inverroche's fynbos-floral is still the featured craft-salmon match.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Filet Mignon": "Monkey 47 with the filet — 47-botanical German gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|French Onion": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Monkey 47 with the grilled Caesar — Schwarzwald forest-herbal gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Monkey 47's complexity meets the char at premium register.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Gumbo": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals reads alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals sits alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|House Wedge": "Monkey 47 with the House Wedge — 47-botanical Black Forest against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Monkey 47's complexity meets the bleu with Schwarzwald depth.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Kansas City": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Monkey 47 Gin on another course instead.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Loaded Potato": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Lobster Mac": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Market Fish": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals reads alongside the fish's clean flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Mocha Creme": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Monkey 47 Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Mushrooms": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Monkey 47 Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Porterhouse": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Monkey 47 Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Prime Tartare": "Monkey 47 with the tartare — 47-botanical Black Forest gin against raw beef. Excellent; Monkey 47's herbal-forest complexity lifts tartare's minerality with Schwarzwald depth — the featured Botanical-Forward Craft Gin tartare pairing across the cluster.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals matches the trout's delicate flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Monkey 47 with the chicken — Black Forest 47-botanical craft against roasted poultry. Works; Monkey 47's forest-herbal supports chicken at premium register.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals plays cleanly against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals holds steady against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals sits alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Seafood Tower": "Monkey 47 with the seafood tower — Black Forest Germany, 47 botanicals including lingonberries, forest-herbal premium craft. Excellent; Monkey 47's layered 47-botanical complexity frames the raw bar with Schwarzwald forest-herbal depth — one of the most complex craft-gin tower pairings on the menu.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Seared Scallops": "Monkey 47 with seared scallops — Black Forest craft gin against scallop sweetness. Excellent; the 47-botanical complexity frames scallops with Schwarzwald layered character.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals sits beside the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals plays cleanly against the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Monkey 47 with shrimp bisque — lingonberry-forest craft gin against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Monkey 47's complexity cuts the bisque with Schwarzwald character.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Monkey 47 with shrimp cocktail — 47-botanical German gin against cold shrimp. Strong; Monkey 47's forest-herbal complexity elevates the classic cold app at premium-craft register.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Swordfish": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals sits with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|The Tomahawk": "Monkey 47 with the Tomahawk — Black Forest craft gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Tomato Basil": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals plays cleanly against the tomato basil's bright acid — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Truffle Fries": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals leans against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals finds register with the tuna's seared rare crust — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Monkey 47 Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Monkey 47 Gin's crisp Black-Forest-botanical botanicals sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Asparagus": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the blended-malt body sits with the asparagus, and the honey rounds the side. Works; the complex profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Monkey Shoulder with the au gratin — triple-Speyside-malt blend against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Monkey Shoulder's Speyside-malt fruit handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Beignets": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the scotch depth settles into the beignets, and the heather brightens the dessert. Works; neutral, not elevating.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Bone Marrow": "Monkey Shoulder with bone marrow — Glenfiddich-Balvenie-Kininvie blend against umami fat. Works; Monkey Shoulder's fruit-and-vanilla sits alongside marrow; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Bone-In Filet": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness finds neutral with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the scotch backbone matches the bone-enhanced filet, and the honey rounds the cut. Works; the complex character gives the dessert a refined frame.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Broccoli Cheddar": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the scotch backbone matches the broccoli cheddar, and the oak threads the cream. Works; Caribbean Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Broccolini": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the broccolini, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the side. Works; KBS distinctiveness is slightly over-specified for the two-cut plate.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Brussels and Belly": "Monkey Shoulder with Brussels + Belly — triple-Speyside-malt blend against maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; Monkey Shoulder's rich-fruit-and-vanilla meets the glaze with distinctive Speyside-malt character.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Burrata": "Monkey Shoulder with burrata — cocktail-friendly Speyside-malt blend against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Butternut Squash": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the malt-driven body wraps the squash bisque, and the oak threads the cream. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet triple-Speyside-malt blend. Monkey Shoulder's Speyside-malt dried-fruit-and-vanilla is the featured blended-Scotch-carrot-cake pairing — the Speyside-malt component brings what Dewar's White's honey-vanilla and JW Red's smoke can't. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Cheesecake": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the Highland register carries the cheesecake, and the oak threads the cream. Works; distinctive character gives the dessert a unique story-frame.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Chilean Seabass": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Monkey Shoulder for the steak course.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Chocolate Brownie": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the brownie, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the dessert. Works; Prohibition narrative wasted on salad.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Chocolate Cake": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the malt-driven body wraps the chocolate cake, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the dessert. Works; annual-release complexity over-specified.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Clam Chowder": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the scotch depth settles into the chowder, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; gentle match.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Cowboy Ribeye": "Monkey Shoulder with the cowboy ribeye — triple-Speyside-malt blend against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Monkey Shoulder's Speyside-malt fruit meets the ribeye at distinctive register; JW Red's bold-smoke is the featured blended-Scotch-ribeye pairing.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Crab Cake": "Monkey Shoulder with crab cake — Speyside-malt blend against delicate crab. Avoid; Scotch wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Creamed Spinach": "Monkey Shoulder with creamed spinach — Glenfiddich-Balvenie blend against dairy-rich side. Strong; Monkey Shoulder's Speyside-malt character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Creamy Potato": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the malt-driven body wraps the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Creme Brulee": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch depth settles into the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; the Millennium's register overshoots the dessert's.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Escargot": "Monkey Shoulder -- blended Speyside malts, soft, honey. The blended-malt body sits with the buttered escargot, and the honey rounds the starter. Strong; balance against richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Faroe Island Salmon": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Monkey Shoulder belongs on the steak course.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Filet Mignon": "Monkey Shoulder with the filet — triple-Speyside-malt blend (Glenfiddich + Balvenie + Kininvie), 40% ABV, cocktail-friendly rich-fruit-and-vanilla. Excellent; Monkey Shoulder's Speyside-malt derived profile frames tenderloin with fruit-and-vanilla character that no other blended Scotch brings — it's literally a blend of the single malts we've already individualized.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|French Onion": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the malt-and-oak threads the French onion, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; the boldness pushes slightly past what the soup asks for.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Grilled Caesar": "Monkey Shoulder with the grilled Caesar — Speyside-malt blend against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Monkey Shoulder's rich-fruit-and-vanilla meets the char with Speyside-malt character — softer than JW Red's smoke, more fruit-forward than Dewar's honey.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Gumbo": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the gumbo, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; AE meets the char at wine-finish register.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Highland register carries the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|House Wedge": "Monkey Shoulder with the House Wedge — triple-Speyside-malt blend against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Monkey Shoulder's Speyside-derived fruit threads the wedge with distinctive blended-malt character.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Kansas City": "Monkey Shoulder with the KC — Speyside-malt blend against an 18oz strip. Strong; Monkey Shoulder handles the strip with Speyside-malt fruit-and-vanilla character; JW Red's bold-smoke is still the featured blended-Scotch-KC pairing.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Loaded Potato": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the loaded potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; AE CS meets the char at premium wine-finish register.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Lobster Mac": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the scotch backbone matches the lobster mac, and the oak softens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Market Fish": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Monkey Shoulder for the steak course.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Mocha Creme": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the scotch backbone matches the mocha creme, and the oak softens the cream. Works; Prohibition narrative wasted on salad.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Mushroom Bisque": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the Highland register carries the mushroom bisque, and the oak threads the umami plate. Works; the complex profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Mushrooms": "Monkey Shoulder with mushrooms — triple-Speyside-malt blend against earthy umami. Excellent; Monkey Shoulder's Glenfiddich-Balvenie-Kininvie Speyside-malt base threads mushroom earthiness with fruit-and-vanilla character.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the malt-and-oak threads the PB brownie, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the dessert. Works; HH 20 is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Porterhouse": "Monkey Shoulder with the porterhouse — Speyside-malt blend against strip-and-filet. Strong; Monkey Shoulder frames the two-cut plate with Speyside-malt character.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Prime Tartare": "Monkey Shoulder's char-honey character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel meets the nut character. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Rainbow Trout": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Monkey Shoulder for the steak course.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Roast Half Chicken": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the scotch depth settles into the roast chicken, and the heather brightens the plate. Works; Remus 15 frames the two-cut plate at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the scotch depth settles into the chickpea soup, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; retailer-pick wasted on salad.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Monkey Shoulder for the steak course.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the Highland register carries the garlic spinach, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Seafood Tower": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Monkey Shoulder for the steak course.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Seared Scallops": "Monkey Shoulder with seared scallops — triple-Speyside-malt blend against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Seasonal Soup": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the scotch depth settles into the seasonal soup, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; Old World complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Seasonal Vegetables": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the seasonal vegetables, and the smoke threads the side. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Shrimp Bisque": "Monkey Shoulder with shrimp bisque — Speyside-malt blend against cream-shrimp soup. Works; Monkey Shoulder rides the bisque at distinctive register.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Shrimp Cocktail": "Monkey Shoulder with shrimp cocktail — Speyside-malt blend against cold shrimp. Avoid; Scotch wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Swordfish": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Monkey Shoulder for the steak course.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|The Tomahawk": "Monkey Shoulder -- blended Speyside malts, soft, honey. The scotch depth settles into the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; JD 12's aged depth frames the two-cut plate at premium register.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Tomato Basil": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the malt-and-oak threads the tomato basil, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Truffle Fries": "Monkey Shoulder with truffle fries — Glenfiddich-Balvenie-Kininvie blend against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Monkey Shoulder's Speyside-malt rich-fruit-and-vanilla meets the earthy-rich with distinctive blended-malt character.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Monkey Shoulder belongs on the steak course.",
  "Monkey Shoulder|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the malt-and-oak threads the curry, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Asparagus": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge doubles down on the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Montenegro Amaro on the steak course instead.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Montenegro Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Beignets": "Montenegro with beignets — approachable amaro against fried dough. Works; vanilla-orange is a classic casual-dessert pairing.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Bone Marrow": "Montenegro with bone marrow — approachable amaro against umami fat. Strong; the vanilla-orange is a gentler digestif than Averna's caramel approach.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Bone-In Filet": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Montenegro Amaro for another course.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Broccoli Cheddar": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Montenegro Amaro on the steak course instead.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Broccolini": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge doubles down on the broccolini's tender green stalks -- bitter herbs and a green vegetable side compound the same register — no contrast. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Montenegro Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Brussels and Belly": "Montenegro with Brussels + Belly — vanilla-orange amaro against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; the sweeter amaro style doubles the dish's sweetness slightly.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Burrata": "Montenegro with burrata — amaro against fresh dairy. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Butternut Squash": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Montenegro Amaro on the steak course instead.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Carrot Cake": "Montenegro with carrot cake — Bologna amaro against cream cheese and cinnamon. Vanilla-orange and cake spice find each other cleanly. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Cheesecake": "Montenegro with cheesecake — Bologna amaro against custard. Vanilla-orange against the cheesecake's richness; the most approachable of the classic amari for dessert. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Chilean Seabass": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Montenegro Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Montenegro Amaro|Chocolate Brownie": "Montenegro with the chocolate brownie — Bologna amaro, vanilla-and-orange forward, the crowd-pleasing classic. More approachable than Nonino, sweeter than Averna. The vanilla-orange character makes the brownie a friendly post-dessert digestif. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
