  "Negroni|Asparagus": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build plays cleanly against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Negroni|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build stays alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Negroni|Beignets": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — equal parts, bitter and herbal. The Campari's edge clashes with beignet sweetness; Negroni's savory-bitter profile belongs with mushrooms or marrow, not with a light dessert. Wrong cocktail here. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Negroni|Bone Marrow": "Gin, Contratto, Campari — equal parts, stirred. The bitter-orange backbone cuts marrow's rendered-fat richness directly and the herbal vermouth complexity plays against roasted bone savor. Peak cocktail for marrow when guest wants bitter structure rather than bourbon sweetness.",
  "Negroni|Bone-In Filet": "Negroni's spirit-forward cocktail register mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Negroni belongs on another course.",
  "Negroni|Broccoli Cheddar": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Negroni|Broccolini": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build sits alongside the broccolini's tender green — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Negroni|Brussels and Belly": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari. The bitter-orange cuts belly's rendered fat and Contratto's herbal depth plays against brussels char. Works well — though the Bowdie's Old Fashioned or Espresso OF peak higher with the belly's sweet glaze.",
  "Negroni|Burrata": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — stirred. The bitter-orange crushes burrata's creamy-delicate build and the cocktail's structure has no match for basil-tomato-cheese delicacy. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco, French 75, or light gin cocktails.",
  "Negroni|Butternut Squash": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build sits alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Negroni|Carrot Cake": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — equal parts, spirit-forward. The Campari bitterness clashes with cream-cheese frosting; the cocktail's weight is wrong for a spiced dessert. Keep Negroni for mushrooms, marrow, or as a pre-dinner opener. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Negroni|Cheesecake": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — equal parts. The Campari's bitter edge clashes with the cream cheese sweetness and the sour-cream tang. Wrong cocktail for cheesecake — save Negroni for mushrooms, marrow, or as a pre-dinner opener. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Negroni|Chilean Seabass": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build reads alongside the seabass's buttery flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Negroni|Chocolate Brownie": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — equal parts, bitter and herbal. The Campari's edge clashes with chocolate frosting sweetness; the cocktail's weight is wrong for dessert. Keep Negroni for mushrooms or marrow where bitter meets savory depth. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Negroni|Chocolate Cake": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — equal parts, bitter-herbal. Campari's bitterness clashes with cake sweetness; wrong cocktail for dessert entirely. Keep Negroni with mushrooms or marrow where bitter meets savory depth. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Negroni|Clam Chowder": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Negroni|Cowboy Ribeye": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari. The bitter-orange backbone cuts the Cowboy's marbled fat and Contratto's herbal depth adds complexity; the stirred structure handles the rendered richness. Strong pairing — though the Bowdie's or Espresso OF peak slightly higher for ribeye.",
  "Negroni|Crab Cake": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari. The bitter-orange profile is too aggressive for crab's sweet-delicate meat and the cocktail's structure crushes the romesco. Never pair — route crab cake to French 75, Bee's Knees, or Sauvignon Blanc, not a bitter red cocktail.",
  "Negroni|Creamed Spinach": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — stirred. The bitter-herbal profile cuts the side's dairy weight cleanly and Campari's orange plays against parmesan. Strong pairing — the Negroni's structure handles cream where sweeter cocktails would compound the weight.",
  "Negroni|Creamy Potato": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Negroni|Creme Brulee": "Equal parts gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari. The Campari's bitterness clashes with vanilla custard and the gin adds nothing helpful. Wrong cocktail for crème brûlée — keep Negroni with the mushrooms or marrow where it meets savory depth. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Negroni|Escargot": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari. The Negroni's bitter-herbal profile cuts garlic-butter escargot and Campari's orange adds aromatic depth against the snails' earth. Peak bitter-cocktail pairing for escargot — sophistication with palate structure.",
  "Negroni|Faroe Island Salmon": "Negroni's manhattan-or-old-fashioned register sits alongside the salmon's rich oily flesh: the bold-mix depth handles the salmon, and the bitters edge frames the plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Negroni|Filet Mignon": "Gin, Contratto, Campari — equal parts, stirred. The cocktail's bitter-orange and herbal complexity can meet butter-tender filet, though heavier steaks suit the Negroni better. Strong alternative to Cab or Manhattan when guest wants bitter structure with filet.",
  "Negroni|French Onion": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the orange brightens the caramel. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Negroni|Grilled Caesar": "Gin, Contratto, Campari — stirred, orange peel. The bitter-orange profile cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and Contratto's herbal complexity plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak bitter cocktail for the grilled salad — structural match through the Negroni's edge.",
  "Negroni|Gumbo": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build reads alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Negroni|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build plays cleanly against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Negroni|House Wedge": "Gin, Contratto, Campari. The bitter-orange and herbal complexity cut iceberg-and-bacon weight and Campari's edge plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as an opener for bitter-cocktail fans — Negroni's structure meets the wedge's layered components.",
  "Negroni|Kansas City": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari. The Negroni's bitter-herbal structure handles the KC's lean-bold strip and Campari's orange adds aromatic lift against the char. Peak bitter cocktail option for the most approachable bold cut — structural match.",
  "Negroni|Loaded Potato": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Negroni|Lobster Mac": "Equal parts gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari. The bitter Campari fights the manchego sweetness and truffle's earthy depth; a structural clash. Keep the Negroni for the tartare course where it peaks. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Negroni|Market Fish": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build plays cleanly against the fish's clean flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Negroni|Mocha Creme": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — equal parts, bitter-herbal. Campari's bitterness fights cream-based custard sweetness; wrong cocktail for dessert entirely. Save Negroni for mushrooms or marrow where bitter meets savory depth. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Negroni|Mushroom Bisque": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Negroni|Mushrooms": "Gin, Contratto, Campari. The Campari's bitter-orange and Contratto's herbal complexity meet the mushroom side's earthy butter-sauté directly; the cocktail's structure handles the side's depth. Strong pairing — the Negroni's edge plays against mushroom umami well.",
  "Negroni|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — equal parts. The Campari bitterness against peanut butter frosting is a bad axis; Negroni's savory-bitter profile belongs with mushrooms or marrow, not with chocolate-and-peanut-butter dessert. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Negroni|Porterhouse": "Negroni's spirit-forward cocktail register mis-pairs with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Negroni for another course.",
  "Negroni|Prime Tartare": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — equal parts, stirred, orange peel. The Campari's bitter orange and Contratto's herbal depth cut raw prime beef's iron richness and add structure against tartare seasoning. Peak bitter cocktail pairing for the hand-cut beef course.",
  "Negroni|Rainbow Trout": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build lifts the trout's delicate flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Negroni|Roast Half Chicken": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the cocktail lift carries the herb-crisped bird, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Negroni|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build sits beside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Negroni|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build sits with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Negroni|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build finds neutral with the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Negroni|Seafood Tower": "Negroni's stirred whiskey-led build sits alongside the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the bold-mix depth handles the seafood tower, and the bitters edge frames the starter. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Negroni|Seared Scallops": "Gin, Contratto, Campari. The bitter-orange and herbal weight obliterates the scallops' delicate sweet-seared profile; Campari has no hook into clean seafood. Never pair — scallops demand crisp whites, French 75, or Vesper, not bitter structure.",
  "Negroni|Seasonal Soup": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build stays alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Negroni|Seasonal Vegetables": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build reads alongside the seasonal garden plate — the cocktail lift sits with the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Negroni|Shrimp Bisque": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build sits alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Negroni|Shrimp Cocktail": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build holds steady against the shrimp's chilled sweetness — the cocktail lift carries the shellfish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Negroni|Swordfish": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build holds steady against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Negroni|The Tomahawk": "Negroni's spirit-forward cocktail register mis-pairs with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Negroni on another course instead.",
  "Negroni|Tomato Basil": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build leans against the tomato basil's bright acid — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Negroni|Truffle Fries": "Gin, Contratto, Campari — stirred, orange peel. The bitter-orange backbone plays against truffle oil's earthy richness and the herbal vermouth adds depth against parmesan. Works as a bitter cocktail-side pairing when guest leads with the Negroni.",
  "Negroni|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build finds register with the tuna's seared rare crust — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Negroni|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Asparagus": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the rye backbone drives the asparagus, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Never Say Die Rye with au gratin potatoes — Yorkshire-aged against cheese-and-cream. Works; novelty maturation at matched register.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Beignets": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the dry rye body wraps the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; GTS is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Bone Marrow": "Never Say Die Rye with bone marrow — novelty UK-aged rye against umami fat. The distinctive maturation character threads through marrow's intensity with a voice no domestic rye matches. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Bone-In Filet": "Never Say Die Rye's crisp oak-spice character matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey character holds register with the tenderloin, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Broccoli Cheddar": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the rye-and-oak weight handles the broccoli cheddar, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Broccolini": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the rye-and-oak weight handles the broccolini, and the rye-pepper note underlines the side. Works; accessible gentle match.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Brussels and Belly": "Never Say Die Rye with Brussels + Belly — Yorkshire-aged rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; distinctive maturation story cuts the glaze with UK-cask voice.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Burrata": "Never Say Die Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against Yorkshire-matured rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Butternut Squash": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the rye-and-oak weight handles the squash bisque, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; aging wasted on the salad.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Carrot Cake": "Never Say Die Rye with carrot cake — UK-matured rye against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; distinctive character gives the dessert a unique story-frame.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Cheesecake": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye spice cuts the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; AE Triple Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Chilean Seabass": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Never Say Die Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Chocolate Brownie": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character leans against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Chocolate Cake": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rye-and-oak weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; distinctive character is worth mentioning tableside, even if the pairing isn't marquee.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Clam Chowder": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the rye backbone drives the chowder, and the oak threads the cream. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Cowboy Ribeye": "Never Say Die Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — UK-aged Kentucky rye against cap fat. Strong; distinctive character capable but Kentucky high-proof ryes (Pikesville, Knob Creek Rye, E.H. Taylor Rye) handle the cut with more grip.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Crab Cake": "Never Say Die Rye with crab cake — novelty UK-aged rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Creamed Spinach": "Never Say Die Rye with creamed spinach — UK-aged rye against dairy. Strong; distinctive character sits with richness at novelty register.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Creamy Potato": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Creme Brulee": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the dry rye body wraps the brulee, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Escargot": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; aged 100-proof covers both cuts with premium rye character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Faroe Island Salmon": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character swallows the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Never Say Die Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Filet Mignon": "Never Say Die Rye with the filet — UK-aged Kentucky rye against the 10oz cut. The Yorkshire cask influence gives the filet a singular character; a conversation pour with real substance behind the story. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|French Onion": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the rye spice cuts the French onion, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; single-barrel depth gives the dessert more than Small Batch.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Grilled Caesar": "Never Say Die Rye with the grilled Caesar — UK-matured rye against charred romaine. The distinctive maturation gives the Caesar a craft-story frame. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Gumbo": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the rye-and-oak weight handles the gumbo, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the dry rye body wraps the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|House Wedge": "Never Say Die Rye with the House Wedge — novelty UK-matured rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; distinctive cask character cuts the bleu with Yorkshire-influence voice.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Kansas City": "Never Say Die Rye with the KC — Kentucky rye aged in Yorkshire, UK. Distinctive maritime-climate maturation; sibling to Never Say Die Bourbon, same UK-aging novelty. The story is the draw; Yorkshire cask influence gives the strip a genuinely unique frame no other rye on the list brings. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Loaded Potato": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the rye-and-oak weight handles the loaded potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; EC 18 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Lobster Mac": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the high-rye spice register matches the lobster mac, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Old Fitz 9 brings more depth here.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Market Fish": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Never Say Die Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Mocha Creme": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the mocha creme, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Mushroom Bisque": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the dry rye body wraps the mushroom bisque, and the oak threads the umami plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Mushrooms": "Never Say Die Rye with the mushrooms — UK-matured rye against earthy umami. Strong; distinctive cask character layers into mushroom depth uniquely.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye backbone drives the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a soup — save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Porterhouse": "Never Say Die Rye with the porterhouse — UK-aged rye across strip and filet. Strong; distinctive character covers both cuts with story behind it.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Prime Tartare": "Never Say Die Rye with the tartare — UK-aged Kentucky rye against raw beef. The Yorkshire cask influence is a distinctive counterpoint with story-driven craft. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Rainbow Trout": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Never Say Die Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the rye spice cuts the roast chicken, and the rye edge frames the plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the rye spice cuts the chickpea soup, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; the pour is a touch ambitious for the salad.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Never Say Die Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye backbone drives the garlic spinach, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; JD 12 handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Seafood Tower": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Never Say Die Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Seared Scallops": "Never Say Die Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against UK-aged rye. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Seasonal Soup": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the rye backbone drives the seasonal soup, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the seasonal vegetables, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; single-barrel depth gives the dessert more than Small Batch.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "Never Say Die Rye with shrimp bisque — UK-aged rye against cream-based seafood. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never Say Die Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against UK-aged rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Swordfish": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Never Say Die Rye for the steak course.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|The Tomahawk": "Never Say Die Rye's crisp oak-spice character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon character meets the rendered fat, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Tomato Basil": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the dry rye body wraps the tomato basil, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Truffle Fries": "Never Say Die Rye with truffle fries — Yorkshire-aged rye against truffle-parmesan. The novelty cask character gives the side a distinctive story-driven frame. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Never Say Die Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Never Say Die Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the rye spice cuts the curry, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the bowl. Works; BH Red Wine Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Never Say Die Small Batch|Asparagus": "Never Say Die Small Batch's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the asparagus, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; AE CS meets the char at premium wine-finish register.",
  "Never Say Die Small Batch|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Never Say Die Small Batch with au gratin potatoes — Yorkshire-aged bourbon against cheese-and-cream. Strong; the novel maturation gives the side a unique voice.",
