  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|Salt-Cured Halibut": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon on the steak course instead.",
  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the oak depth lays under the garlic spinach, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|Seafood Tower": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|Seared Scallops": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with seared scallops — Holland MI beer-barrel against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops.",
  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|Seasonal Soup": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the oak depth lays under the seasonal soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|Seasonal Vegetables": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|Shrimp Bisque": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with shrimp bisque — MI beer-barrel against cream-shrimp soup. Avoid; hop-bitter clashes with seafood soup.",
  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|Shrimp Cocktail": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with shrimp cocktail — Holland MI beer-barrel against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|Swordfish": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon for the steak course.",
  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|The Tomahawk": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with the Tomahawk — Holland MI beer-barrel against 32oz cap-fat. Strong; NH Beer Barrel handles the Tomahawk at Michigan-hometown register.",
  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|Tomato Basil": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|Truffle Fries": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with truffle fries — Michigan beer-barrel finish against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; NH Beer Barrel's hop-character meets the earthy-rich with distinctive Michigan-hometown register.",
  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "New York Sour|Asparagus": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. New York Sour belongs on the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Au Gratin Potatoes": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register doubles down on the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- cocktail whiskey and dairy side meet without contrast — both heavy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. New York Sour lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "New York Sour|Beignets": "New York Sour's stirred whiskey-led build leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bold-mix depth handles the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "New York Sour|Bone Marrow": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- the cocktail's weight is forward for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. New York Sour lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "New York Sour|Bone-In Filet": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the New York Sour for another course.",
  "New York Sour|Broccoli Cheddar": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register flattens the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the soup's cream instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Broccolini": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. New York Sour belongs on the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey's oak and sweetness mirror the side's dairy and umami depth. Works cleanly.",
  "New York Sour|Burrata": "Jameson, lemon, simple, egg white, Daou Cab float — off menu. The Cab float's tannin crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile and the egg-white foam doesn't bridge the cheese-basil axis. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco or a crisp white, not a red-wine-floated sour.",
  "New York Sour|Butternut Squash": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register flattens the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the soup's cream instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Carrot Cake": "New York Sour's citrus-lemon build holds steady against the carrot cake's spiced cream cheese — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "New York Sour|Cheesecake": "New York Sour's citrus-lemon build sits with the cheesecake's custard cream — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "New York Sour|Chilean Seabass": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets New York Sour. Excellent; bourbon-base with red-wine float brings fruit-meets-cocoa dimension to the brownie at cocktail register.",
  "New York Sour|Chocolate Cake": "New York Sour's citrus-lemon build reads alongside the cake's layered chocolate — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "New York Sour|Clam Chowder": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register flattens the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the soup's cream instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. New York Sour belongs on the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. Works — cocktail-on-steak coherence.",
  "New York Sour|Crab Cake": "Crab Cake: this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey's oak and caramel are only going to land if the starter can carry weight — check the plate. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "New York Sour|Creamed Spinach": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register crowds the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the New York Sour on the steak course instead.",
  "New York Sour|Creamy Potato": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. New York Sour lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "New York Sour|Creme Brulee": "New York Sour's citrus-lemon build sits with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "New York Sour|Escargot": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register overshadows the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the herb-bright opener. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the New York Sour on the steak course instead.",
  "New York Sour|Faroe Island Salmon": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- whiskey cocktail and oily fish refuse each other — needs acid, not bitters. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. A headliner steakhouse cocktail. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "New York Sour|French Onion": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- bitter-and-bourbon cocktail and savory broth refuse each other. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Bold whiskey against the Caesar's char and umami — weight matches, but the flavors compete. Strong — both land heavy.",
  "New York Sour|Gumbo": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Honey Roasted Carrots": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register doubles down on the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- bourbon-cocktail sugar and glaze sugar compound without lift. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the New York Sour on the steak course instead.",
  "New York Sour|House Wedge": "Jameson, lemon, simple, egg white, Daou Cab float. The sour profile cuts iceberg's cold crunch and blue-cheese richness; the Cab float adds visual interest and red-wine weight against the wedge. Peak opener cocktail when guest wants a showpiece alongside a classic salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "New York Sour|Kansas City": "Kansas City: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. A headliner steakhouse cocktail. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "New York Sour|Loaded Potato": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. New York Sour belongs on the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Lobster Mac": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register crowds the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. New York Sour belongs on the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Market Fish": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Mocha Creme": "New York Sour's citrus-lemon build frames the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "New York Sour|Mushroom Bisque": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register flattens the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- whiskey-cocktail register buries the soup's cream instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Mushrooms": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register crowds the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Peanut Butter Brownie": "New York Sour's stirred whiskey-led build leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bold-mix depth handles the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "New York Sour|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "New York Sour|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth meets the starter's fat and umami — both land heavy. A headliner opener. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "New York Sour|Rainbow Trout": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the bitters-and-bourbon register has no foil against gentle flesh. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. New York Sour belongs on the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Roast Half Chicken": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- whiskey-cocktail weight buries the chicken's mild frame. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. New York Sour belongs on the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Salt-Cured Halibut": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the cocktail needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Seafood Tower": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- whiskey-cocktail weight is wrong for cold shellfish — the opener needs lift, not bitters. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. New York Sour belongs on the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Seared Scallops": "Jameson, lemon, simple, egg white, Daou Cab float. The Cab float's tannic red-wine character crushes the scallops' delicate sweetness despite the lemon brightness; the cocktail wasn't built for fine seafood. Never pair — scallops demand whites or Champagne, not a red-wine-topped sour.",
  "New York Sour|Seasonal Soup": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- bitter-and-bourbon cocktail and savory broth refuse each other. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Seasonal Vegetables": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register overshadows the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- whiskey-cocktail register crowds the green vegetable plate. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the New York Sour on the steak course instead.",
  "New York Sour|Shrimp Bisque": "Jameson, lemon, simple, egg white, Daou Cab float. The lemon-sour cuts the bisque's cream-and-shellfish richness beautifully and the egg-white foam echoes the soup's texture. Strong unconventional pairing — the sour profile handles bisque where heavier cocktails would fight it.",
  "New York Sour|Shrimp Cocktail": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the cocktail's weight is forward for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Swordfish": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- cocktail bitterness pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. New York Sour lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "New York Sour|The Tomahawk": "Jameson, lemon, simple, egg white, Daou Cab float — off menu. The cocktail's sour profile and Jameson's lighter base can't meet the Tomahawk's size and marbled weight; the Cab float alone doesn't carry it. Never pair at peak — route Tomahawk to Cab-forward cocktails.",
  "New York Sour|Tomato Basil": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- soup-salad course needs lift — bitters-and-whiskey brings weight instead. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "New York Sour|Truffle Fries": "Jameson, lemon, simple, egg white, Daou Cab float. The lemon-sour cuts truffle oil's earth and the egg-white foam adds textural contrast against the crispy fries. Works as an unconventional spirit-forward pairing — though heavier whiskey cocktails peak higher for the truffle depth.",
  "New York Sour|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the New York Sour on the steak course instead.",
  "New York Sour|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- bitter-and-bourbon cocktail and savory broth refuse each other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. New York Sour lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Asparagus": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: structured red meets dairy-driven side: the bold tannin scrubs the cream, the dark fruit lifts the indulgence into balance. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Beignets": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Bone Marrow": "Oakville prestige Cab — deep cassis, blackberry, graphite, subtle oak, concentrated. The structured body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the graphite frames the bone depth. Peak prestige Oakville Cab for marrow — Nickel & Nickel's concentrated terroir handles the rich starter with serious structure. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Bone-In Filet": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's bold oak cassis holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the firm tannin reads cleanly against the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Broccoli Cheddar": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth overwhelms the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Broccolini": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth overshadows the broccolini's tender green stalks -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Burrata": "Prestige Oakville Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — concentrated graphite-cassis body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with deep blackberry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Nickel & Nickel for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Butternut Squash": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Carrot Cake": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Cheesecake": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Chilean Seabass": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Chocolate Brownie": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Chocolate Cake": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Clam Chowder": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Cowboy Ribeye": "Nickel & Nickel — Oakville prestige Cab with deep cassis, blackberry, graphite, subtle oak, concentrated intensity, age-worthy structured finish. The concentrated body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the graphite meets rendered fat directly. Peak prestige Oakville Cab for Cowboy — terroir specificity for the fatted ribeye. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Crab Cake": "Prestige Oakville Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — concentrated graphite-cassis body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Nickel & Nickel for the steak course where its terroir-specific Oakville concentration shines.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Creamy Potato": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Creme Brulee": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Escargot": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's bold oak cassis frames the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Faroe Island Salmon": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Filet Mignon": "Oakville Cab — deep cassis, blackberry, graphite, subtle oak, concentrated structure. The body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the graphite-cassis echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong prestige Cab for filet — Nickel & Nickel terroir specificity, though lighter Cabs like Jordan peak higher on the delicate cut.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|French Onion": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's bold oak cassis finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Tannin and char, umami and dark fruit — the bold red and the Caesar punch at the same level. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Gumbo": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth overwhelms the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|House Wedge": "Nickel & Nickel — Oakville Cab, deep cassis, blackberry, graphite, subtle oak. The concentrated body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the graphite plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as prestige Oakville Cab for the wedge — terroir-specific red, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Kansas City": "Nickel & Nickel — Oakville Cab with deep cassis, blackberry, graphite, subtle oak. The concentrated body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the graphite-cassis plays against the beefy char. Peak prestige Oakville Cab for Kansas City — terroir-specific bottling on the classic strip. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Loaded Potato": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's bold oak cassis finds neutral with the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Lobster Mac": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's bold oak cassis sits beside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the opulent body matches the indulgent side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Market Fish": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Mocha Creme": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Mushroom Bisque": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's bold oak cassis sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the side's mushroom richness pulls the bold red's body; tannin and oak settle into the umami without overshooting. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Prime Tartare": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's bold oak cassis matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Rainbow Trout": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Roast Half Chicken": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's bold oak cassis sits beside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth jars against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Seafood Tower": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Seared Scallops": "Nickel & Nickel Oakville Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — concentrated graphite-cassis obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Nickel & Nickel for the steak course where its prestige Oakville character peaks.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Seasonal Soup": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth overwhelms the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Seasonal Vegetables": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth overshadows the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Shrimp Bisque": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's bold oak cassis holds with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Shrimp Cocktail": "Nickel & Nickel Oakville Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — concentrated graphite-cassis is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Nickel & Nickel for steak where its terroir-specific character belongs.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Swordfish": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|The Tomahawk": "Oakville prestige Cab — deep cassis, blackberry, graphite, subtle oak, concentrated age-worthy. The structured body meets Tomahawk fat and char directly and the graphite frames the theatrical bone-in cut. Peak prestige Oakville Cab for Tomahawk — Nickel & Nickel's terroir-specific concentration stands up to the biggest marbled steak. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Tomato Basil": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit anchors the mushroom's umami weight; firm tannin and oak hold their register. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Nickel & Nickel Cabernet on the steak course instead.",
  "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet belongs on the steak course.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Asparagus": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Beignets": "Nolet's with beignets — contemporary floral against fried dough. Works; the rose-peach-raspberry notes soften the juniper-sugar pairing with contemporary character.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Bone Marrow": "Nolet's with bone marrow — Turkish rose/peach gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Nolets Dry Gin on another course instead.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals leans against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Broccolini": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals sits beside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Burrata": "Nolet's with burrata — Dutch Nolet distillery (Ketel One family), Turkish rose + peach + raspberry botanicals, floral-contemporary. Excellent; Nolet's rose-peach-raspberry profile is the distinctive gin-burrata pairing in the batch — where Plymouth softens and Sapphire is floral-softer, Nolet's is overtly fruit-forward, reading almost like a floral companion to the cream.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Butternut Squash": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals leans against the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Carrot Cake": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Nolets Dry Gin for the steak course.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Cheesecake": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Nolets Dry Gin for the steak course.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Nolets Dry Gin's gin lift with floral-and-pepper matches register with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the herbal gin body sits with the seabass, and the dry gin lift plays against the plate. Strong; Nolets Dry Gin earns its place next to the seabass.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Nolets Dry Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Nolets Dry Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Clam Chowder": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the juniper carries the shellfish. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Nolets Dry Gin for another course.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Crab Cake": "Nolet's with crab cake — Nolet-family fruit-forward gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Nolet's rose-peach-raspberry frames crab with contemporary character British London Drys don't offer.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Creamy Potato": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals finds neutral with the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Creme Brulee": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Nolets Dry Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Escargot": "Nolet's with escargot — Turkish rose + peach + raspberry against garlic-butter snails. Strong; Nolet's fruit-floral meets butter with contemporary character, though flagship Tanq's proof cuts sharper.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Nolet's with the salmon — floral-contemporary Dutch gin against oily salmon. Strong; Nolet's rose-peach lifts the fish with contemporary distinction.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Filet Mignon": "Nolet's with the filet — contemporary Dutch gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified; Nolet's fruit-floral is a distinctive case.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|French Onion": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals stays alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Nolet's with the grilled Caesar — Dutch floral gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Nolet's rose-peach meets the char softly; Tanq 10's citrus is still the sharper gin-Caesar.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Gumbo": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals holds with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals plays cleanly against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|House Wedge": "Nolet's with the House Wedge — floral-contemporary Dutch gin against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Nolet's fruit-forward botanicals meet bleu sharpness with contemporary distinction — the unexpected wedge pairing that actually works.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Kansas City": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Nolets Dry Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Loaded Potato": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals plays cleanly against the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Lobster Mac": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals sits beside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the juniper carries the shellfish. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Market Fish": "Nolet's with the market fish — Dutch Nolet-distillery gin against featured fish. Works; Nolet's fruit-forward botanicals handle fish at distinctive register.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Mocha Creme": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Nolets Dry Gin for the steak course.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals reads alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Mushrooms": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals sits beside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Nolets Dry Gin for the steak course.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Porterhouse": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Nolets Dry Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Prime Tartare": "Nolet's with the tartare — Dutch Nolet-distillery gin against raw beef. Strong; Nolet's fruit-forward floral supports tartare at contemporary register; Bombay Dry's angular juniper still leads the gin-tartare pairing.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals sits with the trout's delicate flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Nolet's with the chicken — Turkish rose/peach/raspberry against roasted poultry. Works; Nolet's fruit-floral supports chicken at contemporary register.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals plays cleanly against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals finds register with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals reads alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Seafood Tower": "Nolet's with the seafood tower — Dutch Turkish-rose gin against the raw bar. Excellent; Nolet's fruit-floral is the conversation-starter gin pairing for the tower — distinctive positioning vs traditional British offerings.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Seared Scallops": "Nolet's with seared scallops — Turkish rose/peach/raspberry Dutch gin against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Nolet's fruit-forward floral profile mirrors scallop sweetness distinctively — the contemporary-gin pairing no British London Dry replicates.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals reads alongside the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Nolet's with shrimp bisque — Turkish rose/peach/raspberry against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Nolet's fruit-floral rides the bisque at contemporary register.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Nolet's with shrimp cocktail — Dutch floral gin against cold shrimp. Excellent; Nolet's rose-peach softness elevates the classic cold app at contemporary register — less traditional than Tanq, more fruit-forward than Sapphire.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Swordfish": "Nolets Dry Gin's gin lift with floral-and-pepper leans against the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the floral edge frames the swordfish, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|The Tomahawk": "Nolet's with the Tomahawk — Dutch floral gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Tomato Basil": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals reads alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Truffle Fries": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals sits beside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals sits with the tuna's seared rare crust — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Nolets Dry Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Nolets Dry Gin's bold char-floral botanicals plays cleanly against the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
