  "Not a Paper Plane|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build finds register with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build reads alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|Seafood Tower": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build holds the line with the tower's chilled shellfish — the cocktail lift carries the shellfish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|Seared Scallops": "Bulleit bourbon, Nonino amaro, Aperol, Angostura — stirred. Spirit-forward amaro-bourbon obliterates scallops' delicate sweet-seared profile; Nonino's bitterness has no hook into clean seafood. Never pair — scallops demand crisp whites, French 75, or Vesper.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|Seasonal Soup": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|Seasonal Vegetables": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build leans against the seasonal garden plate — the cocktail lift sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|Shrimp Bisque": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build holds with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|Shrimp Cocktail": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build finds register with the shrimp's chilled sweetness — the cocktail lift carries the shellfish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|Swordfish": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build plays cleanly against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|The Tomahawk": "Not a Paper Plane's spirit-forward cocktail register mis-pairs with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Not a Paper Plane lands on another course — not here.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|Tomato Basil": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build sits beside the tomato basil's bright acid — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|Truffle Fries": "Bulleit, Nonino amaro, Aperol, Angostura — stirred down, spirit-forward, bittersweet. The Nonino's herbal bitterness and Aperol's orange depth echo truffle's complexity. Grappa-based amaro character rewards attention on this non-obvious cocktail pairing. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build plays cleanly against the tuna's seared rare crust — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Not a Paper Plane|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Oban 14|Asparagus": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the Highland register carries the asparagus, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Oban 14|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Oban 14 with the au gratin — West Highland against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Oban's subtle smoke handles the richness at maritime-Highland register.",
  "Oban 14|Beignets": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the scotch depth settles into the beignets, and the heather brightens the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Oban 14|Bone Marrow": "Oban 14 with bone marrow — tiny-distillery coastal Highland against umami fat. Excellent; Oban's sea-salt-and-honey character is the featured Highland-marrow pairing — the maritime-umami resonance is distinctive, more lively than the sherry-forward or honey-floral alternatives.",
  "Oban 14|Bone-In Filet": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the scotch backbone matches the bone-enhanced filet, and the honey rounds the cut. Works; concentrated blend slightly over-specified.",
  "Oban 14|Broccoli Cheddar": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the malt-driven body wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the oak softens the cream. Works; the story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Oban 14|Broccolini": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the scotch backbone matches the broccolini, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the side. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Oban 14|Brussels and Belly": "Oban 14 with Brussels + Belly — coastal West Highland against maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; Oban's sea-salt-and-honey meets the glaze with distinctive maritime character.",
  "Oban 14|Burrata": "Oban 14 with burrata — West Highland against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Oban 14|Butternut Squash": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the squash bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; regional craft at matched register.",
  "Oban 14|Carrot Cake": "Oban 14 with carrot cake — West Highland single malt against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; Oban's honey-and-sea-salt thread the cake at maritime register; Dalmore 12's sherry-finish is the featured Highland-carrot-cake match.",
  "Oban 14|Cheesecake": "Oban 14 with cheesecake — coastal West Highland against custard. Works; Oban's sea-salt-and-honey threads the cheesecake at maritime register.",
  "Oban 14|Chilean Seabass": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Oban 14 for the steak course.",
  "Oban 14|Chocolate Brownie": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the malt-driven body wraps the brownie, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; the light body doesn't overshoot the salad.",
  "Oban 14|Chocolate Cake": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the scotch backbone matches the chocolate cake, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "Oban 14|Clam Chowder": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the malt-and-oak threads the chowder, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a soup — save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Oban 14|Cowboy Ribeye": "Oban 14 with the cowboy ribeye — West Highland coastal single malt against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Oban's maritime character frames the ribeye; Oban 18's deeper sherry-finish is the featured Highland-ribeye match.",
  "Oban 14|Crab Cake": "Oban 14 with crab cake — maritime Highland against delicate crab. Avoid; even the coastal-terroir doesn't save this — Scotch overwhelms crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Oban 14|Creamed Spinach": "Oban 14 with creamed spinach — West Highland coastal against dairy-rich side. Strong; Oban's sea-salt and honey ride the cream with maritime resonance.",
  "Oban 14|Creamy Potato": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the coastal salinity meets the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Oban 14|Creme Brulee": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the scotch depth settles into the brulee, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; BIB slightly over-specified.",
  "Oban 14|Escargot": "Oban 14's crisp Highland-sea-salt character finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Oban 14|Faroe Island Salmon": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Oban 14 on the steak course instead.",
  "Oban 14|Filet Mignon": "Oban 14 with the filet — West Highland tiny-distillery single malt, coastal-influenced with sea-salt and honey, dried fruit + subtle smoke hint. Excellent; Oban's maritime-Highland character frames tenderloin with coastal terroir — where Glenmorangie 10 is honey-floral-inland and Dalmore is sherry-luxury, Oban is maritime-Highland.",
  "Oban 14|French Onion": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the malt-and-oak threads the French onion, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; the heat slightly overshoots the dessert's delicacy, but the caramel helps the match.",
  "Oban 14|Grilled Caesar": "Oban 14 with the grilled Caesar — maritime Highland single malt against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Oban's subtle smoke hint and sea-salt lift the char-and-anchovy with coastal-Highland resonance — distinct from Glenmorangie's bourbon-cask lift.",
  "Oban 14|Gumbo": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the malt-driven body wraps the gumbo, and the smoke threads the bowl. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "Oban 14|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the malt-and-oak threads the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; Discovery complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Oban 14|House Wedge": "Oban 14 with the House Wedge — coastal Highland against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Oban's maritime character engages the bleu with distinctive coastal register.",
  "Oban 14|Kansas City": "Oban 14 with the KC — coastal West Highland against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Oban's subtle smoke and sea-salt frame strip marbling with maritime character.",
  "Oban 14|Loaded Potato": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the loaded potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the pour's refinement is underused by the salad.",
  "Oban 14|Lobster Mac": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the malt-driven body wraps the lobster mac, and the oak threads the cream. Works; Portwood is over-specified for sides.",
  "Oban 14|Market Fish": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Oban 14 for the steak course.",
  "Oban 14|Mocha Creme": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the scotch backbone matches the mocha creme, and the oak softens the cream. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "Oban 14|Mushroom Bisque": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the scotch depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the oak balances the umami register. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Oban 14|Mushrooms": "Oban 14 with mushrooms — coastal West Highland against earthy umami. Excellent; Oban's sea-salt and subtle smoke notes marry mushroom earthiness with distinctive coastal-umami resonance — the featured Highland Scotch-mushroom pairing.",
  "Oban 14|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the scotch depth settles into the PB brownie, and the malt edge frames the dessert. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "Oban 14|Porterhouse": "Oban 14 with the porterhouse — coastal Highland against strip-and-filet. Strong; Oban's maritime character frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Oban 14|Prime Tartare": "Oban 14's crisp Highland-sea-salt character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Oban 14|Rainbow Trout": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Oban 14 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Oban 14|Roast Half Chicken": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the malt-and-oak threads the roast chicken, and the malt edge frames the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Oban 14|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the malt-and-oak threads the chickpea soup, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; Sinatra Select handles the richness at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Oban 14|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Oban 14 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Oban 14|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the malt-and-oak threads the garlic spinach, and the heather brightens the side. Works; accessible gentle match.",
  "Oban 14|Seafood Tower": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Oban 14 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Oban 14|Seared Scallops": "Oban 14 with seared scallops — coastal Highland against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Oban 14|Seasonal Soup": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the scotch depth settles into the seasonal soup, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; AE CS meets the char at premium wine-finish register.",
  "Oban 14|Seasonal Vegetables": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the scotch backbone matches the seasonal vegetables, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the side. Works; the pour's delicacy doesn't overpower the soup, but doesn't particularly elevate it either.",
  "Oban 14|Shrimp Bisque": "Oban 14 with shrimp bisque — coastal Highland against cream-shrimp soup. Works; Oban's maritime profile cuts the bisque with coastal-terroir resonance — the most thematic bisque pairing in the Highland cluster despite being Works tier due to whisky-meets-soup challenge.",
  "Oban 14|Shrimp Cocktail": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character fights the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Oban 14 for the steak course.",
  "Oban 14|Swordfish": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Oban 14 for the steak course.",
  "Oban 14|The Tomahawk": "Oban 14 -- Highland-coastal, maritime saltiness, honey. The coastal salinity meets the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; clean match at the register.",
  "Oban 14|Tomato Basil": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the Highland register carries the tomato basil, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "Oban 14|Truffle Fries": "Oban 14 with truffle fries — West Highland coastal single malt against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Oban's subtle smoke and sea-salt meet the earthy-rich with distinctive maritime-Highland character.",
  "Oban 14|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Oban 14 on the steak course instead.",
  "Oban 14|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the Highland register carries the curry, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Oban 18|Asparagus": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the malt-and-oak threads the asparagus, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the side. Works; the complex character is wasted on the salad.",
  "Oban 18|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Oban 18 with the au gratin — 18yr aged West Highland against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Oban 18's aged sherry-finish handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Oban 18|Beignets": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the malt-and-oak threads the beignets, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the dessert. Works; gentle match at matched registers.",
  "Oban 18|Bone Marrow": "Oban 18 with bone marrow — premium 18yr coastal Highland against umami fat. Excellent; Oban 18's aged maritime character is the featured Highland-marrow pairing at premium register — the coastal-umami + sherry-depth combination is richer than Oban 14's simpler maritime approach.",
  "Oban 18|Bone-In Filet": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the scotch backbone matches the bone-enhanced filet, and the smoke threads the cut. Works; the light body doesn't overshoot the salad.",
  "Oban 18|Broccoli Cheddar": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the scotch backbone matches the broccoli cheddar, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "Oban 18|Broccolini": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the scotch backbone matches the broccolini, and the smoke threads the side. Works; bonded weight pushes slightly past the soup.",
  "Oban 18|Brussels and Belly": "Oban 18 with Brussels + Belly — aged coastal Highland with sherry-finish against maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; Oban 18's aged maritime + sherry-finish meets the glaze at premium register.",
  "Oban 18|Burrata": "Oban 18 with burrata — 18yr aged coastal Highland against fresh dairy. Avoid; Scotch weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Oban 18|Butternut Squash": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the scotch backbone matches the squash bisque, and the oak threads the cream. Works; bonded weight slightly over-specified.",
  "Oban 18|Carrot Cake": "Oban 18 with carrot cake — aged maritime Highland with sherry-finish against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; Oban 18's sherry-finish dried-fruit marries the cake's spice at premium register; Dalmore 12 still leads the Highland-carrot-cake match.",
  "Oban 18|Cheesecake": "Oban 18 with cheesecake — premium aged maritime Highland against custard. Works; Oban 18's aged maritime + sherry-finish threads the cheesecake at premium register.",
  "Oban 18|Chilean Seabass": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Oban 18 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Oban 18|Chocolate Brownie": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the malt-driven body wraps the brownie, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Oban 18|Chocolate Cake": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the malt-driven body wraps the chocolate cake, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the dessert. Works; gentle match.",
  "Oban 18|Clam Chowder": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the malt-and-oak threads the chowder, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Oban 18|Cowboy Ribeye": "Oban 18 with the cowboy ribeye — premium aged coastal Highland against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Oban 18 is the featured Highland-ribeye pairing — the aged maritime + sherry-finish depth is built for fattier cuts where even Glenmorangie 18 catches up but doesn't surpass.",
  "Oban 18|Crab Cake": "Oban 18 with crab cake — premium aged Highland against delicate crab. Avoid; even the coastal-terroir doesn't save this — Scotch overwhelms crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Oban 18|Creamed Spinach": "Oban 18 with creamed spinach — premium aged coastal Highland against dairy-rich side. Strong; Oban 18's aged character and sherry-finish ride the cream at premium maritime register.",
  "Oban 18|Creamy Potato": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the scotch backbone matches the potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; allocated character doesn't drive.",
  "Oban 18|Creme Brulee": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the Highland register carries the brulee, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; richer profile is slightly over-specified for the dessert.",
  "Oban 18|Escargot": "Oban 18's bold brine-honey character finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Oban 18|Faroe Island Salmon": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a scotch. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Oban 18 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Oban 18|Filet Mignon": "Oban 18 with the filet — premium aged West Highland Oban, 18yr with sherry-cask finish, deeper maritime-plus-sherry character. Excellent; Oban 18's aged maritime + sherry-finish combination is distinctive Highland-premium — where Oban 14 brings coastal-with-subtle-smoke, the 18 adds sherry-finish depth for the tenderloin.",
  "Oban 18|French Onion": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the malt-and-oak threads the French onion, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; the spicy-sweet character gives the dessert a Barton frame.",
  "Oban 18|Grilled Caesar": "Oban 18 with the grilled Caesar — aged maritime Highland with sherry-finish against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Oban 18's aged coastal + sherry-finish meets the char with deeper resonance than Oban 14's entry approach.",
  "Oban 18|Gumbo": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the malt-driven body wraps the gumbo, and the honey rounds the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Oban 18|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Highland register carries the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; the light body doesn't overshoot the salad.",
  "Oban 18|House Wedge": "Oban 18 with the House Wedge — 18yr aged Oban against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Oban 18's aged coastal depth engages the bleu at premium register.",
  "Oban 18|Kansas City": "Oban 18 with the KC — 18yr maritime Highland with sherry-finish against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Oban 18's aged maritime + sherry-cask is distinctive against strip marbling — the coastal-sherry combination no Speyside or other Highland replicates.",
  "Oban 18|Loaded Potato": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the malt-driven body wraps the loaded potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; richer profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Oban 18|Lobster Mac": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the lobster mac, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; the match is gentle and approachable.",
  "Oban 18|Market Fish": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Oban 18 for the steak course.",
  "Oban 18|Mocha Creme": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the malt-driven body wraps the mocha creme, and the oak threads the cream. Works; own-plus-sourced over-specified.",
  "Oban 18|Mushroom Bisque": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the scotch depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the oak threads the umami plate. Works; boutique-blend depth gives composed dessert character.",
  "Oban 18|Mushrooms": "Oban 18 with mushrooms — 18yr aged West Highland against earthy umami. Excellent; Oban 18's aged maritime + sherry-finish depth threads mushroom earthiness at premium coastal-Highland register.",
  "Oban 18|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the scotch depth settles into the PB brownie, and the heather brightens the dessert. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Oban 18|Porterhouse": "Oban 18 with the porterhouse — 18yr aged West Highland against strip-and-filet. Strong; Oban 18's aged maritime + sherry-finish frames the two-cut plate at premium coastal register.",
  "Oban 18|Prime Tartare": "Oban 18 -- aged maritime Highland, spice, honey. The scotch backbone matches the tartare, and the oak threads the umami plate. Strong; decennial-release complexity sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Oban 18|Rainbow Trout": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Oban 18 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Oban 18|Roast Half Chicken": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the malt-and-oak threads the roast chicken, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the plate. Works; boutique-sourced wasted on salad.",
  "Oban 18|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the Highland register carries the chickpea soup, and the oak-and-smoke underlines the bowl. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Oban 18|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Oban 18 for the steak course.",
  "Oban 18|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the scotch depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the malt edge frames the side. Works; distinctive character gives the dessert a unique story-frame.",
  "Oban 18|Seafood Tower": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Oban 18 for the steak course.",
  "Oban 18|Seared Scallops": "Oban 18 with seared scallops — aged maritime Highland against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Oban 18|Seasonal Soup": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the malt-and-oak threads the seasonal soup, and the malt edge frames the bowl. Works; complexity wasted on salad.",
  "Oban 18|Seasonal Vegetables": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the scotch backbone matches the seasonal vegetables, and the honey rounds the side. Works; AE CS handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Oban 18|Shrimp Bisque": "Oban 18 with shrimp bisque — premium maritime Highland against cream-shrimp soup. Works; Oban 18's aged coastal character cuts the bisque with thematic maritime resonance, though over-specified for a soup.",
  "Oban 18|Shrimp Cocktail": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth smothers the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Oban 18 for the steak course.",
  "Oban 18|Swordfish": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Oban 18 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Oban 18|The Tomahawk": "Oban 18 -- aged maritime Highland, spice, honey. The Highland register carries the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; Sinatra Select's grooved-barrel character frames the two-cut plate at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Oban 18|Tomato Basil": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth reads alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the scotch depth settles into the tomato basil, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; curated single-barrel covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Oban 18|Truffle Fries": "Oban 18 with truffle fries — 18yr West Highland against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Oban 18's aged maritime + sherry-finish meets the earthy-rich at premium register.",
  "Oban 18|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Oban 18 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Oban 18|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the Highland register carries the curry, and the heather brightens the bowl. Works; multi-distillery complexity doesn't drive.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Asparagus": "Ocho Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak-aged tequila body threads the asparagus, and the oak threads the side. Works; craft character doesn't compete with dish density.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: cheese-and-cream potatoes meet Ocho Anejo. Works; single-estate character handles richness at craft register.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Beignets": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the añejo register carries the beignets, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Bone Marrow": "Ocho Anejo's agave-char character holds the line with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Bone-In Filet": "Ocho Anejo's agave-char character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-tequila depth carries the cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Broccoli Cheddar": "Ocho Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the barrel-rested body handles the broccoli cheddar, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Broccolini": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the rested-agave depth sits with the broccolini, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the side. Works; clean accessible match.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed meets Ocho Anejo. Excellent; ex-American-whiskey cask character rides the maple glaze at Los-Altos-craft register — whiskey-cask aging echoes bourbon-adjacent notes into the glaze.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Burrata": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Ocho Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Butternut Squash": "Ocho Anejo's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the barrel-rested body handles the squash bisque, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; single-estate character handles richness at craft register.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Ocho Anejo. Strong; ex-whiskey-cask-aged Los-Altos character amplifies cake spice at craft register.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Cheesecake": "Ocho Anejo's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the barrel-rested body handles the cheesecake, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; 1942 aged depth is a touch over-specified for the small plate.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Chilean Seabass": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- agave and oily fish refuse each other — the fish needs acid, not aged spirit. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Ocho Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Ocho Anejo. Excellent; mineral-driven highland character with whiskey-cask aging threads chocolate at craft-vintage register.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Chocolate Cake": "Ocho Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rested-agave depth sits with the chocolate cake, and the soft-spice note softens the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Clam Chowder": "Ocho Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the chowder, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; golden ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Cowboy Ribeye": "Ocho Anejo's rested-agave body matches register with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the añejo register carries the cap-fat ribeye, and the soft-spice note softens the cut. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Crab Cake": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character smothers the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- aged-agave register crowds the delicate shellfish frame. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Ocho Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Creamed Spinach": "Ocho Anejo's agave-char character finds register with the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the añejo weight overshoots the light side, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Creamy Potato": "Ocho Anejo's añejo-pour depth leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rested-agave depth sits with the potato soup, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; the extended maturation gives the side more than standard reposados bring, though not a standout.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Creme Brulee": "Ocho Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak-aged tequila body threads the brulee, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; the bold pour cuts the glaze but is slightly much for the small plate.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Escargot": "Ocho Anejo's agave-char character matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Faroe Island Salmon": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Ocho Anejo on the steak course instead.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: tender lean cut meets Ocho Anejo. Excellent; the field-specific terroir with ex-whiskey-cask aging frames filet at vintage-craft register — where Patron is the recognizable pour, Ocho is the nerd's pick.",
  "Ocho Anejo|French Onion": "Ocho Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the oak-aged tequila body threads the French onion, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; distinctive ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Grilled Caesar": "Ocho Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the barrel-rested body handles the grilled Caesar, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; the pour is a touch over-specified for the side.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Gumbo": "Ocho Anejo's rested-agave body leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the aged-agave weight wraps the gumbo, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; clean and refined but not a marquee pairing.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Ocho Anejo's rested-agave body finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the añejo register carries the honey carrots, and the caramel-agave register brightens the side. Works; multi-cask complexity over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Ocho Anejo|House Wedge": "Ocho Anejo's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the barrel-rested body handles the wedge, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Kansas City": "Ocho Anejo: Los Altos Arandas single-estate vintage-dated tequila from Carlos Camarena — each bottle field-specific, transparent sourcing, 1 year in ex-American-whiskey casks. Excellent on KC strip; the mineral-driven highland character with whiskey-cask aging frames the lean cut at craft-transparency register — Ocho is the connoisseur añejo.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Loaded Potato": "Ocho Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the barrel-rested body handles the loaded potato soup, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Lobster Mac": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the barrel-rested body handles the lobster mac, and the oak threads the side. Works; distinctive composed with this dish.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Market Fish": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- aged-agave weight buries the delicate flesh — agave register is wrong for fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Ocho Anejo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Mocha Creme": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rested-agave depth sits with the mocha creme, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Mushroom Bisque": "Ocho Anejo's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the oak-aged tequila body threads the mushroom bisque, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: earthy umami meets Ocho Anejo. Excellent; Los Altos mineral-agave threads mushroom-earth at field-specific terroir register — single-estate earth meets restaurant earth.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the oak-aged tequila body threads the PB brownie, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Porterhouse": "Ocho Anejo's agave-char character finds register with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Prime Tartare": "Ocho Anejo's agave-char character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Rainbow Trout": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Ocho Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Roast Half Chicken": "Ocho Anejo's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the añejo register carries the roast chicken, and the caramel-agave register brightens the plate. Works; ceremonial over-specified.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Ocho Anejo's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak-aged tequila body threads the chickpea soup, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- aged-agave weight buries the delicate flesh — agave register is wrong for fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Ocho Anejo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Ocho Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the oak-aged tequila body threads the garlic spinach, and the oak threads the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Seafood Tower": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- añejo weight has no foil against cold shellfish — needs sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Ocho Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Ocho Anejo. Avoid; aged-agave heavy for scallops. Reach for a blanco, light rum, or vodka.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Seasonal Soup": "Ocho Anejo's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the seasonal soup, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Seasonal Vegetables": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rested-agave depth sits with the seasonal vegetables, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the side. Works; Ultra over-specified.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Shrimp Bisque": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character reads alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the rested-agave depth sits with the shrimp bisque, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; the wine-cask notes don't compete with the dish's sweetness effectively.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Shrimp Cocktail": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- añejo weight has no foil against cold shellfish — needs sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Ocho Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Swordfish": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- aged tequila is wrong register for a main fish or poultry course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Ocho Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Ocho Anejo|The Tomahawk": "Ocho Anejo's agave-char character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-tequila depth grips the marbled cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Tomato Basil": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the tomato basil, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: Parmesan-and-truffle meets Ocho Anejo. Excellent; mineral-driven highland character with whiskey-cask depth handles truffle-salt-fat at craft register.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the cask-aged agave register pulls against the savory sear. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; the better calls are above. Ocho Anejo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Ocho Anejo|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Ocho Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the curry, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; the wine-cask character adds interest but isn't a standout.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Asparagus": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Odette Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: the structured red's grip carries the rich side's dairy load; oak-driven body absorbs the cream and tannin clears the finish. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Beignets": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Odette Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone-In Filet": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon -- Stags Leap Cab, dark fruit, sage, cedar. The structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccoli Cheddar": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality overwhelms the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccolini": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Burrata": "Refined Stags Leap Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — velvet-glove sage-cedar body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with dark fruit. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Odette for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Butternut Squash": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Carrot Cake": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Odette Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Cheesecake": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Chilean Seabass": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Brownie": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Cake": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Clam Chowder": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Odette Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Cowboy Ribeye": "Stags Leap Cab — dark fruit, sage, cedar, graphite, firm refined tannin. The velvet-glove body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the sage-cedar meets rendered fat. Strong refined Cab for Cowboy — iron-fist-velvet-glove balance, though Silver Oak, Caymus, or Frias peak higher for the fattiest ribeye.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Crab Cake": "Refined Stags Leap Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — velvet-glove sage-cedar body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Odette for the steak course where its iron-fist-velvet-glove character shines.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the structured red's grip carries the rich side's dairy load; oak-driven body absorbs the cream and tannin clears the finish. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamy Potato": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Odette Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Creme Brulee": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Odette Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Escargot": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon -- Stags Leap Cab, dark fruit, sage, cedar. The structured body wraps the buttered escargot, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Faroe Island Salmon": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Odette Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Filet Mignon": "Odette — Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality. Dark fruit, sage, cedar, graphite, firm but remarkably refined tannin. The body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the sage-cedar echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak refined Cab for filet — Stags Leap signature balance on the classic tenderloin cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|French Onion": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's bold sage cassis reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's structure meets the Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold dressing — weight on weight. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Gumbo": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|House Wedge": "Odette — Stags Leap Cab, dark fruit, sage, cedar, graphite, refined tannin. The velvet-glove body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the sage-cedar plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as refined Stags Leap Cab for the wedge — iron-fist-velvet-glove red, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Kansas City": "Kansas City: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Loaded Potato": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the structured body wraps the loaded potato soup, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Lobster Mac": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's bold sage cassis stays alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Market Fish": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Mocha Creme": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Odette Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushroom Bisque": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's bold sage cassis sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the bold red's cassis grip carries the mushroom-and-truffle depth — structured weight on the earthy side. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Porterhouse": "* Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin runs straight into the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the powerful body carries the dual-cut steak, and the tannin grips the cut. Peak mountain Merlot-Cab for filet — worth explaining Mernet to curious guests.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Prime Tartare": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's bold sage cassis holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Rainbow Trout": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Roast Half Chicken": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the structured body wraps the roast chicken, and the cassis frames the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality jars against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Odette Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Seafood Tower": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Odette Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Seared Scallops": "Odette Stags Leap Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — velvet-glove sage-cedar obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Odette for the steak course where its refined Stags Leap character peaks.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Soup": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality overwhelms the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Vegetables": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality overshadows the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Bisque": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's bold sage cassis leans against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Cocktail": "Odette Stags Leap Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — velvet-glove sage-cedar is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Odette for steak where its iron-fist-velvet-glove character belongs.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Swordfish": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Odette Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|The Tomahawk": "Odette — Stags Leap Cab, dark fruit, sage, cedar, graphite, refined tannin. The velvet-glove body meets Tomahawk fat and char and the sage-cedar frames the theatrical bone-in cut. Strong refined Cab for Tomahawk — iron-fist-velvet-glove balance, though Opus or Shafer Hillside peak higher for the biggest steak.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Tomato Basil": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality overwhelms the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the side's mushroom richness pulls the bold red's body; tannin and oak settle into the umami without overshooting. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Odette Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Asparagus": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak depth lays under the asparagus, and the oak frames the side. Works; light body doesn't overshoot.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the au gratin — cigar-paired bourbon against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Old Elk Cigar Cut handles the richness at cigar-companion register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Beignets": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the caramel-spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Bone Marrow": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with bone marrow — Old Elk distillery cigar-paired bourbon against umami fat. Excellent; Old Elk Cigar Cut's cigar-companion character engages marrow — built for rich flavors, it finds its native pairing here.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Bone-In Filet": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the bone-in filet — Old Elk Colorado craft against tenderloin-with-bone. Works; Old Elk Cigar Cut frames the cut at cigar-companion register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Broccoli Cheddar": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon backbone carries the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; bonded weight pushes slightly past the soup.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Broccolini": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the brown-spirit warmth threads the broccolini, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; EHT Full Proof handles the richness at historic Kentucky register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Brussels and Belly": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with Brussels + Belly — Fort Collins craft bourbon designed for cigar pairing against maple-glazed pork belly. Excellent; Old Elk Cigar Cut's purpose-built character meets the glaze at distinctive cigar-companion register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Burrata": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with burrata — Fort Collins craft cigar-paired against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Butternut Squash": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the squash bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; flagship depth gives the dessert premium aged character.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Fort Collins craft bourbon. Works; Old Elk Cigar Cut threads the cake at cigar-companion register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Cheesecake": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with cheesecake — cigar-paired Old Elk against custard. Works; Old Elk Cigar Cut threads the cheesecake at cigar-companion register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Chilean Seabass": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Old Elk Cigar Cut for the steak course.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Chocolate Brownie": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the chocolate brownie — Old Elk cigar-pairing bourbon against cocoa. Excellent; Old Elk Cigar Cut's distinctive character meets cocoa at cigar-companion register — chocolate and cigars share a palate vocabulary.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Chocolate Cake": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Clam Chowder": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon body sits with the chowder, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Cowboy Ribeye": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the cowboy ribeye — Colorado craft bourbon designed for cigar pairing against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Old Elk Cigar Cut's cigar-companion character frames cap-fat with distinctive purpose-built register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Crab Cake": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with crab cake — cigar-paired bourbon against delicate lump crab. Avoid; cigar-paired bourbon is the opposite of seafood-friendly. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Creamed Spinach": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with creamed spinach — Old Elk Colorado craft against dairy-rich side. Strong; Old Elk Cigar Cut's cigar-paired character rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Creamy Potato": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; premium proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Creme Brulee": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; boutique-sourced doesn't drive.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Escargot": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's bold char-chocolate character holds steady against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Faroe Island Salmon": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Old Elk Cigar Cut lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Filet Mignon": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the filet — Colorado distillery cigar-paired bourbon against tenderloin. Strong; Old Elk Cigar Cut frames tenderloin at distinctive cigar-companion register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|French Onion": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; Sinatra Select handles the richness at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Grilled Caesar": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the grilled Caesar — Old Elk Colorado craft against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Old Elk Cigar Cut meets the char at cigar-companion register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Gumbo": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the gumbo, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; milestone-release doesn't drive.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon body sits with the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|House Wedge": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon weight handles the wedge, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; AE CS handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Kansas City": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the KC — Old Elk Distillery (Colorado Fort Collins) bourbon designed specifically for cigar pairing, distinctive cigar-companion character. Excellent; Old Elk Cigar Cut's cigar-designed character is the featured distinctive-finish-bourbon-KC pairing — purpose-built to accompany rich flavors, which makes strip marbling its native territory.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Loaded Potato": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon backbone carries the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Lobster Mac": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon weight handles the lobster mac, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the Dylan-brand story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Market Fish": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Old Elk Cigar Cut on the steak course instead.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Mocha Creme": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mocha creme, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; a gentle composed match at the premium tier.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Mushroom Bisque": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Mushrooms": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with mushrooms — Fort Collins craft bourbon against earthy umami. Strong; Old Elk Cigar Cut's distinctive character threads mushrooms at cigar-companion register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet cigar-paired Old Elk. Strong; Old Elk Cigar Cut's purpose-built character handles dessert density at cigar-companion register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Porterhouse": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the porterhouse — Old Elk cigar-paired against strip-and-filet. Works; Old Elk Cigar Cut frames the two-cut plate at cigar-companion register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Prime Tartare": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's bold char-chocolate character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Rainbow Trout": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Old Elk Cigar Cut on the steak course instead.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Roast Half Chicken": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the plate. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for a side — save the bottle for the main course.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak depth lays under the chickpea soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; BIB slightly over-specified.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Old Elk Cigar Cut lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the oak frames the side. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Seafood Tower": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Old Elk Cigar Cut for the steak course.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Seared Scallops": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with seared scallops — Old Elk cigar-paired against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Seasonal Soup": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Seasonal Vegetables": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon weight handles the seasonal vegetables, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; boutique-blend depth gives composed dessert character.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Shrimp Bisque": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with shrimp bisque — cigar-paired bourbon against cream-shrimp soup. Avoid; cigar-bourbon character clashes with seafood soup.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Shrimp Cocktail": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with shrimp cocktail — cigar-paired Old Elk against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|Swordfish": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Old Elk Cigar Cut for the steak course.",
  "Old Elk Cigar Cut|The Tomahawk": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the Tomahawk — Old Elk cigar-paired against 32oz cap-fat. Strong; Old Elk Cigar Cut handles the Tomahawk at cigar-companion register.",
