  "Old Emmer|Rainbow Trout": "Old Emmer's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Old Emmer belongs on the steak course.",
  "Old Emmer|Roast Half Chicken": "Old Emmer's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the oak depth lays under the roast chicken, and the oak frames the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Old Emmer|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Old Emmer's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Old Emmer|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Old Emmer's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Old Emmer for the steak course.",
