  "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year|Swordfish": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year's aged wheated BIB's mature honey swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Old Fitzgerald 9 Year for the steak course.",
  "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year|The Tomahawk": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads register-aligned with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bourbon body sits with the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla rounds the flame-grilled char. Strong; matched register with curated depth.",
  "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year|Tomato Basil": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year's aged wheated BIB's mature honey leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; complexity wasted on salad.",
  "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year|Truffle Fries": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year with truffle fries — aged wheated BIB against truffle-parmesan. The mature honey gives the side a distinctive sophisticated frame. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year's aged wheated BIB's mature honey overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Old Fitzgerald 9 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Asparagus": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the caramel-spice register matches the asparagus, and the oak frames the side. Works; boutique-blend depth gives composed dessert character.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: cheese-and-cream potatoes meet aged OGD. Strong; OGD 16's rye-spice handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Beignets": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the oak depth lays under the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing is pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Bone Marrow": "OGD 16 with bone marrow — 16yr OGD high-rye against umami fat. Excellent; OGD 16's rye-forward aged spice engages marrow at distinctive aged-Beam register.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Bone-In Filet": "OGD 16 with the bone-in filet — aged OGD high-rye against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; OGD 16's rye-forward aged character frames the cut with distinctive spice at aged-Beam register.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Broccoli Cheddar": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Broccolini": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the broccolini, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; the pour is a touch ambitious for the salad.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet aged OGD. Works; OGD 16's rye-spice meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Burrata": "OGD 16 with burrata — 16yr OGD against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Butternut Squash": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon weight handles the squash bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; grain notes don't really engage the soup but don't clash.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Carrot Cake": "OGD 16 with carrot cake — aged OGD high-rye against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; OGD 16's rye-forward aged spice is the featured aged-bourbon-carrot-cake pairing — the rye spice marries the cake's cinnamon naturally where non-rye aged bourbons don't.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Cheesecake": "OGD 16 with cheesecake — aged high-rye OGD against custard. Works; OGD 16's rye-spice threads the cheesecake at distinctive register.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Chilean Seabass": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's 16yr OGD high-rye bourbon overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Old Grand-Dad 16 Year for the steak course.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Chocolate Brownie": "OGD 16 with the chocolate brownie — aged high-rye OGD against cocoa. Strong; OGD 16's rye-spice meets cocoa at distinctive register.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Chocolate Cake": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon backbone carries the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Clam Chowder": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon depth settles into the chowder, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; bonded weight overpowers this dish.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Cowboy Ribeye": "OGD 16 with the cowboy ribeye — aged high-rye OGD against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; OGD 16's rye-spice frames the ribeye at distinctive register.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Crab Cake": "OGD 16 with crab cake — 16yr OGD against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Creamed Spinach": "OGD 16 with creamed spinach — 16yr OGD high-rye against dairy-rich side. Strong; OGD 16's rye-spice cuts the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Creamy Potato": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon weight handles the potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; aging wasted on the salad.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Creme Brulee": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; boutique-craft doesn't drive.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Escargot": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon backbone carries the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; boutique character covers both cuts; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Faroe Island Salmon": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's 16yr OGD high-rye bourbon overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Old Grand-Dad 16 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Filet Mignon": "OGD 16 with the filet — Old Grand-Dad 16yr, Beam Suntory's aged OGD with high-rye mash bill for rye-forward spice character. Excellent; OGD 16's aged rye-forward spice is the distinctive aged-bourbon-filet pairing — where HH-family brings dried-fruit-aged character, OGD brings aged-rye-spice distinction.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|French Onion": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon body sits with the French onion, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; a composed but not remarkable match.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Grilled Caesar": "OGD 16 with the grilled Caesar — 16yr OGD against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Gumbo": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the brown-spirit warmth threads the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; annual-release complexity over-specified.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; boutique covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|House Wedge": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the brown-spirit warmth threads the wedge, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Kansas City": "OGD 16 with the KC — 16yr OGD high-rye bourbon against an 18oz strip. Excellent; OGD 16's aged rye-forward spice is the featured rye-forward-aged-bourbon-KC pairing — the distinctive spice cuts marbling where dried-fruit-aged bourbons round it.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Loaded Potato": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the brown-spirit warmth threads the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; boutique over-specified.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Lobster Mac": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the brown-spirit warmth threads the lobster mac, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; smooth character is slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Market Fish": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's 16yr OGD high-rye bourbon flattens the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Old Grand-Dad 16 Year for the steak course.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Mocha Creme": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Mushroom Bisque": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the oak depth lays under the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the bourbon's character is distinctive but the pairing isn't a standout.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Mushrooms": "OGD 16 with mushrooms — aged high-rye OGD against earthy umami. Excellent; OGD 16's aged rye-spice threads mushroom earthiness with rye-forward distinction.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet 16yr OGD high-rye. Works; OGD 16 handles dessert density at distinctive register.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Porterhouse": "OGD 16 with the porterhouse — 16yr OGD rye-forward against strip-and-filet. Works; OGD 16 frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Prime Tartare": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads register-aligned with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the brown-spirit warmth threads the tartare, and the oak threads the umami plate. Strong; a composed heritage-blend match; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Rainbow Trout": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's 16yr OGD high-rye bourbon smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Old Grand-Dad 16 Year for the steak course.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Roast Half Chicken": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the oak depth lays under the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the caramel-spice register matches the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the light body doesn't overshoot the salad.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's 16yr OGD high-rye bourbon swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Old Grand-Dad 16 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon body sits with the garlic spinach, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Seafood Tower": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's 16yr OGD high-rye bourbon crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Old Grand-Dad 16 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Seared Scallops": "OGD 16 with seared scallops — aged high-rye OGD against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Seasonal Soup": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon body sits with the seasonal soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for the side — save the rare-cask bottle for the protein course.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Seasonal Vegetables": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon backbone carries the seasonal vegetables, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Shrimp Bisque": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Shrimp Cocktail": "OGD 16 with shrimp cocktail — aged OGD high-rye against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Swordfish": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's 16yr OGD high-rye bourbon flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Old Grand-Dad 16 Year belongs on the steak course.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|The Tomahawk": "OGD 16 with the Tomahawk — aged OGD high-rye against 32oz cap-fat. Works; OGD 16's rye-spice is under-specified for Tomahawk intensity; reach for a barrel-proof bourbon.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Tomato Basil": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Truffle Fries": "OGD 16 with truffle fries — 16yr OGD rye-forward against truffle-parmesan. Strong; OGD 16's aged rye-spice meets the earthy-rich at distinctive register.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's 16yr OGD high-rye bourbon swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Old Grand-Dad 16 Year belongs on the steak course.",
  "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's 16yr OGD high-rye bourbon sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; Caribbean Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Opus One|Asparagus": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against asparagus — firm-tannin prestige Cab against sulfur-forward vegetable is textbook clash territory. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take asparagus with Sauvignon Blanc, Sancerre, or Chablis.",
  "Opus One|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the mocha plays against the lactic richness. Works as collector Napa blend for au gratin — prestige wine on the cheesy side.",
  "Opus One|Beignets": "Mondavi-Rothschild's $950 prestige Cabernet. Extreme mismatch — the beignet cannot carry the wine's structure and the occasion doesn't earn the pour. Never pair; Opus belongs with Tomahawk or Porterhouse where it performs.",
  "Opus One|Bone Marrow": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the mocha-cedar plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak collector Napa blend for marrow — Mondavi-Rothschild prestige on the rich starter. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Opus One|Bone-In Filet": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles bone-in filet's intensified beefy edge and the cedar plays against the bone-deepened cut. Peak collector Napa blend for bone-in filet — Mondavi-Rothschild prestige for the elevated cut. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Opus One|Broccoli Cheddar": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against a cream-and-cheddar broccoli soup — firm-tannin prestige Cab buries the vegetable-cheese classic. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take broccoli cheddar with Chardonnay, Pinot Blanc, or Chablis.",
  "Opus One|Broccolini": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against charred broccolini — firm-tannin prestige Cab is the wrong axis for a vegetable side. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take broccolini with Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Noir.",
  "Opus One|Brussels and Belly": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts pork-belly fat and the mocha plays against charred brussels. Strong collector Napa blend for Brussels and Belly — prestige wine cuts through the rich starter.",
  "Opus One|Burrata": "Collector Napa Cab blend against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Opus One's firm-tannin Bordeaux-blend structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Opus One for the steak course.",
  "Opus One|Butternut Squash": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against a caramelized-squash autumn soup — firm-tannin prestige Cab buries the sweet-cream classic. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take butternut squash with Riesling, Chardonnay, or Pinot Noir.",
  "Opus One|Carrot Cake": "Opus One's Bordeaux-blend allocated-icon power flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Opus One for the steak course.",
  "Opus One|Cheesecake": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet. The dark fruit plays against cheesecake's cream-cheese richness and the mocha frames the graham-cracker crust. Works as collector Napa Cab blend for cheesecake — prestige red-with-dessert works for guest wanting red continuation.",
  "Opus One|Chilean Seabass": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against buttery Chilean seabass — firm-tannin prestige Cab buries the delicate melt-in-mouth fish. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take seabass with Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, or Chablis.",
  "Opus One|Chocolate Brownie": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet. The mocha note echoes chocolate-brownie cocoa directly and the dark fruit sweetens against the dessert. Works as collector Napa Cab blend for chocolate brownie — prestige red-with-chocolate works when guest wants it.",
  "Opus One|Chocolate Cake": "Opus One's Bordeaux-blend allocated-icon power jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Opus One for the steak course.",
  "Opus One|Clam Chowder": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against a cream-based clam chowder — firm-tannin prestige Cab buries the shellfish classic. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take chowder with Chardonnay, Chablis, or Pinot Blanc.",
  "Opus One|Cowboy Ribeye": "Opus One — Oakville Mondavi-Rothschild flagship, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The full body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the dried-violet plays against the beefy char. Peak collector Napa Cab blend for ribeye — Bordeaux-style flagship on the American bold cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Opus One|Crab Cake": "Collector Napa Cab blend against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Opus One's firm-tannin Bordeaux blend buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Opus One for the steak course.",
  "Opus One|Creamed Spinach": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the mocha plays against the spinach base. Strong collector Napa blend for creamed spinach — prestige wine reframes the rich steakhouse side.",
  "Opus One|Creamy Potato": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against a cream-and-potato soup — firm-tannin prestige Cab buries the smooth dairy classic. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk, Porterhouse, or the top steaks; take creamy potato with Chardonnay, Pinot Blanc, or Chablis.",
  "Opus One|Creme Brulee": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet. The dark fruit plays against creme-brulee's vanilla-custard cream and the mocha frames the caramelized crust. Works as collector Napa Cab blend for creme brulee — prestige red-with-dessert for guest wanting red continuation.",
  "Opus One|Escargot": "Mondavi-Rothschild's $950 prestige Cabernet. Same weight-class problem as Caymus but multiplied — the wine flattens every subtle note in the dish. Never pour on the escargot; reserve for the Tomahawk or Porterhouse. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Opus One|Faroe Island Salmon": "Opus One: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets this rich, fatty seafood's buttery flesh and delicate-yet-substantial texture. The bold red's tannin shreds the fish's delicate flake, and the dark fruit clashes with briny sweetness. Textbook avoid — this is the classic red-meat-only territory. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Opus One|Filet Mignon": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin, impeccably balanced. The balanced structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the mocha plays against escargot-butter. Peak collector Napa blend for filet — sell the Mondavi-Rothschild story on the delicate cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Opus One|French Onion": "Opus One's bold cassis-mocha tannin finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Opus One|Grilled Caesar": "Mondavi-Rothschild's $950 prestige Cabernet — the dressing's anchovy and egg yolk flatten the structured tannin. Never pour Opus on the Caesar; the wine loses. Reserve for the Tomahawk or Porterhouse. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Opus One|Gumbo": "Opus One's Bordeaux-blend allocated-icon power jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Opus One on the steak course instead.",
  "Opus One|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against caramelized-honey roasted carrots — firm-tannin prestige Cab is the wrong axis for a sweet-root side. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take honey carrots with Pinot Noir, rosé, or Chardonnay.",
  "Opus One|House Wedge": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark fruit plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as collector Napa blend for the wedge — prestige wine reframes the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "Opus One|Kansas City": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Kansas City's lean-bold strip and the mocha plays against the beefy char. Strong collector Napa blend for the strip — Mondavi-Rothschild on the American steakhouse cut.",
  "Opus One|Loaded Potato": "Opus One's bold cassis-mocha tannin stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis cuts the cheese richness. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Opus One|Lobster Mac": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts lobster-mac's cream-cheese richness and the mocha plays against the dense pasta. Strong collector Napa blend for lobster mac — prestige wine on the indulgent side.",
  "Opus One|Market Fish": "Opus One's Bordeaux-blend allocated-icon power overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Opus One for the steak course.",
  "Opus One|Mocha Creme": "Opus One's Bordeaux-blend allocated-icon power clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Opus One for the steak course.",
  "Opus One|Mushroom Bisque": "Opus One's bold cassis-mocha tannin finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Opus One|Mushrooms": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The cedar-mocha echoes roasted-mushroom glutamate and the dried-violet plays against the earth depth. Works as collector Napa blend for mushrooms — prestige wine on the earthy side.",
  "Opus One|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet. The mocha echoes brownie cocoa and the dark fruit plays against peanut-butter richness. Works as collector Napa Cab blend for peanut butter brownie — prestige red-with-chocolate pairing for guest wanting continuation.",
  "Opus One|Porterhouse": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the cedar plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong collector Napa blend for Porterhouse — Mondavi-Rothschild on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Opus One|Prime Tartare": "Collector Napa Cab blend against raw-beef tartare — Opus One's firm-tannin full-body Bordeaux-blend structure buries raw-beef delicacy. Never pair. Route tartare to Pinot Noir, Beaujolais, lighter reds, or Champagne; keep Opus One for the steak course.",
  "Opus One|Rainbow Trout": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against delicate rainbow trout — firm-tannin prestige Cab buries the freshwater fish. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take trout with Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc.",
  "Opus One|Roast Half Chicken": "Mondavi-Rothschild's $950 prestige Cabernet — wrong weight class for the chicken. The wine buries the bird; the occasion doesn't earn the pour. Save Opus for the steak course where it performs. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Opus One|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against a smoky roasted-pepper chickpea soup — firm-tannin prestige Cab buries the Mediterranean classic. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take the soup with Pinot Noir, rosé, or Rioja.",
  "Opus One|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against salt-cured halibut — firm-tannin prestige Cab buries the delicate cured fish. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take halibut with Chardonnay, Chablis, or Pinot Blanc.",
  "Opus One|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Mondavi-Rothschild's $950 prestige Cabernet. Catastrophic weight mismatch for the lightest side on the sharing menu; the wine crushes the spinach immediately. Never pour — reserve Opus for the Tomahawk or Porterhouse where it earns its price tag. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Opus One|Seafood Tower": "Mondavi-Rothschild's $950 prestige Cabernet — completely wrong weight class for cold shellfish. The wine buries every element of the tower. Never pour here; reserve Opus for the steak course where it can perform. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Opus One|Seared Scallops": "Collector Napa Cab blend against scallop sweetness — Opus One's firm-tannin Bordeaux blend buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Opus One for the steak course.",
  "Opus One|Seasonal Soup": "Opus One's Bordeaux-blend allocated-icon power jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Opus One for the steak course.",
  "Opus One|Seasonal Vegetables": "Opus One's Bordeaux-blend allocated-icon power crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Opus One for the steak course.",
  "Opus One|Shrimp Bisque": "Opus One: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets this rich, creamy seafood soup's sweet-briny depth over dairy-rich base. Bold red meets rich, caramelized depth — the wine's dark fruit threads through the savory base. Not a clean fit — the soup's register clashes. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Opus One|Shrimp Cocktail": "Mondavi-Rothschild's $950 prestige Cabernet — completely overpowering for a cold-shellfish starter. The wine buries the shrimp and the sauces. Never pour here; steer Opus toward the steak course where it performs. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Opus One|Swordfish": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against grilled swordfish — firm-tannin prestige Cab buries the fish even given its meaty character. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take swordfish with Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, or rosé.",
  "Opus One|The Tomahawk": "Opus One — Mondavi-Rothschild Oakville flagship, Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin, full body. The full structure handles Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye and the cedar plays against char. Peak Napa prestige wine for Tomahawk — sell the collaboration story with the cut. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Opus One|Tomato Basil": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against a fresh-herb-and-tomato soup — firm-tannin prestige Cab buries the bright classic. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take tomato basil with Sauvignon Blanc, Sancerre, or Chablis.",
  "Opus One|Truffle Fries": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts truffle-fry potato fat and the cedar-mocha plays against truffle-oil umami. Peak collector Napa Cab blend for truffle fries — prestige wine layered with the truffle hit. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Opus One|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against rare-seared yellowfin tuna — firm-tannin prestige Cab buries the delicate fish. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take tuxedo tuna with Pinot Noir, Pinot Blanc, or Chablis.",
  "Opus One|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Oakville Bordeaux blend against a coconut-milk vegetable curry — firm-tannin prestige Cab buries the spiced classic. Never pair. Route Opus to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take the curry with Riesling, rosé, or Pinot Noir.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Asparagus": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Beignets": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a white wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes for the steak course.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Bone Marrow": "Willamette Trappist Chard against marrow's rendered-fat richness — even rich oak-butterscotch is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Acolytes for salmon, chicken, scallops, crab cake, or the bisque course.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Bone-In Filet": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads light against the cut's weight — a Pinot or Cab would carry it. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes for another course.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Broccoli Cheddar": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity leans against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Broccolini": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bright acid lifts the broccolini, and the citrus cuts the side. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Brussels and Belly": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity leans against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the herbs frame the grilled char. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Burrata": "Willamette Trappist Chardonnay — rich pear, butterscotch, toasted oak. The rich oak weight is heavier than burrata needs — the opulence threatens to overpower the delicate cheese. Strong with restraint; Elk Cove Pinot Blanc or Sancerre peak higher for burrata's light basil-tomato register.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Butternut Squash": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity leans against the squash soup's silky sweet — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Carrot Cake": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Cheesecake": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes for the steak course.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Chilean Seabass": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity sits with the seabass's buttery flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Chocolate Brownie": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Chocolate Cake": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes on the steak course instead.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Clam Chowder": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Cowboy Ribeye": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — rich Oregon Chard crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Acolytes for salmon, chicken, scallops, or the cream-and-bisque course.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Crab Cake": "Willamette Trappist Chardonnay — rich pear, butterscotch, toasted oak, layered. The rich oak weight cuts crab's romesco and the butterscotch plays against the sweet meat directly. Strong Oregon provenance Chardonnay for crab cake — monastery-made opulence on the classic starter.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Creamed Spinach": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Creamy Potato": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Creme Brulee": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Escargot": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes — Willamette Trappist Chard with pear, butterscotch, oak, rich layered. The rich oak-butterscotch cuts escargot's garlic-parsley butter and the toasted oak plays against the French classic. Strong provenance-forward Chardonnay for escargot — monastery story, French dish, opulent pairing.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Faroe Island Salmon": "Willamette Chardonnay made by Trappist monks — rich layered with ripe pear, butterscotch, toasted oak. Mention the provenance — it's a conversation starter. The rich oak weight handles salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen. Peak Oregon Chardonnay for salmon across most seasonal builds. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Filet Mignon": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes — Willamette Trappist Chard with pear, butterscotch, oak, rich layered. The rich oak weight handles butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the butterscotch echoes escargot-butter toppers. Works as opulent Chardonnay-with-steak; classic reds peak higher for filet.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|French Onion": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Grilled Caesar": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes — Willamette Trappist Chard, pear, butterscotch, oak, rich. The rich oak cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the butterscotch plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong opulent Chardonnay for grilled Caesar — monastery provenance adds conversation to the pairing.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Gumbo": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity plays cleanly against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|House Wedge": "Willamette Trappist Chardonnay — rich pear, butterscotch, toasted oak. The rich oak weight cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the butterscotch plays against blue cheese dressing. Strong provenance Chardonnay for the wedge — opulent weight reframes the classic steakhouse salad, mention the monastery.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Kansas City": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes for another course.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Loaded Potato": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity sits beside the loaded potato's richness — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Lobster Mac": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity stays alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Market Fish": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity sits with the fish's clean flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Mocha Creme": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Mushroom Bisque": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Mushrooms": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity stays alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Porterhouse": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- white wine can't stand up to the marbled char — the cut earns a structured red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes on another course instead.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Prime Tartare": "Willamette Trappist Chardonnay — rich pear, butterscotch, oak. The rich oak weight can handle raw prime beef's iron richness but the butterscotch-oak profile is heavier than the delicate tartare needs. Works as unconventional Chardonnay; Sancerre, Chablis, or serious rosé peak higher for tartare.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Rainbow Trout": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity plays cleanly against the trout's delicate flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Roast Half Chicken": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes — Trappist Monastery Chardonnay from Willamette with pear, butterscotch, toasted oak, rich layered. The rich oak weight handles roasted chicken; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak provenance-forward Chardonnay for chicken across most seasonal builds. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity stays alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the caramel rounds the earthy plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes — Willamette Trappist Chard, pear, butterscotch, oak, rich. The rich oak cuts the sauté butter and the butterscotch plays against garlic without clashing with spinach's earth. Works as opulent Chardonnay for the sauté side — Oregon monastery weight on a simple side.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Seafood Tower": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity plays cleanly against the tower's chilled shellfish — the crisp acidity lifts the shellfish without weight, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Seared Scallops": "Willamette Trappist Monastery Chardonnay — rich ripe pear, butterscotch, toasted oak. The rich butterscotch-oak aligns with scallops' pan-sear and the pear frames the sweet shellfish. Peak Oregon Chardonnay for scallops — provenance hook plus opulent match on the classic. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Seasonal Soup": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; capable alongside. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Seasonal Vegetables": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity stays alongside the seasonal garden plate — the crisp acidity sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Shrimp Bisque": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes — Willamette Trappist Chardonnay with ripe pear, butterscotch, toasted oak, rich layered. The rich oak weight aligns with the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and the butterscotch plays against the butter-roux. Peak provenance-forward Chardonnay for bisque — worth mentioning the monastery story. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Shrimp Cocktail": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity holds the line with the shrimp's chilled sweetness — the crisp acidity lifts the shellfish without weight, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Swordfish": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the swordfish's meaty flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|The Tomahawk": "Willamette Trappist Chardonnay against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even rich monastery oak has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Acolytes for salmon, chicken, scallops, crab cake, or bisque.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Tomato Basil": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity leans against the tomato basil's bright acid — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Truffle Fries": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity holds with the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the tuna's seared rare crust — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pablo Sour|Asparagus": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- a light cocktail belongs with the opener or aperitif, not a savory side. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift crowds the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- a light cocktail belongs with the opener or aperitif, not a savory side. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Pablo Sour on the steak course instead.",
  "Pablo Sour|Beignets": "Pablo Sour's lemon-driven cocktail body rests at register with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the gin-or-tequila build matches the beignets, and the bubble-or-tonic top lifts the dessert. Strong; the pairing lands at register without strain against the cut.",
  "Pablo Sour|Bone Marrow": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla, egg white. The Pablo's silky foam has no structural match for marrow's rendered-fat richness — lemon and foam don't cut bone depth. Never pair — keep the Pablo for starters and raw bar, route marrow to whiskey cocktails.",
  "Pablo Sour|Bone-In Filet": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pablo Sour lands on another course — not here.",
  "Pablo Sour|Broccoli Cheddar": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the cocktail and the soup-salad course pull in different directions. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a light cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Pablo Sour on the steak course instead.",
  "Pablo Sour|Broccolini": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- a light cocktail belongs with the opener or aperitif, not a savory side. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pablo Sour belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Brussels and Belly": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift crowds Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- a light cocktail belongs with the opener or aperitif, not a savory side. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a light cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pablo Sour lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pablo Sour|Burrata": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla syrup, egg white. The foam-and-vanilla profile meets burrata's creamy-basil delicacy and lemon cuts through without crushing; reposado adds structure. Strong starter pairing — both dishes share silky, elegant texture as their defining trait.",
  "Pablo Sour|Butternut Squash": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- cocktail acid cuts the soup's cream wrong — needs full-bodied lift instead. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Carrot Cake": "Pablo Sour's citrus-lemon build reads alongside the carrot cake's spiced cream cheese — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Pablo Sour|Cheesecake": "Pablo Sour's citrus-lemon build holds the line with the cheesecake's custard cream — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Pablo Sour|Chilean Seabass": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- cocktail sweetness fights the rich fish's natural oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Chocolate Brownie": "Pablo Sour: Bowdie's signature cocktail. Excellent on chocolate brownie; the build-character threads chocolate at cocktail register.",
  "Pablo Sour|Chocolate Cake": "Pablo Sour's citrus-lemon build holds steady against the cake's layered chocolate — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Pablo Sour|Clam Chowder": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- cocktail acid cuts the soup's cream wrong — needs full-bodied lift instead. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Cowboy Ribeye": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- a light citrus cocktail can't carry the marbled char — needs whiskey or red wine weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Pablo Sour for another course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Crab Cake": "Crab Cake: this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness meets this bright tequila cocktail's citrus lift and agave backbone. Light cocktail and delicate starter share the same register — fresh opener. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Pablo Sour|Creamed Spinach": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- cocktail acid on dairy side cuts the cream wrong — texture reads off. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Creamy Potato": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- cocktail acid cuts the soup's cream wrong — needs full-bodied lift instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a light cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Pablo Sour on the steak course instead.",
  "Pablo Sour|Creme Brulee": "Pablo Sour's citrus-lemon build matches the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Pablo Sour|Escargot": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- this cocktail's register doesn't lift the opener — try a sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Faroe Island Salmon": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla syrup, egg white. The lemon-and-vanilla with silky foam can meet the salmon's fat well; preparation changes seasonally, check with kitchen. Works as a lighter cocktail option — though the Pablo's texture peaks higher on starters than on mains.",
  "Pablo Sour|Filet Mignon": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla syrup, egg white. The cocktail's silky texture and sour profile is wrong for steak — foam has no hook into grilled beef. Strong only when guest leads with the Pablo and stays on it; route filet pairing to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan.",
  "Pablo Sour|French Onion": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- the cocktail and the soup-salad course pull in different directions. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a light cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Grilled Caesar": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla, egg white. The lemon cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the foam adds textural contrast against grilled-romaine char; vanilla softens the edges. Strong unconventional pairing — the Pablo's silkiness holds up to the salad's layered profile.",
  "Pablo Sour|Gumbo": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the cocktail and the soup-salad course pull in different directions. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Pablo Sour on the steak course instead.",
  "Pablo Sour|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- cocktail sweetness and glaze sugar compete instead of complementing. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a light cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|House Wedge": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla, egg white. The foam adds textural contrast against iceberg's crunch and lemon cuts bacon-and-blue-cheese dressing; vanilla softens the edges. Peak textural opener for the wedge — 'the most texturally interesting cocktail on the tequila side' shows well.",
  "Pablo Sour|Kansas City": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla syrup, egg white. The cocktail's sour structure is wrong for the KC's lean-beefy strip — foam and lemon have no hook into char. Works only when guest stays on the cocktail; route KC to Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned.",
  "Pablo Sour|Loaded Potato": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the cocktail and the soup-salad course pull in different directions. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Lobster Mac": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- cocktail acid on dairy side cuts the cream wrong — texture reads off. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pablo Sour belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Market Fish": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- light cocktail register is wrong for a main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pablo Sour lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pablo Sour|Mocha Creme": "Pablo Sour's citrus-lemon build sits with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Pablo Sour|Mushroom Bisque": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the cocktail and the soup-salad course pull in different directions. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Mushrooms": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift crowds the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- a light cocktail belongs with the opener or aperitif, not a savory side. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Pablo Sour's citrus-lemon build matches the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Pablo Sour|Porterhouse": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- a light citrus cocktail can't carry the marbled char — needs whiskey or red wine weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Pablo Sour for another course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this bright tequila cocktail's citrus lift and agave backbone. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Pablo Sour|Rainbow Trout": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the trout's delicate flesh -- light cocktail register is wrong for a main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a light cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Roast Half Chicken": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla syrup, egg white. The cocktail's sour-and-silky profile can handle roasted chicken but seasonal accompaniments drive the pairing — check with kitchen. Works as an approachable agave option alongside the chicken for guests wanting textural interest.",
  "Pablo Sour|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the cocktail and the soup-salad course pull in different directions. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a light cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Pablo Sour on the steak course instead.",
  "Pablo Sour|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- light cocktail register is wrong for a main protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a light cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- a light cocktail belongs with the opener or aperitif, not a savory side. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a light cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Seafood Tower": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla syrup, egg white. The silky foam and lemon brightness handles the raw-bar spread — oysters, shrimp, crab legs — and adds textural interest. Strong tower cocktail when guest wants agave with a conversation-piece texture.",
  "Pablo Sour|Seared Scallops": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla syrup, egg white. The foam carries vanilla-and-lemon aromatics on every sip and meets scallops' clean-seared sweetness without crushing delicacy. Peak textural cocktail for scallops — both dishes lead with silky, clean profiles.",
  "Pablo Sour|Seasonal Soup": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the cocktail and the soup-salad course pull in different directions. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Seasonal Vegetables": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- a light cocktail belongs with the opener or aperitif, not a savory side. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Pablo Sour on the steak course instead.",
  "Pablo Sour|Shrimp Bisque": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla syrup, egg white. The silky foam echoes the bisque's cream-and-shellfish texture and lemon cuts the soup's richness. Peak pairing — the cocktail's foam-on-foam play with bisque is a memorable service moment. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Pablo Sour|Shrimp Cocktail": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- this cocktail's register doesn't lift the opener — try a sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Swordfish": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- light cocktail register is wrong for a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a light cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pablo Sour belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|The Tomahawk": "Silky sour agave cocktail with egg-white foam against the biggest steak on the menu — complete mismatch. The Pablo's textural delicacy is obliterated by marbled Tomahawk weight. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "Pablo Sour|Tomato Basil": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the cocktail and the soup-salad course pull in different directions. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pablo Sour belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Truffle Fries": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift crowds the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- a light cocktail belongs with the opener or aperitif, not a savory side. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a light cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pablo Sour lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pablo Sour|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- light cocktail register is wrong for a main protein. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Pablo Sour|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the cocktail and the soup-salad course pull in different directions. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a light cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Paloma|Asparagus": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build stays alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Paloma|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Reposado tequila, vanilla syrup, fresh grapefruit. The grapefruit's bitterness and the reposado's citrus profile fight our manchego gratin's savory cream. Paloma belongs with chicken, salmon, or lighter seafood — not potato sides. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Paloma|Beignets": "Paloma's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Paloma belongs on the steak course.",
  "Paloma|Bone Marrow": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The Paloma's clean light structure has no match for marrow's rendered-fat richness — grapefruit and vanilla don't cut bone depth. Never pair — keep the Paloma for starters, route marrow to whiskey cocktails or big reds.",
  "Paloma|Bone-In Filet": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build holds with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the cocktail is outweighed by the cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Paloma|Broccoli Cheddar": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build sits beside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Paloma|Broccolini": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build stays alongside the broccolini's tender green — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Paloma|Brussels and Belly": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build stays alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Paloma|Burrata": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The bright grapefruit cuts burrata's cream and vanilla adds aromatic softness against basil-tomato; reposado's light oak keeps it from reading thin. Peak starter-tier agave cocktail for burrata — clean on both sides of the pairing.",
  "Paloma|Butternut Squash": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build sits beside the squash soup's silky sweet — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Paloma|Carrot Cake": "Paloma's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Paloma lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Paloma|Cheesecake": "Paloma's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Paloma for the steak course.",
  "Paloma|Chilean Seabass": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build sits with the seabass's buttery flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Paloma|Chocolate Brownie": "Paloma's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Paloma lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Paloma|Chocolate Cake": "Paloma's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Paloma belongs on the steak course.",
  "Paloma|Clam Chowder": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build plays cleanly against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Paloma|Cowboy Ribeye": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The cocktail's light-bright profile has no weight to meet Cowboy's marbled rendered-fat character. Never pair — route Cowboy to Bowdie's Old Fashioned, Espresso Old Fashioned, or Napa Cab, and keep the Paloma for openers.",
  "Paloma|Crab Cake": "Reposado tequila, fresh grapefruit, vanilla syrup — three ingredients, no tricks. The grapefruit's bite cuts crab's romesco and the vanilla softens against sweet crab meat; reposado's light oak adds structure. Peak easy-drinking cocktail for crab cake when guest wants tequila rather than gin.",
  "Paloma|Creamed Spinach": "Reposado tequila, vanilla syrup, fresh grapefruit. The grapefruit's bitterness fights our creamed spinach's parmesan-roux richness; the cocktail has the wrong job here. Save Paloma for chicken, salmon, or lighter work. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Paloma|Creamy Potato": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the oak frames the cream. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Paloma|Creme Brulee": "Paloma's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Paloma for the steak course.",
  "Paloma|Escargot": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build plays cleanly against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the cocktail lift refreshes the starter, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Paloma|Faroe Island Salmon": "Reposado tequila, fresh grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The bright grapefruit and reposado's clean agave profile can meet the salmon's fat content well; preparation changes seasonally, so check with kitchen. Works as a lighter cocktail alternative to Pinot Noir or rosé for salmon.",
  "Paloma|Filet Mignon": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The cocktail's three-ingredient lightness is wrong for steak — grapefruit has no hook into grilled beef. Works only if guest stays on the Paloma through the entrée; route filet to Cab, Pinot, or Manhattan for peak pairing.",
  "Paloma|French Onion": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup — the Paloma's clean light structure has no match for French Onion's long-cooked beef broth and gruyère crust. Never pair. Route the Paloma to raw bar or lighter starters; take French Onion with Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, or a structured red.",
  "Paloma|Grilled Caesar": "Reposado tequila, fresh grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The grapefruit's bite balances anchovy-parmesan dressing and vanilla softens grilled-romaine char. Strong unconventional pairing — the Paloma's brightness holds its own against the salad's layered smoke-and-salt profile.",
  "Paloma|Gumbo": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The grapefruit citrus plays against gumbo's Cajun spice and the tequila's agave adds dimension against andouille. Works as tequila cocktail for gumbo — Gulf-Coast-adjacent route on the stew classic; wines peak higher.",
  "Paloma|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build stays alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Paloma|House Wedge": "Reposado tequila, fresh grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The grapefruit's bitter-bright cuts iceberg-and-blue-cheese weight and vanilla softens against bacon; reposado handles the wedge's layered components. Peak agave opener for the classic wedge — both deliver clean structure.",
  "Paloma|Kansas City": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build plays cleanly against the strip's lean char — the cocktail is outweighed by the cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Paloma|Loaded Potato": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup — the Paloma's clean light structure has no match for a loaded baked-potato soup's bacon-cheddar weight. Never pair. Route the Paloma to raw bar or lighter starters; take loaded potato with Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, or Champagne.",
  "Paloma|Lobster Mac": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build plays cleanly against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the cocktail lift cleans the rich side, and the oak frames the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Paloma|Market Fish": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build sits with the fish's clean flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Paloma|Mocha Creme": "Paloma's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a light cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Paloma for the steak course.",
  "Paloma|Mushroom Bisque": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build sits alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the cocktail lift refreshes against the hearty soup, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Paloma|Mushrooms": "Reposado tequila, vanilla syrup, fresh grapefruit. The grapefruit's bitterness and citrus profile fight mushroom umami; the cocktail has the wrong job here. Save Paloma for chicken, salmon, or lighter work. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Paloma|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Paloma's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Paloma for the steak course.",
  "Paloma|Porterhouse": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build sits alongside the porterhouse's two-cut character — the cocktail is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Paloma|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this bright tequila cocktail's citrus lift and agave backbone. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. Strong — aligned lift.",
  "Paloma|Rainbow Trout": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build lifts the trout's delicate flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Paloma|Roast Half Chicken": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The cocktail's clean bright-agave profile handles roasted chicken well, though seasonal accompaniments drive the pairing — check with kitchen. Works as an approachable cocktail option alongside the chicken for guests wanting something easy-drinking.",
  "Paloma|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Paloma|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build plays cleanly against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Paloma|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build stays alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; capable alongside. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Paloma|Seafood Tower": "Reposado tequila, fresh grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The bright grapefruit handles raw-bar shellfish — oysters, shrimp, crab legs — and the cocktail's clean build doesn't crush anything. Strong tower cocktail when guest wants agave rather than bubbles.",
  "Paloma|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness meets this bright tequila cocktail's citrus lift and agave backbone. Light cocktail and delicate starter share the same register — fresh opener. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Paloma|Seasonal Soup": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build sits alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Paloma|Seasonal Vegetables": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build sits alongside the seasonal garden plate — the cocktail lift sits with the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Paloma|Shrimp Bisque": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The grapefruit cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and vanilla smooths against the soup's weight. Strong pairing — though Sauvignon Blanc and Champagne peak slightly higher for bisque, the Paloma handles it cleanly.",
  "Paloma|Shrimp Cocktail": "Reposado tequila, fresh grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The grapefruit cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and vanilla softens the cocktail-sauce horseradish bite. Peak cocktail pairing for shrimp cocktail when guest wants agave — bright, clean, approachable across any point in the meal.",
  "Paloma|Swordfish": "Paloma's lemon-driven cocktail body leans against the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the bright build frames the swordfish, and the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Paloma|The Tomahawk": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup. Light agave cocktail against the biggest steak on the menu — zero structural overlap. The Paloma's delicacy is crushed by marbled Tomahawk weight. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
