  "Patron XO Cafe|Shrimp Bisque": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Patron XO Cafe for the steak course.",
  "Patron XO Cafe|Shrimp Cocktail": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Patron XO Cafe for the steak course.",
  "Patron XO Cafe|Swordfish": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Patron XO Cafe lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Patron XO Cafe|The Tomahawk": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Patron XO Cafe for another course.",
  "Patron XO Cafe|Tomato Basil": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Patron XO Cafe on the steak course instead.",
  "Patron XO Cafe|Truffle Fries": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Patron XO Cafe for the steak course.",
  "Patron XO Cafe|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Patron XO Cafe for the steak course.",
  "Patron XO Cafe|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Patron XO Cafe belongs on the steak course.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Asparagus": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity sits alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge frames the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity stays alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Beignets": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a sparkling. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut on the steak course instead.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Bone Marrow": "Bouzy Grand Cru rosé Champagne against marrow's rendered-fat richness — Burgundian body is overwhelmed by bone depth and the red-fruit profile has no hook into the marrow's savory center. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Bone-In Filet": "Paul Bara Grand Cru Pinot Noir rosé — structured Champagne with ripe raspberry, red currant, and minerally finish. The Burgundian weight handles bone-enhanced filet's deeper beef character and the red-fruit depth complements the bone. Strong rosé Champagne alternative to classic Cab for bone-in filet.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Broccoli Cheddar": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity holds with the broccoli cheddar's cream — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Broccolini": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity stays alongside the broccolini's tender green — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge frames the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Brussels and Belly": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's mineral-driven sparkling lift finds neutral with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the fine bubbles lift the pork-belly plate, and the citrus frames the side. Works; the two desserts share the close without crowding.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Burrata": "Paul Bara Grand Cru rosé Champagne — structured Pinot Noir with ripe raspberry, red currant, and minerally finish. The red-fruit weight and fine bubbles cut burrata's cream and play cleanly against basil-tomato-pistachio. Strong grower-rosé for burrata — more depth than typical sparkling pours.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Butternut Squash": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity holds with the squash soup's silky sweet — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Carrot Cake": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut for the steak course.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Cheesecake": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a sparkling. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut on the steak course instead.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Chilean Seabass": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity sits with the seabass's buttery flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Chocolate Brownie": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut for the steak course.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Chocolate Cake": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- brut bubbles and chocolate weight refuse each other — needs sweetness, not crisp acid. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a sparkling. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut belongs on the steak course.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Clam Chowder": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity stays alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Cowboy Ribeye": "Paul Bara Grand Cru rosé Champagne against the marbled bone-in Cowboy ribeye — grower depth is still rosé weight and crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Paul Bara for starters or the raw bar.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Crab Cake": "Bouzy Grand Cru Pinot Noir grower Champagne — structured, ripe raspberry, red currant, minerally finish. The serious Burgundian backbone frames crab's sweet meat and cuts romesco cleanly. Peak rosé Champagne for crab cake — more substance than standard pink bubbles. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Creamed Spinach": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Creamy Potato": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Creme Brulee": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a sparkling. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut for the steak course.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Escargot": "Bouzy Grand Cru Pinot Noir grower Champagne — structured with ripe raspberry, red currant, and minerally finish. The Burgundian weight and fine bubbles cut escargot's garlic-parsley butter richness and the red-fruit complements the French classic. Strong serious rosé Champagne for escargot.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Faroe Island Salmon": "Bouzy Grand Cru Pinot Noir grower Champagne — structured rosé with raspberry, red currant, and mineral finish. The Burgundian weight handles salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as serious rosé Champagne for salmon across most seasonal builds.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Filet Mignon": "Grower Champagne from Bouzy Grand Cru — Pinot Noir rosé, structured and serious with ripe raspberry, red currant, and mineral finish. The Burgundian weight meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness directly and stands up to the steak. Peak rosé Champagne-with-steak choice. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|French Onion": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Grilled Caesar": "Bouzy Grand Cru rosé Champagne — structured Pinot Noir with raspberry, red currant, mineral finish. The Burgundian body can handle anchovy-parmesan and the mineral finish meets grilled-romaine char. Works as a serious rosé alternative to the LP rosé or LP Le Cuvée for Caesar.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Gumbo": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity stays alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|House Wedge": "Bouzy Grand Cru grower rosé Champagne — structured ripe raspberry and red currant with mineral finish. The Burgundian body and fine bubbles cut iceberg-and-bacon weight and the red-fruit frames blue cheese dressing. Strong elegant pairing for the wedge — grower Champagne depth on the classic.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Kansas City": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut for another course.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Loaded Potato": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity sits alongside the loaded potato's richness — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Lobster Mac": "Paul Bara Grand Cru Pinot Noir rosé — structured grower Champagne with raspberry, red currant, minerality. The body handles lobster-mac's lush cream-cheese weight unconventionally; fine bubbles cut the richness without clashing. Works as unconventional rosé Champagne for the lobster mac side.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Market Fish": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's fine-bubble Champagne register leans against the market fish's clean white-fish flesh: the fine bubbles lift the market fish, and the citrus frames the plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Mocha Creme": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut for the steak course.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Mushroom Bisque": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Mushrooms": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity reads alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- brut bubbles and chocolate weight refuse each other — needs sweetness, not crisp acid. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut on the steak course instead.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Porterhouse": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut for another course.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Prime Tartare": "Grower Champagne from Bouzy Grand Cru — Pinot Noir with ripe raspberry, red currant, and minerally finish. The Burgundian weight meets raw prime beef's iron richness directly and the structured mousse holds through each bite. Peak grower-rosé Champagne for tartare — serious pink bubbles. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Rainbow Trout": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity holds the line with the trout's delicate flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Roast Half Chicken": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity plays cleanly against the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the mousse-and-citrus lifts the herb-crisped bird. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity holds steady against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity sits beside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Seafood Tower": "Bouzy Grand Cru Pinot Noir grower Champagne — structured and serious with ripe raspberry, red currant, fine bubbles, and minerally persistent finish. The Burgundian backbone and red-fruit weight elevate the tower's mixed shellfish and reset palate between oysters, shrimp, and crab. Peak grower rosé for the tower.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Seared Scallops": "Bouzy Grand Cru grower Champagne — structured Pinot Noir rosé with ripe raspberry, red currant, and mineral finish. The Burgundian weight frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the red-fruit depth elevates the pan-sear. Peak serious rosé Champagne for scallops — depth meets precision. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Seasonal Soup": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity sits alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Seasonal Vegetables": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's fine-bubble Champagne register leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the fine bubbles lift the seasonal vegetables, and the citrus frames the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Shrimp Bisque": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity sits beside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Shrimp Cocktail": "Bouzy Grand Cru grower Champagne — structured Pinot Noir rosé with raspberry, red currant, mineral finish. The fine bubbles cut chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the Burgundian body meets cocktail-sauce without crumpling. Strong serious rosé Champagne for shrimp — more depth than standard pink.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Swordfish": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's fine-bubble Champagne register leans against the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the fine bubbles lift the swordfish, and the citrus frames the plate. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|The Tomahawk": "Bouzy Grand Cru grower rosé Champagne against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — Burgundian weight is still a light-bodied rosé and can't meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Paul Bara for the opener.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Tomato Basil": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity holds with the tomato basil's bright acid — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Truffle Fries": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity sits beside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity sits with the tuna's seared rare crust — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's crisp mineral-raspberry acidity sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with the PB brownie — Barton small-batch against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; the spicy-sweet character gives the dessert a Barton frame.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon backbone carries the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. 1881 Napa Valley lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. 2 James Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the PB brownie — rare limited-release against peanut butter and cocoa. The boutique character gives the dessert rare-release craft depth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Aberlour 16": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet 16yr double-matured Speyside. Strong; Aberlour 16 handles the dessert density at balanced-premium register; Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-cask brown-sugar is the featured PB-brownie match.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's aged-agave character clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- aged tequila has nothing to bridge into dessert. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Adictivo Cristalino on the steak course instead.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's aged-agave character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the añejo register carries the PB brownie, and the cooked-agave note underlines the dessert. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Alexander Valley Chardonnay belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Alexander Valley Cyrus belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Alexander Valley Homestead Red lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Amaro Nonino": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet Friulian grappa-based amaro. Orange-peel and botanical complexity lift the dessert with elegant digestif character. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness matches the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the smoke crosses the cocoa. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with the PB brownie — flagship AE against peanut butter and cocoa. Excellent; AE's port-finish jammy-fruit handles dessert density with wine-finish character.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with the PB brownie — cask-strength port-finish against peanut butter and cocoa. Excellent; AE CS's cask-strength jammy-fruit handles dessert density at premium wine-finish register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet triple-cask AE. AE Triple Cask's multi-cask layers handle dessert density with distinctive layered character. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Aperol": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet 11% soft bitter. Works; too light for dessert density at this level.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Asparagus": "The asparagus and the PB brownie -- the asparagus' green-vegetal edge hands off cleanly to the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bright register lifts the sweet, the meal closes clean. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes and the PB brownie -- rich dairy yields to dessert weight, the close composes thickly. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Averna Amaro": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet Sicilian caramel-orange amaro. Fuller-body Averna meets the dessert with classic-amaro authority. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Aviation": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Aviation for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Aviation Gin for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Avion 44": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet 44-month aged Avion. Works; extended maturation composed with dessert.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- light spirit can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a richer pour like an aged rum or tawny. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Avion Silver belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the unaged-cane register matches the PB brownie, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Baileys Irish Cream": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut-chocolate stack meets Baileys. Excellent; the vanilla-cocoa-cream profile wraps peanut-chocolate at classic-Baileys dessert register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Baker's": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet 107-proof Beam small-batch. Works; rich vanilla and high-proof give the dessert bolder match than standard Beam brings.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the PB brownie — vanilla-forward Speyside against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; Balvenie's American Oak vanilla marries the dessert density — the best Speyside match in the batch for PB brownie.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the PB brownie — rum-cask-finished 14yr Speyside against peanut butter and cocoa. Excellent; Caribbean Cask's brown-sugar-and-vanilla is the featured Sherried Speyside-PB-brownie pairing — rum-cask sweetness marries PB fat and cocoa at a register Macallan's dried-fruit-sherry doesn't reach.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with the PB brownie — hand-picked American Oak Speyside against peanut butter and cocoa. Excellent; Balvenie 16's deep vanilla marries the dessert density — the featured Speyside-PB-brownie pairing across the whole cluster, where Balvenie 12's vanilla opens it, Balvenie 16's depth closes it.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet ultra-premium 21yr port-cask Speyside. Strong; Portwood's port-richness handles the dessert density at collector register; Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-cask brown-sugar is the featured PB-brownie match.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with the PB brownie — Bardstown Bourbon Co. French-wine finish against peanut butter and cocoa. Excellent; Bardstown Château Laboude's French-wine character handles dessert density at contemporary-craft register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet 6th Discovery release. Craft-blender complexity finds PB-and-cocoa with layered multi-distillery authority; Discovery series' dessert sweet-spot. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with the PB brownie — stout-barrel-finished bourbon against peanut butter and cocoa. The KBS coffee and chocolate notes find the dessert's cocoa and PB in a way no other bourbon on the menu does; standout dessert pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bardstown Foursquare": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet Foursquare rum-cask. Strong; Bardstown Foursquare's tropical-rum-cask character handles dessert density at Caribbean-finish register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with the PB brownie — Fusion blend against peanut butter and cocoa. The own-plus-sourced complexity gives the dessert layered craft-blender character with Bardstown distillery pride. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with the PB brownie — Rishi's-picked Bardstown against peanut butter and cocoa. The specifically-selected character gives the dessert curated single-barrel depth. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; single-barrel depth gives the dessert more than Small Batch.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon backbone carries the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye backbone drives the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with the PB brownie — BH high-rye red-wine finish against peanut butter and cocoa. Excellent; BH Red Wine Cask's rye-spice + red-wine-fruit handles dessert density with dimension.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the oak depth lays under the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; bold intensity slightly over-specified.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bee's Knees": "Bee's Knees' citrus-and-bright cocktail lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- light cocktail citrus is wrong direction for the dessert's richness. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Bee's Knees for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Beefeater Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Belvedere Vodka lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon backbone carries the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with the PB brownie — 103-proof single-barrel against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; the richer pour finds more with the dessert than Original brings.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with the PB brownie — single-barrel caramel-vanilla against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; the pairing is pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet cask-strength Blanton's. Strong; Blanton's SfB handles dessert density at barrel-proof register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with the PB brownie — 10th-Pact milestone against peanut butter and cocoa. The decennial release gives the dessert milestone-collector depth. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with the PB brownie — Pact 8 against peanut butter and cocoa. The master-blender signature with Mizunara depth gives the dessert layered annual-release character. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the PB brownie — Pact 9 wine-cask against peanut butter and cocoa. The fruit-cask character gives the dessert layered annual-Pact depth. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Bluecoat Gin for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Bodega Noemia A Lisa for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Bombay Dry Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Bombay Sapphire Gin for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bone Marrow": "The bone marrow and the PB brownie -- the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness bookends with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bone-In Filet": "After the bone-in filet, the PB brownie -- the marrow-edged cut gives way to peanut-and-chocolate fudge, the vanilla scoop tempering the heat. Works; the warm dessert lands cleanly after the elevated main.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Booker's": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet cask-strength Beam. Strong; Booker's 120+ proof handles dessert density at barrel-proof register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Botanist Gin": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Botanist Gin for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, rum-soaked cherries, orange, bitters. The vanilla backbone meets peanut butter's richness but the cocktail isn't dessert-built — Frangelico or Espresso Old Fashioned hooks into the hazelnut-PB axis more directly. Works only when guest stays on the house OF through dessert.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon weight handles the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; richer profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the PB brownie — Virginia-aged small-batch against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bowmore 12": "Bowmore 12's lighter-Islay peat character finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the scotch backbone matches the PB brownie, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Braida Montebruna Barbera belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Broccoli Cheddar": "The broccoli cheddar alongside the PB brownie -- rich cream gives way to sweet, the close lands generously. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Broccolini": "Broccolini and the PB brownie -- acid plays against sweetness, the close stays unburdened. Works; clean transition or together if the table wants.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich's progressive unpeated Islay finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the PB brownie, and the honey rounds the dessert. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels + Belly and the PB brownie -- Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts hands off cleanly to the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: savory-into-sweet, the classic close lands intact. Works; sequence them or share the table, no clash.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace's rounded vanilla-and-toffee character leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon backbone carries the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut-chocolate stack meets Buffalo Trace Cream. Excellent; the bourbon-cream wraps peanut-chocolate at bourbon-dessert register — where Baileys brings cocoa-cream lightness, Buffalo Trace Cream brings caramel depth.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon's high-rye spice-forward depth finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the distinctive character is underused by the salad's simplicity.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Burrata": "Burrata and the PB brownie -- delicate gives way to sweet, the close lands light. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Butternut Squash": "Butternut Squash and the PB brownie -- two sweet closes, the meal piles sugar without contrast. Works; either timing holds, the table builds either way.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cade Cabernet Sauvignon's Howell Mountain Cab character clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Cade Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila's rested-agave body sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rested-agave depth sits with the PB brownie, and the soft-spice note softens the dessert. Works; covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Campari": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet bitter orange-rhubarb. Works; Campari is lighter than sipping amari want to be against PB density.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the PB brownie, and the light caramel edge frames the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Canadian Club 43 Year": "Canadian Club 43 Year's Canadian whisky character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the PB brownie, and the easy Canadian register softens the dessert. Works; grain notes don't really engage the soup but don't clash.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's spiced-vanilla character holds steady against the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the crisp body underclubs the dessert, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the easygoing Canadian character handles the PB brownie, and the easy Canadian register softens the dessert. Works; HH 20 is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Casa LBV Port": "Late Bottled Vintage Port — Casa LBV, 4-6 year cask, dark cherry, plum, smooth moderate structure, entry-port approachable. The plum bridges peanut-butter-frosting richness and the dark cherry lifts dense fudge-brownie base. Peak LBV for peanut butter brownie — approachable depth on the dense bake. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's espadín-and-smoke register sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the wood-fire body meets the PB brownie, and the earthy backbone threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus — cocoa, mocha, velvety tannins. The cocoa profile is dessert-adjacent but the tannin clashes with peanut butter frosting and dessert in general. Save Caymus for the steak course; steer PB-brownie guest to Espresso Martini or Frangelico. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Chambord": "Peanut Butter Brownie: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Strong when the dessert is restrained.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Chilean Seabass": "After the seabass, the PB brownie -- the silky-flesh fish gives way to peanut-and-chocolate fudge, vanilla ice cream tempering the close. Works; the rich main hands off to the substantial dessert without strain.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Chopin Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with the PB brownie — California brandy against peanut butter and cocoa. Excellent; Christian Bros's sweetness meets the dessert density at value-forward register.",
