  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Epoch Ingenuity": "Epoch Ingenuity's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Epoch Ingenuity on the steak course instead.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Escargot": "Escargot and the PB brownie -- sugar and umami share the table, the contrast carries the meal. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Espresso Martini": "Tito's, espresso, Grind Espresso Liqueur, Averna amaro, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The coffee-and-vanilla profile meets the peanut butter frosting's richness directly; Averna's bitter edge cuts the peanut butter weight. Peak coffee-cocktail pour for the PB brownie.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. Hazelnut via Frangelico and brown sugar meet peanut butter directly; coffee anchors the pairing against the fudge brownie base. Peak spirit-forward dessert cocktail route. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Evening Land Seven Springs' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Evening Land Seven Springs for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon's estate-Cab Napa character flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Far Niente Chardonnay for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Faroe Island Salmon": "After the salmon, the PB brownie -- the oily-rich fish gives way to peanut-and-chocolate fudge, vanilla ice cream tempering the close. Works; the heavy close after a heavy main, both courses share register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi on the steak course instead.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's structured Napa Cab with cassis depth fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Faust Napa Valley Cabernet for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca with the PB brownie — menthol-saffron against peanut butter and cocoa. Excellent; the PB fat and cocoa bitterness are the pairing that can hold up to Branca's intensity.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta with the PB brownie — mint Fernet against peanut butter and cocoa. Excellent; mint-and-chocolate resonance bridges the dessert; the cooling lift is a distinct feature no other amaro offers.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Filet Mignon": "After the filet, the PB brownie -- the lean tenderloin yields to peanut-and-chocolate fudge, the vanilla scoop tempering the close. Works; the peanut richness reads heavier than the filet but the warm pairing settles cleanly.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Fireball Whisky": "Peanut Butter Brownie: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Works as an indulgence.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Fisher Unity Pinot Noir on the steak course instead.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the PB brownie — 104-proof premium against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; the premium Four Roses character gives the dessert small-batch depth Yellow Label can't bring.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the brown-spirit warmth threads the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with the PB brownie — craft small-batch against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; boutique character gives the dessert a craft frame.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet Detroit-based Double Oaked. Fox & Oden's Double Oaked caramel handles dessert density with intensified-vanilla character. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Frangelico": "★ Frangelico's sweet hazelnut-cocoa character anchors against the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the cocoa underlines the chocolate. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Freemark Abbey Bosché for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|French 75": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- citrus-cocktail brightness pulls against chocolate weight — no bridge between sour-sweet and rich-sweet. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a light cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the French 75 on the steak course instead.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|French Onion": "The French onion and the PB brownie -- the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth bookends with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Frias Block 5": "Frias Block 5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Frias Block 5 for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Piedmont Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee, gentle sweetness, effervescent. The bubbles cut peanut butter density and peach softens the fudge. Strong sparkling alternative when guest wants lighter than coffee or amaro. Flag peanut allergies before pouring.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a light spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. G4 Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof's rested-agave body sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the barrel-rested body handles the PB brownie, and the oak threads the dessert. Works; accessible aged composes with the plate.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the malt-grain blend handles the PB brownie, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the dessert. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|George T. Stagg": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet BTAC collector. Works; GTS is over-specified for dessert — save the collector bottle for the ribeye/Tomahawk.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Ghost Block Zinfandel for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Glenfiddich 12": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet gateway Speyside. Works; Fiddich handles the density at approachable register; Balvenie 12's vanilla is the better match.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Glenfiddich 14": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet new-charred-oak Speyside. Works; Fiddich 14 handles the density; Balvenie 16's deeper vanilla is still the featured match.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Glenfiddich 18": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet Small Batch Reserve. Works; Fiddich 18's dried-fruit handles the density; Balvenie 16's vanilla is still the featured match.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23 with the PB brownie — French Cuvée-cask Speyside against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; Gran Cru is over-specified for PB brownie — reach for Balvenie 16 if the guest wants aged Speyside with this dessert.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the PB brownie, and the smoke threads the dessert. Works; Voyage 23 handles the richness at ocean-finish register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Glenlivet 12": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet softer Speyside. Works; Glenlivet handles the density at benchmark register; Balvenie 12's vanilla is the better match.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10's tallest-stills Highland bourbon-cask character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon-cask vanilla settles into the PB brownie, and the honey-floral lifts the dessert. Works; bonded weight pushes past the soup's register slightly.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18's aged Highland with Oloroso finish finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the malt-and-oak threads the PB brownie, and the heather brightens the dessert. Works; milestone-collector wasted on salad.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "10-year oak-cask oxidative Tawny — Graham's entry-point, dried apricot, walnut, toffee, gentle spice. The walnut echoes peanut-butter-frosting nuttiness and the toffee bridges fudge-brownie brown-sugar depth. Peak Tawny for peanut butter brownie — nut-and-caramel match on the dense base. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "* Graham's 20 Year Tawny -- aged tawny port, nutty-caramel, Douro classic. The dessert-wine register matches the PB brownie, and the noble-rot register brightens the dessert. One of the more interesting dessert pairings on the list. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "2017 declared-vintage Port — Graham's bottle-aged, dense inky, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violet, powerful structured. The dark chocolate bridges peanut-butter-frosting richness and the blackcurrant lifts dense fudge-brownie base. Peak Vintage Port for peanut butter brownie — structured depth on the dense bake. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the PB brownie, and the unaged-cane note plays against the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Grand Marnier": "Peanut Butter Brownie: this dense chocolate dessert's deep cocoa richness meets this sweet cream liqueur's dessert-adjacent lushness. Sweet liqueur's dessert-adjacent sweetness amplifies the dessert — doubles up on sugar. Works as an indulgence.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Gray Whale Gin for the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's cask-strength herbal-high-proof sweetness holds the line with the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Grey Goose Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar and the PB brownie -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; sequence them as opener and close or share the table, no clash.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Gumbo": "The gumbo alongside the PB brownie -- two registers in contrast, the meal earns its shape. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
