  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon weight handles the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; boutique-blend meets the brownie cleanly.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with the PB brownie — iconic Double-Oaked benchmark against peanut butter and cocoa. Excellent; Woodford Double Oaked's intensified-caramel handles dessert density with iconic-benchmark Double-Oaked character.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the high-rye spice register matches the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Voyage 23 handles the richness at ocean-finish register.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the Japanese-whisky precision frames the PB brownie, and the silky body rounds the dessert. Works; grain notes don't really engage the soup but don't clash.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the mizunara-cask depth wraps the PB brownie, and the silky precision frames the dessert. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "Peanut Butter Brownie|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness holds steady against the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the rounded sweetness meets the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Asparagus": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Napa Rutherford Cab — dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body cuts au gratin's cheese-and-cream weight and the cocoa plays against the golden crust. Strong elegant Napa Cab for au gratin — family-estate refinement on the decadent side.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Beignets": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone Marrow": "Peju — Rutherford Cab, dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the cocoa frames the bone depth. Strong elegant Napa Cab for marrow — family-estate refinement, though firmer Cabs like Ghost Block or Silver Oak peak higher for the rich starter.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone-In Filet": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-cherry cassis matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the concentrated body carries the strip, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccoli Cheddar": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccolini": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Brussels and Belly": "Rutherford Cab — dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body handles pork-belly fat and the cocoa plays against charred brussels. Strong elegant Napa Cab for Brussels and Belly — family-estate refinement cuts the rich starter with Rutherford-dust depth.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Burrata": "Elegant Rutherford Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — refined dusty-cocoa body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with dark cherry-cassis. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Peju for the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Butternut Squash": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Carrot Cake": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Cheesecake": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Chilean Seabass": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Brownie": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Cake": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Clam Chowder": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Cowboy Ribeye": "Rutherford Cab — dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body can handle Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness but the refined tannin is gentler than rendered fat demands. Strong Peju for Cowboy — bigger Cabs like Silver Oak, Caymus, or Frias peak higher for the fattiest ribeye.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Crab Cake": "Elegant Rutherford Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — refined dusty-cocoa body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Peju for the steak course where its family-estate Rutherford character shines.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamed Spinach": "Peju — Rutherford Cab, dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the cocoa plays against the earthy sauce. Peak elegant Napa Cab for creamed spinach — family-estate Rutherford character on the classic steakhouse side. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamy Potato": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Creme Brulee": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Escargot": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-cherry cassis frames the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Faroe Island Salmon": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Filet Mignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab — dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, balanced acidity, refined tannin. The elegant body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the Rutherford-dust-plus-cocoa echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak elegant Napa Cab for filet — family-estate restraint on the classic tenderloin cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|French Onion": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-cherry cassis sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Grilled Caesar": "Napa Rutherford Cab — dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the cocoa-dust plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as elegant Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — family-estate refinement on the layered classic salad.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Gumbo": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|House Wedge": "Peju — Rutherford Cab, dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the cocoa plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as elegant Napa Cab for the wedge — Rutherford-dust red choice, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Kansas City": "Peju — family-owned Rutherford Cab with dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, balanced acidity, refined tannin. Rutherford's famous \"dust\" character gives a distinctive mid-palate. The elegant body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the cocoa plays against the beefy char. Peak elegant Napa Cab for Kansas City. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Loaded Potato": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-cherry cassis reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Lobster Mac": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-cherry cassis leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the opulent body matches the indulgent side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Market Fish": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Mocha Creme": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushroom Bisque": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the structured body wraps the mushroom bisque, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushrooms": "Napa Rutherford Cab — dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The Rutherford-dust meshes directly with mushroom umami and the elegant body frames the sauté earth. Peak elegant Napa Cab for mushrooms — Peju's dusty-cocoa plus earth on mushroom is a Rutherford-classic earth match. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Porterhouse": "Peju — Rutherford Cab with dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the Rutherford-dust plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong elegant Napa Cab for Porterhouse — family-estate refinement on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Prime Tartare": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon -- cassis, dusty cocoa, balanced acidity, refined tannin. The structured body wraps the tartare, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; the call holds its register at the table.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Rainbow Trout": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Roast Half Chicken": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the structured body wraps the roast chicken, and the cassis frames the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Seafood Tower": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Seared Scallops": "Peju Rutherford Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — elegant dusty-cocoa obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Peju for the steak course where its refined Rutherford character peaks.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Soup": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Vegetables": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Bisque": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-cherry cassis stays alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Cocktail": "Peju Rutherford Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — refined dusty-cocoa is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Peju for steak where its Rutherford refinement belongs.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Swordfish": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|The Tomahawk": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-cherry cassis finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the opulent body meets the cap-fat, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Tomato Basil": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Truffle Fries": "Peju — Rutherford Cab with dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body frames truffle-fry earth and the Rutherford-dust cuts the crisp oil-and-salt with complexity. Peak elegant Napa Cab for truffle fries — family-estate character meets the decadent side. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Peju Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Asparagus": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- cognac on a green vegetable side is wrong direction — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a cognac. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pierre Ferrand Cognac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Pierre Ferrand with the au gratin — artisanal cognac against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Ferrand's craft character handles the richness.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Beignets": "Pierre Ferrand with beignets — unblended vintage-style against fried dough. Works; Ferrand's craft honey-floral is a thoughtful finisher.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Bone Marrow": "Pierre Ferrand with bone marrow — craft Grande Champagne against umami fat. Excellent; Ferrand's floral-honey artisanal profile cuts marrow with unblended character — distinct from Courvoisier's drier oak approach; the thinking-person's cognac-marrow pairing.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Bone-In Filet": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel finds neutral with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the artisan-cognac depth wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the orange-peel frames the cut. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Broccoli Cheddar": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the Limousin-oak weight handles the broccoli cheddar, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Broccolini": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- cognac on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a cognac. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Pierre Ferrand Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Brussels and Belly": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel leans against Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the artisan-cognac depth wraps the pork-belly plate, and the orange-peel frames the side. Works; Rémy's elegance handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Burrata": "Pierre Ferrand with burrata — artisanal cognac against fresh dairy. Avoid; cognac weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Butternut Squash": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the eau-de-vie body carries the squash bisque, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Carrot Cake": "Pierre Ferrand with carrot cake — artisanal Grande Champagne against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Ferrand's honey-floral marries the spice and raisins with craft character.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Cheesecake": "Pierre Ferrand with cheesecake — artisanal Grande Champagne against custard dessert. Excellent; Ferrand's floral-honey threads the cheesecake with craft-premium finesse — parallel to Rémy VSOP's elegance, with distinct unblended character.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Chilean Seabass": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Pierre Ferrand Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Chocolate Brownie": "Pierre Ferrand with the chocolate brownie — unblended cognac against cocoa. Strong; Ferrand's floral-honey marries cocoa cleanly at craft-premium register.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Chocolate Cake": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the cognac register matches the chocolate cake, and the rancio register softens the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Clam Chowder": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the brandy depth wraps the chowder, and the rancio register softens the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Cowboy Ribeye": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's cognac-orange-peel character sits with the ribeye's cap fat and char — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Crab Cake": "Pierre Ferrand with crab cake — artisanal cognac against delicate crab. Avoid; cognac weight wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Creamed Spinach": "Pierre Ferrand with creamed spinach — artisanal cognac against dairy-rich side. Strong; Ferrand's unblended character rides the cream at craft register.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Creamy Potato": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the Limousin-oak weight handles the potato soup, and the oak-and-vanilla edge brightens the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Creme Brulee": "Pierre Ferrand with creme brulee — unblended vintage-style cognac against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Excellent; Ferrand's floral-honey finds the caramel with artisanal elegance — alongside Rémy VSOP's Fine Champagne approach in the featured-pairing register.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Escargot": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's cognac-orange-peel character matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the barrel-aged depth cuts the umami fat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Faroe Island Salmon": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a cognac. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Pierre Ferrand Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Filet Mignon": "Pierre Ferrand with the filet — artisanal Grande Champagne 1er Cru cognac, unblended vintage-style, traditional methods. Excellent; Ferrand's craft-premium floral-honey character frames tenderloin with artisanal polish — the bartender-favorite cognac-steak pairing where Hennessy is democratic and Rémy is elegant-premium.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|French Onion": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the French onion, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Grilled Caesar": "Pierre Ferrand with the grilled Caesar — Grande Champagne 1er Cru against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Ferrand's floral-honey meets the char at functional register.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Gumbo": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the artisan-cognac depth wraps the gumbo, and the orange-peel frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- cognac on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a cognac. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Pierre Ferrand Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|House Wedge": "Pierre Ferrand with the House Wedge — craft-premium cognac against iceberg and bleu. Works; Ferrand's artisanal character is over-specified for the wedge.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Kansas City": "Pierre Ferrand with the KC — artisanal cognac against an 18oz strip. Strong; Ferrand handles the strip with craft-premium character, though Courvoisier's drier oak remains the featured cognac-KC pairing.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Loaded Potato": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the eau-de-vie body carries the loaded potato soup, and the dried-fruit note underlines the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Lobster Mac": "Pierre Ferrand with lobster mac — artisanal Grande Champagne against pasta-and-lobster richness. Strong; Ferrand's craft character frames the richness with unblended polish.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Market Fish": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- cognac's aged-grape weight buries delicate fish — digestif belongs after the meal. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Pierre Ferrand Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Mocha Creme": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the brandy depth wraps the mocha creme, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the dessert. Works; Ferrand's craft character handles the richness.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Mushroom Bisque": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the mushroom bisque, and the caramel balances the umami plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Mushrooms": "Pierre Ferrand with mushrooms — Grande Champagne 1er Cru against earthy umami. Strong; Ferrand's honey-floral threads the mushrooms at craft register.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Peanut Butter Brownie: peanut butter and cocoa meet craft Grande Champagne. Strong; Ferrand's artisanal character handles dessert density with more dimension than the value-brandy Christian Bros.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Porterhouse": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the eau-de-vie body carries the dual-cut steak, and the dried-fruit note underlines the cut. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Prime Tartare": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's cognac-orange-peel character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the barrel-aged depth cuts the umami fat. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Rainbow Trout": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- cognac's aged-grape weight buries delicate fish — digestif belongs after the meal. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a cognac. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Pierre Ferrand Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Roast Half Chicken": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the Limousin-oak weight handles the roast chicken, and the rancio register softens the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the chickpea soup, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- cognac's aged-grape weight buries delicate fish — digestif belongs after the meal. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Pierre Ferrand Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- cognac on a green vegetable side is wrong direction — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a cognac. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Pierre Ferrand Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Seafood Tower": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- cognac weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs sparkling or a crisp white. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Pierre Ferrand Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Seared Scallops": "Pierre Ferrand with seared scallops — Grande Champagne 1er Cru against scallop delicacy. Avoid; cognac overwhelms scallops.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Seasonal Soup": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the Limousin-oak weight handles the seasonal soup, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Seasonal Vegetables": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- cognac on a green vegetable side is wrong direction — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a cognac. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Pierre Ferrand Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Shrimp Bisque": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel leans against the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the brandy depth wraps the shrimp bisque, and the rancio register softens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Shrimp Cocktail": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- cognac is wrong-course energy for the table's opener. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Pierre Ferrand Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Swordfish": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Pierre Ferrand Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|The Tomahawk": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's cognac-orange-peel character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Tomato Basil": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the artisan-cognac depth wraps the tomato basil, and the orange-peel frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Truffle Fries": "Pierre Ferrand with truffle fries — Angeac-Champagne artisanal cognac against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Ferrand's traditional craft character meets the earthy-rich with unblended dimension Hennessy's eaux-de-vie blend doesn't bring.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- cognac's aged register pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a cognac. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Pierre Ferrand Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Pierre Ferrand Cognac|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the eau-de-vie body carries the curry, and the oak-and-vanilla edge brightens the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Asparagus": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the chalk-and-citrus body lifts the asparagus, and the fine bead frames the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity sits alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Beignets": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club 2015 — prestige grower Champagne with citrus, toasted brioche, and creamy texture. The vintage depth cuts beignets' fried-dough weight and the brioche complements pastry directly. Works as elegant Champagne for dessert — though younger NV vintages suit beignets more economically.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Bone Marrow": "Pierre Gimonnet 2015 grower Champagne against marrow's rendered-fat richness — even vintage depth is overwhelmed by bone intensity. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep the Special Club for tartare, seafood tower, or the filet course.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Bone-In Filet": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a sparkling. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut for another course.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Broccoli Cheddar": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Broccolini": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity sits beside the broccolini's tender green — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge frames the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Brussels and Belly": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity reads alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Burrata": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club — 2015 grower Champagne. Citrus zest, toasted brioche, white flowers, creamy texture. The vintage brioche and cream meet burrata's creamy-delicate center and the Côte des Blancs minerality plays against basil-tomato. Strong prestige Champagne pour for burrata — elegance meets cream.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Butternut Squash": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity sits beside the squash soup's silky sweet — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Carrot Cake": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Cheesecake": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut for the steak course.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Chilean Seabass": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut -- blanc de blancs, grand cru Chardonnay, fine bead. The chalk-and-citrus body lifts the seabass, and the fine bead frames the plate. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Chocolate Brownie": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Chocolate Cake": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a sparkling. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut for the steak course.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Clam Chowder": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity stays alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Cowboy Ribeye": "2015 Côte des Blancs prestige Champagne against the marbled bone-in Cowboy ribeye — vintage Chardonnay crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Pierre Gimonnet for starters, seafood tower, or the filet course.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Crab Cake": "2015 Côte des Blancs grower Champagne — prestige cuvée, layered citrus zest, toasted brioche, white flowers, creamy texture. The vintage depth cuts romesco and brioche complements crab's pan-fry. Peak grower Champagne for crab cake — both technical and indulgent in the right measure. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Creamed Spinach": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity sits beside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Creamy Potato": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity sits alongside the potato soup's silky body — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Creme Brulee": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a sparkling. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Escargot": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club 2015 — prestige grower Champagne from Côte des Blancs. Citrus zest, toasted brioche, white flowers, creamy texture. The brioche echoes escargot's buttered-bread tradition and the vintage depth frames the garlic-parsley. Peak Champagne for escargot — classical French pairing elevated. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Faroe Island Salmon": "Pierre Gimonnet 2015 Special Club — prestige grower Champagne with citrus, brioche, white flowers, creamy texture. The vintage depth handles salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong sophisticated Champagne across most seasonal salmon builds.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Filet Mignon": "2015 Côte des Blancs grower Champagne — layered Chardonnay with citrus zest, toasted brioche, white flowers, creamy refined texture. The vintage depth and body handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the brioche echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak prestige Champagne-with-steak pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|French Onion": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity stays alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cheese richness. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Grilled Caesar": "2015 Côte des Blancs Chardonnay Champagne — layered citrus zest, toasted brioche, and white flowers. The creamy texture meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the brioche plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong prestige pairing for grilled Caesar — vintage Champagne elevates the classic salad.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Gumbo": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the chalk-and-citrus body lifts the gumbo, and the fine bead frames the bowl. Works; the side call holds at neutral register.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity plays cleanly against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|House Wedge": "2015 Côte des Blancs prestige cuvée — layered citrus, toasted brioche, white flowers, creamy refined. The vintage body and brioche cut iceberg-and-bacon and the creamy texture complements blue cheese. Strong elegant grower Champagne for the wedge — prestige-level pairing on the classic.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Kansas City": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut for another course.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Loaded Potato": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Lobster Mac": "2015 Côte des Blancs prestige Champagne — layered citrus, brioche, white flowers, and creamy texture. The creamy texture aligns with lobster-mac's rich cream-cheese build and the brioche adds depth. Works as an unconventional indulgence pour for lobster mac — prestige meets richness side-wise.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Market Fish": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity holds the line with the fish's clean flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Mocha Creme": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut on the steak course instead.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Mushroom Bisque": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity reads alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Mushrooms": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut for the steak course.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Porterhouse": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves a structured red. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut for another course.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Prime Tartare": "Pierre Gimonnet 2015 Special Club — prestige grower Champagne, Côte des Blancs Chardonnay with layered citrus zest, toasted brioche, and creamy texture. The vintage weight meets raw prime beef's iron richness and the brioche plays against the seasoned tartare. Peak prestige Champagne for tartare. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Rainbow Trout": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity reads alongside the trout's delicate flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Roast Half Chicken": "Pierre Gimonnet 2015 — prestige grower Champagne from Côte des Blancs. Citrus, brioche, white flowers, creamy. The vintage depth handles roasted chicken; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works well across most seasonal chicken builds as a sophisticated choice.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut -- blanc de blancs, grand cru Chardonnay, fine bead. The chalk-and-citrus body lifts the halibut, and the fine bead frames the plate. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity plays cleanly against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Seafood Tower": "Côte des Blancs Chardonnay grower Champagne — prestige 2015 cuvée, layered with citrus zest, toasted brioche, white flowers, and creamy refined texture. The depth and complexity elevate the tower's mixed shellfish and the creamy texture plays against every element. Peak seafood tower Champagne. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Seared Scallops": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club — 2015 Côte des Blancs Chardonnay. Layered citrus zest, toasted brioche, white flowers, creamy texture. The vintage depth frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the creamy mouthfeel echoes the pan-butter. Peak prestige Champagne for scallops. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Seasonal Soup": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; capable alongside. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Seasonal Vegetables": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity stays alongside the seasonal garden plate — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Shrimp Bisque": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity reads alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Shrimp Cocktail": "2015 Côte des Blancs prestige grower Champagne — layered citrus zest, toasted brioche, white flowers, creamy texture. The vintage precision cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the brioche adds depth against cocktail-sauce. Strong elegant pairing — though lighter Prosecco or LP Le Cuvée are more typical.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Swordfish": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity finds register with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|The Tomahawk": "Pierre Gimonnet 2015 grower Champagne against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even vintage brioche and cream can't meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save the Special Club for the opener or chef's table.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Tomato Basil": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the chalk-and-citrus body lifts the tomato basil, and the fine bead frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Truffle Fries": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity sits alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut -- blanc de blancs, grand cru Chardonnay, fine bead. The chalk-and-citrus body lifts the tuna, and the fine bead frames the plate. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's char-citrus acidity holds with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Asparagus": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the high-rye spice register matches the asparagus, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the side. Works; craft-blend over-specified.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Pikesville Rye with au gratin potatoes — 110-proof Maryland-style against cheese-and-cream. Works; high proof over-specified.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Beignets": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the high-rye spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Bone Marrow": "Pikesville Rye with bone marrow — 110-proof bold Maryland-style rye against umami fat. The high proof and bold rye spice meet marrow with authority at higher impact than standard approachable ryes. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Bone-In Filet": "Pikesville Rye's cask-strength citrus-pepper character frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon depth carries the cut, and the citrus brightens the cream. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Broccoli Cheddar": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the rye backbone drives the broccoli cheddar, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Broccolini": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the rye backbone drives the broccolini, and the rye-pepper note underlines the side. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Brussels and Belly": "Pikesville Rye with Brussels + Belly — 110-proof Maryland-style rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; the high-proof boldness cuts the glaze with serious authority.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Burrata": "Pikesville Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against 110-proof bold rye. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Butternut Squash": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the dry rye body wraps the squash bisque, and the oak softens the cream. Works; distinctive character is worth mentioning tableside, even if the pairing isn't marquee.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Carrot Cake": "Pikesville Rye with carrot cake — 110-proof HH rye against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; bold rye spice finds cake spice at high-impact register.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Cheesecake": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the high-rye spice register matches the cheesecake, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Chilean Seabass": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pikesville Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Chocolate Brownie": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the dry rye body wraps the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th handles the richness at annual-release register.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Chocolate Cake": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the dry rye body wraps the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Clam Chowder": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the high-rye spice register matches the chowder, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; no driver pairing.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Cowboy Ribeye": "Pikesville Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 110-proof HH Maryland-style rye against cap fat. The high proof handles the ribeye with authority; the highest-impact rye in the approachable tier for this cut, alongside Knob Creek Rye (100-proof) and E.H. Taylor Rye (100-proof BIB). Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Crab Cake": "Pikesville Rye with crab cake — 110-proof bold rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Creamed Spinach": "Pikesville Rye with creamed spinach — 110-proof against dairy. Strong; bold rye sits with richness at high-impact register.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Creamy Potato": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rye spice cuts the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Creme Brulee": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye-and-oak weight handles the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the toast adds a warmth the dessert can accept.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Escargot": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the dry rye body wraps the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; covers both cuts cleanly; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Faroe Island Salmon": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pikesville Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Filet Mignon": "Pikesville Rye with the filet — 110-proof HH Maryland-style rye against the 10oz cut. The high proof gives the filet bold rye grip distinct from 90-proof benchmarks; for guests who want high-impact rye. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Pikesville Rye|French Onion": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the rye-and-oak weight handles the French onion, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; the 10-year depth gives the dessert substantial premium frame.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Grilled Caesar": "Pikesville Rye with the grilled Caesar — 110-proof bold rye against charred romaine and anchovy. The high proof earns the Caesar with Maryland-style authority. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Gumbo": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the rye spice cuts the gumbo, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Pikesville Rye|House Wedge": "Pikesville Rye with the House Wedge — 110-proof HH against iceberg and bleu. Strong; high-proof rye cuts the bleu with Maryland-style intensity.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Kansas City": "Pikesville Rye with the KC — Heaven Hill's 110-proof 6-year Maryland-style rye. The highest-proof rye in the approachable tier; bold rye spice with serious intensity. Distinct from HH's Elijah Craig Straight Rye (94 proof) by 16 extra proof points and Maryland-style character. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Loaded Potato": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the dry rye body wraps the loaded potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; curated single-barrel slightly over-specified.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Lobster Mac": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the dry rye body wraps the lobster mac, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; save the BTAC bottle for the protein course — the side doesn't deserve the pour.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Market Fish": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Pikesville Rye for the steak course.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Mocha Creme": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye spice cuts the mocha creme, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Mushroom Bisque": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the rye-and-oak weight handles the mushroom bisque, and the oak threads the umami plate. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Mushrooms": "Pikesville Rye with the mushrooms — 110-proof bold rye against earthy umami. Strong; high proof layers into mushroom depth with Maryland-style intensity.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye-and-oak weight handles the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; rye's brightness is a gentle counterpoint.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Porterhouse": "Pikesville Rye with the porterhouse — 110-proof across strip and filet. Strong; high proof leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Prime Tartare": "Pikesville Rye with the tartare — 110-proof HH rye against raw beef. The bold Maryland-style character is an intense counterpoint to tartare; more assertive than Sazerac Rye's benchmark. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Rainbow Trout": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pikesville Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the rye spice cuts the roast chicken, and the rye edge frames the plate. Works; the pour is a touch ambitious for the salad.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the rye-and-oak weight handles the chickpea soup, and the rye-grain bite cuts the bowl. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pikesville Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the garlic spinach, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; the complex profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Seafood Tower": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Pikesville Rye for the steak course.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Seared Scallops": "Pikesville Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 110-proof. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Seasonal Soup": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the rye spice cuts the seasonal soup, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the dry rye body wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; AE Triple Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "Pikesville Rye with shrimp bisque — 110-proof bold rye against cream-based seafood. Works; high proof over-specified for the soup.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "Pikesville Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against Maryland-style 110-proof. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Swordfish": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pikesville Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pikesville Rye|The Tomahawk": "Pikesville Rye's cask-strength citrus-pepper character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the citrus brightens the cream. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Tomato Basil": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the high-rye spice register matches the tomato basil, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; blend character covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Truffle Fries": "Pikesville Rye with truffle fries — 110-proof bold rye against truffle-parmesan. The high proof cuts through richness with Maryland-style intensity. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pikesville Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pikesville Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the curry, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; boutique character doesn't drive.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Asparagus": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Pineau des Charentes on the steak course instead.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Pineau des Charentes for the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Beignets": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac finish. The warm nutty Cognac clings to hot choux-fried dough and the honey lifts powdered-sugar. Peak Pineau for beignets — honey-on-sugar on the light airy bite. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Bone Marrow": "Never pair Pineau des Charentes with bone marrow — fortified honey drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and the warm nutty Cognac clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. Pineau belongs at aperitif or dessert.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Bone-In Filet": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- a sweet liqueur belongs after the meal, not during the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Pineau des Charentes for another course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Broccoli Cheddar": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Pineau des Charentes on the steak course instead.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Broccolini": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pineau des Charentes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Brussels and Belly": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- sweet liqueur and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no contrast left. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pineau des Charentes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Burrata": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The honey bridges fresh cream density and the white grape lifts tomato-basil brightness. Peak Pineau for burrata — aperitif-mode classic bridge on the caprese composition. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Butternut Squash": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Pineau des Charentes on the steak course instead.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Carrot Cake": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac finish. The warm nutty Cognac matches allspice-cinnamon spice and the honey plays with cream-cheese frosting. Strong Pineau for carrot cake — Tawny's toffee fits the spice-on-spice match harder.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Cheesecake": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac finish. The honey lifts cream-cheese density and the white grape plays with graham-cracker brown-sugar. Peak Pineau for cheesecake — fortified grape-and-honey match on the water-bath bake. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Chilean Seabass": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert-grade sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs a crisp white instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Pineau des Charentes on the steak course instead.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Chocolate Brownie": "Pineau des Charentes's sweet char-honey character sits with the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the dessert-wine sweetness threads the dessert. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Chocolate Cake": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character rests at register with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the syrupy liqueur weight wraps the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Clam Chowder": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Pineau des Charentes for the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Cowboy Ribeye": "Never pair Pineau des Charentes with Cowboy Ribeye — fortified sweet Cognac-blend gets lost against marbled-ribeye char and the honey muddles grilled-beef savory. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. Pineau is a before-meal aperitif or post-dessert pour.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Crab Cake": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The honey bridges romesco richness and the white grape lifts lump-crab delicacy. Works for crab cake — a Sancerre's mineral edge reads cleaner on the delicate shellfish.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Creamed Spinach": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pineau des Charentes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Creamy Potato": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Pineau des Charentes for the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Creme Brulee": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac finish, aperitif-or-dessert. The honey wraps the torched-sugar crust directly and the warm nutty Cognac plays with vanilla-custard. Peak Pineau for creme brulee — honey-on-caramel match. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Escargot": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The warm nutty Cognac plays with garlic-butter richness and the honey lifts parsley-herb. Strong Pineau for escargot — aperitif mode on the French classic, Cognac-region on Cognac-region.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Faroe Island Salmon": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Pineau des Charentes on the steak course instead.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Filet Mignon": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- the liqueur's sugar buries the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Pineau des Charentes for another course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|French Onion": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pineau des Charentes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Grilled Caesar": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The honey bridges anchovy-lemon dressing and the warm nutty Cognac plays with parm-char. Works for grilled caesar — a crisp white handles the salad cleaner.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Gumbo": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Pineau des Charentes for the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character doubles down on the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sweet liqueur and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no contrast left. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pineau des Charentes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|House Wedge": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The honey bridges blue-cheese funk and the warm nutty Cognac plays with bacon-fat char. Strong Pineau for wedge — aperitif-mode honey-on-funk match.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Kansas City": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pineau des Charentes lands on another course — not here.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Loaded Potato": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pineau des Charentes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Lobster Mac": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- the liqueur's sugar buries the side's cream instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Pineau des Charentes for the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Market Fish": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pineau des Charentes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Mocha Creme": "Pineau des Charentes's sweet char-honey character sits with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the dessert-wine sweetness threads the dessert. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Mushroom Bisque": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Pineau des Charentes on the steak course instead.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Mushrooms": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character fights the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- the liqueur's sweet register pulls against the mushroom's umami without bridge. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pineau des Charentes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Pineau des Charentes's sweet char-honey character holds the line with the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the dessert-wine sweetness threads the dessert, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Porterhouse": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pineau des Charentes lands on another course — not here.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Prime Tartare": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The warm nutty Cognac matches raw-beef richness and the honey plays with capers-and-shallot brightness. Strong Pineau for tartare — aperitif-mode classic French match.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Rainbow Trout": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Pineau des Charentes for the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Roast Half Chicken": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pineau des Charentes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pineau des Charentes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pineau des Charentes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Pineau des Charentes for the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Seafood Tower": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Pineau des Charentes on the steak course instead.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Seared Scallops": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The honey plays with browned-butter sweet sear and the white grape lifts delicate shellfish. Strong Pineau for seared scallops — aperitif mode on the delicate cut.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Seasonal Soup": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Pineau des Charentes for the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Seasonal Vegetables": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pineau des Charentes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Shrimp Bisque": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The honey plays with tomato-cream sweetness and the warm nutty Cognac bridges shellfish-coastal depth. Works for bisque — a Chardonnay's cream fits richer.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Shrimp Cocktail": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The warm nutty Cognac warms cold-shrimp coastal sweetness and the honey bridges cocktail-sauce horseradish. Peak Pineau for shrimp cocktail — aperitif bridge on the cold classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Swordfish": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Pineau des Charentes for the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|The Tomahawk": "Never pair Pineau des Charentes with Tomahawk — fortified aperitif-or-dessert style gets bulldozed by the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and honey residual-sugar clashes with smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. Pineau is a before-or-after pour.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Tomato Basil": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Pineau des Charentes for the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Truffle Fries": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Pineau des Charentes for the steak course.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pineau des Charentes lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pineau des Charentes|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Pineau des Charentes on the steak course instead.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Asparagus": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Beignets": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pio Cesare Barbaresco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Bone-In Filet": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's bold Nebbiolo-cherry cassis holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the firm tannin reads cleanly against the lean cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Broccoli Cheddar": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish flattens the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pio Cesare Barbaresco belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Broccolini": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish overshadows the broccolini's tender green stalks -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Burrata": "Approachable-Barolo-sibling Barbaresco against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Pio Cesare's firm-tannin tar weight overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Pio Cesare for aged beef where Barbaresco elegance earns the bottle.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Butternut Squash": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Carrot Cake": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Pio Cesare Barbaresco on the steak course instead.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Cheesecake": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Chilean Seabass": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pio Cesare Barbaresco belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Chocolate Brownie": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pio Cesare Barbaresco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Chocolate Cake": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Pio Cesare Barbaresco on the steak course instead.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Clam Chowder": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pio Cesare Barbaresco belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Crab Cake": "Approachable-Barolo-sibling Barbaresco against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Pio Cesare's firm-tannin tar structure buries the shellfish despite elegant profile. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Pio Cesare for aged beef, filet, or the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Creamy Potato": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pio Cesare Barbaresco belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Creme Brulee": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Escargot": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's bold Nebbiolo-cherry cassis finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Faroe Island Salmon": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Filet Mignon": "Piedmont Barbaresco DOCG Nebbiolo — Pio Cesare Barolo's approachable sibling, floral aromatics, dried cherry, tar, firm-but-elegant tannins, full body, high acid. The elegant firm tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the floral aromatics play against escargot-butter. Peak approachable-Barolo-sibling for filet — earlier-drinking Nebbiolo on the delicate cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|French Onion": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's bold Nebbiolo-cherry cassis sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Grilled Caesar": "Piedmont Barbaresco DOCG Nebbiolo — Pio Cesare Barolo's approachable sibling, floral aromatics, dried cherry, tar, firm-but-elegant tannins, high acid. The high acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the floral aromatics play against grilled-romaine char. Strong approachable-Barolo-sibling for grilled Caesar — Barbaresco elegance on the layered salad.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Gumbo": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish overwhelms the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish crowds the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|House Wedge": "Piedmont Barbaresco DOCG Nebbiolo — Pio Cesare Barolo's approachable sibling, floral aromatics, dried cherry, tar, firm-but-elegant tannins, high acid. The high acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dried cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as approachable-Barolo-sibling for the wedge — Barbaresco elegance reframes the classic salad.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Kansas City": "* Pio Cesare Barbaresco's powerful blackcurrant body meets head-on the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the dark-fruit body sits with the strip steak, and the cassis frames the cut. Peak California-Zin for the strip — bold Napa on the American cut. Gold standard; a confident headline pour.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Loaded Potato": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's bold Nebbiolo-cherry cassis holds with the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Lobster Mac": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's bold Nebbiolo-cherry cassis plays cleanly against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Market Fish": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Mocha Creme": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pio Cesare Barbaresco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Mushroom Bisque": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's bold Nebbiolo-cherry cassis finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the bold red's cassis grip carries the mushroom-and-truffle depth — structured weight on the earthy side. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Pio Cesare Barbaresco on the steak course instead.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Prime Tartare": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's bold Nebbiolo-cherry cassis sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Rainbow Trout": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Roast Half Chicken": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's bold Nebbiolo-cherry cassis finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish overwhelms the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pio Cesare Barbaresco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pio Cesare Barbaresco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Seafood Tower": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Seared Scallops": "Approachable-Barolo-sibling Barbaresco against scallop sweetness — Pio Cesare's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Pio Cesare for aged beef where Barbaresco elegance earns the bottle.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Seasonal Soup": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pio Cesare Barbaresco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Seasonal Vegetables": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Shrimp Bisque": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's bold Nebbiolo-cherry cassis stays alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Shrimp Cocktail": "Approachable-Barolo-sibling Barbaresco against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — firm-tannin Pio Cesare and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Pio Cesare for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Swordfish": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Pio Cesare Barbaresco on the steak course instead.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|The Tomahawk": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's bold Nebbiolo-cherry cassis holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the concentrated tannin grips the marbled cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Tomato Basil": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish overwhelms the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit anchors the mushroom's umami weight; firm tannin and oak hold their register. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Pio Cesare Barbaresco on the steak course instead.",
  "Pio Cesare Barbaresco|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Asparagus": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals plays cleanly against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals sits alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Beignets": "Plymouth with beignets — softer Plymouth-style against fried dough. Works; Plymouth's sweet notes make the juniper-sugar pairing more harmonious than Bombay's angular version.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Bone Marrow": "Plymouth with bone marrow — softer English gin against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Plymouth Gin for another course.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Broccolini": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals leans against the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals reads alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Burrata": "Burrata: fresh dairy meets Plymouth's protected-origin style, softer and earthier than London Dry proper. Plymouth's sweet notes and softer juniper meet burrata's cream where Tanq would cut it and Bombay would contrast it — textural harmony, the gin answer for burrata. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Butternut Squash": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals finds neutral with the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Carrot Cake": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Plymouth Gin for the steak course.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Cheesecake": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Plymouth Gin for the steak course.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals matches the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Plymouth Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Plymouth Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Clam Chowder": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals stays alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Plymouth Gin for another course.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Crab Cake": "Plymouth with crab cake — softer English gin against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Plymouth's earthier juniper is gentle enough to frame crab without overpowering — Tanq lifts harder, Plymouth complements.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Creamy Potato": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals plays cleanly against the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Creme Brulee": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Plymouth Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Escargot": "Plymouth with escargot — softer English gin against garlic-butter snails. Strong; Plymouth's softness meets the butter, though Tanq's proof cuts it harder and Bombay Dry's juniper contrasts more sharply.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Plymouth with the salmon — single-distillery protected gin against oily salmon. Strong; Plymouth's earthier juniper frames the fish softly.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Filet Mignon": "Plymouth with the filet — softer Plymouth-style against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified; Plymouth's softness is the gentlest case.",
  "Plymouth Gin|French Onion": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals stays alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Plymouth with the grilled Caesar — softer English gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Plymouth softens the char where the London Drys cut it.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Gumbo": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals sits beside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Plymouth Gin|House Wedge": "Plymouth with the House Wedge — softer Plymouth-style against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Plymouth's sweet notes match the bleu sharpness with textural harmony the drier London Drys miss.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Kansas City": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Plymouth Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Loaded Potato": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals plays cleanly against the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Lobster Mac": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Market Fish": "Plymouth with the market fish — softer English gin against featured fish. Works; Plymouth's earthier juniper handles fish at gentler register.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Mocha Creme": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Plymouth Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Mushrooms": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Plymouth Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Porterhouse": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Plymouth Gin for another course.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Prime Tartare": "Plymouth with the tartare — Plymouth-style gin against raw beef. Strong; Plymouth's softness supports tartare, though Bombay Dry's juniper-assertiveness is the distinctive gin-tartare match.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Plymouth Gin's coriander-and-juniper register sits alongside the trout's delicate flesh: the floral edge frames the trout, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Neptunia's briny character engages the bleu with distinctive maritime profile.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Plymouth with the chicken — protected single-distillery against roasted poultry. Works; Plymouth's softness supports chicken cleanly.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals sits with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals stays alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Seafood Tower": "Plymouth with the seafood tower — protected single-distillery gin against the raw bar. Excellent; Plymouth's softer character makes it a polished tower pairing when the occasion calls for less aggression than Tanq.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Seared Scallops": "Plymouth with seared scallops — softer English gin against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Plymouth's earthier juniper and sweet notes mirror scallop richness — less aggressive than Tanq, more textural than Beefeater.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals leans against the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Plymouth with shrimp bisque — softer Plymouth-style against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Plymouth rides the bisque at softer register than Tanq cuts it.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Plymouth with shrimp cocktail — single-distillery protected gin against cold shrimp. Excellent; Plymouth's softness elevates the classic pairing without the angular lift of Bombay Dry.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Swordfish": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals plays cleanly against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Plymouth Gin|The Tomahawk": "Plymouth with the Tomahawk — softer gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Tomato Basil": "Plymouth Gin's herbal-botanical edge sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the floral edge frames the tomato basil, and the juniper threads the bowl. Works; gin over-specified; Sapphire's softness is a functional case.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Truffle Fries": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals sits alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals plays cleanly against the tuna's seared rare crust — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Plymouth Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Plymouth Gin's Plymouth-style-rounder botanicals reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Asparagus": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Aldo Conterno Bussia Monforte d'Alba, rose petal, tar, dried cherry, orange peel, iron minerality, firm tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts gruyère-cream weight and the tar plays against the lactic richness. Works as serious-Barolo for au gratin — Aldo Conterno prestige on the cheesy side.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Beignets": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo on the steak course instead.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Bone Marrow": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Aldo Conterno Bussia Monforte d'Alba, rose petal, tar, dried cherry, orange peel, iron minerality, full body, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the iron minerality plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak serious-Barolo for marrow — iron-on-iron mineral match. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Bone-In Filet": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's powerful blackcurrant body reads register-aligned with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the dark-fruit body sits with the bone-enhanced filet, and the iron-mineral plays against the cut. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Broccoli Cheddar": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Broccolini": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo on the steak course instead.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Burrata": "Serious-Barolo Aldo Conterno against burrata's creamy-delicate center — decade-built firm tannin and tar weight overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Aldo Conterno for the grand-cut steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Butternut Squash": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Carrot Cake": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Cheesecake": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Chilean Seabass": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo on the steak course instead.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Chocolate Brownie": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Chocolate Cake": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Clam Chowder": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Cowboy Ribeye": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Aldo Conterno Bussia Monforte d'Alba, rose petal, tar, dried cherry, orange peel, iron minerality, full body, firm tannin. The full body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the tar plays against beefy char. Strong serious-Barolo for ribeye — Aldo Conterno prestige on the bold cut.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Crab Cake": "Serious-Barolo Aldo Conterno against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — decade-built firm tannin and iron-minerality structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Aldo Conterno for aged beef — one of the most serious bottles deserves it.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Creamy Potato": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo on the steak course instead.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Creme Brulee": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo on the steak course instead.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Escargot": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's bold cherry-citrus cassis finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Faroe Island Salmon": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Filet Mignon": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Aldo Conterno Bussia Monforte d'Alba, rose petal, tar, dried cherry, orange peel, iron minerality, full body, firm tannin, high acid, age-worthy. The firm tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the orange peel plays against escargot-butter. Peak serious-Barolo for filet — one of Barolo's greatest names on the delicate cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|French Onion": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's bold cherry-citrus cassis leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the orange brightens the caramel. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. Tannin and char, umami and dark fruit — the bold red and the Caesar punch at the same level. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Gumbo": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip crowds the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|House Wedge": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Aldo Conterno Bussia Monforte d'Alba, rose petal, tar, dried cherry, orange peel, iron minerality, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the orange peel plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as serious-Barolo for the wedge — this bottle deserves aged beef.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Kansas City": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Aldo Conterno Bussia Monforte d'Alba, rose petal, tar, dried cherry, orange peel, iron minerality, full body, firm tannin. The firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the iron minerality plays against beefy char. Peak serious-Barolo for the strip — Aldo Conterno prestige on the American cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Loaded Potato": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's bold cherry-citrus cassis sits beside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Lobster Mac": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's bold cherry-citrus cassis sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Market Fish": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Mocha Creme": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo on the steak course instead.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Mushroom Bisque": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's bold cherry-citrus cassis stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: the structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit anchors the mushroom's umami weight; firm tannin and oak hold their register. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Prime Tartare": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's bold cherry-citrus cassis holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Rainbow Trout": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Roast Half Chicken": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's bold cherry-citrus cassis holds with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overshadows the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Seafood Tower": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Seared Scallops": "Serious-Barolo Aldo Conterno against scallop sweetness — decade-built firm tannin and tar structure buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Aldo Conterno for Tomahawk, Porterhouse, or Bone-In Filet where prestige earns the bottle.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Seasonal Soup": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Seasonal Vegetables": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Shrimp Bisque": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's bold cherry-citrus cassis sits alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Shrimp Cocktail": "Serious-Barolo Aldo Conterno against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — decade-built firm-tannin prestige and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Aldo Conterno for aged beef.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Swordfish": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo on the steak course instead.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Tomato Basil": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the bold red's cassis grip carries the mushroom-and-truffle depth — structured weight on the earthy side. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo for the steak course.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Asparagus": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge frames the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Beignets": "Pommery 2004 Cuvée Louise — 20-year aged Champagne. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich. The aged brioche complements beignets' fried-dough pastry directly and honey plays against powdered sugar. Works as occasion dessert Champagne — though this bottle deserves a more savory course.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Bone Marrow": "Pommery 2004 Cuvée Louise against marrow's rendered-fat richness — aged complexity is overwhelmed by bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep the Cuvée Louise for tartare, scallops, seafood tower, or filet.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Bone-In Filet": "Pommery Cuvée Louise 2004 — 20-year Reims aged Champagne. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich. The aged weight handles bone-enhanced filet and the honey-brioche complements bone depth. Strong occasion Champagne for bone-in filet — rare special-moment pairing worth the pour.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Broccoli Cheddar": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the fine bubbles lift the broccoli cheddar, and the brioche edge frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Broccolini": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity plays cleanly against the broccolini's tender green — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge frames the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Brussels and Belly": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity plays cleanly against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge frames the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Burrata": "2004 Pommery Cuvée Louise — 20-year Reims vintage. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich. The aged complexity cuts burrata's cream and the almond-honey plays against basil-tomato-pistachio. Strong occasion Champagne for burrata — elevated pairing when the meal is a celebration.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Butternut Squash": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the fine bubbles lift the squash bisque, and the brioche edge frames the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Carrot Cake": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Pommery Cuvée Louise for the steak course.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Cheesecake": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a sparkling. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Pommery Cuvée Louise for the steak course.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Chilean Seabass": "Pommery Cuvée Louise: prestige champagne. Excellent on Chilean seabass; tête-de-cuvée depth meets buttery white-fish at prestige register. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Chocolate Brownie": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pommery Cuvée Louise belongs on the steak course.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Chocolate Cake": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Pommery Cuvée Louise for the steak course.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Clam Chowder": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the fine bubbles lift the chowder, and the brioche edge frames the bowl. Works; the opener sits without competing.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Cowboy Ribeye": "2004 Pommery Cuvée Louise against the marbled bone-in Cowboy ribeye — aged Champagne crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep the Pommery for starters, seafood tower, or the filet course where it shines.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Crab Cake": "2004 Pommery Cuvée Louise — aged 20-year Champagne from Reims. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich palate. The aged depth cuts romesco and brioche complements crab's pan-fry; almond plays against sweet meat. Peak occasion Champagne for crab cake — rare indulgence. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Creamed Spinach": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Creamy Potato": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity sits alongside the potato soup's silky body — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Creme Brulee": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- sparkling acid cuts through custard wrong — leaves the dessert reading sharp. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Pommery Cuvée Louise for the steak course.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Escargot": "2004 Pommery Cuvée Louise — aged 20-year Reims Champagne. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich. The aged complexity and brioche echo escargot's buttered-bread French tradition directly; almond adds depth. Peak occasion Champagne for escargot — treat the pairing accordingly. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Faroe Island Salmon": "2004 Pommery Cuvée Louise — 20-year vintage from Reims. Almond, apricot, honey, brioche. The aged complexity handles salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong occasion Champagne for salmon across most seasonal preparations.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Filet Mignon": "Pommery 2004 Cuvée Louise — 20-year-old Reims Champagne. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich. The aged complexity and body handle butter-tender filet's lean sweetness; honey-brioche complements escargot-butter toppers directly. Peak occasion Champagne-with-steak — treat it accordingly. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|French Onion": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity holds with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cheese richness. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Grilled Caesar": "2004 Pommery Cuvée Louise — aged Champagne from Reims. Almond, apricot, honey, brioche. The aged depth meets anchovy-parmesan and the brioche plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as occasion Champagne for Caesar — though this vintage belongs with more substantial courses.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Gumbo": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity plays cleanly against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|House Wedge": "Pommery Cuvée Louise 2004 — 20-year aged Champagne. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich. The aged depth cuts iceberg-and-bacon and honey plays against blue cheese. Strong occasion pairing for wedge — treat the wine accordingly when opening for special moments.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Kansas City": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pommery Cuvée Louise belongs on another course.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Loaded Potato": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity sits beside the loaded potato's richness — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Lobster Mac": "2004 Pommery Cuvée Louise — aged 20-year Champagne. Almond, apricot, honey, brioche-rich. The aged complexity and brioche align with lobster-mac's rich cream-cheese weight unconventionally. Works as indulgence-on-indulgence when the occasion calls for it — though younger Champagne suits the side more economically.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Market Fish": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity sits with the fish's clean flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Mocha Creme": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Pommery Cuvée Louise for the steak course.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Mushroom Bisque": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity stays alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Mushrooms": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity sits alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- brut bubbles and chocolate weight refuse each other — needs sweetness, not crisp acid. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Pommery Cuvée Louise for the steak course.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Porterhouse": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves a structured red. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Pommery Cuvée Louise for another course.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Prime Tartare": "Pommery Cuvée Louise 2004 — 20-year Reims Champagne with toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, and brioche-rich palate. The aged weight meets raw prime beef's iron richness and the honey-almond complexity plays against the seasoned tartare. Peak occasion Champagne for tartare. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Rainbow Trout": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity plays cleanly against the trout's delicate flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Roast Half Chicken": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity sits beside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the mousse-and-citrus lifts the herb-crisped bird. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the fine bubbles lift the chickpea soup, and the brioche edge frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Pommery Cuvée Louise -- prestige Champagne, brioche, fine mousse. The fine bubbles lift the halibut, and the brioche edge frames the plate. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity sits beside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Seafood Tower": "Pommery 2004 Cuvée Louise — 20-year-old Champagne from Reims with toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich palate, and fine persistent bubbles. The extraordinary complexity elevates the tower's mixed shellfish and the honey-almond profile plays against every element. Peak occasion Champagne for the tower. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Seared Scallops": "2004 Pommery Cuvée Louise — 20-year vintage Champagne. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich palate. The aged complexity frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the almond-honey echoes the pan-butter. Peak occasion Champagne for scallops — vintage-on-vintage moment, treat it accordingly. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Seasonal Soup": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity holds with the seasonal soup's house build — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Seasonal Vegetables": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity sits beside the seasonal garden plate — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Shrimp Bisque": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity plays cleanly against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Shrimp Cocktail": "Pommery 2004 Cuvée Louise — 20-year aged Champagne. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich palate. The aged complexity cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the honey-almond depth plays against cocktail-sauce. Strong occasion pairing — though younger Champagnes are more typical for shrimp cocktail.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Swordfish": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity finds register with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|The Tomahawk": "Pommery 2004 Cuvée Louise against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even 20-year vintage complexity can't meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Pommery Louise for the opener or seafood tower course.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Tomato Basil": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity sits beside the tomato basil's bright acid — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the brioche notes meet the herb butter. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Truffle Fries": "* Pommery Cuvée Louise -- prestige Champagne, brioche, fine mousse. The bright effervescence carries the truffle fries, and the bright acidity cuts the side. Peak seafood tower Champagne. Gold standard; a confident headline pour.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna: sesame-crusted rare tuna meets Pommery Cuvée Louise. Excellent; prestige cuvée's mineral-lean character frames the rare-fish-and-sesame at tête-de-cuvée register. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Pommery Cuvée Louise|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's mineral-prestige acidity sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Porterhouse|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with the porterhouse — Barton 1792 across strip and filet. Strong; 93.7-proof balance covers both cuts with spicy-sweet high-rye character.",
  "Porterhouse|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat with the porterhouse — sweet-wheat across strip and filet. Strong; distinctive wheat character handles both cuts.",
  "Porterhouse|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 — Napa Cab, dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, smooth tannin. The approachable body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the cherry-currant plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong entry Napa Cab for Porterhouse — reliable step-up pour on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the 2 James Gin on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the porterhouse — rare craft release across strip and filet. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Porterhouse|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with the porterhouse — 16yr double-matured Speyside against strip-and-filet. Works; Aberlour 16's balance frames the two-cut plate at approachable-premium register.",
  "Porterhouse|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the oak-aged tequila body threads the dual-cut steak, and the oak threads the cut. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Porterhouse|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's añejo-pour depth matches register with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the rested-agave depth sits with the dual-cut steak, and the caramel-agave register brightens the cut. Strong; cristalino depth cuts the garlic-butter with craft-theatrical distinctiveness.",
  "Porterhouse|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- white wine can't stand up to the marbled char — the cut earns a structured red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Alexander Valley Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Porterhouse: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Porterhouse|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Sonoma Bordeaux blend — plum, blackberry, mocha, soft tannin, smooth, medium body. The soft tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet though the medium body reads leaner on the cut than full-bodied Cabs. Works as crowd-pleaser Bordeaux blend for Porterhouse — accessible Sonoma on the dual-cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Amaro Nonino for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Ancho Reyes on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight rests at register with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the brown-spirit warmth threads the dual-cut steak, and the vanilla underlines the rendered char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the bourbon depth settles into the dual-cut steak, and the vanilla balances the char-and-fat. Strong; the 10-year polish covers both cuts with small-batch refinement.",
  "Porterhouse|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the bourbon depth settles into the dual-cut steak, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; composed premium match.",
  "Porterhouse|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Aperol for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Asparagus": "Porterhouse with the asparagus -- bright-acid breaks the weight, the plate finds its lift. Works; the side carries cleanly with the cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Porterhouse with the au gratin -- the strip-and-tender frames the gruyere-baked potato. Excellent; the side that completes the steak plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Porterhouse: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Averna Amaro for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Aviation": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- a light citrus cocktail can't carry the marbled char — needs whiskey or red wine weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Aviation for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Aviation Gin on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Avion 44": "Avion 44 with the porterhouse — 44-month aged across strip and filet. Works; extended-aged Avion over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's green-agave-and-cane lift leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the silver-spirit register lifts the dual-cut steak, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light spirit. Avoid; one of those is the move. Bacardi Rum lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Baileys Irish Cream on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Baker's": "Baker's with the porterhouse — 107-proof high-rye across strip and filet. Strong; bold proof leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "Porterhouse|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with the porterhouse — vanilla-forward Speyside against strip-and-filet. Strong; Balvenie's American Oak richness frames the two-cut plate with more oak depth than the benchmark siblings.",
  "Porterhouse|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with the porterhouse — 14yr rum-cask-finished Speyside against strip-and-filet. Works; Caribbean Cask frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Porterhouse|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with the porterhouse — single-barrel Speyside against strip-and-filet. Strong; Balvenie 16's depth frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Porterhouse|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood with the porterhouse — 21yr port-cask Speyside against strip-and-filet. Strong; Portwood's port-richness frames the two-cut plate at ultra-premium register.",
  "Porterhouse|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the bourbon depth settles into the dual-cut steak, and the vanilla brightens the grilled char-edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with the porterhouse — multi-distillery Discovery across strip and filet. Works; blend character covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Porterhouse|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with the porterhouse — stout-barrel-finished across strip and filet. Works; KBS distinctiveness is slightly over-specified for the two-cut plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare with the porterhouse — Foursquare rum-cask Bardstown against strip-and-filet. Works; Bardstown Foursquare frames the two-cut plate at Caribbean-finish register.",
  "Porterhouse|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with the porterhouse — Fusion blend across strip and filet. Works; own-plus-sourced covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Porterhouse|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with the porterhouse — retailer-pick Bardstown across strip and filet. Works; curated single-barrel covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Porterhouse|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with the porterhouse — the filet side takes the light high-rye beautifully; the strip side is a touch underpowered. Lighter than the Wellers here; a distinct alternate for the porterhouse but not the strongest call. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year with the porterhouse — aged high-rye handles both strip and filet sides more evenly than standard Basil Hayden's. Strong; a refined Basil Hayden's call for this plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with the porterhouse — malted bready character across both cuts. The grain notes give both sides something distinctive; strong and specifically character-forward. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Porterhouse|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the oak depth lays under the dual-cut steak, and the vanilla underlines the rendered char. Strong; EHT Full Proof meets the earthy-rich at historic Kentucky register.",
  "Porterhouse|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast with the porterhouse — toasted caramel across the strip-and-filet split. Works better than standard Basil Hayden's on this plate; the toast character gives both cuts something to meet.",
  "Porterhouse|Bee's Knees": "Bee's Knees' citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- a light citrus cocktail can't carry the marbled char — needs whiskey or red wine weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Bee's Knees for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Beefeater Gin for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Beignets": "After the Porterhouse, the beignets -- the dual strip-and-tender yields to warm choux and powdered sugar, the dipping sauce closing light. Works; the table-share steak hands off to a measured close.",
  "Porterhouse|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Belvedere Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with the porterhouse — heritage blend across strip and filet. Strong; the blend covers both cuts cleanly at the everyday premium register.",
  "Porterhouse|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with the porterhouse — 103-proof across strip and filet. Strong; the extra proof leans toward strip side — Blanton's Original is the more balanced porterhouse call.",
  "Porterhouse|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with the porterhouse — single-barrel across strip and filet. Strong; Blanton's handles both sides cleanly, though Blanton's Gold brings more grip for the strip.",
  "Porterhouse|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's SfB with the porterhouse — export cask-strength Blanton's against strip-and-filet. Strong; Blanton's SfB frames the two-cut plate with distinctive Blanton's character at barrel-proof register.",
  "Porterhouse|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with the porterhouse — decennial Pact 10 across strip and filet. Works; milestone-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Porterhouse|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with the porterhouse — Pact 8 blend across strip and filet. Works; annual-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Porterhouse|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the porterhouse — Pact 9 across strip and filet. Works; annual-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Porterhouse|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Bluecoat Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Porterhouse: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Porterhouse|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Bombay Dry Gin on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Bombay Sapphire Gin for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Bone Marrow": "The porterhouse preceded by the bone marrow -- protein on protein, the table holds register without softening. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding the cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Booker's": "Booker's with the porterhouse — Beam flagship cask-strength against strip-and-filet. Strong; Booker's uncut intensity frames the two-cut plate at barrel-proof register.",
  "Porterhouse|Botanist Gin": "Botanist Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Botanist Gin for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Bowdie's Old Fashioned: this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth meets this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char. Oak, caramel, and spirit heat stand up to the beef's render; the caramelized sweetness mirrors the crust. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with the porterhouse — house-pick depth across strip and filet sides. The stave selection gives both cuts something to work with; more appropriate than standard Maker's for this plate. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the porterhouse — Virginia 90-proof small-batch across strip and filet. Strong; smooth character covers both cuts cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Bowmore 12": "Porterhouse: the cut's split of strip and filet meets Bowmore 12's sherry-touched gentle peat. The filet side actually improves with Bowmore (where Lagavulin and Laphroaig overpower); the strip side handles the gentler profile cleanly. The most versatile peated Islay for this two-cut plate. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin sits with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Porterhouse: the Cab's firm tannin cuts the strip's char and the dark fruit lifts the savory edge — steakhouse symmetry. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Broccoli Cheddar": "Porterhouse with the broccoli cheddar alongside -- cream and protein share the meal, the build reads full. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Broccolini": "The porterhouse into the broccolini -- the bright register cleaves the heavy course, the table breathes. Works; safe alongside, but not the headline pick.",
  "Porterhouse|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich's crisp char-citrus character lifts the porterhouse's two-cut character — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Brussels and Belly": "Porterhouse with Brussels + Belly -- the 40oz dual strip-and-filet meets the maple-pork-belly glaze, the strip side carrying the savory and the filet side meeting the sweet. Strong; the dual cut's range matches the side's range.",
  "Porterhouse|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with the porterhouse — flagship versatility across both strip and filet sides. BT handles the plate cleanly; a middle-ground pour between the Wellers and Eagle Rare 10 Year. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Buffalo Trace Cream for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with the porterhouse — high-rye spice across strip and filet sides. Strong; both cuts take the rye-forward character cleanly, though higher-proof high-ryes (Knob Creek) bring more grip.",
  "Porterhouse|Burrata": "The porterhouse after the burrata -- the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy sets up the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: restrained yields to weighty, the meal arcs from light to full. Works; the starter composes before the cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Butternut Squash": "The porterhouse after the butternut squash -- the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream sets the table for the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: savory-into-sweet, the classic close lands intact. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Porterhouse: the strip's marbled-and-char richness finds the bold red's grip; cassis carries the savory weight cleanly. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the barrel-rested body handles the dual-cut steak, and the oak threads the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Campari": "Campari's aperitivo at its most textbook underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Campari for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the smooth Canadian register carries the dual-cut steak, and the soft profile rounds the cut. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Porterhouse|Canadian Club 43 Year": "Canadian Club 43 Year's Canadian whisky character reads alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the Canadian whisky weight matches the dual-cut steak, and the soft profile rounds the cut. Works; Portwood is over-specified for a side — save for main course.",
  "Porterhouse|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Captain Morgan Rum belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the dual-cut steak, and the gentle rye thread underlines the cut. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Porterhouse|Carrot Cake": "The Porterhouse into the carrot cake -- the dual strip-and-tender hands off to spiced cake and cream-cheese frosting, walnut crunch on the close. Works; the table-share build and the spice cake share the meal across courses.",
  "Porterhouse|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- dessert wine is post-meal — wrong register for the steak course. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Casa LBV Port for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's wood-fire mezcal body rests at register with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the espadín-and-smoke body wraps the dual-cut steak, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Porterhouse|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Caymus — Napa Cab, concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin. The opulent body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the cocoa plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong iconic Napa Cab for Porterhouse — crowd-pleasing lushness on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Chambord for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Cheesecake": "The Porterhouse closes with the cheesecake -- the dual-character cut hands off to dense dairy custard, graham crust and berry compote framing the close. Works; the meal closes substantial, the dual cuts carrying the dairy weight cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Chocolate Brownie": "The Porterhouse closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the dual-character cut yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla scooped over. Strong; both cuts and the warm brownie share the close cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Chocolate Cake": "After the Porterhouse, the chocolate cake -- the dual strip-and-tender hands off to layered cocoa-and-ganache, vanilla ice cream framing the close. Strong; the table-share steak meets the canonical chocolate dessert at full register.",
  "Porterhouse|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Chopin Vodka for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Bros Brandy's rancio-edged cognac weight reads alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the brandy depth wraps the dual-cut steak, and the caramel rounds the cut. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Porterhouse|Château Batailley": "Pauillac 5ème Cru Classé Bordeaux — blackcurrant, tobacco, cedar, graphite, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the graphite plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong classified Bordeaux for Porterhouse — Pauillac structure on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Château Beaucastel": "Porterhouse: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Strong tier. Two cuts, two reads — the Pomerol's velvety Merlot-Cab Franc handles the filet side beautifully, and its truffle-plum core absorbs the strip's char. Biodynamic Bordeaux softer than a Napa Cab, so it flexes across the porterhouse without dominating either half.",
  "Porterhouse|Château Haut Segottes": "Saint-Émilion right-bank Bordeaux — plum, dark cherry, truffle, clayey earth, soft tannin. The soft tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the truffle plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong right-bank Bordeaux for Porterhouse — approachable French on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Château de Rouillac": "Pessac-Léognan left-bank Bordeaux — blackcurrant, cedar, graphite, tobacco, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the graphite plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong left-bank Bordeaux for Porterhouse — French structure on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Clam Chowder": "Porterhouse with the chowder alongside -- cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the oak-aged tequila body threads the dual-cut steak, and the caramel-agave register brightens the cut. Strong; Clase Azul's refinement is slightly underserved by the cut's scale, but the ceremony is still the draw.",
  "Porterhouse|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango with the porterhouse — Durango across strip and filet. Works; ceremonial pour over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold with the porterhouse — ceremonial blend across strip and filet. Works; golden ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal's bold agave-fruit-smoke character holds steady against the porterhouse's two-cut character — the agave smoke meets the cap-fat. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata's silky Jalisco blanco with ceramic-decanter presentation finds neutral with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the silver-spirit register lifts the dual-cut steak, and the green-agave edge brightens the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Porterhouse|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado -- ceramic-bottle reposado, caramel, agave. The ceramic-icon reposado depth wraps the dual-cut steak, and the agave-and-caramel rounds the cut. Strong; the blend's complexity plus ceremonial presentation elevate the dessert.",
  "Porterhouse|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra with the porterhouse — top-tier Clase Azul across strip and filet. Works; 5-year Ultra over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep with the porterhouse — Beam wheated across strip and filet. Strong; handles both cuts cleanly at approachable wheated register.",
  "Porterhouse|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the Rhone-blend body wraps the dual-cut steak, and the peppery edge frames the cut. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Porterhouse|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa's reposado aged in uncharred Napa Cabernet wine barrels sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the barrel-rested body handles the dual-cut steak, and the oak threads the cut. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Porterhouse|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's vermouth-wine-base sweetness holds with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the bitter herb edge cuts the cap-fat richness. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Porterhouse|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body reads alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the blanco-tequila body matches the dual-cut steak, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado's agave-char character holds steady against the porterhouse's two-cut character — the aged-tequila depth grips the marbled cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's rested-agave body finds neutral with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the dual-cut steak, and the caramel-agave register brightens the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Porterhouse|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg's rested-agave body leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the barrel-rested body handles the dual-cut steak, and the soft-spice note softens the cut. Works; 5-year Ultra over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the añejo register carries the dual-cut steak, and the soft-spice note softens the cut. Works; maturation depth composed with this dish.",
  "Porterhouse|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Porterhouse: the Cab's firm tannin cuts the strip's char and the dark fruit lifts the savory edge — steakhouse symmetry. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Porterhouse|Corpse Reviver": "Corpse Reviver's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Corpse Reviver lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the brandy depth wraps the dual-cut steak, and the caramel rounds the cut. Works; Courvoisier's backbone handles the richness.",
  "Porterhouse|Crab Cake": "The porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust: the dual strip-and-filet overshadows the delicate crab cake, and the bold beefy character crowds out the sweet shellfish plate. Avoid; the courses can't share a meal cleanly. Pair the crab cake with Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin on a different visit; pour Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel alongside the porterhouse.",
  "Porterhouse|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 with the porterhouse — layered Speyside against strip-and-filet. Strong; Cragganmore's complexity frames the two-cut plate with Classic Malt character.",
  "Porterhouse|Creamed Spinach": "* Porterhouse alongside the creamed spinach -- the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character carries cleanly into the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens. Gold standard; the unskippable match for this combo.",
  "Porterhouse|Creamy Potato": "The porterhouse after the potato soup -- the potato soup's silky-bodied cream sets the table for the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: cream rounds the protein, the plate composes thick. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Creme Brulee": "After the Porterhouse, the brulee -- the dual strip-and-tender yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the table-share steak meets the steakhouse classic close.",
  "Porterhouse|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée's cherry-earth tannin matches the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Porterhouse|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness reads alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the easygoing Canadian character handles the dual-cut steak, and the easy Canadian register softens the cut. Works; distinctive character gives the dessert a unique story-frame.",
  "Porterhouse|Cucumber Gimlet": "Cucumber Gimlet's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Cucumber Gimlet belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12 with the porterhouse — sherry-finish Highland luxury against strip-and-filet. Strong; Dalmore's Matusalem-cask depth frames the two-cut plate at Highland-premium register.",
  "Porterhouse|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin sits with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Mount Veeder Napa Cab Franc (rare) — Darioush elegant aromatic, violet, dark cherry, dried rose, savory herb, medium tannin. The medium tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the violet plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong rare-Napa CF for Porterhouse — Darioush aromatic elegance on the dual-cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Darioush — Silverado Trail Cab, blackcurrant, dark plum, mocha, spice, rich plush. The opulent body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the mocha-spice plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong opulent Napa Cab for Porterhouse — Persian-inspired estate story on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin's Detroit urban-craft gin falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Detroit City Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City Vodka's hometown craft vodka underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Detroit City Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15 with the porterhouse — aged Highland blend against strip-and-filet. Strong; Dewar's 15 frames the two-cut plate at premium-blend register.",
  "Porterhouse|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label with the porterhouse — honey-vanilla blend against strip-and-filet. Strong; Dewar's White frames the two-cut plate at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Porterhouse|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Disaronno belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the porterhouse — craft blended rye across strip and filet. Strong; boutique character covers both cuts.",
  "Porterhouse|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's high-proof pour register leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the heavy body handles the dual-cut steak, and the deep backbone frames the cut. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Domaine Serene Grand Cheval's char-cherry tannin frames the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Domaine Tempier Bandol's bold earth-mineral cassis finds register with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the opulent body meets the cap-fat. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Porterhouse|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé's crisp white-wine character falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- crisp white reads thin against 26+ ounces of cap-fat ribeye. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Porterhouse: strip-and-filet two-cut plate meets Domaine du Grand Tinel. Excellent; the Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône blend's earth-and-fruit balance handles both the strip's char and the filet's refinement at a single register. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Porterhouse|Don Fulano Anejo": "Don Fulano Anejo's agave-apricot character sits with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the aged-tequila depth grips the marbled cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Porterhouse|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the dual-cut steak, and the green-agave edge brightens the cut. Works; blanco isn't the natural filet call even at premium tier.",
  "Porterhouse|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado's agave-floral character sits with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the aged-tequila depth grips the marbled cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942 with the porterhouse — 1942 across strip and filet. Works; benchmark extra añejo is slightly ambitious for the two-cut plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th's crisp agave-char character holds steady against the porterhouse's two-cut character — the aged-tequila depth grips the marbled cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Porterhouse|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco's workhorse blanco with clean agave snap sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the dual-cut steak, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Porterhouse|Don Julio Reposado": "Don Julio Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the barrel-rested body handles the dual-cut steak, and the oak threads the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Drambuie": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Drambuie belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Dunville's Single Malt": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the approachable Irish character carries the dual-cut steak, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the cut. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Porterhouse|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the porterhouse — BT Bottled-in-Bond against strip-and-filet. Strong; EHT Full Proof frames the two-cut plate at historic Kentucky register.",
  "Porterhouse|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye with the porterhouse — 100-proof BIB across strip and filet. Strong; bonded weight leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "Porterhouse|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the porterhouse — 100-proof BIB across strip and filet. Strong; bonded weight leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "Porterhouse|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Eagle Rare 10 Year with the porterhouse — the 10-year's oak and balance handle both strip and filet sides cleanly. The versatile premium call for this two-cut plate; Weller Millennium is the wheated alternate. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Porterhouse|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Eastern Kille Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Eastern Kille Dry Gin for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|El Cabo": "El Cabo's cooked-agave-and-oak register meets at register with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the rested-agave depth sits with the dual-cut steak, and the oak threads the cut. Strong; 5-year Ultra sits with richness at collector-grade register.",
  "Porterhouse|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "El Mayor Extra Anejo with the porterhouse — accessible aged across strip and filet. Works; covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Porterhouse|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado's agave-char character lifts the porterhouse's two-cut character — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Porterhouse|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Elephant Gin on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 with the porterhouse — HH 18yr against strip-and-filet. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Porterhouse|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel with the porterhouse — house-picked single-barrel across strip and filet. Strong; curated profile covers both cuts with Heaven Hill single-barrel character.",
  "Porterhouse|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel with the porterhouse — Heaven Hill single-barrel across strip and filet. Strong; single-barrel depth covers both cuts with oak-spice character.",
  "Porterhouse|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch with the porterhouse — small-batch character across strip and filet. Strong; covers both cuts cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the porterhouse — 94-proof HH across strip and filet. Strong; HH character covers both cuts at premium register.",
  "Porterhouse|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Elk Cove Five Mountain's cherry-clove tannin matches the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Porterhouse|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Elk Cove Pinot Blanc's crisp white-wine character falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- crisp white reads thin against 26+ ounces of cap-fat ribeye. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Elk Cove Pinot Blanc lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee with the porterhouse — namesake single-barrel across strip and filet. Strong; the 90-proof balance covers both cuts cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Empress 1908 Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Epoch Ingenuity": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis finds register with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the opulent body meets the cap-fat. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Escargot": "Porterhouse after the escargot -- umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; the opener sits cleanly before the steak.",
  "Porterhouse|Espresso Martini": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness mis-pairs with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Espresso Martini on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Espresso Old Fashioned's spirit-forward cocktail register mis-pairs with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Espresso Old Fashioned for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Evening Land Seven Springs's cherry-earth tannin holds the line with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Far Niente Chardonnay's estate Napa Chardonnay character underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Far Niente Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi from the Maremma coast, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The firm tannin handles dual-texture strip-and-tender and the tobacco plays with the mixed profile. Peak Super Tuscan for Porterhouse — Maremma structure bridges both cuts. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Porterhouse|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Faust — Napa Cab, blackcurrant, blueberry, dark chocolate, tobacco, firm tannin. The structured body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the layered complexity plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong value Napa Cab for Porterhouse — brooding depth on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's cherry-earth tannin sits with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity leans against the porterhouse's two-cut character — the bitter herb edge cuts the cap-fat richness. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Porterhouse|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta's crisp mint-menthol edge sits alongside the porterhouse's two-cut character — the bitter herb edge cuts the cap-fat richness. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Fireball Whisky lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Fisher Unity Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin matches the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select with the porterhouse — 104-proof premium across strip and filet. Strong; extra proof leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "Porterhouse|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label with the porterhouse — entry bourbon across strip and filet sides. Strong for the filet side; underpowered for the strip. Buffalo Trace or Woodford Reserve are more balanced calls here.",
  "Porterhouse|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with the porterhouse — craft bourbon across strip and filet. Strong; the boutique character covers both cuts at craft-small-batch register.",
  "Porterhouse|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Fox & Oden Double Oaked with the porterhouse — Fox & Oden Double Oaked against strip-and-filet. Works; Fox & Oden frames the two-cut plate at Double-Oaked register.",
  "Porterhouse|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Frangelico for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Porterhouse: the strip's marbled-and-char richness finds the bold red's grip; cassis carries the savory weight cleanly. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Porterhouse|French 75": "French 75's gin-and-Champagne lift with citrus snap underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the French 75 for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|French Onion": "The porterhouse preceded by the French onion -- the dual strip-and-filet matches the gruyere-crusted onion. Strong; the opener composes cleanly before the cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Frias Block 5": "Porterhouse: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — G.D. Vajra Albe La Morra, tar, rose petal, dried cherry, firm tannic backbone, full body, high acid. The firm tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the rose petal plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong classic Barolo for Porterhouse — Vajra exceptional on the dual-cut.",
  "Porterhouse|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Sweet, effervescent Moscato — peach blossom, apricot, lychee with a honeyed finish. A dessert wine, not a steak wine. The porterhouse's 40oz of marbled beef flattens the aromatics and drowns the delicate sweetness. Save for the end of the meal, not alongside the course. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Porterhouse|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the unaged body brightens the dual-cut steak, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Porterhouse|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof's aged-agave character sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the barrel-rested body handles the dual-cut steak, and the oak threads the cut. Strong; modern cleanness is capable but the starter earns a more character-forward reposado.",
  "Porterhouse|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the gentle Irish body threads the dual-cut steak, and the grain register softens the cut. Works; bold character slightly over-specified for the soup.",
  "Porterhouse|George T. Stagg": "GTS with the porterhouse — BTAC holy-grail against strip-and-filet. Strong; GTS frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Porterhouse|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Ghost Block — Oakville Cab, blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak, firm tannin. The dense body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the graphite-oak plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong Oakville Cab for Porterhouse — Napa's golden-strip structure on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's bold pepper-spice cassis holds the line with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the firm-tannin weight matches the rendered fat, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Porterhouse|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12 with the porterhouse — apple/pear Speyside against strip-and-filet. Strong; Fiddich's gateway lightness frames the two-cut plate without competing.",
  "Porterhouse|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14 with the porterhouse — Bourbon Barrel Reserve against strip-and-filet. Strong; Fiddich 14's vanilla-spice depth frames the two-cut plate with aged character.",
  "Porterhouse|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18 with the porterhouse — Oloroso + bourbon cask marriage against strip-and-filet. Strong; Fiddich 18's depth frames the two-cut plate with premium aged character.",
  "Porterhouse|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23 with the porterhouse — French Cuvée cask finish against strip-and-filet. Strong; Gran Cru's elegance frames the two-cut plate at rare-release register.",
  "Porterhouse|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12 with the porterhouse — recently-reopened Sandend Highland against strip-and-filet. Strong; Glenglassaugh 12's tropical-coastal character frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Porterhouse|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12 with the porterhouse — honey-pineapple Speyside against strip-and-filet. Strong; Glenlivet's softer profile frames the two-cut plate with benchmark elegance.",
  "Porterhouse|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10 with the porterhouse — honey-floral Highland against strip-and-filet. Strong; Glenmorangie 10's vanilla-peach character frames the two-cut plate at entry register.",
  "Porterhouse|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18 with the porterhouse — 18yr Glenmorangie with Oloroso finish against strip-and-filet. Strong; Glenmorangie 18's aged depth frames the two-cut plate at premium register.",
  "Porterhouse|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- dessert wine on prime cut is wrong-course entirely — save it for after. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Graham's 10 Year Tawny lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- dessert wine is post-meal — wrong register for the steak course. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Graham's 20 Year Tawny for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Graham's 2017 Vintage Port's structured vintage port with dark-fruit weight clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- dessert wine on prime cut is wrong-course entirely — save it for after. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Graham's 2017 Vintage Port on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum's agave-citrus character lifts the porterhouse's two-cut character — the light body is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Grand Marnier on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Gray Whale Gin for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's cask-strength herbal-high-proof sweetness stays alongside the porterhouse's two-cut character — the sweet character meets the cap-fat at digestif register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Porterhouse|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Grey Goose Vodka for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Grilled Caesar": "The porterhouse preceded by the Caesar -- the bone-in T-bone plays against the parmesan-Caesar plate. Strong; the opener composes cleanly before the cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Gumbo": "Porterhouse preceded by the gumbo -- spice plays against rich protein, clean contrast. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Porterhouse: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Porterhouse|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. HDW 100 Vodka belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 Year's crisp forest-fresh-Japanese character holds steady against the porterhouse's two-cut character — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Porterhouse|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel with the porterhouse — BT single-barrel across strip and filet. Strong; 88.9-proof balance covers both cuts cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Hangar 1 Vodka for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Head Fake": "Head Fake's sweet citrus-lemon build plays cleanly against the porterhouse's two-cut character — the sweet character meets the cap-fat at digestif register, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the porterhouse — HH 20yr against strip-and-filet. Works; HH 20 frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Porterhouse|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond with the porterhouse — 100-proof across strip and filet sides. Strong; the BIB weight leans toward the strip side, the filet side tolerates it.",
  "Porterhouse|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with the porterhouse — aged 100-proof across strip and filet. Strong; the aged weight leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "Porterhouse|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "Porterhouse: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Porterhouse|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "Porterhouse: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Porterhouse|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Hendricks Flora Adora's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Hendricks Flora Adora for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Hendricks Gin for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Hendricks Grand Cabaret's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Hendricks Grand Cabaret for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Hendricks Neptunia": "Hendricks Neptunia's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Hendricks Neptunia for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Hendricks Orbium": "Hendricks Orbium's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Hendricks Orbium for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy Cognac's classic dried-fruit-and-vanilla cognac leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the cognac register matches the dual-cut steak, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the cut. Works; the close holds, neither soars nor fights.",
  "Porterhouse|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB with the porterhouse — 100-proof 10-year BIB across strip and filet. Strong; bonded weight leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "Porterhouse|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Japanese Harmony's Japanese-blend-honey character sits with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Porterhouse alongside the honey carrots -- the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character meets the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: savory-into-sweet, the classic close lands intact. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Porterhouse|House Wedge": "The porterhouse after the wedge -- the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch sets the table for the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: protein meets umami, the table builds substance. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Porterhouse|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 with the porterhouse — 15yr BT-distilled against strip-and-filet. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Porterhouse|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Il Colle Prosecco Superiore's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Il Colle Prosecco Superiore for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Inhibited": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle mis-pairs with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Inhibited for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Inverroche Gin on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Iron Fish Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon Mezcal Casks with the porterhouse — Iron Fish MI mezcal-cask against strip-and-filet. Works; Ironfish Mezcal frames the two-cut plate at Michigan-hometown register.",
  "Porterhouse|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the bourbon depth settles into the dual-cut steak, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the spicy-sweet character sits with the side's richness.",
  "Porterhouse|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "J Davies Ferrington Vineyards's cherry-coffee tannin reads alongside the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "J. Davies — Diamond Mountain Cab, dark fruit, graphite, mineral, firm tannin. The structured body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the graphite-mineral plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong mountain Cab for Porterhouse — Schramsberg provenance and volcanic complexity on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year with the porterhouse — aged Tennessee against strip-and-filet. Strong; JD 12's aged depth frames the two-cut plate at premium register.",
  "Porterhouse|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded with the porterhouse — 100-proof Bottled-in-Bond against strip-and-filet. Strong; JD Bonded frames the two-cut plate at Bonded-Act register.",
  "Porterhouse|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7 with the porterhouse — charcoal-mellowed Tennessee against strip-and-filet. Strong; Old No. 7 frames the two-cut plate at iconic Tennessee register.",
  "Porterhouse|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "JD Sinatra Select with the porterhouse — Sinatra-tribute Tennessee against strip-and-filet. Strong; Sinatra Select's grooved-barrel character frames the two-cut plate at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Porterhouse|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's classic Irish blend smoothness leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the triple-distilled softness sits with the dual-cut steak, and the malt rounds the cut. Works; curated single-barrel slightly over-specified.",
  "Porterhouse|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir's cherry-earth tannin frames the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis' Burgundy Chablis flinty minerality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- white wine can't stand up to the marbled char — the cut earns a structured red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the porterhouse — limited-edition ocean-finish against strip-and-filet. Works; Marian McLain L.E. frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Porterhouse|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the porterhouse — Voyage #23 against strip-and-filet. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Porterhouse|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve with the porterhouse — blended elegance across both strip and filet sides. Strong; Jefferson's VR Twin Oak is the richer alternate within the family for this plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak with the porterhouse — double-barrel finish handles both strip and filet sides cleanly. The Twin Oak depth covers the plate in a way standard Jefferson's Reserve can't. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label with the porterhouse — aged Beam across strip and filet. Strong; oak handles both cuts capably at the accessible register.",
  "Porterhouse|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label with the porterhouse — 80-proof entry across strip and filet. Strong as a choice; the porterhouse deserves a heavier pour.",
  "Porterhouse|Johnnie Walker Black": "JW Black with the porterhouse — iconic 12yr blended against strip-and-filet. Strong; JW Black frames the two-cut plate at benchmark premium register.",
  "Porterhouse|Johnnie Walker Blue": "JW Blue with the porterhouse — hand-selected rare-cask JW against strip-and-filet. Strong; JW Blue's luxury character frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Porterhouse|Johnnie Walker Red": "JW Red with the porterhouse — 80-proof bold JW against strip-and-filet. Strong; JW Red's smoke-forward frames the two-cut plate with distinctive character.",
  "Porterhouse|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Jordan — Alexander Valley Cab, cassis, plum, cedar, mint, fine tannin. The elegant body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the cedar-mint plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong elegant Cab for Porterhouse — Jordan balance on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre's classic Loire Sauvignon Blanc falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- crisp white reads thin against 26+ ounces of cap-fat ribeye. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Joseph Mellot Sancerre for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the dark chocolate plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong cult Napa for Porterhouse — Colgin on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's bold char-earth character holds the line with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's heavy aged-spirit weight sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the layered spirit body sits with the dual-cut steak, and the layered body brightens the cut. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Kahlua lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Keenan Chardonnay": "Keenan Chardonnay's oaked Spring Mountain Chardonnay underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- white wine can't stand up to the marbled char — the cut earns a structured red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Keenan Chardonnay for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Keenan Mernet": "Porterhouse: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Porterhouse|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye with the porterhouse — 10-year allocated KO across strip and filet. Strong; premium character covers both cuts with collector-register authority.",
  "Porterhouse|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the porterhouse — KO Confiscated across strip and filet. Works; craft-blend covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Porterhouse|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the malt-grain blend handles the dual-cut steak, and the soft malt rounds the cut. Strong; composed namesake match.",
  "Porterhouse|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Rhône Valley Côtes du Rhône Grenache-Syrah-Mourvèdre — Kermit Lynch, red cherry, wild herbs, pepper, garrigue, medium body. Medium body is slightly light for Porterhouse's dual-cut heft, but wild herbs play against grilled beef. Works for Porterhouse — Tempier Bandol or Scavino Barolo handles the cut harder.",
  "Porterhouse|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Ketel One Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Knickerbocker Gin for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek with the porterhouse — 100-proof high-rye across strip and filet. The strip side takes the boldness beautifully; the filet side is a touch over-matched. Strong; the Wellers are lighter for filet, Knob is the better strip-side call.",
  "Porterhouse|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year with the porterhouse — aged high-rye across strip and filet. Strong; 12-year depth leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "Porterhouse|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 with the porterhouse — aged Beam KC against strip-and-filet. Works; KC 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-Beam register.",
  "Porterhouse|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 with the porterhouse — 18yr aged Beam KC against strip-and-filet. Works; KC 18 frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Porterhouse|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye with the porterhouse — blend covers both strip and filet sides. Strong; the bourbon-rye layering handles both cuts with distinctive character.",
  "Porterhouse|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye with the porterhouse — 100-proof bold rye across strip and filet. Strong; Beam intensity leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "Porterhouse|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva's aged-agave character leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the barrel-rested body handles the dual-cut steak, and the oak threads the cut. Strong; distinctive ceremony sits with richness.",
  "Porterhouse|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo's crisp char-floral character holds the line with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Añejo with the porterhouse — aged Komos across strip and filet. Works; luxury ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado's crisp agave-floral character holds steady against the porterhouse's two-cut character — the aged-tequila depth grips the marbled cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Porterhouse|Lagavulin 8": "Porterhouse: two cuts in one plate (filet side + strip side) meet Lagavulin 8's balanced Islay peat. The strip side takes the 8-year's smoke beautifully; the filet side tolerates the brightness better than it would Laphroaig. A solid Islay pour for porterhouse tables. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Porterhouse|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Napa Cab — Lail entry-level, polished blackcurrant, cedar, dark cherry, refined medium tannin. The refined tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the cedar plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong historically-pedigreed Napa Cab for Porterhouse — Lail family polish on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the cassis plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong historically-significant Napa Cab for Porterhouse — Daniel Cuvée pedigree on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver's unaged silver-spirit body leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the dual-cut steak, and the green-agave edge brightens the cut. Works; the opener sits without competing.",
  "Porterhouse|Laphroaig 10": "Laphroaig 10 with the porterhouse — the strip side takes Laphroaig's medicinal punch head-on; the filet side tolerates less but holds. A bold pour for the cut; the dram is never going to recede into the background, and that's the point. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch with the porterhouse — wheated honey across both strip and filet sides. A versatile everyday pour; Heaven Hill's alternate to Weller Special Reserve for this plate. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves a structured red. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut's classic non-vintage Champagne brightness underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Le Garenne Rosé": "Le Garenne Rosé's crisp floral-rose acidity leans against the porterhouse's two-cut character — the white-wine body is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Porterhouse: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Porterhouse|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Porterhouse: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Porterhouse|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- light cocktail lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Lemon Lavender Gin Martini for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Les Pallières Raciné": "Porterhouse: the Cab's firm tannin cuts the strip's char and the dark fruit lifts the savory edge — steakhouse symmetry. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Liberator Gin": "Liberator Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Liberator Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Licor 43 for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Limoncello on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir -- Willamette Pinot, red plum, forest floor. The layered Pinot body matches the dual-cut steak, and the forest-floor edge frames the cut. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Little Book": "Little Book with the porterhouse — Beam Fred Noe annual against strip-and-filet. Strong; Little Book frames the two-cut plate at chapter-series register.",
  "Porterhouse|Loaded Potato": "The porterhouse after the loaded potato -- the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup sets the table for the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: cream and protein share the meal, the build reads full. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Lobster Mac": "Porterhouse with the lobster mac -- the 40oz dual strip-and-filet meets the shellfish-in-cream indulgence, both courses indulgent, neither outpacing the other. Strong; the side reads steakhouse-classic alongside the cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year with the porterhouse — 20yr Highland against strip-and-filet. Strong; Loch Lomond 20's complex-aged depth frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Porterhouse|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the porterhouse — sourced boutique across strip and filet. Strong; blended character covers both cuts at premium-sourced register.",
  "Porterhouse|Long Road Gin": "Long Road Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Long Road Gin on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Los Vecinos Mezcal's wood-fire mezcal body sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the rustic mezcal weight sits with the dual-cut steak, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- an icon cognac is best as a separate occasion — pair the steak with a Cab or bourbon, then close with the cognac. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not an icon cognac. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Louis XIII Cognac for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Lucano Amaro for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Porterhouse: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this medium-bodied red's approachable fruit and food-friendly acidity. The elegant red's medium-light frame gets overrun by the bold, marbled steak — the wine shrinks. Works only if the guest prizes restraint over match.",
  "Porterhouse|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry with the porterhouse — benchmark sherried Speyside against strip-and-filet. Works; Macallan 12 frames the two-cut plate at entry-sherried register.",
  "Porterhouse|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18 with the porterhouse — 18yr aged Oloroso Speyside against strip-and-filet. Works; Macallan 18's aged depth frames the two-cut plate at premium register.",
  "Porterhouse|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate with the porterhouse — Estate-grown-oak Macallan against strip-and-filet. Works; Macallan Estate's distinctive character frames the two-cut plate at premium register.",
  "Porterhouse|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin handles Porterhouse's dual-cut heft and the black pepper works across both filet and strip. Strong Petite-Sirah — exceptional with Porterhouse per the card's call-out.",
  "Porterhouse|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Macauley Pinot Noir's crisp cherry-floral tannin holds steady against the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Porterhouse|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Porterhouse: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Mahon Gin": "Mahon Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Mahon Gin on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark with the porterhouse — soft wheated against the strip-and-filet split. Works cleanly across both sides without standing out; Maker's Cellar Aged and Weller 12 are the stronger calls.",
  "Porterhouse|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Porterhouse: the cut's strip-and-filet split meets Maker's Mark Cellar Aged. The strip side takes the depth; the filet side holds. More appropriate than standard Maker's for this plate; Weller 12 is the alternate premium call. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light spirit. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Malibu Rum belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye with the porterhouse — 16-year Michigan across strip and filet. Strong; extended maturation covers both cuts with home-state distillery character.",
  "Porterhouse|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Marc Brédif Chinon's cherry-earth tannin matches the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Margarita": "Margarita's crisp agave-brine build finds neutral with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the cocktail is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Porterhouse|Marimar Estate Christina": "Marimar Estate Christina's cherry-earth tannin frames the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Porterhouse|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- aperitivo bitterness fights the headline cut's marbled char — bitter and savory pull apart. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Mata Hari Absinthe lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Bourbon with the porterhouse — Michter's allocated 10yr against strip-and-filet. Works; Michter's 10 frames the two-cut plate at prestigious-independent register.",
  "Porterhouse|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye with the porterhouse — aged premium Michter's across strip and filet. Strong; the 10-year polish covers both cuts with small-batch refinement.",
  "Porterhouse|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the porterhouse — smooth small-batch rye across strip and filet. Strong; covers both cuts with Michter's refined polish.",
  "Porterhouse|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco's clean silver register leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the unaged body brightens the dual-cut steak, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Porterhouse|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo's rested-agave body leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the aged-agave weight wraps the dual-cut steak, and the cooked-agave note underlines the cut. Strong; modern cleanness is capable but the starter earns a more character-forward reposado.",
  "Porterhouse|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado's crisp agave-vanilla character finds register with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Porterhouse|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam with the porterhouse — Sazerac Mister Sam tribute against strip-and-filet. Works; Mister Sam frames the two-cut plate at Seagram's-tribute register.",
  "Porterhouse|Mocha Creme": "The Porterhouse into the mocha creme -- the strip-and-tender T-bone hands off to silky coffee-chocolate cream, espresso closing the meal. Strong; both cuts share register with the layered dessert.",
  "Porterhouse|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Monkey 47 Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder with the porterhouse — Speyside-malt blend against strip-and-filet. Strong; Monkey Shoulder frames the two-cut plate with Speyside-malt character.",
  "Porterhouse|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- aperitivo bitterness fights the headline cut's marbled char — bitter and savory pull apart. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Montenegro Amaro belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Mount Gay Rum for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Muga Reserva": "Muga Reserva's cherry-earth tannin matches the porterhouse's two-cut character — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Porterhouse|Mushroom Bisque": "Porterhouse preceded by the mushroom bisque -- protein and umami share the plate, the build reads weighty and clean. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Mushrooms": "Porterhouse alongside the mushrooms -- the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character meets the mushrooms' earthy umami weight: protein meets umami, the table builds substance. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "Porterhouse|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's bold molasses-Jamaican character lifts the porterhouse's two-cut character — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Porterhouse|Negroni": "Negroni's spirit-forward cocktail register mis-pairs with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Negroni for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye with the porterhouse — UK-aged rye across strip and filet. Strong; distinctive character covers both cuts with story behind it.",
  "Porterhouse|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch with the porterhouse — novelty UK-aged across strip and filet. Strong; the distinctive maturation handles both cuts with character-story behind it.",
  "Porterhouse|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with the porterhouse — Holland MI beer-barrel against strip-and-filet. Works; NH Beer Barrel frames the two-cut plate at Michigan-hometown register.",
  "Porterhouse|New York Sour": "Porterhouse: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Porterhouse: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolets Dry Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Nolets Dry Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Not a Paper Plane": "Bulleit bourbon, Nonino Quintessential, Aperol, Angostura. The cocktail's bourbon-and-amaro depth handles the Porterhouse's dual-cut nature; Nonino's bitterness cuts the strip's char and Aperol plays against the filet's delicacy. Strong spirit-forward pour for the house showcase cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Oban 14": "Oban 14 with the porterhouse — coastal Highland against strip-and-filet. Strong; Oban's maritime character frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Porterhouse|Oban 18": "Oban 18 with the porterhouse — 18yr aged West Highland against strip-and-filet. Strong; Oban 18's aged maritime + sherry-finish frames the two-cut plate at premium coastal register.",
  "Porterhouse|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo's agave-char character finds register with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Porterhouse|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "* Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin runs straight into the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the powerful body carries the dual-cut steak, and the tannin grips the cut. Peak mountain Merlot-Cab for filet — worth explaining Mernet to curious guests.",
  "Porterhouse|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the porterhouse — Old Elk cigar-paired against strip-and-filet. Works; Old Elk Cigar Cut frames the two-cut plate at cigar-companion register.",
  "Porterhouse|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer with the porterhouse — emmer-wheat wheated bourbon against strip-and-filet. Strong; Old Emmer frames the two-cut plate at craft register.",
  "Porterhouse|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year with the porterhouse — wheated BIB across strip and filet. Strong; bonded weight leans strip-side, filet tolerates; Old Fitz 9 brings more age for this plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year with the porterhouse — aged wheated BIB across strip and filet. Strong; the 9-year's depth covers both cuts more evenly than Old Fitz 7.",
  "Porterhouse|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with the porterhouse — 16yr OGD rye-forward against strip-and-filet. Works; OGD 16 frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Porterhouse|Opus One": "Opus One — Oakville Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the cedar plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong collector Napa blend for Porterhouse — Mondavi-Rothschild on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes' crisp white-wine character underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- white wine can't stand up to the marbled char — the cut earns a structured red or whiskey. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Pablo Sour": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- a light citrus cocktail can't carry the marbled char — needs whiskey or red wine weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a light cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Pablo Sour for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Paloma": "Paloma's crisp agave-citrus build sits alongside the porterhouse's two-cut character — the cocktail is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye with the porterhouse — Van Winkle Family Reserve across strip and filet. Strong; unicorn Pappy leans strip-side on this plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage 16th Ed with the porterhouse — HH annual collector bourbon against strip-and-filet. Strong; Parker's Heritage 16th frames the two-cut plate at collector cask-strength register.",
  "Porterhouse|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's crisp oak-agave character finds register with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the aged-tequila depth grips the marbled cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Porterhouse|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver's polished silver tequila with agave-forward lift reads alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the silver-spirit register lifts the dual-cut steak, and the agave-citrus thread frames the cut. Works; refined character is slightly over-specified for the dish.",
  "Porterhouse|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Patron XO Cafe on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Peanut Butter Brownie": "After the Porterhouse, the PB brownie -- the dual strip-and-tender gives way to peanut-and-chocolate fudge, the vanilla tempering the heat. Strong; the table-share build meets the substantial close.",
  "Porterhouse|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Peju — Rutherford Cab with dark cherry, cassis, dusty cocoa, refined tannin. The elegant body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the Rutherford-dust plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong elegant Napa Cab for Porterhouse — family-estate refinement on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the eau-de-vie body carries the dual-cut steak, and the dried-fruit note underlines the cut. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Porterhouse|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut's grand cru blanc-de-blancs with mineral lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves a structured red. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye with the porterhouse — 110-proof across strip and filet. Strong; high proof leans strip-side, filet tolerates.",
  "Porterhouse|Pineau des Charentes": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pineau des Charentes lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Porterhouse: the strip's firm beef-and-char meets the structured red's tannic backbone; cassis and dark fruit catch the rendered fat. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Porterhouse|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Plymouth Gin for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Porterhouse: structured red anchors the bold strip: tannin scrubs fat, dark fruit settles into char, oak threads the cut. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery Cuvée Louise's prestige cuvée Champagne elegance underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves a structured red. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Pommery Cuvée Louise for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Prime Tartare": "Porterhouse after the tartare -- umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; the opener sits cleanly before the steak.",
  "Porterhouse|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt — Napa Cab, blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft tannin. The polished tannin frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the chocolate plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong polished Napa Cab for Porterhouse — indulgent anchor on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|RAEN Royal St Robert": "RAEN Royal St Robert: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char. The elegant red's medium-light frame gets overrun by the bold, marbled steak — the wine shrinks. Works only if the guest prizes restraint over match.",
  "Porterhouse|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the Irish whiskey body sits with the dual-cut steak, and the soft malt rounds the cut. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "Porterhouse|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the gentle Irish body threads the dual-cut steak, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the cut. Works; the pour's brightness is a gentle counterpoint to the dessert's richness.",
  "Porterhouse|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Gatsby with the porterhouse — MGP 15yr Gatsby Reserve against strip-and-filet. Works; Remus 15 frames the two-cut plate at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Porterhouse|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Remy VSOP Cognac's VSOP fine-champagne cognac finds neutral with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the brandy depth wraps the dual-cut steak, and the caramel rounds the cut. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Porterhouse|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo with the porterhouse — sun-bottle across strip and filet. Works; distinctive ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reyka Vodka belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique with the porterhouse — retailer-label aged across strip and filet. Works; boutique covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Porterhouse|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Porterhouse preceded by the chickpea soup -- heat lifts the rich course, the table earns the warmer note. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs' bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves a structured red. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Roku Gin": "Roku Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Roku Gin on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum -- Solera 23, Guatemalan, honey-vanilla-caramel. The Solera honey wraps the dual-cut steak, and the caramel rounds the cut. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with the porterhouse — aged Wild Turkey across strip and filet. Strong; the classic Kentucky profile covers both sides cleanly but Eagle Rare 10 is the firmer call for max-strip-impact.",
  "Porterhouse|Sambuca": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Sambuca for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|San Simeon Stormwatch": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin, full body. The medium tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the mocha plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Works as Paso value Bordeaux blend for Porterhouse — solid full-body on the dual-cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the Pinot body brightens the dual-cut steak, and the cherry-and-earth note frames the cut. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Porterhouse|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Porterhouse alongside the garlic spinach -- the 40oz dual strip-and-filet meets the wilted garlic-buttered greens, garlic anchoring both the strip's char and the filet's butter. Strong; a confident table-wide order.",
  "Porterhouse|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- dessert wine on prime cut is wrong-course entirely — save it for after. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Sauternes Glass belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The cocktail handles the Porterhouse's dual-cut nature — rye spice meets the strip, Peychaud's cherry plays against filet. Strong cocktail pour for the house showcase cut when guest wants classic over Cab.",
  "Porterhouse|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye with the porterhouse — iconic rye across strip and filet. Strong; the spice-sweet character covers both cuts with benchmark rye precision.",
  "Porterhouse|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the porterhouse — NZ craft single malt against strip-and-filet. Strong; Fortuna frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Porterhouse|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The powerful tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the tar plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong King-of-Italian for Porterhouse — Scavino prestige on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Off-dry Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol with a lively acid backbone keeping the residual sweetness in check. The acid balance holds, but the light body can't meet 40oz of marbled porterhouse. Redirect to a red; a gateway Riesling for other courses, not this one. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Porterhouse|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin. The medium tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet though the smooth profile reads rounder than firm-tannin Napa on the cut. Works as by-the-glass Cab for Porterhouse — accessible value on the dual-cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Seafood Tower": "Porterhouse after the seafood tower -- iced shellfish opens with showpiece presentation, the 40oz dual strip-and-filet following with two cuts in one, both courses indulgent in their own register. Strong; showpiece into showpiece reads steakhouse-classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Seared Scallops": "The porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character overwhelms the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness: the bold beefy character obliterates the scallops, and the dual strip-and-filet drowns out the seared-rare scallop plate. Avoid; the courses can't share a meal cleanly. Pair the scallops with Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre on a different visit; pour Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel alongside the porterhouse.",
  "Porterhouse|Seasonal Soup": "The porterhouse after the seasonal soup -- the seasonal soup's house seasonal build sets the table for the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Seasonal Vegetables": "The porterhouse with the seasonal vegetables -- the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; the side sits without pulling focus.",
  "Porterhouse|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Porterhouse: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Shafer 1.5": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin. The plush tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the mocha plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong Stags Leap Cab for Porterhouse — Shafer opulence on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Shafer Hillside Select": "Stags Leap collector's Cab — Hillside Select 32-month French oak, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violet, graphite, dense yet silky, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the graphite plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong collector's Napa Cab for Porterhouse — Hillside Select on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Shrimp Bisque": "Porterhouse into the shrimp bisque -- the meal layers rich on rich, the table carries weight cleanly when the registers match. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "Porterhouse|Shrimp Cocktail": "Porterhouse after the shrimp cocktail -- the chilled shrimp's citrus-horseradish snap cleans the palate, the 40oz dual strip-and-filet following with strip char and filet butter, both cuts the opener primed cleanly. Strong; the classic combo reads timeless.",
  "Porterhouse|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the silver-spirit register lifts the dual-cut steak, and the bright blanco register underlines the cut. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado's crisp agave-earth character sits with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Porterhouse|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Alexander Valley Cab — American-oak vanilla, coconut, dill, ripe cassis, plum, soft tannin. The lush body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the vanilla-dill plays against both lean and fatty sides. Peak recognizable Cab for Porterhouse — Silver Oak's American-oak signature on the dual-cut classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Porterhouse|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Boutique Napa family estate Cab — dark plum, cassis, toasted oak, long refined finish, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the cassis plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong boutique Napa Cab for Porterhouse — Simon Family pedigree on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "St. Helena Napa Cab — Spottswoode organic family estate, plum, dark cherry, violet, silky medium tannin. The silky tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the plum plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong organic Napa Cab for Porterhouse — Spottswoode family story on the dual-cut.",
  "Porterhouse|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry — Napa Cab, blackberry, cassis, mocha, full body, firm tannin. The firm tannin frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the cassis plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong by-the-glass Cab for Porterhouse — accessible price for the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- crisp white reads thin against 26+ ounces of cap-fat ribeye. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the espresso plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong estate Meritage for Porterhouse — Napa on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the porterhouse — BT cask-strength against strip-and-filet. Strong; Stagg Jr. frames the two-cut plate at barrel-proof register.",
  "Porterhouse|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Stoli Elit Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, fresh-cut grass with zesty acid. Light-bodied and bright with no body to speak of for a 40oz porterhouse. The wine washes out completely against the marbled strip side. Redirect to a red for the course. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Porterhouse|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Tanq 10 Gin for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Tanqueray Gin on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- honeyed sugar dulls the marbled char — the steak earns a savory pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Tawny on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- dessert wine on prime cut is wrong-course entirely — save it for after. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle on another course instead.",
  "Porterhouse|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with the porterhouse — triple-cask across strip and filet. Works; multi-cask complexity over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Cab — rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth polished finish. The crowd-pleaser body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the mocha plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong mid-tier Napa Cab for Porterhouse — polished concentration on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Porterhouse: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Porterhouse|The BG": "Porterhouse: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth, oak, and sweetness carry beef weight — the drink scrubs fat and echoes char. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "Porterhouse|The Happy Wife": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build matches the porterhouse's two-cut character — the cocktail is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the citrus brightens the cream. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Porterhouse|The Manhattan": "Maker's Mark private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura — stirred, large cube. Spirit-forward richness the porterhouse's marbled strip side can meet. Table-for-two pairing: one red, one cocktail, shared cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|The Prisoner Red Blend": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The full body handles Porterhouse's dual-cut heft and the integrated tannin works across both filet and strip. Strong Prisoner for Porterhouse — Napa lush on the big plate.",
  "Porterhouse|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the porterhouse — BTAC barrel-proof across strip and filet. Strong; uncut intensity leans strip-side.",
  "Porterhouse|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's char-mineral character holds the line with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the bourbon character meets the rendered fat. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Titos Vodka": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Titos Vodka belongs on another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky -- Suntory entry, light, citrus. The entry Suntory body sits with the dual-cut steak, and the citrus lifts the cut. Strong; Kentucky Owl St.",
  "Porterhouse|Tomato Basil": "The porterhouse after the tomato basil -- the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid sets the table for the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the bright register cleaves the heavy course, the table breathes. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "Porterhouse|Transfusion": "Transfusion's crisp citrus-ginger build reads alongside the porterhouse's two-cut character — the cocktail is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Porterhouse|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado's agave-char character holds steady against the porterhouse's two-cut character — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Porterhouse|Truffle Fries": "Porterhouse alongside the truffle fries -- the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character carries cleanly into the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight. Excellent; the headline side pairing for the porterhouse.",
  "Porterhouse|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s gentle Irish blend sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the malt-grain blend handles the dual-cut steak, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the cut. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Porterhouse|Venge Family Reserve": "Porterhouse: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Porterhouse|Venge Scout's Honor": "Napa Zin-led blend — Kirk Venge's Scout's Honor, blackberry, boysenberry, black pepper, vanilla, full body, generous finish. Black pepper plays with dual-texture strip-and-tender and ripe fruit handles the mixed profile. Strong Zin-led for Porterhouse — fruit-forward weight bridges both cuts.",
  "Porterhouse|Venge Silencieux": "Kirk Venge single-vineyard Napa — dark cherry, cassis, chocolate, floral violet, silky soft tannin. The silky structure handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the chocolate plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong winemaker's Napa Cab for Porterhouse — Venge precision on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Vesper": "Vesper's crisp citrus-juniper build holds with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the cocktail is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Porterhouse|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's classic yellow-label Champagne body underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves a structured red. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Veuve Clicquot Brut for another course.",
  "Porterhouse|Vieux Carré": "Porterhouse: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. The whiskey's barrel depth and the steak's flame-grilled fat run at the same intensity — a classic steakhouse move. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "Porterhouse|Villon Cognac": "Villon Cognac's barrel-aged cognac character sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the dual-cut steak, and the caramel rounds the cut. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Porterhouse|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- honeyed sugar dulls the marbled char — the steak earns a savory pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Vin Santo lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|Weller 107": "Porterhouse: the cut's strip-and-filet meets Weller 107. Higher proof handles the strip beautifully; the filet is a touch overpowered. Weller 12 is the more balanced call. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Porterhouse|Weller 12 Year": "* Weller 12 Year -- aged wheated, honey, vanilla. The bourbon backbone carries the dual-cut steak, and the vanilla balances the char-and-fat. A distinctive wheated pour for filet. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Porterhouse|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium with the porterhouse — the Millennium's complexity handles both strip and filet sides. Where Weller 12 leans into strip and fades on filet, the Millennium's polish covers both. The premium wheated for this two-cut plate. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve with the porterhouse — both strip and filet sides meet the gateway wheated cleanly. The most versatile mediator of the Wellers for this two-cut plate, even if Weller 12 brings more refinement. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Porterhouse|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Wheatley Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Porterhouse|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the porterhouse — WP-core across strip and filet. Strong; aged 100-proof covers both cuts with premium rye character.",
  "Porterhouse|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with the porterhouse — 12-year Old World rye across strip and filet. The wine-cask complexity covers both cuts with more fruit-forward character than WP 10 Year single-barrel; a refined porterhouse rye. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Porterhouse|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with the porterhouse — 15-year flagship across strip and filet. Strong; extended maturation covers both cuts with premium WP flagship character.",
  "Porterhouse|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with the porterhouse — barrel-proof allocated WP across strip and filet. Strong; collector-bottle intensity leans strip-side on this plate.",
  "Porterhouse|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with the porterhouse — X Commandments across strip and filet. Strong; 10th-edition leans strip-side like Boss Hog IX.",
  "Porterhouse|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year with the porterhouse — WP 100-proof across strip and filet. Strong; covers both cuts with Vermont-brand character.",
  "Porterhouse|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with the porterhouse — craft high-rye across strip and filet. Strong; the oak spice covers both cuts with distinctive character.",
  "Porterhouse|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with the porterhouse — small-batch character across strip and filet sides. Strong; fruit and floral notes give both cuts something to meet, though they shine more on the filet side.",
  "Porterhouse|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with the porterhouse — iconic KY Double Oaked against strip-and-filet. Works; Woodford Double Oaked frames the two-cut plate at KY-benchmark register.",
  "Porterhouse|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the porterhouse — small-batch rye across strip and filet. Strong; the fruit-forward elegance covers both cuts with small-batch refinement.",
  "Porterhouse|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with the porterhouse — PNW craft rye across strip and filet. Strong; regional character covers both cuts.",
  "Porterhouse|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year -- Suntory, Japanese flagship, silky. The Mizunara-influenced register sits with the dual-cut steak, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the cut. Strong; boutique-blend sits with richness.",
  "Porterhouse|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's bold Mizunara-oak-Japanese character sits with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Porterhouse|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- aperitivo bitterness fights the headline cut's marbled char — bitter and savory pull apart. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Yellow Chartreuse on another course instead.",
  "Prime Tartare|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch -- caramel, spice, 93.7 proof Barton. The bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak threads the umami plate. Strong; GTS meets the earthy-rich at collector register; consider whether the side deserves a BTAC pour; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's butter-caramel character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's bold cherry-coffee cassis holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|2 James Gin": "2 James with the tartare — Detroit craft against raw beef. Strong; 2 James's American juniper supports tartare with hometown character; Bombay Dry's angular juniper still leads the gin-tartare pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's oak-spice character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's chocolate-cocoa character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino with the tartare — filtered aged tequila against raw beef. The aged-but-clear character is a distinctive craft counterpoint to tartare's mineral depth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado with the tartare — near-añejo reposado against raw beef. Works; the aged depth is an unusual but interesting match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay's apple-butter acidity reads alongside the tartare's raw beef and brine — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Prime Tartare|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' concentrated dark-fruit and tannin sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the structured body wraps the tartare, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Prime Tartare|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Prime Tartare|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino with the tartare — Friulian amaro against raw beef. Strong; the grappa-base and orange peel lift the tartare's mineral notes.",
  "Prime Tartare|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes's ancho-chile-smoke sweetness plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the sweet character meets the savory plate at digestif register. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon weight handles the tartare, and the oak balances the umami register. Strong; the 10-year polish covers both cuts with small-batch refinement; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's cask-strength juniper-maple character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Prime Tartare|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's spice-vanilla character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Aperol": "Aperol with the tartare — 11% soft bitter against raw beef. Strong; Aperol is lighter than Campari here but the orange-rhubarb still lifts tartare's mineral notes at aperitivo register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Asparagus": "The tartare and the asparagus -- acid breaks the umami stack, the table earns the brighter note. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Prime Tartare|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The tartare and the au gratin -- dairy plays against umami, the table reads layered. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Prime Tartare|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-coffee cassis reads alongside the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Averna Amaro": "Averna with the tartare — fuller-body classic amaro against raw beef. Strong; caramel-orange lifts the tartare's mineral depth.",
  "Prime Tartare|Aviation": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this gin cocktail's citrus brightness and botanical lift. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. Strong — aligned lift.",
  "Prime Tartare|Aviation Gin": "Aviation with the tartare — soft-juniper Portland craft against raw beef. Strong; Aviation supports tartare at crowd-pleasing register; Monkey 47's 47-botanical complexity is the featured craft-gin-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Avion 44": "Avion 44's aged-agave character leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the oak-aged tequila body threads the tartare, and the caramel-agave register brightens the starter. Strong; a balanced pairing at the everyday tier.",
  "Prime Tartare|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver with the tartare — Jalisco-highlands blanco against raw beef. The clean filtered character is a bright counterpoint at the everyday register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the unaged body brightens the tartare, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the starter. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Prime Tartare|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Baileys Irish Cream on the steak course instead.",
  "Prime Tartare|Baker's": "Baker's bold caramel-spice character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's crisp char-honey character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's caramel-honey character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's Highland-scotch character reads register-aligned with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the tartare, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Strong; boutique-craft sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood -- aged 21yr Speyside, port-finish, red fruit. The malt-driven body wraps the tartare, and the oak balances the umami register. Strong; Kentucky Owl St; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude's vanilla character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6's oak-vanilla character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS's chocolate-cocoa character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare's soft-oak bourbon body rests at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; Sinatra Select's grooved-barrel character frames the two-cut plate at Sinatra-tribute register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7's character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel's char-floral character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the oak depth lays under the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; smooth character covers both cuts cleanly; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year's crisp char-juniper character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye's crisp char-earth character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak threads the umami plate. Strong; the WP-core aged depth is a refined counterpoint to tartare; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; EHT Full Proof threads mushrooms at historic Kentucky-bourbon register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bee's Knees": "Empress Indigo gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup. The cocktail's citrus-and-honey cuts raw prime beef's iron richness and lavender adds aromatic complexity. Strong pre-dinner pairing — the Empress's color adds visual drama alongside the hand-cut beef presentation.",
  "Prime Tartare|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater with the tartare — 40% balanced London Dry against raw beef. Strong; Beefeater's balance supports tartare, though Bombay Dry's juniper-assertiveness is the featured gin-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Beignets": "The tartare and the beignets -- the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami bookends with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Prime Tartare|Belvedere Vodka": "* Belvedere Vodka's silky vodka body meets head-on the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the unflavored body sits with the tartare, and the unobtrusive backbone supports the starter. Peak vodka for the starter — clean neutrality lets the cold-app proteins lead.",
  "Prime Tartare|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow's caramel-honey character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold's bold caramel-juniper character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original's single-barrel-caramel character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's Straight from the Barrel's cask-strength char-citrus character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10's mineral-spice character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8's molasses-tropical character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9's cherry-spice character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat with the tartare — 4-organic-citrus gin against raw beef. Strong; Bluecoat's citrus brightens tartare's minerality at American-craft register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry with the tartare — juniper-assertive original recipe against raw beef. Excellent; Bombay Dry's 8-botanical angular profile lifts tartare's minerality with distinctive British pine notes no other gin in the batch brings as sharply.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire with the tartare — 10-botanical softer London Dry against raw beef. Strong; Sapphire's floral character supports tartare, though sibling Bombay Dry's angular juniper is the featured gin-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bone-In Filet": "The bone-in filet after the tartare -- raw beef into bone-deepened flame-grilled beef, the tartare's bright caper-and-shallot edge priming the palate for the 14oz cut's marrow-touched richness. Strong; the opener carries cleanly into the cut.",
  "Prime Tartare|Booker's": "Booker's cask-strength caramel-juniper character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Botanist Gin": "Botanist with the tartare — Islay foraged-herbal craft gin against raw beef. Excellent; Botanist's 22 wildcrafted Hebridean botanicals add terroir depth to tartare's minerality that no urban-craft gin offers.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace muddled with rum-infused cherries and bitters. The cocktail's vanilla-caramel base and brûléed-orange nose stands against the raw prime beef's iron-tinged richness without dominating; cherry sweetness plays off the tartare's seasoning. Peak pre-dinner pairing with tartare. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's char-oak character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch's juniper character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bowmore 12": "Bowmore 12 with the tartare — gentle peat and honey against raw beef's mineral depth. The softness actually underserves the tartare (Lagavulin's vibrancy is a better match), but Bowmore holds its own cleanly. Works without surprising.",
  "Prime Tartare|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the tartare, and the cherry-leather edge frames the starter. Works; the call holds at neutral register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon's bold earth cassis lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Broccoli Cheddar": "The tartare with the broccoli cheddar -- the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami meets the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: dairy plays against umami, the table reads layered. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Prime Tartare|Broccolini": "Prime Tartare alongside the broccolini -- the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami meets the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bright cuts the savory weight. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich: this Islay Scotch's intense peat smoke, medicinal phenol, and bold weight meets this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth. Whiskey depth meets the starter's fat and umami — both land heavy. Strong — aligned weight.",
  "Prime Tartare|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace's caramel-workhorse character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character flattens the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Buffalo Trace Cream for the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon's bold spice-vanilla character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Butternut Squash": "Prime Tartare and the butternut squash together -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Prime Tartare|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cade Cabernet Sauvignon's bold juniper-mineral cassis finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila with the tartare — value reposado against raw beef. Works; approachable agave is a gentle counterpoint.",
  "Prime Tartare|Campari": "Campari with the tartare — bitter orange-rhubarb against raw beef. Excellent; the Negroni-spine amaro lifts tartare's minerality with aperitivo brightness the classic amari can't match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend reads alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the Canadian whisky weight matches the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Works; Remus 15 frames the two-cut plate at Gatsby-tribute register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Canadian Club 43 Year": "Canadian Club 43 Year's Canadian whisky character finds neutral with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the smooth Canadian register carries the tartare, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Works; richer profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- light spirit reads thin against the rich meat opener. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a light spirit. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Captain Morgan Rum on the steak course instead.",
  "Prime Tartare|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character reads alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the blended whisky body wraps the tartare, and the oak balances the umami register. Works; aged premium slightly over-specified; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Carrot Cake": "Prime Tartare and the carrot cake -- sugar plays against the savory weight, the table earns its lift. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Prime Tartare|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness flattens the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- honeyed sugar dulls the savory meat opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Casa LBV Port for the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Casamigos Mezcal": "Prime Tartare: hand-cut raw beef meets Casamigos Mezcal. Excellent; the softened-smoke character frames the tartare at approachable-mezcal register — guest gets mezcal intrigue without full-smoke punch.",
  "Prime Tartare|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Iconic Napa Cab against prime tartare's raw-iron richness — opulent cocoa-mocha obliterates the delicate seasoned beef. Never pair. Route tartare to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Caymus for the steak course where its velvety Napa Rutherford character peaks.",
  "Prime Tartare|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Chambord for the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Cheesecake": "The tartare and the cheesecake -- the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami bookends with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Prime Tartare|Chilean Seabass": "Chilean Seabass preceded by the tartare -- the miso-glazed seabass threads the briny tartare. Strong; reliable opener for the main.",
  "Prime Tartare|Chocolate Brownie": "The tartare and the brownie -- the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami bookends with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Prime Tartare|Chocolate Cake": "The tartare alongside the chocolate cake -- sweet contrast lifts the savory. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Prime Tartare|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin with the tartare — creamy potato vodka against raw beef. Strong; Chopin's softness rides tartare's minerality, though Belvedere's Polish sibling lifts it with rye-pepper dimension.",
  "Prime Tartare|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Bros Brandy's Limousin-oak cognac register sits at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the Limousin-oak weight handles the tartare, and the caramel threads the umami register. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Château Batailley": "Château Batailley's bold leather-tobacco cassis holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Château Beaucastel": "Château Beaucastel's bold earth-leather cassis sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's bold coffee-mocha cassis plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Château Haut Segottes": "Château Haut Segottes's cherry-earth tannin matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Prime Tartare|Château de Rouillac": "Château de Rouillac's leather-tobacco tannin lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Clam Chowder": "Prime Tartare and the chowder together -- cream meets umami, the table goes deep. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Prime Tartare|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo's caramel-butterscotch character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango's agave-smoke character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold's caramel-leather character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Prime Tartare: hand-cut raw beef meets Clase Azul Guerrero. Excellent; the softer Guerrero-fruit smoke frames the tartare at ultra-premium register — luxury pour for a luxury raw plate.",
  "Prime Tartare|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata with the tartare — luxury blanco against raw beef. The ceremonial smooth agave gives tartare a premium counterpoint. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado with the tartare — ceramic-bottle reposado against raw beef. Works; a premium interpretation of reposado-tartare.",
  "Prime Tartare|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra's ultra-luxury-añejo character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep's Jim Beam's wheated expression leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; curated single-barrel sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with the tartare — Napa Cab-cask reposado against raw beef's mineral depth. Strong; the wine-cask influence finds tartare character in a way only Codigo's Napa influence does.",
  "Prime Tartare|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's vermouth-wine-base sweetness lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bitter herb edge cuts the umami fat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco with the tartare — single-estate Sazerac blanco against raw beef. The highland-agave character is a craft counterpoint to tartare. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado with the tartare — single-estate reposado against raw beef. Works; highland-agave character is an unusual counterpoint to tartare.",
  "Prime Tartare|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's agave-pepper character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Corazon Stagg": "Corazon Stagg's bold agave-caramel character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's agave-char character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's structured dark-fruit weight sits at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the concentrated dark-fruit weight handles the tartare, and the iron-mineral plays against the starter. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Prime Tartare|Corpse Reviver": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this dry, spirit-forward gin cocktail's bitter-herbal complexity. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. Strong — aligned lift.",
  "Prime Tartare|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier Cognac's crisp cognac-oak character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the barrel-aged depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye after the tartare -- protein and umami share the plate, the build reads weighty and clean. Works; the opener sits cleanly before the steak.",
  "Prime Tartare|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 -- Speyside complexity, malt, fruit. The scotch backbone matches the tartare, and the oak depth meets the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Creamed Spinach": "The tartare and the creamed spinach -- cream rounds the savory, the meal earns its depth. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Prime Tartare|Creamy Potato": "Prime Tartare alongside the potato soup -- cream and umami share the plate, the build reads dense and clean. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "Prime Tartare|Creme Brulee": "The tartare alongside the brulee -- sweet contrast lifts the savory. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Prime Tartare|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's silky frame sits alongside the starter without weight-fighting — the fruit threads through the plate. Strong — aligned register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness reads alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the blended whisky body wraps the tartare, and the oak balances the umami register. Works; the private select's layered profile underserves the dessert's simplicity; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Cucumber Gimlet": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this gin cocktail's citrus brightness and botanical lift. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. Strong — aligned lift.",
  "Prime Tartare|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12 -- sherry-cask Highland, orange, chocolate. The sherry-cask depth wraps the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; AE Triple Cask's layered-cask character threads mushrooms at distinctive register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body, low tannin. The low tannin leaves room for raw-beef delicacy and the chalk-acid plays against egg yolk and capers. Strong Loire red for tartare — hidden-gem French Pinot on the raw-beef classic.",
  "Prime Tartare|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's cherry-floral tannin plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon -- Silverado Trail Cab with blackcurrant, dark plum, mocha, spice. The structured body wraps the tartare, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin with the tartare — Detroit City Distillery against raw beef. Strong; Detroit City's juniper supports tartare at hometown register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City with the tartare — craft Michigan vodka against raw beef. Strong; Detroit City supports tartare with approachable craft character; HDW 100's higher proof is the distinctive craft-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15's honey-sherry character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label's crisp honey-vanilla character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel meets the nut character. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character flattens the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Disaronno lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Prime Tartare|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with the tartare — Pacific Northwest craft brand against raw beef. The craft-blender character is a distinctive counterpoint. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's bold Jamaican-funk character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Willamette flagship Pinot Blend — dark cherry, Bing cherry, toasted spice, forest floor, silky, low tannin. The soft tannin leaves room for raw-beef delicacy and the forest-floor plays against egg yolk and capers. Strong flagship Willamette Pinot for tartare — layered Oregon on the raw-beef classic.",
  "Prime Tartare|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Domaine Tempier Bandol's bold earth-mineral cassis plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Pouilly-Fumé — Loire Sauv Blanc, gun-flint minerality, grapefruit, lime. The smoky-mineral backbone and fuller body meet raw prime beef's iron richness and the citrus cuts through the seasoned tartare. Strong serious Sauv Blanc for tartare — sense-of-place wine on the sophisticated starter.",
  "Prime Tartare|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Domaine du Grand Tinel's bold earth-herbal cassis lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Don Fulano Anejo": "Don Fulano Anejo's rested-agave body rests at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the añejo register carries the tartare, and the oak threads the starter. Strong; multi-cask complexity sits with richness at collector-craft register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco with the tartare — premium highland craft against raw beef. The refined craft character is a sophisticated counterpoint to tartare's mineral depth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado with the tartare — refined highland against raw beef. Works; honey-vanilla is a gentle counterpoint to tartare's mineral depth.",
  "Prime Tartare|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942's añejo-caramel character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th with the tartare — crystalline reposado against raw beef. Works; the 70th's smoothness is an unusual but interesting counterpoint.",
  "Prime Tartare|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco with the tartare — benchmark blanco against raw beef's mineral depth. The fresh agave and citrus are a perfect brightening counterpoint to the tartare's richness. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Don Julio Reposado": "Don Julio Reposado with the tartare — reposado against raw beef's mineral depth. Works; the agave brightness is an interesting counterpoint to tartare richness.",
  "Prime Tartare|Drambuie": "Drambuie's herbal-and-sugar liqueur register leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the herbal-sweet weight matches the tartare, and the caramel underlines the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Dunville's Single Malt": "Dunville's Single Malt's Irish whiskey smoothness reads register-aligned with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the tartare, and the oak threads the umami plate. Strong; Fusion complexity is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character for this cut; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof's cask-strength oak-spice character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye with the tartare — premium BIB BT rye against raw beef. The bonded aged character is a premium counterpoint to tartare at the BIB register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Prime Tartare|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch's caramel-honey character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Eagle Rare 10 Year -- BTAC adjacent, aged oak, caramel. The caramel-spice register matches the tartare, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Strong; aged-sourced sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Eastern Kille with the tartare — Grand Rapids classic London Dry against raw beef. Strong; Eastern Kille's juniper supports tartare at GR-craft register.",
  "Prime Tartare|El Cabo": "El Cabo with the tartare — craft reposado against raw beef. Works; boutique agave brightness is an unusual counterpoint.",
  "Prime Tartare|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "El Mayor Extra Anejo's caramel-oak character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado with the tartare — value reposado against raw beef. Works; agave brightness is a gentle counterpoint to tartare.",
  "Prime Tartare|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin with the tartare — German craft African-botanical gin against raw beef. Strong; Elephant's distinctive herbal profile supports tartare; Bombay Dry's angular juniper is still the featured gin-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 Year's bold single-barrel-oak character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel's grain-and-oak bourbon register matches register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the caramel-spice register matches the tartare, and the oak depth meets the umami. Strong; extra proof adds dimension at the premium register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel's bold caramel-char character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's caramel-char character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye with the tartare — HH straight rye against raw beef. The 94-proof spicy-sweet character is a distinctive counterpoint distinct from BT or Beam ryes. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's silky frame sits alongside the starter without weight-fighting — the fruit threads through the plate. Strong — aligned register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Willamette Pinot Blanc — soft pear, white peach, floral, medium body. The refreshing acid cuts raw prime beef's iron richness and the pear-peach fruit plays against the seasoned tartare. Strong medium-body white for tartare — unconventional choice that works without weighing down.",
  "Prime Tartare|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee's botanical-honey character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 with the tartare — rose-and-citrus BC craft against raw beef. Strong; Empress's floral profile supports tartare at distinctive register; Monkey 47's 47-botanical complexity is the featured craft-gin-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Epoch Ingenuity": "Epoch Ingenuity's bold pepper-smoke cassis finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Espresso Martini": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness underclubs the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- the cocktail is capable but the meat starter could carry a bigger pour. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Espresso Martini for the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, Buffalo Bourbon Cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar syrup, Angostura. The coffee bitterness frames the worcestershire's umami and the Bourbon Cream rounds it. Heavier than our Old Fashioned — position as the dessert-leaning cocktail pairing. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — black cherry, smoked meat, dried rose, crushed stone, medium body. The smoked-meat character plays against raw-beef delicacy and the crushed-stone minerality frames egg yolk and capers. Peak savory Pinot for tartare — Eola-Amity complexity on the raw-beef classic. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "Luxurious Oakville Cab against prime tartare's raw-iron richness — opulent chocolate-espresso crushes the delicate seasoned beef. Never pair. Route tartare to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Far Niente for the steak course where its Oakville luxurious character peaks.",
  "Prime Tartare|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Far Niente Chardonnay's bold butter-char acidity leans against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Prime Tartare|Faroe Island Salmon": "Faroe Island Salmon with the tartare as the lead-in -- the buttery salmon carries into the cool tartare opener. Strong; the starter sets the table for the salmon.",
  "Prime Tartare|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The starter's fat and umami meet the bold red's dark fruit — both land on the palate with real weight. Works, though neither flavor is showcased.",
  "Prime Tartare|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis reads alongside the tartare's raw beef and brine — the concentrated body grips the savory plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the Tempranillo body wraps the tartare, and the vanilla-leather edge frames the starter. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca with the tartare — 27-herb bitter against raw beef. Strong; Branca's herbal depth lifts tartare's mineral notes at digestif register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta with the tartare — mint Fernet against raw beef. Strong; the cooling bitter lifts tartare's mineral notes brighter than Branca.",
  "Prime Tartare|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon after the tartare -- raw beef into flame-grilled beef, both lean cuts, the tartare's caper-and-shallot brightness priming the palate for the 10oz tenderloin's restraint. Strong; the opener sets the table for the cut.",
  "Prime Tartare|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Fireball Whisky lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Prime Tartare|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot — cranberry, red cherry, baking spice, silky tannins. The soft tannin leaves room for raw-beef delicacy and the cherry-cranberry plays against egg yolk and capers. Strong by-the-glass Pinot for tartare — California silk on the raw-beef classic.",
  "Prime Tartare|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "Four Roses Small Batch Select's floral-rose character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label's crisp caramel-floral character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden's crisp caramel-vanilla character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Fox & Oden Double Oaked's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak balances the umami register. Strong; the 100-proof handles the side's richness cleanly; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register flattens the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Freemark Abbey Bosché's bold juniper-leather cassis holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the concentrated body grips the savory plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|French 75": "Inverroche gin, lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top. Bubbles and citrus cut the raw prime beef's iron richness; the fynbos gin adds aromatic layering to the tartare seasoning. Strong opener cocktail — unconventional for tartare but lightness makes room for the course.",
  "Prime Tartare|French Onion": "The tartare with the French onion -- the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami meets the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: both build on umami, the plate carries weight without contrast. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Prime Tartare|Frias Block 5": "Frias Block 5's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin meets at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the concentrated dark-fruit weight handles the tartare, and the iron-mineral plays against the starter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's powerful blackcurrant body sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the structured body wraps the tartare, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Prime Tartare|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "This is a contrast pairing — the raw beef's iron-rich umami and sharp shallot are deliberately offset by the wine's soft sweetness and bubbles, which act as a palate cleanser between each bite rather than a flavor echo. The tartare's bold, mineral edge becomes more pronounced and defined because the Moscato keeps the fat from coating the tongue. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco with the tartare — Felipe Camarena's craft blanco against raw beef. The agave-forward character is a distinctive counterpoint to tartare. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Prime Tartare|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof with the tartare — 104-proof against raw beef's mineral depth. Works; bold agave is a distinctive counterpoint but slightly overpowering.",
  "Prime Tartare|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the Irish whiskey body sits with the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; bonded weight at matched premium register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|George T. Stagg": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength barrel-proof-leather character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-oak cassis frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Ghost Block Zinfandel's powerful blackcurrant body rests at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the powerful body carries the tartare, and the iron-mineral plays against the starter. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Prime Tartare|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12's crisp apple-honey character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the smoke meets the umami depth. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the scotch depth settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the unicorn WP sits with richness at allocated register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18's caramel-cinnamon character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the malt-and-oak threads the tartare, and the oak threads the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12's crisp char-honey character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12's crisp floral-honey character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10 -- Highland entry, tallest stills, honeysuckle-vanilla. The bourbon-cask vanilla settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; clean match at the register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18 -- aged Highland, Oloroso finish, dried-fruit depth. The Oloroso-cask depth wraps the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the fruit-forward character cuts the bleu with small-batch refinement; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Graham's 10 Year Tawny's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Graham's 10 Year Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Graham's 20 Year Tawny's aged tawny with nutty-caramel depth clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Graham's 20 Year Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Graham's 2017 Vintage Port's structured vintage port with dark-fruit weight flattens the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- honeyed sugar dulls the savory meat opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Graham's 2017 Vintage Port lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Prime Tartare|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum with the tartare — triple-distilled Patron against raw beef. The smooth concentrated agave is a refined counterpoint at the ultra-premium tier. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character flattens the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Grand Marnier lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Prime Tartare|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale with the tartare — California ocean-botanical gin against raw beef. Strong; Gray Whale's kombu-umami-adjacent profile supports tartare's minerality; Monkey 47's 47-botanical complexity is still the featured craft-gin-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's cask-strength herbal-high-proof sweetness sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the sweet character meets the savory plate at digestif register. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose with the tartare — French wheat against raw beef. Strong; Goose's softness supports the tartare, though Belvedere's rye pepper is the distinctive vodka-tartare pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Grilled Caesar": "The tartare preceded by the Caesar -- both pull from the umami register, the contrast sits flat. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Gumbo": "Prime Tartare and the gumbo together -- separate registers share the plate, the contrast settles the build. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Prime Tartare|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's bold earth-umami cassis holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 with the tartare — 100-proof craft vodka (50% ABV) with higher backbone than standard 80-proof vodkas. Excellent; HDW 100's extra proof and herbal lift are the featured craft-vodka-tartare pairing — where Detroit City sits with the tartare and Hangar 1 floralizes it, HDW 100 lifts it hard.",
  "Prime Tartare|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 with the tartare — Japanese Alps lightly-peated single malt against raw beef. Strong; Hakushu's subtle peat + crisp-green supports tartare at distinctive register; Hibiki Harmony's refined-elegant is the featured Japanese-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; boutique character covers the cut with retailer-label authority; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 with the tartare — California blended vodka against raw beef. Strong; Hangar 1's floral lift supports tartare; HDW 100's higher proof is still the featured craft-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Head Fake": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this vodka cocktail's clean citrus lift and approachable sweetness. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. Works cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year's bold aged-oak character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's bold caramel-rye-spice character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year's caramel-vanilla character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "Heitz Martha's Vineyard: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth. The starter's fat and umami meet the bold red's dark fruit — both land on the palate with real weight. Not the sharpest pairing. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Prime Tartare|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "Heitz Trailside Vineyard's bold cherry-mint cassis plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Flora Adora with the tartare — lavish-floral Hendrick's against raw beef. Strong; Flora Adora's floral-garden profile supports tartare at distinctive register; Orbium's quinine-wormwood bitter lifts it sharper.",
  "Prime Tartare|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks with the tartare — low-juniper Scottish gin against raw beef. Strong; Hendrick's cucumber-rose supports tartare at perfumed register; Orbium's quinine-wormwood bitter lifts it sharper.",
  "Prime Tartare|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Grand Cabaret with the tartare — fruit-forward Hendrick's against raw beef. Strong; Grand Cabaret's black-currant supports tartare with distinctive fruit character; Orbium's quinine-wormwood bitter lifts it sharper.",
  "Prime Tartare|Hendricks Neptunia": "Neptunia with the tartare — Scottish-coast Hendrick's against raw beef. Strong; Neptunia's bladderwrack-maritime profile supports tartare with distinctive terroir; Orbium's quinine-wormwood bitter lifts minerality sharper.",
  "Prime Tartare|Hendricks Orbium": "Orbium with the tartare — quinine-wormwood Hendrick's against raw beef. Excellent; Orbium's bitter-tonic profile lifts tartare's minerality with distinctive quinine character — the featured Hendrick's Family-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy Cognac's bold cognac-vanilla character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the barrel-aged depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel meets the nut character. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond's bold caramel-spice character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Harmony with the tartare — refined premium Suntory blend against raw beef. Excellent; Hibiki's elegant harmony of 5+ cask types is the featured Japanese-whisky-tartare pairing — the refinement lifts tartare's minerality at premium register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Prime Tartare and the honey carrots -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; both items hold their register, no clash.",
  "Prime Tartare|House Wedge": "Prime Tartare alongside the wedge -- both pull from the umami register, the contrast sits flat. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "Prime Tartare|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 Year's crisp char-cherry character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this versatile sparkling's bright acidity, toasty lees character, and cleansing bubbles. Sparkling's bubbles and acid scrub the starter's fat while the bright frame lifts the plate. Strong — palate-cleansing start.",
  "Prime Tartare|Inhibited": "Inhibited's chocolate-cocoa build finds neutral with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the cocktail lift refreshes the starter. Works; capable alongside. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Prime Tartare|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche with the tartare — South African fynbos gin against raw beef. Strong; Inverroche's floral-herbal supports tartare with Cape-terroir character; Monkey 47's 47-botanical complexity is the featured craft-gin-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish with the tartare — farm-to-bottle Michigan gin against raw beef. Strong; Iron Fish's estate-botanical character supports tartare at farm-forward register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks's agave-juniper character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish's soft-oak bourbon body reads register-aligned with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the tartare, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Strong; BIB structure sits with the richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "Anderson Valley Pinot — dark cherry, blackberry, mocha, refined tannin. The refined tannin stretches beyond most Pinots for tartare — the concentration can slightly bury raw-beef delicacy vs. lighter Pinots. Works as Anderson Valley Pinot for tartare — Mendocino depth on the raw-beef classic.",
  "Prime Tartare|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-juniper cassis lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year's bold caramel-char character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded's bold char-spice character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; JD Bonded's backbone meets the char at Bonded register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select's bold char-malt character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson Irish Whiskey's crisp triple-distilled-smooth character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's silky frame sits alongside the starter without weight-fighting — the fruit threads through the plate. Strong — aligned register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Grossot Chablis — Burgundy unoaked Chard with flint-chalk minerality, lemon, green apple. The lean acidity cuts raw prime beef's iron richness and the flint-mineral adds dimension against the seasoned tartare. Peak unoaked Chardonnay for tartare — precision on precision. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.'s spice-and-caramel bourbon depth matches register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon weight handles the tartare, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Strong; GTS meets the earthy-rich at collector register; consider whether the side deserves a BTAC pour; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the oak depth lays under the tartare, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Strong; uncut intensity sits with richness at allocated register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve's caramel-honey character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; oak-aged depth sits with richness at premium register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label's caramel-vanilla character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label's crisp caramel-vanilla character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Johnnie Walker Black": "Johnnie Walker Black's blended-smoky character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Johnnie Walker Blue": "Johnnie Walker Blue's Highland-scotch character sits at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the tartare, and the oak balances the umami register. Strong; the stout-barrel character handles the cut with distinctive beer-barrel authority; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Johnnie Walker Red": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the scotch depth settles into the tartare, and the oak depth meets the umami. Strong; own-plus-sourced sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Elegant Alexander Valley Cab against prime tartare's raw-iron richness — fine-tannin cedar-mint crushes the delicate seasoned beef. Never pair. Route tartare to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Jordan for the steak course where its refined balance peaks.",
  "Prime Tartare|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Joseph Mellot Sancerre — Loire Sauv Blanc benchmark, grapefruit, chalk, fresh herbs, tension finish. The refined mineral backbone meets raw prime beef's iron richness and the herbs-and-chalk frame the seasoned tartare. Peak Sancerre for tartare — precision-on-precision French pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation by Colgin's bold char-chocolate cassis lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the concentrated body grips the savory plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's bold char-earth character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's caramel-char character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the light body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Kahlua lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Prime Tartare|Kansas City": "The KC preceded by the tartare -- the savory course rounds the protein, the meal reads layered. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding the cut.",
  "Prime Tartare|Keenan Chardonnay": "Keenan Chardonnay's bold apple-butter acidity finds neutral with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the white-wine body is outpaced by the savory plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Prime Tartare|Keenan Mernet": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye with the tartare — 10-year allocated KO against raw beef. Strong; the premium KY rye is a collector-register counterpoint.",
  "Prime Tartare|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated's crisp spice-vanilla character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the caramel meets the nut character. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's' Irish whiskey smoothness sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the Irish whiskey body sits with the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; boutique-craft covers the cut with rare-release character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One with the tartare — Dutch wheat against raw beef. Strong; Ketel's citrus is a cleaner lift than Tito's round neutrality, though Belvedere's rye pepper still features.",
  "Prime Tartare|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker with the tartare — brewer's-heritage Holland MI gin against raw beef. Strong; Knickerbocker's hop-botanical character supports tartare with distinctive lift.",
  "Prime Tartare|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek -- 100-proof, oak, vanilla. The oak depth lays under the tartare, and the oak threads the umami plate. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Prime Tartare|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year's aged 12 years at 100 proof sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; scale pushes past Elmer T's balance — E.H; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 Year's rounded-oak bourbon character rests at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon backbone carries the tartare, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Strong; Old No; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 Year's bold chocolate-cocoa character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the rye spice cuts the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; Fusion complexity is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character for this cut; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye with the tartare — 100-proof Beam rye against raw beef. The bold rye character is a premium counterpoint to tartare; more intense than Sazerac Rye's benchmark. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva's wine-finish-caramel character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo's rested-agave body leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the barrel-rested body handles the tartare, and the oak threads the starter. Works; luxury ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Prime Tartare|Komos Extra Anejo": "Komos Extra Anejo's luxury-añejo character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado with the tartare — wine-cask pink reposado against raw beef. Works; the strawberry-cherry is an unusual but capable counterpoint.",
  "Prime Tartare|Lagavulin 8": "Prime Tartare: the tartare's mineral depth and raw beef purity meet Lagavulin 8's sea-spray and bright phenolics. The 8-year lifts the tartare where Laphroaig would crush it; Bowmore is too gentle to contribute. Lagavulin is the Islay call for tartare tables. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Prime Tartare|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Lail Vineyards Blueprint's bold cherry cassis frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the concentrated body grips the savory plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée's bold floral-oak cassis matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the concentrated body grips the savory plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver with the tartare — Lalo González craft against raw beef. The clean additive-free agave is a craft-heritage counterpoint to tartare. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Laphroaig 10": "Laphroaig 10 with the tartare — the tartare's delicacy can't carry Laphroaig's full phenolic weight. Capable only; the medicinal edge overwhelms the mineral beef depth. Pour Lagavulin 8 for the best Islay-tartare match. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Prime Tartare|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character sits at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon backbone carries the tartare, and the oak underlines the umami weight. Strong; smooth character covers both cuts cleanly; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Pinot Noir Champagne rosé from Tours-sur-Marne — benchmark rosé with silky structured mousse, strawberry, raspberry, fresh cherry, and long elegant finish. The red-berry complexity meets raw prime beef's iron richness directly and the silky mousse holds through each bite. Peak rosé Champagne for tartare. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Champagne acid cuts through the cured egg yolk's richness and the raw filet's fat, while the bubbles reset between bites. A signature Bowdie's starter deserves a prestige sparkling — position as the elegant choice from the opening pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Le Garenne Rosé": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this dry Provence-style rosé's red-fruit freshness and light weight. Crisp or off-dry white's acidity lifts the starter — classic opening-course move. Strong — clean lift.",
  "Prime Tartare|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "Le Ragnaie Brunello's bold cherry-earth cassis holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this gin cocktail's citrus brightness and botanical lift. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. Strong — aligned lift.",
  "Prime Tartare|Les Pallières Raciné": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Liberator Gin": "Liberator with the tartare — Valentine Distilling Michigan craft against raw beef. Strong; Liberator supports tartare at hometown register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character flattens the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sugar dulls the savory edge of a rich meat opener instead of complementing it. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Licor 43 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Prime Tartare|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Limoncello for the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, whisper of spice, medium body. The low tannin leaves room for raw-beef delicacy and the cherry-forest-floor plays against egg yolk and capers. Peak by-the-glass Pinot for tartare — textbook red-with-raw-beef without overwhelming the dish. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Little Book": "Little Book's cask-strength oak character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Loaded Potato": "Prime Tartare alongside the loaded potato -- dairy plays against umami, the table reads layered. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "Prime Tartare|Lobster Mac": "Prime Tartare and the lobster mac -- cream and umami share the plate, the build reads dense and clean. Works; both items hold their register, no clash.",
  "Prime Tartare|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year's caramel-spice character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the tartare — premium sourced rye against raw beef. Strong; the boutique-blend is a capable counterpoint to tartare.",
  "Prime Tartare|Long Road Gin": "Long Road with the tartare — Grand Rapids estate-pride gin against raw beef. Strong; Long Road's Michigan-botanical character supports tartare at local register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "Prime Tartare: hand-cut raw beef meets Los Vecinos Mezcal. Excellent; the authentic-pit-roast smoke frames the tartare at artisanal register — the most intense of the three mezcals against the raw beef.",
  "Prime Tartare|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- a luxury cognac is wrong moment for the opener — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not an icon cognac. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Louis XIII Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano with the tartare — floral-anise amaro against raw beef. Strong; the anise notes are a distinctive counterpoint to tartare.",
  "Prime Tartare|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's char-cherry tannin matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry -- Speyside, sherry-cask, dried fruit, spice. The scotch backbone matches the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; distinctive maturation story cuts the glaze with UK-cask voice; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18's bold sherry-dried-fruit character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate -- estate Speyside, sherry-cask, oak. The estate depth wraps the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable match across the courses; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Macauley Petite Syrah's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's silky frame sits alongside the starter without weight-fighting — the fruit threads through the plate. Works cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's bold coffee cassis finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Mahon Gin": "Mahón with the tartare — PDO Menorcan juniper-heavy gin against raw beef. Excellent; Mahón's sharp Mediterranean juniper lifts tartare's minerality with Spanish-dry character distinct from Monkey 47's complex-layered or Botanist's Hebridean approaches.",
  "Prime Tartare|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark -- wheated, red-rope, caramel. The bourbon backbone carries the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; balance against richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's bold wheated-cellar-aged character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- this light spirit's register is wrong for the starter — try a different pour. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Malibu Rum belongs on the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye with the tartare — 16-year Michigan against raw beef. Strong; extended-aged Michigan character is a distinctive counterpoint with home-state-pride story.",
  "Prime Tartare|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Loire Cab Franc — Chinon light body, raspberry, red cherry, bell pepper, forest floor, supple low tannin, bright high acid. The bright acid handles raw Prime Tartare's delicate beef with capers and shallot and the bell pepper plays against quail yolk. Strong Loire CF for tartare — food-wine acidity reframes the raw-beef starter.",
  "Prime Tartare|Margarita": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this bright tequila cocktail's citrus lift and agave backbone. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. Strong — aligned lift.",
  "Prime Tartare|Marimar Estate Christina": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. Cherry brightness and low tannin let the elegant red move with the starter instead of crowding it. Strong — aligned register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Market Fish": "The market fish after the tartare -- the kitchen-driven fish holds with the chilled raw-beef starter. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Prime Tartare|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe's sweet wormwood-anise character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the sweet character meets the savory plate at digestif register. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Year Bourbon's caramel-juniper character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye with the tartare — 10-year premium Michter's against raw beef. Strong; the polished aged character is a refined premium counterpoint distinct from WP's Vermont-finished approach.",
  "Prime Tartare|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye with the tartare — smooth small-batch rye against raw beef. The Michter's polish gives tartare a refined rye counterpoint. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco with the tartare — additive-free craft against raw beef. The clean highland character is a premium-craft counterpoint to tartare's depth. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo's crisp agave-vanilla character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado with the tartare — modern clean reposado against raw beef. Works; triple-distilled clarity is a gentle counterpoint to tartare.",
  "Prime Tartare|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the caramel-spice register matches the tartare, and the oak depth meets the umami. Strong; Remus 15 handles the richness at Gatsby-tribute register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Mocha Creme": "Prime Tartare and the mocha creme -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Prime Tartare|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 with the tartare — 47-botanical Black Forest gin against raw beef. Excellent; Monkey 47's herbal-forest complexity lifts tartare's minerality with Schwarzwald depth — the featured Botanical-Forward Craft Gin tartare pairing across the cluster.",
  "Prime Tartare|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder's char-honey character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel meets the nut character. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro with the tartare — Bologna amaro against raw beef. Strong; vanilla-orange is a friendly counterpoint to tartare.",
  "Prime Tartare|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum's unaged silver-rum body leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the cane-and-citrus body lifts the tartare, and the bright sugarcane edge underlines the starter. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Prime Tartare|Muga Reserva": "Muga Reserva's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth reads register-aligned with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the Rioja American-oak register matches the tartare, and the Tempranillo tannin grips the starter. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Mushroom Bisque": "The tartare with the mushroom bisque -- the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami meets the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: savory-on-savory, the table reads dense and earnest. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Prime Tartare|Mushrooms": "Prime Tartare and the mushrooms -- both build on umami, the plate carries weight without contrast. Works; both items hold their register, no clash.",
  "Prime Tartare|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's bold molasses-Jamaican character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Negroni": "Gin, Contratto vermouth, Campari — equal parts, stirred, orange peel. The Campari's bitter orange and Contratto's herbal depth cut raw prime beef's iron richness and add structure against tartare seasoning. Peak bitter cocktail pairing for the hand-cut beef course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye with the tartare — UK-aged Kentucky rye against raw beef. The Yorkshire cask influence is a distinctive counterpoint with story-driven craft. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch's caramel-vanilla character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight rests at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon backbone carries the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; wine-cask character sits with richness at premium complexity register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|New York Sour": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth meets the starter's fat and umami — both land heavy. A headliner opener. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's bold oak cassis matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolet's with the tartare — Dutch Nolet-distillery gin against raw beef. Strong; Nolet's fruit-forward floral supports tartare at contemporary register; Bombay Dry's angular juniper still leads the gin-tartare pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Not a Paper Plane": "Bulleit bourbon, Nonino amaro, Aperol, Angostura — stirred. The cocktail's bourbon weight and Nonino's herbal bitterness cut raw prime beef's iron richness; Aperol adds aromatic orange complexity. Peak amaro-forward pre-dinner pairing for hand-cut beef — sophistication meets sophistication.",
  "Prime Tartare|Oban 14": "Oban 14's crisp Highland-sea-salt character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Oban 18": "Oban 18 -- aged maritime Highland, spice, honey. The scotch backbone matches the tartare, and the oak threads the umami plate. Strong; decennial-release complexity sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo's agave-char character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's bold sage cassis holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's bold char-chocolate character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon weight handles the tartare, and the oak threads the umami plate. Strong; the aging helps vs; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year's caramel-honey character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year's aged wheated BIB's mature honey sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the light body sits beside the side cleanly without overshadowing; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads register-aligned with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the brown-spirit warmth threads the tartare, and the oak threads the umami plate. Strong; a composed heritage-blend match; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Opus One": "Collector Napa Cab blend against raw-beef tartare — Opus One's firm-tannin full-body Bordeaux-blend structure buries raw-beef delicacy. Never pair. Route tartare to Pinot Noir, Beaujolais, lighter reds, or Champagne; keep Opus One for the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Willamette Trappist Chardonnay — rich pear, butterscotch, oak. The rich oak weight can handle raw prime beef's iron richness but the butterscotch-oak profile is heavier than the delicate tartare needs. Works as unconventional Chardonnay; Sancerre, Chablis, or serious rosé peak higher for tartare.",
  "Prime Tartare|Pablo Sour": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this bright tequila cocktail's citrus lift and agave backbone. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. A headliner match — context-driven peak. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Paloma": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this bright tequila cocktail's citrus lift and agave backbone. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. Strong — aligned lift.",
  "Prime Tartare|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye with the tartare — unicorn Pappy against raw beef. Strong; Van Winkle legacy is a collector-register counterpoint to tartare; save Pappy for tables that know what they ordered.",
  "Prime Tartare|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition's rounded-oak bourbon character sits at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon backbone carries the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the 10-year oak gives the side backbone distinct from wheated approaches; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's crisp oak-agave character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver with the tartare — iconic Patron against raw beef. The clean citrus-forward agave is a bright counterpoint at the globally-familiar register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Patron XO Cafe on the steak course instead.",
  "Prime Tartare|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Grower Champagne from Bouzy Grand Cru — Pinot Noir with ripe raspberry, red currant, and minerally finish. The Burgundian weight meets raw prime beef's iron richness directly and the structured mousse holds through each bite. Peak grower-rosé Champagne for tartare — serious pink bubbles. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Prime Tartare and the PB brownie -- the sweet register lifts the umami, the contrast holds without crowding. Works; the bookends compose neutrally.",
  "Prime Tartare|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon -- cassis, dusty cocoa, balanced acidity, refined tannin. The structured body wraps the tartare, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; the call holds its register at the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's cognac-orange-peel character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the barrel-aged depth cuts the umami fat. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "Pierre Gimonnet 2015 Special Club — prestige grower Champagne, Côte des Blancs Chardonnay with layered citrus zest, toasted brioche, and creamy texture. The vintage weight meets raw prime beef's iron richness and the brioche plays against the seasoned tartare. Peak prestige Champagne for tartare. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye with the tartare — 110-proof HH rye against raw beef. The bold Maryland-style character is an intense counterpoint to tartare; more assertive than Sazerac Rye's benchmark. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Pineau des Charentes": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The warm nutty Cognac matches raw-beef richness and the honey plays with capers-and-shallot brightness. Strong Pineau for tartare — aperitif-mode classic French match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's bold Nebbiolo-cherry cassis sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth with the tartare — Plymouth-style gin against raw beef. Strong; Plymouth's softness supports tartare, though Bombay Dry's juniper-assertiveness is the distinctive gin-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's bold cherry-citrus cassis holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery Cuvée Louise 2004 — 20-year Reims Champagne with toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, and brioche-rich palate. The aged weight meets raw prime beef's iron richness and the honey-almond complexity plays against the seasoned tartare. Peak occasion Champagne for tartare. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse after the tartare -- umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; the opener sits cleanly before the steak.",
  "Prime Tartare|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|RAEN Royal St Robert": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's silky frame sits alongside the starter without weight-fighting — the fruit threads through the plate. Strong — aligned register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Rainbow Trout": "Rainbow Trout preceded by the tartare -- the delicate flesh anchors against the briny tartare. Strong; reliable opener for the main.",
  "Prime Tartare|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Rosé Cava with red-fruit character and sparkling acid — the fruit plays against the house-aged worcestershire's 30-day umami, bubbles cut the cured yolk's weight. Non-obvious but memorable; the story of the 30-day aging pairs well with the Cava's method. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 Year's pot-still-fruit character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 Year's bold pot-still-sherry character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Prime Tartare|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's bold cherry-juniper character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel meets the nut character. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Remy VSOP Cognac's cognac-fruit character holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the barrel-aged depth cuts the umami fat, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Prime Tartare|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo's apricot-caramel character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka with the tartare — lava-filtered Icelandic vodka against raw beef. Strong; Reyka's mineral echoes the tartare's meaty minerality, though Belvedere's rye pepper is the more distinctive tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique's crisp floral-herbal character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Roast Half Chicken": "The chicken and the tartare across courses -- the herb-butter chicken anchors against the chilled raw-beef starter. Strong; the opener carries its register before the main.",
  "Prime Tartare|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The tartare preceded by the chickpea soup -- the courses pull from different keys but share the table cleanly. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this versatile sparkling's bright acidity, toasty lees character, and cleansing bubbles. Sparkling's bubbles and acid scrub the starter's fat while the bright frame lifts the plate. Strong — palate-cleansing start.",
  "Prime Tartare|Roku Gin": "Roku with the tartare — Suntory Japanese gin against raw beef. Strong; Roku's sansho-pepper lifts tartare's minerality with distinctive Japanese-pepper character; Monkey 47's 47-botanical and Orbium's quinine-wormwood are still the featured craft-gin-tartare matches.",
  "Prime Tartare|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the light body is outpaced by the savory plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year's aged Wild Turkey oak sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; BIB structure sits with the richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Salt-Cured Halibut preceded by the tartare -- the firm halibut sits cleanly with the tartare. Strong; reliable opener for the main.",
  "Prime Tartare|Sambuca": "Sambuca's after-dinner liqueur weight leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the herbal-sweet weight matches the tartare, and the caramel underlines the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|San Simeon Stormwatch": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sta. Rita Hills Santa Barbara Pinot — rich, layered, dark cherry, earth, dried herbs. The medium tannin leaves room for raw-beef delicacy and the dried-herbs play against egg yolk and capers. Strong legendary-vineyard Pinot for tartare — Santa Barbara depth on the raw-beef classic.",
  "Prime Tartare|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The tartare and the garlic spinach -- acid lifts the umami, the meal carries lift without losing depth. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Prime Tartare|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Sazerac": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The cocktail's minimal-build purity meets the tartare's raw-beef simplicity; rye spice and Peychaud's cherry note complement the tartare seasoning. Peak pre-dinner pour — 'original American cocktail' meets prime beef course. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye with the tartare — BT's rye against raw beef's mineral depth. The spicy-sweet character is a defining rye-tartare pairing; rye brightness and raw beef find each other cleanly. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon weight handles the tartare, and the oak depth meets the umami. Works; the pour's complexity is underused by the salad; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Scavino Barolo": "King-of-Italian Barolo against raw Prime Tartare — Scavino's powerful firm tannin and tar-leather structure overwhelms the delicate raw beef with capers, shallot, and quail yolk. Never pair. Route tartare to Champagne, Pinot Noir, or a softer red; keep Scavino for Bone-In Filet, Tomahawk, or aged-beef courses.",
  "Prime Tartare|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Rheingau Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry fruit-and-acid cuts raw prime beef's iron richness but the fruit register is less aligned than classical whites for tartare. Works as conversation-starter pairing; Sancerre, Chablis, or grower rosé peak higher.",
  "Prime Tartare|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' concentrated dark-fruit and tannin sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the structured body wraps the tartare, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Seared Scallops": "Prime Tartare with the scallops -- the caper-and-shallot edge holds with the sweet scallop plate. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Prime Tartare|Seasonal Soup": "Prime Tartare and the seasonal soup together -- the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Prime Tartare|Seasonal Vegetables": "The tartare and the seasonal vegetables -- the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Prime Tartare|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's bold cherry-earth cassis holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the concentrated body grips the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Shafer 1.5": "Shafer 1.5's bold coffee-juniper cassis plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Shafer Hillside Select": "Stags Leap collector's Cab against raw Prime Tartare — Hillside Select's firm-tannin graphite-blackcurrant structure overwhelms the delicate raw beef with capers, shallot, and quail yolk. Never pair. Route tartare to Champagne, Pinot Noir, or lighter Cab; keep Hillside Select for the Tomahawk, Cowboy, or Bone-In Filet course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Shrimp Bisque": "The tartare with the shrimp bisque -- the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami meets the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: cream rounds the savory, the meal earns its depth. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Prime Tartare|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco with the tartare — traditional craft against raw beef. The tahona-crushed earthy agave is a distinctive counterpoint to tartare. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado with the tartare — tahona-crushed against raw beef. Works; traditional agave is a distinctive counterpoint to tartare's mineral depth.",
  "Prime Tartare|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak American-oak Cab against prime tartare's raw-iron richness — lush vanilla-dill crushes the delicate seasoned beef. Never pair. Route tartare to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Silver Oak for the steak course where its American-oak signature peaks.",
  "Prime Tartare|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Simon Family Estate Reserve's bold oak cassis reads alongside the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "Spottswoode Lyndenhurst's bold cherry-floral cassis plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-coffee cassis plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "Napa Sauvignon Blanc, grassy and citrus-forward. The bright acid handles the raw beef's richness and the worcestershire's umami weight. Napa profile for guests who want California Sauv Blanc over a Loire pour — clean and reliable here. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's bold cherry-coffee cassis holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's cask-strength cask-strength-oak character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit with the tartare — quadruple-filtered Russian wheat against raw beef. Strong; Elit's clarity supports the tartare cleanly, though Belvedere's rye pepper is the more distinctive vodka-tartare match. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Stoneleigh — Marlborough Sauv Blanc with passionfruit, grapefruit, fresh-cut grass. The zesty acid cuts raw prime beef's iron richness and the tropical-citrus plays against the seasoned tartare. Strong accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for tartare — bright by-the-glass pour on the sophisticated starter.",
  "Prime Tartare|Swordfish": "The swordfish after the tartare -- the grilled swordfish anchors against the tartare. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Prime Tartare|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 with the tartare — small-batch premium Tanq against raw beef. Strong; Tanq 10's citrus-chamomile supports tartare at refined register, though Bombay Dry's angular juniper is the featured gin-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray with the tartare — 47.3% juniper against raw beef. Strong; Tanq's botanical spine lifts tartare's minerality, though Bombay Dry's angular juniper is the featured tartare-gin pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness flattens the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- honeyed sugar dulls the savory meat opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona's oak-sherry character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon -- polished Napa Cab with rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth lingering finish, approachable concentration. The structured body wraps the tartare, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the tartare, and the cherry-leather edge frames the starter. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Prime Tartare|The BG": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth meets the starter's fat and umami — both land heavy. Works cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|The Happy Wife": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this gin cocktail's citrus brightness and botanical lift. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. Works cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|The Manhattan": "Maker's Mark private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The vanilla-softened whiskey meets raw prime beef's iron-tinged richness well; Contratto's herbal sweetness complements the tartare seasoning. Strong pre-dinner pairing for guests leading with a classic whiskey cocktail.",
  "Prime Tartare|The Prisoner Red Blend": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk after the tartare -- the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami sets up the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; the starter composes before the cut.",
  "Prime Tartare|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the tartare — BTAC against raw beef. Strong; the uncut unfiltered Sazerac is a collector-register counterpoint to tartare's mineral depth.",
  "Prime Tartare|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's char-mineral character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Titos Vodka": "Tito's with the tartare — round corn vodka against raw beef. Strong; Tito's doesn't lift tartare's minerality the way Belvedere's rye pepper does, but it's a clean neutral choice.",
  "Prime Tartare|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory with the tartare — entry Suntory blend against raw beef. Strong; Toki's light-floral character supports tartare at crowd-pleasing register; Hibiki Harmony's refined-elegant premium is the featured Japanese-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Tomato Basil": "Prime Tartare alongside the tomato basil -- the bright register pulls against the savory, the contrast lands. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "Prime Tartare|Transfusion": "Hangar 1 vodka, muddled black grapes, lime, ginger beer. The grape's slight tannin and lime brightness cut raw prime beef's iron richness without crushing. Strong unconventional pre-dinner pairing — the Transfusion's fruit adds contrast against the beef.",
  "Prime Tartare|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado with the tartare — cedar-aged reposado against raw beef. Works; cedar is a curious counterpoint to tartare.",
  "Prime Tartare|Truffle Fries": "The tartare and the truffle fries -- both build on umami, the plate carries weight without contrast. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Prime Tartare|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s crisp grain-malt character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "The tuna after the tartare -- the seared-rare yellowfin plays against the briny tartare. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Prime Tartare|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Prime Tartare alongside the curry -- the hand-cut tartare matches the vegetable-and-chickpea bowl. Strong; the openers compose cleanly together.",
  "Prime Tartare|Venge Family Reserve": "Venge Family Reserve's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Venge Scout's Honor": "Venge Scout's Honor's powerful blackcurrant body leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the structured body wraps the tartare, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; the pairing lands at register without strain.",
  "Prime Tartare|Venge Silencieux": "Venge Silencieux's bold cherry-chocolate cassis matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the firm tannin cuts the rich starter, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Prime Tartare|Vesper": "Nolet's gin, Ketel One vodka, Lillet — stirred. The cocktail's clean structure meets raw prime beef's iron richness and Lillet's citrus-bitter adds complexity against tartare seasoning. Peak martini pairing for the hand-cut beef — sophistication matched.",
  "Prime Tartare|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Yellow label Veuve — Reims Champagne with ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse, lively acidity. The Pinot Noir dominance meets raw prime beef's iron richness and the biscuit plays against the seasoned tartare. Peak recognizable Champagne for tartare — everyone's favorite pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Vieux Carré": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto vermouth, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. The cocktail's layered complexity meets raw prime beef's iron richness; cognac and rye echo different facets of the tartare. Peak pre-dinner pour for the guest who wants the historically weighty cocktail.",
  "Prime Tartare|Villon Cognac": "Villon Cognac's rancio-edged cognac weight rests at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the Limousin-oak weight handles the tartare, and the caramel threads the umami register. Strong; Ferrand's craft character frames the richness with unblended polish.",
  "Prime Tartare|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Prime Tartare|Weller 107": "Weller 107's bold caramel-char character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year -- aged wheated, honey, vanilla. The wheated weight wraps the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the curated single-barrel is capable but single-distillery aged bourbons bring more traditional focused character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium -- 20-year wheated bourbon, heavily allocated, the white-whale of the Weller family. The bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; annual-release capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve -- wheated, soft vanilla, gentle oak. The wheated softness matches the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; smooth character sits with richness at matched register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley with the tartare — BT house-made wheat vodka against raw beef. Strong; Wheatley's clean wheat supports tartare cleanly, though Belvedere's rye pepper is the distinctive choice.",
  "Prime Tartare|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the tartare — premium aged rye against raw beef. Strong; the WP-core aged depth is a refined counterpoint to tartare.",
  "Prime Tartare|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with the tartare — Old World fruit-rye against raw beef. Strong; multi-cask complexity is a distinctive counterpoint to tartare.",
  "Prime Tartare|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with the tartare — 15-year premium WP against raw beef. Strong; the flagship extended maturation is a refined premium counterpoint.",
  "Prime Tartare|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with the tartare — allocated WP against raw beef. Strong; the Boss Hog intensity is a collector-register counterpoint to tartare's mineral depth.",
  "Prime Tartare|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with the tartare — X Commandments against raw beef. Strong; 10th-edition is a collector-register counterpoint to tartare.",
  "Prime Tartare|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year with the tartare — WP accessible rye against raw beef. The Vermont-brand Canadian-sourced character is a distinctive counterpoint. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Prime Tartare|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the caramel-spice register matches the tartare, and the oak balances the umami register. Strong; the polished aged character is a refined premium counterpoint distinct from WP's Vermont-finished approach; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's caramel-floral character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Prime Tartare|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; Kentucky Owl St; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Prime Tartare|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the tartare — elegant small-batch rye against raw beef. The fruit-forward character is a refined counterpoint to tartare; distinct from Sazerac Rye's more assertive spicy-sweet. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with the tartare — Washington craft rye against raw beef. The local-grain character is a regional-craft counterpoint distinct from Kentucky. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Prime Tartare|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 with the tartare — Suntory flagship single malt against raw beef. Strong; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak character supports tartare at flagship register; Yamazaki 18's aged Mizunara depth is the featured Yamazaki-tartare match.",
  "Prime Tartare|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 with the tartare — aged Yamazaki against raw beef. Excellent; Yamazaki 18's aged Mizunara-oak with sandalwood + coconut + dried fruit is the featured Japanese-whisky-tartare match at collector register — deeper than Hibiki Harmony's blended refinement.",
  "Prime Tartare|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness reads alongside the tartare's raw beef and brine — the sweet character meets the savory plate at digestif register. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Asparagus": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Napa Cab — ripe blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft tannin. The lush body cuts au gratin's cheese-and-cream weight and the chocolate-vanilla plays against the golden crust. Strong polished Napa Cab for au gratin — indulgent lushness on the decadent side.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Beignets": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone Marrow": "Quilt — Napa Cab with blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft tannin. The lush body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the chocolate frames the bone depth. Strong polished Napa Cab for marrow — indulgent match, though firm-tannin Cabs like Silver Oak or Frias peak higher for the starter.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone-In Filet": "Ripe blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla with polished tannins and an indulgent finish. Parallels the bone-in filet's richness without adding contrast — a match rather than a lift. A default-good pour for guests who asked for \"a nice Cab\" without a specific label in mind. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccoli Cheddar": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccolini": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Brussels and Belly": "Napa Cab — ripe blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla. The lush body handles pork-belly fat and the chocolate-vanilla plays against charred brussels. Strong polished Napa Cab for Brussels and Belly — crowd-pleasing lushness cuts through the rich starter.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Burrata": "Polished Napa Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — lush chocolate-vanilla crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with ripe blackberry. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Quilt for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Butternut Squash": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Carrot Cake": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Cheesecake": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Chilean Seabass": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Brownie": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Cake": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Clam Chowder": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Cowboy Ribeye": "Napa Cab — ripe blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft polished tannin. The lush body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the chocolate-vanilla meets rendered fat. Strong polished Napa Cab for Cowboy — indulgent choice, though Silver Oak, Caymus, or mountain Cabs peak higher for the fattiest ribeye.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Crab Cake": "Polished Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — lush chocolate-vanilla crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Quilt for the steak course where its indulgent Napa character shines.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamed Spinach": "Quilt — Napa Cab with blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft tannin. The lush body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the chocolate plays against the earthy sauce. Peak polished Napa Cab for creamed spinach — crowd-pleasing lushness on the classic steakhouse side. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamy Potato": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Creme Brulee": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Escargot": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds steady against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Faroe Island Salmon": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Filet Mignon": "Napa Cab — ripe blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft tannin, indulgent finish. The polished tannin meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the chocolate-vanilla echoes escargot-butter toppers. Peak lush Napa Cab for filet — crowd-pleasing by-the-bottle anchor on the classic cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|French Onion": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Grilled Caesar": "Napa Cab — blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft tannin. The lush body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the chocolate plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as polished Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — unconventional indulgent red for the classic layered salad.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Gumbo": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|House Wedge": "Quilt — Napa Cab, blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla. The soft tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the chocolate plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as polished Napa Cab for the wedge — indulgent red choice, though whites or rosés peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Kansas City": "Quilt — Napa Cab with ripe blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft polished tannin. The lush body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the vanilla-chocolate plays against the beefy char. Peak polished Napa Cab for Kansas City — indulgent anchor without committing to a prestige bottle. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Loaded Potato": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cream. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Lobster Mac": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Market Fish": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Mocha Creme": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushroom Bisque": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the oak frames the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushrooms": "Napa Cab — ripe blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft tannin. The lush chocolate-vanilla meshes with mushroom umami and the polished tannin frames the sauté cleanly. Peak polished Napa Cab for mushrooms — indulgent body rounds out the earthy side. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Porterhouse": "Quilt — Napa Cab, blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla, soft tannin. The polished tannin frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the chocolate plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong polished Napa Cab for Porterhouse — indulgent anchor on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Prime Tartare": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Rainbow Trout": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Roast Half Chicken": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis plays cleanly against the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Seafood Tower": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Seared Scallops": "Quilt Napa Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — lush chocolate-vanilla obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Quilt for the steak course where its polished Napa character peaks.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Soup": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Vegetables": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Bisque": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis leans against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Cocktail": "Quilt Napa Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — lush chocolate-vanilla is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Quilt for steak where its polished Napa character belongs.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Swordfish": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|The Tomahawk": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the Cab structure carries the bone-in cut, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Tomato Basil": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Truffle Fries": "Quilt — polished Napa Cab with blackberry, dark chocolate, toasted vanilla. The lush chocolate-vanilla frames truffle-fry earth and the ripe fruit cuts the crisp oil-and-salt. Peak indulgent Napa Cab for truffle fries — lush body meets the decadent side without overpowering. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Asparagus": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Au Gratin Potatoes": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin stays alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Beignets": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Bone Marrow": "Sea-influenced Sonoma Coast Pinot against pure collagen-fat marrow — silky RAEN lacks the tannin needed to cut the render. Never pair. Route marrow to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or structured bold reds; keep Royal St Robert for the chicken, salmon, or seafood course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Bone-In Filet": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the medium-body red frames the lean cut, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Broccoli Cheddar": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Broccolini": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin stays alongside the broccolini's tender green — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Brussels and Belly": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin stays alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; capable alongside. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Burrata": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, crushed rock, silky. The silky structure meets burrata's creamy-delicate center and the crushed-rock plays against basil-tomato. Works as Burgundian California Pinot for burrata — elegant red; whites and rosés peak higher.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Butternut Squash": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Carrot Cake": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Cheesecake": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Chilean Seabass": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Chocolate Brownie": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Chocolate Cake": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. RAEN Royal St Robert belongs on the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Clam Chowder": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Cowboy Ribeye": "Sea-influenced Sonoma Coast Pinot against ribeye's big marbled build — silky Burgundian California Pinot falls short of the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Keep RAEN for salmon, chicken, or seafood; route ribeye to Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Crab Cake": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, crushed rock, silky soft tannin. The soft tannin doesn't fight crab's sweet meat and the sea-bred minerality plays against romesco. Peak Burgundian California Pinot for crab cake — elegant red on the shellfish starter. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Creamed Spinach": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Creamy Potato": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin reads alongside the potato soup's silky body — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Creme Brulee": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift flattens the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the RAEN Royal St Robert on the steak course instead.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Escargot": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin reads alongside the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the medium-body red meets the savory plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Faroe Island Salmon": "Faroe Island Salmon: this rich, fatty seafood's buttery flesh and delicate-yet-substantial texture meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's cherry-bright, low-tannin frame threads cleanly through the fish — classic Burgundy logic. A headliner fish pair. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Filet Mignon": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced Burgundian freshness, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, crushed rock, silky, long mineral. The silky structure meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the crushed-rock echoes escargot-butter. Peak Burgundian-style California Pinot for filet — one of California's finest. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|French Onion": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. Cherry-driven red with the Caesar's umami and char — a classic cross-category salad pair. Strong — aligned register.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Gumbo": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Honey Roasted Carrots": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin sits alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|House Wedge": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, crushed rock, high acid. The high acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the crushed-rock plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak Burgundian-style California Pinot for the wedge — one of California's finest reframes the salad. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Kansas City": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, silky, medium body. The silky structure works against Kansas City's lean-bold strip though low tannin falls short of bolder Cabs. Works as Burgundian California Pinot for the strip — elegant red when guest prefers Pinot.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Loaded Potato": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin sits alongside the loaded potato's richness — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Lobster Mac": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin stays alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Market Fish": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Mocha Creme": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the RAEN Royal St Robert on the steak course instead.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Mushroom Bisque": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin reads alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: this earthy, umami-packed side's mushroom-and-truffle depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's acidity cuts the dairy fat while its cherry lifts the earthy umami. Strong — the wine refreshes the side's richness.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Peanut Butter Brownie": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Porterhouse": "RAEN Royal St Robert: this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame meets this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char. The elegant red's medium-light frame gets overrun by the bold, marbled steak — the wine shrinks. Works only if the guest prizes restraint over match.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's silky frame sits alongside the starter without weight-fighting — the fruit threads through the plate. Strong — aligned register.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Rainbow Trout": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Roast Half Chicken": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, crushed rock, silky. The elegant frame plays against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong Burgundian California Pinot for chicken.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin stays alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Salt-Cured Halibut": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, crushed rock. The high acid cuts the sauté butter and the sea-bred minerality plays against garlic without clashing with spinach earth. Works as Burgundian Pinot for the sauté side — elegant red rounds the pairing.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Seafood Tower": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Seared Scallops": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, crushed rock, silky. The sea-bred minerality echoes scallop sweetness and the crushed-rock frames caramelized-exterior char. Peak Burgundian California Pinot for scallops — sea-influenced Pinot for sea-sourced shellfish. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Seasonal Soup": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin sits alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Seasonal Vegetables": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the seasonal garden plate — the medium-body red frames the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Shrimp Bisque": "Fort Ross-Seaview Sonoma Coast Pinot — sea-influenced, red cherry, cranberry, wild strawberry, crushed rock, high acid. The high acid cuts bisque's cream weight and the sea-bred minerality plays against shellfish umami and sherry. Strong Burgundian Pinot for shrimp bisque — elegant against the rich soup.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Shrimp Cocktail": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Swordfish": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|The Tomahawk": "Sea-influenced Sonoma Coast Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — silky low-tannin RAEN has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Royal St Robert for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Tomato Basil": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin plays cleanly against the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the cherry-fruit lifts the herb edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Truffle Fries": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin sits beside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the medium-body red sits with the rich side, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "RAEN Royal St Robert|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "RAEN Royal St Robert's cherry-mineral tannin sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Rainbow Trout|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the 1792 Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. 1792 Sweet Wheat belongs on the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the 1881 Napa Valley on the steak course instead.",
  "Rainbow Trout|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals matches the trout's delicate flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the botanicals lift the savory weight. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Rainbow Trout|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the A Midnight's Winter Dram for the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Aberlour 16 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's caramel-char character finds register with the trout's delicate flesh — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
