  "Rainbow Trout|Venge Scout's Honor": "Venge Scout's Honor's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Venge Scout's Honor for the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Venge Silencieux": "Venge Silencieux's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Venge Silencieux lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Vesper": "Vesper's crisp citrus-juniper build finds register with the trout's delicate flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut -- yellow-label Champagne, pinot-driven blend, restaurant-pour standard. The pinot-driven lift brightens the trout, and the toast-and-citrus edge frames the plate. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Vieux Carré": "Vieux Carré's spirit-forward cocktail register smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the bitters-and-bourbon register has no foil against gentle flesh. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Vieux Carré for the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Villon Cognac": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- cognac's aged-grape weight buries delicate fish — digestif belongs after the meal. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a cognac. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Villon Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- dessert wine on delicate fish is wrong-course energy — needs a dry white, not honeyed sugar. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Vin Santo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Weller 107": "Weller 107's bigger-body wheated buries scallop sweetness flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Weller 107 for the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Weller 12 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Weller Millennium belongs on the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Weller Special Reserve belongs on the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality holds the line with the trout's delicate flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Rainbow Trout|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye for the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. WhistlePig 12 Year Old World belongs on the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the WhistlePig 15 Year for the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Rainbow Trout|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments for the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year for the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's craft high-rye oak spice smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Willett Pot Still Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Woodford Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Woodford Reserve Double Oaked on the steak course instead.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Woodford Reserve Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Woodinville Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character leans against the trout's delicate flesh: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the trout, and the umami-tinged finish frames the plate. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character reads alongside the trout's delicate flesh: the mizunara-cask depth wraps the trout, and the silky precision frames the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Rainbow Trout|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bitter herbs flatten delicate fish — the protein can't lift the amaro's register. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Asparagus": "Catalan Cava rosé — wild strawberry, raspberry, saline finish. The saline finish plays against asparagus's mineral-vegetal character and the bright acid cuts the char. Works as Cava for asparagus — rosé sparkler on the wine-tricky vegetable.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Beignets": "Spanish rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit, clean acid. The rosé fruit plays against the beignet's choux-and-sugar profile; bubbles lift the fried dough. Peak pour when guest wants sparkling that's less sweet than Moscato to finish the meal. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Bone Marrow": "Catalan Cava rosé against marrow's rendered-fat richness — saline-berry bubbles have no weight to meet bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Cava for raw bar, tartare, or starter course where it excels.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Bone-In Filet": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut for another course.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Broccoli Cheddar": "Catalan Cava rosé — wild strawberry, raspberry, saline finish. The bright acid cuts the soup's cheddar-cream weight and the berry profile plays against broccoli vegetal base. Works as Cava for broccoli cheddar — rosé sparkler on the classic.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Broccolini": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge frames the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Brussels and Belly": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity leans against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Burrata": "Rosé Cava — red-fruit character, sparkling acid. The color and fruit character echo the seasonal fruit garnish, and the bubbles cut the burrata cream. Non-obvious sparkling route with real logic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Butternut Squash": "Catalan Cava rosé — wild strawberry, raspberry, saline finish. The bright acid cuts butternut's cream-sweet weight and the berry profile plays against caramelized squash. Works as Cava for butternut squash — rosé sparkler on the autumn classic.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Carrot Cake": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut on the steak course instead.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Cheesecake": "Spanish rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit, clean acid. The rosé fruit plays against the sour-cream tang and the bubbles cut the cream cheese weight. Fun non-obvious pour when guest wants something less sweet than Moscato to finish. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Chilean Seabass": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the seabass's buttery flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Chocolate Brownie": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a sparkling. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut on the steak course instead.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Chocolate Cake": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- brut bubbles and chocolate weight refuse each other — needs sweetness, not crisp acid. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a sparkling. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Clam Chowder": "Catalan Cava rosé — wild strawberry, raspberry, saline finish. The saline finish echoes the chowder's clam brine and the bright acid cuts the cream base. Strong Cava for clam chowder — rosé sparkler on the shellfish classic.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Cowboy Ribeye": "Raventós Cava rosé against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — light body crumples under rendered fat. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Cava for starters, burrata, or seafood tower — it's the opener, not the main event.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Crab Cake": "Raventós de NIT Cava rosé — Catalan blend with wild strawberry, raspberry, mineral spine, and saline finish. The mineral-berry profile cuts romesco and the saline complements crab's sweet meat. Peak Cava for crab cake — sophisticated food-friendly pink at accessible price. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Creamed Spinach": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Creamy Potato": "Catalan Cava rosé — wild strawberry, raspberry, saline finish. The bright acid cuts the soup's cream weight and the berry profile adds lift against the potato's mild base. Works as accessible Cava for creamy potato — rosé sparkler on the classic.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Creme Brulee": "Spanish rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit character, clean acid. Bubbles and fruit cut through vanilla custard cleanly; the rosé lifts the dessert lighter than a Champagne would. Fun non-obvious sparkling finisher when the guest wants to keep it dry-ish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Escargot": "Catalan Cava rosé — dry elegant with wild strawberry, raspberry, and saline finish. The mineral spine cuts escargot's garlic-parsley butter and the saline hooks into the French-Mediterranean tradition directly. Strong rosé Cava for escargot — accessible sophistication on the French classic.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Faroe Island Salmon": "Rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit character, bright acid. The rosé fruit plays against a seared salmon and the bubbles cut the Faroe's fat. Non-obvious sparkling pivot when Champagne isn't the move. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: the filet's lean, buttery tenderness and delicate mineral edge meets this sparkling rosé's strawberry-and-brioche charm with crisp acidity. Bubbles scrub fat well, but sparkling lacks the dark-fruit weight and tannin for steak. Strong — the interaction lands despite the axis challenge.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|French Onion": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Grilled Caesar": "Rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit character. Bubbles cut the dressing's egg-yolk weight, and the rosé fruit survives against our cayenne croutons' heat. Clean sparkling route for a rich salad course — does real work here. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Gumbo": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity reads alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; capable alongside.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Catalan Cava rosé — wild strawberry, raspberry, saline finish. The bright acid cuts carrots' honey-glaze sweetness and the berry profile plays against the roasted char. Works as Cava for honey-roasted carrots — rosé sparkler on the sweet-root classic.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|House Wedge": "Rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit character, bright acid. The bubbles cut the blue cheese dressing weight and the rosé fruit plays against the brown-sugar-and-paprika lardon seasoning. Fun, non-obvious sparkling route for the wedge. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Kansas City": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut lands on another course — not here.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Loaded Potato": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity plays cleanly against the loaded potato's richness — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Lobster Mac": "Spanish rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit character, clean acid. The bubbles cut the truffle-cream roux and the rosé fruit plays against the lobster's sweetness. Fun non-obvious sparkling alternative to Champagne when the table wants something playful. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Market Fish": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's méthode-driven bubbles and toast leans against the market fish's clean white-fish flesh: the fine bubbles lift the market fish, and the citrus frames the plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Mocha Creme": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- brut bubbles and chocolate weight refuse each other — needs sweetness, not crisp acid. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut for the steak course.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Mushroom Bisque": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity holds with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Mushrooms": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity stays alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- a dry sparkling belongs with the opener, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut for the steak course.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Porterhouse": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's bright sparkling effervescence underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut lands on another course — not here.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Prime Tartare": "Rosé Cava with red-fruit character and sparkling acid — the fruit plays against the house-aged worcestershire's 30-day umami, bubbles cut the cured yolk's weight. Non-obvious but memorable; the story of the 30-day aging pairs well with the Cava's method. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Rainbow Trout": "Catalan Cava rosé — wild strawberry, raspberry, saline finish. The saline finish plays against trout's oceanic-adjacent character and the bright acid lifts the flaky flesh. Works as Cava for rainbow trout — rosé sparkler on the delicate classic.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Roast Half Chicken": "Rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit character. Works against roasted chicken but doesn't peak the way the Chards or Pinots do. Fine for the guest who wants sparkling through the main course.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Catalan Cava rosé — wild strawberry, raspberry, saline finish. The bright acid cuts the soup's tomato-pepper base and the berry profile plays against roasted-pepper sweetness. Works as Cava for the soup — rosé sparkler on the smoky classic.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Salt-Cured Halibut: this delicate seafood's mild, flaky flesh meets this sparkling rosé's strawberry-and-brioche charm with crisp acidity. Bubbles and acidity scrub rich fish fat while toasty lees add texture without weight. Works — the classic fit.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity stays alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Seafood Tower": "Rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit character, bright acid. Handles our mignonette's red-wine-vinegar sharpness and the cocktail-sauce horseradish heat without fighting the shellfish. Smart alternative when the table doesn't want Champagne. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Seared Scallops": "Catalan Cava rosé — dry blend with wild strawberry, raspberry, mineral spine, saline finish. The saline cuts scallops' clean-seared edge and the berry frames the sweet pan-sear. Strong Cava-with-scallops pairing — food-friendly sophistication at accessible price point.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Seasonal Soup": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity sits beside the seasonal soup's house build — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Seasonal Vegetables": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity reads alongside the seasonal garden plate — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Shrimp Bisque": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut: this sparkling rosé's strawberry-and-brioche charm with crisp acidity meets this rich, creamy seafood soup's sweet-briny depth over dairy-rich base. Bubbles and acid cut the soup's creamy weight. Strong — brightness-on-richness.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Shrimp Cocktail": "Raventós de NIT Cava rosé — Catalan blend with wild strawberry, raspberry, and mineral spine. The saline finish cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the red-fruit profile plays against cocktail-sauce. Peak Cava for shrimp cocktail — accessible rosé bubbles that complement the classic starter. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Swordfish": "Catalan Cava rosé — wild strawberry, raspberry, saline finish. The saline finish echoes swordfish's oceanic character and the bright acid cuts the grilled-char weight. Works as Cava for swordfish — rosé sparkler on the grilled classic.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|The Tomahawk": "Catalan Cava rosé against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — light dry bubbles have no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Cava de NIT for starters, seafood tower, or accessible rosé moments.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Tomato Basil": "Tomato Basil: this bright, vegetal soup's acidic herbal lift meets this sparkling rosé's strawberry-and-brioche charm with crisp acidity. Sparkling and bright, vegetal soup both lean light — aligned register. Strong — both stay light.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Truffle Fries": "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut's crisp brine-mineral acidity leans against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna: this lean, seared tuna's sesame-sweet crust and rare-pink center meets this sparkling rosé's strawberry-and-brioche charm with crisp acidity. Bubbles and acidity scrub rich fish fat while toasty lees add texture without weight. Strong — bubbles lift the protein.",
  "Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas: this spiced vegetable curry's warm, complex aromatic lift meets this sparkling rosé's strawberry-and-brioche charm with crisp acidity. Sparkling and bright, vegetal soup both lean light — aligned register. Works cleanly.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Asparagus": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the pot-still depth wraps the asparagus, and the sherry-cask spice frames the side. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the gentle Irish body threads the au gratin, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; bold character slightly over-specified for the soup.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Beignets": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the approachable Irish character carries the beignets, and the gentle barley note frames the dessert. Works; boutique character doesn't drive.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Bone Marrow": "Redbreast 12 Year -- single pot still Irish, sherry-cask, spice. The pot-still depth wraps the rich marrow, and the sherry-cask spice frames the starter. Strong; curated character covers the cut; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Bone-In Filet": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the Irish whiskey body sits with the bone-enhanced filet, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the cut. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Broccoli Cheddar": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the pot-still depth wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th handles the richness at annual-release register.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Broccolini": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the broccolini, and the gentle barley note frames the side. Works; pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Brussels and Belly": "Redbreast 12 with Brussels + Belly — single pot still Irish against maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; Redbreast 12's sherry-cask and pot still depth meet the glaze at premium register.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Burrata": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character crowds out the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Redbreast 12 Year for the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Butternut Squash": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the pot-still depth wraps the squash bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the private select's layered profile underserves the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet sherry+bourbon-cask pot still Irish. Works; Redbreast 12's sherry-cask and pot still spice thread the cake at premium register; Redbreast 21's aged depth edges this dessert pairing.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Cheesecake": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Chilean Seabass": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Redbreast 12 Year belongs on the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Chocolate Brownie": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the gentle Irish body threads the brownie, and the gentle barley note frames the dessert. Works; distinctive character is worth mentioning tableside, even if the pairing isn't marquee.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Chocolate Cake": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the Irish whiskey body sits with the chocolate cake, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the dessert. Works; the private select's layered profile underserves the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Clam Chowder": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the pot-still depth wraps the chowder, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; curated single-barrel covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: fatty bone-in cut meets Redbreast 12 Year. Excellent; single-pot-still Irish character frames cap fat at Redbreast's flagship register — Irish-pot-still depth on the most forgiving steak cut.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Crab Cake": "Redbreast 12 with crab cake — premium pot still Irish against delicate lump crab. Works; Redbreast 12 is richer than Jameson so less gentle with crab, but Irish whiskey's triple-distilled-pot-still softness still keeps it in the whisky-curious crab territory.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Creamed Spinach": "Redbreast 12 with creamed spinach — pot still Irish against dairy-rich side. Works; Redbreast 12's rich-oily character rides the cream at premium-Irish register.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Creamy Potato": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the pot-still depth wraps the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; gentle match.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Creme Brulee": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the pour's delicacy doesn't overpower the soup, but doesn't particularly elevate it either.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Escargot": "Redbreast 12 Year's pot-still-fruit character matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Faroe Island Salmon": "Redbreast 12 with the salmon — pot still Irish against oily salmon. Excellent; one of the strong calls on the list.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Filet Mignon": "Redbreast 12 with the filet — premium single pot still Irish against tenderloin. Excellent; Redbreast 12's distinctive pot still character frames tenderloin with rich-spicy-oily gravitas the blended Irish expressions can't match — this is what Irish whiskey becomes when you strip away the grain component.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|French Onion": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the approachable Irish character carries the French onion, and the gentle barley note frames the bowl. Works; premium proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Grilled Caesar": "Redbreast 12 with the grilled Caesar — single pot still Irish against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Redbreast 12's pot still spice is the featured Irish-Caesar pairing — the distinctive spice component lifts char-and-anchovy where Tullamore's drier blend rounds it.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Gumbo": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the pot-still depth wraps the gumbo, and the sherry-cask spice frames the bowl. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the malt-grain blend handles the honey carrots, and the oak threads the caramelized sugar. Works; the intensity overpowers this dish.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|House Wedge": "Redbreast 12 with the House Wedge — pot still Irish against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Redbreast 12's rich-spicy-oily character engages the bleu at premium-Irish register.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Kansas City": "Redbreast 12 with the KC — single pot still Irish whiskey (Ireland's distinctive style — mix of malted + unmalted barley), sherry + bourbon cask, rich-spicy-oily mouthfeel. Excellent; Redbreast 12's pot still oiliness and sherry-cask depth are the featured Irish-KC pairing — the pot still distillation creates a richer body than blended Irish can offer, giving it the backbone for strip marbling.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Loaded Potato": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the gentle Irish body threads the loaded potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Lobster Mac": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the Irish whiskey body sits with the lobster mac, and the oak threads the cream. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Market Fish": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Redbreast 12 Year for the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Mocha Creme": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the Irish whiskey body sits with the mocha creme, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; flagship depth gives the dessert premium aged character.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Mushroom Bisque": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the umami. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Mushrooms": "Redbreast 12 with mushrooms — pot still Irish against earthy umami. Excellent; Redbreast 12's rich-oily pot still mouthfeel is the featured Irish-mushroom pairing — the oiliness marries mushroom earthiness with a texture no Irish blend replicates.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the approachable Irish character carries the PB brownie, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the dessert. Works; Caribbean Cask frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Porterhouse": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the Irish whiskey body sits with the dual-cut steak, and the soft malt rounds the cut. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Prime Tartare": "Redbreast 12 Year's pot-still-fruit character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Rainbow Trout": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Redbreast 12 Year for the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Roast Half Chicken": "Redbreast 12 with the chicken — single pot still Irish against roasted poultry. Excellent; Redbreast 12's pot still depth frames chicken at premium-Irish register — richer than Jameson's iconic-entry, more distinctive than Tullamore's heritage approach.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the chickpea soup, and the grain register softens the bowl. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side; save the rare bottle for the main.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Redbreast 12 Year for the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the approachable Irish character carries the garlic spinach, and the grain register softens the side. Works; EC 18's aged character meets the glaze at aged-HH register.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Seafood Tower": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Redbreast 12 Year belongs on the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Seared Scallops": "Redbreast 12 with seared scallops — single pot still Irish against scallop delicacy. Works; Redbreast 12's pot still richness is heavier for scallops than Jameson's smoothness, but still lighter than most whiskies.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Seasonal Soup": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the seasonal soup, and the grain register softens the bowl. Works; AE Triple Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Seasonal Vegetables": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the grain register softens the side. Works; a gentle composed match.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Shrimp Bisque": "Redbreast 12 with shrimp bisque — pot still Irish against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Redbreast 12's rich-oily character rides the bisque at premium-Irish register.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Shrimp Cocktail": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character fights the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Redbreast 12 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Swordfish": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Redbreast 12 Year belongs on the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|The Tomahawk": "Redbreast 12 with the Tomahawk — premium pot still Irish against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; even rich Irish pot still is too light for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Tomato Basil": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the pot-still depth wraps the tomato basil, and the sherry-cask spice frames the bowl. Works; the complex character gives the dessert a refined frame.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Truffle Fries": "Redbreast 12 with truffle fries — single pot still premium Irish against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Redbreast 12's pot still oiliness meets the earthy-rich at premium register.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Redbreast 12 Year belongs on the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 12 Year|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the approachable Irish character carries the curry, and the soft malt rounds the bowl. Works; extended-aged premium wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Asparagus": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the malt-grain blend handles the asparagus, and the soft malt rounds the side. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the Irish whiskey body sits with the au gratin, and the oak softens the cream. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Beignets": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the malt-grain blend handles the beignets, and the grain register softens the dessert. Works; the pour's assertiveness is a bit much for the soup but doesn't clash.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: umami fat meets Redbreast 21 Year. Excellent; extended-aged single-pot-still Irish character handles marrow at Redbreast-luxury register — the Irish flagship on the umami plate.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Bone-In Filet": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the Irish whiskey body sits with the bone-enhanced filet, and the grain register softens the cut. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Broccoli Cheddar": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the Irish whiskey body sits with the broccoli cheddar, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the pour's sweetness meets the dessert without pushing past.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Broccolini": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the gentle Irish body threads the broccolini, and the grain register softens the side. Works; light body doesn't overshoot.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Brussels and Belly": "Redbreast 21 with Brussels + Belly — aged 21yr pot still Irish against maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; Redbreast 21's aged sherry-cask depth meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Burrata": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish crowds out the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Redbreast 21 Year for the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Butternut Squash": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the gentle Irish body threads the squash bisque, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; Voyage 23 frames the cut at ocean-finish register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet aged sherry-cask pot still Irish. Strong; Redbreast 21's aged sherry-cask dried-fruit is the featured Irish-carrot-cake pairing — 21 years of aging deepens what Redbreast 12 starts.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Cheesecake": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the cheesecake, and the oak softens the cream. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Chilean Seabass": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Redbreast 21 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Chocolate Brownie": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the Irish whiskey body sits with the brownie, and the gentle barley note frames the dessert. Works; regional craft at matched register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Chocolate Cake": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the gentle Irish body threads the chocolate cake, and the grain register softens the dessert. Works; regional craft at matched register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Clam Chowder": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the malt-grain blend handles the chowder, and the oak threads the cream. Works; gentle match.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Cowboy Ribeye": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish reads alongside the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the malt-grain blend handles the cap-fat ribeye, and the gentle barley note frames the cut. Works; Caribou Crossing is lighter than ribeye demands; CC 43 Year handles it better.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Crab Cake": "Redbreast 21 with crab cake — aged premium pot still Irish against delicate lump crab. Works; Redbreast 21's aged-oily character is rich for crab but still within Irish whiskey's category advantage; save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Creamed Spinach": "Redbreast 21 with creamed spinach — aged pot still Irish against dairy-rich side. Works; Redbreast 21 is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Creamy Potato": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the Irish whiskey body sits with the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Creme Brulee": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the brulee, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; smooth character is slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Escargot": "Redbreast 21 Year -- aged single pot still, sherry-cask, rich. The soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the buttered escargot, and the soft malt rounds the starter. Strong; bonded weight at matched premium register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Faroe Island Salmon": "Redbreast 21 with the salmon — aged single pot still Irish against oily salmon. Excellent; one of the strong calls on the list.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets aged 21-year single pot still Irish, deeper sherry-cask depth, collector-register. Redbreast 21 is the featured aged-Irish-filet pairing — 21 years of aging adds depth to the pot still character where Redbreast 12 reaches, the 21 closes with collector-grade gravitas. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|French Onion": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the approachable Irish character carries the French onion, and the grain register softens the bowl. Works; EC 18 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Grilled Caesar": "Redbreast 21 with the grilled Caesar — aged 21yr pot still Irish against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Redbreast 21's aged pot still spice meets the char with collector-register resonance.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Gumbo": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the gentle Irish body threads the gumbo, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the bowl. Works; boutique at matched register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the approachable Irish character carries the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|House Wedge": "Redbreast 21 with the House Wedge — 21-year aged pot still Irish against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Redbreast 21's aged depth engages the bleu at collector register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Kansas City": "Redbreast 21 with the KC — 21-year aged pot still Irish against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Redbreast 21's aged sherry-cask + pot still depth is the featured premium-Irish-KC match at collector register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Loaded Potato": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the loaded potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; gentle match at matched registers.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Lobster Mac": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the gentle Irish body threads the lobster mac, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Market Fish": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Redbreast 21 Year for the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Mocha Creme": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the mocha creme, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; own-plus-sourced over-specified.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Mushroom Bisque": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the malt-grain blend handles the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the umami. Works; JW Black rides the bisque at premium-blend register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Mushrooms": "Redbreast 21 with mushrooms — aged 21yr pot still Irish against earthy umami. Excellent; Redbreast 21's aged-pot-still oiliness and sherry-cask depth thread mushroom earthiness at collector register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the malt-grain blend handles the PB brownie, and the gentle barley note frames the dessert. Works; allocated character doesn't drive.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Porterhouse": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the gentle Irish body threads the dual-cut steak, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the cut. Works; the pour's brightness is a gentle counterpoint to the dessert's richness.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Prime Tartare": "Redbreast 21 Year's bold pot-still-sherry character lifts the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Rainbow Trout": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Redbreast 21 Year for the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Roast Half Chicken": "Redbreast 21 with the chicken — 21-year aged pot still Irish against roasted poultry. Excellent; Redbreast 21's aged depth frames chicken at collector register — the most premium Irish-chicken pairing in the cluster.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the chickpea soup, and the grain register softens the bowl. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Redbreast 21 Year belongs on the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the garlic spinach, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the side. Works; extended-aged home-state pour wasted on salad.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Seafood Tower": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Redbreast 21 Year for the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Seared Scallops": "Redbreast 21 with seared scallops — 21yr aged pot still Irish against scallop delicacy. Works; Redbreast 21's aged pot still is heavy for scallops — save the collector bottle.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Seasonal Soup": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the malt-grain blend handles the seasonal soup, and the orchard-fruit edge underlines the bowl. Works; craft-blender complexity over-specified.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Seasonal Vegetables": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the soft malt-and-grain weight wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the triple-distilled smoothness brightens the side. Works; boutique over-specified.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Shrimp Bisque": "Redbreast 21 with shrimp bisque — 21-year aged pot still Irish against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Redbreast 21 rides the bisque at collector register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Shrimp Cocktail": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish overshadows the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Redbreast 21 Year for the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Swordfish": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Redbreast 21 Year for the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|The Tomahawk": "Redbreast 21 with the Tomahawk — aged pot still Irish against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; even 21yr aged Irish is too light for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Tomato Basil": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the smooth triple-distilled register matches the tomato basil, and the grain register softens the bowl. Works; the aging is wasted on the salad.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Truffle Fries": "Redbreast 21 with truffle fries — aged pot still Irish against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Redbreast 21's aged pot still oiliness meets the earthy-rich at collector register.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Redbreast 21 Year for the steak course.",
  "Redbreast 21 Year|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the malt-grain blend handles the curry, and the soft malt rounds the bowl. Works; the spice is underused by the salad.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Asparagus": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak depth lays under the asparagus, and the oak frames the side. Works; a gentle composed match at the premium tier.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: cheese-and-cream potatoes meet George Remus tribute MGP. Strong; Remus 15 handles the richness at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Beignets": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon depth settles into the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Bone Marrow": "Remus 15 Gatsby with bone marrow — Gatsby-era tribute MGP 15yr against umami fat. Excellent; Remus 15's aged rye-spice + cherry engages marrow at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Bone-In Filet": "Remus 15 Gatsby with the bone-in filet — MGP high-rye 15yr Gatsby Reserve against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Remus 15's aged rye-spice frames the cut at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Broccoli Cheddar": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon weight handles the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Broccolini": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon depth settles into the broccolini, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; the match is gentle and approachable.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet George Remus MGP 15yr. Works; Remus 15's rye-spice meets the glaze at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Burrata": "Remus 15 Gatsby with burrata — George Remus tribute against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Butternut Squash": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the squash bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Carrot Cake": "Remus 15 Gatsby with carrot cake — MGP high-rye Gatsby Reserve against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; Remus 15's rye-spice + cherry is the featured MGP-aged-carrot-cake pairing — where OGD 16 brings aged-Beam rye-spice, Remus brings MGP's rye-and-cherry.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Cheesecake": "Remus 15 Gatsby with cheesecake — 15yr MGP Gatsby Reserve against custard. Works; Remus 15's rye-spice threads the cheesecake at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Chilean Seabass": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve on the steak course instead.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Chocolate Brownie": "Remus 15 Gatsby with the chocolate brownie — MGP high-rye 15yr against cocoa. Strong; Remus 15's rye-spice + cherry meets cocoa with distinctive rye-cocoa resonance.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Chocolate Cake": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon backbone carries the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; curated single-barrel slightly over-specified.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Clam Chowder": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon depth settles into the chowder, and the oak frames the cream. Works; the pour's assertiveness is a bit much for the soup but doesn't clash.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Cowboy Ribeye": "Remus 15 Gatsby with the cowboy ribeye — MGP high-rye 15yr against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Remus 15's aged rye-spice frames the ribeye at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Crab Cake": "Remus 15 Gatsby with crab cake — MGP 15yr Gatsby Reserve against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Creamed Spinach": "Remus 15 Gatsby with creamed spinach — 15yr MGP Gatsby Reserve against dairy-rich side. Strong; Remus 15's rye-spice cuts the cream at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Creamy Potato": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Creme Brulee": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; wine-cask complexity gives the dessert layered premium character.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Escargot": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's bold cherry-juniper character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Faroe Island Salmon": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve belongs on the steak course.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets MGP's Ross & Squibb Distillery, George Remus \"King of Bootleggers\" tribute, 15yr Gatsby-era release, high-rye MGP mash bill with rye-spice + cherry character. Remus 15 Gatsby's high-rye aged-MGP character frames tenderloin with Prohibition-era tribute storytelling — where HH-family brings dried-fruit-aged, Remus brings high-rye-aged-MGP spice. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|French Onion": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Grilled Caesar": "Remus 15 Gatsby with the grilled Caesar — MGP 15yr against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Remus 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Gumbo": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the brown-spirit warmth threads the gumbo, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; AE Triple Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon body sits with the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; boutique-sourced doesn't drive.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|House Wedge": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon backbone carries the wedge, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Kansas City": "Remus 15 Gatsby with the KC — MGP 15yr high-rye Gatsby Reserve against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Remus 15's aged rye-spice is the featured MGP-aged-KC pairing — where OGD 16's high-rye Beam takes this, Remus brings MGP's own Prohibition-tribute approach.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Loaded Potato": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Lobster Mac": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the brown-spirit warmth threads the lobster mac, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve|Market Fish": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve belongs on the steak course.",
