  "Reyka Vodka|Creme Brulee": "Reyka Vodka's crystalline neutrality mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- clean vodka has nothing to add against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reyka Vodka lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Escargot": "Reyka with escargot — small-batch Icelandic against garlic-butter snails. Strong; Reyka's mineral cuts butter, though Belvedere's pepper does it more aggressively.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Faroe Island Salmon": "Reyka with the salmon — lava-filtered Icelandic vodka against Faroe Island salmon. Excellent; the Nordic-adjacent pairing is thematic — glacial vodka meets North Atlantic fish at a register no other vodka on the menu touches.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Filet Mignon": "Reyka with the filet — lava-filtered Icelandic against tenderloin. Works; vodka over-specified; Reyka's mineral adds a distinctive register if a guest insists.",
  "Reyka Vodka|French Onion": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality holds with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Grilled Caesar": "Reyka with the grilled Caesar — Icelandic vodka against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Reyka's mineral-glacial character meets the char cleanly; Ketel's citrus is still the sharper vodka-Caesar.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Gumbo": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality stays alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Reyka Vodka|House Wedge": "Reyka with the House Wedge — small-batch Icelandic against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; the mineral character brightens the wedge with distinctive lift.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Kansas City": "Reyka with the KC — Icelandic vodka against an 18oz strip. Avoid; vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Loaded Potato": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality plays cleanly against the loaded potato's richness — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Lobster Mac": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Market Fish": "Reyka with the market fish — small-batch Icelandic against featured fish. Works; Reyka's mineral pairs thematically with most fish preparations.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Mocha Creme": "Reyka Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a vodka. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Reyka Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Mushroom Bisque": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality leans against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Mushrooms": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Reyka Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Reyka Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Porterhouse": "Reyka Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reyka Vodka belongs on another course.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Prime Tartare": "Reyka with the tartare — lava-filtered Icelandic vodka against raw beef. Strong; Reyka's mineral echoes the tartare's meaty minerality, though Belvedere's rye pepper is the more distinctive tartare match.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Rainbow Trout": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality holds steady against the trout's delicate flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Roast Half Chicken": "Reyka with the chicken — Icelandic glacial vodka against roasted poultry. Works; Reyka's mineral brings a cleaner lift than softer vodkas offer.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality lifts the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality reads alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Seafood Tower": "Reyka with the seafood tower — lava-filtered Icelandic vodka against the raw bar. Excellent; Reyka's mineral-glacial character is the most thematically right vodka for a cold seafood tower — oceanic meets oceanic.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Seared Scallops": "Reyka with seared scallops — Icelandic vodka, lava-filtered glacial water, wheat-and-barley. Excellent; Reyka's mineral character is the distinctive match for scallops — the oceanic pairing where the glacial profile echoes the fish's origin. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Seasonal Soup": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality leans against the seasonal soup's house build — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Seasonal Vegetables": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality plays cleanly against the seasonal garden plate — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Shrimp Bisque": "Reyka with shrimp bisque — Icelandic glacial vodka against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Reyka's mineral cuts the bisque's richness where Chopin's creamy vodka rides it.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Shrimp Cocktail": "Reyka with shrimp cocktail — small-batch Icelandic vodka against cold shrimp. Excellent; Reyka's mineral profile makes the classic pairing distinctive rather than default — a conversation starter.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Swordfish": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality finds register with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Reyka Vodka|The Tomahawk": "Reyka Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Reyka Vodka for another course.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Tomato Basil": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality plays cleanly against the tomato basil's bright acid — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Truffle Fries": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality sits beside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality holds steady against the tuna's seared rare crust — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Reyka Vodka|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Reyka Vodka's crisp Icelandic-glacial neutrality plays cleanly against the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Asparagus": "Rishi's Old Antique's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon depth settles into the asparagus, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Rishi's Old Antique with au gratin potatoes — retailer-label against cheese-and-cream. Works; boutique over-specified.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Beignets": "Rishi's Old Antique's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the caramel-spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Remus 15 handles dessert density at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Bone Marrow": "Rishi's Old Antique with bone marrow — aged retailer-label against umami fat. The boutique-sourced character meets marrow with retailer-label authority. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Bone-In Filet": "Rishi's Old Antique's grain-and-oak bourbon register rests at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the caramel rounds the cut. Strong; the premium character sits with the side at matched register.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Broccoli Cheddar": "Rishi's Old Antique's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; friendly accessible match.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Broccolini": "Rishi's Old Antique's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon depth settles into the broccolini, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; rare-release wasted on salad.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Brussels and Belly": "Rishi's Old Antique with Brussels + Belly — Rishi's label meets maple-glazed pork belly. The aged-sourced character cuts the glaze with retailer-label distinctiveness. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Burrata": "Rishi's Old Antique with burrata — fresh dairy against boutique retailer-label. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Butternut Squash": "Rishi's Old Antique's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon weight handles the squash bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for the side — save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Carrot Cake": "Rishi's Old Antique with carrot cake — retailer-label against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; boutique-sourced finds cake spice with retailer-label character.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Cheesecake": "Rishi's Old Antique's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the caramel-spice register matches the cheesecake, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Chilean Seabass": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Rishi's Old Antique belongs on the steak course.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Chocolate Brownie": "Rishi's Old Antique with the chocolate brownie — aged retailer-label against cocoa. Strong; boutique-sourced meets the brownie at retailer-label register.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Chocolate Cake": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Clam Chowder": "Rishi's Old Antique's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the oak depth lays under the chowder, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Cowboy Ribeye": "Rishi's Old Antique with the Cowboy Ribeye — retailer-label aged bourbon against cap fat. The boutique-label aged character handles the ribeye at premium-sourced register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Crab Cake": "Rishi's Old Antique with crab cake — retailer-label against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Creamed Spinach": "Rishi's Old Antique with creamed spinach — retailer-label against dairy. Strong; aged-sourced sits with richness.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Creamy Potato": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Creme Brulee": "Rishi's Old Antique's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Escargot": "Rishi's Old Antique's crisp floral-herbal character finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Faroe Island Salmon": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Rishi's Old Antique for the steak course.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Filet Mignon": "Rishi's Old Antique with the filet — retailer-label aged bourbon against the 10oz cut. Strong; boutique-label is capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|French Onion": "Rishi's Old Antique's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; the unique maturation story adds interest to a gentle dessert match.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Grilled Caesar": "Rishi's Old Antique with the grilled Caesar — aged retailer-label against charred romaine. Works; boutique-sourced doesn't drive.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Gumbo": "Rishi's Old Antique's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the gumbo, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; gentle match.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Rishi's Old Antique's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon body sits with the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|House Wedge": "Rishi's Old Antique with the House Wedge — Rishi's retailer-label against iceberg and bleu. Works; boutique-sourced wasted on salad.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Kansas City": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets Rishi's Old Antique — Rishi's retailer-label aged sourced bourbon. Premium sourced under Rishi's boutique banner; distinct from Rishi's-picked single-barrel Bardstown by being a label release rather than specific-cask selection. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Loaded Potato": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the brown-spirit warmth threads the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; complex profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Lobster Mac": "Rishi's Old Antique's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon weight handles the lobster mac, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Market Fish": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Rishi's Old Antique on the steak course instead.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Mocha Creme": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon backbone carries the mocha creme, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the toast notes don't really interplay with the soup but don't clash.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Mushroom Bisque": "Rishi's Old Antique's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; milestone-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Mushrooms": "Rishi's Old Antique with the mushrooms — retailer-label aged against earthy umami. Strong; boutique-sourced layers into mushroom depth with retailer-label distinctiveness.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Rishi's Old Antique with the PB brownie — aged retailer-label against peanut butter and cocoa. The boutique-sourced character gives the dessert retailer-label depth. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Porterhouse": "Rishi's Old Antique with the porterhouse — retailer-label aged across strip and filet. Works; boutique covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Prime Tartare": "Rishi's Old Antique's crisp floral-herbal character holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the caramel-oak depth threads the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Rainbow Trout": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Rishi's Old Antique for the steak course.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Roast Half Chicken": "Rishi's Old Antique's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; Remus 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Rishi's Old Antique's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak depth lays under the chickpea soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; Boss Hog's allocation story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Rishi's Old Antique for the steak course.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; annual-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Seafood Tower": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Rishi's Old Antique for the steak course.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Seared Scallops": "Rishi's Old Antique with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against retailer-label. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Seasonal Soup": "Rishi's Old Antique's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Seasonal Vegetables": "Rishi's Old Antique's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; bonded weight pushes past the soup's register slightly.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Shrimp Bisque": "Rishi's Old Antique's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a soup — save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Shrimp Cocktail": "Rishi's Old Antique with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against retailer-label. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Swordfish": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Rishi's Old Antique for the steak course.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|The Tomahawk": "Rishi's Old Antique with the Tomahawk — aged retailer-label against bone-in ribeye. Strong; boutique character covers the cut with retailer-label authority.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Tomato Basil": "Rishi's Old Antique's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the oak depth lays under the tomato basil, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Truffle Fries": "Rishi's Old Antique with truffle fries — retailer-label aged against truffle-parmesan. The boutique-sourced character gives the side a retailer-label frame. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Rishi's Old Antique for the steak course.",
  "Rishi's Old Antique|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Rishi's Old Antique's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon body sits with the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a soup — save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon weight handles the roast chicken, and the rounded sweetness brightens the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon weight handles the roast chicken, and the oak-and-spice register settles the plate. Works; the 10-year depth gives the dessert substantial premium frame.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's bold cherry-coffee cassis reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|2 James Gin": "2 James with the chicken — Michigan craft gin against roasted poultry. Works; 2 James supports chicken at local-craft register.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the malt-driven body wraps the roast chicken, and the malt-driven sweetness softens the plate. Works; extended-aged premium wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the malt-and-oak threads the roast chicken, and the heather brightens the plate. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for the side — save the rare-cask bottle for the protein course.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino with the roast half chicken — filtered aged against roasted chicken. Works; aged-but-clear character composes with the dish.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado with the roast half chicken — aged reposado against roasted chicken. Works; the rich oak is slightly over-specified for the dish.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "* Alexander Valley Chardonnay's crisp white-wine character runs straight into the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bright acid lifts the roast chicken, and the chalky minerality plays against the plate. Peak Napa Chardonnay for chicken across most seasonal accompaniments. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Sonoma Bordeaux blend — plum, blackberry, mocha, soft tannin, smooth. The soft structure plays against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak crowd-pleaser Bordeaux blend for chicken across most seasonal preparations. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness overwhelms the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- amaro bitterness buries the chicken's mild herbed-skin frame. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Amaro Nonino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character buries the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- sugar smothers the chicken's savory frame — wait for dessert. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; Caribbean Cask frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon backbone carries the roast chicken, and the rounded sweetness brightens the plate. Works; Remus 15 frames the two-cut plate at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; flagship depth gives the dessert premium aged character.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge overwhelms the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- amaro bitterness buries the chicken's mild herbed-skin frame. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Asparagus": "Roast Half Chicken into the asparagus -- the herb-butter chicken frames the bright green side. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the main.",
  "Roast Half Chicken|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The chicken and the au gratin on the plate -- the herbed crisp skin meets the gruyere-baked potato. Strong; the side holds the chicken's weight without competing.",
