  "Russell's Reserve 10 Year|Swordfish": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year's aged Wild Turkey oak swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Russell's Reserve 10 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Russell's Reserve 10 Year|The Tomahawk": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year's caramel-honey character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Russell's Reserve 10 Year|Tomato Basil": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the brown-spirit warmth threads the tomato basil, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Russell's Reserve 10 Year|Truffle Fries": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with truffle fries — aged Wild Turkey oak against truffle-parmesan. The classic Kentucky depth frames the truffle without the Wellers' wheat-sweet doubling; a sophisticated non-wheated pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Russell's Reserve 10 Year|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year's aged Wild Turkey oak clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Russell's Reserve 10 Year for the steak course.",
  "Russell's Reserve 10 Year|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the curry, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the toast notes don't really interplay with the soup but don't clash.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the 1792 Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. 1792 Sweet Wheat belongs on the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. 1881 Napa Valley belongs on the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's herbal-botanical edge meets at register with the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the juniper-led botanicals carry the halibut, and the floral-and-herb register brightens the plate. Strong; the gin option when the gold call is spoken for.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. A Midnight's Winter Dram belongs on the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Aberlour 16 for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's caramel-char character sits with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the crisp body frames the fish cleanly, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's barrel-aged tequila weight reads alongside the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the añejo register carries the halibut, and the caramel-agave register brightens the plate. Works; iconic extra añejo is composed with the dessert.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay -- Sonoma with ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth texture. The bright acid lifts the halibut, and the citrus cuts the plate. Strong; the call reads at register without overshooting.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Alexander Valley Cyrus for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Alexander Valley Homestead Red for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bitter herbs flatten delicate fish — the protein can't lift the amaro's register. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Amaro Nonino on the steak course instead.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Ancho Reyes belongs on the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Angel's Envy lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Angel's Envy Cask Strength for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Angel's Envy Triple Cask lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bitter herbs flatten delicate fish — the protein can't lift the amaro's register. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Aperol for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Asparagus": "The halibut alongside the asparagus -- the halibut's salt-cured flesh meets the asparagus' green-vegetal edge cleanly. Strong; the call reads at register without overshooting against the main.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The halibut alongside the au gratin -- the halibut's salt-cured flesh meets the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust cleanly. Strong; the pairing carries its register cleanly against the main.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bitter herbs flatten delicate fish — the protein can't lift the amaro's register. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Aviation": "Aviation's crisp almond-cherry build matches the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals lifts the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Avion 44": "Avion 44's aged-agave character reads alongside the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the rested-agave depth sits with the halibut, and the cooked-agave note underlines the plate. Works; the wine influence is unusual here but doesn't quite drive.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's green-agave-and-cane lift meets at register with the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the halibut, and the bright blanco register underlines the plate. Strong; the filtered clean character provides a through-line at everyday register.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum -- Carta Blanca, workhorse light rum, clean. The bright cane-and-citrus weight carries the halibut, and the Caribbean register brightens the plate. Strong; clean additive-free cuts the garlic-butter at craft register.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Baileys Irish Cream belongs on the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Baker's": "Baker's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Baker's lands on the steak course — not here.",
