  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Burrata": "The halibut after the burrata -- the butter-laced halibut carries into the heirloom burrata plate. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Butternut Squash": "Salt-Cured Halibut after the butternut squash -- the silky sweet squash cream opens with autumnal warmth, the halibut following with firm cured flesh the squash's sweetness primes the salt-cure to land on. Strong; sweet into salt reads composed.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cade Cabernet Sauvignon's Howell Mountain Cab character clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Cade Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila's rested-agave body finds neutral with the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the aged-agave weight wraps the halibut, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the plate. Works; luxury over-specified for small plate.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Campari": "Campari's aperitivo at its most textbook overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bitter herbs flatten delicate fish — the protein can't lift the amaro's register. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Campari for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Canadian Club for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Canadian Club 43 Year": "Canadian Club 43 Year's Canadian whisky character flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Canadian Club 43 Year for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- light spirit reads thin against a main protein — needs a fuller pour. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a light spirit. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Captain Morgan Rum for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Caribou Crossing on the steak course instead.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Carrot Cake": "The halibut into the carrot cake -- the salt-cured firm flesh hands off to spiced cake and cream-cheese frosting, walnut crunch on the close. Works; the cured main and the spiced dessert share the meal at separate registers.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the wine's sweetness buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Casa LBV Port for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Casamigos Mezcal": "Casamigos Mezcal's smoky agave character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- mezcal smoke is wrong register for the main fish course. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a mezcal. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Casamigos Mezcal lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab against salt-cured halibut with sweet soy glaze — concentrated cocoa-mocha body crushes the delicate cured fish. Never pair. Route Caymus to the steak course and take halibut with Chardonnay, Chablis, or Pinot Blanc.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- sugar on delicate fish is wrong direction — the plate needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Chambord lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Cheesecake": "The halibut closes with the cheesecake -- the salt-cured firm flesh hands off to dense dairy custard, graham crust and berry compote framing the close. Works; the cure meets the dairy weight without strain on the close.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Chocolate Brownie": "The halibut closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the salt-cured flesh gives way to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Works; the cured main and the dense close share weight on the plate.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Chocolate Cake": "After the halibut, the chocolate cake -- the salt-cured firm flesh yields to layered cocoa-and-ganache, the vanilla scoop tying the close. Works; the cured-fish depth and the layered chocolate share the meal cleanly.",
