  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Johnnie Walker Red": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Johnnie Walker Red for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon's classical-Napa Cab elegance overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Loire Sancerre — refined tension-filled mineral, citrus, flint. The flinty citrus tension lifts halibut's firm flesh and the mineral plays against the salt-cure. Strong Sancerre for salt-cured halibut — Loire precision on the cured classic.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation by Colgin's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Jubilation by Colgin on the steak course instead.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- heavy spirit weight buries the main protein — needs a wine or lighter spirit. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Jung and Wulff Guyana for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- heavy spirit weight buries the main protein — needs a wine or lighter spirit. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Jung and Wulff Trinidad lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Kahlua on the steak course instead.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Keenan Chardonnay": "Salt-Cured Halibut: this delicate seafood's mild, flaky flesh meets this rich, oak-aged Chardonnay's butter, stone fruit, and full-bodied weight. Oaky Chardonnay's butter and stone fruit lock into rich fish — texture matching texture. A headliner white pour for rich fish. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
