  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Le Volte dell'Ornellaia on the steak course instead.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Lemon Lavender Gin Martini's citrus-floral build holds steady against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the cocktail lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Les Pallières Raciné": "Les Pallières Raciné's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Les Pallières Raciné on the steak course instead.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Liberator Gin": "Liberator Gin's gin lift with floral-and-pepper sits at register with the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the juniper-led botanicals carry the halibut, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; the gin option when the gold call is spoken for.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Licor 43 for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the liqueur's sweet register buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Limoncello lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Willamette Pinot — dark cherry, forest floor, refined structure, low tannin. The low-tannin silk lifts halibut's firm flesh and the cherry plays against sweet soy glaze. Strong Oregon Pinot for salt-cured halibut — unconventional red on the cured classic.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Little Book": "Little Book's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Little Book for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Loaded Potato": "Salt-Cured Halibut into the loaded potato -- the butter-laced halibut sits with the indulgent loaded soup. Works; safe opener, but not the headline pick.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Lobster Mac": "The halibut into the lobster mac -- the lighter course primes the richer, the meal carries register lift. Works; safe alongside, but not the headline pick.",
