  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Woodford Reserve belongs on the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Woodford Reserve Double Oaked lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Woodford Reserve Rye for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Woodinville Rye for the steak course.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character reads alongside the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the halibut, and the green-fruit edge brightens the plate. Works; own-plus-sourced over-specified.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character leans against the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the halibut, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the plate. Works; doesn't drive.",
  "Salt-Cured Halibut|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- aperitivo bitterness fights gentle flesh without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Asparagus": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Sambuca on the steak course instead.",
  "Sambuca|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Beignets": "Beignets: warm sugar-dusted pastry meets Sambuca. Excellent; the sweet-anise character plays with fresh sugar-dust at Italian-digestif register — sweeter pairing than Mata Hari's 60% punch.",
  "Sambuca|Bone Marrow": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sambuca belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Bone-In Filet": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Sambuca for another course.",
  "Sambuca|Broccoli Cheddar": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Sambuca on the steak course instead.",
  "Sambuca|Broccolini": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Sambuca belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Brussels and Belly": "Sambuca's sweet anise-elderflower character matches the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the anise frames the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Sambuca|Burrata": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Sambuca belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Butternut Squash": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Sambuca on the steak course instead.",
  "Sambuca|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Sambuca. Works; elderflower-anise-botanicals amplify cake spice at Italian-dessert register.",
  "Sambuca|Cheesecake": "Sambuca: Italian anise liqueur (Molinari benchmark), star anise + elderflower + botanicals, traditionally served con la mosca (with 3 coffee beans). Excellent on cheesecake; the sweet-anise character threads custard-cream at Italian-digestif register — sweeter, more dessert-friendly than Mata Hari Absinthe.",
  "Sambuca|Chilean Seabass": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert-grade sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs a crisp white instead. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Sambuca on the steak course instead.",
  "Sambuca|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Sambuca. Excellent; sweet-anise-elderflower threads chocolate at Italian register — the coffee-bean tradition (con la mosca) already suggests chocolate-coffee-anise harmony.",
  "Sambuca|Chocolate Cake": "Sambuca's after-dinner liqueur weight rests at register with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the digestif body mirrors the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Sambuca|Clam Chowder": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- sugar piled onto cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Cowboy Ribeye": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Sambuca for another course.",
  "Sambuca|Crab Cake": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Creamed Spinach": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Sambuca on the steak course instead.",
  "Sambuca|Creamy Potato": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Sambuca on the steak course instead.",
  "Sambuca|Creme Brulee": "Creme Brulee: caramelized custard meets Sambuca. Excellent; the sweet star-anise-elderflower profile meets burnt-sugar-cream at Italian-digestif register — the sweeter anise pairing on dessert.",
  "Sambuca|Escargot": "Escargot: garlic-herb butter meets Sambuca. Excellent; the Italian-anise-botanical character engages herb butter at Italian register — sweeter and less bracing than Green Chartreuse or Mata Hari.",
  "Sambuca|Faroe Island Salmon": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the liqueur's sugar piles onto the fish's natural oils with no lift. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Filet Mignon": "Sambuca's syrupy-sweet liqueur body leans against the filet's lean buttery tenderness: the syrupy liqueur weight wraps the lean buttery filet, and the digestif sweetness softens the cut. Works; the two sides share the meal without competing.",
  "Sambuca|French Onion": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: char-romaine with anchovy-parmesan meets Sambuca. Excellent; the sweet-anise-elderflower character meets char-and-brine at Italian register.",
  "Sambuca|Gumbo": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Sambuca on the steak course instead.",
  "Sambuca|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Sambuca on the steak course instead.",
  "Sambuca|House Wedge": "Sambuca's sweet anise-elderflower character lifts the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the anise threads the herb edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Sambuca|Kansas City": "Sambuca's after-dinner liqueur weight sits alongside the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the digestif body mirrors the strip steak, and the soft-spice register brightens the cut. Works; the dessert closes without crowding.",
  "Sambuca|Loaded Potato": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the liqueur's sweetness buries the soup's creamy base instead of supporting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Sambuca lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Sambuca|Lobster Mac": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character doubles down on the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- sweet liqueur and dairy-rich side double the indulgence with no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Sambuca on the steak course instead.",
  "Sambuca|Market Fish": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- sugar on delicate fish is wrong direction — the plate needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Sambuca on the steak course instead.",
  "Sambuca|Mocha Creme": "Sambuca's sweet anise-elderflower character lifts the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the anise underlines the cream. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Sambuca|Mushroom Bisque": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Mushrooms": "Sambuca's sweet anise-elderflower character holds steady against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the sweet character sits at digestif register. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Sambuca|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Sambuca's after-dinner liqueur weight leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the sweet-liqueur register carries the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Strong; the pairing carries its register cleanly against the cut.",
  "Sambuca|Porterhouse": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Sambuca for another course.",
  "Sambuca|Prime Tartare": "Sambuca's after-dinner liqueur weight leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the herbal-sweet weight matches the tartare, and the caramel underlines the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Sambuca|Rainbow Trout": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Roast Half Chicken": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character buries the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- sugar smothers the chicken's savory frame — wait for dessert. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- a sweet liqueur is dessert-course energy — wrong register for any main protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- sugar on a clean vegetable side is wrong direction — needs herb-acid lift, not sweetness. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Sambuca belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Seafood Tower": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Sambuca lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Sambuca|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Sambuca. Avoid; anise liqueur wrong for scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Sambuca|Seasonal Soup": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- sweet liqueur on savory broth jars — sugar pulls one way, the salt-and-spice broth pulls the other. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Seasonal Vegetables": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- a sweet liqueur on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Shrimp Bisque": "Sambuca's after-dinner liqueur weight sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the digestif body mirrors the shrimp bisque, and the honey threads the cream. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Sambuca|Shrimp Cocktail": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character smothers the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sugar buries the delicate shellfish without any acid to balance. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Swordfish": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Sambuca on the steak course instead.",
  "Sambuca|The Tomahawk": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character flattens the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Sambuca for another course.",
  "Sambuca|Tomato Basil": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Sambuca on the steak course instead.",
  "Sambuca|Truffle Fries": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character flattens the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- sugar on starch is dessert territory — wrong course for the supporting plate. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- sugar dulls the crust's clean char instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Sambuca belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sambuca|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the soup-salad course needs lift or acid — sweet liqueur brings neither. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Sambuca lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Asparagus": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis finds neutral with the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the mocha plays against the lactic richness. Strong Paso value Bordeaux blend for au gratin — solid full-body on the cheesy side.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Beignets": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the San Simeon Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Bone Marrow": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis sits with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the coffee threads the cream. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Bone-In Filet": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the Cab structure frames the tenderloin, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Broccoli Cheddar": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Broccolini": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis sits beside the broccolini's tender green — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis underlines the green edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Brussels and Belly": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts pork-belly fat and the mocha plays against charred brussels. Strong Paso value Bordeaux blend for Brussels and Belly — solid full-body cuts through the rich starter.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Burrata": "Paso Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend against burrata's creamy-delicate center — full-body Bordeaux-style structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Butternut Squash": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Carrot Cake": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. San Simeon Stormwatch belongs on the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Cheesecake": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the San Simeon Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Chilean Seabass": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. San Simeon Stormwatch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Chocolate Brownie": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. San Simeon Stormwatch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Chocolate Cake": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. San Simeon Stormwatch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Clam Chowder": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis sits beside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Cowboy Ribeye": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin, full body. The full body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the mocha plays against beefy char. Strong Paso value Bordeaux blend for ribeye — solid full-body on the bold cut at accessible price.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Crab Cake": "Paso Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — full-body mocha-cherry structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Stormwatch for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Creamed Spinach": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the mocha plays against the spinach base. Peak Paso value Bordeaux blend for creamed spinach — solid full-body reframes the rich side. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Creamy Potato": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis stays alongside the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Creme Brulee": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the San Simeon Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Escargot": "San Simeon Stormwatch's powerful blackcurrant body meets at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the concentrated dark-fruit weight handles the buttered escargot, and the espresso-cocoa note rounds the starter. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Faroe Island Salmon": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. San Simeon Stormwatch belongs on the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Filet Mignon": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin, full body. The medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the mocha plays against escargot-butter. Strong Paso value Bordeaux blend for filet — solid full-body on the delicate cut.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|French Onion": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee meets the caramel. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Grilled Caesar": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mocha plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong Paso value Bordeaux blend for grilled Caesar — solid full-body on the layered salad.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Gumbo": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Honey Roasted Carrots": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis reads alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the coffee meets the caramel. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|House Wedge": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark cherry plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as Paso value Bordeaux blend for the wedge — solid full-body reframes the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Kansas City": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin, full body, elegant finish. The full body handles Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the mocha plays against beefy char. Peak Paso value Bordeaux blend for the strip — solid full-body on the American steakhouse cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Loaded Potato": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis sits beside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Lobster Mac": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, no friction.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Market Fish": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the San Simeon Stormwatch on the steak course instead.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Mocha Creme": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the San Simeon Stormwatch on the steak course instead.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Mushroom Bisque": "San Simeon Stormwatch's deep dark-fruit and grippy tannin reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the structured body wraps the mushroom bisque, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Mushrooms": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin. The mocha echoes roasted-mushroom glutamate and the dark cherry plays against earth depth. Peak Paso value Bordeaux blend for mushrooms — solid full-body on the earthy side. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Peanut Butter Brownie": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. San Simeon Stormwatch belongs on the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Porterhouse": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin, full body. The medium tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the mocha plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Works as Paso value Bordeaux blend for Porterhouse — solid full-body on the dual-cut.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Prime Tartare": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Rainbow Trout": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the San Simeon Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Roast Half Chicken": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin, elegant. The elegant structure plays against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak Paso value Bordeaux blend for chicken across most preparations. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Salt-Cured Halibut": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the San Simeon Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis leans against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Seafood Tower": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the San Simeon Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Seared Scallops": "Paso Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend against scallop sweetness — full-body mocha-cherry structure buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Seasonal Soup": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Seasonal Vegetables": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis leans against the seasonal garden plate — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Shrimp Bisque": "San Simeon Stormwatch's structured dark-fruit weight sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the structured body wraps the shrimp bisque, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Shrimp Cocktail": "Paso Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — full-body red and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Swordfish": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the San Simeon Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|The Tomahawk": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis reads alongside the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the velvet-on-fat coherence runs through the plate, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Tomato Basil": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright acid — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis frames the herb butter. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Truffle Fries": "Paso Robles Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend — dark cherry, blackcurrant, mocha, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts truffle-fry potato fat and the mocha plays against the truffle-oil umami. Peak Paso value Bordeaux blend for truffle fries — solid full-body on the umami hit. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. San Simeon Stormwatch belongs on the steak course.",
  "San Simeon Stormwatch|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis holds with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
