  "Sauteed Garlic Spinach|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye backbone drives the garlic spinach, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; BH Red Wine Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Sauteed Garlic Spinach|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye backbone drives the garlic spinach, and the rye-pepper note underlines the side. Works; the blend's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Sauteed Garlic Spinach|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye spice cuts the garlic spinach, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; BIB slightly over-specified.",
  "Sauteed Garlic Spinach|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye backbone drives the garlic spinach, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Sauteed Garlic Spinach|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye-and-oak weight handles the garlic spinach, and the rye-grain bite cuts the side. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Sauteed Garlic Spinach|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon backbone carries the garlic spinach, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; bonded weight pushes past the soup's register slightly.",
  "Sauteed Garlic Spinach|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Sauteed Garlic Spinach|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon body sits with the garlic spinach, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Sauteed Garlic Spinach|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the high-rye spice register matches the garlic spinach, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the side. Works; the bourbon's character is distinctive but the pairing isn't a standout.",
  "Sauteed Garlic Spinach|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye-and-oak weight handles the garlic spinach, and the rye-grain bite cuts the side. Works; craft-blender complexity over-specified.",
  "Sauteed Garlic Spinach|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the garlic spinach, and the umami-tinged finish frames the side. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Sauteed Garlic Spinach|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the garlic spinach, and the umami-tinged finish frames the side. Works; boutique over-specified.",
  "Sauteed Garlic Spinach|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Yellow Chartreuse lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Asparagus": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sweet wine on green vegetable is wrong direction — needs crisp white. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Sauternes Glass on the steak course instead.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Beignets": "Sauternes — Sémillon-Sauvignon Blanc with noble rot (botrytis cinerea), apricot, honey, orange marmalade, luscious unctuous, bright acid. The honey clings to hot choux-fried dough and the bright acid cuts powdered-sugar sweetness. Peak Sauternes for beignets — botrytis-and-acid lift on the airy bite. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Bone Marrow": "Never pair Sauternes Glass with bone marrow — luscious unctuous honey drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and noble-rot sweetness clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. Sauternes is a dessert or cheese-course pour.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Bone-In Filet": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Sauternes Glass lands on another course — not here.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Broccoli Cheddar": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Sauternes Glass belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Broccolini": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- sweet wine on green vegetable is wrong direction — needs crisp white. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Sauternes Glass belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Brussels and Belly": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Sauternes Glass on the steak course instead.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Burrata": "Sauternes — Sémillon-Sauvignon Blanc with noble rot (botrytis cinerea), apricot, honey, orange marmalade, luscious unctuous, bright acid. The honey bridges fresh cream and the bright acid cuts tomato-basil-sweetness cleanly. Strong Sauternes for burrata — Sauternes-on-cheese classic applied to caprese.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Butternut Squash": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Sauternes Glass on the steak course instead.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Carrot Cake": "Sauternes — Sémillon-Sauvignon Blanc with noble rot (botrytis cinerea), apricot, honey, orange marmalade, luscious unctuous, bright acid. The orange marmalade lifts allspice-cinnamon spice and the apricot plays with cream-cheese frosting. Peak Sauternes for carrot cake — botrytis-on-spice match. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Cheesecake": "Bordeaux botrytis — apricot, honey, orange marmalade, bright acid. The acidity cuts dense cream cheese cleanly and the honey aligns with the vanilla-bean cream. One of the great dessert wines on the list for a guest who wants peak sophistication alongside cheesecake. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Chilean Seabass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Sauternes Glass belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Chocolate Brownie": "Sauternes — Sémillon-Sauvignon Blanc with noble rot (botrytis cinerea), apricot, honey, orange marmalade, luscious unctuous, bright acid. The orange marmalade bridges whipped-chocolate frosting, but botrytis honey and fudge cocoa read parallel rather than complementary. Strong Sauternes for chocolate brownie — Vintage Port handles cocoa harder.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Chocolate Cake": "Sauternes — Sémillon-Sauvignon Blanc with noble rot (botrytis cinerea), apricot, honey, orange marmalade, luscious unctuous, bright acid. The orange marmalade plays with fudgy chocolate crumb, but the unctuous sweet reads parallel to cocoa. Works for chocolate cake — Vintage Port's structure handles cocoa-on-cocoa harder.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Clam Chowder": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Cowboy Ribeye": "Never pair Sauternes Glass with Cowboy Ribeye — botrytis honey and orange marmalade muddle marbled-ribeye char and luscious unctuous clashes with grilled-beef savory. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. Sauternes is a finishing pour.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Crab Cake": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sauternes Glass belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Creamed Spinach": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sauternes Glass lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Creamy Potato": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness doubles down on the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Creme Brulee": "Bordeaux botrytis wine — Sémillon and Sauvignon Blanc with noble rot. Apricot, honey, orange marmalade, bright acid keeping it from cloying. The honey-apricot notes align with vanilla custard; the acid cuts the cream. One of the great pairings on the list. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Escargot": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Faroe Island Salmon": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Filet Mignon": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet wine smothers the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Sauternes Glass for another course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|French Onion": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Grilled Caesar": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sauternes Glass belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Gumbo": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Sauternes Glass belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|House Wedge": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Sauternes Glass lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Kansas City": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- dessert wine is post-meal — wrong register for the steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sauternes Glass lands on another course — not here.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Loaded Potato": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Lobster Mac": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Sauternes Glass belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Market Fish": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the wine's sweetness buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Sauternes Glass on the steak course instead.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Mocha Creme": "Bordeaux botrytis — apricot, honey, orange marmalade, bright acid. The acid cuts mocha crème's cream-and-cocoa density. Works alongside — though crème brûlée peaks higher on Sauternes since the torched sugar meets the honey more directly.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Mushroom Bisque": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness doubles down on the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Sauternes Glass on the steak course instead.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Mushrooms": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Sauternes — Sémillon-Sauvignon Blanc with noble rot (botrytis cinerea), apricot, honey, orange marmalade, luscious unctuous, bright acid. The apricot lifts peanut-butter-frosting density and the bright acid cuts fudge-brownie richness. Strong Sauternes for peanut butter brownie — Vintage Port's chocolate-depth fits brownies harder.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Porterhouse": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- dessert wine on prime cut is wrong-course entirely — save it for after. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Sauternes Glass belongs on another course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Prime Tartare": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Rainbow Trout": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- dessert wine on delicate fish is wrong-course energy — needs a dry white, not honeyed sugar. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Roast Half Chicken": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sauternes Glass belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- dessert wine on delicate fish is wrong-course energy — needs a dry white, not honeyed sugar. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Sauternes Glass belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Sauternes Glass on the steak course instead.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Seafood Tower": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Sauternes Glass on the steak course instead.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Seared Scallops": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sauternes Glass lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Seasonal Soup": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- honeyed sugar on savory broth jars — wrong direction for the course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Seasonal Vegetables": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- sweet wine on green vegetable is wrong direction — needs crisp white. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Shrimp Bisque": "Sauternes — Sémillon-Sauvignon Blanc with noble rot (botrytis cinerea), apricot, honey, orange marmalade, luscious unctuous, bright acid. The honey plays with tomato-cream-sweetness and the bright acid cuts shellfish-cream weight. Strong Sauternes for bisque — noble-rot sweetness bridges the rich shellfish soup.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Shrimp Cocktail": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Sauternes Glass lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Swordfish": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sweet wine dulls the sear's clean char — savory main deserves a dry pour. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Sauternes Glass lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Sauternes Glass|The Tomahawk": "Never pair Sauternes Glass with Tomahawk — noble-rot sweetness gets bulldozed by the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and the honey clashes with smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. Sauternes belongs at dessert or the cheese course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Tomato Basil": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- honeyed sugar on savory broth jars — wrong direction for the course. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Truffle Fries": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness flattens the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- dessert sugar on starch is wrong-course energy for the supporting plate. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Sauternes Glass for the steak course.",
  "Sauternes Glass|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- honeyed sugar on savory broth jars — wrong direction for the course. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Sauternes Glass belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Asparagus": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the rye spice cuts the asparagus, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Sazerac Rye with au gratin potatoes — BT rye against cheese-and-cream. Works; the rye's bright character is slightly at odds with the side's richness.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Beignets": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the dry rye body wraps the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Bone Marrow": "Sazerac Rye with bone marrow — iconic rye against umami fat. The rye's pepper and floral notes cut through marrow's richness in a way wheated bourbons don't; a classic rye-starter pairing at the benchmark register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Bone-In Filet": "Sazerac Rye's crisp floral-pepper character holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the agave-pepper carries the char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Broccoli Cheddar": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the rye spice cuts the broccoli cheddar, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Remus 15's rye-spice meets the glaze at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Broccolini": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the high-rye spice register matches the broccolini, and the rye-pepper note underlines the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Brussels and Belly": "Sazerac Rye with Brussels + Belly — iconic rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; rye spice cuts the glaze's sweetness cleanly.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Burrata": "Sazerac Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against rye's bright pepper. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Butternut Squash": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the high-rye spice register matches the squash bisque, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Carrot Cake": "Sazerac Rye with carrot cake — 90-proof iconic rye against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; the rye spice and cake spice find each other but not a marquee pairing.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Cheesecake": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye backbone drives the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; EC 18 handles dessert density at aged-HH register.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Chilean Seabass": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Sazerac Rye for the steak course.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Chocolate Brownie": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rye-and-oak weight handles the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; refined rye sits with richness at matched register.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Chocolate Cake": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the high-rye spice register matches the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; gentle match.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Clam Chowder": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the rye backbone drives the chowder, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the pour's toasted warmth threads through the char without overpowering.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Cowboy Ribeye": "Sazerac Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — BT's rye against cap fat. Strong; the 90-proof spicy-sweet rye handles the cut well but Knob Creek Rye's 100-proof or E.H. Taylor Rye bring more grip for the ribeye's scale.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Crab Cake": "Sazerac Rye with crab cake — iconic rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Creamed Spinach": "Sazerac Rye with creamed spinach — 90-proof rye against dairy. Strong; the spicy-sweet character sits with the side's richness.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Creamy Potato": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rye-and-oak weight handles the potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Remus 15's rye-spice meets the glaze at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Creme Brulee": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the dry rye body wraps the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Escargot": "Sazerac Rye's crisp floral-pepper character matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Faroe Island Salmon": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Sazerac Rye for the steak course.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Filet Mignon": "Sazerac Rye with the filet — BT's iconic rye against the 10oz cut. The 90-proof spicy-sweet rye frames the filet's clean beef with the bright pepper-and-floral character that makes rye distinct from bourbon; the benchmark rye pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Sazerac Rye|French Onion": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the rye spice cuts the French onion, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Grilled Caesar": "Sazerac Rye with the grilled Caesar — iconic rye against charred romaine and anchovy. The rye spice and anchovy salinity are a classic match; the benchmark rye-Caesar pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Gumbo": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the high-rye spice register matches the gumbo, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; bonded weight a touch ambitious for the dessert.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rye backbone drives the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Sazerac Rye|House Wedge": "Sazerac Rye with the House Wedge — BT rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; the rye's bright pepper cuts the bleu's richness differently than bourbon.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Kansas City": "Sazerac Rye with the KC — the iconic rye cocktail benchmark, 90 proof, Buffalo Trace. Spicy-sweet with floral-vanilla notes; the namesake of the Sazerac cocktail. The rye spice finds the strip's char in a way smoother brown spirits can't; the industry-standard rye for strip orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Loaded Potato": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the rye-and-oak weight handles the loaded potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; aged character overshoots delicacy.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Lobster Mac": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the rye-and-oak weight handles the lobster mac, and the oak softens the cream. Works; accessible gentle match.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Market Fish": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Sazerac Rye for the steak course.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Mocha Creme": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the mocha creme, and the oak softens the cream. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Mushroom Bisque": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the rye backbone drives the mushroom bisque, and the oak balances the umami register. Works; craft-blend over-specified.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Mushrooms": "Sazerac Rye with the mushrooms — BT rye against earthy umami. Strong; the rye spice layers into mushroom depth with benchmark character.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye spice cuts the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Porterhouse": "Sazerac Rye with the porterhouse — iconic rye across strip and filet. Strong; the spice-sweet character covers both cuts with benchmark rye precision.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Prime Tartare": "Sazerac Rye with the tartare — BT's rye against raw beef's mineral depth. The spicy-sweet character is a defining rye-tartare pairing; rye brightness and raw beef find each other cleanly. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Rainbow Trout": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character swallows the trout's delicate flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Sazerac Rye for the steak course.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the dry rye body wraps the roast chicken, and the dry-spice register brightens the plate. Works; milestone-edition over-specified.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the rye backbone drives the chickpea soup, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; premium aged rye wasted on the salad's simplicity.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Sazerac Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the dry rye body wraps the garlic spinach, and the rye-grain bite cuts the side. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Seafood Tower": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Sazerac Rye for the steak course.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Seared Scallops": "Sazerac Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 90-proof rye. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Seasonal Soup": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the rye backbone drives the seasonal soup, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for the side — save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye spice cuts the seasonal vegetables, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; GTS is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "Sazerac Rye with shrimp bisque — iconic rye against cream-based seafood. Works; the rye spice is interesting against the bisque but doesn't drive.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "Sazerac Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against iconic rye. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Swordfish": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Sazerac Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Sazerac Rye|The Tomahawk": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character meets at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the dry rye body wraps the 36oz showpiece, and the rye-pepper note underlines the cut. Strong; scale pushes past Elmer T's balance — E.H.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Tomato Basil": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the dry rye body wraps the tomato basil, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; BIB over-specified for side.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Truffle Fries": "Sazerac Rye with truffle fries — 90-proof iconic rye against truffle-parmesan fat. The rye's spice cuts through the richness with benchmark precision. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Sazerac Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Sazerac Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the rye spice cuts the curry, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; GTS is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Sazerac|Asparagus": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build finds neutral with the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; capable alongside. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Sazerac|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the whiskey-build weight settles into the rich side, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Sazerac|Beignets": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The anise-rye edge clashes with powdered-sugar beignets; spirit-forward classics belong pre-dinner or with tartare/marrow. Save Sazerac for those moments; route dessert guest to sparkling or coffee cocktails. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Sazerac|Bone Marrow": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The rye's spice cuts marrow fat and the absinthe-edge nose lifts the savory-bone richness; Peychaud's floral-cherry softens the minerality. Peak spirit-forward pour for marrow with historical cocktail weight.",
  "Sazerac|Bone-In Filet": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build plays cleanly against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey-build weight frames the lean cut, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Sazerac|Broccoli Cheddar": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Sazerac|Broccolini": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build stays alongside the broccolini's tender green — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Sazerac|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey's oak and sweetness mirror the side's dairy and umami depth. Works cleanly.",
  "Sazerac|Burrata": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. Anise-rye profile crushes burrata's creamy-delicate build; the cocktail has no hook into basil-tomato-cheese. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco or a crisp white opener, not the Sazerac.",
  "Sazerac|Butternut Squash": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build reads alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Sazerac|Carrot Cake": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. Spirit-forward classic — the anise-rye edge clashes with the cream-cheese frosting and overwhelms the cake's spice profile. Never pair Sazerac with dessert; keep it as a pre-dinner spirit-forward pour.",
  "Sazerac|Cheesecake": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The absinthe-edge doesn't align with vanilla-bean cheesecake and the rye spice muddies cream cheese. Works only when guest is staying on the cocktail; route dessert-focused guest to Sauternes or Espresso Martini instead.",
  "Sazerac|Chilean Seabass": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Sazerac for the steak course.",
  "Sazerac|Chocolate Brownie": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The anise-rye profile fights cocoa; the cocktail was never built for dessert. Works only when guest insists on finishing with the Sazerac; Espresso Martini or tawny ports peak dramatically higher for chocolate.",
  "Sazerac|Chocolate Cake": "Sazerac rye with Peychaud's bitters, sugar, and absinthe-rinsed glass. The spirit-forward anise-rye profile clashes with chocolate cake's cocoa depth and sweet register. Never pair — save Sazerac for tartare, bone marrow, or pre-dinner service. Note: chocolate cake is currently unavailable.",
  "Sazerac|Clam Chowder": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Sazerac|Cowboy Ribeye": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The rye's spice cuts the Cowboy's marbled fat and the absinthe-edge nose adds complexity to the char; Peychaud's floral-cherry softens the weight. Strong spirit-forward pour for the fattiest cut.",
  "Sazerac|Crab Cake": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- the cocktail's weight is forward for the table's first course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Sazerac for the steak course.",
  "Sazerac|Creamed Spinach": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The rye's spice and anise-edge cut the side's dairy weight cleanly; Peychaud's floral note adds complexity against creamed greens. Strong cocktail pour alongside the classic spinach — rye handles cream well.",
  "Sazerac|Creamy Potato": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build stays alongside the potato soup's silky body — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Sazerac|Creme Brulee": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The anise-rye profile fights vanilla custard; the minimal-build cocktail isn't built for dessert. Works if guest carries the Sazerac through the finish; Moscato and Vin Santo peak far higher on brûlée.",
  "Sazerac|Escargot": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bold-mix depth handles the buttered escargot, and the bitters edge frames the starter. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Sazerac|Faroe Island Salmon": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- whiskey cocktail and oily fish refuse each other — needs acid, not bitters. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Sazerac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sazerac|Filet Mignon": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The anise-rye profile and Peychaud's floral-cherry note play against butter-tender filet elegantly; the spirit-forward structure meets lean beef without crushing delicacy. Peak spirit-forward classic for the most tender cut — historical pairing weight.",
  "Sazerac|French Onion": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the whiskey-build depth matches the hearty soup, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Sazerac|Grilled Caesar": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The anise-edge nose plays against the grilled romaine's smoke directly and the rye's spice handles the anchovy-parmesan dressing. Strong unconventional pairing — the Sazerac's herbal edge meets the salad's char nicely.",
  "Sazerac|Gumbo": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the cherry-fruit frames the umami depth. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Sazerac|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build holds with the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Sazerac|House Wedge": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. Spirit-forward cocktail is heavier than the iceberg wedge strictly demands, but Peychaud's floral-cherry note plays against bacon-and-blue-cheese. Works as an opener when guest leads with the classic.",
  "Sazerac|Kansas City": "Kansas City: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Oak, caramel, and spirit heat stand up to the beef's render; the caramelized sweetness mirrors the crust. A headliner steakhouse cocktail. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Sazerac|Loaded Potato": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build sits beside the loaded potato's richness — the whiskey-build depth matches the hearty soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Sazerac|Lobster Mac": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build stays alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the whiskey-build weight settles into the rich side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Sazerac|Market Fish": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Sazerac for the steak course.",
  "Sazerac|Mocha Creme": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, and absinthe-rinsed glass. The spirit-forward anise-rye profile collides with cream-and-coffee custard — herbal bitterness overrides cocoa softness. Never pair — save Sazerac for tartare, marrow, or pre-dinner spirit service. Mocha Crème is currently off-menu.",
  "Sazerac|Mushroom Bisque": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build leans against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the whiskey-build depth matches the hearty soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Sazerac|Mushrooms": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The rye's spice cuts mushroom-side butter-sauté richness and the absinthe-edge adds aromatic lift against earth. Strong pairing — the Sazerac's structure and nose handles mushroom depth well.",
  "Sazerac|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The anise-rye profile clashes with peanut butter on every axis; spirit-forward classic has no job with dessert. Never pair — save Sazerac for tartare, marrow, or pre-dinner service.",
  "Sazerac|Porterhouse": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The cocktail handles the Porterhouse's dual-cut nature — rye spice meets the strip, Peychaud's cherry plays against filet. Strong cocktail pour for the house showcase cut when guest wants classic over Cab.",
  "Sazerac|Prime Tartare": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The cocktail's minimal-build purity meets the tartare's raw-beef simplicity; rye spice and Peychaud's cherry note complement the tartare seasoning. Peak pre-dinner pour — 'original American cocktail' meets prime beef course. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Sazerac|Rainbow Trout": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Sazerac for the steak course.",
  "Sazerac|Roast Half Chicken": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the whiskey-build depth carries the herb-crisped bird, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Sazerac|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build stays alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Sazerac|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the bitters-and-bourbon register has no foil against gentle flesh. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Sazerac for the steak course.",
  "Sazerac|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build sits beside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Sazerac|Seafood Tower": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the cocktail's weight is forward for the table's first course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sazerac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sazerac|Seared Scallops": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. Spirit-forward weight obliterates the scallops' delicate sweetness and the anise edge clashes with clean seared seafood. Never pair — scallops demand crisp whites or Champagne, not classic rye cocktails.",
  "Sazerac|Seasonal Soup": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Sazerac|Seasonal Vegetables": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build finds neutral with the seasonal garden plate — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Sazerac|Shrimp Bisque": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's bitters, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. Rye's spice against cream-shellfish soup is bold; the anise-edge is unusual with bisque but not clashing. Works for cocktail-first guests — though white wine peaks higher for bisque.",
  "Sazerac|Shrimp Cocktail": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the cocktail's weight is forward for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Sazerac for the steak course.",
  "Sazerac|Swordfish": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- cocktail bitterness pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Sazerac for the steak course.",
  "Sazerac|The Tomahawk": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge sits alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bold-mix depth handles the 36oz showpiece, and the bitters edge frames the cut. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Sazerac|Tomato Basil": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build reads alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Sazerac|Truffle Fries": "Sazerac rye, Peychaud's, sugar, absinthe-rinsed glass. The anise-edge nose plays against truffle oil's earth and the rye's spice meets the parmesan-truffle crust. Peak classic pairing when the fries anchor a spirit-forward cocktail program. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Sazerac|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- cocktail bitterness pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Sazerac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Sazerac|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Sazerac's bold anise-cherry build sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Asparagus": "Scapegrace Fortuna's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak depth lays under the asparagus, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; smooth character is slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the au gratin — New Zealand single malt against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Fortuna handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Beignets": "Scapegrace Fortuna's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the caramel-spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Bone Marrow": "Scapegrace Fortuna with bone marrow — New Zealand single malt against umami fat. Strong; Fortuna's NZ terroir sits alongside marrow at distinctive register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Bone-In Filet": "Scapegrace Fortuna's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the bourbon weight handles the bone-enhanced filet, and the caramel rounds the cut. Works; bonded weight overpowers this dish.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Broccoli Cheddar": "Scapegrace Fortuna's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon backbone carries the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; smooth character is slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Broccolini": "Scapegrace Fortuna's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon backbone carries the broccolini, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; distinctive character is worth mentioning tableside, even if the pairing isn't marquee.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Brussels and Belly": "Scapegrace Fortuna with Brussels + Belly — NZ single malt against maple-glazed pork belly. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Burrata": "Scapegrace Fortuna with burrata — NZ craft single malt against fresh dairy. Avoid; whisky weight wrong for burrata.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Butternut Squash": "Scapegrace Fortuna's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon weight handles the squash bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Carrot Cake": "Scapegrace Fortuna with carrot cake — NZ single malt against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; Fortuna's distinctive NZ terroir threads the cake at craft register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Cheesecake": "Scapegrace Fortuna with cheesecake — New Zealand craft single malt against custard. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir threads the cheesecake at distinctive register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Chilean Seabass": "Scapegrace Fortuna's NZ craft single malt clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Scapegrace Fortuna on the steak course instead.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Chocolate Brownie": "Scapegrace Fortuna's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for the side — save the rare-cask bottle for the protein course.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Chocolate Cake": "Scapegrace Fortuna's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; high proof over-specified.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Clam Chowder": "Scapegrace Fortuna's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon body sits with the chowder, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Cowboy Ribeye": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the cowboy ribeye — New Zealand single malt against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Strong; Fortuna's NZ terroir frames the ribeye at distinctive register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Crab Cake": "Scapegrace Fortuna with crab cake — NZ single malt against delicate lump crab. Avoid; single malt weight wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Creamed Spinach": "Scapegrace Fortuna with creamed spinach — NZ craft single malt against dairy-rich side. Strong; Fortuna's NZ terroir rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Creamy Potato": "Scapegrace Fortuna's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Creme Brulee": "Scapegrace Fortuna's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the toast adds a warmth the dessert can accept.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Escargot": "Scapegrace Fortuna's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the brown-spirit warmth threads the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Works; milestone-release doesn't drive; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Faroe Island Salmon": "Scapegrace Fortuna's NZ craft single malt smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Scapegrace Fortuna on the steak course instead.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Filet Mignon": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the filet — New Zealand single malt (reclassified from American Single Malt / Tennessee — Scapegrace is a New Zealand craft distillery). Excellent; Fortuna's distinctive NZ-single-malt terroir frames tenderloin with southern-hemisphere character no other spirit on the menu can claim — the first NZ whisky on the reference.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|French Onion": "Scapegrace Fortuna's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; AE CS handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Grilled Caesar": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the grilled Caesar — NZ single malt against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Fortuna's distinctive NZ-whisky character meets the char at terroir-driven register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Gumbo": "Scapegrace Fortuna's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Scapegrace Fortuna's NZ craft single malt finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; bonded weight overpowers this dish.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|House Wedge": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the House Wedge — New Zealand single malt against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Fortuna's distinctive NZ character engages the bleu at terroir-driven register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Kansas City": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the KC — NZ single malt against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Fortuna's New Zealand craft-distillery character handles strip marbling at distinctive-terroir register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Loaded Potato": "Scapegrace Fortuna's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon backbone carries the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Lobster Mac": "Scapegrace Fortuna's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the lobster mac, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Market Fish": "Scapegrace Fortuna's NZ craft single malt overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Scapegrace Fortuna lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Mocha Creme": "Scapegrace Fortuna's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the brown-spirit warmth threads the mocha creme, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Mushroom Bisque": "Scapegrace Fortuna's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; boutique character gives the dessert a craft frame.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Mushrooms": "Scapegrace Fortuna with mushrooms — NZ single malt against earthy umami. Excellent; Fortuna's southern-hemisphere terroir threads mushroom earthiness with NZ-whisky distinction.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Scapegrace Fortuna's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the caramel-spice register matches the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Porterhouse": "Scapegrace Fortuna with the porterhouse — NZ craft single malt against strip-and-filet. Strong; Fortuna frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Prime Tartare": "Scapegrace Fortuna's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bourbon weight handles the tartare, and the oak depth meets the umami. Works; the pour's complexity is underused by the salad; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Rainbow Trout": "Scapegrace Fortuna's NZ craft single malt overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Scapegrace Fortuna for the steak course.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Roast Half Chicken": "Scapegrace Fortuna's NZ craft single malt finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; distinctive character gives the dessert a unique story-frame.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Scapegrace Fortuna's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon body sits with the chickpea soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; gentle match at matched registers.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Scapegrace Fortuna's NZ craft single malt overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Scapegrace Fortuna lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Scapegrace Fortuna's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the oak frames the side. Works; Old World complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Seafood Tower": "Scapegrace Fortuna's NZ craft single malt smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Scapegrace Fortuna on the steak course instead.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Seared Scallops": "Scapegrace Fortuna with seared scallops — New Zealand single malt against scallop delicacy. Avoid; single malt overwhelms scallops.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Seasonal Soup": "Scapegrace Fortuna's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; bonded weight overpowers this dish.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Seasonal Vegetables": "Scapegrace Fortuna's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Shrimp Bisque": "Scapegrace Fortuna with shrimp bisque — NZ craft single malt against cream-shrimp soup. Works; Fortuna rides the bisque at distinctive register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Shrimp Cocktail": "Scapegrace Fortuna's NZ craft single malt crowds out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Scapegrace Fortuna on the steak course instead.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Swordfish": "Scapegrace Fortuna's NZ craft single malt overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Scapegrace Fortuna belongs on the steak course.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|The Tomahawk": "Scapegrace Fortuna's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bourbon depth settles into the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla underlines the rendered char. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Tomato Basil": "Scapegrace Fortuna's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the oak depth lays under the tomato basil, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; OGD 16 handles dessert density at distinctive register.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Truffle Fries": "Scapegrace Fortuna with truffle fries — New Zealand craft single malt against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the earthy-rich with distinctive southern-hemisphere character.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna's NZ craft single malt swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Scapegrace Fortuna on the steak course instead.",
  "Scapegrace Fortuna|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Scapegrace Fortuna's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the oak depth lays under the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Asparagus": "Piedmont Barolo's tar-and-tannin intensity against asparagus — firm-tannin Nebbiolo on sulfur-forward vegetable is textbook clash. Never pair. Route Scavino to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take asparagus with Sauvignon Blanc, Sancerre, or Chablis.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts gruyère-cream weight and the leather plays against the lactic richness. Works as King-of-Italian for au gratin — Scavino prestige on the cheesy side.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Beignets": "Piedmont Nebbiolo — tar, dried rose, firm tannin. Barolo crushes the beignet on every axis — tannin against fried choux is incoherent. Never pair. Route beignet guest to sparkling wine, tawny, or a coffee cocktail.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Bone Marrow": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the leather plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak King-of-Italian for marrow — Scavino prestige on the rich starter. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Bone-In Filet": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid, full body. The powerful tannin handles bone-in filet's bone-deep beef flavor and the tar-leather plays against the marrow-infused finish. Peak King-of-Italian for bone-in — Scavino top producer on the aged-beef cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Broccoli Cheddar": "Piedmont Barolo's tar-and-tannin intensity against a cream-and-cheddar soup — firm-tannin Nebbiolo annihilates the vegetable-cheese classic. Never pair. Route Scavino to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take broccoli cheddar with Chardonnay, Pinot Blanc, or Chablis.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Broccolini": "Piedmont Barolo's tar-and-tannin intensity against charred broccolini — firm-tannin Nebbiolo is wildly wrong for a vegetable side. Never pair. Route Scavino to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take broccolini with Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Noir.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Brussels and Belly": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The firm tannin cuts pork-belly fat and the leather plays against charred brussels. Strong King-of-Italian for Brussels and Belly — Scavino prestige on the rich starter.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Burrata": "King-of-Italian Barolo against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Scavino's firm-tannin tar-leather weight overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Scavino for aged beef where the prestige of the King-of-Italian earns the bottle.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Butternut Squash": "Piedmont Barolo's tar-and-tannin intensity against a caramelized-squash autumn soup — firm-tannin Nebbiolo annihilates the sweet-cream classic. Never pair. Route Scavino to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take butternut squash with Riesling, Chardonnay, or Pinot Noir.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Carrot Cake": "Piedmont Nebbiolo — tar, dried rose, firm tannin. Barolo obliterates the cake's spiced sweetness and cream-cheese frosting. Never pair — Barolo belongs with Tomahawk or Porterhouse. Route guest to tawny or Moscato for carrot cake.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Cheesecake": "Piedmont Nebbiolo — tar, dried rose, firm tannin. Barolo obliterates cream cheese and the graham crust gets lost. Never pour with dessert; keep Barolo for the Tomahawk or Porterhouse where the structure earns its place. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Chilean Seabass": "Scavino Barolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Scavino Barolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Chocolate Brownie": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. Dry red Barolo against warm brownie with vanilla ice cream — the cherry-leather can carry chocolate but the firm tannin clashes with sweetness. Works as unusual savory-to-dessert bridge; a dessert wine peaks higher.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Chocolate Cake": "Piedmont Nebbiolo — tar, dried rose, firm tannin. Barolo's structure crushes chocolate cake; tannin-dessert mismatch across the board. Never pair — save Barolo for Tomahawk or Porterhouse where the structure earns its place.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Clam Chowder": "Piedmont Barolo's tar-and-tannin intensity against a cream-based clam chowder — firm-tannin Nebbiolo annihilates the shellfish soup. Never pair. Route Scavino to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take chowder with Chardonnay, Chablis, or Pinot Blanc.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Cowboy Ribeye": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid, full body. The full body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the leather plays against beefy char. Strong King-of-Italian for ribeye — Scavino prestige on the bold cut.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Crab Cake": "King-of-Italian Barolo against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Scavino's powerful firm-tannin tar-leather structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Scavino for aged beef — the bottle demands the meat course.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Creamed Spinach": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the dried rose plays against the spinach base. Strong King-of-Italian for creamed spinach — Scavino prestige reframes the rich side.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Creamy Potato": "Piedmont Barolo's tar-and-tannin intensity against a cream-and-potato soup — firm-tannin Nebbiolo buries the smooth dairy classic. Never pair. Route Scavino to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take creamy potato with Chardonnay, Pinot Blanc, or Chablis.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Creme Brulee": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. Dry red Barolo against crème brûlée's custard-caramelized-sugar — high acid handles the cream but firm tannin clashes with the vanilla sweetness. Works as unusual bridge; a dessert wine peaks higher.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Escargot": "Scavino Barolo: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. Not the sharpest pairing. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Faroe Island Salmon": "Scavino Barolo: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets this rich, fatty seafood's buttery flesh and delicate-yet-substantial texture. The bold red's tannin shreds the fish's delicate flake, and the dark fruit clashes with briny sweetness. Textbook avoid — this is the classic red-meat-only territory. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Filet Mignon": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid, full body. The high acid cuts butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the dried rose plays against escargot-butter. Peak King-of-Italian for filet — top Barolo producer on the delicate cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Scavino Barolo|French Onion": "Scavino Barolo's bold Nebbiolo-tar cassis reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Grilled Caesar": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the dried rose plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak King-of-Italian for grilled Caesar — Scavino's food-demanding structure on the layered salad. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Gumbo": "Scavino Barolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Scavino Barolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Piedmont Barolo's tar-and-tannin intensity against caramelized-honey carrots — firm-tannin Nebbiolo is wildly wrong for a sweet-root side. Never pair. Route Scavino to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take honey carrots with Pinot Noir, rosé, or Chardonnay.",
  "Scavino Barolo|House Wedge": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the leather plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as King-of-Italian for the wedge — Scavino reframes the salad, though this bottle deserves the meat course.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Kansas City": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid, full body. The powerful firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the leather plays against beefy char. Peak King-of-Italian for the strip — Scavino prestige on the American cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Loaded Potato": "Scavino Barolo's bold Nebbiolo-tar cassis sits beside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Lobster Mac": "Scavino Barolo: this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight meets this rich, creamy side's dairy-driven indulgence. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. Directional mismatch at this intersection. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Market Fish": "Scavino Barolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Scavino Barolo for the steak course.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Mocha Creme": "Piedmont Nebbiolo — tar, dried rose, firm tannin. Barolo's structure crushes a cream-based custard; tannin-meets-dessert is incoherent. Never pair — save Barolo for Tomahawk or Porterhouse where structure earns its place.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Mushroom Bisque": "Piedmont Barolo's tar-and-tannin intensity against a cream-based mushroom bisque — firm-tannin Nebbiolo buries the silky umami soup. Never pair. Route Scavino to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take the bisque with Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, or Chablis.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Mushrooms": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The tar echoes roasted-mushroom glutamate and the leather plays against the earth depth. Strong King-of-Italian for mushrooms — Scavino's earth-on-earth match.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Piedmont Nebbiolo — tar, dried rose, firm tannin. Barolo's structure fights dessert completely; tannin against peanut butter frosting is incoherent. Never pair — Barolo belongs with the Tomahawk or Porterhouse steaks.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Porterhouse": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The powerful tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the tar plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong King-of-Italian for Porterhouse — Scavino prestige on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Prime Tartare": "King-of-Italian Barolo against raw Prime Tartare — Scavino's powerful firm tannin and tar-leather structure overwhelms the delicate raw beef with capers, shallot, and quail yolk. Never pair. Route tartare to Champagne, Pinot Noir, or a softer red; keep Scavino for Bone-In Filet, Tomahawk, or aged-beef courses.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Rainbow Trout": "Piedmont Barolo's tar-and-tannin intensity against delicate rainbow trout — firm-tannin Nebbiolo annihilates the freshwater fish. Never pair. Route Scavino to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take trout with Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Roast Half Chicken": "Scavino Barolo's bold Nebbiolo-tar cassis plays cleanly against the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Piedmont Barolo's tar-and-tannin intensity against a smoky roasted-pepper chickpea soup — firm-tannin Nebbiolo crushes the Mediterranean classic. Never pair. Route Scavino to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take the soup with Pinot Noir, rosé, or Rioja.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Piedmont Barolo's tar-and-tannin intensity against salt-cured halibut — firm-tannin Nebbiolo annihilates the delicate cured fish. Never pair. Route Scavino to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take halibut with Chardonnay, Chablis, or Pinot Blanc.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Scavino Barolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Scavino Barolo for the steak course.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Seafood Tower": "Piedmont Nebbiolo with tar, dried rose, and firm tannin. Aggressive structure against delicate cold seafood; the Barolo bullies the tower's nuance. Save this bottle for the Tomahawk or the Filet. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Seared Scallops": "King-of-Italian Barolo against scallop sweetness — Scavino's powerful firm-tannin tar-leather structure buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Scavino for Bone-In Filet or Tomahawk where the prestige earns the bottle.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Seasonal Soup": "Scavino Barolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Scavino Barolo on the steak course instead.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Seasonal Vegetables": "Scavino Barolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Scavino Barolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Shrimp Bisque": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The high acid cuts shrimp bisque's cream-sherry weight and the tar plays against the shellfish base. Works as King-of-Italian for the bisque — Scavino's food-demanding acid on the rich soup.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Shrimp Cocktail": "Piedmont Nebbiolo — dried rose, tar, firm tannin. The structure clashes with delicate chilled shellfish; the Barolo crushes every element of the dish. Save for the Tomahawk or Filet where it can meet real weight. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Swordfish": "Piedmont Barolo's tar-and-tannin intensity against grilled swordfish — firm-tannin Nebbiolo annihilates the fish. Never pair. Route Scavino to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take swordfish with Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, or rosé.",
  "Scavino Barolo|The Tomahawk": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid, full body. The powerful tannin handles Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye and the tar-leather plays against smoky char. Peak King-of-Italian for Tomahawk — Scavino prestige on the grand cut, demands the beef. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Tomato Basil": "Piedmont Barolo's tar-and-tannin intensity against a fresh tomato-basil soup — firm-tannin Nebbiolo annihilates the bright herb classic. Never pair. Route Scavino to the Tomahawk or Porterhouse; take tomato basil with Sauvignon Blanc, Sancerre, or Chablis.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Truffle Fries": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid. The firm tannin cuts truffle-fry potato fat and the tar plays against the truffle-oil umami. Peak King-of-Italian for truffle fries — tar-truffle echo, Scavino prestige on the umami hit. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Scavino Barolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Scavino Barolo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Scavino Barolo|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Scavino Barolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Scavino Barolo on the steak course instead.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Asparagus": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the mineral edge frames the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Beignets": "Schloss Vollrads — Rheingau Riesling, apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry fruit complements beignets' berry jam directly and the acid cuts fried-dough weight; petrol adds unexpected dessert complexity. Strong Riesling-with-beignets pairing — off-dry fruit meets sweet pastry.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Bone Marrow": "Rheingau Riesling against marrow's rendered-fat richness — off-dry German white has no weight to meet bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Schloss Vollrads for bisque, crab cake, scallops, burrata, or the chicken course.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Bone-In Filet": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's off-dry Rheingau Riesling with lime-petrol edge underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Schloss Vollrads Riesling for another course.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Broccoli Cheddar": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Broccolini": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity stays alongside the broccolini's tender green — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the mineral edge frames the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Brussels and Belly": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity leans against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge frames the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Burrata": "Rheingau Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The delicate off-dry fruit meets burrata's creamy-delicate center without crushing and the apricot-peach plays against basil-tomato-pistachio. Peak gateway Riesling for burrata — fruit-lift without the heaviness of Chardonnay. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Butternut Squash": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling -- Rheingau Riesling, off-dry German classic, lime-and-petrol. The bright acid lifts the squash bisque, and the citrus cuts the bowl. Excellent; the starter sets up the cut at full register. Gold standard; the call that earns the regular.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Carrot Cake": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity holds with the carrot cake's spiced cream cheese — the dry white underclubs the dessert sugar, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Cheesecake": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity leans against the cheesecake's custard cream — the dry white underclubs the dessert sugar, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Chilean Seabass": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity holds steady against the seabass's buttery flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Chocolate Brownie": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity plays cleanly against the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the dry white underclubs the dessert sugar, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Chocolate Cake": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity holds with the cake's layered chocolate — the dry white underclubs the dessert sugar, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Clam Chowder": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity sits beside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Cowboy Ribeye": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — light off-dry German white crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Riesling for bisque, crab cake, scallops, salmon, or burrata.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Crab Cake": "Schloss Vollrads — Rheingau Riesling with apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry fruit cuts crab's romesco and the apricot-peach plays against the sweet meat; lively acid cuts the pan-fry. Peak German Riesling for crab cake — fruit-and-acid match, teaching-wine provenance. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Creamed Spinach": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity finds neutral with the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Creamy Potato": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity plays cleanly against the potato soup's silky body — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Creme Brulee": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the dry white underclubs the dessert sugar, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Escargot": "Escargot: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this off-dry Riesling's stone-fruit sweetness and mineral backbone. Crisp or off-dry white's acidity lifts the starter — classic opening-course move. Strong — clean lift.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Faroe Island Salmon": "Schloss Vollrads — Rheingau Riesling with apricot, peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry fruit cuts salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong German Riesling for salmon across most seasonal builds — classical fruit-and-acid match.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Filet Mignon": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's off-dry Rheingau Riesling with lime-petrol edge mis-pairs with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- this white's register is wrong for the filet — a softer red would bridge better. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Schloss Vollrads Riesling for another course.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|French Onion": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cheese richness. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Grilled Caesar": "Schloss Vollrads — Rheingau Riesling, apricot, peach, petrol, off-dry. The off-dry fruit cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the acid plays against grilled-romaine char; petrol adds intrigue. Works as German Riesling for grilled Caesar — conversation-starter choice when guest wants something unexpected.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Gumbo": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling -- Rheingau Riesling, off-dry German classic, lime-and-petrol. The bright acid lifts the gumbo, and the citrus cuts the bowl. Excellent; a textbook match that delivers. Gold standard; the call that earns the regular.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Honey Roasted Carrots: these honey-roasted carrots' caramelized sweetness and earthy warmth meets this off-dry Riesling's stone-fruit sweetness and mineral backbone. Bright acidity lifts green vegetables; mineral lines up with the clean vegetable bite. Works — coherent. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|House Wedge": "Rheingau Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry fruit cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the acid plays against blue cheese dressing; petrol adds intrigue. Strong German Riesling for the wedge — conversation-starter wine reframes the classic steakhouse salad.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Kansas City": "Off-dry Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol with a lively acid backbone keeping the residual sweetness in check. Still, the light body can't meet a bone-in strip like the KC. Redirect to a red — the wine is a gateway Riesling, but not a steak pairing. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Loaded Potato": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Lobster Mac": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity stays alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Market Fish": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling -- Rheingau Riesling, off-dry German classic, lime-and-petrol. The off-dry body brightens the market fish, and the lime-petrol note threads the plate. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Mocha Creme": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the dry white underclubs the dessert sugar, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Mushroom Bisque": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity leans against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the mineral edge cuts the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Mushrooms": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity reads alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity holds with the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the dry white underclubs the dessert sugar, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Porterhouse": "Off-dry Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol with a lively acid backbone keeping the residual sweetness in check. The acid balance holds, but the light body can't meet 40oz of marbled porterhouse. Redirect to a red; a gateway Riesling for other courses, not this one. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Prime Tartare": "Rheingau Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry fruit-and-acid cuts raw prime beef's iron richness but the fruit register is less aligned than classical whites for tartare. Works as conversation-starter pairing; Sancerre, Chablis, or grower rosé peak higher.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Rainbow Trout": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling -- Rheingau Riesling, off-dry German classic, lime-and-petrol. The off-dry body brightens the trout, and the lime-petrol note threads the plate. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Roast Half Chicken": "Rheingau Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry fruit handles roasted chicken; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Strong German Riesling for chicken across most seasonal accompaniments — fruit lifts the pairing, estate story adds conversation.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling -- Rheingau Riesling, off-dry German classic, lime-and-petrol. The off-dry body brightens the halibut, and the lime-petrol note threads the plate. Strong; the call reads at register without overshooting.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Rheingau Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry acid cuts the sauté butter and the fruit plays against garlic without clashing with spinach's earth. Works as German Riesling for the sauté side — unexpected pairing with the provenance story attached.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Seafood Tower": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity sits beside the tower's chilled shellfish — the crisp acidity lifts the shellfish without weight, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Seared Scallops": "Rheingau Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry acidity frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the apricot-peach fruit adds complexity against the pan-sear. Peak German Riesling for scallops — 700-year estate provenance, fruit-and-acid precision. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Seasonal Soup": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Seasonal Vegetables": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity holds with the seasonal garden plate — the crisp acidity sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Shrimp Bisque": "Rheingau Germany Riesling — apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid backbone, off-dry. The off-dry fruit-acid play cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and the apricot-peach plays against the butter-roux. Peak German Riesling for bisque — 700-year estate provenance adds a teaching moment. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Shrimp Cocktail": "Schloss Vollrads — Rheingau Riesling, apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry fruit profile cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the petrol note adds intrigue against cocktail-sauce bite. Peak Riesling for shrimp cocktail — fruit-forward gateway with sommelier-level provenance. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Swordfish": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling: historic German Riesling estate. Excellent on swordfish; off-dry residual-sugar-and-acid balances the meaty fish at German-white register. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|The Tomahawk": "Rheingau Riesling against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — off-dry fruit-and-acid has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Schloss Vollrads for bisque, crab cake, scallops, burrata, or the chicken course.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Tomato Basil": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity holds with the tomato basil's bright acid — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the brioche notes meet the herb butter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Truffle Fries": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's off-dry Rheingau Riesling with lime-petrol edge finds neutral with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight: the off-dry body brightens the truffle fries, and the lime-petrol note threads the side. Works; the two desserts share the close without crowding.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity holds the line with the tuna's seared rare crust — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Schloss Vollrads Riesling|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas: this spiced vegetable curry's warm, complex aromatic lift meets this off-dry Riesling's stone-fruit sweetness and mineral backbone. Crisp, vegetal wine mirrors the soup's bright herbal lift. A same-register headliner. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Asparagus": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' concentrated dark-fruit and tannin finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the structured body wraps the asparagus, and the cassis frames the side. Works; the side call holds at neutral register.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Au Gratin Potatoes": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the dark chocolate plays against the lactic richness. Strong by-the-glass Cab for au gratin — accessible California value on the cheesy side.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Beignets": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Bone Marrow": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis sits with the marrow's umami-fat depth — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Bone-In Filet": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' concentrated dark-fruit and tannin sits at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the concentrated dark-fruit weight handles the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Broccoli Cheddar": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Broccolini": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' concentrated dark-fruit and tannin reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the structured body wraps the broccolini, and the cassis frames the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Brussels and Belly": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts pork-belly fat and the dark chocolate plays against charred brussels. Strong by-the-glass Cab for Brussels and Belly — accessible California value on the rich starter.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Burrata": "By-the-glass California Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — full-body cassis-chocolate structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Butternut Squash": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Carrot Cake": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Cheesecake": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance pulls against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Chilean Seabass": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Chocolate Brownie": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Chocolate Cake": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters on the steak course instead.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Clam Chowder": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis plays cleanly against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Cowboy Ribeye": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin, full body. The full body meets ribeye's big marbled build though medium tannin reads softer than firm-tannin Napa Cabs on the cut. Works as by-the-glass Cab for ribeye — accessible value when guest wants Cab at the price point.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Crab Cake": "By-the-glass California Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — full-body medium-tannin structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Lost Chapters for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Creamed Spinach": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the dark chocolate plays against the spinach base. Strong by-the-glass Cab for creamed spinach — accessible California reframes the rich side.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Creamy Potato": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' concentrated dark-fruit and tannin leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the structured body wraps the potato soup, and the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Creme Brulee": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters belongs on the steak course.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Escargot": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis plays cleanly against the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Faroe Island Salmon": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Filet Mignon": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin, smooth full body. The smooth medium tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the dark chocolate plays against escargot-butter. Strong by-the-glass Cab for filet — accessible California value on the delicate cut.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|French Onion": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis plays cleanly against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Grilled Caesar": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin, smooth. The medium tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the dark chocolate plays against grilled-romaine char. Peak by-the-glass Cab for grilled Caesar — accessible value on the layered salad. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Gumbo": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis finds neutral with the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cocoa deepens the fudge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|House Wedge": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin, smooth. The medium tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the dark chocolate plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak by-the-glass Cab for the wedge — accessible California value reframes the classic salad. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Kansas City": "California Cab by-the-glass value — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin, smooth full finish. The smooth structure meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the dark chocolate plays against beefy char. Peak by-the-glass Cab for the strip — accessible California value on the American cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Loaded Potato": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Lobster Mac": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Market Fish": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters on the steak course instead.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Mocha Creme": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters on the steak course instead.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Mushroom Bisque": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Mushrooms": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin. The dark chocolate echoes roasted-mushroom glutamate and the black cherry plays against earth depth. Peak by-the-glass Cab for mushrooms — accessible California value on the earthy side. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters on the steak course instead.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Porterhouse": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin. The medium tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet though the smooth profile reads rounder than firm-tannin Napa on the cut. Works as by-the-glass Cab for Porterhouse — accessible value on the dual-cut.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Prime Tartare": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' concentrated dark-fruit and tannin sits alongside the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the structured body wraps the tartare, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Rainbow Trout": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Roast Half Chicken": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin, smooth. The smooth full body plays against roasted chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Peak by-the-glass Cab for chicken across most seasonal preparations. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters on the steak course instead.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis plays cleanly against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the opulent body overshoots the light side, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Seafood Tower": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Seared Scallops": "By-the-glass California Cab against scallop sweetness — full-body cassis-chocolate structure buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Seasonal Soup": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis sits beside the seasonal soup's house build — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Seasonal Vegetables": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis stays alongside the seasonal garden plate — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Shrimp Bisque": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis holds with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the cherry-fruit rounds the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Shrimp Cocktail": "By-the-glass California Cab against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — red wine and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Swordfish": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|The Tomahawk": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis reads alongside the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the Cab structure carries the bone-in cut, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Tomato Basil": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis sits alongside the tomato basil's bright acid — the opulent body overshoots the light soup, and the cassis frames the herb butter. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Truffle Fries": "California Cab by-the-glass — black cherry, cassis, dark chocolate, integrated medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts truffle-fry potato fat and the dark chocolate plays against the truffle-oil umami. Strong by-the-glass Cab for truffle fries — accessible California value on the umami hit.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Seafood Tower|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the 1792 Small Batch for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the 1792 Sweet Wheat for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the 1881 Napa Valley on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|2 James Gin": "2 James with the seafood tower — Detroit local distillery against the raw bar. Excellent; 2 James is the most distinctive Michigan-pride tower pairing — the gin choice for guests who want something local and story-driven.",
  "Seafood Tower|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the A Midnight's Winter Dram for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Aberlour 16 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino with the seafood tower — filtered aged across raw oysters, shrimp, lobster. The cristalino's aged depth with clear presentation elevates the tower's premium theatrics; pour for a table that wants the distinctive story. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Seafood Tower|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the rested-agave depth sits with the seafood tower, and the cooked-agave note underlines the starter. Works; multi-cask complexity composed with this dish at distinctive register.",
  "Seafood Tower|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay -- Sonoma with ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth texture. The bright acid lifts the seafood tower, and the citrus cuts the starter. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Seafood Tower|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' red-fruit-and-spice elegance overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Alexander Valley Cyrus on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Alexander Valley Homestead Red belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- amaro bitterness on cold shellfish flattens the delicate brine — needs sparkling instead. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Amaro Nonino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Ancho Reyes on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Angel's Envy lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Angel's Envy Cask Strength for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Angel's Envy Triple Cask belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Aperol belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Asparagus": "The seafood tower and the asparagus -- both crisp and clean, the table reads light from open to handoff. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Seafood Tower|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The seafood tower and the au gratin -- acid lifts the richness, the meal gets the contrast it needs. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Seafood Tower|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- amaro bitterness on cold shellfish flattens the delicate brine — needs sparkling instead. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Averna Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Aviation": "Aviation gin, Luxardo, crème de violette, ginger syrup — pale purple. The cocktail's floral-cherry-ginger profile handles the raw-bar spread and the color adds drama to the tower presentation. Strong tower cocktail when guest wants visual spectacle alongside the shellfish.",
  "Seafood Tower|Aviation Gin": "Aviation with the seafood tower — smooth American craft gin against the raw bar. Strong; Aviation handles the tower at crowd-pleasing register; Gray Whale's ocean-conservation-California-botanicals is the featured craft-tower match.",
  "Seafood Tower|Avion 44": "Avion 44's barrel-aged tequila weight leans against the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the seafood tower, and the soft-spice note softens the starter. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Seafood Tower|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver with the seafood tower — globally-distributed blanco across raw oysters, shrimp, lobster. Strong; the filtered clean character provides a through-line at everyday register.",
  "Seafood Tower|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum -- Carta Blanca, workhorse light rum, clean. The bright cane-and-citrus weight carries the seafood tower, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the starter. Excellent; workhorse light rum on the seafood tower -- the kind of pour that earns a regular. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Seafood Tower|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Baileys Irish Cream for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Baker's": "Baker's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Baker's for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's bourbon-cask Speyside softness drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Balvenie 12 American Oak lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask's rum-finished Speyside character drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's First Fill American Oak fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Balvenie 16 Single Barrel for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood's aged Port-finished Speyside drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Balvenie 21 Portwood on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Bardstown Château Laboude for the steak course.",
