  "Seafood Tower|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18's premium aged 18yr Oloroso Speyside overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Macallan 18 on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate's estate Speyside character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Macallan Estate on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Macauley Petite Syrah's spice-and-dark-fruit Rhone register overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Macauley Petite Syrah for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Macauley Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Macauley Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Mahon Gin": "Mahón with the seafood tower — Xoriguer Mahón, Menorca (Balearic Islands), PDO-protected juniper-heavy Mediterranean gin. Excellent; Mahón's Mediterranean juniper character frames the raw bar with Spanish-island terroir — built for seafood the way its maker's island location would suggest.",
  "Seafood Tower|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark's wheated red-rope sweetness overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Maker's Mark for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's aged wheated depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Maker's Mark Cellar Aged for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- this light spirit's register is wrong for the starter — try a different pour. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a light spirit. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Malibu Rum lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Marc Brédif Chinon's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Marc Brédif Chinon for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Margarita": "Corazon Blanco, lemon, lime, orange, Cointreau. The triple-citrus handles the raw-bar shellfish and the blanco's agave profile adds warmth against oysters and shrimp. Strong tower cocktail when guest wants a classic Margarita rather than Champagne for the full spread.",
  "Seafood Tower|Marimar Estate Christina": "Marimar Estate Christina's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Marimar Estate Christina lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Market Fish": "Market Fish preceded by the seafood tower -- the white-fish body runs alongside the iced shellfish tower. Works; the call holds, neither soars nor fights.",
  "Seafood Tower|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- amaro bitterness on cold shellfish flattens the delicate brine — needs sparkling instead. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Mata Hari Absinthe for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Year Bourbon's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Michter's 10 Year Bourbon for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "Michter's 10 Year Rye's rye-spice character drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Michter's 10 Year Rye for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye's rye-spice character overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Michter's US*1 Rye for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco with the seafood tower — premium craft blanco across raw oysters, shrimp, and lobster. The clean highland agave provides a premium-craft through-line; the additive-free story sells tableside. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Seafood Tower|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo's aged-agave character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- aged-agave register crowds the delicate shellfish frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Milagro Anejo on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado's rested-agave body leans against the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the barrel-rested body handles the seafood tower, and the oak threads the starter. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Seafood Tower|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Mocha Creme": "The seafood tower alongside the mocha creme -- the bright register lifts the sweet, the meal closes clean. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Seafood Tower|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 with the seafood tower — Black Forest Germany, 47 botanicals including lingonberries, forest-herbal premium craft. Excellent; Monkey 47's layered 47-botanical complexity frames the raw bar with Schwarzwald forest-herbal depth — one of the most complex craft-gin tower pairings on the menu.",
  "Seafood Tower|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder's blended Speyside softness fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Monkey Shoulder for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Montenegro Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum's Caribbean-rum register with bright sugarcane leans against the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the unaged body brightens the seafood tower, and the rum-and-lime thread softens the starter. Strong; the premium-craft blanco frames the fish at the Maestra-Tequilera register.",
  "Seafood Tower|Muga Reserva": "Muga Reserva's Tempranillo with vanilla-and-leather depth overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Muga Reserva on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Mushroom Bisque": "Seafood Tower alongside the mushroom bisque -- acid lifts the umami, the meal carries lift without losing depth. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "Seafood Tower|Mushrooms": "The seafood tower and the mushrooms -- the bright cuts the savory weight. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Seafood Tower|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Myers's Rum belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Negroni": "Negroni's stirred whiskey-led build sits alongside the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the bold-mix depth handles the seafood tower, and the bitters edge frames the starter. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Seafood Tower|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye's rye-spice character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Never Say Die Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Never Say Die Small Batch on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|New York Sour": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- whiskey-cocktail weight is wrong for cold shellfish — the opener needs lift, not bitters. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. New York Sour belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Nickel & Nickel Cabernet's single-vineyard Napa Cab depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Nickel & Nickel Cabernet lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolet's with the seafood tower — Dutch Turkish-rose gin against the raw bar. Excellent; Nolet's fruit-floral is the conversation-starter gin pairing for the tower — distinctive positioning vs traditional British offerings.",
  "Seafood Tower|Not a Paper Plane": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build holds the line with the tower's chilled shellfish — the cocktail lift carries the shellfish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Seafood Tower|Oban 14": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Oban 14 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Oban 18": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Oban 18 for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- añejo weight has no foil against cold shellfish — needs sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Ocho Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Odette Cabernet Sauvignon's Stags Leap Cab with iron-fist-in-a-velvet-glove quality overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Odette Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Old Elk Cigar Cut for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Old Emmer for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year's Heaven Hill's wheated heritage bond fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Old Fitzgerald 7 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year's aged wheated BIB's mature honey fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Old Fitzgerald 9 Year belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "Old Grand-Dad 16 Year's 16yr OGD high-rye bourbon crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Old Grand-Dad 16 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Opus One": "Mondavi-Rothschild's $950 prestige Cabernet — completely wrong weight class for cold shellfish. The wine buries every element of the tower. Never pour here; reserve Opus for the steak course where it can perform. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Seafood Tower|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity plays cleanly against the tower's chilled shellfish — the crisp acidity lifts the shellfish without weight, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Seafood Tower|Pablo Sour": "Reposado tequila, lemon, vanilla syrup, egg white. The silky foam and lemon brightness handles the raw-bar spread — oysters, shrimp, crab legs — and adds textural interest. Strong tower cocktail when guest wants agave with a conversation-piece texture.",
  "Seafood Tower|Paloma": "Reposado tequila, fresh grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The bright grapefruit handles raw-bar shellfish — oysters, shrimp, crab legs — and the cocktail's clean build doesn't crush anything. Strong tower cocktail when guest wants agave rather than bubbles.",
  "Seafood Tower|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye's allocated-icon Pappy rye character crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Patron Anejo on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver with the seafood tower — iconic blanco against raw oysters, shrimp, lobster. The clean citrus-forward character provides the through-line at the everyday register. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Seafood Tower|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Patron XO Cafe for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Bouzy Grand Cru Pinot Noir grower Champagne — structured and serious with ripe raspberry, red currant, fine bubbles, and minerally persistent finish. The Burgundian backbone and red-fruit weight elevate the tower's mixed shellfish and reset palate between oysters, shrimp, and crab. Peak grower rosé for the tower.",
  "Seafood Tower|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Seafood Tower and the PB brownie -- the bright register lifts the sweet, the meal closes clean. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Seafood Tower|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Peju Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- cognac weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs sparkling or a crisp white. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Pierre Ferrand Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "Côte des Blancs Chardonnay grower Champagne — prestige 2015 cuvée, layered with citrus zest, toasted brioche, white flowers, and creamy refined texture. The depth and complexity elevate the tower's mixed shellfish and the creamy texture plays against every element. Peak seafood tower Champagne. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Seafood Tower|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye's rye-spice character overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Pikesville Rye for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Pineau des Charentes": "Pineau des Charentes' sweet liqueur character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Pineau des Charentes on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's firm-tannin tar structure buries the delicate shellfish overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Pio Cesare Barbaresco for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth with the seafood tower — protected single-distillery gin against the raw bar. Excellent; Plymouth's softer character makes it a polished tower pairing when the occasion calls for less aggression than Tanq.",
  "Seafood Tower|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo's high-acid Nebbiolo with rose-and-tar grip overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery 2004 Cuvée Louise — 20-year-old Champagne from Reims with toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich palate, and fine persistent bubbles. The extraordinary complexity elevates the tower's mixed shellfish and the honey-almond profile plays against every element. Peak occasion Champagne for the tower. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Seafood Tower|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse after the seafood tower -- iced shellfish opens with showpiece presentation, the 40oz dual strip-and-filet following with two cuts in one, both courses indulgent in their own register. Strong; showpiece into showpiece reads steakhouse-classic.",
  "Seafood Tower|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|RAEN Royal St Robert": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Rainbow Trout": "The trout after the seafood tower -- the herb-finished trout rests against the showpiece tower. Works; the starter sits without crowding the main.",
  "Seafood Tower|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Rosé Cava — sparkling, red-fruit character, bright acid. Handles our mignonette's red-wine-vinegar sharpness and the cocktail-sauce horseradish heat without fighting the shellfish. Smart alternative when the table doesn't want Champagne. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Seafood Tower|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Redbreast 12 Year belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Redbreast 21 Year for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Remy VSOP Cognac's VSOP fine-champagne cognac clashes with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- cognac is wrong-course energy for the table's opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; one of those is the move. Remy VSOP Cognac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo's aged-agave character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- aged-agave register crowds the delicate shellfish frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Rey Sol Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka with the seafood tower — lava-filtered Icelandic vodka against the raw bar. Excellent; Reyka's mineral-glacial character is the most thematically right vodka for a cold seafood tower — oceanic meets oceanic.",
  "Seafood Tower|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Rishi's Old Antique for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Roast Half Chicken": "Roast Half Chicken with the seafood tower as the lead-in -- the herb-butter chicken threads the cool-brine opener. Strong; the starter sets the table for the chicken.",
  "Seafood Tower|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The seafood tower with the chickpea soup -- the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup meets the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bright cuts through the spice cleanly. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Seafood Tower|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Le Mesnil Grand Cru Chardonnay — sommelier's Champagne with chalky minerality, lemon curd, green apple, and almost no dosage. The pure austere precision elevates the tower's mixed shellfish and the chalk-saline character echoes oyster brine. Peak technical Champagne for the seafood tower. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Seafood Tower|Roku Gin": "Roku with the seafood tower — 6-Japanese-botanical Suntory gin against the raw bar. Strong; Roku's yuzu-sansho profile lifts the tower with Japanese-terroir distinction; Gray Whale's California ocean-botanicals and Botanist's Islay-foraged are the featured craft-tower matches, with Roku as the Japanese-story alternative.",
  "Seafood Tower|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey premium-sipping rum overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Ron Zacapa Rum lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year's aged Wild Turkey oak crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Russell's Reserve 10 Year for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Salt-Cured Halibut preceded by the seafood tower -- the firm halibut sits cleanly beside the seafood tower. Works; the call holds, neither soars nor fights.",
  "Seafood Tower|Sambuca": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Sambuca lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|San Simeon Stormwatch": "San Simeon Stormwatch's red-fruit-and-spice elegance overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the San Simeon Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The seafood tower and the garlic spinach -- two bright registers, the table reads fresh across the courses. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Seafood Tower|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Sauternes Glass on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Sazerac": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the cocktail's weight is forward for the table's first course. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sazerac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Sazerac Rye for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna's NZ craft single malt smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Scapegrace Fortuna on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Nebbiolo with tar, dried rose, and firm tannin. Aggressive structure against delicate cold seafood; the Barolo bullies the tower's nuance. Save this bottle for the Tomahawk or the Filet. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Seafood Tower|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads Riesling's crisp apricot-floral acidity sits beside the tower's chilled shellfish — the crisp acidity lifts the shellfish without weight, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Seafood Tower|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters' red-fruit-and-spice elegance overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Seared Scallops": "The seafood tower and the scallops -- shellfish-on-shellfish, the meal goes redundant. Avoid; the table reads heavy without payoff. Suggest Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut for the seafood tower, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre for the scallops.",
  "Seafood Tower|Seasonal Soup": "Seafood Tower alongside the seasonal soup -- the courses share the meal without conflict. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "Seafood Tower|Seasonal Vegetables": "Seafood Tower and the seasonal vegetables -- a clean call, no friction either way. Works; both items hold their register, no clash.",
  "Seafood Tower|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Shafer 1.5": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Shafer 1.5 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Shafer Hillside Select": "Shafer Hillside Select's Stags Leap allocated Cab power overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Shafer Hillside Select lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Shrimp Bisque": "The seafood tower and the shrimp bisque -- both shellfish-forward, the table doubles up on shellfish. Avoid; pick one or the other. Pair the seafood tower with Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut; pair the shrimp bisque with Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay.",
  "Seafood Tower|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco with the seafood tower — traditional craft across raw oysters, shrimp, lobster. Strong; the rustic character gives the tower a distinctive craft thread.",
  "Seafood Tower|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado's añejo-pour depth leans against the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the barrel-rested body handles the seafood tower, and the cooked-agave note underlines the starter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Seafood Tower|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon's American-oak Cab character crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Simon Family Estate Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Simon Family Estate Reserve on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "Spottswoode Lyndenhurst's Napa estate Cab with structured cassis crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Spottswoode Lyndenhurst for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "Napa Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, citrus-forward. Similar function to the Sancerre but California-forward. Bright acid delivers across the full tower of oysters, crab, shrimp, lobster. Pick when the guest prefers American producers over French. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Seafood Tower|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the St Supéry Élu Meritage on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Stagg Jr. Batch #15 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit with the seafood tower — Russian wheat vodka, quadruple-filtered ultra-premium. Excellent; Elit's crystalline clarity is the luxury competitor to Grey Goose on the tower pairing — where Goose is silky, Elit is glacial.",
  "Seafood Tower|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty. The zesty acid meshes with brine across the tower and the tropical-citrus resets palate between oysters, shrimp, crab, and lobster. Strong accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for seafood tower — reliable bright pour for mixed shellfish.",
  "Seafood Tower|Swordfish": "Swordfish with the seafood tower as the lead-in -- the lemon-bright swordfish anchors against the cool-brine opener. Strong; the starter sets the table for the swordfish.",
  "Seafood Tower|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 with the seafood tower — premium Tanqueray No. Ten against the raw bar. Excellent; Tanq 10's citrus-chamomile profile is the martini-gin-collector's seafood tower pairing — more refined than flagship Tanq for the occasion.",
  "Seafood Tower|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray with the seafood tower — iconic London Dry against the raw bar. Excellent; Tanq's juniper and proof are the benchmark gin-and-raw-bar pairing — the classic choice before guests knew to ask for martinis.",
  "Seafood Tower|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the seafood tower, and the soft-spice note softens the starter. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Seafood Tower|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|The BG": "The BG's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- this cocktail's register doesn't lift the opener — try a sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve The BG for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|The Happy Wife": "The Happy Wife's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- this cocktail's register doesn't lift the opener — try a sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a light cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save The Happy Wife for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|The Manhattan": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- whiskey-cocktail weight is wrong for cold shellfish — the opener needs lift, not bitters. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour The Manhattan on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|The Prisoner Red Blend": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve The Prisoner Red Blend for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk after the seafood tower -- showpiece into showpiece, the iced shellfish lineup opens the table with theater, the 36oz long-bone cut following with smoky char that the cold opener primed the palate to anticipate. Strong; both courses earn the spectacle billing.",
  "Seafood Tower|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Titos Vodka": "Tito's with the seafood tower — American corn workhorse against the raw bar. Excellent; Tito's keeps the seafood in focus without pulling attention — the comfortable default when a table orders the tower.",
  "Seafood Tower|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character finds neutral with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the entry Suntory body sits with the seafood tower, and the citrus lifts the starter. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "Seafood Tower|Tomato Basil": "Seafood Tower and the tomato basil together -- both pull from the bright side, the meal carries air through to the close. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Seafood Tower|Transfusion": "Seafood Tower: chilled shellfish lineup meets Transfusion. Excellent; Concord-grape + ginger-ale + vodka cocktail's bright-fruit-ginger character cuts through the salty lineup at approachable register.",
  "Seafood Tower|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado's rested-agave body sits alongside the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the rested-agave depth sits with the seafood tower, and the oak threads the starter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Seafood Tower|Truffle Fries": "The seafood tower and the truffle fries -- acid breaks the umami stack, the table earns the brighter note. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Seafood Tower|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s gentle Irish blend drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Tullamore D.E.W. for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna with the seafood tower as the lead-in -- the sesame-edged tuna composes with the cool-brine opener. Works; the opener composes at neutral register.",
  "Seafood Tower|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Seafood Tower alongside the curry -- the chilled shellfish threads the curry. Strong; the openers compose cleanly together.",
  "Seafood Tower|Venge Family Reserve": "Venge Family Reserve's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Venge Family Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Venge Scout's Honor": "Venge Scout's Honor's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Venge Scout's Honor on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Venge Silencieux": "Venge Silencieux's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Venge Silencieux on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Vesper": "Nolet's gin, Ketel One vodka, Lillet — stirred cold. The classic martini structure with Nolet's softer botanical profile handles the full raw-bar spread; Lillet's citrus cleans each bite. Peak martini pairing for the tower's chilled-shellfish showcase.",
  "Seafood Tower|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut — iconic yellow label Champagne from Reims, Pinot Noir-dominant blend with ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse, lively acidity. The balanced crowd-pleaser resets palate between tower elements and the biscuit plays against rich shellfish. Peak reliable Champagne for the tower. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Seafood Tower|Vieux Carré": "Vieux Carré's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- whiskey-cocktail weight is wrong for cold shellfish — the opener needs lift, not bitters. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Vieux Carré for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Villon Cognac": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- cognac is wrong-course energy for the table's opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Villon Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Weller 107": "Weller 107's bigger-body wheated buries scallop sweetness fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Weller 107 for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Weller 12 Year for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Weller Millennium lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Weller Special Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley with the seafood tower — Buffalo Trace wheat vodka against the raw bar. Excellent; Wheatley handles the tower at value register — a smart call when Goose isn't necessary.",
  "Seafood Tower|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the WhistlePig 12 Year Old World on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the WhistlePig 15 Year for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments for the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Seafood Tower|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's craft high-rye oak spice drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Willett Pot Still Reserve lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Woodford Reserve lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Woodford Reserve Double Oaked lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seafood Tower|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Woodford Reserve Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Woodinville Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seafood Tower|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character sits alongside the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the restrained whisky body threads the seafood tower, and the precision rounds the starter. Works; retailer-pick wasted on salad.",
  "Seafood Tower|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the seafood tower, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the starter. Works; a gentle composed match.",
  "Seafood Tower|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Seared Scallops|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 93.7-proof Barton. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Seared Scallops|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against sweet-wheat. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Seared Scallops|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — mocha-tannin body obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save 1881 for the steak course where its approachable Napa character peaks.",
  "Seared Scallops|2 James Gin": "2 James with seared scallops — Detroit local craft against scallop sweetness. Excellent; 2 James's American interpretation frames scallops with Midwest-craft character — less refined than Tanq 10's citrus-chamomile, more hometown than any British London Dry.",
  "Seared Scallops|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against rare release. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Seared Scallops|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16 with seared scallops — 16yr double-matured Speyside against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino with seared scallops — filtered aged tequila against caramelized scallop. The aged-but-clear character meets the sear with more depth than standard blancos while preserving visual blanco clarity. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Seared Scallops|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado with seared scallops — near-añejo reposado against caramelized scallop. The aged depth meets the sear with more conviction than younger reposados bring. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Seared Scallops|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Alexander Valley Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth texture. The oak-vanilla frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness without overpowering and the apple-pear adds lift against the pan-sear. Strong Chardonnay for scallops — oak-vanilla echoes the pan-butter beautifully.",
  "Seared Scallops|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Sonoma Meritage against scallop sweetness — full-body cassis-cedar-chocolate structure buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Cyrus for the steak course.",
  "Seared Scallops|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Sonoma Bordeaux blend against scallop sweetness — plum-blackberry-mocha red buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Homestead Red for the steak course.",
  "Seared Scallops|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino with seared scallops — Friulian amaro against scallop delicacy. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Seared Scallops|Ancho Reyes": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Ancho Reyes. Avoid; chile-liqueur overwhelms scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Seared Scallops|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy with seared scallops — flagship AE port-finish against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "AE Cask Strength with seared scallops — 117+ proof AE against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "AE Triple Cask with seared scallops — triple-cask-finished AE against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Aperol": "Aperol with seared scallops — soft aperitivo against scallop delicacy. Avoid; even the softest amaro is still wrong for scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Asparagus": "The scallops and the asparagus on different courses -- the starter opens, the side rides with the entrée. Strong; the meal builds cleanly.",
  "Seared Scallops|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The scallops and the au gratin -- cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Seared Scallops|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Paso Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — plush warm-spice obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Austin Hope for the steak course where its Paso richness peaks.",
  "Seared Scallops|Averna Amaro": "Averna with seared scallops — amaro against scallop delicacy. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Seared Scallops|Aviation": "Aviation gin, Luxardo maraschino, crème de violette, ginger syrup. The floral lightness meets scallops' clean-seared sweetness without crushing; cherry-almond from the maraschino adds depth. Peak light cocktail pairing for scallops — pale purple glass makes the table.",
  "Seared Scallops|Aviation Gin": "Aviation with seared scallops — smooth American craft against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Aviation's soft juniper frames scallops with approachable balance — less cut than flagship Tanqueray, less botanical than Monkey 47.",
  "Seared Scallops|Avion 44": "Avion 44 with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 44-month aged. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Seared Scallops|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver with seared scallops — filtered highland blanco against caramelized scallop. The clean agave frames the sear at the approachable global register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bacardi Rum": "Seared Scallops: scallop delicacy meets Bacardi Rum. Excellent; the ultra-clean Bacardi register is the textbook light-rum answer to scallops — neutral enough to let the sear speak.",
  "Seared Scallops|Baileys Irish Cream": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Baileys. Avoid; cream-liqueur wrong for scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Seared Scallops|Baker's": "Baker's with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 107-proof. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Seared Scallops|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 Am Oak with seared scallops — vanilla-forward Speyside against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask with seared scallops — rum-cask-finished Speyside against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel with seared scallops — single-barrel Speyside against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood with seared scallops — 21yr port-cask Speyside against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude with seared scallops — Bardstown Bourbon Co. French-wine finish against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against multi-distillery blend. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against KBS-finish. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare with seared scallops — Foursquare rum-cask against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against Fusion blend. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against retailer-pick. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Seared Scallops|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's with seared scallops — scallop caramelized sweetness against rye pepper. Avoid; the register mismatch is structural.",
  "Seared Scallops|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against aged high-rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Seared Scallops|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye with seared scallops — malted grain against caramelized scallop. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Seared Scallops|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask with seared scallops — Beam BH red-wine finish against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast with seared scallops — caramelized scallop doesn't need the pour's added toast sweetness. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bee's Knees": "Empress Indigo gin, lemon, honey lavender syrup. The three-ingredient purity meets scallops' clean-seared simplicity; honey aligns with the scallops' natural sweetness and lavender adds aromatic depth. Peak minimalist pairing for the cleanest seafood on the menu.",
  "Seared Scallops|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater with seared scallops — 24-hour-steeped London Dry against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Beefeater's balance of juniper and citrus frames scallops at accessible register — where Tanq overpowers slightly and Plymouth softens.",
  "Seared Scallops|Beignets": "The scallops alongside the beignets -- the entree yields to the dessert, the meal arc reads on-key. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Seared Scallops|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere with seared scallops — Polish rye vodka against scallop sweetness. Excellent; the slight rye pepper threads the scallop's natural sweetness at a distinctive register — more character than Tito's round or Goose's silk.",
  "Seared Scallops|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against blend. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Seared Scallops|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against richer single-barrel. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Seared Scallops|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 93-proof single-barrel. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Seared Scallops|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's SfB with seared scallops — cask-strength Blanton's against scallop delicacy. Avoid; barrel-proof wrong for scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 10th-Pact milestone. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Seared Scallops|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against Pact 8 blend. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Seared Scallops|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against Pact 9 blend. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat with seared scallops — Philadelphia citrus-forward craft against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Bluecoat's 4-citrus profile mirrors scallop sweetness with American-artisan character.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Never pair Noemia with seared scallops — graphite and full body overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and the soft tannin still fights browned-butter sweetness. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry with seared scallops — original 8-botanical recipe, juniper-assertive London Dry. Excellent; Bombay Dry's angular juniper is the sharpest gin-scallop lift in the batch — more assertive than Beefeater's balance, less proof-heavy than Tanq.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire with seared scallops — vapor-infused 10-botanical against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Sapphire's softer floral character frames scallops where Bombay Dry's angular juniper contrasts them — the softer sibling for a different pairing philosophy.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow with the scallops -- the roasted bone matches the sweet scallop plate. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Seared Scallops|Booker's": "Booker's with seared scallops — cask-strength Beam against scallop delicacy. Avoid; barrel-proof wrong for scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Botanist Gin": "Botanist with seared scallops — Islay-foraged craft gin against scallop sweetness. Excellent; the 22 wildcrafted Hebridean botanicals frame scallops with oceanic-island terroir — coastal Scotland meets the scallop's marine origin.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, rum-infused cherries, orange, bitters. The spirit-forward bourbon obliterates the scallops' delicate sweetness and the vanilla-caramel muddies the clean seared profile. Never pair — scallops want crisp white or bubbles, not a heavy whiskey cocktail.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select Maker's Mark with seared scallops — custom-stave wheated against scallop delicacy. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 90-proof Virginia. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bowmore 12": "Bowmore 12 with seared scallops — scallops' sweet delicacy gets buried by any peat, Bowmore's gentleness included. Avoid; reach for gin, vodka, or a light white wine.",
  "Seared Scallops|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Italian food-wine Barbera against scallop sweetness — Braida's dark cherry and blackberry buries the delicate shellfish despite light tannin. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Braida for red meat, pasta, or rich starters where the food-wine role peaks.",
  "Seared Scallops|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Brandlin Mount Veeder Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — firm graphite-earth obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Brandlin for the steak course where its Mount Veeder character peaks.",
  "Seared Scallops|Broccoli Cheddar": "Seared Scallops and the broccoli cheddar together -- the caramelized sear meets the cream-and-cheddar soup. Strong; both openers earn their place on the meal.",
  "Seared Scallops|Broccolini": "The scallops opens the meal, the broccolini with the entree -- the rare-center scallop frames the tender green side. Strong; both items.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich's progressive unpeated Islay fights the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not a scotch. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Bruichladdich lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seared Scallops|Brussels and Belly": "Seared Scallops early, Brussels + Belly later with the main -- the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness primes the table for Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts. Strong; the starter sets the table before the side lands.",
  "Seared Scallops|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against BT oak. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Seared Scallops|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Buffalo Trace Cream. Avoid; bourbon-cream weight wrong for scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Seared Scallops|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against rye spice. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Seared Scallops|Burrata": "Burrata with the scallops -- the basil-finished burrata wraps the scallops. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Seared Scallops|Butternut Squash": "The scallops with the butternut squash -- the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness meets the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream. Strong; the opening courses share the table at full register.",
  "Seared Scallops|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "Cade Howell Mountain Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — firm iron-mineral obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Cade for the steak course where its LEED-estate Howell character peaks.",
  "Seared Scallops|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila with seared scallops — value reposado against caramelized scallop. The accessible agave meets the sear at the entry register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Seared Scallops|Campari": "Campari with seared scallops — bitter orange-rhubarb against scallop delicacy. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Seared Scallops|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club with seared scallops — traditional Canadian against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Canadian Club's grain-forward profile is wrong for scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Canadian Club 43 Year": "CC 43 Year with seared scallops — 43yr aged Canadian against scallop delicacy. Avoid; save the collector bottle — scallops demand lighter spirits.",
  "Seared Scallops|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift mis-pairs with the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- this light spirit's register is wrong for the starter — try a different pour. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not a light spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Captain Morgan Rum lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Seared Scallops|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing with seared scallops — Sazerac single-barrel Canadian against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Caribou Crossing's craft profile is wrong for scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Carrot Cake": "Seared Scallops and the carrot cake -- the savory course resolves into sugar, the meal closes with contrast. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Seared Scallops|Casa LBV Port": "Casa LBV Port's dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Casa LBV Port belongs on the steak course.",
  "Seared Scallops|Casamigos Mezcal": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Casamigos Mezcal. Avoid; smoke overwhelms scallops. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Seared Scallops|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Iconic Napa Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — velvety cocoa-mocha obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Caymus for the steak course where the crowd-pleasing Rutherford Cab peaks.",
  "Seared Scallops|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Chambord on the steak course instead.",
  "Seared Scallops|Cheesecake": "Seared Scallops and the cheesecake -- the savory course resolves into sugar, the meal closes with contrast. Works; the bookends compose neutrally.",
  "Seared Scallops|Chocolate Brownie": "Seared Scallops and the brownie -- the entree yields to the dessert, the meal arc reads on-key. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Seared Scallops|Chocolate Cake": "Seared Scallops and the chocolate cake -- savory-into-sweet, the classic close lands intact. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Seared Scallops|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin with seared scallops — creamy Polish potato vodka against scallop sweetness. Excellent; the silky texture mirrors scallop richness with a different softness than Goose's wheat-derived silk.",
  "Seared Scallops|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Brothers with seared scallops — California brandy against scallop delicacy. Avoid; brandy overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Château Batailley": "Classified Pauillac Bordeaux against scallop sweetness — firm-tannin blackcurrant-graphite buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Batailley for the steak course.",
  "Seared Scallops|Château Beaucastel": "Never pair Beaucastel with seared scallops — truffle and Provençal herbs overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and the medium tannin fights browned-butter. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Seared Scallops|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Never pair Château Gombaude-Guillot with seared scallops — plush Pomerol Merlot's dark plum and mocha crush clean-seared shellfish sweetness. Lead with Raventós Cava, Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé. Route Gombaude-Guillot to the filet.",
  "Seared Scallops|Château Haut Segottes": "Right-bank Bordeaux against scallop sweetness — plum-truffle-earth Grand Cru buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Haut Segottes for the steak course.",
  "Seared Scallops|Château de Rouillac": "Left-bank Bordeaux against scallop sweetness — firm-tannin blackcurrant-cedar buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Château de Rouillac for the steak course.",
  "Seared Scallops|Clam Chowder": "The scallops alongside the chowder -- both bring shellfish as the headline. Avoid; one is enough. Pair the scallops with Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre; pair the chowder with Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis.",
  "Seared Scallops|Clase Azul Anejo": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Clase Azul Anejo. Avoid; luxury-añejo too heavy for scallops. Save the bottle — reach for a blanco, light rum, or vodka.",
  "Seared Scallops|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against ceremonial. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Seared Scallops|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against joven blend. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Seared Scallops|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Clase Azul Guerrero. Avoid; smoke overwhelms scallops regardless of softness. Save the bottle.",
  "Seared Scallops|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata with seared scallops — ceramic-bottle luxury against caramelized scallop. The smooth agave frames the sear with premium ceremony. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Seared Scallops|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado with seared scallops — premium reposado against caramelized scallop. The smoothness frames the sear with ceremony; pour when a guest wants the full Clase Azul experience. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Seared Scallops|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against top-tier ceremonial. Avoid; save the Ultra.",
  "Seared Scallops|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against Beam wheated. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Seared Scallops|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Never pair Clos Petit Ona with seared scallops — garrigue and warm spice overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and the kirsch fights browned-butter. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Seared Scallops|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with seared scallops — Napa Cab-cask rosé against caramelized scallop. The red-wine fruit plays with the scallop's sear distinctively. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Seared Scallops|Contratto Vermouth": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Contratto Vermouth. Avoid; vermouth's botanical profile overwhelms scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Seared Scallops|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco with seared scallops — Sazerac single-estate against caramelized scallop. The highland-agave character meets the sear distinctly from Don Julio's benchmark. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Seared Scallops|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado with seared scallops — Sazerac reposado against caramelized scallop. The highland agave's brightness lifts the sear; a clean, character-driven pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Seared Scallops|Corazon Sazerac": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Corazon Sazerac. Avoid; aged-agave rye-cask too heavy for scallops. Reach for a blanco, light rum, or vodka.",
  "Seared Scallops|Corazon Stagg": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Corazon Stagg. Avoid; heavy-caramel-intense-añejo wrong for scallops. Reach for a blanco, light rum, or vodka.",
  "Seared Scallops|Corazon Weller": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Corazon Weller. Avoid; ex-wheated-bourbon-barrel añejo too heavy for scallops. Reach for a blanco, light rum, or vodka.",
  "Seared Scallops|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Never pair Corazón Malbec with seared scallops — jam-forward fruit and full body overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and the velvety tannin fights browned-butter sweetness. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Seared Scallops|Corpse Reviver": "Warner's gin, Lillet, Pierre Ferrand cognac, lemon, absinthe rinse. The layered botanicals with anise-on-the-nose meet scallops' clean-seared character elegantly; lemon cuts, cognac adds depth. Peak sophisticated cocktail pairing for scallops — history meets delicate seafood.",
  "Seared Scallops|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier with seared scallops — traditional cognac against scallop delicacy. Avoid; cognac overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Cowboy Ribeye": "The cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness overwhelms the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness: the rendered fat overshadows the scallops, and the marbled char crowds out the seared-rare scallop plate. Avoid; the courses can't share a meal cleanly. Pair the scallops with Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre on a different visit; pour Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, or Shafer Hillside Select alongside the cowboy ribeye.",
  "Seared Scallops|Crab Cake": "The crab cake and the scallops share shellfish as the primary character. Avoid; the meal doubles down without contrast. Route the crab cake with Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin or the scallops with Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre instead.",
  "Seared Scallops|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12 with seared scallops — layered Speyside against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Creamed Spinach": "Seared Scallops and the creamed spinach -- cream and protein share the meal, the build reads full. Works; both items hold their register, no clash.",
  "Seared Scallops|Creamy Potato": "Seared Scallops alongside the potato soup -- the butter-finished scallop anchors against the potato soup. Strong; the openers compose cleanly together.",
  "Seared Scallops|Creme Brulee": "Seared Scallops and the brulee -- protein hands off to dessert, the close builds without conflict. Works; both bookends earn their place, the meal arc holds.",
  "Seared Scallops|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Eola-Amity Willamette Pinot — cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mineral, low tannin. The soft tannin leaves room for scallop sweetness and the minerality plays against caramelized-exterior char. Strong Willamette Pinot for scallops — benchmark Oregon red on the delicate shellfish.",
  "Seared Scallops|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal with seared scallops — iconic Canadian against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Crown Royal's vanilla-sweetness is wrong for scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Cucumber Gimlet": "Hendrick's muddled with cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda. The clean cocktail profile meets scallops' clean-seared sweet character; cucumber-and-ginger adds aromatic depth without crushing the delicacy. Peak refreshing cocktail for scallops when guest wants something lighter than Champagne.",
  "Seared Scallops|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12 with seared scallops — Matusalem-cask Highland against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Sancerre Rouge — Loire red Pinot, raspberry, chalk, dried herbs, light body. The chalk-minerality plays against scallop sweetness and the dried-herbs frame caramelized-exterior char. Peak Loire red for scallops — distinct French Pinot on the delicate shellfish. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Seared Scallops|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Rare Napa CF against scallop sweetness — Darioush's medium-tannin violet-cherry structure buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Darioush for the steak course.",
  "Seared Scallops|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Darioush Silverado Trail Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — plush mocha-spice obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Darioush for the steak course where its Persian-inspired character peaks.",
  "Seared Scallops|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin with seared scallops — Detroit craft juniper against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Detroit City's American interpretation frames scallops with hometown character.",
  "Seared Scallops|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City with seared scallops — Michigan craft vodka against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Detroit City's approachable craft profile frames scallops at hometown register — less backbone than HDW 100, less floral than Hangar 1.",
  "Seared Scallops|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15 with seared scallops — sherry-finish Highland blend against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label with seared scallops — Highland blend against scallop delicacy. Avoid; Scotch overwhelms scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|Disaronno": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Disaronno. Avoid; amaretto weight wrong for scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Seared Scallops|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against craft rye. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Seared Scallops|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Doctor Bird. Avoid; pot-still funk wrong for scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Seared Scallops|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Willamette flagship Pinot Blend — dark cherry, Bing cherry, toasted spice, forest floor, silky soft tannin. The silky tannin leaves room for scallop sweetness and the toasted-spice plays against caramelized-exterior char. Peak flagship Willamette Pinot for scallops — layered elegance on delicate shellfish. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Seared Scallops|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "Never pair Tempier with seared scallops — game and smoky mineral overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and firm tannin fights browned-butter sweetness. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Seared Scallops|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Loire Valley Sauvignon Blanc from Pouilly-Fumé — gun-flint smoky minerality beneath grapefruit and lime zest. Fuller and more serious than New Zealand Sauv Blanc. The flinty minerality frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the citrus cuts the browned edge. Peak serious Sauv Blanc for scallops. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Seared Scallops|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Never pair Grand Tinel with seared scallops — black pepper and garrigue overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and full body fights browned-butter sweetness. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Seared Scallops|Don Fulano Anejo": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Don Fulano. Avoid; aged-agave heavy for scallops. Reach for a blanco, light rum, or vodka.",
  "Seared Scallops|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco with seared scallops — highland refinement against caramelized scallop. The clean craft character frames the sear with more elegance than Patron Silver's globally-familiar take. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Seared Scallops|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado with seared scallops — highland refinement against caramelized scallop. The honey and vanilla thread through the sear in an elegant register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Seared Scallops|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942 with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against iconic 1942. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Seared Scallops|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th with seared scallops — filtered reposado against caramelized scallop. The clarity of the 70th is the perfect counterpoint to the sear; arguably the best reposado-scallop pairing on the menu. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Seared Scallops|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco with seared scallops — iconic 80-proof blanco against caramelized scallop. The fresh agave and citrus thread through the sear cleanly; the benchmark everyday-premium blanco with this classic seafood. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Seared Scallops|Don Julio Reposado": "Seared Scallops: caramelized scallop meets Don Julio Reposado's bright agave. Vanilla picks up the scallop's sear; signature reposado-and-seafood. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Seared Scallops|Drambuie": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Drambuie. Avoid; scotch-whisky-honey liqueur wrong for scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Seared Scallops|Dunville's Single Malt": "Dunville's Single Malt with seared scallops — Belfast Irish single malt against scallop delicacy. Works; Dunville's is gentle enough for scallops within Irish whiskey's category advantage.",
  "Seared Scallops|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with seared scallops — BT BiB against scallop delicacy. Avoid; barrel-proof wrong for scallops.",
  "Seared Scallops|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 100-proof BIB. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
