  "Seasonal Vegetables|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye-and-oak weight handles the seasonal vegetables, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the side. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Seasonal Vegetables|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year's rye-spice character leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye-and-oak weight handles the seasonal vegetables, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Seasonal Vegetables|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year's rye-spice character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the high-rye spice register matches the seasonal vegetables, and the rye-grain bite cuts the side. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Seasonal Vegetables|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments' rye-spice character leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye-and-oak weight handles the seasonal vegetables, and the rye-pepper note underlines the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Seasonal Vegetables|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye backbone drives the seasonal vegetables, and the rye-grain bite cuts the side. Works; collector-bottle depth gives the dessert ultra-premium character.",
  "Seasonal Vegetables|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's craft high-rye oak spice leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; rye's brightness is a gentle counterpoint.",
  "Seasonal Vegetables|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; Woodford Double Oaked handles the richness at KY-benchmark register.",
  "Seasonal Vegetables|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon backbone carries the seasonal vegetables, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; the boldness pushes slightly past what the soup asks for.",
  "Seasonal Vegetables|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye spice cuts the seasonal vegetables, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the side. Works; the extra proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Seasonal Vegetables|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the dry rye body wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; richer profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Seasonal Vegetables|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the Japanese whisky balance frames the seasonal vegetables, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the side. Works; AE CS handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Seasonal Vegetables|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the umami-tinged finish frames the side. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "Seasonal Vegetables|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Asparagus": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: dark-fruit Cab body settles into the cream-rich side; firm tannin cuts the lactic edge and the oak threads the indulgence. A capable pair; richness aligns if not perfectly. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Beignets": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's tannin scrubs the starter's fat while dark fruit picks up the umami notes. An excellent opening pour — weight and fruit both land. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Bone-In Filet": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's deep dark-fruit and grippy tannin leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Broccoli Cheddar": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Broccolini": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino on the steak course instead.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Burrata": "Never pair Sesti with burrata — firm tannin and high acid clash against fresh cream and the sandalwood earth buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Butternut Squash": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Carrot Cake": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Cheesecake": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Chilean Seabass": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Chocolate Brownie": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino on the steak course instead.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Chocolate Cake": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Clam Chowder": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Cowboy Ribeye": "Tuscany Brunello DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Sesti biodynamic, cherry, dried rose, sandalwood, high acid, firm tannin. The firm tannin meets Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in ribeye and the high acid lifts the rendered fat. Peak biodynamic-Brunello for the ribeye — Sesti's elegance polishes bold char. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Crab Cake": "Never pair Sesti with crab cake — firm tannin, high acid, and sandalwood earth bury delicate lump crab and the savory-rich profile fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: structured red meets dairy-driven side: the bold tannin scrubs the cream, the dark fruit lifts the indulgence into balance. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Creamy Potato": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Creme Brulee": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Escargot": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's firm cassis-and-cocoa tannin meets at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bold Cab register matches the buttered escargot, and the espresso-cocoa note rounds the starter. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Faroe Island Salmon": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino on the steak course instead.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Filet Mignon": "Tuscany Brunello DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Sesti biodynamic, cherry, dried rose, sandalwood, high acid, firm tannin, elegant. The elegance matches butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the sandalwood plays against escargot-butter. Peak biodynamic-Brunello for filet — a refined Montalcino anchor on the delicate cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|French Onion": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's bold cherry-earth cassis leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: the Grilled Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold creamy dressing meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's structure meets the Caesar's charred romaine, anchovy umami, and bold dressing — weight on weight. Works — the Caesar can hold the tannin.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Gumbo": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity crowds the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino on the steak course instead.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|House Wedge": "Tuscany Brunello DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Sesti biodynamic, cherry, dried rose, sandalwood, firm tannin. The firm tannin holds against blue-cheese funk and the dried-rose lift plays with bacon-fat. Works for wedge — cold iceberg dulls Sesti's aromatics, so a lighter Chianti lifts higher.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Kansas City": "Tuscany Brunello DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Sesti biodynamic, cherry, dried rose, sandalwood, high acid, firm tannin. The firm tannin meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the high acid lifts beefy char. Strong Brunello for the strip — Sesti's elegance polishes the American cut.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Loaded Potato": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's bold cherry-earth cassis sits beside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Lobster Mac": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's bold cherry-earth cassis leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the opulent body matches the indulgent side, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Market Fish": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Mocha Creme": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Mushroom Bisque": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's bold cherry-earth cassis sits alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: Cab tannin frames the earthy mushroom-and-truffle depth; dark fruit lifts the umami into the wine's body. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Prime Tartare": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's bold cherry-earth cassis holds the line with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the concentrated body grips the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Rainbow Trout": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Roast Half Chicken": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's bold cherry-earth cassis reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the oak underlines the char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overwhelms the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overshadows the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Seafood Tower": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Sesti with seared scallops — firm tannin and sandalwood earth overwhelm the delicate sweet shellfish and the high acid fights the browned-butter. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Seasonal Soup": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino for the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Seasonal Vegetables": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Shrimp Bisque": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's bold cherry-earth cassis sits alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Sesti with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by firm tannin and high acid and the sandalwood earth has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Swordfish": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Tomato Basil": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Sesti Brunello di Montalcino on the steak course instead.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Truffle Fries": "Tuscany Brunello DOCG Sangiovese Grosso — Sesti biodynamic, cherry, dried rose, sandalwood, firm tannin. The earthy sandalwood echoes truffle-oil mushroom depth and the firm tannin cuts through parm-richness. Peak biodynamic-Brunello for truffle fries — Sesti's earth-on-earth Tuscan match. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Sesti Brunello di Montalcino's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Sesti Brunello di Montalcino belongs on the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Asparagus": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin. The plush tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the vanilla-mocha plays against the lactic richness. Works as Stags Leap Cab for au gratin — Shafer opulence on the cheesy side.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Beignets": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- tannin can't pivot to warm pastry sugar — the pour reads dry, the dessert reads heavy. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Shafer 1.5 on the steak course instead.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Bone Marrow": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin. The plush tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the mocha-vanilla plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak Stags Leap Cab for marrow — Shafer opulence on the rich starter. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Bone-In Filet": "Shafer 1.5's bold coffee-juniper cassis holds steady against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the concentrated body carries the strip, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Broccoli Cheddar": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Broccolini": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Shafer 1.5 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Burrata": "Stags Leap Shafer Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — 1.5's opulent blackberry-vanilla structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Butternut Squash": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Carrot Cake": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Cheesecake": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Chilean Seabass": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Chocolate Brownie": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Chocolate Cake": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Shafer 1.5 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Clam Chowder": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Cowboy Ribeye": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level named for magnum format, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin, smooth full body. The plush body handles ribeye's big marbled build and the mocha plays against beefy char. Peak Stags Leap Cab for ribeye — bridge-to-Hillside-Select on the bold cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Crab Cake": "Stags Leap Shafer Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — 1.5's opulent plush-tannin structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Shafer 1.5 for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Creamy Potato": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Shafer 1.5 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Creme Brulee": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Shafer 1.5 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Escargot": "Shafer 1.5's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the structured body wraps the buttered escargot, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Faroe Island Salmon": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the salmon's rich oily flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Filet Mignon": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin. The plush tannin meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the vanilla-mocha plays against escargot-butter. Strong Stags Leap Cab for filet — Shafer opulence on the delicate cut.",
  "Shafer 1.5|French Onion": "Shafer 1.5's bold coffee-juniper cassis sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Grilled Caesar": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin. The plush tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mocha plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as Stags Leap Cab for grilled Caesar — Shafer opulence on the layered salad.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Gumbo": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Shafer 1.5 on the steak course instead.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Shafer 1.5's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Shafer 1.5|House Wedge": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin. The plush tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the vanilla plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as Stags Leap Cab for the wedge — Shafer opulence reframes the classic salad; whites peak higher.",
  "Shafer 1.5|Kansas City": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin. The plush structure meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the mocha plays against beefy char. Peak Stags Leap Cab for the strip — Shafer opulence on the American cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
