  "Shrimp Cocktail|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Fox & Oden Double Oaked with shrimp cocktail — Detroit Double Oaked against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet liqueur is dessert energy — wrong place for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Frangelico for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "Rutherford Bosché Cab against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — firm-tannin full-body red and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Freemark Abbey Bosché for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|French 75": "Inverroche gin with lemon, vanilla syrup, Prosecco top in a flute. Citrus and bubbles cut the chilled shrimp's sweet brine and lift the cocktail sauce's horseradish edge. Peak classic pairing — bubbles-with-shrimp is textbook and the gin adds aromatic depth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|French Onion": "Shrimp Cocktail alongside the French onion -- acid breaks the umami stack, the table earns the brighter note. Works; the openers share the table cleanly, sequence or share.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Frias Block 5": "Frias Block 5 Spring Mountain Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — firm iron-herbal is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Frias for the steak course where its single-block character belongs.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "Classic Piedmont Barolo against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — firm-tannin Vajra and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Vajra for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Shrimp Cocktail: this delicate seafood starter's briny-fresh sweetness meets the Moscato's peach-and-honey sweetness with delicate effervescence. Moscato's sweetness on a savory plate — wrong register. Strong when the dish plays sweet — not the default.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco with shrimp cocktail — 80-proof G4 against cold shrimp. The craft agave cuts through cocktail sauce with Camarena-family character. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof with shrimp cocktail — high-proof reposado against cold shrimp. Strong; the 104-proof can overpower the cold seafood's delicacy; Don Julio is the balanced call.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue Irish Whiskey's Irish whiskey smoothness crowds out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Garavogue Irish Whiskey on the steak course instead.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|George T. Stagg": "George T. Stagg's cask-strength BTAC bourbon power crowds out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the George T. Stagg on the steak course instead.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Ghost Block Oakville Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — firm graphite-oak is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Ghost Block for the steak course where its Napa-strip structure belongs.",
