  "Shrimp Cocktail|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado with shrimp cocktail — triple-distilled reposado against cold shrimp. The clean modern profile threads through the cocktail sauce cleanly. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam with shrimp cocktail — Sazerac Bronfman tribute against cold shrimp. Avoid; whiskey wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Mocha Creme": "The shrimp cocktail and the mocha creme -- the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish bookends with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 with shrimp cocktail — 47-botanical German gin against cold shrimp. Strong; Monkey 47's forest-herbal complexity elevates the classic cold app at premium-craft register.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder with shrimp cocktail — Speyside-malt blend against cold shrimp. Avoid; Scotch wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- amaro bitterness on cold shellfish flattens the delicate brine — needs sparkling instead. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Montenegro Amaro for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum: world's oldest continuously-operating rum distillery, Barbados since 1703. Excellent on shrimp cocktail; the traditional-craft character with its subtle molasses hint adds tradition-weight to the classic light-rum-on-shellfish move — where Bacardi is the workhorse, Mount Gay is the heritage reach.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Muga Reserva": "Never pair Muga with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by oak-vanilla and leather, and the savory depth has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Mushroom Bisque": "The shrimp cocktail preceded by the mushroom bisque -- the bright cuts the savory weight. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Mushrooms": "The shrimp cocktail and the mushrooms -- acid lifts the umami, the meal carries lift without losing depth. Works; sequence them or share the table, no concern either way.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Myers's Rum belongs on the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Negroni": "Negroni's citrus-herbal build holds steady against the shrimp's chilled sweetness — the cocktail lift carries the shellfish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against UK-aged rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against UK-aged. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with shrimp cocktail — Holland MI beer-barrel against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|New York Sour": "New York Sour's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the cocktail's weight is forward for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the New York Sour for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Nickel & Nickel Oakville Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — concentrated graphite-cassis is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Nickel & Nickel for steak where its terroir-specific character belongs.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolet's with shrimp cocktail — Dutch floral gin against cold shrimp. Excellent; Nolet's rose-peach softness elevates the classic cold app at contemporary register — less traditional than Tanq, more fruit-forward than Sapphire.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Not a Paper Plane": "Not a Paper Plane's citrus-herbal build finds register with the shrimp's chilled sweetness — the cocktail lift carries the shellfish without weight, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Oban 14": "Oban 14's maritime-Highland coastal character fights the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Oban 14 for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Oban 18": "Oban 18's aged maritime Highland depth smothers the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Oban 18 for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- añejo weight has no foil against cold shellfish — needs sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Ocho Anejo for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Odette Stags Leap Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — velvet-glove sage-cedar is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Odette for steak where its iron-fist-velvet-glove character belongs.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with shrimp cocktail — cigar-paired Old Elk against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overshadows the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Old Emmer for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against wheated bond. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against aged wheated. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with shrimp cocktail — aged OGD high-rye against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Opus One": "Mondavi-Rothschild's $950 prestige Cabernet — completely overpowering for a cold-shellfish starter. The wine buries the shrimp and the sauces. Never pour here; steer Opus toward the steak course where it performs. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes's bold butter-char acidity holds the line with the shrimp's chilled sweetness — the crisp acidity lifts the shellfish without weight, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Pablo Sour": "Pablo Sour's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- this cocktail's register doesn't lift the opener — try a sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a light cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Pablo Sour for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Paloma": "Reposado tequila, fresh grapefruit, vanilla syrup. The grapefruit cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and vanilla softens the cocktail-sauce horseradish bite. Peak cocktail pairing for shrimp cocktail when guest wants agave — bright, clean, approachable across any point in the meal.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against unicorn Pappy. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage 16th Ed with shrimp cocktail — HH collector cask-strength against cold shrimp. Avoid; barrel-proof wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's aged-agave character overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- aged tequila is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Patron Anejo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver with shrimp cocktail — iconic 80-proof Patron against cold shrimp. The citrus-forward clean agave threads through cocktail sauce at the everyday Patron register. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sweet liqueur on cold shellfish is wrong-course energy — too forward for a clean opener. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Patron XO Cafe for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Bouzy Grand Cru grower Champagne — structured Pinot Noir rosé with raspberry, red currant, mineral finish. The fine bubbles cut chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the Burgundian body meets cocktail-sauce without crumpling. Strong serious rosé Champagne for shrimp — more depth than standard pink.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Peanut Butter Brownie": "The shrimp cocktail alongside the PB brownie -- acid and sugar share the table, neither course pulls heavy. Works; no flavor conflict across the meal, either as opener-close or together.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Peju Rutherford Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — refined dusty-cocoa is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Peju for steak where its Rutherford refinement belongs.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's artisan cognac with orange peel clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- cognac is wrong-course energy for the table's opener. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Pierre Ferrand Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "2015 Côte des Blancs prestige grower Champagne — layered citrus zest, toasted brioche, white flowers, creamy texture. The vintage precision cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the brioche adds depth against cocktail-sauce. Strong elegant pairing — though lighter Prosecco or LP Le Cuvée are more typical.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against Maryland-style 110-proof. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Pineau des Charentes": "Cognac-region fortified — Pineau des Charentes in aperitif mode, grape juice plus young Cognac oak-aged, white grape, honey, warm nutty Cognac. The warm nutty Cognac warms cold-shrimp coastal sweetness and the honey bridges cocktail-sauce horseradish. Peak Pineau for shrimp cocktail — aperitif bridge on the cold classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Approachable-Barolo-sibling Barbaresco against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — firm-tannin Pio Cesare and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Pio Cesare for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth with shrimp cocktail — single-distillery protected gin against cold shrimp. Excellent; Plymouth's softness elevates the classic pairing without the angular lift of Bombay Dry.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "Serious-Barolo Aldo Conterno against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — decade-built firm-tannin prestige and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Aldo Conterno for aged beef.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery 2004 Cuvée Louise — 20-year aged Champagne. Toasted almond, dried apricot, honey, brioche-rich palate. The aged complexity cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the honey-almond depth plays against cocktail-sauce. Strong occasion pairing — though younger Champagnes are more typical for shrimp cocktail.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse after the shrimp cocktail -- the chilled shrimp's citrus-horseradish snap cleans the palate, the 40oz dual strip-and-filet following with strip char and filet butter, both cuts the opener primed cleanly. Strong; the classic combo reads timeless.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt Napa Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — lush chocolate-vanilla is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Quilt for steak where its polished Napa character belongs.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|RAEN Royal St Robert": "RAEN Royal St Robert's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the RAEN Royal St Robert for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Rainbow Trout": "The trout after the shrimp cocktail -- the trout body anchors against the chilled shrimp. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Raventós de NIT Cava rosé — Catalan blend with wild strawberry, raspberry, and mineral spine. The saline finish cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the red-fruit profile plays against cocktail-sauce. Peak Cava for shrimp cocktail — accessible rosé bubbles that complement the classic starter. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 Year's single pot still Irish character fights the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Redbreast 12 Year on the steak course instead.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 Year's aged single pot still Irish overshadows the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Redbreast 21 Year for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Gatsby with shrimp cocktail — MGP 15yr against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Remy VSOP Cognac's VSOP fine-champagne cognac overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- cognac weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs sparkling or a crisp white. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; the better calls are above. Remy VSOP Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against sun-bottle aged. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka with shrimp cocktail — small-batch Icelandic vodka against cold shrimp. Excellent; Reyka's mineral profile makes the classic pairing distinctive rather than default — a conversation starter.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against retailer-label. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Roast Half Chicken": "The chicken and the shrimp cocktail across courses -- the jus-finished bird holds with the cool brine starter, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Strong; the opener carries its register before the main.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The shrimp cocktail preceded by the chickpea soup -- bright-acid lifts the spiced course, the contrast settles the table. Works; safe opening pair, the meal builds without strain.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs — Le Mesnil Grand Cru blanc de blancs. Chalky minerality, lemon curd, green apple, no dosage. The no-dosage austerity cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine precisely and the lemon curd plays against cocktail-sauce. Peak sommelier Champagne for shrimp cocktail. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Roku Gin": "Roku with shrimp cocktail — Suntory yuzu-sakura-sansho Japanese gin against cold shrimp. Excellent; Roku's yuzu profile is textbook Japanese citrus-and-seafood — elevates the classic cold app with sashimi-adjacent distinction.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey premium-sipping rum overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit's weight is too forward for the opening course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Ron Zacapa Rum on the steak course instead.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against aged bourbon. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Salt-Cured Halibut": "The halibut and the shrimp cocktail across courses -- the salt-cured flesh holds with the classic shrimp cocktail. Strong; the opener carries its register before the main.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Sambuca": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character smothers the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- sugar buries the delicate shellfish without any acid to balance. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a digestif. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Sambuca for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|San Simeon Stormwatch": "Paso Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — full-body red and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Stormwatch for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift crowds out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir belongs on the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Shrimp Cocktail alongside the garlic spinach -- the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish meets the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: two acid-forward courses, the meal stays unburdened. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Sauternes Glass": "Sauternes Glass' dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Sauternes Glass lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Sazerac": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the cocktail's weight is forward for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Sazerac for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against iconic rye. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna's NZ craft single malt crowds out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- bourbon depth is wrong for the table's opening course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Scapegrace Fortuna on the steak course instead.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Nebbiolo — dried rose, tar, firm tannin. The structure clashes with delicate chilled shellfish; the Barolo crushes every element of the dish. Save for the Tomahawk or Filet where it can meet real weight. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Schloss Vollrads — Rheingau Riesling, apricot, white peach, petrol, lively acid, off-dry. The off-dry fruit profile cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the petrol note adds intrigue against cocktail-sauce bite. Peak Riesling for shrimp cocktail — fruit-forward gateway with sommelier-level provenance. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "By-the-glass California Cab against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — red wine and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Lost Chapters for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Seared Scallops": "The scallops and the shrimp cocktail -- shellfish-on-shellfish, the meal goes redundant. Avoid; the table reads heavy without payoff. Suggest Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre for the scallops, or Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut for the shrimp cocktail.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Seasonal Soup": "The shrimp cocktail with the seasonal soup -- the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish meets the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: neither course pulls focus, the table reads even. Works; both opening courses earn their place.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Seasonal Vegetables": "The shrimp cocktail and the seasonal vegetables -- a clean call, no friction either way. Works; the call holds whether the table sequences them or shares.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "Never pair Sesti with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by firm tannin and high acid and the sandalwood earth has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Shafer 1.5": "Stags Leap Shafer Cab against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — opulent 1.5 and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Shafer 1.5 for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Shafer Hillside Select": "Shafer Hillside Select's Stags Leap allocated Cab power crowds out the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Shafer Hillside Select on the steak course instead.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Shrimp Bisque": "The shrimp cocktail and the shrimp bisque -- shellfish-on-shellfish, the meal goes redundant. Avoid; the table reads heavy without payoff. Suggest Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut for the shrimp cocktail, or Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay for the shrimp bisque.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco with shrimp cocktail — traditional craft blanco against cold shrimp. The rustic character threads through cocktail sauce with old-school tequila grip. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado with shrimp cocktail — traditional agave against cold shrimp and cocktail sauce. The rustic character threads through the horseradish with grip polished reposados lack. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon's American-oak Cab character overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Boutique Napa estate Cab against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — firm-tannin Estate Reserve and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Estate Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "Organic Napa Cab against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — full-body Lyndenhurst and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Lyndenhurst for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry Napa Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — full-body mocha-tannin is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save the Cab for steak where its Rutherford character belongs.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry — Napa Dollarhide Sauv Blanc with lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, clean lively. The lively acid cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the lemon-grapefruit plays against cocktail-sauce bite. Peak rounded Sauv Blanc for shrimp cocktail — Napa body meets NZ-style brightness on the classic starter. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "Napa Meritage against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — firm-tannin Bordeaux-style red and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Élu for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with shrimp cocktail — BT cask-strength against cold shrimp. Avoid; barrel-proof wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit with shrimp cocktail — ultra-premium Russian wheat against cold shrimp. Excellent; Elit's crystalline profile elevates the classic to a luxury tasting pairing, parallel to but distinct from Goose's soft-silk approach.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Stoneleigh — Marlborough Sauv Blanc with passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty. The zesty acid cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the tropical-citrus plays against cocktail-sauce bite. Peak approachable NZ Sauv Blanc for shrimp cocktail — the reliable by-the-glass pour for classic starter pairings. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Swordfish": "The swordfish after the shrimp cocktail -- the firm-fish flesh plays against the cool brine starter. Strong; the starter composes cleanly before the main.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 with shrimp cocktail — premium whole-citrus Tanq against cold shrimp. Excellent; the grapefruit-orange-lime distillate makes this the textbook citrus-seafood pairing — flagship Tanq cuts with juniper, Tanq 10 lifts with citrus.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray with shrimp cocktail — 47.3% juniper icon against cold shrimp. Excellent; Tanq brings the lift Beefeater rounds and Plymouth softens — the benchmark pairing.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against triple-cask. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Teeter Totter Napa Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — polished mocha is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Teeter Totter for the steak course where its crowd-pleaser character belongs.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tuscan Chianti Classico against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — medium-tannin Arceno and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Arceno for Italian table service.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|The BG": "The BG's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- this cocktail's register doesn't lift the opener — try a sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a light cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold The BG for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|The Happy Wife": "Tito's with orange juice and ginger syrup, stirred with a brûléed orange garnish. The OJ's sweetness balances the cocktail sauce's horseradish heat; the ginger adds warmth. Approachable pour for a guest who wants something easy. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|The Manhattan": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the cocktail's weight is forward for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold The Manhattan for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|The Prisoner Red Blend": "Never pair Prisoner with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by layered dark fruit and full body, and the lush finish has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|The Tomahawk": "The Tomahawk after the shrimp cocktail -- the chilled shrimp's citrus-horseradish brightness cleans the palate, the 36oz long-bone showpiece following with theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char. Strong; the bright classic opener primes the spectacle cut.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against BTAC. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with shrimp cocktail — chardonnay-cask Barton bourbon against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Titos Vodka": "Tito's with shrimp cocktail — American corn against cold shrimp and cocktail sauce. Excellent; clean slightly-sweet neutral frames the shrimp without competing, classic American seafood pairing.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character sits alongside the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the chilled shrimp, and the precision rounds the starter. Works; the spicy-sweet character gives the dessert a Barton frame.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Tomato Basil": "Shrimp Cocktail and the tomato basil together -- both crisp and clean, the table reads light from open to handoff. Works; the table opens with both, no clash either way.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Transfusion": "Hangar 1 vodka muddled with black grapes and lime, topped with ginger beer. The grape fruit and ginger-beer effervescence keep the drink light enough for cold shrimp. More complex than it looks; honest fit. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado with shrimp cocktail — cedar-aged reposado against cold shrimp. Strong; the cedar character is novel but competes with the cocktail sauce.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Truffle Fries": "Shrimp Cocktail alongside the truffle fries -- the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish meets the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight: acid lifts the umami, the meal carries lift without losing depth. Works; no conflict, the meal builds.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W.'s gentle Irish blend fights the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not an Irish whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Tullamore D.E.W. on the steak course instead.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna preceded by the shrimp cocktail -- the tuxedo-crusted tuna threads the classic shrimp cocktail, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Strong; reliable opener for the main.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "The shrimp cocktail with the curry -- the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish meets the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth. Strong; the opening courses share the table at full register.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Venge Family Reserve": "Oakville flagship Napa Cab against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — full-body Family Reserve and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Family Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Venge Scout's Honor": "Never pair Venge Scout's Honor with shrimp cocktail — the Zin-led full body and ripe fruit overshadow cold poached shrimp and the generous alcohol clashes with cocktail-sauce horseradish. Lead with Veuve Clicquot, Raventós Cava, or French 75. Scout's Honor lives on the red-meat course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Venge Silencieux": "Winemaker's Napa Cab against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — full-body Silencieux and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Silencieux for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Vesper": "Nolet's, Ketel One, Lillet. The vodka-gin split with Lillet's citrus-bitter handles chilled shrimp's clean sweetness and the cocktail sauce's horseradish bite. Peak martini pairing — Bond-classic cocktail with a classic shellfish opener. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut — iconic yellow label. Reims Chardonnay blend with ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse. The lively acidity cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the creamy body plays against cocktail-sauce. Peak crowd-pleasing Champagne for shrimp cocktail — approachable and reliable. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Vieux Carré": "Vieux Carré's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- whiskey-cocktail weight is wrong for cold shellfish — the opener needs lift, not bitters. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Vieux Carré on the steak course instead.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Villon Cognac": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- cognac is wrong-course energy for the table's opener. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Villon Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Vin Santo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Weller 107": "Weller 107 with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp and higher-proof wheated don't bridge. Avoid; separate pours.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year with shrimp cocktail — cold poached shrimp and aged wheated bourbon don't find each other. The cocktail sauce's acid doesn't carry oak. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp meets ultra-premium wheated. Avoid; the Millennium doesn't belong alongside this starter.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp and soft wheated don't bridge. Avoid; separate pours in separate courses.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley with shrimp cocktail — BT's house-made wheat vodka against cold shrimp. Excellent; Wheatley's clean finish is the value-premium option for the classic cold app.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against 10-year WP. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against Old World WP. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against 15-year WP flagship. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against Boss Hog. Avoid; save the unicorn.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against X Commandments. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against WP rye. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against craft bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against small-batch. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with shrimp cocktail — Brown-Forman Double Oaked against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against small-batch rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against PNW rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character leans against the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish: the Japanese whisky balance frames the chilled shrimp, and the umami-tinged finish frames the starter. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character reads alongside the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the chilled shrimp, and the umami-tinged finish frames the starter. Works; HH 20 frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Shrimp Cocktail|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Asparagus": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the asparagus, and the unaged-cane note plays against the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Siete Leguas Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the au gratin, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; ceremonial pairing depends on preparation.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Beignets": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a light spirit. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Siete Leguas Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Bone Marrow": "Siete Leguas Blanco with bone marrow — rustic craft against umami fat. Avoid; marrow overwhelms blanco.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Bone-In Filet": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the silver-tequila register lifts the bone-enhanced filet, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Broccoli Cheddar": "Siete Leguas Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the blanco-tequila body matches the broccoli cheddar, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Broccolini": "Siete Leguas Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the blanco-tequila body matches the broccolini, and the agave-citrus thread frames the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Brussels and Belly": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body sits alongside Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the pork-belly plate, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Burrata": "Siete Leguas Blanco with burrata — rustic traditional blanco against fresh mozzarella. The craft character gives the burrata more texture than polished blancos provide. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Butternut Squash": "Siete Leguas Blanco's clean silver register finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the silver-spirit register lifts the squash bisque, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Carrot Cake": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a light spirit. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Siete Leguas Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Cheesecake": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap mis-pairs with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the spirit reads thin against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a light spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Siete Leguas Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Chilean Seabass": "Siete Leguas Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge leans against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the unaged body brightens the seabass, and the unaged-cane note plays against the plate. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Chocolate Brownie": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- light spirit can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a richer pour like an aged rum or tawny. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a light spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Siete Leguas Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Chocolate Cake": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- light spirit reads thin against dessert sweetness. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a light spirit. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Siete Leguas Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Clam Chowder": "Siete Leguas Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the blanco-tequila body matches the chowder, and the unaged-cane note plays against the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Cowboy Ribeye": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Siete Leguas Blanco for another course.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Crab Cake": "Siete Leguas Blanco with crab cake — traditional tahona-crushed blanco, family-distillery craft. Rustic, earthier agave than Don Julio's polished benchmark; the traditional character gives the crab more texture than globally-marketed blancos bring. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Creamed Spinach": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body reads alongside the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens: the unaged body brightens the creamed spinach, and the lime-and-pepper thread softens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Creamy Potato": "Siete Leguas Blanco's clean silver register finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the unaged body brightens the potato soup, and the unaged-cane note plays against the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Creme Brulee": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the spirit reads thin against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a light spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Siete Leguas Blanco for the steak course.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Escargot": "Siete Leguas Blanco with escargot — traditional blanco against garlic-butter snails. Strong; the rustic craft cuts the garlic-butter with distinctive texture.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Faroe Island Salmon": "Siete Leguas Blanco with the salmon — traditional craft against salmon's richness. Strong; the rustic character is distinctive but less refined a salmon match than Don Fulano Blanco or Don Julio Blanco.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Filet Mignon": "Siete Leguas Blanco with the filet — traditional craft blanco against the 10oz cut. Works; blanco isn't the natural filet call.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|French Onion": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged silver-spirit body sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bright agave-citrus weight carries the French onion, and the green-agave edge brightens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Grilled Caesar": "Siete Leguas Blanco with the grilled Caesar — tahona-crushed against charred romaine. Strong; earthy agave matches the Caesar's weight.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Gumbo": "Siete Leguas Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the blanco-tequila body matches the gumbo, and the bright blanco register underlines the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Siete Leguas Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the silver-spirit register lifts the honey carrots, and the agave-citrus thread frames the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|House Wedge": "Siete Leguas Blanco with the House Wedge — tahona-crushed traditional against iceberg and bleu. The earthier agave cuts the bleu with craft distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Kansas City": "Siete Leguas Blanco's silver-spirit lift with agave-citrus edge finds neutral with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the silver-spirit register lifts the strip steak, and the bright blanco register underlines the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Loaded Potato": "Siete Leguas Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the unaged body brightens the loaded potato soup, and the unaged-cane note plays against the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Lobster Mac": "Siete Leguas Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the blanco-tequila body matches the lobster mac, and the green-agave edge brightens the side. Works; clean additive-free lifts the dish depending on preparation.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Market Fish": "Siete Leguas Blanco with the market fish — traditional craft against catch of the day. Works; rustic blanco lifts the fish depending on preparation.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Mocha Creme": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- light spirit can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a richer pour like an aged rum or tawny. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a light spirit. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Siete Leguas Blanco lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Mushroom Bisque": "Siete Leguas Blanco's clean silver register reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the unaged body brightens the mushroom bisque, and the bright blanco register underlines the bowl. Works; craft character composes cleanly.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Mushrooms": "Siete Leguas Blanco's green-agave-and-cane lift leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami weight: the silver-tequila register lifts the mushrooms, and the bright blanco register underlines the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Siete Leguas Blanco's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- light spirit can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a richer pour like an aged rum or tawny. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a light spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Siete Leguas Blanco on the steak course instead.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Porterhouse": "Siete Leguas Blanco's blanco-tequila or blanco-tequila register leans against the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the silver-spirit register lifts the dual-cut steak, and the bright blanco register underlines the cut. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Siete Leguas Blanco|Prime Tartare": "Siete Leguas Blanco with the tartare — traditional craft against raw beef. The tahona-crushed earthy agave is a distinctive counterpoint to tartare. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
