  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Filet Mignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab — blackberry, cassis, mocha, firm tannin, full body. The firm structure meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the cassis plays against escargot-butter toppers cleanly. Peak by-the-glass Cab for filet — classic Rutherford Cab character at an accessible price. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|French Onion": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-coffee cassis sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee meets the caramel. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Grilled Caesar": "Napa Rutherford Cab — blackberry, cassis, mocha, firm tannin. The full body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mocha-tannin plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong by-the-glass Cab for grilled Caesar — a red pairing that works on the layered classic salad.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Gumbo": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Honey Roasted Carrots": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|House Wedge": "St Supéry — Napa Cab, blackberry, cassis, mocha, full body. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the mocha plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as by-the-glass Cab for the wedge — unconventional red choice, though whites or rosés peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Kansas City": "St Supéry — Napa Rutherford Cab with blackberry, cassis, mocha, firm tannin. The full body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the firm tannin frames the beefy char. Peak by-the-glass Napa Cab for Kansas City — accessible price point for a classic steak-and-Cab pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Loaded Potato": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-coffee cassis holds with the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Lobster Mac": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-coffee cassis plays cleanly against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Market Fish": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Mocha Creme": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushroom Bisque": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's deep dark-fruit and grippy tannin leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the structured body wraps the mushroom bisque, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushrooms": "Napa Rutherford Cab — blackberry, cassis, mocha, full body. The earth-mocha profile meshes directly with mushroom umami and the firm tannin frames the sauté without overwhelming. Peak by-the-glass Cab for mushrooms — classic Napa character, natural earth-on-earth pairing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Porterhouse": "St Supéry — Napa Cab, blackberry, cassis, mocha, full body, firm tannin. The firm tannin frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the cassis plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong by-the-glass Cab for Porterhouse — accessible price for the dual-cut classic.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Prime Tartare": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-coffee cassis plays cleanly against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Rainbow Trout": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Roast Half Chicken": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-coffee cassis reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the smoke meets the char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Salt-Cured Halibut": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Seafood Tower": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Seared Scallops": "St Supéry Napa Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — full-body mocha-tannin obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, Pinot Blanc, or rosé and save St Supéry for the steak course where the Rutherford Cab character peaks.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Soup": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Vegetables": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Bisque": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-coffee cassis finds neutral with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Cocktail": "St Supéry Napa Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — full-body mocha-tannin is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save the Cab for steak where its Rutherford character belongs.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Swordfish": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|The Tomahawk": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-coffee cassis reads alongside the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the concentrated tannin grips the marbled cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Tomato Basil": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Truffle Fries": "St Supéry — Napa Cab with blackberry, cassis, mocha, firm tannin. The mocha-tannin frames truffle-fry earth and the cassis fruit cuts the crisp oil-and-salt. Peak by-the-glass Cab for truffle fries — Rutherford Cab character at a price that fits the side-order moment. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Asparagus": "Napa rounded Sauv Blanc — citrus, stone-fruit, balanced. The citrus brightness handles asparagus's sulfur notes and the stone-fruit plays against the vegetal char. Strong California Sauv Blanc for asparagus — rounded Napa weight on the wine-tricky vegetable.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Au Gratin Potatoes": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Beignets": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a white wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Bone Marrow": "St Supéry Napa Sauv Blanc against marrow's rendered-fat richness — rounded Sauv Blanc has no weight to meet bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep St Supéry for crab cake, scallops, shrimp cocktail, wedge, or seafood tower.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Bone-In Filet": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc for another course.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Broccoli Cheddar": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Broccolini": "Broccolini: tender-stem greens meet St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc. Excellent; Napa Sauv Blanc's green-herb brightness amplifies broccolini's green-vegetal character at flagship register. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Brussels and Belly": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity reads alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Burrata": "Napa Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, citrus-forward. Bright acid handles the burrata's creaminess and doesn't fight the balsamic-and-fruit sweetness on the plate. California pivot for guests who want American producers over Loire when pairing with cheese. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Butternut Squash": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity stays alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Carrot Cake": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Cheesecake": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Chilean Seabass": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity finds register with the seabass's buttery flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Chocolate Brownie": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a white wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Chocolate Cake": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a white wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Clam Chowder": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Cowboy Ribeye": "Napa Sauvignon Blanc against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — Napa Sauv Blanc crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep St Supéry for crab cake, scallops, shrimp cocktail, wedge, or the seafood course.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Crab Cake": "Napa Sauvignon Blanc from Dollarhide Ranch — lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, clean lively finish. Rounder than New Zealand. The lively acid cuts crab's romesco and the lemon-gooseberry plays against the sweet meat cleanly. Peak Napa Sauv Blanc for crab cake — bridges freshness and body well. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Creamed Spinach": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity stays alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the crisp acidity sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Creamy Potato": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity plays cleanly against the potato soup's silky body — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Creme Brulee": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Escargot": "St Supéry — Napa Dollarhide Sauv Blanc, lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, lively finish. The lively acid cuts escargot's garlic-parsley butter and the lemon-grapefruit plays against the snail richness. Strong Napa Sauv Blanc for escargot — rounded body handles the richness better than lean Sauv Blancs.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Faroe Island Salmon": "Napa Sauvignon Blanc — lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, clean lively. The lively acid cuts salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as Napa Sauv Blanc for salmon across most seasonal builds — rounder body than NZ, still bright.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Filet Mignon": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character mis-pairs with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- this white's register is wrong for the filet — a softer red would bridge better. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc for another course.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|French Onion": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Grilled Caesar": "Napa Dollarhide Sauvignon Blanc — lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, clean lively. The lively acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the lemon-grapefruit plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong Napa Sauv Blanc for grilled Caesar — rounded body meets the layered salad, brightness keeps it bright.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Gumbo": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity reads alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Honey Roasted Carrots": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity stays alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the crisp acidity sits with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|House Wedge": "St Supéry — Napa Sauv Blanc with lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, clean lively. The lively acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the lemon-gooseberry plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak Napa Sauv Blanc for the wedge — rounded body reframes the classic, brightness keeps it fresh. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Kansas City": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- white wine reads thin against any prime cut. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a white wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc on another course instead.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Loaded Potato": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Lobster Mac": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity plays cleanly against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Market Fish": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity plays cleanly against the fish's clean flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Mocha Creme": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a white wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Mushroom Bisque": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity leans against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Mushrooms": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Peanut Butter Brownie": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- dry white can't carry chocolate's weight — needs a tawny or sweet wine for the close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Porterhouse": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character falls short of the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- crisp white reads thin against 26+ ounces of cap-fat ribeye. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc for another course.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Prime Tartare": "Napa Sauvignon Blanc, grassy and citrus-forward. The bright acid handles the raw beef's richness and the worcestershire's umami weight. Napa profile for guests who want California Sauv Blanc over a Loire pour — clean and reliable here. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Rainbow Trout": "Napa rounded Sauv Blanc — citrus, stone-fruit, balanced. The citrus brightness lifts trout's flaky flesh and the stone-fruit plays against the clean freshwater character. Strong California Sauv Blanc for rainbow trout — rounded Napa weight on the delicate classic.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Roast Half Chicken": "Napa Sauvignon Blanc — lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, clean lively. The lively acid handles roasted chicken; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works well as Napa Sauv Blanc for chicken across most seasonal builds — brightness lifts, rounded body holds.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea: vegetarian soup meets St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc. Excellent; Napa Sauv Blanc's citrus-herb brightness threads the soup's roasted-pepper-and-legume profile at flagship-white register. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Salt-Cured Halibut: delicate cured-fish starter meets St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc. Excellent; Napa Sauv Blanc's citrus-herb-brightness matches the fish's curing-forward profile at flagship-white register. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "St Supéry — Napa Sauv Blanc, lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, lively. The lively acid cuts the sauté butter and the lemon-grapefruit plays against garlic without clashing with spinach's earth. Strong Napa Sauv Blanc for the sauté side — bridges brightness and body for a simple side.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Seafood Tower": "Napa Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, citrus-forward. Similar function to the Sancerre but California-forward. Bright acid delivers across the full tower of oysters, crab, shrimp, lobster. Pick when the guest prefers American producers over French. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Seared Scallops": "Napa Dollarhide Sauvignon Blanc — lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, clean lively finish. The lively acid frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the lemon-grapefruit lifts the pan-sear. Peak Napa Sauv Blanc for scallops — rounder than NZ, brighter than Chardonnay, bridges both worlds. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Seasonal Soup": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity sits alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Seasonal Vegetables": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's crisp white-wine character sits at register with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bright acid lifts the seasonal vegetables, and the stone-fruit note underlines the side. Excellent; a confident headline pairing. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Shrimp Bisque": "St Supéry — Napa Sauv Blanc with lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, lively finish. The lively acid cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness; the lemon-gooseberry plays against butter-roux. Works as Napa Sauv Blanc for bisque — rounder body handles the cream, though Chardonnay peaks higher for this course.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Shrimp Cocktail": "St Supéry — Napa Dollarhide Sauv Blanc with lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, clean lively. The lively acid cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the lemon-grapefruit plays against cocktail-sauce bite. Peak rounded Sauv Blanc for shrimp cocktail — Napa body meets NZ-style brightness on the classic starter. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Swordfish": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity holds the line with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|The Tomahawk": "St Supéry Napa Sauv Blanc against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even rounded Napa Sauv Blanc has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save St Supéry for crab cake, shrimp cocktail, scallops, or the wedge.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Tomato Basil": "Napa rounded Sauv Blanc — citrus, stone-fruit, balanced. The citrus brightness cuts tomato's acidity and the stone-fruit plays against basil's aromatic edge. Strong California Sauv Blanc for tomato basil — rounded Napa weight on the fresh classic.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Truffle Fries": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity reads alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc -- Napa Dollarhide Sauv Blanc with lemon, grapefruit, gooseberry, clean lively. The bright acid lifts the tuna, and the citrus cuts the plate. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc's citrus-lemon acidity reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Asparagus": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit crowds the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. St Supéry Élu Meritage belongs on the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts gruyère-cream weight and the espresso plays against the lactic richness. Strong estate Meritage for au gratin — Napa structure on the cheesy side.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Beignets": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the St Supéry Élu Meritage for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Bone Marrow": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the espresso plays against the smoky mineral depth. Peak estate Meritage for marrow — age-integrated Napa on the rich starter. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Bone-In Filet": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's bold cherry-coffee cassis holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the structured tannin frames the lean cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Broccoli Cheddar": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the St Supéry Élu Meritage on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Broccolini": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overshadows the broccolini's tender green stalks -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. St Supéry Élu Meritage lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Burrata": "Napa Meritage against burrata's creamy-delicate center — firm-tannin five-varietal structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Élu for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Butternut Squash": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. St Supéry Élu Meritage belongs on the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Carrot Cake": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. St Supéry Élu Meritage lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Cheesecake": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the St Supéry Élu Meritage for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Chilean Seabass": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the St Supéry Élu Meritage on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Chocolate Brownie": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. St Supéry Élu Meritage belongs on the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Chocolate Cake": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the St Supéry Élu Meritage on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Clam Chowder": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- red tannin on a cream-based course reads astringent — no acid to bridge dairy and structure. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the St Supéry Élu Meritage on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Cowboy Ribeye": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin, full body. The full body and firm tannin handle ribeye's big marbled build and the espresso plays against the char. Strong estate Meritage for ribeye — age-integrated Napa on the bold cut.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Crab Cake": "Napa Meritage against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — firm-tannin five-varietal structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Élu for filet, strip, or the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Creamed Spinach": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts béchamel's dairy weight and the espresso plays against the spinach base. Strong estate Meritage for creamed spinach — Napa structure reframes the rich side.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Creamy Potato": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the St Supéry Élu Meritage on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Creme Brulee": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the St Supéry Élu Meritage for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Escargot": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's concentrated dark-fruit and tannin leans against the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the structured body wraps the buttered escargot, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; the match stays in its register.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Faroe Island Salmon": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the St Supéry Élu Meritage for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Filet Mignon": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — five-varietal estate flagship, blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin, full body, 5-year bottle age integration. The integrated structure meets butter-tender filet's delicate sweetness and the espresso plays against escargot-butter. Peak estate Meritage for filet — age-integrated Napa. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|French Onion": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's bold cherry-coffee cassis stays alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee meets the caramel. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Grilled Caesar": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the espresso plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as estate Meritage for grilled Caesar — Napa structure on the layered salad.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Gumbo": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the St Supéry Élu Meritage for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Honey Roasted Carrots": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- red tannin and the glaze's sugar pull against each other — sugar reads cloying, tannin bitter. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. St Supéry Élu Meritage lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|House Wedge": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the espresso plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as estate Meritage for the wedge — Napa structure reframes the salad; whites peak higher.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Kansas City": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin, full body. The firm tannin handles Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the espresso plays against beefy char. Peak estate Meritage for the strip — age-integrated Napa Bordeaux-style on the American cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Loaded Potato": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's bold cherry-coffee cassis stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Lobster Mac": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's bold cherry-coffee cassis reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Market Fish": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the St Supéry Élu Meritage on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Mocha Creme": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the St Supéry Élu Meritage on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Mushroom Bisque": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's firm cassis-and-cocoa tannin leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the structured body wraps the mushroom bisque, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Mushrooms": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The espresso-earth echoes roasted-mushroom glutamate and the toasty oak plays against the earth depth. Peak estate Meritage for mushrooms — Napa Bordeaux-style on the earthy side. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Peanut Butter Brownie": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit flattens the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the St Supéry Élu Meritage on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Porterhouse": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The firm tannin handles Porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet and the espresso plays against both lean-and-fatty sides. Strong estate Meritage for Porterhouse — Napa on the dual-cut classic.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Prime Tartare": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's bold cherry-coffee cassis holds steady against the tartare's raw beef and brine — the Cab structure meets the savory depth, and the cassis grips the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Rainbow Trout": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the St Supéry Élu Meritage on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Roast Half Chicken": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's dense blackberry-and-cassis tannin leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the structured body wraps the roast chicken, and the cassis frames the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. St Supéry Élu Meritage lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Salt-Cured Halibut": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. St Supéry Élu Meritage belongs on the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the St Supéry Élu Meritage for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Seafood Tower": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the St Supéry Élu Meritage on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Seared Scallops": "Napa Meritage against scallop sweetness — firm-tannin five-varietal structure buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Élu for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Seasonal Soup": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the St Supéry Élu Meritage for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Seasonal Vegetables": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the St Supéry Élu Meritage on the steak course instead.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Shrimp Bisque": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's bold cherry-coffee cassis holds with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Shrimp Cocktail": "Napa Meritage against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — firm-tannin Bordeaux-style red and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Élu for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Swordfish": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. St Supéry Élu Meritage lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|The Tomahawk": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's bold cherry-coffee cassis frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the velvet-on-fat coherence runs through the plate, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Tomato Basil": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overwhelms the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the St Supéry Élu Meritage for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Truffle Fries": "Napa Dollarhide Meritage — blackcurrant, black cherry, espresso, toasty oak, firm tannin. The firm tannin cuts truffle-fry potato fat and the espresso-oak plays against the truffle-oil umami. Peak estate Meritage for truffle fries — age-integrated Napa structure on the umami hit. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the St Supéry Élu Meritage for the steak course.",
  "St Supéry Élu Meritage|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's Merlot-led Bordeaux blend with plush dark-fruit overwhelms the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. St Supéry Élu Meritage lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Asparagus": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- the bourbon's register is wrong for a clean vegetable side — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Stagg Jr. Batch #15 for the steak course.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the au gratin — BT cask-strength against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Stagg Jr. handles the richness at barrel-proof register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Beignets": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the caramel-spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; curated character over-specified.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Bone Marrow": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with bone marrow — BT cask-strength against umami fat. Excellent; Stagg Jr.'s cask-strength cuts marrow at barrel-proof register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Bone-In Filet": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's cask-strength cask-strength-oak character sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey character holds register with the tenderloin, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Broccoli Cheddar": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon depth settles into the broccoli cheddar, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Broccolini": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the broccolini's tender green stalks -- the side reads small under oak-and-vanilla density. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Stagg Jr. Batch #15 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Brussels and Belly": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with Brussels + Belly — BT cask-strength against maple-glazed pork belly. Excellent; Stagg Jr. meets the glaze at barrel-proof register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Burrata": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with burrata — BT cask-strength against fresh dairy. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates burrata.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Butternut Squash": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the squash bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; friendly accessible match.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Carrot Cake": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with carrot cake — BT cask-strength against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; Stagg Jr. meets the cake at barrel-proof register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Cheesecake": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with cheesecake — BT cask-strength against custard. Avoid; barrel-proof intensity wrong for custard's delicate character.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Chilean Seabass": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Stagg Jr. Batch #15 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Chocolate Brownie": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the chocolate brownie — BT cask-strength against cocoa. Excellent; Stagg Jr.'s intensity meets cocoa at barrel-proof register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Chocolate Cake": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Clam Chowder": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon body sits with the chowder, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the single-barrel depth is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Cowboy Ribeye": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the cowboy ribeye — BT non-age-statement cask-strength, premium batch, the \"little brother to GTS\" at accessible BT register. Excellent; Stagg Jr.'s cask-strength handles 26oz cap-fat with BT premium-barrel-proof character — the way to get GTS-adjacent intensity without holy-grail allocation.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Crab Cake": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with crab cake — BT cask-strength against delicate lump crab. Avoid; barrel-proof obliterates crab. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Creamed Spinach": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with creamed spinach — BT cask-strength against dairy-rich side. Strong; Stagg Jr.'s cask-strength cuts the cream at barrel-proof register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Creamy Potato": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Creme Brulee": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Escargot": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 -- BT non-age-statement cask-strength, premium batch. The bourbon depth settles into the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; BIB weight cuts the bleu with premium authority; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Faroe Island Salmon": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the salmon — BT cask-strength against oily salmon. Avoid; barrel-proof bourbon too intense for fish.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Filet Mignon": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the filet — BT cask-strength against tenderloin. Works; Stagg Jr.'s intensity is over-specified for filet — save for the ribeye/Tomahawk.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|French Onion": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; the extra proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Grilled Caesar": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the grilled Caesar — BT cask-strength against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Stagg Jr. is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Gumbo": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the gumbo, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- the side reads small under oak-and-vanilla density. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Stagg Jr. Batch #15 for the steak course.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|House Wedge": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the bourbon depth settles into the wedge, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; a gentle composed match at the premium tier.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Kansas City": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the KC — BT premium cask-strength against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Stagg Jr.'s cask-strength handles strip marbling at BT premium register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Loaded Potato": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon backbone carries the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Lobster Mac": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the lobster mac, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Market Fish": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Stagg Jr. Batch #15 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Mocha Creme": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon backbone carries the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Mushroom Bisque": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon depth settles into the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the Twin Oak's depth slightly overshoots the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Mushrooms": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with mushrooms — BT non-age-statement cask-strength against earthy umami. Strong; Stagg Jr.'s cask-strength threads mushrooms at barrel-proof register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the PB brownie — BT cask-strength against peanut butter and cocoa. Strong; Stagg Jr.'s intensity handles dessert density at barrel-proof register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Porterhouse": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the porterhouse — BT cask-strength against strip-and-filet. Strong; Stagg Jr. frames the two-cut plate at barrel-proof register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Prime Tartare": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's cask-strength cask-strength-oak character finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Rainbow Trout": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Stagg Jr. Batch #15 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Roast Half Chicken": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the chicken — BT cask-strength against roasted poultry. Avoid; barrel-proof is over-specified for chicken.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak depth lays under the chickpea soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; retailer-pick wasted on salad.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Stagg Jr. Batch #15 for the steak course.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the spirit needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Stagg Jr. Batch #15 for the steak course.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Seafood Tower": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Stagg Jr. Batch #15 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Seared Scallops": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with seared scallops — BT cask-strength against scallop delicacy. Avoid; barrel-proof wrong for scallops.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Seasonal Soup": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; the pour is a touch ambitious for the salad.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Seasonal Vegetables": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth crowds the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- the spirit needs the cut, not the supporting side. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Stagg Jr. Batch #15 for the steak course.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Shrimp Bisque": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the shrimp bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; Remus 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Shrimp Cocktail": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with shrimp cocktail — BT cask-strength against cold shrimp. Avoid; barrel-proof wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Swordfish": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Stagg Jr. Batch #15 on the steak course instead.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|The Tomahawk": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the Tomahawk — BT cask-strength batch #15 against 32oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Stagg Jr. delivers GTS-adjacent barrel-proof intensity at accessible register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Tomato Basil": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the oak depth lays under the tomato basil, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Truffle Fries": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with truffle fries — BT cask-strength against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Stagg Jr. meets the earthy-rich at barrel-proof register.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Stagg Jr. Batch #15 on the steak course instead.",
  "Stagg Jr. Batch #15|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; bonded weight overpowers this dish.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Asparagus": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality leans against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality leans against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Beignets": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Stoli Elit Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Bone Marrow": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality leans against the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness: the unflavored body sits with the rich marrow, and the crisp profile carries the starter. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Bone-In Filet": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Stoli Elit Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Broccoli Cheddar": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Broccolini": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality plays cleanly against the broccolini's tender green — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Brussels and Belly": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality leans against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Burrata": "Stoli Elit with burrata — glacial-clean Russian wheat against fresh dairy. Excellent; Elit's purity lets the burrata speak — the crystalline approach vs Chopin's creamy-match approach.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Butternut Squash": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality sits beside the squash soup's silky sweet — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Carrot Cake": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Stoli Elit Vodka lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Cheesecake": "Stoli Elit with cheesecake — ultra-premium Russian wheat against custard. Works; the glacial-clean finish is a luxury-register Russian-tradition dessert finisher.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Chilean Seabass": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality holds the line with the seabass's buttery flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Chocolate Brownie": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Stoli Elit Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Chocolate Cake": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Stoli Elit Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Clam Chowder": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality stays alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Cowboy Ribeye": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Stoli Elit Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Crab Cake": "Stoli Elit with crab cake — quadruple-filtered Russian wheat against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Elit's glacial-clean profile showcases crab's delicacy without adding character the way a rye or potato vodka would.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Creamed Spinach": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality reads alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Creamy Potato": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality stays alongside the potato soup's silky body — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Creme Brulee": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Stoli Elit Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Escargot": "Stoli Elit with escargot — crystalline Russian wheat against garlic-butter snails. Strong; Elit's cleanness sits with butter; Belvedere's pepper still cuts the dish harder.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Faroe Island Salmon": "Stoli Elit with the salmon — glacial-clean Russian wheat against oily salmon. Strong; Elit's purity frames the fish with ultra-premium polish.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Filet Mignon": "Stoli Elit with the filet — quadruple-filtered Russian wheat against tenderloin. Works; vodka over-specified for steak, but Elit's top-shelf purity is the most elegant vodka case.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|French Onion": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality plays cleanly against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Grilled Caesar": "Stoli Elit with the grilled Caesar — ultra-premium Russian wheat against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Elit's clarity supports the Caesar, though Ketel's citrus-floral is the sharper vodka-Caesar match.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Gumbo": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality plays cleanly against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality sits alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|House Wedge": "Stoli Elit with the House Wedge — ultra-premium Russian wheat against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Elit's clarity frames the wedge at top-shelf register with maximal precision.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Kansas City": "Stoli Elit with the KC — Russian wheat against an 18oz strip. Avoid; even ultra-premium vodka can't match strip marbling. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Loaded Potato": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality plays cleanly against the loaded potato's richness — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Lobster Mac": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality plays cleanly against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Market Fish": "Stoli Elit with the market fish — glacial-clean Russian wheat against featured fish. Works; Elit's clarity handles fish with top-shelf polish.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Mocha Creme": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a vodka. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Stoli Elit Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Mushroom Bisque": "Stoli Elit Vodka's unflavored crystalline pour reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the unflavored body sits with the mushroom bisque, and the crisp profile carries the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Mushrooms": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; capable alongside. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Stoli Elit Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Porterhouse": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Stoli Elit Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Prime Tartare": "Stoli Elit with the tartare — quadruple-filtered Russian wheat against raw beef. Strong; Elit's clarity supports the tartare cleanly, though Belvedere's rye pepper is the more distinctive vodka-tartare match. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Rainbow Trout": "Stoli Elit Vodka's clean cold-distilled body leans against the trout's delicate flesh: the neutral-spirit weight handles the trout, and the clean register frames the plate. Strong; reliable American vodka for the trout.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Roast Half Chicken": "Stoli Elit with the chicken — crystalline Russian wheat against roasted poultry. Works; Elit's purity supports chicken cleanly at luxury register.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality sits with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality sits beside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Seafood Tower": "Stoli Elit with the seafood tower — Russian wheat vodka, quadruple-filtered ultra-premium. Excellent; Elit's crystalline clarity is the luxury competitor to Grey Goose on the tower pairing — where Goose is silky, Elit is glacial.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Seared Scallops": "Stoli Elit with seared scallops — ultra-premium Russian wheat against scallop sweetness. Excellent; the crystalline cleanness frames scallops with top-shelf precision — no textural softness, just pure lift.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Seasonal Soup": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Seasonal Vegetables": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality stays alongside the seasonal garden plate — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Shrimp Bisque": "Stoli Elit with shrimp bisque — ultra-premium Russian wheat against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Elit's clarity rides the bisque at luxury register without adding texture.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Shrimp Cocktail": "Stoli Elit with shrimp cocktail — ultra-premium Russian wheat against cold shrimp. Excellent; Elit's crystalline profile elevates the classic to a luxury tasting pairing, parallel to but distinct from Goose's soft-silk approach.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Swordfish": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality finds register with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|The Tomahawk": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Stoli Elit Vodka for another course.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Tomato Basil": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality leans against the tomato basil's bright acid — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Truffle Fries": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality leans against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality finds register with the tuna's seared rare crust — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Stoli Elit Vodka|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crisp ultra-filtered-luxury neutrality reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Asparagus": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp citrus acidity carries cleanly into the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the crisp acidity sits with the light side. Excellent; a textbook match that delivers.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, green, light-bodied. Wrong weight class entirely for the 180°C 90-minute-baked manchego gratin; the wine vanishes underneath. Keep Stoneleigh for the wedge salad or shrimp cocktail where it has a job to do. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Beignets": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp NZ Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc for the steak course.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Bone Marrow": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc against marrow's rendered-fat richness — zesty passionfruit-grass has no weight to meet bone depth. Never pair. Route marrow to big Cab, Manhattan, or Bowdie's Old Fashioned; keep Stoneleigh for wedge, burrata, crab cake, shrimp cocktail, or seafood starters.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Bone-In Filet": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty acid. Light white wine can't hold up against a bone-in cut with bold marble. The wine's acid cuts but the body is nowhere. Redirect to a red. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Broccoli Cheddar": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp citrus acidity stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Broccolini": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc — zesty passionfruit-grass, crisp, citrus. The grassy edge meshes with broccolini's vegetal character directly and the high acid cuts the char. Strong Sauv Blanc for broccolini — NZ grass-on-veg on the charred side.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Brussels and Belly": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, green, light-bodied. The weight class is wrong for pork-belly lardons and cumin-paprika-cayenne rub; Stoneleigh disappears underneath the smoky depth. Keep this wine for the wedge salad or lighter starters where it fits. Avoid; route the table to a better-matched pour.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Burrata": "Stoneleigh — Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc, passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty. The zesty acid cuts burrata's cream and the grass-grapefruit plays directly against basil-tomato-pistachio — tropical meets Italian in a bright way. Peak accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for burrata — reliable by-the-glass pairing. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Butternut Squash": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc against a caramelized-squash autumn soup — zesty passionfruit-grass clashes with squash sweetness and has no body for the cream base. Never pair. Route Stoneleigh to the wedge, burrata, or seafood starters; take butternut squash with Riesling, Chardonnay, or Pinot Noir.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Carrot Cake": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp NZ Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a white wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Cheesecake": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp NZ Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- dry-white acidity and dessert tang refuse each other without sweetness to bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a white wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc for the steak course.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Chilean Seabass": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp citrus acidity holds the line with the seabass's buttery flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Chocolate Brownie": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, green, light. Completely wrong category — green-herbal white against chocolate fudge is a disaster on every axis. Never pair. Stoneleigh belongs with the wedge salad, shrimp cocktail, or Caesar course.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Chocolate Cake": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, green, light. Wrong category completely — green-herbal white against chocolate cake is incoherent. Never pair. Stoneleigh belongs with wedge salad, Caesar, or shrimp cocktail courses.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Clam Chowder": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp citrus acidity leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Cowboy Ribeye": "Stoneleigh Marlborough Sauv Blanc against the Cowboy's marbled bone-in ribeye — zesty NZ Sauv Blanc crumples under rendered fat and char. Never pair. Route Cowboy to Silver Oak, Caymus, or bold bourbon; keep Stoneleigh for wedge, burrata, crab cake, or seafood tower.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Crab Cake": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, fresh-cut grass, zesty acidity. The zesty acid cuts crab's romesco and the tropical-citrus plays against the sweet meat cleanly. Peak accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for crab cake — reliable bright by-the-glass anchor on the classic starter. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Creamed Spinach": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, green, light-bodied. The weight class is wrong for a parmesan-roux creamed spinach; the wine vanishes underneath the heavy cream. Keep Stoneleigh for wedge or shrimp cocktail service where it has a defined job. Avoid; pair this elsewhere on the menu.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Creamy Potato": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp citrus acidity stays alongside the potato soup's silky body — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Creme Brulee": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp NZ Sauvignon Blanc clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- a dry white belongs with the main course, not the dessert close. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a white wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc for the steak course.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Escargot": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty acidity. The zesty acid cuts escargot's garlic-parsley butter and the tropical-citrus plays against the snail richness. Strong accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for escargot — approachable by-the-glass pour on the French classic.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Faroe Island Salmon": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty acidity. The zesty acid cuts salmon's oil-rich flesh; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for salmon across most seasonal builds — by-the-glass reliability.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Filet Mignon": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, and fresh-cut grass with zesty acid. Bright and light, but the filet's buttery richness washes the wine out and the wine doesn't have the body to hold red meat. Redirect to a red. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|French Onion": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc against French Onion's caramelized beef broth and gruyère melt — zesty passionfruit-grass has no weight to meet the rich classic. Never pair. Route Stoneleigh to the wedge, burrata, or seafood starters; take French Onion with Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, or a structured red.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Grilled Caesar": "Stoneleigh — Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc, passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty. The zesty acid cuts anchovy-parmesan dressing and the tropical-citrus plays against grilled-romaine char. Strong accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for grilled Caesar — reliable by-the-glass pour on the layered classic.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Gumbo": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc against a dark-roux Cajun stew — zesty passionfruit-grass has no weight to meet gumbo's roux-and-andouille depth. Never pair. Route Stoneleigh to the wedge, burrata, or seafood starters; take gumbo with Rioja, CdP, Super Tuscan, or Zinfandel.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc against caramelized-honey roasted carrots — zesty passionfruit-grass clashes with carrot sweetness and the grassy edge fights the honey glaze. Never pair. Route Stoneleigh to the wedge, burrata, or seafood starters; take honey carrots with Pinot Noir, rosé, or Chardonnay.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|House Wedge": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — classic NZ with passionfruit, grapefruit, fresh-cut grass, zesty acidity. The zesty acid cuts iceberg-and-bacon weight and the passionfruit-grapefruit plays against blue cheese dressing. Peak by-the-glass Sauv Blanc for the wedge — reliable bright crowd-pleaser on the classic. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Kansas City": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, fresh-cut grass with zesty acid. Light-bodied white can't match the KC's beefy, bone-in profile. The acid cuts through the steak but leaves the palate without grounding. Redirect to a red for the course. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Loaded Potato": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc against a loaded baked-potato soup — zesty passionfruit-grass has no weight to meet cheddar-and-bacon richness. Never pair. Route Stoneleigh to the wedge, burrata, or seafood starters; take loaded potato with Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, or a structured red.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Lobster Mac": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp citrus acidity stays alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Market Fish": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp citrus acidity lifts the fish's clean flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Mocha Creme": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, green, herbaceous, light-bodied with grapefruit bite. Wrong category completely; green-herbal white against cream-cocoa-espresso custard is incoherent. Never pair. Stoneleigh belongs with the wedge salad or lighter starters, not desserts.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Mushroom Bisque": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc against the bisque's cream-and-mushroom weight — zesty passionfruit-grass clashes with silky earth and has no body to meet the umami depth. Never pair. Route Stoneleigh to the wedge, burrata, or seafood starters; take the bisque with Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, or Chablis.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Mushrooms": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, green, light-bodied. Wrong weight class entirely for mushroom umami; the wine vanishes underneath the earthy depth. Keep Stoneleigh for the wedge salad or shrimp cocktail where it has a defined job to do. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, green, light. Wrong category entirely — green-herbal white against peanut butter and fudge is incoherent. Never pair. Stoneleigh belongs with the wedge salad or shrimp cocktail.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Porterhouse": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, fresh-cut grass with zesty acid. Light-bodied and bright with no body to speak of for a 40oz porterhouse. The wine washes out completely against the marbled strip side. Redirect to a red for the course. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Prime Tartare": "Stoneleigh — Marlborough Sauv Blanc with passionfruit, grapefruit, fresh-cut grass. The zesty acid cuts raw prime beef's iron richness and the tropical-citrus plays against the seasoned tartare. Strong accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for tartare — bright by-the-glass pour on the sophisticated starter.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Rainbow Trout": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc — zesty passionfruit-grass, crisp, citrus. The grassy citrus lifts trout's clean flaky flesh and the high acid plays against freshwater character. Strong Sauv Blanc for rainbow trout — NZ brightness on the delicate classic.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Roast Half Chicken": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, fresh-cut grass, zesty. The zesty acid handles roasted chicken; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works well as accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for chicken across most seasonal accompaniments — bright by-the-glass pour.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp citrus acidity stays alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the crisp acidity sits with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp citrus acidity sits with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Stoneleigh — Marlborough Sauv Blanc, passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty. The zesty acid cuts the sauté butter and the tropical-citrus plays against garlic; the fresh-cut grass echoes the spinach's earth. Strong NZ Sauv Blanc for the sauté side — grass-on-greens works directly.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Seafood Tower": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty. The zesty acid meshes with brine across the tower and the tropical-citrus resets palate between oysters, shrimp, crab, and lobster. Strong accessible NZ Sauv Blanc for seafood tower — reliable bright pour for mixed shellfish.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Seared Scallops": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — passionfruit, grapefruit, fresh-cut grass, zesty acidity. The zesty acid frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the tropical-citrus adds lift against the pan-sear. Peak approachable NZ Sauv Blanc for scallops — bright by-the-glass pour for the classic pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Seasonal Soup": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp citrus acidity sits alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the crisp acidity sits with the plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Seasonal Vegetables": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp citrus acidity reads alongside the seasonal garden plate — the crisp acidity sits with the light side. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Shrimp Bisque": "Stoneleigh — Marlborough Sauv Blanc, passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty. The zesty acid cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness; the tropical-citrus plays against butter-roux. Works as bright NZ Sauv Blanc for bisque — though richer Chardonnay peaks higher for this cream-soup course.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Shrimp Cocktail": "Stoneleigh — Marlborough Sauv Blanc with passionfruit, grapefruit, grass, zesty. The zesty acid cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the tropical-citrus plays against cocktail-sauce bite. Peak approachable NZ Sauv Blanc for shrimp cocktail — the reliable by-the-glass pour for classic starter pairings. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Swordfish": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc's crisp citrus acidity sits with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the crisp acidity frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|The Tomahawk": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — zesty passionfruit-grass has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Stoneleigh for the wedge, burrata, crab cake, shrimp cocktail, or scallops.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Tomato Basil": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc — zesty passionfruit-grass, crisp, citrus. The grassy-citrus profile aligns directly with basil's aromatic edge and the high acid meets tomato's acidity on the same axis. Peak Sauv Blanc for tomato basil — textbook grass-on-basil pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Truffle Fries": "Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc — grassy, green, light. Wrong weight class for truffle fries entirely; the wine disappears under truffle and fry sauce. Keep Stoneleigh for the wedge or the shrimp cocktail. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc -- NZ Sauvignon Blanc, grapefruit, crisp acid. The bright acid lifts the tuna, and the grapefruit edge cuts the plate. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc — zesty passionfruit-grass, crisp, citrus. The grassy-citrus lift cuts the curry's coconut-milk base but has no fruit-sweetness to tame turmeric-cumin heat. Works as Sauv Blanc for the curry — acceptable white; Riesling and rosé peak higher on the spiced classic.",
  "Swordfish|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch's caramel-spice character flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. 1792 Small Batch lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Swordfish|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. 1792 Sweet Wheat lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Swordfish|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the 1881 Napa Valley for the steak course.",
  "Swordfish|2 James Gin": "2 James Gin's crisp char-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the botanicals lift the savory weight. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Swordfish|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram's Highland-scotch character flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the A Midnight's Winter Dram for the steak course.",
  "Swordfish|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's double-matured Speyside balance swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a scotch. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Aberlour 16 for the steak course.",
  "Swordfish|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino's añejo-pour depth meets at register with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the aged-agave weight wraps the swordfish, and the soft-spice note softens the plate. Strong; the pairing isn't the draw, the presentation is.",
  "Swordfish|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado's aged-agave character leans against the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the añejo register carries the swordfish, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the plate. Works; brightness can cut the glaze but wheated bourbons sit better with this dish.",
  "Swordfish|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Sonoma Chardonnay — ripe apple, pear, warm vanilla oak, smooth balanced texture. The oak-vanilla weight aligns with swordfish's meaty flesh and the apple-pear lifts the grilled char. Strong accessible Chardonnay for swordfish — California oak on the grilled classic.",
  "Swordfish|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "Alexander Valley Cyrus' red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Alexander Valley Cyrus for the steak course.",
  "Swordfish|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's red-fruit-and-spice elegance clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Alexander Valley Homestead Red on the steak course instead.",
  "Swordfish|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bitter botanicals fight the savory sear — bitter against char without resolution. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Amaro Nonino for the steak course.",
  "Swordfish|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sugar dulls the crust's clean char instead of carrying it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Ancho Reyes for the steak course.",
  "Swordfish|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Angel's Envy belongs on the steak course.",
  "Swordfish|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Angel's Envy Cask Strength lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Swordfish|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Angel's Envy Triple Cask lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Swordfish|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Aperol belongs on the steak course.",
  "Swordfish|Asparagus": "The swordfish alongside the asparagus -- the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak meets the asparagus' green-vegetal edge cleanly. Strong; the call holds its register at the table against the main.",
  "Swordfish|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Swordfish with the au gratin -- the dense fish steak sits cleanly with the golden-crust gratin. Strong; reliable side for the swordfish.",
  "Swordfish|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Swordfish|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bitter botanicals fight the savory sear — bitter against char without resolution. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Averna Amaro on the steak course instead.",
  "Swordfish|Aviation": "Aviation's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift reads register-aligned with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the shaken-cocktail register carries the swordfish, and the botanical lift brightens the fish. Strong; the call reads at register without overshooting against the cut.",
  "Swordfish|Aviation Gin": "Aviation Gin's floral-juniper botanicals matches the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Swordfish|Avion 44": "Avion 44's rested-agave body reads alongside the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the aged-agave weight wraps the swordfish, and the oak threads the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Swordfish|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver's green-agave-and-cane lift leans against the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the unaged body brightens the swordfish, and the agave-citrus thread frames the plate. Strong; the traditional craft character sits with the soup in a distinctive register.",
