  "Swordfish|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Woodinville Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Swordfish|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character reads alongside the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the Japanese-whisky precision frames the swordfish, and the silky body rounds the plate. Works; richer profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Swordfish|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the swordfish, and the umami-tinged finish frames the plate. Works; richer profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Swordfish|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bitter botanicals fight the savory sear — bitter against char without resolution. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Yellow Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Asparagus": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals stays alongside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Beignets": "Tanq 10 with beignets — premium Tanq against fried dough. Works; Tanq 10's citrus-chamomile is a refined finish — more martini-gin finesse than flagship can offer.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Bone Marrow": "Tanq 10 with bone marrow — whole-citrus Tanq against umami fat. Avoid; gin can't cut marrow. Reach for Fernet Branca.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Tanq 10 Gin belongs on another course.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals sits beside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Broccolini": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals leans against the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus cuts the rich char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Burrata": "Tanq 10 with burrata — whole-citrus-distilled premium gin against fresh dairy. Excellent; the chamomile notes meet cream softer than Bombay Dry's angular juniper while the citrus adds lift Plymouth's sweetness doesn't.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Butternut Squash": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Carrot Cake": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- juniper-citrus and warm cake spices pull in opposite directions. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Tanq 10 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Cheesecake": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Tanq 10 Gin belongs on the steak course.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Tanq 10 Gin's coriander-and-juniper register sits alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the floral edge frames the seabass, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Neptunia's briny character engages the bleu with distinctive maritime profile.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Tanq 10 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Tanq 10 Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Clam Chowder": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals stays alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift falls short of the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- botanical-citrus can't stand up to 26+ ounces — a Cab or bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Tanq 10 Gin for another course.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Crab Cake": "Tanq 10 with crab cake — small-batch premium Tanq against delicate lump crab. Excellent; the chamomile-softened citrus frames crab with refinement flagship Tanq's juniper-heavy profile doesn't bring.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Creamy Potato": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals leans against the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Creme Brulee": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Tanq 10 Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Escargot": "Tanq 10 with escargot — premium Tanq with whole-citrus against garlic-butter snails. Strong; the chamomile-citrus blend meets butter with refinement, though flagship Tanq's proof cuts it sharper.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Tanq 10 with the salmon — whole-citrus premium Tanq against oily salmon. Strong; the grapefruit-orange lift frames salmon's richness with martini-gin precision.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Filet Mignon": "Tanq 10 with the filet — premium martini gin against tenderloin. Works; gin over-specified for steak; Tanq 10's refinement is the most elegant gin case.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|French Onion": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the orange brightens the caramel. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Tanq 10 with the grilled Caesar — whole-citrus premium gin against charred romaine and anchovy. Excellent; Tanq 10's citrus is the distinctive gin-Caesar pairing — where flagship Tanq cuts with juniper, Tanq 10 lifts with fresh citrus that reads like the lemon wedge.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Gumbo": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals sits beside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|House Wedge": "Tanq 10 with the House Wedge — premium whole-citrus against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Tanq 10's citrus is a refined wedge pairing without Plymouth's sweetness.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Kansas City": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin reads light against the prime cut's weight — needs a red or whiskey. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Tanq 10 Gin on another course instead.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Loaded Potato": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Lobster Mac": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals sits beside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Market Fish": "Tanq 10 with the market fish — whole-citrus premium gin against featured fish. Works; Tanq 10's citrus handles most fish preparations with lift.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Mocha Creme": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Tanq 10 Gin for the steak course.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Mushrooms": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Tanq 10 Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Porterhouse": "Tanq 10 Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Tanq 10 Gin for another course.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Prime Tartare": "Tanq 10 with the tartare — small-batch premium Tanq against raw beef. Strong; Tanq 10's citrus-chamomile supports tartare at refined register, though Bombay Dry's angular juniper is the featured gin-tartare match.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Tanq 10 Gin's herbal-botanical edge sits alongside the trout's delicate flesh: the floral edge frames the trout, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Neptunia's briny character engages the bleu with distinctive maritime profile.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Tanq 10 with the chicken — small-batch premium Tanq against roasted poultry. Works; Tanq 10's citrus-chamomile supports chicken at refined register.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals lifts the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals plays cleanly against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Seafood Tower": "Tanq 10 with the seafood tower — premium Tanqueray No. Ten against the raw bar. Excellent; Tanq 10's citrus-chamomile profile is the martini-gin-collector's seafood tower pairing — more refined than flagship Tanq for the occasion.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Seared Scallops": "Tanq 10 with seared scallops — Tanqueray No. Ten, small-batch premium, whole-citrus-distilled (grapefruit, orange, lime) + chamomile. Excellent; Tanq 10's fresh-citrus lift is the distinctive gin-scallop pairing — where Tanq flagship leads with juniper proof, Tanq 10 leads with bright citrus that mirrors scallop sweetness.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals leans against the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals leans against the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Tanq 10 with shrimp bisque — premium citrus-Tanq against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Tanq 10's citrus brightens the bisque at refined register.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Tanq 10 with shrimp cocktail — premium whole-citrus Tanq against cold shrimp. Excellent; the grapefruit-orange-lime distillate makes this the textbook citrus-seafood pairing — flagship Tanq cuts with juniper, Tanq 10 lifts with citrus.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Swordfish": "Tanq 10 Gin's gin lift with floral-and-pepper leans against the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the floral edge frames the swordfish, and the juniper threads the plate. Strong; Sapphire's softer character frames the fish without the aggression of flagship London Drys.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|The Tomahawk": "Tanq 10 with the Tomahawk — premium citrus gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Tomato Basil": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals plays cleanly against the tomato basil's bright acid — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Truffle Fries": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals stays alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals matches the tuna's seared rare crust — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Tanq 10 Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Tanq 10 Gin's crisp citrus-floral botanicals sits beside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Asparagus": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals finds neutral with the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Beignets": "Tanqueray with beignets — high-proof London Dry against fried dough. Works; the juniper-sugar contrast is historically British-pub territory.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Bone Marrow": "Tanqueray with bone marrow — 47.3% juniper against umami fat. Avoid; juniper can't cut marrow the way Fernet Branca does.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Bone-In Filet": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a gin. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Tanqueray Gin lands on another course — not here.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Broccoli Cheddar": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Broccolini": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals leans against the broccolini's tender green — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Brussels and Belly": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals sits beside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Burrata": "Tanqueray with burrata — high-proof juniper against fresh dairy. Excellent; Tanq's botanical spine cuts cream with precision; Plymouth's sweetness matches it softer but Tanq's contrast wins here.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Butternut Squash": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals sits alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Carrot Cake": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Tanqueray Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Cheesecake": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- sharp botanicals cut through dairy custard without resolving — the dessert reads broken. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not a gin. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Tanqueray Gin for the steak course.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Chilean Seabass": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals matches the seabass's buttery flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the botanicals lift the savory weight. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Chocolate Brownie": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a gin. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Tanqueray Gin for the steak course.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Chocolate Cake": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a gin. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Tanqueray Gin for the steak course.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Clam Chowder": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the juniper carries the shellfish. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Cowboy Ribeye": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Tanqueray Gin for another course.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Crab Cake": "Tanqueray with crab cake — 47.3% juniper-forward London Dry, quadruple-distilled British benchmark. Excellent; Tanq's higher proof and juniper intensity lifts delicate crab with pine-and-citrus precision where lower-proof gins just sit. The default Tanq-seafood pairing. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Creamed Spinach": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Creamy Potato": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals stays alongside the potato soup's silky body — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Creme Brulee": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- gin's botanical-citrus register fights dessert sweetness without a bridge. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a gin. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Tanqueray Gin lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Escargot": "Tanqueray with escargot — juniper-forward Tanq against garlic-butter snails. Excellent; juniper-and-butter is a classic British pairing and Tanq's proof cuts the butter sharper than Plymouth's softer profile can.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Faroe Island Salmon": "Tanqueray with the salmon — 47.3% juniper icon against oily salmon. Strong; Tanq's botanical spine lifts the fish's richness with British precision.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Filet Mignon": "Tanqueray with the filet — high-proof juniper against tenderloin. Works; gin is over-specified for steak; Tanq's proof at least gives it a fighting chance.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|French Onion": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Grilled Caesar": "Tanqueray with the grilled Caesar — juniper-forward Tanq against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Tanq's proof cuts the char, though the Caesar leans toward vodkas (Ketel) cleaner. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Gumbo": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals leans against the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Tanqueray Gin's gin lift with floral-and-pepper sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the floral edge frames the honey carrots, and the juniper threads the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|House Wedge": "Tanqueray with the House Wedge — juniper icon against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Tanq's botanicals cut the bleu where Plymouth's softness matches it differently.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Kansas City": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift mis-pairs with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- gin's clean lift reads thin against the cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a gin. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Tanqueray Gin on another course instead.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Loaded Potato": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals cut the cheese richness. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Lobster Mac": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals stays alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the juniper carries the shellfish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Market Fish": "Tanqueray with the market fish — juniper icon against featured fish. Works; Tanq's botanicals handle most fish preparations with British precision.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Mocha Creme": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- gin botanicals jar against the chocolate's sweetness with no bridge. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a gin. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Tanqueray Gin on the steak course instead.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Mushroom Bisque": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals reads alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Mushrooms": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- juniper and cocoa refuse each other — botanical bitterness reads metallic against chocolate. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Tanqueray Gin for the steak course.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Porterhouse": "Tanqueray Gin's botanical-driven lift underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- gin's light register can't carry the marbled char — the cut deserves a bigger pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a gin. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Tanqueray Gin on another course instead.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Prime Tartare": "Tanqueray with the tartare — 47.3% juniper against raw beef. Strong; Tanq's botanical spine lifts tartare's minerality, though Bombay Dry's angular juniper is the featured tartare-gin pairing.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Rainbow Trout": "Tanqueray Gin's coriander-and-juniper register reads register-aligned with the trout's delicate flesh: the gin lift cuts the trout, and the floral-and-herb register brightens the plate. Strong; gin keeps pace with the trout.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Roast Half Chicken": "Tanqueray with the chicken — 47.3% juniper against roasted poultry. Works; Tanq's botanicals handle chicken at functional register.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals stays alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals finds register with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the botanicals lift the savory weight. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals stays alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the botanical edge sits with the light side, and the botanicals echo the green plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Seafood Tower": "Tanqueray with the seafood tower — iconic London Dry against the raw bar. Excellent; Tanq's juniper and proof are the benchmark gin-and-raw-bar pairing — the classic choice before guests knew to ask for martinis.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Seared Scallops": "Tanqueray with seared scallops — juniper icon against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Tanq's 47.3% proof and angelica-coriander-licorice botanical spine cut scallop richness where Plymouth's softer character just accompanies.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Seasonal Soup": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals stays alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Seasonal Vegetables": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals reads alongside the seasonal garden plate — the botanical edge sits with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Shrimp Bisque": "Tanqueray with shrimp bisque — high-proof London Dry against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Tanq's juniper cuts the bisque's richness sharply.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Shrimp Cocktail": "Tanqueray with shrimp cocktail — 47.3% juniper icon against cold shrimp. Excellent; Tanq brings the lift Beefeater rounds and Plymouth softens — the benchmark pairing.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Swordfish": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals plays cleanly against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the botanicals lift the savory weight. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|The Tomahawk": "Tanqueray with the Tomahawk — juniper icon against 32oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Tomato Basil": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals sits beside the tomato basil's bright acid — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals lift the savory weight. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Truffle Fries": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals sits alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the gin botanicals sit with the plate, and the botanicals frame the umami depth. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals holds steady against the tuna's seared rare crust — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the juniper sharpens the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Tanqueray Gin|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Tanqueray Gin's bold London-Dry-juniper botanicals reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the gin botanicals sit with the plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Asparagus": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sweet wine on green vegetable is wrong direction — needs crisp white. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Beignets": "Classic Douro Tawny — Taylor Fladgate (founded 1692), smooth balanced, dried fruit, almond, warm clean finish. The warm clean finish clings to hot choux-fried dough and the almond lifts powdered-sugar simplicity. Peak heritage-Tawny for beignets — balanced lift on the light airy bite. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Bone Marrow": "Never pair Taylor Fladgate Tawny with bone marrow — smooth balanced sugar drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and the almond oxidative note clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. Tawny belongs at dessert.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Bone-In Filet": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on another course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Broccoli Cheddar": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness doubles down on the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Broccolini": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Brussels and Belly": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness doubles down on Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- sweet wine and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no foil. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Taylor Fladgate Tawny lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Burrata": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness doubles down on the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet wine and fresh dairy compound the richness with no foil. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Butternut Squash": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Carrot Cake": "Classic Douro Tawny — Taylor Fladgate (founded 1692), smooth balanced, dried fruit, almond, warm clean finish. The warm clean finish matches allspice-cinnamon spice and the almond plays with cream-cheese frosting's vanilla bean paste. Peak heritage-Tawny for carrot cake — balanced spice match. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Cheesecake": "Classic Douro Tawny — Taylor Fladgate (founded 1692), smooth balanced, dried fruit, almond, warm clean finish. The dried fruit lifts cream-cheese density and the almond plays with graham-cracker brown-sugar crust. Peak heritage-Tawny for cheesecake — smooth balance handles the water-bath bake. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Chilean Seabass": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Chocolate Brownie": "Classic Douro Tawny — Taylor Fladgate (founded 1692), smooth balanced, dried fruit, almond, warm clean finish. The almond plays with whipped-chocolate frosting and the smooth balance handles fudge-brownie cocoa. Peak heritage-Tawny for chocolate brownie — 1692 Douro on the dense bake. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Chocolate Cake": "Classic Douro Tawny — Taylor Fladgate (founded 1692), smooth balanced, dried fruit, almond, warm clean finish. The smooth balance wraps fudgy chocolate-cake crumb and the almond plays with buttermilk-dairy depth. Strong heritage-Tawny for chocolate cake — Vintage Port's structure handles cocoa harder.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Clam Chowder": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness doubles down on the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Cowboy Ribeye": "Never pair Taylor Fladgate Tawny with Cowboy Ribeye — 1692 heritage Tawny's residual sugar muddles marbled-ribeye char and the smooth balance loses against grilled-beef protein weight. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. Save the Tawny for after.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Crab Cake": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Creamed Spinach": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Creamy Potato": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness doubles down on the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Taylor Fladgate Tawny lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Creme Brulee": "Classic Douro Tawny — Taylor Fladgate (founded 1692), smooth balanced, dried fruit, almond, warm clean finish. The almond echoes the torched-sugar crust's toast and the dried fruit plays with vanilla-custard. Peak heritage-Tawny for creme brulee — 1692 lineage on the caramelized classic. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Escargot": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Taylor Fladgate Tawny lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Faroe Island Salmon": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness doubles down on the salmon's rich oily flesh -- honeyed wine and oily fish double up richness with no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Filet Mignon": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- dessert wine is post-meal — wrong register for the steak course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for another course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|French Onion": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Grilled Caesar": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Gumbo": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- honeyed sugar on savory broth jars — wrong direction for the course. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Taylor Fladgate Tawny lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|House Wedge": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Kansas City": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- dessert wine is post-meal — wrong register for the steak course. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for another course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Loaded Potato": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness doubles down on the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Lobster Mac": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness doubles down on the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Market Fish": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the wine's sweetness buries the gentle flesh's lift. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Mocha Creme": "Classic Douro Tawny — Taylor Fladgate (founded 1692), smooth balanced, dried fruit, almond, warm clean finish. The almond echoes espresso-roast bitterness and the warm finish plays with yolk-cream custard. Strong heritage-Tawny for mocha creme — Vintage Port's chocolate intensity edges higher on espresso.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Mushroom Bisque": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Mushrooms": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Classic Douro Tawny — Taylor Fladgate (founded 1692), smooth balanced, dried fruit, almond, warm clean finish. The almond echoes peanut-butter-frosting nuttiness and the dried fruit bridges fudge-brownie brown-sugar depth. Strong heritage-Tawny for peanut butter brownie — Graham's 10 Year's toffee fits richer.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Porterhouse": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- honeyed sugar dulls the marbled char — the steak earns a savory pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Tawny on another course instead.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Prime Tartare": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Rainbow Trout": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Roast Half Chicken": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness buries the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- dessert wine on chicken is wrong course — sugar smothers the herbed-skin frame. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Taylor Fladgate Tawny lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- dessert wine on delicate fish is wrong-course energy — needs a dry white, not honeyed sugar. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- sweet wine on green vegetable is wrong direction — needs crisp white. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Seafood Tower": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Seared Scallops": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Seasonal Soup": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Seasonal Vegetables": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Shrimp Bisque": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Shrimp Cocktail": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Swordfish": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sweet wine dulls the sear's clean char — savory main deserves a dry pour. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Taylor Fladgate Tawny lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|The Tomahawk": "Never pair Taylor Fladgate Tawny with Tomahawk — heritage Tawny buries the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and sweet oxidation fights smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. Tawny is a finishing pour, not a steak wine.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Tomato Basil": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Taylor Fladgate Tawny belongs on the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Truffle Fries": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness flattens the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- dessert sugar on starch is wrong-course energy for the supporting plate. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Tawny for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Tawny|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Taylor Fladgate Tawny's classic tawny with caramel-nut sweetness clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Tawny on the steak course instead.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Asparagus": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sweet wine on green vegetable is wrong direction — needs crisp white. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Beignets": "Flagship Vintage Port — Taylor Fladgate bottle, extraordinarily rich, dark fruit, chocolate, tobacco, minutes-long evolving finish. The extraordinarily rich structure overwhelms hot choux-fried dough and powdered-sugar simplicity. Strong flagship-Vintage for beignets — the celebrated bottle is over-spent on the light airy bite.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Bone Marrow": "Never pair Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle with bone marrow — flagship sweet structure drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and dark-fruit intensity clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. The Vintage Bottle is a dessert pour.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Bone-In Filet": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- dessert wine is post-meal — wrong register for the steak course. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for another course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Broccoli Cheddar": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness doubles down on the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle on the steak course instead.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Broccolini": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- sweet wine on green vegetable is wrong direction — needs crisp white. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Brussels and Belly": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle on the steak course instead.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Burrata": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Butternut Squash": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Carrot Cake": "Flagship Vintage Port — Taylor Fladgate bottle, extraordinarily rich, dark fruit, chocolate, tobacco, minutes-long evolving finish. The tobacco lifts allspice-cinnamon spice and the chocolate plays with cream-cheese frosting. Strong flagship-Vintage for carrot cake — extraordinarily rich reads heavy, Tawny fits the spice softer.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Cheesecake": "Flagship Vintage Port — Taylor Fladgate bottle, extraordinarily rich, dark fruit, chocolate, tobacco, minutes-long evolving finish. The dark fruit lifts cream-cheese density and the tobacco plays with graham-cracker brown-sugar. Peak flagship-Vintage for cheesecake — Taylor's flagship on the water-bath bake. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Chilean Seabass": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Chocolate Brownie": "Flagship Vintage Port — Taylor Fladgate bottle, extraordinarily rich, dark fruit, chocolate, tobacco, minutes-long evolving finish. The chocolate echoes fudge-brownie cocoa directly and the tobacco plays with whipped-chocolate frosting. Peak flagship-Vintage for chocolate brownie — Taylor's celebrated bottle on the dense bake. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Chocolate Cake": "* Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's sticky-sweet dessert pour meets head-on the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the dessert-wine register matches the chocolate cake, and the honey threads the dessert. One of the more interesting dessert pairings on the list. Gold standard; the call that earns the regular.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Clam Chowder": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Cowboy Ribeye": "Never pair Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle with Cowboy Ribeye — flagship vintage structure is built for chocolate, not grilled-beef protein char, and residual sugar clashes with marbled-ribeye savory. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. The Vintage Bottle belongs at dessert.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Crab Cake": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Creamed Spinach": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle on the steak course instead.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Creamy Potato": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Creme Brulee": "Flagship Vintage Port — Taylor Fladgate bottle, extraordinarily rich, dark fruit, chocolate, tobacco, minutes-long evolving finish. The dark fruit plays with torched-sugar crust, but extraordinarily rich structure overshadows vanilla-custard delicacy. Strong flagship-Vintage for creme brulee — Tawny's toffee fits the caramel harder.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Escargot": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle on the steak course instead.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Faroe Island Salmon": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle on the steak course instead.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Filet Mignon": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet wine smothers the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle belongs on another course.",
