  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Seafood Tower": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Seared Scallops": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Seasonal Soup": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- honeyed sugar on savory broth jars — wrong direction for the course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Seasonal Vegetables": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- sweet wine on green vegetable is wrong direction — needs crisp white. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle on the steak course instead.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Shrimp Bisque": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness doubles down on the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle on the steak course instead.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Shrimp Cocktail": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Swordfish": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|The Tomahawk": "Never pair Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle with Tomahawk — the extraordinarily rich flagship buries the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and residual sugar fights smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. Present the Vintage Bottle with dessert, not steak.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Tomato Basil": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Truffle Fries": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle belongs on the steak course.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle's dessert-wine sweetness clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- honeyed sugar on savory broth jars — wrong direction for the course. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle for the steak course.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Asparagus": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the barrel-rested body handles the asparagus, and the oak threads the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Tears of Llorona with au gratin potatoes — triple-cask aged against cheese-and-cream. Works; multi-cask complexity over-specified.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Beignets": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the aged-agave weight wraps the beignets, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Bone Marrow": "Tears of Llorona with bone marrow — triple-cask aged against umami fat. The Scotch-sherry-cognac cask complexity meets marrow with multi-cask distinctiveness. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Bone-In Filet": "Tears of Llorona with the bone-in filet — triple-cask aged (Scotch/sherry/cognac) against bone-in cut. The multi-cask complexity gives the cut a frame no single-cask extra añejo can deliver. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Broccoli Cheddar": "Tears of Llorona's cooked-agave-and-oak register reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the añejo register carries the broccoli cheddar, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; ceremony over-specified.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Broccolini": "Tears of Llorona's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the añejo register carries the broccolini, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the side. Works; brightness can cut the glaze but wheated bourbons sit better with this dish.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Brussels and Belly": "Tears of Llorona with Brussels + Belly — triple-cask meets maple-glazed pork belly. Works; multi-cask over-specified for small plate.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Burrata": "Tears of Llorona with burrata — fresh dairy against triple-cask aged. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Butternut Squash": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak-aged tequila body threads the squash bisque, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; the extended maturation gives the side more than standard reposados bring, though not a standout.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Carrot Cake": "Tears of Llorona with carrot cake — triple-cask aged against cream cheese and cinnamon. Strong; sherry and cognac cask influences find the cake's raisins at multi-cask craft register.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Cheesecake": "Tears of Llorona with cheesecake — triple-cask against custard. Works; multi-cask depth composed with dessert.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Chilean Seabass": "Tears of Llorona's rested-agave body sits alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the añejo register carries the seabass, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Chocolate Brownie": "Tears of Llorona with the chocolate brownie — triple-cask aged against cocoa. The sherry and cognac cask influences especially thread through the brownie with dessert-aged character no other extra añejo matches. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Chocolate Cake": "Tears of Llorona's barrel-aged tequila weight finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the chocolate cake, and the caramel-agave register brightens the dessert. Works; accessible aged composed with dessert.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Clam Chowder": "Tears of Llorona's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the barrel-rested body handles the chowder, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Cowboy Ribeye": "Tears of Llorona with the Cowboy Ribeye — triple-cask aged against cap fat. Strong; multi-cask depth covers the ribeye with González's legendary craft authority.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Crab Cake": "Tears of Llorona with crab cake — triple-cask aged against delicate crab. Avoid; save the González legendary bottle.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Creamed Spinach": "Tears of Llorona with creamed spinach — triple-cask aged against dairy. Strong; multi-cask complexity sits with richness at collector-craft register.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Creamy Potato": "Tears of Llorona's aged-agave character reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the añejo register carries the potato soup, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; Riedel-luxury over-specified.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Creme Brulee": "Tears of Llorona with creme brulee — multi-cask aged tequila against vanilla custard. Strong; triple-cask depth (especially sherry and cognac notes) mirrors the custard with distinctive multi-cask character.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Escargot": "Tears of Llorona's oak-sherry character finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Faroe Island Salmon": "Tears of Llorona's barrel-aged tequila weight sits alongside the salmon's rich oily flesh: the oak-aged tequila body threads the salmon, and the soft-spice note softens the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Filet Mignon": "Tears of Llorona with the filet — Germán González's extra añejo aged in three different casks: Scotch, sherry, and cognac. One of the most distinctive multi-cask tequilas in the world; González is a legendary tequila maker. The cask-influence layers create a frame no other extra añejo matches. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Tears of Llorona|French Onion": "Tears of Llorona's rested-agave body finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the rested-agave depth sits with the French onion, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; the aged character is a different register than the dish calls for.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Grilled Caesar": "Tears of Llorona's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the grilled Caesar, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; clean character doesn't drive the pairing.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Gumbo": "Tears of Llorona's rested-agave body reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the gumbo, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; refined but not a standout.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Tears of Llorona's rested-agave body finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the barrel-rested body handles the honey carrots, and the soft-spice note softens the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Tears of Llorona|House Wedge": "Tears of Llorona's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch: the añejo register carries the wedge, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Kansas City": "Tears of Llorona with the KC — multi-cask aged against the strip. Strong; triple-cask complexity covers the strip with distinctive multi-cask character.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Loaded Potato": "Tears of Llorona's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the loaded potato soup, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; extended-aged complexity handles richness at Los-Altos register.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Lobster Mac": "Tears of Llorona's rested-agave body finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the añejo register carries the lobster mac, and the oak threads the side. Works; Ultra's ceremonial depth composed with this dish.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Market Fish": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth leans against the market fish's clean white-fish flesh: the añejo register carries the market fish, and the cooked-agave note underlines the plate. Works; luxury ceremony over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Mocha Creme": "Tears of Llorona with mocha creme — triple-cask aged against coffee-chocolate. Works; cognac and sherry cask notes find mocha at distinctive register.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Mushroom Bisque": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the rested-agave depth sits with the mushroom bisque, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; the pink character doesn't quite align with truffle's earth.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Mushrooms": "Tears of Llorona with the mushrooms — multi-cask aged against earthy umami. The Scotch/sherry/cognac cask layers create mushroom-pairing complexity no single-cask aged tequila reaches. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Tears of Llorona with the PB brownie — triple-cask aged against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; multi-cask complexity composed with dessert at distinctive register.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Porterhouse": "Tears of Llorona with the porterhouse — triple-cask across strip and filet. Works; multi-cask complexity over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Prime Tartare": "Tears of Llorona's oak-sherry character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the añejo depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Rainbow Trout": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth reads alongside the trout's delicate flesh: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the trout, and the caramel-agave register brightens the plate. Works; the wine influence is unusual here but doesn't quite drive.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Roast Half Chicken": "Tears of Llorona's cooked-agave-and-oak register finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the barrel-rested body handles the roast chicken, and the oak threads the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Tears of Llorona's rested-agave body sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the barrel-rested body handles the chickpea soup, and the caramel-agave register brightens the bowl. Works; multi-cask complexity over-specified for two-cut plate.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Tears of Llorona's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the aged-agave weight wraps the halibut, and the soft-spice note softens the plate. Works; wheated-soft character handles richness at gentler-Expresiones register.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the aged-agave weight wraps the garlic spinach, and the cooked-agave note underlines the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Seafood Tower": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth finds neutral with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the seafood tower, and the soft-spice note softens the starter. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Seared Scallops": "Tears of Llorona's aged-agave character overwhelms the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- añejo weight has no foil against cold shellfish — needs sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Tears of Llorona for the steak course.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Seasonal Soup": "Tears of Llorona's rested-agave body sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the barrel-rested body handles the seasonal soup, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; the bold agave overpowers the dish's restraint.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Seasonal Vegetables": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the barrel-rested body handles the seasonal vegetables, and the oak threads the side. Works; iconic pour over-specified.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Shrimp Bisque": "Tears of Llorona's aged-agave character sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the añejo register carries the shrimp bisque, and the oak threads the bowl. Works; clean and refined but not a marquee pairing.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Shrimp Cocktail": "Tears of Llorona with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against triple-cask. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Swordfish": "Tears of Llorona's aged-agave character reads alongside the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the barrel-rested body handles the swordfish, and the oak threads the plate. Works; bold agave is a distinctive counterpoint but slightly overpowering.",
  "Tears of Llorona|The Tomahawk": "Tears of Llorona's rested-agave body reads alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the barrel-rested body handles the 36oz showpiece, and the caramel-agave register brightens the cut. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Tomato Basil": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the rested-agave depth sits with the tomato basil, and the cooked-agave note underlines the bowl. Works; clean and refined but not a marquee pairing.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Truffle Fries": "Tears of Llorona with truffle fries — triple-cask Scotch/sherry/cognac against truffle-parmesan. The cask-influence layers create a frame for the earthy side that no other extra añejo replicates. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth leans against the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the barrel-rested body handles the tuna, and the caramel-agave register brightens the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tears of Llorona|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Tears of Llorona's añejo-pour depth leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the barrel-rested body handles the curry, and the soft-spice note softens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Asparagus": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Polished Napa Cab — rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth lingering finish. The crowd-pleaser body cuts au gratin's cheese-and-cream weight and the mocha plays against the golden crust. Works as mid-tier Napa Cab for au gratin — approachable concentration on the decadent side.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Beignets": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone Marrow": "Teeter Totter — Napa Cab with rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth lingering finish. The polished body meets marrow's rendered-fat richness and the mocha frames the bone depth. Peak mid-tier Napa Cab for marrow — crowd-pleaser concentration handles the rich starter confidently. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Bone-In Filet": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's bold coffee-mocha cassis finds register with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the firm tannin reads cleanly against the lean cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccoli Cheddar": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Broccolini": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the broccolini's tender green stalks -- red weight crowds a green side without lifting it — needs crisp register, not tannin. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Burrata": "Polished Napa Cab against burrata's creamy-delicate center — smooth mocha body crushes the milk and basil-tomato-pistachio clashes with rich dark fruit. Never pair. Route burrata to rosé, Pinot Blanc, or Sancerre and keep Teeter Totter for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Butternut Squash": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Carrot Cake": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- red structure flattens the spice cake's cinnamon-and-cream-cheese delicacy. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Cheesecake": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Chilean Seabass": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Reserve the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Brownie": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Chocolate Cake": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Clam Chowder": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Cowboy Ribeye": "Napa Cab — rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth lingering finish, polished. The crowd-pleaser body handles Cowboy's marbled bone-in richness and the mocha meets rendered fat. Peak mid-tier Napa Cab for Cowboy — polished concentration frames fat-and-char without the prestige-bottle price. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Crab Cake": "Polished Napa Cab against crab cake's delicate sweet shellfish — smooth mocha body crushes romesco-and-crab. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chablis, Sancerre, or Champagne and keep Teeter Totter for the steak course where its polished mid-tier Napa character shines.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamed Spinach": "Teeter Totter — Napa Cab, rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth finish. The polished body cuts creamed spinach's dairy weight and the mocha plays against the earthy sauce. Strong mid-tier Napa Cab for creamed spinach — crowd-pleaser concentration on the classic steakhouse side.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Creamy Potato": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the wine's structure sits on top of the bisque's cream instead of integrating. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Creme Brulee": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth pulls against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- the red's dry-fruit register has no bridge to dessert tang or sugar. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Escargot": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's bold coffee-mocha cassis finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the opulent body settles into the umami plate. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Faroe Island Salmon": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Filet Mignon": "Teeter Totter — Napa Cab with rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth lingering finish. The polished body meets butter-tender filet's lean sweetness and the mocha echoes escargot-butter toppers. Strong mid-tier Napa Cab for filet — crowd-pleaser balance, though leaner-style Cabs like Jordan peak similarly on the classic cut.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|French Onion": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's bold coffee-mocha cassis leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee meets the caramel. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Grilled Caesar": "Teeter Totter — Napa Cab, rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth polished finish. The body meets anchovy-parmesan dressing and the mocha plays against grilled-romaine char. Works as mid-tier Napa Cab for grilled Caesar — crowd-pleaser concentration on the classic layered salad.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Gumbo": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|House Wedge": "Teeter Totter — Napa Cab, rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth finish. The polished body cuts iceberg-and-bacon and the mocha plays against blue cheese dressing. Works as mid-tier Napa Cab for the wedge — unconventional crowd-pleaser red, though whites peak higher for the classic wedge.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Kansas City": "Teeter Totter — polished Napa Cab with rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth lingering finish, approachable concentration. The polished body meets Kansas City's lean-bold marbled strip and the mocha plays against the beefy char. Peak mid-tier Napa Cab for Kansas City — crowd-pleaser balance without committing to a prestige bottle. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Loaded Potato": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's bold coffee-mocha cassis plays cleanly against the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Lobster Mac": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's bold coffee-mocha cassis leans against the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Market Fish": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the delicate fish — a crisp white cleans where red sits heavy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Mocha Creme": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth flattens the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushroom Bisque": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's dense blackberry-and-cassis tannin leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the structured body wraps the mushroom bisque, and the cassis frames the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Mushrooms": "Teeter Totter — Napa Cab, rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth finish. The polished body meshes with mushroom umami and the mocha frames the sauté earth. Peak mid-tier Napa Cab for mushrooms — crowd-pleaser concentration on the earthy side without prestige-bottle commitment. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Porterhouse": "Napa Cab — rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth polished finish. The crowd-pleaser body frames Porterhouse's dual-texture strip-and-filet and the mocha plays against both lean and fatty sides. Strong mid-tier Napa Cab for Porterhouse — polished concentration on the dual-cut classic.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Prime Tartare": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon -- polished Napa Cab with rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth lingering finish, approachable concentration. The structured body wraps the tartare, and the cassis frames the starter. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Rainbow Trout": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Roast Half Chicken": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's bold coffee-mocha cassis finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- the red is built for protein weight, not a light soup or salad base. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth crowds the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- the side needs a crisper register to carry it cleanly. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Seafood Tower": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the elegant red is built for the entrée, not the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon on the steak course instead.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Seared Scallops": "Teeter Totter Napa Cab against seared scallops' clean pan-sear — polished mocha obliterates delicate shellfish sweetness. Never layer. Route scallops to Chablis, Sancerre, or Pinot Blanc and save Teeter Totter for the steak course where its crowd-pleaser character peaks.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Soup": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Seasonal Vegetables": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- tannin on raw green vegetable reads astringent without payoff. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Bisque": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's bold coffee-mocha cassis finds neutral with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup, and the coffee threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Shrimp Cocktail": "Teeter Totter Napa Cab against chilled sweet-briny shrimp — polished mocha is the wrong axis for cold shellfish and cocktail sauce. Never layer. Route shrimp cocktail to Champagne, Sancerre, or Chablis and save Teeter Totter for the steak course where its crowd-pleaser character belongs.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Swordfish": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|The Tomahawk": "Napa Cab — rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth finish, polished. The crowd-pleaser body can meet Tomahawk fat and char but the smooth polished profile is rounder than the biggest marbled steak demands. Strong Teeter Totter for Tomahawk — Opus, Shafer, Caymus, or mountain Cabs peak higher for the theatrical cut.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Tomato Basil": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the mushroom's umami depth meets the bold red's structured tannin; dark fruit threads the truffle-and-earth without crowding. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth jars against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the dry-fruit register fights warm broth without resolving — needs crisp white or aperitivo. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon for the steak course.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Asparagus": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the medium-body red frames the green plate, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: the rich-side's cheese-and-cream weight finds the Cab's structured tannin; cassis and dark fruit anchor the pairing without crowding. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Beignets": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico for the steak course.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Bone Marrow": "Tuscany Chianti Classico DOCG Sangiovese — Arceno vibrant cherry, dried herbs, tomato leaf, savory earth, medium body, medium tannin. The medium tannin cuts marrow's collagen fat and the savory earth plays against the smoky mineral depth. Works as Tuscan Sangiovese for marrow — Italian table wine on the rich starter.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Bone-In Filet": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity rests at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the savory Tuscan red carries the bone-enhanced filet, and the savory red register plays against the cut. Strong; the match stays in its register against the cut.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Broccoli Cheddar": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's cherry-earth tannin sits beside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Broccolini": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the broccolini, and the cherry-leather edge frames the side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: the Brussels and Belly's smoky, sweet pork richness over bitter-char sprouts meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The bold red's dark-fruit weight locks into the side's fat and umami; tannin cleans the dairy. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Burrata": "Tuscan Chianti Classico against burrata's creamy-delicate center — Arceno's medium-tannin cherry-earth structure overwhelms the starter. Never pair. Route burrata to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Prosecco; keep Arceno for red meat or Italian table pairings.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Butternut Squash": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's cherry-earth tannin reads alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Carrot Cake": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico for the steak course.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Cheesecake": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico for the steak course.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Chilean Seabass": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the red's tannin and the salmon's rich oils refuse to integrate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico for the steak course.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Chocolate Brownie": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity fights the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the better calls are above. Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Chocolate Cake": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity flattens the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Clam Chowder": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's cherry-earth tannin sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Cowboy Ribeye": "Cowboy Ribeye: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Crab Cake": "Tuscan Chianti Classico against crab cake's delicate sweet meat — Arceno's medium-tannin cherry-earth structure buries the shellfish. Never pair. Route crab cake to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Arceno for Italian table pairings, filet, or the steak course.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Creamed Spinach": "Creamed Spinach: the side's cream pulls the bold red's tannin through; cassis and dark fruit catch the dairy's weight without overshooting. A strong, texturally cohesive match. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Creamy Potato": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the potato soup's silky body — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Creme Brulee": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico belongs on the steak course.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Escargot": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity rests at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the high-acid Tuscan register cuts the buttered escargot, and the savory red register plays against the starter. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
