  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Mushrooms": "Mushrooms: Cab tannin frames the earthy mushroom-and-truffle depth; dark fruit lifts the umami into the wine's body. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity jars against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico for the steak course.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the Cab's oak-driven weight matches the strip's char-and-fat; firm tannin clears the rendered edge. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Prime Tartare": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity leans against the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the tartare, and the cherry-leather edge frames the starter. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Rainbow Trout": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico for the steak course.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Roast Half Chicken": "Tuscany Chianti Classico DOCG Sangiovese — Arceno vibrant cherry, dried herbs, tomato leaf, savory earth, medium body, medium tannin, high acid. The high acid and tomato leaf play against chicken's skin-and-jus build; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as Tuscan Sangiovese for chicken across most seasonal preparations.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Tuscany Chianti Classico DOCG Sangiovese — vibrant cherry, dried herbs, tomato leaf, savory earth, medium body, medium tannin, high acid. The tomato-leaf meets the soup's tomato-pepper base directly and the high acid lifts the chickpea earth. Works tier on this wine's card — solid fit for Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea but below the wine's peaks with the Tomahawk, Porterhouse, and Bone-In Filet.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico on the steak course instead.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the garlic spinach, and the cherry-leather edge frames the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Seafood Tower": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity crowds out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the wine's weight has no business on cold shellfish — needs acid, not structure. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico for the steak course.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Seared Scallops": "Tuscan Chianti Classico against scallop sweetness — Arceno's medium-tannin cherry-earth structure buries the delicate shellfish. Never pair. Route scallops to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, or Rosé; keep Arceno for red meat, pasta, or Italian table service.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Seasonal Soup": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's cherry-earth tannin leans against the seasonal soup's house build — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Seasonal Vegetables": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bright Sangiovese body lifts the seasonal vegetables, and the cherry-leather edge frames the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Shrimp Bisque": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's cherry-earth tannin plays cleanly against the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the medium-body red frames the hearty soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Shrimp Cocktail": "Tuscan Chianti Classico against cold raw shrimp with horseradish — medium-tannin Arceno and chilled shellfish with cocktail sauce is textbook clash. Never pair. Route shrimp cocktail to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Rosé, or Champagne; keep Arceno for Italian table service.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Swordfish": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity overshadows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|The Tomahawk": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's cherry-earth tannin lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Tomato Basil": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's cherry-earth tannin finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright acid — the medium-body red meets the light soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Truffle Fries": "Truffle Fries: the side's mushroom richness pulls the bold red's body; tannin and oak settle into the umami without overshooting. A headliner pair — pour confidently. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's Sangiovese-driven Tuscan acidity clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico on the steak course instead.",
  "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's cherry-earth tannin holds with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the medium-body red meets the light soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "The BG|Asparagus": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build stays alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "The BG|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The BG's lemon-driven cocktail body sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the bright build frames the au gratin, and the citrus cuts the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The BG|Beignets": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build plays cleanly against the beignet's warm sugar-dusted pastry — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "The BG|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. Whiskey depth meets the starter's fat and umami — both land heavy. A headliner opener. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The BG|Bone-In Filet": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the cocktail is outweighed by the cut, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The BG|Broccoli Cheddar": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build leans against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the honey threads the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "The BG|Broccolini": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "The BG|Brussels and Belly": "Traveller whiskey, honey lavender, grapefruit, black walnut bitters, ginger beer top. The grapefruit cuts belly's rendered fat and honey lavender plays against the belly's sweet glaze; black walnut depth meets brussels char. Strong refreshing whiskey pairing for the sweet-savory build.",
  "The BG|Burrata": "Traveller whiskey, honey lavender syrup, whole grapefruit squeeze, black walnut bitters, ginger beer. The whiskey weight and black walnut earth crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco, French 75, or light gin cocktails, not a whiskey cocktail.",
  "The BG|Butternut Squash": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build leans against the squash soup's silky sweet — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the honey threads the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
