  "The Happy Wife|Peanut Butter Brownie": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build stays alongside the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the cocktail acidity underclubs the dessert, and the citrus brightens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "The Happy Wife|Porterhouse": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build matches the porterhouse's two-cut character — the cocktail is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the citrus brightens the cream. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Happy Wife|Prime Tartare": "Prime Tartare: this rich, umami-forward starter's bold fat and concentrated savory depth meets this gin cocktail's citrus brightness and botanical lift. Light cocktail's citrus lift mirrors a delicate starter's brightness. Works cleanly.",
  "The Happy Wife|Rainbow Trout": "The Happy Wife's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the trout's delicate flesh -- light cocktail register is wrong for a main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a light cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve The Happy Wife for the steak course.",
  "The Happy Wife|Roast Half Chicken": "Tito's, orange juice, ginger syrup. The orange-and-ginger profile can handle roasted chicken well, though seasonal accompaniments drive the pairing — check with kitchen. Peak approachable cocktail option for chicken when guest wants something simple and pleasant.",
  "The Happy Wife|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build plays cleanly against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; the match holds without overshooting. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "The Happy Wife|Salt-Cured Halibut": "The Happy Wife's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- light cocktail register is wrong for a main protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a light cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Pour The Happy Wife on the steak course instead.",
  "The Happy Wife|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build sits beside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the cocktail lift sits with the light side, and the citrus sharpens the briny edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Happy Wife|Seafood Tower": "The Happy Wife's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- this cocktail's register doesn't lift the opener — try a sparkling or crisp white. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a light cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save The Happy Wife for the steak course.",
  "The Happy Wife|Seared Scallops": "Tito's, orange juice, ginger syrup. The orange-and-ginger meets scallops' clean-seared sweetness without crushing; Tito's clean vodka keeps the pairing light. Strong approachable cocktail for scallops when guest wants simple over sophisticated — delivers on both ends.",
  "The Happy Wife|Seasonal Soup": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build leans against the seasonal soup's house build — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "The Happy Wife|Seasonal Vegetables": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build sits alongside the seasonal garden plate — the cocktail lift sits with the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "The Happy Wife|Shrimp Bisque": "Tito's, orange juice, ginger syrup. The orange juice cuts the bisque's cream-shellfish richness and ginger's subtle warmth plays against the soup's weight. Strong straightforward pairing — the Happy Wife's simplicity handles the bisque well.",
  "The Happy Wife|Shrimp Cocktail": "Tito's with orange juice and ginger syrup, stirred with a brûléed orange garnish. The OJ's sweetness balances the cocktail sauce's horseradish heat; the ginger adds warmth. Approachable pour for a guest who wants something easy. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Happy Wife|Swordfish": "The Happy Wife's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift mis-pairs with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- light cocktail register is wrong for a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a light cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve The Happy Wife for the steak course.",
  "The Happy Wife|The Tomahawk": "Screwdriver-plus-ginger cocktail against the biggest steak on the menu — zero structural overlap. The Happy Wife's simple profile has no weight for marbled Tomahawk fat. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "The Happy Wife|Tomato Basil": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build sits beside the tomato basil's bright acid — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus lifts the herbs. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "The Happy Wife|Truffle Fries": "The Happy Wife's citrus-and-bright cocktail lift finds neutral with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight: the bright build frames the truffle fries, and the citrus cuts the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "The Happy Wife|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Tito's, orange juice, ginger syrup. The ginger plays against honey sambal's spice and the orange juice lifts rare-seared tuna's lean flesh. Works as light cocktail for tuxedo tuna — simple-and-bright route; wines peak higher as dedicated pairings.",
  "The Happy Wife|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "The Happy Wife's crisp citrus-ginger build stays alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the cocktail lift sits with the light soup, and the citrus brightens the spice. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "The Manhattan|Asparagus": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the rye-vermouth body settles into the asparagus, and the cherry-bitters edge frames the side. Works; the opener sits without competing.",
  "The Manhattan|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Maker's Mark private barrel with Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The vanilla-sweet bourbon wraps around the manchego-baked cream; the cocktail's weight matches the gratin's richness directly. Decadent cocktail route when the guest's committed to indulgence. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "The Manhattan|Beignets": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build reads alongside the beignet's warm sugar-dusted pastry — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Manhattan|Bone Marrow": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The bourbon's vanilla-oak depth cuts marrow's fat richness and the herbal vermouth complexity adds depth against the savory-bone profile. Peak spirit-forward pour for marrow when guest wants classic whiskey cocktail over Old Fashioned.",
  "The Manhattan|Bone-In Filet": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The bone-enhancement deepens the filet's profile and the Manhattan's vanilla-softened structure meets it cleanly; Contratto's Italian vermouth adds herbal complexity. Peak cocktail route for the bone-in filet's elevated preparation.",
  "The Manhattan|Broccoli Cheddar": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "The Manhattan|Broccolini": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build reads alongside the broccolini's tender green — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side, and the rounded sweetness softens the green edge. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "The Manhattan|Brussels and Belly": "Maker's Mark private barrel, Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The bourbon depth meets pork belly's rendered fat and brussels char; Contratto's herbal-sweet side bridges the glaze. Works well — though the Bowdie's Old Fashioned or Espresso version peak higher for this specific sweet-savory build.",
  "The Manhattan|Burrata": "Maker's Mark private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Spirit-forward Manhattan weight crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile; bourbon has no hook into basil-tomato-cheese. Never pair — burrata begs for Prosecco or a crisp white, not a classic whiskey cocktail.",
  "The Manhattan|Butternut Squash": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build reads alongside the squash soup's silky sweet — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "The Manhattan|Carrot Cake": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build holds with the carrot cake's spiced cream cheese — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "The Manhattan|Cheesecake": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye-vermouth body settles into the cheesecake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Manhattan|Chilean Seabass": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour The Manhattan on the steak course instead.",
  "The Manhattan|Chocolate Brownie": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build reads alongside the brownie's warm cocoa depth — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "The Manhattan|Chocolate Cake": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build reads alongside the cake's layered chocolate — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "The Manhattan|Clam Chowder": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the rye-vermouth body settles into the chowder, and the cherry-bitters edge frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "The Manhattan|Cowboy Ribeye": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The Manhattan's bourbon depth stands against the Cowboy's marbled fat but the lighter vanilla-syrup softening doesn't quite match the Bowdie's Old Fashioned or Espresso Old Fashioned's weight here. Strong alternative spirit pour.",
  "The Manhattan|Crab Cake": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Whiskey cocktail weight overwhelms the crab's sweet-delicate meat; the Italian vermouth and vanilla syrup have no hook into the light romesco. Never pair — crab cake belongs with Sauvignon Blanc or Champagne, not the Manhattan.",
  "The Manhattan|Creamed Spinach": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The bourbon depth sits alongside the side's dairy weight without dominating; vanilla syrup softens the contrast against cream-and-parmesan. Strong pairing when guest carries the Manhattan through the entrée course with the spinach.",
  "The Manhattan|Creamy Potato": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build plays cleanly against the potato soup's silky body — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "The Manhattan|Creme Brulee": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the oak frames the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "The Manhattan|Escargot": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build finds register with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey-build depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "The Manhattan|Faroe Island Salmon": "Maker's Mark private barrel with Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Dark, sweet, heavy cocktail that overwhelms the Faroe's clean rich flavor. Save Manhattan for the beef course or the marrow. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "The Manhattan|Filet Mignon": "Maker's Mark private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The vanilla softens what would be a drier Manhattan, making it meet butter-tender filet without overwhelming; Contratto's herbal-sweet depth plays against lean beef. Peak whiskey cocktail for the most delicate cut.",
  "The Manhattan|French Onion": "Maker's Mark private barrel, Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The bourbon-vanilla depth meets French Onion's caramelized onion sweetness and Contratto's herbal edge plays against the gruyère crust. Works as classic cocktail for French Onion — bold whiskey route; wines peak higher as the dedicated pairing.",
  "The Manhattan|Grilled Caesar": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build leans against the Caesar's char-and-anchovy — the whiskey-build weight is misaligned with the leaf-and-acid plate, and the rounded sweetness brightens the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "The Manhattan|Gumbo": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build sits beside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the oak frames the umami. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "The Manhattan|Honey Roasted Carrots": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build plays cleanly against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "The Manhattan|House Wedge": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Cocktail weight is heavier than the iceberg-bacon-blue-cheese wedge strictly wants, but the vanilla softens enough for the pairing to hold. Works as an opener when guest leads with a spirit-forward classic.",
  "The Manhattan|Kansas City": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The cocktail's bourbon depth and herbal-sweet balance stands against the KC's lean-bold strip; vanilla syrup plays against the char. Peak Manhattan pairing for the most approachable bold cut — weight-matches well. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "The Manhattan|Loaded Potato": "Maker's Mark private barrel, Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The bourbon-vanilla depth meets the soup's bacon-cheddar richness and Contratto's herbal edge plays against scallion and sour cream. Strong cocktail pour for loaded potato — classic whiskey on the indulgent bowl.",
  "The Manhattan|Lobster Mac": "Maker's private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The vanilla-bourbon depth meets the lobster mac's cream-and-shellfish richness; Contratto's herbal sweetness bridges the cheese cream. Peak cocktail for rich seafood-indulgent side when guest wants whiskey-forward option.",
  "The Manhattan|Market Fish": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the bitters-and-bourbon register has no foil against gentle flesh. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Hold The Manhattan for the steak course.",
  "The Manhattan|Mocha Creme": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "The Manhattan|Mushroom Bisque": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the whiskey-build depth matches the hearty soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "The Manhattan|Mushrooms": "Maker's Mark private barrel with Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The vanilla-sweet bourbon wraps around mushroom's earthy depth; classic cocktail on a classic side. Heavier cocktail route for committed spirit guests. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Manhattan|Peanut Butter Brownie": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build stays alongside the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the vanilla amplifies the dessert sugar. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "The Manhattan|Porterhouse": "Maker's Mark private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura — stirred, large cube. Spirit-forward richness the porterhouse's marbled strip side can meet. Table-for-two pairing: one red, one cocktail, shared cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "The Manhattan|Prime Tartare": "Maker's Mark private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The vanilla-softened whiskey meets raw prime beef's iron-tinged richness well; Contratto's herbal sweetness complements the tartare seasoning. Strong pre-dinner pairing for guests leading with a classic whiskey cocktail.",
  "The Manhattan|Rainbow Trout": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- whiskey-forward cocktail weight buries delicate fish — the cocktail belongs on red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save The Manhattan for the steak course.",
  "The Manhattan|Roast Half Chicken": "Maker's Mark private barrel with Contratto, vanilla syrup, Angostura. Dark, sweet, heavy — wrong register for roasted chicken's delicate richness. Save for the beef course; route chicken guests to the BG or Paloma. Avoid; the call reads off-register; recommend an alternative.",
  "The Manhattan|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build sits beside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the caramel rounds the earthy plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Manhattan|Salt-Cured Halibut": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. The Manhattan belongs on the steak course.",
  "The Manhattan|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build plays cleanly against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "The Manhattan|Seafood Tower": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- whiskey-cocktail weight is wrong for cold shellfish — the opener needs lift, not bitters. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour The Manhattan on the steak course instead.",
  "The Manhattan|Seared Scallops": "Maker's Mark private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The spirit-forward Manhattan obliterates the scallops' delicate sweetness and the vanilla depth muddies the clean sear. Never pair — scallops demand crisp whites or bubbles, not a classic whiskey cocktail.",
  "The Manhattan|Seasonal Soup": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house build — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "The Manhattan|Seasonal Vegetables": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build sits alongside the seasonal garden plate — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "The Manhattan|Shrimp Bisque": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build finds neutral with the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the whiskey-build depth matches the hearty soup, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "The Manhattan|Shrimp Cocktail": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- the cocktail's weight is forward for the table's first course. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold The Manhattan for the steak course.",
  "The Manhattan|Swordfish": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. The Manhattan lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "The Manhattan|The Tomahawk": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey-build weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Manhattan|Tomato Basil": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the rye-vermouth body settles into the tomato basil, and the cherry-bitters edge frames the bowl. Works; the side call holds at neutral register.",
  "The Manhattan|Truffle Fries": "Maker's Mark private barrel, Contratto vermouth, vanilla syrup, Angostura. The bourbon's oak-vanilla-caramel depth meets truffle oil's earth directly and the Italian vermouth's herbal complexity plays against parmesan. Peak cocktail-side pairing when the fries carry the table's flavor weight. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Manhattan|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "The Manhattan's classic Manhattan with rye-and-vermouth depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour The Manhattan on the steak course instead.",
  "The Manhattan|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build stays alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Asparagus": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis sits beside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The integrated tannin cuts gruyere-cream and the full body handles dairy weight. Works for au gratin — a reliable recognized-label match on the side.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Beignets": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour The Prisoner Red Blend on the steak course instead.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Bone Marrow": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The integrated tannin cuts roasted-marrow unctuousness and the layered fruit echoes toasted-bread char. Peak Prisoner for bone marrow — Napa lush-on-rich harmony. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Bone-In Filet": "The Prisoner Red Blend's structured dark-fruit weight sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the structured body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the cassis frames the cut. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Broccoli Cheddar": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cream — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Broccolini": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis plays cleanly against the broccolini's tender green — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Brussels and Belly": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The integrated tannin cuts pork-belly fat and the layered dark fruit plays with brussels-char. Strong Prisoner for brussels-and-belly — Napa lush polish on fat-and-char.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Burrata": "Never pair Prisoner with burrata — layered dark fruit and full body clash with fresh cream and the lush finish buries tomato-basil brightness. Lead with Santa Margherita Pinot Grigio's clean lift or a Rosé's strawberry-to-tomato-sweetness bridge.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Butternut Squash": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis finds neutral with the squash soup's silky sweet — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Carrot Cake": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift flattens the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- the structured red has nothing to bridge into a dessert finish. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve The Prisoner Red Blend for the steak course.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Cheesecake": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard -- tannin reads astringent next to dairy custard, the custard reads sour back. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Disaronno, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve The Prisoner Red Blend for the steak course.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Chilean Seabass": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. The Prisoner Red Blend belongs on the steak course.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Chocolate Brownie": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift clashes with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- savory tannin sits awkwardly on cocoa weight without resolving. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour The Prisoner Red Blend on the steak course instead.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Chocolate Cake": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not an elegant red. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. The Prisoner Red Blend lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Clam Chowder": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis sits beside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Cowboy Ribeye": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The full body matches Cowboy's marbled 26oz bone-in and the smooth tannin won't tear against rendered fat. Peak Prisoner for ribeye — reliable familiar sell on the marbled cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Crab Cake": "Never pair Prisoner with crab cake — layered dark fruit and full body bury delicate lump crab and the integrated tannin fights the romesco. Lead with Heitz Sauvignon Blanc's citrus lift or Reverdy Sancerre's mineral edge for shellfish clarity.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Creamed Spinach": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The full body handles dairy-heavy cream and the smooth tannin won't tear against spinach-iron. Strong Prisoner for creamed spinach — Napa lush on the rich side.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Creamy Potato": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis sits alongside the potato soup's silky body — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Creme Brulee": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- savory tannin meets dessert sugar with no bridge between them. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour The Prisoner Red Blend on the steak course instead.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Escargot": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the Cab structure meets the savory depth. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Faroe Island Salmon": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift jars against the salmon's rich oily flesh -- tannin on oily fish goes bitter, where a Pinot at the right register would carry it. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. The Prisoner Red Blend lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Filet Mignon": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The smooth tannin matches butter-tender filet's delicate weight and the layered fruit plays with escargot-butter. Strong Prisoner for filet — full body reads heavy but the smoothness threads.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|French Onion": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis sits beside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Grilled Caesar": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The layered fruit plays with anchovy-depth, but the full body reads heavy for the salad. Works for grilled caesar — a Rhône garrigue fits anchovy harder.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Gumbo": "Napa Red Blend — Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The integrated tannin cuts gumbo's roux fat and the layered fruit plays against andouille-shrimp complexity. Strong recognized-label blend for gumbo — accessible red on the Cajun classic.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Honey Roasted Carrots": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis stays alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|House Wedge": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The smooth tannin holds against blue-cheese funk and the layered fruit plays with bacon-fat. Works for wedge — cold iceberg mutes the lushness, a lighter Chianti lifts higher.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Kansas City": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The integrated tannin wraps around Kansas City's lean-bold strip char and the layered dark fruit plays with grilled beef. Peak Prisoner for the strip — one of the country's most recognized labels. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Loaded Potato": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis sits alongside the loaded potato's richness — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Lobster Mac": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis sits beside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the concentrated body settles into the rich side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Market Fish": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour The Prisoner Red Blend on the steak course instead.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Mocha Creme": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift fights the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour The Prisoner Red Blend on the steak course instead.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Mushroom Bisque": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Mushrooms": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The layered dark fruit plays with roasted-mushroom butter-depth and the full body handles glutamate-weight. Peak Prisoner for mushrooms — Napa lush on the earthy side. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Peanut Butter Brownie": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift fights the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- the dry red and the chocolate's sugar refuse each other — tannin reads bitter on the finish. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not an elegant red. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Pour The Prisoner Red Blend on the steak course instead.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Porterhouse": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The full body handles Porterhouse's dual-cut heft and the integrated tannin works across both filet and strip. Strong Prisoner for Porterhouse — Napa lush on the big plate.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Prime Tartare": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis finds register with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the opulent body settles into the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Rainbow Trout": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift clashes with the trout's delicate flesh -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save The Prisoner Red Blend for the steak course.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Roast Half Chicken": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis holds with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the concentrated body carries the herb-crisped bird, and the smoke meets the char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis holds with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Salt-Cured Halibut": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the red's structure is wrong for delicate flesh — built for red meat. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve The Prisoner Red Blend for the steak course.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis leans against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; capable alongside. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Seafood Tower": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- tannin obliterates the delicate shellfish flavor — a crisp white or sparkling carries it cleanly. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve The Prisoner Red Blend for the steak course.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Seared Scallops": "Never pair Prisoner with seared scallops — layered dark fruit and full body overwhelm delicate sweet shellfish and the smooth tannin fights browned-butter sweetness. Lead with Clos de la Chapelle Chardonnay or Rombauer's creamy richness for sear-seared harmony.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Seasonal Soup": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; safe call, no friction. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Seasonal Vegetables": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis reads alongside the seasonal garden plate — the opulent body overshoots the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Shrimp Bisque": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis reads alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the concentrated body matches the heavy soup. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Shrimp Cocktail": "Never pair Prisoner with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp plus cocktail-sauce horseradish gets crushed by layered dark fruit and full body, and the lush finish has nowhere to land. Lead with Laurent Perrier Brut Champagne's bubble-driven palate reset.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Swordfish": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. The Prisoner Red Blend belongs on the steak course.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|The Tomahawk": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the concentrated tannin grips the marbled cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Tomato Basil": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright acid — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Truffle Fries": "Napa Red Blend — The Prisoner, Zin-Cab-Petite Sirah-Syrah-Charbono, layered dark fruit, smooth integrated tannin, full body, lush finish. The layered fruit plays with truffle-oil mushroom depth and the integrated tannin cuts parm-richness. Peak Prisoner for truffle fries — recognized-label lush on the cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "The Prisoner Red Blend's jammy Zinfandel with bramble-and-pepper lift clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin reads metallic against the fish's natural fats. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save The Prisoner Red Blend for the steak course.",
  "The Prisoner Red Blend|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the opulent body overshoots the light soup. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|1792 Small Batch": "1792 Small Batch with the Tomahawk — 93.7-proof Barton against bone-in ribeye. Strong; the balance is capable but Baker's, Four Roses Small Batch Select, or E.H. Taylor are stronger Tomahawk calls.",
  "The Tomahawk|1792 Sweet Wheat": "1792 Sweet Wheat's butter-caramel character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|1881 Napa Valley": "1881 Napa Valley's bold cherry-coffee cassis reads alongside the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the velvet-on-fat coherence runs through the plate, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|2 James Gin": "2 James with the Tomahawk — Detroit local gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|A Midnight's Winter Dram": "A Midnight's Winter Dram with the Tomahawk — rare limited-release against bone-in ribeye. Strong; boutique-craft covers the cut with rare-release character.",
  "The Tomahawk|Aberlour 16": "Aberlour 16's chocolate-cocoa character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Adictivo Cristalino": "Adictivo Cristalino with the Tomahawk — filtered aged against bone-in ribeye. Avoid; bourbon or scotch territory, even cristalino's aged depth doesn't reach.",
  "The Tomahawk|Adictivo Reposado": "Adictivo Reposado with the Tomahawk — extended-aged reposado against massive cut. Avoid; even near-añejo is bourbon-or-scotch territory here.",
  "The Tomahawk|Alexander Valley Chardonnay": "Sonoma Chardonnay against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even oaked California Chard has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Alexander Valley for salmon, chicken, lobster mac, or the bisque course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Alexander Valley Cyrus": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Alexander Valley Homestead Red": "Alexander Valley Homestead Red's coffee-mocha tannin finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Amaro Nonino": "Amaro Nonino's crisp orange-peel-grappa sweetness underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Amaro Nonino belongs on another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Ancho Reyes": "Ancho Reyes' sweet liqueur character flattens the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Ancho Reyes lands on another course — not here.",
  "The Tomahawk|Angel's Envy": "Angel's Envy's crisp vanilla character lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Angel's Envy Cask Strength": "Angel's Envy Cask Strength's cask-strength juniper-maple character finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the juniper sharpens the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Angel's Envy Triple Cask": "Angel's Envy Triple Cask's spice-vanilla character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Aperol": "Aperol's crisp bitter-orange-aperitivo edge underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Aperol on another course instead.",
  "The Tomahawk|Asparagus": "The Tomahawk into the asparagus -- acid lifts the richness, the meal gets the contrast it needs. Works; safe alongside, but not the headline pick.",
  "The Tomahawk|Au Gratin Potatoes": "The Tomahawk alongside the au gratin -- the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char carries cleanly into the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust. Excellent; the headline side pairing for the Tomahawk.",
  "The Tomahawk|Austin Hope Cabernet Sauvignon": "Paso Robles Cab — ripe plum, dark cherry, mocha, warm spice, plush. The bold body can meet Tomahawk fat and char but the warmer-climate softness is rounder than the biggest marbled steak demands firm-tannin-wise. Strong Paso Cab for Tomahawk — Opus, Shafer, or Caymus peak higher for the theatrical bone-in.",
  "The Tomahawk|Averna Amaro": "Averna Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- aperitivo bitterness fights the headline cut's marbled char — bitter and savory pull apart. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Averna Amaro for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Aviation": "Floral pale-purple gin cocktail against the biggest steak on the menu — zero overlap. The Aviation's delicacy is crushed by marbled Tomahawk weight. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "The Tomahawk|Aviation Gin": "Aviation with the Tomahawk — crowd-pleasing craft against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Avion 44": "Avion 44's barrel-aged tequila weight finds neutral with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the aged-agave weight wraps the 36oz showpiece, and the cooked-agave note underlines the cut. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "The Tomahawk|Avion Silver": "Avion Silver with the Tomahawk — 80-proof blanco against bone-in ribeye. Avoid; bourbon or scotch territory.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bacardi Rum": "Bacardi Rum's crisp workhorse-clean character underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a light spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Bacardi Rum for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Baileys Irish Cream": "Baileys Irish Cream's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Baileys Irish Cream for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Baker's": "Baker's with the Tomahawk — 107-proof 7-year against bone-in ribeye. The extra proof handles the cut's scale where standard Knob Creek fades; Baker's and Four Roses Small Batch Select are the premium-small-batch Tomahawk calls. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Balvenie 12 American Oak": "Balvenie 12 American Oak's crisp char-honey character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask": "Balvenie 14 Caribbean Cask -- Speyside, rum-finished, caramel. The rum-cask depth meets the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; decennial-release complexity sits with richness.",
  "The Tomahawk|Balvenie 16 Single Barrel": "Balvenie 16 Single Barrel's First Fill American Oak leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the scotch depth settles into the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; Parker's Heritage 16th's cask-strength character threads mushrooms at annual-release register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Balvenie 21 Portwood": "Balvenie 21 Portwood's berry-juniper character lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon character meets the rendered fat, and the juniper sharpens the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bardstown Château Laboude": "Bardstown Château Laboude's vanilla character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the caramel-and-oak weight rises with the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bardstown Discovery #6": "Bardstown Discovery #6 with the Tomahawk — Discovery blend against bone-in ribeye. Strong; craft-blender complexity covers the cut but Blanton's Gold or E.H. Taylor bring more focused authority.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bardstown Founders KBS": "Bardstown Founders KBS with the Tomahawk — KBS-finished against bone-in ribeye. Strong; the stout-barrel character handles the cut with distinctive beer-barrel authority.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bardstown Foursquare": "Bardstown Foursquare with the Tomahawk — Foursquare rum-cask against 32oz cap-fat. Strong; Bardstown Foursquare's tropical character handles the Tomahawk at Caribbean-finish register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bardstown Fusion #7": "Bardstown Fusion #7 with the Tomahawk — Fusion blend against bone-in ribeye. Strong; own-plus-sourced covers the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel": "Bardstown Rishi's Single Barrel with the Tomahawk — Rishi's-picked single-barrel against bone-in ribeye. Strong; curated character covers the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Basil Hayden's": "Basil Hayden's' oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the brown-spirit warmth threads the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla balances the char-and-fat. Strong; regional character covers both cuts.",
  "The Tomahawk|Basil Hayden's 10 Year": "Basil Hayden's 10 Year's crisp char-juniper character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Basil Hayden's Malted Rye": "Basil Hayden's Malted Rye's crisp char-earth character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask": "Basil Hayden's Red Wine Cask's crisp oak character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the caramel-and-oak weight rises with the cap-fat, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Basil Hayden's Toast": "Basil Hayden's Toast's crisp caramel-smoke character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon character meets the rendered fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bee's Knees": "Light three-ingredient gin cocktail against the biggest steak on the menu — complete mismatch. The Bee's Knees has no weight to handle marbled tomahawk fat. Never pair — route Tomahawk to heavy Cabs, Barolo, or spirit-forward bourbon cocktails.",
  "The Tomahawk|Beefeater Gin": "Beefeater with the Tomahawk — 40% against 32oz bone-in. Avoid; gin has no answer for cap-fat intensity. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Beignets": "After the Tomahawk, the beignets -- the showpiece cut yields to warm fried choux and powdered sugar, the dipping sauce closing the meal cleanly. Works; the table closes light after the table-defining main.",
  "The Tomahawk|Belvedere Vodka": "Belvedere Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Belvedere Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "The Tomahawk|Blade & Bow": "Blade & Bow's caramel-honey character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the caramel-and-oak weight rises with the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Blanton's Gold": "Blanton's Gold with the Tomahawk — 103-proof export Blanton's against the bone-in ribeye. The richer profile and extra proof earn this cut where Blanton's Original is slightly outmatched; a premium single-barrel Tomahawk pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Blanton's Original": "Blanton's Original with the Tomahawk — 93-proof single-barrel against the bone-in ribeye. Strong; the scale is slightly ambitious for Blanton's Original — Blanton's Gold's 103 proof or E.H. Taylor's BIB weight are the stronger Tomahawk calls.",
  "The Tomahawk|Blanton's Straight from the Barrel": "Blanton's SfB with the Tomahawk — export cask-strength Blanton's against 32oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Blanton's SfB handles the Tomahawk with distinctive honey-vanilla-citrus character at 130+ proof.",
  "The Tomahawk|Blood Oath Pact 10": "Blood Oath Pact 10 with the Tomahawk — 10th-Pact milestone against bone-in ribeye. Strong; the decennial release covers the cut with milestone-collector character.",
  "The Tomahawk|Blood Oath Pact 8": "Blood Oath Pact 8 with the Tomahawk — Pact 8 against bone-in ribeye. Strong; Pact 8 annual release covers the cut with master-blender character.",
  "The Tomahawk|Blood Oath Pact 9": "Blood Oath Pact 9 with the Tomahawk — Pact 9 against bone-in ribeye. Strong; annual-release wine-cask covers the cut with master-blender character.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bluecoat Gin": "Bluecoat with the Tomahawk — citrus-forward craft against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bodega Noemia A Lisa": "Bodega Noemia A Lisa's bold earth-floral cassis lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the velvet-on-fat coherence runs through the plate, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bombay Dry Gin": "Bombay Dry with the Tomahawk — juniper-assertive against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bombay Sapphire Gin": "Bombay Sapphire with the Tomahawk — softer floral gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bone Marrow": "The Tomahawk preceded by the bone marrow -- the 36oz showpiece meets cleanly the beefy unctuous opener, and the smoke frames the herb butter. Excellent; the starter sets up the cut at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Booker's": "Booker's with the Tomahawk — Beam cask-strength 125-130 proof against 32oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Booker's is the featured barrel-proof-Tomahawk match — Booker Noe's original cask-strength expression was designed for exactly this kind of intensity meeting.",
  "The Tomahawk|Botanist Gin": "Botanist with the Tomahawk — Islay craft gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bowdie's Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, rum-infused cherries, orange, bitters, large cube. The house Old Fashioned's weight and vanilla-caramel depth stands up to the Tomahawk's size and marbling; brûléed orange adds smoky nose to the crust char. Peak cocktail for the table's statement cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark": "Bowdie's Private Select — Maker's Mark's char-oak character finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bowman Brothers Small Batch": "Bowman Brothers Small Batch with the Tomahawk — 90-proof Virginia small-batch against bone-in ribeye. Strong; cut's scale pushes against the smooth balance — Baker's, Four Roses Small Batch Select, or E.H. Taylor are stronger Tomahawk calls.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bowmore 12": "Bowmore 12 with the Tomahawk — the Tomahawk's scale pushes past Bowmore's gentler peat, so the pairing is capable but not transcendent. Guests who want Bowmore with a big cut will still enjoy this; for max-impact, reach for Lagavulin 8 or Laphroaig 10. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Braida Montebruna Barbera": "Braida Montebruna Barbera's cherry-earth tannin holds with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Tomahawk|Brandlin Estate Cabernet Sauvignon": "Mount Veeder mountain Cab — dark blackberry, graphite, earth, firm age-worthy tannin. The dense mountain-fruit body meets Tomahawk fat and char directly and the graphite-earth frames the theatrical bone-in cut. Peak mountain Cab for Tomahawk — Mount Veeder structure stands up to the biggest marbled steak on the menu. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Broccoli Cheddar": "The Tomahawk into the broccoli cheddar -- cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "The Tomahawk|Broccolini": "The Tomahawk with the broccolini -- acid plays against richness, the contrast holds across the courses. Works; the side carries cleanly with the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bruichladdich": "Bruichladdich's progressive unpeated Islay underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- the bottle's register lands at a lower tier than the cut deserves. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a scotch. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Bruichladdich on another course instead.",
  "The Tomahawk|Brussels and Belly": "The Tomahawk alongside Brussels + Belly -- the 36oz long-bone showpiece's theatrical char meets the maple-glazed pork belly, the bone-in smoke matching the glaze's sweet-savory edge. Strong; the side holds the table's theater.",
  "The Tomahawk|Buffalo Trace": "Buffalo Trace's caramel-workhorse character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Buffalo Trace Cream": "Buffalo Trace Cream's sweet liqueur character flattens the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Buffalo Trace Cream for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Bulleit Bourbon": "Bulleit Bourbon's bold spice-vanilla character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Burrata": "The Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy: the smoky bone-in weight crowds out the burrata, and the marbled char fights the milky cheese plate. Avoid; the courses can't share a meal cleanly. Pair the burrata with Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc on a different visit; pour Opus One, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or George T. Stagg alongside the Tomahawk.",
  "The Tomahawk|Butternut Squash": "The Tomahawk into the butternut squash -- savory gives way to sweet, the close lands cleanly. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "The Tomahawk|Cade Cabernet Sauvignon": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Camarena Tequila": "Camarena Tequila with the Tomahawk — value reposado against massive cut. Avoid; demands bourbon or scotch.",
  "The Tomahawk|Campari": "Campari's aperitivo at its most textbook clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Campari belongs on another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Canadian Club": "Canadian Club's workhorse Canadian blend leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the easygoing Canadian character handles the 36oz showpiece, and the light caramel edge frames the cut. Works; annual-release story wasted on salad.",
  "The Tomahawk|Canadian Club 43 Year": "Canadian Club 43 Year's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the blended whisky body wraps the 36oz showpiece, and the easy Canadian register softens the cut. Works; the complex profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "The Tomahawk|Captain Morgan Rum": "Captain Morgan Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a light spirit. Avoid; the better calls are above. Captain Morgan Rum lands on another course — not here.",
  "The Tomahawk|Caribou Crossing": "Caribou Crossing's Canadian whisky character sits alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the blended whisky body wraps the 36oz showpiece, and the light-grain note brightens the cut. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "The Tomahawk|Carrot Cake": "The Tomahawk into the carrot cake -- the smoky bone-in marbling hands off to spiced cake and cream-cheese frosting, walnut crunch on the close. Works; the showpiece cut and the spiced cake share the meal at separate registers.",
  "The Tomahawk|Casa LBV Port": "Never pair Casa LBV with Tomahawk — entry-port buries the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and residual sugar clashes with smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. LBV is a dessert pour, not a steak wine.",
  "The Tomahawk|Casamigos Mezcal": "The Tomahawk: 36oz bone-on showpiece meets Diageo-backed mezcal polish. Excellent; Casamigos Mezcal's gateway-smoke energy works the dramatic cut at celebrity-brand register — approachable enough to round the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Rutherford Cab — concentrated black fruit, cocoa, mocha, velvety tannin, long warming finish. The opulent body can meet Tomahawk fat and char but the velvety profile is rounder than the biggest marbled steak demands. Strong Caymus for Tomahawk — Opus, Shafer, or Heitz Martha's peak higher for theatrical bone-in.",
  "The Tomahawk|Chambord": "Chambord's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; the better calls are above. Chambord lands on another course — not here.",
  "The Tomahawk|Cheesecake": "The Tomahawk closes with the cheesecake -- the smoky bone-in marbling hands off to dense cream-cheese custard, graham crust holding the dairy weight. Works; the table closes heavy after the heaviest steak, but it carries.",
  "The Tomahawk|Chocolate Brownie": "The Tomahawk closes with the warm fudge brownie -- the smoky char-and-fat yields to dense cocoa fudge, warm vanilla over the top. Excellent; the showpiece into the canonical chocolate close, the meal lands at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Chocolate Cake": "After the Tomahawk, the chocolate cake -- the 36oz long-bone showpiece hands off to layered cocoa-and-ganache, the vanilla scoop closing the table. Excellent; the headline cut earns the layered chocolate close.",
  "The Tomahawk|Chopin Vodka": "Chopin Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Chopin Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "The Tomahawk|Christian Bros Brandy": "Christian Bros Brandy's crisp brandy-caramel character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Château Batailley": "Château Batailley's bold leather-tobacco cassis holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the Cab structure carries the bone-in cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Château Beaucastel": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Château Gombaude-Guillot": "Château Gombaude-Guillot's bold coffee-mocha cassis holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the concentrated tannin grips the marbled cut. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Château Haut Segottes": "Château Haut Segottes's cherry-earth tannin sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Château de Rouillac": "Château de Rouillac's leather-tobacco tannin holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Clam Chowder": "The Tomahawk with the chowder alongside -- two rich courses on the same plate, the table earns its substance. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Clase Azul Anejo": "Clase Azul Anejo: ceramic-decanter añejo, 25 months American oak + 2 months sherry butts. Excellent on the Tomahawk; luxury-icon character matches showpiece cut at ceremonial register — save the bottle.",
  "The Tomahawk|Clase Azul Durango": "Clase Azul Durango's aged-agave character reads alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the añejo register carries the 36oz showpiece, and the caramel-agave register brightens the cut. Works; Durango presentation slightly over-specified.",
  "The Tomahawk|Clase Azul Gold": "Clase Azul Gold's aged-agave character meets at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the 36oz showpiece, and the soft-spice note softens the cut. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal": "The Tomahawk: 36oz bone-on showpiece meets Clase Azul Guerrero Mezcal. Excellent; the ceramic-decanter ceremony matches the dramatic cut — both are occasions, both are plated visually, both are built to be noticed.",
  "The Tomahawk|Clase Azul Plata": "Clase Azul Plata with the Tomahawk — ultra-premium blanco against bone-in ribeye. Avoid; save for a dish that matches presentation.",
  "The Tomahawk|Clase Azul Reposado": "Clase Azul Reposado's ceramic-icon reposado character underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- this expression lands short of the cut's register. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Clase Azul Reposado for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Clase Azul Ultra": "Clase Azul Ultra: ultra-premium 5-year extra añejo, hand-painted platinum-accented decanter. Excellent on the Tomahawk; ceremonial agave meets ceremonial beef at luxury-icon register — save the bottle. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Clermont Steep": "Clermont Steep's crisp caramel-char character frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Clos Petit Ona Grenache": "Clos Petit Ona Grenache's char-earth tannin plays cleanly against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Codigo 1530 Rosa": "Codigo 1530 Rosa with the Tomahawk — Napa Cab-cask reposado against massive cut. Avoid; rosa reposados aren't Tomahawk territory.",
  "The Tomahawk|Contratto Vermouth": "Contratto Vermouth's vermouth-wine-base sweetness reads alongside the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bitter herb edge cuts the cap-fat richness. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "The Tomahawk|Corazon Blanco": "Corazon Blanco with the Tomahawk — single-estate blanco against bone-in ribeye. Avoid; bourbon or scotch territory.",
  "The Tomahawk|Corazon Reposado": "Corazon Reposado with the Tomahawk — 80-proof highland reposado against massive cut. Avoid; the Tomahawk demands bourbon or scotch.",
  "The Tomahawk|Corazon Sazerac": "Corazon Sazerac's agave-pepper character lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Corazon Stagg": "★ Corazon Stagg's bold agave-caramel character meets head-on the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-tequila depth grips the marbled cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "The Tomahawk|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's agave-char character frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's bold floral cassis matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the concentrated tannin grips the marbled cut, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Corpse Reviver": "Classical cocktail against the biggest steak on the menu — complete mismatch. The Corpse Reviver's delicate elegance is obliterated by marbled Tomahawk weight. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "The Tomahawk|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier Cognac's crisp cognac-oak character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Crab Cake": "The Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust: the marbled char overshadows the sweet shellfish plate, and the smoky bone-in weight crowds out the delicate crab cake. Avoid; the courses can't share a meal cleanly. Pair the crab cake with Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin on a different visit; pour Opus One, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or George T. Stagg alongside the Tomahawk.",
  "The Tomahawk|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12's bold char-floral character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Creamed Spinach": "The Tomahawk with the creamed spinach -- the 36oz showpiece's theatrical bone-in marbling meets the rich bechamel-laced greens, the cream catching the rendered fat the long-bone delivers. Strong; the side anchors the spectacle.",
  "The Tomahawk|Creamy Potato": "The Tomahawk preceded by the potato soup -- cream and protein share the meal, the build reads full. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Creme Brulee": "After the Tomahawk, the brulee -- the 36oz showpiece yields to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries brightening the close. Strong; the theatrical steak earns the steakhouse classic close.",
  "The Tomahawk|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Willamette Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — graceful low-tannin Oregon Pinot has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Cristom for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness sits alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the rye-blended Canadian body sits with the 36oz showpiece, and the light caramel edge frames the cut. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "The Tomahawk|Cucumber Gimlet": "Garden-fresh gin cocktail against the biggest steak on the menu — zero structural overlap. The Cucumber Gimlet has no weight to handle marbled tomahawk fat. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or heavy bourbon cocktails.",
  "The Tomahawk|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12's crisp chocolate-citrus character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Loire red Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — light-body low-tannin Sancerre has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold reds; keep Sancerre Rouge for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's cherry-floral tannin matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silverado Trail Cab — blackcurrant, dark plum, mocha, spice, rich plush finish. The opulent body meets Tomahawk fat and char directly and the mocha-spice frames the theatrical bone-in cut. Peak opulent Napa Cab for Tomahawk — Persian-inspired estate story plus plush structure for the biggest marbled steak. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin with the Tomahawk — Detroit craft gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City Vodka's hometown craft vodka underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Detroit City Vodka for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15's honey-sherry character lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label's Highland-scotch character rests at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the 90-proof balance covers both cuts cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character flattens the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Disaronno lands on another course — not here.",
  "The Tomahawk|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the rye-and-oak weight handles the 36oz showpiece, and the dry-spice register brightens the cut. Strong; boutique character covers both cuts.",
  "The Tomahawk|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's high-proof pour register sits alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the dense-spirit weight carries the 36oz showpiece, and the heavy-pour register grips the cut. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "The Tomahawk|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Willamette flagship Pinot Blend against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — silky low-tannin Pinot has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Grand Cheval for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Domaine Tempier Bandol": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé": "Serious Loire Sauv Blanc against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even flinty Pouilly-Fumé structure can't meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Pouilly-Fumé for scallops, crab cake, bisque, or escargot.",
  "The Tomahawk|Domaine du Grand Tinel": "Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rhône GSM blend — Grand Tinel, ripe red fruit, black pepper, garrigue, herbal earth, full body, medium tannin. The full body handles Tomahawk's massive 36oz bone-in and the black pepper plays with smoky char. Strong Châteauneuf for Tomahawk — Scavino Barolo edges higher on the grand cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Don Fulano Anejo": "Don Fulano Anejo's agave-apricot character finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Don Fulano Blanco": "Don Fulano Blanco with the Tomahawk — premium blanco against bone-in ribeye. Avoid; bourbon or scotch territory.",
  "The Tomahawk|Don Fulano Reposado": "Don Fulano Reposado with the Tomahawk — 80-proof highland reposado against massive cut. Avoid; Don Fulano's refinement is wasted here.",
  "The Tomahawk|Don Julio 1942": "Don Julio 1942's icon extra-añejo character finds neutral with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the cooked-agave-and-oak body matches the 36oz showpiece, and the cooked-agave note underlines the cut. Works; the 70th's clarity gets overwhelmed by the dish's density.",
  "The Tomahawk|Don Julio 70th": "Don Julio 70th with the Tomahawk — filtered reposado against massive cut. Avoid; the 70th's refinement is wasted and overwhelmed.",
  "The Tomahawk|Don Julio Blanco": "Don Julio Blanco with the Tomahawk — 80-proof blanco against bone-in ribeye. Avoid; the cut demands bourbon, scotch, or a premium aged reposado.",
  "The Tomahawk|Don Julio Reposado": "Don Julio Reposado with the Tomahawk — 80-proof reposado against the bone-in ribeye's scale. Avoid; the cut overwhelms any reposado. Bourbon or scotch territory.",
  "The Tomahawk|Drambuie": "Drambuie's sweet liqueur character flattens the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Drambuie on another course instead.",
  "The Tomahawk|Dunville's Single Malt": "Dunville's Single Malt with the Tomahawk — Irish single malt against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; Irish whiskey is too light for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|E.H. Taylor Full Proof": "E.H. Taylor Full Proof with the Tomahawk — BT BiB 100-proof against 32oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; EHT Full Proof handles the Tomahawk with historic Kentucky-bourbon register — where Staggs bring 130-proof intensity, EHT brings 100-proof BiB tradition.",
  "The Tomahawk|E.H. Taylor Rye": "E.H. Taylor Rye's rye-spice character meets at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the rye-and-oak weight handles the 36oz showpiece, and the rye-grain bite cuts the cut. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "The Tomahawk|E.H. Taylor Small Batch": "E.H. Taylor Small Batch with the Tomahawk — 100-proof BIB against bone-in ribeye. Strong; the bonded weight handles the cut's scale where 90-proof single-barrels fade — E.H. Taylor and Blanton's Gold are the two bigger-impact BT calls for the Tomahawk.",
  "The Tomahawk|Eagle Rare 10 Year": "Eagle Rare 10 Year's aged-oak character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Eastern Kille Dry Gin": "Eastern Kille with the Tomahawk — GR craft against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|El Cabo": "El Cabo with the Tomahawk — craft reposado against massive cut. Avoid; the cut demands bourbon or scotch.",
  "The Tomahawk|El Mayor Extra Anejo": "El Mayor Extra Anejo's cooked-agave-and-oak register sits alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the añejo register carries the 36oz showpiece, and the cooked-agave note underlines the cut. Strong; agave cuts the dairy at the accessible register.",
  "The Tomahawk|El Mayor Reposado": "El Mayor Reposado with the Tomahawk — value reposado against massive cut. Avoid; demands bourbon or scotch.",
  "The Tomahawk|Elephant Gin": "Elephant Gin with the Tomahawk — craft gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Elijah Craig 18 Year": "Elijah Craig 18 with the Tomahawk — HH aged single-barrel against 32oz cap-fat. Works; EC 18's aged character is under-specified for Tomahawk intensity; reach for a barrel-proof bourbon.",
  "The Tomahawk|Elijah Craig Private Barrel": "Elijah Craig Private Barrel with the Tomahawk — 94-proof house-pick against bone-in ribeye. Strong; the single-barrel intensity handles the cut but Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB's bonded weight is the Heaven Hill-side Tomahawk call.",
  "The Tomahawk|Elijah Craig Single Barrel": "Elijah Craig Single Barrel with the Tomahawk — 94-proof single-barrel against bone-in ribeye. Strong; the cut's scale pushes against 94-proof — Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB or E.H. Taylor handle Tomahawk more fully.",
  "The Tomahawk|Elijah Craig Small Batch": "Elijah Craig Small Batch's caramel-char character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Elijah Craig Straight Rye": "Elijah Craig Straight Rye's rye-spice character leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the rye backbone drives the 36oz showpiece, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the cut. Strong; the 10-year polish covers both cuts with small-batch refinement.",
  "The Tomahawk|Elk Cove Five Mountain": "Willamette Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — medium-tannin Oregon Pinot still has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Elk Cove for the filet, salmon, or chicken course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Elk Cove Pinot Blanc": "Willamette Pinot Blanc against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — delicate pear-peach-floral has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Elk Cove for crab cake, scallops, escargot, or the bisque.",
  "The Tomahawk|Elmer T. Lee": "Elmer T. Lee with the Tomahawk — 90-proof single-barrel against bone-in ribeye. Strong; scale pushes past Elmer T's balance — E.H. Taylor or Blanton's Gold are bigger Tomahawk calls.",
  "The Tomahawk|Empress 1908 Gin": "Empress 1908 with the Tomahawk — color-changing craft against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Epoch Ingenuity": "Paso Robles Rhône blend — Epoch Ingenuity, Templeton Gap Syrah-Grenache-Mourvèdre, blackberry, smoked meat, olive, crushed pepper, full body, medium tannin. The full body handles Tomahawk's massive 36oz bone-in and the smoked meat plays with char. Strong Paso-Rhône for Tomahawk — a firmer-tannin Cab edges higher on the grand cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Escargot": "The Tomahawk after the escargot -- the savory course rounds the protein, the meal reads layered. Works; the opener sits cleanly before the steak.",
  "The Tomahawk|Espresso Martini": "Espresso Martini's roasted-coffee martini richness mis-pairs with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Espresso Martini for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Espresso Old Fashioned": "Buffalo Trace, bourbon cream, Frangelico, espresso, brown sugar, Angostura. The coffee-forward cocktail and Frangelico's hazelnut warmth meet the Tomahawk's char and marbled fat; brown sugar ties into the crust. Peak cocktail for guests wanting dessert-leaning spirit with the biggest cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Evening Land Seven Springs": "Willamette Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — savory Eola-Amity Pinot has some grip but not enough for the cut's fat-and-char demand. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Evening Land for filet, salmon, or tartare.",
  "The Tomahawk|Far Niente Cabernet Sauvignon": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A confident choice — steakhouse symmetry. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Far Niente Chardonnay": "Far Niente opulent Chardonnay against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even Napa weight is the wrong axis for Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Far Niente for scallops, lobster mac, salmon, or the chicken course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi": "Super Tuscan — Le Pupille Saffredi Maremma, Cab-Merlot-Petit Verdot, 2021, blackcurrant, tobacco, graphite, Mediterranean herbs, firm-tannin finish. The tobacco plays with the 36oz bone-in's smoky-char and firm tannin handles the grand cut's weight. Strong Super Tuscan for Tomahawk — Bordeaux-style Italian on the theatrical presentation.",
  "The Tomahawk|Faust Napa Valley Cabernet": "Faust Napa Valley Cabernet's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the velvety body settles into the flame-grilled char, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Fento Ollo de Sapo Mencía": "Never pair Fento Mencía with Tomahawk — light body and high acid get bulldozed by the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and smoky char, leaving the wine tasting thin. Lead with Scavino Barolo's firm tannin or Tempier Bandol's cult-producer density instead.",
  "The Tomahawk|Fernet Branca": "Fernet Branca's nuclear bitter-menthol intensity leans against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bitter herb edge cuts the cap-fat richness. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "The Tomahawk|Fernet Menta": "Fernet Menta's bittersweet rhubarb-and-orange edge leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the herbal cut works the 36oz showpiece, and the amaro backbone frames the cut. Works; the side sits without pulling focus.",
  "The Tomahawk|Fireball Whisky": "Fireball Whisky's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Fireball Whisky belongs on another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Fisher Unity Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — silky California Pinot has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Fisher Unity for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Four Roses Small Batch Select": "The Tomahawk: bone-in ribeye meets 104-proof 6-recipe blend. Extra proof earns the cut where Four Roses Yellow Label can't reach; Small Batch Select and Baker's are premium-small-batch Tomahawk calls. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Four Roses Yellow Label": "Four Roses Yellow Label's crisp caramel-floral character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the caramel-and-oak weight rises with the cap-fat, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Fox & Oden": "Fox & Oden with the Tomahawk — craft small-batch against bone-in ribeye. Strong; the boutique character is capable but Baker's, Four Roses Small Batch Select, or E.H. Taylor bring more Tomahawk grip.",
  "The Tomahawk|Fox & Oden Double Oaked": "Fox & Oden Double Oaked's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bourbon weight handles the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla underlines the rendered char. Strong; Sinatra Select's grooved-barrel character frames the two-cut plate at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Frangelico": "Frangelico's Piedmont hazelnut depth to mocha at after-dinner register clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Frangelico lands on another course — not here.",
  "The Tomahawk|Freemark Abbey Bosché": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|French 75": "Champagne-topped gin cocktail in a flute against the biggest steak on the menu — zero overlap. The cocktail's delicacy is annihilated by the Tomahawk's marbled weight. Never pair — Tomahawk demands Cab, Barolo, or a spirit-forward bourbon cocktail.",
  "The Tomahawk|French Onion": "The Tomahawk with the French onion alongside -- the marbled char anchors against the beef-broth bowl, and the smoke threads the broth. Strong; the opener carries its register against the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Frias Block 5": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo": "G.D. Vajra Albe Nebbiolo's sweet char-cherry tannin matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the sweet character meets the cap-fat at digestif register. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti": "Piedmont sweet sparkling against Bowdie's 35-oz theatrical ribeye — dessert-level sugar and low alcohol have no weight to meet the smoky-beefy signature cut. Never pair. Route Moscato to dessert course; take the Tomahawk with Caymus, Opus One, or Scavino Barolo.",
  "The Tomahawk|G4 Blanco": "G4 Blanco with the Tomahawk — 80-proof craft blanco against bone-in ribeye. Avoid; bourbon or scotch territory.",
  "The Tomahawk|G4 High Proof": "G4 High Proof with the Tomahawk — 104-proof reposado against massive cut. Avoid; even the highest-proof reposado is bourbon-or-scotch territory for this cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Garavogue Irish Whiskey": "Garavogue with the Tomahawk — craft Irish against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; Irish whiskey is too light for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|George T. Stagg": "★ George T. Stagg's cask-strength barrel-proof-leather character is the lock for the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat. Gold standard; recommend without hesitation.",
  "The Tomahawk|Ghost Block Cabernet Sauvignon": "Ghost Block — Oakville Cab with dense blackcurrant, dark cherry, graphite, toasted oak, firm tannin. The structured body meets Tomahawk fat and char and the graphite-oak frames the theatrical bone-in cut. Peak Oakville Cab for Tomahawk — Napa's golden strip structure stands up to the biggest marbled steak. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Ghost Block Zinfandel": "Napa Oakville Zinfandel — Ghost Block, ripe jammy fruit, blackberry preserves, cracked pepper, full body, medium tannin. The full body handles Tomahawk's massive 36oz bone-in and the cracked pepper plays with smoky char. Strong California-Zin for Tomahawk — a firmer-tannin Cab edges higher on the grand cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Glenfiddich 12": "Glenfiddich 12 -- entry Speyside, apple-pear, ex-bourbon-cask. The bourbon-cask apple lifts the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; composed premium match.",
  "The Tomahawk|Glenfiddich 14": "Glenfiddich 14's char-cinnamon character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Glenfiddich 18": "Glenfiddich 18's caramel-cinnamon character finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23": "Glenfiddich Gran Cru 23's bold almond-apple character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Glenglassaugh 12": "Glenglassaugh 12's Highland-scotch character reads register-aligned with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the heather-and-honey weight sits with the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; aged premium at matched register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Glenlivet 12": "Glenlivet 12 -- entry Speyside, pear, honey. The bourbon-cask pear lifts the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; premium aged depth sits with richness.",
  "The Tomahawk|Glenmorangie 10": "Glenmorangie 10 -- Highland entry, tallest stills, honeysuckle-vanilla. The scotch depth settles into the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the 103-proof sits with the richness at the bolder register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Glenmorangie 18": "Glenmorangie 18's extra-mature-Highland character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Graham's 10 Year Tawny": "Never pair Graham's 10 Year Tawny with Tomahawk — sweet oxidative port buries the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and the residual sugar clashes with smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. Tawny is a finishing pour, not a steak wine.",
  "The Tomahawk|Graham's 20 Year Tawny": "Never pair Graham's 20 Year Tawny with Tomahawk — sweet step-up tawny buries the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and smoky char clashes with twenty-year concentrated fruit. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf. Tawny is a finishing pour.",
  "The Tomahawk|Graham's 2017 Vintage Port": "Never pair Graham's 2017 Vintage Port with Tomahawk — powerful bottle-aged structure buries the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and the residual sugar fights smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. Vintage Port lives at dessert.",
  "The Tomahawk|Gran Patron Platinum": "Gran Patron Platinum's unaged blanco-tequila lift with agave-citrus snap underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Gran Patron Platinum lands on another course — not here.",
  "The Tomahawk|Grand Marnier": "Grand Marnier's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Grand Marnier for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Gray Whale Gin": "Gray Whale with the Tomahawk — California ocean-craft against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Green Chartreuse": "Green Chartreuse's cask-strength herbal-high-proof sweetness holds with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the sweet character meets the cap-fat at digestif register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Tomahawk|Grey Goose Vodka": "Grey Goose Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Grey Goose Vodka for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Grilled Caesar": "The Tomahawk into the Caesar -- the marbled char anchors against the anchovy-Caesar plate, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Gumbo": "The Tomahawk with the gumbo alongside -- the spiced register breaks the weight, the table reads round. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet": "Gundlach Bundschu Cabernet's structured dark-fruit weight meets at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the dark-fruit body sits with the 36oz showpiece, and the blackberry edge underlines the cut. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|HDW 100 Vodka": "HDW 100 Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the HDW 100 Vodka for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Hakushu 12 Year": "Hakushu 12 with the Tomahawk — Japanese Alps single malt against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; Japanese whisky's delicate character is wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel": "Hancock's Reserve Single Barrel with the Tomahawk — 88.9-proof BT single-barrel against bone-in ribeye. Strong; scale pushes against the pour — Blanton's Gold, E.H. Taylor, or Henry McKenna 10 BIB are stronger Tomahawk calls.",
  "The Tomahawk|Hangar 1 Vodka": "Hangar 1 Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Hangar 1 Vodka on another course instead.",
  "The Tomahawk|Head Fake": "Sweet-and-citrus lemon-drop cocktail against the biggest steak on the menu — zero structural overlap. Head Fake's delicacy is crushed by marbled Tomahawk weight. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "The Tomahawk|Heaven Hill 20 Year": "Heaven Hill 20 Year with the Tomahawk — HH 20yr collector against 32oz cap-fat. Works; HH 20's aged character is under-specified for Tomahawk intensity; reach for a barrel-proof bourbon.",
  "The Tomahawk|Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond": "Heaven Hill Bottled in Bond's bold caramel-rye-spice character lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the caramel-and-oak weight rises with the cap-fat, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Heaven's Door 10 Year": "Heaven's Door 10 Year with the Tomahawk — 100-proof 10-year against bone-in ribeye. Strong; the aged bonded weight handles the cut — Henry McKenna 10 BIB, E.H. Taylor, or Baker's bring similar authority with different distillery stories.",
  "The Tomahawk|Heitz Martha's Vineyard": "The wine's signature eucalyptus and cedar cut through the thick intramuscular fat of the tomahawk the way fresh herb does in a compound butter, providing aromatic contrast to the beef's Maillard-rich crust. On the palate, the fat coats and softens the tannins while the wine's menthol lift keeps each bite from feeling heavy, turning a powerful steak into something more elegant. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Heitz Trailside Vineyard": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Hendricks Flora Adora": "Flora Adora with the Tomahawk — lavish-floral Hendrick's against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Hendricks Gin": "Hendricks with the Tomahawk — flagship gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Hendricks Grand Cabaret": "Grand Cabaret with the Tomahawk — absinthe-style Hendrick's against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Hendricks Neptunia": "Neptunia with the Tomahawk — bladderwrack-maritime Hendrick's against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Hendricks Orbium": "Orbium with the Tomahawk — bitter-tonic Hendrick's against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Hennessy Cognac": "Hennessy Cognac -- VS/VSOP cognac, dried-fruit, vanilla. The eau-de-vie body carries the 36oz showpiece, and the dried-fruit note underlines the cut. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Henry McKenna 10 Year Bottled in Bond": "Henry McKenna 10 Year BIB with the Tomahawk — 100-proof 10-year BIB against bone-in ribeye. The bonded weight and aging earn the cut where lighter single-barrels fade; the Heaven Hill-side Tomahawk call alongside E.H. Taylor (BT). Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Hibiki Japanese Harmony": "Hibiki Harmony with the Tomahawk — refined Japanese blend against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; Japanese whisky's delicate character is wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Honey Roasted Carrots": "The Tomahawk alongside the honey carrots -- the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char meets the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: savory gives way to sweet, the close lands cleanly. Works; the side reads as a measured plate companion.",
  "The Tomahawk|House Wedge": "The Tomahawk into the wedge -- the 36oz showpiece matches the classic wedge. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|I.W. Harper 15 Year": "I.W. Harper 15 with the Tomahawk — BT-distilled 15yr against 32oz cap-fat. Works; IW Harper 15's aged character is under-specified for Tomahawk intensity; reach for a barrel-proof bourbon.",
  "The Tomahawk|Il Colle Prosecco Superiore": "Light floral Prosecco against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — zero structural overlap. Glera has no weight to meet rendered Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward bourbon, and save the Prosecco for openers or brunch service.",
  "The Tomahawk|Inhibited": "Inhibited's weight and chocolate drizzle mis-pairs with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- the cocktail register is wrong for this cut — try a different pour. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Inhibited belongs on another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Inverroche Gin": "Inverroche with the Tomahawk — Cape fynbos gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Iron Fish Gin": "Iron Fish with the Tomahawk — farm-distilled gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Ironfish Bourbon in Mezcal Casks": "Ironfish Bourbon Mezcal Casks with the Tomahawk — Iron Fish MI mezcal-cask against 32oz cap-fat. Strong; Ironfish Mezcal handles the Tomahawk with distinctive smoky-agave character.",
  "The Tomahawk|Isaac Bowman Port Finish": "Isaac Bowman Port Finish's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth rests at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bourbon body sits with the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla underlines the rendered char. Strong; the premium character sits with the side at matched register.",
  "The Tomahawk|J Davies Ferrington Vineyards": "Anderson Valley Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — concentrated Mendocino Pinot still falls short of the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep J Davies Ferrington for filet, chicken, or salmon.",
  "The Tomahawk|J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon": "J. Davies Cabernet Sauvignon's bold cherry-juniper cassis finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the opulent body meets the cap-fat, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jack Daniel's 12 Year": "Jack Daniel's 12 Year's bold caramel-char character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jack Daniel's Bonded": "Jack Daniel's Bonded's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth rests at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bourbon weight handles the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla rounds the flame-grilled char. Strong; accessible dessert pairing at the entry register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jack Daniel's Old No. 7": "Jack Daniel's Old No. 7's crisp caramel-char character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select": "Jack Daniel's Sinatra Select's bold char-malt character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jameson Irish Whiskey": "Jameson with the Tomahawk — smooth Irish against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; Irish whiskey is too light for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jax Calesa Vineyard Pinot Noir": "Russian River Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — smooth low-tannin Pinot has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Jax Calesa for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis": "Grossot Chablis against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — austere unoaked precision has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Chablis for scallops, escargot, crab cake, bisque, or seafood tower.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E.": "Jefferson's Ocean Marian McLain L.E. with the Tomahawk — limited-edition ocean-finish against 32oz cap-fat. Strong; Marian McLain L.E. handles the Tomahawk at collector register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23": "Jefferson's Ocean Voyage 23 with the Tomahawk — Voyage #23 sea-aged against 32oz cap-fat. Strong; Voyage 23 handles the Tomahawk at ocean-finish register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jefferson's Reserve": "Jefferson's Reserve's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bourbon backbone carries the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla underlines the rendered char. Strong; distinctive character sits with richness at novelty register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak": "Jefferson's Reserve VR Twin Oak's bold double-oak-vanilla character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jim Beam Black Label": "Jim Beam Black Label -- aged Jim Beam, 8 years in the barrel, 86 proof. The bourbon depth settles into the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jim Beam White Label": "Jim Beam White Label's crisp caramel-vanilla character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the caramel-and-oak weight rises with the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Johnnie Walker Black": "Johnnie Walker Black's blended-smoky character frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the caramel-and-oak weight rises with the cap-fat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Johnnie Walker Blue": "Johnnie Walker Blue's Highland-scotch character reads register-aligned with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the malt-and-oak threads the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; classic match with sturdy bonded character.",
  "The Tomahawk|Johnnie Walker Red": "Johnnie Walker Red's Highland-scotch character leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the scotch depth settles into the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; collector intensity at allocated register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon": "Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon -- black cherry, cedar, silky tannins. The silky tannins wrap the 36oz showpiece, and the cherry-fruit frames the cut. Strong; reliable opener for the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Joseph Mellot Sancerre": "Loire Sancerre against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even benchmark Sauv Blanc tension has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Sancerre for scallops, tartare, crab cake, escargot, or salmon.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jubilation by Colgin": "Jubilation — Colgin cult Napa, Pritchard Hill & Howell Mountain, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violets, espresso, firm tannin, extraordinary structure. The structured body handles Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye and the espresso plays against the char. Peak cult Napa for Tomahawk — treat with reverence on the grand cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jung and Wulff Guyana": "Jung and Wulff Guyana's heavy aged-spirit weight finds neutral with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the heavy body handles the 36oz showpiece, and the deep backbone frames the cut. Works; a measured close to the meal.",
  "The Tomahawk|Jung and Wulff Trinidad": "Jung and Wulff Trinidad's heavy aged-spirit weight leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the high-proof register matches the 36oz showpiece, and the heavy-pour register grips the cut. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Kahlua": "Kahlua's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Kahlua for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Keenan Chardonnay": "Spring Mountain Chardonnay against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even mountain structure can't meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Keenan for salmon, chicken, lobster mac, scallops, or the bisque course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Keenan Mernet": "Keenan Mernet's bold cherry-earth cassis lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the Cab structure carries the bone-in cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye": "Kentucky Owl 10 Year Rye with the Tomahawk — Kentucky Owl's allocated 10-year premium rye. Distinctive branding, premium allocation tier; the KO character gives the Tomahawk a unique premium rye frame distinct from WP's Vermont approach or BT's BTAC. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Kentucky Owl Confiscated": "Kentucky Owl Confiscated with the Tomahawk — KO Confiscated against bone-in ribeye. Strong; craft-blend narrative covers the cut with KO authority.",
  "The Tomahawk|Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's": "Kentucky Owl St. Patrick's with the Tomahawk — luxury Irish crossover against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; even luxury Irish is too light for this cut. Save the collector bottle — reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône": "Kermit Lynch Côtes du Rhône's cherry-earth tannin matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Ketel One Vodka": "Ketel One Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Ketel One Vodka on another course instead.",
  "The Tomahawk|Knickerbocker Gin": "Knickerbocker with the Tomahawk — hop-influenced gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Knob Creek": "Knob Creek's bold caramel-spice character finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Knob Creek 12 Year": "Knob Creek 12 Year with the Tomahawk — 12-year 100-proof against bone-in ribeye. Strong; the aged weight handles the cut but Baker's 107-proof or Four Roses Select's 104-proof bring more grip.",
  "The Tomahawk|Knob Creek 15 Year": "Knob Creek 15 with the Tomahawk — Beam aged single-barrel against 32oz cap-fat. Works; KC 15's aged character is under-specified for Tomahawk intensity; reach for a barrel-proof bourbon.",
  "The Tomahawk|Knob Creek 18 Year": "Knob Creek 18 with the Tomahawk — 18yr Beam aged KC against 32oz cap-fat. Works; KC 18's aged character is under-specified for Tomahawk intensity; reach for a barrel-proof bourbon.",
  "The Tomahawk|Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye": "Knob Creek Bourbon/Rye's bold spice character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Knob Creek Rye": "Knob Creek Rye -- Beam's 100-proof bold rye, sibling to Knob Creek Bourbon. The rye spice cuts the 36oz showpiece, and the rye edge frames the cut. Strong; premium proof sits with richness at bold register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Komos Anejo Reserva": "Komos Anejo Reserva's rested-agave body matches register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the oak-aged tequila body threads the 36oz showpiece, and the caramel-agave register brightens the cut. Strong; the craft traditional character sits with the soup in a distinctive register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Komos Crystallino Anejo": "Komos Crystallino Anejo's aged-agave character underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- this expression lands short of the cut's register. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not an añejo tequila. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Komos Crystallino Anejo on another course instead.",
  "The Tomahawk|Komos Extra Anejo": "The Tomahawk: dramatic bone-on showpiece meets Komos Extra Anejo. Excellent; the Komos luxury-icon line's extra-añejo expression matches the tomahawk at ceremonial register — save the bottle.",
  "The Tomahawk|Komos Rosa Reposado": "Komos Rosa Reposado with the Tomahawk — luxury pink reposado against massive cut. Avoid; rosa reposados are cold-seafood and light-cuts territory.",
  "The Tomahawk|Lagavulin 8": "Lagavulin 8 with the Tomahawk — peat smoke in the dram meets grill char on the cut. The 8-year's bright phenolics carry the scale of the bone-in ribeye without feeling heavy. Aggressive, memorable; for guests who want a peated Islay with a max-impact cut but prefer balance over Laphroaig's medicinal wall. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Lail Vineyards Blueprint": "Napa Cab — Lail entry-level, polished blackcurrant, cedar, dark cherry, refined medium tannin, full body. The full body meets Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye though refined medium tannin reads rounder than firm-tannin prestige Cabs on the grand cut. Strong Napa Cab for Tomahawk — Lail pedigree on the grand scale.",
  "The Tomahawk|Lail Vineyards Daniel Cuvée": "Lail flagship multi-appellation Napa Cab — Howell/Calistoga/Oakville/Stags Leap, dark fruit, cassis, graphite, dried violet, firm tannin, full body. The firm tannin handles Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye and the graphite plays against smoky char. Peak historically-significant Napa Cab for Tomahawk — Daniel Cuvée pedigree on the grand cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Lalo Silver": "Lalo Silver with the Tomahawk — craft blanco against bone-in ribeye. Avoid; bourbon or scotch territory.",
  "The Tomahawk|Laphroaig 10": "Laphroaig 10 alongside the Tomahawk — the full weight of Laphroaig's phenolic punch matches the full weight of the cut. Seaweed and iodine cut through marbling in a way no other peated Islay touches. This is the Islay marquee with max-impact beef. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Larceny Small Batch": "Larceny Small Batch's bold caramel-honey character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon character meets the rendered fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Laurent Perrier Brut Rosé": "Silky Pinot Noir rosé Champagne against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — the Tomahawk's rendered fat and char crushes the rosé's delicate strawberry-raspberry structure. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo and save the rosé for starters.",
  "The Tomahawk|Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut": "Chardonnay-blend Champagne against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — precise lemon-apple-brioche has no weight to meet rendered Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo and save LP Le Cuvée for the opener or tartare course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Le Garenne Rosé": "Light Loire rosé against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — delicate strawberry-watermelon-floral has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Le Garenne for burrata, wedge, crab cake, scallops, or shrimp cocktail.",
  "The Tomahawk|Le Ragnaie Brunello": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Le Volte dell'Ornellaia": "Le Volte dell'Ornellaia's cherry-coffee tannin finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Lemon Lavender Gin Martini": "Floral Plymouth-gin martini against the biggest steak on the menu — complete mismatch. The cocktail's delicacy is crushed by marbled Tomahawk weight. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "The Tomahawk|Les Pallières Raciné": "Les Pallières Raciné's bold earth-mineral cassis finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the opulent body meets the cap-fat, and the mineral edge lifts the dish. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Liberator Gin": "Liberator with the Tomahawk — Valentine Distilling Michigan craft against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Licor 43": "Licor 43's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Licor 43 for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Limoncello": "Limoncello's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur is post-meal territory — wrong register for any steak course. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Limoncello belongs on another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir": "Willamette Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — low-tannin Oregon Pinot has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds with structure; keep Lingua Franca for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Little Book": "Little Book with the Tomahawk — Fred Noe annual blended whiskey against 32oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Little Book's cask-strength blended character handles Tomahawk intensity with Fred-Noe-artistic-blend distinction.",
  "The Tomahawk|Loaded Potato": "The Tomahawk with the loaded potato alongside -- two rich courses on the same plate, the table earns its substance. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Lobster Mac": "The Tomahawk alongside the lobster mac -- the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char frames the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence. Excellent; one of the strong calls on the list.",
  "The Tomahawk|Loch Lomond 20 Year": "Loch Lomond 20 Year's caramel-spice character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the caramel-and-oak weight rises with the cap-fat, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Lock Stock & Barrel Rye": "Lock Stock & Barrel Rye with the Tomahawk — sourced rye, often MGP-blended at premium age. Boutique brand sourcing premium liquid; distinct from distillery-specific ryes by blender approach. The Tomahawk gets a premium-blended rye frame. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Long Road Gin": "Long Road with the Tomahawk — GR gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Los Vecinos Mezcal": "The Tomahawk: 36oz bone-on showpiece meets Los Vecinos. Excellent; the traditional-smoke-forward character works the dramatic cut at craft register — authentic smoke on the most dramatic cut we plate.",
  "The Tomahawk|Louis XIII Cognac": "Louis XIII Cognac's luxury-icon cognac depth mis-pairs with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- an icon cognac is best as a separate occasion — pair the steak with a Cab or bourbon, then close with the cognac. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not an icon cognac. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Louis XIII Cognac for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Lucano Amaro": "Lucano Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- aperitivo bitterness fights the headline cut's marbled char — bitter and savory pull apart. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Lucano Amaro lands on another course — not here.",
  "The Tomahawk|Lunaria Coste di Moro": "Lunaria Coste di Moro's silky red-fruit lift leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the silky body carries the 36oz showpiece, and the cherry-pepper note frames the cut. Strong; the side composes cleanly with the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Macallan 12 Sherry": "Macallan 12 Sherry -- Speyside, sherry-cask, dried fruit, spice. The malt-and-oak threads the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; covers both cuts cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Macallan 18": "Macallan 18's bold sherry-dried-fruit character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the dried-fruit notes meet the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Macallan Estate": "Macallan Estate -- estate Speyside, sherry-cask, oak. The estate depth wraps the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; BIB weight cuts the bleu with premium authority.",
  "The Tomahawk|Macauley Petite Syrah": "Napa Petite Sirah — Macauley, inky color, dense powerful tannin (not Syrah), blackberry, blueberry, dark chocolate, black pepper, full body. The dense tannin handles Tomahawk's massive 36oz bone-in and the black pepper plays with smoky char. Peak Petite-Sirah — exceptional with Tomahawk per the card's call-out. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Macauley Pinot Noir": "Sonoma Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — light-body soft-tannin Pinot has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Macauley for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Macauley Stagecoach Vineyard": "Atlas Peak Cab — Stagecoach Vineyard, dark blackberry, blueberry, espresso, iron, powerful tannin. The structured body meets Tomahawk fat and char and the iron-espresso frames the theatrical bone-in cut. Peak celebrated-vineyard Cab for Tomahawk — Stagecoach's extreme diurnal shift gives structure to meet the biggest marbled steak. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Mahon Gin": "Mahón with the Tomahawk — Spanish Mediterranean gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Maker's Mark": "Maker's Mark -- wheated, red-rope, caramel. The oak depth lays under the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla underlines the rendered char. Strong; composed premium match.",
  "The Tomahawk|Maker's Mark Cellar Aged": "Maker's Mark Cellar Aged's bold wheated-cellar-aged character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon character meets the rendered fat. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "The Tomahawk|Malibu Rum": "Malibu Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- light spirit lands short of any prime cut. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a light spirit. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Malibu Rum for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye": "Mammoth 16 Year Single Barrel Rye with the Tomahawk — 16-year aged single-barrel from Mammoth Distilling, Michigan. Home-state distillery; the extended 16-year maturation gives the Tomahawk a local-Michigan rye with serious age. Pour with home-state pride for Michigan guests. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Marc Brédif Chinon": "Light-body Loire Cab Franc against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — Chinon's raspberry-bell pepper delicacy gets buried by the grand cut. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Cabernet Sauvignon, Bordeaux, or prestige Napa Cab; keep Chinon for chicken, salmon, mushrooms, or lighter apps where food-wine acidity peaks.",
  "The Tomahawk|Margarita": "Citrus-forward Margarita against the biggest steak on the menu — zero structural overlap. The cocktail's bright delicacy is crushed by marbled Tomahawk weight. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "The Tomahawk|Marimar Estate Christina": "Russian River Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — plush low-tannin Pinot has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Marimar Christina for salmon, chicken, or seafood.",
  "The Tomahawk|Mata Hari Absinthe": "Mata Hari Absinthe's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- aperitivo bitterness fights the headline cut's marbled char — bitter and savory pull apart. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Mata Hari Absinthe on another course instead.",
  "The Tomahawk|Michter's 10 Year Bourbon": "Michter's 10 Bourbon with the Tomahawk — Michter's flagship 10yr against 32oz cap-fat. Works; Michter's 10's aged character is under-specified for Tomahawk intensity; reach for a barrel-proof bourbon.",
  "The Tomahawk|Michter's 10 Year Rye": "The Tomahawk: bone-in ribeye meets Michter's 10 Year Rye — Michter's aged premium single-barrel, 92.8 proof, 10 years. Sister to Michter's US*1 Rye at premium-aged tier. Aged polish distinct from WP's Vermont-finished approach. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Michter's US*1 Rye": "Michter's US*1 Rye's crisp spice character finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the spice frames the umami depth. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Mijenta Blanco": "Mijenta Blanco with the Tomahawk — premium craft blanco against bone-in ribeye. Avoid; bourbon or scotch territory.",
  "The Tomahawk|Milagro Anejo": "Milagro Anejo -- Highland Jalisco 100% Blue Weber, triple-distilled. The barrel-rested body handles the 36oz showpiece, and the oak threads the cut. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Milagro Reposado": "Milagro Reposado with the Tomahawk — triple-distilled reposado against massive cut. Avoid; demands bourbon or scotch.",
  "The Tomahawk|Mister Sam Tribute Whiskey": "Mister Sam with the Tomahawk — Sazerac tribute blended whiskey against 32oz cap-fat. Works; Mister Sam's aged-blended character is under-specified for Tomahawk intensity; reach for a barrel-proof bourbon.",
  "The Tomahawk|Mocha Creme": "The Tomahawk into the mocha creme -- the smoky bone-in marbling hands off to silky coffee-chocolate cream, espresso depth closing the table. Strong; the theatrical cut meets the layered dessert.",
  "The Tomahawk|Monkey 47 Gin": "Monkey 47 with the Tomahawk — Black Forest craft gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Monkey Shoulder": "Monkey Shoulder -- blended Speyside malts, soft, honey. The scotch depth settles into the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; JD 12's aged depth frames the two-cut plate at premium register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Montenegro Amaro": "Montenegro Amaro's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Montenegro Amaro for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Mount Gay Rum": "Mount Gay Rum's light-rum register with cane and citrus lift underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- light spirit can't stand up to the marbled char — needs a Cab, bourbon, or añejo. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a light spirit. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Mount Gay Rum belongs on another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Muga Reserva": "Muga Reserva's cherry-earth tannin holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Mushroom Bisque": "The Tomahawk after the mushroom bisque -- the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream sets the table for the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Mushrooms": "The Tomahawk with the mushrooms -- umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; the side carries cleanly with the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Myers's Rum": "Myers's Rum's dense-spirit weight finds neutral with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the high-proof register matches the 36oz showpiece, and the heavy-pour register grips the cut. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Negroni": "Negroni's spirit-forward cocktail register mis-pairs with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Negroni on another course instead.",
  "The Tomahawk|Never Say Die Rye": "Never Say Die Rye's crisp oak-spice character matches the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon character meets the rendered fat, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Never Say Die Small Batch": "Never Say Die Small Batch's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bourbon depth settles into the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; EHT Full Proof handles dessert density at historic Kentucky register.",
  "The Tomahawk|New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon": "New Holland Beer Barrel Bourbon with the Tomahawk — Holland MI beer-barrel against 32oz cap-fat. Strong; NH Beer Barrel handles the Tomahawk at Michigan-hometown register.",
  "The Tomahawk|New York Sour": "Jameson, lemon, simple, egg white, Daou Cab float — off menu. The cocktail's sour profile and Jameson's lighter base can't meet the Tomahawk's size and marbled weight; the Cab float alone doesn't carry it. Never pair at peak — route Tomahawk to Cab-forward cocktails.",
  "The Tomahawk|Nickel & Nickel Cabernet": "Oakville prestige Cab — deep cassis, blackberry, graphite, subtle oak, concentrated age-worthy. The structured body meets Tomahawk fat and char directly and the graphite frames the theatrical bone-in cut. Peak prestige Oakville Cab for Tomahawk — Nickel & Nickel's terroir-specific concentration stands up to the biggest marbled steak. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Nolets Dry Gin": "Nolet's with the Tomahawk — Dutch floral gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Not a Paper Plane": "Not a Paper Plane's spirit-forward cocktail register mis-pairs with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- this specific cocktail underclubs the headline cut — a Cab or older bourbon earns it. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Not a Paper Plane lands on another course — not here.",
  "The Tomahawk|Oban 14": "Oban 14 -- Highland-coastal, maritime saltiness, honey. The coastal salinity meets the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; clean match at the register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Oban 18": "Oban 18 -- aged maritime Highland, spice, honey. The Highland register carries the 36oz showpiece, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; Sinatra Select's grooved-barrel character frames the two-cut plate at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Ocho Anejo": "Ocho Anejo's agave-char character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-tequila depth grips the marbled cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Odette Cabernet Sauvignon": "Odette — Stags Leap Cab, dark fruit, sage, cedar, graphite, refined tannin. The velvet-glove body meets Tomahawk fat and char and the sage-cedar frames the theatrical bone-in cut. Strong refined Cab for Tomahawk — iron-fist-velvet-glove balance, though Opus or Shafer Hillside peak higher for the biggest steak.",
  "The Tomahawk|Old Elk Cigar Cut": "Old Elk Cigar Cut with the Tomahawk — Old Elk cigar-paired against 32oz cap-fat. Strong; Old Elk Cigar Cut handles the Tomahawk at cigar-companion register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Old Emmer": "Old Emmer's crisp char-earth character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Old Fitzgerald 7 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 7 Year's caramel-honey character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon character meets the rendered fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Old Fitzgerald 9 Year": "Old Fitzgerald 9 Year's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads register-aligned with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bourbon body sits with the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla rounds the flame-grilled char. Strong; matched register with curated depth.",
  "The Tomahawk|Old Grand-Dad 16 Year": "OGD 16 with the Tomahawk — aged OGD high-rye against 32oz cap-fat. Works; OGD 16's rye-spice is under-specified for Tomahawk intensity; reach for a barrel-proof bourbon.",
  "The Tomahawk|Opus One": "Opus One — Mondavi-Rothschild Oakville flagship, Cab-dominant Bordeaux blend, dark fruit, mocha, cedar, dried violet, firm tannin, full body. The full structure handles Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye and the cedar plays against char. Peak Napa prestige wine for Tomahawk — sell the collaboration story with the cut. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Our Lady of Guadalupe Acolytes": "Willamette Trappist Chardonnay against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even rich monastery oak has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Acolytes for salmon, chicken, scallops, crab cake, or bisque.",
  "The Tomahawk|Pablo Sour": "Silky sour agave cocktail with egg-white foam against the biggest steak on the menu — complete mismatch. The Pablo's textural delicacy is obliterated by marbled Tomahawk weight. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "The Tomahawk|Paloma": "Reposado tequila, grapefruit, vanilla syrup. Light agave cocktail against the biggest steak on the menu — zero structural overlap. The Paloma's delicacy is crushed by marbled Tomahawk weight. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "The Tomahawk|Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye": "Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye with the Tomahawk — the Van Winkle Family Reserve Rye, 13 years, 95.6 proof, Buffalo Trace-produced. Unicorn allocation; one of the rarest ryes on any menu. The Tomahawk meets its unicorn rye counterpart — a pairing that only happens when the table specifically asks for Pappy Rye. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Parker's Heritage Collection 16th Edition": "Parker's Heritage 16th Ed with the Tomahawk — HH annual collector bourbon against 32oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Parker's Heritage 16th's collector cask-strength character handles Tomahawk intensity with HH-annual-release gravitas.",
  "The Tomahawk|Patron Anejo": "Patron Anejo's crisp oak-agave character lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Patron Silver": "Patron Silver with the Tomahawk — 80-proof blanco against bone-in ribeye. Avoid; bourbon or scotch territory.",
  "The Tomahawk|Patron XO Cafe": "Patron XO Cafe's sweet liqueur character clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sweet liqueur on the headline cut is dessert-course energy — save it for after the meal. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Patron XO Cafe for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Paul Bara Grand Rosé Brut": "Bouzy Grand Cru grower rosé Champagne against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — Burgundian weight is still a light-bodied rosé and can't meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Paul Bara for the opener.",
  "The Tomahawk|Peanut Butter Brownie": "After the Tomahawk, the PB brownie -- the 36oz cut yields to peanut-and-chocolate fudge, vanilla ice cream tempering the heat. Strong; the headline steak meets the substantial close.",
  "The Tomahawk|Peju Cabernet Sauvignon": "Peju Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-cherry cassis finds register with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the opulent body meets the cap-fat, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Pierre Ferrand Cognac": "Pierre Ferrand Cognac's cognac-orange-peel character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut": "Pierre Gimonnet 2015 grower Champagne against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even vintage brioche and cream can't meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save the Special Club for the opener or chef's table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Pikesville Rye": "Pikesville Rye's cask-strength citrus-pepper character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the citrus brightens the cream. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Pineau des Charentes": "Never pair Pineau des Charentes with Tomahawk — fortified aperitif-or-dessert style gets bulldozed by the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and honey residual-sugar clashes with smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. Pineau is a before-or-after pour.",
  "The Tomahawk|Pio Cesare Barbaresco": "Pio Cesare Barbaresco's bold Nebbiolo-cherry cassis holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the concentrated tannin grips the marbled cut, and the cherry-fruit cuts the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Plymouth Gin": "Plymouth with the Tomahawk — softer gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Poderi Aldo Conterno Nebbiolo": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Pommery Cuvée Louise": "Pommery 2004 Cuvée Louise against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even 20-year vintage complexity can't meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Pommery Louise for the opener or seafood tower course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Prime Tartare": "The Tomahawk after the tartare -- the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami sets up the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: umami plays against the protein, the table earns its depth. Works; the starter composes before the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon": "Quilt Cabernet Sauvignon's bold chocolate-cocoa cassis frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the Cab structure carries the bone-in cut, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|RAEN Royal St Robert": "Sea-influenced Sonoma Coast Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — silky low-tannin RAEN has no grip for the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Royal St Robert for the salmon, chicken, or seafood course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Raventós Cava de NIT Rosé Brut": "Catalan Cava rosé against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — light dry bubbles have no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Cava de NIT for starters, seafood tower, or accessible rosé moments.",
  "The Tomahawk|Redbreast 12 Year": "Redbreast 12 with the Tomahawk — premium pot still Irish against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; even rich Irish pot still is too light for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Redbreast 21 Year": "Redbreast 21 with the Tomahawk — aged pot still Irish against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; even 21yr aged Irish is too light for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Remus 15 Year Gatsby Reserve": "Remus 15 Gatsby with the Tomahawk — George Remus tribute MGP against 32oz cap-fat. Works; Remus 15's aged rye-spice is under-specified for Tomahawk intensity; reach for a barrel-proof bourbon.",
  "The Tomahawk|Remy VSOP Cognac": "Remy VSOP Cognac's cognac-fruit character frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Rey Sol Anejo": "Rey Sol Anejo's apricot-caramel character frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the añejo weight matches the cap-fat, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Reyka Vodka": "Reyka Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Reyka Vodka for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Rishi's Old Antique": "Rishi's Old Antique with the Tomahawk — aged retailer-label against bone-in ribeye. Strong; boutique character covers the cut with retailer-label authority.",
  "The Tomahawk|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "The Tomahawk into the chickpea soup -- spice and substance share the plate, the contrast settles the meal. Works; safe opener, the meal builds without crowding.",
  "The Tomahawk|Robert Moncuit Les Grand Blancs": "Le Mesnil Grand Cru blanc de blancs against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — austere no-dosage precision has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Moncuit for tartare, seafood tower, or filet course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Roku Gin": "Roku with the Tomahawk — Japanese gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Ron Zacapa Rum": "Ron Zacapa Rum's Solera-honey premium-sipping rum finds neutral with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the layered spirit body sits with the 36oz showpiece, and the dense weight underlines the cut. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Tomahawk|Russell's Reserve 10 Year": "Russell's Reserve 10 Year's caramel-honey character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Sambuca": "Sambuca's sweet liqueur character flattens the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- sugar dulls the marbled char instead of lifting it — the steak deserves a savory call. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a digestif. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Sambuca for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|San Simeon Stormwatch": "San Simeon Stormwatch's bold cherry-coffee cassis reads alongside the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the velvet-on-fat coherence runs through the plate, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Sanford & Benedict Pinot Noir": "Santa Barbara Pinot against Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye — layered Sta. Rita Hills Pinot's medium tannin still falls short of the cut's fat-and-char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Napa Cabs, Bordeaux, Meritage, or bold Italian reds; keep Sanford & Benedict for filet, salmon, or tartare.",
  "The Tomahawk|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "The Tomahawk alongside the garlic spinach -- the 36oz showpiece meets the wilted garlic-buttered greens, the garlic threading the smoky char without competing. Strong; lighter than creamed, still server-confident.",
  "The Tomahawk|Sauternes Glass": "Never pair Sauternes Glass with Tomahawk — noble-rot sweetness gets bulldozed by the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and the honey clashes with smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. Sauternes belongs at dessert or the cheese course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Sazerac": "Sazerac's rye weight and anise-edge sits alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bold-mix depth handles the 36oz showpiece, and the bitters edge frames the cut. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Sazerac Rye": "Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character meets at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the dry rye body wraps the 36oz showpiece, and the rye-pepper note underlines the cut. Strong; scale pushes past Elmer T's balance — E.H.",
  "The Tomahawk|Scapegrace Fortuna": "Scapegrace Fortuna's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bourbon depth settles into the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla underlines the rendered char. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "The Tomahawk|Scavino Barolo": "Piedmont Barolo DOCG Nebbiolo — Scavino Castiglione Falletto, tar, dried rose, cherry, leather, powerful firm tannin, high acid, full body. The powerful tannin handles Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye and the tar-leather plays against smoky char. Peak King-of-Italian for Tomahawk — Scavino prestige on the grand cut, demands the beef. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Schloss Vollrads Riesling": "Rheingau Riesling against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — off-dry fruit-and-acid has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Schloss Vollrads for bisque, crab cake, scallops, burrata, or the chicken course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters": "Scotto Cellars The Lost Chapters's bold cherry-chocolate cassis reads alongside the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the Cab structure carries the bone-in cut, and the cocoa underlines the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Seafood Tower": "The Tomahawk after the seafood tower -- showpiece into showpiece, the iced shellfish lineup opens the table with theater, the 36oz long-bone cut following with smoky char that the cold opener primed the palate to anticipate. Strong; both courses earn the spectacle billing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Seared Scallops": "The Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char overwhelms the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness: the smoky bone-in weight obliterates the scallops, and the marbled char crowds out the seared-rare scallop plate. Avoid; the courses can't share a meal cleanly. Pair the scallops with Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre on a different visit; pour Opus One, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or George T. Stagg alongside the Tomahawk.",
  "The Tomahawk|Seasonal Soup": "The Tomahawk preceded by the seasonal soup -- a clean call, no friction either way. Works; the opener primes the cut without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Seasonal Vegetables": "The Tomahawk with the seasonal vegetables -- neither course pulls focus, the table reads even. Works; the side carries cleanly with the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Sesti Brunello di Montalcino": "The Tomahawk: this marbled ribeye's fatty richness, smoky crust, and bold beef expression meets this structured red's cassis-and-dark-fruit frame, firm tannin, and oak-driven weight. The tannin scrubs rendered fat while dark fruit echoes the savory char. A headliner steakhouse pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Shafer 1.5": "Stags Leap Shafer 1.5 — entry-level, opulent blackberry, mocha, vanilla, plush soft tannin, full body. The full body meets Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye though plush soft tannin reads rounder than firm-tannin prestige Cabs on the grand cut. Strong Stags Leap Cab for Tomahawk — Shafer opulence on the grand scale.",
  "The Tomahawk|Shafer Hillside Select": "Stags Leap collector's Cab — Hillside Select 32-month French oak, blackcurrant, dark chocolate, violet, graphite, dense yet silky, firm tannin, full body. The firm tannin handles Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye and the graphite plays against smoky char. Peak collector's Napa Cab for Tomahawk — one of Napa's most coveted on the grand cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Shrimp Bisque": "The Tomahawk after the shrimp bisque -- the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup sets the table for the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: cream meets protein, the meal builds with weight. Works; the opener carries before the cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Shrimp Cocktail": "The Tomahawk after the shrimp cocktail -- the chilled shrimp's citrus-horseradish brightness cleans the palate, the 36oz long-bone showpiece following with theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char. Strong; the bright classic opener primes the spectacle cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Siete Leguas Blanco": "Siete Leguas Blanco with the Tomahawk — traditional blanco against bone-in ribeye. Avoid; bourbon or scotch territory.",
  "The Tomahawk|Siete Leguas Reposado": "Siete Leguas Reposado with the Tomahawk — traditional reposado against massive cut. Avoid; bourbon or scotch territory.",
  "The Tomahawk|Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon": "Silver Oak — Alexander Valley Cab with American-oak vanilla, coconut, dill, ripe cassis, plum. The soft polished body can meet Tomahawk fat and char and the vanilla-dill frames the theatrical cut with the distinctive American-oak signature. Peak recognizable Cab for Tomahawk — name-recognition plus lushness for the big moment. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Simon Family Estate Reserve": "Boutique Napa family estate Cab — dark plum, cassis, toasted oak, long refined finish, firm tannin, full body. The firm tannin handles Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye and the toasted oak plays against smoky char. Peak boutique Napa Cab for Tomahawk — Simon Family estate-grown fruit and reserve-level winemaking on the grand cut. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Spottswoode Lyndenhurst": "Spottswoode Lyndenhurst's bold cherry-floral cassis lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the velvet-on-fat coherence runs through the plate, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon": "St Supéry Cabernet Sauvignon's bold char-coffee cassis reads alongside the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the concentrated tannin grips the marbled cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc": "St Supéry Napa Sauv Blanc against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even rounded Napa Sauv Blanc has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save St Supéry for crab cake, shrimp cocktail, scallops, or the wedge.",
  "The Tomahawk|St Supéry Élu Meritage": "St Supéry Élu Meritage's bold cherry-coffee cassis frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the velvet-on-fat coherence runs through the plate, and the cassis plays against the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Stagg Jr. Batch #15": "Stagg Jr. Batch #15 with the Tomahawk — BT cask-strength batch #15 against 32oz bone-in cap-fat. Excellent; Stagg Jr. delivers GTS-adjacent barrel-proof intensity at accessible register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Stoli Elit Vodka": "Stoli Elit Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Stoli Elit Vodka for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc": "Marlborough NZ Sauv Blanc against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — zesty passionfruit-grass has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Stoneleigh for the wedge, burrata, crab cake, shrimp cocktail, or scallops.",
  "The Tomahawk|Tanq 10 Gin": "Tanq 10 with the Tomahawk — premium citrus gin against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Tanqueray Gin": "Tanqueray with the Tomahawk — juniper icon against 32oz bone-in cap-fat. Avoid; gin has no answer for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Taylor Fladgate Tawny": "Never pair Taylor Fladgate Tawny with Tomahawk — heritage Tawny buries the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and sweet oxidation fights smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. Tawny is a finishing pour, not a steak wine.",
  "The Tomahawk|Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle": "Never pair Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle with Tomahawk — the extraordinarily rich flagship buries the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and residual sugar fights smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. Present the Vintage Bottle with dessert, not steak.",
  "The Tomahawk|Tears of Llorona": "Tears of Llorona's rested-agave body reads alongside the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the barrel-rested body handles the 36oz showpiece, and the caramel-agave register brightens the cut. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "The Tomahawk|Teeter Totter Cabernet Sauvignon": "Napa Cab — rich dark fruit, mocha, smooth finish, polished. The crowd-pleaser body can meet Tomahawk fat and char but the smooth polished profile is rounder than the biggest marbled steak demands. Strong Teeter Totter for Tomahawk — Opus, Shafer, Caymus, or mountain Cabs peak higher for the theatrical cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico": "Tenuta di Arceno Chianti Classico's cherry-earth tannin lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the medium-body red meets the cap-fat at lighter register, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|The BG": "The BG's crisp citrus-earth build sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the cocktail is outweighed by the cap-fat, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|The Happy Wife": "Screwdriver-plus-ginger cocktail against the biggest steak on the menu — zero structural overlap. The Happy Wife's simple profile has no weight for marbled Tomahawk fat. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "The Tomahawk|The Manhattan": "The Manhattan's bold oak-vanilla build frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey-build weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|The Prisoner Red Blend": "The Prisoner Red Blend's bold char cassis frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the concentrated tannin grips the marbled cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the Tomahawk — BTAC annual barrel-proof rye, uncut and unfiltered, from Buffalo Trace Antique Collection. Sister to Sazerac Rye at its most extreme: uncut version of the cocktail benchmark. Collector-tier pour; the Tomahawk earns BTAC ceremony. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's char-mineral character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Titos Vodka": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Titos Vodka for another course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky": "Toki Suntory with the Tomahawk — light-floral Japanese blend against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; Japanese whisky's delicate character is wrong for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Tomato Basil": "The Tomahawk with the tomato basil alongside -- the bright register cleaves the heavy course, the table breathes. Works; the soup-or-salad call holds, the meal builds cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Transfusion": "Fruit-forward sessionable vodka cocktail against the biggest steak on the menu — zero structural overlap. Ginger beer and grape have no weight to meet marbled Tomahawk fat. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "The Tomahawk|Trombo Cedano Reposado": "Trombo Cedano Reposado with the Tomahawk — cedar-wood reposado against massive cut. Avoid; the cut is bourbon-or-scotch territory.",
  "The Tomahawk|Truffle Fries": "* The Tomahawk alongside the truffle fries -- the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char carries cleanly into the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight. Gold standard; a confident headline pour.",
  "The Tomahawk|Tullamore D.E.W.": "Tullamore D.E.W. with the Tomahawk — heritage Irish against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; Irish whiskey is too light for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Venge Family Reserve": "Kirk Venge flagship Oakville Cab — step above Silencieux, concentrated blackcurrant, dark chocolate, vanilla, seamless polished finish, soft tannin, full body. The full body meets Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye and the dark chocolate plays against smoky char. Peak Oakville flagship for Tomahawk — Venge craftsman pedigree on the grand cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Venge Scout's Honor": "Napa Zin-led blend — Kirk Venge's Scout's Honor, blackberry, boysenberry, black pepper, vanilla, full body, generous finish. Full body approaches the 40oz bone-in but Zin-led fruit reads sweeter than Tomahawk's smoky-char demands. Works for Tomahawk — Silver Oak or Opus One peaks higher on the grand cut.",
  "The Tomahawk|Venge Silencieux": "Kirk Venge single-vineyard Napa — dark cherry, cassis, chocolate, floral violet, silky soft tannin, full body. The full body meets Tomahawk's massive bone-in ribeye though silky soft tannin reads rounder than firm-tannin Cabs on the grand cut. Strong winemaker's Napa Cab for Tomahawk — Venge refinement on the grand scale.",
  "The Tomahawk|Vesper": "Elegant martini against the biggest steak on the menu — complete structural mismatch. The Vesper's clean-dry profile is obliterated by marbled Tomahawk weight. Never pair — route Tomahawk to Cab, Barolo, or spirit-forward whiskey cocktails.",
  "The Tomahawk|Veuve Clicquot Brut": "Yellow label Veuve against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even iconic creamy mousse has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Veuve Brut for the opener, toast, or starter course.",
  "The Tomahawk|Vieux Carré": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey-build weight matches the cap-fat, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "The Tomahawk|Villon Cognac": "Villon Cognac's bold cognac-vanilla character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Vin Santo": "Never pair Vin Santo with Tomahawk — straw-dried dessert wine gets bulldozed by the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and honey residual-sugar clashes with smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. Vin Santo belongs at dessert.",
  "The Tomahawk|Weller 107": "Weller 107's bold caramel-char character frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "The Tomahawk|Weller 12 Year": "Weller 12 Year's wheated-vanilla character frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Weller Millennium": "Weller Millennium's bold wheated-oak character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon character meets the rendered fat, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|Weller Special Reserve": "Weller Special Reserve's caramel-honey character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Wheatley Vodka": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Wheatley Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "The Tomahawk|WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the Tomahawk — WP's core 10-year single-barrel, 100 proof. Vermont-finished Canadian-sourced rye at its WhistlePig-core depth; the aged rye and bone-in ribeye find each other in a classic premium rye-steak pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "The Tomahawk|WhistlePig 12 Year Old World": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with the Tomahawk — 12-year WP finished in Sauternes, Madeira, and Port casks. The fruit-forward wine-cask complexity gives the Tomahawk a layered rye frame distinct from straight WP 10 Year. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|WhistlePig 15 Year": "WhistlePig 15 Year with the Tomahawk — WP's 15-year premium flagship, 92 proof, Vermont oak barrel finish. Extended maturation gives the cut a deeply aged rye frame; the marquee WP pour for the biggest steak on the menu. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|WhistlePig Boss Hog IX 17 Year": "WhistlePig Boss Hog IX with the Tomahawk — WP's allocated annual limited-release, barrel-proof intensity ('The Siren's Song' edition). Collector-bottle territory; the Tomahawk gets WP's absolute top-tier rye with barrel-proof weight. A marquee pour for a marquee cut. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|WhistlePig Boss Hog X Commandments": "WhistlePig Boss Hog X with the Tomahawk — the 10th edition Boss Hog, 'X Commandments'. Allocated luxury annual release; sibling to Boss Hog IX but from the next year's vintage. Pour when a collector orders specifically this edition; the Tomahawk earns the ceremony. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "The Tomahawk|WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year": "WhistlePig PiggyBack 6 Year's rye-spice character leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the high-rye spice register matches the 36oz showpiece, and the rye-grain bite cuts the cut. Strong; OGD 16's aged rye-spice meets the earthy-rich at distinctive register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Willett Pot Still Reserve": "Willett Pot Still Reserve's grain-and-oak bourbon register rests at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bourbon backbone carries the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla underlines the rendered char. Strong; multi-distillery complexity sits with richness at craft-blender register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Woodford Reserve": "Woodford Reserve's caramel-floral character lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the caramel-and-oak weight rises with the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "The Tomahawk|Woodford Reserve Double Oaked": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's rounded-oak bourbon character sits at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bourbon body sits with the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; Sinatra Select's extra oak meets the char at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Woodford Reserve Rye": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the high-rye spice register matches the 36oz showpiece, and the rye edge frames the cut. Strong; the smooth character at matched register.",
  "The Tomahawk|Woodinville Rye": "Woodinville Rye's crisp grain-oak character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "The Tomahawk|Yamazaki 12 Year": "Yamazaki 12 with the Tomahawk — flagship Japanese single malt against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; even Yamazaki's Mizunara-oak depth is too light for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "The Tomahawk|Yamazaki 18 Year": "Yamazaki 18 with the Tomahawk — aged Japanese single malt against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; even aged Yamazaki's Mizunara-oak is too light for this cut. Save the collector bottle for the filet or KC.",
  "The Tomahawk|Yellow Chartreuse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Yellow Chartreuse for another course.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Asparagus": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the rye spice cuts the asparagus, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; the toast notes don't really interplay with the soup but don't clash.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the au gratin, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; premium single-barrel wasted on the salad.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Beignets": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the rye backbone drives the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Bone Marrow": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with bone marrow — BTAC barrel-proof against umami fat. The uncut Sazerac intensity meets marrow with allocated BT authority; a collector-pour marrow pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Bone-In Filet": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's cask-strength spice character frames the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey character holds register with the tenderloin, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Broccoli Cheddar": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the broccoli cheddar, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Broccolini": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the rye-and-oak weight handles the broccolini, and the rye-grain bite cuts the side. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Brussels and Belly": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with Brussels + Belly — BTAC unfiltered rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. The barrel-proof uncut character cuts the glaze with allocated BT authority. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Burrata": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against BTAC barrel-proof. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Butternut Squash": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the high-rye spice register matches the squash bisque, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; multi-distillery complexity doesn't drive.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Carrot Cake": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the rye spice cuts the carrot cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Cheesecake": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye spice cuts the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Chilean Seabass": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye for the steak course.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Chocolate Brownie": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the chocolate brownie — BTAC barrel-proof against cocoa. Works; uncut intensity meets the brownie at allocated register.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Chocolate Cake": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rye-and-oak weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Clam Chowder": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the rye backbone drives the chowder, and the oak threads the cream. Works; annual-release wasted on salad.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Cowboy Ribeye": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — BTAC barrel-proof against cap fat. The uncut unfiltered Sazerac intensity handles the ribeye with BT-allocated authority; an annual-release marquee pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Crab Cake": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with crab cake — BTAC allocated against delicate crab. Avoid; save the Antique Collection bottle.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Creamed Spinach": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with creamed spinach — BTAC barrel-proof against dairy. Strong; uncut intensity sits with richness at allocated register.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Creamy Potato": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; X Commandments story wasted on salad.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Creme Brulee": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye spice cuts the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; curated single-barrel slightly over-specified.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Escargot": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's cask-strength spice character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Faroe Island Salmon": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Filet Mignon": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the filet — BTAC uncut against the 10oz cut. The Antique Collection Sazerac earns the filet at excellent; the BT-allocated Sazerac when the benchmark isn't enough. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|French Onion": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the rye spice cuts the French onion, and the rye-grain bite cuts the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Grilled Caesar": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the grilled Caesar — uncut BT rye against charred romaine. Works; collector-bottle doesn't drive.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Gumbo": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the rye spice cuts the gumbo, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; a gentle composed match at the premium tier.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rye backbone drives the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|House Wedge": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the House Wedge — BTAC allocated against iceberg and bleu. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Kansas City": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the KC — BTAC uncut against the strip. The Handy's barrel-proof Sazerac character gives the strip BT's top-tier rye intensity; the allocated answer to Sazerac Rye's benchmark. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Loaded Potato": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the rye-and-oak weight handles the loaded potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Lobster Mac": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the rye-and-oak weight handles the lobster mac, and the oak threads the cream. Works; cleaner than wheated versions but not exceptional.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Market Fish": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Mocha Creme": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the mocha creme, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for the side — save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Mushroom Bisque": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the rye backbone drives the mushroom bisque, and the oak balances the umami register. Works; craft-blend covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Mushrooms": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the mushrooms — BTAC unfiltered against earthy umami. Strong; the uncut Sazerac layers into mushroom depth at collector register.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the PB brownie — BTAC against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; collector-bottle depth gives ultra-premium character.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Porterhouse": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the porterhouse — BTAC barrel-proof across strip and filet. Strong; uncut intensity leans strip-side.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Prime Tartare": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the tartare — BTAC against raw beef. Strong; the uncut unfiltered Sazerac is a collector-register counterpoint to tartare's mineral depth.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Rainbow Trout": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the rye spice cuts the roast chicken, and the rye-pepper note underlines the plate. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the rye backbone drives the chickpea soup, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; stout-barrel story wasted on salad.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye for the steak course.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye spice cuts the garlic spinach, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; the pour's delicacy doesn't overpower the soup, but doesn't particularly elevate it either.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Seafood Tower": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Seared Scallops": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against BTAC uncut. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Seasonal Soup": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the rye backbone drives the seasonal soup, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the bowl. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the seasonal vegetables, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; save the BTAC bottle for the protein course — the side doesn't deserve the pour.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the shrimp bisque, and the oak softens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against BTAC. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Swordfish": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye for the steak course.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|The Tomahawk": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with the Tomahawk — BTAC annual barrel-proof rye, uncut and unfiltered, from Buffalo Trace Antique Collection. Sister to Sazerac Rye at its most extreme: uncut version of the cocktail benchmark. Collector-tier pour; the Tomahawk earns BTAC ceremony. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Tomato Basil": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the rye backbone drives the tomato basil, and the rye-grain bite cuts the bowl. Works; curated single-barrel covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Truffle Fries": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye with truffle fries — BTAC allocated against truffle-parmesan. The uncut Sazerac intensity gives the side collector-level frame. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Thomas H. Handy Sazerac Rye's rye-spice character leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the rye spice cuts the curry, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; the bourbon's character is distinctive but the pairing isn't a standout.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Asparagus": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon depth settles into the asparagus, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; HH 20 frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the au gratin — chardonnay-cask bourbon against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; TSM Chardonnay Cask handles the richness with chardonnay-butter distinctiveness.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Beignets": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon weight handles the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; gentle composed match.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Bone Marrow": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with bone marrow — Barton chardonnay-cask against umami fat. Works; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay character sits alongside marrow; wine-finish is under-specified for marrow intensity.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Bone-In Filet": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the bone-in filet — Barton chardonnay-cask bourbon against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; TSM Chardonnay Cask's white-wine-finish crisp-apple character frames the cut distinctively.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Broccoli Cheddar": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon body sits with the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Caribbean Cask rides the cream at distinctive register.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Broccolini": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon depth settles into the broccolini, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Brussels and Belly": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone sits alongside Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the caramel-spice register matches the pork-belly plate, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Works; sweet character distinctive but not driving; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Burrata": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with burrata — white-wine-finished bourbon against fresh dairy. Avoid; bourbon weight wrong for burrata even with chardonnay finish.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Butternut Squash": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon body sits with the squash bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Carrot Cake": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with carrot cake — chardonnay-cask bourbon against cream cheese and cinnamon. Excellent; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-derived crisp-apple-butter threads the cake with DISTINCTIVE white-wine character — more subtle than red-wine-finish + raisins bridge, more complementary than port approaches.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Cheesecake": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with cheesecake — white-wine-finished Barton bourbon against custard. Excellent; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-butter + custard resonance is distinctive — white-wine-finish for custard is less common and more harmonious than red-wine finishes here.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Chilean Seabass": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask on the steak course instead.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Chocolate Brownie": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the chocolate brownie — chardonnay-cask bourbon against cocoa. Strong; TSM Chardonnay Cask's white-wine character meets cocoa at distinctive register; AE's port-finish is the featured wine-finished-chocolate match.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Chocolate Cake": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the caramel-spice register matches the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Clam Chowder": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon weight handles the chowder, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; boutique-sourced doesn't drive.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Cowboy Ribeye": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the cowboy ribeye — Barton chardonnay-cask against 26oz bone-in cap-fat. Works; TSM Chardonnay Cask is under-specified for cap-fat — white-wine finish is lighter than red; AE Cask Strength handles ribeye better.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Crab Cake": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with crab cake — chardonnay-cask bourbon against delicate lump crab. Avoid; bourbon wrong for seafood, even with white-wine finish. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Creamed Spinach": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with creamed spinach — white-wine-finished Barton bourbon against dairy-rich side. Works; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-butter rides the cream with distinctive resonance.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Creamy Potato": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Creme Brulee": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with creme brulee — Barton Distilling chardonnay-cask against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Excellent; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-butter + caramel is the featured wine-finished-brulee pairing — the chardonnay-cask apple + vanilla resonance reads distinctively.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Escargot": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon depth settles into the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; curated character covers the cut; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Faroe Island Salmon": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the salmon — chardonnay-cask bourbon against oily salmon. Avoid; even the white-wine finish doesn't rescue bourbon-and-fish.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Filet Mignon": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the filet — Barton Distilling (Sazerac) bourbon finished in chardonnay casks — a white-wine finish, DISTINCTIVE from the port/red-wine crowd. Excellent; TSM Chardonnay Cask's white-wine-finish character is the distinctive-white-wine-finished-bourbon-filet pairing — where every other wine-finish in this cluster brings red/port jammy-fruit, TSM brings chardonnay's crisp-apple-butter notes for a completely different wine-bourbon story.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|French Onion": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the French onion, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; a gentle composed match.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Grilled Caesar": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the grilled Caesar — chardonnay-cask bourbon against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; TSM Chardonnay Cask meets the char with distinctive white-wine-finish character.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Gumbo": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the caramel-spice register matches the gumbo, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; not revelatory but well-matched registers.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; boutique-blend depth gives composed dessert character.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|House Wedge": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the House Wedge — Barton chardonnay-cask against iceberg and bleu. Works; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-butter engages the bleu with distinctive white-wine character.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Kansas City": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the KC — Barton white-wine-finished bourbon against an 18oz strip. Strong; TSM Chardonnay Cask handles the strip with distinctive chardonnay character; AE Cask Strength is the featured wine-finished-KC match.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Loaded Potato": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the oak depth lays under the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for a soup — save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Lobster Mac": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the caramel-spice register matches the lobster mac, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Market Fish": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask for the steak course.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Mocha Creme": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with mocha creme — chardonnay-cask bourbon against coffee-chocolate dessert. Strong; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-butter threads mocha at distinctive register.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Mushroom Bisque": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon backbone carries the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for this dish's register.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Mushrooms": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with mushrooms — Barton chardonnay-cask against earthy umami. Strong; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-butter threads mushroom earthiness with distinctive white-wine character.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with the PB brownie — Barton chardonnay-cask against peanut butter and cocoa. Strong; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-butter handles dessert density with distinctive white-wine character.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Porterhouse": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's char-mineral character holds the line with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the bourbon character meets the rendered fat. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Prime Tartare": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's char-mineral character frames the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the mineral edge mirrors the briny meat. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Rainbow Trout": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask for the steak course.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Roast Half Chicken": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the brown-spirit warmth threads the roast chicken, and the oak-and-spice register settles the plate. Works; rich vanilla and high-proof give the dessert bolder match than standard Beam brings.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask for the steak course.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon backbone carries the garlic spinach, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Seafood Tower": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overshadows the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit weight crowds the delicate shellfish — no acid to clean the brine. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Seared Scallops": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with seared scallops — Barton chardonnay-cask against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops even at white-wine-finish register.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Seasonal Soup": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon weight handles the seasonal soup, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; sweet character distinctive but not driving.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Seasonal Vegetables": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's grain-and-oak bourbon register finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal vegetables, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; grain notes don't really engage the soup but don't clash.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Shrimp Bisque": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with shrimp bisque — white-wine-finished Barton bourbon against cream-shrimp soup. Avoid; chardonnay-and-shrimp is great on paper but bourbon weight breaks the bisque.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Shrimp Cocktail": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with shrimp cocktail — chardonnay-cask Barton bourbon against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Swordfish": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask for the steak course.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|The Tomahawk": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's char-mineral character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Tomato Basil": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; craft-blender complexity over-specified.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Truffle Fries": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask with truffle fries — chardonnay-cask bourbon against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; TSM Chardonnay Cask's chardonnay-butter meets the earthy-rich with distinctive white-wine-finish character.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask for the steak course.",
  "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Thomas S. Moore Chardonnay Cask's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon weight handles the curry, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Titos Vodka|Asparagus": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality reads alongside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Titos Vodka|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality plays cleanly against the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Titos Vodka|Beignets": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a vodka. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Titos Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Titos Vodka|Bone Marrow": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality sits alongside the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Titos Vodka|Bone-In Filet": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Titos Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Titos Vodka|Broccoli Cheddar": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality plays cleanly against the broccoli cheddar's cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Titos Vodka|Broccolini": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality sits beside the broccolini's tender green — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Titos Vodka|Brussels and Belly": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality stays alongside the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Titos Vodka|Burrata": "Tito's with burrata — slightly-sweet American vodka against fresh dairy. Excellent; round mouthfeel sits alongside the cream without friction.",
  "Titos Vodka|Butternut Squash": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Titos Vodka|Carrot Cake": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Titos Vodka belongs on the steak course.",
  "Titos Vodka|Cheesecake": "Tito's with cheesecake — American vodka against custard dessert. Works; Russian-style clean-vodka-with-cheesecake tradition, Tito's roundness is a soft dessert finisher.",
  "Titos Vodka|Chilean Seabass": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality matches the seabass's buttery flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Titos Vodka|Chocolate Brownie": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Titos Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Titos Vodka|Chocolate Cake": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a vodka. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Titos Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Titos Vodka|Clam Chowder": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality stays alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Titos Vodka|Cowboy Ribeye": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Titos Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Titos Vodka|Crab Cake": "Tito's with crab cake — American corn-based workhorse against delicate lump crab. Excellent; Tito's round/slightly-sweet character doesn't overpower crab the way a rye-based vodka like Belvedere can. The default call for a reason here.",
  "Titos Vodka|Creamed Spinach": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality leans against the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Titos Vodka|Creamy Potato": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the round Tito's mouthfeel sits with the potato soup, and the slight sweetness softens the bowl. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Titos Vodka|Creme Brulee": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka mis-pairs with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- clean vodka has nothing to add against the custard's richness. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the Titos Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Titos Vodka|Escargot": "Tito's with escargot — corn-based vodka against garlic-butter snails. Strong; Tito's roundness sits with the butter, though Belvedere's pepper lifts the dish distinctly harder.",
  "Titos Vodka|Faroe Island Salmon": "Tito's with the salmon — Texas corn against oily salmon. Strong; Tito's slightly-sweet character doesn't fight salmon's richness — clean default pairing.",
  "Titos Vodka|Filet Mignon": "Tito's with the filet — American corn against tenderloin. Works; vodka is over-specified for filet, but Tito's roundness won't clash — a functional backup if a guest insists.",
  "Titos Vodka|French Onion": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Titos Vodka|Grilled Caesar": "Tito's with the grilled Caesar — American vodka against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Tito's rounds the char-and-anchovy, though Ketel One's citrus lifts it cleaner.",
  "Titos Vodka|Gumbo": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Titos Vodka|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality stays alongside the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Titos Vodka|House Wedge": "Tito's with the House Wedge — Texas corn vodka against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Tito's American roundness threads the wedge cleanly — this is the most democratic vodka-wedge pairing on the menu, comfortable for any guest.",
  "Titos Vodka|Kansas City": "Tito's with the KC — corn vodka against an 18oz strip. Avoid; vodka has no way to match strip marbling intensity. Reach for a bourbon.",
  "Titos Vodka|Loaded Potato": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality stays alongside the loaded potato's richness — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Titos Vodka|Lobster Mac": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile lets the cheese speak. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Titos Vodka|Market Fish": "Tito's with the market fish — corn-based vodka against whatever's featured. Works; Tito's neutrality handles most fish preparations cleanly.",
  "Titos Vodka|Mocha Creme": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Titos Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Titos Vodka|Mushroom Bisque": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality sits beside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Titos Vodka|Mushrooms": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality leans against the mushrooms' earthy umami — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Titos Vodka|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Titos Vodka lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Titos Vodka|Porterhouse": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Titos Vodka belongs on another course.",
  "Titos Vodka|Prime Tartare": "Tito's with the tartare — round corn vodka against raw beef. Strong; Tito's doesn't lift tartare's minerality the way Belvedere's rye pepper does, but it's a clean neutral choice.",
  "Titos Vodka|Rainbow Trout": "Titos Vodka -- American, corn-based, slightly sweet. The round Tito's mouthfeel sits with the trout, and the slight sweetness softens the plate. Strong; reliable American vodka for the trout.",
  "Titos Vodka|Roast Half Chicken": "Tito's with the chicken — neutral American vodka against roasted poultry. Works; Tito's doesn't compete, comfortable functional pairing.",
  "Titos Vodka|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Titos Vodka|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Titos Vodka -- American, corn-based, slightly sweet. The round Tito's mouthfeel sits with the halibut, and the slight sweetness softens the plate. Strong; reliable American vodka for the halibut.",
  "Titos Vodka|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality sits beside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the neutral profile sits with the light side, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Titos Vodka|Seafood Tower": "Tito's with the seafood tower — American corn workhorse against the raw bar. Excellent; Tito's keeps the seafood in focus without pulling attention — the comfortable default when a table orders the tower.",
  "Titos Vodka|Seared Scallops": "Tito's with seared scallops — round corn-based vodka against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Tito's roundness mirrors scallop's natural sweetness — softer approach than Belvedere's pepper.",
  "Titos Vodka|Seasonal Soup": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality stays alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Titos Vodka|Seasonal Vegetables": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality stays alongside the seasonal garden plate — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Titos Vodka|Shrimp Bisque": "Tito's with shrimp bisque — American vodka against rich cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Tito's roundness rides the bisque's richness without cutting or deepening it.",
  "Titos Vodka|Shrimp Cocktail": "Tito's with shrimp cocktail — American corn against cold shrimp and cocktail sauce. Excellent; clean slightly-sweet neutral frames the shrimp without competing, classic American seafood pairing.",
  "Titos Vodka|Swordfish": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality holds steady against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Titos Vodka|The Tomahawk": "Titos Vodka's American corn-based vodka underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is neutral by design — the marbled char deserves a pour with character. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Titos Vodka for another course.",
  "Titos Vodka|Tomato Basil": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality leans against the tomato basil's bright acid — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate, and the clean profile carries the herb butter. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Titos Vodka|Truffle Fries": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality plays cleanly against the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Titos Vodka|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality plays cleanly against the tuna's seared rare crust — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Titos Vodka|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Titos Vodka's Texas-corn-workhorse neutrality reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Asparagus": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the Japanese whisky balance frames the asparagus, and the umami-tinged finish frames the side. Works; Fortuna handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character finds neutral with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the au gratin, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the blend's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Beignets": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the beignets, and the precision rounds the dessert. Works; pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Bone Marrow": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky -- Suntory entry, light, citrus. The entry Suntory body sits with the rich marrow, and the citrus lifts the starter. Strong; polished aged character sits with richness; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Bone-In Filet": "Toki Suntory with the bone-in filet — entry Japanese blend against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Toki's light-floral Suntory-blend character frames the cut at crowd-pleasing Japanese register.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Broccoli Cheddar": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the broccoli cheddar, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Broccolini": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the restrained whisky body threads the broccolini, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Brussels and Belly": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character leans against Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the pork-belly plate, and the precision rounds the side. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Burrata": "Toki Suntory with burrata — light-floral Japanese entry against fresh dairy. Works; Toki's delicate character is one of the few whiskies gentle enough to sit with burrata; not featured but distinctive.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Butternut Squash": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the restrained whisky body threads the squash bisque, and the oak softens the cream. Works; the complex profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Carrot Cake": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character finds neutral with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the carrot cake, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the dessert. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Cheesecake": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character sits alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; no driver pairing.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Chilean Seabass": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character leans against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the restrained whisky body threads the seabass, and the umami-tinged finish frames the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Chocolate Brownie": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character reads alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the brownie, and the umami-tinged finish frames the dessert. Works; Remus 15 handles dessert density at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Chocolate Cake": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the chocolate cake, and the green-fruit edge brightens the dessert. Works; bold proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Clam Chowder": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the chowder, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; Japanese whisky's delicate category handles the rich dish without fighting it.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Cowboy Ribeye": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character leans against the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the Japanese whisky balance frames the cap-fat ribeye, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the cut. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Crab Cake": "Toki Suntory with crab cake — entry Japanese blend against delicate lump crab. Works; Japanese whisky's delicate category lets Toki sit with crab where most whiskies can't — not featured (vodka/gin still lead) but distinctively possible.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Creamed Spinach": "Toki Suntory with creamed spinach — Suntory entry blend against dairy-rich side. Works; Toki's light-floral character rides the cream at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Creamy Potato": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; aged character overshoots delicacy.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Creme Brulee": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the entry Suntory body sits with the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; Voyage 23 frames the two-cut plate at ocean-finish register.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Escargot": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky -- Suntory entry, light, citrus. The entry Suntory body sits with the buttered escargot, and the citrus lifts the starter. Strong; the unicorn WP sits with richness at allocated register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Faroe Island Salmon": "Toki Suntory with the salmon — entry Japanese blend against oily salmon. Excellent; Toki's light-floral character is gentle enough for fish — Japanese whisky's delicate-elegant-balanced category lets Toki sit with salmon where heavier whiskies overwhelm, parallel to Irish whiskey's category advantage.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: tenderloin meets entry Suntory blend (Hakushu + Yamazaki + Chita grain), light-floral, highball-friendly. Toki's crowd-pleasing Japanese-entry character frames tenderloin with Suntory-blended elegance — the democratic Japanese-whisky-filet pairing, where Hibiki's refinement goes premium and Hakushu's peat goes distinctive. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|French Onion": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the Japanese whisky balance frames the French onion, and the precision rounds the bowl. Works; BIB structure overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Grilled Caesar": "Toki Suntory with the grilled Caesar — Suntory entry blend against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Toki meets the char at entry register; Hakushu's lightly-peated character is the featured Japanese-Caesar match.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Gumbo": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the Japanese whisky balance frames the gumbo, and the precision rounds the bowl. Works; not revelatory but well-matched registers.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; the smooth character gives the dessert a composed frame.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|House Wedge": "Toki Suntory with the House Wedge — light-floral Japanese entry blend against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Toki's delicate character threads the wedge at crowd-pleasing register.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Kansas City": "Toki Suntory with the KC — entry Suntory blend against an 18oz strip. Strong; Toki handles the strip at crowd-pleasing entry register; Yamazaki's Mizunara-oak is the featured Japanese-KC pairing.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Loaded Potato": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the entry Suntory body sits with the loaded potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; 10-year depth overshoots delicacy.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Lobster Mac": "Toki Suntory with lobster mac — entry Japanese blend against pasta-and-lobster richness. Works; Japanese whisky's delicate category handles the rich dish without fighting it.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Market Fish": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character finds neutral with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh: the restrained whisky body threads the market fish, and the umami-tinged finish frames the plate. Works; distinctive finish over-specified.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Mocha Creme": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the restrained whisky body threads the mocha creme, and the oak softens the cream. Works; boutique character doesn't drive.",
  "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky|Mushroom Bisque": "Toki Suntory Japanese Whisky's entry Suntory Toki character leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the entry Suntory body sits with the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the umami. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
