  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Corazon Weller": "Corazon Weller's aged-agave character leans against the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the rested-agave depth sits with the tuna, and the vanilla-and-pepper edge frames the plate. Works; multi-cask complexity handles richness at luxury register.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Corazón del Sol Malbec": "Corazón del Sol Malbec's plush Malbec with plum-and-violet weight overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Corazón del Sol Malbec for the steak course.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Corpse Reviver": "Corpse Reviver's shaken-and-bright cocktail register reads register-aligned with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the high-acid cocktail body sits with the tuna, and the botanical lift underlines the fish. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Courvoisier Cognac": "Courvoisier Cognac's classic VS cognac clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a cognac. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Courvoisier Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Crab Cake": "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna preceded by the crab cake -- the seared-rare flesh sits with the panko-and-lump crab cake. Works; the call holds, neither soars nor fights.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Cragganmore 12": "Cragganmore 12's complex Speyside character overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; the better calls are above. Cragganmore 12 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Creamed Spinach": "The tuna into the creamed spinach -- delicate gives way to rich, the table earns its weight. Works; safe alongside, but not the headline pick.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Creamy Potato": "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna after the potato soup -- the silky leek-and-potato cream opens with quiet richness, the tuna following with seared-rare flesh that the cream primed for. Strong; cream into seared-rare reads refined.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Creme Brulee": "After the tuna, the brulee -- the seared-rare flesh hands off to vanilla-bean custard and torched-sugar crust, berries lifting the close. Strong; the clean ocean main meets the silky-classic finish, the meal arc lands intact.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée": "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna: this lean, seared tuna's sesame-sweet crust and rare-pink center meets this elegant red's cherry-bright, silky-tannin frame. The elegant red's cherry-bright, low-tannin frame threads cleanly through the fish — classic Burgundy logic. A headliner fish pair. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Crown Royal": "Crown Royal's classic Canadian blend smoothness swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a Canadian whisky. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Crown Royal lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Cucumber Gimlet": "Hendrick's, cucumber, lime, ginger syrup, soda. The ginger and cucumber echo Asian-inspired honey-sambal salad and the citrus lifts rare-seared tuna's lean flesh. Works as gin cocktail for tuxedo tuna — fusion-adjacent cocktail route; wines peak higher as dedicated pairings.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Dalmore 12": "Dalmore 12's sherry-finished Highland character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Reserve the Dalmore 12 for the steak course.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge": "Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge's crisp earth-mineral tannin reads alongside the tuna's seared rare crust — the silken tannin frames the fish, and the mineral edge lifts the fish. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Darioush Cabernet Franc": "Darioush Cabernet Franc's herbaceous Cab Franc with red-fruit and bell-pepper clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Darioush Cabernet Franc belongs on the steak course.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon": "Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon's structured Cabernet with cassis-and-cedar depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- tannin against rare-seared crust reads astringent without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Darioush Cabernet Sauvignon belongs on the steak course.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Detroit City Gin": "Detroit City Gin's bold char-juniper botanicals holds steady against the tuna's seared rare crust — the botanical lift frames the fish cleanly, and the smoke meets the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Detroit City Vodka": "Detroit City Vodka's crisp grain-juniper neutrality plays cleanly against the tuna's seared rare crust — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly, and the juniper sharpens the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Dewar's 15": "Dewar's 15's 15yr aged Highland blend swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Dewar's 15 on the steak course instead.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Dewar's White Label": "Dewar's White Label's Highland blend with Aberfeldy-malt core swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a scotch. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Dewar's White Label for the steak course.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Disaronno": "Disaronno's sweet liqueur character fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- sweet liqueur jars against the savory sear — no bridge between dessert sugar and a seared main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a digestif. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Disaronno for the steak course.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye": "Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye's rye-spice character flattens the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Doc Swinson's Alter Ego Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum": "Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum's aged-rum register with molasses and oak depth overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- heavy spirit weight buries the main protein — needs a wine or lighter spirit. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a heavy spirit. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Doctor Bird Jamaica Rum for the steak course.",
  "Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna|Domaine Serene Grand Cheval": "Domaine Serene Grand Cheval's silky Pinot Noir with cherry-and-forest-floor lift overshadows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dark-fruit weight crowds the seared sesame crust — needs a sparkling or crisp white for lift. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not an elegant red. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Domaine Serene Grand Cheval for the steak course.",
