  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Kansas City": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's classic yellow-label Champagne body underclubs the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- sparkling sits short of the prime cut's weight. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a sparkling. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Veuve Clicquot Brut lands on another course — not here.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Loaded Potato": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity reads alongside the loaded potato's richness — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the apple notes lift the custard. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Lobster Mac": "Yellow label Veuve — Reims Champagne, ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse. The creamy body aligns with lobster-mac's rich cream-cheese weight and the lively acidity cuts through. Works as crowd-pleasing Champagne for the lobster mac side — indulgence meets celebration side-by-side.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Market Fish": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity lifts the fish's clean flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the brioche notes meet the herb butter. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Mocha Creme": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's classic yellow-label Champagne body clashes with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- brut bubbles and chocolate weight refuse each other — needs sweetness, not crisp acid. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a sparkling. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Veuve Clicquot Brut for the steak course.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Mushroom Bisque": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity sits alongside the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the apple notes lift the custard. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Mushrooms": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's classic yellow-label Champagne body reads alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami weight: the pinot-driven lift brightens the mushrooms, and the toast-and-citrus edge frames the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's classic yellow-label Champagne body clashes with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- brut bubbles and chocolate weight refuse each other — needs sweetness, not crisp acid. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a sparkling. Avoid; the better calls are above. Veuve Clicquot Brut belongs on the steak course.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Porterhouse": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's classic yellow-label Champagne body underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- sparkling reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves a structured red. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a sparkling. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Veuve Clicquot Brut for another course.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Prime Tartare": "Yellow label Veuve — Reims Champagne with ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse, lively acidity. The Pinot Noir dominance meets raw prime beef's iron richness and the biscuit plays against the seasoned tartare. Peak recognizable Champagne for tartare — everyone's favorite pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Rainbow Trout": "Veuve Clicquot Brut -- yellow-label Champagne, pinot-driven blend, restaurant-pour standard. The pinot-driven lift brightens the trout, and the toast-and-citrus edge frames the plate. Strong; reliable match across the courses.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Roast Half Chicken": "Veuve Clicquot Brut — yellow label, Pinot Noir-dominant Reims Champagne. Apple, peach, biscuit, creamy. The creamy body handles roasted chicken; preparation changes seasonally — check with kitchen for today's build. Works as reliable Champagne pour for chicken across most seasonal builds.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity holds with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity sits with the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the brioche notes meet the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity sits alongside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the lively bubbles sit with the light side, and the apple notes lift the custard. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Seafood Tower": "Veuve Clicquot Brut — iconic yellow label Champagne from Reims, Pinot Noir-dominant blend with ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse, lively acidity. The balanced crowd-pleaser resets palate between tower elements and the biscuit plays against rich shellfish. Peak reliable Champagne for the tower. Gold standard; the recommendation that earns the regular.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Seared Scallops": "Yellow label Veuve — Pinot Noir-dominant Reims Champagne with ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse, lively acidity. The balanced structure frames scallops' clean-seared sweetness and the biscuit echoes the pan-butter. Peak recognizable Champagne for scallops — reliable celebration pour. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Seasonal Soup": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity reads alongside the seasonal soup's house build — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Seasonal Vegetables": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity sits beside the seasonal garden plate — the lively bubbles sit with the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Shrimp Bisque": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity sits alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate, and the apple notes lift the custard. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Shrimp Cocktail": "Veuve Clicquot Brut — iconic yellow label. Reims Chardonnay blend with ripe apple, white peach, biscuit, creamy mousse. The lively acidity cuts chilled shrimp's sweet brine and the creamy body plays against cocktail-sauce. Peak crowd-pleasing Champagne for shrimp cocktail — approachable and reliable. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Swordfish": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity sits with the swordfish's meaty flesh — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the brioche notes meet the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|The Tomahawk": "Yellow label Veuve against the biggest marbled steak on the menu — even iconic creamy mousse has no weight to meet Tomahawk fat and char. Never pair. Route Tomahawk to Caymus, Opus, or Barolo; save Veuve Brut for the opener, toast, or starter course.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Tomato Basil": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright acid — the mousse refreshes against the broth, and the brioche notes meet the herb butter. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Truffle Fries": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity reads alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the mousse-and-acid sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity plays cleanly against the tuna's seared rare crust — the bright acidity frames the fish without weight, and the apple-pear cuts through the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Veuve Clicquot Brut|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Veuve Clicquot Brut's biscuit-apple acidity finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the mousse refreshes against the broth. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Vieux Carré|Asparagus": "Vieux Carré's aromatic spirit-driven cocktail body reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bold-mix depth handles the asparagus, and the bitters edge frames the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Vieux Carré|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Dom Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. NOLA classic — the rye-cognac split and Bénédictine's herbal depth meet manchego's aged character. Complex cocktail for a complex potato side. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Vieux Carré|Beignets": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build sits alongside the beignet's warm sugar-dusted pastry — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Vieux Carré|Bone Marrow": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The rye-cognac split handles marrow's fatty weight from both sides — spice cuts, richness meets. Bénédictine's herbal edge adds complexity. Peak cocktail route for guest wanting sophistication with the marrow course.",
  "Vieux Carré|Bone-In Filet": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build plays cleanly against the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey-build weight frames the lean cut, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Vieux Carré|Broccoli Cheddar": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cream — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Vieux Carré|Broccolini": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build plays cleanly against the broccolini's tender green — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Vieux Carré|Brussels and Belly": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The rye-cognac split handles the belly's rendered fat and the Bénédictine adds herbal depth against brussels char. Works — though the Bowdie's or Espresso Old Fashioned peak higher with the belly's glaze.",
  "Vieux Carré|Burrata": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The cocktail's layered weight crushes burrata's creamy-delicate profile; rye-cognac-Bénédictine has no hook into basil-tomato-cheese. Never pair — burrata belongs with Prosecco or a crisp white, not the Vieux Carré.",
  "Vieux Carré|Butternut Squash": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build finds neutral with the squash soup's silky sweet — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Vieux Carré|Carrot Cake": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build sits alongside the carrot cake's spiced cream cheese — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Vieux Carré|Cheesecake": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. Cognac richness plays against vanilla-bean cream and the herbal complexity adds depth. Works if the guest carries the cocktail through dessert; Sauternes or Espresso Martini peak higher for cheesecake.",
  "Vieux Carré|Chilean Seabass": "Vieux Carré's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Vieux Carré for the steak course.",
  "Vieux Carré|Chocolate Brownie": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto vermouth, Bénédictine, two bitters. The cognac richness meets the brownie's fudge base and Bénédictine's herbal depth plays against cocoa, but the cocktail isn't dessert-built. Works if guest stays with the Vieux Carré; Espresso OF peaks higher.",
  "Vieux Carré|Chocolate Cake": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build leans against the cake's layered chocolate — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Vieux Carré|Clam Chowder": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build stays alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the botanicals lift the shellfish. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Vieux Carré|Cowboy Ribeye": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. The rye-cognac split and Bénédictine's herbal depth meet the Cowboy's marbled fat well; complexity layers with crust character. Strong alternative to Cab-forward or Old Fashioned options for the fatty cut.",
  "Vieux Carré|Crab Cake": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto vermouth, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. Layered spirit-forward weight crushes crab's sweet-delicate meat. Never pair — crab cake belongs with Sauvignon Blanc, Champagne, or a lighter opener, not a complex whiskey-cognac cocktail.",
  "Vieux Carré|Creamed Spinach": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. The cocktail's layered herbal-spirit complexity sits alongside the side's dairy weight; Bénédictine's herbal note plays against creamed greens. Strong cocktail pour alongside the classic spinach preparation.",
  "Vieux Carré|Creamy Potato": "Vieux Carré's aromatic spirit-driven cocktail body finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bold-mix depth handles the potato soup, and the bitters edge frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Vieux Carré|Creme Brulee": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The cocktail's cognac-and-rye split can meet the torched-sugar crust but the Bénédictine's herbal edge doesn't fully align with vanilla custard. Works rather than peaks — Moscato and Vin Santo shine brighter for brûlée.",
  "Vieux Carré|Escargot": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build matches the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey-build depth cuts the umami fat, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Vieux Carré|Faroe Island Salmon": "Vieux Carré's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Vieux Carré for the steak course.",
  "Vieux Carré|Filet Mignon": "Sazerac rye and Pierre Ferrand cognac split, Contratto vermouth, Bénédictine, Angostura and Peychaud's bitters. The rye-cognac split gives both spice and richness; Bénédictine's herbal depth plays against butter-tender filet. Peak sophisticated cocktail for the most delicate cut.",
  "Vieux Carré|French Onion": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The rye-cognac split handles French Onion's deep beef-broth savoriness and Bénédictine's herbal edge plays against the caramelized onion. Works as spirit-forward cocktail for French Onion — sophisticated route; wines peak higher as dedicated pairings.",
  "Vieux Carré|Grilled Caesar": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. The cocktail's herbal complexity and cognac depth can stand alongside grilled romaine's smoke; Bénédictine plays against anchovy-parmesan dressing. Works when the guest wants a spirit-forward opener alongside a charred salad.",
  "Vieux Carré|Gumbo": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build stays alongside the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the spice frames the umami depth. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Vieux Carré|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build plays cleanly against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side, and the herbs echo the green plate. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Vieux Carré|House Wedge": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto vermouth, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. The cocktail's complexity is heavier than the iceberg wedge demands, but Bénédictine's herbal depth plays off the blue cheese. Works as an opener when guest leads with a layered classic.",
  "Vieux Carré|Kansas City": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. The rye's spice cuts the KC's lean-beefy strip while cognac's richness meets the char; Bénédictine's herbal complexity adds depth. Peak Vieux Carré pairing with the most approachable bold cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Vieux Carré|Loaded Potato": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The rye-cognac depth meets the soup's bacon-cheddar weight and Bénédictine's herbal edge plays against scallion. Works as spirit-forward for loaded potato — sophisticated cocktail route; wines peak higher as dedicated pairings.",
  "Vieux Carré|Lobster Mac": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build stays alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the whiskey-build weight settles into the rich side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Vieux Carré|Market Fish": "Vieux Carré's spirit-forward cocktail register smothers the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the bitters-and-bourbon register has no foil against gentle flesh. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Vieux Carré for the steak course.",
  "Vieux Carré|Mocha Creme": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build holds with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the herbs cut the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Vieux Carré|Mushroom Bisque": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The rye-cognac depth meets the bisque's umami weight and Bénédictine's herbal edge plays against the mushroom earth. Works as spirit-forward for the bisque — sophisticated cocktail route; wines peak higher as dedicated pairings.",
  "Vieux Carré|Mushrooms": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The Bénédictine's herbal complexity meets the mushroom side's earth directly and the rye-cognac split handles the butter-sauté richness. Strong pairing — a nuanced cocktail for a nuanced side.",
  "Vieux Carré|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build stays alongside the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the whiskey-build sweetness threads the dessert, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Vieux Carré|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: this bold, beefy strip's firm texture and savory char meets this bold whiskey-forward cocktail's oak, spice, and spirit depth. The whiskey's barrel depth and the steak's flame-grilled fat run at the same intensity — a classic steakhouse move. Strong — steakhouse classic.",
  "Vieux Carré|Prime Tartare": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto vermouth, Bénédictine, Angostura, Peychaud's. The cocktail's layered complexity meets raw prime beef's iron richness; cognac and rye echo different facets of the tartare. Peak pre-dinner pour for the guest who wants the historically weighty cocktail.",
  "Vieux Carré|Rainbow Trout": "Vieux Carré's spirit-forward cocktail register smothers the trout's delicate flesh -- the bitters-and-bourbon register has no foil against gentle flesh. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Vieux Carré for the steak course.",
  "Vieux Carré|Roast Half Chicken": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build holds with the chicken's herbed crisp skin — the whiskey-build depth carries the herb-crisped bird, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Vieux Carré|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Vieux Carré|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Vieux Carré's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Vieux Carré lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Vieux Carré|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build plays cleanly against the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; capable alongside. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Vieux Carré|Seafood Tower": "Vieux Carré's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- whiskey-cocktail weight is wrong for cold shellfish — the opener needs lift, not bitters. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Vieux Carré for the steak course.",
  "Vieux Carré|Seared Scallops": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The cocktail's full spirit weight obliterates the scallops' delicate sweetness; rye spice and cognac richness have no hook into the clean seared profile. Never pair — scallops demand crisp whites.",
  "Vieux Carré|Seasonal Soup": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build leans against the seasonal soup's house build — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Vieux Carré|Seasonal Vegetables": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build holds with the seasonal garden plate — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light side. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Vieux Carré|Shrimp Bisque": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build stays alongside the bisque's creamy shellfish soup — the whiskey-build depth matches the hearty soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Vieux Carré|Shrimp Cocktail": "Vieux Carré's spirit-forward cocktail register overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- whiskey-cocktail weight is wrong for cold shellfish — the opener needs lift, not bitters. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Vieux Carré on the steak course instead.",
  "Vieux Carré|Swordfish": "Vieux Carré's spirit-forward cocktail register fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- cocktail bitterness pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Vieux Carré for the steak course.",
  "Vieux Carré|The Tomahawk": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the whiskey-build weight matches the cap-fat, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Vieux Carré|Tomato Basil": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build holds with the tomato basil's bright acid — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup, and the herbs play the buttery edge. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Vieux Carré|Truffle Fries": "Sazerac rye, Pierre Ferrand cognac, Contratto, Bénédictine, two bitters. The cocktail's herbal complexity from Bénédictine meets truffle oil's earth directly and the rye-cognac depth handles parmesan weight. Peak sophisticated pairing when the fries anchor the table. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Vieux Carré|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Vieux Carré's spirit-forward cocktail register clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- a whiskey-forward cocktail is built for the cut, not the fish or chicken course. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a spirit-forward cocktail. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Vieux Carré for the steak course.",
  "Vieux Carré|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Vieux Carré's bold herbal-spice build finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the whiskey-build weight overshoots the light soup. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Villon Cognac|Asparagus": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- cognac on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a cognac. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Villon Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Villon Cognac|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Villon with the au gratin — cocktail-ready cognac against cheese-and-cream potatoes. Works; Villon handles the richness at approachable register.",
  "Villon Cognac|Beignets": "Villon with beignets — contemporary cognac against fried dough. Works; Villon's approachable sweetness is a casual-dessert finisher.",
  "Villon Cognac|Bone Marrow": "Villon with bone marrow — saffron/elderflower Courvoisier-sibling against umami fat. Strong; Villon's modern profile cuts marrow at contemporary register, though Courvoisier proper's drier oak cuts it harder.",
  "Villon Cognac|Bone-In Filet": "Villon Cognac's barrel-aged cognac character sits alongside the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the bone-enhanced filet, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the cut. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Villon Cognac|Broccoli Cheddar": "Villon Cognac's aged-grape brandy depth finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the eau-de-vie body carries the broccoli cheddar, and the dried-fruit note underlines the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Villon Cognac|Broccolini": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- cognac on a green vegetable side is wrong direction — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a cognac. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Villon Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Villon Cognac|Brussels and Belly": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge reads alongside Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the brandy depth wraps the pork-belly plate, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Villon Cognac|Burrata": "Villon with burrata — contemporary cognac against fresh dairy. Avoid; cognac weight wrong for burrata's cream.",
  "Villon Cognac|Butternut Squash": "Villon Cognac's aged-grape brandy depth leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the brandy depth wraps the squash bisque, and the dried-fruit note underlines the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Villon Cognac|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet saffron/elderflower-infused cognac spirit. Villon's saffron marries the cake's spice with distinctive modern character — the featured Villon pairing where the infusion actively shines. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Villon Cognac|Cheesecake": "Villon with cheesecake — contemporary cognac against custard dessert. Excellent; Villon's modern profile and soft elderflower edge threads the cheesecake at accessible register.",
  "Villon Cognac|Chilean Seabass": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; one of those is the move. Villon Cognac belongs on the steak course.",
  "Villon Cognac|Chocolate Brownie": "Villon with the chocolate brownie — contemporary cocktail-ready cognac against cocoa. Excellent; Villon's accessible-sweet profile and saffron notes find chocolate naturally — more dessert-forward than Hennessy's benchmark.",
  "Villon Cognac|Chocolate Cake": "Villon Cognac's rancio-edged cognac weight sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the chocolate cake, and the oak-and-vanilla edge brightens the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Villon Cognac|Clam Chowder": "Villon Cognac's barrel-aged cognac character reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the brandy depth wraps the chowder, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; Ferrand's craft character handles the richness.",
  "Villon Cognac|Cowboy Ribeye": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge leans against the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the brandy depth wraps the cap-fat ribeye, and the caramel rounds the cut. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Villon Cognac|Crab Cake": "Villon with crab cake — modern cognac spirit against delicate crab. Avoid; cognac weight wrong for seafood. Reach for a vodka or gin.",
  "Villon Cognac|Creamed Spinach": "Villon with creamed spinach — contemporary cognac against dairy-rich side. Strong; Villon's modern profile rides the cream.",
  "Villon Cognac|Creamy Potato": "Villon Cognac's rancio-edged cognac weight sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the cognac register matches the potato soup, and the rancio register softens the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Villon Cognac|Creme Brulee": "Villon with creme brulee — saffron/elderflower Courvoisier-sibling against vanilla custard and caramelized sugar. Excellent; Villon's infusion lifts the caramel with floral-spice distinction.",
  "Villon Cognac|Escargot": "Villon Cognac's Limousin-oak cognac register sits at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the eau-de-vie body carries the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; Ferrand's craft character frames the richness with unblended polish.",
  "Villon Cognac|Faroe Island Salmon": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a cognac. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Villon Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Villon Cognac|Filet Mignon": "Villon with the filet — Courvoisier's modern spirit aimed at younger drinkers, Cognac with saffron + elderflower \"Voyage\" infusion. Excellent; Villon's contemporary accessibility and light spice frame tenderloin at a register Hennessy's benchmark doesn't reach — the younger-guest cognac-steak pairing for guests new to the category.",
  "Villon Cognac|French Onion": "Villon Cognac's eau-de-vie body with oak reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the cognac register matches the French onion, and the oak-and-vanilla edge brightens the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Villon Cognac|Grilled Caesar": "Villon with the grilled Caesar — saffron/elderflower cognac against charred romaine and anchovy. Works; Villon's infusion meets the char at functional register.",
  "Villon Cognac|Gumbo": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the brandy depth wraps the gumbo, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Villon Cognac|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- cognac on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a cognac. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Villon Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Villon Cognac|House Wedge": "Villon with the House Wedge — modern cognac against iceberg and bleu. Works; Villon's infusion is over-specified for the wedge.",
  "Villon Cognac|Kansas City": "Villon with the KC — contemporary cognac spirit against an 18oz strip. Strong; Villon handles the strip at approachable register; Courvoisier's drier oak is still the featured cognac-KC pairing.",
  "Villon Cognac|Loaded Potato": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the brandy depth wraps the loaded potato soup, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Villon Cognac|Lobster Mac": "Villon with lobster mac — saffron-infused cognac spirit against pasta-and-lobster richness. Strong; Villon's saffron notes have a natural seafood-adjacent affinity the other cognacs can't claim.",
  "Villon Cognac|Market Fish": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- cognac is post-meal energy — wrong register for a main protein. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a cognac. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Villon Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Villon Cognac|Mocha Creme": "Villon Cognac's eau-de-vie body with oak reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the Limousin-oak weight handles the mocha creme, and the dried-fruit note underlines the dessert. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Villon Cognac|Mushroom Bisque": "Villon Cognac's barrel-aged cognac character sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the Limousin-oak weight handles the mushroom bisque, and the caramel threads the umami register. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Villon Cognac|Mushrooms": "Villon with mushrooms — cocktail-ready cognac spirit against earthy umami. Strong; Villon's infusion threads the mushrooms at contemporary register.",
  "Villon Cognac|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Villon with the PB brownie — modern cognac spirit against peanut butter and cocoa. Excellent; Villon's approachable sweetness meets the dessert density at contemporary register.",
  "Villon Cognac|Porterhouse": "Villon Cognac's barrel-aged cognac character sits alongside the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the dual-cut steak, and the caramel rounds the cut. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Villon Cognac|Prime Tartare": "Villon Cognac's rancio-edged cognac weight rests at register with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami: the Limousin-oak weight handles the tartare, and the caramel threads the umami register. Strong; Ferrand's craft character frames the richness with unblended polish.",
  "Villon Cognac|Rainbow Trout": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- cognac's aged-grape weight buries delicate fish — digestif belongs after the meal. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a cognac. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Villon Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Villon Cognac|Roast Half Chicken": "Villon Cognac's eau-de-vie body with oak leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the Limousin-oak weight handles the roast chicken, and the rancio register softens the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Villon Cognac|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Villon Cognac's rancio-edged cognac weight sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the chickpea soup, and the orchard-fruit thread frames the bowl. Works; Villon's infusion meets the char at functional register.",
  "Villon Cognac|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- cognac's aged-grape weight buries delicate fish — digestif belongs after the meal. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Villon Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Villon Cognac|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- cognac on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a cognac. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Villon Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Villon Cognac|Seafood Tower": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- cognac is wrong-course energy for the table's opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Villon Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Villon Cognac|Seared Scallops": "Villon with seared scallops — Courvoisier-sibling spirit against scallop delicacy. Avoid; cognac overwhelms scallops.",
  "Villon Cognac|Seasonal Soup": "Villon Cognac's Limousin-oak cognac register leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the cognac register matches the seasonal soup, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Villon Cognac|Seasonal Vegetables": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- cognac on a green vegetable side is wrong direction — needs crisp white instead. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a cognac. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Villon Cognac lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Villon Cognac|Shrimp Bisque": "Villon Cognac's rancio-edged cognac weight sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the shrimp bisque, and the dried-fruit note underlines the bowl. Works; Rémy's elegance handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Villon Cognac|Shrimp Cocktail": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge clashes with the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- cognac is wrong-course energy for the table's opener. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a cognac. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Villon Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Villon Cognac|Swordfish": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge fights the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- cognac's aged register pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a cognac. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Villon Cognac for the steak course.",
  "Villon Cognac|The Tomahawk": "Villon Cognac's bold cognac-vanilla character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the barrel-aged depth meets the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Villon Cognac|Tomato Basil": "Villon Cognac's Limousin-oak cognac register leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the aged-grape brandy body sits with the tomato basil, and the oak-and-vanilla edge brightens the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Villon Cognac|Truffle Fries": "Villon with truffle fries — modern cognac against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Villon's saffron-elderflower infusion meets the earthy-rich with distinctive contemporary character.",
  "Villon Cognac|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Villon Cognac's modern profile and soft elderflower edge fights the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- cognac's aged register pulls against the savory sear without resolving. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a cognac. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Villon Cognac on the steak course instead.",
  "Villon Cognac|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Villon Cognac's rancio-edged cognac weight finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the Limousin-oak weight handles the curry, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Vin Santo|Asparagus": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- sweet wine on green vegetable is wrong direction — needs crisp white. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Vin Santo on the steak course instead.",
  "Vin Santo|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Beignets": "Tuscan Vin Santo — Trebbiano-Malvasia straw-dried, sealed-barrel years, amber, honey, dried apricot, walnut, nutty oxidative. The honey clings to hot choux-fried dough and the walnut lifts powdered-sugar. Peak Vin Santo for beignets — traditional Tuscan dessert wine on the airy bite. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Vin Santo|Bone Marrow": "Never pair Vin Santo with bone marrow — oxidative honey drowns roasted-marrow unctuousness and the dried apricot clashes with beefy-bone savory. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Lail Daniel Cuvée. Vin Santo is a Tuscan finishing pour, not an appetizer.",
  "Vin Santo|Bone-In Filet": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Vin Santo belongs on another course.",
  "Vin Santo|Broccoli Cheddar": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Vin Santo on the steak course instead.",
  "Vin Santo|Broccolini": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Brussels and Belly": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Corazon Weller, Angel's Envy, or Maker's Mark, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Vin Santo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Burrata": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- sweet wine and fresh dairy compound the richness with no foil. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Vin Santo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Butternut Squash": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a dessert wine. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Carrot Cake": "* Vin Santo -- Tuscan dessert wine, dried-fruit and honey, biscotti pour. The sticky-sweet pour sits with the carrot cake, and the late-harvest sugar rounds the dessert. Serious peak dessert pour. Gold standard; the call that earns the regular.",
  "Vin Santo|Cheesecake": "Tuscan straw-dried dessert wine — honey, dried apricot, walnut, oxidative richness. The nutty intensity meets the graham-cracker-and-brown-sugar crust directly and the honey aligns with the vanilla-bean cream cheese body. One of the more interesting dessert pairings on the list. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Vin Santo|Chilean Seabass": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Vin Santo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Vin Santo|Chocolate Brownie": "Tuscan Vin Santo — Trebbiano-Malvasia straw-dried, sealed-barrel years, amber, honey, dried apricot, walnut, nutty oxidative. The walnut plays with whipped-chocolate frosting and the honey wraps fudge-brownie cocoa. Strong Vin Santo for chocolate brownie — Vintage Port's dark-chocolate intensity fits cocoa harder.",
  "Vin Santo|Chocolate Cake": "Tuscan Vin Santo — Trebbiano-Malvasia straw-dried, sealed-barrel years, amber, honey, dried apricot, walnut, nutty oxidative. The walnut plays with fudgy chocolate-cake crumb and the honey plays with buttermilk-dairy depth. Strong Vin Santo for chocolate cake — Vintage Port's structure handles cocoa-on-cocoa harder.",
  "Vin Santo|Clam Chowder": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Cowboy Ribeye": "Never pair Vin Santo with Cowboy Ribeye — Tuscan oxidative dessert wine's honey residual sugar muddles marbled-ribeye char and the walnut clashes with grilled-beef savory. Lead with Silver Oak Cabernet or Caymus for the 26oz bone-in. Vin Santo is a finishing pour.",
  "Vin Santo|Crab Cake": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Vin Santo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Vin Santo|Creamed Spinach": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Vin Santo on the steak course instead.",
  "Vin Santo|Creamy Potato": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Creme Brulee": "* Vin Santo -- Tuscan dessert wine, dried-fruit and honey, biscotti pour. The Sauternes-or-Port body carries the brulee, and the honey threads the cream. Serious peak dessert pour. Gold standard; the call that earns the regular.",
  "Vin Santo|Escargot": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Mata Hari Absinthe, or Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Faroe Island Salmon": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- dessert sugar refuses the rich-fish course — needs acid, not sweetness. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Vin Santo on the steak course instead.",
  "Vin Santo|Filet Mignon": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness overwhelms the filet's lean buttery tenderness -- sweet wine smothers the lean cut's buttery delicacy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Maker's Mark Cellar Aged, or Yamazaki 18 Year, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Vin Santo belongs on another course.",
  "Vin Santo|French Onion": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Vin Santo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Vin Santo|Grilled Caesar": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the Caesar's charred-romaine and anchovy edge -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Green Chartreuse, Tanqueray Gin, or Scavino Barolo, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Vin Santo on the steak course instead.",
  "Vin Santo|Gumbo": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- honeyed sugar on savory broth jars — wrong direction for the course. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- sweet wine and a sweet glaze double the sugar register — no foil. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Save the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|House Wedge": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the wedge's iceberg-and-bleu cool crunch -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Weller 12 Year, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Vin Santo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Kansas City": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain -- dessert wine belongs after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, Pio Cesare Barbaresco, or Pappy Van Winkle 13 Year Rye, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Vin Santo belongs on another course.",
  "Vin Santo|Loaded Potato": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Lobster Mac": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- sweet wine and a dairy side compound the richness — dessert energy on savory plate. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Vin Santo on the steak course instead.",
  "Vin Santo|Market Fish": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Vin Santo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Vin Santo|Mocha Creme": "Tuscan Vin Santo — Trebbiano-Malvasia straw-dried, sealed-barrel years, amber, honey, dried apricot, walnut, nutty oxidative. The walnut echoes espresso-roast bitterness and the honey plays with yolk-cream custard. Strong Vin Santo for mocha creme — Vintage Port's chocolate intensity edges higher on espresso.",
  "Vin Santo|Mushroom Bisque": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a dessert wine. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Mushrooms": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the mushrooms' earthy umami weight -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Los Vecinos Mezcal, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Vin Santo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Tuscan Vin Santo — Trebbiano-Malvasia straw-dried, sealed-barrel years, amber, honey, dried apricot, walnut, nutty oxidative. The walnut echoes peanut-butter-frosting nuttiness and the dried apricot lifts fudge-brownie brown-sugar depth. Peak Vin Santo for peanut butter brownie — nut-on-nut-plus-honey complexity. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Vin Santo|Porterhouse": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness flattens the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- honeyed sugar dulls the marbled char — the steak earns a savory pour. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Vin Santo lands on another course — not here.",
  "Vin Santo|Prime Tartare": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the tartare's raw beef-and-brine umami -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Belvedere Vodka, not a dessert wine. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Rainbow Trout": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- dessert wine on delicate fish is wrong-course energy — needs a dry white, not honeyed sugar. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Vin Santo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Vin Santo|Roast Half Chicken": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- honeyed sugar on savory broth jars — wrong direction for the course. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- dessert wine on delicate fish is wrong-course energy — needs a dry white, not honeyed sugar. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Vin Santo lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Vin Santo|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- sweet wine on green vegetable is wrong direction — needs crisp white. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Seafood Tower": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- dessert wine is wrong-course energy for the opener. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Seared Scallops": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness overwhelms the scallops' caramelized sear-and-sweet richness -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, or Joseph Mellot Sancerre, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Seasonal Soup": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Vin Santo belongs on the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Seasonal Vegetables": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- sweet wine on a savory side is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Shrimp Bisque": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness doubles down on the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup -- sweet wine on cream is dessert-course energy on a savory plate — heavy, no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Bacardi Rum, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Shrimp Cocktail": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- dessert sugar on cold shellfish is wrong direction — needs sparkling or Sancerre. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Swordfish": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- sweet wine dulls the sear's clean char — savory main deserves a dry pour. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a dessert wine. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|The Tomahawk": "Never pair Vin Santo with Tomahawk — straw-dried dessert wine gets bulldozed by the massive 36oz bone-in ribeye and honey residual-sugar clashes with smoky char. Lead with Scavino Barolo or Beaucastel Châteauneuf for the grand cut. Vin Santo belongs at dessert.",
  "Vin Santo|Tomato Basil": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- honeyed sugar on savory broth jars — wrong direction for the course. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Truffle Fries": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness flattens the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- dessert sugar on starch is wrong-course energy for the supporting plate. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a dessert wine. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- dessert wine on a main protein is wrong-course energy. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Vin Santo for the steak course.",
  "Vin Santo|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Vin Santo's Tuscan dried-fruit-and-honey sweetness clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- the soup-salad course needs acid, not dessert sugar. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a dessert wine. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Vin Santo on the steak course instead.",
  "Weller 107|Asparagus": "Weller 107's rounded-oak bourbon character sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak depth lays under the asparagus, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; the bourbon's character is distinctive but the pairing isn't a standout.",
  "Weller 107|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Weller 107 with au gratin potatoes — bigger-body wheated meets cheese-and-cream. Strong; the heat adds interest where Weller Special Reserve just sits beside the side.",
  "Weller 107|Beignets": "Weller 107's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the bourbon depth settles into the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Weller 107|Bone Marrow": "Bone Marrow: the rich umami fat meets Weller 107's heat and body. The highest-impact Weller for this starter; Weller 12 is more refined, Weller 107 matches the marrow's intensity. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Weller 107|Bone-In Filet": "Weller 107's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the brown-spirit warmth threads the bone-enhanced filet, and the oak-and-spice register settles the cut. Strong; aged premium at matched register.",
  "Weller 107|Broccoli Cheddar": "Weller 107's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; neutral, not elevating.",
  "Weller 107|Broccolini": "Weller 107's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon weight handles the broccolini, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Weller 107|Brussels and Belly": "Weller 107 with Brussels + Belly — higher-proof wheat meets maple-glazed pork belly. The heat cuts through the dish's sweetness in a way Weller Special Reserve's softness can't; a more assertive small-plate pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Weller 107|Burrata": "Weller 107 with burrata — fresh dairy cannot carry 107-proof wheat. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Weller 107|Butternut Squash": "Weller 107's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the squash bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the blend's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Weller 107|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: the cake's cream cheese and cinnamon meet Weller 107's higher-proof caramel. More energetic than Weller Special Reserve; heat gives the dessert spice-and-warmth dimension. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Weller 107|Cheesecake": "Weller 107 with cheesecake — clean custard meets 107-proof wheated. Works; the heat slightly overshoots the dessert's delicacy, but the caramel helps the match.",
  "Weller 107|Chilean Seabass": "Weller 107's bigger-body wheated buries scallop sweetness swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Weller 107 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Weller 107|Chocolate Brownie": "Weller 107 with the chocolate brownie — higher-proof wheat and cocoa in a bigger-body dessert pairing. The heat and caramel depth lift the brownie in a way Weller Special Reserve or Weller 12 can't; the Weller for dessert-and-bourbon guests. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Weller 107|Chocolate Cake": "Weller 107's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon backbone carries the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Weller 107|Clam Chowder": "Weller 107's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon body sits with the chowder, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Weller 107|Cowboy Ribeye": "Weller 107 with the Cowboy Ribeye — 107 proof earns the ribeye where Weller Special Reserve falls short. The cap fat meets the pour's bigger body head-on; this is the strongest Weller call for this cut (Weller 12 is the alternate refined pour). Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Weller 107|Crab Cake": "Weller 107 with the crab cake — 107 proof's heat against crab delicacy is a clean miss. Avoid; pour a white wine.",
  "Weller 107|Creamed Spinach": "Weller 107 with creamed spinach — the pour's heat against the dairy's richness. More assertive than Weller Special Reserve; the 107-proof kick gives the side dimension without burying it. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Weller 107|Creamy Potato": "Weller 107's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Weller 107|Creme Brulee": "Weller 107's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon body sits with the brulee, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Weller 107|Escargot": "Weller 107's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits at register with the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; the polished aged character is a refined premium counterpoint distinct from WP's Vermont-finished approach; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Weller 107|Faroe Island Salmon": "Weller 107's bigger-body wheated buries scallop sweetness overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Weller 107 lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Weller 107|Filet Mignon": "Weller 107 with the filet — the higher-proof Weller. The extra 17 proof points give this pour heat and body the Special Reserve doesn't have; frames the filet with more muscle. The Weller for guests who want wheated but also want a hit of alcohol warmth. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Weller 107|French Onion": "Weller 107's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the caramel-spice register matches the French onion, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; craft-blender complexity over-specified.",
  "Weller 107|Grilled Caesar": "Weller 107 with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine meets higher-proof heat. Works; the pour's boldness pushes slightly past what the Caesar needs, but the char absorbs some intensity.",
  "Weller 107|Gumbo": "Weller 107's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon backbone carries the gumbo, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; curated character over-specified.",
  "Weller 107|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Weller 107's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon body sits with the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; the bourbon-and-side match holds — neither soars nor fights.",
  "Weller 107|House Wedge": "Weller 107 with the House Wedge — iceberg and bleu against 107 proof. Works only as a separate sip; the pour's heat overpowers the crisp salad.",
  "Weller 107|Kansas City": "Weller 107 with the KC — the higher proof carries the strip's marbling in ways Weller Special Reserve can't quite reach. More viscous, more caramel-forward, more heat. The bolder wheated call for strip orders. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Weller 107|Loaded Potato": "Weller 107's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "Weller 107|Lobster Mac": "Weller 107's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon backbone carries the lobster mac, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; Boss Hog's allocation story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Weller 107|Market Fish": "Weller 107's bigger-body wheated buries scallop sweetness swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Weller 107 for the steak course.",
  "Weller 107|Mocha Creme": "Weller 107's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; bold intensity slightly over-specified.",
  "Weller 107|Mushroom Bisque": "Weller 107's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the caramel-spice register matches the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; a gentle composed match at the premium tier.",
  "Weller 107|Mushrooms": "Weller 107 with the mushrooms — the extra proof gives the pour weight against umami. Bolder than Weller Special Reserve here; the Weller for a guest who wants wheat character but doesn't want the pour to recede. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Weller 107|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Weller 107's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon depth settles into the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; flagship depth gives the dessert premium aged character.",
  "Weller 107|Porterhouse": "Porterhouse: the cut's strip-and-filet meets Weller 107. Higher proof handles the strip beautifully; the filet is a touch overpowered. Weller 12 is the more balanced call. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Weller 107|Prime Tartare": "Weller 107's bold caramel-char character sits with the tartare's raw beef and brine — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Weller 107|Rainbow Trout": "Weller 107's bigger-body wheated buries scallop sweetness flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Weller 107 for the steak course.",
  "Weller 107|Roast Half Chicken": "Weller 107's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the caramel-spice register matches the roast chicken, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Weller 107|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Weller 107's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak depth lays under the chickpea soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Weller 107|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Weller 107's bigger-body wheated buries scallop sweetness swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Weller 107 belongs on the steak course.",
  "Weller 107|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Weller 107's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the caramel-spice register matches the garlic spinach, and the oak frames the side. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "Weller 107|Seafood Tower": "Weller 107's bigger-body wheated buries scallop sweetness fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Reserve the Weller 107 for the steak course.",
  "Weller 107|Seared Scallops": "Weller 107 with seared scallops — bigger-body wheated buries scallop sweetness. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Weller 107|Seasonal Soup": "Weller 107's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; milestone-collector wasted on salad.",
  "Weller 107|Seasonal Vegetables": "Weller 107's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the brown-spirit warmth threads the seasonal vegetables, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; gentle match.",
  "Weller 107|Shrimp Bisque": "Weller 107 with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood meets higher-proof wheat. Works; the pour's assertiveness is a bit much for the soup but doesn't clash.",
  "Weller 107|Shrimp Cocktail": "Weller 107 with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp and higher-proof wheated don't bridge. Avoid; separate pours.",
  "Weller 107|Swordfish": "Weller 107's bigger-body wheated buries scallop sweetness overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Weller 107 on the steak course instead.",
  "Weller 107|The Tomahawk": "Weller 107's bold caramel-char character frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the aged-oak weight carries the flame-grilled char, and the caramel echoes the grilled char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Weller 107|Tomato Basil": "Weller 107's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the oak depth lays under the tomato basil, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; boutique-sourced wasted on salad.",
  "Weller 107|Truffle Fries": "Weller 107 with truffle fries — bolder wheated against truffle-parmesan fat. The higher proof doesn't overpower the truffle; it matches its earthiness with more heft than Weller Special Reserve. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Weller 107|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Weller 107's bigger-body wheated buries scallop sweetness overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Weller 107 for the steak course.",
  "Weller 107|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Weller 107's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon body sits with the curry, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Asparagus": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character reads alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon body sits with the asparagus, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; distinctive character doesn't drive.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Weller 12 Year with the au gratin potatoes — cheese and cream meet twelve years of oak-softened wheat. The age gives the pour enough weight to sit with the side's richness. A restrained pairing; the side still leads, the 12-year adds dimension. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Beignets": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the caramel-spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; complexity wasted on salad.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Bone Marrow": "Weller 12 Year with bone marrow — aged wheated sweetness against the marrow's umami fat. The 12-year's oak depth threads into the marrow's mineral finish. A richer pour than Weller Special Reserve for this starter; the extra age earns its place. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Bone-In Filet": "Weller 12 Year's wheated-vanilla character matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the whiskey character holds register with the tenderloin, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Broccoli Cheddar": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the wheated weight wraps the broccoli cheddar, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Broccolini": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon backbone carries the broccolini, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; the complex character is wasted on the salad.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Brussels and Belly": "Weller 12 Year -- aged wheated, honey, vanilla. The oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the pork-belly plate, and the vanilla rounds the flame-grilled char. Excellent; EHT Full Proof meets the glaze at historic Kentucky register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Burrata": "Weller 12 Year with burrata — twelve years of oak against fresh dairy and basil oil is a register mismatch. Avoid; the cream can't accommodate the pour's depth.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Butternut Squash": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the wheated weight wraps the squash bisque, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: the cake's raisins, cream cheese frosting, and cinnamon warmth meet Weller 12 Year's aged oak, caramel, and vanilla. A dessert pairing that shows what aged wheated bourbon does with cake spice; balance over intensity. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Cheesecake": "Weller 12 Year with cheesecake — aged oak and vanilla meet the cheesecake's custard. The pairing is composed but doesn't lift either element; Weller Special Reserve does this more cleanly. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Chilean Seabass": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character smothers the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Weller 12 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Chocolate Brownie": "Weller 12 Year with the chocolate brownie — oak and caramel against cocoa. The 12-year's refinement underserves the brownie's boldness; reach for Weller 107 or Weller Millennium for more interplay with chocolate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Chocolate Cake": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon weight handles the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; AE CS meets the char at premium wine-finish register.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Clam Chowder": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the oak depth lays under the chowder, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; Vermont-brand character doesn't drive.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Cowboy Ribeye": "Weller 12 Year with the Cowboy Ribeye — twelve years of aging delivers enough oak-softened depth to carry the ribeye's cap fat and bold char. Richer and more aligned than Weller Special Reserve here; where the younger Wellers are solid, Weller 12 shines. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Crab Cake": "Weller 12 Year with the crab cake — aged bourbon's weight overwhelms the crab's delicacy. Oak and the cake's gentleness don't meet. Reach for a crisp wine or a gin-based cocktail here. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Creamed Spinach": "Weller 12 Year with creamed spinach — the 12-year's aged oak and wheated sweetness thread into the dairy's richness. More refined than Weller Special Reserve here; the oak gives the side a backbone the younger Wellers don't. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Creamy Potato": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the brown-spirit warmth threads the potato soup, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the pour's sweetness meets the dessert without pushing past.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Creme Brulee": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the oak depth lays under the brulee, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; own-plus-sourced over-specified.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Escargot": "Weller 12 Year's wheated-vanilla character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Faroe Island Salmon": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Weller 12 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: the 10oz center-cut's clean beef purity and restraint meet Weller 12 Year's aged wheated oak, honey, and caramel. The 12-year's depth frames the filet without overpowering; the marquee wheated pour for this cut. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Weller 12 Year|French Onion": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon depth settles into the French onion, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Grilled Caesar": "Weller 12 Year with the grilled Caesar — anchovy salinity and charred romaine meet aged honeyed wheat. The 12-year's depth saves this pairing from being too polite; Weller Special Reserve and Weller 107 can't quite earn this Caesar the same way. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Gumbo": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon weight handles the gumbo, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side; save the rare bottle for the main.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the oak depth lays under the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; curated doesn't drive.",
  "Weller 12 Year|House Wedge": "Weller 12 Year with the House Wedge — iceberg's crispness and bleu's tang meet aged wheated oak. The pour overreaches for the salad's simplicity; works as a before-course sip, not a driving pairing. Gold standard; pour without second-guessing.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Kansas City": "Weller 12 Year with the KC — the strip's marbling and char meet twelve years of oak-softened wheat sweetness. Richer than Weller Special Reserve, more refined than Weller 107. The sipping bourbon for guests who want depth without heat on a premium strip. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Loaded Potato": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the brown-spirit warmth threads the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; the unique maturation story adds interest to a gentle dessert match.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Lobster Mac": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon weight handles the lobster mac, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; stout-barrel story wasted on salad.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Market Fish": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Weller 12 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Mocha Creme": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon backbone carries the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; 10-year depth overshoots delicacy.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Mushroom Bisque": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the wheated weight wraps the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Mushrooms": "Weller 12 Year with the mushrooms — aged honey and oak meet earthy umami. The 12-year's complexity gives the side layers; less approachable than Weller Special Reserve with mushrooms, more polished than Weller 107. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the caramel-spice register matches the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Porterhouse": "* Weller 12 Year -- aged wheated, honey, vanilla. The bourbon backbone carries the dual-cut steak, and the vanilla balances the char-and-fat. A distinctive wheated pour for filet. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Prime Tartare": "Weller 12 Year -- aged wheated, honey, vanilla. The wheated weight wraps the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the curated single-barrel is capable but single-distillery aged bourbons bring more traditional focused character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Rainbow Trout": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Weller 12 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Roast Half Chicken": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon depth settles into the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; high proof over-specified.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon body sits with the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; the complex character gives the dessert a refined frame.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Weller 12 Year lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Seafood Tower": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character drowns out the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- sweet-oak depth flattens the briny opener instead of lifting it. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Save the Weller 12 Year for the steak course.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Seared Scallops": "Weller 12 Year with seared scallops — aged oak and scallop delicacy don't bridge. Avoid; the seafood can't carry the bourbon's weight. Choose a restrained white wine instead.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Seasonal Soup": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the caramel-spice register matches the seasonal soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; the toast adds a warmth the dessert can accept.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Seasonal Vegetables": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon weight handles the seasonal vegetables, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; light body doesn't overshoot.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Shrimp Bisque": "Weller 12 Year with the shrimp bisque — aged wheated sweetness against cream-based seafood. The 12-year holds its own but doesn't particularly elevate the soup; it's a neutral companion. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Shrimp Cocktail": "Weller 12 Year with shrimp cocktail — cold poached shrimp and aged wheated bourbon don't find each other. The cocktail sauce's acid doesn't carry oak. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Swordfish": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Hold the Weller 12 Year for the steak course.",
  "Weller 12 Year|The Tomahawk": "Weller 12 Year's wheated-vanilla character frames the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon weight matches the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Tomato Basil": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character reads alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon body sits with the tomato basil, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; annual-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Truffle Fries": "Weller 12 Year with truffle fries — twelve years of oak and vanilla against truffle-parmesan fat. The aged wheated sweetness lifts the truffle's earthiness rather than competing with it. A refined pairing; Maker's Mark Cellar Aged is the alternate premium wheated call here. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Weller 12 Year for the steak course.",
  "Weller 12 Year|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Weller 12 Year's aged wheated honey-vanilla character finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the oak depth lays under the curry, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; bonded weight a touch ambitious for the dessert.",
  "Weller Millennium|Asparagus": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the caramel-spice register matches the asparagus, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Weller Millennium|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Au Gratin Potatoes: cheese and cream richness meet Weller Millennium's honeyed depth. The side holds its own beside the Millennium without either elevating; strong, not celebratory. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Weller Millennium|Beignets": "Weller Millennium's soft-oak bourbon body reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the oak depth lays under the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; a composed but not remarkable match.",
  "Weller Millennium|Bone Marrow": "Weller Millennium with bone marrow — the Millennium's extended oak and deep honey meet marrow's umami fat at a premium register. A ceremonial pour; the marrow's mineral finish picks up the bourbon's aged complexity cleanly. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Weller Millennium|Bone-In Filet": "Weller Millennium: 20-year wheated bourbon, heavily allocated, the white-whale of the Weller family. Excellent on bone-in filet; wheated softness frames the marrow-adjacent cut at luxury register — save the bottle for guests who know the family.",
  "Weller Millennium|Broccoli Cheddar": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon backbone carries the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; Sinatra Select handles the richness at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Weller Millennium|Broccolini": "Weller Millennium's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon weight handles the broccolini, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; AE Triple Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Weller Millennium|Brussels and Belly": "Weller Millennium with Brussels + Belly — the Millennium's honeyed depth against maple-glazed pork belly. More polished than Weller 12's version; the celebratory small-plate pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Weller Millennium|Burrata": "Burrata: fresh dairy and basil oil meet Weller Millennium's aged oak. Avoid; don't pour the Millennium against something that can't carry it.",
  "Weller Millennium|Butternut Squash": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon weight handles the squash bisque, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; narrative-bourbon doesn't drive.",
  "Weller Millennium|Carrot Cake": "Weller Millennium with carrot cake — deep wheated honey and the Millennium's oak against cream cheese and raisins. A dessert pairing that shows what rare wheated bourbon can do; reserved for guests who care about the detail. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Weller Millennium|Cheesecake": "Weller Millennium with cheesecake — custard clean and restrained meets premium wheated depth. Works; the Millennium's register overshoots the dessert's.",
  "Weller Millennium|Chilean Seabass": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Weller Millennium for the steak course.",
  "Weller Millennium|Chocolate Brownie": "Weller Millennium with the chocolate brownie — deep honeyed oak meets cocoa and fudge. The Millennium's complexity does more with the brownie than Weller 12 can; strong, and a more interesting pairing than the baseline suggests. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Weller Millennium|Chocolate Cake": "Weller Millennium's soft-oak bourbon body sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon backbone carries the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Weller Millennium|Clam Chowder": "Weller Millennium's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon depth settles into the chowder, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "Weller Millennium|Cowboy Ribeye": "Weller Millennium with the Cowboy Ribeye — ultra-premium wheated meets ultra-premium cut. The Millennium's deep oak carries the ribeye's cap fat and char with composure; a marquee pairing when the bill isn't the concern. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Weller Millennium|Crab Cake": "Weller Millennium with crab cake — premium bourbon against delicate crab. The Millennium deserves a better pairing; avoid, reach for the right white wine. Avoid; the pairing fights at the table — point the guest elsewhere.",
  "Weller Millennium|Creamed Spinach": "Weller Millennium with creamed spinach — Millennium-level depth meets a dairy side. The bourbon overreaches slightly for the side's register; strong but not the most deserving pour for the Millennium's scarcity. Strong; the call sits without strain.",
  "Weller Millennium|Creamy Potato": "Weller Millennium's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; blended character doesn't drive.",
  "Weller Millennium|Creme Brulee": "Weller Millennium's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon depth settles into the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; extended maturation gives the dessert home-state aged character.",
  "Weller Millennium|Escargot": "Weller Millennium's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon backbone carries the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; extra proof adds dimension at the premium register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Weller Millennium|Faroe Island Salmon": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Weller Millennium belongs on the steak course.",
  "Weller Millennium|Filet Mignon": "Weller Millennium with the filet — a special-occasion pour for a restrained cut. The Millennium release's polished deep oak, honey, and vanilla frame the filet in a way that's unmistakably premium. The top-tier wheated pour for filet when a guest wants the absolute best. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Weller Millennium|French Onion": "Weller Millennium's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the oak depth lays under the French onion, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; annual-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Weller Millennium|Grilled Caesar": "Weller Millennium with the grilled Caesar — the Millennium's aged honey and oak against charred romaine and anchovy. The Caesar actually meets this pour in a way House Wedge doesn't; strong but ambitious. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Weller Millennium|Gumbo": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon backbone carries the gumbo, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Weller Millennium|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Weller Millennium's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon depth settles into the honey carrots, and the oak threads the caramelized sugar. Works; a gentle composed match at the premium tier.",
  "Weller Millennium|House Wedge": "Weller Millennium with the House Wedge — the wedge's simplicity can't carry the Millennium's depth. Works only as a separate sip; a waste of the pour.",
  "Weller Millennium|Kansas City": "* Weller Millennium's grain-and-oak bourbon register runs straight into the KC's lean-bold strip with savory grain: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the strip steak, and the vanilla balances the char-and-fat. Layered conversation-starter pour. Gold standard; the pairing that defines the moment.",
  "Weller Millennium|Loaded Potato": "Weller Millennium's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon depth settles into the loaded potato soup, and the caramel softens the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Weller Millennium|Lobster Mac": "Weller Millennium's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon depth settles into the lobster mac, and the caramel-spice settles the cream. Works; own-plus-sourced over-specified.",
  "Weller Millennium|Market Fish": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Weller Millennium for the steak course.",
  "Weller Millennium|Mocha Creme": "Weller Millennium's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon weight handles the mocha creme, and the vanilla brightens the cream. Works; the blend's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Weller Millennium|Mushroom Bisque": "Weller Millennium's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon body sits with the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; AE CS's cask-strength character rides the cream at premium wine-finish register.",
  "Weller Millennium|Mushrooms": "Weller Millennium with the mushrooms — the Millennium's layered oak meets mushroom umami in a sophisticated interplay. The depth earns the pairing beyond 'just wheated and earth'; this is the premium Weller for this side. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Weller Millennium|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the bourbon body sits with the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; rich vanilla depth meets the brownie with small-batch character.",
  "Weller Millennium|Porterhouse": "Weller Millennium with the porterhouse — the Millennium's complexity handles both strip and filet sides. Where Weller 12 leans into strip and fades on filet, the Millennium's polish covers both. The premium wheated for this two-cut plate. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Weller Millennium|Prime Tartare": "Weller Millennium -- 20-year wheated bourbon, heavily allocated, the white-whale of the Weller family. The bourbon depth settles into the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; annual-release capable but single-distillery premium bourbons bring more focused character; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Weller Millennium|Rainbow Trout": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Weller Millennium belongs on the steak course.",
  "Weller Millennium|Roast Half Chicken": "Weller Millennium's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the oak depth lays under the roast chicken, and the vanilla softens the plate. Works; milestone-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Weller Millennium|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the caramel-spice register matches the chickpea soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; private-selection wasted on the salad.",
  "Weller Millennium|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Weller Millennium on the steak course instead.",
  "Weller Millennium|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Weller Millennium's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon body sits with the garlic spinach, and the oak frames the side. Works; bold proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Weller Millennium|Seafood Tower": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Weller Millennium lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Weller Millennium|Seared Scallops": "Weller Millennium with seared scallops — the Millennium's depth buries scallop delicacy. Avoid; pour a white wine or a clean cocktail.",
  "Weller Millennium|Seasonal Soup": "Weller Millennium's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal soup, and the vanilla softens the bowl. Works; bold intensity slightly over-specified.",
  "Weller Millennium|Seasonal Vegetables": "Weller Millennium's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon backbone carries the seasonal vegetables, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; X Commandments story wasted on salad.",
  "Weller Millennium|Shrimp Bisque": "Weller Millennium with the shrimp bisque — sea-driven cream meets aged wheated honey. Works without elevating either; the Millennium's scarcity suggests saving for a better pairing.",
  "Weller Millennium|Shrimp Cocktail": "Weller Millennium with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp meets ultra-premium wheated. Avoid; the Millennium doesn't belong alongside this starter.",
  "Weller Millennium|Swordfish": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Pour the Weller Millennium on the steak course instead.",
  "Weller Millennium|The Tomahawk": "Weller Millennium's bold wheated-oak character holds the line with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon character meets the rendered fat, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Weller Millennium|Tomato Basil": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; premium proof overshoots delicacy.",
  "Weller Millennium|Truffle Fries": "Weller Millennium with truffle fries — deep honeyed wheat against truffle-parmesan fat. The Millennium brings more gravitas than Weller 12 here; a pairing that feels like it belongs on a special-occasion menu. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Weller Millennium|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Weller Millennium on the steak course instead.",
  "Weller Millennium|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Weller Millennium's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; boutique-blend depth gives composed dessert character.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Asparagus": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the asparagus, and the caramel rounds the side. Works; extended-aged premium wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Weller Special Reserve with au gratin potatoes — approachable wheated meets cheese-and-cream depth. Cleanly matched registers; not revelatory, just composed. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Beignets": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the brown-spirit warmth threads the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; BIB over-specified for side.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Bone Marrow": "Weller Special Reserve with bone marrow — soft wheated sweetness against the marrow's umami fat. A clean, approachable match; Weller 12 is richer, Millennium is ceremonial — this is the everyday pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Bone-In Filet": "Weller Special Reserve -- wheated, soft vanilla, gentle oak. The oak depth lays under the bone-enhanced filet, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the cut. Strong; craft-blend narrative covers the cut with KO authority.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Broccoli Cheddar": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the bourbon body sits with the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Broccolini": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the caramel-spice register matches the broccolini, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; craft-blender complexity over-specified.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Brussels and Belly": "Brussels and Belly: maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly meet Weller Special Reserve's soft wheated sweetness. Approachable without being underwhelming; the gateway Weller handles the small plate cleanly. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Burrata": "Weller Special Reserve with burrata — fresh dairy against gateway wheated caramel. Avoid; the register mismatch is structural across all wheated bourbons.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Butternut Squash": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character sits alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the bourbon body sits with the squash bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; save the BTAC bottle for the protein course — the side doesn't deserve the pour.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: the cake's cream cheese and cinnamon meet Weller Special Reserve's caramel and vanilla. Balanced but accessible; Weller 12 brings more layers here. Strong; reliable across the meal.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Cheesecake": "Weller Special Reserve with cheesecake — soft wheated caramel against clean custard. A gentle match; works cleanly without any particular lift. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Chilean Seabass": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character clashes with the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the oily flesh and the brown-spirit oak refuse to meet cleanly. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Weller Special Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm cocoa and fudge meet Weller Special Reserve's approachable wheat. Works; for more cocoa interplay, reach for Weller 107 or Weller Millennium.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Chocolate Cake": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the bourbon depth settles into the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; light body doesn't overshoot.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Clam Chowder": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the bourbon backbone carries the chowder, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Cowboy Ribeye": "Weller Special Reserve with the Cowboy Ribeye — the cut's cap fat pushes slightly past the 90-proof Weller's register. Capable, but the ribeye earns Weller 12 or Weller 107 for true alignment. Strong as a choice; not the standout.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Crab Cake": "Weller Special Reserve with the crab cake — soft wheated sweetness against delicate crab. Avoid; the lump meat can't find the Weller's profile. A crisp wine or a gin is the better call.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Creamed Spinach": "Weller Special Reserve with creamed spinach — soft caramel-vanilla against dairy richness. Meets the side at its level; wheated honey threads into the cream without the oak depth of Weller 12. Strong; consistent recommendation across the table.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Creamy Potato": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the wheated softness matches the potato soup, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; the VR Twin Oak is over-specified for the soup's register.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Creme Brulee": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the brown-spirit warmth threads the brulee, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; boutique-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Escargot": "Weller Special Reserve's caramel-honey character lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Faroe Island Salmon": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Weller Special Reserve on the steak course instead.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Filet Mignon": "Filet Mignon: the 10oz center-cut's clean beef purity meets Weller Special Reserve's 90-proof caramel-vanilla warmth. The gateway wheated pour for filet; less refined than Weller 12 but perfectly poised for the cut. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|French Onion": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the bourbon weight handles the French onion, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; AE Triple Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Grilled Caesar": "Weller Special Reserve with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against 90-proof Weller. The pairing is capable but the gateway Weller doesn't have the depth Weller 12 brings to this Caesar. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Gumbo": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the bourbon depth settles into the gumbo, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; the distinctive character is underused by the salad's simplicity.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the wheated softness matches the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|House Wedge": "Weller Special Reserve with the House Wedge — iceberg's crispness and bleu's tang against soft wheated sweetness. The Weller works but doesn't lift the salad; a before-course sip more than a driver pairing. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Kansas City": "Weller Special Reserve with the KC — the strip's marbling meets Weller's entry-level caramel and honey. Not as rich as Weller 12, not as bold as Weller 107; the approachable strip pour when a guest wants wheated character without committing to the premium bottles. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Loaded Potato": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the oak depth lays under the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the pairing is pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Lobster Mac": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the bourbon depth settles into the lobster mac, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; bonded weight overpowers this dish.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Market Fish": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character flattens the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Weller Special Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Mocha Creme": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the caramel-spice register matches the mocha creme, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; neutral companion.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Mushroom Bisque": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon weight handles the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Mushrooms": "Weller Special Reserve with the mushrooms — approachable wheated honey meets earthy umami. The 90-proof softness lets the mushrooms lead; a gentle but aligned side pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the brown-spirit warmth threads the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Caribbean Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Porterhouse": "Weller Special Reserve with the porterhouse — both strip and filet sides meet the gateway wheated cleanly. The most versatile mediator of the Wellers for this two-cut plate, even if Weller 12 brings more refinement. Strong; the pairing reads at register.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Prime Tartare": "Weller Special Reserve -- wheated, soft vanilla, gentle oak. The wheated softness matches the tartare, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; smooth character sits with richness at matched register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Rainbow Trout": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Weller Special Reserve belongs on the steak course.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Roast Half Chicken": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the wheated softness matches the roast chicken, and the gentle oak frames the plate. Works; boutique-blend depth gives composed dessert character.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the chickpea soup, and the oak-and-spice register settles the bowl. Works; craft-blend covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Weller Special Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the garlic spinach, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Seafood Tower": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Weller Special Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Seared Scallops": "Weller Special Reserve with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against wheated bourbon. Avoid; even entry Weller overshoots the seafood's register.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Seasonal Soup": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon backbone carries the seasonal soup, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; oak sits beside the dessert gently.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Seasonal Vegetables": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the oak frames the side. Works; the bourbon-with-dessert pairing sits in the middle without contention.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Shrimp Bisque": "Weller Special Reserve with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood meets gateway wheated sweetness. Neutral match; the Weller holds its own but doesn't drive the pairing. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Shrimp Cocktail": "Weller Special Reserve with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp and soft wheated don't bridge. Avoid; separate pours in separate courses.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Swordfish": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Weller Special Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|The Tomahawk": "Weller Special Reserve's caramel-honey character holds steady against the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Tomato Basil": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the brown-spirit warmth threads the tomato basil, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the pairing is pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Truffle Fries": "Weller Special Reserve with truffle fries — caramel-vanilla wheat against truffle-parmesan fat. The entry Weller meets the truffle's earthiness without overreaching; a crowd-pleasing pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Weller Special Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Weller Special Reserve|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Weller Special Reserve's wheated soft-vanilla character sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon backbone carries the curry, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the Twin Oak's depth slightly overshoots the dessert's simplicity.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Asparagus": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality sits beside the asparagus's green-vegetal edge — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality sits beside the gratin's cheese-and-cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the pairing reads neutral at the table. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Beignets": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Ron Zacapa Rum, or G.D. Vajra Moscato d'Asti, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Save the Wheatley Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Bone Marrow": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality leans against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the clean profile cleans the rich starter without crowding. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Bone-In Filet": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a vodka. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Pour the Wheatley Vodka on another course instead.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Broccoli Cheddar": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality stays alongside the broccoli cheddar's cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Broccolini": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality stays alongside the broccolini's tender green — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Brussels and Belly": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality holds with the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Burrata": "Wheatley with burrata — BT wheat vodka against fresh dairy. Excellent; Wheatley's cleanness supports burrata without Chopin's texture-match or Goose's silk — straightforward value.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Butternut Squash": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality plays cleanly against the squash soup's silky sweet — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Carrot Cake": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Vin Santo, Disaronno, or Graham's 10 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Wheatley Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Cheesecake": "Wheatley with cheesecake — BT wheat vodka against custard. Works; Wheatley's cleanness is a soft dessert finisher.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Chilean Seabass": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality reads alongside the seabass's buttery flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Chocolate Brownie": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Louis XIII Cognac, not a vodka. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Pour the Wheatley Vodka on the steak course instead.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Chocolate Cake": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Taylor Fladgate Vintage Bottle, Graham's 2017 Vintage Port, or Baileys Irish Cream, not a vodka. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Wheatley Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Clam Chowder": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality stays alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Cowboy Ribeye": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Wheatley Vodka belongs on another course.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Crab Cake": "Wheatley with crab cake — Buffalo Trace's wheat vodka, 10x-distilled, house-made. Excellent; Wheatley's clean-wheat profile is value-premium character — a BT distillery product built for exactly this kind of delicate seafood pairing.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Creamed Spinach": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality sits alongside the spinach's dairy-rich cream — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Creamy Potato": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality leans against the potato soup's silky body — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Creme Brulee": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the brulee's burnt-sugar custard -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Sauternes Glass, Vin Santo, or Ron Zacapa Rum, not a vodka. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Wheatley Vodka lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Escargot": "Wheatley with escargot — BT's house-made wheat against garlic-butter snails. Strong; Wheatley's cleanness sits with butter; Belvedere's pepper still cuts sharper.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Faroe Island Salmon": "Wheatley with the salmon — Buffalo Trace wheat vodka against oily salmon. Strong; Wheatley's clean profile frames the fish at value register without fighting richness.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Filet Mignon": "Wheatley with the filet — Buffalo Trace wheat vodka against tenderloin. Works; vodka over-specified for steak, but Wheatley's BT pedigree is a clean functional match.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|French Onion": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality stays alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Grilled Caesar": "Wheatley with the grilled Caesar — BT wheat vodka against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Wheatley supports the Caesar cleanly; Ketel's citrus is still the sharper vodka-Caesar.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Gumbo": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced Creole stew — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality leans against the carrots' honeyed sweetness — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|House Wedge": "Wheatley with the House Wedge — Buffalo Trace wheat vodka against iceberg and bleu. Excellent; Wheatley's clean-wheat finish is the democratic pick for guests who like BT — the wedge works comfortably here.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Kansas City": "Wheatley with the KC — Buffalo Trace's wheat vodka against an 18oz strip. Avoid; even the BT pedigree can't make vodka match strip marbling. Reach for the BT bourbon side of the house.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Loaded Potato": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality finds neutral with the loaded potato's richness — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Lobster Mac": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality reads alongside the lobster mac's shellfish cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Market Fish": "Wheatley with the market fish — Buffalo Trace wheat vodka against featured fish. Works; Wheatley's cleanness handles fish at value register.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Mocha Creme": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness -- vodka brings no anchor — the dessert needs a flavor partner, not a clean spirit. The plate deserves Kahlua, Patron XO Cafe, or Frangelico, not a vodka. Avoid; one of those is the move. Reserve the Wheatley Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Mushroom Bisque": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality leans against the mushroom bisque's earthy cream — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Mushrooms": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality stays alongside the mushrooms' earthy umami — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality falls short of the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge -- vodka brings no flavor to bridge the chocolate's weight — neutral but wrong for the close. The plate deserves Frangelico, Disaronno, or Graham's 20 Year Tawny, not a vodka. Avoid; the better calls are above. Save the Wheatley Vodka for the steak course.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Porterhouse": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a vodka. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Wheatley Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Prime Tartare": "Wheatley with the tartare — BT house-made wheat vodka against raw beef. Strong; Wheatley's clean wheat supports tartare cleanly, though Belvedere's rye pepper is the distinctive choice.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Rainbow Trout": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality holds the line with the trout's delicate flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Roast Half Chicken": "Wheatley with the chicken — BT house-made wheat against roasted poultry. Works; Wheatley's cleanness supports chicken at value-premium register.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality holds steady against the halibut's salt-cured flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality sits beside the garlic-spinach's buttered greens — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Seafood Tower": "Wheatley with the seafood tower — Buffalo Trace wheat vodka against the raw bar. Excellent; Wheatley handles the tower at value register — a smart call when Goose isn't necessary.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Seared Scallops": "Wheatley with seared scallops — Buffalo Trace wheat vodka against scallop sweetness. Excellent; Wheatley's clean wheat frames scallops at value-premium register — a smart pick without Goose's price tag.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Seasonal Soup": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality plays cleanly against the seasonal soup's house build — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Seasonal Vegetables": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality plays cleanly against the seasonal garden plate — the neutral profile sits with the light side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Shrimp Bisque": "Wheatley with shrimp bisque — Buffalo Trace wheat vodka against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Wheatley's cleanness rides the bisque's richness without adding character.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Shrimp Cocktail": "Wheatley with shrimp cocktail — BT's house-made wheat vodka against cold shrimp. Excellent; Wheatley's clean finish is the value-premium option for the classic cold app.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Swordfish": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality holds steady against the swordfish's meaty flesh — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|The Tomahawk": "Wheatley Vodka's crystalline neutrality underclubs the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- vodka is too neutral to carry any prime cut. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a vodka. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Wheatley Vodka lands on another course — not here.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Tomato Basil": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality leans against the tomato basil's bright acid — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Truffle Fries": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality stays alongside the truffle fries' Parmesan-and-truffle salt — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality plays cleanly against the tuna's seared rare crust — the neutral profile frames the fish cleanly. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Wheatley Vodka|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Wheatley Vodka's crisp cream neutrality plays cleanly against the curry's spiced chickpea warmth — the vodka neutrality sits with the plate. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Asparagus": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the rye-and-oak weight handles the asparagus, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; the pour's sweetness meets the dessert without pushing past.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Au Gratin Potatoes": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the rye spice cuts the au gratin, and the oak softens the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Beignets": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the high-rye spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; spice over-specified for side's character.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Bone Marrow": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with bone marrow — 100-proof aged WP rye against umami fat. The aged Vermont-finished character meets marrow with premium single-barrel authority. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Bone-In Filet": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's pepper-spice character lifts the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Broccoli Cheddar": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the rye spice cuts the broccoli cheddar, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the complex character is wasted on the salad.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Broccolini": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the rye spice cuts the broccolini, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; milestone-release doesn't drive.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Brussels and Belly": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with Brussels + Belly — aged WP single-barrel meets maple-glazed pork belly. The 10-year Vermont-finished depth cuts the glaze with premium rye authority. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Burrata": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with burrata — fresh dairy against aged single-barrel rye. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Butternut Squash": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the rye backbone drives the squash bisque, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; complexity wasted on salad.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Carrot Cake": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the high-rye spice register matches the carrot cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Cheesecake": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye-and-oak weight handles the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; HH 20 is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Chilean Seabass": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Hold the WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye for the steak course.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Chocolate Brownie": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the chocolate brownie — aged WP rye against cocoa. Works; 10-year depth meets the brownie cleanly at premium rye register.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Chocolate Cake": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the dry rye body wraps the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the spicy-sweet character gives the dessert a Barton frame.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Clam Chowder": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the chowder, and the oak threads the cream. Works; the bourbon-and-side match sits without pulling focus.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Cowboy Ribeye": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the Cowboy Ribeye — 100-proof 10-year single-barrel against cap fat. The aged WP depth and cut's marbling find each other at the premium rye register. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Crab Cake": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with crab cake — aged WP rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Creamed Spinach": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with creamed spinach — aged 100-proof single-barrel against dairy. Strong; Vermont-finished character sits with richness.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Creamy Potato": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the dry rye body wraps the potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Creme Brulee": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye-and-oak weight handles the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Escargot": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's pepper-spice character holds the line with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Faroe Island Salmon": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Filet Mignon": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the filet — aged 100-proof WP single-barrel against the 10oz cut. Strong; the premium rye depth frames the filet well but WP 15 Year or Michter's 10 Year bring more refined aged character for this cut.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|French Onion": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the French onion, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; the pour's brightness is a gentle counterpoint to the dessert's richness.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Grilled Caesar": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the grilled Caesar — 100-proof aged rye against charred romaine. Works; aged character doesn't quite drive the Caesar.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Gumbo": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the rye backbone drives the gumbo, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Honey Roasted Carrots": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the honey carrots, and the oak softens the caramelized sugar. Works; craft-blend covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|House Wedge": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the House Wedge — aged WP single-barrel against iceberg and bleu. Works; premium aged rye wasted on the salad's simplicity.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Kansas City": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the KC — aged 10-year single-barrel rye against the strip's marbling. The WP-core character gives the strip a premium single-barrel rye frame distinct from approachable tier Kentucky ryes. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Loaded Potato": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the rye spice cuts the loaded potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; smooth character is slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Lobster Mac": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the rye backbone drives the lobster mac, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Market Fish": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Mocha Creme": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the dry rye body wraps the mocha creme, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; premium single-barrel wasted on the salad.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Mushroom Bisque": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the mushroom bisque, and the oak threads the umami plate. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Mushrooms": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the mushrooms — aged WP rye against earthy umami. Strong; 10-year depth layers into mushroom character at premium register.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the PB brownie — aged WP single-barrel against peanut butter and cocoa. Works; premium rye gives the dessert substantial depth.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Porterhouse": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the porterhouse — WP-core across strip and filet. Strong; aged 100-proof covers both cuts with premium rye character.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Prime Tartare": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the tartare — premium aged rye against raw beef. Strong; the WP-core aged depth is a refined counterpoint to tartare.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Rainbow Trout": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye for the steak course.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the roast chicken, and the rye-grain bite cuts the plate. Works; master-blender doesn't drive.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the rye-and-oak weight handles the chickpea soup, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character swallows the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye for the steak course.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye-and-oak weight handles the garlic spinach, and the rye-grain bite cuts the side. Works; a safe alongside, no friction.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Seafood Tower": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye for the steak course.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Seared Scallops": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against aged WP. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Seasonal Soup": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the high-rye spice register matches the seasonal soup, and the rye-grain bite cuts the bowl. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye spice cuts the seasonal vegetables, and the rye-pepper note underlines the side. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the dry rye body wraps the shrimp bisque, and the oak threads the cream. Works; Old World complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against 10-year WP. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Swordfish": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|The Tomahawk": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with the Tomahawk — WP's core 10-year single-barrel, 100 proof. Vermont-finished Canadian-sourced rye at its WhistlePig-core depth; the aged rye and bone-in ribeye find each other in a classic premium rye-steak pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Tomato Basil": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the high-rye spice register matches the tomato basil, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; IW Harper 15 handles dessert density at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Truffle Fries": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel with truffle fries — 10-year WP single-barrel against truffle-parmesan. The aged depth gives the side real grip at the WP-core premium register. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Reserve the WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye for the steak course.",
  "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "WhistlePig 10 Year Single Barrel Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the curry, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; the pour's toasted warmth threads through the char without overpowering.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Asparagus": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the rye spice cuts the asparagus, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; GTS is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Au Gratin Potatoes": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the rye-and-oak weight handles the au gratin, and the oak softens the cream. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Beignets": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the rye spice cuts the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; HH 20 handles dessert density at collector register.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Bone Marrow": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with bone marrow — wine-cask-finished WP against umami fat. The Sauternes/Madeira/Port complexity gives marrow a layered rye-and-fruit counterpoint. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Bone-In Filet": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's sweet char-honey character sits with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the sweet character meets the cut at digestif register, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Broccoli Cheddar": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the high-rye spice register matches the broccoli cheddar, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; Voyage 23 frames the cut at ocean-finish register.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Broccolini": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character finds neutral with the broccolini's tender green stalks: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the broccolini, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the side. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Brussels and Belly": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with Brussels + Belly — fruit-cask WP meets maple-glazed pork belly. The wine-cask fruit character and maple find each other in Old World finishing logic; a complex small-plate pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Burrata": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with burrata — fresh dairy against fruit-cask 12-year. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Butternut Squash": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the squash bisque, and the oak threads the cream. Works; JW Black handles the richness at premium-blend register.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Carrot Cake": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character finds neutral with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the rye backbone drives the carrot cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Kentucky Owl is over-specified for the side — save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Cheesecake": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye backbone drives the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Chilean Seabass": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the WhistlePig 12 Year Old World for the steak course.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Chocolate Brownie": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with the chocolate brownie — Sauternes/Madeira/Port-finished rye against cocoa. Works; the Old World fruit gives the brownie layered depth distinct from straight-aged premium ryes.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Chocolate Cake": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character finds neutral with the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; extended-aged premium wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Clam Chowder": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World's rye-spice character sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the dry rye body wraps the chowder, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; rye spice doesn't particularly elevate the soup.",
  "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World|Cowboy Ribeye": "WhistlePig 12 Year Old World with the Cowboy Ribeye — fruit-cask-finished 12-year against cap fat. The Sauternes/Madeira/Port influence gives the ribeye layered fruit-and-oak complexity unique to Old World finishing. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
