  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Rainbow Trout": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Woodford Reserve Double Oaked on the steak course instead.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Roast Half Chicken": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon body sits with the roast chicken, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the plate. Works; the unique maturation story adds interest to a gentle dessert match.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the caramel-spice register matches the chickpea soup, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; the toast adds a warmth the dessert can accept.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth smothers the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- the brown-spirit weight pulls focus off the delicate fish protein. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Woodford Reserve Double Oaked lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon body sits with the garlic spinach, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the side. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Seafood Tower": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Woodford Reserve Double Oaked lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Seared Scallops": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with seared scallops — Brown-Forman Double Oaked against scallop delicacy. Avoid; bourbon overwhelms scallops.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Seasonal Soup": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's grain-and-oak bourbon register leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the bourbon body sits with the seasonal soup, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; the pour's toasted warmth threads through the char without overpowering.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Seasonal Vegetables": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon backbone carries the seasonal vegetables, and the rounded sweetness brightens the side. Works; the boldness pushes slightly past what the soup asks for.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Shrimp Bisque": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with shrimp bisque — iconic Double-Oaked against cream-shrimp soup. Avoid; Double-Oaked caramel clashes with seafood soup.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Shrimp Cocktail": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with shrimp cocktail — Brown-Forman Double Oaked against cold shrimp. Avoid; bourbon wrong for cold seafood.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Swordfish": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth flattens the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the seared crust without lifting it. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Woodford Reserve Double Oaked belongs on the steak course.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|The Tomahawk": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's rounded-oak bourbon character sits at register with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the bourbon body sits with the 36oz showpiece, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; Sinatra Select's extra oak meets the char at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Tomato Basil": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon depth settles into the tomato basil, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; annual-release wasted on salad.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Truffle Fries": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked with truffle fries — Brown-Forman KY Double Oaked against truffle-parmesan. Excellent; Woodford Double Oaked's caramel-oak meets the earthy-rich at KY-benchmark register.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth overwhelms the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Woodford Reserve Double Oaked lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Woodford Reserve Double Oaked's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon depth settles into the curry, and the toffee-vanilla edge underlines the bowl. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Asparagus": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the asparagus, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the side. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Woodford Reserve Rye with au gratin potatoes — elegant small-batch against cheese-and-cream. Works; fruit-forward at matched register.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Beignets": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the high-rye spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Bone Marrow": "Woodford Reserve Rye with bone marrow — fruit-forward rye against umami fat. The Woodford elegance and fruit notes thread through marrow's intensity with small-batch refinement. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Bone-In Filet": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits at register with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness: the dry rye body wraps the bone-enhanced filet, and the rye-grain bite cuts the cut. Strong; sophisticated everyday match.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Broccoli Cheddar": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the rye spice cuts the broccoli cheddar, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; concentrated blend slightly over-specified.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Broccolini": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the rye backbone drives the broccolini, and the rye edge frames the side. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Brussels and Belly": "Woodford Reserve Rye with Brussels + Belly — elegant small-batch rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; fruit-forward character cuts the glaze with refined distinction.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Burrata": "Woodford Reserve Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against fruit-forward rye. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Butternut Squash": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the rye backbone drives the squash bisque, and the oak threads the cream. Works; Boss Hog's allocation story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Carrot Cake": "Woodford Reserve Rye with carrot cake — Woodford fruit-forward rye against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; small-batch fruit and cake spice find each other.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Cheesecake": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the high-rye spice register matches the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Chilean Seabass": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character swallows the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Woodford Reserve Rye for the steak course.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Chocolate Brownie": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character leans against the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rye spice cuts the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Old World complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Chocolate Cake": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the dry rye body wraps the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; IW Harper 15 handles dessert density at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Clam Chowder": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the chowder, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; AE Triple Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Cowboy Ribeye": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — 90.4-proof small-batch against cap fat. Strong; the elegance is slightly ambitious for the ribeye's scale — Knob Creek Rye or E.H. Taylor Rye bring more grip.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Crab Cake": "Woodford Reserve Rye with crab cake — elegant small-batch rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Creamed Spinach": "Woodford Reserve Rye with creamed spinach — 90.4-proof small-batch against dairy. Strong; the Woodford elegance sits with richness.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Creamy Potato": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rye backbone drives the potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; pour slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Creme Brulee": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character leans against the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the high-rye spice register matches the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; KBS distinctiveness is slightly over-specified for the two-cut plate.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Escargot": "Woodford Reserve Rye's floral-spice character lifts the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the whiskey weight reads cleanly against the umami plate, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Faroe Island Salmon": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Woodford Reserve Rye for the steak course.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Filet Mignon": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the filet — elegant small-batch rye against the 10oz cut. The fruit-forward character gives the filet a distinctive rye frame; more refined than Sazerac Rye's benchmark character. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|French Onion": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character leans against the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the French onion, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; boutique at matched register.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Grilled Caesar": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the grilled Caesar — balanced small-batch rye against charred romaine. The fruit-floral character earns the Caesar with small-batch elegance. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Gumbo": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the rye spice cuts the gumbo, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; private-selection wasted on the salad.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rye spice cuts the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; AE Triple Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|House Wedge": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the House Wedge — elegant rye against iceberg and bleu. Strong; the fruit-forward character cuts the bleu with small-batch refinement.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Kansas City": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the KC — 90.4-proof small-batch balanced rye, fruit-forward and elegant. Sister to Woodford Reserve Bourbon; carries the distillery's fruit-floral complexity into rye territory. Distinct from Sazerac Rye's benchmark spicy-sweet — Woodford is more fruit-forward, more elegant. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Loaded Potato": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the dry rye body wraps the loaded potato soup, and the oak threads the cream. Works; novelty maturation at matched register.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Lobster Mac": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the rye spice cuts the lobster mac, and the oak softens the cream. Works; 10-year depth overshoots delicacy.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Market Fish": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character swallows the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Pour the Woodford Reserve Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Mocha Creme": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye spice cuts the mocha creme, and the oak softens the cream. Works; flagship depth gives the dessert premium aged character.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Mushroom Bisque": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the rye-and-oak weight handles the mushroom bisque, and the oak frames the umami. Works; collector-bottle doesn't drive.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Mushrooms": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the mushrooms — fruit-forward rye against earthy umami. Strong; small-batch elegance layers into mushroom depth with Woodford's signature fruit character.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the high-rye spice register matches the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Porterhouse": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the porterhouse — small-batch rye across strip and filet. Strong; the fruit-forward elegance covers both cuts with small-batch refinement.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Prime Tartare": "Woodford Reserve Rye with the tartare — elegant small-batch rye against raw beef. The fruit-forward character is a refined counterpoint to tartare; distinct from Sazerac Rye's more assertive spicy-sweet. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Rainbow Trout": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Woodford Reserve Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the roast chicken, and the rye edge frames the plate. Works; the toast adds a warmth the dessert can accept.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the high-rye spice register matches the chickpea soup, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; high proof over-specified for the soup.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Hold the Woodford Reserve Rye for the steak course.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the high-rye spice register matches the garlic spinach, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the side. Works; the bourbon's character is distinctive but the pairing isn't a standout.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Seafood Tower": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Woodford Reserve Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Seared Scallops": "Woodford Reserve Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against 90.4-proof rye. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Seasonal Soup": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the seasonal soup, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the bowl. Works; KBS distinctiveness is slightly over-specified for the two-cut plate.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the rye spice cuts the seasonal vegetables, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the side. Works; the extra proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "Woodford Reserve Rye with shrimp bisque — small-batch rye against cream-based seafood. Works; fruit-forward interesting but doesn't drive.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "Woodford Reserve Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against small-batch rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Swordfish": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Pour the Woodford Reserve Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|The Tomahawk": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character leans against the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char: the high-rye spice register matches the 36oz showpiece, and the rye edge frames the cut. Strong; the smooth character at matched register.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Tomato Basil": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the rye-and-oak weight handles the tomato basil, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Truffle Fries": "Woodford Reserve Rye with truffle fries — elegant rye against truffle-parmesan. The fruit-forward character gives the side a refined small-batch frame. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory main without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Woodford Reserve Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Woodford Reserve Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Woodford Reserve Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the high-rye spice register matches the curry, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; aging wasted on the salad.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Asparagus": "Woodford Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the bourbon backbone carries the asparagus, and the oak-and-spice register settles the side. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Woodford Reserve with au gratin potatoes — fruit-forward small-batch against cheese-and-cream. Strong; Woodford's complexity adds something BT's straight-line approach doesn't.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Beignets": "Woodford Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pour's sweetness meets the dessert without pushing past.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Bone Marrow": "Woodford Reserve with bone marrow — small-batch character against umami fat. Woodford's fruit and floral notes thread through the marrow's mineral depth in a way Buffalo Trace's cleaner profile doesn't. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Bone-In Filet": "Woodford Reserve's caramel-floral character holds the line with the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the aged-oak weight frames the lean cut, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; the match carries without overshooting.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Broccoli Cheddar": "Woodford Reserve's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the oak depth lays under the broccoli cheddar, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; pour slightly over-specified for this dish.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Broccolini": "Woodford Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth leans against the broccolini's tender green stalks: the bourbon depth settles into the broccolini, and the oak frames the side. Works; the pour's assertiveness is a bit much for the soup but doesn't clash.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Brussels and Belly": "Woodford Reserve with Brussels + Belly — fruit-forward bourbon meets maple-glazed pork belly. Woodford's fruit notes play with the dish's maple in a way BT or Maker's can't; a refined small-plate pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Burrata": "Woodford Reserve with burrata — fresh dairy against fruit-forward bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Butternut Squash": "Woodford Reserve's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone leans against the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the oak depth lays under the squash bisque, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; OGD 16 handles dessert density at distinctive register.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Carrot Cake": "Woodford Reserve with carrot cake — small-batch fruit and floral against cream cheese and cinnamon. The bourbon's fruit character picks up the cake's raisins; a distinctive dessert pairing within the everyday premium tier. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Cheesecake": "Woodford Reserve with cheesecake — small-batch fruit notes against clean custard. Works; the fruit character gives the dessert a subtle counterpoint.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Chilean Seabass": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Woodford Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Chocolate Brownie": "Woodford Reserve with the chocolate brownie — fruit-forward bourbon against cocoa. Works; for cocoa interplay reach for Weller 107 or Weller Millennium; Woodford's fruit is a different pairing logic.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Chocolate Cake": "Woodford Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the caramel-spice register matches the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the extra proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Clam Chowder": "Woodford Reserve's soft-oak bourbon body finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the chowder, and the vanilla folds into the cream. Works; the match reads neutral at the table.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Cowboy Ribeye": "Woodford Reserve with the Cowboy Ribeye — small-batch fruit-forward against cap fat and char. Strong; Woodford's elegance is slightly underserved by the ribeye's scale — BT or Weller 12 are firmer calls for max-impact cuts.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Crab Cake": "Woodford Reserve with crab cake — small-batch against delicate crab. Avoid; choose a white wine.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Creamed Spinach": "Woodford Reserve with creamed spinach — small-batch fruit and floral against dairy. Strong; the non-wheated profile with fruit notes gives the side a different dimension than caramel-wheat does.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Creamy Potato": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the bourbon depth settles into the potato soup, and the oak frames the cream. Works; the 100-proof is a touch over-specified for this dish's register.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Creme Brulee": "Woodford Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character reads alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the bourbon weight handles the brulee, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Escargot": "Woodford Reserve's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the bourbon body sits with the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; multi-distillery complexity sits with richness at craft-blender register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Faroe Island Salmon": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral overwhelms the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bourbon. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Woodford Reserve on the steak course instead.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Filet Mignon": "Woodford Reserve with the filet — Kentucky's small-batch classic. 90.4 proof with fruit-forward and floral notes layered over clean caramel. Distinct from Buffalo Trace's straight-line oak — more interplay, more fruit vocabulary. A refined non-wheated call for filet orders. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Woodford Reserve|French Onion": "Woodford Reserve's grain-and-oak bourbon register reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the brown-spirit warmth threads the French onion, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; Discovery complexity wasted on salad simplicity.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Grilled Caesar": "Woodford Reserve with the grilled Caesar — charred romaine and anchovy against fruit-forward bourbon. Works; Woodford's complexity earns the Caesar a bit more than most everyday bourbons.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Gumbo": "Woodford Reserve's soft-oak bourbon body leans against the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the caramel-spice register matches the gumbo, and the oak frames the bowl. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Woodford Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character leans against the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the bourbon weight handles the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; private-selection wasted on the salad.",
  "Woodford Reserve|House Wedge": "Woodford Reserve with the House Wedge — small-batch against iceberg and bleu. Works; the pour's complexity is underused by the salad.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Kansas City": "Woodford Reserve with the KC — small-batch fruit-forward bourbon meets the strip's marbling. The floral and fruit notes give the strip a dimension BT's straight oak doesn't bring; a sophisticated everyday call. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Loaded Potato": "Woodford Reserve's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth reads alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the bourbon body sits with the loaded potato soup, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; oak sits beside the dessert gently.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Lobster Mac": "Woodford Reserve's grain-and-oak bourbon register sits alongside the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the caramel-spice register matches the lobster mac, and the vanilla underlines the rich cream. Works; Woodford Double Oaked frames the two-cut plate at KY-benchmark register.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Market Fish": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Woodford Reserve lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Mocha Creme": "Woodford Reserve's rounded-oak bourbon character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the bourbon depth settles into the mocha creme, and the vanilla rounds the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Mushroom Bisque": "Woodford Reserve's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the bourbon backbone carries the mushroom bisque, and the vanilla lifts the cream. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th is over-specified for an app; save the collector bottle for the main.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Mushrooms": "Woodford Reserve with the mushrooms — small-batch fruit and floral against earthy umami. Woodford's complexity layers into mushroom depth distinctively; a sophisticated pairing. Excellent; the pairing carries cleanly.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Woodford Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; boutique-blend meets the brownie cleanly.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Porterhouse": "Woodford Reserve with the porterhouse — small-batch character across strip and filet sides. Strong; fruit and floral notes give both cuts something to meet, though they shine more on the filet side.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Prime Tartare": "Woodford Reserve's caramel-floral character matches the tartare's raw beef and brine — the aged-oak depth meets the rich starter, and the caramel rounds the umami plate. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Rainbow Trout": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bourbon. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Reserve the Woodford Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Roast Half Chicken": "Woodford Reserve's spice-and-caramel bourbon depth leans against the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the bourbon backbone carries the roast chicken, and the oak-and-spice register settles the plate. Works; Old Fitz 9 brings more depth here.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Woodford Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the bourbon backbone carries the chickpea soup, and the rounded sweetness brightens the bowl. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral overwhelms the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not a flaky main. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Woodford Reserve belongs on the steak course.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral reads alongside the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the bourbon depth settles into the garlic spinach, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; CC 43 Year is over-specified for a side — save the rare bottle for the main course.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Seafood Tower": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral fights the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- the spirit register reads heavy on a starter's lighter frame. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bourbon. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Woodford Reserve lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Seared Scallops": "Woodford Reserve with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against small-batch oak. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Seasonal Soup": "Woodford Reserve's oak-and-vanilla bourbon depth reads alongside the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the oak-and-vanilla weight wraps the seasonal soup, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; BH Red Wine Cask meets the char at distinctive register.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Seasonal Vegetables": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the bourbon depth settles into the seasonal vegetables, and the vanilla softens the side. Works; Woodford Double Oaked handles the richness at KY-benchmark register.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Shrimp Bisque": "Woodford Reserve with shrimp bisque — cream-based seafood against small-batch. Works neutrally; Woodford holds.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Shrimp Cocktail": "Woodford Reserve with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against small-batch. Avoid; recommend something else for this dish.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Swordfish": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bourbon. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Woodford Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Woodford Reserve|The Tomahawk": "Woodford Reserve's caramel-floral character lifts the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the caramel-and-oak weight rises with the cap-fat, and the vanilla softens the grilled char. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Tomato Basil": "Woodford Reserve's warm-vanilla bourbon backbone finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the bourbon weight handles the tomato basil, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Truffle Fries": "Woodford Reserve with truffle fries — fruit-forward small-batch against truffle-parmesan. The floral complexity meets truffle's earth with more vocabulary than BT or the Wellers bring; a refined side pairing. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Woodford Reserve's small-batch fruit and floral clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bourbon's sweet weight crowds the rare crust's clean char. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bourbon. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Woodford Reserve for the steak course.",
  "Woodford Reserve|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Woodford Reserve's caramel-and-spice bourbon weight reads alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the bourbon weight handles the curry, and the caramel rounds the bowl. Works; OGD 16 frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Asparagus": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the high-rye spice register matches the asparagus, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; the pairing is pleasant but not a standout.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Woodinville Rye with au gratin potatoes — local-grain rye against cheese-and-cream. Works; regional craft at matched register.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Beignets": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the high-rye spice register matches the beignets, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; the pairing holds without overshooting.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Bone Marrow": "Woodinville Rye with bone marrow — PNW craft rye against umami fat. The Washington State character meets marrow with regional-craft distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Bone-In Filet": "Woodinville Rye's crisp grain-oak character matches the bone-in filet's marrow-edged tenderness — the bourbon weight grips the lean cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Broccoli Cheddar": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the rye backbone drives the broccoli cheddar, and the oak softens the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Broccolini": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the dry rye body wraps the broccolini, and the rye-grain bite cuts the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Brussels and Belly": "Woodinville Rye with Brussels + Belly — PNW craft rye meets maple-glazed pork belly. Strong; regional character cuts the glaze with non-Kentucky distinctiveness.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Burrata": "Woodinville Rye with burrata — fresh dairy against Washington craft rye. Avoid; lead with the alts on the menu.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Butternut Squash": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the rye spice cuts the squash bisque, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Carrot Cake": "Woodinville Rye with carrot cake — PNW craft rye against cream cheese and cinnamon. Works; regional rye spice and cake spice find each other.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Cheesecake": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the cheesecake, and the oak threads the cream. Works; OGD 16 handles dessert density at distinctive register.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Chilean Seabass": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bourbon weight needs red meat, not the salmon's already-rich oils. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Woodinville Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Chocolate Brownie": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the rye spice cuts the brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; AE Triple Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Chocolate Cake": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the rye spice cuts the chocolate cake, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; OGD 16 frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Clam Chowder": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the high-rye spice register matches the chowder, and the oak softens the cream. Works; the pour's brightness is a gentle counterpoint to the dessert's richness.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Cowboy Ribeye": "Woodinville Rye with the Cowboy Ribeye — Washington State small-batch against cap fat. Strong; PNW craft capable but higher-proof Kentucky ryes bring more grip for this cut.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Crab Cake": "Woodinville Rye with crab cake — Washington craft rye against delicate crab. Avoid; pour a crisp white wine.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Creamed Spinach": "Woodinville Rye with creamed spinach — Washington craft rye against dairy. Strong; PNW regional character sits with richness.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Creamy Potato": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the rye spice cuts the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Creme Brulee": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the brulee, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the call holds at register.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Escargot": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the escargot's garlic-herb-butter savory weight: the rye spice cuts the buttered escargot, and the caramel rounds the herb butter. Strong; extended-aged Michigan character is a distinctive counterpoint with home-state-pride story; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Faroe Island Salmon": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character smothers the salmon's rich oily flesh -- sweet-spirit register doubles the natural fish-richness — no contrast. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Pour the Woodinville Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Filet Mignon": "Woodinville Rye with the filet — Washington State small-batch rye against the 10oz cut. The PNW craft character gives the filet a non-Kentucky rye voice; distinctive regional-craft pour. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Woodinville Rye|French Onion": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the rye-and-oak weight handles the French onion, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Grilled Caesar": "Woodinville Rye with the grilled Caesar — Pacific Northwest craft against charred romaine. The PNW character earns the Caesar with regional distinctiveness. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Gumbo": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the dry rye body wraps the gumbo, and the dry-spice register brightens the bowl. Works; milestone-release covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the rye-and-oak weight handles the honey carrots, and the oak balances the burnt-sugar edge. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Woodinville Rye|House Wedge": "Woodinville Rye with the House Wedge — Pacific Northwest craft against iceberg and bleu. Strong; regional craft cuts the bleu with PNW distinctiveness.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Kansas City": "Woodinville Rye with the KC — Washington State craft rye from Woodinville Whiskey Co. Pacific Northwest small-batch using local grain; distinct from Kentucky, Heaven Hill, or BT ryes by state of origin alone. A regional-craft rye for strip orders. Excellent; the call holds at full register.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Loaded Potato": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the rye spice cuts the loaded potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Lobster Mac": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence: the dry rye body wraps the lobster mac, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; the pour's sweetness meets the dessert without pushing past.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Market Fish": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character overwhelms the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- bourbon depth belongs with red meat, not flaky white fish. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Reserve the Woodinville Rye for the steak course.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Mocha Creme": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the rye spice cuts the mocha creme, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; blended character doesn't drive.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Mushroom Bisque": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the rye-and-oak weight handles the mushroom bisque, and the oak threads the umami plate. Works; Remus 15 frames the two-cut plate at Gatsby-tribute register.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Mushrooms": "Woodinville Rye with the mushrooms — Washington State craft against earthy umami. Strong; PNW character layers into mushroom depth with regional distinctiveness.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the PB brownie, and the spice threads the dessert. Works; Voyage 23 handles the richness at ocean-finish register.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Porterhouse": "Woodinville Rye with the porterhouse — PNW craft rye across strip and filet. Strong; regional character covers both cuts.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Prime Tartare": "Woodinville Rye with the tartare — Washington craft rye against raw beef. The local-grain character is a regional-craft counterpoint distinct from Kentucky. Excellent; servers pour without second-guessing.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Rainbow Trout": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character flattens the trout's delicate flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Woodinville Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Roast Half Chicken": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character sits alongside the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast: the high-rye spice register matches the roast chicken, and the rye-pepper note underlines the plate. Works; smooth gentle match.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the rye-pepper edge sharpens the chickpea soup, and the rye-pepper note underlines the bowl. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character flattens the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- oak-and-vanilla density rolls over the gentle flesh without lifting it. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Reserve the Woodinville Rye for the steak course.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the rye-and-oak weight handles the garlic spinach, and the rye-grain bite cuts the side. Works; craft-blender complexity over-specified.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Seafood Tower": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character smothers the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bourbon warmth has no foil against cold, clean shellfish. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Woodinville Rye belongs on the steak course.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Seared Scallops": "Woodinville Rye with seared scallops — scallop delicacy against PNW craft rye. Avoid; redirect the order to a more aligned pour.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Seasonal Soup": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the high-rye spice register matches the seasonal soup, and the rye-grain bite cuts the bowl. Works; boutique-blend depth gives composed dessert character.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Seasonal Vegetables": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the dry rye body wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the dry-spice register brightens the side. Works; richer profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Shrimp Bisque": "Woodinville Rye with shrimp bisque — Washington State craft against cream-based seafood. Works; PNW character doesn't drive.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Shrimp Cocktail": "Woodinville Rye with shrimp cocktail — cold shrimp against PNW rye. Avoid; reach for a wine that fits the plate.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Swordfish": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character swallows the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Woodinville Rye on the steak course instead.",
  "Woodinville Rye|The Tomahawk": "Woodinville Rye's crisp grain-oak character sits with the Tomahawk's char-and-fat — the bourbon depth grips the marbled cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Tomato Basil": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the high-rye spice register matches the tomato basil, and the rye edge frames the bowl. Works; a composed but not remarkable match.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Truffle Fries": "Woodinville Rye with truffle fries — Washington craft rye against truffle-parmesan. The local-grain character gives the side a non-Kentucky regional frame. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character swallows the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- the sweet-spirit register fights the savory sear without a bridge. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a rye whiskey. Avoid; one of those is the move. Woodinville Rye lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Woodinville Rye|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Woodinville Rye's rye-spice character finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the high-rye spice register matches the curry, and the spice-and-vanilla thread softens the bowl. Works; milestone-collector wasted on salad.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Asparagus": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the Japanese whisky balance frames the asparagus, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the side. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the restrained whisky body threads the au gratin, and the oak softens the cream. Works; composed but not a standout.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Beignets": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the beignets, and the precision rounds the dessert. Works; AE meets the char at wine-finish register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Bone Marrow": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Japanese-sherry-touched character holds steady against the marrow's umami-fat depth — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the dried-fruit notes meet the grilled char. Strong; the call holds at register without strain.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Bone-In Filet": "Yamazaki 12 with the bone-in filet — iconic Suntory flagship single malt against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak sandalwood-honey character frames the cut with signature Japanese distinction.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Broccoli Cheddar": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the Japanese whisky balance frames the broccoli cheddar, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Broccolini": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the broccolini, and the green-fruit edge brightens the side. Works; EC 18 frames the two-cut plate at aged-HH register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Brussels and Belly": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character sits alongside Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the Japanese-whisky precision frames the pork-belly plate, and the silky body rounds the side. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Burrata": "Yamazaki 12 with burrata — iconic Suntory flagship against fresh dairy. Works; Japanese whisky's delicate category + Yamazaki 12's refinement let it sit with burrata at flagship register; not featured but distinctive.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Butternut Squash": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character reads alongside the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the restrained whisky body threads the squash bisque, and the oak softens the cream. Works; aged premium slightly over-specified.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Carrot Cake": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character reads alongside the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the carrot cake, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the dessert. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Cheesecake": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character leans against the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the cheesecake, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; 10-year depth overshoots delicacy.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Chilean Seabass": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character sits alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the Japanese whisky balance frames the seabass, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the plate. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Chocolate Brownie": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the Japanese whisky balance frames the brownie, and the umami-tinged finish frames the dessert. Works; JW Blue is over-specified for a side — save the bottle for the main course.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Chocolate Cake": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character sits alongside the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the chocolate cake, and the precision rounds the dessert. Works; the pour is a touch ambitious for the salad.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Clam Chowder": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the Japanese whisky balance frames the chowder, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; JD Bonded's backbone handles the richness at Bonded register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Cowboy Ribeye": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the restrained whisky body threads the cap-fat ribeye, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the cut. Works; Caribbean Cask frames the two-cut plate at distinctive register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Crab Cake": "Yamazaki 12 with crab cake — flagship Japanese single malt against delicate lump crab. Works; Japanese whisky's delicate category lets Yamazaki sit with crab at flagship register; not featured but distinctive.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Creamed Spinach": "Yamazaki 12 with creamed spinach — Suntory flagship against dairy-rich side. Works; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak character rides the cream at flagship register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Creamy Potato": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character leans against the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the potato soup, and the oak frames the rich cream. Works; IW Harper 15 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Creme Brulee": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the brulee, and the oak threads the cream. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Escargot": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Japanese-sherry-touched character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon weight grips the savory plate, and the sherry depth threads the umami fat. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Faroe Island Salmon": "Yamazaki 12 with the salmon — Suntory flagship Japanese single malt against oily salmon. Excellent; Yamazaki 12's honey-fruit character and Mizunara-oak finish frame fish with Japanese-single-malt elegance — the category advantage + Yamazaki refinement.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Filet Mignon": "Yamazaki 12 with the filet — Suntory's flagship single malt from Japan's first single malt distillery (opened 1923 by Shinjiro Torii), Mizunara-oak-signature honey-fruit with sandalwood hint. Excellent; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak character frames tenderloin with Japanese-single-malt gravitas — the distillery that started Japanese whisky making its featured filet pairing.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|French Onion": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character reads alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the French onion, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the bowl. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Grilled Caesar": "Yamazaki 12 with the grilled Caesar — Suntory flagship against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Yamazaki 12's honey-fruit character meets the char at flagship register; Hakushu's lightly-peated is the featured Japanese-whisky-Caesar match.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Gumbo": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the Japanese-whisky precision frames the gumbo, and the silky body rounds the bowl. Works; the 10-year holds without lifting.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Japanese whisky balance frames the honey carrots, and the oak frames the caramelized sugar. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|House Wedge": "Yamazaki 12 with the House Wedge — Japan's first single malt distillery against iceberg and bleu. Strong; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak character threads the wedge at flagship register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Kansas City": "Yamazaki 12 with the KC — flagship Suntory single malt against an 18oz strip. Excellent; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak depth is the featured Japanese-whisky-KC pairing — the Mizunara oak is Japan's answer to European oak traditions, and it frames strip marbling with distinctive character.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Loaded Potato": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the loaded potato soup, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; richer profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Lobster Mac": "Yamazaki 12 with lobster mac — Japan's flagship single malt against pasta-and-lobster. Works; Yamazaki 12's honey-fruit elegance handles the rich dish at flagship register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Market Fish": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character leans against the market fish's clean white-fish flesh: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the market fish, and the precision rounds the plate. Works; HH 20's aged character meets the glaze at collector register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Mocha Creme": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the mocha creme, and the oak threads the cream. Works; polished 10-year gives the dessert small-batch premium character.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Mushroom Bisque": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character reads alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the mushroom bisque, and the oak depth meets the umami. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Mushrooms": "Yamazaki 12 with mushrooms — Japan's flagship single malt against earthy umami. Excellent; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak sandalwood-coconut subtlety threads mushroom earthiness at flagship register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character leans against the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the Japanese-whisky precision frames the PB brownie, and the silky body rounds the dessert. Works; grain notes don't really engage the soup but don't clash.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Porterhouse": "Yamazaki 12 Year -- Suntory, Japanese flagship, silky. The Mizunara-influenced register sits with the dual-cut steak, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the cut. Strong; boutique-blend sits with richness.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Prime Tartare": "Yamazaki 12 with the tartare — Suntory flagship single malt against raw beef. Strong; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak character supports tartare at flagship register; Yamazaki 18's aged Mizunara depth is the featured Yamazaki-tartare match.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Rainbow Trout": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character leans against the trout's delicate flesh: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the trout, and the umami-tinged finish frames the plate. Works; the 80-proof softness doesn't overshoot the salad the way heavier bourbons do.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Roast Half Chicken": "Yamazaki 12 with the chicken — Suntory flagship single malt against roasted poultry. Excellent; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak character frames chicken at iconic Japanese-single-malt register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character reads alongside the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the restrained whisky body threads the chickpea soup, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the bowl. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character reads alongside the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the halibut, and the green-fruit edge brightens the plate. Works; own-plus-sourced over-specified.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the garlic spinach, and the umami-tinged finish frames the side. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Seafood Tower": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character sits alongside the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the restrained whisky body threads the seafood tower, and the precision rounds the starter. Works; retailer-pick wasted on salad.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Seared Scallops": "Yamazaki 12 with seared scallops — flagship Japanese single malt against scallop delicacy. Strong; Yamazaki 12's honey-fruit elegance frames scallops at distinguished register; Hakushu's peat is Japanese whisky's more distinctive scallop lift.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Seasonal Soup": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character leans against the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the restrained whisky body threads the seasonal soup, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the bowl. Works; Fortuna's NZ terroir meets the glaze at distinctive register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Seasonal Vegetables": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the Japanese whisky balance frames the seasonal vegetables, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the side. Works; AE CS handles the richness at premium register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Shrimp Bisque": "Yamazaki 12 with shrimp bisque — flagship Japanese single malt against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak elegance rides the bisque at flagship register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Shrimp Cocktail": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character leans against the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish: the Japanese whisky balance frames the chilled shrimp, and the umami-tinged finish frames the starter. Works; OGD 16 is over-specified for an app; save for the main.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Swordfish": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character reads alongside the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the Japanese-whisky precision frames the swordfish, and the silky body rounds the plate. Works; richer profile overshoots the dessert's delicacy.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|The Tomahawk": "Yamazaki 12 with the Tomahawk — flagship Japanese single malt against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; even Yamazaki's Mizunara-oak depth is too light for this cut. Reach for a bourbon or cab.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Tomato Basil": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the Japanese-whisky precision frames the tomato basil, and the silky body rounds the bowl. Works; Sinatra Select handles the richness at Sinatra-tribute register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Truffle Fries": "Yamazaki 12 with truffle fries — Suntory flagship single malt against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Yamazaki 12's Mizunara-oak sandalwood-hint meets the earthy-rich at flagship register.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the tuna, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the plate. Works; the pour's delicacy doesn't overpower the soup, but doesn't particularly elevate it either.",
  "Yamazaki 12 Year|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Yamazaki 12 Year's Suntory Japanese flagship character finds neutral with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the curry, and the green-fruit edge brightens the bowl. Works; boutique-blend depth gives composed dessert character.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Asparagus": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character leans against the asparagus' green-vegetal edge: the restrained whisky body threads the asparagus, and the umami-tinged finish frames the side. Works; namesake pour is wasted on the salad.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust: the restrained whisky body threads the au gratin, and the oak softens the cream. Works; gentle match.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Beignets": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the beignets' warm sugar-dusted pastry: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the beignets, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the dessert. Works; Parker's Heritage 16th handles the richness at annual-release register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Bone Marrow": "Yamazaki 18 Year: Suntory's Japanese flagship. Excellent on marrow; the sherry-cask-driven profile meets umami fat at refined register — save the bottle for the guest who treats whisky like art.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Bone-In Filet": "Yamazaki 18 with the bone-in filet — aged Yamazaki against tenderloin-with-bone. Excellent; Yamazaki 18's aged Mizunara-oak sandalwood-coconut frames the cut at collector register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Broccoli Cheddar": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the broccoli cheddar, and the oak threads the cream. Works; OGD 16 handles dessert density at distinctive register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Broccolini": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character reads alongside the broccolini's tender green stalks: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the broccolini, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the side. Works; Prohibition narrative wasted on salad.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Brussels and Belly": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with Brussels + Belly's maple-pork-belly glaze over charred sprouts: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the pork-belly plate, and the green-fruit edge brightens the side. Works; Remus 15 frames the two-cut plate at Gatsby-tribute register; Fernet Branca still cuts it harder.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Burrata": "Yamazaki 18 with burrata — aged flagship Japanese single malt against fresh dairy. Works; save the collector bottle for the protein course.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Butternut Squash": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the squash bisque, and the oak threads the cream. Works; gentle match.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Carrot Cake": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the carrot cake's cream-cheese-frosted spice cake: the mizunara-cask depth wraps the carrot cake, and the silky precision frames the dessert. Works; polished aged character doesn't drive.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Cheesecake": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the cheesecake's dense tangy dairy custard: the mizunara-cask depth wraps the cheesecake, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; the story is wasted on the salad.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Chilean Seabass": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character reads alongside the seabass' buttery seabass flesh: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the seabass, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the plate. Works; BTAC story wasted on salad.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Chocolate Brownie": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the brownie's fudgy cocoa-and-chocolate weight: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the brownie, and the precision rounds the dessert. Works; distinctive character gives the dessert a unique story-frame.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Chocolate Cake": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character leans against the chocolate cake's layered chocolate body: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the chocolate cake, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the dessert. Works; the 10-year BIB depth gives the dessert substantial weight.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Clam Chowder": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character reads alongside the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base: the Japanese whisky balance frames the chowder, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; single-barrel depth gives the dessert more than Small Batch.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Cowboy Ribeye": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the cap-fat ribeye, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the cut. Works; IW Harper 15 frames the two-cut plate at aged-KY-heritage register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Crab Cake": "Yamazaki 18 with crab cake — aged flagship Japanese single malt against delicate lump crab. Works; save the Yamazaki 18 collector bottle for the protein course — Yamazaki 12 is the whisky-curious crab choice at lower register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Creamed Spinach": "Yamazaki 18 with creamed spinach — aged 18yr Yamazaki against dairy-rich side. Works; Yamazaki 18 is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Creamy Potato": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the potato soup's silky-bodied cream: the restrained whisky body threads the potato soup, and the oak softens the cream. Works; a gentle, everyday match.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Creme Brulee": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the brulee's burnt-sugar custard: the restrained whisky body threads the brulee, and the oak softens the cream. Works; BH Red Wine Cask handles the richness at distinctive register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Escargot": "Yamazaki 18 Year's bold Mizunara-oak-Japanese character sits with the escargot's garlic-herb butter — the bourbon depth cuts the umami fat, and the oak frames the umami. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Faroe Island Salmon": "Yamazaki 18 with the salmon — aged Yamazaki against oily salmon. Strong; Yamazaki 18's aged Mizunara character frames fish at collector register; Yamazaki 12's lighter honey-fruit is still the featured Yamazaki-salmon match, where 18 is over-specified.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Filet Mignon": "Yamazaki 18 Year -- aged Suntory, mizunara cask, silky. The Mizunara-influenced register sits with the lean buttery filet, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the cut. Excellent; JD Bonded's backbone cuts the glaze harder than Old No. Gold standard; the headline call at the table.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|French Onion": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth: the restrained whisky body threads the French onion, and the precision rounds the bowl. Works; the 10-year's restraint lets the soup lead.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Grilled Caesar": "Yamazaki 18 with the grilled Caesar — aged flagship Japanese single malt against charred romaine and anchovy. Strong; Yamazaki 18's aged character meets the char at collector register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Gumbo": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the gumbo's spiced creole stew: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the gumbo, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the bowl. Works; premium proof overpowers this dish.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the honey carrots, and the oak underlines the burnt sugar. Works; Marian McLain L.E.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|House Wedge": "House Wedge: iceberg and bleu meet 18yr aged Yamazaki. Strong; Yamazaki 18's aged Mizunara-oak engages the bleu at collector register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Kansas City": "Kansas City: strip marbling meets 18yr aged Suntory single malt. Yamazaki 18's deeper Mizunara-oak and sherry-cask dried-fruit handle strip marbling at collector register — the premium Japanese-KC match where Yamazaki 12 opens the lane and the 18 completes it. Excellent; reliable headline pairing.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Loaded Potato": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character leans against the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup: the mizunara-cask depth wraps the loaded potato soup, and the oak rounds the cream. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Lobster Mac": "Yamazaki 18 with lobster mac — aged Suntory single malt against pasta-and-lobster. Works; Yamazaki 18's aged character handles the rich dish at collector register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Market Fish": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the market fish's clean white-fish flesh: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the market fish, and the green-fruit edge brightens the plate. Works; the bonded richness gives the dessert more than lighter pours do.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Mocha Creme": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate richness: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the mocha creme, and the oak depth lays under the cream. Works; gentle match at matched registers.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Mushroom Bisque": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the mushroom bisque, and the oak threads the umami plate. Works; high proof over-specified.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Mushrooms": "Yamazaki 18 with mushrooms — aged flagship Japanese single malt against earthy umami. Excellent; Yamazaki 18's deeper Mizunara-oak and aged character thread mushroom earthiness at collector register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character reads alongside the PB brownie's peanut-butter-and-chocolate fudge: the mizunara-cask depth wraps the PB brownie, and the silky precision frames the dessert. Works; pour's narrative is underused by the salad.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Porterhouse": "Yamazaki 18 Year's bold Mizunara-oak-Japanese character sits with the porterhouse's two-cut character — the whiskey weight settles into the bone-in cut, and the oak underlines the char. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Prime Tartare": "Yamazaki 18 with the tartare — aged Yamazaki against raw beef. Excellent; Yamazaki 18's aged Mizunara-oak with sandalwood + coconut + dried fruit is the featured Japanese-whisky-tartare match at collector register — deeper than Hibiki Harmony's blended refinement.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Rainbow Trout": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character reads alongside the trout's delicate flesh: the mizunara-cask depth wraps the trout, and the silky precision frames the plate. Works; safe call, not the headline.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Roast Half Chicken": "Yamazaki 18 with the chicken — aged 18yr Suntory single malt against roasted poultry. Excellent; Yamazaki 18's aged depth frames chicken at collector register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth: the restrained whisky body threads the chickpea soup, and the green-fruit edge brightens the bowl. Works; Japanese whisky's delicate category handles the rich dish without fighting it.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character leans against the halibut's salt-cured flesh: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the halibut, and the Japanese-craft balance underlines the plate. Works; doesn't drive.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character leans against the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the garlic spinach, and the umami-tinged finish frames the side. Works; boutique over-specified.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Seafood Tower": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the seafood tower, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the starter. Works; a gentle composed match.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Seared Scallops": "Yamazaki 18 with seared scallops — aged Suntory single malt against scallop delicacy. Strong; Yamazaki 18's aged character is heavier for scallops than the 12 — save the collector bottle for the main course.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Seasonal Soup": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the seasonal soup, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the bowl. Works; boutique covers both cuts without standing out.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Seasonal Vegetables": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character leans against the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the seasonal vegetables, and the umami-tinged finish frames the side. Works; GTS is over-specified for a side — save the collector bottle.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Shrimp Bisque": "Yamazaki 18 with shrimp bisque — aged Yamazaki against cream-shrimp soup. Strong; Yamazaki 18's Mizunara-oak depth rides the bisque at collector register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Shrimp Cocktail": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character reads alongside the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the chilled shrimp, and the umami-tinged finish frames the starter. Works; HH 20 frames the two-cut plate at collector register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Swordfish": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the swordfish, and the umami-tinged finish frames the plate. Works; richer profile is over-specified for this dish.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|The Tomahawk": "Yamazaki 18 with the Tomahawk — aged Japanese single malt against 32oz cap-fat. Avoid; even aged Yamazaki's Mizunara-oak is too light for this cut. Save the collector bottle for the filet or KC.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Tomato Basil": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid: the precise Japanese craft body wraps the tomato basil, and the precision rounds the bowl. Works; curated single-barrel slightly over-specified.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Truffle Fries": "Yamazaki 18 with truffle fries — aged Suntory single malt against truffle-parmesan. Strong; Yamazaki 18's deeper Mizunara-oak meets the earthy-rich at collector register.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character finds neutral with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust: the Mizunara-influenced register sits with the tuna, and the sandalwood-and-honey register softens the plate. Works; wine-cask complexity gives the dessert layered premium character.",
  "Yamazaki 18 Year|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Yamazaki 18 Year's aged Suntory mizunara character sits alongside the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth: the elegant Suntory/Nikka register matches the curry, and the umami-tinged finish frames the bowl. Works; Vermont-brand at matched register.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Asparagus": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the asparagus' green-vegetal edge -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Joseph Mellot Sancerre, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Au Gratin Potatoes": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the au gratin's cheese-and-cream golden crust -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Beignets": "Beignets: warm sugar-dusted pastry meets Yellow Chartreuse. Excellent; the honey-saffron-herbal character plays with sugar-dust at Carthusian-sister register — sweeter, softer Chartreuse pairing.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Bone Marrow": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge mis-pairs with the bone marrow's unctuous rendered-marrow richness -- this bitter's register fights the rich meat starter — needs warmth or fat, not herbs. The plate deserves Fernet Branca, Lagavulin 8, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Yellow Chartreuse lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Bone-In Filet": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the bone-in filet's bone-enhanced buttery tenderness -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Opus One, Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, or Knob Creek 15 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; lead with the alternatives instead. Reserve the Yellow Chartreuse for another course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Broccoli Cheddar": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the broccoli cheddar's cheddar-and-broccoli cream -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Yellow Chartreuse lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Broccolini": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the broccolini's tender green stalks -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Yellow Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Brussels and Belly": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness matches the maple-glazed Brussels and pork belly — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Burrata": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the burrata's fresh basil-tomato-cream delicacy -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Il Colle Prosecco Superiore, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Save the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Butternut Squash": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the butternut squash's silky sweet squash cream -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Carrot Cake": "Carrot Cake: cream cheese and cinnamon meet Yellow Chartreuse. Works; the honey-saffron-herbal character amplifies the cake's spice with sweeter dimension.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Cheesecake": "Yellow Chartreuse: 40% ABV, 130+ herbs/botanicals with honey-saffron notes, sister to Green but gentler and sweeter. Excellent on cheesecake; the honey-herbal character threads the cream at approachable-Carthusian register — where Green cuts, Yellow caresses.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Chilean Seabass": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the seabass' buttery seabass flesh -- bitter herbs on oily fish read metallic — the rich flesh deserves a crisp white. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Pommery Cuvée Louise, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Yellow Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Chocolate Brownie": "Chocolate Brownie: warm dark chocolate meets Yellow Chartreuse. Excellent; honey-herbal profile threads chocolate at 40% register — gentler than Green's 55% herbal punch.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Chocolate Cake": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness holds the line with the cake's layered chocolate — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the rounded sweetness meets the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Clam Chowder": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the chowder's creamy clam-and-potato base -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Yellow Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Cowboy Ribeye": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge underclubs the cowboy ribeye's marbled char-and-fat richness -- amaro reads light against the marbled char — the cut deserves wine or whiskey weight. The plate deserves Macallan 18, Caymus Cabernet Sauvignon, or Silver Oak Cabernet Sauvignon, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for another course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Crab Cake": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the crab cake's delicate sweet crab in a crisp crust -- amaro bitterness on cold shellfish flattens the delicate brine — needs sparkling instead. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, or Tanqueray Gin, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Pour the Yellow Chartreuse on the steak course instead.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Creamed Spinach": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the creamed spinach's rich bechamel-laced greens -- amaro bitterness on dairy reads metallic — needs warmth or sweetness to balance. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Far Niente Chardonnay, or Weller 12 Year, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; redirect the order — the alternatives carry it. Pour the Yellow Chartreuse on the steak course instead.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Creamy Potato": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the potato soup's silky-bodied cream -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Yellow Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Creme Brulee": "Creme Brulee: caramelized custard meets Yellow Chartreuse. Excellent; the honey-saffron character plays burnt-sugar-cream at lower-proof register — sweeter Chartreuse pairing than Green, more accessible to a guest easing into herbal liqueurs.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Escargot": "Escargot: garlic-herb butter meets Yellow Chartreuse. Excellent; the 130-botanical profile ties to herb butter at honey-sweetened register — softer than Green, still hits the French-classic mark.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Faroe Island Salmon": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the salmon's rich oily flesh -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Elk Cove Five Mountain, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Yellow Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Filet Mignon": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness sits alongside the filet's tender lean cut — the sweet character meets the cut at digestif register, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Works; capable alongside without driving the table.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|French Onion": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the French onion's beef-broth-and-gruyere depth -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Daniel Chotard Sancerre Rouge, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Pour the Yellow Chartreuse on the steak course instead.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Grilled Caesar": "Grilled Caesar: char-romaine with anchovy-parmesan meets Yellow Chartreuse. Excellent; the honey-herbal character meets char-and-brine at gentler-Carthusian register.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Gumbo": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the gumbo's spiced creole stew -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Fattoria Le Pupille Saffredi, or Muga Reserva, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Yellow Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Honey Roasted Carrots": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge fights the honey carrots' honey-glazed carrot sweetness -- aperitivo bitterness and the glaze's sugar refuse each other — bitter wins, dessert reads cloying. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Save the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|House Wedge": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness reads alongside the wedge's iceberg, bacon, and bleu — the sweet character sits at digestif register, and the honey threads the cream. Strong; the call lands cleanly.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Kansas City": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness sits alongside the strip's lean char — the sweet character meets the cut at digestif register, and the honey rounds the grilled char. Works; reliable companion, low risk.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Loaded Potato": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the loaded potato's rich loaded-potato soup -- amaro bitterness on dairy cream reads metallic — no bridge from herbal sharp to soft fat. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Save the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Lobster Mac": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the lobster mac's shellfish-in-cream indulgence -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Save the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Market Fish": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the market fish's clean white-fish flesh -- aperitivo bitterness fights gentle flesh without a bridge. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; recommend one of the alternatives. Reserve the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Mocha Creme": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness reads alongside the mocha creme's coffee-chocolate dessert — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the honey threads the cream. Strong; the pairing reads cleanly.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Mushroom Bisque": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the mushroom bisque's silky earthy mushroom cream -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Keenan Chardonnay, or Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Save the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Mushrooms": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness sits with the mushrooms' earthy umami — the sweet character sits at digestif register. Strong; a steady recommendation at the table.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Peanut Butter Brownie": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness holds steady against the PB brownie's peanut-cocoa stack — the sweet character mirrors the dessert sugar, and the rounded sweetness meets the herb butter. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Porterhouse": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the porterhouse's dual strip-and-filet character -- aperitivo bitterness fights the headline cut's marbled char — bitter and savory pull apart. The plate deserves Odette Cabernet Sauvignon, Weller 12 Year, or Domaine du Grand Tinel, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Pour the Yellow Chartreuse on another course instead.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Prime Tartare": "Yellow Chartreuse's cask-strength honey-saffron sweetness reads alongside the tartare's raw beef and brine — the sweet character meets the savory plate at digestif register. Strong; a confident match across the table.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Rainbow Trout": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the trout's delicate flesh -- bitter herbs flatten delicate fish — the protein can't lift the amaro's register. The plate deserves Joseph Mellot Sancerre, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Domaine de Berthiers Pouilly-Fumé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; one of those is the move. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Roast Half Chicken": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the chicken's herbed crisp-skin roast -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, or Alexander Valley Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Yellow Chartreuse lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Roasted Red Pepper Chickpea": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the chickpea soup's roasted-pepper chickpea warmth -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Cristom Mt Jefferson Cuvée, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; reach for any of those instead. Save the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Salt-Cured Halibut": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the halibut's salt-cured flesh -- aperitivo bitterness fights gentle flesh without a bridge. The plate deserves Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, or Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Sauteed Garlic Spinach": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the garlic spinach's garlic-buttered greens -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Keenan Chardonnay, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Evening Land Seven Springs, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Yellow Chartreuse lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Seafood Tower": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the seafood tower's chilled shellfish lineup -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, Veuve Clicquot Brut, or Pierre Gimonnet Special Club Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; offer the alts at the table — they fit. Save the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Seared Scallops": "Seared Scallops: scallops meet Yellow Chartreuse. Avoid; honey-herbal liqueur wrong for scallops. Reach for a light rum, vodka, or gin.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Seasonal Soup": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the seasonal soup's house seasonal build -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; route the table to a better-fit pour. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Seasonal Vegetables": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the seasonal vegetables' seasonal garden plate -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves St Supéry Sauvignon Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Shrimp Bisque": "Yellow Chartreuse's gentian-driven cut sits alongside the shrimp bisque's creamy shellfish soup: the herbal cut works the shrimp bisque, and the amaro backbone frames the bowl. Strong; consistent recommendation worth pouring.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Shrimp Cocktail": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge overwhelms the shrimp cocktail's cool brine-and-citrus shellfish -- bitter aperitivo flattens the opener's lighter register. The plate deserves Bacardi Rum, Grey Goose Vodka, or Laurent Perrier Le Cuvée Brut, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Swordfish": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the swordfish's meaty swordfish steak -- bitter botanicals fight the savory sear — bitter against char without resolution. The plate deserves Far Niente Chardonnay, Schloss Vollrads Riesling, or Keenan Chardonnay, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the better calls are above. Yellow Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|The Tomahawk": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the Tomahawk's theatrical bone-in marbling and smoky char -- bitter aperitivo belongs before or after the meal, not alongside the steak. The plate deserves Corazon Stagg, Opus One, or George T. Stagg, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; pick from the alternatives. Reserve the Yellow Chartreuse for another course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Tomato Basil": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge jars against the tomato basil's bright tomato-basil acid -- bitter botanicals on savory broth pull against the salt-and-spice frame without resolving. The plate deserves Stoneleigh Sauvignon Blanc, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; steer the table to the alternatives. Hold the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Truffle Fries": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the truffle fries' parmesan-and-truffle fry weight -- bitter aperitivo on the supporting side reads as wrong register for the course. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Belvedere Vodka, or Maker's Mark, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Yellow Chartreuse lands on the steak course — not here.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Tuxedo-Crusted Yellowfin Tuna": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the tuna's seared-rare flesh with sesame crust -- bitter aperitivo is for before or after the meal — not on a main protein. The plate deserves Pommery Cuvée Louise, Jean-Pierre Grossot Chablis, or Elk Cove Pinot Blanc, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the alts above are the call. Save the Yellow Chartreuse for the steak course.",
  "Yellow Chartreuse|Vegetable Curry with Chickpeas": "Yellow Chartreuse's bitter-herb aperitivo edge clashes with the curry's spiced chickpea-curry warmth -- bitter aperitivo crowds the soup-salad course without complement. The plate deserves Schloss Vollrads Riesling, Lingua Franca Avni Pinot Noir, or Le Garenne Rosé, not a bitter aperitivo. Avoid; the listed alts pair where this does not. Yellow Chartreuse belongs on the steak course.",
};

function getPairingNote(itemName, pairingName) {
  return PAIRING_NOTES[itemName + '|' + pairingName] || null;
}

if (typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports) {
  module.exports = { PAIRING_NOTES, getPairingNote };
}
